window.CONCEPTS = [{"id":"8508c727-cef4-4fe3-a99a-64b7319d4e79","l":"0-dimensional systems","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A 0-dimensional system is one that has effectively no spatial extent or spatial degrees of freedom, so all dynamics occur in internal or discrete variables rather than via motion through space. Examples include ideal point particles and fully confined quantum dots, which exhibit discrete energy levels and no spatial dispersion."},{"id":"347c621b-2ddf-4d11-bd25-3380b5623c72","l":"1-dimensional systems","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"One-dimensional systems are physical systems in which particles, excitations, or fields are constrained to move or vary along a single spatial coordinate, so all dynamics and interactions depend only on one spatial dimension. Such systems show enhanced quantum and thermal fluctuations and often display qualitatively different collective behavior from higher-dimensional systems (for example, Luttinger-liquid behavior, absence of conventional long-range order, and strong correlation effects)."},{"id":"7a86b9d8-3162-4279-9ca5-90ebad07be7c","l":"2-dimensional systems","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"2-dimensional systems are physical systems whose motion and interactions are confined to a plane so that only two spatial coordinates are relevant. This geometric restriction\u2014found in examples like graphene, thin films, and 2D electron gases\u2014qualitatively changes thermodynamic and quantum behavior, e.g., enhanced fluctuations, altered phase transitions, and distinct topological phenomena."},{"id":"81194f70-bbca-4041-b57e-29338e588cac","l":"3-dimensional systems","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A 3-dimensional system is a physical system whose positions, fields, and dynamics require three independent spatial coordinates (x, y, z) to specify, giving it three spatial degrees of freedom. Physical quantities in such systems are expressed as scalars, vectors, or tensors defined across three orthogonal directions and evolve according to laws formulated in three-dimensional space."},{"id":"5d582b16-6b34-4e39-b32c-21bd28c9ea7b","l":"Accelerator applications","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Accelerator applications are the practical uses of particle accelerators and their particle or photon beams to perform tasks in science, medicine, industry, and security rather than to study accelerator physics itself. Typical applications include medical therapies and isotope production (e.g., proton therapy, PET isotopes), materials and structural analysis (synchrotron light, neutron scattering), semiconductor lithography and materials processing, radiation sterilization, and security scanning."},{"id":"d0bb99e3-9d07-40c6-80a0-d8858b63da18","l":"Accelerators & storage rings","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Accelerators are machines that use electric and magnetic fields to accelerate and manipulate beams of charged particles to high energies; storage rings are circular accelerator structures that confine and maintain those beams for extended periods using bending magnets, focusing optics, and RF cavities. They enable particle collisions, sustained synchrotron radiation for materials and biological studies, and various medical and industrial applications."},{"id":"4c286df9-b565-4295-b951-49a36d60cad1","l":"Accelerator subsystems","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Accelerator subsystems are the functional groups of equipment and infrastructure that together produce, accelerate, steer, focus, monitor, and protect charged-particle beams in an accelerator facility. Typical subsystems include the injector, RF cavities and power, magnets and magnet power supplies, vacuum and cryogenics, beam diagnostics and feedback, controls and timing, cooling and power distribution, and radiation-safety and shielding systems."},{"id":"40a5bd01-6544-4502-8321-458c33878bf3","l":"Acoustics","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Acoustics is the branch of physics that studies the generation, propagation, interaction, and detection of mechanical waves\u2014especially sound\u2014in gases, liquids, and solids. It encompasses vibration, wave phenomena (reflection, refraction, diffraction, absorption), noise and signal analysis, and auditory perception and applications."},{"id":"07127809-80a6-4cfd-823e-9b331d828173","l":"Acoustic tweezers","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Acoustic tweezers are devices that use shaped sound fields (typically ultrasonic) to generate contactless forces that trap, move, sort, or assemble particles, cells, or droplets. The manipulation arises mainly from acoustic radiation forces and acoustically induced streaming in micro- to millimeter-scale systems, enabling label-free, noninvasive control."},{"id":"709975fc-dae0-44e7-87ab-9ddbf1181412","l":"Advanced accelerator test facilities","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Advanced accelerator test facilities are specialized laboratories that develop, prototype, and evaluate novel particle-acceleration technologies, components, and beam-physics concepts\u2014such as high\u2011gradient RF, plasma and dielectric wakefield acceleration, superconducting systems, and advanced diagnostics. They provide flexible beamlines, lasers and measurement infrastructure to study beam dynamics, stability, materials limits and system integration for future accelerators and their scientific, industrial, and medical applications."},{"id":"a4ca1a7e-639b-4ff5-8cf3-44b60866fb3e","l":"Aerodynamics","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Aerodynamics is the branch of fluid dynamics that studies the behavior of air (and other gases) in motion and how it interacts with solid bodies, determining forces such as lift and drag. It examines flow phenomena\u2014boundary layers, separation, turbulence and compressibility\u2014to predict performance of aircraft, vehicles, and structures."},{"id":"bd680139-bd95-4bfd-a00f-a63e257b9f74","l":"Aging","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Aging is the slow, history-dependent evolution of a nonequilibrium system\u2019s physical properties following its preparation or a rapid change (quench), during which the system progressively relaxes toward equilibrium. It is characterized by broken time-translation invariance\u2014observables and responses depend on both the waiting time since preparation and the observation time\u2014and commonly shows slow relaxation, memory, and dynamical scaling (as in glasses, polymers, and spin systems)."},{"id":"c765ac18-ded5-4ef0-a41a-22be13741559","l":"Applications of soft matter","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Applications of soft matter are the technological and biomedical uses of materials\u2014polymers, colloids, gels, foams, liquid crystals and biomacromolecules\u2014whose mechanical and transport behavior is governed by mesoscale structure and thermal fluctuations. These unique, easily deformable properties are exploited in adhesives and coatings, foods and cosmetics, drug delivery and tissue engineering, soft robotics and flexible electronics, and in self\u2011assembling functional materials."},{"id":"e9c9f6ef-d22d-49af-9833-d58fd54ce0d6","l":"Assessment","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics Education Research"],"def":"Assessment is the systematic process of measuring and evaluating knowledge, skills, performance, or the validity and effectiveness of experiments, models, or instructional methods in physics. It uses defined criteria and instruments (tests, rubrics, diagnostics, measurements) and analysis (including statistics and uncertainty quantification) to inform judgments and guide improvements."},{"id":"27862afe-0467-49b2-a792-5b376b520666","l":"Astronomical black holes","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Astronomical black holes are astrophysical compact objects predicted by general relativity whose gravity is so strong that a boundary called an event horizon forms, beyond which nothing \u2014 not even light \u2014 can escape, with a central region of extreme spacetime curvature. They form by gravitational collapse and grow by accretion and mergers, span stellar to supermassive masses, and are inferred observationally via accretion emission, jets, and gravitational waves."},{"id":"008773e5-bf44-4113-9af5-a7b0a8885ea8","l":"Astronomical masses & mass distributions","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Astronomical masses & mass distributions refers to the amounts of mass in celestial objects and the ways that mass is spatially and statistically apportioned across scales\u2014from planets and stars to galaxies and clusters\u2014characterized by individual masses, mass functions, radial density profiles, and halo models. These distributions determine gravitational dynamics and structure formation and are inferred from observables such as orbital motions, stellar and gas kinematics, gravitational lensing, and emitted radiation."},{"id":"6f6385bb-6b61-4f76-8334-2e26f7644c87","l":"Astrophysical electromagnetic fields","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Astrophysical electromagnetic fields are the electric and magnetic fields produced by charges and currents in astrophysical plasmas and objects (stars, planets, accretion disks, jets, and the interstellar/intergalactic medium) that operate across scales from magnetospheres to galactic and cosmological volumes. They shape dynamics and energy transport through Lorentz forces and radiation, mediating processes such as magnetohydrodynamic flows, reconnection, plasma instabilities, and particle acceleration."},{"id":"6cebc2dd-7a74-488a-9ac5-f6b569e93f07","l":"Astrophysical fluid dynamics","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Astrophysical fluid dynamics is the study of the macroscopic motion and behavior of gases and plasmas in astrophysical systems, described by the equations of hydrodynamics and magnetohydrodynamics together with gravity, radiation, rotation and relativistic effects. It treats processes across scales\u2014from stellar interiors, winds and accretion disks to the interstellar and intergalactic media\u2014governing shocks, turbulence, instabilities and magnetic dynamos."},{"id":"12e1040c-7c2c-478a-b466-c979b57fcaea","l":"Atomic & molecular collisions","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Atomic and molecular collisions are encounters between atoms or molecules in which their mutual forces alter trajectories, internal energy, or chemical identity. Such encounters \u2014 elastic or inelastic (excitation, ionization) and reactive (chemical or charge transfer) \u2014 are described by scattering theory through cross sections and rate coefficients and are governed by quantum-mechanical interactions on potential energy surfaces."},{"id":"ba7ccb8e-d7eb-4e6f-9c1e-b622f5b4e242","l":"Atomic & molecular processes in external fields","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"The study of how applied external fields (electric, magnetic, and time-dependent electromagnetic/optical fields) alter the structure and dynamics of atoms and molecules, modifying their energy levels, transition rates, scattering, ionization and reaction pathways.  It encompasses effects such as Stark and Zeeman shifts, field-induced alignment/orientation, multiphoton and strong-field ionization, high-harmonic generation, and coherent control of quantum states."},{"id":"30e65d01-6cd5-442b-889f-ddc0c3c28157","l":"Atomic & molecular structure","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Atomic & molecular structure is the description of how nuclei and electrons are arranged and occupy quantum-mechanical energy levels in atoms and molecules, including their bonding and spatial geometry; these arrangements determine chemical reactivity, spectra, and many physical properties."},{"id":"ff402d94-d1d8-4207-a321-f935f060b175","l":"Atomic Properties","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Atomic properties are the measurable or calculable physical and chemical characteristics of individual atoms that determine their behavior and interactions. They arise from the nucleus and electron configuration and include quantities such as energy levels and spectral lines, atomic radius, ionization energy, electron affinity, electronegativity, magnetic moment, and polarizability."},{"id":"bd252d88-a608-4897-9416-daf84dee7f6b","l":"Atomic systems","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Atomic systems are physical systems made of nuclei and their bound electrons whose structure and dynamics are governed mainly by Coulomb interactions and quantum mechanics. They range from single atoms and ions to small assemblies and are characterized by discrete electronic energy levels, atomic spectra, and interactions with external fields and other particles."},{"id":"3c3951f1-c856-43f4-9cd6-d058b6261a9d","l":"Auxetic materials","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Auxetic materials are solids with a negative Poisson's ratio: when stretched they expand laterally and when compressed they contract laterally, opposite to ordinary materials. This counterintuitive behavior typically arises from engineered internal geometries (e.g., re-entrant cells, rotating units, or chiral motifs) rather than the chemistry of the constituent material."},{"id":"8ca06aeb-be9b-453e-8323-1dae4ffb0991","l":"Ballistic deposition","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Ballistic deposition is a stochastic surface-growth model in which particles travel along straight (ballistic) trajectories\u2014typically normal to a substrate\u2014and irreversibly stick at the first point of contact with the surface. It models thin-film growth and sedimentation, producing rough, columnar surfaces with shadowing effects and characteristic scaling behavior (e.g., KPZ universality)."},{"id":"cd322dee-17c3-4f48-81b4-5fbca9f2c432","l":"Beam dynamics","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Beam dynamics is the study of the motion, focusing, and stability of charged-particle beams as they travel through accelerators and transport lines, describing how external electromagnetic fields and machine geometry steer and confine particles. It covers single-particle optics (betatron and synchrotron motion), phase-space evolution and emittance, and collective effects (space charge, wakefields, synchrotron radiation) that determine beam size, lifetime, and quality."},{"id":"b3310113-551e-4c5e-9263-28f393ff09f0","l":"Beam techniques","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Beam techniques are experimental methods for generating, shaping, steering, and diagnosing beams of particles or radiation (electrons, ions, photons, neutrons, etc.) used to probe, image, or modify materials and to perform measurements in accelerators and laboratory setups. They encompass source design, beam optics and transport, timing/intensity control, and diagnostics such as profile, energy and emittance measurements."},{"id":"af1f8b12-9181-4c18-a750-d9e9fe621217","l":"Bifurcations","d":0,"p":"15ac2674-419f-4fe1-b73b-99c33fe6b8df","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Nonlinear Dynamics"],"def":"Bifurcations are qualitative changes in the behavior or phase-space structure of a dynamical system that occur when a control parameter crosses a critical value, typically producing or destroying fixed points or periodic orbits or altering their stability. Common examples include saddle-node, transcritical, pitchfork, Hopf, and period-doubling bifurcations."},{"id":"3e27454e-a016-4f0c-98fc-e7428f700f6e","l":"Binary fluids","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"A binary fluid is a mixture of two distinct fluid components whose local composition can vary in space and time. Its macroscopic behavior involves diffusion and advection of the two species and, when they are partially immiscible, phase separation and interfacial dynamics driven by surface tension."},{"id":"21804fac-0546-45ed-98d5-b6af0f0d3228","l":"Biological fluid dynamics","d":0,"p":"b26070f8-32ca-4e02-94d0-ba1961b3480d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Biological fluid dynamics is the study of fluid flows in and around living organisms and of how those flows couple to and drive biological structures and processes. It covers phenomena from microscale locomotion and transport in low\u2011Reynolds\u2011number, often non\u2011Newtonian environments to macroscale blood and airway flows, active suspensions, and fluid\u2013structure interactions across multiple scales."},{"id":"e7ff71e0-89cf-438a-8e28-d449f5629207","l":"Biological materials","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Biological materials are substances produced by living organisms\u2014ranging from proteins, lipids and polysaccharides to cells, tissues and biominerals\u2014characterized by hierarchical, heterogeneous and often composite microstructures. Their physical behavior is governed by multiscale organization and active, out-of-equilibrium processes, giving rise to distinctive mechanical, transport, optical and adaptive properties (self-assembly, remodeling, healing)."},{"id":"d87d9c51-bdc2-43bb-99c3-6e5345a7f631","l":"Biological networks","d":0,"p":"b4d51608-61b8-4e73-8186-ba02e5a41a85","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Biological networks are graph-based representations of interacting biological components\u2014such as genes, proteins, neurons, or species\u2014where nodes denote entities and edges denote biochemical, physical, or ecological interactions. Their structure and dynamics are studied with tools from network theory, statistical physics, and dynamical systems to understand information flow, robustness, and emergent, system-level behaviors."},{"id":"8b374c75-8448-476e-a872-f15b0cf5a5fe","l":"Biological neural networks","d":0,"p":"d87d9c51-bdc2-43bb-99c3-6e5345a7f631","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Biological neural networks are assemblies of neurons linked by synapses in living organisms that process and transmit information via electrochemical signals. Their collective activity exhibits nonlinear, stochastic dynamics and adapts through synaptic plasticity, enabling learning and emergent computation."},{"id":"5be420fe-48f0-4430-a63d-9d611d837d6e","l":"Biomolecular & subcellular processes","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Biomolecular and subcellular processes are the physical, chemical, and mechanical activities of biomolecules (proteins, nucleic acids, lipids, carbohydrates) and organelles that underlie cellular functions such as metabolism, signaling, transport, assembly, and motility. They are governed by thermodynamics and kinetics and often operate far from equilibrium, involving stochastic fluctuations, conformational dynamics, self\u2011assembly, enzymatic catalysis, and active force generation by molecular motors."},{"id":"5f0d4d9a-0a6d-4a84-befa-8e2ee579e060","l":"Bipartite graphs","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"A bipartite graph is a graph whose vertices can be partitioned into two disjoint sets such that every edge connects a vertex in one set to a vertex in the other. Equivalently, it contains no odd-length cycles and is often used to model pairwise relations between two distinct classes of objects."},{"id":"0a74688b-2eee-4349-a9bd-e93851fe2c5a","l":"Body & organ systems","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Body and organ systems are organized assemblies of cells, tissues, and organs that work together to perform coordinated physiological functions (e.g., circulation, respiration, digestion). In physics, they are treated as multiscale systems governed by mechanics, fluid and mass transport, energy conversion, and feedback/control processes that maintain homeostasis."},{"id":"f6dd23e2-5253-4255-9d6c-db58471a2cdc","l":"Boolean networks","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Boolean networks are discrete dynamical systems of nodes that take binary values (0 or 1) and update their states according to fixed Boolean functions of a specified set of input nodes. They serve as minimal models of complex regulatory systems (e.g., gene or neural networks), exhibiting state-space structure with attractors and order\u2013chaos transitions that depend on topology and update rules."},{"id":"0c2f74cc-42b0-4b3a-b470-cd50aa7d0f89","l":"Boundary layers","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Boundary layers are thin regions in a fluid next to a solid surface where viscous forces dominate and the flow velocity changes rapidly from the surface value (often zero) to the free-stream value, producing large velocity gradients and shear. Their structure\u2014laminar or turbulent\u2014controls skin friction, heat and mass transfer, and phenomena such as flow separation."},{"id":"15ddc38a-7602-4107-8a76-2d51b4893f07","l":"Brownian ratchet","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"A Brownian ratchet (or Feynman ratchet) is a device or model that uses an asymmetric potential or mechanical pawl to try to rectify random Brownian (thermal) motion into directed motion or useful work. It cannot produce net work at thermal equilibrium because of the second law of thermodynamics; sustained rectification requires nonequilibrium conditions (e.g., temperature differences or time-dependent driving)."},{"id":"2bc9b8a7-9fd8-4787-8660-cdabebc20cbb","l":"Buoyancy","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Buoyancy is the upward force a fluid exerts on an object immersed in it, caused by the pressure increasing with depth so the fluid pushes more strongly on the bottom than the top. By Archimedes' principle its magnitude equals the weight of the fluid displaced by the object."},{"id":"884ba888-9ed7-43f7-ae3d-360ffe6d2df0","l":"Cavitation","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Cavitation is the formation of vapor-filled cavities (bubbles) in a liquid when the local pressure drops below the liquid\u2019s vapor pressure, followed by their rapid collapse when pressure recovers. The collapse produces intense local shock waves, noise, and can cause pitting, erosion, and performance loss in devices such as propellers, pumps, and ultrasonic transducers."},{"id":"b26070f8-32ca-4e02-94d0-ba1961b3480d","l":"Cellular organization, physiology & dynamics","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Cellular organization, physiology & dynamics is the study of how a cell\u2019s structural components (membranes, cytoskeleton, organelles, macromolecular assemblies) are arranged and coordinated to perform life-sustaining functions, and how those structures change in time. It focuses on the physical mechanisms\u2014mechanical forces, biochemical reactions, transport, signaling, stochastic fluctuations and active, far-from-equilibrium processes\u2014that govern cellular behavior, adaptation and self-organization."},{"id":"bb2351de-d2a6-4d4c-a33e-9396bc991c32","l":"Chaos","d":0,"p":"15ac2674-419f-4fe1-b73b-99c33fe6b8df","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Nonlinear Dynamics"],"def":"Chaos: the aperiodic, effectively unpredictable behavior of certain deterministic nonlinear dynamical systems, characterized by extreme sensitivity to initial conditions \u2014 nearby trajectories diverge exponentially (positive Lyapunov exponent). Such systems often display complex, fractal phase-space structures (e.g., strange attractors) despite being governed by simple equations."},{"id":"4455b073-c3a2-4a0b-923e-98d7ec0f3b9d","l":"Chemical Physics & Physical Chemistry","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Chemical physics and physical chemistry are closely related disciplines that apply the laws and methods of physics to understand chemical systems and processes. Chemical physics emphasizes physical principles and techniques (e.g., spectroscopy, quantum mechanics, and statistical mechanics) to study molecular structure and dynamics, while physical chemistry focuses on applying those principles to explain thermodynamics, kinetics, and macroscopic properties of matter."},{"id":"4cea3ccf-5ca5-4998-a997-0f054a738162","l":"Chirality","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Chirality is the geometric property of an object or system that makes it distinguishable from its mirror image: a chiral object cannot be superposed onto its mirror image by rotations and translations, giving rise to \"handedness\" (left vs. right). In physics the term also denotes the intrinsic handedness of fermions under parity/Lorentz transformations \u2014 for massless particles chirality coincides with helicity, while for massive particles they differ."},{"id":"026384c2-5a28-4069-b909-e6872fec566d","l":"Classical fluids","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Classical fluids are macroscopic liquids and gases treated as continuous media whose flow and thermodynamic behavior are described by classical mechanics and statistical physics. Their dynamics follow conservation laws (mass, momentum, energy) plus constitutive relations such as the Navier\u2013Stokes equations and are characterized by properties like density, pressure, viscosity and thermal conductivity, excluding quantum-dominated fluids (e.g., superfluids)."},{"id":"b43baaca-e8a9-4440-a55f-9f2a3459e510","l":"Classical mechanics","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nonlinear Dynamics"],"def":"Classical mechanics is the branch of physics that describes the motion of macroscopic bodies and the forces acting on them, using principles such as Newton's laws and equivalent Lagrangian and Hamiltonian formalisms. It provides deterministic predictions of trajectories and conserved quantities when quantum and relativistic effects are negligible."},{"id":"f7ea083c-ea2d-4e8c-907b-0f625c3776d8","l":"Classical spin models","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Classical spin models are statistical-mechanical lattice models in which magnetic moments (\"spins\") at lattice sites are treated as classical variables\u2014either discrete (e.g., Ising \u00b11) or continuous unit vectors (e.g., XY, Heisenberg)\u2014that interact via specified coupling terms in a Hamiltonian. They are used to study collective phenomena such as magnetization, phase transitions, critical behavior, and domain formation driven by thermal fluctuations."},{"id":"465c2601-2756-41b3-8518-ca6f24f11411","l":"Classical statistical mechanics","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Classical statistical mechanics is the framework that derives macroscopic thermodynamic and transport properties of many-particle systems by averaging over probability distributions on the system's phase space, assuming the particles follow classical (Newtonian or Hamiltonian) dynamics. It uses ensembles (microcanonical, canonical, grand canonical), Liouville's theorem and kinetic equations (e.g., Boltzmann equation) and neglects quantum effects."},{"id":"46e2a993-8dce-4c28-beab-fdabe4378664","l":"Cold atoms & matter waves","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Cold atoms are neutral atoms cooled to microkelvin\u2013nanokelvin temperatures so that their thermal de Broglie wavelength becomes comparable to interparticle spacing, producing strong quantum coherence and enabling regimes such as Bose\u2013Einstein condensation and degenerate Fermi gases. Matter waves denote the wave-like quantum behavior of particles (characterized by the de Broglie wavelength), which in cold-atom systems gives rise to coherent interference, atom optics, and precision interferometry."},{"id":"da8795cf-dd0b-446f-bab1-986dcb2703c6","l":"Collective behavior","d":0,"p":"bb9d205a-1327-40a1-a9f3-91d677ea5de3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Collective behavior is the emergence of coherent macroscopic patterns or dynamics in a system of many interacting units, arising from their interactions rather than the properties of individual components. Examples include phase transitions, spontaneous symmetry breaking, synchronization, flocking and pattern formation, and such phenomena are often described by coarse-grained order parameters and universal laws."},{"id":"5b214695-f1bb-4a5b-a178-f1c242e9546e","l":"Collective behavior in networks","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Collective behavior in networks is the emergence of coordinated, large-scale dynamics or patterns from interactions among many interconnected units (nodes), where the whole exhibits properties not present in individual elements. Examples include synchronization, cascades, consensus, and phase-transition\u2013like changes, whose form and stability depend jointly on the node dynamics, coupling rules, and network topology."},{"id":"5e87cd7f-17e3-40b7-98ce-29c38a8b217f","l":"Collective effects in atomic physics","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Collective effects in atomic physics are phenomena that arise when many atoms interact coherently via the common electromagnetic field or through interatomic (e.g., dipole\u2013dipole) couplings, so that the ensemble's optical and dynamical response cannot be described as the sum of independent single-atom behaviors. Examples include superradiance and subradiance, cooperative frequency shifts, and altered scattering/absorption in dense or correlated atomic ensembles."},{"id":"ef0a1bee-2a1f-4f47-aca2-6302db18e67f","l":"Colloids","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Colloids are heterogeneous mixtures in which particles of one substance (the dispersed phase), typically in the range ~1 nm\u20131 \u03bcm, are finely distributed throughout another continuous medium. The particles are small enough to remain suspended by Brownian motion yet large enough to scatter light (Tyndall effect) and show surface- and interface-dominated behaviors (e.g., electrostatic stabilization, capillarity)."},{"id":"52399a41-c27b-49c2-96c3-120524795d0b","l":"Complex fluids","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Complex fluids are fluids whose macroscopic flow and transport behavior is controlled by internal microscopic or mesoscopic structure (e.g., polymers, colloids, emulsions, foams, surfactant solutions) rather than only by molecular viscosity. That microstructure produces non-Newtonian responses\u2014viscoelasticity, shear thinning or thickening, yield stresses, and slow structural relaxation\u2014over a range of length and time scales."},{"id":"8252f005-8412-4e8e-a2c6-0f6ff6c1b7fa","l":"Complex media","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Complex media are materials whose macroscopic behavior is governed by heterogeneous, often hierarchical microstructure and many-body interactions, leading to emergent, nontrivial responses such as nonlinearity, dispersion, disorder-induced localization, memory effects, and anomalous transport. Examples include glasses, granular media, colloidal suspensions, biological tissues, composites, and engineered metamaterials."},{"id":"d55c7570-5cb1-4f1a-b729-2647e666ffe8","l":"Complex systems","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Complex systems are assemblies of many interacting parts whose collective dynamics produce emergent behaviors and structures that cannot be straightforwardly predicted from the properties of individual components. They typically exhibit nonlinear interactions, multiscale organization, robustness, self-organization, and phenomena such as phase transitions, criticality, and adaptation."},{"id":"c2ed1d79-919b-4642-8549-2394977dd60c","l":"Composite materials","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Composite materials are engineered solids made from two or more physically distinct constituent phases (commonly a continuous matrix and a dispersed reinforcement) whose combined structure yields mechanical, thermal, electrical or other properties different from, and often superior to, those of the individual components. Their macroscopic behavior depends on the properties, volume fractions and microstructure (arrangement and interfaces) of the constituents."},{"id":"26766f08-226c-4d5c-8c0e-796e6d7b7bba","l":"Composition of astronomical objects","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Composition of astronomical objects is the elemental, isotopic and molecular makeup plus the physical phases (gas, plasma, liquid, solid, dust) and relative abundances that constitute stars, planets, comets, asteroids and the interstellar/intergalactic media. It is determined by spectroscopy, sample and in\u2011situ measurements and fundamentally controls an object's opacity, energy generation, chemistry and evolutionary behavior."},{"id":"7a4e6b3d-d685-4a65-a562-4720bd830099","l":"Compressible flows","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Compressible flows are fluid motions in which variations in density are significant and must be included in the governing equations, typically occurring when flow speeds approach or exceed about Mach 0.3. They require conservation of mass, momentum and energy together with an equation of state, and can produce phenomena such as sound waves, expansion fans and shock waves."},{"id":"1e0c099a-9f2a-4e47-b3f1-7faa28b6a506","l":"Computational Techniques","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Techniques"],"dl":[],"def":"Computational techniques are numerical algorithms, simulation methods, and software tools used to model, simulate, and analyze physical systems when analytic solutions are unavailable. They include discretization and spectral methods, Monte Carlo sampling, numerical linear algebra, optimization and high-performance computing to obtain approximate solutions and quantitative predictions from models and data."},{"id":"45348f65-04ba-45a1-8d14-bbc7a025737a","l":"Concepts & principles","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics Education Research"],"def":"Concepts & principles are the fundamental ideas and general rules\u2014such as conservation laws, symmetries, idealizations, and governing assumptions\u2014that capture the essential structure of physical systems. They provide the explanatory frameworks used to build models, derive equations, and predict or interpret experimental phenomena."},{"id":"f033dcc0-0f19-42c3-b48f-bfe11a85a24b","l":"Confinement","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Confinement is the phenomenon whereby particles carrying a non\u2011observable charge (most notably quarks and gluons with color charge in QCD) cannot be isolated as free asymptotic states but remain permanently bound into color\u2011neutral composite particles. More generally, \"confinement\" denotes the restriction of a particle's motion or degrees of freedom to a finite region by a potential, boundary conditions, or strong interactions."},{"id":"233556f2-7015-49bd-ad5c-867dda4753e1","l":"Conformation & topology","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Conformation: the instantaneous spatial arrangement of a molecule or flexible object determined by bond lengths, angles and rotations (e.g., dihedral angles, end-to-end distance), which can change continuously through thermal motion without breaking bonds.  \nTopology: the global connectivity or knotting properties (knot type, linking number, genus) that are invariant under continuous deformations that do not cut or pass strands through one another, and which constrain possible conformations of polymers and macromolecules."},{"id":"fe033bd7-dfe8-4d73-93c8-3acd8685eac2","l":"Continuum particle models","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Continuum particle models are theoretical and computational frameworks that represent localized, particle-like objects as smooth, finite-width features of continuum fields (e.g., density, order parameter, displacement), allowing one to treat discrete entities within continuum mechanics or field theory. Examples include solitons and topological defects in field theories, vortices and droplets in fluids, and particle-based continuum methods such as smoothed-particle hydrodynamics."},{"id":"2d276ce6-c803-4ba3-813c-b989f2d4bfde","l":"Convection","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Convection is the transfer of heat (and accompanying mass) in a fluid by bulk fluid motion, occurring either from buoyancy-driven flows caused by temperature-dependent density differences (natural convection) or from externally imposed flows such as pumps or winds (forced convection)."},{"id":"678197d1-8bf1-4c16-86cc-8133fba03b86","l":"Cosmic rays & astroparticles","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Cosmic rays are high\u2011energy particles\u2014primarily protons and atomic nuclei, with some electrons and secondary photons and neutrinos\u2014accelerated in astrophysical sources (the Sun, supernovae, active galactic nuclei, etc.) that arrive at Earth and produce cascades of secondary particles when they interact with the atmosphere. Astroparticles refers to the ensemble of particles of astrophysical origin (cosmic rays, gamma rays, neutrinos, and candidate dark\u2011matter particles) and the field that studies their production, propagation, detection, and role in probing high\u2011energy astrophysical processes and fundamental physics."},{"id":"26ed7afc-8635-4637-a339-8d0a7cb75be1","l":"Cosmology","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Cosmology is the branch of physics and astronomy that studies the Universe as a whole \u2014 its origin, large-scale structure, composition, dynamics, and ultimate fate \u2014 using observations and theoretical frameworks (notably general relativity and particle physics) to model phenomena such as the Big Bang, cosmic expansion, dark matter, and dark energy."},{"id":"11f78b53-5856-41bb-a6f2-78a7da00bcbf","l":"Coupled oscillators","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Nonlinear Dynamics"],"def":"Coupled oscillators are two or more oscillating systems that interact so energy can be transferred between them, causing their motion to be interdependent. The coupling alters the natural frequencies and produces collective normal modes (eigenmodes), giving rise to phenomena such as mode splitting, beats, and synchronization."},{"id":"7853f3c2-c02d-4fc9-aae8-1eb75aaf35b8","l":"Cryogenics & vacuum technology","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Cryogenics is the science and engineering of producing, controlling, and using very low temperatures (typically below about 120 K) to reduce thermal motion and access unique material or quantum behaviors. Vacuum technology is the creation, measurement, and maintenance of low\u2011pressure environments (from rough to ultra\u2011high vacuum) and the pumps, seals, gauges and materials used; the two fields commonly overlap in cryostats, cryopumping and systems requiring both thermal isolation and low collision rates."},{"id":"1128a9f3-70c1-409b-84b2-44063aa12108","l":"Crystalline systems","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Crystalline systems are materials whose constituent particles (atoms, ions, or molecules) are arranged in a periodic, long-range-ordered lattice characterized by a repeating unit cell and discrete translational and rotational symmetries. This structural periodicity governs their mechanical, optical and electronic properties and supports phenomena such as phonons, electronic band structures, and characteristic defects."},{"id":"e521b9c7-1f62-48e4-a3fc-9428f629d078","l":"Crystal phenomena","d":0,"p":"15f520a3-5c6c-4d9e-8975-81d399140562","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Crystal phenomena are the physical behaviors and properties that arise from a material\u2019s long-range periodic arrangement of atoms or molecules in a crystal lattice. They include diffraction and wave scattering, electronic band structures and Bloch states, lattice vibrations (phonons), anisotropic mechanical and transport responses, defect- and surface-mediated effects, and symmetry-driven phase transitions such as ferroelectricity or superconductivity."},{"id":"7123b794-372f-434f-a158-302812f5c4f6","l":"Deterministic networks","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Deterministic networks are network models whose topology and/or node dynamics evolve according to fixed, non\u2011random rules, so the entire future state of the network is uniquely determined by its initial conditions. They contrast with stochastic networks and are used to study predictable or chaotic behavior in systems such as deterministic graph-growth algorithms, Boolean networks, and coupled-map lattices."},{"id":"fe72c849-ad3f-4968-bc46-177716503f13","l":"Directed networks","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Directed networks are graphs in which each link has an orientation, so connections are ordered pairs from a source node to a target node rather than symmetric ties. This directionality (and the resulting asymmetric adjacency matrix and distinct in-/out-degrees) produces nonreciprocal flows, directed cycles, and connectivity structures that strongly affect transport, spreading, and dynamical behavior."},{"id":"373a7206-3c07-47bb-9078-efd80ed70556","l":"Disordered systems","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Disordered systems are materials or model systems whose microscopic structure or interaction parameters vary randomly or lack translational symmetry, so spatial homogeneity is absent. This randomness alters equilibrium and dynamical behavior\u2014giving rise to phenomena such as Anderson localization, glassy slow dynamics, modified transport, and unconventional phase transitions\u2014and includes examples like amorphous solids, alloys, and spin glasses."},{"id":"6be88c0c-f628-412d-9fd4-8ab448a5ecb6","l":"Distances, redshifts, & velocities","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"In cosmology, distances are defined by different measures (proper, comoving, luminosity, angular-diameter) to account for an expanding spacetime, and redshift z is the fractional increase in a photon's wavelength caused by relative motion or cosmic expansion, related to the scale factor by 1+z = a0/ae. At low z the redshift approximates a Doppler shift v \u2248 cz and the dominant recession is Hubble flow v_H \u2248 H0\u00b7d, while peculiar velocities are local deviations from that expansion."},{"id":"560043c3-4ee2-48af-9d6c-08819ef45fff","l":"Diversity & inclusion","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics Education Research"],"def":"Diversity & inclusion: the intentional cultivation of representation, access, and a sense of belonging for people of varied identities, perspectives, and backgrounds within the physics community, implemented through policies and practices that remove structural barriers to participation and advancement. These efforts improve scientific creativity, rigor, and fairness by broadening talent, viewpoints, and collaborative opportunities."},{"id":"b72a2d57-f47e-4e87-9a43-f8320d817bae","l":"Drop & bubble phenomena","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Drop & bubble phenomena are the formation, dynamics, and interactions of liquid droplets and gas bubbles in fluids, controlled by interfacial tension together with buoyancy, viscosity, inertia, and wetting. They encompass processes such as nucleation, rise/fall, deformation and oscillation, breakup and coalescence, and surface\u2011tension\u2013driven (Marangoni) flows that appear in natural and engineering systems."},{"id":"98cac094-ec9c-4f37-adf7-eed968f89359","l":"Drops & bubbles","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Drops are finite volumes of liquid surrounded by another fluid, and bubbles are pockets of gas enclosed by a liquid; both are separated from their surroundings by a deformable interface. Their shapes and dynamics (motion, oscillation, coalescence, breakup, evaporation) are governed primarily by surface tension and interfacial curvature (Laplace pressure), together with gravity, inertia, viscosity, wetting, and Marangoni (surface-tension-gradient) effects."},{"id":"c485f44d-2cc4-4e6d-a20b-0899fb9e53a1","l":"Dynamical systems","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Nonlinear Dynamics"],"def":"A dynamical system is a mathematical description of how the state of a physical system changes in time, usually given by differential or difference equations that map points in a phase space to their time derivatives or future states. Such systems can be continuous or discrete, deterministic or stochastic, and are used to analyze stability, long-term behavior, attractors, and chaotic dynamics."},{"id":"27c445f4-a41d-4b4c-881f-aa74fc493d6d","l":"Dynamics of networks","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nonlinear Dynamics"],"def":"Dynamics of networks studies the time-dependent behavior of processes that occur on networked systems and how those processes are shaped by \u2014 and can in turn reshape \u2014 the network's topology. It includes phenomena such as diffusion, spreading, synchronization, flows and cascading failures, and uses tools from statistical physics and nonlinear dynamics to characterize macroscopic outcomes like phase transitions, stability, and resilience."},{"id":"d5c377ae-7fd9-40cc-af51-fc16be6d879a","l":"Educational policy","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics Education Research"],"def":"Educational policy comprises the laws, regulations, guidelines, and administrative decisions that determine how education is organized, funded, staffed, and assessed\u2014covering curricula, standards, teacher qualifications, resource allocation, and accountability. In the physics context, educational policy shapes what physics is taught, how it is taught and assessed, and how resources and training for physics teachers and researchers are provided."},{"id":"41dd58dd-d35c-46e8-9594-d7e05b458da6","l":"Electrical properties","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Electrical properties are the characteristics of materials and systems that determine how they respond to electric fields and currents\u2014including conductivity, resistivity, permittivity, dielectric constant, charge mobility, and work function. They describe how charge is transported, distributed, polarized, stored, and dissipated under applied voltages."},{"id":"e4ee958b-eaac-46c0-8788-7d521b0f9dbd","l":"Electric field effects","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Electric field effects are the physical influences exerted by an electric field E on charges and materials, producing forces F = qE on point charges and altering their potential energy and motion. They include electrostatic attraction or repulsion, dielectric polarization, charge redistribution in conductors, induced currents, and energy storage in capacitors."},{"id":"17d6ffbe-b5db-4c92-b602-93b78f871d79","l":"Electrokinetic flows","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Electrokinetic flows are fluid motions produced when electric fields act on mobile ions in the electrical double layer at a charged solid\u2013liquid interface, creating a slip velocity that drives bulk fluid (electroosmosis) or moves charged particles (electrophoresis). The term also covers reciprocal effects such as streaming currents and streaming potentials, where pressure-driven flow convects charge in channels or porous media."},{"id":"be790dcd-2f91-4896-8328-00953746f53a","l":"Electromagnetic field calculations","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Electromagnetic field calculations are the analytical and numerical methods used to determine the spatial and temporal distributions of electric and magnetic fields produced by charges, currents, and materials, subject to Maxwell's equations and boundary/initial conditions. They range from closed-form laws (Coulomb, Biot\u2013Savart, Green's functions) to computational techniques (finite-element, finite-difference time-domain, integral-equation methods) for radiation, waveguide, and scattering problems."},{"id":"893ecc24-c678-4687-bd3a-0b4a1bcb4cfc","l":"Electromagnetic radiation astronomy","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Electromagnetic radiation astronomy is the study of astronomical objects and processes by detecting and analyzing the electromagnetic radiation they emit, absorb, or scatter across the spectrum from radio to gamma rays. Measurements of intensity, spectrum, polarization, spatial distribution, and time variability are used to infer physical properties such as composition, temperature, motion, magnetic fields, and distances."},{"id":"9e019803-f9d8-4802-b1ac-d2c035d9508f","l":"Electronic structure","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Electronic structure is the set of quantum states and corresponding energies that electrons can occupy in an atom, molecule, or solid, together with their occupation; it controls the system's electrical, optical, magnetic and chemical properties. It is determined by (approximately) solving the many-electron Schr\u00f6dinger equation or using effective theories such as band theory and density functional theory."},{"id":"ee487445-39e4-4995-9543-fea1735bcbb2","l":"Electroweak interaction","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"The electroweak interaction is the unified quantum field theory of the electromagnetic and weak nuclear forces, described by an SU(2)L \u00d7 U(1)Y gauge symmetry whose spontaneous breaking via the Higgs mechanism gives mass to the W\u00b1 and Z0 bosons while leaving the photon massless. It governs electromagnetic processes and flavor-changing weak processes (e.g., beta decay) and features parity-violating weak currents."},{"id":"803dacbd-5e2d-43eb-933b-9493ef3d5a24","l":"Emulsions","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Emulsions are dispersions of one immiscible liquid as microscopic droplets within another (e.g., oil-in-water or water-in-oil). They are thermodynamically unstable but can be kinetically stabilized by surfactants or particles that lower interfacial tension and inhibit droplet coalescence, giving characteristic optical and rheological behavior."},{"id":"af9f5a05-6917-40cf-a2ff-5520ff506935","l":"Energy sources","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Energy sources are physical reservoirs or ongoing processes that supply usable energy by converting stored forms (chemical, nuclear, gravitational, thermal) or ambient flows (solar, wind, hydro) into work, heat, or electrical energy. They are characterized by their energy content, power output (rate of delivery), and the efficiency and pathways by which that energy is converted."},{"id":"80fe725c-ae9b-4701-a9f2-db29c094a8ea","l":"Energy storage","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Energy storage is the capture of energy produced at one time or place and its retention in a physical, chemical, thermal, or electrical form for controlled release when needed. Common mechanisms include chemical batteries and fuels, mechanical systems (flywheels, pumped hydro), thermal reservoirs, capacitors, and gravitational storage."},{"id":"39ac0edc-a645-42f8-bbf9-04e80cb0460a","l":"Energy utilization","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Energy utilization is the process by which available energy sources are converted, transported, stored, and consumed to produce useful work or services. It is characterized by the ratio of useful output to supplied energy and is limited by thermodynamic inefficiencies and losses (e.g., waste heat and irreversibility)."},{"id":"b008f863-15c1-4914-98e1-27735c8991bb","l":"Engineering","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Engineering is the application of physics, mathematics, and materials science to design, construct, and optimize devices, systems, and processes that perform specified functions while satisfying constraints such as cost, safety, reliability, and manufacturability."},{"id":"118dcea0-ad7e-42d7-b32c-2c9f38256be6","l":"Environmental research","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Environmental research is the interdisciplinary scientific study of Earth's physical, chemical, and biological systems and their responses to natural variability and human activities, using observation, experiment, theory, and modeling to quantify processes and impacts. It aims to understand and predict phenomena such as climate change, pollution, resource flows, and ecosystem dynamics to inform mitigation and management."},{"id":"c9842cda-c46d-4212-827a-640f14883d5a","l":"Epistemology, attitudes, & beliefs","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics Education Research"],"def":"Epistemology, attitudes, & beliefs refers to learners' views about the nature and justification of physics knowledge (what counts as knowing, how certainty and evidence are judged), together with their affective dispositions and convictions (interest, motivation, self\u2011efficacy) about physics; these cognitive and affective stances shape how students approach, interpret, and apply physics concepts."},{"id":"55c6e92f-4705-4854-9517-43fb71e31015","l":"Equations of state","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Equations of state are relations among macroscopic state variables (such as pressure, temperature, volume or density, and sometimes internal energy) that characterize a material's thermodynamic equilibrium response. They can be empirical or derived from microscopic theory (e.g., the ideal-gas law, van der Waals equation, or more complex models for solids and dense nuclear matter) and are used to close thermodynamic or fluid-dynamical problems."},{"id":"7123f9db-cc4b-4132-8fef-2ded0d50be87","l":"Evolving networks","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Evolving networks are networks whose nodes, edges, or link/vertex attributes change in time due to processes such as growth, deletion, rewiring, or adaptive coupling between node states and connections. They are modeled to capture how these temporal and coevolutionary changes alter structural properties and dynamical processes (e.g., spreading, synchronization) in complex systems."},{"id":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","l":"Experimental Techniques","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Techniques"],"dl":[],"def":"Experimental techniques are the methods, instruments, and procedures used to design, perform, and measure physical experiments so that hypotheses can be tested and physical quantities extracted. They include apparatus design and control, sample preparation, calibration and metrology, data acquisition and signal processing, and error analysis to ensure accurate, reproducible results."},{"id":"70beb0c8-6828-4903-a460-d25abd7212aa","l":"Exponential networks","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Exponential networks are networks of trajectories on a complex curve determined by the phase of an exponential differential; they generalize spectral networks by allowing multivalued exponential data instead of meromorphic differentials. They are used to encode and compute BPS/soliton spectra, wall-crossing, and aspects of mirror symmetry for certain supersymmetric and Calabi\u2013Yau systems."},{"id":"dd3a6865-1735-4fc2-912e-a015609d910c","l":"Faculty","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Professional Topics"],"dl":["Physics Education Research"],"def":"Faculty: the academic staff of a university or research institution\u2014professors, lecturers, researchers and similar appointments\u2014responsible for teaching, conducting research, mentoring students, and performing institutional service; the term can also denote an administrative division (e.g., Faculty of Science) that groups related departments."},{"id":"fd960e5c-b282-401a-8c54-4998af6cee06","l":"Fibers","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Fibers are slender, high\u2013aspect-ratio filaments (natural or synthetic) whose length greatly exceeds their diameter, giving them distinctive mechanical, thermal, electrical or optical properties. They serve as structural reinforcements in composites and as functional elements such as electrical conductors or waveguides (e.g., optical fibers), with behavior set by composition, cross\u2011section, and microstructure."},{"id":"b7f686b6-3282-4e50-85e4-f19b2db66d82","l":"Flow control","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Flow control is the deliberate modification of a fluid flow field\u2014using passive geometry or active forcing and feedback\u2014to achieve desired outcomes such as reduced drag, delayed separation, enhanced mixing, controlled heat transfer, or noise suppression. Techniques range from fixed devices (vortex generators, surface roughness) to active inputs (blowing/suction, synthetic jets, plasma actuators, closed-loop control) that alter boundary layers, shear layers, and vortex dynamics."},{"id":"d2fd5563-5077-41b4-9d06-f0da03883073","l":"Flow instability","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Flow instability is the tendency of a fluid flow state (steady, laminar, or simple time\u2011dependent) to amplify small perturbations so that the flow evolves into a qualitatively different pattern. Such instabilities\u2014driven by mechanisms like shear, buoyancy, or centrifugal forces and studied with linear and nonlinear stability theory\u2014often produce vortices, waves, or transition to turbulence."},{"id":"cc73b723-a501-45c2-a121-f8c9757c5573","l":"Flows in porous media","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Flows in porous media are the motion of fluids through the interconnected voids of a solid matrix, characterized macroscopically by porosity and permeability and driven by pressure gradients and body forces. At the continuum scale they are commonly described by Darcy\u2019s law (with extensions for inertial, multiphase, or reactive transport), while pore-scale behavior follows the Navier\u2013Stokes equations and includes capillarity and interfacial effects."},{"id":"c6d9db47-7b2d-4d3b-868a-606b65695e3c","l":"Fluctuations & noise","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Fluctuations are spontaneous, often stochastic, temporal or spatial deviations of a physical quantity from its mean value caused by thermal, quantum, or dynamical processes. Noise denotes the portion of those random fluctuations that interferes with signals or measurements and is described statistically (variance, correlation functions, power spectral density) and, in equilibrium systems, related to dissipation by the fluctuation\u2013dissipation theorem."},{"id":"cf6b1eea-2e6d-498c-afd5-ebc77fd47d64","l":"Foams","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Foams are disordered, multi-scale soft materials made of gas bubbles separated by thin liquid or solid films, where surface tension and interfacial forces control the geometry and stability of the bubble network. They show out-of-equilibrium dynamics (drainage, coarsening by gas diffusion, and bubble rearrangements) and a rheology intermediate between viscous liquids and elastic solids."},{"id":"da24955e-c4af-4c5f-81b5-3d0a92703f84","l":"Formation & evolution of stars & galaxies","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"The formation and evolution of stars and galaxies is the study of how primordial density fluctuations and interstellar gas collapse, cool, and fragment under gravity, hydrodynamics, and radiation to form stars and assemble into galaxies within dark-matter halos. It also encompasses the subsequent changes\u2014stellar evolution and feedback, gas accretion and cooling, mergers, and chemical enrichment\u2014that reshape their structure and populations over cosmic time."},{"id":"97615b28-ff3a-41d1-98a5-6d7c64e3300b","l":"Fractional dimension","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Fractional dimension is a non-integer measure of a set's or system's effective dimensionality that quantifies how geometric or physical quantities (like mass, length, or number of covering boxes) scale with resolution. It characterizes fractal-like complexity and anomalous scaling behaviors that lie between the familiar integer dimensions (e.g., Hausdorff or box-counting dimensions)."},{"id":"a2f3cef9-614c-4f7a-8f5b-009885b53abb","l":"Galaxies","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Galaxies are gravitationally bound systems of stars, stellar remnants, gas, dust and dark matter\u2014typically containing millions to trillions of stars and often hosting a central supermassive black hole\u2014whose visible shapes range from spirals to ellipticals and irregulars. They are the primary building blocks of cosmic large-scale structure, formed and evolved through gravitational collapse, mergers, and interactions in the expanding universe."},{"id":"4c9828a9-11a8-4826-9d87-a34a3280b7f4","l":"Galaxy clusters","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Galaxy clusters are the largest gravitationally bound structures in the Universe, containing hundreds to thousands of galaxies embedded in a hot, X-ray\u2013emitting intracluster plasma and a dominant dark matter halo, with typical masses ~10^14\u201310^15 solar masses and sizes of a few megaparsecs. They form by hierarchical merging of smaller structures and occupy the nodes of the cosmic web."},{"id":"556e52f5-6040-4273-b1a4-5e864b903fa5","l":"Gauge bosons","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Gauge bosons are spin-1 particles that arise as the quanta of gauge fields associated with local symmetries in quantum field theories and serve as the force carriers by coupling to conserved charges. Examples include the massless photon and gluons and the massive W\u00b1 and Z bosons (whose masses result from electroweak symmetry breaking)."},{"id":"06891d57-1044-487c-ae20-21366d161a97","l":"Gels","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Gels are soft materials in which a percolating network of polymers or particles traps and immobilizes a large fraction of a liquid, producing a material that is solid-like (elastic) yet mostly solvent. Their mechanical response reflects the elastic network together with viscous flow of the trapped fluid, and networks can be formed by permanent (chemical) or reversible (physical) bonds."},{"id":"9990b97d-b485-4ad7-ac0a-33ab94e0e794","l":"Genomes","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"A genome is the complete set of hereditary information in an organism, encoded in its DNA (or RNA for some viruses) and comprising all genes plus noncoding sequences that together specify its structure, function, and replication."},{"id":"d003cb63-dde8-403d-84dd-985e04b1fb25","l":"Geophysical fluid dynamics","d":0,"p":"29ab1ea0-dd7e-46b7-87dd-0ca29c6be062","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Geophysical fluid dynamics is the study of fluid motion in planetary environments\u2014particularly the atmosphere, oceans, and planetary interiors\u2014where rotation, stratification, buoyancy, and topography strongly shape flow. It focuses on the dynamics of large-scale circulation, waves, instabilities, and turbulence driven by Coriolis forces, density variations, and boundary interactions."},{"id":"29ab1ea0-dd7e-46b7-87dd-0ca29c6be062","l":"Geophysics","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Geophysics is the application of physical principles and quantitative methods to study the Earth's internal structure, surface and near-surface processes, and the interactions among the solid Earth, oceans, atmosphere, and cryosphere. It encompasses fields such as seismology, gravimetry, geomagnetism, heat-flow and mantle dynamics, geodesy, and remote sensing to observe and model Earth's physical behavior."},{"id":"f7719ae3-8715-4b68-a9bc-df0e56f8033c","l":"Glassy systems","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Glassy systems are amorphous materials that lack long-range crystalline order yet behave as rigid solids; when cooled or compressed they fall out of equilibrium and exhibit very slow, non\u2011exponential relaxation (glassy dynamics). Their behavior is governed by a rugged energy landscape with many metastable minima, producing history dependence, aging, and broad distributions of relaxation times."},{"id":"2f065729-56f9-49ab-b8e6-f2daa86321bd","l":"Global Positioning System","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics","Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"The Global Positioning System (GPS) is a satellite-based radio navigation system that determines a user\u2019s position, velocity, and time by measuring signal travel times from multiple satellites with known orbits and applying trilateration. It depends on precise onboard atomic clocks and requires relativistic corrections (special and general) to maintain meter-scale accuracy."},{"id":"0af55013-5004-4528-bd2d-a27fb2d1359c","l":"Globular clusters","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Globular clusters are roughly spherical, gravitationally bound assemblies of tens of thousands to millions of stars that orbit in the halos of galaxies. They are typically very old, low-metallicity stellar populations whose dense, dynamically evolved cores make them useful probes of stellar evolution and galactic formation."},{"id":"17fc7dd5-c7c1-4cdb-ac6d-0165d5a1d343","l":"Graduate students","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Professional Topics"],"dl":["Physics Education Research"],"def":"Graduate students are individuals enrolled in postgraduate programs (master\u2019s or doctoral) in physics or related fields who pursue advanced coursework, pass qualifying examinations, and conduct original research under faculty supervision. They often serve as teaching or research assistants while working toward professional credentials and research competence."},{"id":"8da11568-256e-4eba-ac10-f750c272b3ca","l":"Granular flows","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Granular flows are the collective motion of large numbers of macroscopic particles (grains) driven by gravity, shear, or external forcing, in which interparticle contact forces, friction, and inelastic collisions govern the dynamics. They display solid-, liquid-, and gas-like behaviors (e.g., jamming, avalanches, shear bands, segregation) and non-Newtonian rheology depending on density and forcing."},{"id":"e8836b5d-a348-41e4-9d9e-30e950115672","l":"Granular materials","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Granular materials are assemblies of macroscopic particles (e.g., sand, grains, powders) whose behavior is governed primarily by interparticle contact forces, friction, and inelastic collisions rather than thermal motion. They can behave like solids, liquids, or gases and exhibit rich non-equilibrium phenomena such as jamming, flow, segregation, and force-chain networks."},{"id":"756e1ce2-8f47-4e38-ab1a-17a68b30f843","l":"Granular packing","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Granular packing is the spatial arrangement of macroscopic particles (grains) in a confined volume, described by the packing (volume) fraction and the contact network that govern force transmission and mechanical stability. Packings range from ordered crystalline lattices to disordered \"random close\" packings, and their geometry and coordination number control macroscopic behavior such as rigidity, jamming, and flow."},{"id":"124f9214-0176-475c-b706-99df707e8411","l":"Gravitation","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Gravitation is the universal attractive interaction by which masses (and more generally energy\u2013momentum) cause bodies to accelerate toward one another.  In Newtonian physics it appears as an inverse\u2011square force proportional to the product of masses, while in general relativity it is described as the curvature of spacetime produced by energy\u2013momentum."},{"id":"39cd8c29-4ed1-4f17-9a75-defb541f7cab","l":"Growth processes","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Growth processes are the physical mechanisms and models that describe how structures\u2014such as surfaces, interfaces, clusters, crystals, or biological tissues\u2014increase in size or advance over time, typically far from equilibrium. They are characterized by kinetics, fluctuations, instabilities and scaling laws, and are studied with continuum equations (e.g., diffusion, KPZ) and discrete stochastic models (e.g., Eden, diffusion-limited aggregation)."},{"id":"ca7e6e75-307c-4709-ad9e-9bb1f153fede","l":"Hadrons","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Hadrons are composite particles made of quarks bound together by the strong force, with the binding mediated by gluons as described by quantum chromodynamics (QCD). They come in two main classes\u2014baryons (three quarks, e.g., protons and neutrons) and mesons (a quark\u2013antiquark pair)."},{"id":"1384cf6f-6b2c-4bc6-ab8f-60f200af3eed","l":"Hamiltonian systems","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Nonlinear Dynamics"],"def":"A Hamiltonian system is a dynamical system on phase space defined by a scalar Hamiltonian function H(q,p,t) whose evolution is given by Hamilton\u2019s equations dq/dt = \u2202H/\u2202p, dp/dt = \u2212\u2202H/\u2202q; it preserves the symplectic form (phase-space volume) and, if H is time-independent, conserves energy."},{"id":"9099c760-673a-40a9-a10f-410acf85f0ee","l":"Heat engines","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"A heat engine is a device that operates in a thermodynamic cycle to convert heat taken from a high-temperature reservoir into mechanical work while rejecting some heat to a lower-temperature reservoir. Its performance is governed by the laws of thermodynamics, with an upper efficiency limit given by the Carnot efficiency determined by the two reservoir temperatures."},{"id":"4e212348-481b-40ed-a4c8-b7cf31405640","l":"Higgs bosons","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Higgs bosons are the quantum excitations (spin-0 scalar particles) of the Higgs field, the field whose nonzero vacuum expectation value breaks electroweak symmetry in the Standard Model. This mechanism gives mass to the W and Z gauge bosons and, via Yukawa couplings, to fermions; the Standard Model Higgs was observed at the LHC in 2012 with a mass of about 125 GeV."},{"id":"0a7309c9-e0e7-473b-ad96-b83da6960810","l":"High-energy-density plasmas","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"High-energy-density plasmas are plasmas whose internal energy density (or equivalently pressure) exceeds roughly 10^11 J/m^3 (\u22481 megabar), so that radiation, strong coupling, extreme ionization, and often relativistic or collective many-body effects play a dominant role. They occur in inertial-confinement-fusion and pulsed-power experiments and in energetic astrophysical environments such as stellar cores and supernovae."},{"id":"e429096f-dcb1-45db-8ccb-80767dfde58b","l":"History of physics","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"The history of physics is the study of how physical concepts, theories, experiments, instruments, and methods have developed over time. It examines major discoveries, shifts in scientific paradigms, and the roles of individuals, institutions, and cultural and technological contexts in shaping the discipline."},{"id":"fd7cfd2b-a957-4dcd-b32d-b9d00e6aba33","l":"Homogeneous networks","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"A homogeneous network is a graph whose nodes and/or links are identical in their properties or connectivity statistics so that the structure is invariant under node permutation (e.g., regular lattices or networks with narrow degree distributions). This uniformity yields spatially uniform dynamical behavior and simplifies analytical treatments, in contrast to heterogeneous networks with broad degree or weight distributions."},{"id":"670457a5-ade7-47cf-b508-6ff87f3d30e0","l":"Hydrostatic stellar nucleosynthesis","d":0,"p":"38c3eaa1-e9a7-4590-9a93-cbdfd9fe162f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics","Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Hydrostatic stellar nucleosynthesis is the production of new nuclei by nuclear fusion and slow neutron-capture reactions that occur in a star's cores and shells while the star is in hydrostatic equilibrium (pressure balancing gravity). These quiescent burning stages (e.g., hydrogen, helium, carbon, neon, oxygen, silicon burning and the s-process) build elements up to the iron group and supply the energy that supports the star against collapse, in contrast to explosive nucleosynthesis in supernovae."},{"id":"cdaf7295-546e-4fba-85c5-9d6435b40253","l":"Hypothetical particle physics models","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Theoretical frameworks that extend or modify the Standard Model by postulating new particles, forces, or symmetries to explain unexplained phenomena (e.g., dark matter, neutrino masses, or the hierarchy problem). They provide mathematically consistent interactions and parameters that yield testable predictions for experiments and observations."},{"id":"3981a374-dbc8-42de-bdba-73ea858650c6","l":"Hypothetical particles","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Hypothetical particles are elementary or composite entities postulated by theoretical models but not yet confirmed by experiment. They are proposed to explain unexplained observations or to address theoretical problems (examples include axions, WIMPs, magnetic monopoles, and tachyons) and are characterized by predicted properties that experiments seek to detect or constrain."},{"id":"fd7300dc-49e9-458d-9121-de94f7446fdb","l":"Information & communication theory","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Information and communication theory is the study of how information is quantified, encoded, transmitted, and recovered reliably in the presence of noise, encompassing measures such as Shannon entropy and limits like channel capacity as well as practical source and channel coding methods. It includes classical and quantum formulations and connects to statistical mechanics, computation, and the thermodynamics of information."},{"id":"88e20708-8615-4f4b-a25b-314a280250b6","l":"Instructional materials development","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics Education Research"],"def":"Instructional materials development is the systematic process of designing, producing, and evaluating teaching and assessment resources\u2014such as lectures, laboratory activities, simulations, problem sets, and multimedia\u2014so they align with learning objectives, curriculum standards, and evidence-based pedagogical strategies. It typically involves iterative testing and revision using learner feedback and assessment data to improve clarity, engagement, accessibility, and learning outcomes."},{"id":"51b7feeb-da35-4a0b-b0bf-ff6ba0aa1cc7","l":"Instructional strategies","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics Education Research"],"def":"Instructional strategies are the deliberate methods and approaches instructors use to design, deliver, and assess learning activities that promote students' conceptual understanding and problem\u2011solving skills in physics. Examples include lecturing, active learning and peer instruction, inquiry- and problem\u2011based learning, and formative-assessment techniques."},{"id":"cc59c6fb-acbb-4237-953b-b874fe5dbd4a","l":"Interactions & forces","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Interactions are the physical processes by which bodies or fields influence one another, typically transmitted by fields or the exchange of particles; forces are the vector quantities that describe the local effect of an interaction on a body's motion, causing acceleration (classically via F = ma). The four known fundamental interactions are gravity, electromagnetism, the strong force, and the weak force."},{"id":"52c158bb-fb1e-4d1b-a117-b6b279c3bc67","l":"Interactions in fluids","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Interactions in fluids are the forces and momentum/energy exchanges among fluid elements and between fluids and immersed bodies that determine flow, transport, and structure. They include pressure and viscous (shear) stresses, surface tension and interfacial forces, long-range hydrodynamic coupling through the flow field, and molecular-scale attractions/repulsions (e.g., van der Waals, electrostatic) that set viscosity, diffusion, and phase behavior."},{"id":"a47b235a-072e-4d30-94cc-43b2451d6bcd","l":"Interconnected & interdependent networks","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Interconnected and interdependent networks are systems of two or more network layers that are linked by interlayer connections so that nodes or links in one layer can directly affect those in another; interdependence specifically means a node's state or function in one network depends on nodes in a different network. Such coupling alters collective behavior and robustness, enabling phenomena like cascading failures, modified percolation thresholds, and cross-layer synchronization or spreading."},{"id":"f5372742-fa5e-4bda-96ba-87e45d98bae6","l":"Interfacial flows","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Interfacial flows are fluid motions that occur at or are dominated by the boundary between two phases (e.g., liquid\u2013gas or liquid\u2013liquid), where surface tension, capillarity, and interfacial stresses set the dominant dynamics. They include free-surface, thin-film, multiphase and contact-line phenomena driven by pressure and viscous forces together with surface-tension and Marangoni (surface-tension-gradient) effects."},{"id":"ab0f7862-2ef6-42c8-84a2-c663fb498e19","l":"Irreversible processes","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Irreversible processes are thermodynamic changes that cannot be undone so as to restore both the system and its surroundings to their original states; they involve net entropy production. Examples include heat flow from hot to cold, frictional dissipation, viscosity, and inelastic deformation, all of which convert organized energy into unrecoverable thermal disorder."},{"id":"7f0a64a2-c123-4a5e-8b50-e3df97beb4ff","l":"K-12 students","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Professional Topics"],"dl":["Physics Education Research"],"def":"K\u201312 students are learners enrolled in kindergarten through 12th grade (roughly ages 5\u201318), covering elementary, middle, and high school pupils who receive pre\u2011college science and physics instruction. In physics contexts the term denotes the pre\u2011college population targeted by curriculum design, pedagogy research, and outreach."},{"id":"f10caf23-9d24-4d75-8f23-cac63aa14b67","l":"K-12 teachers","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Professional Topics"],"dl":["Physics Education Research"],"def":"K\u201312 teachers are educators who provide formal instruction to students from kindergarten through twelfth grade. In the physics context they design and deliver age\u2011appropriate science and physics curricula, assess and support student learning, and use pedagogical strategies to develop foundational conceptual understanding and scientific inquiry skills."},{"id":"99934578-7c4c-4b59-9edd-cd7dc8988a58","l":"Kinetic theory","d":0,"p":"6f991c28-112d-4bdf-9c2c-50cec44f48fe","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Kinetic theory is the statistical framework that explains macroscopic properties of gases and fluids by modeling them as large ensembles of microscopic particles whose motions and collisions determine observable quantities. It connects pressure, temperature, and transport coefficients to particle velocity distributions (e.g., Maxwell\u2013Boltzmann) and dynamical equations such as the Boltzmann equation."},{"id":"1d80a56f-19ce-4fd1-b088-20fed49f32c4","l":"Laboratory plasma","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"A laboratory plasma is an ionized gas produced and confined under controlled experimental conditions for study or application, in which free electrons and ions exhibit collective behavior and strong coupling to electric and magnetic fields. Laboratory plasmas span a wide range of temperatures and densities\u2014from low-temperature glow discharges and plasma processing reactors to high-temperature fusion devices and laser-produced plasmas."},{"id":"26f5f3dd-19a2-4ea0-8d21-f9c30a8898d5","l":"Laboratory studies of space & astrophysical plasmas","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Laboratory studies of space and astrophysical plasmas are controlled experiments that recreate and scale key plasma processes found in environments like the solar corona, planetary magnetospheres, jets, accretion disks, and shocks so they can be measured and tested under reproducible conditions. Using devices such as plasma toruses, pulsed-power machines, and high-power lasers together with diagnostics and similarity scaling (e.g., matching dimensionless numbers like plasma beta or Lundquist number), these experiments probe phenomena such as magnetic reconnection, turbulence, waves, and particle acceleration to validate theory and complement observations and simulations."},{"id":"0ade2a4b-c4b3-40d0-9e34-83344578c4f2","l":"Laminar flows","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Laminar flows are fluid motions in which fluid moves in smooth, orderly layers or streamlines with little cross\u2011stream mixing, so the velocity at each point is steady and predictable. They typically occur at low Reynolds numbers where viscous forces dominate inertial forces (for example, Poiseuille flow in a pipe)."},{"id":"8bf49057-d087-4218-b1ad-0678d26c2751","l":"Lattice models in statistical physics","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Lattice models in statistical physics are simplified theoretical models in which microscopic degrees of freedom (e.g., spins, particle occupations, or heights) sit on the sites or links of a regular lattice and interact via specified local rules. They provide tractable frameworks\u2014examples include the Ising, Potts and lattice-gas models\u2014for studying collective phenomena such as phase transitions, critical behavior, and universality."},{"id":"d3d5f00e-7f9b-4fd8-ac3a-9caba1c0c589","l":"Learning environment","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics Education Research"],"def":"A learning environment is the physical, virtual, social and instructional setting in which students engage with physics content\u2014e.g., classrooms, laboratories, online platforms, resources, activities, and assessment. It organizes interactions, supports or constrains inquiry and problem solving, and thereby shapes students' engagement, skill development, and conceptual understanding."},{"id":"a15f9e32-c63a-4c5a-b73a-cd30286d4b6f","l":"Learning theory","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics Education Research"],"def":"Learning theory is the mathematical study of how algorithms infer patterns or predictive models from data, quantifying their generalization, sample complexity, and convergence using probability, statistics, optimization, and information\u2011theoretic tools. It includes frameworks such as PAC learning and VC dimension that provide formal performance guarantees and bounds on error rates and required training data."},{"id":"a45e2665-2df6-42e0-99dc-30bd93812c2c","l":"Leptons","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Leptons are a family of fundamental spin-1/2 particles in the Standard Model that do not feel the strong interaction; they come in three generations, each consisting of a charged lepton (electron, muon, tau) and its associated neutrino. Charged leptons interact electromagnetically and via the weak force, while neutrinos interact only weakly (and gravitationally)."},{"id":"63f7a3cf-31b7-4d5c-bbcc-58d679dd5139","l":"Liquid crystals","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Liquid crystals are materials whose constituent anisotropic molecules maintain long-range orientational order like a crystal while still flowing like a liquid, producing intermediate \"mesophases.\" Different mesophases (e.g., nematic, smectic, cholesteric) vary in degrees of positional and orientational order and yield anisotropic optical and electro-optical properties used in devices such as displays."},{"id":"e944fbb4-e706-4095-9a43-5b3a586a6b25","l":"Lower undergraduate students","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Professional Topics"],"dl":["Physics Education Research"],"def":"Lower undergraduate students are those in the first one to two years of a bachelor's program, typically taking introductory physics courses. They usually have high-school physics and basic calculus/algebra and are building foundational understanding of mechanics, waves, thermodynamics, and introductory electromagnetism."},{"id":"6aed0168-d867-4733-835f-da82de3c65f5","l":"Low Reynolds number flows","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Low Reynolds number flows occur when the Reynolds number Re = \u03c1UL/\u03bc is \u226a 1, so viscous forces dominate inertial forces; the fluid motion is laminar, typically time-reversible and governed by the linear Stokes equations (creeping flow). They arise for very small length scales or slow velocities, as in microfluidics, lubrication layers, and microorganism locomotion."},{"id":"e3dc4b55-d412-4f8d-bf41-19db11969e69","l":"Macromolecules","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Macromolecules are very large molecules composed of many covalently linked repeating units (monomers) or long biological chains (e.g., proteins, DNA). Their extended size and connectivity give rise to distinctive physical behaviors\u2014chain conformations, entropic elasticity, viscoelasticity, entanglements and collective phase behavior\u2014central to polymer and soft-matter physics."},{"id":"508e580d-f2db-4627-8d3a-663a299ccf2f","l":"Magnetic fluids","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Magnetic fluids are liquids whose mechanical and magnetic behavior is strongly controlled by applied magnetic fields, typically realized as colloidal suspensions of nanoscale ferromagnetic or ferrimagnetic particles dispersed in a carrier liquid and stabilized by surfactants. They become magnetized, form field-aligned microstructures (e.g., spikes) and show field-dependent properties such as viscosity and surface tension."},{"id":"58071c68-730c-4a3e-982c-abc9f0cedf67","l":"Magnetic systems","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Magnetic systems are physical systems whose behavior is dominated by magnetic fields and magnetic interactions between moving charges or localized magnetic moments, so that magnetization, magnetic susceptibility, and magnetic energy govern their properties. They include materials with collective spin order (ferromagnets, antiferromagnets, paramagnets, diamagnets), current-carrying circuits and coils, and coupled-spin models studied with Maxwell's equations and spin Hamiltonians."},{"id":"b35238d0-83c7-4412-b293-c7d42fa92c62","l":"Magnetism","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Magnetism is the set of phenomena in which moving electric charges and intrinsic magnetic moments (e.g., electron spin) produce magnetic fields that exert forces and torques on other moving charges and magnetic dipoles. In materials these interactions give rise to behaviors such as ferromagnetism, paramagnetism, and diamagnetism."},{"id":"c25108e7-4697-4565-b484-221b808d454e","l":"Magnetohydrodynamics","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Plasma Physics"],"def":"Magnetohydrodynamics (MHD) is the theory of the dynamics of electrically conducting fluids\u2014such as plasmas, liquid metals, and electrolytes\u2014coupling fluid motion to electromagnetic fields by combining the Navier\u2013Stokes and Maxwell equations. It describes how flows advect and are driven by magnetic fields, giving rise to phenomena like Alfv\u00e9n waves, magnetic pressure/tension, field-line \"freezing\" in ideal MHD, and magnetic reconnection when non-ideal effects are important."},{"id":"be085c40-d877-4e85-9c63-504357581b86","l":"Mathematical physics","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Mathematical physics is the branch of physics that formulates, analyzes, and solves physical theories using rigorous mathematical methods. It aims both to provide precise foundations and proofs for physical concepts (e.g., quantum mechanics, statistical mechanics, field theory) and to motivate the development of new mathematics."},{"id":"9e001bda-d32b-4a59-a216-bfd4c1af8c25","l":"Mechanical & acoustical properties","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Mechanical and acoustical properties are the material characteristics that determine how a body responds to mechanical loads (deformation, stress, and energy dissipation) and how it transmits, reflects, absorbs, or attenuates sound waves. Typical mechanical properties include elastic modulus, strength, hardness, density and damping, while acoustical properties include sound speed, acoustic impedance, absorption coefficient and attenuation/scattering."},{"id":"b2c47d4b-a2d2-4f91-aa08-6029cd34a4a7","l":"Medical physics & public health","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"An interdisciplinary field that applies physical principles, measurement techniques, devices, and modeling (e.g., imaging, dosimetry, sensors, and simulation) to diagnose, treat, prevent disease, and optimize health-care delivery at the population level. It also encompasses assessment and mitigation of environmental and radiological risks, development and evaluation of screening and intervention technologies, and quantitative support for public\u2011health policy and epidemiology."},{"id":"4722b240-3d0b-4ab1-831a-75302ab4cf86","l":"Mesoscopics","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Mesoscopics is the study of systems whose size lies between atomic and macroscopic scales, where quantum coherence and phase-sensitive effects remain important while thermal averaging and disorder also play roles. It focuses on transport and fluctuation phenomena in nanostructures and devices (e.g., conductance quantization, universal conductance fluctuations, weak localization, Coulomb blockade) that depend on sample size and geometry."},{"id":"141061f5-f02c-492c-af56-81c3c49e8b65","l":"Metrology","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Metrology is the science of measurement, covering the development, realization and maintenance of measurement units and standards, the design and calibration of instruments, and the quantification of measurement uncertainty. It ensures measurements are accurate, comparable and traceable to agreed references (such as the SI) across time and space."},{"id":"63764576-5e03-40cf-9680-5796e144da49","l":"Microfluidic devices","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Microfluidic devices are engineered systems that manipulate and analyze very small fluid volumes (picoliter to microliter) in networks of channels and chambers with characteristic dimensions from micrometers to a few hundred micrometers. At these scales fluid behavior is dominated by viscous forces, surface tension, capillarity and diffusion (rather than inertia), enabling highly controlled laminar flows, mixing, and transport for applications like lab-on-a-chip analysis, single-cell assays, and microscale synthesis."},{"id":"edff6507-ef6e-4655-88ac-33fa3e31b6fb","l":"Microfluidics","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Microfluidics is the study and engineering of fluid behavior and manipulation in channels or structures with characteristic dimensions of micrometers, where flows are typically laminar and surface, viscous, and interfacial forces dominate over inertia. It enables precise handling of tiny volumes for applications like lab-on-a-chip analysis, chemical synthesis, and biological assays."},{"id":"a831f274-1508-4eca-a092-fdeb3a36e2ed","l":"Micromechanical & nanomechanical oscillators","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Micromechanical and nanomechanical oscillators are tiny mechanical resonators\u2014beams, membranes, cantilevers or suspended structures with dimensions in the micrometer to nanometer range\u2014that support discrete vibrational modes with characteristic resonance frequencies and quality factors. Their small mass and stiffness yield high frequencies and extreme sensitivity to forces, masses, and fields, making them useful for precision sensing, signal processing, and experiments in nano-/quantum optomechanics and NEMS."},{"id":"e2b8e2c2-4beb-41db-b473-1e2501888502","l":"Minerals","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Minerals are naturally occurring, inorganic solids with a definite (but sometimes variable) chemical composition and an ordered atomic (crystalline) structure. Their macroscopic physical properties\u2014density, elasticity, optical and electrical behavior, magnetism, and fracture/cleavage\u2014derive from atomic bonding, crystal structure, and defects, making them subjects of study in condensed-matter physics, materials science, and geophysics."},{"id":"817070d7-63da-46d5-bea2-fea4348a2c4a","l":"Multicomponent systems","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Multicomponent systems are physical systems composed of two or more distinguishable constituents (particle species, chemical components, phases, or coupled order-parameter fields) whose interactions and exchanges of mass, charge, momentum, or energy produce behavior not seen in single-component systems. Their dynamics and thermodynamics are described by coupled conservation laws and fields (e.g., concentrations and chemical potentials), leading to phenomena such as diffusion, phase separation, alloying, and collective modes."},{"id":"4a8be40b-d6a1-4a8c-8fbe-d74eebf86a21","l":"Multilayer & multiplex networks","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Multilayer networks are generalized graphs made of multiple layers, where each layer encodes a different type of relation, time slice, or context and nodes are linked by both intra-layer and inter-layer edges to capture cross-layer coupling. A multiplex network is a special case in which the same set of nodes appears in every layer and inter-layer links typically connect each node to its counterparts across layers, representing multiple relation types among the same entities."},{"id":"fcb85338-c881-4bd4-98a9-1563b1194606","l":"Multi-organism systems","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Multi-organism systems are assemblies of interacting living organisms whose collective dynamics and function emerge from exchanges of matter, energy, and information and from behavioral or biochemical interactions, producing nonequilibrium phenomena such as pattern formation, synchronization, and collective motion. Physics approaches\u2014e.g., active matter, statistical mechanics, and network theory\u2014are used to model and predict behaviors in examples like animal flocks, insect swarms, microbial communities, and biofilms."},{"id":"c577bd62-ea57-43b7-9607-1ef45dba3afb","l":"Multiphase flows","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Multiphase flows are fluid flows containing two or more distinct phases (gas, liquid, or solid particles) that interact through interfaces and exchange mass, momentum, and energy. They produce complex behaviors\u2014bubbles, droplets, slurries, or aerosols\u2014and often involve surface tension, phase change, and multi-scale coupling requiring specialized models and measurements."},{"id":"85d6e22c-4376-40a6-9451-9c66745c1955","l":"Multipole matrix elements","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Multipole matrix elements are the quantum-mechanical matrix elements \u27e8\u03c8_f|O_{LM}|\u03c8_i\u27e9 of electric or magnetic multipole operators O_{LM} (dipole, quadrupole, etc.) between initial and final states. They quantify the amplitude, selection rules, and angular/ parity dependence of transitions and interactions mediated by Lth-order multipole fields."},{"id":"42f217c5-a6d5-4143-ae9d-147042953c9e","l":"Nanofluidic devices","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Nanofluidic devices are engineered channels, pores or networks with characteristic dimensions of a few to a few hundred nanometers that control the flow and transport of liquids, ions, and nanoparticles. At these scales surface forces, electric double layers and molecular discreteness dominate transport\u2014leading to phenomena such as Debye-layer overlap, strong electrokinetic coupling, ion selectivity and altered slip\u2014which are exploited for sensing, separation, single\u2011molecule analysis and energy conversion."},{"id":"c7102703-2c55-4648-bf60-11f0f66a1256","l":"Nanofluidics","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Nanofluidics is the study of fluid flow, transport, and interfacial phenomena in confinements with characteristic dimensions at the nanometer scale (typically <100 nm). In this regime surface forces, molecular discreteness, electrostatic interactions and confinement effects dominate over bulk continuum behavior, requiring molecular or modified-continuum descriptions."},{"id":"a38f8c2b-eff3-4e4d-8146-e17ef6bde9dd","l":"Nanostructures","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Nanostructures are materials or objects with one or more dimensions in the nanometer range (roughly 1\u2013100 nm) where quantum confinement and a high surface-to-volume ratio produce size-dependent electronic, optical, mechanical, and chemical properties different from the bulk. Examples include nanoparticles, quantum dots, nanowires, nanotubes and thin films used to tailor transport, optical and catalytic behavior."},{"id":"c37d3be0-1372-4113-9b24-5bcc1216769b","l":"Naturalness","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Naturalness is the heuristic that dimensionless parameters and ratios in a fundamental theory should be of order unity unless their smallness or largeness is explained by a symmetry or dynamical mechanism. A theory is called unnatural when low-energy observables require precise cancellations (fine-tuning) of high-energy parameters, motivating new physics to stabilize those quantities."},{"id":"da80b6c6-baf7-49b0-922d-bf031c66389e","l":"Network evolution","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Network evolution is the time-dependent change of a network's topology and link weights driven by processes such as growth, rewiring, attachment rules, adaptation, or stochastic fluctuations, often coupled to dynamics on the nodes. It is studied to understand how microscopic update rules produce macroscopic structural, statistical, and functional properties."},{"id":"dbd74b4d-3b4a-4458-8abf-2847fba2fe6d","l":"Network formation & growth","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Network formation & growth is the study of the dynamical processes by which nodes and links are created, removed, and rewired, producing evolving topologies in systems from materials and biological tissues to social and technological networks. It examines mechanistic growth rules (e.g., random or preferential attachment, spatial embedding, aggregation) and their statistical consequences\u2014degree distributions, clustering, community structure, and percolation thresholds\u2014using tools from dynamical systems and statistical physics."},{"id":"31408bf9-9618-464c-ae16-cedacfc7a9eb","l":"Network optimization","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Network optimization is the study of finding the best allocation of resources, routing, or topology in a network to maximize performance (e.g., throughput, efficiency, or robustness) or minimize cost subject to constraints. It combines graph theory, optimization algorithms, and statistical-physics methods to model and solve problems of flow, congestion, and resilience in engineered and natural networks."},{"id":"6e3ddc0a-24fd-4682-bb1b-137944166a4a","l":"Network phase transitions","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Network phase transitions are abrupt qualitative changes in a network's global behavior that occur when a control parameter crosses a threshold \u2014 for example the sudden appearance of a giant connected component in percolation, the onset of global synchronization, or an epidemic outbreak. These transitions, which can be continuous or discontinuous, show critical phenomena and are analyzed with statistical\u2011physics concepts adapted to graph topology and interactions."},{"id":"b29359c1-cac2-4880-a9dc-f957b13a3e72","l":"Network searches","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Network searches are processes and algorithms for locating targets, nodes, or information on graphs, studied as dynamical processes on network topologies. Physicists analyze these searches using tools like random walks, biased navigation, and optimization to quantify efficiency measures such as first-passage times, success probability, and search cost on complex networks."},{"id":"b25ae464-8a45-4e8e-8efe-63d0f4714f01","l":"Networks & random structures","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Networks & random structures is the study of systems modeled as discrete elements (nodes) connected by links, where the pattern of connectivity\u2014often drawn from probabilistic ensembles\u2014determines collective behavior. It focuses on the statistical properties and dynamics (e.g., percolation, transport, synchronization, robustness) that arise from deterministic or random network architectures."},{"id":"f6b0d9b5-f1f6-42b0-89df-1b70c8ec3e8b","l":"Network stability","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Network stability is the ability of a networked physical system to maintain its function or return to a desired state after perturbations to node states or to the network topology. It includes dynamical stability (return to attractors, often diagnosed via eigenvalues or Lyapunov exponents of the system Jacobian) and structural robustness (persistence of connectivity/function under node/link failures, characterized by percolation thresholds or related metrics)."},{"id":"41033e78-6742-4486-9c09-f1efa60f9d9d","l":"Network structure","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Network structure is the arrangement of nodes and links (edges) in a graph \u2014 the connectivity pattern that specifies how components are connected. Its measurable features (e.g., degree distribution, clustering, path lengths, motifs and community organization) determine the network's topology and strongly influence dynamical processes and function."},{"id":"376c5d7d-24b3-40a2-a82d-a517ac25dbad","l":"Neuroscience, neural computation & artificial intelligence","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"An interdisciplinary field that studies how biological neural circuits represent, transmit, and transform information and how those principles can be captured in computational models. It combines experiment and theory\u2014drawing on dynamical systems, statistical physics, and information theory\u2014to explain neural coding, learning, and decision-making and to design artificial neural networks and AI algorithms."},{"id":"7dd874c0-5ab0-4de4-9919-10775f22748a","l":"Nonequilibrium statistical mechanics","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Nonequilibrium statistical mechanics is the branch of statistical physics that describes how macroscopic, time-dependent and irreversible behavior\u2014such as relaxation, transport, and steady-state currents\u2014emerges from the microscopic dynamics of many-particle systems driven away from thermodynamic equilibrium. It links microscopic laws to observable fluxes and fluctuations using time-dependent probability distributions, kinetic equations, linear response, and fluctuation relations."},{"id":"63bdecc6-b616-484d-b99d-2de7fbc78c6b","l":"Nonequilibrium systems","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Nonequilibrium systems are physical systems that are not in thermodynamic equilibrium, meaning their macroscopic variables change in time or they sustain steady flows of energy, matter, or momentum. They typically exhibit net currents and positive entropy production and must be described by nonequilibrium statistical mechanics or dynamical laws rather than by equilibrium thermodynamics."},{"id":"6d0b28b1-a3eb-40f1-bf96-43308f5d2c56","l":"Non-Hermitian systems","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Non-Hermitian systems are physical systems described by operators (often effective Hamiltonians) that are not equal to their own Hermitian conjugate, typically modeling open, dissipative or gain\u2013loss situations; their eigenvalues can be complex, with real parts giving energy/frequency and imaginary parts giving decay or growth rates. Unlike Hermitian systems, their eigenvectors need not be orthogonal and can coalesce at exceptional points, producing unconventional spectral and dynamical behavior."},{"id":"15ac2674-419f-4fe1-b73b-99c33fe6b8df","l":"Nonlinear dynamics in fluids","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Nonlinear dynamics in fluids is the study of fluid motion governed by nonlinear equations (notably the Navier\u2013Stokes and Euler equations), where nonlinear advection and interactions across scales produce phenomena such as instabilities, shocks, pattern formation, turbulence, and chaotic behavior."},{"id":"fad16b8f-e0c2-42ac-b5a2-6a9a82436c09","l":"Nonlinear phenomena in plasmas","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Nonlinear phenomena in plasmas are behaviors that arise when the plasma response depends nonlinearly on fields or perturbations, so different modes no longer superpose and small disturbances can grow or interact strongly. They include instabilities, turbulence, shocks, solitons, wave\u2013particle and wave\u2013wave coupling, and magnetic reconnection, which redistribute energy and particles across scales and drive anomalous transport and acceleration."},{"id":"2375c1d2-a3fc-4d5b-8fde-4f9029004962","l":"Nonlinear waves","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Nonlinear Dynamics"],"def":"Nonlinear waves are wave motions governed by equations in which the medium's response depends nonlinearly on the wave amplitude, so wave speed, shape, and interactions depend on amplitude and the superposition principle fails. This nonlinearity produces phenomena such as harmonic generation, modulation instability, solitons, shocks, and energy transfer between modes."},{"id":"b14c11c6-6e81-44c1-a1ff-2ec4d1a95359","l":"Non-Newtonian fluids","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Non-Newtonian fluids are fluids that do not obey Newton\u2019s law of viscosity\u2014i.e., their shear stress is not simply proportional to shear rate, so their apparent viscosity depends on shear rate, shear history, or time. Examples include shear-thinning (pseudoplastic) and shear-thickening (dilatant) fluids, thixotropic and rheopectic materials, and viscoelastic polymer solutions and suspensions."},{"id":"38c3eaa1-e9a7-4590-9a93-cbdfd9fe162f","l":"Nuclear astrophysics","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Nuclear astrophysics is the study of nuclear reactions and the properties of atomic nuclei as they occur in astrophysical environments, determining how stars generate energy and how elements and isotopes are synthesized in processes like stellar burning, supernovae, and neutron-star mergers. It combines laboratory nuclear physics, astronomical observations, and theoretical models to calculate reaction rates, decays, and their influence on stellar evolution and cosmic chemical evolution."},{"id":"8ad76784-af63-4e0f-a57c-e8483afce6c5","l":"Nuclear engineering","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Nuclear engineering is the application of nuclear physics, radiation science, and related engineering principles to design, analyze, and control systems that produce, harness, or are affected by nuclear energy and ionizing radiation. It encompasses reactor and fuel-cycle design, radiation detection and shielding, nuclear safety and materials under irradiation, medical and industrial isotope production, and waste management."},{"id":"68e48847-c427-4c28-a6e0-01e33a33e926","l":"Nuclear mass ranges","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Nuclear mass ranges denote the span of masses exhibited by atomic nuclei across all isotopes, determined by the numbers of protons and neutrons and the nuclear binding energy. They are usually given in atomic mass units (u) or energy units (MeV/c^2) and extend from about 1 u (proton) up to a few hundred u for the heaviest known nuclei."},{"id":"bbb0ce0d-36da-4da5-a443-df064f36aa17","l":"Nuclear reactions","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Nuclear reactions are processes in which a nucleus and another nucleus or subatomic particle interact and rearrange nucleons, producing different nuclei and/or emitted particles while exchanging large amounts of nuclear binding energy. They include induced processes (fission, fusion, neutron capture) and spontaneous transformations (radioactive decay), and obey conservation laws such as energy, momentum, charge, nucleon number and angular momentum."},{"id":"1cf75de2-e14b-41cd-b733-8f704652db33","l":"Nuclear structure & decays","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Nuclear structure is the study of how protons and neutrons are arranged and interact inside the atomic nucleus, determining its energy levels, spins, parities, shapes and collective or single\u2011particle excitations. Nuclear decays are the processes by which unstable nuclei transform to more stable configurations\u2014via alpha, beta, gamma emissions, nucleon emission or fission\u2014characterized by specific decay modes, energies, selection rules and lifetimes (half\u2011lives)."},{"id":"c348f41e-fe2b-4ff6-ae11-c12c1a63e4c7","l":"Nuclear tests of fundamental interactions","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Nuclear tests of fundamental interactions are experiments that use nuclear processes\u2014such as decays, transitions, scattering, and reactions\u2014to probe the strengths, symmetries, and detailed properties of the fundamental forces. They test Standard Model predictions and search for new physics via precision measurements and rare processes (e.g., parity and CP violation, superallowed beta decays, neutrinoless double beta decay, and nuclear electric dipole moments)."},{"id":"4c4f4d87-e837-4d65-970d-f5323698a5f1","l":"Optical & microwave phenomena","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Optical and microwave phenomena are the behaviors and interactions of electromagnetic waves in the optical (roughly 10^14\u201310^15 Hz; visible/IR/UV) and microwave (\u224810^8\u201310^11 Hz) frequency bands. They encompass propagation, interference, diffraction, polarization, scattering, absorption and emission, and the ways these waves couple to materials and devices (lenses, waveguides, antennas, resonators, and nonlinear or quantum media)."},{"id":"23cdcd6b-ae22-4b6d-92cd-24a9a8bd0577","l":"Optics & lasers","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Optics is the branch of physics that studies the generation, propagation, interaction and detection of light and other electromagnetic radiation, including phenomena such as reflection, refraction, diffraction, interference, polarization and imaging. Lasers are devices that produce highly directional, coherent (often monochromatic) light by stimulated emission in a gain medium, enabling precise spectroscopy, metrology, communication and nonlinear optical applications."},{"id":"b96ba7a9-cce1-470c-b80c-6dadb2c6b8e9","l":"Optimization problems","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Optimization problems are mathematical tasks of choosing values of one or more variables that minimize or maximize a given objective function (e.g., energy, cost, or performance) subject to any constraints on the variables. In physics they appear in finding equilibrium states, least-action trajectories, or optimal parameters, and can be continuous or discrete and constrained or unconstrained, with local and global optima."},{"id":"6f618be6-815c-40bf-bd8a-cb8ffc96bfd5","l":"Organismal, population, evolutionary & ecological systems","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"The study of biological systems as dynamical, multi-scale networks\u2014from individual organisms through populations to whole ecosystems\u2014focusing on interactions, energy and material flows, and feedbacks among components. It encompasses population dynamics, species interactions, evolutionary processes (selection, drift, adaptation), and the emergent properties (stability, resilience, pattern formation) that arise from those coupled processes."},{"id":"91278658-51b4-4d8e-a936-7cfad1266625","l":"Organisms","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Organisms are individual living systems\u2014ranging from single cells to complex multicellular beings\u2014that maintain, grow, and reproduce organized structure by exchanging matter and energy with their environment. From a physics perspective they are open, far-from-equilibrium, self\u2011organizing systems that dissipate energy to sustain order, process information, and respond to stimuli."},{"id":"a32482f9-6a8e-410f-80c0-204520778cbf","l":"Organs","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Organs are anatomically distinct assemblies of tissues that perform specific physiological functions within multicellular organisms. As physical systems they are mesoscopic, heterogeneous structures whose behavior is governed by mechanics (solid and fluid), transport phenomena, thermodynamics, and bioelectrical processes."},{"id":"cc5d7ae7-fbc6-4c73-8de5-c5ce3015cd05","l":"Packing & jamming problems","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Packing problems concern how particles or shapes can be arranged in space to maximize density or satisfy geometric constraints, e.g., the densest or most efficient filling of a volume. Jamming problems study the nonequilibrium transition by which disordered assemblies of particles become mechanically rigid and load-bearing (without crystalline order) as density, applied stress, friction, or constraints prevent further rearrangements."},{"id":"c21efba1-aa17-434c-92b2-2b72f6f67670","l":"Parity","d":0,"p":"7de7a12f-2bec-4394-98aa-26aebe36d88f","fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Parity is the symmetry operation that inverts all spatial coordinates (r \u2192 \u2212r), and in quantum mechanics is represented by a parity operator whose eigenstates have eigenvalues \u00b11 (even or odd parity). Parity is a conserved symmetry in many interactions (electromagnetic and strong) but is violated by the weak interaction."},{"id":"dbece4d4-f30b-402f-a8c3-85285ff51d0b","l":"Particle accelerator facilities","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Particle accelerator facilities are complexes that produce, accelerate, transport, and deliver beams of charged particles (and derived secondary beams) to collision points or targets for fundamental physics experiments, medical therapies, materials science, and industrial applications. They combine accelerator structures (RF cavities, magnets), beamlines, targets and detectors with supporting systems such as vacuum, cryogenics, radiation shielding, and control/diagnostics."},{"id":"c037721f-32bc-4ef4-88de-c8374b706edb","l":"Particle astrophysics","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Particle astrophysics is the study of elementary particles and their interactions in astrophysical and cosmological settings, using observations of cosmic rays, neutrinos, gamma rays, and the cosmic microwave background to learn about both particle physics and the high-energy universe. It connects laboratory particle physics with astronomical phenomena to investigate topics such as dark matter, neutrino properties, and particle processes in the early universe and extreme astrophysical sources."},{"id":"b338ef70-aa71-4ec1-a73a-03a275bda631","l":"Particle-beam sources","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Particle-beam sources are devices or systems that generate and deliver directed streams of charged or neutral particles (e.g., electrons, ions, neutrons) with controlled species, energy, intensity, spatial and temporal phase-space properties for use in accelerators, experiments, and applications. Common examples include thermionic and photocathode electron guns, ion or plasma sources, laser-driven injectors, and neutron spallation or reactor sources."},{"id":"9583ddb0-9593-4dfd-92b6-0f49ff1282d4","l":"Particle phenomena","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Particle phenomena are the observable processes and behaviors of elementary and composite particles, including their creation and annihilation, scattering and binding interactions, decays, and quantum properties such as spin, statistics, and entanglement. They include both single-particle effects studied in high-energy and nuclear physics and collective, many-particle emergent behavior seen in condensed matter and plasma physics."},{"id":"7de7a12f-2bec-4394-98aa-26aebe36d88f","l":"Particle properties","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Particle properties are the measurable intrinsic and interaction-defining attributes of elementary and composite particles that determine their behavior and how they couple to forces. Typical examples include mass, electric charge, spin and magnetic moment, lifetime and decay modes, and internal quantum numbers such as flavor, parity, isospin or color charge."},{"id":"c256fda5-8fd3-4be9-b68b-bb7b2cc9855a","l":"Patterning","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Patterning is the spontaneous emergence of spatial or spatiotemporal structure from an initially homogeneous system, driven by instabilities, nonlinear interactions, and symmetry breaking. It appears across physics in phenomena such as Rayleigh\u2013B\u00e9nard convection rolls, Turing reaction\u2013diffusion patterns, phase separation, and crystallization."},{"id":"d9490ac8-a0d2-4d97-8dfc-19020e49aeb9","l":"Percolation","d":0,"p":"36754399-ec2b-4962-bb07-ca12c79834e8","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Percolation is the study of how connectivity emerges in a disordered system: sites or bonds of a lattice (or pores in a material) are randomly occupied and one asks whether long-range connected clusters form. As the occupation probability is increased, a sharp geometric phase transition occurs at a critical value (the percolation threshold) where a system-spanning cluster appears, accompanied by universal critical scaling."},{"id":"5eb78870-d202-4a15-9cf0-1e9e04b107ac","l":"Phase behavior","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Phase behavior describes how a material's thermodynamic phases (solid, liquid, gas, and other ordered or disordered states) and their relative amounts change with control variables such as temperature, pressure, composition, and applied fields, encompassing phase coexistence, phase transitions (including critical points) and metastable states."},{"id":"f2a9db78-1651-4a94-b66e-3e3d72056760","l":"Phase transitions","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Phase transitions are transformations between distinct states of matter (phases) that occur when external control parameters (such as temperature, pressure, or field) are varied, and are marked by qualitative changes in macroscopic properties.  Thermodynamically they appear as nonanalyticities in the free energy and are classified as first-order (discontinuous order parameter and latent heat) or continuous/second-order (no latent heat but critical phenomena with diverging correlation lengths)."},{"id":"3797f4c0-5a77-4d24-a693-c12daf6cd3d3","l":"Phenomenology","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Phenomenology is the branch of physics that builds and analyzes theoretical models to produce concrete, testable predictions and observable signatures, serving as the interface between fundamental theory and experiment. It uses effective descriptions, parameter extraction, and computational tools to connect abstract principles with measurable quantities and to propose experimental tests that distinguish competing theories."},{"id":"019e106f-f2bb-47ed-a0d5-2d513dc9ac5e","l":"Physics & society","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Physics & society is the study of the reciprocal interactions between physics and the wider public: how physics research, technologies, and expertise affect social, economic, ethical and policy issues, and how societal needs, values, institutions, and public understanding shape the direction, practice, funding, and communication of physics."},{"id":"c743ca36-88d6-4f26-adea-cdfd0732c4f9","l":"Planets & planetary systems","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Planets are bodies orbiting a star (or stellar remnant) that are massive enough for their self-gravity to make them approximately round but not massive enough to sustain hydrogen fusion, and that on planetary-system scales are dynamically distinct from smaller debris. A planetary system is the ensemble of a star and its gravitationally bound companions \u2014 planets, moons, asteroids, comets and dust \u2014 formed from a protoplanetary disk and evolving through accretion and dynamical interactions."},{"id":"4b4294b5-d03f-4662-9886-cea042ce7d8c","l":"Plasma acceleration & new acceleration techniques","d":0,"p":"d0bb99e3-9d07-40c6-80a0-d8858b63da18","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams","Plasma Physics"],"def":"Plasma acceleration and new acceleration techniques are methods that use collective electromagnetic fields in plasmas or engineered structures to produce accelerating gradients much larger than conventional radio\u2011frequency cavities, enabling acceleration of charged particles over dramatically shorter distances. Examples include laser\u2011 or beam\u2011driven plasma wakefield acceleration, dielectric/photonic\u2011structure and laser\u2011based accelerators, and advanced injection or staging schemes for compact, high\u2011energy beams."},{"id":"b30d0b9e-de6d-4de6-bfad-565f513573b7","l":"Plasma discharges","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Plasma discharges are processes in which an applied electric field ionizes a gas, producing a conducting mixture of electrons and ions that sustains an electric current and often emits light. They occur in many regimes\u2014from low-current corona and glow discharges to high-current sparks and arcs\u2014and are governed by electron-impact ionization, excitation, collisions, and boundary conditions."},{"id":"888306e1-6391-43a8-a318-3434270aafd5","l":"Plasma fusion","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Plasma fusion is nuclear fusion that occurs in an ionized gas (plasma), where free nuclei collide and combine to form heavier nuclei, releasing energy. It requires sufficiently high temperature, density, and confinement so that thermal motion or quantum tunneling overcomes the Coulomb repulsion between positively charged nuclei, as in stars and in laboratory devices like tokamaks and inertial-confinement experiments."},{"id":"04f1f8c5-11c4-434d-9561-f9c615ba5813","l":"Plasma interactions","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Plasma interactions are the processes by which the charged particles, neutral atoms, electromagnetic fields, waves, and material boundaries in a plasma exchange momentum, energy and charge, governed both by long-range collective (electromagnetic) forces and by short-range collisions. These interactions give rise to phenomena such as Debye shielding, wave\u2013particle coupling, instabilities, transport, sheath formation, and magnetic reconnection."},{"id":"76e4ad69-7a84-4616-a27b-2eb4298f279b","l":"Plasma ionization","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Plasma ionization is the process by which neutral atoms or molecules lose or gain electrons to produce free charged particles (ions and electrons), thereby converting a neutral gas into an electrically conducting plasma. It occurs via mechanisms such as collisional (thermal) ionization, photoionization, or field ionization, and is quantified by the degree of ionization (the fraction of particles that are charged)."},{"id":"162881a6-3f70-4bbc-a447-f2bafc56dd18","l":"Plasma opacity","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Plasma opacity quantifies how strongly a plasma attenuates electromagnetic radiation, usually given as an absorption-plus-scattering coefficient \u03ba (per unit length or per unit mass) that depends on frequency, temperature, density, and composition. It determines the optical depth (\u03c4 = \u222b\u03ba\u03c1 ds) and thus controls radiative transfer through processes such as bound\u2013bound, bound\u2013free, free\u2013free transitions and electron scattering."},{"id":"6efa6e17-f6ea-40bb-a2dd-6fd722219dd2","l":"Plasma optics","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Plasma optics is the study of how electromagnetic waves propagate through and are modified by a plasma, where the collective response of free charges determines the medium's refractive index, dispersion, and absorption. Because plasmas are intrinsically dispersive and strongly nonlinear (and can become relativistic at high intensities), they produce effects such as reflection and refraction at plasma surfaces, self\u2011focusing, wavebreaking, harmonic generation and can serve as plasma-based lenses, mirrors and waveguides."},{"id":"3741a35f-83a8-4e50-b7a2-38e600034925","l":"Plasma production & heating","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Plasma production and heating are the processes that ionize a neutral gas and supply energy to its free electrons and ions, thereby creating, sustaining, and raising the temperature or bulk kinetic energy of a plasma. Energy is delivered by electrical discharges and applied fields (ohmic/inductive), electromagnetic waves (RF/microwave/laser), particle or neutral\u2011beam injection, collisions and shocks, and magnetic processes such as reconnection or compressional heating."},{"id":"52f61d50-21d6-438b-9aa1-a8dc29ba6c06","l":"Plasma thermodynamics","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Plasma thermodynamics is the study of the thermodynamic properties and laws that govern ionized gases, treating electrons, ions and neutrals as interacting subsystems. It addresses how pressure, temperature, internal energy, entropy and equilibrium (or non\u2011equilibrium) behavior arise and evolve through processes such as ionization/recombination, collisions, radiation and transport."},{"id":"f5482cec-6a04-4ecc-9c61-7a0fc9771f2f","l":"Plasma transport","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Plasma transport is the set of physical processes that move and redistribute particles, momentum, energy, and charge within a plasma. It encompasses collisional diffusion and conduction, field-driven drifts and flows, and collective/turbulent mechanisms (including neoclassical and anomalous transport) that determine parallel and cross-field transport and thus plasma confinement and profile evolution."},{"id":"cad250bc-84eb-4c9d-82f1-ce01969ff172","l":"Plasma waves","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Plasma waves are collective oscillations of a plasma in which the motions of electrons and ions are coupled self-consistently to the electric and magnetic fields, producing propagating disturbances in charge density, current and fields. Depending on frequency, wavelength and magnetization they include electrostatic modes (e.g., Langmuir and ion-acoustic waves) and electromagnetic modes (e.g., Alfv\u00e9n and magnetosonic waves), each with characteristic dispersion relations."},{"id":"df01dc89-5a4e-46fb-8e28-b8b68e4da784","l":"Polydisperse materials","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Polydisperse materials are assemblies of particles or macromolecules whose a key property (most often size, but also shape, mass or charge) varies across the population and is described by a distribution rather than a single value. This heterogeneity, unlike in monodisperse systems, strongly affects their phase behavior, rheology and transport properties and must be included in theoretical and experimental analyses."},{"id":"4027c276-74b3-4e04-a448-3f6e74df71ae","l":"Polydisperse systems","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Polydisperse systems are collections of particles or components that have a distribution (continuous or discrete) in one or more intrinsic properties\u2014such as size, mass, shape, charge, or composition\u2014rather than being identical. This heterogeneity alters their thermodynamic, structural and transport behavior, affecting phase equilibria, kinetics, rheology and self-assembly compared with monodisperse systems."},{"id":"5114e95c-b4a7-458f-a2f7-b49a98b828cd","l":"Polymer behavior","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Polymer behavior describes the mechanical, thermal and transport responses of materials made from long-chain macromolecules, determined by chain conformation, connectivity, entanglements, crystallinity and intermolecular interactions. It includes phenomena such as entropic elasticity, viscoelasticity and reptation-controlled flow, glass transition and melting, and swelling or phase separation, with dynamics spanning many length- and time-scales."},{"id":"fea036a1-9c29-4872-ab2c-d052949f7c03","l":"Polymers","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Polymers are large macromolecules made of many repeating monomer units covalently linked into long chains. Their chain connectivity, conformational flexibility, and tendency to entangle give rise to distinctive mechanical, thermal and rheological behaviors (e.g., viscoelasticity, glass and melting transitions) important in soft condensed matter."},{"id":"50cc79d6-1a27-4ef3-9ea8-40f8f29ce40e","l":"Population dynamics","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Nonlinear Dynamics"],"def":"Population dynamics is the study of how the sizes and spatial distributions of populations (of organisms, particles, or agents) change over time due to processes such as birth, death, migration and interactions (competition, predation, cooperation). It uses mathematical and statistical models\u2014from deterministic differential equations to stochastic master equations and reaction\u2013diffusion formalisms\u2014to analyze stability, fluctuations, pattern formation and extinction phenomena."},{"id":"7656e510-f579-4768-9a96-51bb544218eb","l":"Porous media","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Porous media are materials made of a solid matrix containing a network of interconnected pores or voids that can hold and transmit fluids or gases. Their macroscopic behavior is characterized by properties like porosity (void fraction) and permeability, which govern flow, diffusion, and multiphase transport through the pore space."},{"id":"358a880c-10d7-4a15-9fbe-9e182dc66d6b","l":"Quantum algorithms & computation","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum algorithms and computation is the study and design of information-processing tasks carried out by quantum systems, where information is encoded in qubits and manipulated by unitary operations and measurements using quantum phenomena such as superposition, entanglement, and interference. It covers quantum algorithm design (e.g., Shor, Grover), computational complexity for quantum models (e.g., BQP), and supporting techniques like quantum error correction and fault-tolerant architectures."},{"id":"9eb3a3f6-79e2-49e8-ae1e-813c0f319225","l":"Quantum chaotic systems","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Nonlinear Dynamics"],"def":"Quantum chaotic systems are quantum-mechanical systems whose dynamics, especially in the semiclassical limit, exhibit signatures analogous to classical chaos\u2014most notably complex, delocalized eigenstates and energy-level statistics that follow random-matrix theory rather than Poisson statistics. Although quantum evolution is unitary and lacks classical trajectory divergence, quantum chaos is diagnosed by features such as level repulsion, eigenstate thermalization, scarred wavefunctions, and rapid growth of out-of-time-order correlators."},{"id":"06b2397a-c546-4dad-89bc-a90c7df4b439","l":"Quantum communication, protocols & technology","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum communication is the use of quantum states and correlations (especially single photons and entanglement) to encode, transmit and process information, enabling tasks such as provably secure cryptography, quantum teleportation and capacity advantages unattainable classically. Protocols and technology denotes the theoretical schemes (e.g., QKD, teleportation, entanglement swapping, error correction) and the enabling hardware (single-photon sources and detectors, quantum memories and repeaters, integrated photonics and free-space/satellite links) for generating, distributing, protecting and measuring quantum information over distances."},{"id":"fa706013-023b-4901-95ca-2e16b9c7a0af","l":"Quantum compass model","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"The quantum compass model is a lattice spin model in which the nearest-neighbor exchange is bond-directional so that different spin components are coupled on different bonds (e.g., S^xS^x on x-bonds and S^zS^z on z-bonds). This bond-dependent anisotropy produces directional frustration, large degeneracies and rich quantum phases relevant to orbital order, frustrated magnetism and Kitaev-like topological physics."},{"id":"40e874c7-7e74-4151-8977-81c8667cffc0","l":"Quantum complex networks","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Quantum complex networks are networks whose nodes are quantum systems and whose links represent interactions or quantum communication channels. The field adapts complex-network methods to study how nontrivial topology affects quantum dynamics, entanglement and coherence distribution, transport, and quantum information processing."},{"id":"01f70d47-cbac-4b5a-8ff9-0768e722511b","l":"Quantum correlations, foundations & formalism","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum correlations are statistical dependencies between outcomes of measurements on spatially separated or subsystems of a quantum system that cannot be accounted for by classical probability, manifested in phenomena such as entanglement, Bell nonlocality, and quantum discord. The \"foundations & formalism\" area develops and analyzes the mathematical framework (Hilbert spaces, density operators, tensor products, measurement theory) and conceptual principles (locality, realism, contextuality, resource theories and axiomatic reconstructions) used to characterize, quantify, and interpret these nonclassical correlations."},{"id":"ee82ad04-6c47-46a4-a70d-c9a7d7989f6a","l":"Quantum field theory","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Quantum field theory is the framework that combines quantum mechanics and special relativity by treating particles as quantized excitations of underlying fields that permeate space. It describes the creation and annihilation of particles and their interactions using field operators, Lagrangians, and path-integral or operator methods."},{"id":"db7ba2bd-700a-4b0b-b444-b873c97ff818","l":"Quantum fluids & solids","d":0,"p":"b5f8885e-3ae5-44dd-acd2-28a17709a6ff","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Quantum fluids and solids are phases of matter whose macroscopic properties are dominated by quantum statistics and zero\u2011point motion rather than thermal behavior, leading to coherent quantum phenomena such as superfluidity, Bose\u2013Einstein condensation, and Fermi degeneracy. Examples include superfluid helium-4 and helium-3, ultracold atomic Bose and Fermi gases, superconducting electron fluids, and quantum crystals like Wigner solids."},{"id":"b5f8885e-3ae5-44dd-acd2-28a17709a6ff","l":"Quantum many-body systems","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Quantum many-body systems are assemblies of a large number of interacting quantum particles whose behavior is governed by strong correlations and entanglement, producing collective phenomena that cannot be understood by treating particles independently. They require specialized theoretical and computational many-body methods because the system's Hilbert space grows exponentially with particle number, giving rise to emergent phases (e.g., superconductivity, magnetism, topological order) and quasiparticles."},{"id":"9085ba40-141a-438f-8b33-179b23ba11cf","l":"Quantum spin models","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Quantum spin models are many-body lattice Hamiltonians that represent localized quantum angular-momentum degrees of freedom (spins, often spin-1/2) interacting via exchange couplings, anisotropies, and external fields. They serve as minimal theoretical descriptions of magnetism, quantum phase transitions, collective excitations, and entanglement (examples include the Ising, Heisenberg, and XXZ models)."},{"id":"1addd209-da18-41de-9a48-46f5f0f11651","l":"Quantum statistical mechanics","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Quantum statistical mechanics is the framework that combines quantum mechanics with statistical methods to predict the thermodynamic and statistical behavior of systems with many quantum degrees of freedom. It uses tools like the density operator and partition function to compute ensemble averages and yields quantum equilibrium distributions (Bose\u2013Einstein, Fermi\u2013Dirac), correlations, and phenomena such as quantum degeneracy and phase transitions."},{"id":"683890e6-25e0-4d29-9686-308d5800fda7","l":"Quarks","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Quarks are elementary spin-1/2 fermions that are the fundamental constituents of hadrons (such as protons and neutrons), coming in six flavors\u2014up, down, charm, strange, top, and bottom\u2014and carrying fractional electric charge and a color charge. They interact via the strong force (mediated by gluons) and the electroweak force, and because of color confinement they are never observed in isolation but only in color-neutral bound states (mesons and baryons)."},{"id":"d46f32eb-17b3-4d09-be59-fc3461e542db","l":"Quasiparticles & collective excitations","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Quasiparticles are emergent, particle-like excitations in interacting many-body systems that carry energy, momentum and quantum numbers and behave like weakly interacting particles with renormalized properties (e.g., effective mass, charge and finite lifetime). Collective excitations are coherent modes of many degrees of freedom\u2014such as phonons, magnons or plasmons\u2014formed by correlated motion of many constituents, and often appear in the low-energy spectrum as quasiparticles that simplify the description of complex interactions."},{"id":"a7a00bcb-5b8d-46f1-876d-978833fa4036","l":"Radiation & particle generation in plasmas","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Radiation & particle generation in plasmas describes the processes by which an ionized gas emits electromagnetic radiation and creates or energizes particle populations. Radiation comes from accelerating charges and atomic transitions (e.g., bremsstrahlung, cyclotron/synchrotron, line and recombination emission), while particle production and acceleration arise from collisional ionization/recombination, pair or nuclear reactions, and mechanisms such as electric fields, shocks, and wave\u2013particle interactions."},{"id":"5f099028-76e7-4a1c-b549-61c441f6241f","l":"Radio frequency techniques","d":0,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Radio frequency (RF) techniques are methods that generate, manipulate, transmit, detect and measure electromagnetic signals in the radio\u2011frequency band (roughly 3 kHz\u2013300 GHz), using components such as oscillators, amplifiers, mixers, filters, transmission lines, resonators and antennas. They encompass modulation/demodulation, impedance matching, signal conditioning and RF spectroscopy/imaging methods that underpin communications, radar, NMR/MRI and sensing."},{"id":"73187d36-0e90-42c1-b531-7b85d8beefd4","l":"Rare-earth doped crystals","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Rare-earth doped crystals are solid-state host lattices in which small concentrations of trivalent lanthanide ions substitute for cations, creating localized 4f electronic states that are well shielded by outer shells and therefore exhibit narrow optical transitions and long spin/optical coherence times. These properties make them useful for lasers, optical amplifiers, frequency converters, and quantum information applications."},{"id":"6f991c28-112d-4bdf-9c2c-50cec44f48fe","l":"Rarefied flows","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Rarefied flows are gas flows in which the molecular mean free path is comparable to or larger than the characteristic length scale of the system, so the continuum (Navier\u2013Stokes) approximation breaks down. They are characterized by moderate-to-high Knudsen numbers and require kinetic descriptions (e.g., the Boltzmann equation or DSMC) and show effects such as velocity slip and temperature jump at surfaces."},{"id":"5ad87d23-7dd2-4d60-bc90-68055febea41","l":"Reacting flows","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Reacting flows are fluid flows in which chemical reactions change the composition, temperature, and thermodynamic properties of the fluid, so that reaction heat release and species production feed back on the motion and transport. They are described by coupled conservation equations for mass, momentum, energy and species with reaction-rate source terms, and include phenomena such as combustion, detonation, and chemically driven atmospheric or plasma flows."},{"id":"1c4f20f1-446b-4875-abc0-744966470931","l":"Reaction diffusion systems","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Nonlinear Dynamics"],"def":"Reaction\u2013diffusion systems are mathematical models, usually written as coupled partial differential equations, that describe how concentrations of one or more chemical or biological species change in space and time due to local nonlinear reactions and spatial transport by diffusion. They commonly produce spatiotemporal phenomena such as pattern formation (Turing patterns), traveling waves, and propagating fronts and are used to model chemical, biological, and ecological processes."},{"id":"b4d51608-61b8-4e73-8186-ba02e5a41a85","l":"Real world networks","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Real-world networks are graphs that represent components and their interactions in natural, social, or technological systems (e.g., neurons, people, power grids), studied as physical systems whose structure and dynamics emerge from many interacting parts. They typically show nontrivial statistical features\u2014heterogeneous (often heavy-tailed) degree distributions, high clustering, short path lengths, and community structure\u2014which strongly affect processes such as spreading, synchronization, and robustness."},{"id":"7321e0f7-3b12-45a2-aa4a-28c2a7e03149","l":"Relativistic heavy-ion collisions","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Relativistic heavy-ion collisions are experiments in which atomic nuclei are accelerated to velocities near the speed of light and collided to create extremely hot, dense matter where quarks and gluons can become deconfined, forming a quark\u2013gluon plasma. Performed at facilities like RHIC and the LHC, these collisions probe the properties of strongly interacting (QCD) matter, collective flow, and energy loss phenomena under conditions similar to the early universe."},{"id":"7bb4589f-b819-4599-8390-26e4fcd6c72a","l":"Research methodology","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics Education Research"],"def":"Research methodology is the systematic framework of principles and procedures used to plan, carry out, analyze, and interpret scientific investigations. In physics it includes experimental design and measurement (with uncertainty analysis), theoretical modeling and simulation, data-analysis and statistical testing, and practices for validation and reproducibility."},{"id":"1932821b-d848-4b37-8209-74c6a9556c07","l":"Rheological properties","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Rheological properties are the material characteristics that describe how a substance deforms and flows under applied stress or strain. They encompass measures like viscosity, elasticity and yield behavior and capture dependencies on shear rate, time, temperature and deformation history (e.g., viscoelasticity, shear thinning/thickening, thixotropy)."},{"id":"bc8cb665-b339-4b1d-9d63-7cab69b3ae1d","l":"Robotics","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Robotics is the interdisciplinary field concerned with the design, construction, operation, and use of robots\u2014engineered systems that sense their environment, compute decisions, and actuate forces to perform tasks. It combines mechanics, dynamics, control theory, sensors and actuators, materials, and computation to model, predict, and control a robot's physical interaction with its environment."},{"id":"0dc06882-40b4-4da9-b489-9706a97e3e25","l":"Scale free & inhomogeneous networks","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Scale-free networks are networks whose degree distribution follows a power law, P(k) \u221d k^\u2212\u03b3, so there is no characteristic node degree and a few highly connected hubs coexist with many low-degree nodes. Such networks are structurally inhomogeneous\u2014nodes differ widely in connectivity or other attributes\u2014leading to heterogeneous dynamics and robustness properties (e.g., resilience to random failure but vulnerability to targeted attacks)."},{"id":"2ec60b08-2f2a-4a83-af01-efcd3973f4b2","l":"Scientific reasoning & problem solving","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics Education Research"],"def":"Scientific reasoning and problem solving is the process of using logical inference, evidence, mathematical models, and strategic heuristics to formulate questions, construct and test explanations, and derive quantitative predictions in physics. It combines critical evaluation of data, translation between representations (equations, graphs, diagrams), and iterative refinement to reach justified solutions."},{"id":"bb18132a-8998-4023-a343-725c6803d24c","l":"Self-assembly","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Self-assembly is the spontaneous organization of components into ordered structures or patterns driven by their local interactions and the surrounding thermodynamic conditions, without external direction. It is typically governed by minimization of free energy (or by sustained non-equilibrium drives in active systems) and can occur across molecular to macroscopic length scales."},{"id":"5d390ffe-6779-4a6f-a2bf-460b869c6fdd","l":"Self-avoiding walks","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"A self-avoiding walk is a path on a lattice or in continuous space that takes successive steps without ever revisiting a site or crossing itself (no self-intersections). It is the canonical statistical-mechanics model of a linear polymer in a good solvent and exhibits nontrivial, scale-dependent behavior (e.g., end-to-end distance ~ N^\u03bd with \u03bd \u2260 1/2)."},{"id":"69e160e2-bae4-4d3e-88f0-378a5479d00a","l":"Self-organized systems","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Self-organized systems are assemblies of interacting components that spontaneously develop macroscopic order or patterns without centralized control, driven by local interactions and nonlinear feedback typically far from thermodynamic equilibrium. They exhibit emergent collective behaviors\u2014such as pattern formation, synchronization, or scale-free structure\u2014arising from the system's internal dynamics rather than external tuning."},{"id":"9c5b281b-03e8-44e5-bd2e-5ba452b0bd1a","l":"Shear flows","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Shear flows are fluid motions in which the velocity changes across a direction perpendicular to the main flow, producing a velocity gradient (shear) and hence internal shear stresses and strain rates. These gradients govern momentum transport and deformation in phenomena such as boundary layers, Couette and Poiseuille flows, and influence whether the flow is laminar or becomes turbulent."},{"id":"82d0ef36-63e0-4a7c-8dc3-a5292d2d338e","l":"Small-world networks","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Small-world networks are graphs that combine high local clustering (many tightly connected neighbors) with short average path lengths between nodes, typically produced by adding a small fraction of long-range \"shortcuts\" to a regular lattice. Introduced by Watts and Strogatz, they exhibit rapid communication (average distance growing only logarithmically with system size) while preserving local structure."},{"id":"3f908345-cb08-47c2-bb62-3196134223aa","l":"Social dynamics","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Social dynamics is the study of how interactions among individuals produce collective, time\u2011dependent patterns (opinions, norms, cooperation, segregation, etc.) in populations, using tools from statistical physics, network theory and agent\u2011based modeling to analyze emergence, phase\u2011transition\u2011like behavior and scaling laws."},{"id":"37c11ef8-b242-46ad-b0c0-f8e391c074a2","l":"Social systems","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Social systems are ensembles of interacting agents (individuals, groups, or institutions) whose collective behaviors emerge from local interactions, information exchange, and adaptive decision rules. They are treated as open, often far-from-equilibrium complex systems studied with tools from statistical mechanics, network theory, and dynamical systems to model phenomena such as opinion formation, contagion, cooperation, and collective motion."},{"id":"88a5cd35-609c-4f0a-8994-5a4cbe488296","l":"Space & astrophysical plasma","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Space and astrophysical plasmas are ionized gases that pervade astronomical environments and whose dynamics are governed primarily by collective electromagnetic forces rather than by individual particle collisions. They are typically threaded by magnetic fields and exhibit phenomena across many scales \u2014 e.g., the solar wind, magnetospheres, stellar coronae, accretion disks, and the interstellar/intergalactic media \u2014 described by magnetohydrodynamic and kinetic plasma physics."},{"id":"95c602a8-4c67-4eda-8ac0-6f0f080007cb","l":"Space weather","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Space weather is the time-varying state of the space environment driven primarily by solar activity\u2014including the solar wind, coronal mass ejections, flares, and energetic particles\u2014which alters the magnetosphere, ionosphere, and thermosphere. These changes can affect satellites, radio and radar communications, navigation systems, and ground-based electrical infrastructure."},{"id":"22580d6a-8d20-4653-b2d1-a7de314bc162","l":"Specific kinds of plasmas","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Specific kinds of plasmas are classes of ionized matter distinguished by particular combinations of physical parameters (temperature, density, degree of ionization, composition, magnetic field, collisionality or relativistic/coupling effects) or by their environment. Examples include cold laboratory plasmas, hot fusion plasmas, astrophysical plasmas, dusty plasmas, pair plasmas and the quark\u2013gluon plasma, each showing distinct collective behaviors and dominant processes."},{"id":"3eb201bd-796e-4985-836d-af5c7e873190","l":"Spreading","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Spreading is the increase in the spatial extent of a physical quantity or object (e.g., mass, energy, probability amplitude, or a fluid) over time, caused by processes such as diffusion, dispersion, advection, or surface wetting. It is characterized by how fast and how far the quantity becomes distributed, often quantified by measures like the variance of a distribution or the position of a moving front."},{"id":"b6d90b06-7836-486e-8e77-221ad2624e1e","l":"Stars","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Stars are massive, self-gravitating spheres of hot plasma that produce light and heat by converting mass to energy through thermonuclear fusion (primarily hydrogen into helium) in their cores. Their structure and evolution are set by the balance of gravity and pressure (hydrostatic equilibrium) together with energy generation and transport processes, and they form from the gravitational collapse of interstellar gas clouds."},{"id":"21802c03-692c-4093-a35c-11955f447cd4","l":"Stochastic networks","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Stochastic networks are graphs whose structure and/or dynamics are governed by randomness\u2014nodes, edges, or link weights are treated as random variables and processes on the network (e.g., random walks, percolation, epidemic spreading) evolve with probabilistic rules. They model transport, communication, and interacting systems under uncertainty and are analyzed using tools from probability theory, statistical mechanics, and network science."},{"id":"860c01ce-5b2c-4bd5-9148-88f0b1d585e2","l":"Stochastic processes","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"A stochastic process is a family of random variables indexed by time or space that describes how a system evolves under intrinsic randomness. It specifies the probabilistic rules (distributions, correlations, or transition probabilities) for possible trajectories\u2014examples include Brownian motion, Poisson processes, and Markov chains used to model noise, fluctuations, and transport in physics."},{"id":"cbc41431-fba0-4ff0-8134-2ded6e598922","l":"Strings & branes","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"In string theory, \"strings\" are fundamental one-dimensional objects whose quantized vibrational modes correspond to the different particles and forces, replacing pointlike particles of quantum field theory. \"Branes\" (short for membranes) are their higher-dimensional generalizations \u2014 p\u2011dimensional extended objects (e.g., D\u2011branes, M\u2011branes) that can carry charges, host gauge fields and string endpoints, and serve as essential nonperturbative ingredients in dualities, compactification, and holographic descriptions of spacetime."},{"id":"0b08c33f-27b1-4bbf-90f9-1eb1bf63d1eb","l":"Strong interaction","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"The strong interaction is the fundamental force that binds quarks together into protons, neutrons and other hadrons and, via its residual effect, holds atomic nuclei together. It is described by quantum chromodynamics (QCD), mediated by gluons that carry color charge, and is characterized by confinement at low energies and asymptotic freedom at high energies."},{"id":"29bd219f-a624-4ef5-b4c5-f9049f2be66e","l":"Strongly correlated systems","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Strongly correlated systems are many-body quantum systems in which interparticle interactions are comparable to or larger than the particles' kinetic energy, so that single-particle or weakly interacting quasiparticle descriptions fail. This strong coupling produces collective, emergent behaviors\u2014e.g., Mott insulating states, heavy-fermion physics, magnetism, and unconventional superconductivity\u2014that require nonperturbative theoretical approaches."},{"id":"15f520a3-5c6c-4d9e-8975-81d399140562","l":"Structural properties","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Structural properties describe the arrangement, connectivity, and internal architecture of a material or object\u2014ranging from atomic lattice and defects to grain structure and macroscopic geometry. These features determine mechanical behavior (stiffness, strength, fracture) and strongly influence thermal, electrical, and optical responses."},{"id":"b63a8374-45ac-4029-80ff-46f191b5d486","l":"Structured light","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Structured light refers to electromagnetic fields whose spatial amplitude, phase, polarization, or spectral content is deliberately shaped to produce nontrivial transverse or longitudinal field patterns. Such engineered beams (for example optical vortices carrying orbital angular momentum, Bessel, or Airy beams) have tailored propagation, focusing, and torque/force properties used in imaging, manipulation, and communications."},{"id":"1ad4c70d-5644-4a14-a97e-973953744ade","l":"Student preparation","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics Education Research"],"def":"Student preparation: the collection of prior knowledge, skills, and pre-class activities that ready learners to engage productively with physics instruction and assessment\u2014including mathematical background, conceptual understanding, laboratory techniques, and study/metacognitive strategies. Adequate preparation reduces cognitive load and enables deeper, more efficient learning."},{"id":"308f3fec-1dd8-4797-979a-f6f07630285c","l":"Sum rules","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Sum rules are exact relations that equate a sum or integral over a spectrum or response function (e.g., transition strengths, cross sections, or spectral weight) to a simple quantity fixed by symmetries, conservation laws, or operator commutation relations. They follow from fundamental principles (like charge or energy conservation, causality, or analyticity) and are used to check consistency between theory and experiment or to extract static properties from dynamic spectra."},{"id":"ca8ddaad-441c-481c-93b3-65f46125fb93","l":"Superconductivity","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Superconductivity is a phase of matter occurring below a material-dependent critical temperature in which electrical resistance vanishes and magnetic fields are expelled (the Meissner effect). It is a macroscopic quantum phenomenon typically arising from the formation of bound electron pairs (Cooper pairs) that condense into a coherent quantum state."},{"id":"2250b5f3-dfa1-4ca7-bedf-fff5d41bd6ea","l":"Superconductors","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Materials that, below a characteristic critical temperature, conduct electric current with exactly zero electrical resistance and expel magnetic fields (the Meissner effect), allowing persistent, lossless currents. In conventional superconductors this behavior reflects a macroscopic quantum state formed by paired electrons (Cooper pairs); the superconducting state is destroyed above a critical temperature, magnetic field, or current."},{"id":"733b28ec-b147-4e13-b2a5-d8a767d2d6f3","l":"Superfluidity","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Superfluidity is a phase of matter in which a fluid flows with effectively zero viscosity, allowing frictionless, persistent flow and phenomena like creeping up container walls and sustained currents. It is a macroscopic quantum effect arising from long-range coherence of the wavefunction (e.g., Bose\u2013Einstein condensation in helium-4 or paired fermion condensation in helium-3), and shows features such as quantized vortices and second sound."},{"id":"7c2da745-d748-43e9-9f3b-5dc4cd2a6c99","l":"Surface growth","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Surface growth is the time evolution of a material interface as atoms or molecules are added to or removed from a surface, which changes its morphology, roughness, and patterning. It is governed by microscopic processes (adsorption, diffusion, nucleation, step propagation) and described by kinetic and continuum models (e.g., ballistic deposition, solid-on-solid models, KPZ/Edwards\u2013Wilkinson equations) that predict growth modes and scaling behavior."},{"id":"eef7c13b-c67f-4d33-be97-76cee58e700a","l":"Surface & interfacial phenomena","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Surface and interfacial phenomena are the physical behaviors that occur at the boundary between two phases (e.g., solid\u2013liquid, liquid\u2013gas, solid\u2013solid) because molecules at an interface experience different forces and symmetries than in the bulk. They include effects such as surface tension, wetting and contact angles, capillarity, adsorption and interfacial energy, which govern adhesion, transport, and mechanical behavior at and near interfaces."},{"id":"16158b58-5493-4fb9-901c-54f841cc4524","l":"Surfactants, micelles & vesicles","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Surfactants are amphiphilic molecules (having both hydrophobic and hydrophilic parts) that lower interfacial tension and, above a critical concentration, spontaneously self\u2011assemble to minimize free energy. Common self\u2011assembled structures are micelles\u2014typically compact, single\u2011layer aggregates with hydrophobic cores that solubilize nonpolar material\u2014and vesicles (liposomes)\u2014closed bilayer shells that enclose an aqueous interior and act as nanoscale compartments."},{"id":"f10e180c-1ed7-4aee-9c62-1a863581317c","l":"Suspensions","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Suspensions are heterogeneous mixtures in which solid particles (typically \u22731 \u03bcm) are dispersed throughout a fluid (liquid or gas); the particles are large enough to scatter light and to sediment under gravity unless kept suspended by flow, Brownian motion, or interparticle forces. Their macroscopic behavior is governed by hydrodynamic interactions, collisions and surface forces, producing phenomena such as settling, shear-dependent rheology (e.g., shear thinning or yield stress) and jamming."},{"id":"6dca4b7f-ff80-45b8-ab18-bbba041e3393","l":"Sustainability","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Sustainability is the practice of organizing human and natural systems so they can provide needed energy, materials, and services indefinitely without degrading the environment or exhausting resources. From a physics perspective it means operating within conservation and thermodynamic limits\u2014minimizing irreversible entropy production, maximizing energy and material efficiency, closing material loops, and shifting to renewable energy flows."},{"id":"54b389aa-8ccf-49ee-a54e-478ccc2616eb","l":"Symmetries","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Symmetries are transformations (spatial rotations, translations, time shifts, internal/gauge rotations, or discrete operations) under which a physical system or its governing laws remain unchanged. These invariances constrain possible dynamics and, via Noether's theorem for continuous symmetries, lead to conserved quantities and selection rules used throughout classical and quantum physics."},{"id":"5b3df46f-4812-4196-8c68-7fb8577cdd80","l":"Synchronization","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nonlinear Dynamics","Networks","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Synchronization is the phenomenon in which two or more oscillating systems, when coupled to each other or to a common drive, adjust their frequencies and phases so they oscillate at a common frequency or maintain a fixed phase relationship (phase locking or entrainment). It occurs widely in physical, biological, and engineered systems \u2014 e.g., coupled pendula, synchronized flashing of fireflies, and phase-locked electronic oscillators."},{"id":"dba0f19a-3cbb-43dd-a780-1190b1a5d8ae","l":"Synthesis","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Synthesis is the controlled creation or assembly of physical systems\u2014materials, structures, or particles\u2014by experimental or engineered processes to achieve a specified composition, structure, or set of properties. It spans scales from nuclear and atomic (nucleosynthesis, chemical synthesis) to meso- and macroscopic fabrication (crystal growth, thin-film deposition, nanofabrication)."},{"id":"0fe307b1-cfb7-401a-8d94-be4064d16e8d","l":"Synthesis of new superheavy elements","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Synthesis of new superheavy elements is the creation of atomic nuclei with very high proton number (typically Z > 104) by inducing nuclear reactions\u2014most commonly heavy-ion fusion, multi-nucleon transfer, or neutron capture\u2014in accelerators or reactors to fuse projectile and target nuclei. These experiments, producing only a few short-lived atoms at a time, extend the periodic table and probe nuclear structure and shell effects (including the predicted \"island of stability\") by identifying new elements through their characteristic decay chains and radiation."},{"id":"faf0fcca-3e2a-490a-89c5-cc4c610b73f5","l":"Technology","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics Education Research"],"def":"Technology: the application of scientific knowledge\u2014especially physical principles\u2014to design, build, and optimize tools, instruments, materials, and processes that solve practical problems and extend human capabilities. It encompasses the methods, devices, and engineering practices used to implement and refine those applications."},{"id":"233a6cd0-b7fe-491b-b5fd-85fd6b2a7c79","l":"Theoretical & Computational Techniques","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Techniques"],"dl":[],"def":"Theoretical & Computational Techniques are the mathematical frameworks and numerical algorithms used to formulate, analyze, and solve models of physical systems, turning physical principles into quantitative predictions. They encompass analytical methods (perturbation theory, mean-field and renormalization-group approaches), computational algorithms and simulations (Monte Carlo, molecular dynamics, finite-element and spectral methods), and the high-performance computing and data-analysis tools that implement them."},{"id":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","l":"Theoretical Techniques","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Techniques"],"dl":[],"def":"Theoretical techniques are the mathematical frameworks, approximation schemes, and computational tools physicists use to formulate models, derive analytic results, and compute predictions for physical systems. Examples include perturbation and variational methods, symmetry and group-theoretic analysis, renormalization and effective-field theory, and numerical approaches like Monte Carlo and finite-element simulations."},{"id":"25bdd3c1-c3ec-4e4e-a84e-6d4152bec3b2","l":"Thermal properties","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Thermal properties are material characteristics that determine how a system stores, transports, and exchanges thermal energy and how its temperature and microscopic state respond to changes in heat. Examples include specific heat (heat capacity), thermal conductivity and diffusivity, thermal expansion coefficient, emissivity, and latent heat of phase transitions, all rooted in the material's microscopic degrees of freedom and interactions."},{"id":"fc65882e-b316-4cf1-a96a-ec8557624d80","l":"Tissues","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Tissues are organized ensembles of cells plus extracellular matrix that cooperate to perform specific biological functions in multicellular organisms. In physics they are treated as complex soft-matter and active materials with characteristic mechanical, transport, and dynamical properties (e.g., viscoelasticity, poroelasticity, and collective cell motility)."},{"id":"0026b3bc-1a2f-45df-8025-1243dac91047","l":"Topological materials","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Topological materials are solids whose electronic or quasiparticle band structures are characterized by global topological invariants (e.g., Chern numbers or Z2 indices) that cannot change without closing a gap. By the bulk\u2013boundary correspondence these invariants enforce robust, symmetry-protected boundary modes or protected band crossings (edge/surface conduction or Dirac/Weyl points) that are insensitive to weak disorder or perturbations."},{"id":"bfe403ee-cd34-466b-b8bf-f90b4d4431fc","l":"Topological phases of matter","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Topological phases of matter are quantum phases characterized not by local symmetry-breaking order parameters but by global topological invariants of the ground-state wavefunction that remain unchanged under continuous deformations that do not close the energy gap. They exhibit robust, quantized responses and protected edge or surface states (via bulk\u2013boundary correspondence) that are insensitive to weak disorder or perturbations, and in some cases are protected by symmetries."},{"id":"7b8486b8-c51f-4359-a150-61019b3522d8","l":"Total cross sections","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"The total cross section is the effective target area that quantifies the probability for an incident particle to undergo any interaction with a target; it equals the integral of the differential cross section over all scattering angles and the sum over all final channels. It has dimensions of area (e.g., barns) and is the sum of elastic and inelastic cross sections."},{"id":"62dc4b9b-2c00-4b5c-bf19-74f4d37293fd","l":"Traffic","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Traffic is the collective motion of self-driven agents (vehicles or pedestrians) on transportation networks, characterized by macroscopic quantities such as flow, density, and average speed. It exhibits emergent behaviors\u2014traffic jams, stop-and-go waves, and phase transitions\u2014and is studied with continuum, kinetic, and microscopic models (hydrodynamic, car-following, cellular automata) to analyze stability and control."},{"id":"b64db5c0-c54c-4fe6-a142-d089ceb55b3f","l":"Transient & explosive astronomical phenomena","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Transient and explosive astronomical phenomena are short-lived astrophysical events marked by sudden, often violent releases of energy that produce dramatic brightening, fading, or new sources on timescales from milliseconds to years (e.g., supernovae, gamma\u2011ray bursts, novae, kilonovae, fast radio bursts, tidal disruption events). They arise from extreme processes\u2014catastrophic nuclear burning, compact\u2011object mergers, accretion instabilities, or relativistic jets in strong gravity\u2014and are studied through rapid, multiwavelength observations."},{"id":"cc1c57bd-55a6-4bc9-92a7-1c4c1c873ee5","l":"Transportation research","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Transportation research is the interdisciplinary study of the movement of people and goods and of the systems, infrastructure, technologies, and policies that enable that movement, using modeling, measurement, and optimization to improve safety, efficiency, accessibility, and sustainability. It applies physics principles (dynamics, fluid mechanics, energy/thermodynamics, materials, and control theory) together with data analysis and engineering to analyze and design vehicles, infrastructure, and transport networks."},{"id":"f23d87e8-fae8-49d5-9be0-45ccf8ac97f9","l":"Transport in networks","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Transport in networks is the study of how particles, energy, or information propagate across discrete nodes and links, using models such as random walks, flow equations, and percolation to quantify conductance, throughput, and transit times. It focuses on how network topology (degree distribution, clustering, modularity) and link/node dynamics (capacities, delays, failures) create pathways, bottlenecks, and emergent large-scale transport behavior."},{"id":"36754399-ec2b-4962-bb07-ca12c79834e8","l":"Transport phenomena","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Transport phenomena is the study of how conserved quantities\u2014mass, momentum, and energy\u2014are transferred within and between physical systems. It describes the mechanisms (diffusion, conduction, convection), the governing conservation equations (e.g., continuity, Navier\u2013Stokes, heat equation), and the constitutive laws and transport coefficients (diffusivity, viscosity, thermal conductivity) that relate fluxes to driving gradients."},{"id":"7ce4d01a-e821-4adc-a47b-3ac109e970c4","l":"Tree network","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"A tree network is a connected graph with no cycles, so there is exactly one simple path between any two nodes and a tree with N nodes has N\u22121 edges. Such cycle-free, branching architectures commonly model physical systems like electrical distribution, river or vascular branching, and hierarchical resistor or flow networks."},{"id":"beaa54ca-9008-483f-afb7-74caff3d61a2","l":"Triangular network","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"A triangular network is a graph or lattice in which the basic repeating unit is a triangle, typically realized as the two-dimensional triangular (equilateral-triangle) lattice in which each site has six nearest neighbors. It is a common geometry for modeling resistor meshes, mechanical trusses, crystalline lattices, and geometrically frustrated spin or percolation problems."},{"id":"062f0300-c33d-4782-b24c-43996b0469f3","l":"Turbulence","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Turbulence is a disordered, strongly nonlinear state of fluid flow characterized by chaotic fluctuations and a hierarchy of vortices and eddies across a broad range of scales; it typically occurs at high Reynolds number where inertial forces dominate viscosity, producing enhanced mixing and a cascade of kinetic energy from large to small scales that is ultimately dissipated by viscosity."},{"id":"9124ceaf-786d-437d-a17d-59ba0f26ad44","l":"Uncorrelated network","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"An uncorrelated network is a network with no degree\u2013degree correlations, meaning the degrees of the two nodes at either end of a randomly chosen edge are statistically independent. Equivalently, the conditional probability factorizes as P(k'|k)=k'P(k')/\u27e8k\u27e9 (where P(k) is the degree distribution and \u27e8k\u27e9 its mean)."},{"id":"6db94357-3eba-4ebe-9e0b-1a84c152804b","l":"Undergraduate research","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Professional Topics"],"dl":["Physics Education Research"],"def":"Undergraduate research is a mentored, inquiry-driven project in which a physics student works on open-ended experimental, theoretical, or computational problems under faculty or laboratory supervision to develop research skills and contribute to new or ongoing knowledge. These projects typically occur during a semester, summer, or as a capstone and often lead to presentations, reports, or coauthored publications."},{"id":"ac5659ab-e45c-49ef-b879-75394058a63b","l":"Upper undergraduate students","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Professional Topics"],"dl":["Physics Education Research"],"def":"Upper undergraduate students are those in the later years (typically the third and fourth years) of a bachelor's physics program who have completed foundational lower-division courses. They are taking advanced and specialized coursework and often begin research projects or capstone work in preparation for graduate study or professional careers."},{"id":"9fa410c9-2586-4115-8dca-190b22421a01","l":"Vortex flows","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"A vortex flow is a fluid motion in which streamlines circulate about a common axis or core, producing nonzero circulation and vorticity (local rotation) of the fluid. Such flows\u2014ranging from bathtub drains and tornadoes to vortex rings and superfluid quantized vortices\u2014are characterized by a rotating core and distinctive velocity profiles (e.g., ideal free/potential vortex v \u221d 1/r versus solid-body/forced vortex v \u221d r)."},{"id":"0a677b11-f06d-4ced-9385-cf0bf1061a59","l":"Waves and free surface flows","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Waves and free surface flows concern fluid motion in which a deformable interface (usually liquid\u2013air) is free to move, so the shape of the surface evolves dynamically under forces such as gravity, pressure and surface tension. It encompasses the generation, propagation and interaction of surface waves (gravity and capillary), and nonlinear phenomena of open-channel flows (breaking waves, solitons, hydraulic jumps, tsunamis) described by fluid equations with moving-boundary conditions."},{"id":"93b6be1a-96f6-44e9-8a88-4e1498419285","l":"Weighted networks","d":0,"p":null,"fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Weighted networks are graphs in which edges (and sometimes nodes) carry numerical weights that quantify the strength, capacity, cost, or probability of connections, so that network behavior depends not just on topology but on weighted link values. These weights \u2014 which may be positive, signed, or time-dependent \u2014 are essential for accurately modeling transport, flow, synchronization, and influence in real systems (e.g., transportation, neural, social, and metabolic networks)."},{"id":"68d7090d-127f-44dd-bda5-5ee1aee2a7a2","l":"150 \u2264 A \u2264 189","d":1,"p":"68e48847-c427-4c28-a6e0-01e33a33e926","fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Denotes nuclei whose mass number A (total number of protons plus neutrons) lies between 150 and 189 \u2014 the medium\u2011heavy, rare\u2011earth and neighboring region of the nuclear chart. Nuclei in this range are often strongly quadrupole\u2011deformed and show pronounced collective phenomena (rotational bands, vibrations, shape coexistence) and rich nuclear spectroscopy."},{"id":"67e66b8d-f8c2-4782-a35e-9bae3e0d8664","l":"190 \u2264 A \u2264 219","d":1,"p":"68e48847-c427-4c28-a6e0-01e33a33e926","fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"190 \u2264 A \u2264 219 denotes the set of atomic mass numbers A from 190 to 219 inclusive \u2014 i.e., nuclei containing 190\u2013219 nucleons. This heavy-mass region contains many stable and radioactive isotopes important for nuclear-structure, decay and spectroscopy studies."},{"id":"2dbc1b50-90ad-46a1-827a-18bb66c9f1c2","l":"1-dimensional spin chains","d":1,"p":"347c621b-2ddf-4d11-bd25-3380b5623c72","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A 1-dimensional spin chain is a model consisting of discrete spin degrees of freedom (e.g., S = 1/2) placed on the sites of a linear lattice, with specified interactions\u2014typically nearest-neighbor exchange, anisotropy, and/or external fields\u2014between neighboring spins. These classical or quantum models (Ising, Heisenberg, XY, etc.) are used to study collective magnetic behavior, excitations, and quantum many-body phenomena such as spin waves, entanglement, and quantum phase transitions, and in many cases admit exact solutions."},{"id":"12ad5a64-13ab-439d-8a12-071c089de2bb","l":"20 \u2264 A \u2264 38","d":1,"p":"68e48847-c427-4c28-a6e0-01e33a33e926","fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"20 \u2264 A \u2264 38 denotes the set of atomic nuclei whose mass number A (total number of protons plus neutrons) lies between 20 and 38 inclusive. This mass range covers light-to-medium nuclei commonly studied for shell effects, clustering, and reaction dynamics."},{"id":"12aae757-568b-4acf-995c-1497aca36937","l":"39 \u2264 A \u2264 58","d":1,"p":"68e48847-c427-4c28-a6e0-01e33a33e926","fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"39 \u2264 A \u2264 58 denotes atomic nuclei whose mass number A is between 39 and 58 inclusive \u2014 i.e., isotopes from 39K to 58Ni. This mass range spans the upper sd and lower pf shells (the iron-group nuclei) and is important in nuclear-structure studies and stellar nucleosynthesis."},{"id":"9ebaacc9-f580-461c-ae18-8686659f5421","l":"59 \u2264 A \u2264 89","d":1,"p":"68e48847-c427-4c28-a6e0-01e33a33e926","fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"59 \u2264 A \u2264 89: the set of atomic nuclei whose mass number A (total number of protons plus neutrons) lies between 59 and 89 inclusive; this A\u224860\u201390 region comprises medium-mass nuclei (e.g., 59Co to 89Y) commonly studied in nuclear structure, collective excitations, and reaction dynamics."},{"id":"303ae788-385a-47c3-8e25-031548d8677f","l":"6 \u2264 A \u2264 19","d":1,"p":"68e48847-c427-4c28-a6e0-01e33a33e926","fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"6 \u2264 A \u2264 19 denotes the nuclear mass number A (total number of protons plus neutrons) restricted to the range 6 through 19; it labels the class of light nuclei (from 6Li up to 19F) relevant in nuclear-structure, reaction, and astrophysical studies."},{"id":"3346188e-2ca7-426d-a1c6-9da0e28f25c4","l":"90 \u2264 A \u2264 149","d":1,"p":"68e48847-c427-4c28-a6e0-01e33a33e926","fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"90 \u2264 A \u2264 149 denotes nuclei whose mass number A (the total number of protons plus neutrons) lies between 90 and 149 inclusive. This mid-mass region contains many isotopes that exhibit a rich variety of nuclear-structure behavior (shell effects, deformation, collective excitations) and is important for fission and nucleosynthesis studies."},{"id":"4a4df734-a188-4394-bb81-42c9f3872454","l":"A \u2265 220","d":1,"p":"68e48847-c427-4c28-a6e0-01e33a33e926","fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"A \u2265 220 denotes atomic nuclei whose mass number A (the total number of protons plus neutrons) is 220 or larger. These are very heavy nuclei\u2014typically actinides and beyond\u2014whose structure and decay are strongly affected by large Coulomb repulsion and which commonly undergo alpha decay and spontaneous fission."},{"id":"305753ba-ca44-404e-80f2-7c992baa34ba","l":"A \u2264 5","d":1,"p":"68e48847-c427-4c28-a6e0-01e33a33e926","fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"A \u2264 5 denotes an inequality meaning the quantity A is at most 5 \u2014 it can equal 5 or be any smaller value. For a real-valued A this restricts A to the interval (\u2212\u221e, 5]."},{"id":"f5523cbb-df53-402a-8b99-d9a6efb8c1e4","l":"Ab initio calculations","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Ab initio calculations are computational, first-principles methods that predict the properties of atoms, molecules, and materials by solving the quantum many-body problem using only fundamental constants and no empirical fitting. Common approaches include density functional theory, coupled-cluster, and quantum Monte Carlo, which approximate the electronic-structure solution to obtain energies, geometries, and response properties."},{"id":"b6bfb821-0163-4e17-8cf3-4191147a3a02","l":"Accelerator/storage ring control systems","d":1,"p":"b3310113-551e-4c5e-9263-28f393ff09f0","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Integrated hardware and software systems that monitor, regulate and synchronize an accelerator or storage ring\u2019s subsystems\u2014magnets, RF, timing, vacuum, beam diagnostics and instrumentation\u2014to produce and maintain stable, correctly focused and timed particle beams. They provide closed-loop feedback, automation and interlocks for orbit and energy control, beam stability, machine protection and operator control."},{"id":"31ddfc50-4455-4ecd-8460-458cc870e0eb","l":"Accretion disk & black-hole plasma","d":1,"p":"88a5cd35-609c-4f0a-8994-5a4cbe488296","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"An accretion disk is a flattened, rotating assemblage of gas and plasma that forms as matter with angular momentum spirals inward toward a compact object (such as a black hole), with viscous and magnetic stresses transporting angular momentum outward and converting gravitational energy into heat and radiation. Black-hole plasma denotes the hot, ionized material in and around such disks and the black hole's magnetosphere, whose dynamics are shaped by strong gravity, relativistic effects and magnetic fields and which can produce high-energy emission and relativistic jets."},{"id":"bef62928-e018-4ea1-ac11-204556a6b63b","l":"Acoustic forces","d":1,"p":"40a5bd01-6544-4502-8321-458c33878bf3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Acoustic forces are the time-averaged mechanical forces exerted by sound waves on particles, surfaces, or fluids, produced by acoustic radiation pressure and gradients in the pressure and velocity fields. They can trap, push, or aggregate objects and drive steady flows (acoustic streaming), with strength and direction set by the wave amplitude, wavelength, and the properties of the objects and medium."},{"id":"96696bfb-21b8-42a8-bb0e-9ce10790a04a","l":"Acoustic interactions","d":1,"p":"cc59c6fb-acbb-4237-953b-b874fe5dbd4a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Acoustic interactions are the physical processes by which sound waves exchange energy, momentum, or information with media, objects, or other waves. They encompass generation, propagation, reflection, scattering, absorption and nonlinear effects (e.g., harmonic generation, radiation forces), and are determined by the medium's mechanical properties and boundary conditions."},{"id":"53354177-c126-47af-94c7-a4cf5764b664","l":"Acoustic measurements","d":1,"p":"40a5bd01-6544-4502-8321-458c33878bf3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Acoustic measurements are experimental determinations of sound-field properties\u2014such as pressure, intensity, frequency spectrum, phase, propagation speed, absorption, and scattering\u2014using transducers (microphones, hydrophones, laser vibrometers) and signal-analysis methods. They are used to characterize sound sources, material acoustic properties, and propagation environments for applications from noise control to imaging and nondestructive testing."},{"id":"2a5345ea-2bd1-45f5-818c-bf056b4f6500","l":"Acoustic metamaterials","d":1,"p":"77fabbae-1f98-4300-996c-9158ab52e6fc","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Fluid Dynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Acoustic metamaterials are artificially structured media whose subwavelength architecture\u2014rather than chemical composition\u2014engineers effective acoustic parameters and wave behavior not found in natural materials. By using resonant elements and tailored geometry they can produce effects such as negative effective density or bulk modulus, band gaps, anomalous refraction, cloaking, and superlensing for sound."},{"id":"bd30d628-af2a-41ac-ab9f-31c06a9b70d9","l":"Acoustic modeling","d":1,"p":"40a5bd01-6544-4502-8321-458c33878bf3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Acoustic modeling is the construction of mathematical and computational representations of sound generation and propagation\u2014usually based on the acoustic wave equation or its approximations\u2014to predict pressure, particle velocity, and energy transport in a medium. It incorporates effects of boundaries, reflections, scattering, absorption, and (when needed) nonlinear or statistical behavior for applications from room acoustics to sonar and speech."},{"id":"12eef432-bdf5-4247-bbf7-9af3cb7bbb4c","l":"Acoustic signal processing & noise","d":1,"p":"40a5bd01-6544-4502-8321-458c33878bf3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Acoustic signal processing is the measurement, representation and algorithmic transformation of sound-pressure variations to extract information, enhance or separate sources, and enable transmission, sensing or synthesis in applications such as audio, sonar and speech. Noise refers to unwanted random or structured disturbances superimposed on acoustic signals that degrade fidelity or detectability\u2014described by statistics (e.g., SNR, spectra) and mitigated by filtering, beamforming and denoising methods."},{"id":"29146d99-215f-436f-9dbc-6dd06cd063c4","l":"Acoustic techniques","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Acoustic techniques are experimental and analytical methods that use sound (mechanical) waves\u2014from audible through ultrasonic and hypersonic\u2014to probe, image, characterize, or manipulate materials and systems. They encompass approaches such as sonar and medical ultrasound, acoustic microscopy and spectroscopy, nondestructive testing, and acoustic manipulation (levitation, particle sorting, microfluidics)."},{"id":"30d2c20a-5fa5-4852-ac74-7545085f50c8","l":"Acoustic wave phenomena","d":1,"p":"40a5bd01-6544-4502-8321-458c33878bf3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Acoustic wave phenomena are the generation, propagation, and interaction of mechanical pressure and particle-velocity oscillations (sound) in gases, liquids, and solids. They are described by the acoustic wave equation and include reflection, refraction, diffraction, interference, scattering, dispersion, attenuation, and nonlinear effects such as shock formation and acoustic streaming."},{"id":"4716dc85-b01b-49ac-82b8-2dcfb975f092","l":"Acoustofluidics","d":1,"p":"40a5bd01-6544-4502-8321-458c33878bf3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Acoustofluidics is the study and application of interactions between acoustic waves and fluids (and suspended particles), using sound-induced forces and acoustic streaming to control fluid motion and manipulate particles, droplets, or cells\u2014typically at the microscale. It exploits acoustic radiation forces and viscous streaming for noncontact tasks such as particle sorting, mixing, pumping, and biosensing."},{"id":"ce52ebb4-8fab-48e9-8a00-a3bc21c01fdb","l":"Active & peculiar galaxies","d":1,"p":"a2f3cef9-614c-4f7a-8f5b-009885b53abb","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Active galaxies are galaxies whose unusually bright, variable nonstellar cores (active galactic nuclei, or AGN) are powered by accretion onto a supermassive black hole and often produce strong emission lines, jets, and high-energy radiation. Peculiar galaxies are systems with atypical morphology or spectral properties\u2014such as tidal tails, warped disks, rings, or disturbed kinematics\u2014usually resulting from interactions, mergers, or internal instabilities."},{"id":"2a4f166c-10ae-4975-b17f-6f3e204376b1","l":"Adaptive methods","d":1,"p":"141061f5-f02c-492c-af56-81c3c49e8b65","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Adaptive methods are numerical techniques that dynamically adjust discretization or algorithm parameters (such as mesh resolution, time step, or basis functions) during a simulation based on error estimates or solution features. They concentrate computational effort where it is most needed to achieve a desired accuracy with lower cost than a uniformly fine discretization."},{"id":"03235f9c-9981-4bb3-adf5-6852e9646614","l":"Additive manufacturing","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Additive manufacturing (3D printing) is the layer-by-layer fabrication of three-dimensional parts directly from a digital model by depositing or consolidating material in successive cross-sections. It uses physical processes such as material extrusion, powder-bed fusion, or photopolymerization to produce complex geometries with reduced material waste compared with subtractive machining."},{"id":"fe83f0b4-fdcd-4678-9ead-9a735befaecb","l":"Adhesion","d":1,"p":"eef7c13b-c67f-4d33-be97-76cee58e700a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Adhesion is the attraction between unlike materials or surfaces that makes them stick together at their interface. It arises from intermolecular forces (van der Waals, hydrogen bonding, electrostatic or chemical bonds) and from mechanical interlocking, and determines contact, wetting, and bond strength."},{"id":"996b083a-805f-4898-8621-c2ac0562cd8e","l":"Adhesives","d":1,"p":"c765ac18-ded5-4ef0-a41a-22be13741559","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Adhesives are materials that join two surfaces by forming an interfacial layer that transmits mechanical loads through interfacial forces (van der Waals, hydrogen bonding, covalent/ionic bonding) and/or mechanical interlocking. Their effectiveness depends on surface wetting and energy, contact area, and the adhesive\u2019s bulk rheology and cure chemistry (e.g., thermosetting, thermoplastic, pressure-sensitive)."},{"id":"d9bd0a93-7579-4d12-a152-7a475cadedca","l":"Adiabatic approximation","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"The adiabatic approximation is the assumption that a system's external parameters change so slowly compared with its internal dynamical timescales that the system continuously follows the instantaneous solution (e.g., an instantaneous eigenstate in quantum mechanics) and transitions between different states are negligible. In practice it implies conservation of adiabatic invariants (quantum: staying in the same eigenstate up to a phase; classical: conserved action variables) when parameter change rates are small compared with level splittings or oscillation frequencies."},{"id":"229eca6d-30f1-4c05-9a78-c352de8326b1","l":"Adsorption","d":1,"p":"eef7c13b-c67f-4d33-be97-76cee58e700a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Adsorption is the accumulation of atoms, ions, or molecules (adsorbates) at the surface of a solid or liquid (the adsorbent), where they are held by surface forces and form a thin interfacial film, reducing their concentration in the bulk. It differs from absorption and occurs either by weak physical (physisorption, van der Waals) interactions or by formation of chemical bonds (chemisorption)."},{"id":"265f614d-082f-4a91-b6c5-4335720667fc","l":"Advocacy","d":1,"p":"019e106f-f2bb-47ed-a0d5-2d513dc9ac5e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Advocacy in physics is the organized effort by physicists and physics organizations to influence public policy, funding decisions, institutional practices, and public understanding in ways that support scientific research, education, and equitable participation. Activities include outreach, communication with policymakers, lobbying for resources, and programs to broaden access and diversity in the field."},{"id":"3a9b26c3-a981-4a36-b479-8c3acde06f89","l":"Aeroacoustics","d":1,"p":"40a5bd01-6544-4502-8321-458c33878bf3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Aeroacoustics is the study of how fluid flows (especially turbulent flows, vortices, and flow\u2013structure interactions) generate and radiate sound, and how that sound propagates and can be predicted or controlled. It combines fluid dynamics and acoustics to analyze and mitigate flow-induced noise in systems like aircraft, engines, and wind turbines."},{"id":"4da3f7ed-7c00-40e3-bb14-55c39886e2cc","l":"Aerodynamic noise","d":1,"p":"a4ca1a7e-639b-4ff5-8cf3-44b60866fb3e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Aerodynamic noise is sound produced by unsteady airflow and its interaction with solid surfaces, where turbulent fluctuations, vortices, or flow separation create time-varying pressure fields that radiate as acoustic waves. It includes broadband turbulence noise and discrete tones (e.g., vortex shedding, blade or trailing-edge noise) and is modeled by aeroacoustic theories such as Lighthill's acoustic analogy."},{"id":"7434f2cd-2824-4424-b1cb-b672ea9b2eb2","l":"Aerogel","d":1,"p":"06891d57-1044-487c-ae20-21366d161a97","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Aerogel is a highly porous, ultra-low-density solid formed by removing the liquid from a gel while preserving its interconnected solid network (usually by supercritical drying), so the pores are filled with gas rather than liquid. This structure gives aerogels extremely high porosity and surface area, very low thermal conductivity and refractive index, and exceptional insulating properties."},{"id":"6aa2c3dc-ffa3-48ce-98a1-be48517886ee","l":"Aerosols","d":1,"p":"98cac094-ec9c-4f37-adf7-eed968f89359","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Aerosols are dispersions of microscopic solid particles or liquid droplets suspended in a gas (usually air). Their physical behavior\u2014set mainly by particle size (nanometers to tens of micrometers), Brownian motion, settling, coagulation, and evaporation/condensation\u2014controls their transport, lifetime, and optical/radiative effects."},{"id":"0345bd94-5ea4-4f97-9316-4923eb67aa07","l":"Aggregates","d":1,"p":"16158b58-5493-4fb9-901c-54f841cc4524","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Aggregates are assemblies of discrete constituents (particles, grains, molecules, etc.) that adhere or bond to form larger structures whose mechanical, optical, and transport properties depend on both the constituents and their spatial arrangement. They occur in contexts from colloids and granular materials to aerosols and astrophysical dust, with morphologies ranging from compact to fractal."},{"id":"3a918ab3-927d-4051-b15a-777fcfe551ab","l":"Alignment & survey","d":1,"p":"4c286df9-b565-4295-b951-49a36d60cad1","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Alignment & survey refers to the measurement, control, and adjustment techniques used to determine and maintain the precise positions and orientations of experimental or accelerator components and reference points to required tolerances. Typical methods include geodetic surveying, optical tooling and laser trackers, fiducialization, coordinate transforms, and continuous monitoring to ensure proper beamline, detector, or optical performance."},{"id":"96973abf-f1b5-44c5-9549-9f0f4dfe234f","l":"Alloys","d":1,"p":"81194f70-bbca-4041-b57e-29338e588cac","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"An alloy is a material composed of two or more elements, at least one of which is a metal, whose atoms form solid solutions and/or intermetallic phases. The resulting microstructure gives mechanical, electrical, thermal, and chemical properties that differ from those of the pure constituent metals."},{"id":"85ba6e9e-eac7-4639-b158-04c76b56397b","l":"Alluvial dynamics","d":1,"p":"c577bd62-ea57-43b7-9607-1ef45dba3afb","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Alluvial dynamics is the study of how flowing water transports, sorts, deposits, and erodes sediment in rivers, floodplains, deltas and related landscapes, governed by coupled fluid\u2013sediment interactions and bedform evolution. It focuses on the physical processes and feedbacks that control sediment transport rates, channel morphology, and landscape change across short (storms/floods) to long (geologic) timescales."},{"id":"34a274ff-a677-40a6-b390-dfcea4340aa6","l":"Alpha decay","d":1,"p":"1cf75de2-e14b-41cd-b733-8f704652db33","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Alpha decay is a radioactive process in which an unstable nucleus emits an alpha particle (two protons and two neutrons, i.e., a helium-4 nucleus), reducing its atomic number by two and its mass number by four. It commonly occurs in heavy nuclei and proceeds via quantum tunneling of the preformed alpha particle through the nuclear Coulomb barrier."},{"id":"5fa985c3-dc5a-43a0-8579-426758f721cf","l":"Altermagnets","d":1,"p":"58071c68-730c-4a3e-982c-abc9f0cedf67","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Altermagnets are a class of collinear, compensated magnets (zero net magnetization) that break time-reversal symmetry and crystalline symmetries so as to produce a momentum-dependent, sign-alternating spin splitting of electronic bands. This symmetry-driven k\u2011space spin polarization yields spin-polarized transport and Hall effects without a net magnetic moment, distinct from both ferromagnets and conventional antiferromagnets."},{"id":"19eb7adc-6b41-43d6-b3a1-ccdd0a973e87","l":"Alternative gravity theories","d":1,"p":"124f9214-0176-475c-b706-99df707e8411","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Alternative gravity theories are theoretical frameworks that modify or extend Einstein's general relativity\u2014by changing the gravitational action, adding new fields or dimensions, or altering the properties of the graviton\u2014in order to explain phenomena or resolve theoretical problems that GR plus known matter does not fully address. They are motivated by issues like dark matter, dark energy and cosmic acceleration, singularities, and the quest for a quantum gravity, with examples including scalar\u2013tensor theories, f(R) gravity, massive gravity, and MOND."},{"id":"34548dbc-f720-4a9d-982f-aa10e01b74d9","l":"Amorphous materials","d":1,"p":"81194f70-bbca-4041-b57e-29338e588cac","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Amorphous materials are solids in which atoms or molecules lack long-range periodic order, showing only short-range local coordination instead of a crystalline lattice. They therefore do not have a sharp melting point and often exhibit glassy behavior and properties (e.g., isotropy, history-dependent structure) determined by their formation process."},{"id":"4c886248-148a-41d4-888c-de09e60e1e87","l":"Analog computation","d":1,"p":"fd7300dc-49e9-458d-9121-de94f7446fdb","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Analog computation is the use of continuously varying physical quantities (such as voltages, currents, mechanical displacements, optical intensities or fluid pressures) to represent variables and to perform arithmetic and functional operations directly via the governing physical laws. It is embodied in devices like electronic, mechanical, or optical analog computers that solve equations (notably differential equations) in real time and with inherent parallelism, but with limited precision and sensitivity to noise and component drift."},{"id":"cb2f25c7-cb3c-4847-8c5e-0da35f6df26b","l":"Analytical chemistry","d":1,"p":"4455b073-c3a2-4a0b-923e-98d7ec0f3b9d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Analytical chemistry is the branch of chemistry that develops and applies methods and instruments (e.g., separation, spectroscopic, and electrochemical techniques) to identify and quantify the chemical composition of samples, with emphasis on measurement accuracy, precision, and data interpretation."},{"id":"295dd02d-5447-477d-8f0b-0760bd7f1e39","l":"Anderson localization","d":1,"p":"7d9b7d96-c9e6-455f-9fa7-86088b7df853","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Anderson localization is the disorder-induced inhibition of wave transport, where multiple scattering and destructive interference cause eigenstates to become spatially localized with exponentially decaying amplitude. Originally formulated for electrons in disordered lattices, the phenomenon applies generally to waves (e.g., light, sound, cold atoms) and leads to suppression of diffusion and conductance."},{"id":"1ecde7a0-ec5a-4350-95d1-0f22db60e1ea","l":"Andreev bound states","d":1,"p":"ca8ddaad-441c-481c-93b3-65f46125fb93","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Andreev bound states are discrete quasiparticle energy levels that form inside the superconducting gap at interfaces or weak links because electrons are coherently retro-reflected as holes (Andreev reflection), producing standing-wave interference. Their energies depend on the superconducting phase difference and junction scattering and they are the microscopic carriers of the Josephson supercurrent."},{"id":"a010a47c-8170-4527-b93d-8ff3af720a89","l":"Andreev reflection","d":1,"p":"ca8ddaad-441c-481c-93b3-65f46125fb93","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Andreev reflection is the process at a normal\u2013superconductor interface in which an electron incident from the normal side with energy below the superconducting gap is reflected as a hole while a Cooper pair is transferred into the superconductor, allowing subgap charge transport. The reflected hole retraces the electron's path (retroreflection) and carries opposite charge, converting normal current into supercurrent."},{"id":"b0037bda-552b-43be-b7a5-b2c17477f85c","l":"Angular momentum","d":1,"p":"7de7a12f-2bec-4394-98aa-26aebe36d88f","fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Angular momentum is a vector quantity that measures an object's rotational motion and its resistance to changes in that rotation; for a point particle L = r \u00d7 p (position cross linear momentum) and for a rigid body about an axis L = I\u03c9. In an isolated system angular momentum is conserved when the net external torque is zero."},{"id":"7fc8e960-d2c3-425f-9bc5-2990bf2b3fa1","l":"Angular momentum of light","d":1,"p":"23cdcd6b-ae22-4b6d-92cd-24a9a8bd0577","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Angular momentum of light is the mechanical angular momentum carried by an electromagnetic field that can exert torques on matter. It decomposes into spin angular momentum (tied to polarization, e.g., \u00b1\u0127 per photon for circular polarization) and orbital angular momentum (tied to the spatial phase/wavefront structure, e.g., l\u0127 per photon for an optical vortex with topological charge l)."},{"id":"dc706191-bd70-4d20-8944-0217b12cd7d9","l":"Animal behavior, social interactions & emergent phenomena","d":1,"p":"6f618be6-815c-40bf-bd8a-cb8ffc96bfd5","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"The study of how individual animals' behavioral rules and local interactions produce collective, often unexpected, group-level patterns and functions. Using tools from statistical physics, nonlinear dynamics and network theory, it analyzes mechanisms (alignment, attraction\u2013repulsion, signaling, noise) that drive emergent phenomena like flocking, schooling, swarming, synchronization and collective decision-making."},{"id":"639669e6-7ffb-474f-8aea-0bfb5175c495","l":"Anomalies","d":1,"p":"ee82ad04-6c47-46a4-a70d-c9a7d7989f6a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Anomalies are phenomena in quantum field theory where a symmetry of the classical action is broken by quantum effects\u2014typically through regularization/renormalization or noninvariance of the path-integral measure\u2014so the corresponding classical current is not conserved. They have observable consequences (e.g., the chiral anomaly causing \u03c00 \u2192 \u03b3\u03b3) and can impose consistency constraints on gauge theories."},{"id":"6c171ad4-fba1-453f-a76e-dac7e815dbac","l":"Anthropic considerations","d":1,"p":"26ed7afc-8635-4637-a339-8d0a7cb75be1","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Anthropic considerations are selection effects in cosmology and fundamental physics that note we can only observe universes (or regions) whose physical constants and initial conditions permit the existence of observers, and are therefore used to explain apparent fine-tuning of parameters\u2014often in the context of multiverse or ensemble hypotheses."},{"id":"c560211a-e486-4a78-ab36-d9ac2ddc82a3","l":"Antibubbles","d":1,"p":"98cac094-ec9c-4f37-adf7-eed968f89359","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"An antibubble is a droplet of liquid encased by a thin shell of gas and suspended in the same surrounding liquid, giving a liquid\u2013gas\u2013liquid structure that is the inverse of an ordinary gas bubble. The thin air film that separates the inner and outer liquids is metastable and typically drains and ruptures rapidly, so antibubbles form only under dynamic conditions (e.g., pouring or splashing)."},{"id":"2844da54-3053-4dc4-a594-668ba3fded21","l":"Antiferroelectricity","d":1,"p":"41dd58dd-d35c-46e8-9594-d7e05b458da6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Antiferroelectricity is a long-range ordered state in which neighboring unit cells carry electric dipole moments that align antiparallel and cancel, producing no net spontaneous polarization. Under a sufficiently strong external electric field the antiparallel arrangement can switch to a parallel (ferroelectric) alignment, often producing a characteristic double hysteresis loop in the polarization\u2013field response."},{"id":"12e5d5fb-76a6-45d8-bd73-142f26c69131","l":"Antiferromagnets","d":1,"p":"58071c68-730c-4a3e-982c-abc9f0cedf67","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Antiferromagnets are materials in which the atomic magnetic moments (spins) order into a regular pattern with neighboring moments aligned antiparallel, so the bulk has zero or very small net magnetization below a characteristic N\u00e9el temperature. This staggered order is stabilized by exchange interactions and leads to magnetic excitations and response properties distinct from ferromagnets."},{"id":"dd65d266-d9c3-4789-8591-dae48c6e6742","l":"Antihydrogen","d":1,"p":"bd252d88-a608-4897-9416-daf84dee7f6b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Antihydrogen is the antimatter counterpart of the hydrogen atom, a neutral bound state formed by an antiproton (the negatively charged antiparticle of the proton) and a positron (the positively charged antiparticle of the electron). It is expected to mirror hydrogen\u2019s mass and energy-level structure, and is produced and trapped in experiments to test fundamental symmetries (like CPT) and antimatter\u2019s behavior under gravity."},{"id":"74a2f68b-37df-4018-9750-8e16a9660c21","l":"Anyons","d":1,"p":"d46f32eb-17b3-4d09-be59-fc3461e542db","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Anyons are quasiparticles that occur in two-dimensional systems whose exchange statistics interpolate continuously between bosons and fermions. Exchanging anyons multiplies the many-body wavefunction by an arbitrary phase (abelian anyons) or by a noncommuting unitary transformation (nonabelian anyons); they appear in systems like the fractional quantum Hall effect and are central to proposals for topological quantum computing."},{"id":"89598015-ae35-4a6b-80c6-1bb4f0f4fb08","l":"Archea","d":1,"p":"91278658-51b4-4d8e-a936-7cfad1266625","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Archaea are a domain of single-celled, prokaryotic microorganisms that are genetically and biochemically distinct from Bacteria and Eukarya. Many are extremophiles and are characterized by unique membrane lipids (ether-linked isoprenoids) and distinctive molecular machinery (e.g., ribosomal RNA and metabolic pathways)."},{"id":"519093fc-f1a2-4530-96e2-6518bc4c4f37","l":"Architectural acoustics","d":1,"p":"40a5bd01-6544-4502-8321-458c33878bf3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Architectural acoustics is the study and engineering of how sound is produced, propagates, reflects, is absorbed, diffused, and transmits within and through buildings and rooms. It applies wave physics to design spaces and choose materials and treatments that achieve desired speech intelligibility, musical quality, reverberation times, and noise isolation."},{"id":"c2727c77-55dd-4107-8035-76ef5fb18d70","l":"Asteroids, meteors, & meteorites","d":1,"p":"c743ca36-88d6-4f26-adea-cdfd0732c4f9","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Asteroids are small rocky and metallic bodies that orbit the Sun, most commonly found in the main asteroid belt between Mars and Jupiter. A meteoroid is a smaller fragment of such bodies in space; if it enters Earth's atmosphere and vaporizes it produces a bright streak called a meteor (a \"shooting star\"), and if any piece survives to reach the ground it is a meteorite."},{"id":"0a18edd1-9df1-4a54-a10a-39cb27e00431","l":"Astrophysical jets","d":1,"p":"88a5cd35-609c-4f0a-8994-5a4cbe488296","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics","Plasma Physics"],"def":"Astrophysical jets are narrow, collimated outflows of plasma launched at high\u2014often relativistic\u2014speeds from the vicinity of compact objects (black holes, neutron stars) or young stars; they can extend from AU to galactic or intergalactic scales and transport energy, momentum, and magnetic flux into their surroundings. They are typically powered and collimated by accretion disks and magnetic fields and are observed across the electromagnetic spectrum."},{"id":"fbd1b02e-26c9-4a37-a5b3-3b1271fccc07","l":"Atmospheric electricity","d":1,"p":"b30d0b9e-de6d-4de6-bfad-565f513573b7","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Atmospheric electricity is the study of electrical charges, electric fields, and currents in Earth's atmosphere \u2014 how they are generated (by ionization, convection and collisional charge separation), transported, and dissipated. It covers phenomena from the fair\u2011weather electric field and air conductivity to thunderstorm charge separation and lightning, the global electric circuit, and transient luminous events (sprites, jets)."},{"id":"ad1edc29-450c-4872-86ea-0034e0815b2e","l":"Atmospheric fluid dynamics","d":1,"p":"358b7565-3e15-46e9-859e-1254bd4a9e7f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Interdisciplinary Physics","Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Atmospheric fluid dynamics is the study of the motion of Earth's atmosphere using the principles of fluid mechanics and thermodynamics, usually expressed by the Navier\u2013Stokes or primitive equations on a rotating, stratified sphere. It explains how pressure gradients, Coriolis forces, buoyancy, friction, moisture and radiation produce winds, turbulence, waves and large\u2011scale atmospheric circulation across scales from the boundary layer to planetary waves."},{"id":"358b7565-3e15-46e9-859e-1254bd4a9e7f","l":"Atmospheric science","d":1,"p":"29ab1ea0-dd7e-46b7-87dd-0ca29c6be062","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics","Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Atmospheric science is the study of the physical, chemical and dynamical processes in planetary atmospheres\u2014especially Earth's\u2014including composition, thermodynamics, radiation, fluid dynamics, clouds/aerosols, and their coupled interactions with the ocean, land and space that produce weather and climate."},{"id":"785303ac-6cda-4258-aa03-fed17f2bba54","l":"Atomic ensemble","d":1,"p":"bd252d88-a608-4897-9416-daf84dee7f6b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"An atomic ensemble is a macroscopic collection of atoms whose collective degrees of freedom (population, polarization, spin coherence) are treated and manipulated as a single effective system. Such ensembles are used in atomic, molecular and optical physics for collective phenomena and applications like coherent spectroscopy, superradiance, quantum memories, and precision sensing."},{"id":"01af01cc-b889-4c31-9073-c4ec759cd252","l":"Atomic gases","d":1,"p":"bd252d88-a608-4897-9416-daf84dee7f6b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Atomic gases are ensembles of neutral atoms in the gaseous phase whose macroscopic properties arise from atomic motion and interatomic collisions. Depending on temperature and density they range from classical ideal gases to quantum-degenerate systems exhibiting Bose\u2013Einstein condensation or Fermi degeneracy."},{"id":"df4cc751-ec6a-4a07-81fa-3761146e3af1","l":"Atomic & molecular beams","d":1,"p":"bd252d88-a608-4897-9416-daf84dee7f6b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Atomic and molecular beams are collimated streams of neutral atoms or molecules (or ions) traveling through high vacuum with well-defined velocities and narrow angular spreads. They are produced by effusive or supersonic sources and manipulated with apertures, fields, or lasers to provide controlled samples for spectroscopy, collision studies, surface science, atom optics, and precision measurements."},{"id":"cac7f673-4cd1-47b8-9de3-9f9785aea585","l":"Atomic & molecular clusters","d":1,"p":"30e65d01-6cd5-442b-889f-ddc0c3c28157","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Atomic and molecular clusters are finite aggregates of a modest number of atoms or molecules (from a few up to thousands) held together by chemical or physical bonds, lying between single molecules and bulk solids. They show size-dependent structural, electronic and optical properties \u2014 often discrete energy levels and behaviors not found in the bulk \u2014 making them important model systems for nanoscale physics and chemistry."},{"id":"c7b43954-94a1-45d2-9290-481c815e62ba","l":"Atomic, optical & lattice clocks","d":1,"p":"ba7ccb8e-d7eb-4e6f-9c1e-b622f5b4e242","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Atomic clocks measure time by locking an oscillator to a highly reproducible atomic transition (microwave or optical), using the atom\u2019s quantized energy difference as the reference frequency. Optical clocks exploit much higher-frequency, narrower optical transitions for superior accuracy and stability; optical lattice clocks in particular trap many neutral atoms in a standing-wave \u201cmagic-wavelength\u201d lattice to suppress motional and light-shift perturbations while maintaining high signal-to-noise."},{"id":"60df05a3-1a26-4f09-9925-244dfd169c6c","l":"Atomic orbital","d":1,"p":"30e65d01-6cd5-442b-889f-ddc0c3c28157","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"An atomic orbital is a quantum-mechanical wavefunction (an eigenfunction of the atomic Hamiltonian) for an electron bound to a nucleus, whose squared magnitude |\u03c8(r)|^2 gives the probability density for finding the electron in space. Orbitals are labeled by quantum numbers (principal n, orbital l, magnetic m, and spin) that determine their energy, angular shape, and orientation."},{"id":"e4236efc-fc7b-4d50-bc84-76ddf5e65856","l":"Atomic spectra","d":1,"p":"30e65d01-6cd5-442b-889f-ddc0c3c28157","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Atomic spectra are the characteristic patterns of electromagnetic radiation that atoms emit or absorb when their electrons jump between quantized energy levels, producing discrete spectral lines whose frequencies satisfy \u0394E = h\u03bd. These line patterns identify elements and encode information about atomic structure and interactions (fine/hyperfine splitting, external fields, and broadening)."},{"id":"1d433fa8-4cdd-4e66-ae51-df1797f18d56","l":"Atomic techniques","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Atomic techniques are experimental methods that exploit the internal energy levels, transitions, motion, and interactions of single atoms or atomic ensembles\u2014using tools like lasers, magnetic and electric fields, and vacuum systems\u2014to manipulate, control, measure, or probe physical systems with high precision. Examples include laser cooling and trapping, atomic spectroscopy and clocks, atom interferometry, and magnetic resonance, which underpin precision metrology, quantum information, and sensing."},{"id":"f20282e8-1a0a-4a4e-aff5-eeb680fd2808","l":"Atom impact & scattering","d":1,"p":"eef7c13b-c67f-4d33-be97-76cee58e700a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Atom impact & scattering is the study of collisions in which an incident atom interacts with another atom, molecule, ion, or a surface and is deflected and/or exchanges momentum and energy. These processes are characterized by quantities like impact parameter, differential and total scattering cross sections, and include elastic, inelastic, and reactive channels that reveal interatomic potentials and surface dynamics."},{"id":"88cd30f5-62cf-441c-b7a3-8fd2f7f0fac5","l":"Atom interferometry","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Atom interferometry exploits the quantum wave nature of atoms by coherently splitting and recombining their matter-wavefunctions (typically using laser pulses or gratings) to produce interference. The resulting phase difference, sensitive to accelerations, rotations, gravity, electromagnetic potentials, or fundamental interactions, is measured for precision sensing and tests of physics."},{"id":"7f15ad20-b739-48a5-86df-d5118d1a4d89","l":"Atomization","d":1,"p":"b72a2d57-f47e-4e87-9a43-f8320d817bae","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Atomization is the breakup of a continuous material\u2014typically a liquid or molten metal\u2014into a dispersed ensemble of fine droplets or particles by mechanical, aerodynamic, or thermal forces, producing sprays or powders with characteristic size distributions. It underlies processes such as fuel injection, aerosol generation, spray coating, and powder production."},{"id":"754977c0-04c0-447a-aa2f-4f1ec1c514f0","l":"Atom lasers","d":1,"p":"46e2a993-8dce-4c28-beab-fdabe4378664","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"An atom laser is a device that emits a coherent, directional beam of atoms\u2014a matter wave\u2014by outcoupling atoms from a Bose\u2013Einstein condensate, analogous to an optical laser but with massive particles. It produces a narrow, phase-coherent atomic beam with low momentum spread useful for precision interferometry and sensing."},{"id":"f9069b5c-203a-4184-b8f5-f0cd7f0fcda1","l":"Atom optics","d":1,"p":"46e2a993-8dce-4c28-beab-fdabe4378664","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Atom optics is the study and manipulation of the wave-like behavior of neutral atoms, treating atomic motion with tools analogous to optical elements (lenses, mirrors, beam splitters) to control atomic trajectories and coherence. These elements are implemented using laser light, magnetic/electric fields, and material or evanescent-wave surfaces, enabling techniques such as atom interferometry and precision measurements."},{"id":"3f61c3b5-a96a-428b-b171-2888d28a2f6d","l":"Atom or molecule scattering from surfaces","d":1,"p":"12e1040c-7c2c-478a-b466-c979b57fcaea","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"The process in which an incident atom or molecule interacts with a solid surface, exchanging momentum, energy, internal excitation, or charge and thereby being reflected, trapped (adsorbed), scattered inelastically, or reacting with the surface. Measurements of angular and energy distributions of the scattered particles are used to probe the surface potential, structure, and dynamical excitations (phonons, electronic states) and to study adsorption/desorption and reaction dynamics."},{"id":"37d66e56-71be-4e9e-b517-e2e52e14b6df","l":"Atoms","d":1,"p":"bd252d88-a608-4897-9416-daf84dee7f6b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Atoms are the basic units of chemical elements, each consisting of a dense nucleus of protons and neutrons surrounded by electrons bound by electromagnetic forces. Their structure and behavior are governed by quantum mechanics, which produces discrete energy levels, atomic spectra, and the basis for chemical bonding."},{"id":"ea26d181-6bf5-47aa-9f45-098e3e073f4f","l":"Atomtronics","d":1,"p":"46e2a993-8dce-4c28-beab-fdabe4378664","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Atomtronics is the design and implementation of circuit elements and devices using ultracold neutral atoms (often Bose\u2013Einstein condensates or degenerate Fermi gases) confined and guided by tailored optical, magnetic or microfabricated potentials, where coherent matter-wave flow and interactions play the role of electronic currents. It creates atomic analogues of resistors, diodes, transistors and SQUIDs for applications in quantum simulation, sensing and information processing."},{"id":"f014e3f5-6876-4e32-a7b6-c7ca1c6eb27b","l":"Attacks on networks","d":1,"p":"f6b0d9b5-f1f6-42b0-89df-1b70c8ec3e8b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Attacks on networks are deliberate actions that compromise the confidentiality, integrity, or availability of communication or control networks by exploiting vulnerabilities of nodes, links, or protocols\u2014examples include denial-of-service, spoofing, routing manipulation, and eavesdropping. They are studied and modeled (e.g., via percolation, epidemic/cascading-failure models, information theory, and game theory) to quantify network robustness and design defenses."},{"id":"393ec13e-de28-4e91-acc2-89299c4f21b1","l":"Auditory system","d":1,"p":"0a74688b-2eee-4349-a9bd-e93851fe2c5a","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"The auditory system is the biological sensor and neural pathway that detects airborne pressure waves and converts them into electrical signals the brain can interpret as sound. It includes the outer ear (collects and filters sound), the middle ear (ossicles for impedance matching), and the inner ear (the cochlea mechanically decomposes frequencies along the basilar membrane and hair cells transduce motion into neural impulses), with central pathways encoding intensity, frequency, and timing for perception and localization."},{"id":"20becfdd-ee51-4ca3-9191-827518624f1c","l":"Autoionization & Auger processes","d":1,"p":"ba7ccb8e-d7eb-4e6f-9c1e-b622f5b4e242","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Autoionization is a nonradiative electronic decay in which an excited atom, ion, or molecule transfers the energy of an internal electronic transition to a second bound electron, causing that electron to be ejected and producing further ionization without photon emission. The Auger process is a common autoionization pathway in which a core-level vacancy is filled by a higher\u2011energy electron and the excess energy ejects an \"Auger\" electron, whose kinetic energy equals the difference of the involved binding energies."},{"id":"88e498c7-ee2e-4169-8786-5462f72db423","l":"Axion-like particles","d":1,"p":"3981a374-dbc8-42de-bdba-73ea858650c6","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Axion-like particles (ALPs) are hypothetical light, neutral pseudoscalar bosons similar to the QCD axion but not constrained to its specific mass\u2013coupling relation. They interact extremely weakly (notably via a two-photon coupling) and appear in many extensions of the Standard Model, making them targets for dark-matter and laboratory/astrophysical searches."},{"id":"da511145-476f-4251-9fd4-bf7b19745f5c","l":"Axions","d":1,"p":"3981a374-dbc8-42de-bdba-73ea858650c6","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Axions are hypothetical light pseudoscalar bosons predicted by the Peccei\u2013Quinn mechanism to dynamically cancel the CP-violating theta term in quantum chromodynamics, thereby solving the strong CP problem. They interact extremely weakly with ordinary matter and radiation and are considered a viable cold dark matter candidate produced nonthermally in the early universe."},{"id":"e42c088f-e8e5-4f90-87bb-048238240d13","l":"Bacteria","d":1,"p":"91278658-51b4-4d8e-a936-7cfad1266625","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Bacteria are single-celled, prokaryotic microorganisms (typically ~0.2\u201310 \u00b5m) that lack membrane-bound organelles and reproduce asexually; in physics they are often treated as self-propelled, active particles that generate nonequilibrium flows, chemotactic responses, and collective behaviors."},{"id":"e06001fa-a168-42a9-bdb5-b955d85794d4","l":"Ballistic transport","d":1,"p":"36754399-ec2b-4962-bb07-ca12c79834e8","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Ballistic transport is the motion of carriers (electrons, phonons, etc.) through a material or device with negligible scattering, occurring when the carriers' mean free path exceeds the device dimensions. In this regime transport is governed by boundary conditions and quantum transmission (e.g., conductance quantization) rather than by resistive scattering."},{"id":"3817eab7-2f24-46f8-ba4a-aa6433722b4a","l":"Band structure methods","d":1,"p":"233a6cd0-b7fe-491b-b5fd-85fd6b2a7c79","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Band structure methods are theoretical and computational techniques for finding the allowed electronic energy versus crystal momentum E(k) in a periodic solid by solving electrons in a periodic potential (exploiting Bloch's theorem). They range from simple analytic models (nearly\u2011free electron, tight\u2011binding) to first\u2011principles approaches (DFT, GW) and provide the energy bands and gaps that determine a material's electronic, optical, and transport properties."},{"id":"33517878-e26f-47df-ab9f-fd9b1ef2898c","l":"Barabasi-Albert network","d":1,"p":"0dc06882-40b4-4da9-b489-9706a97e3e25","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"A Barab\u00e1si\u2013Albert (BA) network is a model of a growing random graph in which new nodes attach to existing nodes with probability proportional to the existing nodes' degrees (preferential attachment), producing a scale-free network whose degree distribution follows a power law P(k) \u221d k^{-\u03b3} (typically \u03b3 \u2248 3). It captures the \"rich-get-richer\" mechanism underlying many real-world networks."},{"id":"da83048e-674c-4cc1-8aad-9050f4707734","l":"Baryogenesis & leptogenesis","d":1,"p":"26ed7afc-8635-4637-a339-8d0a7cb75be1","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Baryogenesis is the set of theoretical mechanisms in the early universe that produced the observed excess of baryons over antibaryons, which requires baryon-number violation, C and CP violation, and a departure from thermal equilibrium (the Sakharov conditions). Leptogenesis is a class of such scenarios in which a CP-violating lepton asymmetry\u2014often generated by decays of heavy Majorana neutrinos\u2014is converted into a baryon asymmetry by electroweak sphaleron processes."},{"id":"c975168d-1a1f-4be7-917a-f16a8fd755ee","l":"Baryon acoustic oscillations","d":1,"p":"26ed7afc-8635-4637-a339-8d0a7cb75be1","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Baryon acoustic oscillations are pressure-driven sound waves that propagated in the coupled photon\u2013baryon plasma of the early universe before recombination. They left a characteristic comoving length scale (about 150 Mpc) in the cosmic microwave background and the large-scale distribution of galaxies, providing a standard ruler for cosmological distance measurements."},{"id":"b0ebe8a2-ed86-4c14-bfdd-5860e15b18bc","l":"Baryons","d":1,"p":"ca7e6e75-307c-4709-ad9e-9bb1f153fede","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Baryons are composite hadrons made of three valence quarks bound by the strong interaction (QCD), forming color\u2011singlet, half\u2011integer\u2013spin fermions that carry baryon number +1; common examples are the proton and neutron."},{"id":"5456ab2d-da9c-42aa-8940-44ae73ce2005","l":"Beam code development & simulation techniques","d":1,"p":"cd322dee-17c3-4f48-81b4-5fbca9f2c432","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Beam code development & simulation techniques are the design and implementation of computational tools and numerical methods for modeling the dynamics, transport, and interactions of particle or radiation beams in accelerators, beamlines, plasmas, and materials. They cover algorithm choices (e.g., particle\u2011in\u2011cell, envelope, Vlasov, and tracking codes), numerical accuracy and stability, parallelization, diagnostics, and validation/benchmarking to predict beam optics, collective effects (space\u2011charge, wakefields), and experimental performance."},{"id":"f8f21b09-696c-40cb-a4d6-b7f8505fba32","l":"Beam control","d":1,"p":"b3310113-551e-4c5e-9263-28f393ff09f0","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Beam control is the set of techniques and devices used to steer, focus, shape, modulate, and stabilize beams of particles or electromagnetic radiation so they have prescribed spatial, temporal, spectral, and polarization properties. It includes magnetic/electrostatic optics, lenses and mirrors, adaptive optics and phase/amplitude modulators, plus diagnostics and feedback systems for alignment and stability."},{"id":"94544fbf-91a0-41ad-8ff2-375e55bd4ae0","l":"Beam countermeasures","d":1,"p":"cd322dee-17c3-4f48-81b4-5fbca9f2c432","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Beam countermeasures are techniques and technologies used to reduce, deflect, or neutralize the effects of an incident electromagnetic or particle beam by absorbing, scattering, disrupting its coherence, or diverting its trajectory. Examples include passive absorbers and reflective coatings, active adaptive optics or plasma/ionized gas generation, opposing beams or electromagnetic fields, and induced turbulence to attenuate or disperse beam propagation."},{"id":"18b5e187-7220-48af-8739-8746959278d5","l":"Beam diagnostics","d":1,"p":"b3310113-551e-4c5e-9263-28f393ff09f0","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Beam diagnostics are the instruments and measurement techniques used to monitor and characterize particle or photon beams in accelerators and beamlines. They provide quantitative information on beam intensity/current, position, size and profile, emittance (phase-space), energy and energy spread, bunch length/timing and polarization to enable beam tuning, stabilization and machine protection."},{"id":"49f5ead8-f4f2-4f66-bba5-08bc3c5a9b50","l":"Beam impedance","d":1,"p":"cd322dee-17c3-4f48-81b4-5fbca9f2c432","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Beam impedance is the complex, frequency-dependent transfer function that relates a beam\u2019s current to the electromagnetic voltage (longitudinal) or transverse force induced in accelerator structures, i.e., how the beam excites and is affected by wakefields in its surroundings. It quantifies energy loss, tune shifts, and the tendency toward collective instabilities in a particle accelerator."},{"id":"fa96d67a-a31e-48ef-9ea7-b536ed3eb1c8","l":"Beam injection, extraction & transport","d":1,"p":"b3310113-551e-4c5e-9263-28f393ff09f0","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Beam injection, extraction, and transport are the techniques and systems used to introduce a charged-particle beam into an accelerator or beamline, guide and preserve its properties as it travels, and then remove or deliver it to a target or another stage. These processes use beam optics and electromagnetic elements (dipoles, quadrupoles, RF cavities, septa/kickers), vacuum and diagnostics to control trajectory, focus, timing, energy and losses so beam quality and alignment are maintained."},{"id":"78e3e9c0-dd10-495e-bf2b-851b1d23180f","l":"Beam instabilities","d":1,"p":"cd322dee-17c3-4f48-81b4-5fbca9f2c432","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Beam instabilities are processes in charged-particle beams where small perturbations grow in time due to collective self-fields and coupling to the accelerator environment, degrading beam quality or causing particle loss. They occur in transverse or longitudinal modes and are driven by mechanisms such as space charge, wakefields/impedance, resistive-wall effects, and beam\u2013beam interactions."},{"id":"09143feb-ebc3-4fb5-b41d-931b440d05b2","l":"Beam loss","d":1,"p":"b3310113-551e-4c5e-9263-28f393ff09f0","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Beam loss is the escape or removal of particles from a charged-particle beam in an accelerator or transport line when they deviate from the intended trajectory or energy (due to scattering, interactions with residual gas, magnetic errors, etc.), causing those particles to strike accelerator components. It is quantified as a fraction or rate/power of particles lost and is minimized to avoid component damage, radiation activation, and degradation of beam quality."},{"id":"a9c20b4d-69a1-40f1-81c1-86b01859b80c","l":"Beam optics","d":1,"p":"cd322dee-17c3-4f48-81b4-5fbca9f2c432","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Beam optics is the study of how beams of particles or light propagate, are focused, steered and shaped by optical or electromagnetic elements. It treats beam trajectories and envelopes using ray/phase\u2011space methods (e.g., transfer matrices, Twiss parameters, Gaussian beam theory) to control size, divergence and emittance."},{"id":"ab248660-57b1-49b9-aac8-c4590f1bc10a","l":"Beam processes","d":1,"p":"cd322dee-17c3-4f48-81b4-5fbca9f2c432","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Beam processes are the physical mechanisms and dynamics that govern the creation, acceleration, transport, focusing, stability, and interactions of particle or photon beams. They include single-particle motion and collective effects, scattering and energy loss, radiation emission, beam\u2013matter and beam\u2013beam interactions, and instabilities important in accelerators, light sources, and plasma devices."},{"id":"f32e5062-d52a-407d-817e-8bf76cb9cd35","l":"Bent-core liquid crystals","d":1,"p":"63f7a3cf-31b7-4d5c-bbcc-58d679dd5139","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Bent-core (or \"banana-shaped\") liquid crystals are mesophases formed by anisotropic molecules whose rigid cores are bent rather than rodlike. The molecular bend promotes transverse dipoles and distinctive packing that can produce polar, chiral, biaxial, and ferroelectric or antiferroelectric smectic and columnar phases even from achiral molecules."},{"id":"486fd198-240a-4fef-a8a8-60d4cb9ed665","l":"Beta decay","d":1,"p":"1cf75de2-e14b-41cd-b733-8f704652db33","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Beta decay is a weak-interaction radioactive process in which a nucleus converts a neutron to a proton (\u03b2\u2212 decay) or a proton to a neutron (\u03b2+ decay), emitting respectively an electron or positron together with an antineutrino or neutrino; the mass number is unchanged while the atomic number changes by \u00b11. A related mode is electron capture, where an orbital electron is captured by the nucleus converting a proton to a neutron and emitting a neutrino."},{"id":"39e56bd1-5df4-4310-86f7-0652e86ef10c","l":"Bethe-Salpeter equation","d":1,"p":"a0dcc4fb-34a0-4892-a60a-cfb14c0f73e8","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Particles & Fields","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Bethe\u2013Salpeter equation is a covariant integral equation in quantum field theory that determines the two-particle Green's function and the bound-state (or scattering) amplitude by relating it to a two-particle irreducible interaction kernel. Its solutions \u2014 the Bethe\u2013Salpeter amplitudes \u2014 generalize nonrelativistic wavefunctions to include relativistic, retardation, and particle\u2011creation/annihilation effects."},{"id":"7e4f22e5-2958-4e9f-8810-793a09459b61","l":"Biaxial liquid crystals","d":1,"p":"63f7a3cf-31b7-4d5c-bbcc-58d679dd5139","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Biaxial liquid crystals are mesophases in which the anisotropic molecules or local ordering have two distinct, mutually perpendicular preferred orientations, so the material has three unequal principal directions. This lower-symmetry ordering (unlike uniaxial nematics) produces different physical and optical properties along each principal axis and is described by a second-rank alignment tensor with three distinct eigenvalues."},{"id":"087aaf8a-99a5-414e-b2a3-db1b3b8383ae","l":"Bifurcation analysis","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Bifurcation analysis studies how the qualitative behavior of a dynamical system (the number and stability of fixed points, periodic orbits, or other invariant sets) changes as one or more parameters are varied. It identifies and classifies bifurcation points where small parameter changes produce sudden structural changes, such as the creation or annihilation of equilibria or the onset of oscillations."},{"id":"7077df72-2381-46cd-a858-2305519b9fbd","l":"Big bang nucleosynthesis","d":1,"p":"26ed7afc-8635-4637-a339-8d0a7cb75be1","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Big Bang nucleosynthesis is the set of nuclear reactions in the hot, dense early universe (roughly from seconds to a few minutes after the Big Bang) that built the light nuclei\u2014mainly deuterium, helium-3, helium-4 and lithium-7\u2014as the plasma expanded and cooled. The predicted primordial abundances are sensitive to the baryon-to-photon ratio and other cosmological parameters, providing a key observational test of cosmology and particle physics."},{"id":"db452e3b-02bf-444d-b532-06ce3bf23bdf","l":"Binary stars","d":1,"p":"b6d90b06-7836-486e-8e77-221ad2624e1e","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Binary stars are two stars gravitationally bound to each other that orbit their common center of mass. Their orbital motions (observed as visual, spectroscopic, eclipsing, or contact systems) allow direct determination of stellar masses and, in close pairs, can lead to mass transfer and other interaction phenomena."},{"id":"80225ff1-75fb-4e39-9e94-f565c3933b4e","l":"Binding energy & masses","d":1,"p":"1cf75de2-e14b-41cd-b733-8f704652db33","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Binding energy is the amount of energy that must be supplied to separate a bound system into its free constituents; it is taken positive and equals the energy required to break the binding. Because energy contributes to mass, a bound system\u2019s rest mass is less than the sum of its constituents\u2019 rest masses by the mass defect \u0394m = E_binding/c^2, i.e. M_system = \u2211m_constituents \u2212 E_binding/c^2."},{"id":"eae7e56e-ad28-455e-9169-552cf35f1508","l":"Bioacoustics","d":1,"p":"40a5bd01-6544-4502-8321-458c33878bf3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Bioacoustics is the study of sound production, propagation, and reception in living organisms and biological systems, applying acoustic principles to understand communication, sensing (e.g., echolocation), and physiological mechanisms. It combines acoustics, physiology, and ecology for applications from animal behavior and environmental monitoring to medical diagnostics."},{"id":"1fd60b07-6823-4217-b0c4-df9ad90d9ea0","l":"Biochemistry","d":1,"p":"4455b073-c3a2-4a0b-923e-98d7ec0f3b9d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Biochemistry is the study of the chemical substances and reactions that occur in living organisms, focusing on the structure, function, and interactions of biomolecules (proteins, nucleic acids, lipids, and carbohydrates) that underlie metabolism, signaling, and genetic information flow. It applies chemical and physical principles\u2014thermodynamics, kinetics, and structural methods\u2014to explain how molecular processes produce biological function."},{"id":"5dae75ae-8760-4316-baad-414c2eeaf2be","l":"Bioconvection","d":1,"p":"21804fac-0546-45ed-98d5-b6af0f0d3228","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Bioconvection is the spontaneous formation of convective flows and patterns in a fluid containing motile microorganisms, caused when biased swimming (e.g., upward gravitaxis or phototaxis) concentrates cells into a dense upper layer that becomes gravitationally unstable and overturns. It couples the organisms' orientation and motility to the fluid's hydrodynamics, producing plumes, cellular patterns, or chaotic mixing."},{"id":"3ec5c202-988d-4eb9-bd75-d51f77fbe15a","l":"Biofuels","d":1,"p":"af9f5a05-6917-40cf-a2ff-5520ff506935","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Biofuels are energy carriers produced from biomass\u2014recently living plants, algae, or organic waste\u2014converted into liquid (e.g., ethanol, biodiesel), gaseous (biogas) or solid fuels by biological, chemical or thermochemical processes. They are used to supply heat, electricity and transportation energy and are considered renewable because the CO2 released on combustion is at least partly offset by the carbon absorbed during feedstock growth."},{"id":"06acf1f4-0f88-4c17-bc06-52d0e4a3dfbd","l":"Bioinformatics","d":1,"p":"1e0c099a-9f2a-4e47-b3f1-7faa28b6a506","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Bioinformatics is the interdisciplinary field that develops and applies computational, statistical, and mathematical tools to store, analyze, and interpret large-scale biological data such as DNA/RNA sequences, protein structures, and gene expression profiles. It aims to discover patterns and relationships, predict biological function and structure, and build quantitative models of biological systems."},{"id":"5151d67c-cd9b-47c1-8098-5d41f48d8bf0","l":"Biological information processing","d":1,"p":"376c5d7d-24b3-40a2-a82d-a517ac25dbad","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Biological information processing is the study of how living systems physically encode, transmit, store and transform information\u2014from molecular recognition and gene regulatory networks to electrical and chemical signaling in neurons\u2014using mechanisms like binding, chemical reactions and ion flows. It emphasizes how these processes perform computation and communication reliably and efficiently despite noise, limited resources and thermodynamic constraints."},{"id":"f17debef-8633-40da-9a61-440f1f15ad06","l":"Biological liquid crystals","d":1,"p":"63f7a3cf-31b7-4d5c-bbcc-58d679dd5139","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Biological liquid crystals are liquid\u2011crystalline (typically lyotropic) phases formed by anisotropic biomolecules\u2014lipids, DNA, filamentous proteins and polysaccharides\u2014that exhibit long\u2011range orientational (and sometimes positional) order while remaining fluid. These self\u2011assembled states (nematic, smectic, cholesteric, columnar, etc.) organize cellular structures and tissues and govern mechanical and optical properties in many biological systems."},{"id":"566341e3-164a-49b8-93ee-5881dfce1666","l":"Biological network methods","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Biological network methods represent biological systems as graphs (nodes such as genes, proteins, cells, or organisms and edges as interactions or flows) and apply graph theory, statistical inference, and dynamical-systems tools to characterize their structure, function, and dynamics. They encompass network reconstruction/inference, topology and motif analysis, community detection, centrality measures, and modeling of processes like signaling, gene regulation, and epidemic spread."},{"id":"477d725e-5189-40d4-8763-6f9b580b1113","l":"Biomimetic & bio-inspired materials","d":1,"p":"c765ac18-ded5-4ef0-a41a-22be13741559","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Biomimetic (bio-inspired) materials are engineered materials whose composition, structure, or fabrication is deliberately modeled on biological systems\u2014using hierarchical architecture, self\u2011assembly, and responsive chemistry\u2014to reproduce or extend natural functions such as toughness, adhesion, self\u2011healing, actuation, wetting control, and sensing. They translate biological design principles into synthetic systems to achieve multifunctional, adaptive, and energy\u2011efficient behavior across length scales."},{"id":"00960c4d-78bb-469c-8ffb-cda89f4fe20c","l":"Biomolecular processes","d":1,"p":"5be420fe-48f0-4430-a63d-9d611d837d6e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Biomolecular processes are the physical and chemical interactions and transformations of biological molecules (proteins, nucleic acids, lipids, carbohydrates and small metabolites) \u2014 including folding, binding, catalysis, transport and self-assembly \u2014 that produce cellular function. They are governed by thermodynamics, kinetics and stochastic fluctuations and are analyzed using concepts from molecular physics, statistical mechanics and physical chemistry."},{"id":"6c0e8bb5-a5a0-4f28-8d4a-4e7ace85a023","l":"Biomolecules","d":1,"p":"e7ff71e0-89cf-438a-8e28-d449f5629207","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Biomolecules are organic molecules produced by living organisms\u2014chiefly proteins, nucleic acids, carbohydrates, and lipids\u2014that serve as the structural, catalytic, informational, and energetic components of cells. Their functions and assemblies arise from molecular structure and intermolecular forces and are described by chemical kinetics, thermodynamics, and statistical mechanics."},{"id":"66fe8601-6dc2-4a97-8f17-30c93bdb1a49","l":"Birds","d":1,"p":"91278658-51b4-4d8e-a936-7cfad1266625","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Birds are warm-blooded, feathered, winged vertebrates (class Aves) with lightweight skeletons and high power-to-weight ratios; many species generate lift and thrust by flapping wings to achieve powered flight. Their wing kinematics, wing and body morphology, and respiratory and muscle physiology are central to studies in aerodynamics, biomechanics, and fluid dynamics."},{"id":"10c33644-0171-4613-927f-9fa35e3f6f00","l":"Block copolymers","d":1,"p":"fea036a1-9c29-4872-ab2c-d052949f7c03","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Block copolymers are macromolecules made of two or more chemically distinct polymer blocks joined covalently in a single chain. Because the different blocks are mutually incompatible but tethered together, they undergo microphase separation and self-assemble into periodic nanoscale morphologies (e.g., lamellae, cylinders, spheres, gyroid) whose form and spacing are set by chain architecture, degree of polymerization, and the interaction strength between blocks."},{"id":"74b33442-1c5d-43fc-b908-5a2e0537c9b7","l":"Boiling","d":1,"p":"b72a2d57-f47e-4e87-9a43-f8320d817bae","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Boiling is the bulk vaporization of a liquid that occurs when its vapor pressure at a given temperature equals the external pressure, allowing vapor bubbles to form throughout the liquid and rise to the surface. It takes place at the pressure-dependent boiling point and requires input of the latent heat of vaporization, distinct from surface evaporation."},{"id":"c56eb365-c1a7-44a2-b81d-37845faabac2","l":"Boolean satisfiability problem","d":1,"p":"b96ba7a9-cce1-470c-b80c-6dadb2c6b8e9","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"The Boolean satisfiability problem (SAT) asks whether there exists an assignment of true/false values to the variables of a given Boolean formula that makes the formula evaluate to true. It is the canonical NP-complete decision problem and underpins many algorithms and complexity results in computer science and physics-based computational problems."},{"id":"acc87e43-4992-4905-bf8d-10451a110790","l":"Boron nitride","d":1,"p":"7a86b9d8-3162-4279-9ca5-90ebad07be7c","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Boron nitride is a binary ceramic compound (BN) of boron and nitrogen that occurs in several polymorphs\u2014most notably hexagonal BN (h\u2011BN, a layered, graphene\u2011like insulator), cubic BN (c\u2011BN, a diamond\u2011like ultrahard material), and wurtzite BN. These forms share high thermal and chemical stability, a wide electronic band gap, excellent thermal conductivity, and, depending on structure, lubricating (h\u2011BN) or extreme hardness (c\u2011BN) properties."},{"id":"4254adf6-872d-4868-9931-a2a3e84b566e","l":"Bose-Einstein condensates","d":1,"p":"db7ba2bd-700a-4b0b-b444-b873c97ff818","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"A Bose\u2013Einstein condensate (BEC) is a phase of matter formed when a dilute gas of bosons is cooled to temperatures near absolute zero so that a macroscopic fraction of the particles occupies the system's lowest quantum state. The result is a single coherent matter wave exhibiting macroscopic quantum behavior such as long-range coherence and, in some systems, superfluidity."},{"id":"977ff312-56f0-422a-ae7a-f21bcc27b831","l":"Bose gases","d":1,"p":"01af01cc-b889-4c31-9073-c4ec759cd252","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"A Bose gas is a quantum gas made of bosons (integer-spin particles) that obey Bose\u2013Einstein statistics, allowing many particles to occupy the same single-particle quantum state. At low temperatures this can produce a Bose\u2013Einstein condensate \u2014 a macroscopic occupation of the ground state \u2014 and, when interactions are present, phenomena like superfluidity."},{"id":"b9043522-5a73-41b5-89d0-a22194666868","l":"Bosonization","d":1,"p":"a1083ad2-a154-449d-a9ff-59bf46db1c7f","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Bosonization is a nonperturbative method in one spatial dimension that maps fermionic degrees of freedom onto equivalent bosonic fields, representing fermion creation/annihilation operators as exponentials of collective bosonic modes. This exact 1D duality converts interacting fermion problems into bosonic theories (e.g., Luttinger liquid or sine\u2013Gordon), enabling tractable calculations of low-energy correlations and dynamics."},{"id":"71e104e2-448f-42ec-a9be-bbaec6758896","l":"Bosons","d":1,"p":"d46f32eb-17b3-4d09-be59-fc3461e542db","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Bosons are particles with integer spin that obey Bose\u2013Einstein statistics, which allow any number of identical bosons to occupy the same quantum state. Examples include force carriers (photons, gluons, W and Z bosons), the Higgs, and composite bosons (e.g., helium-4 atoms), and they underlie phenomena such as Bose\u2013Einstein condensation."},{"id":"d9cf5fe6-bb7c-429c-8f10-327b2e72673f","l":"Bottom quark","d":1,"p":"683890e6-25e0-4d29-9686-308d5800fda7","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"The bottom (or beauty) quark is a third-generation, down-type elementary quark with electric charge \u22121/3 e and a mass of order 4.2 GeV/c\u00b2; it participates in the strong, weak, and electromagnetic interactions and hadronizes into B mesons and bottom baryons. Its weak decays (through CKM mixing) are important probes of flavor physics and CP violation."},{"id":"09561f32-b014-40f0-8600-f7dd008af7bf","l":"Boundary element method","d":1,"p":"1e0c099a-9f2a-4e47-b3f1-7faa28b6a506","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"The boundary element method (BEM) is a numerical technique that reformulates a linear partial differential equation as an integral equation over the problem boundary using the problem's Green's function, thereby reducing the spatial dimensionality by one. By discretizing only the boundary into elements and solving the resulting dense system for boundary values, BEM is efficient for homogeneous or infinite-domain problems (e.g., electrostatics, acoustics, elastostatics) but is less convenient for strongly inhomogeneous or nonlinear problems."},{"id":"6c97eab8-c8f6-4c2e-9228-deb3732737c0","l":"Boundary layer receptivity, stability & separation","d":1,"p":"0c2f74cc-42b0-4b3a-b470-cd50aa7d0f89","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Boundary-layer receptivity and stability describe how the thin viscous layer of fluid along a solid surface converts external perturbations (acoustic waves, freestream turbulence, surface roughness) into internal disturbances and whether those disturbances grow or decay, thereby controlling the laminar-to-turbulent transition. Boundary-layer separation occurs when an adverse pressure gradient decelerates the near-wall flow enough to cause flow reversal and detachment from the surface, strongly modifying the local instability and transition dynamics."},{"id":"0b2ca6ea-0697-4de2-b157-68ce3ac12983","l":"Bound states","d":1,"p":"ee82ad04-6c47-46a4-a70d-c9a7d7989f6a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"A bound state is a configuration of two or more particles that remain confined together by their mutual interactions and cannot separate without supplying extra energy. In quantum mechanics it appears as a normalizable stationary wavefunction with energy below the continuum (dissociation) threshold, producing discrete energy levels."},{"id":"5b07db24-f5c0-4db4-b846-6ea707d0dc41","l":"Brain","d":1,"p":"a32482f9-6a8e-410f-80c0-204520778cbf","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"The brain is the central organ of the nervous system: a soft-matter network of neurons and glial cells whose structured connectivity and synapses implement information processing and memory. It functions as a nonlinear, dissipative, nonequilibrium system that uses electrochemical signaling and metabolic energy to generate coordinated behavior, cognition, and homeostatic control."},{"id":"9d738c0f-a468-453c-9fdc-a96d033ea104","l":"Brain network","d":1,"p":"d87d9c51-bdc2-43bb-99c3-6e5345a7f631","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"A brain network is a representation of the brain as a graph of nodes (cells, neuronal populations, or anatomical regions) linked by edges that denote physical connections (synapses, axonal tracts) or statistical/causal interactions (functional or effective connectivity). Such networks are analyzed with graph-theoretic and dynamical-systems methods to characterize topology, information flow, and collective activity underlying perception, cognition, and behavior."},{"id":"23492d7c-51a8-42d3-a642-e2c904cbbfc5","l":"Braneworlds","d":1,"p":"cbc41431-fba0-4ff0-8134-2ded6e598922","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Braneworlds are theoretical models in which our observable universe is a lower-dimensional \"brane\" (a membrane-like surface) embedded in a higher-dimensional space called the bulk, with Standard Model fields confined to the brane while gravity can propagate into the extra dimensions. These scenarios are used to address problems like the hierarchy between the weak and Planck scales and predict signatures such as modified gravity at short distances and Kaluza\u2013Klein excitations."},{"id":"0fd0ac3b-8eee-41e1-8fa0-2ec7ac1488af","l":"Breakup reactions","d":1,"p":"bbb0ce0d-36da-4da5-a443-df064f36aa17","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Breakup reactions are collision processes in which a composite projectile or target (for example a nucleus, molecule, or weakly bound cluster) dissociates into two or more fragments due to its interaction with another particle or an external field. They provide information on internal structure and reaction dynamics and can occur promptly (direct breakup) or via intermediate sequential decay."},{"id":"4280af65-b2d4-4298-942f-0f11b52a4e1f","l":"Breathers","d":1,"p":"2375c1d2-a3fc-4d5b-8fde-4f9029004962","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Nonlinear Dynamics"],"def":"Breathers are spatially localized, time-periodic nonlinear excitations of continuous or discrete media\u2014solutions of nonlinear wave or lattice equations that trap energy locally while oscillating without dispersing. They arise from a balance of nonlinearity and dispersion or lattice discreteness (e.g., sine\u2013Gordon breathers and discrete intrinsic localized modes)."},{"id":"6efb152f-e313-4269-b7dd-a830f5523c53","l":"Brownian motion","d":1,"p":"c6d9db47-7b2d-4d3b-868a-606b65695e3c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Brownian motion is the random, jittery movement of microscopic particles suspended in a fluid caused by incessant, thermally driven collisions with the fluid's molecules. It is modeled as a stochastic (Wiener) process whose mean-square displacement grows linearly in time (\u27e8\u0394x^2\u27e9 = 2Dt in one dimension) and underlies diffusion."},{"id":"a82823da-f6dd-4de8-a2a9-c3bd144204e2","l":"Bubble dynamics","d":1,"p":"b72a2d57-f47e-4e87-9a43-f8320d817bae","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Bubble dynamics is the study of the motion and time\u2011evolution of gas or vapor bubbles in a surrounding fluid, including their nucleation, growth, oscillation, translation, deformation, breakup and collapse, driven by pressure, inertia, surface tension, viscosity and heat/mass transfer. It encompasses single- and multi-bubble behavior and phenomena such as cavitation, acoustic emissions and sonoluminescence."},{"id":"d7032c56-439d-473f-8f03-eecad62b08e4","l":"Cabibbo\u2013Kobayashi\u2013Maskawa matrix","d":1,"p":"ee487445-39e4-4995-9543-fea1735bcbb2","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"The Cabibbo\u2013Kobayashi\u2013Maskawa (CKM) matrix is the unitary 3\u00d73 matrix in the Standard Model that relates quark mass eigenstates to weak (flavor) eigenstates and therefore parameterizes the strengths of charged-current transitions between up-type and down-type quarks. Its complex phase provides the source of CP violation observed in the quark sector."},{"id":"5cec7977-528a-4d9f-98fe-4ce85b717b60","l":"Capacitance","d":1,"p":"41dd58dd-d35c-46e8-9594-d7e05b458da6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Capacitance is the property of a conductor or pair of conductors that quantifies the amount of electric charge stored per unit potential difference, defined by C = Q/V and measured in farads (F). It depends on the conductors' geometry and the intervening dielectric, and a capacitor of capacitance C stores electrostatic energy U = \u00bdCV^2."},{"id":"246ded35-faf5-4395-a28d-c69e648998a0","l":"Capillary interactions","d":1,"p":"cc59c6fb-acbb-4237-953b-b874fe5dbd4a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Capillary interactions are forces between particles or objects mediated by deformations of a fluid interface, produced by surface tension and wetting (contact-line) effects. Their magnitude and sign depend on the shape and overlap of the menisci, contact angles, and external fields (e.g., gravity), and they drive assembly and motion of particles at interfaces."},{"id":"7a497938-f622-436c-a518-05921f104f95","l":"Capillary waves","d":1,"p":"0a677b11-f06d-4ced-9385-cf0bf1061a59","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Capillary waves are short-wavelength surface waves on a liquid whose primary restoring force is surface tension rather than gravity, producing the fine ripples seen on water. For deep water their dispersion relation (neglecting gravity) is \u03c9\u00b2 = (\u03c3/\u03c1) k\u00b3, and including gravity the general form is \u03c9\u00b2 = gk + (\u03c3/\u03c1) k\u00b3, where \u03c3 is surface tension, \u03c1 the fluid density and k the wavenumber."},{"id":"bfbc8007-4c38-4ccb-a698-eeb79bc1651c","l":"Carbon-based materials","d":1,"p":"81194f70-bbca-4041-b57e-29338e588cac","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Carbon-based materials are solids and polymers whose structure and properties are dominated by networks of carbon atoms bonded together in different hybridizations (sp, sp2, sp3), including allotropes such as diamond, graphite/graphene, fullerenes, and carbon nanotubes as well as amorphous carbon and organic polymers. The variety of carbon bonding and bonding geometries gives rise to a wide range of mechanical, electronic, thermal, and chemical behaviors, from insulating to metallic and from ultra-hard to flexible."},{"id":"2e9e0c49-ce2d-4029-a89d-c5d0535574a2","l":"Carbon capture & utilization","d":1,"p":"6dca4b7f-ff80-45b8-ab18-bbba041e3393","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Carbon capture and utilization (CCU) refers to technologies that capture CO2 from point sources or the atmosphere and convert it into useful products\u2014such as fuels, chemicals, or building materials\u2014using chemical, electrochemical, or photochemical processes. Unlike carbon capture and storage (CCS), CCU emphasizes reuse rather than permanent sequestration, and its net climate benefit depends on the energy inputs and lifecycle emissions."},{"id":"5224a1aa-fe0a-47eb-b2f6-9e06d8ac3025","l":"Cardiovascular networks","d":1,"p":"d87d9c51-bdc2-43bb-99c3-6e5345a7f631","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Cardiovascular networks are the interconnected system of the heart and branching blood vessels that transport blood throughout an organism, modeled as a pulsatile fluid-flow network whose flows and pressures are set by vessel geometry, elasticity, and regulatory feedback. Physicists study them with fluid dynamics, network theory and multiscale models to understand flow distribution, energy dissipation, mass/solute transport, and adaptive remodeling."},{"id":"de286550-5a0b-44c3-b275-9d22a1e2b7f1","l":"Carrier dynamics","d":1,"p":"36754399-ec2b-4962-bb07-ca12c79834e8","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Carrier dynamics is the time-dependent behavior and transport of mobile charge carriers (electrons, holes, ions) in a material or device under applied fields and interactions. It encompasses processes such as generation, scattering, relaxation, diffusion and recombination and is characterized by parameters like mobility, diffusion coefficient, scattering rates and carrier lifetimes."},{"id":"c279e478-0d89-436c-a293-ccef08fff0ac","l":"Carrier generation & recombination","d":1,"p":"36754399-ec2b-4962-bb07-ca12c79834e8","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Carrier generation and recombination are the processes that create and annihilate charge carriers (electrons and holes) in a semiconductor: generation produces electron\u2013hole pairs via thermal excitation, optical absorption, or impact ionization, while recombination removes them through radiative emission, defect-assisted (Shockley\u2013Read\u2013Hall) or Auger mechanisms. The competition between these rates sets the carrier densities, lifetimes, and thus the electrical and optical behavior of semiconductor devices."},{"id":"3d42af0b-696d-4683-a2c7-10f55239f3f0","l":"Cascades","d":1,"p":"f6b0d9b5-f1f6-42b0-89df-1b70c8ec3e8b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Cascades are processes in which a quantity (energy, particles, excitations, or reactions) is transferred, redistributed, or multiplied through a sequence of successive interactions or stages, often producing a chain reaction or scale-to-scale transfer. Examples include energy cascades in turbulence (direct or inverse transfer across wavenumbers), particle showers from high-energy collisions or cosmic rays, and sequential decay chains in nuclear and particle physics."},{"id":"217a1e00-6ed1-4b16-ae8c-736a95e4da00","l":"Casimir effect & related phenomena","d":1,"p":"30e65d01-6cd5-442b-889f-ddc0c3c28157","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"The Casimir effect is a quantum electrodynamic phenomenon in which boundary conditions or material properties alter the spectrum of vacuum (zero-point) electromagnetic fluctuations, producing a measurable force between neutral objects (famously, closely spaced conducting plates). Related phenomena include the Casimir\u2013Polder force between atoms and surfaces, the Lifshitz description of van der Waals/Casimir interactions in real materials and at finite temperature, and the dynamical Casimir effect where time\u2011dependent boundaries or media convert vacuum fluctuations into real photons."},{"id":"ae595808-9b56-482f-9583-acb10acf7da2","l":"Cavity methods","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Cavity methods are analytical and computational techniques, developed in spin-glass theory, that analyze disordered many-body systems by removing a site or degree of freedom (creating a \"cavity\") and deriving self-consistent equations for the distribution of the effective fields when the site is reinserted. They yield mean-field descriptions and message-passing algorithms (e.g., belief propagation) used to compute thermodynamic properties and phase behavior on sparse random graphs and constraint-satisfaction problems."},{"id":"3e04a8a2-1199-4410-801c-ac848b0bbda8","l":"Cells","d":1,"p":"e7ff71e0-89cf-438a-8e28-d449f5629207","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"A cell is a small, bounded unit of structure or function: in condensed-matter physics a unit cell is the smallest repeating volume whose periodic arrangement generates a crystal lattice, while in biophysics a (biological) cell is the membrane-bound microscopic unit of life containing the molecular machinery and compartments whose physical behavior is studied."},{"id":"f86aed6f-8ade-4beb-a7f1-aa568e640cd6","l":"Cellular automata","d":1,"p":"1e0c099a-9f2a-4e47-b3f1-7faa28b6a506","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"A cellular automaton is a discrete dynamical system consisting of a regular lattice of cells, each of which takes a value from a finite set and is updated synchronously in discrete time according to a local rule that depends on the states of a specified neighborhood. Simple local update rules (deterministic or stochastic) can generate complex, emergent spatiotemporal patterns, making cellular automata useful models in statistical physics, pattern formation, and computation."},{"id":"63a150d6-b0c6-4af9-a20c-21c91b4e8c1e","l":"Chain stiffness","d":1,"p":"5114e95c-b4a7-458f-a2f7-b49a98b828cd","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Chain stiffness is the resistance of a filament or polymer chain to bending, determining how quickly its tangent direction decorrelates along the contour; it is commonly quantified by the persistence length or by the bending modulus (flexural rigidity). Stiffer chains have larger persistence lengths and behave more rodlike, while flexible chains have small persistence lengths and behave like random coils."},{"id":"a00bed04-dbe9-4c1c-aa71-7e82a61f7a96","l":"Channel flow","d":1,"p":"0a677b11-f06d-4ced-9385-cf0bf1061a59","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Channel flow is the motion of a fluid confined to a long passage bounded by solid walls (for example between parallel plates, inside a duct, or in a river channel), driven by a pressure gradient, gravity, or an imposed inlet flow. It is often studied in steady, fully developed regimes \u2014 laminar (Poiseuille) or turbulent \u2014 and is characterized by wall shear, boundary layers, and streamwise velocity profiles."},{"id":"1ec03196-e8d2-40b7-891a-fc8e4a1cbbdb","l":"Chaos & nonlinear dynamics","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Chaos and nonlinear dynamics is the branch of physics that studies systems governed by nonlinear equations whose interactions produce qualitatively new behavior\u2014sensitivity to initial conditions, multiple attractors, bifurcations, and complex spatiotemporal patterns\u2014that cannot be understood by linear superposition. It explains how deterministic rules can yield unpredictable, aperiodic motion (chaos) and organizes the transitions between regular and chaotic regimes."},{"id":"3618e253-41e1-4314-9ce0-e8db6246126a","l":"Chaotic advection","d":1,"p":"bb2351de-d2a6-4d4c-a33e-9396bc991c32","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Nonlinear Dynamics"],"def":"Chaotic advection is the phenomenon in which passive tracers or fluid particles follow deterministic but chaotic trajectories in a flow, so nearby particles separate exponentially and trajectories show sensitive dependence on initial conditions. It typically arises in time-dependent or spatially complex laminar flows and produces efficient stretching-and-folding that greatly enhances mixing of scalars."},{"id":"4b51a2bf-3140-4387-90ec-91ffb4f9d381","l":"Chaotic systems","d":1,"p":"c485f44d-2cc4-4e6d-a20b-0899fb9e53a1","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Nonlinear Dynamics"],"def":"Chaotic systems are deterministic dynamical systems whose trajectories show extreme sensitivity to initial conditions, so that arbitrarily small differences grow rapidly (positive Lyapunov exponents) and make long-term prediction effectively impossible. They typically arise from nonlinear interactions and display features such as topological mixing and a dense set of unstable periodic orbits."},{"id":"0c41737e-03d9-4afc-9c7d-33eb99ffbbea","l":"Charge","d":1,"p":"41dd58dd-d35c-46e8-9594-d7e05b458da6","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Charge is an intrinsic property of matter that determines how particles couple to the electromagnetic field; it comes in positive and negative values (quantized in units of the elementary charge e), is conserved, and is measured in coulombs. Charged particles produce electric fields and experience electromagnetic forces (Coulomb and Lorentz forces) that govern their interactions."},{"id":"d5f72b52-2d02-421f-abc9-6c9b9775c5ec","l":"Charged colloids","d":1,"p":"ef0a1bee-2a1f-4f47-aca2-6302db18e67f","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Charged colloids are microscopic particles (nanometre\u2013micrometre) dispersed in a fluid that carry a surface electric charge and are surrounded by a compensating cloud of counterions, together forming an electric double layer. Their interactions are dominated by screened Coulomb (Yukawa) repulsion (and usually van der Waals attraction), which control suspension stability, structure, and dynamics."},{"id":"ff0e7d3e-fab2-47fa-8ea2-17be4df28c44","l":"Charge density waves","d":1,"p":"f708cc58-38ab-4944-8e59-aba8cebdece7","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Charge density waves are collective electronic states in which the conduction-electron density spontaneously develops a spatially periodic modulation, typically accompanied by a matching periodic lattice distortion. They arise from Fermi-surface instabilities or strong electron\u2013phonon coupling (Peierls-like), breaking translational symmetry and producing characteristic collective excitations and transport phenomena such as pinning and sliding."},{"id":"6a67c968-5a26-448e-a158-d4eef9233cab","l":"Charged-particle multiplicity","d":1,"p":"7321e0f7-3b12-45a2-aa4a-28c2a7e03149","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Charged-particle multiplicity is the number of charged particles produced in a single particle-collision event, usually reported either as a total count or as a density (e.g., dN_ch/d\u03b7) versus kinematic variables like pseudorapidity. It is a basic observable used to quantify event activity and study particle-production mechanisms and their dependence on collision energy and geometry."},{"id":"a85bf8ea-b104-41e1-9274-80d62856c0d9","l":"Charged polymers","d":1,"p":"fea036a1-9c29-4872-ab2c-d052949f7c03","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Charged polymers (polyelectrolytes and polyampholytes) are macromolecules whose repeat units carry fixed or dissociable electric charges, so the chain interacts via long-range Coulomb forces and attracts mobile counterions in solution. These electrostatic interactions and ion screening strongly modify chain conformation, dynamics, osmotic properties, and phase behavior compared with neutral polymers."},{"id":"40afdc3c-400c-4d0f-b441-2eb654c8ca9d","l":"Charge dynamics","d":1,"p":"41dd58dd-d35c-46e8-9594-d7e05b458da6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Charge dynamics is the study of how distributions of electric charge and charged particles evolve in time under forces from electric and magnetic fields and from inter-particle interactions, producing currents and collective phenomena. It covers the governing laws (continuity equation, Lorentz force, Maxwell's equations) and material-specific transport processes such as drift, diffusion, recombination, and plasma oscillations."},{"id":"809153e1-3335-4eca-a6da-ed975994dda4","l":"Charge-exchange reactions","d":1,"p":"bbb0ce0d-36da-4da5-a443-df064f36aa17","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Charge-exchange reactions are collisions in which interacting particles exchange electric charge so that one species is converted into another (for example a proton becomes a neutron) while the total mass number of the system stays the same. They occur via exchange of charged carriers (e.g., charged mesons in strong interactions or W bosons in weak processes) and are used to probe isospin and spin\u2013isospin excitations in nuclei and hadrons."},{"id":"56d5d666-6f28-4ad8-afe4-5f9bc776c85c","l":"Charge-transfer insulators","d":1,"p":"29bd219f-a624-4ef5-b4c5-f9049f2be66e","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A charge-transfer insulator is a strongly correlated material whose insulating gap is set by moving an electron from ligand (typically oxygen) p states to transition\u2011metal d states, rather than by excitations between d orbitals. In the Zaanen\u2013Sawatzky\u2013Allen scheme it occurs when the charge\u2011transfer energy \u0394 is smaller than the on\u2011site Coulomb repulsion U (\u0394 < U), as in many transition\u2011metal oxides such as cuprates and nickelates."},{"id":"96fee9f0-72f0-4394-a88a-f0206663000f","l":"Charm quark","d":1,"p":"683890e6-25e0-4d29-9686-308d5800fda7","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"The charm quark is a second-generation elementary fermion in the Standard Model: a color-triplet quark with electric charge +2/3 e and charm quantum number +1 that participates in the strong, electromagnetic and weak interactions. Its mass is roughly 1.3 GeV/c^2, and it appears bound in hadrons such as D mesons and charmonium states (e.g., J/\u03c8)."},{"id":"145dbac8-d9f8-4397-96f0-2372254d18f4","l":"Chemical binding","d":1,"p":"30e65d01-6cd5-442b-889f-ddc0c3c28157","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Chemical binding is the electromagnetic interaction that holds atoms or molecules together by arranging their electrons and nuclei into lower-energy bound states. Different mechanisms\u2014covalent (electron sharing), ionic (electron transfer), metallic, and weaker van der Waals or hydrogen bonds\u2014produce characteristic binding energies and spatial structures."},{"id":"7c6cdbf5-0145-41f4-b17c-5725b4146ab4","l":"Chemical bonding","d":1,"p":"15f520a3-5c6c-4d9e-8975-81d399140562","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Chemical bonding is the set of electromagnetic interactions that hold atoms together in molecules and solids, producing stable bound states at potential-energy minima. These bonds arise from quantum-mechanical effects\u2014principally electron sharing (covalent), electron transfer (ionic), and electron delocalization (metallic and van der Waals-like interactions)\u2014and are characterized by bond energies and lengths determined by Coulomb forces and wavefunction overlap."},{"id":"47c36d5f-7065-49ab-96f1-6ea82575855b","l":"Chemical kinetics, dynamics & catalysis","d":1,"p":"4455b073-c3a2-4a0b-923e-98d7ec0f3b9d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Chemical kinetics, dynamics & catalysis is the study of how and how fast chemical reactions occur, focusing on reaction rates, molecular-level pathways and motions on potential-energy surfaces that determine transition states and mechanisms. Catalysis concerns species or surfaces that provide alternative, lower\u2011energy reaction pathways\u2014stabilizing transition states or enhancing reactant encounters\u2014to increase reaction rates without being consumed."},{"id":"54a532df-b087-4763-b83c-cb2a0c875763","l":"Chemical networks","d":1,"p":"b4d51608-61b8-4e73-8186-ba02e5a41a85","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Chemical networks are representations of interacting chemical species connected by reactions, typically depicted as graphs where nodes are species and edges denote reaction pathways. They include stoichiometry and rate laws to model the kinetics and dynamics of concentration changes, allowing study of steady states, oscillations, and emergent behavior in systems from combustion and atmospheric chemistry to biology and astrochemistry."},{"id":"a8c79e8a-73ed-4190-8e3c-6fa1bef601d1","l":"Chemical reactions","d":1,"p":"12e1040c-7c2c-478a-b466-c979b57fcaea","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Chemical reactions are processes in which atoms and molecules are rearranged by breaking and forming chemical bonds, converting reactants into different products while conserving elements and charge. They involve energy transfer (exothermic or endothermic) and are governed by thermodynamic driving forces and kinetic rates."},{"id":"ede4996f-6a03-4e72-b493-2d674b4ab4ff","l":"Chimera states","d":1,"p":"5b3df46f-4812-4196-8c68-7fb8577cdd80","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nonlinear Dynamics","Networks","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Chimera states are self-organized symmetry-breaking patterns in networks of identical, symmetrically coupled oscillators in which spatially coexisting domains of phase-locked (coherent) oscillation and desynchronized (incoherent) activity persist. They arise in nonlocal or complex coupling and demonstrate how identical units can spontaneously split into synchronous and asynchronous subpopulations."},{"id":"8343e5a7-1846-4f96-9cdd-7b51f99f626c","l":"Chiral magnets","d":1,"p":"58071c68-730c-4a3e-982c-abc9f0cedf67","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Magnetic materials in which broken inversion symmetry and spin\u2013orbit coupling produce an antisymmetric exchange (the Dzyaloshinskii\u2013Moriya interaction), causing spin configurations with a fixed handedness. Such interactions stabilize chiral spin textures like helices, N\u00e9el walls and magnetic skyrmions, giving rise to unusual static and dynamical properties useful in spintronics."},{"id":"78a566e1-e017-4a65-8a92-f8984736bb76","l":"Cholesteric liquid crystals","d":1,"p":"63f7a3cf-31b7-4d5c-bbcc-58d679dd5139","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Cholesteric (or chiral nematic) liquid crystals are a class of liquid-crystalline phase formed by chiral rod-like molecules whose average orientation (director) rotates in space to produce a helical, periodic structure with a well-defined pitch and handedness. This helical ordering causes strong, wavelength- and circular-polarization-selective Bragg reflection, giving rise to vivid structural colors and optical tunability."},{"id":"bacc2571-6830-4a8b-9494-f44c0f8b3a39","l":"Circulatory system","d":1,"p":"0a74688b-2eee-4349-a9bd-e93851fe2c5a","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"The circulatory system is the organism's closed-loop fluid-transport network\u2014a pump (heart), conduits (arteries, veins, capillaries) and a working fluid (blood)\u2014that moves mass, heat, momentum and solutes by pressure gradients. Its behavior is governed by pulsatile, often non-Newtonian fluid dynamics plus vessel resistance and compliance that control flow and exchange at the microvascular level."},{"id":"42791fef-2a1a-4002-81dd-c04d56ae9618","l":"Classical optics","d":1,"p":"23cdcd6b-ae22-4b6d-92cd-24a9a8bd0577","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Classical optics is the branch of optics that describes the propagation and behavior of light\u2014including reflection, refraction, interference, diffraction, polarization, and imaging\u2014using macroscopic ray and wave models. It is based on Maxwell's equations and related wave theory and deliberately neglects inherently quantum phenomena such as photon discreteness."},{"id":"cf6fcf92-5d05-4722-a1b3-c076e8b22b9e","l":"Classical solutions in field theory","d":1,"p":"a1083ad2-a154-449d-a9ff-59bf46db1c7f","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Classical solutions in field theory are field configurations that satisfy the classical Euler\u2013Lagrange field equations (equivalently, stationary points of the action) for given boundary or initial conditions. They include vacua, waves, solitons and instantons and serve as the background trajectories for perturbation theory and semiclassical quantization."},{"id":"ac80909d-0962-428a-b719-a7a28aa28a1d","l":"Classical transport","d":1,"p":"36754399-ec2b-4962-bb07-ca12c79834e8","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Classical transport is the study of how mass, momentum, charge, and energy are carried and redistributed in materials and fluids when quantum effects are negligible. It is described macroscopically by conservation laws and constitutive relations (e.g., Fick's law, Fourier's law, Newtonian viscosity, Navier\u2013Stokes) and microscopically by classical kinetic theory (e.g., the Boltzmann equation), which yield transport coefficients like diffusivity, viscosity, and conductivity."},{"id":"edf4f30c-1a9e-447e-b8ef-444f132ed08c","l":"Clay gels","d":1,"p":"06891d57-1044-487c-ae20-21366d161a97","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Clay gels are colloidal gels formed when charged, plate-like clay particles (e.g., montmorillonite, Laponite) suspended in a solvent attract and arrest into a space-spanning, percolated network, giving a soft solid with a finite yield stress and viscoelastic response. They are non-equilibrium, often thixotropic materials that show slow aging and shear-dependent restructuring (shear-thinning and reversible yielding), relevant in geology and many industrial formulations."},{"id":"0a4e4f65-eef5-4f6d-bc2f-1c25a3173c67","l":"Climate research","d":1,"p":"118dcea0-ad7e-42d7-b32c-2c9f38256be6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics","Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Climate research is the interdisciplinary study of Earth's climate system\u2014atmosphere, oceans, cryosphere, land surface, and biosphere\u2014focusing on the physical processes, energy and mass exchanges, variability, and long-term change driven by natural and external forcings (including anthropogenic greenhouse gases). It uses observations, experiments, theory, and numerical models to understand, attribute, and predict climate behavior across time scales from seasons to millennia."},{"id":"ee57bda0-62db-4110-872d-c84d206dca0d","l":"Cloaking","d":1,"p":"4c4f4d87-e837-4d65-970d-f5323698a5f1","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Cloaking is the deliberate manipulation of wave or field propagation so an object produces little or no detectable signature, typically by steering incident waves around it or canceling its scattered fields. It is implemented for specific probes (e.g., electromagnetic, acoustic, thermal) using engineered structures such as metamaterials or scattering-cancellation devices."},{"id":"c5b596fc-84f5-4464-b9ae-bc11ff6d2b0a","l":"Closed-orbit theory","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Closed-orbit theory is a semiclassical framework that explains oscillatory structures in atomic and molecular photoabsorption and photodetachment spectra by relating them to classical electron trajectories that originate at the atomic/ionic core, propagate in external fields, and return to the core (closed orbits). Quantum spectral features are obtained as a coherent sum over these closed orbits, with phases given by the classical action and amplitudes determined by each orbit's stability."},{"id":"d842863f-eee1-451c-9028-a5bd499698c3","l":"Clustering","d":1,"p":"41033e78-6742-4486-9c09-f1efa60f9d9d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Clustering is the tendency of particles, objects, or data points in a physical system to form localized, higher-density groups or aggregates as a result of interactions, correlations, or external driving, producing spatial or temporal inhomogeneities relative to a random distribution. It occurs across scales and contexts\u2014from atomic and molecular clusters and nucleation to clusters of galaxies and collective phenomena in statistical and dynamical systems."},{"id":"decec525-9bef-4d01-872b-6fcc208dc890","l":"Cluster models","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Cluster models are theoretical descriptions of many\u2011body systems that treat the system as an assembly of strongly correlated subunits (\"clusters\"), each with its own internal structure, interacting via effective intercluster forces. They simplify dynamics and structure by focusing on cluster degrees of freedom (e.g., alpha clusters in nuclei or molecular/atomic groups in condensed matter) to explain binding, spectra, and reaction behavior."},{"id":"468f8fa5-904a-436c-83fc-6f1eed405239","l":"Clusters","d":1,"p":"bd252d88-a608-4897-9416-daf84dee7f6b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Clusters are finite, bound aggregates of atoms, molecules, or other particles whose electronic, structural, and dynamical properties interpolate between single constituents and bulk matter and depend sensitively on size, shape, and composition. The term also applies generically to similar finite groupings at other scales (e.g., alpha clusters in nuclei or star clusters) exhibiting emergent collective behavior."},{"id":"b2c4323c-b907-4ff3-974d-3c810a643abd","l":"Coarse graining","d":1,"p":"1e0c099a-9f2a-4e47-b3f1-7faa28b6a506","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Coarse graining is the procedure of replacing a detailed microscopic description by a lower-resolution one obtained by averaging or integrating out short-distance or fast degrees of freedom, yielding effective variables and equations that describe behavior on larger length or time scales. This reduction discards fine-grained information and often produces emergent, irreversible or stochastic macroscopic dynamics important in statistical mechanics and renormalization."},{"id":"c010e4a5-3da8-4c05-998f-245d70dedb52","l":"Coatings","d":1,"p":"c765ac18-ded5-4ef0-a41a-22be13741559","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Coatings are thin layers of material applied to a substrate to modify or protect its surface\u2014altering properties such as wear resistance, corrosion resistance, optical reflectivity, electrical conductivity, or wettability. Their performance depends on interfacial physics (adhesion, stress, diffusion, and surface energy), film thickness, and microstructure, and they are produced by techniques ranging from chemical and physical vapor deposition to electroplating and painting."},{"id":"077a93b4-7d90-4c93-a713-d8322eac29c9","l":"Coherence length","d":1,"p":"ca8ddaad-441c-481c-93b3-65f46125fb93","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Coherence length is the distance over which a wave (e.g., light) maintains a well-defined phase relationship so that interference remains visible. For temporal coherence it equals Lcoh = c \u03c4coh and is inversely related to the source linewidth (approximately Lcoh \u2248 c/\u0394\u03bd \u2248 \u03bb^2/\u0394\u03bb for light)."},{"id":"da893acc-f204-480b-8a7e-386479c50687","l":"Coherent control","d":1,"p":"ba7ccb8e-d7eb-4e6f-9c1e-b622f5b4e242","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Coherent control is the use of phase-stable electromagnetic fields or tailored waveforms to steer the evolution of a quantum system by exploiting interference between alternative transition pathways. By shaping amplitudes, phases, and timing of the driving fields (e.g., laser pulses), one can enhance or suppress specific outcomes such as reaction channels, population transfer, or quantum gate operations."},{"id":"2024288f-7ee4-445f-9db6-918294c9649e","l":"Coherent Raman in a low pressure gas","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Coherent Raman in a low\u2011pressure gas refers to the induced, phase\u2011locked oscillation of molecular vibrational or rotational modes by interacting laser fields (e.g., pump and Stokes), which produces coherent Raman signals such as coherent anti\u2011Stokes Raman scattering (CARS). At low pressure collisions are rare, so collisional dephasing is reduced and the Raman coherence lifetime is long, yielding narrow spectral lines and a response dominated by Doppler broadening and macroscopic phase\u2011matching."},{"id":"b3e622be-e85e-4c06-9002-b65185f94fcc","l":"Coherent structures","d":1,"p":"c485f44d-2cc4-4e6d-a20b-0899fb9e53a1","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Nonlinear Dynamics"],"def":"Coherent structures are persistent, spatially and temporally correlated patterns that emerge in turbulent or nonlinear systems and retain phase coherence and identity over timescales longer than the surrounding fluctuations. They dominate transport, mixing, and energy transfers and include features such as vortices, jets, plumes, solitons and coherent wavepackets."},{"id":"b7ae9785-f32b-4c42-a5f3-727b6f30a208","l":"Cohesion","d":1,"p":"eef7c13b-c67f-4d33-be97-76cee58e700a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Cohesion is the mutual attraction between like molecules or atoms within a substance, caused by intermolecular forces (e.g., hydrogen bonding, van der Waals interactions, or chemical bonds) that hold the material together. In liquids, cohesion produces phenomena such as surface tension and resistance to separation."},{"id":"72665b27-7e2b-4eaa-ae70-6bc714a8e22e","l":"Cold and ultracold molecules","d":1,"p":"46e2a993-8dce-4c28-beab-fdabe4378664","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Cold and ultracold molecules are molecules whose translational motion has been cooled so far that their de Broglie wavelength is large and quantum mechanical effects dominate their motion, collisions, and state control.  Roughly speaking, \"cold\" refers to temperatures of order 1 K down to millikelvin, while \"ultracold\" denotes millikelvin to microkelvin/nanokelvin regimes where only a few (often s\u2011wave) scattering channels contribute and coherent quantum control is possible."},{"id":"f3757435-4c4f-4297-9efb-4249f0bd8774","l":"Cold gases in optical lattices","d":1,"p":"46e2a993-8dce-4c28-beab-fdabe4378664","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Cold gases in optical lattices are ultracold atomic or molecular ensembles loaded into spatially periodic potentials formed by interfering laser beams, so the particles are localized on and tunnel between well-defined lattice sites. This platform realizes highly tunable lattice Hamiltonians (e.g., Bose- and Fermi-Hubbard models) with controllable tunneling, interactions, and dimensionality, enabling clean quantum simulation of many-body phenomena."},{"id":"be788987-3638-4630-ab9b-2d10b1fac65f","l":"Collective dynamics","d":1,"p":"c485f44d-2cc4-4e6d-a20b-0899fb9e53a1","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Nonlinear Dynamics"],"def":"Collective dynamics is the time-dependent behavior of many interacting components in which local interactions produce coordinated, emergent motion or patterns not present for isolated particles. It includes phenomena such as collective modes, waves, synchronization, and phase transitions, and is often described by macroscopic fields or reduced dynamical equations rather than single-particle laws."},{"id":"63ac8383-f353-4343-9c94-de98d9631340","l":"Collective flow","d":1,"p":"7321e0f7-3b12-45a2-aa4a-28c2a7e03149","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Collective flow is the correlated bulk motion of many particles in an interacting many-body system driven by common pressure gradients or mean fields. In relativistic heavy\u2011ion collisions it appears as anisotropic patterns in the final\u2011state particle momenta (flow harmonics v_n) that reflect the hydrodynamic expansion of the created medium."},{"id":"14ae8e80-c205-4119-b0b3-744d0fb178d0","l":"Collective levels","d":1,"p":"1cf75de2-e14b-41cd-b733-8f704652db33","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Collective levels are quantized excited states of a many-body system that involve coherent motion of many constituents (collective degrees of freedom) \u2014 for example rotational and vibrational states of a nucleus or phonon/plasmon excitations in solids \u2014 as opposed to excitations of individual particles."},{"id":"17c60544-56fd-45e2-853d-30c986f5ab63","l":"Collective models","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Collective models are theoretical frameworks that describe the coherent, correlated motion of many interacting particles by a few emergent macroscopic degrees of freedom. Examples include the nuclear liquid\u2011drop/Bohr\u2013Mottelson models for nuclear rotations and vibrations and condensed\u2011matter descriptions of collective excitations such as phonons and plasmons."},{"id":"ecae6ccf-7ff5-4445-a235-0149e3effae0","l":"Collimation","d":1,"p":"b3310113-551e-4c5e-9263-28f393ff09f0","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Collimation is the process of making a beam of waves or particles (e.g., light, electrons, neutrons) travel in nearly parallel paths, i.e., reducing its angular divergence so the beam has a well-defined direction. It is achieved with lenses, mirrors, apertures or dedicated collimators and is important for imaging, spectroscopy, and particle experiments."},{"id":"3c5b4e65-e2c2-487e-aea2-cadfba125a55","l":"Colloidal crystal","d":1,"p":"ef0a1bee-2a1f-4f47-aca2-6302db18e67f","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"A colloidal crystal is a three-dimensional, long-range ordered lattice formed by colloidal particles (nanometer\u2013micrometer scale) suspended in a fluid, analogous to an atomic crystal but with lattice spacing set by the particle size. These structures self-assemble through interparticle forces (electrostatic, van der Waals, depletion, entropic packing) and can diffract light and exhibit photonic or mechanical properties determined by their periodicity."},{"id":"0b8399fb-bcb7-4f39-9db8-8a65927a41e4","l":"Colloidal gel","d":1,"p":"ef0a1bee-2a1f-4f47-aca2-6302db18e67f","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"A colloidal gel is a space-spanning, percolated network of colloidal particles that bond together (via van der Waals forces, depletion, polymer bridging, etc.) and trap solvent, forming a solid-like structure at relatively low particle concentrations. Its formation involves arrested aggregation so the material exhibits elastic, viscoelastic behavior and yield stress despite being mostly fluid by volume."},{"id":"e33a2eba-c5b0-47cd-b894-fdd1ee6f3c05","l":"Colloidal glass","d":1,"p":"ef0a1bee-2a1f-4f47-aca2-6302db18e67f","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"A colloidal glass is a disordered, solid-like state of a suspension in which colloidal particles (typically micron- to nanometer-scale) are so crowded or strongly interacting that their Brownian motion becomes arrested and each particle is trapped in a cage formed by its neighbors. It shows glassy dynamics\u2014slow relaxation, aging, and mechanical rigidity\u2014despite lacking long-range crystalline order and is a colloidal analogue of the molecular glass transition."},{"id":"87ca58a8-a316-4438-a430-36d3d0a0f6b6","l":"Color confinement","d":1,"p":"0b08c33f-27b1-4bbf-90f9-1eb1bf63d1eb","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Color confinement is the property of quantum chromodynamics (the non\u2011Abelian SU(3) gauge theory of the strong interaction) that color\u2011charged particles (quarks and gluons) cannot be isolated and observed individually. At long distances the strong field forms flux tubes whose energy grows with separation, so attempts to separate color charges produce new quark\u2013antiquark pairs and only color\u2011neutral hadrons are detected."},{"id":"72fd3888-dcb4-4d9f-89c9-f9b1d9768204","l":"Columnar liquid crystals","d":1,"p":"63f7a3cf-31b7-4d5c-bbcc-58d679dd5139","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Columnar liquid crystals are mesophases in which anisotropic molecules (commonly disc-like) stack into one-dimensional columns that then arrange into a two-dimensional lattice (e.g., hexagonal or rectangular). They thus exhibit long-range positional order in the plane perpendicular to the column axis while retaining fluid-like mobility along the columns."},{"id":"1b56e7d8-2aa9-4576-83f0-44c3f8d016ed","l":"Combinatorics","d":1,"p":"1e0c099a-9f2a-4e47-b3f1-7faa28b6a506","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Combinatorics is the branch of mathematics concerned with counting, arranging, and selecting discrete objects and structures (permutations, combinations, partitions, graphs, etc.). In physics it provides the tools to enumerate microstates, compute degeneracies and combinatorial symmetry factors in statistical mechanics, quantum many-body theory, and diagrammatic expansions."},{"id":"764981db-0bdd-4f07-be10-46c52b4bb9e6","l":"Combustion","d":1,"p":"5ad87d23-7dd2-4d60-bc90-68055febea41","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Combustion is a rapid exothermic oxidation reaction between a fuel and an oxidizer (typically O2) that converts chemical energy into heat, light and combustion products. Its behavior depends on reaction kinetics and transport (heat, mass, momentum), producing flames, deflagrations, or detonations depending on conditions."},{"id":"0f07d301-a4ed-4538-aca3-aea5d70bc1b2","l":"Communication measures","d":1,"p":"fd7300dc-49e9-458d-9121-de94f7446fdb","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Communication measures are quantitative metrics that characterize the amount, fidelity, reliability, and capacity of information transferred through a communication channel. Examples include Shannon information and mutual information, channel capacity, signal-to-noise ratio and bit-error rate for classical channels, and fidelity and quantum capacity for quantum channels."},{"id":"df62af0e-1882-4457-aecc-9402b0736a56","l":"Communication schemes","d":1,"p":"fd7300dc-49e9-458d-9121-de94f7446fdb","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Communication schemes are the physical and algorithmic methods used to encode, transmit, and decode information over a communication channel, specifying modulation, multiplexing, signaling, and error-control techniques. They determine how information is mapped onto physical carriers (electromagnetic waves, photons, electrons) to maximize data rate, reliability, and efficiency under constraints like noise, bandwidth, latency, and power, and encompass both classical and quantum protocols."},{"id":"6baffbd1-395d-4194-9fe9-8212a409b5a7","l":"Community structure","d":1,"p":"41033e78-6742-4486-9c09-f1efa60f9d9d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Community structure is the mesoscale organization of a network in which nodes are partitioned into groups (communities or modules) that have many internal links but relatively few links between groups. This modular structure, often quantified by measures like modularity and found by clustering algorithms, influences the network's function and dynamics."},{"id":"0aa3b7b5-77fa-4cd5-8d16-e7cd9fcc47e7","l":"Compactification","d":1,"p":"cbc41431-fba0-4ff0-8134-2ded6e598922","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Compactification is the procedure in high-energy and gravitational theories of making one or more spatial dimensions compact (finite and typically periodic or curved), e.g., replacing an extra dimension by a circle or compact manifold, so that the full theory appears effectively lower-dimensional at long distances. It produces discrete Kaluza\u2013Klein spectra for fields and generically yields moduli parameters that describe the shape and size of the compact space."},{"id":"cc06f43c-faf2-4a1b-b418-6d399e1f47fc","l":"Competition","d":1,"p":"da80b6c6-baf7-49b0-922d-bf031c66389e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Competition in physics denotes a situation where two or more processes, interactions, or energy scales favor different states or outcomes, so the actual behavior is set by their relative strengths, timescales, or length scales. This interplay often produces phase competition, coexistence, frustration, metastability, or nontrivial dynamics (e.g., ferromagnetic vs antiferromagnetic exchange, kinetic vs thermodynamic control, or inertia vs damping)."},{"id":"f111f126-342c-48fd-baa7-6124af4142eb","l":"Complex materials","d":1,"p":"81194f70-bbca-4041-b57e-29338e588cac","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Complex materials are materials whose macroscopic properties are governed not just by atomic composition but by the interactions, disorder, and hierarchical structure of heterogeneous constituents across multiple length- and time-scales. They therefore exhibit nonlinear, history-dependent, collective and emergent behaviors (examples include glasses, polymers and gels, colloids and granular media, biological tissues, and metamaterials)."},{"id":"74a6313f-90ff-426a-9577-538d006bde5e","l":"Complex networks","d":1,"p":"b25ae464-8a45-4e8e-8efe-63d0f4714f01","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Complex networks are graphs whose connectivity shows nontrivial, system-specific patterns\u2014such as broad (often heavy-tailed) degree distributions, high clustering, community (modular) structure, and short path lengths\u2014that distinguish them from simple random graphs and strongly influence emergent collective behavior and dynamics on the network."},{"id":"193d5d6f-2e94-4abe-9d67-23697e0e115e","l":"Composite models","d":1,"p":"cdaf7295-546e-4fba-85c5-9d6435b40253","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Composite models are theoretical frameworks in which particles ordinarily treated as elementary (for example quarks, leptons, or the Higgs) are instead bound states of more fundamental constituents held together by new interactions. They aim to explain observed particle properties (masses, family structure, couplings) via new strong dynamics at higher energy scales (e.g., preon models, technicolor, composite Higgs)."},{"id":"9060ff55-19bc-4aec-99f0-51522983f45d","l":"Composition","d":1,"p":"15f520a3-5c6c-4d9e-8975-81d399140562","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Composition is the identity, relative amounts, and spatial arrangement of the constituent parts (atoms, molecules, phases, or subsystems) that make up a physical system or material. It largely determines the system's macroscopic properties and behavior."},{"id":"f131484a-7ef1-4fc0-829a-e92944b0a11a","l":"Compressible boundary layers","d":1,"p":"0c2f74cc-42b0-4b3a-b470-cd50aa7d0f89","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Compressible boundary layers are the thin regions of fluid next to a solid surface where density and temperature change significantly (usually at high Mach numbers or with strong heating), so compressibility alters the velocity and thermal profiles. In these layers momentum and energy transport are coupled and variable fluid properties, acoustic effects, and possible shock\u2013boundary-layer interactions play important roles."},{"id":"d3d64ff8-bda0-4b94-9731-f1cf6498c5ef","l":"Compressible turbulence","d":1,"p":"062f0300-c33d-4782-b24c-43996b0469f3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Compressible turbulence is turbulent flow in which variations in density and pressure are dynamically significant, so that the flow cannot be treated as incompressible. It features velocity divergence, acoustic waves and often shocklets, with strong coupling between kinetic and internal (thermal) energy that alters the energy cascade and statistical properties of the turbulence."},{"id":"51c41310-42da-42fb-a8ec-0d6ed96e76cb","l":"Compressive strength","d":1,"p":"9e001bda-d32b-4a59-a216-bfd4c1af8c25","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Compressive strength is the maximum uniaxial compressive stress a material can withstand before it fails (by crushing, fracture, yielding, or buckling). It is typically reported as the peak load sustained divided by the specimen's original cross\u2011sectional area (units: pascals or N/m\u00b2)."},{"id":"a2dbb963-65c6-4422-bfbc-8ae66bdf131d","l":"Computational complexity","d":1,"p":"fd7300dc-49e9-458d-9121-de94f7446fdb","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Computational complexity is the study of how the resources needed to solve computational problems\u2014typically time (number of steps) and memory\u2014scale with the size of the input, used to quantify the intrinsic difficulty of problems and efficiency of algorithms. It organizes problems into classes (e.g., P, NP, BQP) and distinguishes tractable from intractable tasks by their asymptotic resource growth."},{"id":"abed11a1-af1b-4a1c-ba3c-01328ae70df1","l":"Conducting polymers","d":1,"p":"fea036a1-9c29-4872-ab2c-d052949f7c03","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Conducting polymers are organic polymers whose conjugated (alternating single\u2013double bond) backbones support delocalized \u03c0-electrons, enabling electronic charge transport. Their conductivity can be widely tuned by chemical or electrochemical doping and structural order, producing materials that range from insulating to metallic and are used in electronics, sensors, and energy devices."},{"id":"2a8a83b8-d729-4edf-83b0-15a9d73affc9","l":"Confinement-induced resonance","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Confinement-induced resonance is a scattering phenomenon in tightly confined (quasi-1D or quasi-2D) quantum gases where transverse quantization couples to the relative motion, producing a resonance in the effective low-dimensional interaction strength (i.e., the 1D/2D coupling can diverge or change sign). It arises when the 3D scattering length is comparable to the transverse confinement length\u2014via virtual excitation of transverse modes\u2014and is commonly observed in ultracold atoms in waveguides."},{"id":"41fd0ba6-886f-4dbc-bfba-6b3a3fdde355","l":"Confocal imaging","d":1,"p":"a38dc017-3236-4084-afc5-eb59c5de51e0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Confocal imaging is a microscopy technique that uses focused (usually laser) point illumination together with a spatial pinhole placed in a plane conjugate to the sample focal plane to reject out\u2011of\u2011focus light, producing optically sectioned images with improved contrast and axial resolution. Images are formed by raster\u2011scanning the focal spot (and stacking focal planes for 3D reconstructions)."},{"id":"b59fbf99-aaba-4b28-b58a-6104444592fb","l":"Conformal field theory","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Particles & Fields","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A conformal field theory (CFT) is a quantum field theory invariant under conformal transformations\u2014angle-preserving local rescalings of spacetime\u2014so it has no intrinsic length scale and its local operators are classified by scaling dimensions and constrained by the conformal symmetry. CFTs govern universal critical behavior in statistical systems, describe string worldsheet dynamics, and play a central role in holographic dualities such as AdS/CFT."},{"id":"d79c4601-fdf0-411e-935b-f0cf28d98789","l":"Conformal foams","d":1,"p":"cf6b1eea-2e6d-498c-afd5-ebc77fd47d64","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Conformal foams are foam-like two-dimensional cell complexes (networks of faces meeting along edges and vertices) equipped with a conformal or local scale-invariant structure. They serve as discrete or categorical models of surfaces and their cobordisms in contexts such as conformal field theory, topological quantum field theory, and low-dimensional topology."},{"id":"5bb65267-e5d4-4303-a8de-492fc855e9a3","l":"Conjugated polymers","d":1,"p":"fea036a1-9c29-4872-ab2c-d052949f7c03","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Conjugated polymers are macromolecules whose backbone contains alternating single and double (or aromatic) bonds, producing extended \u03c0-electron delocalization along the chain. This delocalization yields narrow electronic bandgaps and tunable semiconducting or metallic optical and charge-transport properties, which can be modified by chemical doping or oxidation."},{"id":"dd4a3a9f-bc98-4d86-bad2-97e7ece032ab","l":"Connectome","d":1,"p":"8b374c75-8448-476e-a872-f15b0cf5a5fe","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"A connectome is the comprehensive map of neural connections in a nervous system, represented as a network (graph) whose nodes are neurons or brain regions and whose edges are synapses or fiber tracts. It can be defined at micro-, meso-, or macro-scales and is used to relate the brain\u2019s structural wiring to its functional dynamics."},{"id":"7dfdd1db-3f72-4352-ae84-b3a05e0543ac","l":"Contact line dynamics","d":1,"p":"f5372742-fa5e-4bda-96ba-87e45d98bae6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Contact-line dynamics is the study of how the line where liquid, solid and gas meet moves, determined by the balance of capillary (surface tension) forces and dissipative mechanisms such as viscous flow, microscopic slip or precursor films and contact-angle hysteresis. It describes velocity-dependent contact angles and laws (e.g., Cox\u2013Voinov, Tanner) and the microscale physics needed to resolve the stress singularity at a moving contact line."},{"id":"6a6a8f1d-0b89-4739-8693-2ae6aa3a5db8","l":"Continuous phase transition","d":1,"p":"6e3ddc0a-24fd-4682-bb1b-137944166a4a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"A continuous phase transition (also called a second-order transition) is a change of state where the order parameter varies continuously through the transition and no latent heat is released. It is accompanied by diverging correlation length and susceptibilities and critical, scale-invariant fluctuations described by power-law behavior and universal critical exponents."},{"id":"fadc87f6-ee6b-49d6-9128-da9737e4d165","l":"Continuous symmetries","d":1,"p":"54b389aa-8ccf-49ee-a54e-478ccc2616eb","fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Continuous symmetries are transformations that depend smoothly on one or more real parameters and leave a physical system's laws (Lagrangian, Hamiltonian, or equations of motion) unchanged, forming Lie groups (e.g., time and spatial translations, rotations). By Noether's theorem each continuous symmetry of the action corresponds to a conserved quantity (energy, momentum, angular momentum)."},{"id":"f92e7081-1a69-4c36-8918-0df841923a46","l":"Continuum mechanics","d":1,"p":"b43baaca-e8a9-4440-a55f-9f2a3459e510","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nonlinear Dynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Continuum mechanics is the study of the deformation and motion of materials modeled as continuous media, described by field quantities (e.g., displacement, velocity, stress, strain) that satisfy conservation laws. It uses constitutive relations to predict mechanical response of solids and fluids at scales where discrete microstructure can be neglected."},{"id":"c0994213-aa21-4664-bcc9-34473da8eaa9","l":"Control & applications of chaos","d":1,"p":"bb2351de-d2a6-4d4c-a33e-9396bc991c32","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Nonlinear Dynamics"],"def":"Control and applications of chaos is the study and practice of deliberately influencing chaotic dynamical systems\u2014by small, timely perturbations, feedback loops, or parameter modulation\u2014to stabilize desired behaviors, synchronize or steer trajectories, or suppress unwanted irregularity.  It also encompasses exploiting chaotic dynamics for useful tasks such as improved mixing and transport, secure communications, random-number generation, and sensitive detection."},{"id":"935a3c34-30ed-47cf-8ef9-071c7e3dcd02","l":"Control theory","d":1,"p":"b7f686b6-3282-4e50-85e4-f19b2db66d82","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Control theory is the study of how to influence the behavior of dynamical systems by designing inputs or controllers so the system attains desired objectives (stability, regulation, or tracking of reference signals). It emphasizes feedback\u2014measuring outputs to adjust inputs\u2014to ensure robustness and performance despite disturbances and model uncertainty."},{"id":"5b47e517-ce07-4799-90b8-8599a2a4a1af","l":"Convection in porous media","d":1,"p":"cc73b723-a501-45c2-a121-f8c9757c5573","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Convection in porous media is the transport of fluid (and hence heat and solutes) by bulk motion through a permeable solid matrix, driven either by buoyancy from density/temperature/concentration differences (natural convection) or by imposed pressure/flow (forced convection); it is described by coupled flow and transport equations (e.g., Darcy's law plus advection\u2013diffusion)."},{"id":"0b6b113c-8f3d-421e-a149-940af26931cf","l":"Cooling & trapping","d":1,"p":"ba7ccb8e-d7eb-4e6f-9c1e-b622f5b4e242","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Cooling and trapping are techniques for reducing the kinetic energy (effective temperature) of atoms, ions, or particles and for confining them in space using controlled forces from light, magnetic, or electric fields. Common implementations include laser Doppler and evaporative cooling together with magneto\u2011optical, magnetic, optical\u2011dipole, and radiofrequency/Paul traps, enabling long interaction times and quantum control for precision measurements and many-body experiments."},{"id":"0355255e-2e87-4364-be03-5e84d1ff3c26","l":"Core-annular flows","d":1,"p":"c577bd62-ea57-43b7-9607-1ef45dba3afb","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Core\u2013annular flow is a two-phase flow in a pipe or channel in which one fluid occupies the central \u201ccore\u201d while a second fluid forms a concentric annulus along the walls. The structure and stability of the arrangement are set by viscosity and density contrasts, interfacial tension, shear and buoyancy, and it commonly appears in oil\u2013water transport, microfluidics, and geophysical flows."},{"id":"3ee273d1-fee7-4f09-aaf7-ec5484dd6a95","l":"Correlation function measurements","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Correlation function measurements determine how a physical observable at one point in space or time is statistically related to the same or another observable at a different point/time, quantified by two-point (autocorrelation) and higher-order correlation functions. They are used to characterize coherence, fluctuations, ordering, and dynamical length- and time-scales in systems from fluids and solids to optical and quantum fields."},{"id":"91244abe-9e78-413f-9e74-189aa7d92a58","l":"Cortical networks","d":1,"p":"d87d9c51-bdc2-43bb-99c3-6e5345a7f631","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Cortical networks are the interconnected populations of excitatory and inhibitory neurons in the cerebral cortex whose synaptic connections and recurrent dynamics implement sensory processing, cognition, and motor control. They are treated as complex dynamical networks and graphs, showing structured connectivity, collective activity (oscillations, waves, attractors) and activity-dependent plasticity."},{"id":"db7d3062-4276-4845-8d68-9f2d062e79d7","l":"Cosmetics","d":1,"p":"c765ac18-ded5-4ef0-a41a-22be13741559","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Cosmetics are formulated products applied to the human body to cleanse, protect, enhance, or alter appearance. From a physics perspective they are soft-matter and particulate systems whose optical behavior (color, scattering, gloss), rheology and tribology (spreadability, touch, adhesion), and interfacial/colloidal stability (emulsions, suspensions, film formation) determine performance."},{"id":"dce9f5ad-b7ee-4c48-b463-1d6621ba628e","l":"Cosmic microwave background","d":1,"p":"b3ac6303-2e15-4b1b-bd7e-242d57d2b1d7","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"The cosmic microwave background (CMB) is the relic thermal radiation from the hot, dense early universe, now observed as an almost isotropic blackbody at about 2.725 K. It was released when photons decoupled from matter at recombination (~380,000 years after the Big Bang) and its small temperature anisotropies encode the primordial density fluctuations that seeded large-scale structure."},{"id":"1086ed72-6170-407e-aa7a-1815ae993a5d","l":"Cosmic ray acceleration","d":1,"p":"678197d1-8bf1-4c16-86cc-8133fba03b86","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Cosmic ray acceleration is the set of astrophysical processes that drive charged particles to relativistic (cosmic\u2011ray) energies by repeated interactions with moving magnetized plasma and electromagnetic fields\u2014most notably first\u2011order Fermi acceleration at shocks (e.g., supernova remnants), but also stochastic (second\u2011order) Fermi processes, magnetic reconnection, and acceleration in jets and pulsar winds. These mechanisms typically produce the power\u2011law energy spectra observed in cosmic rays and depend on the source environment and magnetic turbulence."},{"id":"b144b8b6-c6a6-4178-81b5-8f63f6e17c20","l":"Cosmic ray & astroparticle detectors","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Cosmic ray & astroparticle detectors are instruments that detect and characterize energetic particles and photons from space\u2014primary cosmic rays, air\u2011shower secondaries, gamma rays and neutrinos\u2014by measuring their interactions with matter or electromagnetic fields. They employ technologies such as tracking chambers, scintillators, calorimeters, Cherenkov and radio detectors, deployed on ground, underground, balloons or satellites, to determine energy, arrival direction, timing and particle type for astrophysical and particle\u2011physics studies."},{"id":"fb81690f-0f98-4212-b6fa-b5f0a3b9b0d3","l":"Cosmic ray composition & spectra","d":1,"p":"678197d1-8bf1-4c16-86cc-8133fba03b86","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Cosmic ray composition & spectra refers to the measurement and characterization of which particles (protons, heavier nuclei, electrons, positrons, gamma rays, neutrinos) make up cosmic rays and how their relative abundances vary with energy. The spectra are the energy-dependent fluxes of those particles, typically shown as power laws with characteristic features (knees, ankles, cutoffs) that encode information about source acceleration, composition at injection, and propagation through interstellar and intergalactic space."},{"id":"87e89de2-57b5-4443-a423-33c8d474d00a","l":"Cosmic ray propagation","d":1,"p":"678197d1-8bf1-4c16-86cc-8133fba03b86","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Cosmic ray propagation is the transport and evolution of high-energy charged particles through interstellar and intergalactic media under the influence of magnetic fields and electromagnetic turbulence. It includes processes such as diffusion, convection, energy losses, spallation and reacceleration, which together determine the particles' spatial distribution and energy spectrum observed at Earth."},{"id":"e56d3e00-8711-4a46-a2cf-8a7a3cd9e8de","l":"Cosmic ray sources","d":1,"p":"678197d1-8bf1-4c16-86cc-8133fba03b86","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Cosmic ray sources are astrophysical objects and environments that produce and accelerate charged particles (mostly protons and atomic nuclei, plus electrons and photons) to high and often relativistic energies. Typical sources include supernova remnants, pulsar wind nebulae, active galactic nuclei, gamma\u2011ray bursts and solar flares, where acceleration occurs via mechanisms such as diffusive shock acceleration, magnetic reconnection, or acceleration in relativistic jets."},{"id":"145999c8-4da5-4041-9999-a96c1dd88fa2","l":"Cosmic strings & domain walls","d":1,"p":"26ed7afc-8635-4637-a339-8d0a7cb75be1","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Cosmic strings are line-like (one-dimensional) topological defects produced when a symmetry-breaking phase transition in the early universe traps energy along narrow, massive filaments that can gravitationally lens and influence structure formation. Domain walls are sheet-like (two-dimensional) defects that form where regions of different vacuum (typically from breaking a discrete symmetry) meet, carrying surface energy and being cosmologically problematic if abundant."},{"id":"6b54bc50-477c-4c8d-b365-2e630d3eab20","l":"Cosmological parameters","d":1,"p":"26ed7afc-8635-4637-a339-8d0a7cb75be1","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Cosmological parameters are a set of measured numbers that specify the large\u2011scale properties of the universe\u2014its expansion rate, composition (matter, radiation, dark energy), spatial curvature, and the statistical properties of primordial fluctuations\u2014used as input to cosmological models. Common examples include the Hubble constant H0, density parameters \u03a9m, \u03a9\u039b, \u03a9k, the primordial spectral index ns and amplitude As, and the reionization optical depth \u03c4."},{"id":"c3346a7a-9719-46d6-baf5-df9a9fe6a7aa","l":"Coulomb blockade","d":1,"p":"4722b240-3d0b-4ab1-831a-75302ab4cf86","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Coulomb blockade is the suppression of electron tunneling through a small conducting island or tunnel junction when the island\u2019s single-electron charging energy (E_C = e^2/2C) exceeds the thermal and applied-bias energies, so adding an extra electron is energetically forbidden until the bias passes a threshold. It is observed in nanoscale devices (quantum dots, single-electron transistors) at low temperatures and produces conductance gaps and quantized single-electron transport."},{"id":"34aa4f0d-f19a-4ec4-9726-d5a23295f84c","l":"Coulomb dissociation","d":1,"p":"bbb0ce0d-36da-4da5-a443-df064f36aa17","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Coulomb dissociation is the breakup of a fast projectile nucleus caused by the strong electromagnetic (Coulomb) field of a heavy target nucleus, which excites the projectile via exchange of virtual photons. It is used experimentally to infer electromagnetic transition strengths and, by detailed balance, radiative-capture cross sections important in nuclear astrophysics."},{"id":"2e2be214-1eff-49e0-a4c9-e38af3680db2","l":"Coulomb energies & analogue states","d":1,"p":"1cf75de2-e14b-41cd-b733-8f704652db33","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Coulomb energies are the parts of a nucleus's energy arising from electrostatic repulsion among protons, which shift binding energies and level spacings. Analogue states (isobaric analogue states) are energy levels in different nuclei of the same isobaric multiplet that have the same internal structure and isospin quantum numbers but different proton/neutron composition, so their energy differences predominantly reflect Coulomb and other charge-dependent effects."},{"id":"e98c6636-816e-48aa-89c2-042aabeaa5df","l":"Coulomb excitation","d":1,"p":"bbb0ce0d-36da-4da5-a443-df064f36aa17","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Coulomb excitation is the process in which a nucleus is raised to an excited state by the time-dependent electromagnetic (Coulomb) field of a passing charged particle\u2014typically another nucleus\u2014during a peripheral collision with negligible nuclear contact. It can be viewed as absorption of virtual photons and is widely used to probe electromagnetic transition strengths (e.g., B(E\u03bb), B(M\u03bb)) and nuclear structure."},{"id":"7450c470-b69d-4469-a36c-26b870fbea4b","l":"Cracking","d":1,"p":"eef7c13b-c67f-4d33-be97-76cee58e700a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Cracking is the nucleation and growth of fractures (cracks) in a material under mechanical, thermal, or chemical loading, often initiated at stress concentrators and leading to partial or complete failure. Its behavior is described by fracture mechanics concepts such as stress intensity factors, energy release rates, and material toughness."},{"id":"0281cbad-580b-4603-ad9c-a55fec6d3db1","l":"Crazing","d":1,"p":"eef7c13b-c67f-4d33-be97-76cee58e700a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Crazing is a localized fracture process in glassy polymers where a network of microvoids separated by stretched polymer fibrils forms under tensile stress, producing whitish, crack-like zones that relieve stress but can grow and coalesce into cracks and eventual failure."},{"id":"7fe6e80b-3869-4bd6-a81c-62d4076447f0","l":"Critical current","d":1,"p":"ca8ddaad-441c-481c-93b3-65f46125fb93","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Critical current is the maximum electric current a material or device can carry while remaining in its non-dissipative ordered state; in a superconductor it is the largest current that can flow with zero resistance, and in a Josephson junction it is the maximum supercurrent that can flow with zero voltage. It depends on temperature, magnetic field and sample geometry."},{"id":"6faac3e6-cda0-43ca-be0f-4ea65fd16e81","l":"Critical phenomena","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Networks","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Critical phenomena are the universal, scale-invariant behaviors exhibited by many-body systems as they approach a continuous (second-order) phase transition. They are characterized by a diverging correlation length, singular response functions, and power-law scaling with critical exponents that depend only on macroscopic symmetries and dimensionality, not microscopic details."},{"id":"c100a8da-558a-4cb9-ab99-44fe731820c9","l":"Critical race theory","d":1,"p":"560043c3-4ee2-48af-9d6c-08819ef45fff","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics Education Research"],"def":"Critical race theory (CRT) is an interdisciplinary academic framework, originating in U.S. legal scholarship, that analyzes how race and racism are embedded in laws, policies, institutions, and social structures rather than being only matters of individual prejudice. It emphasizes the role of power, historical context, and the lived experiences of marginalized groups, using concepts like intersectionality to examine how race intersects with other forms of inequality."},{"id":"22b17e02-28a0-4b88-ae3b-2520aa22cfb9","l":"Cryogenics","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Cryogenics is the science and technology of producing and studying very low temperatures\u2014typically below about 120 K\u2014and the behavior of materials and systems under those conditions. It includes cryogenic refrigeration and liquefaction (e.g., liquid nitrogen and helium), measurement and thermal management, and low\u2011temperature phenomena such as superconductivity and superfluidity."},{"id":"a4ec9597-5725-4a4e-9c3e-aca409c09285","l":"Cryogenic technology","d":1,"p":"7853f3c2-c02d-4fc9-aae8-1eb75aaf35b8","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Cryogenic technology is the set of methods and systems for producing, controlling, measuring, and sustaining very low temperatures\u2014typically below about 120 K (\u2248\u2212153 \u00b0C)\u2014to exploit altered material properties and reduced thermal noise. It encompasses cryogens (liquid nitrogen, helium), cryocoolers, vacuum and multilayer insulation, and instrumentation used in research, superconducting devices, space engineering, and medical applications."},{"id":"a6f70b5d-d56e-4730-b33f-511b442882d2","l":"Crystal binding","d":1,"p":"e521b9c7-1f62-48e4-a3fc-9428f629d078","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Crystal binding is the set of interatomic and intermolecular forces that hold atoms, ions, or molecules fixed at regular positions in a crystalline lattice, producing a cohesive (binding) energy. It arises from combinations of ionic, covalent, metallic and van der Waals interactions whose balance of attraction and short-range repulsion determines the lattice structure, spacing, and stability."},{"id":"20650477-6cf6-463c-b6eb-97c00584b564","l":"Crystal defects","d":1,"p":"e521b9c7-1f62-48e4-a3fc-9428f629d078","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Crystal defects are deviations from the perfect periodic arrangement of atoms in a crystalline solid, such as point defects (vacancies, interstitials, substitutional impurities), line defects (dislocations), planar defects (grain boundaries, stacking faults), and volume defects (voids, precipitates). These imperfections strongly affect a material\u2019s mechanical, electronic, optical, and thermal properties by introducing local strains and scattering centers for electrons and phonons."},{"id":"1a4745c4-fb72-4dd5-9dfc-c2c6ce55fb06","l":"Crystal field excitations","d":1,"p":"15f520a3-5c6c-4d9e-8975-81d399140562","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Crystal field excitations are transitions between the discrete electronic levels of localized d- or f-electrons that result when the degeneracy of atomic orbitals is lifted by the anisotropic electrostatic (crystal) field of surrounding ions. These level splittings and their excitations govern and can be probed via a material's magnetic, optical, and spectroscopic properties."},{"id":"b23c6297-9e2d-413e-84ae-84e597abac27","l":"Crystal forms","d":1,"p":"e521b9c7-1f62-48e4-a3fc-9428f629d078","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Crystal forms are the characteristic external shapes of crystals produced by sets of symmetrically equivalent crystal faces. Each form corresponds to a family of crystallographic planes (specified by Miller indices) and reflects the symmetry and lattice parameters of the crystal's internal structure."},{"id":"843259d3-c0d8-4f11-a577-8cf874f3a636","l":"Crystal growth","d":1,"p":"e521b9c7-1f62-48e4-a3fc-9428f629d078","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Crystal growth is the process by which a crystalline solid forms and enlarges by the ordered addition of atoms, ions, or molecules onto a periodic lattice, starting with nucleation and proceeding by material transport and attachment at crystal surfaces. It is governed by thermodynamics (free-energy minimization) and kinetics (supersaturation/undercooling, diffusion, interface attachment rates and defect incorporation) and occurs from melts, solutions, or vapors."},{"id":"44635c9c-bd9e-4678-9d9a-d82435b78716","l":"Crystallography","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Crystallography is the branch of science that studies the atomic-scale arrangement, symmetry, and repeating order of atoms in crystalline solids. It encompasses experimental and theoretical methods (e.g., x-ray, neutron, and electron diffraction) used to determine unit cells, lattice symmetries, defects, and how these structural features control material properties."},{"id":"3a0ef4f7-a3bb-4e6b-b5b3-40011e2da825","l":"Crystal melting","d":1,"p":"e521b9c7-1f62-48e4-a3fc-9428f629d078","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Crystal melting is the thermodynamic phase transition in which a crystalline solid loses its long-range periodic order and becomes a liquid; at the melting temperature the crystal lattice breaks down and absorbs latent heat as atoms or molecules gain mobility. This process is typically first-order (solid\u2013liquid coexistence) and is strongly affected by defects, surfaces, pressure, and finite-size effects (e.g., premelting and nanoscale melting)."},{"id":"adc77711-945c-4bd1-990c-5deef3b055d7","l":"Crystal orientation","d":1,"p":"e521b9c7-1f62-48e4-a3fc-9428f629d078","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Crystal orientation is the alignment of a crystal's lattice \u2014 its crystallographic axes and planes \u2014 relative to an external reference frame or sample coordinates, specifying which lattice directions or planes point along given sample directions. It is commonly described by Miller indices for planes and directions or by Euler angles/rotation matrices for the full unit-cell orientation."},{"id":"c4de79ff-0d40-4258-ac52-180805a7188d","l":"Crystal stoichiometry","d":1,"p":"e521b9c7-1f62-48e4-a3fc-9428f629d078","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Crystal stoichiometry is the elemental composition of a crystalline solid expressed as the integer ratio of atoms per formula unit or per unit cell. Deviations from ideal stoichiometry (nonstoichiometry) occur when point defects\u2014vacancies, interstitials, or substitutional impurities\u2014change those ratios and thereby alter the material's electronic, optical, and transport properties."},{"id":"2a766a58-c72f-40f1-a41e-7bd38b251b86","l":"Crystal structure","d":1,"p":"e521b9c7-1f62-48e4-a3fc-9428f629d078","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Crystal structure is the ordered, periodic arrangement of atoms, ions, or molecules in a solid, described by a lattice of translational symmetry together with a basis (motif) at each lattice point. The smallest repeating unit is the unit cell, and the lattice symmetry strongly influences the material's physical properties."},{"id":"6af5be88-489f-4a50-9abf-998e8912254a","l":"Crystal structures","d":1,"p":"1128a9f3-70c1-409b-84b2-44063aa12108","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Crystal structures are the three-dimensional, long-range periodic arrangements of atoms, ions, or molecules in a solid, described by a repeating unit cell (a lattice plus a basis) and the symmetry operations that map it onto itself. The specific lattice type and basis determine the material's geometric symmetry and many of its physical properties."},{"id":"ed672f46-7dd2-4242-9fbc-aaf78fcd252f","l":"Crystal symmetry","d":1,"p":"e521b9c7-1f62-48e4-a3fc-9428f629d078","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Crystal symmetry is the set of geometric operations that leave a crystal's infinite atomic arrangement unchanged \u2014 including discrete lattice translations together with point operations (rotations, reflections, inversion) and combined operations such as glide planes and screw axes. These symmetries are classified by the 230 three-dimensional space groups and strongly constrain allowable atomic arrangements and physical properties (e.g., band degeneracies, selection rules, and tensor forms)."},{"id":"63f3d640-b8e1-43e0-80e6-0492da6087b6","l":"Current drive","d":1,"p":"888306e1-6391-43a8-a318-3434270aafd5","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Current drive is the generation and sustained maintenance of a net electric current in a conducting medium\u2014particularly a plasma\u2014using external, usually non\u2011inductive, sources. In magnetic\u2011confinement fusion this is achieved by radio\u2011frequency waves, neutral\u2011beam injection, or the bootstrap effect to sustain the toroidal plasma current needed for confinement and steady\u2011state operation."},{"id":"a647eb01-cfb2-4629-8a84-76bea4dc2b95","l":"Cycles","d":1,"p":"41033e78-6742-4486-9c09-f1efa60f9d9d","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Cycles are processes or trajectories that repeat periodically, returning a system to its initial state after a fixed interval. In physics they appear as closed sequences of state changes\u2014e.g., thermodynamic engine cycles, oscillatory motions, or closed orbits (limit cycles) in dynamical systems."},{"id":"adf8950c-3d99-4e88-b7f3-76ecf2764c1e","l":"Dark energy","d":1,"p":"26ed7afc-8635-4637-a339-8d0a7cb75be1","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Dark energy is the name for the unknown component driving the observed accelerated expansion of the Universe; it contributes roughly 68% of the cosmic energy density and behaves as a source of negative pressure (equation-of-state parameter w \u2248 \u22121). It is inferred from type Ia supernova distances, the cosmic microwave background, and large-scale structure, and could be a true cosmological constant (\u039b) or a dynamical field, though its fundamental nature remains unknown."},{"id":"024cf552-b967-4a51-8b54-d209e10f0bc7","l":"Dark matter","d":1,"p":"26ed7afc-8635-4637-a339-8d0a7cb75be1","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Dark matter is a nonluminous form of matter that does not emit, absorb, or scatter appreciable electromagnetic radiation but is inferred from its gravitational effects on galaxies, galaxy clusters, the cosmic microwave background, and large-scale structure. It makes up most of the Universe's matter (about 85% of the total matter content), though its particle identity and non-gravitational properties remain unknown."},{"id":"9cb101d6-4b02-4f69-948e-f42b9bea9fe3","l":"Data analysis","d":1,"p":"1e0c099a-9f2a-4e47-b3f1-7faa28b6a506","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Data analysis is the process of processing, summarizing, and interpreting experimental or simulated data to extract physical parameters, test models and hypotheses, and quantify uncertainties using statistical, computational, and signal-processing methods. It encompasses preprocessing (cleaning and filtering), modeling, parameter estimation, hypothesis testing, and visualization to draw reliable, reproducible conclusions."},{"id":"559fd52a-dd8b-4c6c-92e6-e32968f05074","l":"Decoherence in quantum gases","d":1,"p":"46e2a993-8dce-4c28-beab-fdabe4378664","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Decoherence in quantum gases is the process by which quantum superpositions and long-range phase coherence (e.g., in a Bose\u2013Einstein condensate) are suppressed, so that the system's density matrix loses its off-diagonal coherence and approaches a classical statistical mixture. This loss of coherence is driven by interactions with external environments or internal degrees of freedom\u2014collisions with thermal atoms, atom\u2013light coupling, spontaneous emission, or technical noise\u2014which randomize relative phases and destroy interference on a characteristic decoherence timescale."},{"id":"7060bde3-9f16-411d-9d68-3328cf14b019","l":"Defects","d":1,"p":"15f520a3-5c6c-4d9e-8975-81d399140562","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Defects are deviations from an ideal, perfectly ordered structure in a material\u2014localized or extended disruptions such as vacancies, interstitials, dislocations, grain boundaries, and topological defects\u2014that strongly influence mechanical, electronic, optical, and thermal properties. They are classified by dimensionality (point, line, planar, bulk) and topology and often govern processes like diffusion, plastic deformation, and phase transitions."},{"id":"aabb3867-8c42-46b7-8bb5-8dc194e448a0","l":"Degree correlations","d":1,"p":"41033e78-6742-4486-9c09-f1efa60f9d9d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Degree correlations describe the statistical relationship between the degrees (number of links) of neighboring nodes in a network, indicating whether high-degree nodes preferentially connect to other high-degree nodes (assortative) or to low-degree nodes (disassortative). They are quantified by measures such as the average neighbor degree k_nn(k) or the Pearson assortativity coefficient r (r>0 assortative, r<0 disassortative)."},{"id":"62c10226-6a6c-4ea4-94c2-15016ad9c99f","l":"Degree distributions","d":1,"p":"41033e78-6742-4486-9c09-f1efa60f9d9d","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"The degree distribution of a network is the probability distribution P(k) (or empirical fraction) that a randomly chosen node has degree k, i.e., k incident edges; for directed graphs one can define separate in\u2011 and out\u2011degree distributions. It compactly characterizes a network's connectivity pattern and strongly influences processes on the network (e.g., percolation, diffusion, epidemics)."},{"id":"466328b7-dffc-4b1c-ace1-668fe7f7dbb8","l":"Demagnetization","d":1,"p":"b35238d0-83c7-4412-b293-c7d42fa92c62","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Demagnetization is the process of reducing or removing a material's net magnetization or external magnetic field by disrupting the alignment of its magnetic domains, for example by heating above the Curie temperature, applying a reverse or alternating magnetic field (degaussing), mechanical shock, or through time-dependent magnetic relaxation."},{"id":"ad932eb6-9cc0-4b84-a8e9-91cd43df0e7f","l":"Dense plasma focus","d":1,"p":"3741a35f-83a8-4e50-b7a2-38e600034925","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"A dense plasma focus is a pulsed plasma device in which a rapid electrical discharge between coaxial electrodes drives a plasma sheath that first accelerates axially and then implodes radially to form a compact, magnetically confined, high\u2011density, high\u2011temperature plasma pinch (a Z\u2011pinch). The resulting hotspot emits intense X\u2011rays and often neutrons, making the device a compact source for fusion studies and pulsed radiation experiments."},{"id":"aaff2519-9891-4d4e-a067-1ea36b6b08d4","l":"Density of states","d":1,"p":"9e019803-f9d8-4802-b1ac-d2c035d9508f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The density of states (DOS) g(E) is the number of quantum states available per unit energy (and usually per unit volume) at energy E, so g(E) dE equals the number of states in the interval [E, E + dE]. It is determined by a system's dimensionality and dispersion relation and governs how particles occupy energies, affecting thermodynamic and transport properties."},{"id":"235bce6d-6c5a-46d2-b5fa-7cfdbbbeab55","l":"Depletion interactions","d":1,"p":"cc59c6fb-acbb-4237-953b-b874fe5dbd4a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Depletion interactions are effective, usually attractive forces between large colloidal particles that arise when smaller particles or nonadsorbing polymers (depletants) are present in the suspension. They are entropic in origin: overlap of the excluded volumes as big particles approach increases the free volume available to depletants, producing an osmotic-pressure imbalance that pushes the large particles together (Asakura\u2013Oosawa mechanism)."},{"id":"db63fb69-a07b-4b9e-9425-1ff135a94db7","l":"Desorption","d":1,"p":"eef7c13b-c67f-4d33-be97-76cee58e700a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Desorption is the process by which atoms, molecules, or ions previously adsorbed on a surface detach and return to the surrounding gas or liquid phase, i.e., the reverse of adsorption. It can be driven by thermal energy, light, electric fields or chemical reactions, and its rate depends on the adsorption binding energy and mechanism (physisorption vs chemisorption)."},{"id":"a17bad0f-2766-4b9e-9a99-de31988242f0","l":"Determination of fundamental constants","d":1,"p":"141061f5-f02c-492c-af56-81c3c49e8b65","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Determination of fundamental constants is the experimental and theoretical process of measuring the numerical values of the universal parameters that appear in physical laws (e.g., c, h, G, e, \u03b1) and assigning best-value estimates with quantified uncertainties. It combines high\u2011precision experiments, theoretical calculations, and statistical evaluation (e.g., CODATA adjustments) to provide reliable values used for tests of physical theory and for defining units."},{"id":"0717b49f-02c4-4d11-8faf-a35c86bc736a","l":"Detonation","d":1,"p":"5ad87d23-7dd2-4d60-bc90-68055febea41","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"A detonation is a self\u2011sustaining, supersonic combustion wave in which a leading shock front compresses and heats a reactive medium, triggering extremely rapid exothermic chemical reactions that in turn sustain the shock. It differs from slower, subsonic deflagration and is commonly modeled by Chapman\u2013Jouguet and Zeldovich\u2013von Neumann\u2013D\u00f6ring descriptions of the shock\u2013reaction structure."},{"id":"93b8baac-44de-4a26-a175-357ad9d8744c","l":"Developmental biology","d":1,"p":"6f618be6-815c-40bf-bd8a-cb8ffc96bfd5","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Developmental biology studies how multicellular organisms form and acquire shape and function from a single cell, by examining the molecular, cellular, and tissue-level processes\u2014gene regulation, cell signaling, differentiation, morphogenesis, and pattern formation\u2014that control timing, size, and spatial organization. It emphasizes dynamical, self-organizing processes across scales that are often described using physical principles (e.g., mechanics, reaction\u2013diffusion, and pattern-formation theory)."},{"id":"2360e96e-a12b-4d21-9b65-36ed1177c0e6","l":"Devices","d":1,"p":"c765ac18-ded5-4ef0-a41a-22be13741559","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Devices are engineered physical systems or components that exploit specific physical principles to perform tasks such as sensing, signal processing, actuation, or energy conversion, translating physical phenomena into useful outputs. They range from macroscopic instruments (motors, sensors) to microscopic and quantum components (transistors, detectors, qubits)."},{"id":"37b01aab-cccf-4089-b0c4-219f60e671bd","l":"Diamagnets","d":1,"p":"58071c68-730c-4a3e-982c-abc9f0cedf67","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Diamagnets are materials that develop an induced magnetic moment opposite to an applied magnetic field, causing a weak repulsion and a small negative magnetic susceptibility. This effect comes from changes in the orbital motion of bound electrons (Lenz's law) and is present in all substances but dominates only when there are no unpaired electron spins (no permanent magnetic moments)."},{"id":"3c920234-cbfc-497b-a8e6-99592df75499","l":"Dielectric properties","d":1,"p":"41dd58dd-d35c-46e8-9594-d7e05b458da6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Dielectric properties describe how an insulating material responds to an applied electric field by polarizing\u2014i.e., redistributing bound charges\u2014which is quantified by its permittivity (or dielectric constant) and electric susceptibility. These properties include dielectric loss and frequency-dependent dispersion, and they determine how the material stores and dissipates electric energy and how fields propagate through it."},{"id":"96cf4e19-746a-4d9b-b2ef-b76773008bbc","l":"Diffusion","d":1,"p":"36754399-ec2b-4962-bb07-ca12c79834e8","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Diffusion is the transport process by which particles, heat, or other conserved quantities spread out due to microscopic random motion, producing a net flux from regions of higher concentration or chemical potential to regions of lower. Macroscopically it is described by Fick's laws (or the diffusion equation) and characterized by a diffusion coefficient that quantifies the rate of spreading."},{"id":"cbf5d6c2-2523-4ba3-abb5-133219e2ce5a","l":"Diffusion & random walks","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Diffusion is the macroscopic spreading of particles, heat, or other conserved quantities from regions of high to low concentration driven by microscopic random motion and described in the continuum by the diffusion (heat) equation. A random walk is a discrete stochastic process of successive random steps taken by an individual particle; in the limit of many small steps it converges to diffusion / Brownian motion, characterized by a mean-squared displacement proportional to time."},{"id":"75066a36-2241-43ef-937e-cfbb9e778a41","l":"Diffusiophoresis","d":1,"p":"36754399-ec2b-4962-bb07-ca12c79834e8","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Diffusiophoresis is the directed motion of suspended (typically colloidal) particles induced by a gradient in solute concentration; interactions between the solute and the particle\u2019s surface produce an asymmetric interfacial pressure that drives a fluid slip and thus particle drift relative to the solvent. In electrolyte gradients, an additional diffusional electric field can produce a charged-particle electrophoretic contribution to the motion."},{"id":"db1e0a86-41cd-4171-bbc2-d6e09da5ac60","l":"Diffusive wave spectroscopy","d":1,"p":"382676e6-fd8f-4fc6-b8e0-3abbc45c379c","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Diffusive wave spectroscopy (also called diffusing-wave spectroscopy) is an optical technique that probes microscopic dynamics in strongly scattering (turbid) media by measuring temporal fluctuations of the scattered coherent light speckle. By analyzing the intensity autocorrelation of multiply scattered photons and using a photon-diffusion approximation, DWS relates the measured signal to the scatterers' mean-squared displacement and thus to microscopic motion or viscoelastic properties."},{"id":"130b51f2-d186-4d8c-bfd2-24111492cf79","l":"Dipolar gases","d":1,"p":"46e2a993-8dce-4c28-beab-fdabe4378664","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Dipolar gases are (typically ultracold) gases of particles\u2014atoms, molecules, or quasiparticles\u2014with permanent or induced electric or magnetic dipole moments, so that long-range, anisotropic dipole\u2013dipole interactions dominate their behavior. Those interactions produce qualitatively different many-body phenomena (e.g., anisotropic scattering, roton-like excitations, collapse, and stripe or supersolid ordering) compared with short-range interacting gases."},{"id":"a631ea74-7753-41a3-96c3-09841aad6030","l":"Dipole approximation","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Dipole approximation is the truncation of the multipole expansion of an electromagnetic interaction to its leading (electric dipole) term, treating the external field as spatially uniform over the system so the interaction reduces to H_int \u2248 \u2212d\u00b7E. It is valid when the system size is much smaller than the wavelength of the field (kr \u226a 1), so higher multipoles and retardation can be neglected."},{"id":"b8f736e2-6707-4b3d-98aa-236ca0e716a0","l":"Dirac semimetal","d":1,"p":"0026b3bc-1a2f-45df-8025-1243dac91047","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A Dirac semimetal is a three-dimensional crystalline material whose low-energy electronic excitations behave like massless Dirac fermions because conduction and valence bands touch at isolated, linearly dispersing Dirac points in the bulk. These fourfold-degenerate nodes are protected by crystal and time-reversal/inversion symmetries and can split into pairs of Weyl nodes if those symmetries are broken."},{"id":"73fe1dbd-ff9a-4072-800a-d8e7600e1232","l":"Direct drive","d":1,"p":"888306e1-6391-43a8-a318-3434270aafd5","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Direct drive is a configuration in which a driver transmits force, motion, or energy straight to the load without intermediate mechanical or energy\u2011conversion stages, minimizing transmission losses and latency. Examples include direct\u2011drive electric motors (no gearbox) and direct\u2011drive inertial\u2011confinement fusion, where lasers irradiate the fuel capsule surface directly."},{"id":"def3e8c1-b28b-493b-9595-67a492f25e02","l":"Directed percolation","d":1,"p":"d9490ac8-a0d2-4d97-8dfc-19020e49aeb9","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Directed percolation is a stochastic lattice model of connectivity or spreading in which links or activity can propagate only along a preferred direction (often interpreted as time or a flow), so clusters grow anisotropically. It exhibits a non-equilibrium phase transition between an absorbing (extinct) state and an active (percolating) state and defines a robust universality class with characteristic critical exponents."},{"id":"2103846f-aab6-4a12-b95b-fcb1670a1a82","l":"Direct numerical simulations","d":1,"p":"1e0c099a-9f2a-4e47-b3f1-7faa28b6a506","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Direct numerical simulations (DNS) are numerical solutions of the full governing equations (e.g., Navier\u2013Stokes) in which all relevant spatial and temporal scales of the flow are explicitly resolved so that no turbulence or subgrid models are used. DNS yields near\u2013exact, high-fidelity data for studying turbulent and multiscale fluid phenomena but is computationally very expensive and therefore limited to modest Reynolds numbers or small domains."},{"id":"21601513-6c94-4bc8-a1ee-6cbff0bbaac6","l":"Direct reactions","d":1,"p":"bbb0ce0d-36da-4da5-a443-df064f36aa17","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Direct reactions are nuclear reactions in which the incoming projectile interacts with only a few nucleons in a single, fast, peripheral collision, producing immediate processes such as elastic or inelastic scattering, nucleon transfer, or breakup. They occur on short timescales compared with compound-nucleus formation and are characterized by strong angular dependence and selectivity to specific quantum states."},{"id":"0afc4f8e-b1e7-4c16-a049-ca2ed082b939","l":"Discontinuous phase transition","d":1,"p":"6e3ddc0a-24fd-4682-bb1b-137944166a4a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"A discontinuous phase transition (also called a first-order transition) is one in which an order parameter jumps abruptly at the transition point, rather than changing continuously, and the transition involves latent heat and a regime of coexistence and metastability between phases. It is typically accompanied by hysteresis and an energy barrier separating the competing phases."},{"id":"7e09caea-4f02-4729-8a60-f706ea5820de","l":"Discotic liquid crystals","d":1,"p":"63f7a3cf-31b7-4d5c-bbcc-58d679dd5139","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Discotic liquid crystals are materials made of disk-shaped (planar) molecules that stack face-to-face into one-dimensional columns which then arrange into ordered arrays (columnar phases) or orient collectively (discotic nematic). They combine fluidity with long-range orientational and positional order, giving strong anisotropic properties\u2014often enhanced charge or energy transport\u2014along the column axes."},{"id":"d0171af5-5603-4934-b23f-d95de0287b81","l":"Discrete symmetries","d":1,"p":"54b389aa-8ccf-49ee-a54e-478ccc2616eb","fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Discrete symmetries are invariances of physical laws under transformations that are not continuously connected to the identity, i.e., they form a discrete (finite or countable) group such as parity (spatial inversion), time reversal, charge conjugation, finite rotations, or permutations. Because they cannot be generated by infinitesimal operations, discrete symmetries impose specific selection rules, classify states and phases, and can be broken in ways distinct from continuous symmetries."},{"id":"bbcb2483-b22c-4bb6-bb81-beb53ff1b1ed","l":"Diseases, physiology & pathology","d":1,"p":"b2c47d4b-a2d2-4f91-aa08-6029cd34a4a7","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Physiology is the study of the normal physical, chemical and functional processes in cells, tissues and whole organisms. Pathology and diseases concern the causes, mechanisms and structural or functional changes that deviate from normal physiology, producing clinical signs, progression and outcomes."},{"id":"e1cb5760-2177-41c4-a04b-b31dabed13bf","l":"Displacement of immiscible fluids","d":1,"p":"cc73b723-a501-45c2-a121-f8c9757c5573","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Displacement of immiscible fluids is the process in which one fluid (the displacing phase) pushes aside another non\u2011mixing fluid (the displaced phase), creating and moving a sharp fluid\u2013fluid interface. The dynamics are set by pressure gradients, gravity, viscous stresses, interfacial tension and wettability, and often produce capillary effects and interfacial instabilities (e.g., viscous fingering) in pores or channels."},{"id":"e49ae35d-10c5-4c0b-a622-135645bf942f","l":"Dissipative dynamics","d":1,"p":"36754399-ec2b-4962-bb07-ca12c79834e8","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Dissipative dynamics is the time evolution of systems in which energy of the degrees of freedom of interest is irreversibly lost (e.g., by friction, viscosity, or coupling to a heat bath), causing motion to be damped and the system to relax toward equilibrium or a nonequilibrium steady state. Such dynamics are governed by non\u2011Hamiltonian equations (damped oscillators, Langevin equations, viscous hydrodynamics), produce entropy, and can display attractors and irreversible behavior."},{"id":"3331008f-47b2-4908-92ad-bc9bd7cd815e","l":"Distortions & defects","d":1,"p":"233556f2-7015-49bd-ad5c-867dda4753e1","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Distortions and defects are deviations from an ideal, periodic or symmetric structure\u2014ranging from local atomic-scale point defects (vacancies, interstitials, substitutions), line defects (dislocations), and planar defects (grain boundaries, stacking faults), to extended or topological defects and local lattice distortions (strain, symmetry-lowering displacements). These imperfections break translational or rotational symmetry and strongly affect a material\u2019s mechanical, electronic, optical, and thermal properties."},{"id":"f4109589-64cd-4a7a-8fcc-ea73fb2d29a9","l":"Diversity","d":1,"p":"019e106f-f2bb-47ed-a0d5-2d513dc9ac5e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Diversity (in physics) denotes the presence and representation of varied personal, social, and intellectual characteristics\u2014such as gender, race, ethnicity, nationality, socioeconomic background, disability status, sexual orientation, age, culture, and disciplinary training\u2014within the physics community. A diverse community brings multiple perspectives and experiences that improve creativity, problem-solving, and the relevance and equity of scientific practice."},{"id":"be0e6945-4b97-4b3d-8435-81912a4d3b07","l":"Domains","d":1,"p":"15f520a3-5c6c-4d9e-8975-81d399140562","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Domains are contiguous regions inside a material in which an order parameter (for example magnetization, electric polarization, or crystal orientation) is uniformly aligned; adjacent domains are separated by narrow transition zones called domain walls and form to lower the system's total free energy (e.g., by reducing long-range fields or accommodating strain)."},{"id":"c2d3e33e-a796-4db2-9b67-22bb3cff586b","l":"Doping effects","d":1,"p":"15f520a3-5c6c-4d9e-8975-81d399140562","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Doping effects are the changes in a material's electronic, optical, magnetic and transport properties caused by intentionally adding small concentrations of impurity atoms or defects; in semiconductors these impurities act as donors or acceptors that introduce energy levels, change carrier concentration and shift the Fermi level to produce n- or p-type behavior. Doping also modifies scattering and carrier mobility, can alter band structure, and may induce or suppress phases such as superconductivity, magnetism or metal\u2013insulator transitions."},{"id":"d3548b79-3e27-4d04-8a34-a9b8ac8cc3b2","l":"Doublon","d":1,"p":"d46f32eb-17b3-4d09-be59-fc3461e542db","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A doublon is a lattice excitation consisting of two particles occupying the same site (a doubly occupied site) in strongly correlated systems such as the Hubbard model. It carries the on-site interaction energy U and, when U is large compared with the bandwidth, can behave as a long-lived, repulsively bound pair that propagates through the lattice."},{"id":"c70636d1-85ee-4b51-835c-1ca3ea7597aa","l":"Doubly diffusive convection","d":1,"p":"2d276ce6-c803-4ba3-813c-b989f2d4bfde","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Doubly diffusive convection is buoyancy-driven fluid motion that occurs when density depends on two scalar fields (typically temperature and composition or salinity) that diffuse at different rates, so competing stabilizing and destabilizing gradients interact. The unequal diffusion produces characteristic instabilities (e.g., salt fingering or layered/thermohaline staircases) that strongly affect mixing in oceans, lakes, planetary interiors and stars."},{"id":"a7fe602e-d99d-424a-b668-a19010a2cc29","l":"Down quark","d":1,"p":"683890e6-25e0-4d29-9686-308d5800fda7","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"The down quark is an elementary spin-1/2 fermion of the first quark generation that carries electric charge \u22121/3 e, color charge (three QCD colors), and baryon number 1/3, and it participates in the strong, weak, and electromagnetic interactions. Together with up quarks it makes up nucleons (proton = uud, neutron = udd); its current mass is a few MeV/c^2 while its effective (constituent) mass inside hadrons is much larger."},{"id":"89adf617-7556-4943-aedc-d4704372aae2","l":"Drag reduction","d":1,"p":"b7f686b6-3282-4e50-85e4-f19b2db66d82","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Drag reduction is the decrease in the fluid-dynamic resistance (drag force or drag coefficient) experienced by a body moving through a fluid. It is achieved by changing the object, the fluid, or the flow (e.g., streamlined shaping, surface treatments, polymers, bubbles, or boundary-layer control) to reduce momentum transfer, skin friction, or pressure-induced form drag and thereby lower the energy needed to overcome the resistance."},{"id":"5bed1a7d-eddf-4741-8258-45cbe8de6361","l":"Drift waves","d":1,"p":"cad250bc-84eb-4c9d-82f1-ce01969ff172","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Drift waves are low-frequency electrostatic oscillations in magnetized plasmas that propagate across the magnetic field due to pressure or density gradients, driven by differential drift motions such as the diamagnetic drift and the E\u00d7B drift. They are often unstable (drift-wave instability) and play a central role in anomalous cross-field particle and heat transport in confined plasmas."},{"id":"4c251551-f31e-43ab-a789-701616a6cea1","l":"Drop breakup","d":1,"p":"b72a2d57-f47e-4e87-9a43-f8320d817bae","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Drop breakup is the fragmentation of a liquid droplet into two or more smaller droplets or fragments when external stresses overcome the droplet\u2019s surface-tension cohesion. It occurs by mechanisms such as capillary (Rayleigh\u2013Plateau) instability, aerodynamic shear or acceleration-driven instabilities (Kelvin\u2013Helmholtz, Rayleigh\u2013Taylor) or electrostatic (Coulomb) fission, with outcomes set by the balance of inertia, viscosity and surface tension (e.g., Weber and Ohnesorge numbers)."},{"id":"927bd7d0-b9d5-46e6-add6-7fa614075c21","l":"Drop coalescence","d":1,"p":"b72a2d57-f47e-4e87-9a43-f8320d817bae","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Drop coalescence is the process by which two or more liquid droplets merge into a single larger droplet after their interfaces make contact and the intervening thin film of gas or liquid drains and ruptures. The subsequent dynamics are driven mainly by surface tension (capillary pressure) and are influenced by inertia and viscosity, often characterized by rapid growth of a liquid \"bridge\" connecting the drops."},{"id":"30ee2f3d-e006-4dd1-93d9-dd85f7de78f2","l":"Drop interactions","d":1,"p":"b72a2d57-f47e-4e87-9a43-f8320d817bae","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Drop interactions are the physical processes that occur when liquid drops encounter each other, solid surfaces, or surrounding fluids\u2014manifesting as collisions, coalescence, bouncing, breakup, spreading or splashing\u2014governed by surface tension, viscosity, inertia, contact-line dynamics and interfacial forces. These interactions determine droplet outcomes in phenomena ranging from raindrop impacts and spray formation to microfluidic manipulation and inkjet printing."},{"id":"a9a585dd-c63b-4f55-b590-8c6538e00a5f","l":"Drop or bubble formation","d":1,"p":"b72a2d57-f47e-4e87-9a43-f8320d817bae","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Drop or bubble formation is the process by which a continuous fluid phase breaks up into discrete liquid droplets or gas bubbles due to the competition between surface tension, inertia, viscosity, pressure differences and buoyancy. Drops typically arise from capillary instabilities and pinch\u2011off (e.g., Rayleigh\u2013Plateau breakup), while bubbles form by nucleation and growth (including cavitation) in supersaturated or depressurized liquids."},{"id":"51fd4190-cd29-4fa9-afea-7274fbad0ce6","l":"Drug delivery","d":1,"p":"c765ac18-ded5-4ef0-a41a-22be13741559","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Drug delivery is the design and implementation of systems and methods to transport pharmaceutical agents to specific locations in the body and control their temporal and spatial release so therapeutic concentrations are achieved where and when needed. It relies on physical transport and interface processes\u2014diffusion, convection, membrane permeation, adsorption/desorption\u2014and the mechanics and transport properties of carriers such as nanoparticles, liposomes, and hydrogels."},{"id":"7b5faa1f-2c6c-4047-84b3-2f4b22882590","l":"Dry granular materials","d":1,"p":"e8836b5d-a348-41e4-9d9e-30e950115672","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Dry granular materials are assemblies of macroscopic solid grains (sand, powders, beads) whose dynamics are governed by contact forces\u2014normal and tangential (frictional) forces and inelastic collisions\u2014while thermal motion and interstitial-fluid or cohesive forces are negligible. They are athermal, dissipative systems that can behave like solids, liquids, or gases (e.g., jam, flow, avalanche) and form heterogeneous force chains."},{"id":"63a2a77a-68e2-4596-8244-acd84717308e","l":"Dualities in field theory","d":1,"p":"ee82ad04-6c47-46a4-a70d-c9a7d7989f6a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Dualities in field theory are precise mappings that show two apparently different quantum field theories\u2014or different descriptions of the same theory\u2014are physically equivalent, meaning they reproduce the same spectra and observable correlation functions. They often relate weakly and strongly coupled regimes or exchange different degrees of freedom (e.g., electric\u2194magnetic, particles\u2194vortices), giving nonperturbative insight."},{"id":"25b79ec2-418a-4f57-bd93-13d46b44ac5d","l":"Ductility","d":1,"p":"9e001bda-d32b-4a59-a216-bfd4c1af8c25","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Ductility is the material property that describes how much plastic (permanent) tensile deformation a substance can undergo before fracturing, commonly quantified by percent elongation or reduction in cross\u2011section and often illustrated by a material's ability to be drawn into wire."},{"id":"f8c39702-4ddd-449c-bd17-093154c1ddf2","l":"Dusty or complex plasma","d":1,"p":"22580d6a-8d20-4653-b2d1-a7de314bc162","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"A dusty or complex plasma is a plasma that contains micron- to submicron-sized solid particles (\u201cdust\u201d) that acquire substantial electric charge by collecting electrons and ions and thereby couple both to the background plasma and to each other. This additional charged component introduces new length- and time-scales and rich collective phenomena, such as strong coupling, Coulomb crystallization, dust-acoustic waves, and sheath- and wake-mediated structures."},{"id":"551e0669-ff26-4709-a8ac-ede312a52a5d","l":"Dynamical phase transitions","d":1,"p":"f2a9db78-1651-4a94-b66e-3e3d72056760","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Dynamical phase transitions are sharp, qualitative changes in a many-body system's time-evolution or long-time non\u2011equilibrium behavior that are analogous to equilibrium phase transitions but occur in time or in trajectory space. They are typically diagnosed either by nonanalyticities in time-dependent quantities such as the Loschmidt-echo rate after a quench or by changes in dynamical order parameters or large-deviation (trajectory) statistics."},{"id":"908b92a6-5196-437c-a2f3-aa1885f5396a","l":"Dynamical symmetry breaking models","d":1,"p":"cdaf7295-546e-4fba-85c5-9d6435b40253","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Dynamical symmetry breaking models are theories in which a symmetry of the fundamental Lagrangian is spontaneously broken not by an explicit scalar potential but by strong, typically nonperturbative interactions that drive the vacuum to develop a nonzero order parameter (a condensate).  This dynamical condensate then generates masses and breaks gauge or global symmetries (for example chiral symmetry breaking in QCD or technicolor models that produce a composite Higgs)."},{"id":"07958120-4b17-455d-84e7-3bb720f69031","l":"Dynamo theory","d":1,"p":"c25108e7-4697-4565-b484-221b808d454e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Plasma Physics"],"def":"Dynamo theory describes how the motion of an electrically conducting fluid or plasma converts kinetic energy into magnetic energy by electromagnetic induction, producing and sustaining self-excited magnetic fields. Key processes are the stretching, twisting and folding of magnetic field lines by the flow, leading to field amplification and eventual nonlinear saturation in bodies such as planets, stars and galaxies."},{"id":"d4ce293a-8c78-4f5c-a4ec-9fd0f295d3ee","l":"Earth's atmosphere","d":1,"p":"88a5cd35-609c-4f0a-8994-5a4cbe488296","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Earth's atmosphere is the gravitationally bound envelope of gases surrounding Earth, composed mainly of nitrogen (~78%), oxygen (~21%), argon (~0.9%) and trace greenhouse and reactive gases, with pressure and density that decrease roughly exponentially with altitude. It is stratified into layers (troposphere, stratosphere, mesosphere, thermosphere, exosphere) with different temperature profiles and mediates heat transfer, weather and climate while shielding the surface from harmful solar ultraviolet radiation and meteoroids."},{"id":"6d4f4a05-dc90-419b-b647-fb63c97e0a62","l":"Earth's ionosphere","d":1,"p":"88a5cd35-609c-4f0a-8994-5a4cbe488296","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"The ionosphere is the electrically conducting part of Earth's upper atmosphere, extending roughly from about 60 km to 1,000 km altitude, where solar and cosmic radiation ionize neutral gases to produce free electrons and ions. Its charged-particle content and currents strongly affect radio-wave propagation, satellite drag, and coupling between the atmosphere and magnetosphere."},{"id":"993587b9-4472-41fd-9896-2df938e75b92","l":"Earth's magnetosphere","d":1,"p":"88a5cd35-609c-4f0a-8994-5a4cbe488296","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"The Earth's magnetosphere is the region of near-Earth space where the planet's intrinsic magnetic field governs the motion of charged particles and carves out a cavity in the incoming solar wind. Shaped by the solar wind into a dayside magnetopause, bow shock and an extended nightside magnetotail, it contains trapped particle populations (radiation belts, plasmasphere) and current systems that largely shield the atmosphere from solar and cosmic charged particles."},{"id":"01f18d6a-0e76-4d44-a8db-946934037ffc","l":"Ecological networks","d":1,"p":"b4d51608-61b8-4e73-8186-ba02e5a41a85","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Ecological networks are graph representations of ecosystems in which nodes are species or functional groups and edges are biotic interactions (predation, competition, mutualism, parasitism), often weighted by interaction strength or biomass/energy flow. Their topology and weights, analyzed with network theory and dynamical systems, govern emergent properties such as stability, persistence, robustness to perturbations, and patterns of energy and nutrient transfer."},{"id":"0f53e9c0-21db-4e21-9bf2-a3dcab5a14fc","l":"Ecological phenomena","d":1,"p":"6f618be6-815c-40bf-bd8a-cb8ffc96bfd5","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Ecological phenomena are the patterns and processes that emerge from interactions among organisms and between organisms and their physical environment\u2014including population dynamics, energy and nutrient flows, spatial pattern formation, and ecosystem responses to disturbances. They typically exhibit multiscale, nonlinear, and emergent behavior that can be analyzed using physical tools such as dynamical systems, statistical mechanics, fluid dynamics, and network theory."},{"id":"61db6117-6a7a-4412-bfa5-9e2d7d79dc5d","l":"Ecologies","d":1,"p":"fcb85338-c881-4bd4-98a9-1563b1194606","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Ecologies are assemblies of interacting organisms together with their physical and chemical environment, treated as complex, adaptive, non\u2011equilibrium systems that exhibit emergent patterns and dynamics. Physicists study ecologies by applying tools from nonlinear dynamics, statistical mechanics, stochastic processes, and network theory to model population dynamics, stability, resilience, and pattern formation."},{"id":"644c1e48-d1c8-48e8-8680-00f0da5a950e","l":"Economic games","d":1,"p":"3f908345-cb08-47c2-bb62-3196134223aa","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Economic games are mathematical models of strategic interaction in which multiple agents choose strategies to maximize payoffs, with outcomes characterized by solution concepts such as Nash equilibria. They provide simplified frameworks\u2014e.g., Prisoner\u2019s Dilemma or coordination games\u2014used in economics, game theory, and physics (econophysics) to study competition, cooperation, and collective dynamics."},{"id":"d5be53e5-a8e2-4cb6-a523-8a8b341cae80","l":"Econophysics","d":1,"p":"37c11ef8-b242-46ad-b0c0-f8e391c074a2","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Econophysics is an interdisciplinary field that applies tools and concepts from statistical physics, nonlinear dynamics, and complex systems to study economic and financial phenomena by treating markets and interacting agents as many-body systems. It emphasizes empirical regularities\u2014such as heavy-tailed return distributions, scaling laws, and volatility clustering\u2014and models them with methods like random walks, agent-based simulations, and network theory."},{"id":"eba4897b-35d4-4eed-9c36-a08ca406d79e","l":"Edge states","d":1,"p":"9e019803-f9d8-4802-b1ac-d2c035d9508f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Edge states are modes localized at the boundary of a finite system whose energies lie in the bulk band gap and which often provide conducting or propagating channels confined to the edge. In many systems (e.g., quantum Hall effects, topological insulators, photonic/phononic crystals) these edge states are robust against disorder or perturbations because they are protected by topology or symmetry."},{"id":"25bc4713-6ee8-4a66-8ab3-a3e33090c90e","l":"Effective field theory","d":1,"p":"a1083ad2-a154-449d-a9ff-59bf46db1c7f","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"An effective field theory (EFT) is a low-energy quantum-field description obtained by integrating out heavy degrees of freedom, keeping only the relevant light fields while encoding high-energy effects as higher-dimension operators suppressed by powers of a cutoff scale. It is organized as a systematic expansion in E/\u039b and constrained by the symmetries of the system, allowing controlled predictions without knowing the full high-energy theory."},{"id":"70cba0b1-a5ec-474b-a023-2ab4c3e484b9","l":"Effects of atomic coherence on light propagation","d":1,"p":"23cdcd6b-ae22-4b6d-92cd-24a9a8bd0577","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Atomic coherence\u2014the creation of well-defined quantum superpositions between atomic energy levels\u2014alters a medium's optical response by causing interference between different absorption and emission pathways. This interference changes the medium's absorption and refractive index (dispersion), producing effects such as electromagnetically induced transparency, slow and fast light, coherent population trapping, and enhanced nonlinear interactions."},{"id":"3869a702-3d03-4e81-a88f-f0fad773d160","l":"Efficiency at maximum power","d":1,"p":"9099c760-673a-40a9-a10f-410acf85f0ee","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"The efficiency at maximum power is the thermodynamic efficiency a heat engine attains when it is operated at the point of maximum output power, i.e., the ratio of useful work power delivered to heat power absorbed at that operating point. It is generally lower than the reversible (Carnot) efficiency \u2014 for many simple endoreversible models it takes the Curzon\u2013Ahlborn form \u03b7 = 1 \u2212 \u221a(Tc/Th)."},{"id":"5800a911-ec6a-422c-875d-14e5808c02b9","l":"Eigenstate thermalization","d":1,"p":"1addd209-da18-41de-9a48-46f5f0f11651","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Eigenstate thermalization is the hypothesis that in generic (nonintegrable, quantum-chaotic) many-body systems the expectation value of any few-body or local observable in a single energy eigenstate equals the thermal (microcanonical) value appropriate to that eigenstate's energy. It also asserts that off-diagonal matrix elements between eigenstates are exponentially small in system size, so unitary evolution from a typical initial state yields steady thermal expectation values."},{"id":"5b2fff9c-516d-44a5-870a-c2502e376353","l":"Elastic forces","d":1,"p":"cc59c6fb-acbb-4237-953b-b874fe5dbd4a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Elastic forces are internal restoring forces that develop in a material when it is deformed, acting to return the material toward its equilibrium shape and storing elastic potential energy. For small deformations they are proportional to displacement (Hooke's law F = \u2212kx), while larger deformations follow nonlinear laws determined by the material's elastic properties."},{"id":"e085ebce-3a96-4bf8-ad67-054c2a4f7e73","l":"Elasticity","d":1,"p":"15f520a3-5c6c-4d9e-8975-81d399140562","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Elasticity is the property of a material that allows it to deform under applied stress and recover its original shape and size when the stress is removed. It is characterized by elastic constants (e.g., Young's, shear, and bulk moduli) and, in the linear regime, follows Hooke's law relating stress proportionally to strain."},{"id":"4f7c2ea6-bed8-48dd-b1c0-cd3e20b556cd","l":"Elastic scattering reactions","d":1,"p":"bbb0ce0d-36da-4da5-a443-df064f36aa17","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Elastic scattering reactions are collisions in which the interacting particles keep their identities and internal states, so no energy is transferred into excitations or particle transmutation. Consequently total kinetic energy is conserved in the center-of-mass frame and only the directions (and hence the momentum distribution) of the particles change; such processes are characterized by scattering cross sections."},{"id":"97b39ca6-7943-41af-a89b-59916cc477dc","l":"Elastic waves","d":1,"p":"0a677b11-f06d-4ced-9385-cf0bf1061a59","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Elastic waves are mechanical disturbances that propagate through a solid or fluid by the medium's elastic restoring forces, carrying stress and strain (but not bulk mass) from place to place. They occur as longitudinal and transverse body waves or as surface waves (e.g., sound in solids, seismic P- and S-waves) and are described by the elastic wave equation."},{"id":"81d98e90-dd34-4852-a1a4-e375304b5585","l":"Elastocaloric effect","d":1,"p":"25bdd3c1-c3ec-4e4e-a84e-6d4152bec3b2","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The elastocaloric effect is the reversible temperature and entropy change that a solid exhibits when subjected to mechanical stress or strain under (approximately) adiabatic conditions, typically due to a stress-induced phase transformation (for example the martensitic transformation in shape\u2011memory alloys). It is a mechanically driven analogue of the magnetocaloric/electrocaloric effects and is exploited for solid\u2011state cooling by converting mechanical work into heat absorption or release."},{"id":"79581256-9db1-48bc-bda9-67f6da9e9b82","l":"Elastomers","d":1,"p":"fea036a1-9c29-4872-ab2c-d052949f7c03","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Elastomers are lightly cross-linked polymer networks that can undergo very large, reversible elastic deformations because their macromolecular chains stretch and retract, giving a low modulus and strongly nonlinear, entropy-driven \"rubber\" elasticity. They typically exhibit viscoelastic (time- and temperature-dependent) behavior and, unlike uncross-linked polymers, do not flow because the crosslinks prevent long-range chain disentanglement."},{"id":"21992cc9-b289-4c96-8daa-2f131e29fb0c","l":"Electrical conductivity","d":1,"p":"36754399-ec2b-4962-bb07-ca12c79834e8","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Electrical conductivity is a material property that quantifies how readily electric charge flows under an applied electric field, defined by J = \u03c3E and equal to the reciprocal of resistivity (\u03c3 = 1/\u03c1), with SI units siemens per meter (S\u00b7m\u207b\u00b9). It is set by the density, charge, and mobility of the material's charge carriers (electrons, holes, or ions)."},{"id":"427070f0-a28d-4ad3-a3ae-961d1c1dfffb","l":"Electrical techniques","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Electrical techniques are experimental methods that use controlled electrical signals, circuits, and fields to probe, measure, or manipulate the properties and behavior of materials and devices. They include transport and conductivity measurements, impedance and dielectric spectroscopy, electrostatic gating and biasing, and electrical detection methods such as lock\u2011in amplification and noise analysis."},{"id":"f51bc5b1-a67c-46ca-80df-c0873f9cdba5","l":"Electric field alignment","d":1,"p":"bb18132a-8998-4023-a343-725c6803d24c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Electric field alignment is the orientation of an electric field (or of electric dipoles/polarization within a material) relative to a chosen reference direction, typically set by an applied external field. It is quantified by the angle between the field vector(s) and that direction and governs direction-dependent forces and polarization responses."},{"id":"5ea59c5d-e69f-4ebb-82c6-d7d38af67089","l":"Electric moment","d":1,"p":"ff402d94-d1d8-4207-a321-f935f060b175","fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Particles & Fields"],"def":"An electric moment is a multipole quantity that characterizes how electric charge is distributed in space and how that distribution produces an external electric potential; the lowest-order electric moment is the dipole moment p = \u222b r \u03c1(r) d^3r, while higher-order moments (quadrupole, octupole, etc.) are tensors describing finer details of the charge geometry. These moments determine the leading terms in the multipole expansion of the potential and field at distances large compared with the charge distribution."},{"id":"78cb9147-814a-4a0f-b143-67b1ddf5c5a4","l":"Electric polarization","d":1,"p":"41dd58dd-d35c-46e8-9594-d7e05b458da6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Electric polarization is the vector field P(r) that gives the electric dipole moment per unit volume of a material, produced by microscopic separation or orientation of bound charges in response to an electric field. It creates bound charge densities (\u03c1_b = \u2212\u2207\u00b7P, \u03c3_b = P\u00b7n) and enters the macroscopic relation D = \u03b50E + P (for linear dielectrics often P = \u03b50\u03c7_eE)."},{"id":"b580bc4d-63fc-4636-9ef6-76e79ea68085","l":"Electric power transmission","d":1,"p":"39ac0edc-a645-42f8-bbf9-04e80cb0460a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Electric power transmission is the bulk transfer of electrical energy from generation plants to substations near load centers, carried over high-voltage lines and associated equipment to move large power flows efficiently. Raising voltage (AC or HVDC) reduces I^2R losses and, together with transformers, switches and control systems, enables long-distance transport while maintaining grid stability and safety."},{"id":"0b00fd00-42c8-4d0a-a0ec-3cdc9925bec4","l":"Electrides","d":1,"p":"81194f70-bbca-4041-b57e-29338e588cac","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Electrides are ionic solids or molecular salts in which one or more electrons occupy interstitial cavities or channels in the crystal lattice and serve as anions. These trapped or quasi\u2011delocalized \u201canionic\u201d electrons give electrides unusual electronic, magnetic and catalytic properties (e.g., low work function, high conductivity)."},{"id":"4e568cff-559b-4f8b-b30b-4e1f4bd7aee2","l":"Electrocaloric effect","d":1,"p":"41dd58dd-d35c-46e8-9594-d7e05b458da6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The electrocaloric effect is the reversible change in temperature and entropy of a dielectric material produced by applying or removing an electric field. It arises because the field alters dipole ordering (and thus configurational entropy), giving an adiabatic temperature change that is often largest near ferroelectric phase transitions."},{"id":"ee0d0060-68a8-4531-b245-540151bb4484","l":"Electrochemical properties","d":1,"p":"41dd58dd-d35c-46e8-9594-d7e05b458da6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Electrochemical properties describe how materials and interfaces participate in coupled electrical and chemical processes involving charge transfer and ion transport; key examples include redox (electrochemical) potentials, ionic and electronic conductivities, electrode kinetics (exchange current density and overpotential), capacitance, and corrosion rates. These properties govern the behavior and performance of devices and phenomena such as batteries, fuel cells, sensors, electroplating, and corrosion."},{"id":"0a3d04fa-1b55-4721-9cd3-44415c98b7c1","l":"Electrochemistry","d":1,"p":"4455b073-c3a2-4a0b-923e-98d7ec0f3b9d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Electrochemistry is the study of chemical processes that produce or are driven by electric currents, centered on redox reactions at electrode\u2013electrolyte interfaces and the accompanying ion transport. It covers how chemical and electrical energy interconvert, describing potentials, currents, charge transfer kinetics, and electrochemical cells."},{"id":"4e209806-75d9-4135-9ccd-573459761ba5","l":"Electrohydrodynamic effects","d":1,"p":"b72a2d57-f47e-4e87-9a43-f8320d817bae","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Electrohydrodynamic effects are the interactions between electric fields and fluid motion, where electric forces (from free charges, induced polarization, or Maxwell stresses) act on a fluid to produce flows, stresses, or instabilities. These couplings underlie phenomena such as electroosmosis, electrophoresis, dielectrophoresis, charge-injection flows, and electrically driven vortices or jets in dielectric or conducting liquids."},{"id":"ed4399a5-3c89-4c22-9e8f-eab258fdca8b","l":"Electroluminescence","d":1,"p":"4c4f4d87-e837-4d65-970d-f5323698a5f1","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Electroluminescence is the emission of light from a material in response to an applied electric current or strong electric field, produced when charge carriers are injected or excited and release photons as they recombine or relax to lower energy states. It is the operating principle of devices such as LEDs and OLEDs, distinct from photoluminescence because the excitation is electrical rather than optical."},{"id":"5aa012f6-4b0c-46d3-b8f7-dbd64d676609","l":"Electromagnetic interactions","d":1,"p":"cc59c6fb-acbb-4237-953b-b874fe5dbd4a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Electromagnetic interactions are the forces and energy exchanges between electrically charged particles and currents, mediated by the electromagnetic field (electric and magnetic fields). Classically described by Maxwell's equations and the Lorentz force law and quantum mechanically by photon exchange in quantum electrodynamics, they are long-range and underlie phenomena from atomic binding to light and radiation."},{"id":"824ae77e-7d83-4b9e-8bbe-1a1d2dcd7cd6","l":"Electromagnetic transitions","d":1,"p":"1cf75de2-e14b-41cd-b733-8f704652db33","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Electromagnetic transitions are changes between discrete quantum states of atoms, nuclei, molecules, or particles that occur through the emission or absorption of photons and are described by the matrix elements of electromagnetic operators. They are classified by multipolarity (electric or magnetic dipole, quadrupole, etc.), which along with selection rules determines the allowedness and rates of spontaneous, stimulated, or induced radiative transitions."},{"id":"4f513e0c-b639-4505-9116-7a0613323cf4","l":"Electromagnetic waves & oscillations","d":1,"p":"cad250bc-84eb-4c9d-82f1-ce01969ff172","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Electromagnetic waves and oscillations are time\u2011varying, typically periodic, electric and magnetic fields that satisfy Maxwell's equations; oscillations refer to local periodic variations or resonant modes, while waves are the propagating solutions that carry energy and momentum through space at the speed of light (in vacuum)."},{"id":"c0187e7a-ee93-4e5b-a1fc-9a55cfde0587","l":"Electron affinity","d":1,"p":"ff402d94-d1d8-4207-a321-f935f060b175","fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Electron affinity is the energy change when a neutral atom or molecule in the gas phase accepts an extra electron to form a negative ion. It is usually quoted as the energy released (a positive value for exothermic attachment, often given in eV), though some sources use the opposite sign convention."},{"id":"b86ebcb8-9aed-4896-be7c-f17b8729bb06","l":"Electron beams & optics","d":1,"p":"23cdcd6b-ae22-4b6d-92cd-24a9a8bd0577","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Streams of electrons and the methods used to generate, shape, steer, focus, and image them\u2014using electric and magnetic fields, electrodes, and materials\u2014analogous to light optics but including charged-particle dynamics. It covers devices and concepts such as electron guns, lenses and deflectors, emittance and coherence, aberrations and space\u2011charge effects, and their use in microscopes, accelerators, displays, and lithography."},{"id":"33873714-13e6-431c-95e4-2aa31fbe7348","l":"Electron correlation calculations for atoms & ions","d":1,"p":"30e65d01-6cd5-442b-889f-ddc0c3c28157","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Electron correlation calculations for atoms and ions are theoretical and computational methods that account for the correlated, Coulomb-driven motion of electrons beyond independent-particle (mean-field) models like Hartree\u2013Fock. They employ approaches such as configuration interaction, many-body perturbation theory, multiconfiguration self-consistent-field, and coupled-cluster methods to obtain accurate energies, spectra, and other atomic properties."},{"id":"9b06df9f-a1aa-438f-9cbe-fcbe4fcea4fa","l":"Electron emission","d":1,"p":"41dd58dd-d35c-46e8-9594-d7e05b458da6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Electron emission is the process by which electrons are liberated from a material's surface into vacuum or another medium when they gain enough energy to overcome the material's work function or are dislodged by particle impact. Common mechanisms include thermionic (heat), photoelectric (photons), field or tunnel emission (strong electric fields), and secondary emission (impact by other particles)."},{"id":"74721827-c4e3-4da0-90ca-dba52158fc6b","l":"Electronically polarized systems","d":1,"p":"c765ac18-ded5-4ef0-a41a-22be13741559","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Electronically polarized systems are materials or assemblies in which an applied electric field produces polarization primarily by displacing or redistributing the electronic charge cloud relative to the nuclei, creating induced dipoles. This electronic polarization is very fast (optical\u2013ultrafast timescales) and governs the high\u2011frequency dielectric response and refractive index, distinct from slower ionic or orientational polarization mechanisms."},{"id":"339a8cdb-9337-4b8e-9138-cc7fcf112782","l":"Electronic excitation & ionization","d":1,"p":"12e1040c-7c2c-478a-b466-c979b57fcaea","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Electronic excitation and ionization describe processes in which electrons in atoms, molecules, or solids gain energy and either move to higher bound energy levels (electronic excitation) or are removed entirely into the continuum as free charges (ionization). These processes occur by absorbing photons, undergoing inelastic collisions, or exposure to strong fields, and require specific energy quanta\u2014discrete level spacings for excitation and at least the material's ionization energy (or work function) to free an electron."},{"id":"cba86ec0-6406-4f81-9400-bb0ca403e79f","l":"Electronic materials","d":1,"p":"c765ac18-ded5-4ef0-a41a-22be13741559","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Electronic materials are solids whose electronic structure and microstructure are engineered to control charge transport, storage, or electromagnetic response for use in electronic devices. Their relevant properties include band structure, carrier concentration and mobility, dielectric and magnetic response, and the effects of defects and interfaces."},{"id":"fb5cc8ef-a0ce-410f-9e08-108c11fe2341","l":"Electronic phase separation","d":1,"p":"41dd58dd-d35c-46e8-9594-d7e05b458da6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Electronic phase separation is the spontaneous division of an electronic system into spatially distinct regions with different electronic properties (for example metallic vs insulating, or different charge, spin, or orbital orders). It arises from competing interactions, frustration, or disorder and can occur on nano- to microscale without necessarily involving separate chemical or structural phases."},{"id":"7eb769ec-4d58-48b3-ac80-5b56c91008ec","l":"Electronic structure of atoms & molecules","d":1,"p":"30e65d01-6cd5-442b-889f-ddc0c3c28157","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"The electronic structure of atoms and molecules is the quantum-mechanical description of how electrons are arranged\u2014specifying their allowed energy levels, spatial wavefunctions (orbitals), and occupations. It determines chemical bonding, spectra, and material properties, and is obtained by solving (usually approximately) the electronic many-body Schr\u00f6dinger equation using methods like Hartree\u2013Fock, configuration interaction, or density functional theory."},{"id":"d71b76c7-1d45-4e0c-b301-13ef8e115d2b","l":"Electronic transitions","d":1,"p":"30e65d01-6cd5-442b-889f-ddc0c3c28157","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Electronic transitions are changes in an electron's quantum state between discrete energy levels (in atoms or molecules) or between energy bands (in solids), caused by absorption or emission of energy. They typically occur via photon absorption/emission or by nonradiative processes (collisions, phonons) and produce the characteristic lines and bands observed in optical and electronic spectra."},{"id":"7a5ddfe4-a73c-47e3-9fc9-a649c78862d3","l":"Electron-ion collisions","d":1,"p":"bbb0ce0d-36da-4da5-a443-df064f36aa17","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Electron-ion collisions are interactions in which free electrons scatter from ions, exchanging energy and momentum via the Coulomb (electromagnetic) force. Depending on energy, they produce elastic scattering, excitation, ionization, recombination or charge exchange, and are quantified by cross sections and collision rates that govern transport and emission in plasmas and astrophysical systems."},{"id":"392f3bdf-86fc-4e76-947b-91479f6ebcdf","l":"Electron & muon capture","d":1,"p":"1cf75de2-e14b-41cd-b733-8f704652db33","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Electron capture is a weak-interaction process in which an orbital (typically K- or L-shell) electron is absorbed by a proton in the nucleus, converting the proton to a neutron and emitting an electron neutrino. Muon capture is the analogous process for a negative muon bound to an atom: the muon is captured by a proton, producing a neutron and a muon neutrino (and providing a probe of nuclear structure and weak-interaction physics)."},{"id":"97e9ea90-9266-4c82-b375-c25fbee92488","l":"Electron-positron plasmas","d":1,"p":"22580d6a-8d20-4653-b2d1-a7de314bc162","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Electron-positron plasmas are ionized gases composed mainly of electrons and their antiparticles, positrons, so the positive and negative charge carriers have equal mass; this mass symmetry produces distinctive collective behavior (altered wave modes and instabilities) and makes pair processes (annihilation and creation) and relativistic effects important in astrophysical and laboratory settings."},{"id":"14a9ce15-69d4-43cb-838c-cd132236246f","l":"Electron & positron scattering","d":1,"p":"12e1040c-7c2c-478a-b466-c979b57fcaea","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Electron & positron scattering is the set of processes in which an electron and its antiparticle interact via the electromagnetic (and at high energies electroweak) force. They can scatter elastically (Bhabha scattering) or annihilate into photons or other particle pairs, with rates and angular distributions predicted by quantum electrodynamics using s- and t-channel amplitudes."},{"id":"f5052eec-9da6-4c10-8956-e8fa4dfdd021","l":"Electron relaxation","d":1,"p":"36754399-ec2b-4962-bb07-ca12c79834e8","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Electron relaxation is the process by which an electron in an excited or non\u2011equilibrium state loses energy and returns toward its ground or thermal equilibrium distribution. This occurs via radiative recombination (photon emission) or nonradiative channels such as electron\u2013electron scattering, electron\u2013phonon coupling, Auger processes, or trapping at defects, and is characterized by characteristic relaxation times (lifetimes) that depend on the system and excitation conditions."},{"id":"62da4b33-cdaf-4c35-bde0-c0c01ca50927","l":"Electrons","d":1,"p":"a45e2665-2df6-42e0-99dc-30bd93812c2c","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Electrons are elementary, negatively charged leptons with charge \u2212e (\u22121.602\u00d710\u207b\u00b9\u2079 C), rest mass \u22489.109\u00d710\u207b\u00b3\u00b9 kg, and intrinsic spin 1/2. They obey quantum (Fermi\u2013Dirac) statistics, exhibit wave\u2013particle duality, and are the primary carriers of electric current and the key players in atomic structure and chemical bonding."},{"id":"9d1d2bab-9293-426e-b822-7a72fdfae578","l":"Electron sources","d":1,"p":"b338ef70-aa71-4ec1-a73a-03a275bda631","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Electron sources are devices or physical processes that generate free electrons or directed electron beams with controlled properties (current, energy, emittance, brightness and temporal or spatial profile) for use in accelerators, electron microscopes, lithography and vacuum electronics. Common types include thermionic cathodes, photoemission guns, field-emission tips, secondary-emission and plasma-based sources."},{"id":"b6850136-0dff-41c9-85da-b6bcf1518811","l":"Electron techniques","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Electron techniques are experimental methods that use electrons\u2014as beams, pulses, or ensembles\u2014as probes or tools to image, scatter from, excite, or manipulate matter, revealing structural, electronic, chemical, and magnetic properties down to atomic scales. Common examples include electron microscopy, electron diffraction/scattering, electron spectroscopy, electron-beam lithography, and electron spin/resonance methods."},{"id":"236b12d9-b512-4a2e-ac80-ced0e0722daf","l":"Electron wave interferometry","d":1,"p":"46e2a993-8dce-4c28-beab-fdabe4378664","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Electron wave interferometry is a set of techniques that split and recombine coherent electron beams so their quantum wavefunctions interfere, producing interference patterns whose fringes encode relative phase shifts. By analyzing those patterns one can sensitively measure electromagnetic potentials, fields, material structure, or decoherence effects at nanometer-to-atomic scales."},{"id":"e6f724c4-622f-421d-8bbb-a758445e4720","l":"Electro-optical spectra","d":1,"p":"ba7ccb8e-d7eb-4e6f-9c1e-b622f5b4e242","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Electro-optical spectra are optical spectra (absorption, reflectivity, emission, or refractive index vs wavelength/frequency) measured while an external electric field is applied, showing how a material's optical response changes with field. They reveal field-induced phenomena (Stark and Franz\u2013Keldysh shifts, Pockels/Kerr birefringence, etc.) and are used to probe electronic structure, excitons, and charge-carrier dynamics."},{"id":"ed385286-31ab-418f-bc3e-be9030396a19","l":"Electrostatic interactions","d":1,"p":"cc59c6fb-acbb-4237-953b-b874fe5dbd4a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Electrostatic interactions are the forces between stationary electric charges or charge distributions, described macroscopically by Coulomb's law. They scale with the product of the charges and fall off roughly as the inverse square of their separation (modified by the intervening dielectric), obey superposition, and determine attraction/repulsion and the system's electrostatic potential energy."},{"id":"b39526d9-02d4-4caf-a280-518aed206059","l":"Electrostatic waves & oscillations","d":1,"p":"cad250bc-84eb-4c9d-82f1-ce01969ff172","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Electrostatic waves and oscillations are collective charge-density motions in a medium (typically a plasma) whose restoring force is the Coulomb field, so the oscillating electric field is essentially irrotational (longitudinal) and magnetic effects are negligible. Examples include Langmuir (electron plasma) oscillations and ion-acoustic waves, whose frequencies and dispersion are set by the plasma density, species masses, and temperatures."},{"id":"93f4f147-531c-49fe-9bb2-5886b99cae8b","l":"Electrostriction","d":1,"p":"41dd58dd-d35c-46e8-9594-d7e05b458da6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Electrostriction is the property of dielectric materials to change shape (strain) when exposed to an electric field, with the induced strain proportional to the square of the local electric field or polarization and therefore independent of field sign. It arises from field-induced changes in intermolecular forces and is distinct from piezoelectricity, which is a linear, symmetry-restricted effect."},{"id":"57034c64-1440-4f6c-a380-b9545d77c82a","l":"Electroweak interactions in nuclear physics","d":1,"p":"1cf75de2-e14b-41cd-b733-8f704652db33","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Electroweak interactions in nuclear physics are the electromagnetic and weak-force processes, as described by the unified electroweak theory, that act on nucleons and nuclei via photon, W, and Z exchange. They govern phenomena such as beta decay, neutrino\u2013nucleus scattering, radiative transitions and parity-violating observables, and are used as probes of nuclear structure and weak currents."},{"id":"4d182066-72ff-497a-9a3c-d9d61d8f50db","l":"Electroweak phase transition","d":1,"p":"ee487445-39e4-4995-9543-fea1735bcbb2","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"The electroweak phase transition is the thermal symmetry-breaking event in the early universe, at temperatures of order 100 GeV, when the Higgs field acquired a nonzero vacuum expectation value and the unified electroweak force separated into distinct electromagnetic and weak interactions, giving masses to the W and Z bosons and fermions. The transition's order\u2014smooth crossover in the Standard Model or first-order in many beyond-Standard-Model scenarios\u2014has important implications for electroweak baryogenesis and potential gravitational-wave signals."},{"id":"cc8a7491-c6ef-4aea-b0c6-284dc689898d","l":"Electroweak radiative corrections","d":1,"p":"ee487445-39e4-4995-9543-fea1735bcbb2","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Electroweak radiative corrections are the quantum (loop-level) modifications to tree-level electroweak interaction amplitudes produced by virtual photons, W and Z bosons, the Higgs, and fermions. They alter couplings, masses, and cross sections (including running and mixing effects) and must be included via renormalized perturbation theory for precision tests of the Standard Model."},{"id":"19d18904-15d7-4639-813e-977ce2369690","l":"Electroweak symmetry breaking","d":1,"p":"ee487445-39e4-4995-9543-fea1735bcbb2","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Electroweak symmetry breaking is the process in the Standard Model by which the Higgs field acquires a nonzero vacuum expectation value, spontaneously breaking the SU(2)L \u00d7 U(1)Y electroweak gauge symmetry to the electromagnetic U(1)EM. This Higgs mechanism gives mass to the W and Z bosons (and, via Yukawa couplings, to fermions) while leaving the photon massless."},{"id":"aa36bec0-0f65-4f5d-bdf2-bc77986b4d0e","l":"Elemental materials","d":1,"p":"81194f70-bbca-4041-b57e-29338e588cac","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Elemental materials are substances made entirely of a single chemical element (e.g., pure metals, elemental semiconductors, or allotropes such as diamond and graphite). Their physical properties are determined by that element's atomic species, bonding, and the material's crystal, molecular, or defect structure."},{"id":"42614004-1a41-47ca-85d0-bc8b88399580","l":"Embodiment","d":1,"p":"376c5d7d-24b3-40a2-a82d-a517ac25dbad","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Embodiment is the physical instantiation of an abstract model, algorithm, or information in a particular material system or device, i.e., the mapping of logical degrees of freedom onto real physical degrees of freedom. It emphasizes that the substrate's specific properties, constraints, interactions and noise determine how the idealized behavior is realized in practice."},{"id":"b22015a2-3968-40e4-befc-d3e24e8f320f","l":"Embrittlement","d":1,"p":"9e001bda-d32b-4a59-a216-bfd4c1af8c25","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Embrittlement is the loss of ductility and fracture toughness in a material that makes it prone to sudden, brittle fracture at much lower strains than before. It results from changes in microstructure or chemistry\u2014for example grain\u2011boundary segregation, hydrogen uptake, radiation damage, or phase transformations\u2014that impede plastic deformation."},{"id":"ae9bb97e-75e5-4183-a8e8-281ddf26e234","l":"Emergence of patterns","d":1,"p":"da8795cf-dd0b-446f-bab1-986dcb2703c6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Emergence of patterns is the spontaneous formation of organized spatial or temporal structures at macroscopic scales that arises from local interactions, instabilities, nonlinear dynamics, or symmetry breaking in many-component systems. Examples include convection rolls in Rayleigh\u2013B\u00e9nard cells, Turing chemical patterns, striped phases in reaction\u2013diffusion systems, and crystal lattices."},{"id":"bb9d205a-1327-40a1-a9f3-91d677ea5de3","l":"Emergent biological functions from complex interactions","d":1,"p":"6f618be6-815c-40bf-bd8a-cb8ffc96bfd5","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Emergent biological functions are system-level behaviors or capabilities (e.g., robustness, pattern formation, homeostasis, decision-making) that arise from nonlinear, multiscale interactions among components (genes, proteins, cells, organisms) and cannot be inferred directly from the properties of individual parts. They typically result from feedback, cooperation, and self-organization and are studied using tools from statistical physics, nonlinear dynamics, and network theory."},{"id":"8cf58117-5bb6-424f-8ef6-910004303f59","l":"Encapsulation","d":1,"p":"bb18132a-8998-4023-a343-725c6803d24c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Encapsulation is the enclosure of a physical system, device, or material within a protective shell or matrix to isolate it from the environment or to confine its constituents. This controlled enclosure modifies boundary conditions and thus affects interactions such as thermal, chemical, mechanical, and electromagnetic transport, used to protect, stabilize, or tune device/material properties."},{"id":"36cf574e-f3b6-477e-8e91-e2d3a7660b70","l":"Endocrine system","d":1,"p":"0a74688b-2eee-4349-a9bd-e93851fe2c5a","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"The endocrine system is the body's network of glands that secrete hormones into the bloodstream to coordinate physiology and behavior (growth, metabolism, reproduction, stress responses). It functions via feedback-regulated chemical signaling pathways that act over seconds to days to maintain homeostasis."},{"id":"35ae50a0-e7bc-4000-be54-958196034818","l":"Energy cascade","d":1,"p":"062f0300-c33d-4782-b24c-43996b0469f3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Energy cascade \u2014 the nonlinear transfer of energy across length or frequency scales in a dynamical system, typically from the scale where energy is injected toward the scale where it is dissipated. In fluid turbulence this often appears as a forward cascade (large to small scales) with a constant flux through an inertial range, while in some systems (e.g., two-dimensional turbulence) an inverse cascade moves energy to larger scales."},{"id":"9320b25b-5182-484c-866d-c379c6650fb1","l":"Energy conversion technologies","d":1,"p":"39ac0edc-a645-42f8-bbf9-04e80cb0460a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Technologies that convert energy from one form or carrier into another to produce useful work, power, heat, or stored energy. Examples include heat engines, turbines and generators, photovoltaic cells, fuel cells, batteries and thermoelectric devices, whose performance is governed by material properties, power density and thermodynamic limits (efficiency and losses)."},{"id":"aa8ef099-e628-4144-b624-c3c6529abb74","l":"Energy efficient accelerators & components","d":1,"p":"d0bb99e3-9d07-40c6-80a0-d8858b63da18","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Energy-efficient accelerators and components are accelerator designs and hardware that minimize net power consumption and maximize the fraction of input energy converted into useful beam energy or recovered for reuse. They achieve this through high-efficiency RF sources and superconducting or permanent magnets, energy-recovery linacs, low-loss power electronics and cryogenics, and system-level measures such as beam-loss reduction and heat reclamation."},{"id":"02eb6e91-8409-4ef3-ad57-d76b9c554e4e","l":"Energy-efficient infrastructure","d":1,"p":"6dca4b7f-ff80-45b8-ab18-bbba041e3393","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Energy-efficient infrastructure comprises the design, materials, and control systems of buildings, transport, and energy networks optimized to provide required services using the least primary energy. It applies thermodynamic and energy-conversion principles to reduce losses (thermal, electrical, leakage), recover waste energy, and match supply to demand, thereby lowering operational and embodied energy per useful output."},{"id":"3bab53f4-af17-4278-95a1-fa1b42b9231c","l":"Energy harvesting devices","d":1,"p":"af9f5a05-6917-40cf-a2ff-5520ff506935","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Energy harvesting devices are systems that capture ambient environmental energy (mechanical vibrations, thermal gradients, light, radiofrequency fields, etc.) and convert it into usable electrical power. They use transduction mechanisms such as photovoltaic, thermoelectric, piezoelectric/triboelectric, or electromagnetic converters to power low\u2011power electronics, sensors, or to recharge energy storage."},{"id":"2c208d45-2118-46a2-b406-a78838263ea7","l":"Energy levels","d":1,"p":"1cf75de2-e14b-41cd-b733-8f704652db33","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Energy levels are the allowed values of a system's total energy; in quantum systems they are the discrete eigenvalues of the Hamiltonian corresponding to stationary states. Transitions between energy levels release or absorb quanta equal to the energy difference, producing characteristic spectral lines."},{"id":"fc959fe9-5962-49a0-bb17-cf856ea49724","l":"Energy transport","d":1,"p":"39ac0edc-a645-42f8-bbf9-04e80cb0460a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Energy transport is the transfer of energy from one location or subsystem to another by mechanisms such as conduction, convection, radiation, mass flow, work, or wave propagation, carried by particles, fields, or waves. It redistributes energy to reduce spatial or thermodynamic gradients (e.g., temperature) and drive systems toward equilibrium."},{"id":"38f275ef-91f9-4e8a-9daa-26438881a5a5","l":"Enstrophy","d":1,"p":"062f0300-c33d-4782-b24c-43996b0469f3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Enstrophy is the spatial integral of the squared vorticity field\u2014commonly defined as E = (1/2) \u222b \u03c9^2 dV\u2014so it quantifies the intensity of rotational motion and small-scale vorticity in a fluid. It is an important diagnostic in turbulence (especially 2D), where it is conserved in the inviscid limit and governs viscous dissipation at small scales."},{"id":"f7a2acc3-d505-4e8e-879d-ddf287210522","l":"Entanglement in field theory","d":1,"p":"ee82ad04-6c47-46a4-a70d-c9a7d7989f6a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Entanglement in field theory is the quantum correlation between the degrees of freedom of a quantum field associated with different spatial regions or modes, such that the global field state cannot be factorized into independent subsystem states. It is characterized by reduced density matrices and measures like entanglement entropy\u2014often showing UV divergences and area-law scaling in local QFTs\u2014and plays a central role in vacuum structure, thermalization, and black hole/holographic physics."},{"id":"04ad919a-2e27-40d8-a505-ce6522d22b99","l":"Entanglement in quantum gases","d":1,"p":"46e2a993-8dce-4c28-beab-fdabe4378664","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Entanglement in quantum gases is the presence of nonclassical, many-body correlations among atoms (or field modes) in an ultracold Bose or Fermi gas such that the joint quantum state cannot be written as a product of single-particle states. It underlies collective phenomena like superfluidity and quantum-phase transitions and is quantified by measures such as bipartite entanglement entropy, particle-number entanglement, and the entanglement spectrum."},{"id":"5cbf417b-4973-4970-94eb-b0f2f418dc74","l":"Entropy","d":1,"p":"25bdd3c1-c3ec-4e4e-a84e-6d4152bec3b2","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Entropy is a thermodynamic state function that quantifies the logarithm of the number of microscopic configurations (microstates) consistent with a system's macroscopic state (Boltzmann: S = k_B ln \u03a9), and so measures microscopic disorder or multiplicity.  Thermodynamically it also represents the part of a system's energy unavailable for doing work, and for an isolated system entropy does not decrease (Second Law)."},{"id":"a1c60fe3-47ab-4c61-a925-5edab473b8f9","l":"Epidemic","d":1,"p":"3eb201bd-796e-4985-836d-af5c7e873190","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"An epidemic is a rapid increase in the number of cases of an infectious disease in a population above the expected baseline, driven by person-to-person transmission. Its spread is determined by contact patterns, transmission and recovery rates, and typically grows when the effective reproduction number exceeds one."},{"id":"be115003-1382-4161-8dbb-1949cec703c5","l":"Epidemiology, prevention & mitigation of disease spreading","d":1,"p":"b2c47d4b-a2d2-4f91-aa08-6029cd34a4a7","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"The study of how infectious agents spread through populations using mathematical, statistical and computational models (compartmental, stochastic, network and agent\u2011based) to characterize transmission dynamics and predict outbreaks. It also addresses design and assessment of interventions\u2014vaccination, quarantine, contact tracing, social distancing and environmental controls\u2014to reduce transmission (e.g., the effective reproduction number) and overall disease burden."},{"id":"f93b82cb-9073-4a1d-97bd-80b6ce2bd6ea","l":"Epidermis","d":1,"p":"fc65882e-b316-4cf1-a96a-ec8557624d80","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"The epidermis is the outermost layer of vertebrate skin: a stratified, avascular epithelium made chiefly of keratinocytes that forms the primary mechanical and permeability barrier against fluid loss, pathogens, and abrasion. It contains specialized cells (melanocytes, Langerhans cells, Merkel cells), undergoes continuous turnover, and its thickness and mechanical properties vary by location."},{"id":"c8cff238-7425-4c03-996c-41e9cc76aa4b","l":"Equilibrium lattice models","d":1,"p":"8bf49057-d087-4218-b1ad-0678d26c2751","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Equilibrium lattice models are statistical-mechanical systems in which microscopic variables live on the sites or bonds of a fixed discrete lattice and interact via a specified Hamiltonian, with thermodynamic properties obtained from the Boltzmann-weighted ensemble (partition function) at thermal equilibrium. They are paradigmatic tools for studying phase transitions and critical phenomena (examples: Ising, Potts, lattice-gas, XY, and Heisenberg models)."},{"id":"a98d4f12-3f2d-469b-a454-f5adc9cfc9fb","l":"Erdos-Renyi graph","d":1,"p":"21802c03-692c-4093-a35c-11955f447cd4","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"An Erd\u0151s\u2013R\u00e9nyi graph is a simple random-graph model where N labeled vertices are connected either by including each possible edge independently with probability p (the G(N,p) ensemble) or by choosing uniformly at random from all graphs with exactly M edges (the G(N,M) ensemble). It is a fundamental ensemble for studying typical graph properties and network phase transitions, such as the emergence of a giant connected component when the mean degree crosses unity."},{"id":"3b5d20ee-4905-4150-9973-5e00559589da","l":"Ergodic theory","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Ergodic theory studies the long-term statistical behavior of measure-preserving dynamical systems, characterizing when time averages along typical trajectories equal ensemble (phase\u2011space) averages. In physics it underlies the ergodic hypothesis used in statistical mechanics, which justifies replacing long-time measurements by phase\u2011space averages for systems that are ergodic."},{"id":"566fb91b-474e-4600-8770-c3101ca0f5d1","l":"Escape problems","d":1,"p":"c6d9db47-7b2d-4d3b-868a-606b65695e3c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Escape problems study how and how fast a system leaves a specified region of state or phase space (or a metastable state), typically quantifying escape probability, escape rate, and mean first\u2011passage time. They arise for deterministic dynamics (e.g., chaotic scattering and basin escape) and for stochastic dynamics (e.g., thermal activation over a potential barrier described by Kramers' theory)."},{"id":"208ce93e-1ae4-4cd3-a079-73ff926dc869","l":"Euler equation","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"The Euler equations are the governing partial differential equations for an ideal (inviscid) fluid, expressing conservation of mass, momentum (and energy) obtained by neglecting viscous and heat-conduction terms in the Navier\u2013Stokes equations."},{"id":"c8e4bac8-e39c-4159-818b-688b72e5e1c9","l":"Eulerian flow field","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"An Eulerian flow field describes a fluid by specifying its properties (velocity, pressure, density, etc.) as functions of fixed spatial positions and time, e.g. v(x,t). It is the viewpoint in which one examines how those field values change at points in space, rather than following individual fluid parcels (the Lagrangian description)."},{"id":"706a1337-b4b5-43a3-92b3-bc12fc41cec1","l":"Evaporation","d":1,"p":"c577bd62-ea57-43b7-9607-1ef45dba3afb","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Evaporation is the surface-driven phase transition in which individual liquid molecules acquire enough kinetic energy to overcome cohesive forces and escape into the gas phase at temperatures below the boiling point. It is an endothermic process whose rate depends on temperature, ambient vapor pressure (humidity), and surface area, and it cools the remaining liquid."},{"id":"c5151ee0-7663-447a-aa31-c098b6b3860e","l":"Evolutionary games","d":1,"p":"3f908345-cb08-47c2-bb62-3196134223aa","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Evolutionary games study how the frequencies of strategies in a population change over time when an individual's reproductive success (fitness) depends on interactions with others, so payoffs determine selection rather than fixed fitness values. They are modeled with dynamical and stochastic methods\u2014e.g., replicator equations, birth\u2013death processes, and spatial/network models\u2014to predict equilibria (evolutionarily stable strategies), coexistence, and pattern formation."},{"id":"346899f4-4f01-4d98-87a3-1668696a3d48","l":"Evolutionary & population dynamics","d":1,"p":"6f618be6-815c-40bf-bd8a-cb8ffc96bfd5","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Evolutionary and population dynamics studies how the sizes and genetic or phenotypic composition of populations change over time under processes such as natural selection, mutation, genetic drift, migration, and species interactions. It employs mathematical and statistical-physics tools\u2014stochastic processes, dynamical systems, and spatial models\u2014to predict allele/trait frequencies, fixation probabilities, coexistence, and spatio-temporal patterns like waves and cycles."},{"id":"925cfd27-f632-4564-9ef8-ed4b01ff7143","l":"Evolution of the Universe","d":1,"p":"26ed7afc-8635-4637-a339-8d0a7cb75be1","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Evolution of the Universe is the time-dependent sequence of changes in the Universe\u2019s geometry, energy content, temperature, and structure as governed by general relativity, particle physics, and thermodynamics. It includes early rapid expansion (inflation), a hot radiation-dominated phase with primordial nucleosynthesis and recombination (the CMB), the growth of structure under gravity, and the current era of accelerated expansion driven by dark energy."},{"id":"81c76a73-f0ec-420b-8026-5cb1b9a2da46","l":"Exceptional points","d":1,"p":"6d0b28b1-a3eb-40f1-bf96-43308f5d2c56","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Exceptional points are parameter values in non-Hermitian (open or dissipative) systems where two or more eigenvalues and their corresponding eigenvectors coalesce, so the operator becomes non-diagonalizable. They act as branch-point singularities in parameter space, producing characteristic square-root eigenvalue splitting, mode exchange on encirclement, and enhanced sensitivity to perturbations."},{"id":"29e56ec8-6039-4a4b-9832-1f48fa943ea1","l":"Excitons","d":1,"p":"d46f32eb-17b3-4d09-be59-fc3461e542db","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"An exciton is a neutral quasiparticle formed when an electron and the positively charged hole it leaves behind in a semiconductor or insulator are bound together by Coulomb attraction, behaving like a hydrogen-like bound state with a characteristic binding energy and radius. Excitons mediate optical absorption and emission and occur in types such as delocalized Wannier\u2013Mott excitons (large radius) and localized Frenkel excitons (small radius)."},{"id":"c707c576-383f-44ea-9901-52ff36b21376","l":"Excluded volume interaction","d":1,"p":"cc59c6fb-acbb-4237-953b-b874fe5dbd4a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Excluded-volume interaction is the steric repulsion that arises because real particles or monomers have finite size and therefore cannot occupy the same region of space, preventing overlap. It is often modeled as a hard-core or short-range repulsive potential and causes nonideal thermodynamic and scaling behavior in gases, liquids, polymers (self-avoiding chains), and colloids."},{"id":"a11d0539-905e-4f28-94f9-e5f20816de30","l":"Exhaustive network search","d":1,"p":"b29359c1-cac2-4880-a9dc-f957b13a3e72","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Exhaustive network search is a brute-force computational method that systematically enumerates and checks every possible node, edge, path, or subgraph configuration of a network to locate a desired structure, solution, or property. It guarantees completeness but typically has exponential time and/or memory complexity, becoming infeasible for large networks."},{"id":"01c724a2-6f97-442f-a3ac-c9410ca76035","l":"Exotic atoms & molecules","d":1,"p":"bd252d88-a608-4897-9416-daf84dee7f6b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Exotic atoms and molecules are bound systems in which one or more ordinary constituents (electrons or nuclei) are replaced by other particles\u2014such as muons, positrons, antiprotons or their antiparticles\u2014or where matter and antimatter bind together (examples: muonic atoms, positronium, antihydrogen, positronium molecules). These species have altered energy levels, sizes and lifetimes compared with ordinary atoms and are used to test QED, measure nuclear structure, and probe fundamental symmetries."},{"id":"050430d9-e7b1-4e3f-bbb3-975081181c3c","l":"Exotic phases of matter","d":1,"p":"f2a9db78-1651-4a94-b66e-3e3d72056760","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Exotic phases of matter are states whose collective behavior cannot be described by the traditional symmetry-breaking and local order-parameter framework; instead they arise from topology or strong correlations and exhibit emergent phenomena. Examples include topological order, fractionalized quasiparticles (anyons), long-range quantum entanglement, and symmetry-protected surface or edge modes."},{"id":"ef090024-1dd2-47eb-8058-f7e000a440d0","l":"Experimental studies of gravity","d":1,"p":"124f9214-0176-475c-b706-99df707e8411","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Experimental studies of gravity are empirical investigations that measure gravitational interactions and test theories of gravity (Newtonian, general relativity, and proposed alternatives) across laboratory, astronomical, and cosmological scales. They use precision techniques\u2014torsion balances, atom interferometry, lunar/planetary ranging, pulsar timing, and gravitational-wave detectors\u2014to probe the inverse-square law, the equivalence principle, gravitational radiation, and possible new forces or couplings."},{"id":"d0b30d9d-44fd-4920-9123-7116745f5e43","l":"Extensions of fermion sector","d":1,"p":"cdaf7295-546e-4fba-85c5-9d6435b40253","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Extensions of the fermion sector are theoretical modifications of a particle-physics model that introduce additional or nonstandard fermionic fields beyond the known quarks and leptons\u2014examples include sterile neutrinos, vector-like or mirror fermions, or extra generations. These additions alter mass spectra, mixing and interaction patterns and are motivated by phenomena the Standard Model does not explain (neutrino masses, dark matter, flavor puzzles, anomalies, etc.)."},{"id":"8b7ac13b-98e1-4d2a-847d-3e71280ff41b","l":"Extensions of gauge sector","d":1,"p":"cdaf7295-546e-4fba-85c5-9d6435b40253","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Extensions of the gauge sector are theoretical modifications that add new gauge symmetries or gauge bosons beyond those of the Standard Model, thereby introducing additional force carriers and interaction terms. Such extensions (e.g., extra U(1) \"dark\" photons, SU(2)R, or larger GUT groups) change symmetry-breaking and coupling structures and are motivated by phenomena like dark matter, neutrino masses, or unification."},{"id":"f9878664-4f0f-49a5-ae1c-9d7d9ce789f6","l":"Extensions of Higgs sector","d":1,"p":"cdaf7295-546e-4fba-85c5-9d6435b40253","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Extensions of the Higgs sector are theoretical models that enlarge the Standard Model\u2019s single scalar doublet by adding extra scalar fields (extra doublets, singlets, triplets, etc.) or altering their quantum numbers, producing additional physical Higgs bosons and changing how electroweak symmetry breaking occurs. Such extensions are proposed to address open problems (dark matter, baryogenesis, naturalness, CP violation) and lead to modified Higgs couplings and characteristic signals in collider and precision experiments."},{"id":"669f5aa1-86be-40b6-8aed-bb6ad02c98fc","l":"Extensions of scalar sector","d":1,"p":"cdaf7295-546e-4fba-85c5-9d6435b40253","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Extensions of the scalar sector are theoretical modifications that add or change the spin\u20110 (Higgs-like) fields and their potential beyond the Standard Model's single Higgs doublet, yielding extra scalar particles and altered electroweak symmetry\u2011breaking and coupling patterns. Such extensions \u2014 e.g., singlet or triplet scalars, two\u2011Higgs\u2011doublet models, or supersymmetric Higgs sectors \u2014 are introduced to address phenomena like CP violation, dark matter, neutrino masses, and baryogenesis."},{"id":"76de2c5d-48bc-43bc-948d-e811ca2cea44","l":"Extraordinary optical transmission","d":1,"p":"4c4f4d87-e837-4d65-970d-f5323698a5f1","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Extraordinary optical transmission is the unexpectedly large transmission of light through subwavelength apertures (often a periodic array of holes) in a thin metal film, far exceeding predictions of classical aperture theory. The enhancement occurs at particular wavelengths due to resonant coupling of the incident light to surface-bound modes (e.g., surface plasmons) and diffracted orders of the periodic structure."},{"id":"fd918032-3589-4778-9f84-ab13d1639204","l":"Extrasolar neutrino astronomy","d":1,"p":"678197d1-8bf1-4c16-86cc-8133fba03b86","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Extrasolar neutrino astronomy is the observational study of neutrinos produced by astrophysical sources beyond the Sun\u2014such as supernovae, gamma\u2011ray bursts, active galactic nuclei, and cosmic\u2011ray interactions\u2014to learn about high\u2011energy processes and fundamental neutrino properties. Because neutrinos interact very weakly and travel essentially unimpeded and undeflected, large-volume detectors (e.g., IceCube, Super\u2011Kamiokande, KM3NeT) use their arrival directions and energies to localize and characterize distant sources."},{"id":"9832e31f-7c2a-430e-b365-b73ff7c40a95","l":"Extrasolar planets","d":1,"p":"c743ca36-88d6-4f26-adea-cdfd0732c4f9","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Extrasolar planets (exoplanets) are planetary-mass objects that orbit stars other than the Sun. The term is sometimes extended to include free-floating planetary-mass bodies not gravitationally bound to any star."},{"id":"6e1181e1-dadd-4fd7-875b-34fda8a1085f","l":"Eye","d":1,"p":"a32482f9-6a8e-410f-80c0-204520778cbf","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"The eye is a biological optical instrument that collects and focuses incoming light with a cornea and adjustable lens onto a photosensitive retina, where photoreceptor cells convert photon absorption into neural signals (phototransduction). Its optical performance is governed by aperture, focal length and accommodation, diffraction and aberrations, and by physical limits such as photon noise and the spatial sampling set by retinal receptor spacing."},{"id":"5cd71ba4-3a51-4625-ad26-8ed87b93f128","l":"Faraday waves","d":1,"p":"0a677b11-f06d-4ced-9385-cf0bf1061a59","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Faraday waves are standing wave patterns on a fluid's free surface that are excited when the container is driven vertically with sufficient periodic acceleration, arising from a parametric instability of the surface. They commonly exhibit subharmonic oscillations (the surface oscillates at half the driving frequency) and can form a variety of spatial patterns set by drive amplitude, frequency, fluid properties, and geometry."},{"id":"afce6bb5-4b42-4ae1-b0fe-7dfe73edcb14","l":"Fast ignition","d":1,"p":"888306e1-6391-43a8-a318-3434270aafd5","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Fast ignition is an inertial confinement fusion approach in which the fuel capsule is first compressed to high density and then ignited by a separate, ultra-short, ultra-intense pulse (usually a petawatt laser) that generates energetic electrons or ions to rapidly heat a small region of the dense core to thermonuclear temperatures. By decoupling compression and heating, fast ignition lowers the required implosion energy and relaxes symmetry constraints compared with conventional central-hot-spot ignition."},{"id":"0d77610f-437a-4fde-af28-204cd0c24886","l":"Fast particle effects in plasmas","d":1,"p":"4b4294b5-d03f-4662-9886-cea042ce7d8c","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams","Plasma Physics"],"def":"Fast particle effects in plasmas are the influences exerted by a minority population of energetic charged particles (e.g., fusion-born alpha particles, neutral-beam ions, or RF-accelerated electrons) on the behavior of the bulk plasma. Through resonant wave\u2013particle interactions and nonlinear orbit dynamics they can drive or damp instabilities (such as Alfv\u00e9nic modes), modify heating, current and transport profiles, and cause energetic-particle losses or redistribution of energy."},{"id":"09309868-ff87-4509-9ba1-048a25b427e3","l":"Fermi gases","d":1,"p":"01af01cc-b889-4c31-9073-c4ec759cd252","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"A Fermi gas is an idealized many-particle system of fermions that obey Fermi\u2013Dirac statistics and the Pauli exclusion principle, usually treated as noninteracting or weakly interacting. At low temperatures it becomes quantum-degenerate, filling single-particle states up to a Fermi energy and exhibiting a Fermi surface and characteristic behaviors (degeneracy pressure, linear-in-T electronic specific heat), as in conduction electrons, neutron gases, and ultracold atomic Fermi gases."},{"id":"cc5c83e5-f441-4d09-8e38-37113c091dcd","l":"Fermionic condensates","d":1,"p":"db7ba2bd-700a-4b0b-b444-b873c97ff818","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Fermionic condensates are quantum phases in which fermions pair up (so the pairs behave effectively like bosons) and occupy a single coherent macroscopic wavefunction, enabling superfluid or superconducting behavior. They occur in systems such as ultracold Fermi gases and electronic superconductors and exemplify the BEC\u2013BCS crossover between tightly bound molecule condensation and Cooper-pairing."},{"id":"4fcc035e-7eb4-46f6-b347-5cc975e5ed95","l":"Fermions","d":1,"p":"d46f32eb-17b3-4d09-be59-fc3461e542db","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Fermions are particles with half-integer intrinsic spin whose many-particle wavefunction is antisymmetric under exchange, so they obey Fermi\u2013Dirac statistics and the Pauli exclusion principle (no two identical fermions can occupy the same quantum state). Examples include electrons, protons, neutrons and quarks, which constitute ordinary matter."},{"id":"78d12677-c7dc-427e-acd7-cbff78a1713f","l":"Fermi surface","d":1,"p":"9e019803-f9d8-4802-b1ac-d2c035d9508f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Fermi surface is the surface in momentum (reciprocal) space that separates occupied from unoccupied electronic states at zero temperature, defined by the set of wavevectors k for which the single-particle energy equals the Fermi energy. Its shape and topology largely determine a metal's electronic, thermal, and transport properties."},{"id":"7c3da561-dbb4-4505-9bb7-1f16ae1f42b5","l":"Ferrimagnets","d":1,"p":"58071c68-730c-4a3e-982c-abc9f0cedf67","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Ferrimagnets are materials in which two or more magnetic sublattices are ordered antiparallel but carry unequal magnetic moments, producing a net spontaneous magnetization. Unlike antiferromagnets (equal opposing moments) or ferromagnets (parallel moments), ferrimagnets often show temperature-dependent net magnetization and can exhibit a compensation point where the net moment vanishes."},{"id":"c4e3b6f2-4f8a-47a1-8774-da5c1339b26f","l":"Ferroelasticity","d":1,"p":"15f520a3-5c6c-4d9e-8975-81d399140562","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Ferroelasticity is the property of certain solids to develop a spontaneous, symmetry-breaking strain (shape distortion) below a structural phase transition, producing distinct orientation variants (domains) that can be reoriented by applied mechanical stress. It is the mechanical analogue of ferroelectricity/ferromagnetism, characterized by domain formation, switchable strain, and usually hysteresis."},{"id":"153de13f-2f91-40e7-8553-022f50949969","l":"Ferroelectricity","d":1,"p":"41dd58dd-d35c-46e8-9594-d7e05b458da6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Ferroelectricity is the property of certain insulating materials to exhibit a spontaneous electric polarization that can be reversed by an applied electric field. It arises from a non\u2011centrosymmetric arrangement of charges in the crystal, producing a switchable polarization and characteristic hysteresis below a Curie temperature."},{"id":"cc09d9c8-369d-43bf-adf2-d091210ad1bb","l":"Ferroelectric liquid crystals","d":1,"p":"63f7a3cf-31b7-4d5c-bbcc-58d679dd5139","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Ferroelectric liquid crystals are liquid-crystalline materials (most commonly the chiral smectic-C or SmC* phase) in which the tilted, chiral arrangement of molecules produces a spontaneous electric polarization that can be reversed by an applied electric field. This broken-inversion symmetry combines fluidity with ferroelectric order, enabling very fast electro\u2011optic switching."},{"id":"851cc311-3592-47a6-a279-398405f42105","l":"Ferrofluids","d":1,"p":"ef0a1bee-2a1f-4f47-aca2-6302db18e67f","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Ferrofluids are stable colloidal suspensions of nanometer-scale single\u2011domain ferromagnetic or ferrimagnetic particles coated with surfactant and dispersed in a carrier liquid, which behave as a magnetizable continuum: they become strongly magnetized in an applied magnetic field yet remain fluid and typically show negligible remanent magnetization. The coupling of magnetic and hydrodynamic responses gives rise to effects like field-induced surface spikes, magnetoviscous behavior, and enables uses in seals, dampers, speakers and biomedical applications."},{"id":"356c6d11-0d3f-4ac4-ac5c-0b870035bf69","l":"Ferromagnetic liquid crystals","d":1,"p":"63f7a3cf-31b7-4d5c-bbcc-58d679dd5139","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Ferromagnetic liquid crystals are phases of matter that combine liquid-crystalline orientational order with spontaneous, long-range magnetization, so the average molecular director and a net magnetic moment coexist and are strongly coupled. They can arise either from liquid crystals doped with ferromagnetic nanoparticles or from molecules with permanent magnetic moments, giving rise to magneto-optic and magneto-mechanical responses."},{"id":"104316dd-ea29-4eb8-a4b9-63df1768b615","l":"Ferromagnetic superconductors","d":1,"p":"2250b5f3-dfa1-4ca7-bedf-fff5d41bd6ea","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Ferromagnetic superconductors are materials that simultaneously exhibit long-range ferromagnetic order and zero-resistance superconductivity in the same phase. This rare coexistence\u2014which ordinarily should destroy Cooper pairs\u2014typically arises from unconventional pairing (often spin\u2011triplet equal\u2011spin pairing) or electronic phase separation and is found in some heavy\u2011fermion compounds (e.g., UGe2, URhGe, UCoGe)."},{"id":"dadd0f02-6a35-463b-997f-67d64338d639","l":"Ferromagnets","d":1,"p":"58071c68-730c-4a3e-982c-abc9f0cedf67","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Ferromagnets are materials whose atomic magnetic moments interact via the quantum-mechanical exchange interaction and align parallel over macroscopic regions, producing a spontaneous net magnetization even without an applied field. They form magnetic domains, exhibit hysteresis and large susceptibility, and lose their spontaneous order above a characteristic Curie temperature."},{"id":"18c1757b-a4b9-40f2-adf4-1ddc448c713c","l":"Feshbach resonance","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"A Feshbach resonance is a scattering resonance that occurs when a bound (or quasi-bound) state in a closed channel is tuned into energetic alignment with the scattering continuum of an open channel, so that coupling between the channels strongly enhances the scattering amplitude. In practice (especially in ultracold atoms) this resonance lets one magnetically tune the effective interaction strength, with the low-energy scattering length varying rapidly and even diverging near resonance."},{"id":"23dc1816-95ac-4954-b14c-f497299effe7","l":"Few-body systems","d":1,"p":"1cf75de2-e14b-41cd-b733-8f704652db33","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Few-body systems are physical systems made up of a small, finite number of interacting particles (typically two to a few), whose dynamics and correlations must be treated explicitly rather than by statistical or mean-field many-body methods. Studies of few-body physics\u2014classical or quantum\u2014focus on solving these interactions exactly or with high accuracy (e.g., the three-body problem, Efimov states), revealing behavior intermediate between single-particle and many-body regimes."},{"id":"10704ce5-dc5d-4865-9577-fbe7a7b59d16","l":"Feynman diagrams","d":1,"p":"a1083ad2-a154-449d-a9ff-59bf46db1c7f","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Feynman diagrams are pictorial shorthand used in quantum field theory to represent and organize the terms in a perturbative expansion of scattering amplitudes and correlation functions. Lines denote particle propagation and vertices denote interaction events, with each diagram corresponding to a specific mathematical integral (via the theory's Feynman rules) whose sum yields physical probabilities and cross sections."},{"id":"b07540f0-2cfb-413d-82a6-e7f9ae151ff2","l":"FFLO","d":1,"p":"ca8ddaad-441c-481c-93b3-65f46125fb93","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"FFLO (Fulde\u2013Ferrell\u2013Larkin\u2013Ovchinnikov) refers to a superconducting or superfluid phase in which Cooper pairs carry a nonzero center-of-mass momentum due to strong spin imbalance or Zeeman splitting, causing the pairing order parameter to vary periodically in space and break translational symmetry. The Fulde\u2013Ferrell variant has a single plane-wave phase of the order parameter, while the Larkin\u2013Ovchinnikov variant exhibits a real-space oscillatory amplitude with sign-changing domains."},{"id":"a0b93f4a-6671-448b-bf2d-b2f3f512bf24","l":"Field emission","d":1,"p":"41dd58dd-d35c-46e8-9594-d7e05b458da6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Field emission is the quantum-mechanical tunneling of electrons from a solid surface into vacuum driven by a strong external electric field, producing a current that rises steeply with field strength (described by Fowler\u2013Nordheim theory). It is most pronounced at high local fields such as sharp tips or nanostructures and is used in electron sources and vacuum devices."},{"id":"a1083ad2-a154-449d-a9ff-59bf46db1c7f","l":"Field & string theory models & techniques","d":1,"p":"233a6cd0-b7fe-491b-b5fd-85fd6b2a7c79","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Field and string theory models and techniques are theoretical frameworks that describe particles and interactions using quantum fields or one-dimensional strings (rather than point particles) and are applied across particle physics, cosmology, and condensed matter. They include modeling tools (Lagrangians, gauge symmetry, effective field theory) and computational methods (path integrals, renormalization, perturbation theory, nonperturbative methods and dualities, and worldsheet/conformal-field techniques) for deriving predictions and connecting theory to experiment."},{"id":"d93a03e0-cbd7-4a22-8232-591d01e6a765","l":"Financial networks","d":1,"p":"b4d51608-61b8-4e73-8186-ba02e5a41a85","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Financial networks are network representations of financial systems in which nodes denote agents (banks, firms, markets, or assets) and edges encode interactions such as loans, exposures, ownership, transactions, or correlations. They are analyzed as weighted, directed, and often multilayer or temporal networks to study contagion, systemic risk, stability, and the dynamics of money, credit, and shocks."},{"id":"26b262fb-2b60-4c48-b9fd-d2ecfd50dd7a","l":"Fine & hyperfine structure","d":1,"p":"30e65d01-6cd5-442b-889f-ddc0c3c28157","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Fine structure: the small splittings of atomic electronic energy levels produced by relativistic corrections to the electron motion (kinetic-energy correction and Darwin term) and spin\u2013orbit coupling, typically much smaller than the principal level separations. Hyperfine structure: even finer splittings arising from interactions between the electron cloud and nuclear moments (mainly the nuclear magnetic dipole and, if present, electric quadrupole), which depend on the nuclear spin and are much smaller than the fine-structure splittings."},{"id":"94ed178f-c482-4e32-ac00-ee7ea9291dcc","l":"Fingering instability","d":1,"p":"d2fd5563-5077-41b4-9d06-f0da03883073","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"A fingering instability is an interfacial instability in which a formerly smooth boundary between two fluids or phases breaks up into narrow, finger\u2011like protrusions because small perturbations are amplified by contrasts in material properties or by the driving forces (pressure, gravity, concentration gradients, etc.). A classic example is viscous (Saffman\u2013Taylor) fingering, where a less viscous fluid injected into a more viscous one in a porous medium or Hele\u2013Shaw cell produces growing fingers."},{"id":"f0877d7c-bf46-4559-8629-e4eb678c2679","l":"Finite-element method","d":1,"p":"45a22032-e27f-4922-9d0c-10c4aa294db5","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"The finite-element method is a numerical technique for approximating solutions of partial differential equations and boundary-value problems by dividing the spatial domain into many small, simple elements and representing the unknown fields with local basis functions on each element. Using a variational or Galerkin formulation, the method converts the PDE into a finite system of algebraic equations that are assembled and solved for the element coefficients, widely used in structural, fluid, thermal, and electromagnetic problems."},{"id":"c55000d0-ab5e-4ac5-a655-7a25c91ff13b","l":"Finite-size scaling","d":1,"p":"1e0c099a-9f2a-4e47-b3f1-7faa28b6a506","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Finite-size scaling is the framework that describes how thermodynamic observables near a continuous phase transition depend on the finite linear size L of the system, by expressing their singular behavior as universal scaling functions of the ratio L/\u03be (with \u03be the correlation length). It enables collapse of data from different sizes and extraction of critical exponents and the thermodynamic-limit behavior."},{"id":"4374ce32-cb3c-4b9c-9806-5925f10f6c00","l":"Finite temperature field theory","d":1,"p":"a1083ad2-a154-449d-a9ff-59bf46db1c7f","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Finite-temperature field theory is the extension of quantum field theory to systems in thermal equilibrium at nonzero temperature, where observables are computed as thermal expectation values with respect to the Gibbs ensemble. It is formulated either in imaginary (Matsubara) time\u2014introducing periodicity and discrete Matsubara frequencies\u2014or in real-time (Schwinger\u2013Keldysh) formalism, and is used to describe thermal excitations, screening, transport, and phase transitions in many-body and high-energy systems."},{"id":"55bba34b-0d31-428f-a430-4f502667434a","l":"First-principles calculations","d":1,"p":"233a6cd0-b7fe-491b-b5fd-85fd6b2a7c79","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"First-principles (ab initio) calculations are computational methods that predict the properties of atoms, molecules, and materials directly from fundamental physical laws\u2014typically quantum mechanics\u2014without relying on empirical fitting parameters. They do this by solving or approximating the many\u2011body Schr\u00f6dinger equation (for example via density\u2011functional theory, Hartree\u2013Fock, or quantum Monte Carlo) to obtain electronic structure, energies, and forces."},{"id":"f4b24eda-ccb4-40bb-8d06-baaa0ccde712","l":"First-principles calculations in plasma physics","d":1,"p":"233a6cd0-b7fe-491b-b5fd-85fd6b2a7c79","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"First-principles (ab initio) calculations in plasma physics are methods that predict plasma behavior directly from fundamental microscopic laws\u2014Maxwell's equations together with classical or quantum particle dynamics\u2014without using empirical closures or fitted transport coefficients. They accomplish this by solving kinetic or many-body equations (e.g., Vlasov\u2013Maxwell, Boltzmann, molecular dynamics, or quantum many-body formulations) numerically with techniques such as particle-in-cell, continuum kinetic, gyrokinetic, or ab initio quantum simulations."},{"id":"0597c026-33ee-4e63-8193-361a6015a83e","l":"Fish","d":1,"p":"91278658-51b4-4d8e-a936-7cfad1266625","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Fish \u2014 aquatic, gill-bearing vertebrates (usually ectothermic) that locomote using fins and body undulations. In physics contexts they are treated as self-propelled bodies interacting with fluids, serving as models for hydrodynamics, bio-locomotion, and collective/active-matter behavior."},{"id":"93b0bf15-0828-42c6-a43a-92f11091a0d8","l":"Fitness","d":1,"p":"da80b6c6-baf7-49b0-922d-bf031c66389e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Fitness: the expected reproductive success or per-capita growth rate of an organism, genotype, phenotype, or strategy in a given environment, usually expressed relative to others; it quantifies how selection changes frequencies and is often represented as a fitness landscape or selection coefficient."},{"id":"29dcd859-9f61-4f1d-bfd9-58eeb4b78832","l":"Flat bands","d":1,"p":"9e019803-f9d8-4802-b1ac-d2c035d9508f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Flat bands are dispersionless energy bands in a periodic system where the band energy is essentially independent of crystal momentum, so quasiparticles have zero group velocity and the band yields a macroscopically large density of states. They typically arise from lattice geometry or fine-tuned hopping interference and strongly amplify the effects of interactions and localization."},{"id":"e24c96b1-1d9e-450c-8694-9342fe98f484","l":"Flavor symmetries","d":1,"p":"54b389aa-8ccf-49ee-a54e-478ccc2616eb","fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Flavor symmetries are internal symmetries of a particle theory that permute or rotate different \"flavors\" (species or generations) of fermions, acting on the generation indices of quarks and leptons and leaving the interaction Lagrangian invariant. They (continuous or discrete, exact or approximate) constrain the form of mass matrices and mixing angles, and their breaking\u2014for example by Yukawa couplings\u2014gives rise to the observed fermion masses and flavor-changing phenomena."},{"id":"29cb32d7-935f-4b5b-b0bd-68e22f864921","l":"Flexoelectricity","d":1,"p":"41dd58dd-d35c-46e8-9594-d7e05b458da6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Flexoelectricity is the linear coupling between electric polarization and a spatial gradient of mechanical strain: a nonuniform deformation (e.g., bending) induces an electric polarization, and conversely an electric-field or polarization gradient produces stress. Unlike piezoelectricity, flexoelectricity is symmetry-allowed in all dielectrics and becomes especially important at small (nanoscale) dimensions where strain gradients are large."},{"id":"c6ce82ed-4c61-454c-8a5d-e184e0925326","l":"Flexomagnetics","d":1,"p":"58071c68-730c-4a3e-982c-abc9f0cedf67","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Flexomagnetics is the coupling between magnetic order and gradients of mechanical strain: spatial variations of strain can induce magnetization or modify magnetic anisotropy, and conversely nonuniform magnetization can produce mechanical torque or bending. It is a nonuniform magnetoelastic effect\u2014analogous to flexoelectricity\u2014that becomes especially important at interfaces and in nanoscale or inversion-asymmetric systems."},{"id":"8d4a7997-b1a2-496a-973d-6b0e1c89f692","l":"Flocking","d":1,"p":"da8795cf-dd0b-446f-bab1-986dcb2703c6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Flocking is the emergent collective motion of many self-propelled agents that locally align their velocities, producing coherent group movement (e.g., bird flocks, fish schools, or active colloids). It is an active-matter phenomenon often modeled by Vicsek-type models and characterized by spontaneous symmetry breaking and long-range order despite noise."},{"id":"7bdaeb8e-1441-4a9d-b96b-e66dafe5e248","l":"Flocks","d":1,"p":"fcb85338-c881-4bd4-98a9-1563b1194606","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Flocks are cohesive groups of self-propelled agents (birds, fish, insects, cells, or synthetic particles) that exhibit coherent collective motion arising from local alignment, attraction, and repulsion interactions. They are paradigmatic examples of active matter and nonequilibrium collective behavior, often modeled by minimal rules such as the Vicsek model."},{"id":"6a7a2e30-ea84-4c4d-8546-31bc89091c80","l":"Floquet systems","d":1,"p":"63bdecc6-b616-484d-b99d-2de7fbc78c6b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Nonlinear Dynamics"],"def":"Floquet systems are physical systems driven by a time-periodic Hamiltonian; by Floquet theory their solutions are characterized by time-periodic Floquet states with associated quasi-energies, and their stroboscopic evolution can be described by an effective (Floquet) Hamiltonian. Such periodically driven systems allow engineered nonequilibrium phases and band structures (Floquet engineering) and can exhibit unique effects like Floquet topological states and drive-induced heating."},{"id":"4f790e9a-7d91-478f-bff6-3554dd01511b","l":"Flow-structure interactions","d":1,"p":"a4ca1a7e-639b-4ff5-8cf3-44b60866fb3e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Flow-structure interactions are coupled dynamical processes in which a fluid flow and a solid structure mutually influence each other: fluid forces cause motion or deformation of the structure, and that motion in turn modifies the flow field. This two-way coupling can produce complex phenomena such as vibrations, instabilities, noise and fatigue, and is analyzed using multiphysics models and experiments."},{"id":"d6399422-830b-4243-9809-a90ec123ab75","l":"Fluctuation-dissipation theorem","d":1,"p":"c6d9db47-7b2d-4d3b-868a-606b65695e3c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"The fluctuation\u2013dissipation theorem states that, for a system in thermal equilibrium, the spontaneous microscopic fluctuations are quantitatively related to the system\u2019s linear dissipative response to small external perturbations. Practically, it connects equilibrium correlation functions (noise) to the dissipative part of the response (susceptibility), with a proportionality set by temperature (k_B T) \u2014 for example, Johnson\u2013Nyquist electrical noise is tied to resistance."},{"id":"9bf053fc-c2f1-45a4-9549-981c8ac75739","l":"Fluctuation mediated interactions","d":1,"p":"cc59c6fb-acbb-4237-953b-b874fe5dbd4a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Fluctuation-mediated interactions are effective forces between bodies that arise because the presence of those bodies modifies and correlates the local fluctuations of a surrounding field or medium (quantum vacuum, thermal order-parameter, concentration, or hydrodynamic field), producing an interaction even without direct contact. Their sign, range and magnitude depend on the fluctuation spectrum and boundary conditions; examples include the quantum Casimir force, critical Casimir forces, and entropic depletion forces."},{"id":"082e0bd7-290e-4ff7-9cdb-0a838c18ee8e","l":"Fluctuation theorems","d":1,"p":"c6d9db47-7b2d-4d3b-868a-606b65695e3c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Fluctuation theorems are exact relations in nonequilibrium statistical mechanics that quantify the probabilities of transient fluctuations of thermodynamic quantities (like entropy production, work, or heat), including events that momentarily appear to violate the macroscopic second law. They relate probabilities of forward and reverse trajectories (e.g., Crooks relation, Jarzynski equality, Gallavotti\u2013Cohen theorem) via exponential factors and imply the usual second law when averaged."},{"id":"38bac9c1-bf64-489f-ae94-8434562d24a2","l":"Fluidized beds","d":1,"p":"c577bd62-ea57-43b7-9607-1ef45dba3afb","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"A fluidized bed is a system in which a bed of solid particles is made to behave like a fluid by passing a gas or liquid upward through it at a velocity high enough to suspend and mobilize the particles. This fluid-like particle\u2013fluid mixture gives excellent mixing and heat/mass transfer and is widely used in reactors, combustion, and particle-processing operations."},{"id":"d1dc41a8-e63f-419e-88a5-8e7aeb3cd01d","l":"Fluid-particle interactions","d":1,"p":"52c158bb-fb1e-4d1b-a117-b6b279c3bc67","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Fluid-particle interactions are the coupled mechanical, thermal, and mass-exchange processes by which discrete particles (solid grains, droplets, bubbles) and a surrounding fluid mutually influence each other's motion and state. They include hydrodynamic forces (drag, lift, added\u2011mass, history), collisions and interphase heat/mass transfer, and underpin phenomena such as sedimentation, dispersion, particle-laden turbulence, and breakup/coalescence."},{"id":"d1c2f095-8593-4b1d-bff5-4a14f67ef2ad","l":"Fluid planets","d":1,"p":"c743ca36-88d6-4f26-adea-cdfd0732c4f9","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Fluid planets are planetary bodies whose bulk is dominated by fluids (gases and compressible liquids) rather than a rigid solid surface, so their shape, internal structure, and evolution are set by hydrostatic equilibrium and fluid dynamics. Examples include gas and ice giants, where convection, rotation, and magnetohydrodynamic processes control winds, heat transport, and magnetic-field generation."},{"id":"2d6115d9-d0e5-4378-b470-059c8b2b1804","l":"Flying","d":1,"p":"21804fac-0546-45ed-98d5-b6af0f0d3228","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Flying is the sustained motion of an object through a fluid (usually air) in which forces generated by the object\u2014principally lift and thrust\u2014overcome gravity and drag. Lift is produced either by pressure differences around wings or rotors (aerodynamic lift) or by buoyancy in lighter\u2011than\u2011air craft, while thrust propels the object and counters aerodynamic resistance."},{"id":"31c5a865-17fb-43e5-9323-da27fcd5f1dd","l":"Fokker-Planck & Vlasov model","d":1,"p":"233a6cd0-b7fe-491b-b5fd-85fd6b2a7c79","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"The Fokker\u2013Planck equation is a kinetic (phase\u2011space) evolution equation for a probability or distribution function that includes deterministic drift and stochastic diffusion terms to model small\u2011angle collisions or noise and resulting collisional relaxation and transport. The Vlasov equation is the collisionless, mean\u2011field kinetic equation for a one\u2011particle distribution that evolves under self\u2011consistent electromagnetic or gravitational fields produced by the distribution itself."},{"id":"5b273a16-b77a-41f4-a4f1-6ccb151ad416","l":"Food additives","d":1,"p":"c765ac18-ded5-4ef0-a41a-22be13741559","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Food additives are substances intentionally added to foods to alter or preserve their physical, chemical, microbiological, or sensory properties. From a physics perspective they modify phase behavior, rheology, colloidal and interfacial stability, optical and thermal properties, and mass\u2011 and heat\u2011transport during processing, storage, and consumption."},{"id":"0c4e512d-9ed7-483f-95ca-a7cfebb3b973","l":"Foraging","d":1,"p":"da8795cf-dd0b-446f-bab1-986dcb2703c6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Foraging is the behavior by which organisms search for and acquire resources (e.g., food) and is modeled in physics as a search process. Physicists analyze foraging using statistical and dynamical tools\u2014random walks, L\u00e9vy flights, and energy/information\u2013optimization frameworks\u2014to predict encounter rates, movement patterns, and optimal search strategies."},{"id":"a85fe3bc-6d49-4059-b83e-a83bb9e995f8","l":"Form factors","d":1,"p":"7de7a12f-2bec-4394-98aa-26aebe36d88f","fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Form factors are functions that quantify how an object's internal structure or spatial distribution modifies its interaction compared with a point-like particle. Typically they are the Fourier transform of charge, current, or matter densities and appear in scattering amplitudes, decay matrix elements, and structure-factor calculations to change cross sections and angular distributions."},{"id":"025d4a06-2dc1-4a4e-93cc-f8638752de8a","l":"Fossil fuels","d":1,"p":"af9f5a05-6917-40cf-a2ff-5520ff506935","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Fossil fuels are energy-rich hydrocarbons (coal, crude oil, natural gas) formed from buried remains of ancient plants and microorganisms that were transformed by heat and pressure over geological timescales. They store chemical potential energy derived from ancient sunlight and, when combusted, release that energy as heat while emitting CO2 and other combustion products."},{"id":"b08d3359-5c6f-43d4-af94-4921af6477e3","l":"Fractal analysis","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Fractal analysis is the set of quantitative methods used to characterize complex, often self-similar structures or signals by their scale-invariant properties. It typically measures scaling behavior with metrics such as fractal dimensions (e.g., box-counting, Hausdorff) and multifractal spectra to describe roughness, clustering, or heterogeneity across scales."},{"id":"8fc18485-2858-482d-b6ba-c26e741dc913","l":"Fractal dimension characterization","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Fractal dimension characterization quantifies how the apparent size or detail of a geometric set or pattern scales with the resolution of measurement, yielding an effective (often non-integer) dimension that captures self-similarity and complexity. Common measures include Hausdorff, box-counting, correlation and information dimensions, used to describe rough surfaces, porous media, chaotic attractors and scaling near critical points."},{"id":"72e394dc-f8d4-44ff-ac08-c74d10d24d9a","l":"Fractals","d":1,"p":"15ac2674-419f-4fe1-b73b-99c33fe6b8df","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Fractals are geometric objects or patterns that exhibit self-similarity and complex detail at arbitrarily small scales, so that portions resemble the whole when rescaled. They are often generated by iterative or recursive rules and are characterized by non-integer (fractal) dimensions that quantify their scaling behavior."},{"id":"edd3593e-02f7-4cf6-a7d6-3f1cd7432e63","l":"Fractionalization","d":1,"p":"d46f32eb-17b3-4d09-be59-fc3461e542db","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Fractionalization is an emergent many-body phenomenon in which the low-energy excitations of a system carry fractional quantum numbers (charge, spin, or statistics) compared with the constituent particles. It occurs in strongly correlated or topologically ordered systems \u2014 for example, fractional electric charge in the fractional quantum Hall effect or spinons and holons in one-dimensional conductors."},{"id":"ea5486d7-baba-459b-bf6a-145a4f675537","l":"Fractons","d":1,"p":"d46f32eb-17b3-4d09-be59-fc3461e542db","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Fractons are emergent quasiparticles in certain strongly correlated quantum many-body systems that are either strictly immobile or can move only along lower-dimensional subspaces, a restriction enforced by exotic conservation laws (e.g., charge and dipole moment) or tensor gauge symmetries. They underpin \"fracton\" topological orders and lead to unusual features such as subextensive ground-state degeneracy and highly constrained, glassy dynamics."},{"id":"a246d7fc-29fc-400a-adad-45f2a4738994","l":"Fracture","d":1,"p":"9e001bda-d32b-4a59-a216-bfd4c1af8c25","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Fracture is the process by which a solid body separates into two or more pieces due to the initiation and growth of cracks under applied stress or deformation. It occurs when local stresses or energy release at a crack tip exceed the material's resistance, and is characterized by mechanisms such as brittle cleavage, ductile tearing, or fatigue and quantified by measures like fracture toughness and energy-release rate."},{"id":"9fd2c50d-f43d-4bca-978d-05a33bff2fae","l":"Free-electron devices","d":1,"p":"3741a35f-83a8-4e50-b7a2-38e600034925","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Free-electron devices are radiation sources and amplifiers that use beams of unbound (free) electrons interacting with electromagnetic structures or magnetic undulators to generate, amplify, or manipulate coherent radiation (examples: free-electron lasers, klystrons, traveling-wave tubes). Because the electrons are not bound to atomic states, the emitted frequency and power are set by the electron energy and interaction geometry, giving high power and wide tunability from microwave to X-ray."},{"id":"8630c41a-a825-46b6-b635-0b356fc9ebf6","l":"Free-electron laser acceleration","d":1,"p":"4b4294b5-d03f-4662-9886-cea042ce7d8c","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams","Plasma Physics"],"def":"Free-electron laser acceleration is the process of increasing electron energy by coupling electrons to the electromagnetic fields of a free-electron laser. In the common inverse free-electron-laser (IFEL) scheme, a high-power laser co-propagates with an electron beam through a periodic magnetic undulator, and the resulting ponderomotive (beat) field produces a resonant longitudinal force that transfers energy from the laser to the electrons."},{"id":"851e2668-1545-43d9-9d52-8e84432e655f","l":"Friction","d":1,"p":"4e823153-8e04-4bdb-a7ca-0f608ecd9453","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Friction is the resistive force that opposes relative motion (or the tendency to move) between contacting bodies or within a fluid. It originates from microscopic surface roughness, adhesion and intermolecular forces, dissipates mechanical energy as heat, and is often characterized empirically by a coefficient relating tangential frictional force to the normal load."},{"id":"614263c8-f762-4e14-8347-932c5ac234c8","l":"Front propagation","d":1,"p":"2375c1d2-a3fc-4d5b-8fde-4f9029004962","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Nonlinear Dynamics"],"def":"Front propagation is the motion of a localized interface (front) that separates two distinct spatial states or phases\u2014e.g., reacted vs unreacted, burned vs unburned, or ordered vs disordered\u2014through a medium. Its speed and shape are set by the interplay of local dynamics (reaction or phase-change kinetics) and transport (diffusion, advection, curvature, fluctuations), and are often analyzed in reaction\u2013diffusion or hydrodynamic models with fronts classified qualitatively as pulled (linear spreading-determined) or pushed (nonlinearity-determined)."},{"id":"da2cf824-a50d-4c47-a974-efa09b98c860","l":"Functional materials","d":1,"p":"c765ac18-ded5-4ef0-a41a-22be13741559","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Functional materials are solids whose intrinsic physical or chemical properties are engineered to perform a specific technological function (for example electrical conduction, magnetism, optical activity, catalysis, sensing, or actuation). Their behavior is determined by designed composition and micro-/nanostructure so they produce tailored responses to external stimuli such as fields, stress, temperature, or light."},{"id":"7d2c6bed-7d52-4257-8a81-13afa07c4405","l":"Fusion products","d":1,"p":"888306e1-6391-43a8-a318-3434270aafd5","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Fusion products are the atomic nuclei, particles, and radiation produced by a nuclear fusion reaction\u2014for example an alpha particle and a neutron in D\u2013T fusion. These products carry off most of the reaction\u2019s released energy as kinetic energy and electromagnetic radiation and determine how that energy is deposited in surrounding material."},{"id":"0e78bc28-1d07-4a4f-b2b3-6f89f7eead59","l":"Fusion reactors","d":1,"p":"1d80a56f-19ce-4fd1-b088-20fed49f32c4","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Fusion reactors are devices that confine and heat ionized fuel to the temperature and pressure needed for controlled nuclear fusion of light nuclei (e.g., deuterium and tritium), releasing energy. They aim to produce net usable power and typically use magnetic confinement (tokamaks, stellarators) or inertial confinement (laser-driven) methods to achieve those conditions."},{"id":"ed707a76-a57e-4d23-bf2b-77bc38de4b31","l":"Gain properties of lasers","d":1,"p":"23cdcd6b-ae22-4b6d-92cd-24a9a8bd0577","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Gain properties of lasers describe how the active medium amplifies light via stimulated emission, typically quantified by a gain coefficient (gain per unit length), gain cross section and gain bandwidth. These properties depend on the population inversion and saturate at high intensity, and together with cavity losses determine the lasing threshold, output power, spectral width and dynamic behavior."},{"id":"aec9de5c-118c-450a-b9e5-199b09cfb063","l":"Galactic disks","d":1,"p":"a2f3cef9-614c-4f7a-8f5b-009885b53abb","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Galactic disks are the flattened, rotationally supported components of disk galaxies (spirals and lenticulars) made of stars, interstellar gas, and dust, typically exhibiting an approximately exponential radial surface-density profile and differential rotation. They are maintained by the balance of gravity and centrifugal forces, often show spiral arms or bars, and are the primary sites of ongoing star formation."},{"id":"3724958d-c1dd-4fcd-ab51-41d9a5893748","l":"Galactic halos","d":1,"p":"a2f3cef9-614c-4f7a-8f5b-009885b53abb","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Galactic halos are extended, roughly spherical regions surrounding galaxies that contain most of their mass\u2014primarily dark matter\u2014and extend well beyond the visible stellar disk. They determine the galaxy's gravitational potential and also host sparse hot gas, globular clusters, and a population of old stars."},{"id":"7d316bf9-a88e-4f5e-853c-9ff4230017ac","l":"Galactic nuclei & quasars","d":1,"p":"a2f3cef9-614c-4f7a-8f5b-009885b53abb","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Galactic nuclei are the central regions of galaxies, and when their emission is dominated by gas accreting onto a central supermassive black hole they are called active galactic nuclei (AGN). Quasars are the most luminous class of AGN\u2014often distant and compact\u2014whose intense accretion-powered radiation can outshine the host galaxy across the electromagnetic spectrum."},{"id":"6183fd44-36b1-475a-bb3f-9457c5bc8bed","l":"Gamma ray astronomy","d":1,"p":"893ecc24-c678-4687-bd3a-0b4a1bcb4cfc","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Gamma-ray astronomy is the observational study of the universe using gamma-ray photons\u2014the highest-energy electromagnetic radiation\u2014to detect and characterize energetic, nonthermal astrophysical processes. Using space- and ground-based detectors covering roughly keV to TeV energies, it probes sources like pulsars, supernova remnants, active galactic nuclei and gamma-ray bursts to study particle acceleration, nuclear interactions, and extreme physical conditions."},{"id":"d3f72522-490a-4ce8-b02c-02cc16495485","l":"Gamma ray bursts","d":1,"p":"b64db5c0-c54c-4fe6-a142-d089ceb55b3f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Gamma-ray bursts (GRBs) are extremely luminous, short-lived flashes of gamma-ray photons from distant extragalactic sources, produced when relativistic jets formed in catastrophic events\u2014core collapse of massive stars (long GRBs) or mergers of compact objects like neutron stars (short GRBs)\u2014dissipate their kinetic energy; they last from milliseconds to minutes and are typically followed by multiwavelength afterglows."},{"id":"96d90b72-2e0b-473b-a719-07e970717753","l":"Gamma-ray generation in plasmas","d":1,"p":"a7a00bcb-5b8d-46f1-876d-978833fa4036","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Gamma-ray generation in plasmas is the production of high\u2011energy photons (keV\u2013GeV and above) when energetic charged particles in a plasma interact with electromagnetic fields, other particles, or matter. Common mechanisms are bremsstrahlung, inverse Compton scattering, synchrotron/curvature radiation (including nonlinear Compton/synchrotron in ultra\u2011intense laser\u2013plasma interactions), and hadronic channels such as \u03c00 decay from energetic ion collisions."},{"id":"e8874e1c-ffb5-4c6c-9b28-8f667e783ccc","l":"Gamma-ray strength functions","d":1,"p":"bbb0ce0d-36da-4da5-a443-df064f36aa17","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Gamma-ray strength functions quantify the average, energy-dependent probability for a nucleus to emit or absorb a gamma ray, giving the mean reduced transition probability per unit gamma energy (for a given multipolarity). They are used in statistical nuclear-reaction models to predict radiative widths, gamma spectra, and photoabsorption cross sections."},{"id":"d199d86e-97af-4dd1-9fe7-fdb00b298b96","l":"Gamma-ray techniques","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Gamma-ray techniques are experimental and observational methods that use high-energy photons (typically keV\u2013MeV energies) and their interactions with matter to probe nuclear and atomic structure, detect and image materials, and study high-energy processes. Common implementations include gamma-ray spectroscopy, radiography/tomography, scattering and attenuation measurements, activation analysis, and astrophysical gamma-ray observations."},{"id":"fad3b2c6-780d-43b4-bca9-d7200d937635","l":"Gauge-gravity dualities","d":1,"p":"cbc41431-fba0-4ff0-8134-2ded6e598922","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Gauge-gravity dualities (holographic dualities) are conjectured equivalences between certain quantum gauge field theories and higher-dimensional gravitational (often string) theories, in which the strongly coupled dynamics of a d-dimensional gauge theory is encoded by a dual (d+1)-dimensional gravity description \u2014 the best-known example being the AdS/CFT correspondence."},{"id":"7e256d71-43dd-4793-87f0-7c8de704416c","l":"Gauge theories","d":1,"p":"ee82ad04-6c47-46a4-a70d-c9a7d7989f6a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Gauge theories are field theories whose laws are invariant under continuous local symmetry transformations (gauge transformations), so part of the field description is a redundancy rather than physical degrees of freedom. Requiring gauge invariance introduces gauge fields that mediate forces between matter fields\u2014examples include Maxwell's electromagnetism (U(1)) and the non\u2011Abelian theories of the weak and strong interactions (SU(2), SU(3))."},{"id":"6287ac05-d956-4c3c-9882-8ea7a798f964","l":"General relativity","d":1,"p":"124f9214-0176-475c-b706-99df707e8411","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"General relativity is Einstein\u2019s geometric theory of gravity in which mass and energy curve four\u2011dimensional spacetime, and free-falling objects move along geodesics in that curved spacetime. Its dynamics are given by the Einstein field equations, which relate the spacetime curvature to the stress\u2013energy content."},{"id":"b42c75b9-b893-45c3-bf01-2539026851d0","l":"Gene regulatory networks","d":1,"p":"d87d9c51-bdc2-43bb-99c3-6e5345a7f631","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Gene regulatory networks are systems of genes, transcription factors, and regulatory DNA elements connected by activation and repression interactions that control when and how strongly genes are expressed. They are modeled as directed networks whose nonlinear and often stochastic dynamics (feedback, switching, oscillations) determine cellular states and responses."},{"id":"fe77b1aa-a279-45e8-ac45-5b89c1facd4d","l":"Genes","d":1,"p":"9990b97d-b485-4ad7-ac0a-33ab94e0e794","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Genes are discrete segments of DNA (or RNA in some viruses) that contain nucleotide sequences specifying the information to make functional products (proteins or functional RNAs) and the regulatory elements controlling when and how much they are produced. They reside at defined loci on chromosomes and serve as the basic units of heredity."},{"id":"8027cc97-dc8a-441c-bdd6-172d0b1f6856","l":"Genome organization","d":1,"p":"5be420fe-48f0-4430-a63d-9d611d837d6e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Genome organization is the three-dimensional, hierarchical arrangement of DNA and its associated proteins inside the cell nucleus\u2014from nucleosomes and chromatin fibers to loops, domains (TADs), and whole chromosome territories\u2014which determines accessibility and regulation of genes. Its structure and dynamics are shaped by physical mechanisms such as polymer folding, protein-mediated loop extrusion, and phase separation."},{"id":"1a3b4d60-b5e0-4a92-9631-9c79d27566d1","l":"Geoengineering","d":1,"p":"6dca4b7f-ff80-45b8-ab18-bbba041e3393","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Geoengineering is the deliberate, large-scale manipulation of Earth's climate system to counteract anthropogenic climate change, typically by altering the planetary radiative balance (e.g., solar radiation management) or by removing and sequestering atmospheric greenhouse gases (carbon dioxide removal)."},{"id":"d289f2d6-db43-409b-b292-ca21c8739364","l":"Geomagnetism","d":1,"p":"29ab1ea0-dd7e-46b7-87dd-0ca29c6be062","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Geomagnetism is the study of Earth's magnetic field\u2014its structure, sources, and temporal changes. It encompasses the field's generation by the fluid\u2011iron dynamo in the outer core, crustal and lithospheric magnetic anomalies, secular variation and reversals, and its interaction with the solar wind and magnetosphere."},{"id":"9f7a4cce-0ded-4976-b8ad-cc75830297fa","l":"Geometric & topological phases","d":1,"p":"4722b240-3d0b-4ab1-831a-75302ab4cf86","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Geometric phases are phase shifts acquired by a system\u2019s wavefunction when its parameters are varied around a closed path, depending only on the path in parameter space (e.g., the Berry phase) rather than on dynamical details. Topological phases are quantum states of matter characterized by global, quantized invariants (e.g., Chern numbers) and robust boundary phenomena that depend on the system\u2019s topology rather than local symmetry-breaking order."},{"id":"6ec86497-6eda-4672-9845-336e089a5f69","l":"Geophysical networks","d":1,"p":"29ab1ea0-dd7e-46b7-87dd-0ca29c6be062","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks","Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Geophysical networks are spatially distributed arrays of sensors and their connecting links (e.g., seismic, GPS, magnetometer, and tide-gauge stations) deployed to observe and monitor Earth's physical processes. The term also refers to network representations of geophysical systems\u2014such as river basins or fault systems\u2014used to analyze connectivity, transport, and dynamic interactions."},{"id":"3e08aa2f-f8bd-4a09-ae45-3dddb833f61f","l":"Geothermal energy","d":1,"p":"af9f5a05-6917-40cf-a2ff-5520ff506935","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Geothermal energy is the heat stored in the Earth\u2019s interior\u2014from residual primordial heat, radioactive decay, and tidal dissipation\u2014manifesting as a geothermal gradient, hot fluids, and magma. It can be accessed via wells and subsurface reservoirs, using conduction or fluid convection to provide direct heating or to drive turbines for electricity generation."},{"id":"552916cc-0bc2-4398-ad56-f8a9fff65e1f","l":"Germanene","d":1,"p":"7a86b9d8-3162-4279-9ca5-90ebad07be7c","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Germanene is a two-dimensional allotrope of germanium forming a honeycomb lattice similar to graphene but with a buckled geometry, typically realized on supporting substrates rather than as a free-standing layer. Its low-energy carriers exhibit Dirac-like dispersion and relatively strong spin\u2013orbit coupling, producing a tunable band gap and potential quantum-spin-Hall/topological-insulator behavior."},{"id":"ffe22c08-57d2-4806-a07d-0e5b18da52e8","l":"Giant resonances","d":1,"p":"1cf75de2-e14b-41cd-b733-8f704652db33","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Giant resonances are high-frequency collective excitations of atomic nuclei in which a large fraction of nucleons move coherently (for example, the giant dipole resonance where protons oscillate against neutrons). They appear as broad peaks in electromagnetic or hadronic response at energies of order 10\u201330 MeV and probe bulk nuclear properties such as compressibility and symmetry energy."},{"id":"251bf3dc-c99f-4de0-a9e9-c412d90ab994","l":"Glow & corona plasma discharges","d":1,"p":"b30d0b9e-de6d-4de6-bfad-565f513573b7","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Glow discharge: a low- to medium-pressure gas discharge in which a steady current produces a luminous, quasineutral plasma with distinct spatial regions (cathode fall, negative glow, positive column) sustained by electron-impact ionization and secondary electron emission.  \nCorona discharge: a localized, low-current ionization that forms at sharp electrodes or strong field gradients (typically at atmospheric pressure), producing ionized layers or filamentary streamers and light without forming a continuous arc, often accompanied by ozone and reactive species."},{"id":"7d99283e-8f70-4d54-80b8-3607295678ae","l":"Gluons","d":1,"p":"556e52f5-6040-4273-b1a4-5e864b903fa5","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Gluons are the massless spin-1 gauge bosons of quantum chromodynamics (QCD) that mediate the strong force binding quarks into hadrons. They themselves carry color charge (there are eight independent color combinations in the SU(3) adjoint representation), so they self-interact\u2014giving rise to confinement at long distances and asymptotic freedom at short distances."},{"id":"8156b91d-56d6-4229-8354-70199155696f","l":"Governance","d":1,"p":"019e106f-f2bb-47ed-a0d5-2d513dc9ac5e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Governance is the framework of rules, procedures, and organizational structures that guide decision-making and oversight of physics institutions, collaborations, and research programs, covering resource allocation, safety, ethics, data and IP policies, and accountability. Effective governance ensures scientific integrity, reproducibility, equitable participation, and responsible use of resources."},{"id":"44cb6cdc-fac7-40d9-ba38-49017dd22ef7","l":"Grafted colloids","d":1,"p":"ef0a1bee-2a1f-4f47-aca2-6302db18e67f","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Grafted colloids are colloidal particles whose surfaces are decorated with chemically tethered polymer chains or ligands, forming a surface \"brush\" of attached molecules. This grafted layer alters the particles' effective size and interparticle forces\u2014providing steric stabilization, tunable interactions, and control over self-assembly and responsiveness."},{"id":"b4e07db7-127a-44d1-9ea2-1b67c695613a","l":"Grand unified models","d":1,"p":"cdaf7295-546e-4fba-85c5-9d6435b40253","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Grand unified models are quantum field theories that embed the Standard Model gauge groups into a single larger gauge group so that the strong, weak, and electromagnetic interactions arise from one force at very high energy. They predict gauge-coupling unification at a GUT scale (~10^15\u201310^16 GeV) and typically imply new particles and phenomena such as heavy gauge bosons, proton decay, and mechanisms for neutrino masses."},{"id":"1a306da3-c882-48b1-b354-26853de1d57e","l":"Granular avalanches","d":1,"p":"8da11568-256e-4eba-ac10-f750c272b3ca","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Granular avalanches are gravity-driven downslope flows of granular material that occur when the applied shear or slope angle exceeds the material\u2019s static yield threshold. Their dynamics are set by grain-scale frictional contacts and collisions (and sometimes interstitial fluids), producing fronts, segregation, and transitions between intermittent and steady flow."},{"id":"7b511424-2876-47df-97bb-1c60d495269b","l":"Granular chains","d":1,"p":"e8836b5d-a348-41e4-9d9e-30e950115672","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Granular chains are linear assemblies of macroscopic particles (grains) \u2014 either physically linked beads (granular polymers) or emergent sequences of contacting grains (force chains) \u2014 whose chain-like connectivity and geometry govern packing, jamming, and stress transmission in granular media."},{"id":"468e38a1-b16f-4618-875d-b481375e6012","l":"Granular compaction","d":1,"p":"756e1ce2-8f47-4e38-ab1a-17a68b30f843","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Granular compaction is the process by which a granular assembly increases its packing fraction (solid-volume fraction) when driven by external perturbations such as tapping, vibration, or shear, as particles rearrange and voids are reduced. The densification is often slow and history-dependent, showing effects like logarithmic relaxation, aging, and changes in force-chain structure and mechanical response."},{"id":"6fc3b2bd-4821-4ade-a389-6384321a438c","l":"Granular fluids","d":1,"p":"e8836b5d-a348-41e4-9d9e-30e950115672","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Granular fluids are collections of macroscopic solid grains that, when externally driven (e.g., shaken or sheared), flow and transport momentum like a fluid even though thermal motion is negligible. Their dynamics are dominated by dissipative contacts (inelastic collisions and friction), making them intrinsically out of equilibrium and described by modified kinetic/hydrodynamic theories that exhibit clustering, size segregation, and jamming."},{"id":"5bbf1eff-0bb0-41a3-8b6e-6630155d7606","l":"Granular gases","d":1,"p":"e8836b5d-a348-41e4-9d9e-30e950115672","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Granular gases are dilute collections of macroscopic particles whose dynamics are dominated by short-range, inelastic collisions so that, unlike molecular gases, kinetic energy is continually dissipated. They are intrinsically nonequilibrium systems that typically require external driving to reach steady states and exhibit behaviors such as clustering, velocity correlations, and non\u2011Maxwellian velocity distributions."},{"id":"1f5271dc-41c2-4520-a44d-44eb205e4e55","l":"Granular mixing","d":1,"p":"8da11568-256e-4eba-ac10-f750c272b3ca","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Granular mixing is the process by which different particles in a granular assembly are redistributed toward spatial homogeneity under external driving (e.g., shaking, stirring, or shear), mediated by collisions, friction, and collective flows. It is distinct from molecular diffusion because contact mechanics, inelasticity and size/shape-dependent effects govern transport and it often competes with segregation mechanisms (e.g., percolation or convective segregation)."},{"id":"cd94673c-b042-444e-8fed-5864866b7aa9","l":"Granular monolayers","d":1,"p":"e8836b5d-a348-41e4-9d9e-30e950115672","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"A granular monolayer is a single-particle-thick assembly of macroscopic grains confined to move effectively in two dimensions (e.g., on a vibrating plate or fluid interface). Its dynamics are governed by inelastic collisions, friction, and external driving, producing out-of-equilibrium collective phenomena such as jamming, segregation, clustering, and pattern formation."},{"id":"39bed7a0-3273-4d2a-a261-5f80d6011fb6","l":"Granular segregation","d":1,"p":"8da11568-256e-4eba-ac10-f750c272b3ca","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Granular segregation is the spontaneous separation of a mixed particulate system into spatially distinct regions according to particle properties (size, density, shape, friction) when the material is agitated, sheared, or flowed. It results from mechanisms such as kinetic sieving/percolation, convection, inertia and buoyancy, producing effects like the \u201cBrazil\u2011nut\u201d phenomenon in vibrated mixtures."},{"id":"8cd02d93-0edd-4259-95f1-79e4eaa1d433","l":"Granular solids","d":1,"p":"e8836b5d-a348-41e4-9d9e-30e950115672","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Granular solids are densely packed assemblies of macroscopic particles whose mechanical behavior is governed by interparticle contact forces, friction, and geometric constraints rather than thermal motion. They can support static shear stresses via inhomogeneous force chains and rigid packing, yet are athermal and dissipative and can yield, flow, or jam under applied stress."},{"id":"a0106d33-6e0e-4b73-8190-a953b55d037a","l":"Graphene","d":1,"p":"7a86b9d8-3162-4279-9ca5-90ebad07be7c","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Graphene is a single atomic layer of sp2-bonded carbon atoms arranged in a two-dimensional honeycomb (hexagonal) lattice. Its electronic band structure has linear \"Dirac cone\" dispersions at the K points, so charge carriers behave like massless Dirac fermions, giving graphene exceptional electrical, mechanical, and thermal properties."},{"id":"08c17dbe-f526-463c-8d53-f3b2a081be60","l":"Gravitational lenses","d":1,"p":"893ecc24-c678-4687-bd3a-0b4a1bcb4cfc","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Gravitational lenses are massive objects (like galaxies or clusters) whose curvature of spacetime bends and focuses light from more distant sources, producing distorted, magnified, and sometimes multiple images and time delays. They are used observationally to map intervening mass (including dark matter) and to measure cosmological parameters."},{"id":"9f7ebc25-d4a6-42d0-832a-25f4382b7208","l":"Gravitational wave detectors","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Gravitational wave detectors are instruments that measure ripples in spacetime\u2014gravitational waves\u2014by detecting extremely small changes in the relative separation or timing between widely separated test masses or clocks. Implementations include kilometer-scale laser interferometers (LIGO/Virgo/KAGRA), resonant-mass antennas, pulsar timing arrays, and planned space-based interferometers (LISA), which convert the wave-induced strain into measurable optical or timing signals."},{"id":"453f63ad-c881-451c-b024-5d2b5aab7199","l":"Gravitational waves","d":1,"p":"124f9214-0176-475c-b706-99df707e8411","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Gravitational waves are propagating ripples in the curvature of spacetime produced by accelerating mass-energy distributions that have a changing quadrupole (or higher) moment. Predicted by general relativity, they travel at the speed of light, carry energy and momentum, and have been directly detected from events like black-hole and neutron-star mergers."},{"id":"997c43cc-8b98-4bc8-a038-3436675ebf88","l":"Gravitons","d":1,"p":"3981a374-dbc8-42de-bdba-73ea858650c6","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Gravitons are the hypothetical massless spin-2 bosons that would mediate the gravitational interaction in a quantum theory of gravity, corresponding to quantized perturbations of the spacetime metric that propagate at the speed of light. They have not been experimentally detected, and a consistent description requires a complete quantum-gravity framework beyond perturbative general relativity."},{"id":"f9d14734-0a69-45d8-8243-9975e835110f","l":"Gravity in dimensions other than four","d":1,"p":"124f9214-0176-475c-b706-99df707e8411","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Gravity in dimensions other than four studies gravitational theories in spacetimes with a number of spatial and/or temporal dimensions different from the physical 3+1. The behavior changes: in N spatial dimensions the Newtonian force scales as 1/r^{N-1} (so the potential \u221d1/r^{N-2}, logarithmic for N=2), general relativity has different propagating degrees of freedom (e.g. no local gravitons in 2+1), and new phenomena arise such as higher-dimensional black holes and nontrivial effects from higher-curvature terms."},{"id":"d32ebb8f-d6ea-4fbe-8115-d00b2924ef90","l":"Growth","d":1,"p":"eef7c13b-c67f-4d33-be97-76cee58e700a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Growth \u2014 in physics, the time-dependent increase of a system's size, extent, amplitude, or number of constituents, typically quantified by a growth rate or law (e.g., exponential, linear, or power-law). It describes processes ranging from crystal and thin-film formation to instability amplification and coarsening during phase ordering."},{"id":"6d551114-557b-413d-acf5-87446c2dc782","l":"Guided network search","d":1,"p":"b29359c1-cac2-4880-a9dc-f957b13a3e72","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Guided network search is a method for exploring a graph to find nodes, paths, or substructures by biasing the search with external information or heuristics so it concentrates on promising regions instead of scanning blindly. In physics applications the guidance may come from physical fields, dynamical flows, node centrality or learned policies, enabling more efficient routing, detection of communities, or solution of inverse problems than random or exhaustive searches."},{"id":"e5c8fc20-498a-4484-8dc7-1dc6d0bbc0ae","l":"Gyrofluid & gyrokinetics","d":1,"p":"233a6cd0-b7fe-491b-b5fd-85fd6b2a7c79","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Gyrokinetics is a reduced kinetic theory for strongly magnetized plasmas that averages over the fast cyclotron (gyro) motion to evolve the particle distribution in five-dimensional phase space, retaining finite-Larmor-radius effects, parallel dynamics, and resonant wave\u2013particle interactions needed to describe microturbulence and transport.  \nGyrofluid models are fluid-like closures derived from the gyrokinetic equation that evolve a finite set of velocity moments (density, flow, pressures, etc.) with finite-Larmor-radius and kinetic (e.g., Landau-damping) corrections to capture essential kinetic behavior at reduced computational cost."},{"id":"a02ccc4e-637b-4bef-aabd-063305a81ca2","l":"Gyromagnetic ratio","d":1,"p":"7de7a12f-2bec-4394-98aa-26aebe36d88f","fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"The gyromagnetic ratio (magnetogyric ratio) is the ratio of an object's magnetic dipole moment \u03bc to its angular momentum J, \u03b3 = \u03bc/J, and for a classical charged rotor equals q/2m (with a g\u2011factor correction for intrinsic spin). It sets the Larmor precession frequency in a magnetic field via \u03c9 = \u03b3B."},{"id":"eb3165eb-0e43-42a0-a62d-4e08aca7d291","l":"Half-metals","d":1,"p":"58071c68-730c-4a3e-982c-abc9f0cedf67","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Half-metals are magnetic materials whose electronic band structure is metallic for one spin orientation but gapped (insulating or semiconducting) for the opposite spin, yielding (ideally) 100% spin polarization of carriers at the Fermi level and making them valuable for spintronic applications."},{"id":"99c1e71a-1639-42a5-8e00-6e258fc0b4f5","l":"Hall effect","d":1,"p":"36754399-ec2b-4962-bb07-ca12c79834e8","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Hall effect is the development of a transverse electric field (Hall voltage) across a current-carrying conductor or semiconductor placed in a perpendicular magnetic field, caused by the Lorentz-force deflection of charge carriers. The sign and magnitude of the Hall voltage reveal the carriers' sign and density and are widely used to measure magnetic fields, carrier concentration, and mobility."},{"id":"41b9e865-2d85-4871-8f13-565775ce0359","l":"Hardness","d":1,"p":"9e001bda-d32b-4a59-a216-bfd4c1af8c25","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Hardness is a measure of a solid's resistance to localized permanent deformation, such as indentation or scratching. It is quantified by empirical tests and scales (e.g., Vickers, Brinell, Rockwell for indentation; Mohs for scratch resistance) and depends on bonding and microstructure rather than being a fundamental material constant."},{"id":"bc0c832d-ff8b-438a-8c02-81fa6c15ed25","l":"Hard scattering","d":1,"p":"7321e0f7-3b12-45a2-aa4a-28c2a7e03149","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Hard scattering is a high-energy collision with large momentum transfer (Q^2 \u226b \u039b_QCD^2) that probes short-distance, pointlike constituents of a target so that perturbative methods (e.g., perturbative QCD) apply. It typically produces high-transverse-momentum particles or jets and contrasts with low-Q^2 \"soft\" processes."},{"id":"85bf0bbf-6b72-45cc-824d-030177187546","l":"Hard sphere colloids","d":1,"p":"ef0a1bee-2a1f-4f47-aca2-6302db18e67f","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Hard-sphere colloids are suspensions of nearly spherical particles whose interactions are dominated by short-range excluded-volume (hard-core) repulsion, so particles behave like impenetrable spheres. They are widely used as model systems to study entropy-driven phenomena such as fluid\u2013crystal transitions, packing, and glass formation."},{"id":"ddb44c70-ff82-4054-9055-e8bc39b91eaf","l":"Heart","d":1,"p":"a32482f9-6a8e-410f-80c0-204520778cbf","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"The heart is a muscular organ that acts as a cyclic pressure-generating pump, propelling blood through the circulatory system via coordinated contractions. Its operation involves electromechanical coupling (cardiac action potentials triggering contraction), valvular mechanics, and the fluid dynamics of pulsatile blood flow."},{"id":"1df66481-700c-4dff-8592-5b40122c2e7a","l":"Heat radiation","d":1,"p":"25bdd3c1-c3ec-4e4e-a84e-6d4152bec3b2","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Heat radiation (thermal radiation) is electromagnetic radiation emitted by matter due to the thermal motion of charged particles, carrying energy away and allowing heat transfer even through vacuum. Its spectral distribution and intensity depend on temperature and emissivity\u2014described for ideal emitters by Planck\u2019s law and integrated by the Stefan\u2013Boltzmann law."},{"id":"95b916ee-a0ff-4079-9e14-69d436d0e6db","l":"Heat transfer","d":1,"p":"25bdd3c1-c3ec-4e4e-a84e-6d4152bec3b2","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Heat transfer is the physical process by which thermal energy flows from regions of higher temperature to regions of lower temperature, driven by temperature differences. It occurs via three primary mechanisms\u2014conduction (microscopic transfer through material interactions and carriers), convection (energy carried by bulk fluid motion), and thermal radiation (energy emitted as electromagnetic waves)."},{"id":"b18fe941-d388-4e68-b11f-11c128179eb7","l":"Heavy-fermion systems","d":1,"p":"29bd219f-a624-4ef5-b4c5-f9049f2be66e","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Heavy-fermion systems are intermetallic compounds (typically containing Ce, Yb, or U) in which strong hybridization and correlations between localized f-electrons and conduction electrons produce low-temperature quasiparticles with anomalously large effective masses. They are characterized by huge electronic specific-heat coefficients and Pauli susceptibilities and often show Kondo screening, unconventional superconductivity, and quantum critical behavior."},{"id":"2c4f193a-b4fc-40c0-aca9-6a615eef1b40","l":"Heavy neutrinos","d":1,"p":"3981a374-dbc8-42de-bdba-73ea858650c6","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Heavy neutrinos are hypothetical neutral fermions similar to the known light (active) neutrinos but with much larger masses and typically only very weak couplings to Standard Model particles. They commonly appear as right-handed or \u201csterile\u201d neutrinos in extensions of the Standard Model (e.g., seesaw models), can be Dirac or Majorana, and are invoked to explain neutrino masses, leptogenesis, and sometimes dark matter."},{"id":"d7555320-c35e-4bfc-b17d-6e2fe467463b","l":"Heavy-particle decays","d":1,"p":"1cf75de2-e14b-41cd-b733-8f704652db33","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Heavy-particle decays are processes in which an unstable particle whose mass is large compared with its decay products transforms into lighter particles via the fundamental interactions (strong, electromagnetic, or weak); each decay mode is characterized by a rate (lifetime or width) and a branching fraction and obeys conservation laws. Studying these decays\u2014their final states, kinematics and symmetries\u2014reveals the particle's internal structure, interaction strengths, and possible physics beyond the Standard Model."},{"id":"d0a4d1a0-837a-49cc-b912-168c41221d7a","l":"Hele-Shaw cell","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"A Hele\u2013Shaw cell is two closely spaced parallel plates forming a narrow gap filled with fluid, producing quasi-two-dimensional, low\u2011Reynolds\u2011number flow whose gap\u2011averaged velocity is proportional to the pressure gradient (Hele\u2011Shaw/Darcy law). It is widely used to study interfacial phenomena such as viscous fingering and to model porous\u2011media flows."},{"id":"c7f91fe1-fd36-453e-afcb-718083b656cb","l":"Hele-shaw flows","d":1,"p":"6aed0168-d867-4733-835f-da82de3c65f5","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Hele-Shaw flows are viscous, low\u2011Reynolds\u2011number flows confined to the thin gap between two closely spaced parallel plates, so that motion is effectively two\u2011dimensional and dominated by viscous (lubrication) forces. Under the gap\u2011averaging approximation the in\u2011plane velocity is proportional to the pressure gradient (an analogue of Darcy\u2019s law) and the pressure field satisfies a Laplace/Poisson equation, making Hele\u2011Shaw cells a canonical model for phenomena such as viscous fingering and porous\u2011media flow."},{"id":"8839c11e-d1d8-494a-a16a-cdd94168af72","l":"Helicity","d":1,"p":"062f0300-c33d-4782-b24c-43996b0469f3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Helicity is a measure of handedness: for a particle it is the projection of its spin angular momentum onto its momentum direction (a quantum number that equals chirality for massless particles but is frame\u2011dependent for massive ones). In continuum fields or fluids helicity is a pseudoscalar functional\u2014e.g., \u222bA\u00b7B in electromagnetism or \u222bv\u00b7(\u2207\u00d7v) in fluid dynamics\u2014that quantifies the linkage or knottedness of field lines and is often conserved in ideal dynamics."},{"id":"589b3c81-ae46-4c4f-869b-ffec28a737df","l":"Helimagnets","d":1,"p":"58071c68-730c-4a3e-982c-abc9f0cedf67","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Helimagnets are magnetic materials whose lowest-energy spin configuration is a noncollinear helix in which the local magnetization rotates continuously with a fixed pitch (wavevector) along a propagation direction, forming a periodic spiral spin density wave. Such helical order typically arises from competing exchange interactions or chiral Dzyaloshinskii\u2013Moriya coupling in noncentrosymmetric crystals and leads to distinctive magnetic and transport phenomena (e.g., conical states and skyrmion lattices)."},{"id":"7366f21e-d7d6-4f68-b1c2-d7642c6ea4bf","l":"Heliosphere","d":1,"p":"88a5cd35-609c-4f0a-8994-5a4cbe488296","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"The heliosphere is the bubble-shaped region of space around the Sun dominated by the outward-flowing solar wind and the Sun's magnetic field, within which solar plasma and fields outweigh the influence of the local interstellar medium. Its outer boundary, the heliopause, marks where solar wind pressure balances the interstellar medium (after passing the termination shock and heliosheath), and it extends roughly on the order of 100 astronomical units from the Sun."},{"id":"735be4e0-f261-4524-8c0a-f133d560764a","l":"H & He burning","d":1,"p":"670457a5-ade7-47cf-b508-6ff87f3d30e0","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics","Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Hydrogen (H) burning is the set of stellar nuclear fusion reactions\u2014primarily the proton\u2013proton chains and the CNO cycles\u2014that convert hydrogen into helium in stellar cores or shells and release the energy that powers the star. Helium (He) burning is the subsequent fusion of helium nuclei into carbon and heavier elements (chiefly via the triple\u2011alpha process and alpha captures), occurring at higher temperatures and marking later stages of stellar evolution."},{"id":"0dcb4eb8-43eb-4766-a3a4-83f1360cabe2","l":"H & He induced nuclear reactions","d":1,"p":"bbb0ce0d-36da-4da5-a443-df064f36aa17","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"H & He induced nuclear reactions are processes in which light charged particles\u2014hydrogen isotopes (protons, deuterons, tritons) or helium isotopes (3He, \u03b1 particles)\u2014are used as projectiles to excite, fuse with, or eject constituents of target nuclei, producing different residual nuclei and emitted particles or radiation. The reaction channels (capture, transfer, (p,n), (\u03b1,n), fusion, breakup, etc.) and their cross sections are strongly energy-dependent because of the Coulomb barrier, and these reactions are important for nuclear-structure studies, isotope production, and astrophysical nucleosynthesis."},{"id":"74d0f5e2-aeeb-4223-ae42-57dbbeb66dfb","l":"Hierarchical equations of motion","d":1,"p":"233a6cd0-b7fe-491b-b5fd-85fd6b2a7c79","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Hierarchical equations of motion (HEOM) are a numerically exact, nonperturbative framework for open quantum systems that represent the reduced system dynamics by an infinite hierarchy of coupled equations for the reduced density operator and a set of auxiliary density operators encoding system\u2013bath correlations. Truncating the hierarchy provides a systematically convergent approximation that accurately captures strong system\u2013bath coupling and non\u2011Markovian memory effects for Gaussian environments."},{"id":"ee16507b-1c24-48b2-a7a0-b5f4e920a097","l":"Hierarchy problem","d":1,"p":"ee487445-39e4-4995-9543-fea1735bcbb2","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"The hierarchy problem is the question of why the electroweak scale (set by the Higgs mass, ~10^2 GeV) is enormously smaller than the Planck or grand-unification scales (~10^19\u201310^16 GeV). In the Standard Model quantum radiative corrections to the Higgs mass are quadratically sensitive to high-energy physics, so keeping the Higgs light appears to require extreme fine-tuning unless new dynamics (e.g., supersymmetry, compositeness, or other mechanisms) stabilizes the scale."},{"id":"7f9be16c-1f39-44fe-82ad-836b8f4a4cb1","l":"High dimensional systems","d":1,"p":"c485f44d-2cc4-4e6d-a20b-0899fb9e53a1","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Nonlinear Dynamics"],"def":"High-dimensional systems are physical systems whose state, phase, or configuration space involves a large number of degrees of freedom (many variables or dimensions, typically N \u226b 1). Their collective behavior, geometry, and statistical properties can differ qualitatively from low-dimensional cases, so they are usually analyzed with statistical, mean-field, or dimensional-reduction methods."},{"id":"226db421-ad24-4e1c-9fbf-5de508711fa2","l":"High-energy accelerators & colliders","d":1,"p":"d0bb99e3-9d07-40c6-80a0-d8858b63da18","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"High-energy accelerators and colliders are machines that use electromagnetic fields to accelerate charged particles (electrons, protons, heavy ions) to relativistic energies and then either collide counter\u2011propagating beams (colliders) or direct beams onto fixed targets. These high-energy interactions concentrate large center\u2011of\u2011mass energy into tiny spacetime regions, producing new particles and enabling precision studies of fundamental forces, particle properties, and a range of applied sciences."},{"id":"040d9a12-e746-4f8e-9ac9-f50f445a29b2","l":"Higher-dimensional field theories","d":1,"p":"ee82ad04-6c47-46a4-a70d-c9a7d7989f6a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Higher-dimensional field theories are classical or quantum field theories formulated on spacetimes that include more than the usual three spatial and one time dimension, so fields depend on additional spatial (or temporal) coordinates. When those extra dimensions are compactified or hidden, the theory reduces to an effective lower-dimensional description with towers of Kaluza\u2013Klein modes and altered interactions, and is used in unification and beyond\u2013Standard-Model frameworks such as Kaluza\u2013Klein, string, and braneworld models."},{"id":"02e50a8d-9a83-451f-a05a-ec435f0af9c2","l":"High-frequency & RF discharges in plasmas","d":1,"p":"b30d0b9e-de6d-4de6-bfad-565f513573b7","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"High-frequency and RF discharges are plasmas sustained by oscillating electric and/or magnetic fields at radio-to-microwave frequencies (kHz\u2013GHz), in which energy is coupled to electrons via capacitive (E\u2011field), inductive (H\u2011field) or wave-absorption mechanisms. These driven, typically non\u2011equilibrium plasmas exhibit sheath rectification, skin\u2011depth effects and frequency\u2011dependent power deposition that control electron heating, plasma density and uniformity for applications such as materials processing and lighting."},{"id":"99517ef4-1e39-4a40-b393-843b91429df3","l":"High Hartmann number flows","d":1,"p":"c25108e7-4697-4565-b484-221b808d454e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Plasma Physics"],"def":"High Hartmann number flows are magnetohydrodynamic (MHD) flows in electrically conducting fluids under a strong imposed magnetic field, quantified by Ha = B L sqrt(\u03c3/(\u03c1 \u03bd)) \u226b 1 (B = field, L = length scale, \u03c3 = conductivity, \u03c1 = density, \u03bd = kinematic viscosity). In this regime Lorentz forces dominate viscous forces, producing thin Hartmann boundary layers of thickness ~L/Ha at walls normal to the field and a quasi-two-dimensional, anisotropic core flow with suppressed turbulence."},{"id":"3934db16-fe69-4d69-82c8-9bc346490273","l":"High intensity beam dynamics","d":1,"p":"cd322dee-17c3-4f48-81b4-5fbca9f2c432","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"High-intensity beam dynamics studies the motion and stability of charged-particle beams in accelerators when the beam\u2019s own electromagnetic self-fields and interactions with the surrounding structures are comparable to the applied focusing forces. It focuses on collective effects\u2014space charge, wakefields, resonances and instabilities\u2014that drive emittance growth, halo formation and beam loss, and on modeling and mitigation strategies."},{"id":"36db86d0-3e82-4100-b91a-f02485f325ba","l":"High magnetic fields","d":1,"p":"b35238d0-83c7-4412-b293-c7d42fa92c62","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"High magnetic fields are magnetic fields whose magnitude is large enough to dominate the relevant energy scales or dynamics of particles and materials, producing strong effects such as Landau quantization, large Zeeman splitting, magnetic pressure, and non\u2011linear magnetization. What counts as \"high\" depends on context: in condensed-matter physics a few tesla can be significant, specialized labs reach 10^2\u201310^3 T in pulsed experiments, while astrophysical objects (e.g., magnetars) exhibit fields of 10^8\u201310^11 tesla."},{"id":"4e3734fe-a81e-4bf0-9915-128158382566","l":"High-pressure, high-current plasma production","d":1,"p":"3741a35f-83a8-4e50-b7a2-38e600034925","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"High-pressure, high-current plasma production is the generation of a dense, strongly ionized gas by driving very large electrical currents through a gas at or above atmospheric pressure, typically in short-duration arcs or pulsed discharges. Such plasmas are highly collisional and rapidly heated, producing strong magnetic forces, intense radiation, electrode erosion and shock formation, and are used in pulsed-power, plasma processing and certain fusion or high-energy-density experiments."},{"id":"0657186e-71bf-48f1-906b-fa75e792afac","l":"High-speed flow","d":1,"p":"a4ca1a7e-639b-4ff5-8cf3-44b60866fb3e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"High-speed flow refers to fluid motion in which the flow velocity is large enough that compressibility and associated compressible-gas phenomena become important (rule of thumb: Mach number M \u2273 0.3), encompassing transonic, supersonic (M>1) and hypersonic (M\u22735) regimes. Such flows exhibit shocks and expansion waves, strong temperature and density changes, and\u2014at very high Mach numbers\u2014high-enthalpy and non\u2011equilibrium effects that require compressible fluid dynamics for accurate description."},{"id":"41832898-7313-44fe-a1fe-258cbf4a5ec9","l":"High spin states","d":1,"p":"1cf75de2-e14b-41cd-b733-8f704652db33","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"High-spin states are quantum states of atoms, molecules, nuclei, or other systems whose total angular momentum (spin) is much larger than that of the ground state. They are typically produced by aligning individual particle angular momenta or by rotational excitation and therefore show characteristic high rotational energy, collective behavior (rotational bands, deformation), and often shortened lifetimes."},{"id":"bf9744b9-e389-44c0-ac97-fddd7f3a42ab","l":"High-temperature superconductors","d":1,"p":"2250b5f3-dfa1-4ca7-bedf-fff5d41bd6ea","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"High-temperature superconductors are materials that exhibit superconductivity\u2014zero electrical resistance and full magnetic flux expulsion\u2014at critical temperatures much higher than those of conventional, phonon-mediated superconductors, often above about 30 K and in many famous cases above liquid\u2011nitrogen temperature (~77 K). They include families such as cuprates and iron\u2011based compounds and generally involve unconventional pairing mechanisms not fully captured by traditional BCS theory."},{"id":"d7a8b8f5-832c-42c1-99d9-3810e4bf4a99","l":"Holon","d":1,"p":"d46f32eb-17b3-4d09-be59-fc3461e542db","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A holon is an emergent quasiparticle in strongly correlated electron systems that carries the electron's charge but no spin, produced by spin\u2013charge separation (for example in one-dimensional Luttinger liquids or slave-particle descriptions of Hubbard/t\u2013J models). Holons may behave as bosons or fermions depending on the representation and are typically paired conceptually with spinons (spin-carrying, charge-neutral excitations)."},{"id":"ad1a55c6-b679-4930-af7d-7ab924199b11","l":"Homogeneous turbulence","d":1,"p":"062f0300-c33d-4782-b24c-43996b0469f3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Homogeneous turbulence is a turbulent flow whose statistical properties are invariant under spatial translations, so ensemble averages and correlation functions depend only on separation vectors, not on absolute position. It is an idealized condition (often used in theory and periodic-box simulations) and is distinct from inhomogeneous turbulence produced by boundaries or mean gradients."},{"id":"ff0242d5-cf24-47c4-a2ff-e4730bfaa1f5","l":"Hopping transport","d":1,"p":"36754399-ec2b-4962-bb07-ca12c79834e8","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Hopping transport is a conduction mechanism in disordered or strongly localized materials where charge carriers (or excitations/ions) move by thermally activated, often phonon-assisted, quantum jumps between localized sites instead of by extended band states. It produces characteristic temperature-dependent conductivities, e.g. Arrhenius behavior for nearest-neighbor hopping or stretched-exponential (Mott) variable-range hopping."},{"id":"08994d1d-c41e-4a9f-94a2-4823dae72292","l":"Hot dense plasma","d":1,"p":"22580d6a-8d20-4653-b2d1-a7de314bc162","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"A hot dense plasma is a state of ionized matter in which both the temperature and particle density are high enough that atoms are largely ionized and charged particles interact frequently and strongly. In this regime collective electromagnetic fields, collisional (non\u2011ideal) effects, radiation transport and often quantum or degeneracy physics become important, as in stellar interiors and high\u2011energy\u2011density fusion experiments."},{"id":"ee920d8d-1ab6-4dbf-9886-21939f02ff2b","l":"Hot-wire anemometry","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Hot-wire anemometry is a fluid-velocity measurement technique that infers local instantaneous speed from the convective cooling of a thin electrically heated wire: flow-dependent heat loss changes the wire's temperature and therefore its electrical resistance or current, which is calibrated to velocity. It is commonly implemented in constant-temperature or constant-current modes and provides high temporal resolution for turbulence and unsteady-flow measurements."},{"id":"f08e5121-17ea-4f77-8e1e-ac3a3b3304b3","l":"Hybrid model","d":1,"p":"233a6cd0-b7fe-491b-b5fd-85fd6b2a7c79","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"A hybrid model is a theoretical or computational framework that combines two or more distinct descriptions (for example microscopic and macroscopic, quantum and classical, or kinetic and fluid) so each component handles the regime where it is most accurate. The components are coupled through interface conditions or shared variables to produce a unified representation that captures phenomena across scales or regimes more efficiently or accurately than a single approach."},{"id":"9d5d8dc3-42bd-4f98-8535-3de25a94f98a","l":"Hydrodynamic models","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Hydrodynamic models are continuum descriptions of many-particle systems that evolve macroscopic fields (density, velocity, pressure/energy) according to conservation laws and constitutive relations (e.g., Euler or Navier\u2013Stokes equations). They arise by coarse-graining microscopic dynamics and incorporate dissipative effects such as viscosity and thermal conductivity, and can be generalized to relativistic, quantum, or multi-component fluids."},{"id":"db9edff6-ca20-4c19-84ae-e68c27a8a88d","l":"Hydrodynamic noise","d":1,"p":"40a5bd01-6544-4502-8321-458c33878bf3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Hydrodynamic noise are random fluctuations of macroscopic fluid fields (velocity, pressure, density, etc.) that arise from microscopic thermal motion and molecular discreteness. In continuum theory they are modeled by stochastic stress and flux terms (Landau\u2013Lifshitz fluctuating hydrodynamics), producing effects such as long-time tails, enhanced diffusion, and observable fluctuations in small or near-critical systems."},{"id":"5512c79b-f40a-44ca-ae16-8ceef3dda771","l":"Hydrodynamics","d":1,"p":"233a6cd0-b7fe-491b-b5fd-85fd6b2a7c79","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Hydrodynamics is the branch of fluid mechanics that studies the macroscopic motion of fluids (liquids and gases) and the forces and stresses they experience. It describes flows using continuum conservation laws\u2014mass, momentum and energy\u2014leading to governing equations such as the Navier\u2013Stokes equations and covers phenomena from laminar flow and waves to turbulence."},{"id":"42faf1b1-780c-431c-9a74-5d330820f7f7","l":"Hydrodynamic waves","d":1,"p":"0a677b11-f06d-4ced-9385-cf0bf1061a59","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Hydrodynamic waves are propagating disturbances in a fluid or at a fluid interface governed by the equations of fluid dynamics, arising from the balance of inertia and restoring forces such as pressure, gravity, or surface tension. Examples include surface gravity waves, internal waves, shallow-water waves and nonlinear/dispersive waveforms, all of which transport energy and momentum through the fluid."},{"id":"4c12fcde-ed75-4e1c-bd19-253623703054","l":"Hydroelectricity","d":1,"p":"af9f5a05-6917-40cf-a2ff-5520ff506935","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Hydroelectricity is electrical power generated by converting the gravitational potential and kinetic energy of flowing or falling water into mechanical rotation in turbines, which drive generators that produce electricity by electromagnetic induction. It typically uses rivers, dams or pumped-storage reservoirs to control water flow and provide a renewable, dispatchable source of grid-scale power."},{"id":"1c31e6d2-dd5b-4e16-995a-a4a7f659f9c5","l":"Hydrogels","d":1,"p":"06891d57-1044-487c-ae20-21366d161a97","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Hydrogels are three-dimensional, crosslinked polymer networks that absorb and retain large amounts of water, forming soft, porous materials with solid-like elasticity and fluid-like transport. Their swelling, mechanical, and transport behavior is governed by a balance of osmotic pressure, polymer elasticity, and network structure, often giving rise to viscoelasticity and stimuli-responsive changes."},{"id":"1ed07d2a-b99b-4e10-91d9-31d61dc0c4ad","l":"Hydrogenated materials","d":1,"p":"81194f70-bbca-4041-b57e-29338e588cac","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Hydrogenated materials are solids in which hydrogen atoms are incorporated\u2014chemically bonded or occupying interstitial sites\u2014so that hydrogenation (formation of hydrides or surface/passivation bonds) alters the material's electronic, optical, chemical, and mechanical properties (e.g., a\u2011Si:H, metal hydrides, graphane)."},{"id":"cffba4e3-189f-447a-a221-8460f2206047","l":"Hydrogen bonds","d":1,"p":"cc59c6fb-acbb-4237-953b-b874fe5dbd4a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Hydrogen bonds are directional, relatively strong noncovalent interactions in which a hydrogen atom covalently bound to an electronegative donor atom (commonly O, N, or F) is attracted to a lone-pair-bearing electronegative acceptor atom. They have mixed electrostatic and partial covalent character, are stronger than typical van der Waals/dipole forces but weaker than covalent bonds, and critically influence molecular structure and properties."},{"id":"7d448998-51f2-4304-867e-db5833197487","l":"Hydrogen energy","d":1,"p":"af9f5a05-6917-40cf-a2ff-5520ff506935","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Hydrogen energy refers to using hydrogen as an energy carrier: hydrogen stores chemical energy that can be released as heat or converted to electricity (e.g., by combustion or in fuel cells) and can be stored and transported for later use. When produced by low\u2011carbon methods (electrolysis using renewable or low\u2011carbon electricity, or reforming with CCS) it provides a route for decarbonized energy storage, transport, and industrial feedstock."},{"id":"cd19be01-0612-4cfe-a3e4-a80d4baa0db0","l":"Hydrophilic interactions","d":1,"p":"cc59c6fb-acbb-4237-953b-b874fe5dbd4a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Hydrophilic interactions are the favorable attractions between water molecules and polar or charged molecular groups (e.g., OH, NH, carbonyls, ions) that promote solvation and formation of hydration shells. They arise mainly from hydrogen bonding, electrostatic and dipole\u2013dipole forces and are typically enthalpically favorable, contrasting with entropy-driven hydrophobic effects."},{"id":"164db802-de53-43d0-86bd-d8eb3c7d4ae1","l":"Hydrophobic interactions","d":1,"p":"cc59c6fb-acbb-4237-953b-b874fe5dbd4a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Hydrophobic interactions are solvent-mediated, effective attractions between nonpolar molecules or surfaces in water that drive their aggregation. They arise mainly from the increase in water entropy (and corresponding free-energy decrease) when ordered hydration layers around hydrophobic groups are reduced, and underlie processes like protein folding and micelle formation."},{"id":"482bc2f7-a957-4f5b-b9da-255c67b43e8f","l":"Hyperbolic lattice","d":1,"p":"7a86b9d8-3162-4279-9ca5-90ebad07be7c","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A hyperbolic lattice is a discrete graph or regular tessellation of the hyperbolic plane (or higher\u2011dimensional hyperbolic space) by congruent polygons or cells with uniform vertex coordination, embedded in a space of constant negative curvature. Unlike Euclidean lattices (Schl\u00e4fli symbol {p,q} with (p\u22122)(q\u22122)=4), hyperbolic lattices satisfy (p\u22122)(q\u22122)>4 and exhibit exponential growth of the number of sites with distance."},{"id":"0845f541-b7a3-402b-b779-736d06a64443","l":"Hypernuclei & mesic nuclei","d":1,"p":"1cf75de2-e14b-41cd-b733-8f704652db33","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Hypernuclei are atomic nuclei in which one or more nucleons are replaced by hyperons\u2014baryons containing strange quarks (e.g., \u039b, \u03a3, \u039e)\u2014that are bound to the remaining nucleons by the strong force and thus allow study of hyperon\u2013nucleon and hyperon\u2013hyperon interactions and strangeness in nuclear matter.  \nMesic nuclei are nuclei that host a bound or quasi\u2011bound meson (e.g., \u03c0, K, \u03b7), held by the attractive strong interaction (with Coulomb binding relevant for charged mesons), providing probes of in\u2011medium meson properties and meson\u2013nucleon forces."},{"id":"187af2f2-b6a9-40be-83d2-546148dec617","l":"Hyperspherical method","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"The hyperspherical method is a technique for solving few- and many-body problems by rewriting particle coordinates in hyperspherical coordinates\u2014one collective length (the hyperradius) plus a set of angular hyperangles\u2014and expanding the system's wavefunction in hyperspherical harmonics. This transforms the problem into coupled ordinary differential (radial) equations in the hyperradius, simplifying treatment of correlations, bound states and scattering phenomena."},{"id":"07f621ab-cce4-47c5-80e7-9562850e47e0","l":"Hypothetical fermions","d":1,"p":"3981a374-dbc8-42de-bdba-73ea858650c6","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Hypothetical fermions are proposed particles or quasiparticles with half-integer spin that obey Fermi\u2013Dirac statistics and the Pauli exclusion principle but have not yet been experimentally confirmed. They appear in theories beyond the Standard Model (e.g., sterile neutrinos, neutralinos, gravitinos) or as emergent excitations in condensed matter (e.g., Majorana modes) and are invoked to explain phenomena such as dark matter, neutrino masses, or unconventional superconductivity."},{"id":"5ff69d0e-ec0b-4cec-a68b-df16f595eb9e","l":"Hypothetical gauge bosons","d":1,"p":"3981a374-dbc8-42de-bdba-73ea858650c6","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Hypothetical gauge bosons are proposed force-carrying particles associated with additional gauge symmetries beyond those of the Standard Model. They would mediate new interactions (for example a Z' or dark photon), characterized by their quantum charges, masses and coupling strengths, and are the subject of collider and precision searches."},{"id":"bc72354b-fe26-4424-b723-bd6dd9e33e3c","l":"Hypothetical scalars","d":1,"p":"3981a374-dbc8-42de-bdba-73ea858650c6","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Hypothetical scalars are proposed spin-0 particles described by scalar fields that are invariant under rotations and Lorentz transformations, carrying no intrinsic directionality. They arise in many extensions of the Standard Model (e.g., extra Higgs-like bosons, inflatons, axion-like particles or moduli) and are invoked to explain phenomena such as electroweak symmetry breaking, inflation, or dark matter."},{"id":"e42c3b84-6ccd-46ed-a6a6-56e8b3ee653f","l":"Imaging & diagnostics","d":1,"p":"b2c47d4b-a2d2-4f91-aa08-6029cd34a4a7","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Imaging & diagnostics are the instruments, measurement techniques, and analysis methods used to produce spatially resolved images and quantitative measurements of physical systems, often noninvasively, to reveal internal structure, composition, and dynamics. They combine probes (light, X\u2011rays, electrons, particles, acoustic waves, etc.), detectors, and reconstruction/signal\u2011processing algorithms to translate measured signals into interpretable images or diagnostic parameters."},{"id":"cebeca7e-1bae-4fd3-8619-1791162a21ab","l":"Imbibition & injection","d":1,"p":"cc73b723-a501-45c2-a121-f8c9757c5573","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Imbibition is the spontaneous uptake of a wetting fluid into a porous or porous-like medium driven by capillary forces, causing the wetting phase to displace a non\u2011wetting phase and increase its saturation. Injection is the externally driven introduction of a fluid (by pressure or pumping) into a system or porous medium, producing forced displacement and pressure\u2011driven flow that can compete with capillary and viscous effects."},{"id":"7cd48089-be71-4809-9288-ffdf82e55a20","l":"Immersed boundary methods","d":1,"p":"1e0c099a-9f2a-4e47-b3f1-7faa28b6a506","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Immersed boundary methods are numerical techniques for simulating fluid\u2013structure interaction by representing flexible or moving boundaries with Lagrangian markers embedded in a fixed Eulerian fluid grid; boundary forces are spread to the fluid and velocities are interpolated back to the markers using smoothed delta kernels. Originally developed by Peskin for cardiac valves, they let one handle complex, deforming geometries without body-fitted meshes."},{"id":"14f68069-7de8-4b48-a6d4-d9a35b713f66","l":"Immune system","d":1,"p":"0a74688b-2eee-4349-a9bd-e93851fe2c5a","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"The immune system is the integrated network of cells, tissues, and soluble molecules that detects, neutralizes, and removes pathogens and damaged or malignant host cells, operating through innate (rapid, nonspecific) and adaptive (slower, specific, memory-forming) responses. It functions as a dynamic information\u2011processing and regulatory system that discriminates self from nonself to maintain organismal integrity."},{"id":"58475ff8-10a7-4f39-b4ad-9ded34881784","l":"Impact craters","d":1,"p":"e8836b5d-a348-41e4-9d9e-30e950115672","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Impact craters are bowl-shaped depressions produced when a solid projectile (meteorite, asteroid, or comet) strikes a planetary surface at hypervelocity, generating shock waves that excavate, melt, and eject target material. Their size and morphology\u2014ranging from simple bowls to complex craters with raised rims, ejecta blankets and central peaks\u2014depend on impact energy, target properties, gravity, and atmospheric effects."},{"id":"758a338d-fb5d-440d-83e4-3a6ba7f80e7f","l":"Impedance","d":1,"p":"41dd58dd-d35c-46e8-9594-d7e05b458da6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Impedance is the complex, frequency-dependent generalization of electrical resistance that relates phasor voltage to phasor current in linear AC circuits: Z = V/I. Its real part (resistance) dissipates energy, its imaginary part (reactance) stores and returns energy, and the magnitude (in ohms) and phase of Z give the amplitude ratio and phase shift between voltage and current."},{"id":"81da9c66-6ca6-4c98-a0e7-3ea080e407e6","l":"Implosion symmetry","d":1,"p":"888306e1-6391-43a8-a318-3434270aafd5","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Implosion symmetry is the degree to which an imploding system (material, shell, or shock front) converges uniformly in space and time toward its geometric center, preserving the intended shape (e.g., spherical). High symmetry maximizes peak compression and temperature, while asymmetries produce distortions, jets, and mixing that degrade performance (e.g., in inertial confinement fusion)."},{"id":"a0b28422-f2e6-47e1-aaa2-64743b1a4885","l":"Impurities in superconductors","d":1,"p":"ca8ddaad-441c-481c-93b3-65f46125fb93","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Impurities in superconductors are foreign atoms, vacancies or structural defects that scatter electrons and locally alter the pairing potential, thereby changing transport, spectral and thermodynamic superconducting properties. Nonmagnetic impurities mainly shorten the electronic mean free path and (by Anderson's theorem) do not strongly suppress Tc in conventional s-wave superconductors, whereas magnetic or strong-scattering impurities break Cooper pairs and reduce Tc (and in unconventional superconductors any impurity can strongly suppress pairing); impurities can also serve as vortex-pinning centers that raise the critical current."},{"id":"ccb1456a-b59d-40bc-bad3-7690b7895fd1","l":"Indirect drive","d":1,"p":"888306e1-6391-43a8-a318-3434270aafd5","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Indirect drive is an inertial-confinement fusion approach in which driver beams (typically lasers) heat the inside of a high\u2011Z cavity (a hohlraum) so that the cavity re-emits the energy as an X\u2011ray bath; those X\u2011rays then symmetrically ablate the outer layer of a fuel capsule to drive its implosion."},{"id":"ef098eda-722a-4e25-8996-aa19116b725a","l":"Inelastic scattering reactions","d":1,"p":"bbb0ce0d-36da-4da5-a443-df064f36aa17","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Inelastic scattering reactions are collisions in which kinetic energy is not conserved because part of the initial kinetic energy is converted into internal excitations, ionization, or the creation of new particles; total energy and momentum are still conserved. Unlike elastic scattering, inelastic processes change the internal states or particle content of the colliding system."},{"id":"e558a15a-1b9e-4f48-ae30-625c83ce421f","l":"Inertial confinement fusion","d":1,"p":"888306e1-6391-43a8-a318-3434270aafd5","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Inertial confinement fusion is a method of achieving thermonuclear fusion by rapidly compressing and heating a small fuel capsule (typically deuterium\u2013tritium) with an intense, short energy pulse\u2014usually lasers, particle beams, or X-rays\u2014so the fuel reaches the extreme temperature and density needed for fusion. The fuel is held together only by its own inertia for a very short time (\u223cnanoseconds) so fusion reactions occur before the capsule disassembles."},{"id":"fd233cf5-7ec1-4c82-90c6-2bad6ab2f982","l":"Inertially confined plasmas","d":1,"p":"1d80a56f-19ce-4fd1-b088-20fed49f32c4","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Inertially confined plasmas are dense, hot plasmas held together transiently by the inertia of surrounding mass after a rapid implosion (typically driven by lasers or particle beams), so that they remain compressed long enough\u2014on the order of nanoseconds, set by the hydrodynamic disassembly time\u2014for thermonuclear reactions to occur rather than by magnetic or steady-state equilibrium."},{"id":"c31987f6-4337-45cb-9265-1fe19f826be7","l":"Inflation","d":1,"p":"26ed7afc-8635-4637-a339-8d0a7cb75be1","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Inflation is a brief period in the very early universe during which space underwent extremely rapid (approximately exponential) expansion driven by the vacuum-like potential energy of a scalar \"inflaton\" field. This rapid stretching converted microscopic quantum fluctuations into macroscopic density perturbations and explains the observed large-scale homogeneity, near-flatness, and origin of structure in the universe."},{"id":"af5106b0-a8c7-47c8-94f7-419ac16b076b","l":"Information scrambling","d":1,"p":"1addd209-da18-41de-9a48-46f5f0f11651","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Information scrambling is the unitary process in a quantum many-body system by which information initially localized in a few degrees of freedom becomes rapidly delocalized over the entire system, so that it cannot be accessed by local measurements despite being globally preserved. It is often diagnosed by the growth of out-of-time-order correlators and defines a scrambling time (with black holes conjectured to be among the fastest scramblers)."},{"id":"0cb3aade-0b32-4cc8-ab97-361d839ce3f3","l":"Information theory","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Information theory is the mathematical framework for quantifying, encoding, transmitting, and decoding information, using measures such as Shannon entropy to quantify uncertainty and mutual information to quantify statistical dependence. It establishes fundamental limits (e.g., channel capacity and coding theorems) on reliable communication and connects closely to statistical mechanics via formal analogies between information and thermodynamic entropy."},{"id":"8156d7cb-6ab9-4097-be2b-4aa71aad93a3","l":"Infrared techniques","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Infrared techniques are experimental and analytical methods that use infrared radiation\u2014wavelengths longer than visible light but shorter than microwaves\u2014to probe, image, or manipulate matter. They include spectroscopic (absorption, emission, reflectance, Fourier\u2011transform IR), imaging (thermal cameras, IR microscopy), and interferometric approaches used to identify chemical bonds, measure vibrational and electronic properties, map temperature, and perform remote sensing in materials science, chemistry, and astronomy."},{"id":"c26b8972-c54f-4039-a987-ee7fe4645fff","l":"Infrastructures & instrumentation","d":1,"p":"4c286df9-b565-4295-b951-49a36d60cad1","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Infrastructures & instrumentation refers to the physical facilities, specialized instruments, and supporting systems that enable experimental and observational physics\u2014including laboratories, telescopes, accelerators, detectors, cryogenics, and computing resources. It also encompasses the design, fabrication, calibration, operation and maintenance of those tools and the data-acquisition, storage and metrology systems that make precise measurements and analyses possible."},{"id":"aa3bdd09-6576-4fe1-9664-44f151015951","l":"Inorganic chemistry","d":1,"p":"4455b073-c3a2-4a0b-923e-98d7ec0f3b9d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Inorganic chemistry is the branch of chemistry that studies the synthesis, structure, properties, and reactivity of inorganic compounds\u2014those not primarily based on C\u2013H frameworks\u2014such as metals, minerals, coordination complexes, and solid-state materials. It overlaps with condensed-matter physics and materials science in its focus on electronic structure, bonding, crystal lattices, and resulting magnetic, optical, and catalytic properties."},{"id":"7be50bbd-fa68-4e84-986f-bdad7ef2c678","l":"Insects","d":1,"p":"91278658-51b4-4d8e-a936-7cfad1266625","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Insects are six-legged arthropods of the class Insecta, typically with a three-part body (head, thorax, abdomen), compound eyes, and often one or two pairs of wings. In physics they are studied as small-scale mechanical and fluid-dynamic systems \u2014 for example in biomechanics and locomotion, flapping-wing aerodynamics, sensory transduction, and collective behavior."},{"id":"9cf04270-c27c-4351-8840-9d94a7d19eea","l":"Instability control","d":1,"p":"d2fd5563-5077-41b4-9d06-f0da03883073","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Instability control is the set of methods used to prevent, suppress, or limit the growth of perturbations that would drive a physical system away from a desired state or operating regime. Techniques include passive design choices (damping, geometry, material selection) and active interventions (feedback control, targeted forcing, field shaping) tailored to the system's specific instability mechanisms."},{"id":"50adc7d0-14e7-4a61-82ce-46d54feb45a9","l":"Instability of free-surface flows","d":1,"p":"f5372742-fa5e-4bda-96ba-87e45d98bae6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Instability of free-surface flows is the tendency for small disturbances at a fluid interface (liquid\u2013gas) in a flow to grow rather than decay, producing surface waves, fingering, rivulets or breakup into droplets. Such instabilities arise from competing effects of inertia, gravity, viscosity, surface tension and interfacial stresses (e.g., shear, density differences, thermal or surfactant gradients) and are characterized by dispersion relations and critical stability criteria."},{"id":"1b170e0a-a62a-406a-a007-e5218ade416c","l":"Insulators","d":1,"p":"81194f70-bbca-4041-b57e-29338e588cac","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Insulators are materials in which charge carriers are localized so that electrical conductivity is vanishingly small; in band theory this occurs when the valence band is full and there is a large energy gap to the conduction band. In some materials (Mott insulators) strong electron\u2013electron interactions can likewise prevent conduction even when bands are partially filled."},{"id":"6eb87115-a3d1-4110-9b0c-b33a9b1998c2","l":"Integrability in field theory","d":1,"p":"ee82ad04-6c47-46a4-a70d-c9a7d7989f6a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Integrability in field theory means the theory has infinitely many independent conserved quantities (typically in involution), which so strongly constrain the dynamics that it becomes exactly solvable. In practice this yields structures like Lax pairs, factorized scattering without particle production, and exact solutions or S-matrices for both classical and quantum integrable models."},{"id":"fdd36ae4-ff85-4541-958c-c6ea84bb5b45","l":"Integrable systems","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"An integrable system is a dynamical system that possesses as many independent conserved quantities as degrees of freedom, typically in involution, so its equations of motion can be solved exactly (by quadratures) and cast into action\u2013angle variables (Liouville integrability). In the quantum context, an integrable model has an extensive set of commuting conserved operators (often solvable by techniques like the Bethe ansatz), leading to exact spectra and the absence of generic chaotic behavior."},{"id":"4dc047b5-2d27-4b49-b3d1-f8fa2fb4552d","l":"Interatomic & molecular potentials","d":1,"p":"12e1040c-7c2c-478a-b466-c979b57fcaea","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Interatomic and molecular potentials are functions or potential-energy surfaces that give the potential energy of a collection of atoms or molecules as a function of their relative positions, encapsulating chemical bonding, short-range repulsion, and long-range (electrostatic, dispersion) interactions arising from the underlying electronic structure. They determine the forces, equilibrium geometries, vibrational spectra and reaction pathways of systems and are represented by empirical pairwise forms (e.g., Lennard-Jones, Morse) or by ab initio computed potential-energy surfaces."},{"id":"77517a5e-a88f-4e90-b565-3dff8fe797bc","l":"Intercalation","d":1,"p":"15f520a3-5c6c-4d9e-8975-81d399140562","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Intercalation is the reversible insertion of guest atoms, ions, or molecules into the interstitial sites or between the layers/planes of a host solid with little disruption of the host crystal framework. It typically involves charge transfer and lattice expansion and is important for tunable electronic properties and technologies such as battery electrodes and superconductors."},{"id":"628f7190-cb71-4692-8cda-ebc544f9e9fb","l":"Interfaces","d":1,"p":"7a86b9d8-3162-4279-9ca5-90ebad07be7c","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"An interface is the physical boundary between two distinct phases or materials where material properties (density, composition, permittivity, etc.) change abruptly. It governs localized phenomena\u2014reflection/refraction, surface tension, charge accumulation, transport and chemical reactions\u2014and determines the boundary conditions for fields and flows."},{"id":"d4074cef-1ff9-4a93-9cfa-dc4d7e4e111a","l":"Interferometry","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Interferometry is a technique that combines two or more coherent waves so their superposition produces interference fringes, and measurements of fringe shifts yield precise differences in optical path, phase, or frequency. It is widely used to measure tiny displacements, refractive-index changes, surface shapes, and to synthesize large-aperture telescopes and precision metrology."},{"id":"43327d0f-b976-4ae9-9dcb-1a71193b8cb5","l":"Intergalactic medium","d":1,"p":"88a5cd35-609c-4f0a-8994-5a4cbe488296","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics","Plasma Physics"],"def":"The intergalactic medium (IGM) is the diffuse, mostly ionized gas and plasma that fills the space between galaxies, arranged along the cosmic web's filaments, sheets and voids and containing the bulk of the universe's baryonic matter. Its density, temperature and ionization\u2014shaped by cosmic expansion, reionization and feedback from galaxies and quasars\u2014are probed by absorption (e.g., the Lyman-\u03b1 forest) and X-ray/UV emission and play a key role in structure formation and galaxy evolution."},{"id":"61345e7f-0892-4281-8197-34ced4f1268b","l":"Intermittency","d":1,"p":"062f0300-c33d-4782-b24c-43996b0469f3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Intermittency is the tendency of a dynamical system to display irregular, sporadic bursts of intense activity separated by relatively calm intervals, producing strong spatial or temporal inhomogeneity and non-Gaussian statistics. In turbulence it specifically refers to the concentration of dissipation in rare, intense structures that cause deviations from simple scaling laws (multifractal behavior)."},{"id":"f0e2a1b3-ef9a-491f-b610-4506f0cc4020","l":"Interparticle interactions","d":1,"p":"52c158bb-fb1e-4d1b-a117-b6b279c3bc67","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Interparticle interactions are the forces or corresponding potential-energy terms between particles that determine their relative motion, correlations, and collective behavior. They range from fundamental forces (e.g., Coulomb, gravitational, nuclear) to effective model interactions (e.g., Lennard\u2011Jones, hard\u2011sphere, screened Coulomb) used to describe atoms, molecules, and many-body systems."},{"id":"b0bfa6aa-ff2d-4535-97e9-7c296724369b","l":"Interplanetary magnetic field","d":1,"p":"88a5cd35-609c-4f0a-8994-5a4cbe488296","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"The interplanetary magnetic field (IMF) is the Sun\u2019s magnetic field carried outward through the heliosphere by the conducting solar wind, where it is effectively \"frozen into\" the plasma and is wound into a Parker (Archimedean) spiral by solar rotation. This pervasive magnetic field controls the transport of charged particles in interplanetary space and mediates interactions between the solar wind and planetary magnetospheres."},{"id":"66bd6c99-625b-42e0-adc8-ccb04a05da59","l":"Interstellar medium","d":1,"p":"88a5cd35-609c-4f0a-8994-5a4cbe488296","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics","Plasma Physics"],"def":"The interstellar medium (ISM) is the diffuse mixture of gas (primarily hydrogen and helium), dust grains, cosmic rays, and magnetic fields that fills the space between stars within a galaxy. It exists in multiple temperature\u2011density phases\u2014from cold molecular clouds to hot ionized plasma\u2014and mediates star formation, energy transfer, and chemical enrichment."},{"id":"0c684545-f2d3-4d6e-a772-573cfc39d09c","l":"Inverse problems","d":1,"p":"be085c40-d877-4e85-9c63-504357581b86","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Inverse problems seek to infer unknown causes, parameters, or structures of a physical system from observed data\u2014the reverse of a forward (predictive) model. They are often ill-posed (non\u2011unique or unstable to noise), so regularization or prior information is normally required to obtain stable, meaningful solutions."},{"id":"e84f5a9e-6934-4738-a3b2-19c2d7eaab86","l":"Ionic fluids","d":1,"p":"026384c2-5a28-4069-b909-e6872fec566d","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Ionic fluids are liquids whose constituent particles are charged ions rather than neutral molecules, so their structure, dynamics, and transport are dominated by long\u2011range Coulomb interactions and electrostatic screening. Examples include molten salts, room\u2011temperature ionic liquids, and concentrated electrolytes, which show strong ion pairing, collective correlations, and ionic conduction."},{"id":"e0e306f2-7361-431b-9840-ae0903f75320","l":"Ionic transport","d":1,"p":"36754399-ec2b-4962-bb07-ca12c79834e8","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Ionic transport is the motion of charged ions through a medium (liquid, gas, membrane, or solid) driven by electric fields, concentration (chemical\u2011potential) gradients, and/or fluid flow, and is described by drift and diffusion (e.g., the Nernst\u2013Planck equation). It is characterized by ion mobility, conductivity and diffusion coefficients and in solids proceeds by mechanisms such as vacancy or interstitial hopping while in fluids it also couples to convection and electro\u2011osmotic flow."},{"id":"a9c7dc8c-3efe-420e-8539-3021eb28955c","l":"Ion impact & scattering","d":1,"p":"eef7c13b-c67f-4d33-be97-76cee58e700a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Ion impact and scattering describes collisions in which energetic ions interact with atoms, molecules, or surfaces, exchanging momentum and energy and undergoing deflection, charge transfer, excitation, or ionization. The process is characterized by scattering angles and energy-loss distributions (differential cross sections) and can produce effects such as sputtering and secondary electron emission."},{"id":"1f9cf92d-3ba5-4de6-9516-b51579bded3a","l":"Ions","d":1,"p":"bd252d88-a608-4897-9416-daf84dee7f6b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"An ion is an atom or molecule that carries a net electric charge because it has an unequal number of protons and electrons; loss of electrons produces a positive ion (cation) and gain produces a negative ion (anion). Ions occur in plasmas, electrolytes, and ionic solids and are the charge carriers responsible for electrical conduction and many chemical reactions."},{"id":"7979d35a-4fe7-40ae-a391-fb9bb192e57b","l":"Iron-based superconductors","d":1,"p":"2250b5f3-dfa1-4ca7-bedf-fff5d41bd6ea","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Iron-based superconductors are a family of layered, high-temperature superconductors whose conducting planes contain iron coordinated with pnictogen (e.g., As) or chalcogen (e.g., Se, Te) atoms, exhibiting superconducting transition temperatures up to about 55 K and often coexisting or competing with antiferromagnetic order. Their superconductivity is unconventional and multiband, typically described by a sign-changing (s\u00b1) order parameter believed to be mediated by spin fluctuations rather than conventional phonons."},{"id":"b9ef303e-f2a9-4a3b-8b81-23a6799d7823","l":"Irradiation effects","d":1,"p":"eef7c13b-c67f-4d33-be97-76cee58e700a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Irradiation effects are the changes in a material's structure, composition, and properties produced by exposure to energetic radiation (particles or photons). These arise from processes such as atomic displacement, ionization and transmutation and can lead to defects, heating, embrittlement, swelling, altered electrical/optical behavior, or radioactivation."},{"id":"c7ab6671-1a61-46c3-90b7-bb80847079ca","l":"Isomer decays","d":1,"p":"1cf75de2-e14b-41cd-b733-8f704652db33","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Isomer decays are radioactive transitions of nuclear isomers\u2014metastable excited states of a nucleus\u2014to lower-energy states or the ground state, occurring by gamma emission (isomeric transition), internal conversion, or by competing particle decays (\u03b2, proton, neutron) or fission. These decays are often much slower than ordinary excited-state de-excitation because large changes in angular momentum or nuclear configuration (e.g., spin/parity or K-forbiddenness) inhibit the transition."},{"id":"d362f997-0773-44ba-9c32-1275c537dad4","l":"Isotope effect","d":1,"p":"ca8ddaad-441c-481c-93b3-65f46125fb93","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Isotope effect: the change in a material's physical or chemical properties caused by substituting atoms with their isotopes, because different nuclear masses alter nuclear motion (zero-point energies, vibrational and phonon spectra) even though the electronic potential is essentially unchanged. Examples include kinetic isotope effects on reaction rates and the BCS isotope effect on superconducting transition temperatures."},{"id":"39add935-c036-4b4b-8555-5b1ba27062f3","l":"Isotope separation & enrichment","d":1,"p":"8ad76784-af63-4e0f-a57c-e8483afce6c5","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Isotope separation and enrichment are processes that increase the fraction of a specific isotope in a material by exploiting small differences in mass or atomic/molecular behavior to partition isotopes (separation) and produce a product with a higher concentration of the desired isotope (enrichment). Common methods include gas centrifuges, gaseous diffusion, electromagnetic separation, laser isotope separation, and chemical or thermal techniques, used in applications from medicine and research to nuclear fuel production."},{"id":"46a2d535-54ca-45a6-8330-d9adb2e81e6b","l":"Isotropic turbulence","d":1,"p":"062f0300-c33d-4782-b24c-43996b0469f3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Isotropic turbulence is a turbulent flow whose statistical properties are invariant under arbitrary rotations and reflections of the coordinate system, so there is no preferred direction in the statistics. When the statistics are also translationally invariant it is called homogeneous isotropic turbulence; instantaneous velocity fields remain anisotropic, but their ensemble averages or correlation functions depend only on separation magnitude, not orientation."},{"id":"9f8fee62-cbe8-4e7e-b0f5-c47a7cc9f70c","l":"Jahn-Teller effect","d":1,"p":"15f520a3-5c6c-4d9e-8975-81d399140562","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Jahn\u2013Teller effect is the spontaneous geometric distortion of a non\u2011linear molecule or crystal that lifts a degenerate electronic state and lowers the total energy via coupling between electronic degrees of freedom and nuclear (vibrational) motions. It produces characteristic symmetry\u2011breaking bond elongations/compressions (static Jahn\u2013Teller) or rapid tunneling among equivalent distortions (dynamic Jahn\u2013Teller) in ions, molecules, and solids."},{"id":"2f0fa360-7d8e-42e4-b6e1-5b48675950d1","l":"Jamming","d":1,"p":"da8795cf-dd0b-446f-bab1-986dcb2703c6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Physics of Living Systems","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Jamming is the transition in dense, disordered particulate systems (granular media, foams, emulsions, dense colloids) from a fluid-like, flowing state to a mechanically rigid, solid-like state when particle motion is arrested by crowding or applied stress. It is typically controlled by packing fraction, stress or temperature and at the jamming point the system develops a percolating contact network, a finite shear modulus, and a loss of long-range particle mobility."},{"id":"97562943-8ef0-43e6-b83c-a2a7f83706db","l":"Janus materials","d":1,"p":"7a86b9d8-3162-4279-9ca5-90ebad07be7c","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Janus materials are engineered particles or sheets whose two faces have different chemical compositions or physical properties, producing an intrinsic front\u2013back asymmetry. This broken mirror symmetry gives rise to directional or anisotropic behaviors (for example, amphiphilic Janus colloids, catalytic particles, or 2D monolayers with different atoms on opposite sides)."},{"id":"6a14bc62-47b7-4faf-b1c0-3cdbf460f79b","l":"Janus particles","d":1,"p":"ef0a1bee-2a1f-4f47-aca2-6302db18e67f","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Janus particles are micro- or nanoparticles whose surface is divided into two or more regions with different chemical or physical properties, giving them an intrinsic anisotropy. This surface contrast produces directional interactions and responses, enabling controlled self-assembly, patchy bonding, and active behaviors such as self-phoretic propulsion."},{"id":"42ce52af-cb27-42e1-b268-0cb89ed0a09d","l":"Jet quenching","d":1,"p":"7321e0f7-3b12-45a2-aa4a-28c2a7e03149","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Jet quenching is the suppression and modification of high-energy particle jets produced in heavy-ion collisions, caused by the energy loss of fast partons as they traverse the hot, dense quark\u2013gluon plasma. This in-medium energy loss, primarily from medium-induced gluon radiation and elastic collisions, leads to reduced jet energy, softer fragmentation, and imbalanced dijet or high-pT hadron yields."},{"id":"1e0f3a18-3230-45e8-8804-5b9654465bd5","l":"Jets & heavy flavor physics","d":1,"p":"7321e0f7-3b12-45a2-aa4a-28c2a7e03149","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Jets are collimated sprays of hadrons that arise when high-energy quarks or gluons fragment and hadronize, and are used to reconstruct the underlying parton-level dynamics in collider experiments. Heavy-flavor physics studies the production, decay, and interactions of heavy quarks (primarily charm and bottom, and often top), using signatures such as tagged jets and displaced vertices to probe QCD, flavor-changing processes, CP violation, and potential new physics."},{"id":"1aba4855-3b5e-41d4-b42f-99812a5d1e84","l":"Josephson effect","d":1,"p":"ca8ddaad-441c-481c-93b3-65f46125fb93","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Josephson effect is the coherent tunneling of superconducting Cooper pairs across a weak link (e.g., a thin insulating barrier) between two superconductors, producing a nondissipative supercurrent whose magnitude depends on the phase difference of the superconducting order parameters (I = I_c sin\u03c6). If a constant voltage V is applied the phase evolves in time and the current oscillates at the Josephson frequency f = 2eV/h (AC Josephson effect)."},{"id":"f495cfcf-afde-4cee-8daa-3ffffaa0e3a6","l":"Junctions","d":1,"p":"c765ac18-ded5-4ef0-a41a-22be13741559","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Junctions are interfaces between two distinct materials, regions, or phases where physical properties change and which control coupling and transport (of charge, heat, spin, or superconducting phase) across the boundary. Examples include semiconductor p\u2013n junctions that rectify and separate carriers and Josephson junctions that couple superconducting phases and allow coherent tunneling."},{"id":"e983e33c-85f7-465b-9b21-c832849dcf59","l":"Kane-Mele model","d":1,"p":"36754399-ec2b-4962-bb07-ca12c79834e8","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Kane\u2013Mele model is a tight-binding model on the honeycomb lattice (graphene) that includes an intrinsic spin\u2013orbit coupling term to produce a two-dimensional, time-reversal\u2013symmetric Z2 topological insulator. It can be viewed as two Haldane models with opposite chirality for the two spin species and predicts a quantum spin Hall state with protected helical edge states."},{"id":"14c29735-a28a-4020-8ed7-0b04d1061c47","l":"Kelvin, coastal, & edge waves","d":1,"p":"0a677b11-f06d-4ced-9385-cf0bf1061a59","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Kelvin waves are boundary\u2011trapped gravity waves in a rotating fluid where the Coriolis force balances the cross\u2011boundary pressure gradient, causing them to propagate along a coast or the equator with amplitude that decays exponentially away from the boundary and (in the shallow\u2011water long\u2011wave limit) with non\u2011dispersive speed c = \u221a(gH). Coastal and edge waves are the broader class of trapped shoreline or topographic modes \u2014 including coastal Kelvin waves and topographic edge waves \u2014 whose dispersion and modal structure depend on the shoreline geometry and bathymetry."},{"id":"7a703b9c-4515-4961-bd94-eda6e6a05fe5","l":"Kelvin-Helmholtz instability","d":1,"p":"d2fd5563-5077-41b4-9d06-f0da03883073","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Plasma Physics"],"def":"The Kelvin\u2013Helmholtz instability is a shear-driven instability that arises at the interface between two fluid (or plasma) layers moving at different velocities, producing growing wave-like perturbations that roll up into vortices and enhance mixing. It occurs when the destabilizing effect of velocity shear exceeds stabilizing forces such as density stratification, surface tension, or magnetic tension, with growth rates set by the velocity difference and density contrast."},{"id":"6cee676f-f03b-4091-8bd6-a0433baf26ba","l":"Kerr effect","d":1,"p":"4c4f4d87-e837-4d65-970d-f5323698a5f1","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Kerr effect \u2014 any optical phenomenon in which an applied field or the light itself changes a material's optical response. Common usages: the optical Kerr effect is a third\u2011order nonlinearity that makes the refractive index depend on light intensity (n = n0 + n2I), while the magneto\u2011optic Kerr effect is the rotation/ellipticity induced in light reflected from a magnetized surface."},{"id":"38d21c20-f30b-46b6-957d-d852cd4cdf53","l":"Kinetically constrained models","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Kinetically constrained models are simplified lattice or spin/particle models in which the equilibrium energy (or static interactions) is trivial but the allowed dynamics are restricted by local rules: a site may change state only if neighboring sites meet specified conditions. These kinetic constraints produce slow, cooperative relaxation and glassy phenomena (e.g., dynamic heterogeneity and facilitated dynamics) despite simple thermodynamics."},{"id":"b15ea032-1cbc-4fe9-8319-050c5dfe7305","l":"Kondo effect","d":1,"p":"36754399-ec2b-4962-bb07-ca12c79834e8","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Kondo effect is the anomalous increase in electrical resistance of a metal with dilute magnetic impurities as temperature is lowered, arising from enhanced spin-flip scattering of conduction electrons off the impurity. Below a characteristic Kondo temperature T_K, many-body correlations screen the impurity spin to form a Kondo singlet, producing a logarithmic rise of the scattering amplitude and a crossover to a strong-coupling ground state."},{"id":"0f633032-e23f-4056-93ef-230c584acf1c","l":"Kondo insulators","d":1,"p":"29bd219f-a624-4ef5-b4c5-f9049f2be66e","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Kondo insulators are strongly correlated materials in which conduction electrons undergo many-body Kondo screening of a lattice of localized magnetic (typically f\u2011shell) moments, producing a low\u2011temperature hybridization between localized and itinerant states that opens a narrow energy gap at the Fermi level. The result is a small\u2011gap insulating ground state with heavy quasiparticles and unusual temperature-dependent transport (for example SmB6)."},{"id":"db028e42-1084-4ec9-8243-e03ccf74e026","l":"Lagrangian dynamics","d":1,"p":"b43baaca-e8a9-4440-a55f-9f2a3459e510","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nonlinear Dynamics"],"def":"Lagrangian dynamics is a formulation of classical mechanics in which a system's motion is derived from a scalar function, the Lagrangian L = T \u2212 V (kinetic minus potential energy), by requiring the action integral to be stationary. Applying the principle of stationary action yields the Euler\u2013Lagrange equations, which give the equations of motion in generalized coordinates and naturally incorporate constraints and symmetries."},{"id":"a09ae68e-edc5-4a6f-a5a6-a5251baab29c","l":"Lagrangian flow field","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"A Lagrangian flow field is the time-dependent mapping X(a,t) that assigns to each fluid parcel (labeled by its initial position a) its position at time t, thereby specifying the parcel trajectories. Its time derivative dX/dt equals the Eulerian velocity evaluated at the parcel's location, so the flow field encodes the material motion of the fluid."},{"id":"c21e7551-f63c-435d-8997-614152843b63","l":"Lagrangian particle tracking","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Lagrangian particle tracking is the technique of following individual fluid parcels or discrete particles through space and time by recording or integrating their trajectories in the Lagrangian frame (i.e., tracking each particle's position and velocity as it moves). It contrasts with the Eulerian approach (fields at fixed points) and is used in experiments and simulations to study advection, mixing, dispersion, and particle\u2013fluid interactions."},{"id":"71b83667-ebe2-4f20-bedc-691fd7f4117c","l":"Laminar reacting flows","d":1,"p":"5ad87d23-7dd2-4d60-bc90-68055febea41","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Laminar reacting flows are fluid flows in which the velocity field remains smooth and orderly (low-Reynolds-number) while chemical reactions occur, so that reaction kinetics, species diffusion, and heat release are tightly coupled to the hydrodynamics. They include canonical cases such as laminar premixed and nonpremixed flames and are modeled by the Navier\u2013Stokes equations together with species and energy conservation."},{"id":"6d37cf9f-c2cd-4175-94bc-801b32ee1dd7","l":"Landau levels","d":1,"p":"9e019803-f9d8-4802-b1ac-d2c035d9508f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Landau levels are the discrete, quantized energy eigenstates of a charged particle confined to motion perpendicular to a uniform magnetic field, arising from the quantum cyclotron motion; for a free particle in a plane they have energies E_n = \u0127\u03c9_c(n + 1/2) with \u03c9_c = |q|B/m. Each Landau level has a macroscopic degeneracy proportional to the magnetic flux through the sample (degeneracy per unit area = |q|B/h), which underlies phenomena like the quantum Hall effect."},{"id":"e27a6b66-9dd3-4f91-b93c-3d9937233c54","l":"Landau theory","d":1,"p":"f2a9db78-1651-4a94-b66e-3e3d72056760","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Landau theory is a phenomenological mean-field framework for phase transitions that introduces an order parameter and expresses the free energy as a symmetry-allowed power-series expansion in that order parameter; minimizing this \"Landau free energy\" yields criteria for continuous or first-order transitions and gives mean-field critical behavior. It classifies transitions by symmetry and order-parameter coupling but neglects critical fluctuations, so it can fail near lower critical dimensions or close to critical points."},{"id":"c80b3aa7-f3e9-4d73-98bb-d9ae7740549e","l":"Landau-Zener effect","d":1,"p":"9e019803-f9d8-4802-b1ac-d2c035d9508f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Landau\u2013Zener effect describes nonadiabatic transitions that occur when a quantum two\u2011level system is swept through an avoided level crossing: instead of following a single adiabatic eigenstate, the system can jump between states with a finite probability. For a linear sweep the transition probability has the exponential Landau\u2013Zener form P = exp(\u22122\u03c0\u0393), where \u0393 \u221d (coupling^2)/(\u0127 \u00d7 sweep rate) (i.e., the squared matrix element divided by the rate of change of the diabatic energy difference at the crossing)."},{"id":"e2a20295-65f0-4837-814d-2cdf19da3f25","l":"Langmuir-Blodgett films","d":1,"p":"16158b58-5493-4fb9-901c-54f841cc4524","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Langmuir\u2013Blodgett films are ordered molecular monolayers or multilayers produced by transferring amphiphilic molecules from a compressed monolayer at an air\u2013water interface onto a solid substrate using controlled vertical dipping (Langmuir\u2013Blodgett deposition). This layer-by-layer method yields nanometer-thick films with tunable molecular packing and orientation for studies and applications in surface science, optics, and thin-film devices."},{"id":"d6f9e7ea-3f94-4dde-9f6d-28385ddcc249","l":"Language models","d":1,"p":"3f908345-cb08-47c2-bb62-3196134223aa","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Language models are probabilistic models that assign probabilities to sequences of words or tokens and are trained to predict missing or next tokens given context. Modern large language models are typically neural networks (especially transformer architectures) that learn statistical patterns of text to generate, complete, or represent natural language."},{"id":"41b50226-3385-4c74-bd7c-bc19624298ff","l":"Large deviation & rare event statistics","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Large deviation and rare event statistics is the theory that quantifies the probabilities of atypical, exponentially unlikely fluctuations in stochastic systems, by giving their asymptotic decay rates as system size or time grows. It characterizes these probabilities through a rate function or action (via results like Cram\u00e9r\u2019s theorem, Gartner\u2013Ellis, and Freidlin\u2013Wentzell) and identifies the most likely pathways by which rare events occur in statistical mechanics, dynamical systems, and stochastic processes."},{"id":"66a15d90-d02d-4467-9726-35dd0f4d43fa","l":"Large eddy simulations","d":1,"p":"1e0c099a-9f2a-4e47-b3f1-7faa28b6a506","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Large eddy simulation (LES) is a computational technique for turbulent flows that explicitly resolves the large, energy-containing eddies and models the effects of the smaller, dissipative scales via a subgrid-scale model after applying a spatial filter to the Navier\u2013Stokes equations. By resolving the dominant motions and parameterizing only the unresolved scales, LES gives more accurate turbulence predictions than Reynolds-averaged models while being far cheaper than direct numerical simulation at high Reynolds numbers."},{"id":"6cacb9c1-8000-4368-b2e6-b7d540567281","l":"Large-N expansion in field theory","d":1,"p":"a1083ad2-a154-449d-a9ff-59bf46db1c7f","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A systematic approximation method that treats the number of field components or the rank N of an internal symmetry group as a large parameter and reorganizes the theory as a power series in 1/N.  In the N\u2192\u221e limit one typically obtains a solvable saddle-point or mean-field theory while subleading 1/N corrections give controlled quantum or fluctuation effects (e.g., planar-diagram dominance in matrix models)."},{"id":"4bf3e4a6-7faa-4fea-a196-1e2e031a4d3f","l":"Large scale structure of the Universe","d":1,"p":"26ed7afc-8635-4637-a339-8d0a7cb75be1","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"The large-scale structure of the Universe is the network-like spatial distribution of matter on scales from a few megaparsecs to gigaparsecs, comprising galaxies, galaxy clusters, filaments and vast voids (the \"cosmic web\"). It arises from the gravitational growth of primordial density fluctuations in dark matter and baryons and is characterized statistically (e.g., by the power spectrum or correlation functions) and shaped by cosmic expansion and dark energy."},{"id":"0a28ea4d-8308-4866-a594-794292453473","l":"Larynx","d":1,"p":"a32482f9-6a8e-410f-80c0-204520778cbf","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"The larynx is the airway organ in the throat that houses the vocal folds and regulates airflow between the lungs and the mouth or nose. From a physics perspective it converts aerodynamic energy into sound via fluid\u2013structure interaction: airflow drives vibration of the vocal folds, whose tension, mass, and geometry set the fundamental frequency and amplitude of the produced acoustic source."},{"id":"19e76224-3711-45a4-afff-fb4a04e1a075","l":"Laser-cluster interaction","d":1,"p":"ba7ccb8e-d7eb-4e6f-9c1e-b622f5b4e242","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Laser-cluster interaction is the study of how coherent laser fields couple to nanometer-scale aggregates of atoms or molecules, driving collective electronic excitation and rapid ionization. These interactions produce intense heating, Coulomb-driven explosion, energetic electrons and ions, and high-energy radiation (e.g., X-rays, high harmonics), with dynamics set by cluster size, laser intensity, wavelength, and pulse duration."},{"id":"47bd3c10-1a4c-49e7-bc7d-b9b4cff04f32","l":"Laser driven electron acceleration","d":1,"p":"4b4294b5-d03f-4662-9886-cea042ce7d8c","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams","Plasma Physics"],"def":"Laser-driven electron acceleration is the family of techniques that use intense laser pulses interacting with plasma, dielectric structures, or magnetic undulators to produce very large accelerating electric fields that transfer energy to electrons. Examples include laser wakefield acceleration, direct laser acceleration, and inverse free-electron laser, which enable compact, high-gradient accelerators with ultrashort electron bunches."},{"id":"8876ec95-1c25-4a82-bfe6-9cd30de4298a","l":"Laser driven ion acceleration","d":1,"p":"4b4294b5-d03f-4662-9886-cea042ce7d8c","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams","Plasma Physics"],"def":"Laser-driven ion acceleration is the generation of high-energy ion beams by irradiating a target with an ultra-intense laser pulse, which produces hot electrons and strong quasi-static electromagnetic and space-charge fields that transfer momentum to ions. Typical acceleration mechanisms include target-normal sheath acceleration, radiation-pressure acceleration, and Coulomb explosion, enabling compact sources for applications in medicine, imaging, and inertial fusion research."},{"id":"49d8e5d2-8bff-4203-8886-0af2a31d8401","l":"Laser-induced cavitation","d":1,"p":"884ba888-9ed7-43f7-ae3d-360ffe6d2df0","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Laser-induced cavitation is the formation of vapor or gas bubbles in a liquid caused by rapid, localized energy deposition from a laser pulse (via strong heating, vaporization, or optical breakdown/plasma formation). The bubbles then expand and collapse violently on microsecond\u2013millisecond timescales, emitting shock waves and microjets that can produce mechanical effects used in applications from medical surgery to materials processing."},{"id":"2917e3bd-b25c-4e89-8796-5c7e77dfda5a","l":"Laser-plasma interactions","d":1,"p":"04f1f8c5-11c4-434d-9561-f9c615ba5813","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Laser-plasma interactions are the processes by which an intense laser electromagnetic field couples energy to an ionized gas, driving collective plasma responses and single-particle effects such as heating, generation of plasma waves, scattering, particle acceleration, and nonlinear instabilities. They are described by Maxwell\u2019s equations coupled to plasma fluid or kinetic models and underlie applications from inertial confinement fusion to plasma-based accelerators and high-energy-density physics."},{"id":"dfb4980b-4281-4a8f-830b-8822ca3477ba","l":"Lasers","d":1,"p":"23cdcd6b-ae22-4b6d-92cd-24a9a8bd0577","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams","Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"A laser (Light Amplification by Stimulated Emission of Radiation) is a device that generates coherent, monochromatic, and highly directional electromagnetic radiation by stimulated emission from an excited gain medium. Population inversion is created by external pumping and optical feedback from a resonant cavity, producing amplified, narrow-linewidth, high-intensity light."},{"id":"cd4c012e-bacf-4bcf-8358-a6573a330ec4","l":"Laser systems","d":1,"p":"c765ac18-ded5-4ef0-a41a-22be13741559","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Laser systems are engineered devices that generate coherent, highly directional, and often nearly monochromatic electromagnetic radiation by stimulated emission from an excited gain medium, with optical feedback provided by a resonator and energy supplied by a pump. They comprise the gain medium, pump source, cavity (mirrors or waveguide), output coupler and auxiliary optics/electronics for beam shaping, modulation, and power control, and are configured for continuous-wave or pulsed operation depending on application."},{"id":"8465cb2f-4ac4-46d9-ab59-363c9b75133c","l":"Laser wakefield acceleration","d":1,"p":"4b4294b5-d03f-4662-9886-cea042ce7d8c","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams","Plasma Physics"],"def":"Laser wakefield acceleration is a plasma-based technique where an intense, ultrashort laser pulse propagating through a plasma expels electrons via the ponderomotive force, creating a trailing plasma wake with extremely strong longitudinal electric fields (GV/m) that can trap and rapidly accelerate electrons to relativistic energies over millimeter\u2013centimeter distances."},{"id":"bbb4472b-21d7-4e9a-a5e1-b08007b9f820","l":"Lattice-Boltzmann methods","d":1,"p":"1e0c099a-9f2a-4e47-b3f1-7faa28b6a506","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Physics of Living Systems","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Lattice-Boltzmann methods are computational fluid-dynamics techniques that simulate fluids by evolving discrete particle distribution functions on a regular lattice using alternating streaming and local collision steps derived from a discrete Boltzmann equation. In the macroscopic limit they recover the Navier\u2013Stokes equations and are particularly convenient for complex boundaries, multiphase flows, porous media, and micro- or nonequilibrium transport phenomena."},{"id":"a06c5357-8167-4e9e-8fef-1a0304cf08d2","l":"Lattice dynamics","d":1,"p":"15f520a3-5c6c-4d9e-8975-81d399140562","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Lattice dynamics is the study of the collective vibrational motions of atoms in a crystal lattice and their quantized excitations (phonons). It uses the dynamical matrix to obtain normal modes and dispersion relations, which determine a solid's thermal, elastic, and transport properties."},{"id":"81b06dce-2254-4dd1-99ad-cf1e3db77cdf","l":"Lattice field theory","d":1,"p":"ee82ad04-6c47-46a4-a70d-c9a7d7989f6a","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Lattice field theory is the formulation of quantum field theories on a discrete spacetime lattice, providing a nonperturbative ultraviolet regulator that makes path integrals and operator products well-defined. It is used chiefly for numerical (often Euclidean Monte Carlo) studies of strongly coupled systems\u2014e.g., quantum chromodynamics\u2014recovering continuum physics by extrapolating the lattice spacing to zero."},{"id":"a088bad1-4904-4f08-a625-a9840fdf32c7","l":"Lattice models in condensed matter","d":1,"p":"233a6cd0-b7fe-491b-b5fd-85fd6b2a7c79","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Lattice models in condensed matter are simplified theoretical descriptions that place degrees of freedom (spins, electrons, orbitals, etc.) on the sites or bonds of a regular lattice and specify local interactions and hopping between them. They capture essential microscopic physics to study collective phenomena and phase transitions in solids\u2014examples include the Ising, Heisenberg, and Hubbard models."},{"id":"6862afdd-9e07-4061-910a-da1b08618723","l":"Lattices","d":1,"p":"fd7cfd2b-a957-4dcd-b32d-b9d00e6aba33","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"A lattice is a discrete, regular array of points in space generated by all integer linear combinations of a set of primitive translation vectors, which encodes the translational symmetry of a crystal. In physics it provides the framework of sites where atoms, ions, spins or fields reside to build crystal structures and lattice models."},{"id":"8bc71f5b-3de8-4994-8de2-1a300cec36b6","l":"Lattice types","d":1,"p":"7a86b9d8-3162-4279-9ca5-90ebad07be7c","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Lattice types classify periodic arrangements of points or atoms by the geometry of their unit cell and the symmetries of the pattern. In three dimensions this classification yields the seven crystal systems and 14 Bravais lattices (with analogous families in 2D) that describe the distinct translational and point-group symmetries crystals can have."},{"id":"8eecedc1-2c26-4e97-a2c8-9b26c4f41ee2","l":"Layered crystals","d":1,"p":"7a86b9d8-3162-4279-9ca5-90ebad07be7c","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Layered crystals are materials whose atoms are strongly bonded within two-dimensional sheets but are held together between sheets by much weaker interlayer forces (often van der Waals), producing pronounced structural and electronic anisotropy. This stacking allows easy cleavage and exfoliation into single- or few-layer sheets, as in graphite and transition-metal dichalcogenides (e.g., MoS2)."},{"id":"553e95dc-7be7-47a1-96b2-a1dc984c90ff","l":"Learning","d":1,"p":"8b374c75-8448-476e-a872-f15b0cf5a5fe","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Learning is the process by which a system\u2014biological or artificial\u2014acquires, refines, or improves knowledge, skills, or predictive models from experience or data by updating internal representations or parameters. In physics contexts it is formalized by statistical and algorithmic frameworks (e.g., Bayesian inference, optimization, and machine learning) and used for tasks such as pattern recognition, model discovery, data-driven modeling, and control."},{"id":"02260c5f-466c-46d0-a687-826d188b22a4","l":"Learning & adaptation without a brain","d":1,"p":"6f618be6-815c-40bf-bd8a-cb8ffc96bfd5","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"The capacity of non-neural physical, chemical, or biological systems to store information about past stimuli via changes in their internal state or structure and thereby alter future responses\u2014i.e., memory, habituation, or learning-like behavior without a central nervous system. Mechanisms include hysteretic or plastic dynamics in materials (memristors, phase-change media), self-organized reaction\u2013diffusion or chemomechanical processes, and structural/physiological reconfiguration in organisms such as slime molds and plants."},{"id":"0accc7a1-8d4a-49d6-9069-4aafaabf158f","l":"Leaves","d":1,"p":"a32482f9-6a8e-410f-80c0-204520778cbf","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Leaves are the typically thin, flat organs of plants that intercept light for photosynthesis and mediate gas and water exchange with the environment. In physics they are treated as thin, deformable surfaces whose optical, thermal, mechanical and fluid-dynamic interactions (light absorption/scattering, heat conduction/convection, transpiration-driven vapor flux, and bending under loads) illustrate coupled transport and soft-matter phenomena."},{"id":"68c60832-eee5-48ba-9d07-30d258848b6b","l":"Lee-Yang & Fisher zeroes","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Lee\u2013Yang zeros are the zeros of the partition function viewed as a function of a complex external field or fugacity (originally the complex magnetic field), while Fisher zeros are the zeros of the partition function in the complex temperature (or coupling) plane. In the thermodynamic limit the accumulation of these zeros onto the real parameter axis produces nonanalyticities in the free energy, signaling phase transitions."},{"id":"38c8cc9d-4cb4-4528-b4bb-1ffb6ea5b346","l":"Lepton induced nuclear reactions","d":1,"p":"bbb0ce0d-36da-4da5-a443-df064f36aa17","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Lepton-induced nuclear reactions are nuclear processes triggered when a lepton (for example an electron, muon, or neutrino) scatters from or is absorbed by a nucleus, transferring energy and quantum numbers via electromagnetic or weak interactions and thereby exciting, ejecting, or transmuting nucleons. Because leptons do not participate in the strong force, such reactions provide relatively clean probes of nuclear structure and of weak-interaction physics."},{"id":"220c487e-cc69-4cc9-b7cd-4d176626d4ef","l":"Leptoquarks","d":1,"p":"3981a374-dbc8-42de-bdba-73ea858650c6","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Leptoquarks are hypothetical bosons that couple directly to a lepton and a quark, thereby carrying both lepton and baryon number and mediating lepton\u2013quark transitions. They appear in many beyond\u2013Standard-Model theories (e.g., grand unified, composite, or technicolor models), can be scalar or vector with various electric charges, and would give distinctive signals in collider and rare-decay experiments."},{"id":"913be659-e5cb-4203-a3a6-0d6ba9c783b6","l":"Level densities","d":1,"p":"1cf75de2-e14b-41cd-b733-8f704652db33","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Level density is the number of quantum energy levels per unit energy interval at a given excitation energy in a many-body system (most often a nucleus). It can be resolved by quantum numbers such as spin and parity and is a key input for statistical models of reaction rates and thermodynamic properties."},{"id":"4474a071-3610-4651-a0e2-5a399dd970fb","l":"Levitation","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Levitation is the suspension of an object against gravity without mechanical contact, achieved by balancing its weight with other forces so the net force (and often net torque) on the object is zero. Typical mechanisms include magnetic (diamagnetic or superconducting), electrostatic, aerodynamic, acoustic, optical, or quantum effects, and achieving stable levitation generally requires a restoring force or active feedback (Earnshaw\u2019s theorem limits passive static stability for purely inverse-square forces)."},{"id":"ab2988c5-040b-48a5-aa19-5de80e3f7cb4","l":"Light-induced magnetic effects","d":1,"p":"4c4f4d87-e837-4d65-970d-f5323698a5f1","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Light-induced magnetic effects are phenomena in which electromagnetic radiation modifies or generates a material's magnetic properties or net magnetization, either transiently or permanently. They arise via mechanisms such as the inverse Faraday effect, photoexcited carriers and spin\u2013orbit coupling, or ultrafast heating, and can produce optically driven magnetization, spin currents, domain switching, or rapid magnetic dynamics."},{"id":"3123cbf9-460d-467a-9306-b4fb316daddd","l":"Light-matter interaction","d":1,"p":"23cdcd6b-ae22-4b6d-92cd-24a9a8bd0577","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Light\u2013matter interaction is the set of physical processes by which electromagnetic radiation exchanges energy, momentum, or information with material systems, changing the state of the light, the matter, or both. It encompasses absorption, spontaneous and stimulated emission, scattering (elastic and inelastic), reflection and refraction, and nonlinear and quantum effects that excite electronic, vibrational, or collective modes."},{"id":"27e56848-caac-4c58-8c76-d7dbc2651974","l":"Light quarks","d":1,"p":"683890e6-25e0-4d29-9686-308d5800fda7","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Light quarks are the up, down (and often the strange) quark flavors whose current masses are small compared with the QCD scale \u039bQCD, so their dynamics exhibit an approximate chiral symmetry. They dominate the composition and low-energy properties of ordinary hadrons (protons, neutrons, pions, kaons) and are treated as nearly massless in effective theories like chiral perturbation theory."},{"id":"e2327894-cf0b-403f-ab6b-1bcf3a35e546","l":"Lindblad equation","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"The Lindblad equation (also called the Gorini\u2013Kossakowski\u2013Sudarshan\u2013Lindblad or GKSL master equation) is the most general time-homogeneous Markovian master equation that generates a completely positive, trace-preserving quantum dynamical semigroup for a system density operator \u03c1(t). It has the canonical form d\u03c1/dt = \u2212i[H,\u03c1] + \u2211_k (L_k \u03c1 L_k\u2020 \u2212 1/2{L_k\u2020L_k,\u03c1}), where H is an effective Hamiltonian and the Lindblad (jump) operators L_k encode dissipation and decoherence."},{"id":"294d6167-d6db-4d49-87f4-59493bfb4620","l":"Linear acoustics","d":1,"p":"40a5bd01-6544-4502-8321-458c33878bf3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Linear acoustics describes propagation of small-amplitude sound waves in a medium, obtained by linearizing the continuity, momentum (Euler) and constitutive equations so that pressure, density and velocity fluctuations obey a linear wave equation. Because the equations are linear, disturbances superpose and frequency content is preserved, while nonlinear effects (harmonics, shocks, etc.) are neglected."},{"id":"c8af0d4b-24a3-4374-9cc3-b8de5f2dd341","l":"Linear response theory","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Linear response theory describes how a system near equilibrium reacts to a small external perturbation by assuming the change in any observable is, to first order, proportional to the applied perturbing field via a causal response function (or susceptibility) that can depend on time or frequency. It is formalized by Kubo-type response functions and links dissipation to equilibrium fluctuations through the fluctuation\u2013dissipation theorem."},{"id":"94702848-fa1d-45fb-ba9c-9130705746da","l":"Liquid bridges","d":1,"p":"f5372742-fa5e-4bda-96ba-87e45d98bae6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Liquid bridges are volumes of liquid that span and connect two solid surfaces or droplets, held in place by surface tension and wetting (contact) angles. They produce capillary forces and torques that depend on the bridge volume, curvature, and separation, and whose stability determines when the bridge coalesces or ruptures."},{"id":"9b9b580d-d364-4a9d-b72c-8ee0697b5720","l":"Liquid crystal blue phase","d":1,"p":"63f7a3cf-31b7-4d5c-bbcc-58d679dd5139","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"A liquid crystal blue phase is a chiral liquid-crystal state in which double-twist regions organize into a three-dimensional periodic (usually cubic) lattice separated by a network of disclination lines, giving rise to Bragg diffraction of visible light and vivid colors. Blue phases typically appear in a narrow temperature window between the chiral nematic and isotropic phases."},{"id":"61eb5a43-a9da-4836-a15c-e9b6e6b78635","l":"Liquid crystalline polymers","d":1,"p":"fea036a1-9c29-4872-ab2c-d052949f7c03","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Liquid crystalline polymers are macromolecules that incorporate rigid, anisotropic mesogenic units in their backbone or side chains so that their melts or solutions exhibit liquid-crystal phases (e.g., nematic or smectic) with long-range orientational\u2014and sometimes positional\u2014order while maintaining polymeric connectivity. They combine the mechanical and processing characteristics of polymers with the anisotropic optical, electrical and rheological properties of liquid crystals, and are used in high-strength fibers and functional anisotropic materials."},{"id":"93461c37-4b2d-4092-9572-0faf35224cb9","l":"Liquids","d":1,"p":"81194f70-bbca-4041-b57e-29338e588cac","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Liquids are a condensed state of matter with a definite volume but no fixed shape: their molecules are closely packed and held by intermolecular forces yet can move past one another so the substance flows. They are relatively incompressible and exhibit properties like viscosity, surface tension, and only transient resistance to shear (no static shear modulus)."},{"id":"ba8221e8-b186-4db2-abb4-bcb0cc7f5b9d","l":"Living matter & active matter","d":1,"p":"6f618be6-815c-40bf-bd8a-cb8ffc96bfd5","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Living matter: biological materials (cells, tissues, organisms) whose components continuously convert chemical energy to sustain metabolism, structure and function, so they maintain organized, adaptive states far from thermodynamic equilibrium.  \nActive matter: collections of self-driven units (molecular motors, cells, synthetic swimmers, animals) that individually consume energy to generate persistent motion or stresses, producing collective nonequilibrium phenomena like flocking, swarming, and motility\u2011induced phase separation."},{"id":"7d9b7d96-c9e6-455f-9fa7-86088b7df853","l":"Localization","d":1,"p":"36754399-ec2b-4962-bb07-ca12c79834e8","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Localization is the confinement of a wave, particle, or excitation to a limited region of space so that it does not propagate freely; in quantum systems this can arise from disorder (Anderson localization), confinement potentials, or strong interactions and leads to suppressed transport."},{"id":"e7b35736-ee8c-4961-b852-1ac40c2f4144","l":"Long-range interactions","d":1,"p":"30e65d01-6cd5-442b-889f-ddc0c3c28157","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Long-range interactions are forces whose pair potential decays so slowly with distance that contributions from distant particles remain significant even in the thermodynamic limit \u2014 typically characterized by V(r) \u221d 1/r^\u03b1 with \u03b1 \u2264 d in d spatial dimensions. Examples include gravity and Coulomb forces; they produce non-additive thermodynamics and pronounced collective effects (e.g., ensemble inequivalence and slow relaxation)."},{"id":"561ecfd0-965f-4ce8-9569-2fcbd0c828e0","l":"Low-dimensional models","d":1,"p":"15ac2674-419f-4fe1-b73b-99c33fe6b8df","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Low-dimensional models are theoretical descriptions in which a system\u2019s degrees of freedom are confined to one or two spatial dimensions (or to a small set of effective modes), giving rise to physics\u2014enhanced fluctuations, modified phase transitions, and prominent quantum or topological effects\u2014that can differ qualitatively from three-dimensional behavior. They are used both as simplified toy models and as realistic descriptions of quasi-1D/2D systems like chains, films, and nanowires."},{"id":"8bcaeaa2-a127-4368-aecb-0f449b3fdbe4","l":"Low-energy multiple-particle dynamics","d":1,"p":"cd322dee-17c3-4f48-81b4-5fbca9f2c432","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"The study of how systems of two or more interacting particles evolve and scatter when the available kinetic or thermal energies are small compared with the system's intrinsic scales (e.g., excitation gaps or particle rest energies). In this low-energy regime quantum effects \u2014 such as long-wavelength scattering characterized by scattering lengths, bound-state formation, coherence and collective modes, and universal few-body phenomena \u2014 dominate and are described by effective interactions, few-body equations, and many-body approximations."},{"id":"69362314-bc07-4c73-9ac8-6b72d12e5215","l":"Lower-dimensional field theories","d":1,"p":"ee82ad04-6c47-46a4-a70d-c9a7d7989f6a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Lower-dimensional field theories are classical or quantum field theories defined on spacetimes with fewer dimensions (for example 1+1 or 2+1 instead of 3+1). They often show qualitatively different behavior\u2014stronger quantum/infrared fluctuations, enhanced solvability, and prominent topological effects\u2014so they serve as useful simplified models in condensed matter, statistical mechanics, and high-energy theory."},{"id":"a7ef156f-2ab6-450f-860b-31231fe17248","l":"Low-field & Townsend plasma discharges","d":1,"p":"b30d0b9e-de6d-4de6-bfad-565f513573b7","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Low-field plasma discharges occur in gases under relatively small reduced electric fields (E/N) so that electrons gain only modest energy between collisions and transport and ionization are collision-dominated rather than runaway. The Townsend discharge is the prototypical low-current, low-field regime in which small primary electron avalanches grow exponentially with distance according to the first Townsend ionization coefficient \u03b1 and the discharge is sustained only when secondary electron emission from electrodes (coefficient \u03b3) compensates losses; space-charge effects are negligible and no visible glow forms."},{"id":"4eec2798-da70-4fc2-8371-f6aa5f6180d7","l":"Low- & intermediate-energy accelerators","d":1,"p":"d0bb99e3-9d07-40c6-80a0-d8858b63da18","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Low- and intermediate-energy accelerators are machines that accelerate electrons, protons, or ions to kinetic energies from a few kiloelectronvolts up to a few hundred megaelectronvolts (i.e., below the GeV scale) for experiments and applications where fully relativistic beams are not required. Typical technologies include electrostatic generators, low-energy cyclotrons and RF linacs, used for nuclear and atomic physics, materials analysis and modification, medical isotope production, and radiation testing."},{"id":"7949bfe4-7221-4501-8608-7b5bd77fbb7a","l":"Low & intermediate energy heavy-ion reactions","d":1,"p":"bbb0ce0d-36da-4da5-a443-df064f36aa17","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Low and intermediate energy heavy-ion reactions are collisions between nuclei heavier than helium at energies from around the Coulomb barrier (a few MeV per nucleon) up to a few hundred MeV per nucleon. In this regime nuclear structure and collective dynamics control outcomes such as complete or incomplete fusion, nucleon transfer, deep-inelastic scattering and multifragmentation."},{"id":"d6f75a76-98bb-4917-9e09-b6da28d5266d","l":"Low-temperature plasma","d":1,"p":"22580d6a-8d20-4653-b2d1-a7de314bc162","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"A low-temperature plasma is a partially ionized gas that is quasi-neutral but far from thermal equilibrium, with electrons much hotter than the heavy species (ions and neutrals). Electron temperatures are typically ~0.1\u201310 eV while ions and neutrals remain near room temperature to a few thousand kelvin, yielding low ionization fractions common in gas discharges and plasma-processing systems."},{"id":"70da3cfc-db10-4e3f-8d94-2646155ceaf5","l":"Low-temperature superconductors","d":1,"p":"2250b5f3-dfa1-4ca7-bedf-fff5d41bd6ea","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Low-temperature superconductors are materials that exhibit zero electrical resistance and the Meissner effect only below relatively low critical temperatures (typically \u227230 K), requiring cryogenic cooling (e.g., liquid helium); their behavior is usually described by BCS electron\u2013phonon pairing and includes elemental superconductors (Nb, Pb) and practical alloys/compounds like NbTi and Nb3Sn."},{"id":"1e0cc69d-0062-48ed-964b-8ad5b9c8b30e","l":"Lubricants","d":1,"p":"c765ac18-ded5-4ef0-a41a-22be13741559","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Lubricants are substances (liquids, greases, gases, or solids) placed between contacting surfaces to reduce friction and wear by forming a film that separates the surfaces and carries away heat. Their effectiveness depends on properties such as viscosity, temperature stability, and chemical interaction with the surfaces, giving rise to regimes like hydrodynamic, elastohydrodynamic, and boundary lubrication."},{"id":"2cea1c8c-c8ee-4576-a697-73e0371d9428","l":"Lubrication","d":1,"p":"4e823153-8e04-4bdb-a7ca-0f608ecd9453","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Lubrication is the physical process of reducing friction and wear between contacting surfaces in relative motion by placing a thin layer of lubricant (liquid, solid, or gas) that separates and supports the surfaces. Its effectiveness depends on fluid properties (viscosity), film thickness, surface roughness, and load, producing hydrodynamic, elastohydrodynamic, or boundary lubrication regimes."},{"id":"51947716-36c7-4efe-a335-6d19f313711b","l":"Lubrication theory","d":1,"p":"6aed0168-d867-4733-835f-da82de3c65f5","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Lubrication theory is the asymptotic simplification of the Navier\u2013Stokes equations for viscous flow in thin films or gaps where the film thickness is much smaller than the lateral dimensions, so transverse velocity gradients and viscous stresses dominate while inertia is negligible. The resulting reduced equations (e.g., the Reynolds equation) relate pressure, film thickness and flow to predict load support, friction and film evolution in bearings, coatings and thin-film flows."},{"id":"125ca8eb-8023-4aca-80ad-2101f5bcd325","l":"Luminescence","d":1,"p":"4c4f4d87-e837-4d65-970d-f5323698a5f1","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Luminescence is the emission of light by a material that arises from radiative relaxation of electronically excited states and is not due to its high temperature (i.e., not blackbody/thermal radiation). It includes processes such as fluorescence, phosphorescence, chemiluminescence and electroluminescence, distinguished by how the excitations are produced and by their lifetimes."},{"id":"99668e65-fdae-43c5-b265-3b3af92ccddc","l":"Lunar plasmas","d":1,"p":"88a5cd35-609c-4f0a-8994-5a4cbe488296","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Lunar plasmas are the ionized gases and charged-particle populations in the Moon\u2019s immediate environment, supplied by the solar wind, photoionization of a tenuous exosphere, and sputtering from the surface. Their interactions with the airless regolith produce surface charging, plasma sheaths, a downstream wake and localized mini\u2011magnetospheres, and drive electrostatic dust mobilization."},{"id":"b2a30868-6c3c-4e85-a4d9-73456ef016dc","l":"Lyotropic liquid crystals","d":1,"p":"63f7a3cf-31b7-4d5c-bbcc-58d679dd5139","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Lyotropic liquid crystals are mesophases that form when amphiphilic or anisotropic molecules dissolved in a solvent self-assemble into structured aggregates (bilayers, micelles, rods, etc.), producing partial orientational and/or positional order intermediate between a liquid and a solid. Their specific phases (e.g., lamellar, hexagonal, cubic, nematic) depend primarily on concentration and temperature rather than only on molecular structure."},{"id":"1547f603-847b-4a6f-a90d-b04ed1374ebb","l":"Machine protection","d":1,"p":"4c286df9-b565-4295-b951-49a36d60cad1","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Machine protection is the set of hardware, software and operational procedures that detect faults and promptly mitigate or stop delivery of stored energy (e.g., particle beams, RF power, magnets) to prevent damage to accelerator components, experiments, and infrastructure. It combines active measures (sensors, interlocks, beam-abort systems) and passive elements (collimators, absorbers) plus controlled shutdown protocols."},{"id":"5b4f3bb4-8db6-4156-a472-4c9988d6e313","l":"Magnetically confined plasmas","d":1,"p":"1d80a56f-19ce-4fd1-b088-20fed49f32c4","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Magnetically confined plasmas are ionized gases whose charged particles are steered and trapped by engineered magnetic fields so that their motion is mainly along field lines and contact with material walls is minimized. This confinement\u2014used in devices like tokamaks, stellarators and magnetic mirrors\u2014relies on the Lorentz force and tailored field geometry to reduce cross-field transport and sustain the high temperatures needed for applications such as controlled fusion."},{"id":"dd3e5997-1821-48c1-8e5d-d122961272cf","l":"Magnetic anisotropy","d":1,"p":"b35238d0-83c7-4412-b293-c7d42fa92c62","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Magnetic anisotropy is the directional dependence of a material's magnetic properties: the energy required to magnetize a sample varies with the direction of magnetization, producing preferred \"easy\" and \"hard\" axes. It arises from sources such as crystal symmetry (magnetocrystalline anisotropy), sample shape (demagnetizing fields), strain (magnetoelasticity), and interfacial exchange."},{"id":"cefb274f-e193-42e2-91b8-a74548a21575","l":"Magnetic colloids","d":1,"p":"ef0a1bee-2a1f-4f47-aca2-6302db18e67f","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Magnetic colloids are suspensions of nano- or micron-scale particles that carry a magnetic moment (ferro-, ferri- or superparamagnetic) dispersed in a carrier fluid. Their behavior reflects the interplay of thermal (Brownian), magnetic dipole, and hydrodynamic interactions, so external magnetic fields can reversibly tune their microstructure and rheology (e.g., ferrofluids and magnetorheological fluids)."},{"id":"9f6334f7-2a72-4198-9ee3-8e7ca4d0b7f4","l":"Magnetic confinement fusion","d":1,"p":"888306e1-6391-43a8-a318-3434270aafd5","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Magnetic confinement fusion is a method of producing controlled thermonuclear fusion by using strong magnetic fields to confine and insulate a high\u2011temperature, ionized plasma so ions can undergo fusion before the energy is lost. Devices such as tokamaks and stellarators shape the magnetic field to keep charged particles away from material walls and achieve the temperature\u2013density\u2013confinement-time conditions needed for net energy gain."},{"id":"492228d3-c5ce-4eac-922a-b8158c777c9b","l":"Magnetic coupling","d":1,"p":"b35238d0-83c7-4412-b293-c7d42fa92c62","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Magnetic coupling is the interaction between two magnetic systems (such as current-carrying coils or permanent magnets) mediated by their magnetic fields, whereby a change in one produces forces or induces a voltage or current in the other. In electrical circuits it is quantified by mutual inductance, while between permanent magnets it is described by magnetic dipole\u2013dipole forces and torques."},{"id":"a1f3a6a6-9b93-4121-ab19-4d8204582852","l":"Magnetic field alignment","d":1,"p":"bb18132a-8998-4023-a343-725c6803d24c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Magnetic field alignment is the tendency for magnetic field vectors or magnetic dipoles to orient parallel to a reference direction or to one another. This alignment arises when external fields, currents, or material anisotropies favor a particular orientation, producing net magnetization or a coherent field geometry."},{"id":"cb9f8d3f-f452-4970-a552-29fcd074bb29","l":"Magnetic field generation & plasma dynamo","d":1,"p":"c25108e7-4697-4565-b484-221b808d454e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Plasma Physics"],"def":"Magnetic field generation & plasma dynamo: the process by which motions of an electrically conducting fluid or plasma convert kinetic energy into magnetic energy via electromagnetic induction, amplifying and sustaining magnetic fields against resistive decay. Dynamo action arises when flow patterns (e.g., turbulence, shear, differential rotation) stretch, twist and fold field lines, producing both large-scale organized fields and small-scale turbulent fields in astrophysical and laboratory plasmas."},{"id":"4256a82c-21ec-4a71-a5b0-e732abaa848b","l":"Magnetic insulators","d":1,"p":"58071c68-730c-4a3e-982c-abc9f0cedf67","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Magnetic insulators are electrically insulating materials that nonetheless host localized magnetic moments and long-range magnetic order (ferro-, antiferro-, or more complex orders) arising from exchange interactions between ions. Because they lack mobile charge carriers, they decouple spin and charge degrees of freedom and support spin excitations (magnons), making them important for quantum magnetism and spintronic/magnonic applications."},{"id":"1f68adf2-72ef-4afa-8536-fd5094c2edd5","l":"Magnetic interactions","d":1,"p":"cc59c6fb-acbb-4237-953b-b874fe5dbd4a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Magnetic interactions are the forces and torques that arise between moving charges, electrical currents, and magnetic dipoles, mediated by the magnetic field and depending on relative motion and orientation. Classically they are described by the Lorentz force (F = q v \u00d7 B) and dipole\u2013dipole coupling (U = \u2212\u03bc\u00b7B), while in materials they also include quantum exchange interactions that align atomic spins."},{"id":"8568a1b2-908f-465b-9013-9f8ec543070c","l":"Magnetic moment","d":1,"p":"ff402d94-d1d8-4207-a321-f935f060b175","fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Particles & Fields"],"def":"The magnetic moment is a vector quantity that characterizes the strength and orientation of a magnetic source (such as a current loop, bar magnet, or atomic electron) and determines the torque it experiences in an external magnetic field (\u03c4 = \u03bc \u00d7 B). It governs the source's dipole field at large distances and, for a current loop, equals \u03bc = I A n (units A\u00b7m^2 or J/T), while for particles it is related to angular momentum by the gyromagnetic ratio."},{"id":"08f3be87-c0e8-436a-a49d-d67842795583","l":"Magnetic monopoles","d":1,"p":"3981a374-dbc8-42de-bdba-73ea858650c6","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Magnetic monopoles are hypothetical particles that carry isolated magnetic charge (a net north or south pole), meaning the magnetic field has a nonzero divergence at their location, analogous to electric charges for the electric field. They appear in theoretical models (Dirac, 't Hooft\u2013Polyakov) and as emergent quasiparticles in spin ices; their existence would modify Maxwell's equations and imply electric charge quantization, but no elementary monopole has been observed."},{"id":"ff65e95b-d8de-402b-a6cb-4f266682e4c6","l":"Magnetic multilayers","d":1,"p":"58071c68-730c-4a3e-982c-abc9f0cedf67","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Magnetic multilayers are nanoscale stacks of alternating magnetic and nonmagnetic (or different magnetic) thin films whose electronic and magnetic behavior is determined by interlayer exchange, spin-dependent scattering, and dipolar coupling. These engineered stacks exhibit collective effects such as giant magnetoresistance, exchange bias, and tunable magnetic anisotropy that are central to magnetic sensors and spintronic devices."},{"id":"b6e4cc90-18f3-4427-841d-c667375bbca0","l":"Magnetic order","d":1,"p":"979074fa-fadd-4ebe-acf1-0a9c92fb1848","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Magnetic order is the spatial arrangement of atomic or electronic magnetic moments in a material, produced by interactions (exchange, dipolar, anisotropy) that lock spins into correlated patterns such as ferromagnetic, antiferromagnetic, ferrimagnetic, or more complex textures. It typically appears below a characteristic ordering temperature (e.g., Curie or N\u00e9el temperature) and represents a spontaneous breaking of spin-rotation symmetry."},{"id":"92927025-018a-4b9f-b617-c4a768530654","l":"Magnetic phase transitions","d":1,"p":"19f26688-0955-4fd9-85d8-aaf71c4a581f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Magnetic phase transitions are changes in a material's collective magnetic order\u2014for example between ferromagnetic, antiferromagnetic, spin-glass, and paramagnetic states\u2014driven by variations in temperature, pressure, or applied magnetic field. They are marked by the emergence or disappearance of a magnetic order parameter (e.g., magnetization or staggered magnetization), often involve spontaneous symmetry breaking, and can be continuous (with critical behavior) or discontinuous."},{"id":"dd2278fa-b529-4b57-8abb-7b1cf81f0e4e","l":"Magnetic susceptibility","d":1,"p":"b35238d0-83c7-4412-b293-c7d42fa92c62","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Magnetic susceptibility \u03c7 is the (dimensionless) proportionality between a material's magnetization M (magnetic moment per unit volume) and the applied magnetic field H in the linear response regime, defined as \u03c7 = M/H. It indicates whether a material is paramagnetic/ferromagnetic (\u03c7 > 0) or diamagnetic (\u03c7 < 0), and in anisotropic or dynamic cases \u03c7 may be a tensor or a frequency-dependent complex quantity."},{"id":"0003fc3f-1522-4fca-bf3f-bcef0dcec405","l":"Magnetic techniques","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Magnetic techniques are experimental methods that use applied magnetic fields or the magnetic response of materials, particles, or spins to probe, image, manipulate, or control physical systems. Common examples include magnetometry (SQUIDs, Hall probes), magnetic resonance (NMR, ESR), magneto\u2011optical effects (MOKE), magnetoresistance measurements, and magnetic trapping or transport of atoms and charges."},{"id":"1bd1a9ab-ec4f-40a8-9f81-9f1005587d58","l":"Magnetic texture","d":1,"p":"b35238d0-83c7-4412-b293-c7d42fa92c62","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A magnetic texture is the spatial arrangement of the local magnetization (its direction and, when relevant, magnitude) within a magnetic material, describing how magnetic moments vary from point to point. Examples include domain walls, vortices, helices and skyrmions, some of which carry nontrivial topological character and distinct dynamical properties."},{"id":"3e80884b-56d9-4ed5-9f94-7e835761cb6e","l":"Magnetization dynamics","d":1,"p":"b35238d0-83c7-4412-b293-c7d42fa92c62","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Magnetization dynamics is the time-dependent behavior of a material\u2019s magnetization vector under the action of effective magnetic fields, torques (e.g., applied fields, anisotropy, exchange, spin-transfer or spin-orbit torques) and thermal fluctuations. It is commonly described by the Landau\u2013Lifshitz\u2013Gilbert equation, which captures precessional motion around effective fields and damping toward equilibrium."},{"id":"46f9ab71-6dde-4941-a500-06f82f4482ed","l":"Magnetized plasma","d":1,"p":"22580d6a-8d20-4653-b2d1-a7de314bc162","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"A magnetized plasma is an ionized gas whose particle motion and collective dynamics are strongly controlled by a magnetic field. Quantitatively it occurs when charged-particle gyrofrequencies exceed collision frequencies or their Larmor radii are small compared with system scales, producing anisotropic transport and magnetic-field\u2013dominated behavior (e.g., guiding-center motion and Alfv\u00e9n waves)."},{"id":"664caf62-07df-471a-824f-b0ed7f326bf4","l":"Magnetoacoustic effect","d":1,"p":"b35238d0-83c7-4412-b293-c7d42fa92c62","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The magnetoacoustic effect is the coupling between magnetic fields (or magnetization) and mechanical sound/elastic waves: changing magnetic fields or magnetization can generate or modify acoustic waves (e.g., via magnetostriction or Lorentz forces), and conversely acoustic strain alters magnetic properties through magnetoelastic coupling. This interaction produces phenomena such as magnetostriction-driven sound in solids and the magnetoacoustic (fast and slow) wave modes in plasmas and magnetized media."},{"id":"635a1f62-c411-4b5c-909f-504a3187f491","l":"Magneto-dielectric effect","d":1,"p":"b35238d0-83c7-4412-b293-c7d42fa92c62","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Magneto-dielectric effect \u2014 the change in a material's dielectric permittivity (or dielectric response) induced by an applied magnetic field or by changes in magnetization, arising from coupling between magnetic and electric degrees of freedom. It is commonly seen in multiferroics, magnetoelectric materials, and engineered composites and is exploited for tunable microwave and sensor applications."},{"id":"2747139a-722e-4167-a03a-e2671889b160","l":"Magnetohydrodynamic techniques","d":1,"p":"233a6cd0-b7fe-491b-b5fd-85fd6b2a7c79","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Magnetohydrodynamic (MHD) techniques are the theoretical, computational and experimental methods used to model and analyze the behavior of electrically conducting fluids (plasmas, liquid metals) interacting with magnetic fields, based on the coupled Navier\u2013Stokes and Maxwell equations (the MHD equations). They encompass analytic approaches (e.g., linear stability and wave analyses), numerical MHD codes and reduced models (ideal, resistive, Hall MHD), and laboratory diagnostics, with applications in astrophysics, fusion, and liquid\u2011metal engineering."},{"id":"f713ae72-a647-44e9-ac36-df249c5fd2c3","l":"Magnetohydrodynamic turbulence","d":1,"p":"c25108e7-4697-4565-b484-221b808d454e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Plasma Physics"],"def":"Magnetohydrodynamic (MHD) turbulence is the chaotic, multiscale motion of an electrically conducting fluid or plasma in which nonlinear coupling between velocity and magnetic-field fluctuations\u2014via the induction equation and the Lorentz force\u2014drives anisotropic energy cascades, Alfv\u00e9nic wave behavior, and intermittent structures such as current sheets."},{"id":"8b3dead2-5a21-4f0e-b8e3-0fe7c260c606","l":"Magnetohydrodynamic waves","d":1,"p":"cad250bc-84eb-4c9d-82f1-ce01969ff172","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Magnetohydrodynamic (MHD) waves are collective oscillations in a conducting fluid or plasma in which fluid motion and magnetic fields are coupled by the Lorentz force and electromagnetic induction.  The magnetic tension and pressure (together with gas pressure) act as restoring forces, giving characteristic modes such as Alfv\u00e9n waves and slow and fast magnetosonic waves whose speeds depend on the magnetic field strength, density, and sound speed."},{"id":"d7ffe50d-52c2-4e3d-b88b-fc29e5e04b8b","l":"Magnetoinertial fusion","d":1,"p":"888306e1-6391-43a8-a318-3434270aafd5","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Magnetoinertial fusion (also called magnetized target fusion) is a hybrid approach in which a plasma is pre-seeded with a magnetic field and then rapidly compressed on inertial timescales so the field suppresses thermal conduction and helps confine charged particles long enough to reach fusion conditions. By combining magnetic insulation with implosion-driven heating, it aims to achieve fusion with lower driver energy and relaxed steady-state requirements compared with pure inertial or magnetic confinement."},{"id":"47152936-51d3-496a-baaa-2fe108b6ad58","l":"Magnetometry","d":1,"p":"141061f5-f02c-492c-af56-81c3c49e8b65","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Magnetometry is the measurement and quantitative characterization of magnetic fields and magnetic moments\u2014their magnitude, direction, and spatial variation\u2014using instruments called magnetometers. Techniques range from simple compass-based sensors to highly sensitive devices (SQUIDs, atomic and solid-state magnetometers) used in geophysics, materials science, biomedical imaging, and fundamental-physics experiments."},{"id":"415100c9-6a50-4f96-952c-e047c73976ff","l":"Magneto-optical spectra","d":1,"p":"ba7ccb8e-d7eb-4e6f-9c1e-b622f5b4e242","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Magneto-optical spectra are measurements of a material's optical response (absorption, transmission, reflectivity and polarization rotation) as a function of photon energy while an external magnetic field is applied. These spectra show field-induced features\u2014such as Zeeman splitting, Landau-level transitions, cyclotron resonance and magneto-excitons\u2014that reveal electronic structure, carrier dynamics and magnetic interactions."},{"id":"e5cd96a4-a0d5-4b3a-a477-8ed31cf31830","l":"Magneto-optics","d":1,"p":"4c4f4d87-e837-4d65-970d-f5323698a5f1","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Magneto-optics is the study of how magnetic fields and material magnetization affect the propagation, polarization, and absorption of light, and conversely how optical fields can probe or influence magnetic properties. It encompasses phenomena such as the Faraday and Kerr rotation, magnetic circular dichroism, and magnetically induced birefringence used in spectroscopy, sensing, and magnetic imaging."},{"id":"0e380d9d-ccad-467d-abf7-7ab154e42c86","l":"Magnetotransport","d":1,"p":"b35238d0-83c7-4412-b293-c7d42fa92c62","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Magnetotransport is the study of how magnetic fields modify a material's transport properties (especially electrical and thermoelectric), producing effects such as magnetoresistance, the Hall effect, and quantum oscillations. It probes carrier dynamics, scattering, and band or topological structure via the Lorentz force, Landau quantization, and field-dependent changes in carrier motion and scattering."},{"id":"51a71a1e-b8e4-4a7f-8266-2cc5a0b67c81","l":"Magnons","d":1,"p":"d46f32eb-17b3-4d09-be59-fc3461e542db","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Magnons are the quantized collective excitations (quasiparticles) of spin waves in magnetically ordered materials, representing coherent precession of aligned electron magnetic moments. They are bosonic excitations with a dispersion set by exchange interactions and anisotropy, carrying energy and spin angular momentum and contributing to thermal and spin transport."},{"id":"4893232d-4f6c-4097-a4a9-98817e7dab41","l":"Majorana neutrinos","d":1,"p":"3981a374-dbc8-42de-bdba-73ea858650c6","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Majorana neutrinos are neutral spin-1/2 particles that are identical to their antiparticles \u2014 i.e., they can be described by a self-conjugate Majorana field rather than a distinct Dirac field. If neutrinos are Majorana, lepton number is not conserved, allowing Majorana mass terms that can explain small neutrino masses and produce signatures such as neutrinoless double beta decay."},{"id":"8a14a3c2-b839-48bf-85ee-2ca6c041b548","l":"Mammals","d":1,"p":"91278658-51b4-4d8e-a936-7cfad1266625","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Mammals are a class of vertebrate animals characterized chiefly by the presence of mammary glands, which produce milk to nourish their young, and by hair or fur. They also typically have three middle-ear bones and an endothermic (warm-blooded) metabolism, and most species give live birth (with egg-laying monotremes as exceptions)."},{"id":"05a47dcc-71c0-4ebc-9d4e-79a40a191efb","l":"Many-body techniques","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Many-body techniques are theoretical and computational methods for describing and predicting the properties of systems containing large numbers of interacting particles (quantum or classical), where collective behavior and correlations determine macroscopic phenomena. They encompass approaches such as mean-field theory, Green's functions and diagrammatic perturbation, renormalization-group methods, density-functional theory, quantum Monte Carlo, exact diagonalization, and tensor-network techniques."},{"id":"19ed05c8-4543-4d77-801f-a8978c87387c","l":"Marangoni convection","d":1,"p":"2d276ce6-c803-4ba3-813c-b989f2d4bfde","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Marangoni convection is flow driven by gradients in surface tension along a liquid interface: differences in temperature, concentration, or surfactant coverage create a tangential stress that pulls fluid along the surface (typically from low to high surface tension), producing circulating motions and possible instabilities."},{"id":"194715d0-408a-40dc-990d-423fdb28bdad","l":"Masers","d":1,"p":"23cdcd6b-ae22-4b6d-92cd-24a9a8bd0577","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Masers (Microwave Amplification by Stimulated Emission of Radiation) are devices that generate or amplify coherent microwave-frequency electromagnetic waves by stimulated emission from a population-inverted ensemble of atoms, molecules, or solids, producing highly monochromatic, low-noise, phase-coherent output. They operate on the same fundamental principles as lasers but at microwave/radio frequencies and are used in precision spectroscopy, low-noise amplification, and frequency standards."},{"id":"a164d72c-84d8-4332-a3b1-a9199a4f50ad","l":"Mass","d":1,"p":"7de7a12f-2bec-4394-98aa-26aebe36d88f","fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Mass is a scalar property of matter that quantifies an object's resistance to acceleration (inertial mass) and its coupling to the gravitational field (gravitational mass). In special relativity the invariant or rest mass equals the object's energy in its rest frame divided by c^2."},{"id":"e1fdd9bf-9507-4d67-ad27-da8630cead94","l":"Material failure","d":1,"p":"9e001bda-d32b-4a59-a216-bfd4c1af8c25","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Material failure is the loss of a material's ability to carry the intended load or perform its function due to irreversible damage\u2014such as fracture, plastic yielding, fatigue, creep, buckling, corrosion, or wear\u2014so that it no longer meets design requirements. Failures can be sudden (e.g., brittle fracture) or progressive (e.g., fatigue or corrosion) and are typically manifested by cracks, excessive deformation, or material separation."},{"id":"4db2768f-9069-4cad-901f-a8001738ab66","l":"Materials","d":1,"p":"29ab1ea0-dd7e-46b7-87dd-0ca29c6be062","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Materials are physical substances\u2014natural or engineered\u2014whose composition and internal structure at the atomic, nano-, and micro-scales determine their macroscopic mechanical, electrical, magnetic, optical, and thermal properties. Materials physics studies these structure\u2013property relationships, including the roles of defects and processing, and how to design and synthesize materials for specific applications."},{"id":"053cbd96-42ec-4b6c-be35-f9115be3b6b4","l":"Materials analysis & modification by accelerators","d":1,"p":"5d582b16-6b34-4e39-b32c-21bd28c9ea7b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Materials analysis & modification by accelerators is the use of energetic particle and photon beams produced by accelerators to probe and quantify a material\u2019s composition, structure and defects with high sensitivity and depth resolution. The same beams (ions, electrons, neutrons, X-rays, etc.) can also be used to deliberately alter materials\u2014by ion implantation, radiation-induced defects, sputtering or phase changes\u2014enabling precise, controllable modification of chemical, structural and functional properties."},{"id":"93a57375-877e-4150-991e-2c8b966b3185","l":"Materials modeling","d":1,"p":"233a6cd0-b7fe-491b-b5fd-85fd6b2a7c79","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Materials modeling is the use of theoretical and computational methods to predict and explain the structure, properties, and behavior of materials across length and time scales. It spans electronic\u2011structure calculations and atomistic simulations to mesoscale and continuum models, often combined with experiments and data\u2011driven techniques to design and optimize materials."},{"id":"b5ff1838-0ecd-41a5-a90b-bae4acb69fcf","l":"Mathematical physics methods","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Mathematical physics methods are the mathematical techniques and tools used to formulate, analyze, and solve physical problems and theories, providing rigorous structure and calculational procedures that connect equations to physical predictions. They include analytical frameworks (e.g., differential equations, functional and group theory, variational principles, perturbation and asymptotic methods) and numerical algorithms for computation."},{"id":"caacfd22-969a-48da-905d-e17156f58569","l":"MAX phase compounds","d":1,"p":"81194f70-bbca-4041-b57e-29338e588cac","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"MAX phase compounds are a family of nanolaminated, hexagonal ternary carbides and nitrides with general formula M_{n+1}AX_n (n = 1\u20133), where M is an early transition metal, A is a group\u201113\u201316 element, and X is C or N. Their layered atomic structure gives a unique combination of metallic properties (electrical/thermal conductivity, damage tolerance) and ceramic attributes (high stiffness, thermal stability, chemical/oxidation resistance)."},{"id":"4f95a01c-85bf-4b22-916d-6e9fcc586377","l":"Mean-field & cluster methods","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Mean-field methods approximate a many-body system by replacing the interactions on each degree of freedom with an average, self-consistently determined effective field, thereby neglecting most inter-particle correlations. Cluster methods systematically improve on mean-field theory by treating a finite group of sites or particles exactly (or with higher accuracy) while embedding that cluster in a mean-field environment, capturing short-range correlations and fluctuations."},{"id":"77d491f8-55ad-47a6-a0a7-cd24ffce7602","l":"Mechanical deformation","d":1,"p":"9e001bda-d32b-4a59-a216-bfd4c1af8c25","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Mechanical deformation is the change in a body's shape or size produced by applied forces or internal stresses, quantified locally by the strain (and described mathematically by the strain tensor) and accompanied by internal stresses. It can be reversible (elastic), permanent (plastic), or time-dependent (viscoelastic) and occurs in modes such as tension, compression, shear, and bending."},{"id":"7f29b291-aab9-4d63-a15c-1b98193cc0ca","l":"Mechanical energy storage","d":1,"p":"80fe725c-ae9b-4701-a9f2-db29c094a8ea","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Mechanical energy storage is the retention of energy in a mechanical system by converting it into forms such as elastic or gravitational potential or rotational kinetic energy (e.g., in springs, raised masses, or flywheels) so it can be recovered later as mechanical work. It is typically reversible and characterized by the system\u2019s configuration (position, deformation, or motion) and its ability to deliver force or torque."},{"id":"1cfa904e-db22-4c06-8f68-e1cf8fc103bb","l":"Mechanical spectroscopy","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Mechanical spectroscopy is an experimental technique that measures a material's frequency- and temperature-dependent mechanical response\u2014usually the complex elastic modulus and internal friction\u2014by applying small oscillatory stress or strain. It is used to identify and characterize relaxation processes, defects, phase transitions, and microstructural dynamics through their signatures in stored and dissipated mechanical energy."},{"id":"b777b985-75b5-420a-9f13-21bb83461999","l":"Mechanical testing","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Mechanical testing is the experimental characterization of a material's response to controlled mechanical loads or deformations to determine properties such as stiffness, strength, ductility, toughness, hardness, and fatigue life. Typical tests (tensile, compression, bending, hardness, impact, fatigue) measure stress\u2013strain behavior and failure modes to quantify performance and inform design or modeling."},{"id":"f5fed3b8-a2ea-4008-971e-62d179ae3b06","l":"Mechanobiology","d":1,"p":"b26070f8-32ca-4e02-94d0-ba1961b3480d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Mechanobiology is the study of how mechanical forces, deformations, and material properties of cells and tissues influence biological function and behavior and how cells sense and transduce those mechanical cues into biochemical signals. It combines concepts from continuum and cellular mechanics with molecular biology to quantify force generation, transmission, and feedback in processes such as development, homeostasis, and disease."},{"id":"f3560812-6ca5-43b8-a0e6-25d4f08e3454","l":"Medical applications of accelerators","d":1,"p":"5d582b16-6b34-4e39-b32c-21bd28c9ea7b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Medical applications of accelerators use machines such as linear accelerators, cyclotrons and synchrotrons to generate energetic particle beams or radionuclides for healthcare. These beams and isotopes are used for diagnosis and imaging (e.g., PET/SPECT), external-beam radiotherapy and precision proton/ion therapy for cancer, and for sterilization and other medical research applications."},{"id":"65266af4-488c-4bbf-a84a-36626bc9aac8","l":"Meissner effect","d":1,"p":"ca8ddaad-441c-481c-93b3-65f46125fb93","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Meissner effect is the complete expulsion of magnetic flux from the bulk of a material when it transitions into the superconducting state below its critical temperature, producing perfect diamagnetism. It arises from dissipationless surface screening currents that cancel applied fields inside the superconductor, leaving only a shallow field penetration set by the London penetration depth."},{"id":"d0c53e76-4b73-46f9-a799-d1b3712a330e","l":"Memory","d":1,"p":"8b374c75-8448-476e-a872-f15b0cf5a5fe","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Memory in physics is the property of a system to retain information about past states, inputs, or perturbations so that its current and future behavior depends on that history. It appears as hysteresis, aging, non\u2011Markovian dynamics, or engineered information storage in materials and devices (e.g., spin glasses, polymers, magnetic domains, and memristors)."},{"id":"a34a5512-047d-46b1-8957-eb4be1543024","l":"Mesenchyme","d":1,"p":"fc65882e-b316-4cf1-a96a-ec8557624d80","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Mesenchyme is a loosely organized embryonic connective tissue composed of multipotent, often spindle-shaped mesenchymal cells embedded in a fibrous extracellular matrix. Its cells are highly migratory and mechanically active, generating forces and remodeling the matrix during morphogenesis and wound healing, making it a key subject in tissue mechanics and biophysics."},{"id":"e83448a2-8715-4268-93b1-1712750e5a5c","l":"Meson & hyperon induced nuclear reactions","d":1,"p":"bbb0ce0d-36da-4da5-a443-df064f36aa17","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Meson- and hyperon-induced nuclear reactions are collisions in which an incoming meson (e.g., \u03c0, K, \u03b7) or hyperon (e.g., \u039b, \u03a3, \u039e) interacts with a nucleus, causing elastic or inelastic scattering, absorption, particle production, or conversion of nucleons into strange baryons. These reactions probe hadron\u2013nucleon and hyperon\u2013nucleon interactions, can produce strangeness (e.g., hypernuclei) via strangeness-exchange processes, and are used to study nuclear structure and the strong force in the strange sector."},{"id":"5400167e-3abc-44f8-b432-0dc88cab84cf","l":"Mesons","d":1,"p":"ca7e6e75-307c-4709-ad9e-9bb1f153fede","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Mesons are hadronic particles made of a quark and an antiquark bound together by the strong interaction (QCD). They are bosons with integer spin, generally unstable, and often act as carriers of the residual strong force between nucleons (e.g., pions)."},{"id":"ec491c6b-9fe9-4b07-bfdf-e96570733898","l":"Metal halides","d":1,"p":"81194f70-bbca-4041-b57e-29338e588cac","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Metal halides are compounds formed by a metal atom bonded to one or more halogen atoms (fluorine, chlorine, bromine, iodine). They range from ionic to covalent salts with diverse crystal structures and electronic/optical properties important in catalysis, lighting, and electronic materials."},{"id":"10f6f4ab-6944-4d26-a799-52ed9e725571","l":"Metallotropic liquid crystals","d":1,"p":"63f7a3cf-31b7-4d5c-bbcc-58d679dd5139","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Metallotropic liquid crystals (also called metallomesogens) are liquid-crystalline materials whose mesophase formation and properties are governed by metal-containing mesogens or metal\u2013ligand/metal\u2013metal interactions. They combine the orientational/positional order of liquid crystals with electronic, magnetic or photophysical functionality imparted by coordinated metal centers, enabling stimuli-responsive and multifunctional mesophases."},{"id":"651dd708-9dbe-4012-a04e-ee9a8332a9f2","l":"Metal-organic framework","d":1,"p":"fea036a1-9c29-4872-ab2c-d052949f7c03","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"A metal\u2013organic framework (MOF) is a crystalline, porous coordination network formed by metal ions or clusters bridged by multidentate organic ligands, producing a periodic scaffold with very high internal surface area. Its modular structure gives tunable pore size and chemistry, enabling applications in gas storage, separation, catalysis and sensing."},{"id":"721bc5e5-4148-408b-91b5-fb760c1278ab","l":"Metals","d":1,"p":"81194f70-bbca-4041-b57e-29338e588cac","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Metals are materials whose atoms are held together by metallic bonding: a lattice of positive ions immersed in a sea of delocalized conduction electrons (a partially filled conduction band). This electronic structure gives metals high electrical and thermal conductivity, optical reflectivity, and mechanical ductility."},{"id":"77fabbae-1f98-4300-996c-9158ab52e6fc","l":"Metamaterials","d":1,"p":"23cdcd6b-ae22-4b6d-92cd-24a9a8bd0577","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Metamaterials are artificially structured materials whose macroscopic electromagnetic, acoustic, or mechanical response is determined by the geometry of engineered subwavelength unit cells rather than the intrinsic properties of their constituent substances. This design freedom lets them exhibit unusual effective parameters \u2014 for example negative refractive index, extreme anisotropy, or wavefront\u2011control capabilities \u2014 not found in natural materials."},{"id":"82f6c5fd-bbef-4ff9-a5f9-de62d3f5dfeb","l":"Methods in electromagnetism","d":1,"p":"233a6cd0-b7fe-491b-b5fd-85fd6b2a7c79","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Methods in electromagnetism are the analytical, approximation, and computational techniques used to formulate and solve Maxwell\u2019s equations and predict electromagnetic fields, waves, and forces for given sources and boundary conditions. They include tools such as separation of variables, Green\u2019s functions and multipole expansions, variational and perturbative/asymptotic methods, and numerical schemes like finite-element, boundary-element, and finite-difference time-domain methods."},{"id":"ba55d8a8-d049-4550-b264-530f74618214","l":"Methods in magnetism","d":1,"p":"233a6cd0-b7fe-491b-b5fd-85fd6b2a7c79","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Methods in magnetism are the experimental, theoretical, and computational techniques used to create, measure, model, and control magnetic materials and phenomena. They include experimental probes (e.g., magnetometry, SQUIDs, neutron/X-ray scattering, magnetic resonance and various microscopies) and theoretical/computational frameworks (e.g., micromagnetics and the Landau\u2013Lifshitz\u2013Gilbert equation, spin models, Monte Carlo and molecular/first-principles simulations)."},{"id":"7299559c-15c4-47f3-b866-5633b1030453","l":"Methods in superconductivity","d":1,"p":"233a6cd0-b7fe-491b-b5fd-85fd6b2a7c79","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Methods in superconductivity are the experimental and theoretical techniques used to detect, characterize, and understand superconducting materials and devices. They include low-temperature transport and magnetic measurements, spectroscopies and scattering probes (STM/ARPES, muon spin rotation, neutron scattering), Josephson and microwave techniques, and theoretical frameworks from phenomenological Ginzburg\u2013Landau and BCS/Eliashberg theories to microscopic Bogoliubov\u2013de Gennes and numerical many-body methods."},{"id":"f4585613-4c82-4c78-a57d-515fa4774cb6","l":"Methods in transport","d":1,"p":"233a6cd0-b7fe-491b-b5fd-85fd6b2a7c79","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Methods in transport are the theoretical, computational, and experimental techniques used to describe and predict the flow and exchange of mass, charge, momentum, or energy in physical systems. They span continuum and kinetic theories (e.g., diffusion, Navier\u2013Stokes, Boltzmann), linear-response and non\u2011equilibrium formalisms (Green\u2013Kubo, Boltzmann transport, NEGF), numerical simulations (molecular dynamics, Monte Carlo, finite-element) and measurement protocols for fluxes and transport coefficients."},{"id":"af53b7f0-3191-45c2-9be4-e04b69db0666","l":"Microbial multicellular systems","d":1,"p":"e7ff71e0-89cf-438a-8e28-d449f5629207","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Microbial multicellular systems are assemblies of interacting microbes that self-organize into cohesive, often functionally differentiated structures (e.g., biofilms, colonies, filaments, fruiting bodies). Their collective dynamics\u2014shaped by chemical signaling, extracellular matrix production, mechanical forces and transport processes\u2014give rise to emergent physical behaviors (pattern formation, collective motion, altered rheology) distinct from single cells."},{"id":"91d8d762-4b89-4c5c-8d00-66550c6f6af3","l":"Microbubbles","d":1,"p":"98cac094-ec9c-4f37-adf7-eed968f89359","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Microbubbles are gas-filled spherical cavities in a liquid, typically ranging from tens of nanometers to a few hundred micrometers in diameter and often stabilized by surfactants or thin shells. Their small size makes them highly compressible and responsive to pressure or acoustic forcing, producing strong oscillations, cavitation and enhanced interfacial transport used in applications from ultrasound contrast imaging to sonochemistry."},{"id":"705276f4-9fe7-4d6d-ba93-645eb50a4ebf","l":"Microgels","d":1,"p":"06891d57-1044-487c-ae20-21366d161a97","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Microgels are solvent-swollen, crosslinked polymer-network particles with sizes from tens of nanometers to micrometers that behave as soft, deformable colloids. They are stimulus-responsive (e.g., to temperature, pH, or ionic strength), exhibiting reversible swelling/deswelling (volume phase transitions) and rich mechanical and rheological behavior used in soft-matter studies and applications."},{"id":"d602a656-c57b-4004-b015-230b610f2bb2","l":"Micromagnetism","d":1,"p":"b35238d0-83c7-4412-b293-c7d42fa92c62","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Micromagnetism is a continuum theoretical framework for describing the spatial distribution and dynamics of the magnetization in ferromagnetic materials at nanometer-to-micrometer scales, treating magnetization as a smoothly varying vector field of fixed magnitude. It determines equilibrium patterns (domains, domain walls, vortices) and time evolution by balancing exchange, anisotropy, magnetostatic (demagnetizing) and Zeeman energies, with dynamics governed by the Landau\u2013Lifshitz\u2013Gilbert equation."},{"id":"e2a0a4cc-d573-40f1-b1cd-78ed5b91459e","l":"Microstructure","d":1,"p":"15f520a3-5c6c-4d9e-8975-81d399140562","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Microstructure is the arrangement and morphology of phases, grains, defects, and interfaces in a material at length scales from nanometers to micrometers, usually revealed by microscopy. These microstructural features\u2014grain size and shape, phase distribution, precipitates, dislocations and porosity\u2014govern the material's mechanical, thermal, electrical and optical properties."},{"id":"62b17a2f-3001-4254-982f-0f01726f510b","l":"Microwave techniques","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Microwave techniques are experimental and engineering methods for generating, guiding, controlling, and detecting electromagnetic waves in the microwave band (roughly 0.3\u2013300 GHz) using sources, waveguides/cables, resonators, and detectors. They are employed for spectroscopy and materials characterization, communication and radar, signal processing, and coherent control/readout of quantum and electronic devices."},{"id":"024e482f-2738-428a-b77e-6402088ab85e","l":"Mixed ionic electronic conduction","d":1,"p":"41dd58dd-d35c-46e8-9594-d7e05b458da6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Mixed ionic\u2013electronic conduction is the property of a material that allows significant simultaneous transport of mobile ions and electronic charge carriers (electrons and/or holes) through the same phase. Such mixed conductors support coupled ionic and electronic currents and are essential in devices like fuel cells, batteries, membranes and sensors where electrochemical reactions and charge neutrality link the two transport modes."},{"id":"ba7558eb-409a-47e3-bd55-78866b42a059","l":"Mixing enhancement","d":1,"p":"b7f686b6-3282-4e50-85e4-f19b2db66d82","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Mixing enhancement is the increase in the rate at which a scalar field (e.g., concentration or temperature) is homogenized by fluid motion or external forcing beyond what molecular diffusion alone would produce, usually quantified by an effective diffusivity or faster decay of scalar variance. Mechanisms include turbulence, chaotic advection, shear and instabilities, and it is often measured by metrics such as mixing efficiency or enhanced transport coefficients."},{"id":"0312c66e-757d-4763-abec-f20801d32015","l":"Mixing in geophysical flows","d":1,"p":"d003cb63-dde8-403d-84dd-985e04b1fb25","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Mixing in geophysical flows is the process by which fluid motions\u2014large-scale advection, mesoscale stirring, turbulent eddies, and molecular diffusion\u2014redistribute and homogenize tracers such as heat, salt, and chemical species in rotating, stratified systems like the atmosphere and oceans. It includes reversible stirring and irreversible cross-density (diapycnal) mixing that alter buoyancy distributions, convert available potential energy to background potential energy, and thereby control heat, mass, and momentum transport on climate-relevant scales."},{"id":"b7445287-a978-40fe-8f6c-2804e07201e0","l":"Mixtures of atomic and/or molecular quantum gases","d":1,"p":"46e2a993-8dce-4c28-beab-fdabe4378664","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Mixtures of atomic and/or molecular quantum gases are ultracold, multi-component systems of two or more distinct atomic or molecular species (or internal states) brought to quantum degeneracy, whose properties are governed by quantum statistics and inter- and intra-species interactions. These mixtures exhibit rich behavior \u2014 e.g., sympathetic cooling, phase separation, heteronuclear pairing, and coupled collective excitations \u2014 not present in single-component gases."},{"id":"4dbd8e97-7735-45c1-a1bf-ad9db4e24fad","l":"Mode coupling theory","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Mode-coupling theory is a self-consistent framework for describing the time evolution of collective fluctuations in interacting many-body systems, in which nonlinear feedback between slow modes (for example, density fluctuations) leads to dramatically slowed relaxation. It provides closed equations for correlation functions that predict two-step relaxation, scaling laws, and an idealized dynamical glass transition, though it is an approximate theory valid near the onset of glassy dynamics."},{"id":"9af709f6-5f1d-4c71-a663-0c66c9db09d3","l":"Models based on symmetries","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Models based on symmetries are theoretical frameworks that construct physical laws by imposing invariance under specified transformations (e.g., rotations, translations, discrete permutations, or gauge changes), using group theory to classify states and restrict allowed interactions. By enforcing those symmetries they yield conservation laws (via Noether's theorem), selection rules, and predictive relations that reduce the number of free parameters in a model."},{"id":"c98434b0-e98e-49be-9355-5de4b1799898","l":"Models & methods for nuclear reactions","d":1,"p":"bbb0ce0d-36da-4da5-a443-df064f36aa17","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Models & methods for nuclear reactions are the theoretical frameworks and computational techniques used to describe, predict, and analyze how atomic nuclei interact, scatter, and transform during collisions, covering mechanisms from direct reactions to compound-nucleus and statistical decay. They connect nuclear structure and reaction dynamics to experimental observables using approaches such as the optical model, R\u2011matrix and Hauser\u2013Feshbach formalisms, DWBA and coupled\u2011channels methods, Glauber and transport theories, and numerical many\u2011body or Monte Carlo simulations."},{"id":"6090aec3-29d2-4724-93d3-f3dc024b5a9d","l":"Molecular condensates","d":1,"p":"db7ba2bd-700a-4b0b-b444-b873c97ff818","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Molecular condensates are quantum-degenerate states in which bound molecules (typically diatomic) occupy a single macroscopic quantum state, forming a coherent matter wave analogous to a Bose\u2013Einstein condensate. They are usually realized in ultracold atomic gases by pairing atoms (e.g., via Feshbach resonances) and are important for studies of superfluidity and the BCS\u2013BEC crossover."},{"id":"26d668bf-9c1d-42d0-aef5-45f15df3847b","l":"Molecular dissociation","d":1,"p":"ba7ccb8e-d7eb-4e6f-9c1e-b622f5b4e242","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Molecular dissociation is the process by which a molecule's chemical bonds are broken and it separates into two or more smaller species (atoms, radicals, ions, or other molecules). It typically requires input of energy (thermal, photonic, or collisional) to overcome bond energies and can proceed via homolytic (radical) or heterolytic (ionic) pathways."},{"id":"7e2374d5-2769-48f1-bd04-1d8f3a57e280","l":"Molecular dynamics","d":1,"p":"45a22032-e27f-4922-9d0c-10c4aa294db5","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Molecular dynamics is a computational simulation method that computes the time evolution of a many-particle system by numerically integrating the classical equations of motion for atoms or molecules, using forces derived from interatomic potentials or force fields. It yields microscopic trajectories used to predict structural, thermodynamic and dynamical properties, and can be extended to include quantum effects when needed."},{"id":"8e68401e-adc9-4b5f-b66d-5ae80024c90d","l":"Molecular evolution","d":1,"p":"6f618be6-815c-40bf-bd8a-cb8ffc96bfd5","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Molecular evolution is the change over time in the sequences and structures of biomolecules (DNA, RNA, and proteins) driven by mutation, natural selection, genetic drift, recombination, and other molecular processes. It is studied using population\u2011genetic and phylogenetic models to quantify rates, patterns, and functional consequences of sequence change."},{"id":"4f2351e6-16aa-42b0-9a6d-8673e1558679","l":"Molecular magnetism","d":1,"p":"b35238d0-83c7-4412-b293-c7d42fa92c62","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Molecular magnetism is the study of magnetic properties that originate within individual molecules and small molecular assemblies, arising from unpaired electron spins, spin\u2013orbit coupling, and exchange interactions among magnetic centers. It covers phenomena from simple paramagnetism and diamagnetism to cooperative ordering and single\u2011molecule magnet behavior, emphasizing how molecular structure controls magnetic anisotropy and spin relaxation."},{"id":"3f3d275f-3133-4b36-990a-59ca878fa208","l":"Molecular magnets","d":1,"p":"58071c68-730c-4a3e-982c-abc9f0cedf67","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Molecular magnets are materials whose fundamental magnetic units are discrete molecules or small molecular assemblies (e.g., transition\u2011metal or lanthanide complexes) that carry a stable or bistable magnetic moment. They can show magnetic hysteresis, slow relaxation of magnetization and quantum tunneling at the molecular scale, enabling magnetic behavior without a conventional extended magnetic lattice."},{"id":"d131918f-eedf-4f58-914a-6af82b44409e","l":"Molecular networks","d":1,"p":"b26070f8-32ca-4e02-94d0-ba1961b3480d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Molecular networks are assemblies in which molecules serve as nodes connected by covalent bonds or reversible/noncovalent interactions (edges) to form extended, often branched or cross-linked structures. Their topology and interaction strengths determine collective properties such as transport, mechanical response, dynamics, and self-assembly."},{"id":"7b1d5a31-bb4e-4b73-ae62-1c68e9c6006e","l":"Molecular solids","d":1,"p":"81194f70-bbca-4041-b57e-29338e588cac","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Molecular solids are solids whose lattice sites are occupied by neutral molecules held together by relatively weak intermolecular forces (van der Waals, dipole\u2013dipole, and hydrogen bonding). Because the constituent molecules interact weakly, these solids typically have low melting points, are mechanically soft, and are electrical insulators."},{"id":"2f9b8e36-83be-438b-83e8-0321500dfa28","l":"Molecular spectra","d":1,"p":"30e65d01-6cd5-442b-889f-ddc0c3c28157","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Molecular spectra are the characteristic patterns of electromagnetic radiation absorbed or emitted by molecules when they undergo quantized transitions between electronic, vibrational, and rotational energy levels. These patterns\u2014appearing as discrete lines or bands\u2014depend on molecular structure and selection rules and are used to identify species and determine properties like bond strengths, temperatures, and concentrations."},{"id":"42e66168-abfd-4328-aa9d-f6fc3a077f75","l":"Molecules","d":1,"p":"bd252d88-a608-4897-9416-daf84dee7f6b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Molecules are assemblies of two or more atoms held together by chemical bonds, forming a discrete bound system of nuclei and electrons described by molecular quantum mechanics. They possess quantized internal degrees of freedom (electronic, vibrational, rotational) that determine their spectroscopic, thermodynamic, and transport properties."},{"id":"eb9bd2e1-eedd-4bd0-997d-58b44ffa3ebb","l":"Monte Carlo methods","d":1,"p":"45a22032-e27f-4922-9d0c-10c4aa294db5","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics","Networks","Particles & Fields","Physics of Living Systems","Plasma Physics","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Monte Carlo methods are computational techniques that use repeated random sampling to estimate numerical quantities (integrals, averages, distributions, or solutions of complex problems), converting deterministic problems into statistical ones. The desired value is approximated from the sample statistics, with accuracy improving as the number of samples increases and variance-reduction strategies (e.g., importance sampling, Markov-chain sampling) are applied."},{"id":"df7eb531-d279-4bfc-ba84-d757e8526955","l":"Motor system","d":1,"p":"0a74688b-2eee-4349-a9bd-e93851fe2c5a","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"A motor system is an assembly of components\u2014an energy source, a transducer (actuator), and control elements\u2014that converts stored or supplied energy into directed mechanical work or motion; examples range from electric and robotic motors to biological systems of muscles, motor proteins, and neural controllers."},{"id":"8a966c56-0a58-4e8b-970e-aadbf8fbd108","l":"Mott insulators","d":1,"p":"29bd219f-a624-4ef5-b4c5-f9049f2be66e","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A Mott insulator is a material that band theory predicts should be metallic (typically because a band is partially filled) but instead is an insulator because strong electron\u2013electron (Coulomb) repulsion localizes electrons and opens an interaction-driven energy gap (a \"Mott gap\"). Such correlation-driven insulating states, often at or near half-filling, are commonly described by models like the Hubbard model and frequently show magnetic ordering."},{"id":"a715e20a-854e-4f9e-8b32-5cb4c2e1d89a","l":"M-theory","d":1,"p":"cbc41431-fba0-4ff0-8134-2ded6e598922","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"M-theory is a conjectured eleven-dimensional quantum framework that unifies the five consistent superstring theories and 11D supergravity by including higher-dimensional extended objects (notably M2- and M5-branes) and strong/weak dualities. It is believed to be the nonperturbative completion of string theory but is not yet fully formulated."},{"id":"35e2663d-6746-4003-965a-d8aa004753b8","l":"Multiband superconductivity","d":1,"p":"ca8ddaad-441c-481c-93b3-65f46125fb93","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Multiband superconductivity occurs when electrons in two or more distinct energy bands at the Fermi level form Cooper pairs, producing more than one superconducting gap. Interband coupling and possible phase differences between gaps modify critical temperatures, thermodynamic and transport properties, and can give rise to collective modes such as the Leggett mode."},{"id":"a6f53d1e-2623-4ed4-8b7d-cf70a51e246d","l":"Multicomponent & negative-ion plasma","d":1,"p":"22580d6a-8d20-4653-b2d1-a7de314bc162","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"A multicomponent plasma is an ionized gas whose charge and neutral population must be treated as several distinct species (e.g., electrons, multiple positive ion types, neutrals and/or dust), so that each species\u2019 density, temperature and flow affect the collective behavior. A negative-ion plasma specifically contains a nonnegligible population of negatively charged ions (formed by electron attachment or surface processes), which alters charge balance, screening lengths, transport, wave modes and sheath formation compared with a simple electron\u2013positive-ion plasma."},{"id":"d858d084-693b-468e-b437-3df9c9c7a560","l":"Multiphoton or tunneling ionization & excitation","d":1,"p":"ba7ccb8e-d7eb-4e6f-9c1e-b622f5b4e242","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Multiphoton ionization/excitation is a nonlinear light-matter process in which an atom or molecule absorbs two or more photons nearly simultaneously so that their combined energy promotes an electron to an excited bound state or into the continuum. Tunneling ionization/excitation occurs in very strong fields that deform the binding potential so an electron can quantum-mechanically tunnel through the barrier to a higher state or into the continuum; the Keldysh parameter (\u03b3) is commonly used to distinguish the multiphoton (\u03b3\u226b1) and tunneling (\u03b3\u226a1) regimes."},{"id":"aebdfaec-03e5-4237-a386-b634fc2b69de","l":"Multiple time scale dynamics","d":1,"p":"c485f44d-2cc4-4e6d-a20b-0899fb9e53a1","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Nonlinear Dynamics"],"def":"Multiple time scale dynamics describes systems in which different degrees of freedom or processes evolve on widely separated characteristic times (fast and slow), so their interaction produces phenomena such as slow manifolds, adiabatic invariants, and resonant or secular behavior. Such systems are analyzed with singular-perturbation, averaging, or multiple-scale methods to capture the long-term evolution of the slow variables while accounting for fast dynamics."},{"id":"b2cfd7a5-6ecd-4dd7-9ab1-150ab32fc687","l":"Multiquark bound states","d":1,"p":"0b08c33f-27b1-4bbf-90f9-1eb1bf63d1eb","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Multiquark bound states are color-singlet hadrons in quantum chromodynamics that contain more than the minimal quark content of ordinary mesons (quark\u2013antiquark) or baryons (three quarks), for example tetraquarks (qqq\u0304q\u0304), pentaquarks (qqqqq\u0304) or hexaquarks. They are held together by the strong interaction (gluons and confinement) and can appear as compact exotic configurations or as more loosely bound hadronic molecules."},{"id":"6558d12a-54c2-4ef7-8a5b-67ba06aaab9d","l":"Multiscale modeling","d":1,"p":"45a22032-e27f-4922-9d0c-10c4aa294db5","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Multiscale modeling is the construction and coupling of physical models that represent the same system at different spatial and/or temporal scales so that fine-scale mechanisms (e.g., atomic, molecular, microstructural) inform and determine coarse-grained, emergent behavior (e.g., continuum properties). It achieves this by transferring information between scales through techniques such as coarse-graining, homogenization, or concurrent coupling to produce predictive, scale-bridging simulations."},{"id":"e546a0d8-12a1-407d-9a97-153f61a487e5","l":"Muonic atoms & molecules","d":1,"p":"bd252d88-a608-4897-9416-daf84dee7f6b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Muonic atoms are atoms in which one or more electrons are replaced by a negatively charged muon (\u03bc\u2212), so that bound-state radii and energy levels are scaled by the muon\u2019s \u2248207\u00d7 greater mass, yielding much tighter orbits and greatly increased overlap with the nucleus useful for probing nuclear structure and testing QED. Muonic molecules are molecular complexes in which a muon plays the binding role (often replacing an electron), bringing nuclei unusually close together and enabling phenomena such as muon\u2011catalyzed fusion."},{"id":"680c1e46-6348-41dd-bb1e-e2fbe5675eb5","l":"Muonium","d":1,"p":"bd252d88-a608-4897-9416-daf84dee7f6b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Muonium is an exotic \"atom\" formed by a positive muon (\u03bc+) bound to an electron (e\u2212), analogous to hydrogen but with the proton replaced by a much lighter muon. It has Bohr-like energy levels and a short lifetime set by the muon (~2.2 \u03bcs), and is used for precision tests of QED and muon properties."},{"id":"45891beb-bda2-4d87-a0ab-b16654e52dbe","l":"Muons","d":1,"p":"a45e2665-2df6-42e0-99dc-30bd93812c2c","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Muons are second-generation charged leptons (\u03bc\u2212 and \u03bc+) \u2014 elementary spin-1/2 particles about 207 times heavier than the electron (mass \u2248105.66 MeV/c^2) that participate in electromagnetic and weak interactions but not the strong force. They are unstable with a mean lifetime of \u22482.2 \u03bcs, typically decaying to an electron and two neutrinos, and are commonly produced in cosmic-ray air showers and particle accelerators."},{"id":"ddccd292-4b0f-4372-9de3-7ac85a63c89a","l":"Muscle","d":1,"p":"fc65882e-b316-4cf1-a96a-ec8557624d80","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Muscle is a biological soft tissue that generates force and motion by converting chemical (ATP) and electrochemical energy into mechanical work via molecular motors (actin\u2013myosin) in sarcomeres. In physics and biomechanics it is modeled as an active, contractile, viscoelastic actuator with characteristic force\u2013length and force\u2013velocity behavior."},{"id":"041f6b58-cdfc-488c-aaf0-fc11558fda75","l":"Musical acoustics","d":1,"p":"40a5bd01-6544-4502-8321-458c33878bf3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Musical acoustics is the branch of acoustics that studies the physical production, propagation, and perception of musical sound. It covers vibrating sources and resonances in instruments, sound spectra (harmonics and overtones), pitch, timbre and loudness, and how rooms and the human ear shape musical tone."},{"id":"1deaff1d-2567-446c-9cf3-2f51c13d2409","l":"MXenes","d":1,"p":"7a86b9d8-3162-4279-9ca5-90ebad07be7c","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"MXenes are a family of two-dimensional materials made of early transition-metal carbides or nitrides (general formula Mn+1XnTx, with M = transition metal, X = C and/or N) produced by selectively etching the A layer from layered MAX-phase precursors; Tx denotes surface terminations (e.g., O, OH, F) that strongly influence their behavior. They combine high conductivity, large surface area and tunable composition/surface chemistry, making them useful for energy storage, catalysis and electronic applications."},{"id":"95440bc1-8f5e-4053-84d7-9d47f5bf7902","l":"Nanoantennas","d":1,"p":"4c4f4d87-e837-4d65-970d-f5323698a5f1","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Nanoantennas are nanoscale structures that couple localized electromagnetic fields to free-space radiation, acting as antennas at optical and near\u2011infrared frequencies. They typically use plasmonic or high\u2011index dielectric resonances to concentrate, enhance, and direct light at subwavelength scales for sensing, emission control, and nanophotonic integration."},{"id":"b527731c-278f-452a-a490-309b290cb528","l":"Nanobubbles","d":1,"p":"98cac094-ec9c-4f37-adf7-eed968f89359","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Nanobubbles are gas-filled cavities in a liquid with characteristic dimensions in the nanometer range (typically tens to a few hundred nanometers). Although Laplace pressure predicts rapid dissolution, many nanobubbles\u2014particularly those pinned at interfaces\u2014can be unexpectedly long-lived due to charge, contamination, gas oversaturation, or interfacial-structure stabilization."},{"id":"60c386aa-2681-4c99-be18-e4412729d107","l":"Nanoclusters","d":1,"p":"a38f8c2b-eff3-4e4d-8146-e17ef6bde9dd","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Nanoclusters are small aggregates of atoms (from a few up to thousands) with dimensions roughly in the 1\u2013100 nm range whose electronic, optical and chemical behavior differs from both single molecules and bulk solids due to quantum confinement and a large surface-to-volume ratio. They often exhibit discrete energy levels, strong size-dependent properties, and enhanced reactivity compared with the bulk."},{"id":"a5d90e16-ba22-43fb-b104-ffff32672032","l":"Nanocrystals","d":1,"p":"e521b9c7-1f62-48e4-a3fc-9428f629d078","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Nanocrystals are solid particles whose internal crystal lattice extends across at least one dimension in the nanometer range (typically ~1\u2013100 nm). Their small size and large surface-to-volume ratio give rise to size-dependent electronic, optical and mechanical properties\u2014e.g., quantum confinement in semiconductor nanocrystals (quantum dots)."},{"id":"ef3366fa-f2f2-4674-9cc2-3c30fb286a38","l":"Nanodisks","d":1,"p":"a38f8c2b-eff3-4e4d-8146-e17ef6bde9dd","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Nanodisks are nanoscale, disk-shaped objects\u2014typically tens to hundreds of nanometers in diameter and a few nanometers thick\u2014made from metals, semiconductors, or dielectrics. Their confined geometry produces size- and shape-dependent electronic, optical (e.g., plasmonic), or magnetic resonances, making them basic elements in nanophotonics, plasmonics, and spintronic devices."},{"id":"e547b306-ed29-4ac2-ace8-e04a1a845c81","l":"Nanofibers","d":1,"p":"a38f8c2b-eff3-4e4d-8146-e17ef6bde9dd","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Nanofibers are fibers with diameters on the nanometer scale\u2014typically a few to a few hundred nanometers\u2014whose extremely high surface-area-to-volume ratios give them size-dependent mechanical, electrical, optical, and transport properties distinct from bulk materials. They can be made from polymers, ceramics, or metals by methods such as electrospinning, self-assembly, or template synthesis and are used in filtration, sensing, tissue engineering, and nanoelectronics."},{"id":"4fd30a9d-7de5-44a0-82dd-d4b18d88721b","l":"Nanoparticles","d":1,"p":"8508c727-cef4-4fe3-a99a-64b7319d4e79","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Nanoparticles are particles with one or more dimensions in the nanometer range (roughly 1\u2013100 nm). They show size-dependent physical, chemical, and optical properties that differ from the bulk due to large surface-to-volume ratios and quantum-confinement effects."},{"id":"7a97482d-2279-423f-a808-431276be5d5b","l":"Nanoribbon","d":1,"p":"a38f8c2b-eff3-4e4d-8146-e17ef6bde9dd","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A nanoribbon is a quasi-one-dimensional strip of material with nanometer-scale width (often atomic-scale thickness) and much larger length, whose properties are dominated by quantum confinement and its atomic-scale edges. Because width and edge structure strongly affect electronic band structure and scattering, nanoribbons can exhibit discrete subbands, edge states, and width-dependent bandgaps (e.g., graphene nanoribbons)."},{"id":"52c3b50b-f97a-4c17-9f47-f9d59c728c80","l":"Nanorods","d":1,"p":"a38f8c2b-eff3-4e4d-8146-e17ef6bde9dd","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Nanorods are nanoscale, rod-shaped structures whose length exceeds their diameter (typical diameters ~1\u2013100 nm and lengths from tens to several hundred nanometers) and can be made from metals, semiconductors, or dielectrics. Their high aspect ratio produces anisotropic mechanical, electronic, and optical behavior\u2014e.g., polarization-dependent plasmon resonances and transverse quantum confinement\u2014useful in plasmonics, photonics, and nanoscale devices."},{"id":"8b79010b-e848-47f1-955a-8bbaa72c2de0","l":"Nanotechnology","d":1,"p":"a38f8c2b-eff3-4e4d-8146-e17ef6bde9dd","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Nanotechnology is the engineering and use of materials, devices, and systems by controlling matter at the nanometer scale (roughly 1\u2013100 nm), where quantum confinement, large surface-to-volume ratios, and other size-dependent effects produce novel physical, chemical, and biological properties. It encompasses the design, fabrication, characterization, and integration of nanoscale structures for applications in electronics, photonics, medicine, energy, and sensing."},{"id":"894b691f-ea17-4b23-8c9d-dae9518314a5","l":"Nanotubes","d":1,"p":"347c621b-2ddf-4d11-bd25-3380b5623c72","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Nanotubes are cylindrical nanostructures with diameters on the order of a nanometer and lengths that can span micrometers or more, formed by one or more concentric atomic layers (most famously carbon) rolled into seamless tubes. Their quasi-one-dimensional geometry produces exceptional mechanical strength and distinctive electronic and thermal properties, which depend sensitively on their composition and atomic structure (e.g., single- vs multi-walled, chirality)."},{"id":"a59454f2-d66b-4382-ae7d-2be19eb82af3","l":"Nanowires","d":1,"p":"347c621b-2ddf-4d11-bd25-3380b5623c72","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Nanowires are quasi-one-dimensional structures with diameters in the nanometer range and lengths up to micrometers, whose strong transverse confinement and large surface-to-volume ratio produce quantum- and surface-dominated electrical, optical, thermal, and mechanical properties distinct from the bulk. They are made from metals, semiconductors, or oxides and are used in nanoelectronics, sensors, photonics, and energy devices."},{"id":"a3ff37a7-c0a4-48a9-a32f-0dbc060c2746","l":"Navier-Stokes equation","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"The Navier\u2013Stokes equations are the fundamental momentum-balance partial differential equations for a viscous (typically Newtonian) fluid, relating the time evolution of the velocity field to pressure gradients, viscous (diffusive) stresses, advective inertia, and external body forces. Together with the continuity (mass-conservation) equation they form a nonlinear PDE system that governs fluid motion."},{"id":"368a208a-7a87-480d-b995-c872b34aac50","l":"Near-critical & underdense plasmas","d":1,"p":"22580d6a-8d20-4653-b2d1-a7de314bc162","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Near-critical and underdense plasmas are plasmas whose electron density n_e is, respectively, comparable to or smaller than the electromagnetic-wave critical density n_cr = \u03b50 m_e \u03c9^2/e^2 for a wave of angular frequency \u03c9. In an underdense plasma (n_e < n_cr) the wave can propagate (with refractive index \u221a(1\u2212\u03c9_p^2/\u03c9^2)), while near-critical densities (n_e \u2272 n_cr) produce strong dispersion, partial reflection, enhanced absorption and nonlinear collective effects that strongly influence wave\u2013plasma coupling."},{"id":"617c64e5-251e-4aa9-bc38-a419fd7a8522","l":"Near-field optics","d":1,"p":"23cdcd6b-ae22-4b6d-92cd-24a9a8bd0577","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Near-field optics studies light\u2013matter interactions occurring within roughly one wavelength of a source or surface, where evanescent (non\u2011propagating) field components dominate and far\u2011field approximations break down. It exploits these localized fields and strong confinement to achieve subwavelength imaging and control (e.g., near\u2011field scanning optical microscopy, surface\u2011plasmon coupling)."},{"id":"0c383605-4e3f-46fa-90d0-4b63324f4a84","l":"Nematic liquid crystals","d":1,"p":"63f7a3cf-31b7-4d5c-bbcc-58d679dd5139","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Nematic liquid crystals are a fluid phase in which anisotropic (typically rod-like) molecules exhibit long-range orientational order but no long-range positional order: on average the molecules align along a common axis called the director while their centers of mass remain disordered. They are described by a head\u2013tail symmetric director field (n \u2261 \u2212n) and an orientational order parameter S, giving rise to anisotropic optical, electric, and elastic properties and characteristic defects (disclinations)."},{"id":"46a7669c-cbb5-4333-bc3a-0ac87484b7f4","l":"Nervous system","d":1,"p":"0a74688b-2eee-4349-a9bd-e93851fe2c5a","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"The nervous system is the biological network of neurons and supporting cells that generates, transmits, and processes electrical and chemical signals (action potentials and synaptic transmission) to sense the environment, regulate internal states, and coordinate behavior. It is organized into the central nervous system (brain and spinal cord) and the peripheral nervous system (nerves and ganglia) to enable rapid long-range signaling and computation."},{"id":"32c21b1f-3011-4d4a-816c-b548ac037e73","l":"Network diffusion","d":1,"p":"f23d87e8-fae8-49d5-9be0-45ccf8ac97f9","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Network diffusion is the process by which a physical quantity, information, or a dynamical state spreads among the nodes of a network along its edges. It is typically modeled by diffusion equations or random walks on the graph and is governed by the graph Laplacian, whose spectrum determines the characteristic rates and spatial patterns of spreading."},{"id":"5406bb1e-ef35-4289-9429-1d553f918344","l":"Network flow","d":1,"p":"f23d87e8-fae8-49d5-9be0-45ccf8ac97f9","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Network flow describes the distribution of a conserved quantity through the edges of a graph, where each edge has a capacity and flows at nodes satisfy conservation except at designated sources and sinks. It is a mathematical framework used to model and optimize transport processes (e.g., fluids, currents, traffic), with central results such as the max-flow/min-cut theorem."},{"id":"720fbd6c-ad91-46b1-9958-296aa626f983","l":"Network flow optimization","d":1,"p":"31408bf9-9618-464c-ae16-cedacfc7a9eb","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Network flow optimization studies how to route a conserved quantity (e.g., mass, current, data, or traffic) through a graph so as to optimize an objective (such as maximize throughput or minimize transport cost) while satisfying node conservation and arc capacity constraints. It is formalized with graph-theoretic and linear-programming methods (e.g., max-flow/min-cut, minimum-cost flow, network simplex) and applies to fluids, electricity, transport, and supply networks."},{"id":"6f3f5cf1-363e-4d04-ac63-ca4ae90b47fc","l":"Network Models","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Network models are mathematical representations of complex systems that encode components as nodes and their interactions as edges, capturing the system's topology and interaction strengths. They provide a framework for studying how structural features (degree distribution, clustering, modularity) and edge properties (weights, directionality, temporal or multilayer structure) govern collective phenomena such as transport, synchronization, spreading, and robustness."},{"id":"3a7bb933-1b5a-40aa-95fe-cf28063cff16","l":"Network navigation","d":1,"p":"b29359c1-cac2-4880-a9dc-f957b13a3e72","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Network navigation is the study of how agents, information, or signals find and traverse routes between nodes in a network, using local or global information to choose paths. It focuses on algorithms and dynamics (e.g., greedy routing, search protocols) and on metrics of performance\u2014efficiency, success probability, stretch and robustness\u2014as determined by the network's topology and geometry."},{"id":"d45f0099-ce2a-4de2-a7b2-0ff5d44d8c07","l":"Network resilience","d":1,"p":"f6b0d9b5-f1f6-42b0-89df-1b70c8ec3e8b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Network resilience is the capacity of a networked system to withstand, absorb, and recover from component failures, attacks, or external perturbations while preserving its essential functions. It is characterized by how topology, redundancy, dynamics, and adaptive responses affect metrics like connectivity, throughput, cascade size, and recovery time."},{"id":"7b9db112-8475-42b4-ad22-f00f6b8635f8","l":"Networks Analysis Tools","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Analytical and computational methods for representing, quantifying, and modeling complex systems as graphs, used to extract structural and dynamical properties of networks. These tools include graph-theoretic measures (degree, centrality, clustering), spectral and statistical techniques, community detection, percolation and diffusion models, and algorithms for inference, visualization, and simulation."},{"id":"ce1c6c0a-58ee-41a7-a3b2-83d268ffbda6","l":"Neural basis of learning & memory","d":1,"p":"376c5d7d-24b3-40a2-a82d-a517ac25dbad","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"The neural basis of learning and memory is the set of cellular, synaptic, and circuit mechanisms by which the brain encodes, stores, and retrieves information\u2014principally by activity-dependent changes in synaptic strength and connectivity (e.g., long\u2011term potentiation and depression) and by altered patterns of neuronal activity. These changes are consolidated by structural remodeling, intracellular signaling and gene expression to produce persistent memory traces or engrams across timescales from seconds to years."},{"id":"2fc1722f-3725-4f4b-8c0c-4c05c97204b2","l":"Neural mechanisms of decision making","d":1,"p":"376c5d7d-24b3-40a2-a82d-a517ac25dbad","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Neural mechanisms of decision making are the brain circuits and computations that select actions by integrating sensory evidence, prior knowledge, and reward signals, often modeled as noisy dynamical systems that accumulate information until a threshold triggers a choice. These mechanisms are studied experimentally and mathematically using concepts like stochastic accumulation (drift-diffusion), attractor networks, synaptic plasticity, and reinforcement learning to link neural dynamics to observed behavior."},{"id":"e0fd4bc2-ece7-410f-a393-59c646a5cef5","l":"Neural network simulations","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Neural network simulations are computational experiments in which artificial neural networks are trained and executed to model, predict, or approximate the behavior of physical systems (as surrogates, solvers, or data-driven models) or to emulate and study the dynamics of neural-network models themselves. They are used to accelerate numerical calculations, infer parameters or latent variables from data, and discover or encode governing relationships in physics problems."},{"id":"422ebc8c-e0eb-4e3c-ba60-bf7becf9371d","l":"Neuroimaging","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Neuroimaging is the collection of measurement and imaging techniques that map the structure and function of the nervous system by detecting physical signals produced by neural tissue and tracers. Common modalities exploit different physical principles\u2014X\u2011rays/attenuation (CT), nuclear magnetic resonance (MRI/fMRI), positron emission and gamma detection (PET/SPECT), and electric/magnetic fields from neuronal activity (EEG/MEG)."},{"id":"1cef0413-aa47-4526-8ef8-785465baa968","l":"Neuronal dynamics","d":1,"p":"27c445f4-a41d-4b4c-881f-aa74fc493d6d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nonlinear Dynamics"],"def":"Neuronal dynamics is the study of the time-dependent electrical and chemical activity of neurons and neural networks \u2014 how membrane potentials, ion-channel kinetics, action potentials (spikes), and synaptic currents evolve and interact. It uses dynamical-systems and statistical-physics models (e.g., Hodgkin\u2013Huxley, integrate-and-fire) to explain single-cell excitability and collective behaviors such as oscillations, synchronization, and wave propagation."},{"id":"205e6918-12a2-4f4c-9795-8f723f476866","l":"Neuronal network activity","d":1,"p":"8b374c75-8448-476e-a872-f15b0cf5a5fe","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Neuronal network activity is the collective spatiotemporal pattern of electrical and chemical signaling produced by interconnected neurons, manifested as sequences of action potentials, synaptic currents, and extracellular field potentials. These dynamics\u2014including oscillations, waves, synchronization, and transient firing patterns\u2014underlie information processing, learning, and behavior in nervous systems."},{"id":"db3d74af-cef8-4eeb-a6da-910a42929da6","l":"Neutrinos","d":1,"p":"a45e2665-2df6-42e0-99dc-30bd93812c2c","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Neutrinos are electrically neutral, nearly massless elementary fermions in the lepton family that interact only via the weak force and gravity; they come in three flavors (electron, muon, tau) and can change flavor through quantum oscillations. They are produced in processes like beta decay, nuclear reactions in the Sun and supernovae, and particle accelerators, and their tiny masses and oscillations reveal physics beyond the simplest Standard Model."},{"id":"756837f2-dbd1-49c3-afb6-ad3560f2c49b","l":"Neutron decay time","d":1,"p":"1cf75de2-e14b-41cd-b733-8f704652db33","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Neutron decay time is the average lifetime of a free neutron before it undergoes beta decay into a proton, electron and antineutrino. The mean lifetime is about 880 seconds (corresponding to a half-life \u2248 10.2 minutes); neutrons bound in nuclei can be stable or have different decay times."},{"id":"14fede99-f5aa-4e3e-94be-167116d8c322","l":"Neutron physics","d":1,"p":"8ad76784-af63-4e0f-a57c-e8483afce6c5","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Neutron physics is the branch of physics that studies the neutron \u2014 its intrinsic properties (mass, spin, magnetic moment, lifetime), internal structure, and fundamental interactions such as beta decay and scattering with nuclei. It also covers neutron production and transport and the use of neutrons as probes and agents in applications like nuclear reactors, neutron scattering, and tests of fundamental symmetries."},{"id":"389efcc8-e090-497e-b414-8c90e6a69cd7","l":"Neutron skin thickness","d":1,"p":"1cf75de2-e14b-41cd-b733-8f704652db33","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Neutron skin thickness is the difference between the root-mean-square radii of the neutron and proton distributions in a nucleus, \u0394R = R_n \u2212 R_p, quantifying how much neutrons extend beyond protons at the nuclear surface. It measures the neutron-rich surface layer and is sensitive to the density dependence of the nuclear symmetry energy and related astrophysical properties."},{"id":"3986eac4-6a3f-45f6-8377-8dc19ff2c541","l":"Neutron star cooling","d":1,"p":"b64db5c0-c54c-4fe6-a142-d089ceb55b3f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Neutron star cooling is the time-dependent loss of a neutron star\u2019s thermal energy, dominated at early times by neutrino emission from dense-core weak processes (e.g., direct and modified Urca, Cooper-pair breaking) and at later times by photon emission from the surface. The cooling rate and thermal evolution depend sensitively on interior microphysics \u2014 equation of state, composition, superfluidity, thermal conductivity, envelope composition, and magnetic fields \u2014 so observed temperatures constrain dense-matter physics."},{"id":"c17001d7-c0bd-41d6-b970-ea4269e7ec06","l":"Neutron stars & pulsars","d":1,"p":"b6d90b06-7836-486e-8e77-221ad2624e1e","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Neutron stars are the compact (~1\u20132 solar mass, ~10 km radius) remnants of massive stars that collapsed in supernovae, with interior densities near nuclear density and pressure support from neutron degeneracy and strong nuclear forces. Pulsars are rapidly rotating, strongly magnetized neutron stars whose lighthouse-like beams of radio and higher-frequency emission from their magnetic poles sweep past Earth as very regular pulses."},{"id":"fd8cf2dd-2362-4994-a45c-a9b453fe508d","l":"Neutron techniques","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Neutron techniques are experimental methods that use beams of neutrons\u2014in scattering, diffraction, spectroscopy, imaging and activation experiments\u2014to probe the structure, dynamics and composition of materials. Because neutrons are electrically neutral, deeply penetrating, isotope-sensitive and carry a magnetic moment, they are uniquely suited to reveal atomic positions, light elements, isotopic contrasts and magnetic order and excitations across a wide range of length- and time-scales."},{"id":"8a933584-7dcb-4e59-a210-5db79ca404eb","l":"Node-line semimetals","d":1,"p":"0026b3bc-1a2f-45df-8025-1243dac91047","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Node-line semimetals are topological semimetals in which the conduction and valence bands touch along one-dimensional lines or loops in momentum space rather than at isolated points, yielding band degeneracies extended in k\u2011space. These line nodes are typically protected by crystalline or combined symmetries and produce characteristic low-energy behavior and surface \"drumhead\" states due to linear dispersion perpendicular to the nodal lines."},{"id":"38e21fa2-d4a0-4206-a623-ab3eb9ba82a8","l":"Noise","d":1,"p":"c6d9db47-7b2d-4d3b-868a-606b65695e3c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Noise is the random or unwanted fluctuations in a physical quantity or signal that obscure or distort the information of interest. It arises from thermal motion, discrete charge or photon events, quantum uncertainty, and external disturbances, and is characterized statistically (e.g., by its power spectral density) and compared to the signal by the signal-to-noise ratio."},{"id":"d339ab27-1f68-44cc-a855-690e5f192c17","l":"Noise control","d":1,"p":"40a5bd01-6544-4502-8321-458c33878bf3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Noise control is the science and engineering of reducing unwanted sound and vibration by modifying the source, the transmission path, or the receiver to lower sound levels and mitigate adverse effects. Techniques include passive measures (absorption, damping, isolation) and active methods (phase\u2011cancellation, feedback/feedforward control), together with measurement and standards for evaluation."},{"id":"775f40ec-0ad3-4379-bca0-5890bc96c932","l":"Noise-induced transitions","d":1,"p":"c6d9db47-7b2d-4d3b-868a-606b65695e3c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Noise-induced transitions are changes in a system's macroscopic state or qualitative behavior driven by random fluctuations rather than by deterministic parameter changes. Examples include noise-driven switching between metastable states (activation over barriers, Kramers escape) and noise-induced ordering or phase transitions in which stochasticity alters stability and the steady-state probability distribution."},{"id":"b1fdd414-792d-43b4-a94b-79fc9c655f04","l":"Noncollinear magnets","d":1,"p":"58071c68-730c-4a3e-982c-abc9f0cedf67","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Noncollinear magnets are magnetic materials whose ordered magnetic moments (spins) on different sites point in different directions, so the moments are not all parallel or antiparallel to a single axis. Such noncollinear spin structures\u2014e.g., spirals, helices, canted or 120\u00b0 arrangements\u2014typically arise from competing exchange interactions, geometric frustration, or antisymmetric Dzyaloshinskii\u2013Moriya interactions."},{"id":"dcf3805a-edb6-475a-b1b9-b826c3eb7481","l":"Noncommutative field theories","d":1,"p":"ee82ad04-6c47-46a4-a70d-c9a7d7989f6a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Noncommutative field theories are quantum field theories defined on a spacetime whose coordinate operators fail to commute\u2014typically [x^\u03bc,x^\u03bd]=i \u03b8^{\u03bc\u03bd}\u2014so ordinary pointwise multiplication of fields is replaced by a nonlocal star (Moyal) product. This deformation of locality and symmetry leads to modified interaction rules and phenomena like UV/IR mixing and naturally arises in contexts such as string theory with background B-fields and in models of quantum spacetime."},{"id":"7c31fb13-2185-47b2-bc3d-7efe4061ddb9","l":"Noncontinuum effects","d":1,"p":"6f991c28-112d-4bdf-9c2c-50cec44f48fe","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Noncontinuum effects are departures from continuum behavior that occur when a system\u2019s characteristic length or time becomes comparable to microscopic scales (molecular mean free path, interatomic spacing, or carrier wavelength), so smooth fields and local constitutive laws (e.g., Navier\u2013Stokes, Fourier) break down. They are quantified by dimensionless measures like the Knudsen number and require kinetic or discrete-particle descriptions\u2014examples include rarefied-gas slip/free-molecular flow and ballistic phonon or electron transport."},{"id":"3c88d04a-17da-443c-8428-781247d46a8a","l":"Nonequilibrium lattice models","d":1,"p":"8bf49057-d087-4218-b1ad-0678d26c2751","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Nonequilibrium lattice models are discrete-space models in which degrees of freedom (spins, particles, heights, etc.) reside on lattice sites and evolve under stochastic or deterministic rules that violate detailed balance or are externally driven, producing steady states with sustained currents, entropy production, and behaviors not described by equilibrium statistical mechanics. Examples include driven lattice gases, exclusion processes, the contact process, and sandpile/avalanche models, which exhibit nonequilibrium phase transitions and universal scaling laws."},{"id":"6ea23702-ddf3-44e8-b35b-c1f1345c10aa","l":"Nonlinear acoustics","d":1,"p":"40a5bd01-6544-4502-8321-458c33878bf3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Nonlinear acoustics is the study of sound propagation when wave amplitudes are large enough that nonlinear terms in the medium's equations of motion and constitutive relations significantly affect the behavior. These nonlinearities cause amplitude-dependent sound speed, waveform distortion and harmonic generation, and give rise to effects such as shock formation, parametric arrays, acoustic streaming, and cavitation."},{"id":"bb27c0c8-04af-471e-8122-24961354a177","l":"Nonlinear beam dynamics","d":1,"p":"cd322dee-17c3-4f48-81b4-5fbca9f2c432","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Nonlinear beam dynamics is the study of charged-particle beam motion and stability in accelerators when the forces and interactions contain nonlinear terms so that linear approximations fail. It analyzes consequences such as amplitude-dependent tune shifts, resonances, chaotic trajectories, emittance growth and limits on dynamic aperture arising from sextupoles/octupoles, space charge, wakefields and beam\u2013beam interactions."},{"id":"bd03ea46-2406-4a24-8307-05b2b498fb4e","l":"Nonlinear optics","d":1,"p":"23cdcd6b-ae22-4b6d-92cd-24a9a8bd0577","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Nonlinear optics is the branch of optics that studies light\u2013matter interactions in which the material polarization responds nonlinearly to the applied electromagnetic field (often described by higher-order susceptibilities \u03c7^(2), \u03c7^(3), \u2026). This nonlinear response, typically observed at high light intensities, produces effects such as harmonic generation, frequency mixing and parametric amplification, intensity-dependent refractive index (Kerr effect), self-focusing, and optical solitons."},{"id":"63fb114b-dd1d-4d61-896b-3a58926c168b","l":"Nonlinear time-delay systems","d":1,"p":"c485f44d-2cc4-4e6d-a20b-0899fb9e53a1","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Nonlinear Dynamics"],"def":"Nonlinear time-delay systems are dynamical systems whose evolution depends on nonlinear functions of their past states at one or more finite time delays, typically modeled by delay differential equations. The explicit dependence on delayed variables gives them an effectively infinite-dimensional state space and can produce rich behaviors\u2014oscillations, multistability, and chaos\u2014relevant in control, optics, and biological feedback processes."},{"id":"13567a42-9280-4cdd-bef0-e84a90020e2b","l":"Nonneutral plasmas","d":1,"p":"22580d6a-8d20-4653-b2d1-a7de314bc162","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Nonneutral plasmas are ionized gases in which one sign of charge strongly dominates so the net charge density is appreciable and quasi-neutrality is violated, making long-range Coulomb fields and space\u2011charge effects central to their behavior. They are typically realized as pure electron or ion clouds confined by external electromagnetic fields (e.g., in Penning traps), with equilibrium set by a balance of rotation, confinement fields, and space charge."},{"id":"4c71fe1e-5232-4a04-8170-51673a93b27c","l":"Nonperturbative effects in field theory","d":1,"p":"ee82ad04-6c47-46a4-a70d-c9a7d7989f6a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Nonperturbative effects in field theory are contributions to observables that cannot be obtained from a power\u2011series expansion in a small coupling (i.e., from perturbation theory) and therefore require different methods. They arise from strong coupling or topologically nontrivial field configurations\u2014examples include solitons, instantons, confinement and dynamical mass gaps\u2014and typically scale nonanalytically in the coupling (e.g., \u223ce^{-const/g^2})."},{"id":"7fd2a423-32c8-433f-8425-ff2941c9c247","l":"Nonperturbative methods","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Nonperturbative methods are analytical or numerical techniques for solving physical problems without assuming a small expansion parameter, i.e., not based on perturbation theory. They are used to capture phenomena\u2014such as bound states, solitons and instantons, confinement, tunneling, and phase transitions\u2014that perturbative expansions cannot reliably describe."},{"id":"7ce654be-4660-49bf-9a82-ccf25e18f73d","l":"Non-reciprocal propagation","d":1,"p":"23cdcd6b-ae22-4b6d-92cd-24a9a8bd0577","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Non-reciprocal propagation is directional asymmetry in the transmission of waves or signals through a medium or device, meaning the transmission from point A to B differs from the transmission from B to A (e.g., S_AB \u2260 S_BA). It requires breaking time\u2011reversal symmetry or the conditions of the reciprocity theorem\u2014achieved by magnetic biasing, time modulation, or nonlinearities\u2014and is the basis for isolators and circulators."},{"id":"075f4f46-08d8-483f-a01e-5124e1a928e0","l":"Non-reciprocal transmission","d":1,"p":"23cdcd6b-ae22-4b6d-92cd-24a9a8bd0577","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Non-reciprocal transmission is the property of a system or device where waves or signals transmit differently depending on direction, so the transmission from port A to B is not equal to that from B to A. Achieving non-reciprocity requires breaking reciprocity/time-reversal symmetry (for example with magnetic bias, spatiotemporal modulation, or certain nonlinear or active media) and is used in isolators and circulators."},{"id":"a84969d7-a3f9-43b9-8746-d6b4e77576ff","l":"Nonrelativistic wave equations","d":1,"p":"01f70d47-cbac-4b5a-8ff9-0768e722511b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Nonrelativistic wave equations are partial differential equations that describe wave-like behavior of fields or particles in the regime where velocities are much smaller than the speed of light and relativistic effects can be neglected. They include Schr\u00f6dinger-type equations (linear and nonlinear, e.g. Schr\u00f6dinger, Pauli, Gross\u2013Pitaevskii) and are typically Galilean\u2011invariant rather than Lorentz\u2011invariant, omitting relativistic corrections such as time dilation, spin\u2013orbit coupling, and particle\u2013antiparticle creation."},{"id":"022c3abb-9915-43bc-b7d9-0fe95ecf3d55","l":"Normal galaxies","d":1,"p":"a2f3cef9-614c-4f7a-8f5b-009885b53abb","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Normal galaxies are galaxies whose total electromagnetic output is dominated by starlight, gas and dust (the normal interstellar medium) rather than by a luminous active galactic nucleus (AGN) or by an extreme starburst. They include ordinary spirals, ellipticals and irregulars, exhibiting typical star-formation rates and spectra dominated by stellar absorption features and thermal dust emission."},{"id":"0f2ae25e-a9ef-412f-ac42-2158123660ca","l":"Normal stars","d":1,"p":"b6d90b06-7836-486e-8e77-221ad2624e1e","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Normal stars are self-gravitating gaseous spheres in hydrostatic and thermal equilibrium that produce most of their luminosity by sustained nuclear fusion (primarily hydrogen burning) in their cores or shells. The term excludes compact degenerate remnants (white dwarfs, neutron stars, black holes) and substellar objects that cannot sustain steady fusion."},{"id":"ec69b3ef-eaca-44df-ab99-464871cd24f8","l":"Novae & supernovae","d":1,"p":"b64db5c0-c54c-4fe6-a142-d089ceb55b3f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Novae are sudden brightenings produced when a white dwarf in a close binary accretes enough hydrogen from its companion to trigger a thermonuclear runaway on its surface, ejecting a shell but leaving the white dwarf intact. Supernovae are catastrophic stellar explosions that either result from core collapse of a massive star or from thermonuclear disruption of a white dwarf, releasing enormous energy, synthesizing heavy elements, and leaving a compact remnant (neutron star or black hole) or no remnant."},{"id":"929c7207-7174-4a78-b4f8-d26c87110620","l":"NP-hard problems","d":1,"p":"b96ba7a9-cce1-470c-b80c-6dadb2c6b8e9","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"NP-hard problems are problems at least as difficult as the hardest problems in NP: formally, every problem in NP can be transformed (via a polynomial-time reduction) into an NP-hard problem. They need not be decision problems or lie in NP (and could even be undecidable), and if any NP-hard problem that lies in NP were solvable in polynomial time it would imply P = NP."},{"id":"531d572f-5343-4cf1-910a-3657ba30aa75","l":"Nuclear binding","d":1,"p":"1cf75de2-e14b-41cd-b733-8f704652db33","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Nuclear binding is the net attractive interaction that holds protons and neutrons together in an atomic nucleus, primarily arising from the residual strong (nuclear) force which overcomes their electrostatic repulsion. The associated nuclear binding energy is the energy required to separate the nucleus into individual nucleons, equal to the mass defect times c^2."},{"id":"f837d92b-2b47-45d3-8803-a555715c1b78","l":"Nuclear charge distribution","d":1,"p":"1cf75de2-e14b-41cd-b733-8f704652db33","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"The nuclear charge distribution is the spatial arrangement of positive electric charge inside an atomic nucleus, given by a charge density \u03c1_charge(r) (or a radial profile for spherical nuclei) that reflects the locations of the protons. It is probed by electromagnetic interactions (e.g., electron scattering, muonic atoms) and characterized by moments such as the root-mean-square charge radius and multipole moments."},{"id":"cd1858f1-89e4-4b0e-864a-b5a4a73de5a8","l":"Nuclear data analysis & compilation","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"The systematic collection, evaluation, validation and organization of experimental and theoretical nuclear physics quantities (e.g., decay properties, level schemes, reaction and fission cross sections, and reaction rates) into consistent, uncertainty-characterized datasets. Its goal is to produce recommended, standardized nuclear data and covariance information for research and applications (reactors, medical isotopes, astrophysics), typically maintained in evaluated libraries such as ENSDF, EXFOR and ENDF."},{"id":"f3901c9b-bf9c-44a0-8c9e-b9c2b0b89339","l":"Nuclear density functional theory","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Nuclear density functional theory is a self-consistent many-body framework that models an atomic nucleus by expressing its total energy as a functional of local proton and neutron densities and currents, enabling calculation of ground-state energies, radii, shapes and collective excitations across the nuclear chart. In practice, because the nuclear interaction is strong and short-ranged, NDFT uses phenomenological energy density functionals (e.g., Skyrme, Gogny, covariant) and solves mean-field (Hartree\u2013Fock or Hartree\u2013Fock\u2013Bogoliubov) equations, often augmented by pairing, symmetry restoration, and beyond-mean-field correlations."},{"id":"5e960526-d9f1-401b-90f6-d23adb05a997","l":"Nuclear & electron resonance","d":1,"p":"ba7ccb8e-d7eb-4e6f-9c1e-b622f5b4e242","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Nuclear resonance (NMR) is the resonant absorption and emission of radio\u2011frequency radiation by nuclear magnetic moments placed in a static magnetic field when the radiation matches the nuclei's Larmor precession frequency, driving transitions between nuclear spin states.  \nElectron resonance (EPR/ESR) is the analogous process for unpaired electron magnetic moments, using microwave radiation in a magnetic field to induce transitions between electronic spin states and probe local magnetic environments."},{"id":"5174619b-f9e9-495a-a296-83a14ffd3087","l":"Nuclear explosions","d":1,"p":"8ad76784-af63-4e0f-a57c-e8483afce6c5","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"A nuclear explosion is the extremely rapid, uncontrolled release of nuclear binding energy by a self-sustaining fission chain reaction, a fusion reaction, or a staged combination of both. It produces an intense burst of thermal and ionizing radiation, prompt high-energy neutrons and gamma rays, a high-pressure shock (blast) wave, and radioactive fallout from activated and dispersed material."},{"id":"72fd2bae-0362-40c5-91a3-36083a6477ba","l":"Nuclear forces","d":1,"p":"1cf75de2-e14b-41cd-b733-8f704652db33","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Nuclear forces are the interactions that bind protons and neutrons into atomic nuclei, arising as the residual strong interaction between color\u2011neutral nucleons (ultimately due to quark\u2013gluon dynamics and effectively mediated by meson exchange). They are short\u2011range (\u223c1\u20133 fm), predominantly attractive at intermediate distances to hold the nucleus together but strongly repulsive at very short separations, and depend on spin, isospin and include a noncentral (tensor) component."},{"id":"a1ea7e3f-96ef-45c6-9f78-8fc5f688f044","l":"Nuclear fragmentation","d":1,"p":"bbb0ce0d-36da-4da5-a443-df064f36aa17","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Nuclear fragmentation is the process in which an atomic nucleus breaks up into two or more smaller nuclei, nucleons, or light clusters, typically as a result of high-energy collisions or strong excitation. It ranges from spallation (loss of a few nucleons) to multifragmentation (simultaneous breakup into many fragments)."},{"id":"bade400c-827b-4a33-87b2-695354930d18","l":"Nuclear many-body theory","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Nuclear many-body theory is the quantum-mechanical framework and set of methods used to describe atomic nuclei as systems of strongly interacting protons and neutrons, including their correlations and collective behavior. It aims to predict nuclear structure and reactions from realistic nuclear forces using approaches such as ab initio calculations, mean-field and shell models, Green\u2019s functions, coupled-cluster theory, and density functional theory."},{"id":"63a9c708-1efc-49bb-9b67-fb6a39269235","l":"Nuclear matter","d":1,"p":"1cf75de2-e14b-41cd-b733-8f704652db33","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Nuclear matter is an idealized infinite, homogeneous system of interacting nucleons (protons and neutrons) in which Coulomb and surface effects are neglected so that only the strong interaction governs the dynamics. It is a theoretical model\u2014with variants like symmetric nuclear matter and pure neutron matter\u2014used to study bulk properties (equilibrium density, energy per particle, equation of state) relevant to nuclei and neutron stars."},{"id":"a71bd8c0-903c-4991-9877-3a8eaf171967","l":"Nuclear matter in neutron stars","d":1,"p":"38c3eaa1-e9a7-4590-9a93-cbdfd9fe162f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Nuclear matter in neutron stars is the extremely dense, strongly interacting material in their interiors, composed mainly of a degenerate Fermi sea of neutrons with a smaller fraction of protons, electrons (and muons), whose properties at densities from nuclear saturation to several times that are shaped by nuclear forces, superfluidity, and possible exotic components (hyperons, meson condensates, or deconfined quarks). Its equation of state \u2014 the pressure\u2013density relation set by these microphysics \u2014 determines the star\u2019s mass\u2013radius relation, stability, and observable behavior such as cooling and glitches."},{"id":"b10d176e-09d6-4bcb-bd82-bb884d5964df","l":"Nuclear physics of explosive environments","d":1,"p":"38c3eaa1-e9a7-4590-9a93-cbdfd9fe162f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"The study of nuclear reactions, properties, and weak/neutrino interactions occurring in extreme, rapidly changing conditions (high temperature, density, and neutron richness) found in explosive events\u2014such as supernovae, neutron-star mergers, novae, X\u2011ray bursts, and man-made detonations\u2014which control energy release, nucleosynthesis, and the dynamical evolution of the explosion. It encompasses measurement and modeling of reaction and decay rates, nuclear statistical equilibrium, photodisintegration, fission, and the equation of state under transient, out-of-equilibrium conditions."},{"id":"8c48a332-f38c-4fde-affa-82f036e2aa25","l":"Nuclear power","d":1,"p":"af9f5a05-6917-40cf-a2ff-5520ff506935","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Nuclear power is the generation of usable energy\u2014usually electricity\u2014by converting the nuclear binding energy released in controlled nuclear reactions (most commonly the fission of heavy nuclei) into thermal energy, which is used to produce steam that drives turbines. It also broadly includes prospective fusion-based systems that would similarly convert fusion energy into heat and electricity, with reactor design and shielding used to control reactions and radiation."},{"id":"2b534c54-ace7-425b-8282-798d56312fc2","l":"Nuclear radii","d":1,"p":"1cf75de2-e14b-41cd-b733-8f704652db33","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Nuclear radii quantify the spatial extent of a nucleus, usually given as the root-mean-square (rms) charge radius (proton distribution) or the matter radius (all nucleons), and are typically a few femtometers (10^-15 m). To leading order they scale with mass number A as R \u2248 R0 A^(1/3) with R0 \u2248 1.2 fm, though precise values depend on shell structure, deformation and neutron skins."},{"id":"dfad8fb2-ba51-4845-ac3d-8a4f1065044b","l":"Nuclear reaction rates","d":1,"p":"bbb0ce0d-36da-4da5-a443-df064f36aa17","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"The nuclear reaction rate is the number of nuclear reactions occurring per unit time per unit volume, determined by the reactant number densities and the probability of reaction when particles meet. Quantitatively, for two species it is R = n1 n2 <\u03c3v>/(1+\u03b412) (or for a beam R = \u03a6 n_target \u03c3), where \u03c3 is the reaction cross section, v the relative velocity, and <\u03c3v> denotes the velocity average (which introduces the temperature dependence in thermal plasmas)."},{"id":"d9e32a6d-d42e-430a-9665-10aaf0776e26","l":"Nuclear reactors","d":1,"p":"8ad76784-af63-4e0f-a57c-e8483afce6c5","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Nuclear reactors are engineered systems that sustain and control a chain fission reaction in fissile material to convert nuclear binding energy into heat, which is typically used to generate electricity. They regulate neutron population and heat removal using components such as moderators, control rods, coolant, and biological shielding to ensure safe, steady operation."},{"id":"8692716f-f11a-403b-ac30-f80a2c9c9651","l":"Nuclear shapes and moments","d":1,"p":"1cf75de2-e14b-41cd-b733-8f704652db33","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Nuclear shapes describe the spatial distribution of a nucleus's mass and charge, which can be spherical or deformed (e.g., prolate, oblate, octupole/pear-shaped) and are parameterized by multipole deformation coefficients. Nuclear moments are the expectation values of multipole operators\u2014most commonly the electric quadrupole and magnetic dipole moments\u2014that quantify the magnitude and orientation of those shape and current distributions and are observable via electromagnetic probes."},{"id":"aff93223-66f4-4a9a-8285-2bd2b8a714a9","l":"Nuclear spin & parity","d":1,"p":"1cf75de2-e14b-41cd-b733-8f704652db33","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Nuclear spin is the total intrinsic angular momentum of a nucleus, given by the quantized vector sum of individual nucleon spins and orbital angular momenta and labeled by the quantum number I (or J), which can be integer or half-integer. Nuclear parity is the eigenvalue \u00b11 of the spatial inversion operator (r \u2192 \u2212r) acting on the nuclear wavefunction \u2014 \"even\" (+) or \"odd\" (\u2212) \u2014 determined by the orbital angular momenta of the constituent nucleons and governing selection rules for transitions."},{"id":"323d8bba-d268-41a6-b135-ffec787e4244","l":"Nuclear transition rates","d":1,"p":"bbb0ce0d-36da-4da5-a443-df064f36aa17","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Nuclear transition rates are the probabilities per unit time that a nucleus in one energy state will change to another (by \u03b3 emission, internal conversion, \u03b2 or particle emission, etc.), with each decay channel having a partial rate and the sum giving the total rate. They equal the inverse of the mean lifetime and, in theory, are computed from the square of the transition matrix element and the density of final states (Fermi's golden rule)."},{"id":"521744c9-c58a-45e8-a39a-c85bfc40ea69","l":"Nucleation","d":1,"p":"f2a9db78-1651-4a94-b66e-3e3d72056760","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Nucleation is the initial process by which a new phase or structure (e.g., a crystal, droplet, or bubble) appears within a metastable parent phase through formation of small clusters or \"nuclei\" that must reach a critical size to become stable and grow. It is controlled by a competition between bulk free-energy gain and interfacial (surface) energy, creating an activation barrier that can be reduced by defects or surfaces (heterogeneous nucleation)."},{"id":"3c1c2bbb-a966-456c-aedf-203158147afc","l":"Nucleation on surfaces","d":1,"p":"eef7c13b-c67f-4d33-be97-76cee58e700a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Nucleation on surfaces is the process by which a new phase (for example a solid island, crystalline nucleus, or liquid droplet) first forms and becomes stable at a solid interface through adsorption and aggregation of atoms or molecules until a critical nucleus size is reached. It is a heterogeneous nucleation pathway with an energy barrier typically lower than bulk (homogeneous) nucleation and is strongly controlled by surface energy, defects, supersaturation, and surface diffusion."},{"id":"8e083471-2fd1-49cc-88e4-8c35ddff33be","l":"Nucleic acid replication & information processes","d":1,"p":"5be420fe-48f0-4430-a63d-9d611d837d6e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Nucleic acid replication and information processes are the molecular mechanisms by which DNA and RNA sequences are copied, read and maintained\u2014including template-directed replication, transcription, translation, proofreading and repair\u2014that preserve and transmit genetic information. Physically, these processes are analyzed with kinetics, stochastic thermodynamics, statistical mechanics and information theory to quantify rates, error rates, energetic costs and fidelity of information transfer."},{"id":"8d086d50-9e53-4988-b836-cf1108d32c93","l":"Nucleon distribution","d":1,"p":"1cf75de2-e14b-41cd-b733-8f704652db33","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Nucleon distribution is the probability density that describes how protons and neutrons (nucleons) are arranged inside a nucleus, typically given as spatial densities \u03c1_p(r), \u03c1_n(r). Complementary momentum-space distributions n(k) characterize the nucleons' momentum content and both types of distributions are probed experimentally by scattering and knockout reactions."},{"id":"624a78a4-af11-4f01-8bb8-5a0a81905d3d","l":"Nucleon induced nuclear reactions","d":1,"p":"bbb0ce0d-36da-4da5-a443-df064f36aa17","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Nucleon-induced nuclear reactions are processes that occur when a free nucleon (a proton or neutron) strikes a target nucleus and induces outcomes such as elastic or inelastic scattering, capture, particle emission (e.g., (n,\u03b3), (p,\u03b1), (n,p)), spallation, or fission, thereby changing the nucleus\u2019s energy or composition. The reaction type and probability (cross section) depend strongly on the incident nucleon species, its energy, and the target nucleus."},{"id":"4214927b-d43a-4eac-8975-f610cb1cc8e9","l":"Nucleon-nucleon interactions","d":1,"p":"1cf75de2-e14b-41cd-b733-8f704652db33","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Nucleon-nucleon interactions are the forces between protons and neutrons that bind nuclei, arising as the residual strong force of quantum chromodynamics. They are short-range with a strong repulsive core and intermediate attraction, depend on spin and isospin (including a tensor component), and are modeled by meson-exchange or phenomenological potentials and by chiral effective field theory, with three-nucleon forces required for precise many-body descriptions."},{"id":"29903cc6-9078-49fa-8c8f-9416eb5644fb","l":"Nucleosynthesis in explosive environments","d":1,"p":"38c3eaa1-e9a7-4590-9a93-cbdfd9fe162f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics","Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Nucleosynthesis in explosive environments is the formation of new atomic nuclei that occurs during rapid, high-temperature, high-density astrophysical explosions\u2014such as core-collapse and thermonuclear supernovae, neutron-star mergers, and X-ray bursts\u2014where conditions change on short timescales. Under these extremes, fast nuclear reaction pathways (notably the rapid neutron-capture r-process, rapid proton-capture rp-process, and photodisintegration-driven p-process) produce many of the Universe's heavy and proton-rich isotopes."},{"id":"42778a2b-d265-4877-9f98-99161aa3dbe3","l":"Nucleus-neutrino interactions","d":1,"p":"bf8ac618-dcbe-4098-91c1-f781ef555e5d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Nucleus\u2013neutrino interactions are weak-force processes in which a neutrino scatters from or is absorbed by an atomic nucleus, either via charged-current (converting a neutron to a proton or vice versa and producing a charged lepton) or neutral-current (leaving the neutrino unchanged) reactions. These rare, energy-dependent processes probe nuclear structure, determine neutrino detection signals, and play key roles in astrophysical environments like supernovae."},{"id":"e5bf5e43-7610-48b1-b1e9-dd26e460a500","l":"Numerical approximation & analysis","d":1,"p":"1e0c099a-9f2a-4e47-b3f1-7faa28b6a506","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Numerical approximation and analysis is the development and study of computational methods that produce approximate solutions to mathematical problems (e.g., differential equations, integrals, linear systems) when exact solutions are unavailable. It focuses on designing algorithms and quantifying their accuracy, stability, convergence, and efficiency, providing error estimates and criteria for reliable numerical results."},{"id":"ef675df4-aadf-4446-af41-0955eee689fb","l":"Numerical simulations in gravitation & astrophysics","d":1,"p":"1e0c099a-9f2a-4e47-b3f1-7faa28b6a506","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Numerical simulations in gravitation & astrophysics are computational models that solve the equations governing gravity, fluid dynamics, radiation, and particle dynamics (e.g., Einstein field equations, hydrodynamics/MHD, radiative transfer, and N-body gravity) to study the nonlinear, multi-scale behavior of astrophysical systems. Using numerical methods such as finite-difference, spectral, particle-based schemes and adaptive mesh refinement, they predict observables, test theories, and interpret data for phenomena from black-hole mergers and accretion disks to star and galaxy formation and cosmic structure."},{"id":"45a22032-e27f-4922-9d0c-10c4aa294db5","l":"Numerical techniques","d":1,"p":"233a6cd0-b7fe-491b-b5fd-85fd6b2a7c79","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Numerical techniques are computational algorithms for obtaining approximate solutions to mathematical problems in physics\u2014such as ordinary and partial differential equations, integrals, eigenvalue problems, and optimizations\u2014when analytic solutions are unavailable or impractical. They rely on discretization, iteration, and error/stability control to produce efficient, controllable approximations."},{"id":"6a87a113-159a-46b4-8ebb-ebc179f00384","l":"Ocean energy","d":1,"p":"af9f5a05-6917-40cf-a2ff-5520ff506935","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Ocean energy is the renewable energy derived from the oceans' mechanical motion and stored gradients\u2014converted into electricity or useful work by exploiting waves, tides, currents, temperature differences, or salinity differences. Common technologies extract it using turbines and oscillating bodies for mechanical flows, heat engines for ocean thermal gradients (OTEC), and membrane or pressure-driven systems for salinity-gradient power."},{"id":"787d6f81-9f93-4997-9978-cba2419977ca","l":"Oceanography","d":1,"p":"29ab1ea0-dd7e-46b7-87dd-0ca29c6be062","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Oceanography is the scientific study of Earth's oceans, encompassing the physical, chemical, biological, and geological processes that govern their behavior. From a physics perspective it emphasizes fluid dynamics, wave mechanics, thermodynamics, and transport processes that control currents, tides, mixing, and air\u2013sea interactions."},{"id":"ddc498ca-b065-4e93-b1da-5ad08d37aa65","l":"Odd-frequency superconductivity","d":1,"p":"ca8ddaad-441c-481c-93b3-65f46125fb93","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Odd-frequency superconductivity is a pairing state in which the Cooper-pair amplitude is an odd function of the relative time (or frequency) between the two electrons, i.e., it changes sign under exchange of time coordinates. Because fermionic antisymmetry must be preserved, this temporal oddness permits unconventional combinations of spin and spatial symmetry (for example spin-triplet s-wave or spin-singlet p-wave) and implies a vanishing equal-time order parameter and intrinsically dynamical pairing."},{"id":"6b5443fa-9849-4d5b-966b-7d6e08113e36","l":"Omics","d":1,"p":"b26070f8-32ca-4e02-94d0-ba1961b3480d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Omics refers to the systematic, high-throughput measurement and analysis of complete sets of molecular species in a biological system (e.g., genomics for genes, transcriptomics for RNAs, proteomics for proteins, metabolomics for metabolites), aimed at characterizing system-wide composition, interactions, and dynamics. These large, heterogeneous datasets are typically analyzed with statistical, network and computational-physics methods to uncover emergent, systems-level behavior."},{"id":"a34a6241-eab6-4358-b980-ef67fe59c533","l":"Onsager relations","d":1,"p":"36754399-ec2b-4962-bb07-ca12c79834e8","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Onsager reciprocal relations state that, in the linear-response regime near thermodynamic equilibrium, the phenomenological transport coefficients relating thermodynamic fluxes J_i to forces X_j (J_i = \u03a3_j L_{ij} X_j) obey symmetry relations following from microscopic time\u2011reversal invariance: L_{ij} = L_{ji}, or more generally L_{ij} = \u03b5_i \u03b5_j L_{ji} where \u03b5_k = \u00b11 is the parity of variable k under time reversal. These relations are a consequence of detailed balance and ensure reciprocity between coupled irreversible processes (e.g., thermoelectric and diffusion effects)."},{"id":"37d54ed9-50d7-491e-a874-5adb42615c2b","l":"Open quantum systems","d":1,"p":"860c01ce-5b2c-4bd5-9148-88f0b1d585e2","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"An open quantum system is a quantum system that interacts with external degrees of freedom (the environment), so its state evolution is generally nonunitary and can exhibit decoherence and dissipation. Its dynamics are described by the reduced density operator (obtained by tracing out the environment) and are modeled by master equations, quantum channels, or stochastic Schr\u00f6dinger equations."},{"id":"fce63f54-4c30-4bd4-b3b6-1a4d58bdaceb","l":"Open quantum systems & decoherence","d":1,"p":"01f70d47-cbac-4b5a-8ff9-0768e722511b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Open quantum systems are quantum systems that interact with external degrees of freedom (the environment), so their evolution is generally nonunitary and must be described by mixed states and master equations rather than isolated Schr\u00f6dinger dynamics. Decoherence is the environment-induced loss of phase coherence between components of a quantum superposition, caused by entanglement with environmental modes and leading to the suppression of interference and the emergence of effectively classical statistical mixtures."},{"id":"f5039ec3-c828-46cd-8f53-0c482841abc3","l":"Optical coherence","d":1,"p":"23cdcd6b-ae22-4b6d-92cd-24a9a8bd0577","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Optical coherence quantifies how well an optical field maintains a fixed phase relationship at different points in space and instants in time; it is a measure of the correlation of the electromagnetic field and determines the visibility of interference. Temporal coherence refers to phase correlation over time (linked to spectral linewidth), while spatial coherence refers to phase correlation across the wavefront (linked to source size)."},{"id":"05ff9faf-4943-45b0-b627-49eb8a9404c6","l":"Optical conductivity","d":1,"p":"4c4f4d87-e837-4d65-970d-f5323698a5f1","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Optical conductivity is the complex, frequency-dependent conductivity \u03c3(\u03c9) that quantifies a material's linear current response J(\u03c9)=\u03c3(\u03c9)E(\u03c9) to an oscillating electric field at optical frequencies. Its real part \u03c31(\u03c9) measures dissipative absorption while the imaginary part \u03c32(\u03c9) describes reactive (dispersive/polarization) response, and it is related to the dielectric function by \u03b5(\u03c9)=1 + i\u03c3(\u03c9)/(\u03b50\u03c9)."},{"id":"0593c217-5a51-4a64-bac7-e9f379367df6","l":"Optical, coupled-channel & distorted wave models","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"These are theoretical frameworks for describing scattering and reaction processes (especially in nuclear and atomic physics) by modeling how an incident wave is scattered and redistributed among possible final channels. The optical model uses a complex effective potential to represent elastic scattering plus absorption into nonelastic channels; coupled-channel models solve coupled Schr\u00f6dinger equations to treat explicit couplings between elastic, inelastic and reaction channels; and distorted-wave approaches (e.g., DWBA) compute transition amplitudes using incoming and outgoing waves already distorted by an optical potential."},{"id":"5f185b0e-83b0-452c-9602-799066bdcc87","l":"Optical forces","d":1,"p":"4c4f4d87-e837-4d65-970d-f5323698a5f1","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Optical forces are forces exerted on atoms, charges, or particles by electromagnetic radiation, arising from the transfer of photon momentum and from interactions with spatially varying electromagnetic fields. They include radiation\u2011pressure (scattering/absorption) forces that push along the beam and gradient (dipole) forces that draw particles toward or away from regions of high intensity, underlying effects like optical trapping and laser cooling."},{"id":"dcf48409-af3f-4517-b58d-7ec6c95351f3","l":"Optical materials & elements","d":1,"p":"23cdcd6b-ae22-4b6d-92cd-24a9a8bd0577","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Optical materials & elements are the substances (glasses, crystals, semiconductors, polymers, coatings) and engineered components (lenses, mirrors, prisms, fibers, filters, polarizers, gratings, waveplates, modulators) used to guide, shape and control light in the visible to ultraviolet/near\u2011IR spectral ranges. They are characterized by optical properties\u2014refractive index, dispersion, absorption, birefringence and nonlinear response\u2014that determine how they transmit, reflect, refract, polarize and spectrally or temporally modify light."},{"id":"88358b16-0fb9-43f8-b82d-cc149645b812","l":"Optical pumping","d":1,"p":"ba7ccb8e-d7eb-4e6f-9c1e-b622f5b4e242","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Optical pumping is a technique in which resonant (often polarized) light is used to drive selective electronic transitions in atoms or molecules, redistributing population among quantum states to produce non-thermal level populations or spin polarization. It relies on selection rules and spontaneous decay to accumulate population in particular sublevels and is widely used to prepare polarized ensembles for lasers, magnetometry, and atomic-physics experiments."},{"id":"a38dc017-3236-4084-afc5-eb59c5de51e0","l":"Optical techniques","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Optical techniques are experimental methods that use light (from radio to ultraviolet, often lasers) to probe, image, measure, or manipulate physical systems. They include spectroscopy, interferometry, microscopy, scattering, polarimetry and laser trapping, and are used to determine structural, dynamical, spectral or material properties with high spatial, temporal, or spectral resolution."},{"id":"ac65595b-19dc-4e41-959d-842bf8d48a31","l":"Optical transient phenomena","d":1,"p":"ba7ccb8e-d7eb-4e6f-9c1e-b622f5b4e242","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Optical transient phenomena are short-lived, time-dependent changes in the properties of light\u2014such as intensity, spectrum, phase or polarization\u2014produced by rapid physical processes (e.g., pulses, flashes, flares, or ultrafast electronic and nuclear dynamics) and occurring on timescales from femtoseconds to seconds. They are probed with time-resolved techniques (ultrafast lasers, fast photodetectors, transient spectroscopy) to reveal dynamic processes in materials, plasmas, and astrophysical sources."},{"id":"b088dbe4-5fc5-48f1-a650-fd33d24db135","l":"Optical, UV, & IR astronomy","d":1,"p":"893ecc24-c678-4687-bd3a-0b4a1bcb4cfc","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Optical, ultraviolet (UV), and infrared (IR) astronomy are branches of observational astronomy that detect and analyze electromagnetic radiation in the visible (~400\u2013700 nm), UV (~10\u2013400 nm), and IR (~0.7 \u03bcm\u20131 mm) wavelength bands. Observations in these bands\u2014using ground- and space-based telescopes and spectrometers\u2014probe temperatures, chemical composition, velocities, dust content and star-formation activity of celestial objects, with UV and much of the IR often needing space platforms because of atmospheric absorption."},{"id":"3d71d9d8-fab6-4338-be83-7624e12f1438","l":"Optoelectronics","d":1,"p":"4c4f4d87-e837-4d65-970d-f5323698a5f1","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Optoelectronics is the field that studies and designs devices and systems which convert between electrical signals and light (photons) by exploiting photon\u2013electron interactions in materials. It encompasses components such as LEDs, lasers, photodiodes and modulators used in communications, sensing, imaging and display technologies."},{"id":"2d7a7b42-9db0-4027-8b95-43b276a198c0","l":"Optomechanics","d":1,"p":"23cdcd6b-ae22-4b6d-92cd-24a9a8bd0577","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Optomechanics is the study of interactions between light and mechanical motion\u2014typically in optical cavities where radiation pressure or photothermal forces couple an optical mode to a mechanical oscillator\u2014enabling sensitive measurement, cooling, amplification and quantum control of mechanical degrees of freedom."},{"id":"4915ee7f-20c8-4fc1-ae82-82613da144df","l":"Order diagnosis","d":1,"p":"f2a9db78-1651-4a94-b66e-3e3d72056760","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Order diagnosis is the set of theoretical and experimental methods used to detect, identify, and characterize the presence and type of order in a physical system\u2014e.g., crystalline, magnetic, superconducting, or topological\u2014by measuring order parameters, correlation functions, structure factors, symmetry-breaking patterns, and topological invariants. It determines whether and how a system exhibits long-range, quasi-, or topological order and distinguishes competing ordered phases."},{"id":"3502cc4a-fa49-4558-90ea-e743a42e00b3","l":"Ordered compounds","d":1,"p":"81194f70-bbca-4041-b57e-29338e588cac","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Ordered compounds are crystalline solids in which different chemical species occupy specific lattice sites in a regular, long-range repeating arrangement (chemical ordering), yielding a definite stoichiometry and physical properties distinct from disordered solid solutions or random alloys."},{"id":"979074fa-fadd-4ebe-acf1-0a9c92fb1848","l":"Order parameters","d":1,"p":"f2a9db78-1651-4a94-b66e-3e3d72056760","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"An order parameter is a macroscopic quantity that takes distinct values in different phases\u2014typically zero in the disordered phase and nonzero in the ordered phase\u2014used to distinguish and characterize phase transitions. It usually equals the expectation value of a microscopic operator (scalar, vector, or tensor), encoding broken symmetry and critical behavior (e.g., magnetization, density difference, superconducting gap)."},{"id":"c215fb56-b919-42c5-9267-13846d715223","l":"Organic electronics","d":1,"p":"c765ac18-ded5-4ef0-a41a-22be13741559","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Organic electronics is the study and application of electronic materials and devices built from organic (carbon\u2011based) semiconductors\u2014conjugated polymers and small molecules\u2014whose charge transport arises from \u03c0\u2011conjugation and often involves hopping or band\u2011like mechanisms. It encompasses devices such as organic light\u2011emitting diodes (OLEDs), organic field\u2011effect transistors (OFETs), and organic photovoltaics (OPVs), and is prized for chemical tunability, solution processing, and mechanical flexibility."},{"id":"555f77ad-7319-492f-ad89-8b07dfd06f86","l":"Organic & organometallic chemistry","d":1,"p":"4455b073-c3a2-4a0b-923e-98d7ec0f3b9d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Organic chemistry is the branch of chemistry that studies the structure, properties, synthesis and reactions of carbon\u2011containing molecules (including those with H, O, N, halogens and other heteroatoms). Organometallic chemistry focuses on compounds with direct metal\u2013carbon bonds, examining their bonding, electronic structure and reactivity\u2014especially in catalysis and functional materials that bridge inorganic and organic behavior."},{"id":"d3847428-6e20-4054-92c9-f8b44e36cffd","l":"Organogels","d":1,"p":"06891d57-1044-487c-ae20-21366d161a97","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Organogels are soft, viscoelastic materials in which an organic liquid is immobilized by a three-dimensional network of gelator molecules (either low\u2011molecular\u2011weight self\u2011assembled fibers or polymeric/crosslinked networks), giving the material solid\u2011like behavior despite high solvent content. The network is formed and stabilized by noncovalent interactions (e.g., hydrogen bonding, \u03c0\u2013\u03c0 stacking, van der Waals) or covalent crosslinks, and can be reversible (physical) or permanent (chemical)."},{"id":"f3d6eecd-4d03-4fad-94da-a7c089947f2f","l":"Origami & Kirigami","d":1,"p":"bb18132a-8998-4023-a343-725c6803d24c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Origami and kirigami are the study and design of thin sheets whose shapes and mechanical responses are controlled by prescribed fold patterns (origami) or by folds combined with cuts (kirigami). Using geometry, topology, and elasticity, these techniques enable programmable deformations, deployable structures, and unusual mechanical properties (e.g., auxetic response, stiffness tuning, and strain localization)."},{"id":"fa2c0c80-ce03-4933-9dcf-0269b613e73f","l":"Osmotic interactions","d":1,"p":"cc59c6fb-acbb-4237-953b-b874fe5dbd4a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Osmotic interactions are forces between particles, surfaces or membranes in a fluid that arise from imbalances in solute chemical potential (osmotic pressure), producing solvent flow or net mechanical forces. They are fundamentally entropic in origin and include effects such as swelling against a semipermeable membrane and depletion-induced attractions between colloidal particles."},{"id":"a072b9ca-e7cd-4f67-90fd-984fd0db57c2","l":"Packaging materials","d":1,"p":"c765ac18-ded5-4ef0-a41a-22be13741559","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Packaging materials are materials engineered to enclose, protect and preserve products during storage, handling and transport. Their performance is characterized physically by mechanical (strength, toughness, impact absorption), thermal (conductivity, stability), barrier (permeability to gases, moisture, and light) and electromagnetic (optical clarity, shielding) properties."},{"id":"d180315b-fe7b-449b-958a-ea6fdb8c4a9f","l":"Paints","d":1,"p":"c765ac18-ded5-4ef0-a41a-22be13741559","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Paints are multi-component colloidal coatings made of pigment particles dispersed in a liquid vehicle (binder, solvent and additives) that form a solid thin film on a surface by solvent evaporation or binder polymerization. Physically, they control a surface\u2019s optical (absorption, scattering, color, reflectance/emissivity), mechanical (adhesion, hardness, elasticity) and thermal properties, with behavior governed by rheology, drying/film\u2011formation kinetics and interfacial forces."},{"id":"78b459cf-9dc6-45ac-bc92-9c675ef1c9c3","l":"Pair density wave","d":1,"p":"ca8ddaad-441c-481c-93b3-65f46125fb93","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A pair density wave (PDW) is a superconducting state in which the superconducting order parameter is spatially modulated, so Cooper pairs carry a finite center-of-mass momentum and the local pair density oscillates. This breaks translational symmetry and can coexist or intertwine with charge- and spin-density waves."},{"id":"57c8eac7-41d5-4fed-8ba5-d8cb38d1ad03","l":"Pairing mechanisms","d":1,"p":"ca8ddaad-441c-481c-93b3-65f46125fb93","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Pairing mechanisms are the physical interactions or processes that cause two fermions (e.g., electrons or nucleons) to form bound or strongly correlated pairs, leading to a paired ground state with collective behavior such as superconductivity or superfluidity and typically an excitation gap. Examples include phonon-mediated attraction in conventional (BCS) superconductors and electronic or spin-fluctuation-driven pairing in unconventional superconductors and other paired fermion systems."},{"id":"ea2dec8b-86df-4e72-99a0-c6fc6bb35f51","l":"Paramagnets","d":1,"p":"58071c68-730c-4a3e-982c-abc9f0cedf67","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Paramagnets are materials whose atoms or ions have permanent magnetic dipole moments (unpaired electron spins) that tend to align with an applied magnetic field, producing a small, positive magnetization proportional to the field. Thermal agitation prevents spontaneous ordering in zero field, so their susceptibility is positive and typically follows Curie\u2019s law (\u03c7 \u221d 1/T) or Curie\u2013Weiss behavior if interactions are present."},{"id":"1762a8ce-6759-4d70-bc6f-96a105fc190d","l":"Partially composite materials","d":1,"p":"c2ed1d79-919b-4642-8549-2394977dd60c","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"A partially composite material is a heterogeneous solid in which only part of the volume or microstructure contains reinforcing phases (fibers, particles, laminates) while the remainder remains the unreinforced matrix, so the material exhibits a mix of composite and base-material behavior. Such partial reinforcement produces spatially varying mechanical, thermal, or electrical properties used to tailor performance, weight, and cost."},{"id":"03927be6-184c-42fb-93da-1b1772a1345e","l":"Particle acceleration in plasmas","d":1,"p":"4b4294b5-d03f-4662-9886-cea042ce7d8c","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams","Plasma Physics"],"def":"Particle acceleration in plasmas refers to processes that increase the kinetic energy of charged particles through interactions with electromagnetic fields, collective plasma waves, and large-scale plasma structures. Typical mechanisms include direct acceleration by electric fields, Fermi acceleration at shocks or in turbulence, resonant wave\u2013particle interactions, and magnetic reconnection."},{"id":"f55defd6-c6c9-4557-af91-e0b0f467ffe5","l":"Particle accelerators","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Particle accelerators are machines that use electric fields to speed up charged particles and magnetic fields to steer and focus their trajectories, producing controlled high-energy beams. These beams are used to probe the structure of matter, produce collisions for particle physics, generate medical isotopes and radiation for cancer treatment, and for various industrial and research applications."},{"id":"656297b3-33eb-4690-985f-5f5b34cffca4","l":"Particle beams","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Particle beams are directed, often collimated streams of elementary or composite particles (electrons, protons, ions, neutrons, muons, photons) produced and transported by accelerators and beamlines and steered or focused by electromagnetic elements. They are characterized by parameters such as energy, intensity (current), emittance/brightness and energy spread, and are used in experiments, materials processing, imaging and medical therapy."},{"id":"a923556f-ddb8-48c1-a730-a10a924b691c","l":"Particle correlations & fluctuations","d":1,"p":"7321e0f7-3b12-45a2-aa4a-28c2a7e03149","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Particle correlations describe statistical dependencies between particles\u2019 properties (position, momentum, spin, charge, etc.), typically quantified by correlation functions that depend on relative coordinates or other variables. Fluctuations are the sample- or time-dependent deviations of observables from their mean (variance and higher cumulants); together correlations and fluctuations reveal interactions, collective behavior, and critical phenomena and are related by fluctuation\u2013dissipation relations."},{"id":"83481d17-bac2-4c24-b875-f19596028e90","l":"Particle dark matter","d":1,"p":"c037721f-32bc-4ef4-88de-c8374b706edb","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Particle dark matter is hypothetical non-baryonic matter made of new, stable or long\u2011lived particle species that interact primarily through gravity and only very weakly (if at all) with light and ordinary matter, invoked to explain galaxy rotation curves, gravitational lensing, the cosmic microwave background, and large\u2011scale structure. Typical candidate classes include weakly interacting massive particles (WIMPs), axions, and sterile neutrinos."},{"id":"db0eac92-d73c-4c77-ad1b-6c2517418354","l":"Particle data analysis","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Particle data analysis is the process of converting raw signals from particle detectors into reconstructed events and quantitative physics results by calibrating detectors, reconstructing particles, selecting signal samples, and estimating backgrounds. It combines Monte Carlo simulation, statistical inference, and uncertainty evaluation to measure particle properties, cross sections, and to search for new phenomena."},{"id":"270c18a9-5bde-4c79-9f83-8453d38dc9c8","l":"Particle decays","d":1,"p":"9583ddb0-9593-4dfd-92b6-0f49ff1282d4","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Particle decay is the spontaneous transformation of an unstable elementary particle or bound state into other particles mediated by the fundamental interactions. It is described statistically by a decay rate (or mean lifetime) and branching ratios for possible channels, and proceeds subject to conservation of energy, momentum and relevant quantum numbers."},{"id":"66ce7b40-3091-4fb4-a468-0170ee73cbed","l":"Particle detection signatures","d":1,"p":"9583ddb0-9593-4dfd-92b6-0f49ff1282d4","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Particle detection signatures are the characteristic measurable signals produced when a particle interacts with a detector\u2014examples include ionization and energy deposition, scintillation or Cherenkov light, electronic pulses, tracks in tracking detectors, and timing information. By combining these observables (energy, topology, timing, and secondary-particle patterns) experiments identify the particle species and infer its energy, momentum, and other properties."},{"id":"be8bd934-5bc9-4a6d-ae73-f4753ce5c128","l":"Particle detectors","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Particle detectors are instruments that observe and measure elementary and nuclear particles by converting the particles' interactions with matter (ionization, scintillation, Cherenkov light, charge collection, etc.) into electrical or optical signals. From those signals they determine particle properties such as identity, energy, momentum, trajectory, and arrival time for applications ranging from collider experiments to astroparticle and nuclear physics."},{"id":"e9784296-0926-438e-8981-c17b0cc74f11","l":"Particle image velocimetry","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Particle image velocimetry (PIV) is an optical measurement technique that maps fluid velocity fields by seeding the flow with tracer particles, illuminating them (commonly with a laser sheet), recording successive images, and determining particle displacements\u2014typically by cross-correlation of interrogation regions\u2014to compute instantaneous velocity vectors. It yields planar or volumetric, time-resolved flow-field measurements with high spatial resolution for experimental fluid-dynamics studies."},{"id":"7abf7854-8c1d-41eb-9d7e-82106d345d24","l":"Particle-in-cell methods","d":1,"p":"233a6cd0-b7fe-491b-b5fd-85fd6b2a7c79","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Particle-in-cell (PIC) methods are numerical simulation techniques that represent a plasma or many-body system by a finite number of \"macro\" particles whose charges/currents are deposited onto a spatial grid where the electromagnetic (or electrostatic) field equations are solved, and those grid fields are interpolated back to advance the particle motions. They efficiently capture kinetic and collective dynamics (e.g., wave\u2013particle interactions, instabilities) without computing pairwise forces between all particles."},{"id":"83c64883-5507-43f8-92bc-ae9f5ae03f02","l":"Particle interactions","d":1,"p":"9583ddb0-9593-4dfd-92b6-0f49ff1282d4","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Particle interactions are processes by which particles influence each other's motion, internal states, or existence via the fundamental forces (electromagnetic, weak, strong, and gravitational). In quantum field theory these interactions are described by exchange of mediator (gauge) bosons and manifest as scattering, decay, annihilation, or creation events constrained by conservation laws and coupling strengths."},{"id":"63f8b3b2-4f01-4ed3-8256-e8c63afc6ddb","l":"Particle-laden flows","d":1,"p":"c577bd62-ea57-43b7-9607-1ef45dba3afb","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Particle-laden flows are fluid flows that carry a dispersed phase of particles (solid grains, droplets, or bubbles) whose motion and presence exchange momentum, heat and mass with the carrier fluid and can alter the flow structure. They are treated as multiphase systems whose behavior depends on particle size, concentration and coupling strength (e.g., Stokes number), and may include collisions, settling, and turbulence modulation."},{"id":"5adb0193-f741-4713-8010-4270b7bf5d9c","l":"Particle mixing & oscillations","d":1,"p":"9583ddb0-9593-4dfd-92b6-0f49ff1282d4","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Particle mixing occurs when the states produced and observed in interactions (e.g., \"flavor\" eigenstates) are quantum superpositions of the states that propagate with definite mass or energy (mass eigenstates), due to a non-diagonal mass or Hamiltonian. Because the mass eigenstates acquire different quantum phases as they propagate, the superposition changes in time or distance and the probability to observe a given flavor oscillates\u2014the phenomenon seen in neutrino oscillations and neutral meson mixing."},{"id":"97069ede-42dd-4f4d-b552-9fc8562cb02d","l":"Particle production","d":1,"p":"9583ddb0-9593-4dfd-92b6-0f49ff1282d4","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Particle production is the creation of one or more particles from the energy present in interactions, collisions, decays, or time-varying fields, converting kinetic or field energy into rest mass and particle excitations. In quantum field theory it is described by interaction terms that allow transitions between states, subject to conservation laws (energy, momentum, charge, etc.)."},{"id":"ad204586-a9a9-49e4-af2f-58f1d8ea2e70","l":"Particle & resonance production","d":1,"p":"7321e0f7-3b12-45a2-aa4a-28c2a7e03149","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Particle and resonance production denotes the creation of new particles or short-lived excited (resonant) states in collisions or decays of other particles. Resonances are unstable, appear as peaks in scattering cross sections or invariant-mass spectra, and are characterized by production cross sections and decay widths determined by the underlying strong, electromagnetic, or weak interactions."},{"id":"7e0090ff-4a5c-44e2-b167-0ac747e5e728","l":"Particle sources & targets","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Particle sources & targets are the paired experimental elements that produce and intercept particles: sources generate controlled particle emissions or beams (e.g., accelerators, radioactive isotopes, reactors, neutron generators, laser-produced plasmas), while targets are the materials or assemblies placed in the beam (solid, liquid, gas, or moving) to study interactions, induce reactions, or produce secondary particles. The choice and design of source and target determine the beam properties, reaction yields, and experimental outcomes."},{"id":"0c11d1c6-a6c0-43df-a612-d2c47121f7ef","l":"Patchy particles","d":1,"p":"ef0a1bee-2a1f-4f47-aca2-6302db18e67f","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Patchy particles are colloidal or nanoparticle building blocks whose surfaces are decorated with a small number of distinct interaction sites (\"patches\") that produce anisotropic, direction-specific attractions. This patchiness gives controllable, valence-limited bonding and enables programmable self-assembly into targeted structures and phases."},{"id":"cb8280f7-1169-4173-8638-64316b821a5b","l":"Path-integral methods","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Path-integral methods are a formulation of quantum mechanics and statistical physics in which transition amplitudes or partition functions are written as integrals (sums) over all possible histories\u2014paths for particles or configurations for fields\u2014each weighted by exp(iS/\u0127) (or exp(\u2212S/\u0127) in imaginary time), with S the classical action. They provide a unified framework for perturbation theory, semiclassical approximations, and nonperturbative phenomena in quantum and statistical systems."},{"id":"4bf8a023-3d5f-4e87-8e97-921c3d4cba16","l":"Path integrals","d":1,"p":"a1083ad2-a154-449d-a9ff-59bf46db1c7f","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Path integrals are a formulation of quantum mechanics and field theory in which a transition amplitude or partition function is obtained by summing (integrating) over all possible histories (paths or field configurations) between given endpoints, with each history weighted by exp(iS[history]/\u0127) (or exp(\u2212S/kT) in statistical contexts). In this approach the classical limit emerges from destructive interference except near paths that make the action stationary."},{"id":"8e35265c-459a-43fc-9eb8-73e1dd82b47c","l":"Pattern formation","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Nonlinear Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Pattern formation is the spontaneous emergence of spatial or spatiotemporal order from an initially homogeneous or disordered system, usually driven far from equilibrium. It arises when instabilities and nonlinear interactions break symmetry and select characteristic wavelengths and structures (e.g., convection rolls, Turing chemical spots, or stripe patterns on animal skins)."},{"id":"dd01af76-b910-470d-b460-65d6ff3229d3","l":"Patterns in complex systems","d":1,"p":"d55c7570-5cb1-4f1a-b729-2647e666ffe8","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Patterns in complex systems are recurring spatial or temporal structures that arise spontaneously from interactions among many components, rather than from a central controller. They result from nonlinear dynamics, feedback and symmetry breaking, and appear as waves, oscillations, synchronization, spirals, stripes or scale\u2011invariant (fractal) organization."},{"id":"f708cc58-38ab-4944-8e59-aba8cebdece7","l":"Peierls transition","d":1,"p":"15f520a3-5c6c-4d9e-8975-81d399140562","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Peierls transition: in a one-dimensional metal, a spontaneous lattice distortion (often doubling the unit cell) and accompanying charge-density wave form at low temperature, opening an energy gap at the Fermi level and turning the metal into an insulator. This occurs because electron\u2013phonon coupling and Fermi-surface nesting at wavevector 2k_F lower the electronic energy enough to overcome the elastic cost of the distortion."},{"id":"753cc095-4421-4a9f-9668-8c05cbe45bfa","l":"Penning discharges","d":1,"p":"b30d0b9e-de6d-4de6-bfad-565f513573b7","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"A Penning discharge is a low\u2011pressure gas glow discharge sustained in crossed electric and magnetic fields that confine electrons on long helical or closed trajectories, increasing their path length and ionization probability so the discharge can be maintained at much lower pressures than without the field. It is the operating principle of devices such as Penning (cold\u2011cathode) gauges, sputter\u2011ion pumps, and some types of ion sources."},{"id":"08ee99a2-4964-4690-8cd4-213bbba951b5","l":"Percolation phase transition","d":1,"p":"6e3ddc0a-24fd-4682-bb1b-137944166a4a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Percolation phase transition is the geometric transition in a disordered network or lattice where, as the fraction of occupied sites or bonds is increased past a critical threshold pc, a connected cluster suddenly spans the system. It is a continuous (second-order) transition characterized by the onset of a nonzero spanning-cluster density (order parameter) and universal critical scaling of cluster sizes and correlation length."},{"id":"7f613135-db34-40bd-b19e-e520cf4b29f4","l":"Percolation theory","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Percolation theory is the statistical-mechanical study of how connected clusters form in random systems\u2014typically modeled by occupying sites or bonds of a lattice (or particles in the continuum) with probability p\u2014and it characterizes the percolation threshold p_c at which a system-spanning (infinite) cluster appears and the universal critical behavior near that transition. It underlies phenomena such as fluid flow in porous media, electrical conduction in disordered materials, and epidemic spreading."},{"id":"7132f665-8c46-47a5-9b17-7150169f25b5","l":"Permanent magnets","d":1,"p":"58071c68-730c-4a3e-982c-abc9f0cedf67","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Permanent magnets are materials that produce a persistent external magnetic field without applied current because they retain a spontaneous magnetization from aligned atomic magnetic moments (spins and orbital contributions) stabilized by magnetic anisotropy and domain structure. High-coercivity permanent magnets (e.g., NdFeB, Alnico, ferrites) resist demagnetization and are characterized by hysteresis parameters such as remanence and coercive field."},{"id":"6dabc2b8-af3b-4156-b7b4-27b0d4c8519f","l":"Permittivity","d":1,"p":"41dd58dd-d35c-46e8-9594-d7e05b458da6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Permittivity is a material constant that quantifies how an electric field induces polarization in a medium and how the medium modifies the field; it enters Maxwell\u2019s equations via D = \u03b5E. The vacuum permittivity \u03b50 is the value for free space and the relative permittivity \u03b5r = \u03b5/\u03b50 describes a material\u2019s dielectric response."},{"id":"a7f95210-907a-454d-bde2-b3d2f7ca2f70","l":"Persistence length","d":1,"p":"5114e95c-b4a7-458f-a2f7-b49a98b828cd","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Persistence length is the characteristic length scale over which the tangent directions of a polymer or filament remain correlated in the presence of thermal fluctuations; mathematically, <t(s)\u00b7t(0)> = exp(\u2212s/\u2113p). It quantifies bending stiffness: \u2113p much larger than the contour length behaves like a rigid rod, while \u2113p much smaller indicates a flexible coil."},{"id":"a41d49ce-09be-4880-b90a-cc746141bb9f","l":"Perturbation theory","d":1,"p":"a0dcc4fb-34a0-4892-a60a-cfb14c0f73e8","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Particles & Fields","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Perturbation theory is a set of methods for obtaining approximate solutions to a physical problem by starting from an exactly solvable reference problem and expanding the desired solution in powers of a small parameter that measures the deviation. The resulting order-by-order corrections (often in a power or asymptotic series) are widely used across quantum mechanics, celestial mechanics, fluid dynamics, and other areas when exact solutions are unavailable."},{"id":"66c20921-8f2f-4e97-bbb4-394a2ce02a61","l":"Perturbative methods","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics"],"def":"Perturbative methods are techniques for finding approximate solutions to problems that cannot be solved exactly by expanding the solution in a series around a solvable reference case, treating the difference as a small parameter and computing successive corrections. They are widely used in quantum mechanics, quantum field theory, and classical dynamics, and are accurate when the perturbation is small though the resulting series may be asymptotic or require resummation."},{"id":"609eef8d-00d9-44dd-b81a-627290cdfb23","l":"Phase diagrams","d":1,"p":"f2a9db78-1651-4a94-b66e-3e3d72056760","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Phase diagrams are graphical maps of the equilibrium states of a substance (or mixture) as functions of external variables\u2014typically temperature, pressure and/or composition\u2014showing regions where distinct phases are stable and lines or surfaces where phases coexist or transform.  They allow prediction of which phase(s) occur under given conditions and locate features like coexistence lines, critical points, and triple points."},{"id":"ed1232cd-9cdc-4da9-a1bf-572e24c3058c","l":"Phase-field modeling","d":1,"p":"45a22032-e27f-4922-9d0c-10c4aa294db5","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Phase-field modeling is a diffuse-interface computational approach that describes different phases or microstructural states by a smoothly varying order-parameter (the phase field) whose evolution is driven by a free-energy functional. The resulting partial differential equations (e.g., Allen\u2013Cahn or Cahn\u2013Hilliard) let interfaces move, merge, and form complex topology without explicit boundary tracking, and are widely used for solidification, phase separation, fracture, and multiphase flows."},{"id":"d0f6584f-c5d7-4011-99bb-2a370e8f2b99","l":"Phase separation","d":1,"p":"f2a9db78-1651-4a94-b66e-3e3d72056760","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Phase separation is the process by which a once-uniform system spontaneously decomposes into distinct coexisting regions (phases) with different composition or order parameters when the homogeneous state becomes thermodynamically unstable and lowering the free energy is possible. It can occur by mechanisms such as nucleation and growth or spinodal decomposition and is common in mixtures, alloys, polymers, and other condensed-matter systems."},{"id":"b1610fb7-dc8d-45d6-b262-15a5fa16b5e8","l":"Phase separation driven self-assembly","d":1,"p":"bb18132a-8998-4023-a343-725c6803d24c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Phase separation\u2013driven self-assembly is the spontaneous formation of organized micro- or nano-scale structures when a multi-component system demixes into coexisting phases (via nucleation-and-growth or spinodal decomposition), with components segregating to minimize free energy and interfacial costs. The resulting domains (droplets, bicontinuous networks, etc.) can template further ordering by selective partitioning, confinement, or interfacial interactions."},{"id":"b34f5fe1-9689-4ea2-8eb2-5db6f915cbc7","l":"Phase slips","d":1,"p":"4722b240-3d0b-4ab1-831a-75302ab4cf86","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Phase slips are localized events in a coherent ordered state (superconductor or superfluid) where the phase of the macroscopic order parameter changes abruptly by 2\u03c0, allowing the system to change its quantized winding number and dissipate a persistent current. They occur when the amplitude of the order parameter is transiently suppressed (e.g., a vortex crossing) and can be induced thermally or by quantum tunneling."},{"id":"db4ea3ff-b6e5-43b2-90eb-8a966a5bc1b0","l":"Phase space methods","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Nonlinear Dynamics"],"def":"Phase-space methods represent a physical system by points in a space whose axes are its generalized coordinates and conjugate momenta, and analyze dynamics as trajectories or flows of points or of probability/distribution functions on that space. They provide unified tools\u2014Hamiltonian/Liouville flow, symplectic geometry, Poincar\u00e9 maps, and quantum phase-space distributions (e.g., Wigner, Husimi)\u2014for studying dynamical behavior, statistical ensembles, and semiclassical approximations."},{"id":"d2c655da-6260-45e7-bc14-26fba976f03b","l":"Phase transitions by order","d":1,"p":"f2a9db78-1651-4a94-b66e-3e3d72056760","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Phase transitions by order classify transitions according to the lowest derivative of the thermodynamic free energy that is discontinuous at the transition (the Ehrenfest scheme). For example, first\u2011order transitions show discontinuous first derivatives (latent heat, volume jump), whereas second\u2011order or continuous transitions have continuous first derivatives but singular second (or higher) derivatives (diverging specific heat, correlation length) and are described by symmetry breaking and renormalization\u2011group critical phenomena."},{"id":"5fddad64-3302-472f-9945-d256974cb278","l":"Phase transitions in biological systems","d":1,"p":"b26070f8-32ca-4e02-94d0-ba1961b3480d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Phase transitions in biological systems are collective reorganizations of biomolecules, cells, or tissues in which the material or functional state changes abruptly as control parameters (e.g., concentration, temperature, chemical activity) are varied, analogous to thermodynamic phase transitions such as liquid\u2013liquid demixing or membrane melting. These transitions often occur far from equilibrium and are shaped by biochemical reactions, active forces, and heterogeneity, giving rise to living-specific phenomena like energy-driven condensate formation, tissue jamming, or cytoskeletal order."},{"id":"5142791a-87c4-4cb8-ad5b-3c2d5c54187c","l":"Phasons","d":1,"p":"d46f32eb-17b3-4d09-be59-fc3461e542db","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Phasons are collective excitations that change the phase of a spatially modulated order (e.g., incommensurate crystals, quasicrystals, or charge-density waves), corresponding to shifts of the modulation pattern rather than local atomic vibrations. They are Goldstone-like, low-energy modes (often diffusive or weakly propagating) distinct from phonons, which are ordinary lattice displacement modes."},{"id":"66d2d40f-9387-444a-8986-9728f461ba3b","l":"Phononics","d":1,"p":"9e001bda-d32b-4a59-a216-bfd4c1af8c25","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Phononics is the study and engineering of phonons\u2014quantized lattice vibrations or vibrational waves in solids\u2014to control, guide, and manipulate thermal and acoustic energy. It encompasses phonon transport and devices (phononic crystals, thermal diodes, transistors, waveguides, metamaterials) that manage heat and sound at micro- to nanoscale."},{"id":"684c842a-625c-4cd3-bf2a-c64bbcbc1c24","l":"Phonons","d":1,"p":"d46f32eb-17b3-4d09-be59-fc3461e542db","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Phonons are the quantized normal modes of vibration in a crystal lattice \u2014 bosonic quasiparticles that represent collective oscillations of the atoms and carry energy \u0127\u03c9 and crystal momentum. They determine many thermal and transport properties of solids (e.g., heat capacity, thermal conductivity, and electron-phonon interactions)."},{"id":"87c0c99e-6a56-44eb-857b-25b1beafcac1","l":"Phosphorene","d":1,"p":"7a86b9d8-3162-4279-9ca5-90ebad07be7c","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Phosphorene is a two-dimensional semiconducting allotrope of phosphorus consisting of single- or few-atom-thick layers of black phosphorus with a puckered lattice. Its anisotropic structure produces direction-dependent electronic and optical properties, including a thickness-tunable direct band gap and relatively high carrier mobility."},{"id":"38fde934-8dcd-4f53-9d09-2aa630d7863d","l":"Photoassociation","d":1,"p":"ba7ccb8e-d7eb-4e6f-9c1e-b622f5b4e242","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Photoassociation is the resonant, light-induced formation of a bound molecular state from two colliding atoms by absorption of a photon (or photons), typically carried out in ultracold atomic gases to transfer the atom pair into an excited molecular potential. It is used to create ultracold molecules and to probe interatomic potentials and collision dynamics."},{"id":"03d36887-85f0-4045-a51f-93c21e08df23","l":"Photochemistry","d":1,"p":"4455b073-c3a2-4a0b-923e-98d7ec0f3b9d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Photochemistry is the study of chemical reactions and physical transformations that are initiated or driven by the absorption of light (photons). It focuses on the behavior and reactivity of electronically excited states and the pathways\u2014photophysical (e.g., fluorescence, intersystem crossing) and photochemical (e.g., photodissociation, photoisomerization)\u2014by which absorbed energy produces chemical change."},{"id":"57934ca6-f76c-4b88-9513-12b414e4e105","l":"Photoconductivity","d":1,"p":"4c4f4d87-e837-4d65-970d-f5323698a5f1","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Photoconductivity is the increase in a material's electrical conductivity when it absorbs light (or other electromagnetic radiation), caused by the generation of mobile charge carriers (electron\u2013hole pairs) or the release of carriers from traps. The effect can be transient or persistent depending on carrier recombination and trapping dynamics and is commonly observed in semiconductors and some insulators."},{"id":"0ac4a408-a5fa-4282-9aca-76faf6cfb000","l":"Photocurrent","d":1,"p":"4c4f4d87-e837-4d65-970d-f5323698a5f1","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Photocurrent is the electric current produced when incident photons generate mobile charge carriers (electrons and holes) in a material. It arises from photoelectric, photoconductive, or photovoltaic processes and is carried away by built\u2011in or applied electric fields."},{"id":"635ceb3e-28a3-4d4d-acbe-174bb896a844","l":"Photodetachment","d":1,"p":"ba7ccb8e-d7eb-4e6f-9c1e-b622f5b4e242","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Photodetachment is the process in which a photon ejects an electron from a negative ion, producing a neutral atom or molecule and a free electron. It occurs only when the photon energy exceeds the ion's electron affinity (the detachment threshold) and is characterized by photodetachment cross sections and threshold laws."},{"id":"3788a748-c446-4dd4-b8bb-4d01612f7815","l":"Photodissociation","d":1,"p":"ba7ccb8e-d7eb-4e6f-9c1e-b622f5b4e242","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Photodissociation is the breakup of a molecule into two or more atoms, radicals, or ions after it absorbs one or more photons whose energy exceeds the chemical bond dissociation energy. It can occur by direct excitation to a repulsive electronic state or via predissociation and plays a key role in atmospheric, combustion, and astrophysical chemistry."},{"id":"90223563-a9ed-483d-ad68-68ab7c3b3161","l":"Photoelectron techniques","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Photoelectron techniques are experimental methods that exploit the photoelectric effect to eject electrons from atoms, molecules, or solids by photon absorption and then measure the emitted electrons' kinetic energy, emission angle, and/or arrival time. From those measurements they infer electronic binding energies, momentum (band structure), chemical state, and ultrafast electronic dynamics (examples include XPS, UPS, ARPES, and time-resolved photoelectron spectroscopy)."},{"id":"96384c37-66b9-4126-8aa1-d6e984cbe3c3","l":"Photoemission","d":1,"p":"ba7ccb8e-d7eb-4e6f-9c1e-b622f5b4e242","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Photoemission is the process in which photons incident on a material transfer energy to electrons, causing those electrons to be emitted from the material if the photon energy exceeds the electrons' binding energy (or the material's work function). The emitted electrons' kinetic energies reflect the photon energy minus that binding energy (h\u03bd \u2212 \u03a6), a principle used in photoelectron spectroscopy to probe electronic structure."},{"id":"581a0b5b-0b7f-4ff7-a192-8b5ad1c6252c","l":"Photoinduced effect","d":1,"p":"4c4f4d87-e837-4d65-970d-f5323698a5f1","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Photoinduced effect: any change in a material's physical, chemical, electronic, or magnetic properties that is caused by absorption of photons. Examples include photoconductivity, photochromism, photochemical reactions, and light-driven structural or phase transitions, which can be transient or permanent."},{"id":"d9fbef9a-71f6-4881-9f69-8d81c5749cff","l":"Photonic crystals","d":1,"p":"23cdcd6b-ae22-4b6d-92cd-24a9a8bd0577","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Photonic crystals are materials with a spatially periodic modulation of the dielectric constant on length scales comparable to optical wavelengths, which gives rise to photonic band structures and frequency ranges (photonic band gaps) where propagation of certain electromagnetic modes is forbidden. By engineering their geometry and dimensionality (1D, 2D, or 3D) they can control, confine, and enhance or suppress light propagation and emission, analogous to how electronic band structures govern electrons in solids."},{"id":"99f32be9-888e-42c5-ba4a-1fdcea060307","l":"Photonics","d":1,"p":"23cdcd6b-ae22-4b6d-92cd-24a9a8bd0577","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Photonics is the science and technology of generating, controlling, transmitting, and detecting photons (light), covering both classical and quantum optical phenomena. It encompasses devices and systems such as lasers, optical fibers, lenses and waveguides, detectors, and nonlinear- and quantum-optical components used in communication, sensing, imaging, and computing."},{"id":"0ecc27c0-3b2d-413b-8780-fe9dde80ed68","l":"Photon, lepton & quark production","d":1,"p":"7321e0f7-3b12-45a2-aa4a-28c2a7e03149","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Photon, lepton & quark production refers to the creation of photons, charged leptons (e.g., electrons, muons) and quarks from available energy in particle interactions or decays, subject to conservation laws (energy, momentum, charge, etc.).  Typical mechanisms include electromagnetic processes (bremsstrahlung, annihilation) for photons and electroweak or strong interactions in high\u2011energy collisions or decays for leptons and quarks, with quarks subsequently fragmenting into hadrons and jets."},{"id":"a93bbd60-566f-4c4c-b6df-e93921384a8d","l":"Photons","d":1,"p":"556e52f5-6040-4273-b1a4-5e864b903fa5","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Photons are the elementary quanta of the electromagnetic field \u2014 massless, spin-1 bosons that mediate the electromagnetic interaction and carry energy E = h\u03bd and momentum p = h/\u03bb, propagating at the speed of light in vacuum. They exhibit both wave and particle behavior and can be created or annihilated in electromagnetic processes."},{"id":"c728a028-1878-419b-9d01-0f8959e06b4a","l":"Photonuclear reactions","d":1,"p":"bbb0ce0d-36da-4da5-a443-df064f36aa17","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Photonuclear reactions are nuclear processes initiated when a high-energy photon (typically a gamma ray) is absorbed by a nucleus, producing excitation that leads to emission of one or more nucleons or light fragments, photofission, or complete breakup (photodisintegration).  They commonly occur via the giant dipole resonance in the ~10\u201330 MeV range, with higher-energy regimes governed by quasi-deuteron absorption and photopion production."},{"id":"a4d8732e-5e4a-4246-924f-3db7507537aa","l":"Photosynthesis","d":1,"p":"af9f5a05-6917-40cf-a2ff-5520ff506935","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Photosynthesis is the process by which plants, algae, and some bacteria convert light (photon) energy into chemical energy, using pigment-driven charge separation and electron transport to generate ATP and reducing power that fix CO2 into sugars while releasing O2 from water."},{"id":"abc3dcc9-16b4-40a1-9694-092bdca2bcbd","l":"Physics of computation","d":1,"p":"fd7300dc-49e9-458d-9121-de94f7446fdb","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Physics of computation is the study of how physical laws govern the processing, transmission, and storage of information, determining fundamental limits on energy, speed, error rates, and resource costs of computation. It includes the thermodynamics of information (e.g., Landauer's principle), reversible and quantum computing, and the implementation of algorithms in real physical systems."},{"id":"12f6f3a9-8096-40e2-9170-e8f3a45d6bd9","l":"Physiological flows","d":1,"p":"21804fac-0546-45ed-98d5-b6af0f0d3228","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Physiological flows are the fluid motions inside living organisms\u2014such as blood in vessels, air in the lungs, lymph, cerebrospinal and interstitial fluids\u2014that transport mass, momentum, heat and biochemical species. They differ from idealized flows by features like compliant and evolving boundaries, non-Newtonian rheology, pulsatile or peristaltic driving, multiscale geometries, and strong coupling to biological processes."},{"id":"5677f3e5-3422-45c5-b460-00fc041663ee","l":"Piezoelectricity","d":1,"p":"41dd58dd-d35c-46e8-9594-d7e05b458da6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Piezoelectricity is the linear electromechanical coupling in non-centrosymmetric materials whereby applied mechanical stress produces an electric polarization or surface charge (direct effect) and an applied electric field produces mechanical strain (converse effect)."},{"id":"ea69605a-7cae-476a-a7da-28f3c186d709","l":"Planetary plasmas","d":1,"p":"88a5cd35-609c-4f0a-8994-5a4cbe488296","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Planetary plasmas are ionized gases found in and around planets, consisting of electrons and ions produced by processes such as solar ultraviolet ionization, particle precipitation, atmospheric escape, and surface sputtering. Their collective electromagnetic behavior in the presence of planetary magnetic and electric fields governs ionospheres, magnetospheres, plasma tori, and solar-wind\u2013planet interactions."},{"id":"79af0a5d-bb5e-41d4-ac34-634895fa02f5","l":"Planetary satellites & rings","d":1,"p":"c743ca36-88d6-4f26-adea-cdfd0732c4f9","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Planetary satellites and rings are the natural bodies and particulate systems gravitationally bound to a planet\u2014satellites (moons) are discrete solid bodies, while rings are flattened distributions of dust, ice and small particles orbiting the planet. Their structure and evolution are governed by gravity and orbital mechanics, including tidal forces, resonances, collisions, and accretion or disruption processes."},{"id":"9c136a51-04ad-4752-84f1-5c508214f321","l":"Plants","d":1,"p":"91278658-51b4-4d8e-a936-7cfad1266625","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Plants are multicellular, primarily photosynthetic organisms that convert sunlight, water, and CO2 into chemical energy and biomass via chloroplast-driven photochemistry. In physics they are studied as systems of light harvesting and energy conversion, fluid transport and porous-media flow, soft-matter mechanics of growth, and active/nonequilibrium biological processes."},{"id":"962ae19c-a556-4278-ba6c-8705953789a2","l":"Plasma-beam interactions","d":1,"p":"04f1f8c5-11c4-434d-9561-f9c615ba5813","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Plasma\u2013beam interactions are the collective and collisional processes by which a propagating charged-particle beam exchanges energy, momentum and charge with a plasma, driving plasma waves, wakefields and scattering while modifying the beam (e.g., deceleration, thermalization or neutralization). These interactions commonly produce instabilities such as the two\u2011stream (beam\u2011plasma) instability and are central to accelerators, fusion, and space-plasma phenomena."},{"id":"fa06d5b1-45b2-49f7-9315-6f97e3170bd1","l":"Plasma crystals","d":1,"p":"22580d6a-8d20-4653-b2d1-a7de314bc162","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Plasma crystals (or dusty plasma crystals) are ordered, solid-like lattices formed when micron-scale charged dust particles become suspended in a plasma and self-organize under mutual screened-Coulomb (Yukawa) repulsion plus external confinement (electric fields, gravity). They provide a visible, tunable model system for studying crystalline order, phase transitions, waves and defects in strongly coupled many-body plasmas."},{"id":"a5866236-c99c-4828-b499-4f50cccf7a2b","l":"Plasma diagnostic techniques","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Plasma diagnostic techniques are experimental methods used to measure and infer the physical properties and dynamics of plasmas\u2014such as electron/ion temperatures, particle densities, composition, electric and magnetic fields, and flow velocities. They employ a variety of invasive and non\u2011invasive tools (e.g., probes, optical and spectroscopic methods, Thomson scattering, interferometry, microwave reflectometry, and particle detectors) to extract quantitative information."},{"id":"bb8943b3-7db5-4825-96b5-9b4ae1da76cc","l":"Plasma discharges in liquid & solid","d":1,"p":"b30d0b9e-de6d-4de6-bfad-565f513573b7","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Plasma discharges in liquids and solids are electrical-breakdown events in which a strong applied field creates a transient, highly ionized region (a plasma channel or surface plasma) within or on the surface of a dense dielectric medium. These discharges are short-lived, highly localized, and strongly coupled to the surrounding material, producing extreme temperatures, pressures, shocks and reactive species that drive structural and chemical changes."},{"id":"9c96dfbb-393b-4413-b747-6be5e5051e5c","l":"Plasma discharges in vacuum","d":1,"p":"b30d0b9e-de6d-4de6-bfad-565f513573b7","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Plasma discharges in vacuum are electrical breakdown phenomena that produce and sustain ionized particle flows in environments with extremely low neutral-gas pressure. They arise when electrons emitted from surfaces (by field, thermionic or secondary emission) ionize residual gas or surface vapor and generate dense electron\u2013ion regions (cathode spots or vacuum arcs) that carry current across an electrode gap despite the lack of a background gas."},{"id":"9bbcc3c5-16bb-4a7f-adea-c23acc893cde","l":"Plasma instabilities","d":1,"p":"fad16b8f-e0c2-42ac-b5a2-6a9a82436c09","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Plasma instabilities are collective processes in which small perturbations to a plasma's equilibrium grow in time because free energy (e.g., pressure or temperature gradients, currents, beams, or velocity anisotropies) can be converted into waves or flows. They typically produce exponential amplification of fluctuations and lead to enhanced transport, wave emission, turbulence, or large-scale reconfiguration of the plasma."},{"id":"2df080ee-0735-49e9-b36b-e6da5f86a49c","l":"Plasma interactions with antennas","d":1,"p":"04f1f8c5-11c4-434d-9561-f9c615ba5813","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Plasma interactions with antennas are the coupling between an antenna\u2019s electromagnetic fields and the surrounding ionized gas, where the plasma's free charges and collective behavior modify wave propagation, antenna impedance, radiation pattern, and power absorption through dispersion, cutoffs/resonances, and sheath formation. These effects are important for space and ionospheric communications, active plasma experiments, and RF heating in fusion devices, because plasma loading, damping, and nonlinear sheath physics determine antenna performance and efficiency."},{"id":"0fe92aa1-a9c4-416f-bff0-5003e75bcf44","l":"Plasma kinetic theory","d":1,"p":"233a6cd0-b7fe-491b-b5fd-85fd6b2a7c79","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Plasma kinetic theory describes the time evolution of the phase\u2011space distribution functions of charged particles interacting with self\u2011consistent electromagnetic fields. It uses kinetic equations (e.g., Vlasov, Boltzmann, Fokker\u2013Planck) to capture collisions, collective effects, and wave\u2013particle interactions that lie beyond fluid descriptions."},{"id":"52218215-091d-40c7-96d0-ac828f418ddb","l":"Plasma oscillations","d":1,"p":"4c4f4d87-e837-4d65-970d-f5323698a5f1","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Plasma oscillations are collective, longitudinal oscillations of the electron density in a plasma, where electrons oscillate against a nearly fixed ion background under Coulomb restoring forces. In the small-amplitude, electrostatic limit these are called Langmuir waves and have a characteristic plasma frequency \u03c9p = sqrt(n e^2/(\u03b50 me))."},{"id":"4ba23eca-4c62-4056-9e29-a1b961c02a3e","l":"Plasma-particle interactions","d":1,"p":"04f1f8c5-11c4-434d-9561-f9c615ba5813","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Plasma-particle interactions are the processes by which individual particles (electrons, ions, neutrals, dust grains, or injected beams) exchange energy, momentum and charge with a plasma through collisions and through forces from the plasma's collective electromagnetic fields. These interactions produce effects such as scattering, ionization and recombination, particle charging, sputtering or implantation at surfaces, and wake or sheath formation around objects in the plasma."},{"id":"0d3c1c52-f6e3-474e-bb5d-f841c81a859b","l":"Plasma production & heating by high-voltage diodes","d":1,"p":"3741a35f-83a8-4e50-b7a2-38e600034925","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Plasma production and heating by high-voltage diodes is the generation and energization of plasma when a diode driven at large voltage produces intense electron emission and current that ionize a background gas or target surface. The energetic electron beams and accompanying return currents deposit energy into the forming plasma via collisions (electron-impact ionization and heating), Ohmic dissipation, and collective electromagnetic effects (instabilities and beam-plasma interactions)."},{"id":"31fddab4-6549-4d71-b7f9-2afaf3ab2b3e","l":"Plasma production & heating by laser beams, laser-foil, laser-cluster","d":1,"p":"3741a35f-83a8-4e50-b7a2-38e600034925","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Plasma production and heating by laser beams is the creation of ionized matter and the transfer of laser energy to its electrons and ions when intense optical pulses strike a target, via ionization (multiphoton, tunneling, impact) and coupling mechanisms such as inverse bremsstrahlung, resonant absorption and ponderomotive or collisionless heating. In laser\u2013foil interactions this produces dense hot plasmas with energetic electrons and sheath fields that can drive ion acceleration, while in laser\u2013cluster interactions compact nanoplasmas form that heat rapidly and undergo Coulomb-driven explosion yielding energetic ions and electrons."},{"id":"35fef4b0-79f5-4800-a8d0-ab43e384fb74","l":"Plasma production & heating by particle beams","d":1,"p":"3741a35f-83a8-4e50-b7a2-38e600034925","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Plasma production and heating by particle beams is the creation and energetic charging of a plasma by injecting a focused stream of energetic particles (electrons, ions or neutrals) that ionize neutral gas and transfer kinetic energy to plasma electrons and ions. Energy deposition occurs via binary collisions (Coulomb stopping and ionization), induced return currents and Ohmic heating, and collective beam\u2013plasma processes (wave excitation and instabilities), and is used in fusion drivers, materials processing, and laboratory astrophysics."},{"id":"cd816ccf-4217-43ca-b8a5-e9b147e23fb8","l":"Plasma production & heating by shock waves & compression","d":1,"p":"3741a35f-83a8-4e50-b7a2-38e600034925","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Plasma production and heating by shock waves and compression is the process by which rapid mechanical compression or a propagating shock converts bulk kinetic energy into internal energy, raising gas density and temperature until atoms dissociate and ionize. In collisional media this occurs via adiabatic compression and viscous/thermodynamic jump heating, while in collisionless or magnetized plasmas additional heating and particle energization arise from wave\u2013particle interactions, turbulence, and electromagnetic compression (e.g., in inertial confinement, shock tubes, or astrophysical shocks)."},{"id":"1608f2b3-a536-43c2-9df7-ecb4d24ce298","l":"Plasma sheaths & surfaces","d":1,"p":"22580d6a-8d20-4653-b2d1-a7de314bc162","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Plasma sheaths are thin (typically a few Debye lengths) non-neutral boundary layers that form where a plasma meets a material or electrode; they sustain a potential drop that repels electrons and accelerates ions, setting the particle and energy fluxes to the surface. Plasma-facing surfaces then undergo charging and modification (sputtering, deposition, secondary electron emission, chemical reactions), with the sheath controlling erosion, deposition, and overall plasma\u2013surface interaction."},{"id":"5d23a3ea-babf-44d8-802d-16728e5acf6c","l":"Plasma-solid interactions","d":1,"p":"04f1f8c5-11c4-434d-9561-f9c615ba5813","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Plasma\u2013solid interactions are the processes by which an ionized gas (plasma) exchanges particles, energy and charge with a solid surface, producing effects such as sheath formation, surface charging, sputtering, implantation, deposition and chemical modification. These interactions, governed by electromagnetic fields and particle kinetics across atomic-to-macroscopic scales, determine material erosion, contamination and performance in applications from fusion and space environments to semiconductor processing."},{"id":"31d16456-2d2e-457a-8073-496213e6a31c","l":"Plasma sources","d":1,"p":"3741a35f-83a8-4e50-b7a2-38e600034925","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Plasma sources are devices or processes that create and sustain plasmas\u2014ionized gases with free electrons and ions\u2014by supplying energy (electrical, electromagnetic, or optical) to ionize and heat a working gas. Common types include DC and RF discharges, inductively or capacitively coupled plasmas, microwave- or laser-produced plasmas, arcs and plasma jets, and they are characterized by operating pressure, power coupling, and degree of ionization."},{"id":"c3c70482-883f-4acd-ada3-7f9659dad973","l":"Plasma stability","d":1,"p":"c25108e7-4697-4565-b484-221b808d454e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Plasma Physics"],"def":"Plasma stability describes whether a plasma equilibrium resists or amplifies small perturbations: a stable plasma damps disturbances and returns toward equilibrium, while an unstable plasma develops growing modes that reconfigure the flow or magnetic structure. Stability is assessed by linear and nonlinear perturbation analysis and depends on factors such as magnetic geometry, pressure and current profiles, flows, and dissipative effects (e.g., ideal versus resistive instabilities)."},{"id":"4004f9fc-e9a4-4b78-95f4-5c8e513aed6c","l":"Plasma surface high-order harmonics","d":1,"p":"a7a00bcb-5b8d-46f1-876d-978833fa4036","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Plasma surface high-order harmonics are high-frequency components produced when an intense laser pulse strikes an overdense plasma surface, driving nonlinear oscillations of the surface electrons that re-radiate light at integer multiples of the incident frequency. In the relativistic regime this \"oscillating mirror\" (and related mechanisms like coherent synchrotron or wake emission) yields coherent extreme-ultraviolet/soft\u2011x\u2011ray harmonics and ultrashort (attosecond) pulses."},{"id":"f865bc1c-195f-4bbb-a69b-ccb81565563a","l":"Plasma turbulence","d":1,"p":"fad16b8f-e0c2-42ac-b5a2-6a9a82436c09","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Plasma turbulence is the chaotic, multiscale state of a plasma in which nonlinear interactions among waves, fields, and particle motions produce broadband fluctuations that transfer energy across scales (a cascade) and drive enhanced transport, mixing, and heating. It arises from instabilities and wave\u2013particle coupling and is central to behavior in fusion devices, the solar wind, and astrophysical plasmas."},{"id":"19fa9995-0e6c-4549-936d-f16ccc0e73a5","l":"Plasma-wall interactions","d":1,"p":"04f1f8c5-11c4-434d-9561-f9c615ba5813","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Plasma\u2013wall interactions are the collection of physical and chemical processes that occur where a plasma contacts a material surface, mediated by a thin electric sheath and by fluxes of ions, electrons, neutrals, and radiation. These processes \u2014 including surface heating, sputtering and erosion, implantation and adsorption, secondary electron emission, and chemical reactions \u2014 determine impurity release and surface modification and thus strongly affect the performance and lifetime of fusion devices, spacecraft, and plasma-processing systems."},{"id":"e3571632-af1b-4c60-95c4-77d762f9fbc6","l":"Plasmonics","d":1,"p":"23cdcd6b-ae22-4b6d-92cd-24a9a8bd0577","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Plasmonics is the study of plasmons\u2014collective oscillations of a free electron gas in metals or doped semiconductors\u2014and their coupling to electromagnetic fields, which allows light to be confined and guided at subwavelength (nanometer) scales. It underpins technologies such as nanoscale waveguides, surface-enhanced spectroscopies, and plasmonic metamaterials."},{"id":"0b46d855-14dd-4d47-8df1-76e3e60ebfd3","l":"Plasmons","d":1,"p":"d46f32eb-17b3-4d09-be59-fc3461e542db","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Plasmons are collective oscillations of the free-electron (charge) density in a conductor or plasma, driven by Coulomb restoring forces and often treated as bosonic quasiparticles. They couple to electromagnetic fields and occur as bulk modes in the volume or as surface modes localized at interfaces (surface plasmons)."},{"id":"ec4add19-c185-4aa3-80b4-8e95f6775eed","l":"Plasticity","d":1,"p":"15f520a3-5c6c-4d9e-8975-81d399140562","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Plasticity is the property of a material to undergo permanent, non\u2011recoverable deformation when applied stresses exceed its elastic limit (yield point). In solids this irreversible flow is typically carried by mechanisms such as dislocation motion in crystals or localized shear transformations in amorphous materials and depends on stress, temperature, strain rate and microstructure."},{"id":"73578754-5267-4c14-8bc3-b692910d824f","l":"Plumes & thermals","d":1,"p":"2d276ce6-c803-4ba3-813c-b989f2d4bfde","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Plumes and thermals are buoyancy\u2011driven coherent flows in a fluid created by localized heating or compositional differences that transport heat, mass, and momentum by rising (or sinking) columns or blobs while entraining ambient fluid. A plume is a sustained source\u2011driven jet that forms a continuous, self\u2011similar column, whereas a thermal is an isolated buoyant \u201cbubble\u201d or vortex\u2011ring that rises, expands and mixes with its surroundings."},{"id":"41cab86b-d3ca-479d-a3df-a98515b02344","l":"Point contacts","d":1,"p":"8508c727-cef4-4fe3-a99a-64b7319d4e79","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Point contacts are localized interfaces where two bodies or conductors touch over a region whose dimensions are negligible compared with other relevant length scales, so the junction can be treated as an essentially single point. They serve as useful idealizations in mechanics (concentrated forces, Hertzian contact) and in electronic transport (narrow constrictions that yield ballistic or quantum conduction, as in quantum point contacts and point\u2011contact spectroscopy)."},{"id":"20026a42-2299-4239-b21b-8ee23c3b20c4","l":"Poisson degree distribution networks","d":1,"p":"fd7cfd2b-a957-4dcd-b32d-b9d00e6aba33","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Networks in which the probability that a node has degree k is given by a Poisson distribution, P(k) = e^{-\u27e8k\u27e9}\u27e8k\u27e9^k/k!, typically arising in sparse random graphs such as Erd\u0151s\u2013R\u00e9nyi models. Such networks have a narrow degree distribution with variance equal to the mean and thus lack the high-degree hubs seen in heavy-tailed networks."},{"id":"27ddfc6a-bdbc-4a96-b380-735af822565d","l":"Polariton condensate","d":1,"p":"db7ba2bd-700a-4b0b-b444-b873c97ff818","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"A polariton condensate is a macroscopic coherent state of exciton\u2013polaritons\u2014hybrid light\u2013matter quasiparticles formed in semiconductor microcavities\u2014where many particles occupy a single quantum state, analogous to Bose\u2013Einstein condensation. Because polaritons have a short lifetime, these condensates are typically driven-dissipative, non-equilibrium systems that display long-range coherence and superfluid-like collective phenomena."},{"id":"69adcacb-6270-4cfc-8cf5-60097ec74d8b","l":"Polaritons","d":1,"p":"d46f32eb-17b3-4d09-be59-fc3461e542db","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Polaritons are hybrid quasiparticles that arise when electromagnetic modes (photons) strongly couple to collective excitations in a material (e.g., excitons, phonons, plasmons, or magnons), producing new mixed light\u2013matter eigenmodes. They combine photon-like dispersion with matter-like interactions, giving altered dispersion relations, reduced effective mass, and enhanced nonlinear and coherence effects (e.g., slowed light or Bose\u2013Einstein\u2013like condensation)."},{"id":"9758c79f-f66f-4b0d-b45f-50ef0f22a789","l":"Polarizability","d":1,"p":"ff402d94-d1d8-4207-a321-f935f060b175","fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Polarizability is a measure of how easily an object's charge distribution (e.g., an atom, molecule, or dielectric) is distorted by an external electric field, quantified by the induced dipole moment p per applied field E (p = \u03b1E). In general \u03b1 is a tensor (reducing to a scalar for isotropic particles) and can depend on frequency and other conditions."},{"id":"60e8a165-69d6-4413-9242-82a2199f29af","l":"Polarization","d":1,"p":"ff402d94-d1d8-4207-a321-f935f060b175","fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Polarization is the property of a transverse wave that specifies the orientation and relative phase of its oscillations perpendicular to the direction of propagation \u2014 for light this means the direction and phase of the electric field and can be linear, circular, or elliptical. More generally, in materials physics \"polarization\" denotes a macroscopic alignment or imbalance of a vector quantity (e.g., electric dipole moment density or spin), indicating a preferred direction."},{"id":"707ca316-ba1c-4a57-bcfd-815a2356b0a9","l":"Polarization phenomena","d":1,"p":"bbb0ce0d-36da-4da5-a443-df064f36aa17","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Polarization phenomena are effects that depend on the orientation of a directional property of a system\u2014most commonly the oscillation direction of an electromagnetic wave's electric field or the alignment of particle spins\u2014so that propagation, absorption, scattering, or interactions vary with that orientation. Examples include linear and circular polarization of light (and resulting birefringence, dichroism, and optical activity) and spin polarization in solids and particle beams."},{"id":"adf7e946-b75b-480d-8c59-a141650ae46c","l":"Polarons","d":1,"p":"d46f32eb-17b3-4d09-be59-fc3461e542db","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A polaron is a quasiparticle formed when a charge carrier (electron or hole) interacts with and polarizes its surrounding lattice, dragging a cloud of phonons with it; this dressing by lattice distortions renormalizes the carrier's energy, effective mass, and transport properties. Depending on the strength of the electron\u2013phonon coupling and material parameters, polarons can be \"large\" and delocalized or \"small\" and self-trapped, with distinct conduction behavior."},{"id":"81dbff5e-d110-4acf-8973-0d85e54516d9","l":"Polycrystalline materials","d":1,"p":"1128a9f3-70c1-409b-84b2-44063aa12108","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Polycrystalline materials are solids made up of many small crystalline grains (crystallites), each a single crystal with its own orientation, joined together by grain boundaries. Because the grains are misoriented relative to one another, the bulk lacks the single-crystal\u2019s long-range orientational order and its properties depend strongly on grain size, shape, and boundary structure."},{"id":"3b122d7b-8212-4bdf-8c44-1862dc0fcd5a","l":"Polycrystals","d":1,"p":"e521b9c7-1f62-48e4-a3fc-9428f629d078","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Polycrystals are solids composed of many small crystallites or grains, each a region of uniform lattice orientation, joined together by grain boundaries. The distribution of grain orientations and the structure of the boundaries strongly affect macroscopic properties (mechanical strength, electrical and thermal transport) and often yield more isotropic behavior than a single crystal."},{"id":"549639d8-2272-4363-acfb-a16c31816771","l":"Polyelectrolytes","d":1,"p":"fea036a1-9c29-4872-ab2c-d052949f7c03","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Polyelectrolytes are polymers bearing ionizable groups that dissociate in polar solvents to produce charged macromolecular chains and mobile counterions. Their conformations and interactions are dominated by long-range electrostatic forces, counterion screening/condensation, and strong sensitivity to ionic strength and pH."},{"id":"54fd3d71-4f0d-4073-9f8e-0d437584221f","l":"Polymer blends","d":1,"p":"fea036a1-9c29-4872-ab2c-d052949f7c03","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Polymer blends are physical mixtures of two or more different polymers combined to produce materials with tailored mechanical, thermal, or chemical properties. Their macroscopic behavior depends on thermodynamic compatibility\u2014ranging from a single miscible phase to phase-separated multiphase morphologies whose domain size and interfaces (often modified by compatibilizers) control properties like strength, toughness, and transport."},{"id":"dac7e77a-5a64-4b55-8cc0-c707d63baaad","l":"Polymer brushes","d":1,"p":"fea036a1-9c29-4872-ab2c-d052949f7c03","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Polymer brushes are dense layers of polymer chains chemically or physically tethered by one end to a surface or interface at a grafting density high enough that neighboring chains crowd and stretch away from the surface, producing a brushlike coating. Their thickness, mechanical and interfacial properties depend on chain length, grafting density and solvent quality, giving rise to effects like steric stabilization, lubrication, and stimulus-responsive behavior."},{"id":"910b0011-1066-4b4c-871a-357e24b7243d","l":"Polymer composites","d":1,"p":"fea036a1-9c29-4872-ab2c-d052949f7c03","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Polymer composites are engineered heterogeneous materials in which a continuous polymer matrix binds and transfers load to dispersed reinforcing elements (fibers, particles, or nanoscale fillers), combining the polymer\u2019s processability and toughness with the reinforcements\u2019 stiffness and strength. Their macroscopic mechanical, thermal, and electrical properties depend on reinforcement geometry, volume fraction, and interface quality, and are often anisotropic."},{"id":"0746cce9-22be-4e46-a152-53fb5b25c647","l":"Polymer conformation changes","d":1,"p":"5114e95c-b4a7-458f-a2f7-b49a98b828cd","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Polymer conformation changes are reversible alterations in a polymer chain\u2019s three-dimensional shape produced by rotations about backbone bonds (without changing chemical connectivity), driven by thermal fluctuations, solvent quality, interactions, or external fields. They are quantified by measures like end-to-end distance or radius of gyration and underlie phenomena such as coil\u2013globule transitions, swelling, and mechanical unfolding."},{"id":"8da34010-6805-4ff1-93ed-6692da601029","l":"Polymer conformation & topology","d":1,"p":"233556f2-7015-49bd-ad5c-867dda4753e1","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Polymer conformation is the instantaneous three\u2011dimensional arrangement of a polymer chain\u2019s monomers set by bond lengths, bond angles and torsional rotations, usually described statistically (e.g., end\u2011to\u2011end distance or radius of gyration) because thermal fluctuations continually change it. Polymer topology refers to the chain\u2019s global connectivity and entanglement invariants\u2014linear vs. cyclic, branching, knots or links\u2014that cannot be changed by continuous deformations without cutting bonds and which strongly affect entropic, rheological and transport properties."},{"id":"487333ce-9de7-4bab-a01d-6747b994802f","l":"Polymer entanglement","d":1,"p":"5114e95c-b4a7-458f-a2f7-b49a98b828cd","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Polymer entanglement refers to the topological constraints that arise when long flexible polymer chains in a melt or concentrated solution interpenetrate and become mutually hindered, so that each chain's motion is restricted by its neighbors. These non\u2011chemical, transient constraints give rise to slowed, viscoelastic dynamics (described by tube/reptation models) and are quantified by parameters such as the entanglement molecular weight and plateau modulus."},{"id":"992fdbaf-efdb-4139-8181-7504c4f3a226","l":"Polymer folding","d":1,"p":"5114e95c-b4a7-458f-a2f7-b49a98b828cd","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Polymer folding is the collapse and rearrangement of a polymer chain from an extended coil into a compact, often ordered three-dimensional conformation driven by intramolecular interactions and environmental factors (solvent quality, temperature), reflecting a balance between enthalpic gains (favorable contacts) and the entropic cost of reduced configurational freedom. In sequence-specific biopolymers (e.g., proteins, RNA) this can produce a unique native structure with specific function, while in simple polymers it often appears as a coil\u2013globule transition."},{"id":"df5355b1-5d9f-4da8-ad47-98013d224293","l":"Polymer gels","d":1,"p":"fea036a1-9c29-4872-ab2c-d052949f7c03","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Polymer gels are soft, viscoelastic materials made of a crosslinked network of polymer chains that entrap and swell with a large fraction of solvent. Their mechanical and transport behavior is governed by the network's elastic (entropic) response balanced against osmotic swelling and solvent flow (poroelasticity)."},{"id":"c26fc9ac-2da9-4baf-a68e-f0848b24c3d3","l":"Polymer glasses","d":1,"p":"fea036a1-9c29-4872-ab2c-d052949f7c03","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Polymer glasses are amorphous, non\u2011crystalline polymer solids in which chain\u2011segment mobility is kinetically frozen below the glass transition temperature (Tg), giving the material a rigid, glassy state. They show viscoelastic behavior and slow structural relaxation (physical aging) as the frozen conformations gradually approach equilibrium."},{"id":"68d1b001-0a62-46d3-beb6-c41043b31279","l":"Polymerization","d":1,"p":"dba0f19a-3cbb-43dd-a780-1190b1a5d8ae","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Polymerization is the chemical process in which small molecules (monomers) covalently bond to form long-chain or network macromolecules (polymers) composed of repeating units. It occurs by mechanisms such as step-growth or chain-growth (e.g., radical, ionic, or coordination) and produces materials with markedly different mechanical, thermal, and rheological properties than the monomers."},{"id":"9e8c2f04-b59a-44fa-95ce-8c513cdb9c37","l":"Polymer melts","d":1,"p":"fea036a1-9c29-4872-ab2c-d052949f7c03","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Polymer melts are solvent-free, dense liquids of long-chain macromolecules held at temperatures above their glass transition (and above melting for semicrystalline polymers), where the chains are mobile and interpenetrate. Their flow and mechanical response are dominated by chain connectivity and topological entanglements, giving rise to viscoelastic, often strongly non-Newtonian, behavior."},{"id":"2303d382-65bb-4b93-94b8-62a73502ec4b","l":"Polymers by architecture","d":1,"p":"fea036a1-9c29-4872-ab2c-d052949f7c03","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Polymers by architecture classifies polymers according to the connectivity and topology of their macromolecular chains \u2014 e.g., linear, branched, star, comb, graft, bottlebrush, cyclic, dendrimer, crosslinked/network, and block copolymer architectures. Molecular architecture strongly controls the polymer's physical behavior (rheology, dynamics, mechanical and thermal properties, and self-assembly/phase behavior) for a given chemical composition and molecular weight."},{"id":"e70f3138-b003-4fe8-af25-d63ecec3255c","l":"Polymer solutions","d":1,"p":"fea036a1-9c29-4872-ab2c-d052949f7c03","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Polymer solutions are homogeneous mixtures in which long, flexible macromolecular chains are dissolved in a solvent so that the chains adopt coil-like conformations and interact with each other and the solvent. Because of the polymers' large size and chain connectivity, their thermodynamic and transport properties (osmotic pressure, viscosity, diffusion, and mechanical response) are strongly concentration- and interaction-dependent, showing regimes (dilute, semidilute, concentrated), scaling laws, entanglements, and viscoelastic, often non-Newtonian, behavior."},{"id":"83e4b22f-a9c3-4334-8eb2-a3a0e437fa41","l":"Polymer suspensions","d":1,"p":"fea036a1-9c29-4872-ab2c-d052949f7c03","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Polymer suspensions are two\u2011phase fluids in which polymer chains or polymeric particles (e.g., coils, microgels, latex) are dispersed in a solvent. Their microstructure\u2014polymer conformation, entanglements and hydrodynamic/colloidal interactions\u2014gives rise to complex, typically non\u2011Newtonian and viscoelastic rheological behavior."},{"id":"21d58e93-c4e1-4a67-a283-e1ce49414290","l":"Polymer synthesis","d":1,"p":"dba0f19a-3cbb-43dd-a780-1190b1a5d8ae","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Polymer synthesis is the chemical process by which small monomer molecules are covalently linked to form long-chain or network macromolecules (polymers). It includes mechanisms such as chain-growth (addition) and step-growth (condensation) polymerization, with molecular weight, architecture and composition controlled by monomer choice, catalysts, reaction kinetics and conditions."},{"id":"c267a3f3-0c34-4fc1-9825-947683caf4dd","l":"Polymer theory","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Polymer theory is the branch of statistical physics and soft-matter science that models the structure, thermodynamics, and dynamics of long-chain macromolecules by treating them as connected sequences of repeating units (monomers). It uses concepts like random and self-avoiding walks, scaling and mean-field theories, and dynamical models (Rouse, Zimm, reptation) to predict polymer conformations, viscoelastic behavior, and phase behavior in solutions and melts."},{"id":"2479507d-e3a5-4c91-83f9-1b0d92c27d19","l":"Polymer thin films","d":1,"p":"fea036a1-9c29-4872-ab2c-d052949f7c03","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Polymer thin films are layers of polymeric material with thicknesses from a few nanometers to micrometers where confinement and surface/interface interactions significantly alter chain conformations and collective properties. As a result they show modified glass\u2011transition and viscoelastic behavior, anisotropic structure, dewetting and instability phenomena, and altered transport/optical properties important for coatings, electronics, and membranes."},{"id":"14c40874-6a7c-4ca6-8710-701fc295550e","l":"Polymer translocation","d":1,"p":"5114e95c-b4a7-458f-a2f7-b49a98b828cd","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Polymer translocation is the process by which a long flexible macromolecule (e.g., DNA, RNA, or a synthetic polymer) threads through a narrow pore or constriction from one side of a membrane or barrier to the other. Its kinetics are governed by entropic barriers, polymer\u2013pore interactions and any external driving forces (electric fields, pressure, binding), and are often described by scaling laws for translocation time versus chain length."},{"id":"7954ba21-04fe-4967-ae52-4779f4f7cf8d","l":"Populations","d":1,"p":"fcb85338-c881-4bd4-98a9-1563b1194606","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Populations are the numbers or fractional occupancies of entities (particles, atoms, energy levels, quantum states, or species) in specific states or categories within a physical system. They are described by statistical distributions (e.g., Boltzmann, Fermi\u2013Dirac, Bose\u2013Einstein) and by dynamical rate or master equations, and they determine macroscopic observables and system evolution."},{"id":"84d35b4d-ab71-42a6-89e7-f25e73d8401f","l":"Porous materials","d":1,"p":"81194f70-bbca-4041-b57e-29338e588cac","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Porous materials are solids that contain an internal network of pores (voids) distributed throughout the solid matrix. Their porosity, pore-size distribution and connectivity (e.g., open versus closed pores) govern fluid and heat transport, permeability and mechanical properties."},{"id":"4503aa80-d8c3-4d77-b13f-ed0c8065fb28","l":"Positron diffraction","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Positron diffraction is the coherent elastic scattering of a beam of positrons from a periodic atomic arrangement (surface or bulk), producing interference patterns that reveal the crystal or surface structure. Because positrons are positively charged and interact differently with the ionic potential and electron density than electrons, their diffraction gives complementary information about surface geometry, defects and electronic environments."},{"id":"c682c3d2-f4af-486d-986d-8dfc0fb784f9","l":"Positronium","d":1,"p":"bd252d88-a608-4897-9416-daf84dee7f6b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Positronium is a hydrogen-like exotic atom made of an electron and its antiparticle, a positron, bound by their Coulomb attraction; it is metastable and eventually annihilates into gamma rays. It occurs in a spin-singlet (para-positronium, decays mainly into two photons with lifetime ~10^-10 s) and a spin-triplet (ortho-positronium, decays mainly into three photons with lifetime ~10^-7 s) state."},{"id":"569c41ec-e899-478e-a59f-b94e7abc2111","l":"Positrons","d":1,"p":"a45e2665-2df6-42e0-99dc-30bd93812c2c","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"A positron (e+), or antielectron, is the electron's antiparticle: it has the same mass and spin as an electron but opposite electric charge (+e). Positrons are produced in processes like \u03b2+ (beta-plus) decay and pair production, and when they meet electrons they annihilate, typically yielding two 511 keV gamma photons."},{"id":"a873b833-e42e-448a-8981-b2ab2996ee19","l":"Potential energy surfaces","d":1,"p":"30e65d01-6cd5-442b-889f-ddc0c3c28157","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"A potential energy surface (PES) is the multidimensional function giving the potential energy of a molecular or atomic system as a function of its nuclear coordinates, usually computed within the Born\u2013Oppenheimer approximation where the electronic energy depends parametrically on nuclear geometry. Minima on the surface correspond to stable structures and saddle points to transition states that govern reaction pathways, while different electronic states produce distinct coupled surfaces relevant for nonadiabatic processes."},{"id":"3d537e22-0ca1-46d7-821d-0e52f3a11e6c","l":"Power supplies","d":1,"p":"4c286df9-b565-4295-b951-49a36d60cad1","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Power supplies are devices or systems that convert energy from a primary source into controlled electrical voltage and current for powering circuits and equipment, while regulating output to remain stable under changing input or load conditions. Common forms include batteries, AC\u2013DC rectifiers with regulators, linear regulators, and switching (DC\u2013DC or AC\u2013DC) converters."},{"id":"09bdc129-b92c-4bd4-80a5-358508775cde","l":"Precision measurements","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Precision measurements are experimental determinations made with exceptionally small uncertainties and high reproducibility, aimed at resolving tiny effects or tightly constraining physical parameters. They rely on stringent control of statistical and systematic errors, careful calibration, and often long integration or large datasets to achieve the required sensitivity."},{"id":"f6791e7c-5735-454e-9149-7ee3d36e1f69","l":"Preferential attachment","d":1,"p":"720fbd6c-ad91-46b1-9958-296aa626f983","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Preferential attachment is a network-growth rule in which new nodes are more likely to connect to existing nodes with higher degree (links), so that \"the rich get richer.\" This mechanism generically produces hubs and power-law (scale-free) degree distributions, as in the Barab\u00e1si\u2013Albert model."},{"id":"adba71aa-d89f-407f-b563-b2226d3e370c","l":"Pre-main-sequence stars","d":1,"p":"b6d90b06-7836-486e-8e77-221ad2624e1e","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Pre-main-sequence stars are young stellar objects that have finished most protostellar accretion and are contracting quasi-statically toward the main sequence, radiating primarily by gravitational (Kelvin\u2013Helmholtz) contraction until core temperatures become high enough for sustained hydrogen fusion. On the Hertzsprung\u2013Russell diagram they lie above the main sequence and follow Hayashi or Henyey evolutionary tracks (examples include T Tauri and Herbig Ae/Be stars)."},{"id":"8fa26c4d-d115-4c5c-9822-9e90b9248d1b","l":"Pressure effects","d":1,"p":"15f520a3-5c6c-4d9e-8975-81d399140562","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Pressure effects are the changes in a material's structural, electronic, magnetic, optical, mechanical, and transport properties induced by applied pressure (hydrostatic or anisotropic). By altering interatomic distances and interaction energies, pressure can drive phase transitions and modify band structures, superconductivity, conductivity, magnetism, optical gaps, and reaction rates."},{"id":"3bbe5675-f826-44e6-8e86-f17066618893","l":"Pressure techniques","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Pressure techniques are experimental methods that apply controlled static or dynamic pressure to a material or system to modify and probe its structural, electronic, magnetic, optical, and mechanical properties and to map phase behavior and equations of state. Common implementations range from gas or piston-cylinder cells and multi\u2011anvil presses to diamond\u2011anvil cells and shock\u2011compression setups, spanning pressures from ambient to millions of atmospheres."},{"id":"1051268c-9a10-41db-954f-19696de65ec6","l":"Primordial black holes","d":1,"p":"27862afe-0467-49b2-a792-5b376b520666","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Primordial black holes are hypothetical black holes that formed in the very early universe from the direct gravitational collapse of large density fluctuations, phase transitions, or collapsing topological defects rather than from dying stars. They could span a very wide mass range \u2014 from subatomic scales to many solar masses \u2014 and are studied as possible dark-matter candidates and sources of Hawking radiation."},{"id":"9012336e-01ae-41cc-bfde-5b3167929379","l":"Primordial magnetic fields","d":1,"p":"26ed7afc-8635-4637-a339-8d0a7cb75be1","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Primordial magnetic fields are magnetic fields generated in the early universe\u2014during or before recombination\u2014by processes such as inflation, cosmological phase transitions, or plasma effects, and which could serve as the seeds of the magnetic fields observed in galaxies and clusters today. Their amplitude, coherence scale, and spectrum are uncertain but they can affect cosmic microwave background anisotropies and the growth of large-scale structure."},{"id":"bcbbe936-13ac-4859-a252-5ea05e14aa01","l":"Probabilistic computing","d":1,"p":"fd7300dc-49e9-458d-9121-de94f7446fdb","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Probabilistic computing is a computing paradigm that represents and manipulates information as probability distributions and stochastic variables instead of deterministic bits, using random processes or noise as a native resource. It exploits physical randomness (thermal, quantum, or engineered stochastic devices) to perform tasks like sampling, inference, optimization, and machine learning more efficiently or robustly than fully deterministic architectures."},{"id":"c2ea9e2c-0e15-49ee-b336-111976b0b965","l":"Processes in cells, tissues & organoids","d":1,"p":"b26070f8-32ca-4e02-94d0-ba1961b3480d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Processes in cells, tissues & organoids are the physical mechanisms that govern the dynamics, structure, and function of individual cells, their multicellular assemblies, and lab-grown organoid models. They include molecular transport and signaling, reaction\u2013diffusion patterning, mechano-chemical coupling, active force generation, stochastic fluctuations, and collective behaviors underlying growth, morphogenesis, and homeostasis."},{"id":"591a9de6-1564-49a1-9319-fb676c8353a8","l":"Promoters","d":1,"p":"9990b97d-b485-4ad7-ac0a-33ab94e0e794","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Promoters are substances added in small amounts to a catalyst or material to enhance its activity, selectivity, or stability without being the primary active component. They modify the catalyst\u2019s surface electronic structure, adsorption properties, or morphology (via electronic, geometric, or chemical effects) to improve performance."},{"id":"6e1bbe22-c6fb-42b5-8ff6-1f61db13fdc4","l":"Protein dynamics, structure & function","d":1,"p":"5be420fe-48f0-4430-a63d-9d611d837d6e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Protein dynamics, structure & function concerns how a protein\u2019s three-dimensional fold and its time-dependent conformational fluctuations determine and enable biological activity such as catalysis, binding, and allosteric regulation. It uses physical concepts\u2014energy landscapes, thermal fluctuations, and statistical mechanics\u2014plus tools like molecular dynamics to link atomic-scale motions across femtosecond-to-second timescales to function."},{"id":"8b9018cc-53c1-499d-af37-bcd57cd080ee","l":"Protein interaction networks","d":1,"p":"d87d9c51-bdc2-43bb-99c3-6e5345a7f631","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Protein interaction networks are graph representations in which nodes are proteins and edges denote physical or functional interactions (binding, complex formation, post\u2011translational regulation, or signaling) between them. They provide a framework for applying network theory and dynamical models to study cellular organization, information flow, robustness, and emergent behaviors."},{"id":"3985b0f8-5a86-4628-b81a-d283a4f93b76","l":"Proton emission","d":1,"p":"1cf75de2-e14b-41cd-b733-8f704652db33","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Proton emission is a radioactive decay mode in which an unstable, proton-rich nucleus ejects a single proton, reducing its atomic number by one. It usually occurs in nuclei beyond the proton drip line via quantum tunneling through the Coulomb and centrifugal barriers and is used to probe nuclear structure and single-particle states."},{"id":"f3ca6e92-ba48-475b-a2f2-e5bc9e7181a1","l":"Proton & ion sources","d":1,"p":"b338ef70-aa71-4ec1-a73a-03a275bda631","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Proton and ion sources are devices that produce beams of charged particles by ionizing neutral atoms or molecules (e.g., via plasmas, electron impact, laser ablation, or field ionization) and extracting the resulting ions with electric fields. They deliver controlled ion species, currents and energies for use in accelerators, ion implantation, mass spectrometry, materials processing and beam experiments."},{"id":"661fd1bf-0214-438d-8b02-415f5f1e0924","l":"Proximity effect","d":1,"p":"ca8ddaad-441c-481c-93b3-65f46125fb93","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The proximity effect is the induction of one material's order (most often superconducting pair correlations) into an adjacent, non\u2011superconducting material when they are in good contact, altering the latter's electronic properties over a characteristic decay length. Reciprocally, the superconductor's order parameter can be suppressed near the interface; the phenomenon is mediated by processes such as Andreev reflection."},{"id":"cb28db21-062a-4267-aa8e-75f235749305","l":"PT-symmetric quantum mechanics","d":1,"p":"01f70d47-cbac-4b5a-8ff9-0768e722511b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"PT-symmetric quantum mechanics is an extension of quantum theory in which the Hamiltonian need not be Hermitian but instead commutes with the combined parity (P) and time-reversal (T) operations; such PT-symmetric non-Hermitian Hamiltonians can nevertheless have entirely real spectra and generate unitary time evolution while the PT symmetry is unbroken. When PT symmetry is spontaneously broken the eigenvalues occur in complex-conjugate pairs, and a modified positive-definite inner product (e.g., via a C operator or pseudo-Hermitian map) is required to restore unitary evolution."},{"id":"1427a2d4-7758-4109-93fa-7690418fb149","l":"Pulsed-power accelerators","d":1,"p":"d0bb99e3-9d07-40c6-80a0-d8858b63da18","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Pulsed-power accelerators are systems that store electrical energy over relatively long times and release it in very short, intense pulses to produce extremely large voltages and currents, creating the strong electric and magnetic fields needed to accelerate charged particles or compress plasmas. They employ capacitor banks, pulse-forming networks and fast switches (e.g., Marx generators, water or solid-state switches) to convert stored energy into high-power, short-duration bursts for applications in particle beams, z-pinches and high-energy-density physics."},{"id":"ba28b2de-1199-404d-a4b3-0eb53a3a0ca9","l":"Pyroelectricity","d":1,"p":"41dd58dd-d35c-46e8-9594-d7e05b458da6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Pyroelectricity is the property of certain non-centrosymmetric, polar dielectric crystals whereby a change in temperature alters their spontaneous electric polarization, producing a temporary surface charge or voltage. This temperature-driven polarization change yields a measurable current while the temperature is changing and is related to (but distinct from) piezoelectricity."},{"id":"53dbf388-0773-4e60-83b9-13de84487845","l":"QCD in nuclear reactions","d":1,"p":"bbb0ce0d-36da-4da5-a443-df064f36aa17","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"QCD in nuclear reactions is the application of quantum chromodynamics\u2014the theory of quarks, gluons, and color interactions\u2014to describe how the strong force builds nucleons and nuclei and governs their scattering, breakup, and fusion. At low energies it appears through effective hadronic forces between nucleons, while at high energies it controls partonic processes such as deep-inelastic scattering, nuclear modifications of parton distributions (EMC effect), color transparency, and quark\u2013gluon plasma formation."},{"id":"b9110fa6-0c37-4c22-b39b-e90d8cacdc6f","l":"QCD phase transitions","d":1,"p":"7321e0f7-3b12-45a2-aa4a-28c2a7e03149","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"QCD phase transitions are changes in the equilibrium state of strongly interacting matter predicted by quantum chromodynamics as temperature or baryon density is varied \u2014 most notably the confinement\u2013deconfinement change from a hadron gas to a quark\u2013gluon plasma and the simultaneous restoration of chiral symmetry. They can occur as first-order transitions, second-order transitions, or smooth crossovers (with a possible critical point) and are relevant to heavy-ion collisions, the early universe, and neutron-star interiors."},{"id":"519fd613-c37e-4ce3-b9f4-8971cb6273e8","l":"Quantum billiards","d":1,"p":"9eb3a3f6-79e2-49e8-ae1e-813c0f319225","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Nonlinear Dynamics"],"def":"Quantum billiards are quantum-mechanical systems in which a particle is confined to a bounded region (typically two-dimensional) with impenetrable walls, so its stationary states are solutions of the Schr\u00f6dinger equation with hard-wall (Dirichlet) boundary conditions. They serve as paradigmatic models for studying eigenvalue spectra, eigenfunctions (including scarring), and quantum signatures of classically regular or chaotic dynamics."},{"id":"e8e055f8-8711-4305-9995-7b3789b1a63b","l":"Quantum chaos","d":1,"p":"bb2351de-d2a6-4d4c-a33e-9396bc991c32","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Nonlinear Dynamics"],"def":"Quantum chaos is the study of quantum systems whose classical counterparts display chaotic dynamics, focusing on how features of classical chaos appear in quantum observables. Typical signatures include universal level-spacing statistics (random-matrix behavior), nonrandom eigenstate structure (e.g., scars), and sensitive dynamical responses (fidelity/Loschmidt-echo decay) arising from semiclassical correspondence."},{"id":"74f884e0-6764-4962-a081-b198cca267c3","l":"Quantum chromodynamics","d":1,"p":"0b08c33f-27b1-4bbf-90f9-1eb1bf63d1eb","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Quantum chromodynamics (QCD) is the quantum field theory of the strong interaction: an SU(3) non\u2011Abelian gauge theory in which quarks carry one of three \"color\" charges and gluons mediate their interactions. Its defining properties are gluon self\u2011interaction, asymptotic freedom (weaker coupling at high energy), and confinement (color\u2011charged particles do not exist as isolated free states at low energy)."},{"id":"27d61d4c-d801-4705-b4f5-e93e4926bbf1","l":"Quantum circuits","d":1,"p":"358a880c-10d7-4a15-9fbe-9e182dc66d6b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum circuits are the circuit-model framework for quantum computation in which quantum information is stored in qubits and transformed by sequences of quantum gates\u2014unitary operations that generate superposition and entanglement\u2014followed by measurements that produce classical outcomes. They serve as both an abstract language for designing quantum algorithms and a blueprint for physical implementations (e.g., superconducting qubits, trapped ions)."},{"id":"fa74ced7-fb7d-48bb-a88e-d601c6ae2333","l":"Quantum coherence & coherence measures","d":1,"p":"01f70d47-cbac-4b5a-8ff9-0768e722511b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum coherence is the property of a quantum state arising from superpositions of basis states with well-defined relative phases, which gives rise to interference and enables uniquely quantum effects and protocols. Coherence measures quantify how much such superposition a state contains (and are basis-dependent), assigning a nonnegative number to a state\u2014common examples are the l1\u2011norm of off\u2011diagonal density\u2011matrix elements, the relative entropy of coherence, and the robustness of coherence."},{"id":"8ec9d84c-4a4c-499f-81f9-e6e703c50033","l":"Quantum communication","d":1,"p":"06b2397a-c546-4dad-89bc-a90c7df4b439","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum communication is the transmission and processing of information using quantum states (typically of photons or atoms), exploiting superposition and entanglement to perform tasks such as secure key distribution, quantum teleportation, and entanglement distribution. It enables capabilities unavailable classically\u2014most notably provable security from the no-cloning and measurement-disturbance principles and the creation of quantum networks."},{"id":"2ddffb19-8fa7-4c61-83a8-af6b9be80b5a","l":"Quantum computation","d":1,"p":"358a880c-10d7-4a15-9fbe-9e182dc66d6b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum computation is the study and implementation of information processing that uses quantum-mechanical systems (qubits) and their properties\u2014superposition, entanglement, and interference\u2014to perform logic operations and algorithms via unitary evolutions and measurements. It exploits the exponentially large state space of entangled qubits to solve certain problems more efficiently than classical computers."},{"id":"c9f142c1-4ecb-437a-8586-97957a1ebdba","l":"Quantum control","d":1,"p":"06b2397a-c546-4dad-89bc-a90c7df4b439","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum control is the deliberate manipulation of a quantum system's dynamics using external fields, shaped pulses, measurements, and feedback to drive it into desired states or implement target operations while minimizing errors from decoherence and noise. It encompasses open-loop (precomputed pulses) and closed-loop (measurement- or feedback-based) protocols, often designed with optimal control techniques."},{"id":"d599eade-2dc5-4572-84ac-54f7a980fbfb","l":"Quantum correlations in quantum information","d":1,"p":"01f70d47-cbac-4b5a-8ff9-0768e722511b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum correlations are nonclassical statistical dependencies between parts of a composite quantum system\u2014examples include entanglement, Bell nonlocality, steering, and quantum discord\u2014that cannot be fully described by classical probability models. These correlations act as resources for quantum information tasks (e.g., teleportation, secure key distribution, superdense coding, and quantum-enhanced sensing) that have no classical equivalents."},{"id":"3bbee7e8-e8e3-4261-8f9d-3f04dd351948","l":"Quantum cosmology","d":1,"p":"26ed7afc-8635-4637-a339-8d0a7cb75be1","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Quantum cosmology applies quantum principles to the universe as a whole, treating spacetime geometry and matter fields as quantum degrees of freedom to describe the very early, Planck\u2011scale regime and possible initial conditions. It aims to provide a quantum wavefunction or path\u2011integral for the universe (e.g., via the Wheeler\u2013DeWitt equation, no\u2011boundary proposals, or loop quantum cosmology) and to explain how classical spacetime and cosmic structure emerge."},{"id":"daf1bbbc-2b32-42e3-89b6-e280ffa32030","l":"Quantum crystals","d":1,"p":"1128a9f3-70c1-409b-84b2-44063aa12108","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Quantum crystals are solids whose lattice structure and thermodynamic and dynamical properties are dominated by quantum effects\u2014especially large zero\u2011point motion, tunneling, and quantum statistics\u2014so that particles are strongly delocalized and classical descriptions fail. Examples include solid helium, with highly delocalized atoms and mobile quantum defects, and electron (Wigner) crystals, where Coulomb repulsion plus quantum fluctuations produce an ordered electron lattice."},{"id":"709d46e1-304b-4708-a494-d27c13e9ca2c","l":"Quantum dots","d":1,"p":"8508c727-cef4-4fe3-a99a-64b7319d4e79","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Quantum dots are nanoscale semiconductor particles that confine electrons and holes in all three spatial dimensions, producing discrete, atom-like energy levels. Due to quantum confinement their optical and electronic properties (notably absorption and emission wavelengths) are strongly size-dependent, enabling tunable colors and applications in devices and bioimaging."},{"id":"abc8b5e1-9768-40d2-8d64-1bc2f0c09a89","l":"Quantum electrodynamics","d":1,"p":"ee487445-39e4-4995-9543-fea1735bcbb2","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Quantum electrodynamics (QED) is the relativistic quantum field theory that describes how charged particles (notably electrons and positrons) interact by exchanging quantized electromagnetic fields (photons), incorporating both quantum mechanics and special relativity. Its perturbative framework, expressed with Feynman diagrams and based on U(1) gauge symmetry, provides extremely precise predictions such as the electron's anomalous magnetic moment and the Lamb shift."},{"id":"2f62a37c-e96c-420d-87c7-afa0af4b24a2","l":"Quantum engineering","d":1,"p":"06b2397a-c546-4dad-89bc-a90c7df4b439","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum engineering is the interdisciplinary discipline that applies quantum mechanics to design, build, and optimize devices and systems that exploit quantum phenomena\u2014such as superposition, entanglement, and tunneling\u2014for computing, communication, sensing, and simulation. It combines quantum theory with control and measurement techniques, materials science, and systems engineering to realize, scale, and validate practical quantum technologies."},{"id":"c3c8c4f2-ab1b-4b6d-8556-3cbf5c77a5f2","l":"Quantum error correction","d":1,"p":"358a880c-10d7-4a15-9fbe-9e182dc66d6b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum error correction is the set of protocols that protect quantum information from decoherence and operational faults by encoding logical qubits into entangled states of multiple physical qubits and using measurements that detect (but do not read out) error syndromes so errors can be reversed. It enables fault-tolerant quantum computation by correcting a limited number of errors through redundant encoding and recovery operations without destroying the encoded quantum state."},{"id":"d26f935c-8076-4407-85fc-299a523edb77","l":"Quantum feedback","d":1,"p":"06b2397a-c546-4dad-89bc-a90c7df4b439","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum feedback is the real-time use of information about a quantum system\u2014obtained either by measurement or by coupling to another quantum device\u2014to adjust controls and thereby steer its evolution, stabilize target states, or suppress noise. It comes in measurement-based form, where classical signals from measurements drive actuators, and in coherent (autonomous) form, where quantum signals are routed through quantum controllers without explicit measurement."},{"id":"389751fd-e0d6-4e4e-ac36-87d973b40bc9","l":"Quantum field theory (low energy)","d":1,"p":"01f70d47-cbac-4b5a-8ff9-0768e722511b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum field theory (low energy) is the effective-field-theory description of quantum fields and their particle excitations that is valid at energies much smaller than some underlying cutoff or heavy mass scale.  It keeps only the light degrees of freedom and the leading interactions; effects of heavier physics enter indirectly as renormalized couplings and higher\u2011dimension operators suppressed by powers of the large scale."},{"id":"4d51cd7d-ac58-4f1e-a07d-191986546ede","l":"Quantum formalism","d":1,"p":"01f70d47-cbac-4b5a-8ff9-0768e722511b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum formalism is the mathematical framework of quantum mechanics that represents physical systems by state vectors or density operators in a Hilbert space and represents measurable quantities by Hermitian operators. It prescribes unitary time evolution (Schr\u00f6dinger equation) and probabilistic measurement outcomes via the Born rule, encoding superposition, noncommutativity, uncertainty, and entanglement."},{"id":"48672257-b4c7-4558-b1ed-49a79dfe913e","l":"Quantum foundations","d":1,"p":"01f70d47-cbac-4b5a-8ff9-0768e722511b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum foundations is the study of the conceptual, mathematical, and operational basis of quantum mechanics, asking what the formalism means and which principles or axioms give rise to phenomena like superposition, entanglement, contextuality, and nonlocal correlations. It addresses problems such as the measurement problem, the origin of quantum probabilities, and alternative reconstructions or modifications of the theory."},{"id":"6d5b9a32-cad0-4d9f-a837-b0f239f5cdc6","l":"Quantum gravity","d":1,"p":"124f9214-0176-475c-b706-99df707e8411","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Quantum gravity is the field of theoretical physics that seeks to describe gravity using the principles of quantum mechanics, reconciling general relativity with quantum field theory and characterizing the quantum structure of spacetime at the Planck scale. It aims to explain phenomena where both quantum effects and strong gravity are important (e.g., black-hole interiors and the Big Bang) and to resolve classical singularities."},{"id":"6ac80df9-0d8b-43c7-968f-ae3077f9a31a","l":"Quantum harmonic oscillator","d":1,"p":"01f70d47-cbac-4b5a-8ff9-0768e722511b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"The quantum harmonic oscillator is the quantum analogue of a mass on a spring\u2014a particle confined to a one-dimensional quadratic potential V(x) = \u00bd m \u03c9^2 x^2. Its energy eigenstates are discrete and equally spaced, E_n = \u0127\u03c9(n + \u00bd), with a nonzero zero-point energy and eigenfunctions given by Hermite-polynomial\u2013weighted Gaussians; ladder operators provide an elegant algebraic solution."},{"id":"9684747e-16c1-46af-b4cd-9f5de2c66708","l":"Quantum heat engines & refrigerators","d":1,"p":"9099c760-673a-40a9-a10f-410acf85f0ee","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Quantum heat engines and refrigerators are devices that use a small quantum system (the working substance) undergoing cyclic operations while coupled to thermal reservoirs to convert heat into work or to pump heat against a temperature gradient. In the quantum regime their behavior is shaped by discrete energy levels, coherence, entanglement and quantum statistics, so performance, fluctuations and fundamental limits are described by quantum thermodynamics and can differ from classical engines."},{"id":"2c450d36-1070-4190-8d54-aa17a4ef7f8b","l":"Quantum information processing","d":1,"p":"358a880c-10d7-4a15-9fbe-9e182dc66d6b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum information processing is the storage, manipulation, and transmission of information using quantum systems and operations that exploit quantum superposition and entanglement. It encompasses tasks such as quantum computation, communication, and sensing, and enables information-processing capabilities and algorithmic advantages that have no classical analog."},{"id":"076ac9ef-5d09-42b3-a0ea-b99b6ab4f5df","l":"Quantum information theory","d":1,"p":"358a880c-10d7-4a15-9fbe-9e182dc66d6b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum information theory studies the encoding, transmission, and processing of information using quantum systems, extending classical information concepts to quantum states, operations, and channels. It quantifies resources like qubits, entanglement, and coherence (e.g., via von Neumann entropy) and provides the foundations for quantum computation, communication, cryptography, and error correction."},{"id":"ab2c3182-dcb7-4cdd-a237-d806fda33f30","l":"Quantum interference effects","d":1,"p":"4722b240-3d0b-4ab1-831a-75302ab4cf86","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Quantum interference effects occur when the complex probability amplitudes for different quantum paths or histories superpose so that their relative phases produce constructive or destructive changes in the probabilities of measurement outcomes. They require coherence between alternatives and give rise to nonclassical phenomena such as double\u2011slit fringes, Aharonov\u2013Bohm oscillations, and weak localization in electronic transport."},{"id":"01cf0c9b-65f1-4ffa-bc8a-14973227bf67","l":"Quantum kicked rotor","d":1,"p":"9eb3a3f6-79e2-49e8-ae1e-813c0f319225","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Nonlinear Dynamics"],"def":"The quantum kicked rotor is a paradigmatic Floquet model consisting of a quantum particle on a ring subjected to a spatially periodic potential applied as instantaneous, periodic \"kicks.\" Its classical counterpart can exhibit chaotic momentum diffusion, while the quantum system displays phenomena like dynamical localization (suppression of diffusion in momentum space) and quantum resonances."},{"id":"384786ca-6bf6-41a8-981e-6546cdcae948","l":"Quantum kinetic theory","d":1,"p":"99934578-7c4c-4b59-9edd-cd7dc8988a58","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Quantum kinetic theory is the framework that describes the nonequilibrium time evolution and transport of quantum many-body systems by following quantum distribution functions or reduced density matrices rather than single-particle trajectories. It generalizes classical kinetic equations (e.g., Boltzmann) to include quantum coherence, statistics, collisions and correlations using tools such as Wigner functions, Green\u2019s functions (Kadanoff\u2013Baym) or quantum master equations."},{"id":"90bf01d7-2a57-43ee-962e-c320e30b586c","l":"Quantum master equation","d":1,"p":"db32a5c3-4277-4836-b044-5bc4a7aef527","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Particles & Fields","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A quantum master equation is an equation of motion for the density operator of a system coupled to an environment that combines the system's unitary evolution with irreversible effects such as dissipation and decoherence. In the Markovian regime it is often written in the Gorini\u2013Kossakowski\u2013Sudarshan\u2013Lindblad form, a linear generator that yields completely positive, trace-preserving dynamics, while more general master equations include memory (non\u2011Markovian) integro-differential terms."},{"id":"53cd9599-5951-4fa6-af4e-2ff2c0e53fe4","l":"Quantum metrology","d":1,"p":"06b2397a-c546-4dad-89bc-a90c7df4b439","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum metrology uses quantum states and measurements to estimate physical parameters (like phase, time, or fields) with precision that can surpass classical limits. It exploits quantum resources such as entanglement and squeezing and is analyzed via (quantum) Fisher information, yielding sensitivities that can approach the Heisenberg limit rather than the classical shot-noise limit."},{"id":"632f74d1-d16b-4b92-a295-b24729a93d8d","l":"Quantum optics","d":1,"p":"23cdcd6b-ae22-4b6d-92cd-24a9a8bd0577","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Quantum optics is the study of the quantum properties of light and its interactions with matter, describing electromagnetic fields in terms of quantized photons and field modes. It encompasses phenomena such as single-photon effects, coherence, squeezing, entanglement, and their applications in quantum information, precision metrology, and tests of quantum mechanics."},{"id":"700abfaa-cb00-4bd6-a420-a95c27b8f7be","l":"Quantum orbits theory","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Quantum-orbits theory is a semiclassical framework in strong-field and attosecond physics that associates dominant contributions to processes like above-threshold ionization and high-harmonic generation with stationary-phase (saddle-point) solutions of the electron action. These solutions are interpreted as complex-valued, classical-like trajectories (\"quantum orbits\") connecting ionization and (re)collision events, whose phases and amplitudes determine observed spectra and interference features."},{"id":"3e0549b8-0105-4383-84a3-ba1f47652af0","l":"Quantum oscillation techniques","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Quantum oscillation techniques are experimental methods that detect periodic variations of observables (magnetization, resistivity, torque, etc.) as a function of inverse magnetic field, produced by Landau quantization of electronic states. Analysis of these oscillations (e.g., de Haas\u2013van Alphen and Shubnikov\u2013de Haas effects) gives precise information about Fermi-surface cross sections, carrier effective masses, and scattering rates in metals and semimetals."},{"id":"48110e4b-fc3c-4ef7-89ed-77ff13bf554d","l":"Quantum parameter estimation","d":1,"p":"06b2397a-c546-4dad-89bc-a90c7df4b439","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum parameter estimation is the task of inferring unknown parameters that characterize a quantum state or process by making measurements and applying statistical estimation, using quantum resources and measurement strategies to minimize the estimation uncertainty. It formalizes ultimate precision limits through the quantum Fisher information and the quantum Cram\u00e9r\u2013Rao bound, and can exploit entanglement or other nonclassical states to outperform classical measurement limits."},{"id":"520d3361-6e3b-4612-895f-98a7259acfe3","l":"Quantum phase transitions","d":1,"p":"f2a9db78-1651-4a94-b66e-3e3d72056760","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Quantum phase transitions are transitions between distinct ground states of a many-body quantum system that occur at zero temperature when a non\u2011thermal control parameter (e.g., pressure, magnetic field, interaction strength) is varied and quantum fluctuations drive the change. They are characterized by a quantum critical point where the ground-state properties become nonanalytic and long-range quantum correlations and dynamical critical scaling govern the low\u2011temperature behavior."},{"id":"656406f1-8851-4606-bb8d-c01290f80f56","l":"Quantum plasmas","d":1,"p":"22580d6a-8d20-4653-b2d1-a7de314bc162","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Quantum plasmas are ionized many-particle systems whose collective behavior is controlled by quantum mechanics rather than classical statistics\u2014occurring when the electron thermal de Broglie wavelength or Fermi energy is comparable to interparticle spacing or excitation wavelengths. They show Fermi degeneracy and pressure, quantum diffraction/tunneling (often modeled by a Bohm term), exchange\u2013correlation and spin effects, which modify screening, dispersion, and transport in settings like white-dwarf interiors, warm dense matter, and nanoscale electronic plasmas."},{"id":"830eaa37-9813-4972-9351-1846c659b20b","l":"Quantum protocols","d":1,"p":"06b2397a-c546-4dad-89bc-a90c7df4b439","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum protocols are prescribed sequences of preparation, manipulation, transmission, and measurement of quantum states (often combined with classical communication) designed to perform information-processing tasks such as secure key distribution, teleportation, or distributed computation. They exploit inherently quantum features\u2014superposition, entanglement, and nonlocal correlations\u2014to achieve functionalities or efficiencies unattainable classically."},{"id":"703c2c2b-3d50-4906-a638-5995d34cd6db","l":"Quantum quench","d":1,"p":"7dd874c0-5ab0-4de4-9919-10775f22748a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"A quantum quench is a rapid change in a quantum system's Hamiltonian parameters (for example an interaction strength or external field) occurring so fast that the state is effectively frozen and then evolves unitarily under the new Hamiltonian. It generates non-equilibrium dynamics in isolated many-body systems used to probe relaxation, thermalization, and dynamical phase transitions."},{"id":"ee56797a-591b-49de-b19c-1eda6eb00339","l":"Quantum ratchets","d":1,"p":"9eb3a3f6-79e2-49e8-ae1e-813c0f319225","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Nonlinear Dynamics"],"def":"Quantum ratchets are systems that generate directed particle or charge motion (a net current) in the absence of any net force or bias by exploiting broken spatial or temporal symmetries under non\u2011equilibrium (often periodic) driving. In the quantum regime effects such as tunneling, discrete energy levels, coherence and quantum noise modify the classical ratchet picture and produce phenomena like current reversals and resonant transport in cold atoms, quantum dots and superconducting circuits."},{"id":"2e741b31-7ee0-4bf2-b3dd-a2713fd8e154","l":"Quantum rings","d":1,"p":"8508c727-cef4-4fe3-a99a-64b7319d4e79","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Quantum rings are nanoscale or mesoscopic ring-shaped structures that confine quantum particles to circular motion, causing their angular momentum and energy to be quantized. Because the particle wavefunction encloses a loop, observable properties (energy spectra, conductance, and equilibrium currents) show flux-dependent quantum-interference effects such as Aharonov\u2013Bohm oscillations and persistent currents."},{"id":"200ea9a8-54ca-4f8f-a7e1-f186bb326056","l":"Quantum simulation","d":1,"p":"358a880c-10d7-4a15-9fbe-9e182dc66d6b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum simulation is the use of a well-controlled quantum system to reproduce the dynamics or equilibrium properties of another quantum system that is intractable to simulate on classical computers. It can be implemented analogously by engineering equivalent Hamiltonians or digitally with gate-based quantum circuits, and is used to study many-body physics, chemistry, and materials."},{"id":"0440f4ec-bada-4748-a612-b580f85ef27d","l":"Quantum state transfer","d":1,"p":"06b2397a-c546-4dad-89bc-a90c7df4b439","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum state transfer is the process of moving the full quantum information contained in a system's state from one physical subsystem or location to another without measuring or destroying it, thereby preserving amplitudes, phase coherence, and any entanglement. It is implemented via direct dynamical coupling or engineered quantum channels (spin chains, cavities, waveguides) or by quantum teleportation, and is characterized by the fidelity with which the target reproduces the original state."},{"id":"db32a5c3-4277-4836-b044-5bc4a7aef527","l":"Quantum stochastic processes","d":1,"p":"860c01ce-5b2c-4bd5-9148-88f0b1d585e2","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Quantum stochastic processes are time-indexed families of quantum states or observables whose evolution includes both intrinsic quantum uncertainty and extrinsic randomness from environmental noise or measurements. They are described in the framework of noncommutative probability and open quantum systems, typically via completely positive trace-preserving maps, quantum trajectories, or quantum stochastic differential equations (e.g., Hudson\u2013Parthasarathy formalism)."},{"id":"ebe276d7-1de5-4ac3-8a32-be0e18eb6592","l":"Quantum-to-classical transition","d":1,"p":"01f70d47-cbac-4b5a-8ff9-0768e722511b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"The quantum-to-classical transition is the process by which a quantum system loses manifestly quantum features\u2014superposition, interference and entanglement\u2014and begins to exhibit definite, effectively classical properties and trajectories. This emergence of classical behavior is typically explained by environment-induced decoherence, coarse-graining/measurement and limiting procedures (e.g., large action or \u0127\u21920) that suppress quantum coherence and yield classical probabilities and equations of motion."},{"id":"e3dde6b7-dff0-4e1a-8edf-f090633360fb","l":"Quantum transport","d":1,"p":"36754399-ec2b-4962-bb07-ca12c79834e8","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Quantum transport is the study of how quantum particles (electrons, phonons, excitons, etc.) carry charge, energy, or spin through materials and nanostructures when quantum coherence, interference, tunneling, discrete energy levels, or quantum statistics dominate conduction. It uses frameworks such as the Landauer\u2013B\u00fcttiker approach, the Kubo formula, and non-equilibrium Green's functions to describe ballistic, mesoscopic, and coherent/incoherent transport regimes."},{"id":"c11c0316-b13a-4bf0-bb55-1f650a059352","l":"Quantum wells","d":1,"p":"8508c727-cef4-4fe3-a99a-64b7319d4e79","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A quantum well is a thin layer of lower-potential material (often a narrow-bandgap semiconductor) sandwiched between higher-potential barriers that confines charge carriers (electrons and holes) in one dimension, with thickness comparable to their de Broglie wavelength. This confinement produces discrete, quantized energy levels and alters optical and transport properties relative to the bulk, underpinning devices like quantum\u2011well lasers and detectors."},{"id":"ebc3ff81-e1a7-4484-844f-9a8bbeb06e20","l":"Quantum wires","d":1,"p":"347c621b-2ddf-4d11-bd25-3380b5623c72","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Quantum wires are quasi\u2013one-dimensional conductors in which electrons are confined in the two transverse directions to length scales comparable to their de Broglie wavelength, so motion is effectively along a single axis and transverse energy levels form discrete subbands. This confinement produces characteristic phenomena such as quantized conductance, ballistic transport, and enhanced electron\u2013electron interaction effects (e.g., Luttinger\u2011liquid behavior)."},{"id":"a6961570-e424-490c-8b49-f5a6af0b778b","l":"Quantum Zeno dynamics","d":1,"p":"01f70d47-cbac-4b5a-8ff9-0768e722511b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum Zeno dynamics is the modification of a quantum system's time evolution produced by very frequent measurements or by strong continuous coupling, which suppresses transitions out of certain subspaces and thereby confines the state to evolve within a \"Zeno subspace.\" Within that subspace the system undergoes an effective (often unitary) evolution governed by a projected Hamiltonian, rather than the original full-system dynamics."},{"id":"ed47e543-18fb-4597-9384-a5fc4c7921f5","l":"Quark-gluon plasma","d":1,"p":"0b08c33f-27b1-4bbf-90f9-1eb1bf63d1eb","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Quark\u2013gluon plasma (QGP) is a high-temperature, high-density phase of quantum chromodynamics in which quarks and gluons are no longer confined inside hadrons but form a deconfined, strongly interacting medium. It is produced briefly in relativistic heavy-ion collisions and in the early universe and behaves like a nearly perfect fluid with very low shear viscosity."},{"id":"b94f60a0-02d3-4d70-a109-22eec108ba26","l":"Quark matter","d":1,"p":"0b08c33f-27b1-4bbf-90f9-1eb1bf63d1eb","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Quark matter is a phase of strongly interacting matter in which quarks (and usually gluons) are no longer confined inside individual hadrons but form an extended, interacting medium. It appears at extreme temperatures or baryon densities\u2014e.g., the quark\u2013gluon plasma in heavy-ion collisions or putative quark cores of neutron stars\u2014and can show variants such as color-superconducting or strange-quark matter."},{"id":"d6c8011e-0457-41cc-a9c4-13718c357417","l":"Quark model","d":1,"p":"0b08c33f-27b1-4bbf-90f9-1eb1bf63d1eb","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"The quark model is a classification and constituent model in which hadrons are bound states of fundamental quarks and antiquarks (mesons = quark\u2013antiquark, baryons = three quarks) that carry fractional electric charge, flavor quantum numbers, and color.  It organizes hadron spectroscopy and quantum numbers via SU(3) (and extended) flavor symmetries and is realized microscopically by quantum chromodynamics, which binds quarks through color forces."},{"id":"88bfc025-b710-4235-9a62-55a67c34ac8c","l":"Quasicrystalline structure","d":1,"p":"e521b9c7-1f62-48e4-a3fc-9428f629d078","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A quasicrystalline structure is a solid that has well-defined long-range order without translational periodicity, producing sharp Bragg diffraction peaks and often showing non\u2011crystallographic rotational symmetries (e.g., fivefold). Such structures can be described mathematically by aperiodic tilings (like the Penrose tiling) or as projections of a higher\u2011dimensional periodic lattice."},{"id":"3fbf5f17-17b4-46c2-ae49-e9a24c3648f2","l":"Quasicrystals","d":1,"p":"1128a9f3-70c1-409b-84b2-44063aa12108","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Quasicrystals are solids with long-range, ordered but nonperiodic (quasiperiodic) atomic arrangements that produce sharp Bragg diffraction peaks while lacking conventional translational symmetry and permitting rotational symmetries (e.g., fivefold) forbidden in periodic crystals. They are often described mathematically by quasiperiodic tilings or as projections of higher-dimensional lattices."},{"id":"f27a3e9c-1897-4ee8-9270-edd2d637fe86","l":"Radiation detectors","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Radiation detectors are instruments that sense and measure ionizing radiation by converting interactions of particles or photons with matter into observable electrical or optical signals. They use transduction mechanisms such as gas ionization, scintillation light, or semiconductor charge collection to provide counts, energy spectra, timing, or dose information."},{"id":"43592360-fefb-426e-9ce8-50769ea0f43e","l":"Radiation monitoring & safety","d":1,"p":"4c286df9-b565-4295-b951-49a36d60cad1","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Radiation monitoring and safety is the set of measurement techniques, instruments, procedures and regulations used to detect and quantify ionizing radiation and radioactive contamination to protect people and the environment. It encompasses environmental and personal dosimetry, surveys with detectors, contamination control, shielding, administrative controls and dose limits guided by the ALARA (as low as reasonably achievable) principle."},{"id":"80ae744e-f34d-41f9-ba7b-e8e33f479c4b","l":"Radiation pressure acceleration","d":1,"p":"4b4294b5-d03f-4662-9886-cea042ce7d8c","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams","Plasma Physics"],"def":"Radiation pressure acceleration is the acceleration of matter produced by the force exerted when electromagnetic radiation transfers momentum to an object via absorption, reflection, or scattering of photons. The acceleration scales with the incident radiation flux divided by c and depends on the object's optical properties and mass per unit area, and it underlies phenomena from solar sails and comet tails to laser-driven ion/plasma acceleration."},{"id":"33dfb6ab-f1f6-41e8-b219-1e819e5b63d3","l":"Radiative capture","d":1,"p":"bbb0ce0d-36da-4da5-a443-df064f36aa17","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Radiative capture is a process in which a particle (often a neutron, proton, or light nucleus) is absorbed by a target nucleus and the excess energy is emitted as electromagnetic radiation (typically one or more gamma rays), producing a more tightly bound final nucleus. It conserves energy and angular momentum by converting the incoming kinetic and binding energy into photons and is important in nuclear physics and astrophysical nucleosynthesis."},{"id":"9dbfd6fe-b782-4f11-826e-82ae5084a744","l":"Radioactive beams","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Radioactive beams are directed streams of unstable (radioactive) nuclei that have been produced and accelerated to act as projectiles in experiments. They let physicists probe the structure and reactions of isotopes far from stability and are typically made by techniques such as in-flight fragmentation or isotope-separation-online (ISOL)."},{"id":"444a77f6-f64c-40d8-a801-39f2292fa723","l":"Radio frequency calculations","d":1,"p":"5f099028-76e7-4a1c-b549-61c441f6241f","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Radio frequency calculations are computations applied to electromagnetic waves in the radio-frequency band (roughly 3 kHz\u2013300 GHz) to quantify wave properties and system behavior. They determine quantities such as frequency, wavelength (\u03bb = c/f), period, photon energy, power and spectral bandwidth, and are used to analyze propagation losses, antenna dimensions, and impedance matching."},{"id":"9dd35c94-fb02-4a0f-af31-48bb3ba146fe","l":"Radio frequency power sources","d":1,"p":"5f099028-76e7-4a1c-b549-61c441f6241f","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Radio frequency power sources are devices or systems that convert electrical energy into controlled electromagnetic power at radio frequencies (roughly 3 kHz\u2013300 GHz) for use in communications, radar, plasma heating, and particle accelerators. Common examples include vacuum-tube devices (magnetrons, klystrons, traveling-wave tubes) and solid-state RF amplifiers, which deliver continuous-wave or pulsed RF output with specified amplitude, frequency, and phase."},{"id":"b3ac6303-2e15-4b1b-bd7e-242d57d2b1d7","l":"Radio, microwave, & sub-mm astronomy","d":1,"p":"893ecc24-c678-4687-bd3a-0b4a1bcb4cfc","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Radio, microwave, and submillimeter astronomy is the study of celestial objects using electromagnetic radiation at wavelengths from meters and centimeters down through millimeters to submillimeter scales (~m\u20130.1 mm), observed with radio dishes, interferometers, and space/balloon platforms. These bands uniquely probe nonthermal synchrotron and free-free emission, molecular rotational lines, thermal dust emission, and the cosmic microwave background, revealing magnetic fields, cold gas, star formation, and early-universe signals."},{"id":"82b8e261-a123-4136-86da-06474a9f3927","l":"Random & disordered media","d":1,"p":"8252f005-8412-4e8e-a2c6-0f6ff6c1b7fa","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Random and disordered media are materials or systems whose structure or material parameters vary unpredictably on microscopic or mesoscopic scales, so they lack long-range order and must be described statistically. This randomness profoundly modifies transport and wave phenomena\u2014producing enhanced scattering, diffusion, Anderson localization, and percolation behavior\u2014and is treated with effective-medium, scaling, and statistical theories."},{"id":"f38e82e9-5671-4568-a915-1b4d5355f4d8","l":"Random graphs","d":1,"p":"21802c03-692c-4093-a35c-11955f447cd4","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Random graphs are ensembles of graphs generated by a specified probabilistic rule for placing edges between a set of vertices, used to study the typical structural and dynamical properties of networks. A canonical example is the Erd\u0151s\u2013R\u00e9nyi G(n,p) model, in which each pair of n vertices is connected independently with probability p, exhibiting phenomena such as the emergence of a giant component and sharp phase transitions."},{"id":"e004c5bf-0efa-40fc-a2f4-bdb72f7d77cb","l":"Random matrix theory","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Nuclear Physics","Nonlinear Dynamics"],"def":"Random matrix theory studies the statistical properties of matrices with randomly chosen entries, focusing on the distributions and correlations of their eigenvalues and eigenvectors. By classifying ensembles according to symmetry, it predicts universal spectral fluctuations that model complex systems (e.g., nuclei, quantum-chaotic systems, disordered conductors) and connect to areas like number theory and statistical mechanics."},{"id":"b52b4f11-cad6-4cea-997f-36a603349fea","l":"Random walks","d":1,"p":"c6d9db47-7b2d-4d3b-868a-606b65695e3c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"A random walk is a stochastic process in which a particle or variable undergoes a sequence of successive random steps in space or state, either on a lattice or in continuous space and time. Simple random walks model diffusion and Brownian motion: for uncorrelated steps the displacement distribution approaches a Gaussian (by the central limit theorem) and the mean-square displacement grows linearly with time, with qualitative differences (e.g., recurrence vs. transience) that depend on dimensionality."},{"id":"818d42b3-189e-49dd-a2b3-a273c93278c5","l":"Rare-earth ions","d":1,"p":"bd252d88-a608-4897-9416-daf84dee7f6b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Rare-earth ions are positively charged atoms of the lanthanide series (often including yttrium and scandium) that typically occur in the trivalent state and have partially filled 4f electron shells. The shielded 4f electrons produce narrow, atom-like optical transitions, large magnetic moments and strong spin\u2013orbit coupling, properties exploited in lasers, magnets, phosphors and quantum technologies."},{"id":"900680de-e59d-4b2f-9fe7-34992c1ce9df","l":"Rare-earth magnetic materials","d":1,"p":"58071c68-730c-4a3e-982c-abc9f0cedf67","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Materials that contain rare-earth (lanthanide) elements whose localized 4f electrons produce large magnetic moments and strong magnetocrystalline anisotropy, giving rise to high-performing permanent magnets and unusual magnetic order. Their magnetic behavior is governed by strong spin\u2013orbit coupling and crystal-field splitting of the 4f states and by indirect exchange (e.g., RKKY) with conduction electrons."},{"id":"fba210d7-a749-425f-a730-515392fa9ae3","l":"Rare & new isotopes","d":1,"p":"1cf75de2-e14b-41cd-b733-8f704652db33","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Rare and new isotopes are atomic nuclei of an element that have uncommon or newly synthesized neutron-to-proton ratios \u2014 often far from the valley of stability and typically short-lived. They are produced at accelerator or reactor facilities and used to probe nuclear structure, fundamental interactions, and astrophysical nucleosynthesis."},{"id":"67e3ab83-61f8-49be-b813-2cd2a5e49784","l":"Rayleigh-B\u00e9nard convection","d":1,"p":"2d276ce6-c803-4ba3-813c-b989f2d4bfde","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Rayleigh\u2013B\u00e9nard convection is buoyancy-driven fluid motion that develops in a horizontal layer heated from below and cooled from above, producing organized cellular patterns (rolls or hexagons) once the vertical temperature gradient exceeds a critical value. Its onset and structure are governed mainly by the Rayleigh number, which measures the ratio of thermal buoyancy to viscous and diffusive damping."},{"id":"9170a878-285e-4fd7-8e22-5b526a6e0ac3","l":"Rayleigh-Taylor instability","d":1,"p":"d2fd5563-5077-41b4-9d06-f0da03883073","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Plasma Physics"],"def":"Rayleigh\u2013Taylor instability is the fluid-dynamical instability that occurs at the interface between fluids of different density when the lighter fluid accelerates the heavier (for example, a heavier fluid lying above a lighter fluid in a gravitational field), causing small perturbations to grow. Those perturbations evolve into rising bubbles of light fluid and falling spikes of heavy fluid, producing vigorous mixing and appearing in contexts from supernovae to inertial-confinement fusion."},{"id":"7162fca3-31c8-4dcd-a715-b7c5ec07b943","l":"Reacting multiphase flows","d":1,"p":"c577bd62-ea57-43b7-9607-1ef45dba3afb","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Reacting multiphase flows are fluid flows containing two or more coexisting phases (gas, liquid, solid) in which chemical reactions occur within a phase or at phase interfaces, so that reaction kinetics, interphase mass and heat transfer, and momentum exchange are tightly coupled. These systems feature strong interactions between transport, turbulence, phase change and heat release that jointly determine composition, temperature and flow structure."},{"id":"cf113451-83bd-4f3a-9884-a14d38899ecf","l":"Reactions at surfaces & chemisorption","d":1,"p":"4455b073-c3a2-4a0b-923e-98d7ec0f3b9d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Reactions at surfaces are chemical processes that occur at the interface between phases (typically gas\u2013solid or liquid\u2013solid) where molecules adsorb, diffuse on the surface, react with surface atoms or other adsorbates, and desorb. Chemisorption is the adsorption mode in which an adsorbate forms a strong chemical bond with the surface (involving electron sharing or transfer and changes in local electronic structure), in contrast to weak, physical (van der Waals) physisorption, and it often controls catalytic activity and reaction kinetics."},{"id":"5ef62623-4765-4b21-8fce-7542d68c9faa","l":"Refraction","d":1,"p":"4c4f4d87-e837-4d65-970d-f5323698a5f1","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Refraction is the change in direction of a wave when it crosses the interface between two media because its propagation speed (and wavelength) changes while its frequency remains the same. For light, the angles of incidence and refraction are related by Snell\u2019s law, n1 sin\u03b81 = n2 sin\u03b82, where n is the refractive index."},{"id":"810c52c2-69a9-4ac0-9c5b-41c1954c6e7f","l":"Relativistic aspects of cosmology","d":1,"p":"26ed7afc-8635-4637-a339-8d0a7cb75be1","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Relativistic aspects of cosmology are the application of special and primarily general relativity to the universe\u2019s large-scale geometry and dynamics\u2014how spacetime curvature and Einstein\u2019s equations govern cosmic expansion, horizons, and the propagation of light and matter. This includes FLRW metrics, relativistic fluids and perturbation theory used to model structure formation and observational effects like redshift, gravitational lensing, and CMB anisotropies."},{"id":"8c924dda-2265-44a8-9f05-24dcd1673123","l":"Relativistic kinetic theory","d":1,"p":"99934578-7c4c-4b59-9edd-cd7dc8988a58","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Relativistic kinetic theory is the extension of kinetic theory to systems where particle speeds or energy densities require special or general relativity, describing the statistical evolution of a Lorentz\u2011invariant single-particle distribution function on phase space. It uses relativistic transport equations (e.g., the Vlasov or Boltzmann equation with a covariant collision term) to derive macroscopic quantities such as the energy\u2011momentum tensor and transport coefficients."},{"id":"33a18d37-b325-4ba4-8ee2-a5cb6149a751","l":"Relativistic multiple-particle dynamics","d":1,"p":"cd322dee-17c3-4f48-81b4-5fbca9f2c432","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Relativistic multiple-particle dynamics is the theoretical description of the motion and interaction of systems of two or more particles when velocities or energies require special-relativistic treatment, formulated so that equations of motion and interactions are Lorentz\u2011covariant and respect causality. It covers covariant N\u2011body equations, field\u2011mediated forces, radiation reaction and related kinetic or Hamiltonian/Lagrangian frameworks for many\u2011particle systems."},{"id":"75ab024b-8571-432b-a0b3-5f69433b4789","l":"Relativistic plasmas","d":1,"p":"22580d6a-8d20-4653-b2d1-a7de314bc162","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Relativistic plasmas are ionized gases in which particle thermal energies or bulk-flow velocities are comparable to or exceed rest-mass energy (kT \u2273 mc^2 or Lorentz factor \u03b3 \u226b 1), so special-relativistic effects strongly influence single-particle motion and collective dynamics. This leads to altered dispersion relations, Lorentz-transformed fields, strong radiation reaction and, in extreme cases, pair creation, requiring relativistic kinetic or fluid descriptions."},{"id":"50076b93-d7f3-4add-b6b4-9a7353517869","l":"Relativistic & quantum electrodynamic effects in atoms, molecules,& ions","d":1,"p":"30e65d01-6cd5-442b-889f-ddc0c3c28157","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Relativistic and quantum electrodynamic effects in atoms, molecules, and ions are the modifications to their electronic structure, spectra, and interactions that arise from special relativity and from the quantization of the electromagnetic field. They include relativistic corrections (e.g., Dirac kinetic energy, spin\u2013orbit, mass\u2013velocity, Darwin terms) and QED corrections (self\u2011energy, vacuum polarization, Lamb shift) that appreciably shift energy levels, transition rates, and hyperfine structure, especially for high\u2011Z systems and precision spectroscopy."},{"id":"386ccc1a-0265-4f5d-a1d9-3f815cbd1334","l":"Relativistic quantum information","d":1,"p":"01f70d47-cbac-4b5a-8ff9-0768e722511b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Relativistic quantum information is the study of quantum information theory when special- or general-relativistic effects are significant, combining quantum field theory with information-theoretic concepts. It analyzes how motion, gravity, and spacetime causal structure affect entanglement, coherence, communication channels, and quantum protocols (e.g., via Unruh/Hawking effects and observer-dependent vacuum/particle notions)."},{"id":"490ba97f-fb85-47ee-a794-ef8d687bd74c","l":"Relativistic wave equations","d":1,"p":"01f70d47-cbac-4b5a-8ff9-0768e722511b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Relativistic wave equations are Lorentz\u2011invariant generalizations of the Schr\u00f6dinger equation that enforce the relativistic energy\u2013momentum relation (E^2 = p^2c^2 + m^2c^4) and govern quantum particles or fields. Examples include the Klein\u2013Gordon equation (spin\u20110), the Dirac equation (spin\u20111/2), the Proca equation (massive spin\u20111) and Maxwell\u2019s equations (massless spin\u20111), and their structure leads naturally to antiparticles and the quantum field theory description."},{"id":"3db3e08f-6e6d-4376-8752-7a5c0e44ec4d","l":"Reliability & operability","d":1,"p":"4c286df9-b565-4295-b951-49a36d60cad1","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Reliability and operability quantify how well a system can perform and be used over time: reliability is the probability that equipment will perform its required function without failure under specified conditions for a given period, while operability describes how easily, safely, and effectively the system can be started, controlled, maintained, and restored to service."},{"id":"14cf446f-88e3-46b3-acad-c44037a0603a","l":"Renormalization","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields","Networks"],"def":"Renormalization is the set of methods used in quantum field theory and statistical physics to remove divergences by absorbing infinite or cutoff-dependent pieces into redefinitions of the theory's parameters (masses, couplings, field normalizations), producing finite predictions for measurable quantities. It also describes how those effective parameters change with the observation scale (the renormalization-group flow), thereby linking microscopic and macroscopic behavior."},{"id":"50ba3f74-7f31-4857-8a75-b2b007348981","l":"Renormalization group","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields","Physics of Living Systems","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The renormalization group is a framework of methods for studying how the effective description and parameters of a physical system change with observation scale (length, momentum, or energy), typically by coarse-graining or integrating out short-distance degrees of freedom. It yields flow equations for parameters whose fixed points and scaling behavior explain universality and critical phenomena in statistical mechanics and the scale dependence (running) of couplings in quantum field theory."},{"id":"54189ada-9320-4fe3-9a6c-30ba36d377c4","l":"Repeat sequences","d":1,"p":"9990b97d-b485-4ad7-ac0a-33ab94e0e794","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Repeat sequences are ordered motifs (sets of symbols, events, or structural units) that recur consecutively in a system, producing a periodic or quasi\u2011periodic pattern. They are characterized by a repeat unit (motif) and repetition length (period) and appear in time\u2011series (pulse trains), spatial lattices (unit cells), and polymer/DNA motifs."},{"id":"8cec8081-7bee-49b5-b6a0-fb4e86a69c88","l":"Replica methods","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"The replica method is a technique in statistical physics for computing disorder-averaged quantities (most commonly the quenched free energy \u27e8ln Z\u27e9) by introducing n identical copies (\"replicas\") of the system, computing \u27e8Z^n\u27e9 for integer n, and obtaining \u27e8ln Z\u27e9 via the analytic continuation \u27e8ln Z\u27e9 = lim_{n\u21920}(\u27e8Z^n\u27e9\u22121)/n. It is widely used in spin glasses, random optimization and random-matrix problems and often depends on assumptions about replica symmetry or replica-symmetry breaking."},{"id":"76c3b5eb-2e1d-46c1-8c98-55924b738d6c","l":"Reproductive system","d":1,"p":"0a74688b-2eee-4349-a9bd-e93851fe2c5a","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"The reproductive system is the collection of organs, tissues, and hormones that produce and transport gametes (sperm or eggs), enable fertilization, and support early development of offspring. In animals it includes gonads, ducts, accessory glands, and regulatory endocrine controls that coordinate reproductive cycles and pregnancy."},{"id":"033555f3-f2e5-4e9d-afdf-ff358604c622","l":"Reptation","d":1,"p":"5114e95c-b4a7-458f-a2f7-b49a98b828cd","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Reptation is the snake-like motion of a polymer chain constrained to move along an effective \"tube\" formed by topological entanglements with neighboring chains, so that its long-time dynamics are dominated by sliding along its own contour. This mechanism, introduced by de Gennes, governs the diffusion and viscoelastic relaxation of entangled polymer melts and solutions."},{"id":"67ad5b80-e842-426f-925e-78ce7c5fa066","l":"Resistive switching","d":1,"p":"41dd58dd-d35c-46e8-9594-d7e05b458da6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Resistive switching is the reversible change of a material or device between distinct high- and low-resistance states induced by an applied voltage or current. It typically arises from processes such as ionic migration, redox reactions, interface-charge modulation, or the formation/rupture of conductive filaments, and is the basis for nonvolatile memory devices (RRAM/memristors)."},{"id":"720ce597-013c-47b2-8ad2-83166ee2e256","l":"Resonance fluorescence","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Resonance fluorescence is the light emitted by an atom or molecule that has been driven by incident radiation tuned to one of its allowed transition frequencies; the system re\u2011emits photons at the driving frequency (elastic or Rayleigh scattering) and, through quantum processes, at shifted frequencies (inelastic components, e.g., the Mollow triplet under strong driving)."},{"id":"74a8caf9-1e57-49c0-bc03-27b219a10e6a","l":"Resonance reactions","d":1,"p":"bbb0ce0d-36da-4da5-a443-df064f36aa17","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Resonance reactions are processes that proceed through the formation of a short-lived intermediate (resonant) state whose energy matches the incoming particles, producing a pronounced, energy-dependent enhancement of the reaction cross section. The resonance is characterized by its resonance energy and width (inverse lifetime), which determine the position and shape (typically Breit\u2013Wigner) of the peak; this phenomenon occurs in nuclear, atomic, molecular, and particle scattering."},{"id":"6fab7f86-2de1-4f52-9425-ed4f9aec008b","l":"Resonance techniques","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Resonance techniques are experimental methods that probe or manipulate a system by driving it at frequencies matching its natural or transition frequencies, producing an enhanced, frequency\u2011selective response. They are widely used (e.g., NMR, ESR, optical and mechanical resonances) to sensitively measure energy levels, relaxation times, coupling strengths, and dynamical properties."},{"id":"63bb3c62-4694-40c2-880b-49a5eebb0887","l":"Resummation methods","d":1,"p":"ee82ad04-6c47-46a4-a70d-c9a7d7989f6a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Resummation methods are techniques that reorganize a perturbative expansion to sum infinite subsets of terms (for example leading logarithms or factorially growing contributions) to restore convergence or capture effects beyond fixed-order calculations. Examples include Borel resummation, Pad\u00e9 approximants, and renormalization-group or effective-field-theory based resummations used in quantum field theory and statistical physics."},{"id":"07d61576-c924-4d92-9a86-7806f78a518f","l":"Reynolds-averaged Navier Stokes","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"The Reynolds-averaged Navier\u2013Stokes (RANS) equations are the Navier\u2013Stokes equations applied to turbulent flows after decomposing instantaneous flow variables into mean and fluctuating parts and then taking a time or ensemble average. This averaging produces additional Reynolds stress terms that represent turbulent momentum transport and must be closed with a turbulence model to solve the mean-flow equations."},{"id":"87e64a0f-c935-44dd-a3ef-ae868d3a6389","l":"Rheology","d":1,"p":"9e001bda-d32b-4a59-a216-bfd4c1af8c25","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Rheology is the study of how materials deform and flow under applied forces, focusing on the relationships between stress, strain, and time for fluids, soft solids, and complex materials. It quantifies behaviors such as viscosity, elasticity, viscoelasticity, and plasticity, and connects macroscopic flow responses to microstructure and deformation history."},{"id":"2a7e97bc-0a5f-4b55-a310-75aae0ede940","l":"Rheology techniques","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Rheology techniques are experimental methods for measuring how materials deform and flow under applied stresses or strains, producing quantities such as viscosity, viscoelastic moduli, yield stress, relaxation times, and normal stresses. Common techniques include rotational and oscillatory rheometry, capillary and extensional rheometry, creep and stress\u2011relaxation tests, and microrheology (passive and active) to probe different length and time scales and linear versus nonlinear response."},{"id":"66325c3d-b9b9-4435-a048-3d7c4d709178","l":"Richtmyer-Meshkov instability","d":1,"p":"d2fd5563-5077-41b4-9d06-f0da03883073","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Richtmyer\u2013Meshkov instability is the growth of perturbations on the interface between fluids of different density caused by an impulsive acceleration (typically a shock), where misaligned pressure and density gradients generate baroclinic vorticity that amplifies corrugations and drives interpenetration and mixing. It is the impulsive-acceleration analogue of the Rayleigh\u2013Taylor instability and commonly occurs in shock-driven flows such as supernovae and inertial-confinement fusion."},{"id":"7ed91357-33af-4995-bdd6-a84259d4f8f0","l":"Robustness","d":1,"p":"f6b0d9b5-f1f6-42b0-89df-1b70c8ec3e8b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Robustness is the degree to which a physical system, observable, or theoretical prediction retains its qualitative behavior and quantitative values when subjected to perturbations, noise, parameter variation, or modeling uncertainty. It is often quantified by the range or magnitude of disturbances the system can tolerate before its behavior changes significantly."},{"id":"54fe4359-1113-4cc5-8302-78e8e614825e","l":"Room-temperature magnet calculations","d":1,"p":"be790dcd-2f91-4896-8328-00953746f53a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Calculations aimed at predicting or characterizing a material's magnetic behavior at around 300 K, including Curie/N\u00e9el temperatures, magnetic anisotropy, exchange interactions, and thermal stability. They typically combine first-principles electronic-structure methods (e.g., density functional theory) with statistical-mechanical or spin-dynamics models (Monte Carlo, mean-field, atomistic spin dynamics) to include finite-temperature magnetic fluctuations."},{"id":"734da0f2-e51e-4afb-a3b7-c235658ee967","l":"Room temperature RF","d":1,"p":"5f099028-76e7-4a1c-b549-61c441f6241f","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Room-temperature RF refers to radio\u2011frequency electromagnetic signals and the sources, detectors, and components that operate at ordinary ambient temperatures (roughly 20\u201325 \u00b0C) rather than under cryogenic cooling. It typically covers the RF/microwave band (about 3 kHz\u2013300 GHz) and highlights device performance (noise, loss, sensitivity) achievable without temperature-controlled refrigeration."},{"id":"0bcf3ac1-4647-4b3c-9ee5-d09eb8a1bde6","l":"Rotating geophysical flows","d":1,"p":"d003cb63-dde8-403d-84dd-985e04b1fb25","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Rotating geophysical flows are fluid motions in Earth's atmosphere, oceans, or planetary interiors whose dynamics are strongly influenced by the planet's rotation: the Coriolis force alters trajectories and momentum balance. At large scales (small Rossby number) this produces nearly geostrophic flow and characteristic phenomena such as Ekman boundary layers, Rossby waves, and baroclinic instabilities."},{"id":"806835c4-96ac-4871-8ec3-b1fc5c201fa1","l":"Rotating turbulence","d":1,"p":"062f0300-c33d-4782-b24c-43996b0469f3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Rotating turbulence is turbulent fluid motion in a frame with significant background rotation, where Coriolis forces and inertial waves alter nonlinear interactions and make the flow anisotropic, often producing columnar (Taylor column) structures and a tendency toward quasi-two-dimensional dynamics. Its behavior and energy transfer (including possible inverse cascades) are controlled by nondimensional numbers such as the Rossby and Ekman numbers."},{"id":"7896b7fd-f1e6-4540-ab5a-dbaf7caa8b47","l":"Rotating wave approximation","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"The rotating wave approximation (RWA) is an approximation used in driven oscillatory systems (e.g., light\u2013matter interactions or two-level atoms) that neglects rapidly oscillating \"counter-rotating\" terms in the interaction Hamiltonian, keeping only near-resonant, energy-conserving terms. It is valid when the drive frequency is close to the system's transition frequency and the coupling is weak compared with those frequencies, so the fast terms average to zero on the timescale of the dynamics."},{"id":"50ddc138-cd85-4a3e-bb57-07181509b6da","l":"Rotational states","d":1,"p":"30e65d01-6cd5-442b-889f-ddc0c3c28157","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Rotational states are the distinct configurations of a physical system distinguished by its rotation, characterized by angular momentum and the associated rotational energy. In quantum mechanics they are eigenstates of the angular\u2011momentum operators labeled by quantum numbers (e.g., J, M) and, for a rigid rotor, have quantized energies E_J = \u0127^2 J(J+1)/2I."},{"id":"52532366-b9d9-48fd-8bc7-f791390b9c8b","l":"Roughness","d":1,"p":"eef7c13b-c67f-4d33-be97-76cee58e700a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Roughness is the small-scale deviation of a surface or interface from an ideal smooth plane, characterized by the height variations of its profile. It is usually quantified by statistical measures (e.g., RMS height or average roughness) and strongly influences contact mechanics, friction, scattering, adhesion and interfacial transport."},{"id":"df25e539-cf94-40a1-a90f-53453eed4846","l":"Rumor","d":1,"p":"3eb201bd-796e-4985-836d-af5c7e873190","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Rumor: a piece of information of uncertain veracity whose transmission through a population is studied as a dynamical spreading process; in physics and network science it is modeled (e.g., with ignorant\u2013spreader\u2013stifler compartments) using tools from statistical mechanics, stochastic processes and percolation to analyze spreading speed, thresholds, and final prevalence."},{"id":"375e9370-f563-487e-9a65-a532b6cf20f6","l":"Rydberg atoms & molecules","d":1,"p":"bd252d88-a608-4897-9416-daf84dee7f6b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Rydberg atoms and molecules are species in which one or more electrons occupy states with very large principal quantum number n, giving them enormous spatial extent, long radiative lifetimes, large polarizabilities, and energies that scale roughly as \u2212Ry/n^2 with near-classical orbital motion. Rydberg molecules are bound states involving such highly excited electrons \u2014 for example ultralong-range molecules formed when a Rydberg electron scatters from a nearby ground-state atom, or dimers bound by long-range dipole/van der Waals interactions, resulting in exceptionally large bond lengths and exaggerated interaction strengths."},{"id":"63b254a3-fa6a-4e2b-8a7c-ebe4e32c2c77","l":"Rydberg gases","d":1,"p":"46e2a993-8dce-4c28-beab-fdabe4378664","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Rydberg gases are ensembles of atoms in highly excited Rydberg states (large principal quantum number), often prepared at low temperature so atomic motion is slow compared with internal dynamics. Such Rydberg atoms have exaggerated size, long lifetimes, and enormous dipole moments/polarizabilities, giving rise to strong long-range dipole\u2013dipole or van der Waals interactions that dominate their collective and many-body behavior."},{"id":"3a52ce65-ada8-44c5-94c7-4701731accab","l":"Saffman-Taylor instability","d":1,"p":"d2fd5563-5077-41b4-9d06-f0da03883073","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"The Saffman\u2013Taylor instability (viscous fingering) is the hydrodynamic instability that occurs when a less viscous fluid displaces a more viscous one in a porous medium or between closely spaced plates (Hele\u2013Shaw cell), causing the interface to break up into finger-like protrusions. The growth and pattern selection are controlled by viscosity contrast and driving pressure/flow, with interfacial tension stabilizing short-wavelength perturbations."},{"id":"c4ed35b0-1090-409c-9667-85f9ac03e9c0","l":"Sample preparation","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Sample preparation is the set of controlled procedures used to produce a specimen with the required size, geometry, surface quality, composition, and environmental state so it can be accurately measured or analyzed by a particular experimental technique. Its goal is to preserve intrinsic properties, remove contaminants, and avoid artifacts so results are reproducible and representative of the material."},{"id":"d5fbe3ba-804d-43f1-a653-2e0c104cdba7","l":"Satellite data analysis","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Satellite data analysis is the processing, calibration, and interpretation of measurements from spaceborne instruments (imagers, spectrometers, radars, etc.) to derive physical properties of Earth, the atmosphere, or space. It includes sensor calibration and geolocation, retrieval algorithms, uncertainty characterization, time\u2011series/statistical analysis, and assimilation of observations into models for scientific and operational use."},{"id":"f751f157-1d34-4071-b0a4-9b1d33142fca","l":"Scaling laws of complex systems","d":1,"p":"d55c7570-5cb1-4f1a-b729-2647e666ffe8","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Scaling laws of complex systems are simple mathematical relationships\u2014often power laws\u2014that describe how macroscopic observables (such as correlations, fluctuations, or rates) change when the system is rescaled in size, time, or resolution. They arise from multi-scale interactions or criticality, imply approximate scale invariance and universality, and capture emergent behavior across physical, biological, and social systems."},{"id":"d7fb198e-02ff-4cb4-abf1-458dd06eebc4","l":"Scaling methods","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems","Plasma Physics"],"def":"Scaling methods are techniques that exploit dimensional analysis, similarity transformations, and scale invariance to reduce variables, identify dominant balances, and derive power-law or self-similar relationships between physical quantities. They range from simple nondimensionalization and similarity solutions to renormalization-group and finite-size scaling approaches used to relate and predict behavior across different length, time, or energy scales."},{"id":"3f6c9d4a-a53c-42ac-9f57-f8eb1dedb31d","l":"Scaling of energy technologies","d":1,"p":"39ac0edc-a645-42f8-bbf9-04e80cb0460a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Scaling of energy technologies is the study and practice of how an energy device or system\u2019s performance, efficiency, reliability, and cost change as its physical size, production volume, or deployment extent is increased or decreased (from lab prototype to utility scale). It combines physical scaling laws (e.g., power density, heat and mass transfer), material and resource limits, manufacturing learning curves, and system-level effects (grid integration, storage) that determine the technical and economic feasibility of large-scale deployment."},{"id":"a0889633-1e24-48ce-80d9-98b0139e10cb","l":"Scanning techniques","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Scanning techniques are experimental methods that obtain spatially resolved information by moving a localized probe (tip, beam, or sensor) or steering it across a sample and recording a signal at successive positions to build an image or map of physical properties. Examples include scanning probe microscopy, scanning electron and scanning near-field optical methods; their spatial resolution is set by probe size, wavelength, and scanning precision."},{"id":"ee59d387-70e9-41e3-a4a9-be5b6413e9be","l":"Scattering amplitudes","d":1,"p":"ee82ad04-6c47-46a4-a70d-c9a7d7989f6a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Scattering amplitudes are the complex-valued matrix elements of the S\u2011matrix that give the quantum-mechanical probability amplitude for a specified set of incoming particles to evolve into a specified set of outgoing particles. Their squared modulus (with kinematic factors) yields observable cross sections and decay rates, and they depend on particle momenta, spins, and the underlying interactions."},{"id":"7fb8ecb8-7f22-49e6-bd57-959dc9757c32","l":"Scattering of atoms, molecules, clusters & ions","d":1,"p":"12e1040c-7c2c-478a-b466-c979b57fcaea","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Scattering of atoms, molecules, clusters & ions is the study of collisions between neutral or charged small particles, focusing on how an incident particle is deflected, excites, ionizes, undergoes charge transfer, or fragments after interacting with a target. It characterizes the interaction potentials and reaction dynamics by measuring and calculating differential and total cross sections, rate coefficients, and final-state energy and angular distributions using classical and quantum methods."},{"id":"d72773f6-a8d3-4130-a6cd-b070d4487dcd","l":"Scattering theory","d":1,"p":"12e1040c-7c2c-478a-b466-c979b57fcaea","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Scattering theory is the theoretical framework that describes how waves or particles are deflected, absorbed, or transformed by interactions with targets or potentials, relating incoming to outgoing states. It characterizes these interactions quantitatively using scattering amplitudes, cross sections, and the S\u2011matrix in both classical and quantum contexts."},{"id":"66104f37-e222-448c-a3f0-00e2a71c36cc","l":"Schwinger-Dyson equations","d":1,"p":"ee82ad04-6c47-46a4-a70d-c9a7d7989f6a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"The Schwinger\u2013Dyson equations are an infinite hierarchy of exact relations among a quantum field theory's correlation (Green's) functions, obtained by applying the field equations or functional derivatives to the generating functional. They encode the full quantum dynamics nonperturbatively but form an unclosed set of coupled integral equations that must be truncated or approximated to be solved in practice."},{"id":"3e560f09-232b-4d98-8f5d-6ebe4bf10610","l":"Schwinger effect","d":1,"p":"ee82ad04-6c47-46a4-a70d-c9a7d7989f6a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Schwinger effect is the nonperturbative production of particle\u2013antiparticle pairs (e.g., electron\u2013positron) from the quantum vacuum induced by a very strong electric field, interpreted as tunneling across the mass gap.  It was predicted in QED by Schwinger and has a rate exponentially suppressed as ~exp(\u2212\u03c0E_c/E), where E_c = m^2c^3/(e\u0127) \u2248 1.3\u00d710^18 V/m for electrons."},{"id":"7162745c-b552-4f22-831d-b85673e0ea43","l":"Search strategy","d":1,"p":"860c01ce-5b2c-4bd5-9148-88f0b1d585e2","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Search strategy: a systematic plan for locating a targeted signal, phenomenon, or region of parameter space in experimental data or theoretical studies, specifying which observables to use and how to select or weight events. It includes choice of selection criteria or algorithms, background estimation and statistical tests, all optimized to maximize sensitivity or discovery potential while controlling false positives and uncertainties."},{"id":"71f01859-61dd-4dcb-9a16-324da77397db","l":"Secondary beams","d":1,"p":"b3310113-551e-4c5e-9263-28f393ff09f0","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Secondary beams are streams of particles produced when a primary beam (e.g., protons or ions) strikes a target or beamline element, generating new particle species (pions, muons, neutrons, neutrinos, etc.) with different energies and directions. These products are then momentum- and charge-selected and transported by magnets, collimators and decay channels for experiments or applications."},{"id":"d32d1654-92f7-41bb-a38e-060b89d49ec0","l":"Security applications of accelerators","d":1,"p":"5d582b16-6b34-4e39-b32c-21bd28c9ea7b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Security applications of accelerators are the use of particle accelerators and the beams they produce (X\u2011/gamma rays, neutrons, charged particles) to non\u2011destructively detect, image, and identify contraband, explosives, and illicit nuclear materials. Typical techniques include radiographic cargo and luggage scanning, active interrogation that induces characteristic nuclear or prompt\u2011gamma signatures, and spectroscopic methods that exploit material\u2011dependent responses for reliable threat identification."},{"id":"a8a34aa1-565d-4c0e-bef0-96d4c60c5be5","l":"Sediment transport","d":1,"p":"d003cb63-dde8-403d-84dd-985e04b1fb25","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Sediment transport is the process by which solid particles (e.g., sand, silt, gravel) are entrained, carried, and ultimately deposited by a moving fluid (water or air) or by gravity along a bed. It occurs in modes such as bed load, suspended load, and saltation and is governed by the balance between fluid shear stress and particle resistance, controlling the evolution of channels, coasts, and dunes."},{"id":"97bfddcd-43e2-47b5-9b1e-bfc0318f69a8","l":"Seismology","d":1,"p":"29ab1ea0-dd7e-46b7-87dd-0ca29c6be062","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Seismology is the study of seismic waves generated by earthquakes, explosions, or other sources to investigate the elastic properties, structure, and dynamic processes of the Earth and other planetary bodies. By analyzing wave travel times, amplitudes, and spectra, seismologists infer interior layering, material properties, and earthquake source mechanisms."},{"id":"938dcf10-206b-41c4-9d73-b6ff11504ddf","l":"Self-organized criticality","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Self-organized criticality is the tendency of driven, dissipative systems with many interacting degrees of freedom and threshold dynamics to evolve spontaneously to a critical state characterized by scale invariance and power-law distributions of event sizes, without fine-tuning of external parameters. Classic examples are the sandpile model, earthquakes, and forest fires, where slow driving and fast relaxation produce avalanches spanning a broad range of scales."},{"id":"d33e8a5a-85e6-45cb-abbb-47c88e1e3e6b","l":"Self-similarity","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Self-similarity is the property of an object or process whose structure or behavior remains invariant under a change of scale, so that parts resemble the whole when magnified or rescaled. It can be exact (as in mathematical fractals) or statistical (as in critical phenomena and turbulence), and typically leads to power-law scaling."},{"id":"c82a3805-4f41-4d31-97e7-6814e977a7cc","l":"Semiclassical physics","d":1,"p":"01f70d47-cbac-4b5a-8ff9-0768e722511b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Semiclassical physics encompasses methods that approximate quantum systems by using classical trajectories or variables augmented with leading quantum corrections (typically via expansions in Planck\u2019s constant \u0127), providing a bridge between classical mechanics and full quantum mechanics. It is valid when actions are large compared with \u0127 and includes techniques such as WKB, stationary-phase/path-integral approximations, and Bohr\u2013Sommerfeld quantization to capture interference and tunneling effects."},{"id":"12721ec5-11d3-484a-9ea9-a7bea1986722","l":"Semiconductors","d":1,"p":"81194f70-bbca-4041-b57e-29338e588cac","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Semiconductors are materials whose electrical conductivity lies between that of insulators and conductors, determined by a modest electronic band gap that allows thermal or optical excitation of electrons from the valence to the conduction band. Their charge-carrier density and type (electrons and holes) can be widely controlled by temperature, light, and intentional doping, enabling electronic and optoelectronic devices such as diodes and transistors."},{"id":"ac47229e-a05a-48bc-aab6-b2475df16eac","l":"Semicrystalline polymers","d":1,"p":"fea036a1-9c29-4872-ab2c-d052949f7c03","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Semicrystalline polymers are polymeric solids in which portions of the chains pack into ordered crystalline domains (often lamellae and spherulites) coexisting with amorphous, disordered regions. The relative fraction and morphology of these regions determine mechanical, thermal and transport properties, producing both a glass transition (amorphous) and a melting transition (crystalline)."},{"id":"76b35a9f-a4d3-4483-ab66-406f2cf056a8","l":"Semimetals","d":1,"p":"81194f70-bbca-4041-b57e-29338e588cac","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Semimetals are solids in which the valence and conduction bands either slightly overlap or touch at discrete points, so there is no full bandgap but the carrier density and density of states at the Fermi level are very small. As a result they have both electron and hole carriers with small Fermi surfaces and often show distinctive transport and topological behaviors (e.g., bismuth, graphite, Dirac/Weyl semimetals)."},{"id":"7b59f715-a6b8-4141-b762-668edfb84dc3","l":"Sensory processes","d":1,"p":"376c5d7d-24b3-40a2-a82d-a517ac25dbad","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Sensory processes are the physical and biophysical mechanisms by which organisms detect external or internal stimuli (light, sound, pressure, chemicals, temperature) and transduce them into electrical signals in sensory neurons. They include the receptor-level transduction, amplification, filtering and encoding of signals that make them suitable for neural processing."},{"id":"c8a21c51-a881-4efa-bcbe-3d0377b1a631","l":"Series expansions & exact enumeration","d":1,"p":"1e0c099a-9f2a-4e47-b3f1-7faa28b6a506","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Series expansions and exact enumeration are complementary analytical and computational techniques for studying many\u2011body systems: series expansions express physical observables as power series in a small parameter (e.g., temperature, coupling, field), with the series coefficients encoding contributions from microscopic configurations and revealing singular behavior like phase transitions. Exact enumeration obtains those coefficients or finite\u2011size thermodynamic quantities by exhaustively summing over all possible states of a finite system, giving exact results that validate, extend, or seed series analyses."},{"id":"3f586fc2-2119-4133-8746-1e078bf35be0","l":"S-factor","d":1,"p":"bbb0ce0d-36da-4da5-a443-df064f36aa17","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"The astrophysical S-factor S(E) is a redefined nuclear reaction cross section that removes the strong low\u2011energy suppression from Coulomb barrier tunneling, defined by S(E) = \u03c3(E) E exp(2\u03c0 \u03b7), where \u03c3(E) is the cross section and \u03b7 (the Sommerfeld parameter) \u221d Z1 Z2/(\u0127v) characterizes Coulomb repulsion. S(E) is typically a slowly varying function of energy and is used to extrapolate measured cross sections down to the low energies relevant in stars."},{"id":"e9bf3b02-5f76-41ca-b772-3ac5ef388ab6","l":"Shannon entropy","d":1,"p":"fd7300dc-49e9-458d-9121-de94f7446fdb","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Shannon entropy quantifies the uncertainty or information content of a probability distribution: for discrete outcomes with probabilities p_i it is defined as H = \u2212\u2211_i p_i log p_i (log base 2 gives units of bits). It equals the average number of binary (yes/no) questions \u2014 or the minimal average number of bits \u2014 needed to specify an outcome under an optimal encoding."},{"id":"6c13c1a4-c709-42e7-ab47-ef1692dd2d52","l":"Shape memory effect","d":1,"p":"15f520a3-5c6c-4d9e-8975-81d399140562","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Shape memory effect is the ability of certain materials to recover a previously imposed shape when triggered by a change in temperature (or sometimes a magnetic field). It arises from a reversible, diffusionless phase transformation\u2014typically a thermoelastic martensitic transformation in alloys like Nitinol or in shape-memory polymers\u2014that stores deformation in martensite variants and restores the original shape on reverse transformation to austenite."},{"id":"8e117b54-7f83-4338-928f-e41ed5d2de22","l":"Shear layer turbulence","d":1,"p":"062f0300-c33d-4782-b24c-43996b0469f3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Shear layer turbulence is the turbulent flow that develops in a thin region of strong velocity gradient (a shear layer) separating two fluid streams moving at different speeds or directions. It typically arises from Kelvin\u2013Helmholtz instabilities that roll up into vortical structures which break down into a cascade of eddies, producing intense mixing and enhanced momentum and energy transport."},{"id":"e82cb5d9-e645-4c84-bb57-6e789cdaa4c2","l":"Shear thickening","d":1,"p":"1932821b-d848-4b37-8209-74c6a9556c07","fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Shear thickening is a non-Newtonian rheological behavior in which a fluid's apparent viscosity increases with increasing shear rate or applied stress. It commonly occurs in dense particle suspensions when hydrodynamic forces and/or frictional particle contacts form transient clusters or force networks, producing either gradual (continuous) or abrupt (discontinuous) increases in flow resistance."},{"id":"c319f2f4-d9d0-44b4-b0c2-f7df005ff2e4","l":"Shear thinning","d":1,"p":"1932821b-d848-4b37-8209-74c6a9556c07","fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Shear thinning is a non-Newtonian rheological behavior in which a fluid's apparent viscosity decreases as the applied shear rate (or shear stress) increases. It typically arises from flow-induced microstructural changes such as alignment of polymer chains, disentanglement, or break-up of particle or network structures."},{"id":"d278d3d9-1f0d-4e6a-9bfc-b452285021f0","l":"Shear waves","d":1,"p":"0a677b11-f06d-4ced-9385-cf0bf1061a59","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Shear waves (transverse waves) are elastic waves in which particle displacement is perpendicular to the direction of propagation, producing shear strain that is restored by the material's shear rigidity. Their speed in solids is v_s = sqrt(G/\u03c1) (G = shear modulus, \u03c1 = density), and they do not propagate in ideal (inviscid) fluids because fluids cannot sustain static shear stress."},{"id":"85f8341d-5dbc-427c-96ab-511220fd44b6","l":"Shell model","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"The nuclear shell model describes the nucleus as nucleons moving independently in an average (mean-field) potential that produces quantized single-particle energy levels or \"shells,\" with filled shells giving extra stability at magic numbers. Residual nucleon\u2013nucleon interactions among valence nucleons above closed shells determine detailed nuclear spectra and transition properties."},{"id":"3b9c7cd1-014b-4adb-882c-aadc3dd737a2","l":"Shock waves","d":1,"p":"7a4e6b3d-d685-4a65-a562-4720bd830099","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Shock waves are sharply defined, propagating fronts in a compressible medium across which pressure, density, temperature and flow velocity change abruptly over a very thin region. They form when a disturbance moves faster than the local sound speed and are dissipative, irreversible structures governed by the Rankine\u2013Hugoniot jump conditions."},{"id":"6f92bd2f-a884-4762-b3c4-2d9c011a118f","l":"Sigma models","d":1,"p":"a1083ad2-a154-449d-a9ff-59bf46db1c7f","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Sigma models are field theories whose dynamical variables are maps \u03c6(x) from spacetime into a target manifold M, with an action typically given by the integral of the pullback of a metric on M (e.g., S \u221d \u222b d^dx g_{ij}(\u03c6) \u2202_\u03bc\u03c6^i \u2202^\u03bc\u03c6^j). They describe nonlinear degrees of freedom such as Goldstone bosons, condensed-matter order-parameter textures, and string worldsheets, and can include topological or Wess\u2013Zumino terms."},{"id":"b80f25ca-a9c7-4544-9499-95f7a4418f4b","l":"Signaling networks","d":1,"p":"d87d9c51-bdc2-43bb-99c3-6e5345a7f631","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Signaling networks are systems of interacting molecular components (receptors, enzymes, transcription factors, second messengers) whose biochemical interactions transmit and regulate information within and between cells. Their function is determined by network topology, reaction kinetics, feedback and noise, producing behaviors such as amplification, filtering, adaptation and decision-making."},{"id":"8f4732c2-6f6d-46e8-80a5-bd70b68b858e","l":"Silicene","d":1,"p":"7a86b9d8-3162-4279-9ca5-90ebad07be7c","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Silicene is a two-dimensional allotrope of silicon formed by a single layer of silicon atoms arranged in a honeycomb lattice that adopts a low-buckled geometry due to mixed sp2\u2013sp3 hybridization. It exhibits Dirac-like electronic bands with relatively large spin\u2013orbit coupling, making it promising for novel electronic and topological effects, though it is typically grown on substrates and is chemically less stable than graphene."},{"id":"66a5b16a-927f-478c-bba9-67c89856c8dd","l":"Sine-Gordon equation","d":1,"p":"c485f44d-2cc4-4e6d-a20b-0899fb9e53a1","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Nonlinear Dynamics"],"def":"The Sine\u2013Gordon equation is a nonlinear relativistic wave equation for a scalar field \u03d5(x,t), typically written \u2202^2_t \u03d5 \u2212 \u2202^2_x \u03d5 + sin\u03d5 = 0, in which the restoring force is sinusoidal. It arises in systems such as coupled pendula and long Josephson junctions and is notable for admitting stable topological solitons (kinks) and localized breather solutions."},{"id":"4054c254-9f83-4a0b-a8d1-1d2a99e11213","l":"Single- and few-photon ionization & excitation","d":1,"p":"ba7ccb8e-d7eb-4e6f-9c1e-b622f5b4e242","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Single-photon excitation or ionization occurs when an atom or molecule absorbs one photon whose energy matches a bound\u2013bound transition (excitation) or exceeds the ionization threshold (ejecting an electron to the continuum). Few-photon (multiphoton) excitation/ionization requires absorption of two or more photons whose combined energy reaches the same transition and therefore typically occurs only at high light intensity, with a probability that scales nonlinearly with the light intensity and can proceed via simultaneous (coherent) or sequential steps."},{"id":"0deacd9c-13eb-4a87-9371-42809bbdb980","l":"Single-component plasmas","d":1,"p":"22580d6a-8d20-4653-b2d1-a7de314bc162","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"A single-component plasma (also called a one-component plasma, OCP) is a theoretical model consisting of mobile charged particles of a single sign interacting by Coulomb forces while a uniform neutralizing background (\"jellium\") provides overall charge neutrality. It isolates the collective and correlation physics of one species (e.g., electrons or ions) and is widely used to study screening, transport, and strong-coupling phenomena."},{"id":"6a71ea1b-05e8-49de-92a8-87e5306cc3e3","l":"Single crystal materials","d":1,"p":"1128a9f3-70c1-409b-84b2-44063aa12108","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A single-crystal material is a solid in which the atomic lattice is continuous and uniformly oriented throughout the entire sample, so the periodic arrangement of atoms has a single crystallographic orientation with no grain boundaries. Because the structure is homogeneous at the macroscopic scale, many mechanical, electrical, and optical properties are anisotropic and depend on the crystal orientation."},{"id":"d21f7e96-ea6a-4c54-bed5-d62e78986605","l":"Single molecule techniques","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Single-molecule techniques are experimental methods that detect, image, or manipulate individual molecules to reveal dynamics, conformational changes, interactions, and heterogeneity that are obscured by ensemble averaging. They include approaches such as single-molecule fluorescence (including FRET), force spectroscopy with optical or magnetic tweezers and AFM, and nanopore or electrical sensing, offering high sensitivity and temporal resolution for tracking molecular trajectories and kinetics."},{"id":"6f3f4438-0693-479b-8df0-30374134e926","l":"Single-particle dynamics","d":1,"p":"cd322dee-17c3-4f48-81b4-5fbca9f2c432","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Single-particle dynamics is the study of the motion and time evolution of a single particle under applied forces or external fields. It uses classical (Newtonian, Lagrangian, Hamiltonian) or quantum (single-particle Schr\u00f6dinger/Dirac) descriptions to predict trajectories, energy changes, and scattering behavior."},{"id":"f3ad2159-b3a9-4c96-bd88-6b789b7d6a37","l":"Single polymer chains","d":1,"p":"fea036a1-9c29-4872-ab2c-d052949f7c03","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Single polymer chains are individual long macromolecules made of repeating monomer units, considered in isolation to probe molecular-scale conformations and dynamics rather than bulk material properties. Their physics is described by statistical measures (end-to-end distance, radius of gyration), stiffness scales (persistence or Kuhn length), and entropic effects that govern elasticity and diffusion."},{"id":"b33bd01e-839a-42d6-8cce-6bf2e3e1560a","l":"Skeletal system","d":1,"p":"0a74688b-2eee-4349-a9bd-e93851fe2c5a","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"The skeletal system is the internal framework of bones and connective tissues that supports body shape, protects organs, stores minerals, and\u2014via joints and muscle attachments\u2014enables and transmits forces for movement. In physics/biomechanics it is modeled as a network of rigid bodies and elastic joints/tissues that bear, distribute, and dissipate loads and moments."},{"id":"491643bd-6b81-4fb8-8b4b-7c30910e26e3","l":"Skin effect","d":1,"p":"36754399-ec2b-4962-bb07-ca12c79834e8","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Skin effect is the tendency of alternating current (or time-varying fields) in a conductor to concentrate near its surface, with current density decaying approximately exponentially into the bulk over a characteristic skin depth \u03b4 = sqrt(2/(\u03c9\u03bc\u03c3)). It arises from self-induced magnetic fields and eddy currents and leads to a higher effective resistance at higher frequencies."},{"id":"f718ea50-5533-4096-b60b-b2f3af362de4","l":"Sky surveys","d":1,"p":"893ecc24-c678-4687-bd3a-0b4a1bcb4cfc","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Sky surveys are systematic, wide-area astronomical observations that map large portions or the whole celestial sphere to detect, catalog, and measure the positions, brightnesses, spectra, and other properties of astronomical objects. Conducted across different wavelengths and timescales (photometric, spectroscopic, and time-domain surveys), they enable statistical studies, discovery of rare or transient phenomena, and mapping of large-scale cosmic structure."},{"id":"ef2580ba-7b3d-4445-9719-cce1d541b955","l":"Slender body theory","d":1,"p":"6aed0168-d867-4733-835f-da82de3c65f5","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Slender-body theory is an asymptotic method in fluid mechanics that approximates the flow and hydrodynamic forces on bodies whose length is much larger than their cross\u2011section by replacing the 3D body with a 1D distribution of singularities (e.g., stokeslets or sources) along its centerline. It reduces the boundary\u2011value problem to integral equations for the line strength, yielding leading\u2011order force and velocity relations (often with logarithmic dependence on aspect ratio) valid for high\u2011aspect\u2011ratio bodies in Stokes or potential flows."},{"id":"3c9ff605-5540-4e27-b7fb-a7fefc4047cb","l":"Slidetronics","d":1,"p":"9e019803-f9d8-4802-b1ac-d2c035d9508f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Slidetronics is the emerging area of electronics that uses lateral sliding between stacked two-dimensional (van der Waals) layers as an active control knob: changing the relative registry alters interlayer coupling, moir\u00e9 patterns, polarization and band structure, thereby tuning conductance, optical response and other device properties."},{"id":"095b7316-525f-4a29-a371-7526dd01993c","l":"Smectic liquid crystals","d":1,"p":"63f7a3cf-31b7-4d5c-bbcc-58d679dd5139","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Smectic liquid crystals are layered mesophases in which anisotropic molecules form regularly spaced planes, producing one-dimensional positional order along the layer normal while retaining fluid, liquid-like mobility within each layer. They also have orientational order of the molecular long axes, with variants (e.g., SmA, SmC) distinguished by the average tilt of the molecules and by the degree of in-layer positional ordering."},{"id":"18aaf314-921a-463e-aacb-1b7898708dc4","l":"Social networks","d":1,"p":"b4d51608-61b8-4e73-8186-ba02e5a41a85","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Social networks are graphs that represent social entities (people, groups, organizations) as nodes and the social ties or interactions between them (friendship, communication, collaboration) as edges. Physicists study their topology and dynamics\u2014e.g., degree distributions, clustering, small-world or scale-free structure\u2014and processes on them such as information or disease spreading, synchronization, and collective behavior using tools from statistical mechanics and network theory."},{"id":"917de97e-071f-44ab-aca0-64e0be0dcb4f","l":"Soft colloids","d":1,"p":"ef0a1bee-2a1f-4f47-aca2-6302db18e67f","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Soft colloids are mesoscopic dispersed particles whose internal structure, surface layers, or liquid interfaces allow them to deform, compress, swell or interpenetrate, so they do not behave like rigid hard spheres. Examples include polymeric micelles, microgels, star polymers and vesicles; their softness produces tunable, often long-range or bounded effective interactions and leads to distinctive phase behavior (e.g., reentrant melting, clustering, gelation)."},{"id":"d2b62966-4eb1-4cbd-8ea1-4b34ea070e52","l":"Solar energy","d":1,"p":"af9f5a05-6917-40cf-a2ff-5520ff506935","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Solar energy is the electromagnetic radiation produced by the Sun (primarily from nuclear fusion in its core) that arrives at the Earth across a broad spectrum of wavelengths. It can be absorbed as heat or converted into electrical power or chemical energy using technologies like solar thermal collectors and photovoltaic cells."},{"id":"4501b22f-36e3-4024-8fe8-7222b02313eb","l":"Solar neutrinos","d":1,"p":"38c3eaa1-e9a7-4590-9a93-cbdfd9fe162f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics","Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Solar neutrinos are the nearly massless, weakly interacting electron neutrinos produced in the Sun's core by nuclear fusion reactions (primarily the proton\u2013proton chain and the CNO cycle) that accompany proton-to-neutron conversions. Because they escape the Sun unimpeded, their flux and energy spectrum provide a direct probe of solar energy generation, and they undergo flavor oscillations on the way to Earth."},{"id":"f712db32-2bc9-4e97-9f42-a16c12832ff8","l":"Solar plasma","d":1,"p":"88a5cd35-609c-4f0a-8994-5a4cbe488296","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Solar plasma is the Sun's state of matter: a hot, ionized gas of ions, electrons (and some neutrals) that is electrically conducting and nearly charge-neutral. Its collective electromagnetic and fluid behavior (governed by magnetohydrodynamics) produces convection, magnetic fields, the solar wind, flares, and coronal structures."},{"id":"ae59b377-6cb1-47fa-8200-b6b1aa41a27b","l":"Solar system & its planets","d":1,"p":"c743ca36-88d6-4f26-adea-cdfd0732c4f9","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"The Solar System is the Sun together with the ensemble of natural bodies\u2014planets, dwarf planets, moons, asteroids, comets, dust, and interplanetary gas and plasma\u2014that are gravitationally bound to it and orbit it. Its planets are the major bodies (eight by IAU definition) that orbit the Sun, are massive enough for self-gravity to make them nearly spherical (hydrostatic equilibrium), and have cleared their orbital neighborhoods of other comparable-mass objects."},{"id":"d0c80de4-78ce-49eb-9bdb-d55be50a1b77","l":"Solid hydrogen","d":1,"p":"db7ba2bd-700a-4b0b-b444-b873c97ff818","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Solid hydrogen is hydrogen in its condensed, crystalline state\u2014typically composed of H2 molecules arranged in a lattice at low temperature and moderate pressure\u2014characterized by large quantum zero\u2011point motion and ortho/para spin isomers. Under extreme (megabar) pressures it undergoes phase transitions and is predicted to dissociate into an atomic, metallic form with very different electronic properties."},{"id":"2420e949-77f8-42a5-99aa-41d65c54e190","l":"Solid-state chemistry","d":1,"p":"4455b073-c3a2-4a0b-923e-98d7ec0f3b9d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Solid-state chemistry is the study of the synthesis, composition, crystal structure, and bonding of solid materials and how these atomic-scale features determine their electronic, magnetic, optical, and mechanical properties. It combines chemical methods for making and modifying solids with characterization of phases, defects, and phase transitions to understand and control material behavior."},{"id":"289f39c0-4d9a-4945-a239-40ac02d09bf6","l":"Solid-surface planets","d":1,"p":"c743ca36-88d6-4f26-adea-cdfd0732c4f9","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Solid-surface planets are planetary bodies whose outermost layer is a solid\u2014rock, metal, or high-pressure ice\u2014so that a definable physical surface exists, unlike gas or ice giants dominated by thick fluid envelopes. Examples include terrestrial planets (Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars) and icy worlds (Europa, Ganymede, Titan)."},{"id":"50db6239-887e-452f-9588-1545b9f3612e","l":"Solitary waves","d":1,"p":"0a677b11-f06d-4ced-9385-cf0bf1061a59","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"A solitary wave is a single, localized wave pulse that travels at constant speed without changing shape, typically because nonlinear effects (which tend to steepen the pulse) balance dispersion (which tends to spread it). If such pulses also emerge unchanged from mutual collisions they are called solitons."},{"id":"d56fd9aa-2ab9-4bee-9475-10e35c4ef897","l":"Solitons","d":1,"p":"cad250bc-84eb-4c9d-82f1-ce01969ff172","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Nonlinear Dynamics","Particles & Fields","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Plasma Physics"],"def":"Solitons are stable, localized wave packets that propagate without changing shape in nonlinear dispersive media. They occur when nonlinearity balances dispersion (or diffraction), producing particle-like waves that retain their form and can emerge unchanged from collisions, as described by equations like the Korteweg\u2013de Vries or nonlinear Schr\u00f6dinger equations."},{"id":"14a956fa-8d1b-487a-8e4d-93bb1ff7f8a5","l":"Solvation","d":1,"p":"4455b073-c3a2-4a0b-923e-98d7ec0f3b9d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Solvation is the process by which solvent molecules surround and interact with a dissolved species (ion, molecule, or atom), forming a structured solvation shell that stabilizes the solute through electrostatic, hydrogen-bonding, and dispersion forces. The associated rearrangement and interactions determine solubility and contribute a solvation energy that affects the system's thermodynamics and kinetics."},{"id":"852a9d7d-4874-4ad9-bbc4-6957aa7b1d18","l":"Sonoluminescence","d":1,"p":"884ba888-9ed7-43f7-ae3d-360ffe6d2df0","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Sonoluminescence is the emission of short, intense flashes of broadband light from a gas bubble in a liquid that is driven to rapid collapse by an acoustic (sound) field. The violent cavitation and implosion of the bubble focus energy into the gas, producing extremely high local temperatures and pressures and resulting in picosecond\u2013nanosecond light pulses."},{"id":"803a843e-f2fa-464f-9e82-ac35c3e286c5","l":"Sound waves","d":1,"p":"cad250bc-84eb-4c9d-82f1-ce01969ff172","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Sound waves are mechanical longitudinal waves of compression and rarefaction that propagate through a material medium (gas, liquid, or solid) via local pressure and density variations. They transport energy and momentum without net mass flow and are characterized by frequency, wavelength, amplitude, and a propagation speed set by the medium's elastic and inertial properties."},{"id":"d794d6b2-dac3-40e5-906e-24fc867d825f","l":"Sound wave techniques","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Sound wave techniques are experimental and analytical methods that generate, manipulate, and detect acoustic waves (audible to ultrasonic) to probe, image, or measure the properties, motion, and geometry of materials, fluids, and structures. They exploit reflection, transmission, scattering, absorption and Doppler effects to infer composition, distance, flow, stress, or other physical parameters (e.g., in sonar, medical ultrasound, acoustic spectroscopy, and nondestructive testing)."},{"id":"ac85bff7-027d-4815-8944-d82d92505713","l":"Space science","d":1,"p":"29ab1ea0-dd7e-46b7-87dd-0ca29c6be062","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Space science is the interdisciplinary study of physical processes and phenomena occurring beyond Earth's atmosphere \u2014 including the formation and behavior of planets, stars, interplanetary and interstellar matter, and the space environment (radiation, magnetic fields, microgravity). It uses observations, in situ spacecraft measurements, laboratory experiments, and theoretical models to understand the origins, dynamics, and effects of space on matter and technology."},{"id":"8eec6905-8553-46b9-881f-5ba007405a6c","l":"Spacetime symmetries","d":1,"p":"54b389aa-8ccf-49ee-a54e-478ccc2616eb","fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Spacetime symmetries are transformations of the space and time coordinates that leave the spacetime structure or the dynamical laws invariant\u2014examples include translations, rotations, and Lorentz boosts (the Poincar\u00e9 group) and, in GR, general coordinate diffeomorphisms. They constrain the allowed form of physical laws and, via Noether\u2019s theorem, correspond to conserved quantities such as energy, momentum, and angular momentum."},{"id":"3bb6bdb7-a4c6-40fa-8637-1c077aaf1d2d","l":"Spatiotemporal chaos","d":1,"p":"bb2351de-d2a6-4d4c-a33e-9396bc991c32","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Nonlinear Dynamics"],"def":"Spatiotemporal chaos is the irregular, aperiodic behavior of spatially extended dynamical systems in which both the temporal evolution and the spatial patterns are unpredictable and sensitive to initial conditions. It typically involves many interacting degrees of freedom and is diagnosed by positive Lyapunov exponents (often an extensive spectrum), coexistence of local coherent structures, and complex, time-varying spatial correlations."},{"id":"de7c563b-63ee-4b54-b76c-be410edf4051","l":"Specific heat","d":1,"p":"25bdd3c1-c3ec-4e4e-a84e-6d4152bec3b2","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Specific heat (specific heat capacity) is the amount of heat required to raise the temperature of one unit mass of a substance by one kelvin (or one degree Celsius), typically given in J\u00b7kg\u207b\u00b9\u00b7K\u207b\u00b9. It is an intensive, material-specific property that can depend on temperature and on the thermodynamic constraint (e.g., cp at constant pressure or cv at constant volume)."},{"id":"19f26688-0955-4fd9-85d8-aaf71c4a581f","l":"Specific phase transitions","d":1,"p":"f2a9db78-1651-4a94-b66e-3e3d72056760","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Specific phase transitions are particular kinds of changes between distinct states of matter or phases, classified by their microscopic mechanisms and universal behavior\u2014for example first\u2011order transitions (latent heat and a discontinuous order parameter), continuous/second\u2011order transitions (no latent heat, divergent correlation length and critical exponents), topological transitions (e.g., Kosterlitz\u2013Thouless), or quantum phase transitions (driven by quantum fluctuations at zero temperature). Each type is characterized by how thermodynamic quantities and symmetries change (e.g., discontinuities in derivatives of the free energy, emergence of long\u2011range order, or topological order)."},{"id":"97e2d545-ee8b-42a3-bb91-36c49d6c5ffc","l":"Spectrometers & spectroscopic techniques","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Spectrometers are instruments that disperse and measure the intensity of electromagnetic radiation (or particles) as a function of wavelength, frequency, or energy to produce a spectrum. Spectroscopic techniques use those spectra\u2014via absorption, emission, scattering, fluorescence, Raman, etc.\u2014to infer material composition, electronic/rotational/vibrational energy levels, temperatures, densities, velocities (Doppler shifts) and magnetic or electric field effects."},{"id":"cd8557b1-6114-4e5a-a78b-6612f197dbf9","l":"Spectroscopic factors & electromagnetic moments","d":1,"p":"1cf75de2-e14b-41cd-b733-8f704652db33","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Spectroscopic factors are quantities (squared overlaps) that measure how strongly a many-body nucleus (or other quantum system) contains a given single-particle configuration, and are extracted from transfer or knockout reaction cross sections to indicate orbital occupancies and correlation effects. Electromagnetic moments are the static multipole moments (e.g., magnetic dipole, electric quadrupole) of a system\u2019s charge and current distribution that characterize its shape and magnetization and determine its coupling to external electromagnetic fields."},{"id":"d8d9518f-91ee-40ab-9529-2c3f98431a0f","l":"Spectroscopy","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics"],"def":"Spectroscopy is the study of how matter interacts with electromagnetic radiation as a function of wavelength or frequency. By measuring absorption, emission, or scattering spectra, it reveals the energy levels, composition, structure, and dynamical properties of atoms, molecules, and materials."},{"id":"8fc05421-d713-43be-b560-4bca5de50eab","l":"Spin","d":1,"p":"7de7a12f-2bec-4394-98aa-26aebe36d88f","fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Spin is an intrinsic quantum-mechanical form of angular momentum carried by elementary particles, nuclei, and composite systems, quantized in units of \u210f and taking integer or half-integer values that label representations of the rotation group. It is not literal rotation of matter, but an inherent property that determines magnetic moments and whether a particle obeys Fermi\u2011Dirac (fermion) or Bose\u2011Einstein (boson) statistics."},{"id":"03fcb893-9d89-43e7-8eda-c1a20edfbec7","l":"Spin blockade","d":1,"p":"4722b240-3d0b-4ab1-831a-75302ab4cf86","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Spin blockade is the suppression of electronic transport through a small system (commonly a double quantum dot) that occurs when spin conservation and the Pauli exclusion principle prevent occupied spin states from tunneling into available final states, so charge flow is blocked until a spin flip occurs. It is typically observed when electrons form a triplet configuration that cannot convert to the accessible singlet state without spin relaxation or mixing by mechanisms such as spin\u2013orbit coupling or hyperfine interactions."},{"id":"1fa8daba-8a48-4d9f-ada9-03a9d3259ca3","l":"Spin dynamics","d":1,"p":"b35238d0-83c7-4412-b293-c7d42fa92c62","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Spin dynamics is the study of how intrinsic angular momentum (spin) evolves in time under applied fields and interactions, including coherent precession, relaxation, and decoherence. It encompasses quantum and semiclassical descriptions (Schr\u00f6dinger or density-matrix evolution, Bloch equations, Landau\u2013Lifshitz\u2013Gilbert) and underlies phenomena in NMR, spintronics, and quantum information."},{"id":"12590779-553a-4c0b-80ab-4798eefd261f","l":"Spin glasses","d":1,"p":"373a7206-3c07-47bb-9078-efd80ed70556","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Spin glasses are disordered magnetic systems in which localized spins interact through competing (ferromagnetic and antiferromagnetic) couplings and quenched disorder, so that no conventional long-range order is established. Below a characteristic glass temperature the spins freeze into a highly degenerate, non\u2011ergodic state with many metastable configurations and slow, history\u2011dependent dynamics."},{"id":"5c251acc-774e-4f23-a66c-42180874bd08","l":"Spinon","d":1,"p":"d46f32eb-17b3-4d09-be59-fc3461e542db","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A spinon is an emergent quasiparticle excitation in strongly correlated quantum magnets that carries spin-1/2 but no electric charge, produced by the fractionalization of an electron's spin degree of freedom (common in 1D spin chains and quantum spin liquids). Depending on the host system, spinons can obey fermionic, bosonic, or anyonic statistics and govern spin transport and magnetic response."},{"id":"4201a122-4a87-4b76-af1c-5e52bb109c78","l":"Spin-orbit coupling","d":1,"p":"9e019803-f9d8-4802-b1ac-d2c035d9508f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Spin-orbit coupling is a relativistic interaction that links a particle's intrinsic spin to its orbital motion (often written \u221d L\u00b7S), so the orientation of the spin affects the particle's orbital energy. In atoms it stems from an electron moving in the nucleus' electric field seeing an effective magnetic field that interacts with its magnetic moment, and in solids the same effect couples electron spin to momentum, producing spin-dependent band splittings and related phenomena."},{"id":"a475f4df-cfe6-4370-8745-220c6daada66","l":"Spin-phonon coupling","d":1,"p":"9e019803-f9d8-4802-b1ac-d2c035d9508f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Spin-phonon coupling is the interaction between a solid's lattice vibrations (phonons) and its electronic spin degrees of freedom, where atomic displacements alter magnetic interactions (e.g., exchange or spin\u2013orbit couplings) and spins in turn affect the lattice. This mutual coupling shifts and broadens phonon frequencies and can link magnetic and structural dynamics (manifesting as magnetoelastic effects, magnetostriction, or phonon-mediated spin relaxation)."},{"id":"37878882-17a8-4ac8-b077-4ed365a5d7ac","l":"Spin-singlet pairing","d":1,"p":"ca8ddaad-441c-481c-93b3-65f46125fb93","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Spin-singlet pairing is a two-fermion bound state with total spin zero, in which the particles have antiparallel spins and an antisymmetric spin wavefunction. By Fermi statistics the orbital (spatial) part is even-parity (symmetric), as in conventional s-wave BCS superconductors (and also d-wave singlet states in cuprates)."},{"id":"c1bebd2f-48a5-42c0-b301-3228bcf0151e","l":"Spin-triplet pairing","d":1,"p":"ca8ddaad-441c-481c-93b3-65f46125fb93","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Spin-triplet pairing is a form of Cooper pairing in which two fermions combine to a total spin S = 1 (three spin projections m = \u22121, 0, +1), so the spin part of the pair wavefunction is symmetric. For identical fermions the Pauli principle then forces an antisymmetric (odd-parity, e.g. p-wave) spatial orbital, a pairing symmetry that underlies superfluid 3He and some unconventional superconductors and often permits equal-spin pairing and nonzero spin susceptibility."},{"id":"29a5aa4f-d2c0-43ce-98a8-21cbbf2ae916","l":"Spintronics","d":1,"p":"b35238d0-83c7-4412-b293-c7d42fa92c62","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Spintronics (spin electronics) is the study and technology of using the electron's spin degree of freedom\u2014and its associated magnetic moment\u2014alongside charge to carry, manipulate, and store information. It exploits spin-dependent transport and magnetic phenomena (e.g., giant magnetoresistance, spin-transfer torque, spin\u2013orbit coupling) to build faster, lower-power, and nonvolatile electronic devices."},{"id":"014da66e-408d-481d-98a5-1d3fac1c2a07","l":"Spin valves","d":1,"p":"58071c68-730c-4a3e-982c-abc9f0cedf67","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A spin valve is a layered spintronic device consisting of two ferromagnetic films separated by a nonmagnetic spacer, where the electrical resistance depends on the relative orientation of the films' magnetizations (low resistance when parallel, high when antiparallel). This magnetoresistive behavior arises from spin-dependent scattering or transport (giant magnetoresistance) and is used in magnetic sensors and hard\u2011drive read heads, with one ferromagnet typically \"pinned\" and the other free to switch."},{"id":"eb3619b8-4516-48de-a078-d037f5c7578a","l":"Spontaneous emission","d":1,"p":"ba7ccb8e-d7eb-4e6f-9c1e-b622f5b4e242","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Spontaneous emission is the quantum process by which an excited atom, molecule, or other emitter decays to a lower energy state and emits a photon without any external driving field. It is intrinsically probabilistic and driven by the emitter's coupling to the vacuum electromagnetic field, characterized by a decay rate (the Einstein A coefficient) that determines the excited-state lifetime."},{"id":"a097ac4a-8104-4f6f-a5ad-6928cc82e502","l":"Spontaneous symmetry breaking","d":1,"p":"ee82ad04-6c47-46a4-a70d-c9a7d7989f6a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Spontaneous symmetry breaking occurs when the equations governing a system are symmetric under some transformation but the system\u2019s lowest-energy (ground) state settles into one of several equivalent configurations that are not symmetric, so the symmetry is not realized by the state. This produces degenerate ground states and an order parameter, and for continuous broken symmetries yields gapless collective excitations (Goldstone modes)\u2014for example, a ferromagnet below its Curie temperature chooses a magnetization direction despite rotationally symmetric interactions."},{"id":"74239a90-9c27-4056-b15e-1f8f41ed0e03","l":"s process","d":1,"p":"670457a5-ade7-47cf-b508-6ff87f3d30e0","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics","Nuclear Physics"],"def":"The s-process (slow neutron-capture process) is a stellar nucleosynthesis mechanism in which nuclei capture neutrons on timescales long compared with their beta-decay lifetimes, so the path of synthesis follows stable isotopes near the valley of beta stability. It occurs mainly in asymptotic giant branch and some massive stars and produces roughly half of the elements heavier than iron up to bismuth."},{"id":"dc4cb24f-7c96-4fad-ad33-1c7abe6e1f8c","l":"Stable compressible flows","d":1,"p":"7a4e6b3d-d685-4a65-a562-4720bd830099","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Stable compressible flows are fluid motions in which density variations and acoustic effects are important, and small perturbations either decay or remain bounded so the flow returns to or stays close to a reference state. Stability may be assessed in linear or nonlinear senses, depending on whether infinitesimal or finite disturbances are considered."},{"id":"94c56f5f-9451-4c87-b737-e69975215ca3","l":"Stanene","d":1,"p":"7a86b9d8-3162-4279-9ca5-90ebad07be7c","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Stanene is a two-dimensional monolayer of tin atoms arranged in a graphene-like honeycomb lattice (typically with a slight buckling). It is predicted to be a strong spin\u2013orbit-coupled topological insulator (quantum spin Hall system), with a large band gap that could enable dissipationless edge conduction near room temperature."},{"id":"8ddbaf7e-007c-44c0-9f88-c7195c390f92","l":"Stark effect","d":1,"p":"ba7ccb8e-d7eb-4e6f-9c1e-b622f5b4e242","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"The Stark effect is the shifting and splitting of atomic or molecular energy levels (and thus their spectral lines) induced by an external electric field, caused by the interaction of the field with the system's electric dipole moment and polarizability. Depending on level symmetry and degeneracy, the energy shift can be linear or quadratic in the applied field strength."},{"id":"0660b3fe-654b-49c9-a7fa-79f18efe2aa0","l":"Statistical field theory","d":1,"p":"ee82ad04-6c47-46a4-a70d-c9a7d7989f6a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Statistical field theory is the framework that describes many-body systems by promoting microscopic degrees of freedom to continuous fields and expressing thermodynamic averages and correlation functions as functional (path) integrals over those fields. It uses field-theoretic tools\u2014perturbation theory, functional renormalization, and stochastic equations\u2014to analyze fluctuations, critical phenomena, and phase transitions in both classical and quantum systems."},{"id":"6f7e48fc-bee9-46d1-9e16-06a774ac336e","l":"Statistical hydrodynamics","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Statistical hydrodynamics is the branch of fluid dynamics that uses statistical and probabilistic methods to describe and predict the macroscopic properties of turbulent and fluctuating flows, focusing on ensemble averages, correlation functions, probability distributions, and scaling laws rather than individual deterministic realizations. It applies ideas from statistical mechanics and stochastic processes to analyze energy cascades, fluctuations, and closure problems in the Navier\u2013Stokes equations."},{"id":"695da376-c44a-41dd-b968-d200c31e9d5e","l":"Statistical methods","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems","Plasma Physics"],"def":"Statistical methods are mathematical techniques based on probability theory used to summarize data, estimate model parameters, test hypotheses, and quantify uncertainties in experiments and simulations. They encompass tools such as descriptive statistics, regression and model fitting, hypothesis testing, Bayesian inference, and Monte Carlo sampling to extract reliable conclusions from noisy measurements."},{"id":"dd79c77f-fc7a-4956-acbe-5e680c90fb06","l":"Statistical phenomena & chaos","d":1,"p":"bbb0ce0d-36da-4da5-a443-df064f36aa17","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Statistical phenomena are behaviors of systems with many degrees of freedom or strong fluctuations that are described by probabilistic laws and ensemble averages rather than exact microstates. Chaos describes deterministic dynamics that show extreme sensitivity to initial conditions and complex phase-space structure (e.g., positive Lyapunov exponents and strange attractors), making long-term prediction effectively impossible and often requiring statistical descriptions like invariant measures."},{"id":"9b591609-822d-4be9-93a8-dff9f7f3722c","l":"Stellar plasmas","d":1,"p":"88a5cd35-609c-4f0a-8994-5a4cbe488296","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Stellar plasmas are the hot, ionized gases that make up stars, consisting of free electrons, ions, and radiation whose dynamics are governed by electromagnetic forces, particle collisions, and gravity. Their collective properties\u2014pressure (including radiation pressure), energy transport by radiation and convection, and conditions enabling nuclear fusion\u2014set a star's structure and evolution."},{"id":"07e7cee9-576b-4952-9dac-421fe196fa96","l":"Stem cell networks","d":1,"p":"d87d9c51-bdc2-43bb-99c3-6e5345a7f631","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Stem cell networks are the interconnected systems of stem cells, their niche cells, and the molecular regulatory links (cell\u2013cell signaling, gene regulatory and signaling pathways) that control self\u2011renewal, differentiation, and tissue organization. They are commonly modeled as networks (nodes = cells, genes or signals; edges = interactions) to analyze dynamical behaviors such as stability, bifurcations, pattern formation, and robustness."},{"id":"7b287382-c7f9-480a-bc80-3ac6fe07b665","l":"Stereochemical interactions","d":1,"p":"cc59c6fb-acbb-4237-953b-b874fe5dbd4a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Stereochemical interactions are the forces and energy differences that depend on the three\u2011dimensional arrangement of atoms in a molecule; because geometry alters steric repulsion, orbital overlap and electrostatic contacts, these interactions control conformational stability, reaction pathways and chiral-specific binding."},{"id":"20a16e31-69dd-4e1b-9a44-2df3b053d9fd","l":"Stereochemistry","d":1,"p":"fea036a1-9c29-4872-ab2c-d052949f7c03","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Stereochemistry is the branch of chemistry that studies the three-dimensional arrangement of atoms in molecules and how that spatial organization determines their physical, chemical, and biological properties. It focuses on stereoisomers (e.g., enantiomers and diastereomers) and conformational changes, where differences in geometry \u2014 such as chirality \u2014 lead to distinct behaviors despite identical bonding connectivity."},{"id":"aadb696a-cc64-4503-8983-e6283e12f3af","l":"Sterile neutrinos","d":1,"p":"3981a374-dbc8-42de-bdba-73ea858650c6","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Sterile neutrinos are hypothetical neutral fermions that do not participate in Standard Model weak interactions \u2014 they are gauge-singlet (often right-handed) neutrino states. They can mix with the ordinary \"active\" neutrinos, providing mechanisms for neutrino masses and oscillation anomalies and, depending on their mass, can be candidates for heavy neutrinos or dark matter."},{"id":"5fc3d219-4226-4833-b792-af16fe373dbb","l":"Stochastic analysis methods","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Stochastic analysis methods are mathematical tools for modeling and analyzing physical systems driven by randomness or noise, using probability theory and stochastic processes to predict statistical behavior and fluctuations. They include techniques such as stochastic differential equations (e.g., Langevin and Fokker\u2013Planck equations), master equations, and Monte Carlo simulations to describe time evolution, steady states, and statistical moments."},{"id":"9130d4e0-dfe3-483f-bc4c-2447daf3b2d0","l":"Stochastic dynamical systems","d":1,"p":"c485f44d-2cc4-4e6d-a20b-0899fb9e53a1","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Nonlinear Dynamics"],"def":"Stochastic dynamical systems are systems whose time evolution combines deterministic laws with random fluctuations, so the state follows probability distributions rather than a single trajectory. They are modeled by stochastic differential or jump/Markov equations and are characterized by transition probabilities, invariant measures, and noise-driven phenomena (e.g., fluctuations, switching, or escape)."},{"id":"c307ba1f-5316-4861-80ea-cf921f8a1327","l":"Stochastic inference","d":1,"p":"b96ba7a9-cce1-470c-b80c-6dadb2c6b8e9","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Stochastic inference is the process of estimating unknown parameters, states, or models from noisy data by formulating the problem probabilistically and using randomized algorithms (e.g., Monte Carlo, MCMC, particle filters) to produce probability distributions or samples that quantify uncertainty. It emphasizes uncertainty-aware estimates rather than single deterministic solutions."},{"id":"ecf441e1-8a3d-4516-a7a5-2474481e09bd","l":"Stochastic resetting","d":1,"p":"860c01ce-5b2c-4bd5-9148-88f0b1d585e2","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Stochastic resetting is a stochastic process in which a system's dynamics are intermittently interrupted and the system is returned (reset) to a prescribed state or distribution at random times (commonly Poisson-distributed). This mechanism drives the system out of equilibrium, often producing a non\u2011equilibrium steady state and strongly modifying statistics such as first\u2011passage times and search efficiencies."},{"id":"56533bc5-19ba-40e4-aae2-8fd8bc7c0a83","l":"Stochastic resonance","d":1,"p":"860c01ce-5b2c-4bd5-9148-88f0b1d585e2","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Stochastic resonance is a phenomenon in which an optimal amount of random noise enhances a nonlinear system's ability to detect or amplify a weak periodic signal, producing a resonance-like peak in response as noise intensity is varied. It typically occurs in systems with thresholds or bistability, where noise-driven transitions become synchronized with the periodic forcing."},{"id":"7d7cf43c-3151-4c5a-a05c-fa6577013893","l":"Stochastic restart","d":1,"p":"860c01ce-5b2c-4bd5-9148-88f0b1d585e2","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Stochastic restart is the mechanism of intermittently interrupting a random dynamical process and returning it to a specified initial state (either at random or scheduled times), thereby modifying its completion or first-passage statistics. Properly tuned restart can shorten mean completion times, change distribution tails, and is used to optimize search, reaction, and algorithmic processes."},{"id":"a63601d3-32c0-40e0-b1ce-57d81e1edf02","l":"Stokes equations","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"The Stokes equations are the linearized form of the Navier\u2013Stokes equations that govern slow (low\u2013Reynolds-number), viscous, incompressible flow, obtained by neglecting the nonlinear convective term. They express force balance as \u2212\u2207p + \u03bc\u2207\u00b2u + f = 0 (steady case) together with the incompressibility constraint \u2207\u00b7u = 0 (with \u03c1\u2202u/\u2202t retained for the unsteady Stokes problem), where u is the velocity, p the pressure, \u03bc the dynamic viscosity and f body forces."},{"id":"44a16592-4eaa-402d-a24c-41399258fbd4","l":"Stokesian dynamics","d":1,"p":"0ac46efd-0f43-4786-a998-8f9d0042ffd8","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Plasma Physics","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Stokesian dynamics is a particle-based numerical method for simulating the collective motion of suspended particles in creeping (low\u2013Reynolds-number) flows by explicitly accounting for many-body hydrodynamic interactions. It combines far-field mobility (multipole) approximations with near-field lubrication corrections and optionally Brownian forces to compute particle velocities from applied forces and torques."},{"id":"db8cf878-165f-4ddd-aca4-0adc2002945c","l":"Straintronics","d":1,"p":"c765ac18-ded5-4ef0-a41a-22be13741559","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Straintronics is the study and application of controlling a material's electronic, magnetic, or optical properties by applying mechanical strain, thereby tuning its band structure, magnetic anisotropy, or coupling. It exploits strain-mediated effects (e.g., via piezoelectric substrates or in 2D materials) to achieve functions such as low-energy switching, sensing, and reconfigurable electronic or spintronic devices."},{"id":"0e6266fe-017a-447d-925e-67b21355b5c3","l":"Strange quark","d":1,"p":"683890e6-25e0-4d29-9686-308d5800fda7","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"The strange quark (symbol s) is a second-generation elementary fermion in the quark family with electric charge \u22121/3 e, spin 1/2, and a flavor quantum number called strangeness; it participates in the strong, electromagnetic, and weak interactions and forms strange hadrons such as kaons and hyperons. It is heavier than the up and down quarks (current mass \u224895 MeV/c^2) and was named \"strange\" because its presence leads to long-lived, unusually produced particles."},{"id":"133ff954-fb67-4d15-ae83-a684a1463dd6","l":"Stratified geophysical flows","d":1,"p":"d003cb63-dde8-403d-84dd-985e04b1fb25","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Stratified geophysical flows are fluid motions in the atmosphere or ocean in which a stable vertical density gradient (from temperature, salinity, or composition) imposes strong buoyancy forces. This stratification suppresses vertical mixing and supports internal gravity waves and layered dynamics that, together with Earth's rotation, control large-scale circulation and instabilities."},{"id":"a4908f4f-f225-4785-a82d-e2090248686d","l":"Stratified turbulence","d":1,"p":"062f0300-c33d-4782-b24c-43996b0469f3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Stratified turbulence is turbulent motion in a fluid with a stable density gradient (e.g., due to temperature or salinity) in which buoyancy forces and internal gravity waves strongly modify and often suppress vertical motions, producing direction-dependent (layered) eddies and mixing along density surfaces. Its behavior is controlled by the buoyancy (Brunt\u2013V\u00e4is\u00e4l\u00e4) frequency and nondimensional parameters such as the Froude and Richardson numbers."},{"id":"4fb63b7b-53b3-49e8-923f-9b158d32ef0e","l":"Stress","d":1,"p":"9e001bda-d32b-4a59-a216-bfd4c1af8c25","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Stress is the internal force per unit area transmitted across an imagined surface inside a material that quantifies how the material resists deformation; it is a second\u2011rank tensor whose components include normal and shear stresses. Locally, stress is defined as the limit of force divided by area as the area element shrinks to zero, with SI unit pascal (N/m^2)."},{"id":"47915832-4789-42cf-8f76-2031fa668729","l":"String dualities","d":1,"p":"cbc41431-fba0-4ff0-8134-2ded6e598922","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"String dualities are exact equivalences showing that apparently different string theories\u2014or different parameter regimes of the same theory\u2014are physically the same, related by maps between their fields, couplings, and geometric data. Examples include T\u2011duality (which exchanges compactification radius with winding versus momentum modes) and S\u2011duality (which relates weak and strong coupling)."},{"id":"504172b6-f4f3-4044-a5d8-3ea5d8dcfdcf","l":"String phenomenology","d":1,"p":"cbc41431-fba0-4ff0-8134-2ded6e598922","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"String phenomenology is the subfield that builds and analyzes low-energy effective models derived from string theory with the goal of connecting its higher-dimensional, quantum-gravitational framework to observable particle physics and cosmology. It studies compactifications, moduli stabilization, supersymmetry breaking and other mechanisms to derive particle spectra, dark-matter candidates, inflationary dynamics and experimental signatures that could test string theory."},{"id":"2007366a-9fc5-499c-90bd-02ba78a14762","l":"Strong electromagnetic field effects","d":1,"p":"ba7ccb8e-d7eb-4e6f-9c1e-b622f5b4e242","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Strong electromagnetic field effects occur when electromagnetic fields are so intense that linear, perturbative approximations fail and the fields induce nonlinear, relativistic, or nonperturbative responses in matter or the vacuum. Examples include relativistic electron motion in ultra-strong lasers, nonlinear optical responses, radiation reaction, vacuum polarization, and electron\u2013positron pair production near the QED (Schwinger) critical field."},{"id":"b7d922eb-c308-425c-8664-93cd3b064849","l":"Strong-field approximation","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"The strong-field approximation (SFA) is a theoretical method for intense laser\u2013matter interactions that models ionization by treating the liberated electron exactly in the strong external laser field while neglecting (or treating perturbatively) the atomic or molecular binding potential after ionization.  It underlies Keldysh\u2013Faisal\u2013Reiss theory and is widely used to calculate tunneling/multiphoton ionization, above-threshold ionization, and high-harmonic generation."},{"id":"9205e2b9-1b26-46a0-9fd4-d90e7f0592c9","l":"Strongly-coupled plasmas","d":1,"p":"22580d6a-8d20-4653-b2d1-a7de314bc162","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Strongly-coupled plasmas are plasmas in which the typical Coulomb potential energy between neighboring charged particles is comparable to or larger than their thermal kinetic energy (coupling parameter \u0393 \u2273 1), so particles are strongly correlated and the plasma shows non\u2011ideal, liquid\u2011 or solid\u2011like ordering rather than the weakly interacting, Debye\u2011screened gas behavior. Examples include dusty plasmas, ultracold plasmas, warm dense matter, and planetary interior plasmas."},{"id":"10c138e7-fbf9-4875-938c-c1e84ad5e598","l":"Structure & turbulence of boundary layers","d":1,"p":"0c2f74cc-42b0-4b3a-b470-cd50aa7d0f89","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Structure and turbulence of boundary layers refers to the spatial organization of velocity, vorticity and scalar fields in the thin region of fluid flow adjacent to a solid surface, which can be laminar or turbulent and typically exhibits distinct sublayers (viscous sublayer, buffer layer, logarithmic layer) and an outer wake. Turbulent boundary layers are populated by chaotic, multi-scale eddies and coherent structures that produce Reynolds stresses, enhance momentum and heat transfer, and govern the transition and scaling behavior (e.g., the law of the wall)."},{"id":"9ccf11df-5b72-4ce0-92e6-e48f8dda172b","l":"Subcellular structures","d":1,"p":"e7ff71e0-89cf-438a-8e28-d449f5629207","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Subcellular structures are the organized components inside a cell\u2014such as organelles, membrane domains, cytoskeletal filaments, and macromolecular complexes\u2014typically ranging from nanometers to micrometers in size. They carry out specific biochemical, transport, and mechanical functions and have characteristic physical properties (shape, stiffness, dynamics) that determine cellular behavior."},{"id":"c6751f54-a0e7-44a2-aad8-cc8e5a5df651","l":"Supercapacitors","d":1,"p":"80fe725c-ae9b-4701-a9f2-db29c094a8ea","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Supercapacitors are electrochemical energy-storage devices that store charge largely at the electrode\u2013electrolyte interface, either by electrostatic electric-double-layer formation or by fast surface redox (pseudocapacitive) reactions on high\u2011surface\u2011area electrodes. They provide very high power density and long cycle life with rapid charge/discharge, but generally lower energy density than batteries."},{"id":"d258071b-9340-4ff2-9a2d-dc3f21514409","l":"Superconducting fluctuations","d":1,"p":"ca8ddaad-441c-481c-93b3-65f46125fb93","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Superconducting fluctuations are temporal and spatial variations of the superconducting order parameter that occur near the superconducting transition, producing short-lived Cooper-pair correlations even above the critical temperature. These fluctuations give rise to precursor effects such as enhanced (paraconductivity), diamagnetism, and Nernst signals, and can be driven by thermal noise near Tc or by quantum effects in low-dimensional or strongly disordered systems."},{"id":"8ef7f47d-a47b-4652-967c-4176ffb5a96c","l":"Superconducting gap","d":1,"p":"ca8ddaad-441c-481c-93b3-65f46125fb93","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The superconducting gap is the energy gap in the single-particle excitation spectrum that opens at the Fermi level when electrons form Cooper pairs below the superconducting transition temperature; its magnitude \u0394 is the minimum energy needed to break a pair and create quasiparticle excitations. It appears as a suppression of the electronic density of states near the Fermi energy, can be momentum-dependent (having nodes in unconventional superconductors), and in weak-coupling BCS theory satisfies roughly 2\u0394 \u2248 3.52 kB Tc for an isotropic s-wave gap."},{"id":"9dcc3dca-e603-4c7c-a137-fb7d5293409e","l":"Superconducting magnet calculations","d":1,"p":"be790dcd-2f91-4896-8328-00953746f53a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Superconducting magnet calculations are the analytical and numerical methods used to design and predict the electromagnetic, thermal and mechanical behavior of magnets wound with superconductors, yielding field maps, inductance, stored energy and Lorentz forces for a given coil geometry and current. They also include stability and safety analyses\u2014critical\u2011current and temperature margins, quench initiation/propagation, cooling requirements and protection circuitry\u2014to ensure reliable operation within superconducting limits."},{"id":"c402b896-072d-4b06-b27e-c8b5388d7e0f","l":"Superconducting RF","d":1,"p":"5f099028-76e7-4a1c-b549-61c441f6241f","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Superconducting RF is the technology of using superconducting materials for radio-frequency resonant cavities and components so they can support high-gradient electromagnetic fields with extremely low surface resistance and very high quality factors while operated at cryogenic temperatures. It is widely used in particle accelerators and other RF systems to achieve low losses, high duty cycles, and efficient power handling."},{"id":"01e53456-ac5a-47f0-b6f8-8cd9e395c737","l":"Supercritical fluids","d":1,"p":"81194f70-bbca-4041-b57e-29338e588cac","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A supercritical fluid is a single-phase substance achieved when temperature and pressure exceed the fluid's critical point (T_c and P_c), so liquid and gas phases are indistinguishable. It combines gas-like transport (high diffusivity, low viscosity) with liquid-like density and solvating power, and its thermodynamic properties change continuously with pressure and temperature."},{"id":"78017460-1d24-4041-bc27-e547620d05b7","l":"Superfluid density","d":1,"p":"ca8ddaad-441c-481c-93b3-65f46125fb93","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Superfluid density, \u03c1_s, is the portion of a fluid's mass density that participates in dissipationless, irrotational flow and therefore carries the supercurrent \u2014 quantitatively it is the coefficient in j_s = \u03c1_s v_s. It is temperature-dependent (vanishing at the transition) and, in charged systems, determines properties like the London penetration depth, generally differing from the Bose-condensate fraction in interacting systems."},{"id":"9284057b-d22f-4c66-bad2-a840fcc697c9","l":"Superfluids","d":1,"p":"db7ba2bd-700a-4b0b-b444-b873c97ff818","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Superfluids are fluids that, below a critical temperature, flow without viscosity or energy dissipation and exhibit macroscopic quantum phenomena such as persistent currents, the fountain effect, and quantized vortices. They occur when many particles occupy a single coherent quantum state\u2014e.g., Bose\u2013Einstein condensation in helium\u20114 or Cooper pairing in fermionic superfluids like helium\u20113 and ultracold atomic gases."},{"id":"c498259b-a3bb-401e-8760-5dbd86a99a6d","l":"Supergravity","d":1,"p":"124f9214-0176-475c-b706-99df707e8411","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Supergravity is the gauge theory of local supersymmetry: an extension of general relativity in which spacetime symmetry relates bosons and fermions, so the graviton is accompanied by a spin-3/2 superpartner called the gravitino. Different supergravity models (in various dimensions and numbers of supercharges) provide frameworks for unifying gravity with other interactions and appear as low-energy limits of superstring/M-theory."},{"id":"ed553949-45c5-443e-b9c7-31f06f219232","l":"Supernova remnants","d":1,"p":"a2f3cef9-614c-4f7a-8f5b-009885b53abb","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Supernova remnants are the expanding shells of stellar ejecta and swept-up interstellar gas produced when a star explodes as a supernova. Their hot, shocked plasma and magnetic shocks emit across the electromagnetic spectrum and drive chemical enrichment, turbulence, and cosmic-ray acceleration in the interstellar medium."},{"id":"2c20d571-e9ad-4a5b-930c-6e4479344e77","l":"Superpartners","d":1,"p":"3981a374-dbc8-42de-bdba-73ea858650c6","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Superpartners are hypothetical particles predicted by supersymmetry that pair every known fermion with a boson and every boson with a fermion, sharing the same gauge charges and interactions but differing in spin by 1/2. In exact supersymmetry they would have the same masses as their partners, but realistic (broken) SUSY makes them heavier, and none have yet been observed."},{"id":"ce0ea996-386c-41b3-b9f6-b322b9c3b6d2","l":"Super-resolution techniques","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Super-resolution techniques are imaging methods that achieve spatial resolution finer than the classical diffraction (Abbe/Rayleigh) limit. They do this by exploiting nonlinear optical responses, structured or patterned illumination, single\u2011molecule localization, temporal modulation, or computational reconstruction to extract high\u2011frequency spatial information beyond the microscope's point\u2011spread function."},{"id":"8c8ef32c-a457-4777-b4c3-caa510d854f0","l":"Supersolids","d":1,"p":"db7ba2bd-700a-4b0b-b444-b873c97ff818","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"A supersolid is a phase of matter that simultaneously has crystalline (spatially periodic) order and superfluid-like macroscopic phase coherence, so the system shows both a density modulation and dissipationless flow. It therefore breaks translational symmetry while exhibiting off-diagonal long-range order, leading to phenomena such as nonclassical rotational inertia."},{"id":"8999260c-1728-4643-8a2d-0f5b6012664d","l":"Supersymmetric field theories","d":1,"p":"ee82ad04-6c47-46a4-a70d-c9a7d7989f6a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Supersymmetric field theories are quantum field theories invariant under supersymmetry, a symmetry that exchanges bosonic and fermionic degrees of freedom and thus pairs each particle with a superpartner whose spin differs by one-half. This symmetry strongly constrains interactions and quantum corrections, improving ultraviolet behavior and serving as a framework for many models beyond the Standard Model and in string theory."},{"id":"a4ad7b42-3b1a-4ab3-b6ac-b02300ec7c5e","l":"Supersymmetric models","d":1,"p":"cdaf7295-546e-4fba-85c5-9d6435b40253","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Supersymmetric models are extensions of quantum field theory that postulate a symmetry\u2014supersymmetry\u2014relating bosons and fermions, so every known particle has a partner (a \"superpartner\") whose spin differs by 1/2. They were developed to address theoretical problems such as the Higgs mass hierarchy, facilitate gauge coupling unification, and often provide viable dark-matter candidates, though no superpartners have yet been observed."},{"id":"1618738e-4041-49a3-b141-93368eff72c9","l":"Supersymmetry","d":1,"p":"54b389aa-8ccf-49ee-a54e-478ccc2616eb","fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Supersymmetry is a proposed extension of spacetime symmetry that relates bosons and fermions by adding fermionic (anticommuting) generators to the symmetry algebra, so that each particle has a superpartner whose spin differs by 1/2. It is used in particle-physics models to stabilize the Higgs mass (address the hierarchy problem), help unify gauge couplings, and provide dark-matter candidates."},{"id":"ec10d2e2-db4c-40ee-967d-e05b3e4abba2","l":"Supramolecular phase separation","d":1,"p":"bb18132a-8998-4023-a343-725c6803d24c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Supramolecular phase separation is the spontaneous demixing of a mixture into coexisting dense and dilute phases driven by reversible, multivalent noncovalent interactions among molecules (e.g., proteins, polymers or small supramolecular building blocks), producing fluid- or gel-like condensates (membraneless compartments). It is governed by a balance of attractive enthalpic interactions and entropic costs and can be tuned by concentration, valency, temperature, and solvent conditions."},{"id":"2dc9a369-bb59-4ed6-a292-a499e9a6db27","l":"Surface diffusion","d":1,"p":"eef7c13b-c67f-4d33-be97-76cee58e700a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Surface diffusion is the thermally activated migration of adsorbed atoms or molecules (adatoms/adsorbates) across a solid surface, occurring by discrete hops between adsorption sites. It is characterized by an effective diffusion coefficient and typically follows an Arrhenius dependence on the activation energy determined by the surface potential landscape."},{"id":"37f35216-d113-4666-8b59-e87868aaf363","l":"Surface gravity waves","d":1,"p":"0a677b11-f06d-4ced-9385-cf0bf1061a59","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Surface gravity waves are waves that travel along the free surface of a fluid (typically the water\u2013air interface) whose restoring force is gravity acting on the displacement of the fluid, producing oscillations of the surface. Their speed and behavior are dispersive and depend on wavelength and fluid depth (with surface tension becoming important only at very short wavelengths)."},{"id":"42ec6252-ac7f-42bd-9ddc-08ac45a4b4e9","l":"Surface instabilities","d":1,"p":"eef7c13b-c67f-4d33-be97-76cee58e700a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Surface instabilities are phenomena in which small perturbations of a material interface (often between fluids or a fluid and a gas/solid) grow rather than decay because competing forces\u2014gravity, inertia, surface tension, viscosity, or tangential stresses\u2014are imbalanced. They produce characteristic pattern formation and breakup such as waves, droplets, fingering, or interfacial mixing (examples: Rayleigh\u2013Taylor, Rayleigh\u2013Plateau, Kelvin\u2013Helmholtz, Marangoni instabilities)."},{"id":"e78983d4-5cc6-4736-b53c-73baef7e05f0","l":"Surface reconstruction","d":1,"p":"eef7c13b-c67f-4d33-be97-76cee58e700a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Surface reconstruction is the rearrangement of atoms in the top layers of a crystalline solid so that the surface adopts a different atomic arrangement and periodicity than the bulk termination, lowering the surface free energy. This change, driven by broken bonds, strain, temperature, or adsorbates, can strongly modify the surface\u2019s structural, electronic, and chemical properties."},{"id":"d85793ae-45ce-470f-a61f-7dce117f9dd8","l":"Surfaces","d":1,"p":"7a86b9d8-3162-4279-9ca5-90ebad07be7c","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Surfaces are the two-dimensional boundaries or interfaces between different phases or materials (mathematically, 2D manifolds bounding 3D bodies) where material properties differ from the bulk. Because atomic coordination and symmetry change at a surface, it has distinct energetic, electronic, chemical and mechanical behavior (e.g., surface tension, adsorption, surface states) that controls wetting, catalysis, friction and thin-film phenomena."},{"id":"0030808d-6a1a-4ce5-974a-748b5ef3eeba","l":"Surface states","d":1,"p":"9e019803-f9d8-4802-b1ac-d2c035d9508f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Surface states are quantum states or modes (commonly electronic, but also vibrational, etc.) localized at a material's boundary whose wavefunctions decay into the bulk; they arise because the bulk periodic potential is abruptly terminated, producing energies and dispersions distinct from bulk bands. Such states can lie in bulk band gaps or overlap bulk bands and often dominate surface chemical reactivity, optical response, and transport (including topologically protected conducting surface states)."},{"id":"8f585b75-23e9-4388-afe5-364824c412da","l":"Surface tension effects","d":1,"p":"52c158bb-fb1e-4d1b-a117-b6b279c3bc67","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Surface tension effects are the mechanical consequences of the surface free energy (or equivalently a force per unit length) at a fluid interface, arising from unbalanced cohesive forces on molecules at the boundary. They make the interface behave like a stretched elastic sheet and produce phenomena such as capillary rise, meniscus curvature and Laplace pressure, droplet formation, wetting, and Marangoni flows."},{"id":"8150336c-16c0-470c-bf2d-5d7be72e0c48","l":"Surfactant bilayers","d":1,"p":"16158b58-5493-4fb9-901c-54f841cc4524","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Surfactant bilayers are self-assembled two-layer sheets of amphiphilic molecules in water, with hydrophobic tails packed inward and hydrophilic head groups facing the aqueous interfaces. They form nanometer-thick membranes that separate two aqueous regions and exhibit soft-matter elastic properties (bending rigidity, surface tension) important in membrane physics and colloidal/soft-condensed-matter systems."},{"id":"0eab3d97-bf5e-43a1-9cd8-4fa0e3dc3652","l":"Surfactant composites","d":1,"p":"16158b58-5493-4fb9-901c-54f841cc4524","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Surfactant composites are materials in which amphiphilic surfactant molecules are combined with polymers, nanoparticles, or inorganic matrices so that the surfactant\u2019s self\u2011assembly and surface activity create nanostructured, interface\u2011dominated architectures. The surfactant-driven morphology and interfacial chemistry control the composite\u2019s stability, mechanical, transport, and surface properties."},{"id":"8d4cce2e-64a3-4401-8e0e-3195de19b1b1","l":"Surfactant monolayers","d":1,"p":"16158b58-5493-4fb9-901c-54f841cc4524","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Surfactant monolayers are single-molecule-thick films of amphiphilic molecules adsorbed at a liquid\u2013gas or liquid\u2013liquid interface, with hydrophilic headgroups toward the polar phase and hydrophobic tails into the nonpolar phase. They reduce surface tension and exhibit two-dimensional phase behavior and interfacial rheology that strongly influence wetting, capillary phenomena, and transport at interfaces."},{"id":"b4e35d81-1c0e-4b5b-982e-ac42e1ff0c00","l":"Sustainable accelerators","d":1,"p":"d0bb99e3-9d07-40c6-80a0-d8858b63da18","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Sustainable accelerators are accelerator designs and operating practices that aim to minimize environmental impact, resource use, and lifetime carbon footprint while preserving or enhancing scientific performance. This is achieved through energy-efficient technologies (e.g., superconducting RF, energy\u2011recovery linacs), waste-heat recovery, optimized cooling and cryogenics, use of renewable power, and materials recycling and lifecycle planning."},{"id":"b5af5135-b5da-419f-9f2d-a71517a39cf1","l":"Swarming","d":1,"p":"da8795cf-dd0b-446f-bab1-986dcb2703c6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Swarming is the emergent, collective motion of many self-propelled agents (animals, robots, or particles) that coordinate through predominantly local interactions to produce coherent large-scale patterns such as flocks, mills, or streams. It is studied as a non-equilibrium, many-body phenomenon in active matter, often modeled by simple rules (e.g., alignment, attraction/repulsion) that generate complex collective behavior."},{"id":"963f61af-35ba-4d8a-9a68-7133715e5e2b","l":"Swimming","d":1,"p":"21804fac-0546-45ed-98d5-b6af0f0d3228","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Swimming is self-propelled locomotion through a fluid produced by time-dependent body deformations or force actuation that transfer momentum to the fluid to generate net thrust overcoming drag. The hydrodynamic mechanisms depend on the Reynolds number: at high Re inertia, wakes and lift dominate, while at low Re viscous stresses require non\u2011reciprocal strokes (Purcell's scallop theorem)."},{"id":"eac8c4d5-07af-40a1-92d6-f32abca831a1","l":"Symmetries in condensed matter","d":1,"p":"233a6cd0-b7fe-491b-b5fd-85fd6b2a7c79","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Symmetries in condensed matter are invariances of a material's Hamiltonian or many-body state under transformations such as lattice translations and rotations, point-group operations, time-reversal, spin or charge rotations, and particle\u2013hole or gauge changes. These symmetries constrain allowed phases and excitations, enforce conservation laws and degeneracies, and\u2014when broken explicitly or spontaneously\u2014lead to distinct ordered states and characteristic collective modes."},{"id":"a93d3915-2117-4060-9ef7-33c194ab0e7a","l":"Symmetry protected topological states","d":1,"p":"bfe403ee-cd34-466b-b8bf-f90b4d4431fc","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Symmetry-protected topological (SPT) states are gapped quantum phases with no intrinsic bulk topological order but that host robust gapless or degenerate boundary states whose stability relies on one or more global symmetries. These boundary modes can be removed only by breaking the protecting symmetry or closing the bulk gap, and the bulk is short-range entangled and can be adiabatically connected to a trivial phase if the symmetry is not enforced."},{"id":"55539a3d-11b8-4ce7-b556-02c59dcba864","l":"Synchronization transition","d":1,"p":"6e3ddc0a-24fd-4682-bb1b-137944166a4a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"A synchronization transition is a collective dynamical change in a population of interacting oscillators where the system goes from incoherent, independent motion to macroscopic phase or frequency locking as a control parameter (e.g., coupling strength, noise) passes a critical value. It is characterized by the emergence of a nonzero synchronization order parameter and can be continuous, discontinuous, or show hysteresis depending on the interactions and disorder."},{"id":"7e4e985f-d2fb-46cf-9183-c6e4b4c1c61b","l":"Synchrotron radiation & free-electron lasers","d":1,"p":"d0bb99e3-9d07-40c6-80a0-d8858b63da18","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Synchrotron radiation is the broadband, highly collimated and polarized electromagnetic radiation emitted when relativistic charged particles (usually electrons) are accelerated transverse to their velocity by magnetic fields in bending magnets, wigglers, or undulators. Free-electron lasers (FELs) exploit relativistic electron beams passing through periodic magnetic undulators to induce microbunching and stimulated emission of this synchrotron-like radiation, producing intense, tunable, and coherent light from the infrared up to X-ray wavelengths."},{"id":"47ef0a25-3230-46db-828a-7b4613a6cd9a","l":"Synthetic gauge fields","d":1,"p":"46e2a993-8dce-4c28-beab-fdabe4378664","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Synthetic gauge fields are engineered, effective gauge potentials (Abelian or non\u2011Abelian) imposed on neutral particles or quasiparticles\u2014such as cold atoms, photons, or phonons\u2014so they behave as if coupled to real electromagnetic or more general gauge fields. They are created by tailored laser couplings, lattice geometry, or time\u2011periodic driving that produce Berry\u2011phase\u2011dependent forces, enabling simulation of charged\u2011particle dynamics, topological band structures, and gauge\u2011theory phenomena."},{"id":"17e985e4-5274-418e-953c-66b0458bde5c","l":"Target design & fabrication","d":1,"p":"888306e1-6391-43a8-a318-3434270aafd5","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Target design & fabrication is the engineering of the material samples or structures placed in particle, ion, photon, or laser beams to produce specified interactions or signals for an experiment. It encompasses selecting composition, thickness and geometry and implementing mounting, cooling and manufacturing methods (machining, deposition, lithography, microfabrication, additive manufacturing) to meet constraints on purity, mechanical strength, thermal load, radiation damage and vacuum compatibility."},{"id":"e92ed128-fb18-436f-8475-14187623b338","l":"Target normal sheath acceleration","d":1,"p":"4b4294b5-d03f-4662-9886-cea042ce7d8c","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams","Plasma Physics"],"def":"Target normal sheath acceleration (TNSA) is an ion-acceleration mechanism in intense laser\u2013solid interactions where laser-heated \"hot\" electrons stream through and escape the rear surface of a thin target, creating a strong quasi-static electrostatic sheath field normal to the target; this sheath field pulls and accelerates surface ions (commonly protons from adsorbed contaminants) to multi\u2011MeV energies."},{"id":"95315740-215c-4ac3-b411-1ec656d636f9","l":"Targets","d":1,"p":"4c286df9-b565-4295-b951-49a36d60cad1","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Targets are the physical materials or assemblies placed in the path of a beam (particles, photons, or lasers) in an experiment to produce controlled interactions\u2014scattering, reactions, or emissions\u2014whose outcomes are measured. Their composition, thickness, geometry and mounting are chosen to control interaction probability, energy loss, kinematics and background signals."},{"id":"c5aa1c20-1d7e-4ea7-a71b-294edf87a52b","l":"Tau leptons","d":1,"p":"a45e2665-2df6-42e0-99dc-30bd93812c2c","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Tau leptons are the third-generation charged leptons (\u03c4\u2212 and \u03c4+) with spin 1/2, electric charge \u00b1e and a mass \u22481.777 GeV/c^2, substantially heavier than the electron and muon and with a short lifetime (~2.9\u00d710^\u221213 s). They are unstable, decaying via the weak interaction into lighter leptons and hadrons together with a tau neutrino, and are important probes of lepton universality and beyond-Standard-Model physics."},{"id":"5c58f19a-f24c-4029-a15a-c007eef0d130","l":"Taylor-Couette system","d":1,"p":"062f0300-c33d-4782-b24c-43996b0469f3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"The Taylor-Couette system is the flow of a viscous fluid confined between two concentric, independently rotating cylinders and is characterized by the cylinder radii, their angular velocities, and the fluid's viscosity. It is a canonical laboratory setup for studying shear-driven and centrifugal instabilities, showing transitions from laminar circular Couette flow to axisymmetric Taylor vortices, wavy states, and turbulence as rotation rates increase."},{"id":"96ee91b1-3ba0-47ad-b628-6a89155e08e7","l":"Technological networks","d":1,"p":"b4d51608-61b8-4e73-8186-ba02e5a41a85","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Technological networks are networks whose nodes are engineered components (power plants, routers, sensors, vehicles, etc.) and whose edges are physical or logical links that carry energy, information, or material (e.g., power lines, communication links, roads). Physicists model them as graphs to analyze their structure, dynamics, performance, robustness to failures and attacks, and strategies for control and optimization."},{"id":"a98c3246-39c9-4072-944f-4bd5eda11baa","l":"Telescopes","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Telescopes are instruments that collect and concentrate electromagnetic radiation (or other incoming particles) from distant sources to form images or measure spectral and temporal properties, thereby increasing light\u2011gathering power and angular resolution to reveal faint or small-scale features. They accomplish this with optical elements (lenses or mirrors), antennas, or specialized detectors and operate across the electromagnetic spectrum from radio to gamma rays."},{"id":"6e0b1993-5af9-4ebe-8b3d-54134808f5ad","l":"Temperature","d":1,"p":"25bdd3c1-c3ec-4e4e-a84e-6d4152bec3b2","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Temperature (measured in kelvins, K) is the thermodynamic variable that determines the direction of heat flow between bodies and is equalized at thermal equilibrium (zeroth law). In statistical mechanics it is defined by 1/T = \u2202S/\u2202E (at fixed volume and particle number) and, for a classical ideal gas, is proportional to the average translational kinetic energy per particle."},{"id":"e8a8de05-c55c-438e-a793-8116459e4621","l":"Terahertz generation in plasmas","d":1,"p":"a7a00bcb-5b8d-46f1-876d-978833fa4036","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Terahertz generation in plasmas is the production of electromagnetic radiation in the ~0.1\u201310 THz band by converting energy in an ionized gas via collective plasma response and nonlinear laser\u2013plasma interactions. Typical mechanisms include laser-driven transient photocurrents (e.g., two\u2011color pulses), plasma wakefields and oscillations, transition or coherent transition radiation from accelerated electrons, and ponderomotive or nonlinear mixing processes."},{"id":"07ec0e31-6cd2-4c32-97e1-ba06f99a5d87","l":"Terahertz techniques","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Terahertz techniques are experimental and theoretical methods for generating, shaping, detecting, and measuring electromagnetic radiation in the terahertz band (roughly 0.1\u201310 THz), which bridges the microwave and infrared regimes. They encompass time-domain spectroscopy, imaging, and communications approaches using specialized sources (e.g., photoconductive switches, optical rectification, quantum-cascade lasers) and detectors (e.g., electro\u2011optic sampling, bolometers) for material characterization, noninvasive imaging, ultrafast dynamics, and sensing."},{"id":"9334bc7d-f378-4deb-ab64-61c7a83aaf2c","l":"Test-particle methods","d":1,"p":"233a6cd0-b7fe-491b-b5fd-85fd6b2a7c79","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Test-particle methods are computational or theoretical techniques in which individual particles are propagated through a prescribed background field or distribution while their own influence on that background is neglected (or treated perturbatively), enabling study of trajectories, transport, and phase-space evolution without solving the full self-consistent many-body problem. They are commonly used to probe diffusion, heating, resonances, and diagnostic responses in plasmas, astrophysical systems, and accelerator physics."},{"id":"6290591e-f42a-4e01-a29e-64d8645600e1","l":"Tests of fundamental symmetries","d":1,"p":"01f70d47-cbac-4b5a-8ff9-0768e722511b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Experimental and observational probes that verify whether the laws of physics are invariant under symmetry operations\u2014such as parity (P), charge conjugation (C), time reversal (T), Lorentz transformations, and the combined CPT symmetry\u2014by looking for predicted conservation or tiny violations. These tests (for example measurements of electric dipole moments, atomic parity violation, neutral-meson CP violation, and precision CPT/Lorentz comparisons of particles and antiparticles) validate the Standard Model and search for new physics."},{"id":"d7c049d9-95bf-40ab-804d-a50f21faaf04","l":"Theories of collective dynamics & active matter","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Theories of collective dynamics and active matter are theoretical frameworks that describe how many interacting, self-driven units (cells, animals, synthetic swimmers) continuously consume energy at the microscopic scale and produce emergent, large-scale motion, patterns, and phase behavior. They use agent-based models, kinetic equations and continuum hydrodynamics within nonequilibrium statistical mechanics to predict phenomena such as flocking, swarming, clustering and anomalous transport."},{"id":"a24a2d8a-58a4-4cbc-81aa-4cd953e73f4b","l":"Therapeutics","d":1,"p":"b2c47d4b-a2d2-4f91-aa08-6029cd34a4a7","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Therapeutics are interventions used to prevent, treat, or alleviate disease or injury, including drugs, biologics, medical devices, and physical modalities such as radiation, thermal, or mechanical therapies. In a physics context, therapeutics refers to the design and optimization of these interventions through understanding energy\u2013matter interactions, transport processes, and device physics."},{"id":"bd003430-3153-4fb0-bc2c-2cc2ced4ec96","l":"Thermal boundary conductance","d":1,"p":"25bdd3c1-c3ec-4e4e-a84e-6d4152bec3b2","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Thermal boundary conductance is the heat flux across an interface per unit area per unit temperature difference (units W\u00b7m\u207b\u00b2\u00b7K\u207b\u00b9), i.e., the inverse of thermal boundary (Kapitza) resistance. It quantifies how effectively energy carriers (phonons, electrons) transmit across an interface and depends on material properties, bonding, and interface structure."},{"id":"1d1e18e6-170c-403e-8786-d06fe58ddc94","l":"Thermal conductivity","d":1,"p":"25bdd3c1-c3ec-4e4e-a84e-6d4152bec3b2","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Thermal conductivity is a material property that quantifies how effectively heat is conducted by a substance: in Fourier\u2019s law the heat flux density q is q = \u2212k \u2207T, where k is the thermal conductivity. Its SI units are W\u00b7m\u207b\u00b9\u00b7K\u207b\u00b9, and its value depends on temperature and the material\u2019s microscopic carriers of heat (e.g., phonons, electrons)."},{"id":"58297430-53b1-485c-8093-bb0100102ed4","l":"Thermal convection","d":1,"p":"2d276ce6-c803-4ba3-813c-b989f2d4bfde","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Thermal convection is heat transport in a fluid by bulk motion driven when temperature differences produce density variations and buoyancy forces, causing warmer fluid to rise and cooler fluid to sink. It can occur naturally (buoyancy-driven) or be forced by external means (pumps, fans) and typically produces convective currents or cells that greatly enhance heat transfer compared with pure conduction."},{"id":"09c4be7a-362a-4452-b732-6c8755bee303","l":"Thermal energy storage","d":1,"p":"80fe725c-ae9b-4701-a9f2-db29c094a8ea","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Thermal energy storage is the capture and retention of heat for later use by storing energy in a material\u2014typically via sensible heating (temperature change), latent heat (phase change), or reversible thermochemical reactions\u2014so that thermal power can be shifted in time to match supply and demand."},{"id":"dfd0c9e0-2b06-41eb-92d9-03cb607aa5f5","l":"Thermal expansion","d":1,"p":"25bdd3c1-c3ec-4e4e-a84e-6d4152bec3b2","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Thermal expansion is the tendency of a material to change its size (length, area, or volume) when its temperature changes, caused by an increase in the average separation of its atoms or molecules as internal energy rises. For small temperature changes the dimensional change is approximately proportional to the temperature change and is characterized by a linear or volumetric coefficient of thermal expansion."},{"id":"e4482ea2-2cf0-451a-9152-bb25295fda85","l":"Thermal & statistical models","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Thermal and statistical models are theoretical frameworks that use statistical mechanics and thermodynamics to relate microscopic degrees of freedom to macroscopic observables by constructing ensembles and partition functions. They are used to predict equilibrium and nonequilibrium properties, fluctuations, transport, and phase behavior of many-body systems."},{"id":"c2ea95f8-c209-44d7-b8f7-a6baf632c46a","l":"Thermal techniques","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Thermal techniques are experimental and analytical methods used to measure, control, and characterize heat, temperature, and thermal transport or thermodynamic properties of materials and devices. Examples include thermometry and calorimetry, thermal conductivity/diffusivity measurements (3-omega, laser-flash, time-domain thermoreflectance), thermoreflectance and scanning thermal microscopy, and cryogenic/heating stages for temperature control across scales from bulk to nanoscale."},{"id":"c4174927-851c-4123-b194-67c453cda393","l":"Thermionic emission","d":1,"p":"41dd58dd-d35c-46e8-9594-d7e05b458da6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Thermionic emission is the process by which electrons are liberated from the surface of a material\u2014typically a metal\u2014when thermal energy raises their energies enough to overcome the material's work function and escape into vacuum or another medium. The emitted current rises steeply with temperature and, for metals, is described approximately by the Richardson\u2013Dushman law J = A T^2 exp(\u2212\u03c6/kT), where \u03c6 is the work function."},{"id":"40415d25-cc96-4c77-8191-60f7614ee35d","l":"Thermoacoustic effect","d":1,"p":"9e001bda-d32b-4a59-a216-bfd4c1af8c25","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Thermoacoustic effect is the coupling between thermal and acoustic energy in a compressible medium: temperature gradients or heat fluxes can generate pressure oscillations (sound), and conversely acoustic waves can transport heat and produce temperature differences. This coupling arises from the interaction of compression/expansion with heat transfer in the fluid and its boundary layers and is the basis for thermoacoustic engines and refrigerators."},{"id":"d4a28035-660a-4cf1-a45f-ab539830c8dd","l":"Thermocapillarity","d":1,"p":"b72a2d57-f47e-4e87-9a43-f8320d817bae","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Thermocapillarity is the Marangoni-driven flow that arises when temperature gradients create surface-tension variations along a fluid interface; because surface tension typically decreases with temperature, this produces tangential stresses that drive fluid from hot (low\u2011\u03b3) toward cold (high\u2011\u03b3) regions and induce interfacial and bulk motion."},{"id":"609a2adc-099f-463f-9e2a-dd85c0101044","l":"Thermodynamics","d":1,"p":"4455b073-c3a2-4a0b-923e-98d7ec0f3b9d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Thermodynamics is the branch of physics that describes how heat, work, energy, and entropy govern the macroscopic behavior and equilibrium of matter, and how these quantities change in physical processes. It codifies these relationships into state variables and the laws of thermodynamics, with statistical mechanics providing the microscopic underpinning."},{"id":"da7a0c81-bb8c-4503-ab5b-8ac6eb2ff8db","l":"Thermoelasticity","d":1,"p":"25bdd3c1-c3ec-4e4e-a84e-6d4152bec3b2","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Thermoelasticity is the theory of how elastic deformation and temperature fields are coupled in solids: mechanical strains produce temperature changes (thermoelastic heating or cooling) and temperature changes induce stresses and strains (thermal expansion). In its common linear form it couples the elastic equilibrium equations to the heat equation through thermal expansion coefficients and material-specific thermoelastic constants, assuming reversible (nonplastic, nondissipative) behavior."},{"id":"6e14e903-9322-41db-b946-0b518ecafdf7","l":"Thermoelectric effects","d":1,"p":"25bdd3c1-c3ec-4e4e-a84e-6d4152bec3b2","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Thermoelectric effects are phenomena in which heat and charge transport are directly coupled, so a temperature difference can produce an electric voltage or current and conversely an electric current can create heating or cooling. The main manifestations are the Seebeck effect (temperature gradient \u2192 voltage), the Peltier effect (current \u2192 heating/cooling at a junction), and the Thomson effect (distributed heating/cooling in a conductor), exploited for power generation and solid-state refrigeration."},{"id":"1f82b746-c2b2-4f2c-a864-4d9adcd6ef80","l":"Thermoelectric transport","d":1,"p":"36754399-ec2b-4962-bb07-ca12c79834e8","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Thermolectric transport is the coupled flow of electrical charge and heat in a material, driven by temperature gradients and electric fields and mediated by electrons, holes and phonons. It includes the Seebeck, Peltier and Thomson effects and is characterized by transport coefficients (electrical and thermal conductivities and the Seebeck coefficient) and Onsager reciprocity, which together determine a material\u2019s ability to convert heat to electricity (figure of merit ZT)."},{"id":"0b7b4b69-44ec-4f3f-b04c-f8272798b97a","l":"Thermomagnetic effects","d":1,"p":"25bdd3c1-c3ec-4e4e-a84e-6d4152bec3b2","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Thermomagnetic effects are transport phenomena in which temperature gradients, heat currents, or electric currents couple with magnetic fields to produce transverse electric voltages, heat flows, or changes in magnetization. Classic examples are the Nernst effect (a transverse voltage from a temperature gradient in a magnetic field), the Ettingshausen effect (a transverse temperature gradient produced by a current in a magnetic field), and the Righi\u2013Leduc effect (the thermal analog of the Hall effect)."},{"id":"28fa9065-1282-4fdc-a024-0ed06223588b","l":"Thermomechanical effects","d":1,"p":"25bdd3c1-c3ec-4e4e-a84e-6d4152bec3b2","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Thermomechanical effects are phenomena arising from the coupling between thermal and mechanical fields in materials: changes in temperature produce mechanical strains and stresses (e.g., thermal expansion, thermoelastic stress) and conversely mechanical deformation or work produces temperature changes (e.g., frictional heating, thermoelastic heating/cooling)."},{"id":"f7f8d0e5-5d79-44a2-998b-6ed48fe0d6a4","l":"Thermophoresis","d":1,"p":"36754399-ec2b-4962-bb07-ca12c79834e8","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Thermophoresis (thermal migration) is the directed motion of suspended particles in a fluid induced by a temperature gradient, typically causing particles to migrate from hot toward cold regions. It arises from asymmetric momentum and energy transfer between fluid molecules and the particle surface and depends on particle size, surface properties, and the flow/mean-free-path regime."},{"id":"96da2685-a67e-42eb-9210-7d401d99f41a","l":"Thermosetting polymers","d":1,"p":"fea036a1-9c29-4872-ab2c-d052949f7c03","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Thermosetting polymers are polymers that undergo an irreversible curing reaction (by heat, radiation, or chemical crosslinking) to form a permanent three\u2011dimensional network. Once cured they are rigid and dimensionally stable with high thermal and chemical resistance and will char or decompose rather than melt, so they cannot be reshaped."},{"id":"c0c5a682-8287-4e5e-9e62-b2cdf7fc959c","l":"Thermotropic liquid crystals","d":1,"p":"63f7a3cf-31b7-4d5c-bbcc-58d679dd5139","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Thermotropic liquid crystals are materials made of anisotropic (often rod- or disk-shaped) molecules that exhibit one or more intermediate mesophases\u2014such as nematic, smectic, or cholesteric\u2014with long-range orientational and sometimes partial positional order over a temperature range between the crystalline solid and isotropic liquid. Their phase behavior is driven primarily by temperature changes, in contrast to lyotropic liquid crystals whose ordering depends on concentration or solvent."},{"id":"08087671-73b2-4a66-a8ae-079c75cb9b33","l":"Thin films","d":1,"p":"7a86b9d8-3162-4279-9ca5-90ebad07be7c","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Thin films are layers of material whose thickness (typically from a few atomic layers up to a few micrometers) is small enough that surface, interface and confinement effects strongly influence their behavior compared with the bulk. They show phenomena such as optical interference, quantum confinement, altered mechanical/thermal/electrical properties, and are widely used in coatings, optics, sensors and microelectronics."},{"id":"cd96a15b-7a3d-41a0-b914-072e0941bbb8","l":"Thin fluid films","d":1,"p":"f5372742-fa5e-4bda-96ba-87e45d98bae6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Thin fluid films are liquid layers whose thickness is much smaller than their lateral extent, so their behavior is dominated by surface tension, viscous stresses and intermolecular (disjoining) forces rather than bulk inertia. In this lubrication regime their evolution is described by thin-film equations and shows phenomena like capillary-driven spreading, rupture/dewetting, and interfacial instabilities common in coatings, foams, and biological membranes."},{"id":"b5125e12-6b59-49c6-8172-9f3c79372a35","l":"Three-dimensional turbulence","d":1,"p":"062f0300-c33d-4782-b24c-43996b0469f3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Three-dimensional turbulence is the chaotic, multiscale state of a fluid flow in which velocity fluctuates in all three spatial directions and nonlinear interactions (notably vortex stretching) dominate. It is characterized by a forward energy cascade that transfers kinetic energy from large scales to progressively smaller scales until viscous dissipation at the Kolmogorov scale."},{"id":"3de03435-78a7-4920-820d-670b0f08b5bc","l":"Time & frequency standards","d":1,"p":"141061f5-f02c-492c-af56-81c3c49e8b65","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Time and frequency standards are physical systems and measurement techniques that realize the SI second (defined by 9,192,631,770 cycles of the cesium\u2011133 hyperfine transition) and provide highly stable, accurate reference frequencies and time signals. They are implemented with devices such as cesium fountains, hydrogen masers and optical clocks, and underpin synchronized time scales (e.g., UTC) and precision applications in navigation, communications and fundamental metrology."},{"id":"e9607b79-fd31-4535-bbdd-afc2ab9963fb","l":"Time series analysis","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Nonlinear Dynamics"],"def":"Time series analysis is the set of statistical and signal-processing methods used to characterize, model, and predict data recorded sequentially in time. It includes tools such as autocorrelation, spectral (Fourier/wavelet) analysis and stochastic or dynamical models (e.g., ARMA, state-space) to extract trends, periodicities, noise properties, and causal/forecasting information from temporal measurements."},{"id":"53302127-660d-40df-859a-f44839151b21","l":"Timing & synchronization","d":1,"p":"4c286df9-b565-4295-b951-49a36d60cad1","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Timing & synchronization: the set of techniques, hardware and and protocols used to establish, distribute and maintain a common time reference and to coordinate the occurrence and ordering of events or signals across instruments, subsystems or networks. It ensures events occur at prescribed times or phases with the required precision and stability\u2014compensating for clock drift, latency and jitter\u2014and is essential in experiments, control systems, communications and data acquisition."},{"id":"98b70077-cbaa-4810-afdf-05f1146d6e01","l":"Tissue engineering","d":1,"p":"c765ac18-ded5-4ef0-a41a-22be13741559","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Tissue engineering is an interdisciplinary field that designs and builds biological substitutes to restore, maintain, or improve tissue function by combining living cells, biomaterial scaffolds, and biochemical/biophysical cues. It applies principles from biology, materials science, and mechanics (e.g., mass transport, scaffold mechanics, and mechanotransduction) to control cell behavior and guide tissue formation."},{"id":"18b744bf-c79c-4dc9-a0b6-a8939e4b5c3d","l":"Topolectrical circuits","d":1,"p":"bfe403ee-cd34-466b-b8bf-f90b4d4431fc","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Topolectrical circuits are electrical networks whose linear response (admittance or circuit Laplacian) is engineered to map onto the band Hamiltonian of a topological system. By driving and measuring voltages and impedances at specific frequencies they realize and probe topological phenomena such as robust edge or corner modes and associated quantized invariants."},{"id":"daac46e9-4226-443f-b976-9afdf466bdfe","l":"Topological effects in acoustic systems","d":1,"p":"a06c5357-8167-4e9e-8fef-1a0304cf08d2","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Topological effects in acoustic systems occur when the global topology of a phononic crystal or acoustic metamaterial bandstructure gives rise to boundary- or interface-localized sound modes that are robust against disorder and defects. These protected modes\u2014characterized by topological invariants (e.g., Chern numbers, Zak phase, valley or spin-like indices)\u2014enable one\u2011way or backscattering\u2011immune sound propagation and other defect\u2011tolerant acoustic functionalities."},{"id":"326fc0a4-1326-4cca-abec-2109dabaaf6b","l":"Topological field theories","d":1,"p":"ee82ad04-6c47-46a4-a70d-c9a7d7989f6a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"A topological field theory (TFT) is a quantum (or classical) field theory whose observables and correlation functions depend only on the topology of the underlying spacetime manifold, not on its metric, so they are invariant under smooth deformations. Such theories\u2014axiomatized as topological quantum field theories (TQFTs)\u2014compute topological invariants (e.g., knot and manifold invariants) and include examples like Chern\u2013Simons and BF theories with applications in mathematics and condensed-matter physics."},{"id":"c9fb88dd-03ca-453d-b78c-5b9bb956df7f","l":"Topological insulators","d":1,"p":"bfe403ee-cd34-466b-b8bf-f90b4d4431fc","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Topological insulators are materials whose bulk electronic states are gapped (insulating) while their surfaces or edges host gapless, conducting states that are protected by the global topology of the band structure. These boundary states are robust against disorder and perturbations (often when time-reversal symmetry holds), exhibit properties like spin\u2013momentum locking, and are classified by topological invariants (e.g., Z2)."},{"id":"003aba47-6994-48d4-b8f6-ddf0152cd5e4","l":"Topological mechanics","d":1,"p":"bfe403ee-cd34-466b-b8bf-f90b4d4431fc","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Topological mechanics is the study of mechanical systems whose vibrational or deformation spectra are characterized by topological invariants, giving rise to robust boundary or interface modes (e.g., localized zero modes, edge phonons, or one-way waves) that persist despite disorder or defects. It applies ideas from topological band theory to design mechanical metamaterials and lattices with unusual, defect-immune responses such as protected waveguiding, programmable softness, and tailored rigidity."},{"id":"e9bf9554-e639-478f-b833-99c948d8db6d","l":"Topological order","d":1,"p":"bfe403ee-cd34-466b-b8bf-f90b4d4431fc","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Topological order is a type of quantum many-body phase that cannot be described by conventional symmetry breaking or local order parameters, but instead is characterized by global patterns of long-range entanglement. It manifests through features such as ground-state degeneracy tied to the system's spatial topology and exotic quasiparticles (anyons) with fractional or non\u2011Abelian statistics that are robust to local perturbations."},{"id":"58e53b96-a1ff-46ed-8659-fc631cef385d","l":"Topological phase transition","d":1,"p":"bfe403ee-cd34-466b-b8bf-f90b4d4431fc","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A topological phase transition is a change between phases of matter that are distinguished by global topological invariants\u2014properties insensitive to smooth local perturbations\u2014rather than by symmetry breaking. It typically requires a closing of the bulk energy gap (or another singularity) so the invariant can change and is accompanied by the appearance or disappearance of protected edge or surface states."},{"id":"68cf8652-f610-49f1-91be-0b5229c168ba","l":"Topological superconductors","d":1,"p":"bfe403ee-cd34-466b-b8bf-f90b4d4431fc","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Topological superconductors are superconducting states whose Cooper-pair condensate has nontrivial topological order, giving a gapped bulk and symmetry-protected gapless boundary or defect modes, often realized as Majorana zero modes. These boundary states are robust against local perturbations that do not close the bulk gap or break the protecting symmetries."},{"id":"d8307e14-25ac-4f8f-a9a2-828e4f960932","l":"Top quark","d":1,"p":"683890e6-25e0-4d29-9686-308d5800fda7","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Top quark \u2014 the heaviest of the six quark flavors, an up-type (electric charge +2/3 e) third-generation elementary fermion with a mass of about 173 GeV/c^2. It has an extremely short lifetime, decays predominantly to a W boson and a bottom quark, and through its large Yukawa coupling is important for precision tests of the Standard Model."},{"id":"8f33b140-6e59-4b37-b124-bdd0633377d5","l":"Transfer reactions","d":1,"p":"bbb0ce0d-36da-4da5-a443-df064f36aa17","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Transfer reactions are direct nuclear reactions in which one or more nucleons or a small cluster are transferred between the projectile and target, changing the composition of both nuclei (examples: (d,p), (p,d), (\u03b1,3He)). They are widely used to probe single-particle structure and quantum numbers of nuclear states because the transferred particle carries specific angular momentum and occupancy information."},{"id":"b3c9462a-2b5a-4fd3-b4ac-b9eea44d3784","l":"Transition metal dichalcogenides","d":1,"p":"7a86b9d8-3162-4279-9ca5-90ebad07be7c","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Transition metal dichalcogenides (TMDs) are layered van der Waals materials with chemical formula MX2 (M = transition metal, X = chalcogen such as S, Se, Te), in which a plane of metal atoms is sandwiched between two planes of chalcogen atoms. They exhibit a broad range of electronic phases\u2014monolayer TMDs are semiconductors with direct band gaps, strong spin\u2013orbit coupling and valley-dependent physics, while multilayers can be metallic, superconducting, or host charge-density waves."},{"id":"94120a94-675b-43b0-af4a-ad63cd3b5cc6","l":"Transition metal trichalcogenides","d":1,"p":"7a86b9d8-3162-4279-9ca5-90ebad07be7c","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Transition metal trichalcogenides (TMTs) are inorganic compounds with formula MX3 (M = transition metal, X = S, Se, Te) that form van der Waals layered crystals built from chains or trigonal-prismatic metal\u2013chalcogen units, giving them a quasi-one- to two-dimensional structure. Their low-dimensional, anisotropic lattice and strong electron\u2013lattice interactions lead to rich electronic and magnetic behavior, including charge-density waves, magnetism, superconductivity, and topological phases."},{"id":"6e948b10-2e0d-44da-a550-5cb78041392c","l":"Transition temperature","d":1,"p":"f2a9db78-1651-4a94-b66e-3e3d72056760","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Transition temperature: the temperature at which a system undergoes a phase transition, marked by a qualitative change in its macroscopic properties (e.g., melting, magnetic ordering, onset of superconductivity). For continuous (second\u2011order) transitions it is the critical temperature Tc where an order parameter goes to zero and response functions may diverge, while for first\u2011order transitions it denotes the temperature at which a discontinuous change and latent heat occur."},{"id":"e6ada997-f540-4edc-acae-073c259e6e78","l":"Transition to turbulence","d":1,"p":"d2fd5563-5077-41b4-9d06-f0da03883073","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Transition to turbulence is the process by which a fluid flow changes from smooth, laminar motion to chaotic, vortical, broadband fluctuations as a control parameter (typically the Reynolds number) is increased beyond a threshold. It occurs via amplification of disturbances through linear and nonlinear instabilities (e.g., Tollmien\u2013Schlichting or bypass routes), often showing spatial/temporal intermittency and the onset of an energy cascade to smaller scales."},{"id":"0e2146b4-5954-4ac1-8055-77630568f181","l":"Transmutation & power generation with accelerators","d":1,"p":"5d582b16-6b34-4e39-b32c-21bd28c9ea7b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Use of high-energy particle beams to induce nuclear reactions\u2014principally spallation-produced neutrons\u2014to convert long-lived radioactive isotopes into shorter-lived or stable ones (transmutation). The same accelerator-driven neutron source can sustain a subcritical reactor (an accelerator-driven system) to generate electricity, offering potential waste reduction and enhanced safety compared with conventional critical reactors."},{"id":"67c386cf-8c3b-42e1-b49a-c763c1e52698","l":"Transportation","d":1,"p":"62dc4b9b-2c00-4b5c-bf19-74f4d37293fd","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Transportation (physics): The study of how objects, people, goods, or energy are moved through space and media, analyzed using physical principles such as kinematics, dynamics, fluid mechanics, and transport phenomena (momentum, heat, and mass transfer). It encompasses the behavior of moving bodies and fluids and the design and optimization of systems\u2014vehicles, channels, and networks\u2014that convey them."},{"id":"ca680354-e15b-45ba-add1-7f966f2c0ae2","l":"Transportation networks","d":1,"p":"b4d51608-61b8-4e73-8186-ba02e5a41a85","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Transportation networks are spatially embedded graphs whose nodes (e.g., stations, intersections) and links (e.g., roads, rails) carry flows of people, vehicles, goods, or energy. Physicists model their flow dynamics, capacity constraints, congestion, and resilience using network theory, statistical mechanics, and dynamical-systems methods to analyze connectivity, efficiency, and phase transitions."},{"id":"7e57ef0c-07de-4919-8fd8-d27d99a1cd6d","l":"Transport in heavy-ion collisions","d":1,"p":"7321e0f7-3b12-45a2-aa4a-28c2a7e03149","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Transport in heavy-ion collisions refers to the microscopic and macroscopic processes by which energy, momentum, conserved charges, and particle excitations are exchanged, dissipated, and relax toward (or away from) equilibrium in the quark\u2013gluon plasma produced by the collision. It is characterized by transport coefficients (shear and bulk viscosity, diffusion constants, electrical conductivity) and described by kinetic theory, Langevin dynamics and relativistic hydrodynamics, with experimental signatures such as collective flow, thermalization timescales, jet quenching, and heavy\u2011flavor diffusion."},{"id":"675773f0-6ff3-44de-8600-2e9034eea9f0","l":"Transport techniques","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Transport techniques are experimental and theoretical methods used to measure, analyze, and model the movement of particles, charge, heat, and momentum in materials and fluids. They include laboratory measurement protocols (e.g., four\u2011point probe, Hall effect, tracer diffusion, particle imaging velocimetry, time\u2011domain thermoreflectance) and analytical/numerical frameworks (Boltzmann transport, Green\u2013Kubo linear response, Landauer formalism, molecular dynamics) that determine transport coefficients such as conductivity, diffusivity, and viscosity."},{"id":"e880fe46-da59-4d4b-915e-b50409c6ce78","l":"Trapped atoms","d":1,"p":"bd252d88-a608-4897-9416-daf84dee7f6b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Trapped atoms are atoms that are localized and held in a small region of space by externally applied fields or potentials (e.g., magnetic or magnetic-gradient traps, optical dipole traps and lattices, or radio-frequency/electric ion traps). They are often laser-cooled to suppress thermal motion so they can be used for precision spectroscopy, quantum information, and studies of cold-atom many-body physics."},{"id":"a24536c5-e01e-429d-a29a-f43fa8bbf23f","l":"Trapped ions","d":1,"p":"bd252d88-a608-4897-9416-daf84dee7f6b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Trapped ions are charged atoms confined in space by static and/or oscillating electromagnetic fields so their motion can be precisely controlled and isolated from the environment. Implemented in devices such as Paul (radiofrequency) and Penning traps, they enable high-precision spectroscopy, atomic clocks, and quantum information experiments by allowing preparation, manipulation, and measurement of internal and motional states."},{"id":"9794a926-9d59-4c60-a64a-e2a88ff3b421","l":"Traveling waves","d":1,"p":"2375c1d2-a3fc-4d5b-8fde-4f9029004962","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Nonlinear Dynamics"],"def":"A traveling wave is a disturbance that propagates through space or a medium, conveying energy and momentum without net transport of the medium's mass. It is described by a waveform that moves with a definite speed, frequency, wavelength, and phase (e.g., y(x,t) = A cos(kx \u2212 \u03c9t + \u03c6) for a rightward-traveling sinusoidal wave)."},{"id":"4e823153-8e04-4bdb-a7ca-0f608ecd9453","l":"Tribology","d":1,"p":"eef7c13b-c67f-4d33-be97-76cee58e700a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Tribology is the study of friction, wear, and lubrication between surfaces in relative motion, focusing on the mechanisms and material, chemical, and structural factors that govern energy dissipation and surface degradation. It spans scales from atomic contacts to engineering components and informs design of surfaces and lubricants to reduce wear and improve efficiency."},{"id":"cc57ec76-3e4c-4a48-bd64-43233f1d463b","l":"Trions","d":1,"p":"d46f32eb-17b3-4d09-be59-fc3461e542db","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Trions are three-particle charged excitons formed when an exciton (an electron\u2013hole pair) binds an additional charge carrier, producing a negative trion (two electrons + one hole) or a positive trion (two holes + one electron). They act as quasiparticles in semiconductors and low-dimensional materials, with a characteristic binding energy that alters optical spectra and charge-transport properties."},{"id":"5d7958f1-1a37-4155-a3e5-b424f2e5b949","l":"Triplon","d":1,"p":"d46f32eb-17b3-4d09-be59-fc3461e542db","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A triplon is a collective S = 1 quasiparticle excitation in a gapped quantum magnet, produced when a local spin singlet (e.g., a dimer or valence-bond pair) is promoted to a triplet state. It behaves as a dispersing bosonic mode with an energy gap set by the singlet\u2013triplet splitting and can Bose-condense (e.g., under applied magnetic field or pressure) to produce magnetic order."},{"id":"27dabd93-768d-449c-9f89-ea90b7aa38ca","l":"Truncated conformal space approach","d":1,"p":"1e0c099a-9f2a-4e47-b3f1-7faa28b6a506","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"The truncated conformal space approach is a numerical method for studying two-dimensional quantum field theories obtained by perturbing a conformal field theory: one represents the Hamiltonian on a finite spatial circle in the CFT eigenbasis, truncates to states below a chosen energy cutoff, and diagonalizes the resulting finite matrix to approximate the spectrum and observables. Truncation introduces systematic errors that are controlled by increasing the cutoff or using extrapolation/renormalization techniques."},{"id":"b256d081-6757-4d5e-bded-0cdfd1de180c","l":"Tumors","d":1,"p":"e7ff71e0-89cf-438a-8e28-d449f5629207","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Tumors are abnormal masses of tissue arising from uncontrolled proliferation of cells that form a localized growth. They can be benign (noninvasive) or malignant (invasive and capable of metastasis) and often exhibit altered mechanical, transport, and metabolic properties that are of interest in physical studies."},{"id":"b50c74bd-87b6-4235-a5b9-085699353e2a","l":"Tunneling & traversal time","d":1,"p":"01f70d47-cbac-4b5a-8ff9-0768e722511b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Tunneling is the quantum-mechanical process by which a particle or wave penetrates and emerges past a classically forbidden potential barrier because of its wave-like probability amplitude. Traversal (or tunneling) time is the characteristic time associated with how long the particle or wave effectively spends in the barrier or the delay introduced by the barrier; it is not uniquely defined and is described by several operational measures (e.g., Wigner phase/group delay, dwell time, Larmor time), which can give different, sometimes counterintuitive, results without violating causality."},{"id":"5e7749ee-62eb-414c-8265-5d11f7281fb3","l":"Turbulence control","d":1,"p":"062f0300-c33d-4782-b24c-43996b0469f3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Turbulence control is the deliberate manipulation of transitional or turbulent fluid flows to achieve desired outcomes\u2014such as reducing drag and noise, delaying transition, or enhancing mixing\u2014by modifying instabilities and coherent structures using passive elements or active forcing and feedback control. Techniques range from surface shaping and roughness to actuators (jets, plasma, oscillatory forcing) and control algorithms that target specific flow dynamics."},{"id":"5738bede-c7fe-499b-80f7-acbf475bb806","l":"Turbulence modeling","d":1,"p":"062f0300-c33d-4782-b24c-43996b0469f3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Turbulence modeling is the development and application of mathematical and computational models that approximate the effects of chaotic, multi-scale turbulent velocity and scalar fluctuations on the averaged or filtered flow so that practical predictions can be made without resolving every turbulent scale. Common approaches include Reynolds-averaged Navier\u2013Stokes (RANS) closure models, Large-Eddy Simulation (LES) subgrid models, and hybrid or scale-resolving methods."},{"id":"5219bf18-1a3e-43ac-8eee-2f4f7447c470","l":"Turbulence simulations","d":1,"p":"062f0300-c33d-4782-b24c-43996b0469f3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Turbulence simulations are computational methods that solve or approximate the governing fluid equations (typically the Navier\u2013Stokes equations) to reproduce the chaotic, multi\u2011scale velocity and scalar fields and their transport. They span approaches from Direct Numerical Simulation (resolving all turbulent scales) to Large\u2011Eddy and Reynolds\u2011averaged models that explicitly simulate large motions while modeling unresolved small\u2011scale turbulence to predict flow statistics and engineering quantities."},{"id":"eb9d76dd-7037-4876-ae94-bb866f0d41ef","l":"Turbulent boundary layers","d":1,"p":"062f0300-c33d-4782-b24c-43996b0469f3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"A turbulent boundary layer is the thin region of fluid next to a solid surface in a high\u2011Reynolds\u2011number flow where the mean velocity rises from zero at the wall to the free stream while chaotic, three\u2011dimensional turbulent eddies produce greatly enhanced momentum and energy transport. It typically contains a viscous sublayer, buffer layer and logarithmic layer and yields higher skin friction and mixing than a laminar boundary layer."},{"id":"ac14c9a3-5556-4c02-9a9d-f93584ddc15a","l":"Turbulent convection","d":1,"p":"2d276ce6-c803-4ba3-813c-b989f2d4bfde","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Turbulent convection is buoyancy-driven fluid flow arising from unstable temperature or density gradients that becomes chaotic and multi-scale, producing vigorous mixing and greatly enhanced transport of heat, momentum, and scalars. It typically occurs at high Rayleigh and Reynolds numbers and is characterized by coherent plumes, thin thermal/viscous boundary layers, and a broad spectrum of eddy sizes."},{"id":"a49bad99-06c9-48b4-b069-653681722e53","l":"Turbulent multiphase flows","d":1,"p":"c577bd62-ea57-43b7-9607-1ef45dba3afb","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Turbulent multiphase flows are fluid flows containing two or more coexisting phases (e.g., gas, liquid, solid particles, droplets or bubbles) in which at least one phase exhibits chaotic, multi-scale turbulent motion. The turbulence strongly couples to interfaces or dispersed elements, producing complex interphase momentum, heat and mass transfer (and often phase change) across a wide range of spatial and temporal scales."},{"id":"63c13e79-725d-4367-940f-08f36df1593d","l":"Turbulent reacting flows","d":1,"p":"5ad87d23-7dd2-4d60-bc90-68055febea41","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Turbulent reacting flows are fluid flows in which chemical reactions (for example combustion) occur inside a turbulent, eddying velocity field so that turbulent mixing, molecular transport, heat release, and chemical kinetics are strongly coupled. This multiscale interaction determines local reaction rates, flame structure, and pollutant formation in engineering systems such as engines, gas turbines, and industrial burners."},{"id":"3bd34535-a0eb-4c20-9080-8877f534f6b5","l":"Twisted films","d":1,"p":"7a86b9d8-3162-4279-9ca5-90ebad07be7c","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Twisted films are layered thin-film heterostructures in which one layer is rotated by a controlled \"twist angle\" relative to another, creating a moir\u00e9 superlattice that modifies the system's electronic, optical, and mechanical properties. Tuning the twist angle can produce flat bands, strong electronic correlations, topological phases, and altered excitonic/optical responses, as famously observed in twisted bilayer graphene and other van der Waals heterostructures."},{"id":"d555061c-b102-456c-b1ab-8656ac6054a0","l":"Twistronics","d":1,"p":"9e019803-f9d8-4802-b1ac-d2c035d9508f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Twistronics is the study of how the relative twist angle between stacked two-dimensional crystals (most famously graphene) controls their electronic, optical, and mechanical properties by creating moir\u00e9 superlattices. The resulting moir\u00e9 bands can be dramatically reshaped\u2014producing flat bands, enhanced correlations and topology, and emergent phenomena like correlated insulators and superconductivity."},{"id":"8fc60c42-47b7-46f9-85a7-8c09b9e3cdbd","l":"Two-dimensional electron system","d":1,"p":"7a86b9d8-3162-4279-9ca5-90ebad07be7c","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A two-dimensional electron system is an assembly of electrons confined so their motion is effectively restricted to a plane\u2014perpendicular motion is quantized while in-plane motion is free\u2014realized in semiconductor quantum wells, interfaces (2DEGs), or atomically thin materials like graphene. Such systems have electronic properties distinct from 3D conductors (e.g., a constant 2D density of states and phenomena like the quantum Hall effect)."},{"id":"bf453e7b-14ff-46ab-8dbf-2e3a32cccdef","l":"Two-dimensional turbulence","d":1,"p":"062f0300-c33d-4782-b24c-43996b0469f3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Two-dimensional turbulence is turbulent fluid motion effectively confined to two spatial dimensions (as in thin layers, soap films, or strongly rotating/stratified flows), where vortex stretching is suppressed. Its hallmark dynamics are an inverse cascade of kinetic energy to larger scales and a forward cascade of enstrophy (mean-square vorticity) to smaller scales, leading to characteristic spectra (Kraichnan) such as k^\u22125/3 for the inverse cascade and k^\u22123 for the enstrophy cascade."},{"id":"348b1423-f570-4472-8683-65891ace6435","l":"Two-fluid & multi-fluid model","d":1,"p":"233a6cd0-b7fe-491b-b5fd-85fd6b2a7c79","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"A two-fluid or multi-fluid model describes a mixture by treating each constituent as an interpenetrating continuum with its own density, velocity, pressure (and possibly temperature) fields that obey their own continuity and momentum equations, coupled through inter\u2011species exchange terms (collisional drag, momentum and energy transfer, and electromagnetic forces). Such models are used for plasmas (separate ion and electron fluids), superfluids (normal and superfluid components), dusty gases, and other multi-phase or multi-component flows."},{"id":"cb4dcf82-80bb-4305-94f4-12a11959d840","l":"Two-level models","d":1,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Two-level models are quantum systems restricted to two discrete states and described by a 2\u00d72 Hamiltonian, used to represent spin-1/2 particles, atomic two-level transitions, and qubits. Their dynamics include coherent superposition and Rabi oscillations under driving fields and are conveniently visualized on the Bloch sphere."},{"id":"77b93d42-947a-429f-b237-95747ee8bf14","l":"Type-II superconductors","d":1,"p":"2250b5f3-dfa1-4ca7-bedf-fff5d41bd6ea","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Type-II superconductors are materials that exhibit two critical magnetic fields, Hc1 and Hc2, and, for fields between them, enter a mixed (Shubnikov) state in which quantized magnetic flux penetrates as vortices while the material remains superconducting (zero resistance). They tolerate much higher magnetic fields than type-I superconductors and are the basis for most practical superconducting applications."},{"id":"a60c8c1b-964b-4b9d-8c3c-5d9ccfcb6b52","l":"Type-I superconductors","d":1,"p":"2250b5f3-dfa1-4ca7-bedf-fff5d41bd6ea","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Type-I superconductors are materials that exhibit a complete Meissner effect and undergo an abrupt transition from superconducting to normal state at a single critical magnetic field Hc, expelling magnetic flux entirely below Hc and becoming normal above it. They are typically pure elemental superconductors characterized by a Ginzburg\u2013Landau parameter \u03ba < 1/\u221a2 and often form macroscopic normal/superconducting domains (the intermediate state) near Hc."},{"id":"8016865a-76b7-47f2-99b3-e6b43bee40e7","l":"Ultracold chemistry","d":1,"p":"4455b073-c3a2-4a0b-923e-98d7ec0f3b9d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Ultracold chemistry is the study of chemical interactions and reactions at temperatures near absolute zero (typically microkelvin to nanokelvin), where translational motion is quantum mechanical and long de Broglie wavelengths, quantum statistics, and threshold laws dominate reaction dynamics. It uses state-prepared ultracold atoms and molecules in traps and external fields to achieve coherent, state-resolved control of collisions, resonances, and tunneling pathways."},{"id":"f57e61a6-b97a-4ffc-8a42-4f109d1b266a","l":"Ultracold collisions","d":1,"p":"12e1040c-7c2c-478a-b466-c979b57fcaea","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Ultracold collisions are scattering events between atoms, molecules, or ions at temperatures near absolute zero (microkelvin to nanokelvin) where the particles' de Broglie wavelengths exceed the range of their interactions so quantum effects govern the dynamics. In this regime only a few partial waves (often s-wave) contribute and quantities like scattering lengths and Feshbach resonances determine cross sections, reaction rates, and coherence."},{"id":"b5856dca-6e2d-4d5a-819c-c31543640df8","l":"Ultracold gases","d":1,"p":"db7ba2bd-700a-4b0b-b444-b873c97ff818","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Ultracold gases are dilute atomic or molecular ensembles cooled to microkelvin\u2013nanokelvin temperatures so that the thermal de Broglie wavelength is comparable to the interparticle spacing and quantum-statistical effects (e.g., Bose\u2013Einstein condensation or Fermi degeneracy) dominate their behavior. They are created and trapped using laser and evaporative cooling techniques and often studied as tunable, clean realizations of quantum many-body systems."},{"id":"b1d88900-8a47-4260-993c-945e7b002cf1","l":"Ultrafast magnetic effects","d":1,"p":"b35238d0-83c7-4412-b293-c7d42fa92c62","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Ultrafast magnetic effects are changes in a material's magnetic order and spin-related phenomena that occur on femtosecond-to-picosecond timescales following a rapid perturbation (e.g., a femtosecond laser pulse or ultrafast current). They involve the coupled dynamics of electrons, spins, and the lattice\u2014manifesting as ultrafast demagnetization, coherent spin precession, spin-transfer/spin-orbit torques, and light-induced magnetization switching."},{"id":"ce61da82-4786-4ab2-a822-cf232bd0c760","l":"Ultrafast optics","d":1,"p":"23cdcd6b-ae22-4b6d-92cd-24a9a8bd0577","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Ultrafast optics is the branch of optics that produces, manipulates, and measures extremely short light pulses\u2014typically picosecond to attosecond durations\u2014to probe and control physical processes on their natural (ultrafast) timescales. It encompasses technologies and techniques such as mode-locked lasers, pulse compression and shaping, pump\u2013probe time\u2011resolved spectroscopy, and high\u2011harmonic generation."},{"id":"2fd5a7a1-32f9-4efa-b4d9-368e9619d7ed","l":"Ultrafast phenomena","d":1,"p":"ba7ccb8e-d7eb-4e6f-9c1e-b622f5b4e242","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Ultrafast phenomena are physical processes that evolve on extremely short timescales\u2014typically picoseconds (10^-12 s), femtoseconds (10^-15 s) or attoseconds (10^-18 s)\u2014in which electrons, phonons, spins, and chemical bonds undergo non\u2011equilibrium, often coherent dynamics. They are probed and controlled with ultrashort laser pulses and time\u2011resolved techniques (pump\u2013probe, streaking) to resolve transient states and fundamental interactions."},{"id":"2bda776d-04a8-43ee-b1d9-0ed4f743ee07","l":"Ultrasonics","d":1,"p":"40a5bd01-6544-4502-8321-458c33878bf3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Ultrasonics is the study and application of mechanical sound waves with frequencies above the audible range (\u227320 kHz), including their generation, propagation, interaction with matter, and detection in solids, liquids, and gases. Typical ultrasonic work spans kHz\u2013GHz frequencies, where short wavelengths enable high-resolution imaging, precise material characterization, nondestructive testing, and therapeutic medical uses."},{"id":"501b1d4f-3b84-4cbd-935a-dc6cc5eccc78","l":"Ultrasound attenuation","d":1,"p":"9e001bda-d32b-4a59-a216-bfd4c1af8c25","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Ultrasound attenuation is the decrease in amplitude and intensity of an ultrasonic wave as it propagates through a medium, caused by absorption of acoustic energy into heat and by scattering, reflection, and mode conversion at inhomogeneities. It is quantified by an attenuation coefficient (usually frequency-dependent) that gives the loss per unit distance, commonly expressed in dB/cm or Np/m."},{"id":"7d961f4e-e5a1-4405-826f-e054c0e90dff","l":"Unconventional superconductors","d":1,"p":"2250b5f3-dfa1-4ca7-bedf-fff5d41bd6ea","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Unconventional superconductors are materials whose superconducting state cannot be explained by the conventional BCS mechanism of phonon-mediated, isotropic (s-wave) Cooper pairing. Instead they exhibit pairing with non\u2011s\u2011wave order-parameter symmetry (e.g., d\u2011 or p\u2011wave), often driven by electronic correlations or spin fluctuations and frequently showing anisotropic or nodal energy gaps."},{"id":"46bf48b6-815b-4ce5-b3f5-d1c972135b10","l":"Underwater acoustics","d":1,"p":"40a5bd01-6544-4502-8321-458c33878bf3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Underwater acoustics is the study of the generation, propagation, and reception of sound in water, including its interactions with the sea surface, seafloor, and inhomogeneities (absorption, scattering, reflection and refraction). It underlies sonar, underwater communication, marine biology, seafloor mapping, and oceanographic sensing and noise studies."},{"id":"f7596119-caf1-4d2d-9f87-7a727621e77c","l":"Unstable nuclei induced nuclear reactions","d":1,"p":"bbb0ce0d-36da-4da5-a443-df064f36aa17","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Nuclear reactions induced by unstable nuclei are processes in which a radioactive (short\u2011lived) nucleus serves as the projectile striking a target nucleus. These collisions produce distinctive channels (e.g., breakup, transfer, fusion, fragmentation) whose outcomes reflect the exotic structure and decay properties of the projectile and are used to study nuclei far from stability and astrophysical reaction rates."},{"id":"61afd5a3-dff1-41ee-a640-82061a64477a","l":"Up quark","d":1,"p":"683890e6-25e0-4d29-9686-308d5800fda7","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"An up quark is a fundamental spin-1/2 fermion of the first quark generation carrying electric charge +2/3 e and a color charge, which participates in strong, weak and electromagnetic interactions. Together with down quarks it makes up nucleons (proton = uud, neutron = udd)."},{"id":"369bf8a3-d3ed-406b-a16d-99fb6d679081","l":"Vacuum stability","d":1,"p":"ee487445-39e4-4995-9543-fea1735bcbb2","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Vacuum stability is the question of whether a quantum field theory's vacuum corresponds to the true global minimum of its potential energy or only a local (false) minimum that can decay to a lower-energy state. If decay can occur (by quantum tunneling or thermal activation) the vacuum is metastable or unstable, with a lifetime determined by the tunneling/decay rate compared to cosmological timescales."},{"id":"6750d8d2-4f40-4bc7-9047-ebceacfd1d9f","l":"Vacuum technology","d":1,"p":"7853f3c2-c02d-4fc9-aae8-1eb75aaf35b8","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Vacuum technology is the science and engineering of creating, measuring, and maintaining environments at pressures below atmospheric pressure, and of controlling gas composition and gas\u2013surface interactions in those environments. It covers pumps, seals, materials and procedures (leak-tight assembly, outgassing control) plus pressure and residual-gas diagnostics used in applications from thin-film deposition and surface science to particle accelerators and space simulation."},{"id":"bec339e2-f302-4cca-9afd-bf3022974809","l":"Valley degrees of freedom","d":1,"p":"9e019803-f9d8-4802-b1ac-d2c035d9508f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Valley degrees of freedom are the quantum label that distinguishes which inequivalent local extremum (\"valley\") of a crystal's electronic band structure an electron (or hole) occupies in momentum space. Because these valleys are separated in k\u2011space and often related by symmetry, the valley index acts like a pseudospin that can be selectively addressed and manipulated (by strain, electric/magnetic fields, or light) and used as an information carrier in \"valleytronics.\""},{"id":"d596397a-e9a6-4916-8ae6-a98fef9e526f","l":"Valleytronics","d":1,"p":"9e019803-f9d8-4802-b1ac-d2c035d9508f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Valleytronics is the use of the \"valley\" degree of freedom\u2014distinct local extrema (energy minima or maxima) in a material's electronic band structure at different crystal momenta\u2014as an information carrier, analogous to charge or spin. It relies on creating and manipulating valley populations (valley polarization) with symmetry-breaking fields, polarized light, strain, or electric/magnetic control to produce valley-dependent transport and device functionality."},{"id":"bd91321f-4f48-4fcc-ada9-9233a0b95bd6","l":"van der Waals interaction","d":1,"p":"30e65d01-6cd5-442b-889f-ddc0c3c28157","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Van der Waals interactions are weak, noncovalent forces between neutral atoms or molecules that arise from electrostatic attraction of permanent or induced dipoles and from correlated quantum fluctuations of the electron cloud (Keesom, Debye, and London dispersion contributions). The dominant dispersion component is quantum-mechanical and typically decays rapidly with separation (for neutral particles ~1/r^6), and it plays a key role in cohesion, adhesion, and condensed-matter and biological phenomena."},{"id":"3c193fc5-099c-4dff-85bf-964191243cad","l":"Variable & peculiar stars","d":1,"p":"b6d90b06-7836-486e-8e77-221ad2624e1e","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Variable stars are stars whose apparent brightness changes with time due to intrinsic processes (pulsations, eruptions, rotational spots) or extrinsic effects (eclipses, occultations in binaries). Peculiar stars show unusual spectral lines, surface chemical abundances, magnetic fields, rotation or emission properties that set them apart from normal stellar types (e.g., Ap/Am, Be, Wolf\u2013Rayet stars)."},{"id":"348d253c-d4f2-4413-bfe5-d9589499f118","l":"Vascular flow","d":1,"p":"21804fac-0546-45ed-98d5-b6af0f0d3228","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Vascular flow is the motion of blood through the network of arteries, veins, and capillaries driven by pressure gradients generated by the heart and modulated by vessel geometry, branching, and wall elasticity. Its behavior is governed by fluid dynamics (e.g., Navier\u2013Stokes and continuum models) and is characterized by pulsatility, low-to-moderate Reynolds numbers, non-Newtonian blood rheology, and fluid\u2013structure interactions that set shear stresses and transport."},{"id":"025824d5-cdd3-499f-9bc7-6c7fb9f6b09b","l":"Vascular system","d":1,"p":"0a74688b-2eee-4349-a9bd-e93851fe2c5a","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"The vascular system is the network of blood vessels\u2014arteries, arterioles, capillaries, venules, and veins\u2014that distributes blood throughout an organism. It functions as a branching, compliant fluid circuit whose flow is governed by hemodynamic principles such as pressure gradients, resistance, vessel compliance, and the heart's pulsatile pumping."},{"id":"4572b029-0a50-46d4-b166-31d348363df4","l":"Velocimetry","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Velocimetry is the experimental measurement and mapping of velocity (speed and direction) in fluids or moving objects, typically providing spatially and temporally resolved velocity fields. Common methods include particle imaging/tracking, laser Doppler, acoustic, and magnetic-resonance techniques that infer local flow velocities."},{"id":"ff5d90d2-4959-4829-b1ac-b56c5dc765df","l":"Vibrational states","d":1,"p":"30e65d01-6cd5-442b-889f-ddc0c3c28157","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Vibrational states are the quantized energy eigenstates of a system's oscillatory degrees of freedom\u2014e.g., the relative motion of atoms in a molecule or phonon modes in a crystal. Each state corresponds to a normal mode with a discrete energy (labeled by vibrational quantum numbers) and a characteristic wavefunction, with the ground state possessing nonzero zero-point energy."},{"id":"ff02297d-5b93-4c34-bf76-2a789ed5f327","l":"Vibrofluidized granular materials","d":1,"p":"e8836b5d-a348-41e4-9d9e-30e950115672","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Vibrofluidized granular materials are assemblies of macroscopic particles that are driven by external vibration so that sustained collisions and particle rearrangements produce a fluid-like, rapidly flowing state. Their behavior is a nonequilibrium steady state set by the balance between energy input from the vibrations and dissipation in inelastic collisions, yielding phenomena such as granular temperature, convection, segregation, and pattern formation."},{"id":"faef96f6-0066-4ff1-86cd-1fc89ed97320","l":"Virology & self-assembly","d":1,"p":"5be420fe-48f0-4430-a63d-9d611d837d6e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Virology & self-assembly is the study of how viral components (capsid proteins, genomes, and membranes) spontaneously organize into functional virions, driven by physical forces such as electrostatics, hydrophobic interactions, and entropy. It focuses on the thermodynamics, kinetics, mechanics, and multiscale modeling of assembly pathways, stability, and error correction in virus formation."},{"id":"8c304260-0dba-4a63-908f-bab534c0a876","l":"Viscoelasticity","d":1,"p":"1932821b-d848-4b37-8209-74c6a9556c07","fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Viscoelasticity is the property of materials that combine elastic (instantaneously reversible) and viscous (time-dependent, dissipative) responses to deformation, so the stress depends on both the current strain and its history. Such materials exhibit phenomena like creep, stress relaxation, and frequency-dependent moduli, reflecting simultaneous energy storage and dissipation."},{"id":"9f3853c9-0774-4176-941e-a7a9c9b1c9a3","l":"Viscoplasticity","d":1,"p":"1932821b-d848-4b37-8209-74c6a9556c07","fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Viscoplasticity is the material behavior in which irreversible (plastic) deformation depends on the rate of loading: materials have a yield-like threshold for permanent flow and, once exceeded, deform viscously with a stress-dependent strain rate."},{"id":"6a49e85d-b47b-40df-bcd7-8d6e74992c4e","l":"Viscosity","d":1,"p":"1932821b-d848-4b37-8209-74c6a9556c07","fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Viscosity is a measure of a fluid\u2019s resistance to flow or to relative motion between adjacent layers, arising from internal friction that transports momentum. Quantitatively, the dynamic (shear) viscosity \u03b7 relates shear stress \u03c4 to the velocity gradient du/dy via \u03c4 = \u03b7\u2009(du/dy), while the kinematic viscosity \u03bd = \u03b7/\u03c1 describes momentum diffusion per unit density."},{"id":"e4b03cec-c3c6-4950-a2f7-bb1089e50194","l":"Visual system","d":1,"p":"0a74688b-2eee-4349-a9bd-e93851fe2c5a","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"The visual system is the ensemble of biological optics and neural circuitry that captures light, converts it to electrical signals, and processes those signals to produce spatial and temporal representations of the environment. It includes the eye's optical elements and retina (photoreceptors and phototransduction) plus downstream neural circuits that perform image formation, contrast enhancement, motion detection, and higher-level visual processing."},{"id":"239b6ffa-1153-47d6-ae56-8d11442d7999","l":"Volume of fluid method","d":1,"p":"1e0c099a-9f2a-4e47-b3f1-7faa28b6a506","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"The volume-of-fluid (VOF) method is a numerical technique for tracking and capturing the interface between immiscible fluids in computational fluid dynamics by advecting a scalar volume-fraction field that gives the fraction of each computational cell occupied by one fluid. It conserves mass and typically reconstructs the local interface geometry (e.g., piecewise-linear) from the volume fractions to compute advection and interfacial forces."},{"id":"1ca87c3f-9b02-46d5-ba30-88c6ac4a8910","l":"Vortex breakdown","d":1,"p":"9fa410c9-2586-4115-8dca-190b22421a01","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Vortex breakdown is an abrupt reorganization of a concentrated swirling flow in which the axial velocity near the vortex core decelerates to a stagnation point and can reverse, producing a large recirculating bubble or helical distortion and a sudden expansion of the core. It occurs when swirl strength and adverse pressure gradients exceed a threshold, converting a stable columnar vortex into a stalled or unstable structure."},{"id":"805187f4-ce6b-41f3-9306-f56534b732d0","l":"Vortex dynamics","d":1,"p":"9fa410c9-2586-4115-8dca-190b22421a01","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Vortex dynamics is the study of the motion, formation, interaction, and stability of vortices\u2014localized regions of concentrated vorticity (local fluid rotation)\u2014in classical and quantum fluids. It covers models and phenomena from point vortices and vortex filaments with Biot\u2013Savart interactions to vortex shedding, reconnection, and their role in turbulence, governed by the vorticity evolution equations and conservation of circulation."},{"id":"a8263374-1be1-4b82-9ff3-5344e503a183","l":"Vortex interactions","d":1,"p":"9fa410c9-2586-4115-8dca-190b22421a01","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Vortex interactions are the dynamical influences between vortices mediated by their induced velocity and pressure fields, causing mutual advection, deformation, pairing, merging, reconnection, or annihilation. In classical fluids they are described by vorticity transport and the Biot\u2013Savart law, while in superfluids or superconductors they involve quantized circulation and forces such as the Magnus force."},{"id":"027af302-e3b1-4233-98d4-d9a090508dc3","l":"Vortex shedding","d":1,"p":"9fa410c9-2586-4115-8dca-190b22421a01","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Vortex shedding is the periodic formation and release of vortices from the sides of a bluff body placed in a flowing fluid, producing an alternating pattern of swirling regions (a K\u00e1rm\u00e1n vortex street) in the wake and inducing oscillating lift and drag forces on the body. It occurs when separated flow at certain Reynolds numbers becomes unstable and is often characterized by the Strouhal number, which relates shedding frequency to flow speed and body size."},{"id":"b9961030-23e4-40ac-8ef7-76c89ffabeeb","l":"Vortex streets","d":1,"p":"0a677b11-f06d-4ced-9385-cf0bf1061a59","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"A vortex street (commonly a von K\u00e1rm\u00e1n vortex street) is a stable, repeating wake pattern of alternating counter-rotating vortices shed downstream of a bluff body in a flow. It arises from shear-layer instability, produces periodic forces and oscillations on the body, and has a characteristic shedding frequency related to flow speed and object size (Strouhal scaling)."},{"id":"ed63100a-e62b-42e6-9405-93c81012cd30","l":"Vortices in superconductors","d":1,"p":"ca8ddaad-441c-481c-93b3-65f46125fb93","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Vortices in superconductors are quantized tubes of magnetic flux that penetrate Type-II materials in the mixed state, each carrying one flux quantum \u03a60 = h/2e and having a normal (non\u2011superconducting) core of size ~coherence length \u03be surrounded by circulating supercurrents that decay over the penetration depth \u03bb. Their static arrangement (Abrikosov lattice) and dynamics\u2014pinning by defects or motion that produces dissipation\u2014control the material's magnetic and transport behavior."},{"id":"5ae21d4b-32b4-4b16-8601-9ff68dadd496","l":"Vortices in superfluids","d":1,"p":"733b28ec-b147-4e13-b2a5-d8a767d2d6f3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Vortices in superfluids are topological line defects where the complex order parameter vanishes and its phase winds by an integer multiple of 2\u03c0, so the circulation around the core is quantized in units of h/m (Planck's constant over the particle mass). They concentrate the system's angular momentum and enable rotational, persistent flow in an otherwise inviscid fluid, with a nonsuperfluid core of size set by the coherence length."},{"id":"4c984e2a-a745-480a-8a33-f96574b3bf03","l":"Wakes & jets","d":1,"p":"062f0300-c33d-4782-b24c-43996b0469f3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Wakes are regions of disturbed flow downstream of a body or obstacle in a fluid, typically containing separated shear layers, vortices and a momentum deficit relative to the free stream. Jets are concentrated high\u2011velocity streams issuing from an orifice or nozzle into a surrounding fluid, characterized by shear\u2011layer mixing, entrainment of ambient fluid, and downstream spreading."},{"id":"a92733e5-5aca-4175-8295-42bfa5f7f64d","l":"Warm-dense matter","d":1,"p":"22580d6a-8d20-4653-b2d1-a7de314bc162","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Warm-dense matter (WDM) is the regime of matter at near-solid densities and temperatures of order 1\u2013100 eV (10^4\u201310^6 K) where ions are strongly coupled and electrons are partially degenerate and partially ionized, so neither condensed\u2011matter nor ideal\u2011plasma theories alone apply. It occurs in planetary interiors, laser\u2011compressed targets and inertial\u2011confinement fusion experiments and is characterized by strong correlations, transient bound states and continuum lowering that require quantum many\u2011body and plasma treatments."},{"id":"3fefccff-ee2d-49bf-8e41-af4a405aa9ce","l":"Watts-Strogatz network","d":1,"p":"82d0ef36-63e0-4a7c-8dc3-a5292d2d338e","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"A Watts\u2013Strogatz network is a model of a small-world graph constructed by starting from a regular ring lattice of N nodes each connected to k nearest neighbors and randomly rewiring each edge with probability p; for small p the resulting networks combine high local clustering with short average path lengths. It was introduced by Watts and Strogatz (1998) to capture the intermediate structure between ordered lattices and random graphs."},{"id":"fe6ed61a-9874-4209-a345-e422004f8b7f","l":"Wave breaking","d":1,"p":"0a677b11-f06d-4ced-9385-cf0bf1061a59","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Wave breaking is the nonlinear instability in which a propagating wave\u2019s crest steepens, overturns, and collapses, converting organized wave energy into turbulence, mixing, and often spray. For water waves it typically occurs when particle velocity at the crest approaches or exceeds the wave phase speed or when wave steepness/height-to-depth ratios pass a critical threshold, producing spilling, plunging, or surging breakers."},{"id":"59d67d30-fc63-4c84-84d0-2ef50dbec5a3","l":"Wave chaos","d":1,"p":"bb2351de-d2a6-4d4c-a33e-9396bc991c32","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Nonlinear Dynamics"],"def":"Wave chaos is the study of wave systems whose short-wavelength (ray) limit is governed by chaotic classical dynamics, so that interference produces complex spatial patterns and irregular mode spectra. It describes universal signatures\u2014such as level repulsion, random-matrix statistics, and scarred eigenmodes\u2014seen in quantum, optical, acoustic, and microwave resonators with chaotic ray trajectories."},{"id":"1198f0b2-1f1a-416c-94f0-5e80ea9bc191","l":"Waveguides","d":1,"p":"c765ac18-ded5-4ef0-a41a-22be13741559","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Waveguides are physical structures that confine and direct the propagation of waves (commonly electromagnetic or acoustic) by using boundaries or refractive-index contrast so the energy is carried along a preferred path. They support discrete guided modes whose field patterns, cutoff frequencies, and dispersion are set by the guide's geometry and material properties."},{"id":"48733425-bcb4-4465-93db-43fca0958c29","l":"Wave scattering","d":1,"p":"0a677b11-f06d-4ced-9385-cf0bf1061a59","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Wave scattering is the process by which a propagating wave is redirected, redistributed, or otherwise altered in amplitude, phase, or frequency when it encounters inhomogeneities, obstacles, or other waves. It covers elastic and inelastic interactions for electromagnetic, acoustic, and matter waves and is characterized theoretically by quantities like the scattering amplitude, S\u2011matrix, and cross sections."},{"id":"a7011185-c6c2-43a7-ac3a-e05634095239","l":"Wave-structure interactions","d":1,"p":"0a677b11-f06d-4ced-9385-cf0bf1061a59","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Wave-structure interactions are the coupled processes by which incident waves (hydrodynamic, acoustic, elastic, or electromagnetic) are scattered, reflected, transmitted, or absorbed by solid bodies and in turn exert forces and moments that deform, translate, or vibrate those structures. These interactions determine loading, energy transfer, resonance and nonlinear effects (e.g., impact or wave breaking) that control structural response and performance."},{"id":"cedc5f76-c617-4961-a6c4-37cf4db80961","l":"Wave-turbulence interactions","d":1,"p":"062f0300-c33d-4782-b24c-43996b0469f3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Wave-turbulence interactions are nonlinear processes in which coherent wave motions and disordered turbulent fluctuations exchange energy, momentum, and phase information, thereby modifying each other's spectra and dynamics. These interactions\u2014including wave scattering, generation, damping, and turbulence modulation\u2014play key roles in fluids, plasmas, and geophysical flows by altering transport and cascade pathways."},{"id":"15b0e018-46e8-4acf-8e67-9d54fc70c1b1","l":"Wave-wave, wave-particle interactions","d":1,"p":"04f1f8c5-11c4-434d-9561-f9c615ba5813","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Wave-wave interactions are nonlinear processes in which two or more waves exchange energy and momentum, producing new frequencies or changing amplitudes and phases (e.g., three-wave coupling and parametric instabilities). Wave-particle interactions are processes by which waves exchange energy and momentum with particles\u2014often via resonant mechanisms such as Landau or cyclotron resonance\u2014leading to wave damping or growth and particle heating or acceleration."},{"id":"4fc00508-239b-4914-b626-03709fbdb725","l":"Weakly interacting massive particles","d":1,"p":"3981a374-dbc8-42de-bdba-73ea858650c6","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Weakly interacting massive particles (WIMPs) are hypothetical, electrically neutral and stable particles with masses roughly in the GeV\u2013TeV range and interaction strengths comparable to the weak nuclear force, proposed as a leading candidate for cold dark matter. They would have been thermally produced in the early universe and left a relic abundance via freeze-out, and are searched for by direct detection (nuclear recoils), indirect detection (annihilation/decay products), and collider production."},{"id":"a25c6e35-bdeb-4379-ab7e-d321f17d60f1","l":"Weak measurements","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"A weak measurement is a quantum measurement performed with a deliberately very small coupling between system and probe so that each individual readout yields little information and only negligibly disturbs the system. By averaging many such weak readouts\u2014often after selecting runs with a particular final (postselected) outcome\u2014one obtains a \"weak value\" that can reveal information about pre- and postselected states and can lie outside the operator's eigenvalue spectrum."},{"id":"52453e56-cc10-42a1-a17f-aa4e76684d83","l":"Weak turbulence","d":1,"p":"062f0300-c33d-4782-b24c-43996b0469f3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Weak turbulence is the regime in wave-bearing fluids, plasmas, or other nonlinear wave systems where amplitudes are small so nonlinear interactions are weak compared with linear wave dynamics, and energy is transferred slowly via resonant interactions among many weakly nonlinear waves. This separation of timescales permits a perturbative statistical description (wave turbulence theory) and kinetic equations that predict cascade spectra and evolution."},{"id":"5e45434a-0657-44e7-93f6-25bebdb379ee","l":"Weak values & weak measurements","d":1,"p":"01f70d47-cbac-4b5a-8ff9-0768e722511b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Weak measurements are quantum measurements made with an interaction so weak that each trial disturbs the system negligibly and yields only a small amount of information; when combined with pre-selection of an initial state and post-selection on a final state, the average pointer shift reflects the system's weak value. The weak value of an observable A for pre-selected |\u03c8\u27e9 and post-selected |\u03c6\u27e9 is \u27e8\u03c6|A|\u03c8\u27e9/\u27e8\u03c6|\u03c8\u27e9, which can be complex and may lie outside the observable's eigenvalue spectrum."},{"id":"4aae0c88-756b-4d69-92a5-c152b9e412d7","l":"Wet granular materials","d":1,"p":"e8836b5d-a348-41e4-9d9e-30e950115672","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Wet granular materials are assemblies of macroscopic particles containing a small amount of liquid that forms capillary bridges and films between grains, producing cohesion and viscous forces. These capillarity- and wetting-driven interactions strongly alter their mechanical and rheological behavior (e.g., yield stress, angle of repose, clustering, and flow) and depend sensitively on liquid saturation and distribution."},{"id":"131dafdf-93a4-4e0a-bba0-a8b40cb90b6c","l":"Wetting","d":1,"p":"f5372742-fa5e-4bda-96ba-87e45d98bae6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Wetting is the tendency of a liquid to spread on or adhere to a solid surface, set by the balance of intermolecular forces at the solid\u2013liquid\u2013gas contact line and characterized by the contact angle a droplet makes with the surface.  The contact angle \u03b8 is determined by Young\u2019s equation, \u03b3_SG = \u03b3_SL + \u03b3_LG cos\u03b8, so \u03b8\u21920 corresponds to complete wetting (film formation) while 0<\u03b8<180\u00b0 gives partial wetting (finite droplet)."},{"id":"1b9904d7-622a-4f2e-abd8-3432401748b7","l":"Weyl semimetal","d":1,"p":"0026b3bc-1a2f-45df-8025-1243dac91047","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A Weyl semimetal is a three-dimensional crystal whose conduction and valence bands cross at isolated linear points (Weyl nodes) where low-energy quasiparticles behave as massless, chiral Weyl fermions. These nodes act as sources or sinks of Berry curvature (requiring broken inversion or time-reversal symmetry) and give rise to topological signatures such as surface Fermi arcs and anomalous transport from the chiral anomaly."},{"id":"6ceabb22-d8b0-4f3a-8163-65439392c898","l":"Wind energy","d":1,"p":"af9f5a05-6917-40cf-a2ff-5520ff506935","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Wind energy is the conversion of the kinetic energy of moving air\u2014created by atmospheric pressure differences driven mainly by solar heating\u2014into useful mechanical or electrical energy, typically using wind turbines. The extractable power scales with air density, turbine swept area, and the cube of wind speed, and practical conversion is limited by the Betz limit (~59%)."},{"id":"32796252-1a8b-490e-983b-e6605973bf5a","l":"Wireless power transfer","d":1,"p":"41dd58dd-d35c-46e8-9594-d7e05b458da6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Wireless power transfer is the transmission of electrical energy from a source to a load without physical conductive contacts, achieved by coupling through electromagnetic fields. Common mechanisms are near-field coupling (inductive, resonant inductive, or capacitive) for short-range transfer and far-field radiative methods (microwave or laser) for longer-range transfer."},{"id":"44354e99-eeb1-4239-9cd1-af63ae10127e","l":"W & Z bosons","d":1,"p":"556e52f5-6040-4273-b1a4-5e864b903fa5","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"W and Z bosons are the massive gauge bosons that mediate the weak (electroweak) interaction: the charged W+ and W\u2212 carry charged-current processes (changing particle flavor and electric charge, e.g., beta decay), while the neutral Z0 mediates neutral-current scattering. Their large masses (~80\u201391 GeV/c^2), generated by the Higgs mechanism, make the weak force short-ranged and parity-violating."},{"id":"0d7cc395-f949-4313-ae9b-1fb6d9e00a3c","l":"Xerogels","d":1,"p":"06891d57-1044-487c-ae20-21366d161a97","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"A xerogel is a porous solid formed by evaporative (ambient-pressure) drying of a gel, in which capillary forces during solvent removal cause partial collapse and shrinkage of the gel network so the result is a densified, low-to-moderate porosity material. Unlike an aerogel (obtained by supercritical drying that preserves the network), a xerogel typically has smaller pores and higher density due to pore collapse."},{"id":"365fe75e-6ce9-49e4-bfd0-072c655aebcd","l":"X ray astronomy","d":1,"p":"893ecc24-c678-4687-bd3a-0b4a1bcb4cfc","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"X-ray astronomy is the branch of observational astronomy that detects and analyzes X-ray photons (roughly 0.1\u2013100 keV) from celestial sources to probe high-energy processes such as hot plasmas, accretion onto compact objects, supernova remnants, and galaxy clusters. Because Earth's atmosphere absorbs X-rays, observations are made from space using grazing-incidence X-ray telescopes and specialized detectors."},{"id":"3ce4437a-2887-4132-a4e0-849e077ad779","l":"X-ray beams & optics","d":1,"p":"23cdcd6b-ae22-4b6d-92cd-24a9a8bd0577","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"X\u2011ray beams are streams of photons with wavelengths of order 0.01\u201310 nm (energies ~100 eV\u2013100 keV) whose short wavelength and high penetration make them powerful probes for imaging, diffraction and spectroscopy of matter. X\u2011ray optics is the set of components and methods\u2014grazing\u2011incidence mirrors, crystals and Bragg monochromators, multilayers, zone plates and compound refractive elements\u2014that steer, focus, monochromate and polarize X\u2011ray beams while contending with weak refraction and strong absorption."},{"id":"6c49b855-3f2b-40be-a043-d92c4f6bee27","l":"X ray bursts","d":1,"p":"b64db5c0-c54c-4fe6-a142-d089ceb55b3f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"X\u2011ray bursts are sudden, short-lived increases in X\u2011ray brightness from an accreting compact object\u2014most commonly thermonuclear flashes on the surface of a neutron star in a low\u2011mass X\u2011ray binary. They arise when accumulated hydrogen/helium ignites unstably, releasing energy on timescales of seconds to minutes (with recurrence hours to days); a rarer class (\"Type II\") is instead caused by spasmodic accretion."},{"id":"86ce7260-1ecb-4be2-82bb-4a9b14b11cc7","l":"X-ray generation in plasmas","d":1,"p":"a7a00bcb-5b8d-46f1-876d-978833fa4036","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"X-ray generation in plasmas is the production of X\u2011ray photons when charged particles in an ionized gas are accelerated, decelerated or change electronic states. Major mechanisms include thermal bremsstrahlung (free\u2013free), radiative recombination (free\u2013bound) and bound\u2013bound line emission in hot plasmas, while nonthermal processes such as synchrotron radiation and inverse Compton scattering can dominate in relativistic or magnetized plasmas."},{"id":"18d10b28-37f1-434d-a95b-19cf71fc6f05","l":"X-ray techniques","d":1,"p":"705f7ed8-5e20-47e1-8ca4-fa7feea8ad84","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"X-ray techniques are experimental methods that use X-ray photons to probe the structure, composition, electronic states, and dynamics of matter by measuring how X-rays are absorbed, scattered, emitted, or transmitted. Common examples include X-ray diffraction/crystallography, X-ray absorption spectroscopy (XAS), X-ray fluorescence (XRF), scattering and small-angle X-ray scattering (SAXS), tomography/imaging, and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS)."},{"id":"dffa4a12-1571-43ac-b36e-9696658e149c","l":"Zeeman effect","d":1,"p":"ba7ccb8e-d7eb-4e6f-9c1e-b622f5b4e242","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"The Zeeman effect is the splitting of atomic or molecular spectral lines into multiple components when an external magnetic field is applied, produced by the interaction of the field with the magnetic moments associated with electron orbital and spin angular momentum. This interaction lifts the degeneracy of energy levels, with the frequency shifts proportional to the magnetic-field strength and producing characteristic polarization of the split components."},{"id":"ea65698b-ef77-42f2-9477-df2fa8057ccf","l":"Zero-range process","d":1,"p":"7dd874c0-5ab0-4de4-9919-10775f22748a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"A zero-range process is a continuous-time stochastic lattice model of interacting particles in which each particle hops from a site at a rate that depends only on the number of particles at that departure site (not on occupancies of neighboring sites). Because the jump rates are \"zero-range,\" the steady state often factorizes into a product measure and can exhibit phenomena such as condensation of mass onto a single site."},{"id":"19978591-c155-46f3-b130-d40df5bf95ab","l":"Ab initio molecular dynamics","d":2,"p":"7e2374d5-2769-48f1-bd04-1d8f3a57e280","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Ab initio molecular dynamics (AIMD) is a simulation method in which atomic nuclei are propagated classically in time while the interatomic forces are computed on-the-fly from quantum-mechanical electronic-structure calculations (i.e., from first principles rather than empirical potentials). Common AIMD schemes include Born\u2013Oppenheimer and Car\u2013Parrinello approaches, which differ in how the electronic degrees of freedom are treated during the nuclear dynamics."},{"id":"22b7a6fa-d34b-46db-948d-5e2e4fc84c80","l":"Abraham-Minkowski debate","d":2,"p":"3123cbf9-460d-467a-9306-b4fb316daddd","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"The Abraham\u2013Minkowski debate concerns which expression correctly describes the momentum of light in a dielectric: Minkowski's momentum density g_M = D \u00d7 B (giving photon momentum p_M = n\u0127\u03c9/c) or Abraham's g_A = E \u00d7 H / c^2 (giving p_A = \u0127\u03c9/(n c)). The modern understanding is that both are correct in different senses\u2014Minkowski represents the canonical/wave momentum while Abraham gives the kinetic/force-transferring momentum\u2014and total momentum conservation requires including the medium's mechanical contribution."},{"id":"331e1526-4a92-4ab8-b268-47f47ebf4410","l":"Abrasion","d":2,"p":"4e823153-8e04-4bdb-a7ca-0f608ecd9453","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Abrasion is the progressive removal of material from a solid surface by mechanical action during relative motion with another surface, particle, or fluid, occurring via mechanisms like plowing, cutting, adhesion and micro\u2011fracture. It produces surface roughening and mass loss and is commonly classified as two\u2011body abrasion (direct contact) or three\u2011body abrasion (loose abrasive particles)."},{"id":"3929cb76-c8aa-4e9a-b518-618307f8df83","l":"Abstract algebra","d":2,"p":"b5ff1838-0ecd-41a5-a90b-bae4acb69fcf","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Abstract algebra is the branch of mathematics that studies algebraic structures\u2014such as groups, rings, fields, modules and algebras\u2014by specifying operations and the axioms they satisfy. In physics it provides the language for symmetries, conservation laws, particle statistics and the algebraic structure of observables in classical and quantum theories."},{"id":"11289c21-1e4a-4cda-aa88-8928cfe88e95","l":"Acoustic levitation","d":2,"p":"4474a071-3610-4651-a0e2-5a399dd970fb","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Acoustic levitation is the suspension and manipulation of objects using sound waves\u2014typically high\u2011intensity standing (often ultrasonic) waves\u2014where acoustic radiation pressure and gradient forces counteract gravity and hold particles at stable positions."},{"id":"cde55fa7-ad7b-4693-bd97-dd785920c9dc","l":"Acoustic phonons","d":2,"p":"684c842a-625c-4cd3-bf2a-c64bbcbc1c24","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Acoustic phonons are quantized collective vibrations of a crystal lattice in which neighboring atoms move in phase, producing sound-like propagating modes whose frequency tends to zero as the wavelength becomes infinite. At long wavelengths they have approximately linear dispersion and govern a solid\u2019s elastic response and much of its heat and sound transport, distinct from optical phonons where sublattices move out of phase."},{"id":"8a3aa2cc-92d3-464f-9ce1-e1a1e1625137","l":"Acoustic vortices","d":2,"p":"30d2c20a-5fa5-4852-ac74-7545085f50c8","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Acoustic vortices are sound fields whose phase fronts wind helically around a line singularity, producing a central pressure null and a ring-shaped intensity pattern; the phase winding is quantized by an integer topological charge. They carry orbital angular momentum and can transfer torque to particles, enabling acoustic tweezing, manipulation, and communications."},{"id":"6af3ffb5-57e8-4052-9bf5-58a4111536d6","l":"Actinides","d":2,"p":"aa36bec0-0f65-4f5d-bdf2-bc77986b4d0e","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Actinides are the 15 chemical elements with atomic numbers 89\u2013103 (actinium through lawrencium) that occupy the 5f block of the periodic table. They are typically radioactive, have complex 5f electron configurations and multiple oxidation states, and include key nuclei used in nuclear physics and technology (e.g., uranium, plutonium)."},{"id":"062092a5-396d-41e2-9af3-891ddbc4ea6e","l":"Action potential propagation","d":2,"p":"ce1c6c0a-58ee-41a7-a3b2-83d268ffbda6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Action potential propagation is the transmission of a regenerative, transient change in membrane potential along an excitable cell (neuron or muscle) by local ionic currents that depolarize adjacent membrane to threshold and trigger voltage-gated ion channels. Its speed and waveform are determined by ion-channel kinetics and passive cable properties (membrane resistance and capacitance), and in myelinated axons the signal propagates rapidly by saltatory conduction between nodes of Ranvier."},{"id":"7e15b2f2-fbff-4712-b89b-5946e4320e24","l":"Active gels","d":2,"p":"ba8221e8-b186-4db2-abb4-bcb0cc7f5b9d","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Active gels are soft, fluid-permeated polymer networks whose mechanical and flow behavior is driven out of equilibrium by internally generated forces from energy-consuming agents (such as molecular motors or self-propelled particles). These active stresses produce spontaneous flows, nonstandard rheology, and self-organized patterns (e.g., contractile actomyosin networks in cells)."},{"id":"47b7862a-2008-41c9-91a9-2d29828acc1b","l":"Active liquid films","d":2,"p":"ba8221e8-b186-4db2-abb4-bcb0cc7f5b9d","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Active liquid films are thin fluid layers that contain internally driven, energy-consuming constituents (such as motile cells, motor\u2013filament mixtures, or synthetic swimmers) which generate active stresses and flows, keeping the film intrinsically out of equilibrium. Their behavior merges thin-film hydrodynamics with active-matter physics, producing spontaneous flows, pattern formation, modified wetting, and instabilities absent in passive films."},{"id":"f3ad8cfa-a536-49e0-bf56-ff47aeb98e3f","l":"Active nematics","d":2,"p":"ba8221e8-b186-4db2-abb4-bcb0cc7f5b9d","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Active nematics are nonequilibrium liquid-crystalline systems made of elongated, apolar units that align nematically but continuously consume energy to generate internal stresses and flows. This activity drives spontaneous flows, continual creation and annihilation of topological defects (notably self-propelled +1/2 defects), and fluctuation-driven chaotic \"active turbulence\" even at low Reynolds number."},{"id":"631e3eff-745a-4774-a2f2-4c1af47ff02a","l":"Adaptive immunity","d":2,"p":"02260c5f-466c-46d0-a687-826d188b22a4","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Adaptive immunity is the branch of the vertebrate immune system that learns and remembers specific molecular features (antigens) of pathogens and mounts targeted responses by clonally expanding lymphocytes\u2014B cells (which produce antibodies) and T cells (which kill infected cells or help other immune cells). It provides high specificity and immunological memory, giving faster and stronger protection on re\u2011exposure."},{"id":"6ee1dc83-255a-4d34-9298-b3ca889f9673","l":"Adiabatic demagnetization","d":2,"p":"22b17e02-28a0-4b88-ae3b-2520aa22cfb9","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Adiabatic demagnetization is a cryogenic cooling method in which a magnetized material is thermally isolated and the applied magnetic field is reduced; as the magnetic moments gain entropy during demagnetization, the system's temperature falls to conserve total entropy (a magnetocaloric effect). It is commonly implemented with paramagnetic salts or nuclear spins to reach millikelvin temperatures."},{"id":"e9085fb5-4218-423e-bc89-a47523d290d6","l":"Adsorbed biomolecules","d":2,"p":"00960c4d-78bb-469c-8ffb-cda89f4fe20c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Adsorbed biomolecules are biological macromolecules (proteins, nucleic acids, lipids, polysaccharides) that become attached to a surface or interface by physical or chemical interactions rather than entering the bulk phase. Their interfacial binding\u2014driven by electrostatic, van der Waals, hydrogen-bonding, hydrophobic and sometimes covalent forces\u2014can change their conformation and activity and is central to biointerfaces, sensors, and fouling phenomena."},{"id":"020035d3-c88b-49ea-9d53-0df5782a9a28","l":"Adsorption isotherm","d":2,"p":"229eca6d-30f1-4c05-9a78-c352de8326b1","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"An adsorption isotherm is the equilibrium relationship between the amount of a substance adsorbed on a surface (typically expressed per unit mass or area of adsorbent) and the pressure (for gases) or concentration (for liquids) of that substance in the contacting phase at a fixed temperature. It characterizes surface adsorption behavior and is used to extract parameters like adsorption capacity and interaction strength (e.g., via Langmuir or Freundlich models)."},{"id":"5e4d1def-a8c7-4d47-b944-dbcf8659e6dc","l":"Aharonov-Bohm effect","d":2,"p":"ab2c3182-dcb7-4cdd-a237-d806fda33f30","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Aharonov\u2013Bohm effect is a quantum phenomenon in which charged particles are influenced by electromagnetic potentials even when traveling through regions where the electric and magnetic fields are zero, producing measurable phase shifts and interference effects. In the canonical magnetic case, electrons encircling a confined magnetic flux \u03a6 acquire a phase difference \u0394\u03c6 = (q/\u0127)\u03a6 (for charge q), shifting interference fringes despite never passing through the magnetic field."},{"id":"71e86fac-91ea-4cf8-9b6f-fcdb233464e9","l":"Aharonov-Casher effect","d":2,"p":"ab2c3182-dcb7-4cdd-a237-d806fda33f30","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Aharonov\u2013Casher effect is a quantum topological phenomenon in which a neutral particle carrying a magnetic dipole moment acquires a measurable phase shift when it encircles a line of electric charge or otherwise moves through an electric field, despite feeling no classical force. It is the electromagnetic dual of the Aharonov\u2013Bohm effect, demonstrating that electromagnetic potentials can produce observable quantum phases."},{"id":"182f0850-82d5-4b3e-ac5e-46d025770aab","l":"Air lasing","d":2,"p":"dfb4980b-4281-4a8f-830b-8822ca3477ba","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams","Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Air lasing is laser action that occurs directly in ambient atmospheric gases (primarily nitrogen and its ions) when a population inversion is created\u2014typically by intense ultrashort laser pulses or electrical discharges\u2014so that stimulated emission produces coherent light. It often happens without a conventional optical cavity, yielding directional, cavity-free emission at characteristic wavelengths useful for remote sensing."},{"id":"a4f31156-a256-4411-9f5a-34d2b0f69f32","l":"Alfv\u00e9n waves","d":2,"p":"8b3dead2-5a21-4f0e-b8e3-0fe7c260c606","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Alfv\u00e9n waves are transverse magnetohydrodynamic waves in a conducting plasma that propagate along magnetic-field lines with the magnetic tension acting as the restoring force. In ideal MHD they travel (to leading order) nondispersively at the Alfv\u00e9n speed v_A = B/\u221a(\u03bc0\u03c1) (B/\u221a(4\u03c0\u03c1) in cgs), with velocity and magnetic perturbations perpendicular to the background field and propagation direction."},{"id":"e8409747-7d73-4b19-afc3-5178af8a1b7f","l":"Algal fuel","d":2,"p":"3ec5c202-988d-4eb9-bd75-d51f77fbe15a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Algal fuel is a renewable biofuel produced from algal biomass\u2014typically microalgae\u2014by converting their lipids, carbohydrates, or whole biomass into liquid fuels (biodiesel, bio-oil, bioethanol) or biogas via chemical, thermal, or biochemical processes. Algae have high photosynthetic efficiency and rapid growth in ponds or photobioreactors, offering potentially large fuel yields per area with lower land use and reduced net CO2 emissions compared with many terrestrial biofuel crops."},{"id":"ce89e5bd-5c66-45d8-bbc6-d11b42be6766","l":"Altermagnetism","d":2,"p":"b6e4cc90-18f3-4427-841d-c667375bbca0","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Altermagnetism is a recently identified class of magnetic order in which collinear, compensated magnetic moments combined with specific crystal symmetries produce large, momentum-dependent spin splitting of electronic bands, with the spin polarization alternating sign across the Brillouin zone. This yields ferromagnet-like spin-dependent transport (for example intrinsic anomalous Hall and spin currents) despite a vanishing net magnetization."},{"id":"4aabe9ae-12ad-4c2e-a578-fb7239d3b285","l":"Alternative feedstock chemicals","d":2,"p":"2e9e0c49-ce2d-4029-a89d-c5d0535574a2","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Alternative feedstock chemicals are raw materials used as inputs to chemical and materials production that come from nontraditional, lower\u2011carbon or waste sources (e.g., biomass, recycled plastics, captured CO2, or electrolytically produced intermediates) instead of conventional fossil\u2011fuel feedstocks. They enable alternative reaction pathways and process designs aimed at reducing greenhouse\u2011gas emissions, conserving resources, and supporting circular economy strategies."},{"id":"b831af16-b533-40eb-aaf9-bc03e374dff8","l":"Amorphous semiconductors","d":2,"p":"12721ec5-11d3-484a-9ea9-a7bea1986722","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Amorphous semiconductors are noncrystalline solids whose atoms lack long-range periodic order but still show a semiconductor-like energy gap between occupied and empty electronic states. Structural disorder produces localized tail and defect states in the gap, so charge transport is dominated by thermally activated excitation and hopping or mobility-edge-limited conduction rather than the extended-band conduction of crystalline semiconductors."},{"id":"226301c6-088c-4692-bb1f-5773be28ccf4","l":"Andreev point contact spectroscopy","d":2,"p":"b6850136-0dff-41c9-85da-b6bcf1518811","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Andreev point contact spectroscopy is an experimental technique that probes a superconductor by measuring the I\u2013V (or dI/dV) characteristics of a small, ballistic point contact between a normal metal (or ferromagnet) and the superconductor, where subgap transport is dominated by Andreev reflection\u2014the process in which an incoming electron is retroreflected as a hole while a Cooper pair is transferred into the superconductor. Analysis of the bias-dependent conductance features yields spectroscopic information about the superconducting energy gap, order-parameter symmetry, quasiparticle scattering, and spin polarization."},{"id":"bcea0b75-cf56-42ec-956c-13c0e36fd2c5","l":"Anelastic deformation","d":2,"p":"77d491f8-55ad-47a6-a0a7-cd24ffce7602","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Anelastic deformation is a time-dependent, but fully recoverable, strain in a material where stress and strain are not instantaneously proportional because internal relaxation processes (e.g., defect motion, diffusion) cause a delayed elastic response and energy dissipation; it is characterized by creep, stress relaxation, and a frequency-dependent (complex) modulus."},{"id":"c6078832-7861-4f34-9058-a32f0f8abd77","l":"Anharmonic lattice dynamics","d":2,"p":"a06c5357-8167-4e9e-8fef-1a0304cf08d2","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Anharmonic lattice dynamics is the study of vibrational motion in crystalline solids that includes non\u2011quadratic (anharmonic) terms in the interatomic potential, which cause phonons to interact and couple. These anharmonic effects produce temperature-dependent frequency shifts and finite phonon lifetimes, and they underlie phenomena such as thermal expansion, thermal conductivity, and nonlinear lattice responses."},{"id":"278b9d25-e618-4a6f-abe5-bc10e5c41dc2","l":"Annealing","d":2,"p":"c4ed35b0-1090-409c-9667-85f9ac03e9c0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Annealing is a heat-treatment process in which a material is heated to an elevated temperature and then cooled slowly to allow atoms to diffuse and the microstructure to relax. This reduces defects and internal stresses and drives the system toward a lower-energy state, often restoring ductility and changing mechanical or electrical properties (e.g., recrystallization and grain growth in metals)."},{"id":"56dcff00-4e4c-4240-acec-a4c05b5daf4e","l":"Anomalous diffusion","d":2,"p":"96cf4e19-746a-4d9b-b2ef-b76773008bbc","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Anomalous diffusion refers to transport processes in which the mean-squared displacement does not grow linearly in time but instead scales as \u27e8\u0394x^2(t)\u27e9 \u221d t^\u03b1 with \u03b1 \u2260 1 (\u03b1<1 for subdiffusion, \u03b1>1 for superdiffusion). Such behavior arises from heterogeneity, traps, long-range correlations or jump-length distributions with heavy tails and is modeled by non\u2011Brownian stochastic frameworks (e.g., continuous-time random walks, L\u00e9vy flights, or fractional diffusion equations)."},{"id":"8adc9a72-2460-4d3d-ad53-a44092c6a198","l":"Anomalous Hall effect","d":2,"p":"99c1e71a-1639-42a5-8e00-6e258fc0b4f5","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Anomalous Hall effect: the generation of a transverse voltage (Hall resistivity) in a ferromagnetic or magnetized conductor even without an external magnetic field, usually scaling with the sample magnetization. Microscopically it arises from spin\u2013orbit coupling that asymmetrically deflects carriers, via extrinsic scattering mechanisms (skew scattering and side-jump) and an intrinsic contribution from the Berry curvature of the electronic bands."},{"id":"95f961de-1536-4f4a-8036-8bbf790eee2e","l":"Antiferromagnetism","d":2,"p":"b6e4cc90-18f3-4427-841d-c667375bbca0","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Antiferromagnetism is a type of magnetic order in which neighboring atomic magnetic moments (spins) align in a regular antiparallel pattern due to antiferromagnetic exchange interactions, so the opposing sublattice magnetizations cancel and produce little or no net macroscopic magnetization. This ordered state sets in below a characteristic N\u00e9el temperature and has distinct excitations and responses to applied fields compared with ferromagnets."},{"id":"1d6a0c10-dfd6-4747-bcec-fae5b2c960a1","l":"Antiprotons","d":2,"p":"b0ebe8a2-ed86-4c14-bfdd-5860e15b18bc","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"An antiproton (p\u0304) is the proton's antiparticle: it has the same mass and spin as a proton but opposite electric charge (\u2212e) and baryon number (\u22121). It is made of antiquarks (two anti-up and one anti-down), is produced in high-energy processes or accelerators, and annihilates with protons into mesons and photons."},{"id":"a0dcc4fb-34a0-4892-a60a-cfb14c0f73e8","l":"Approximation methods for many-body systems","d":2,"p":"05a47dcc-71c0-4ebc-9d4e-79a40a191efb","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Approximation methods for many-body systems are analytical and numerical techniques that replace the intractable full interacting problem of many particles by a simpler, effective description that captures the essential collective behavior. Examples include mean-field and Hartree\u2013Fock theories, perturbative and diagrammatic expansions, variational and density-functional approaches, renormalization-group schemes, and Monte Carlo sampling, which trade exactness for controlled estimates of correlations and emergent phenomena."},{"id":"c9ff2600-b1bc-49bb-8ae0-d30f9f85d0df","l":"Arc discharge","d":2,"p":"c4ed35b0-1090-409c-9667-85f9ac03e9c0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"An arc discharge is a sustained, low-voltage, high-current electrical discharge in which the gas between electrodes becomes ionized and forms a hot, highly conductive plasma channel that emits bright light and heat. It is maintained by processes such as ionization, thermionic emission and localized cathode spots, and often causes strong heating and electrode erosion."},{"id":"2226691f-74b8-410b-b732-715bb1c8964b","l":"Artificial intelligence","d":2,"p":"233a6cd0-b7fe-491b-b5fd-85fd6b2a7c79","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology","Fluid Dynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Artificial intelligence is the study and implementation of computational algorithms that enable machines to perform tasks that normally require human-like cognition\u2014such as learning, perception, reasoning, and decision-making\u2014typically by extracting patterns from data using methods like machine learning and neural networks. In physics, AI techniques are applied for tasks including data analysis, surrogate modeling, experiment optimization and control, and automated discovery of models and solutions to inverse problems."},{"id":"e3cdbf4d-724b-4f92-ad56-fd2ee003b34d","l":"Asperomagnetism","d":2,"p":"b6e4cc90-18f3-4427-841d-c667375bbca0","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Asperomagnetism is a disordered magnetic state in which a nonzero average magnetization coexists with strong, static, randomly oriented transverse components of the local moments: spins have an overall preferred direction but are locally canted by site disorder or random anisotropy. It therefore shows a reduced net moment and diffuse magnetic scattering, distinct from both a conventional ferromagnet and a spin glass."},{"id":"337785d9-d19f-4212-860f-5730bf71fa5e","l":"Astrochemistry","d":2,"p":"925cfd27-f632-4564-9ef8-ed4b01ff7143","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Astrochemistry is the study of the formation, destruction, and distribution of chemical species (atoms, molecules, ions and dust) in astronomical environments such as the interstellar medium, circumstellar envelopes, protoplanetary disks, comets, and planetary atmospheres. It combines observations, laboratory experiments, and chemical-kinetics and radiative-transfer models to interpret molecular spectra and to trace the physical conditions and evolution of these environments."},{"id":"a17ae4d6-fb4b-487e-a753-8e62d5794f5f","l":"Astrophysical & cosmological simulations","d":2,"p":"ef675df4-aadf-4446-af41-0955eee689fb","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Astrophysical and cosmological simulations are computational models that numerically solve the governing physics (gravity, hydrodynamics, radiation, chemistry, and sometimes relativity) to follow the formation and evolution of structures from stars and galaxies up to the large-scale universe. They combine methods such as N\u2011body dynamics, grid or particle hydrodynamics, and radiative transfer with subgrid prescriptions for unresolved processes to predict observables and test theoretical models."},{"id":"1ac3d4b9-5aed-4e19-b076-832a26ba778a","l":"Astrophysical studies of gravity","d":2,"p":"ef090024-1dd2-47eb-8058-f7e000a440d0","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Astrophysical studies of gravity examine how gravitational interactions determine the dynamics, structure, and evolution of astronomical systems from planets and stars to galaxies and the Universe. They use observations\u2014orbital motions, gravitational lensing, compact-object phenomena, and gravitational waves\u2014to measure masses and spacetime properties, test general relativity and alternative theories, and probe dark matter and dark energy."},{"id":"314cc56e-57b2-4cde-a602-3aae9949766c","l":"Asymmetric nuclear matter","d":2,"p":"63a9c708-1efc-49bb-9b67-fb6a39269235","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Asymmetric nuclear matter is an idealized infinite, uniform system of interacting nucleons with unequal neutron and proton densities, quantified by the isospin asymmetry \u03b4 = (\u03c1n \u2212 \u03c1p)/\u03c1. It is used to study the isospin dependence of the nuclear equation of state and the symmetry energy relevant for neutron-rich nuclei and neutron stars."},{"id":"24d2edc1-911e-428d-b58d-d7a815949b4c","l":"Asymmetric simple exclusion process","d":2,"p":"695da376-c44a-41dd-b968-d200c31e9d5e","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems","Plasma Physics"],"def":"The asymmetric simple exclusion process (ASEP) is a prototypical stochastic lattice model in which particles hop randomly to neighboring sites with a preferred direction while obeying hard-core exclusion (at most one particle per site). Despite its simple rules it generates nonequilibrium steady states, boundary-driven phase transitions, and connections to the KPZ universality class, making it central to studies of driven transport and nonequilibrium statistical mechanics."},{"id":"8b1a7c4d-dc38-4fa4-9a44-597480f3e9d6","l":"Atactic polymers","d":2,"p":"20a16e31-69dd-4e1b-9a44-2df3b053d9fd","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Atactic polymers are polymers whose stereogenic centers (or pendant-group orientations) are arranged randomly along the backbone, so there is no regular stereochemical order (tacticity). This irregularity prevents efficient chain packing, making atactic polymers typically amorphous, noncrystalline, and different in thermal/mechanical properties from isotactic or syndiotactic counterparts."},{"id":"3c9c01fe-fe82-4861-a59d-3da21432adb7","l":"Atom diffraction","d":2,"p":"1d433fa8-4cdd-4e66-ae51-df1797f18d56","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Atom diffraction is the diffraction of atomic matter waves\u2014because atoms have a de Broglie wavelength\u2014when they encounter apertures, gratings, crystal lattices, or periodic potentials, producing interference patterns analogous to optical diffraction. It demonstrates the wave nature of atoms and is used to probe surfaces, measure coherence, and test quantum dynamics."},{"id":"f2622e6a-5f55-4f27-804c-aed3aebdbe02","l":"Atomic force microscopy","d":2,"p":"a0889633-1e24-48ce-80d9-98b0139e10cb","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Atomic force microscopy (AFM) is a scanning probe technique that maps a surface at nanometer-to-atomic resolution by rastering a sharp tip mounted on a flexible cantilever over the sample and measuring tip\u2013sample interaction forces via cantilever deflection. A feedback loop maintains a set interaction (contact, tapping, or noncontact), allowing reconstruction of topography and measurement of local mechanical, electrical, and magnetic properties."},{"id":"1f7fbe75-9eaa-44b4-a717-2a6d23a0e093","l":"Atom & ion cooling","d":2,"p":"0b6b113c-8f3d-421e-a149-940af26931cf","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Atom and ion cooling refers to techniques that reduce the translational (and sometimes internal) energy of neutral atoms or charged ions to very low temperatures by controlled interactions such as photon scattering, selective removal of hot particles, or thermalizing collisions. Common methods include Doppler and sideband laser cooling, evaporative cooling, and sympathetic cooling, enabling trapping, precision measurement, and quantum-state control at microkelvin to nanokelvin scales."},{"id":"5a33e436-16a9-42a6-9f48-cac57ee1f645","l":"Atom & ion trapping & guiding","d":2,"p":"0b6b113c-8f3d-421e-a149-940af26931cf","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Atom & ion trapping & guiding are techniques that use static and time-varying electric and magnetic fields, and/or resonant and far-off-resonant laser light, to confine, steer, and control the motion of neutral atoms or charged ions. Common implementations include Paul and Penning traps for ions and magneto-optical, magnetic, and optical-dipole traps and waveguides for atoms, typically combined with laser cooling for precision measurements and quantum information applications."},{"id":"e57f27ff-c67a-4974-bcc3-24cac3036ec6","l":"Atoms, ions, & molecules in cavities","d":2,"p":"3123cbf9-460d-467a-9306-b4fb316daddd","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"The study of atoms, ions, and molecules placed inside optical or microwave resonators, where the confinement of electromagnetic modes strongly modifies their radiative properties, level structure, and dynamics. Such cavity QED effects produce phenomena like the Purcell-enhanced emission, vacuum Rabi splitting, and cavity-mediated coherent or collective interactions useful for quantum control and sensing."},{"id":"bf7626bc-fac0-45d3-ac13-aa181bacba5b","l":"Auger electron spectroscopy","d":2,"p":"b6850136-0dff-41c9-85da-b6bcf1518811","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Auger electron spectroscopy (AES) is a surface-sensitive analytical technique that detects the kinetic energies of electrons emitted when an atom with a core-level vacancy relaxes by transferring energy to a second electron (the Auger process) after excitation by an electron beam or X-ray. The characteristic Auger peak energies and intensities reveal elemental composition and chemical/oxidation-state information from the top few atomic layers of a sample."},{"id":"e437f686-6729-4719-9785-38ba8e2f1618","l":"Auroral phenomena in magnetosphere","d":2,"p":"993587b9-4472-41fd-9896-2df938e75b92","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Auroral phenomena in the magnetosphere are luminous displays in the high\u2011latitude upper atmosphere produced when magnetospheric and solar\u2011wind charged particles are accelerated along geomagnetic field lines and collide with atmospheric atoms and molecules, exciting them and producing light. Their morphology and variability (arcs, diffuse and pulsating aurora, substorms) reflect magnetospheric dynamics such as reconnection, field\u2011aligned (Birkeland) currents and wave\u2013particle interactions."},{"id":"00d83659-a918-4cca-bb38-e0fab7b44213","l":"Auroras","d":2,"p":"d4ce293a-8c78-4f5c-a4ec-9fd0f295d3ee","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Auroras are luminous displays in the high-latitude upper atmosphere (aurora borealis/australis) produced when energetic charged particles from the solar wind, guided by Earth's magnetic field into the polar magnetosphere, collide with and excite atmospheric atoms and molecules, which then emit characteristic photons. Variations in particle energy, species (electrons, protons), and magnetospheric currents determine the aurora's colors, shapes, and dynamics."},{"id":"d4a122a5-211c-4cb1-a7c5-eb7f979ab1b7","l":"Autocorrelation","d":2,"p":"bd03ea46-2406-4a24-8307-05b2b498fb4e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Autocorrelation quantifies how similar a signal or stochastic process is to a time-shifted copy of itself as a function of the time lag. For a (wide-sense) stationary process X(t) it is R(\u03c4)=\u27e8X(t)X(t+\u03c4)\u27e9, whose Fourier transform yields the power spectral density (Wiener\u2013Khinchin theorem)."},{"id":"bf5e915a-2eef-4e0a-a4dc-893de5d08918","l":"Bacterial communities","d":2,"p":"02260c5f-466c-46d0-a687-826d188b22a4","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Bacterial communities are spatially structured assemblages of bacterial cells (one or more species) that live in close proximity and interact through physical contact, chemical signaling, nutrient exchange, and horizontal gene transfer. These interactions create dynamic, emergent behaviors\u2014such as biofilm formation, pattern formation, collective motility, and altered transport or mechanical properties\u2014that are studied in physics as examples of active, interacting many-body systems."},{"id":"16575cae-b51f-473c-975c-dcbf05d2de80","l":"Band gap","d":2,"p":"aaff2519-9891-4d4e-a067-1ea36b6b08d4","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The band gap is the energy range in a solid where no electronic states exist, usually the energy difference between the highest occupied valence band and the lowest empty conduction band. Its magnitude determines whether a material is a conductor, semiconductor, or insulator and sets the minimum photon energy required to excite an electron across the gap."},{"id":"30a20e86-5b71-4324-9e42-bdf37f8ef704","l":"Barabasi-Albert model","d":2,"p":"6f3f5cf1-363e-4d04-ac63-ca4ae90b47fc","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"The Barab\u00e1si\u2013Albert (BA) model is a generative network model in which nodes are added one-by-one and attach to existing nodes with probability proportional to their degree (preferential attachment), producing scale-free networks with a power-law degree distribution (P(k) \u221d k^\u22123). It captures the \"rich-get-richer\" mechanism that explains heavy-tailed connectivity in many real-world networks."},{"id":"831a9088-c788-4f91-983d-bc6f18985b03","l":"Baryon & lepton number symmetries","d":2,"p":"fadc87f6-ee6b-49d6-9128-da9737e4d165","fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Baryon and lepton number symmetries are global U(1) quantum-number conservations that assign baryon number B=+1 to baryons (\u22121 to antibaryons) and lepton number L=+1 to leptons (\u22121 to antileptons), so that in many reactions the total B and L are unchanged. In the Standard Model these are classical symmetries but are violated by nonperturbative electroweak (sphaleron) processes\u2014which conserve B\u2212L\u2014and can also be broken by beyond\u2011Standard\u2011Model effects such as proton decay or Majorana neutrino masses."},{"id":"7e6b9882-5ee7-49c9-abdf-a4b202524e08","l":"Bayesian methods","d":2,"p":"db0eac92-d73c-4c77-ad1b-6c2517418354","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Bayesian methods are a probabilistic framework that use Bayes' theorem to update prior beliefs with new data by combining a prior distribution and the data likelihood to produce a posterior distribution, thereby providing principled parameter estimation, model comparison, and uncertainty quantification."},{"id":"a2fa0cd1-7d5f-4a5f-8c5e-7581ad0a7d93","l":"BCS theory","d":2,"p":"7299559c-15c4-47f3-b866-5633b1030453","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"BCS theory (Bardeen\u2013Cooper\u2013Schrieffer) is the microscopic theory of conventional superconductivity in which an effective attractive interaction (typically phonon-mediated) causes electrons near the Fermi surface to form Cooper pairs that condense into a coherent many-body ground state. This paired condensate has an energy gap for single-particle excitations and long-range phase coherence, explaining zero resistance and the Meissner effect."},{"id":"c124a697-13c6-4edb-9987-d706a96d9e6b","l":"Beam cooling","d":2,"p":"3934db16-fe69-4d69-82c8-9bc346490273","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Beam cooling is the reduction of a particle beam's phase-space volume (emittance)\u2014a decrease in its momentum spread and/or transverse size and divergence\u2014to increase brightness or luminosity. It is achieved by dissipative or feedback processes (e.g., radiation damping, electron cooling, stochastic cooling, ionization cooling) that remove random motion without proportionally losing particles."},{"id":"d1f090ce-4f37-4169-bd3f-40c3dd29a60a","l":"Beam halos","d":2,"p":"3934db16-fe69-4d69-82c8-9bc346490273","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Beam halos are the low-density population of particles that lie outside the bright core of a charged-particle or laser beam, forming a diffuse peripheral envelope. They arise from effects such as scattering, nonlinear focusing, space-charge forces, mismatch, and collective instabilities, and are important because they lead to beam loss, radiation, and damage to accelerator components."},{"id":"22e84288-8154-40ed-b53b-bbf136c947b6","l":"Beam optics correction schemes","d":2,"p":"a9c20b4d-69a1-40f1-81c1-86b01859b80c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Beam optics correction schemes are sets of measurement-and-actuation procedures used in particle accelerators to detect and restore the designed transverse focusing and phase-space properties of a beam (beta functions, dispersion, coupling, chromaticity and closed orbit) when they are perturbed by magnet errors or misalignments. They combine beam-based measurements (orbit/response matrices, turn-by-turn or phase-advance data, dispersion scans) with model-based algorithms to compute and apply adjustments to quadrupoles, skew quadrupoles, sextupoles and steering elements, often in automated feedback loops."},{"id":"1130bd3e-e2f4-450f-b54b-2bd7de51100e","l":"Beam optics transport","d":2,"p":"a9c20b4d-69a1-40f1-81c1-86b01859b80c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Beam optics transport is the theory and practice of guiding and manipulating the spatial and angular distribution (the phase-space) of charged-particle beams as they propagate through accelerator elements (drifts, lenses, dipoles, RF cavities). It uses optical analogues and transfer maps or matrices to predict beam envelopes, focusing and dispersion, and to design beamlines that control emittance and match beam conditions between sections."},{"id":"68d9d702-23e8-45a3-ba20-a5c2aedc587f","l":"Beam polarization","d":2,"p":"f8f21b09-696c-40cb-a4d6-b7f8505fba32","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Beam polarization is the preferred orientation of a vector property carried by a beam \u2014 for light, the direction and phase relation of the electric field; for particle beams, the spin orientation \u2014 describing how strongly that property is aligned across the ensemble. It is quantified by a degree of polarization (from 0 to 1) and by the polarization state (e.g., linear, circular, elliptical for light; longitudinal or transverse for spins)."},{"id":"c0286d12-d981-4273-a80e-a74fba72b26c","l":"Beam resonances","d":2,"p":"bb27c0c8-04af-471e-8122-24961354a177","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Beam resonances are conditions where a beam (mechanical or charged-particle) exhibits a strongly amplified response because an applied or intrinsic periodic perturbation matches one of its natural oscillation frequencies or mode combinations, producing large coherent oscillations. In accelerator physics, examples include betatron and synchrotron resonances, which occur when integer combinations of transverse and longitudinal tunes and lattice periodicities satisfy resonance relations, leading to emittance growth and possible beam loss."},{"id":"544c9851-59dc-42c6-9e4a-388fc061ffa1","l":"Beam tracking","d":2,"p":"bb27c0c8-04af-471e-8122-24961354a177","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Beam tracking is the computation or measurement of the trajectories and phase-space evolution of particles in a charged-particle or optical beam as it propagates through an accelerator, transport line, or optical system. It encompasses single-particle and multi-particle (macro-particle) simulations or real-time diagnostics used to predict and monitor beam position, momentum, emittance, and collective effects."},{"id":"7e629cc7-28e3-49e4-8b37-43efb9a40f1e","l":"Bechgaard salts","d":2,"p":"7b1d5a31-bb4e-4b73-ae62-1c68e9c6006e","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Bechgaard salts are a family of quasi-one-dimensional organic charge-transfer crystals with general formula (TMTSF)2X (TMTSF = tetramethyltetraselenafulvalene, X = inorganic anion) whose conduction is highly anisotropic. They are famous for a rich low-temperature phase diagram\u2014including spin-density-wave states, pressure-induced (often unconventional) superconductivity and field-induced phases\u2014arising from strong electron correlations and Fermi-surface nesting."},{"id":"998363e1-a4e5-4cc9-9c5f-bdf46e9b5f17","l":"BEC tunneling","d":2,"p":"4254adf6-872d-4868-9931-a2a3e84b566e","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Quantum Information, Science & Technology","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"BEC tunneling is the quantum-mechanical passage of a Bose\u2013Einstein condensate's coherent matter wave through a classically forbidden potential barrier or between separated wells. Because the condensate is a macroscopic, phase-coherent quantum state, the process shows collective phenomena (e.g., Josephson oscillations and macroscopic quantum self-trapping) and is strongly affected by atom\u2013atom interactions and nonlinear mean-field dynamics."},{"id":"477aba68-21e5-42ea-9f6d-693b0a9fc1d9","l":"Bending","d":2,"p":"77d491f8-55ad-47a6-a0a7-cd24ffce7602","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Bending is the deformation of a slender structural element (like a beam or plate) caused by transverse loads or applied moments that make it curve, so material fibers on one side are stretched (tension) and on the other compressed. The resulting internal bending moment and shear stresses resist the curvature and are described for small deflections by beam theories such as Euler\u2013Bernoulli and Timoshenko."},{"id":"143e44a2-352f-4dc6-9aae-b90f7d3d878f","l":"Bend & torsion tests","d":2,"p":"b777b985-75b5-420a-9f13-21bb83461999","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Bend and torsion tests are mechanical experiments that apply bending (flexural) or twisting (torsional) loads to a specimen to measure its mechanical response\u2014e.g., flexural strength and modulus, shear (torsional) modulus, ductility, fracture and fatigue behavior. Typical implementations include three- or four-point bending rigs and torsion fixtures that record force/torque and deformation to extract stress\u2013strain relations."},{"id":"5620754f-a506-4da1-83a9-86e213b10ddb","l":"Bernstein plasma waves","d":2,"p":"4f513e0c-b639-4505-9116-7a0613323cf4","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Bernstein plasma waves are electrostatic waves in a magnetized plasma that propagate primarily across the magnetic field and occur at frequencies near integer harmonics of the cyclotron frequency; they arise from finite Larmor\u2011radius (kinetic) effects and do not appear in fluid models. They can exist in frequency bands inaccessible to ordinary electromagnetic waves and are important for cyclotron resonance heating and plasma diagnostics."},{"id":"31fe39dc-47a9-4b33-9e8e-e0e426b947a0","l":"Biexcitons","d":2,"p":"29e56ec8-6039-4a4b-9832-1f48fa943ea1","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A biexciton is a four-particle quasiparticle formed when two excitons (each an electron bound to a hole by Coulomb attraction) bind together, effectively acting like a molecule of excitons. Its characteristic biexciton binding energy is the energy needed to split it into two separate excitons, and it plays a key role in the nonlinear optical and photoluminescence properties of semiconductors and low-dimensional materials."},{"id":"e03ea2aa-7eb3-470a-9d7e-a18944e5502e","l":"Bilayer films","d":2,"p":"08087671-73b2-4a66-a8ae-079c75cb9b33","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Bilayer films are engineered thin-film structures consisting of two layers of different (or identical) materials stacked on a substrate, with layer thicknesses typically in the nanometer-to-micrometer range. Their overall behavior is dominated by interfacial effects\u2014such as charge transfer, strain, proximity coupling, and interdiffusion\u2014which can produce electronic, optical, magnetic, or mechanical properties distinct from the bulk constituents."},{"id":"f01be782-7e48-4aa0-9f2a-7ca0c74e2520","l":"Bilayer graphene","d":2,"p":"a0106d33-6e0e-4b73-8190-a953b55d037a","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Bilayer graphene consists of two graphene monolayers stacked on top of each other (most commonly in Bernal/AB stacking), with interlayer hopping that alters the single-layer electronic structure. This coupling produces low-energy massive, chiral quasiparticles with a roughly parabolic dispersion, and a perpendicular electric field can open a tunable band gap."},{"id":"e4359de3-c27e-4118-91d3-9eb55efc224e","l":"Billiards","d":2,"p":"4b51a2bf-3140-4387-90ec-91ffb4f9d381","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Nonlinear Dynamics"],"def":"Billiards are idealized dynamical systems in which a point particle moves freely inside a fixed region and undergoes perfectly elastic, specular reflections off the rigid boundary; they serve as simple models for studying classical and quantum chaos, ergodicity, and transport."},{"id":"7a2142f6-2dcc-4e0a-b910-e8554b7342e2","l":"Biodiesel","d":2,"p":"3ec5c202-988d-4eb9-bd75-d51f77fbe15a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Biodiesel is a renewable liquid fuel made of fatty acid methyl or ethyl esters produced by transesterifying vegetable oils or animal fats, used as a drop-in substitute or blend for petroleum diesel. It has combustion properties similar to diesel but typically a bit lower energy density and higher viscosity, and generally produces lower sulfur and particulate emissions while being biodegradable."},{"id":"7b165f78-b636-495d-aea2-01023f34a353","l":"Bioenergetics","d":2,"p":"00960c4d-78bb-469c-8ffb-cda89f4fe20c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Bioenergetics is the study of how living systems capture, convert, store, and use energy, applying thermodynamics and kinetics to biological processes such as metabolism, ATP synthesis, and molecular motors. It quantifies energy flow and work in cells through concepts like free-energy changes, reaction coupling, and electrochemical gradients across membranes."},{"id":"aa41a029-6867-47db-a636-f4a8071d0423","l":"Bioethanol","d":2,"p":"3ec5c202-988d-4eb9-bd75-d51f77fbe15a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Bioethanol is ethanol (C2H5OH) produced from biomass\u2014typically by fermentation of sugars or by thermochemical conversion of plant materials\u2014and used as a renewable liquid fuel. It is commonly blended with gasoline for spark\u2011ignition engines and has a lower volumetric energy density but higher octane rating, giving different combustion and emissions characteristics than gasoline."},{"id":"2d145e00-2891-45ce-b55c-6d295e385e81","l":"Biofilms","d":2,"p":"af53b7f0-3191-45c2-9be4-e04b69db0666","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Biofilms are surface-associated communities of microorganisms encased in a self-produced, hydrated polymeric matrix (extracellular polymeric substances) that anchors them to each other and to surfaces. From a physics perspective they behave as heterogeneous, viscoelastic active materials with complex transport, mechanical and collective dynamics driven by growth, chemical gradients and microbial activity."},{"id":"8f860fdb-9b91-4d64-889d-38c8b741a595","l":"Biogas","d":2,"p":"3ec5c202-988d-4eb9-bd75-d51f77fbe15a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Biogas is a combustible gas mixture produced by anaerobic microbial decomposition of organic matter, typically consisting of methane (\u224850\u201370%) and carbon dioxide (\u224830\u201350%) with traces of hydrogen sulfide, nitrogen and water vapor. It is a renewable fuel\u2014generated in landfills and engineered digesters\u2014that has significant calorific value and can be used for heat, electricity, or as vehicle fuel after purification."},{"id":"d56b5277-e328-4221-a086-720804ca6adf","l":"Biological movement","d":2,"p":"bb9d205a-1327-40a1-a9f3-91d677ea5de3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Biological movement is the generation and control of motion by living systems, including whole-organism locomotion and the transport of materials within and between cells. It spans mechanisms from molecular motors, cytoskeletal dynamics, muscles, cilia, and flagella, and is analyzed with physical principles such as mechanics, fluid dynamics, elasticity, and nonequilibrium (active-matter) physics."},{"id":"747386d5-d15e-43fe-890c-abafe7aad8f2","l":"Biological self-organization","d":2,"p":"bb9d205a-1327-40a1-a9f3-91d677ea5de3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Biological self-organization is the spontaneous emergence of ordered structures and functions in living systems driven by local interactions, feedback loops, and flows of energy and matter that keep the system far from thermodynamic equilibrium. It underlies molecular assemblies, cell-patterning and morphogenesis, and collective behaviors, and is modeled using nonlinear dynamics, stochastic processes, and pattern-formation theory."},{"id":"3fb70e7e-fce8-4ec4-820c-cf126dde5a22","l":"Biomechanics","d":2,"p":"00960c4d-78bb-469c-8ffb-cda89f4fe20c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Biomechanics is the application of mechanics to biological systems, studying how forces, motions, and deformations determine the structure, function, and behavior of organisms and tissues across scales. It uses principles of solid and fluid mechanics, material properties, and dynamical modeling to analyze phenomena from molecular and cellular mechanics to locomotion and cardiovascular flow."},{"id":"e53ee31f-27a6-46a0-829d-6c99796b3627","l":"Biomolecular diffusion","d":2,"p":"00960c4d-78bb-469c-8ffb-cda89f4fe20c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Biomolecular diffusion is the thermally driven, random motion of biomolecules (proteins, nucleic acids, metabolites) that produces net transport down concentration gradients; it is characterized by a diffusion coefficient and described by Fick\u2019s laws and Brownian motion, though in cells it is often altered by crowding, binding, confinement, or active processes (yielding hindered or anomalous diffusion)."},{"id":"deef9592-7e3e-4949-9bca-ffa9fa9d1e46","l":"Biomolecular dynamics","d":2,"p":"00960c4d-78bb-469c-8ffb-cda89f4fe20c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Biomolecular dynamics is the study of the time-dependent motions and conformational changes of biological macromolecules (proteins, nucleic acids, lipids) and their assemblies, driven by thermal fluctuations, intermolecular forces, and solvent interactions. It covers processes from atomic vibrations and side\u2011chain rearrangements to folding, binding, and large-scale conformational transitions, analyzed with statistical mechanics, molecular simulations, and time-resolved experiments."},{"id":"b9a7848b-4a90-4912-94b2-4d9c05b0a736","l":"Biomolecular interactions","d":2,"p":"00960c4d-78bb-469c-8ffb-cda89f4fe20c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Biomolecular interactions are the physical forces and energetic processes that govern association, recognition, folding, and dynamics of biological macromolecules (proteins, nucleic acids, lipids, carbohydrates). They arise from covalent and primarily noncovalent forces\u2014electrostatics, hydrogen bonds, van der Waals, hydrophobic and \u03c0-type interactions\u2014and are described by thermodynamics and kinetics on complex energy landscapes that set binding affinities, reaction rates, and assembly behavior."},{"id":"9c437e89-cf6a-4250-8738-b43ded1eb4ff","l":"Biomolecular optics","d":2,"p":"00960c4d-78bb-469c-8ffb-cda89f4fe20c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Biomolecular optics is the study of how light interacts with biological molecules, encompassing their optical properties, photophysics, and photochemistry. It covers optical methods\u2014spectroscopy, fluorescence and single-molecule microscopy, plasmonics, nonlinear optics, and optical trapping\u2014used to probe, image, and manipulate the structure and dynamics of proteins, nucleic acids, lipids and other biomolecules."},{"id":"9988d7c1-f3e7-4fec-b85f-8fa6df6487bb","l":"Biomolecular self-assembly","d":2,"p":"00960c4d-78bb-469c-8ffb-cda89f4fe20c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Biomolecular self-assembly is the spontaneous, thermodynamically driven organization of biological macromolecules into defined higher-order structures via noncovalent interactions (hydrogen bonds, electrostatics, van der Waals, hydrophobic effect) that minimize free energy. It is often hierarchical and reversible across nano\u2013micron scales and underlies processes such as protein folding, membrane formation, ribosome assembly, and virus capsid formation."},{"id":"9fe8a5bc-b93d-46bc-9faa-208a23f0d923","l":"Biomolecular structure","d":2,"p":"00960c4d-78bb-469c-8ffb-cda89f4fe20c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Biomolecular structure is the three-dimensional arrangement of atoms in biological molecules (proteins, nucleic acids, lipids, carbohydrates) described at hierarchical levels\u2014primary, secondary, tertiary, and quaternary\u2014and set by covalent bonds and noncovalent interactions (hydrogen bonds, electrostatics, van der Waals, hydrophobic effect), which together determine the molecule's stability, dynamics, and biological function."},{"id":"f9781150-9f5c-4996-9642-458e01110653","l":"Biomolecule manipulation","d":2,"p":"00960c4d-78bb-469c-8ffb-cda89f4fe20c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Biomolecule manipulation is the controlled use of physical forces, fields, and confinements to position, stretch, rotate, or otherwise control single molecules or molecular assemblies so as to probe or change their structure, dynamics, and interactions. Common experimental implementations include optical and magnetic tweezers, atomic-force microscopy, electric fields and nanopores, and microfluidic confinement."},{"id":"f3009e0b-4c3d-442e-acf7-ca8a96209cb0","l":"Biosensing","d":2,"p":"00960c4d-78bb-469c-8ffb-cda89f4fe20c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Biosensing is the detection and quantification of biological molecules, cells, or processes by converting a specific biochemical recognition event into a measurable physical signal. It couples a biological recognition element (e.g., antibody, enzyme, nucleic acid, cell) to a physical transducer (optical, electrical, mechanical, acoustic, magnetic) to provide sensitive, selective, and often real-time readout for diagnostics, environmental monitoring, and research."},{"id":"213035ab-7d9e-4de0-b601-0733da9694b4","l":"Birefringence","d":2,"p":"5ef62623-4765-4b21-8fce-7542d68c9faa","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Birefringence is an optical property of anisotropic materials in which the refractive index depends on light\u2019s polarization and propagation direction. As a result, an incident beam is split into two orthogonally polarized rays (ordinary and extraordinary) that travel at different speeds and/or along different paths, producing double refraction and a relative phase retardation."},{"id":"707164f9-c279-429e-b986-2d1647b0d1ef","l":"BKT transition","d":2,"p":"d2c655da-6260-45e7-bc14-26fba976f03b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Berezinskii\u2013Kosterlitz\u2013Thouless (BKT) transition is a topological phase transition in two-dimensional systems with a continuous symmetry (e.g., the 2D XY model or thin superfluids) where the low-temperature phase has quasi-long-range order and the high-temperature phase is disordered. It is driven by thermal unbinding of vortex\u2013antivortex pairs and is characterized by an essential singularity in the correlation length and a universal jump in the stiffness at the transition temperature."},{"id":"ca6ea60d-96c0-4784-83a0-1e17843f0edb","l":"Bloch-Floquet theorem","d":2,"p":"3817eab7-2f24-46f8-ba4a-aa6433722b4a","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Bloch\u2013Floquet theorem states that for a linear differential operator with coefficients periodic in space or time, its solutions can be written as a product of a plane-wave (or exponential in time) factor and a function with the same period; e.g., spatially periodic systems admit Bloch states \u03c8_k(x)=e^{ikx}u_k(x) with u_k(x+a)=u_k(x), and time-periodic systems admit Floquet states \u03c8(t)=e^{-i\u03b5t/\u0127}\u03c6(t) with \u03c6(t+T)=\u03c6(t). This structure yields band (or quasi-energy) spectra and underlies phenomena in crystals, photonic lattices, and driven quantum systems."},{"id":"a0431a9c-276a-4f91-962e-ea2b0b275b16","l":"Bloch wave theory","d":2,"p":"3817eab7-2f24-46f8-ba4a-aa6433722b4a","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Bloch wave theory describes the quantum states of particles in a spatially periodic potential (such as a crystal): Bloch's theorem says each eigenstate can be written as \u03c8_k(r)=e^{i k\u00b7r}u_k(r), where u_k(r) has the lattice periodicity and k is the crystal momentum. This structure implies energy eigenvalues form bands as functions of k (the band structure in the Brillouin zone), which govern electronic and vibrational transport properties."},{"id":"71f63abf-95bd-4353-989b-179eff053880","l":"Block models","d":2,"p":"7b9db112-8475-42b4-ad22-f00f6b8635f8","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Block models are piecewise\u2011constant discretizations of a physical domain in which the region is partitioned into a finite set of contiguous blocks, each assigned uniform material or field parameters. They provide a simple, computationally convenient way to approximate spatially varying properties for forward simulations, inversion, and parameter estimation."},{"id":"356040c7-f8a2-4afa-a9e4-c6f22fd80281","l":"Blood","d":2,"p":"3e04a8a2-1199-4410-801c-ac848b0bbda8","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Blood is a complex biological fluid of plasma (a water-based solution of proteins, ions, and solutes) suspending red and white blood cells and platelets. Physically it behaves as a multiphase, non-Newtonian (shear-thinning), viscoelastic suspension whose rheology, transport properties, and flow-dependent behaviors (aggregation, margination, clotting) depend on hematocrit, cell deformability, and shear rate."},{"id":"1c51a1d6-f1ea-4920-add7-5200be585089","l":"Blood flow","d":2,"p":"12f6f3a9-8096-40e2-9170-e8f3a45d6bd9","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Blood flow is the transport of blood through the cardiovascular system driven by pressure differences generated mainly by the heart. Physically it is a pulsatile, often laminar flow of a complex, shear\u2011thinning suspension whose behavior is governed by the conservation laws and fluid dynamics (e.g., Navier\u2013Stokes/Poiseuille approximations) modified by vessel geometry, branching, resistance, and compliance."},{"id":"b40be3c9-d5da-42ba-9c7a-7e33cd8f38af","l":"Body-centered cubic","d":2,"p":"6af5be88-489f-4a50-9abf-998e8912254a","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Body-centered cubic (BCC) is a cubic Bravais lattice with lattice points at the eight corners of a cube and one additional point at the cube center, giving two lattice points per unit cell. It is common in metals, has coordination number 8, a packing fraction \u22480.68, and nearest-neighbor distance equal to (\u221a3/2) times the cube edge length."},{"id":"0977007d-64b8-4e82-8ba0-f00ebd3c6a16","l":"Bogoliubov-de Gennes equations","d":2,"p":"7299559c-15c4-47f3-b866-5633b1030453","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Bogoliubov\u2013de Gennes (BdG) equations are a mean-field, self-consistent eigenvalue problem that describe superconducting quasiparticles by coupling electron-like and hole-like amplitudes u(r), v(r) through the pair potential \u0394(r) and the single-particle Hamiltonian.  They generalize BCS theory to spatially inhomogeneous systems (vortices, interfaces, finite or topological superconductors), yielding symmetric eigenvalues \u00b1E that are the energies of Bogoliubov quasiparticles."},{"id":"b7549bc9-c07f-4ee2-91b6-7ff30a88a264","l":"Bolometers","d":2,"p":"a38dc017-3236-4084-afc5-eb59c5de51e0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Bolometers are thermal detectors that measure incident electromagnetic radiation (or deposited particle energy) by absorbing it and sensing the resulting tiny temperature rise of an absorber element that is weakly thermally linked to a cold bath via a thermometer. Their sensitivity and time response are set by the absorber's heat capacity, the thermal conductance to the bath, and the thermometer noise, and they are widely used in infrared, submillimeter and particle detection (often at cryogenic temperatures)."},{"id":"1d643b36-603c-4c69-a0bc-4b9e87bf1479","l":"Boltzmann theory","d":2,"p":"f4585613-4c82-4c78-a57d-515fa4774cb6","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Boltzmann theory is the kinetic theory that describes the time evolution of a dilute gas via a single-particle distribution function obeying the Boltzmann equation, which includes free streaming and a collision integral for binary interactions. It connects microscopic particle dynamics to macroscopic transport and irreversibility, predicting relaxation toward the Maxwell\u2013Boltzmann equilibrium and enabling calculation of transport coefficients."},{"id":"59cf390c-9a8f-41bd-830c-8554677924f3","l":"Boojums","d":2,"p":"3331008f-47b2-4908-92ad-bc9bd7cd815e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"A boojum is a surface-bound topological defect in an ordered medium that appears where a bulk singularity (for example a vortex or disclination) terminates at a boundary or interface, producing a localized point singularity with characteristic topological charge. The term, introduced by N. D. Mermin, is used to describe such defects in superfluids, liquid crystals and related systems."},{"id":"d087a960-b5b7-4fb4-ac88-6e150de91ccb","l":"Bose-Bose mixtures","d":2,"p":"b7445287-a978-40fe-8f6c-2804e07201e0","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"A Bose-Bose mixture is a quantum gas containing two distinguishable bosonic components (different atomic species, isotopes, or internal states) that can each form a Bose-Einstein condensate; its equilibrium and dynamical properties\u2014miscibility, phase separation, and collective modes\u2014are governed by the competition between intra- and interspecies interactions. It is commonly modeled by coupled Gross\u2013Pitaevskii equations for the two condensate wavefunctions."},{"id":"12f23909-b343-41da-b30c-88d06cbf738b","l":"Bose-Fermi mixtures","d":2,"p":"b7445287-a978-40fe-8f6c-2804e07201e0","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"A Bose\u2013Fermi mixture is a many-body system containing coexisting bosonic and fermionic particles whose distinct quantum statistics and mutual interactions govern the system's collective behavior. In ultracold-atom realizations this interplay leads to effects such as phase separation, Bose-mediated effective interactions that can induce fermion pairing, and formation of polarons or mixed superfluid phases."},{"id":"520c13af-c184-43ce-b48a-09434b9732af","l":"Boson sampling","d":2,"p":"830eaa37-9813-4972-9351-1846c659b20b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Boson sampling is a specialized quantum sampling task in which many indistinguishable bosons (typically single photons) are injected into a linear-optical interferometer and the pattern of output detections is sampled; the probabilities are determined by the squared moduli of matrix permanents of submatrices of the interferometer\u2019s unitary. It is not a universal quantum computer but is believed to be intractable for classical computers for sufficiently large systems, making it a candidate for demonstrating quantum computational advantage."},{"id":"8d15a528-54fb-4e89-89fa-e4fc9ddbdd47","l":"Bottom quark production","d":2,"p":"97069ede-42dd-4f4d-b552-9fc8562cb02d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Bottom quark production is the creation of bottom (b) quarks in high\u2011energy processes, most commonly by strong\u2011interaction pair production (e.g., gluon fusion or quark\u2013antiquark annihilation) in particle collisions or by electroweak single\u2011b processes. The produced b quarks quickly hadronize into B hadrons, and measurements of their production rates and kinematics test QCD and electroweak predictions."},{"id":"d136f161-f968-4c15-8bf1-25823f7fd837","l":"Bound states in the continuum","d":2,"p":"0b2ca6ea-0697-4de2-b157-68ce3ac12983","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Bound states in the continuum (BICs) are spatially localized, square-integrable eigenstates whose discrete energy eigenvalues lie inside the continuous spectrum of extended scattering states but nevertheless remain nonradiating and decoupled from that continuum. They arise when symmetry, destructive interference, parameter tuning, or topological constraints cancel the coupling to outgoing modes, giving the state an effectively infinite lifetime despite lying within the continuum."},{"id":"182daf39-1dac-4eae-a4ee-e8217d74edec","l":"Bragg spectroscopy","d":2,"p":"d8d9518f-91ee-40ab-9529-2c3f98431a0f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics"],"def":"Bragg spectroscopy is an experimental probe that transfers a well-defined momentum q and energy \u210f\u03c9 to a sample via scattering from a moving periodic potential (commonly two phase-coherent laser beams) and measures the induced response. By recording the scattered intensity or excitation rate as a function of q and \u03c9 one obtains the dynamic structure factor S(q,\u03c9), revealing the system's excitation spectrum and correlations."},{"id":"f9d3e8bd-295a-42fa-814c-caffe4307d81","l":"Bragg structures","d":2,"p":"dcf48409-af3f-4517-b58d-7ec6c95351f3","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Bragg structures are periodic arrangements of scattering planes or layers (for example dielectric multilayers or crystal lattice planes) with spacing comparable to the wavelength of interest, which produce strong constructive interference (Bragg reflection) for waves satisfying the Bragg condition. They create wavelength-selective stop bands or photonic band gaps and are used as mirrors, filters, and in photonic- and phononic-crystal devices."},{"id":"7a9902f6-9ec0-44df-9bec-8f1e1feb9dcb","l":"Branching fraction","d":2,"p":"270c18a9-5bde-4c79-9f83-8453d38dc9c8","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Branching fraction (or branching ratio) is the probability that an unstable particle or excited state decays via a particular channel, equal to the partial decay width for that channel divided by the total decay width; the branching fractions for all possible channels sum to one."},{"id":"5e1dedab-bd52-4ebd-95ca-64b8c28718c1","l":"Brewster reflection spectroscopy","d":2,"p":"8156d7cb-6ab9-4097-be2b-4aa71aad93a3","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Brewster reflection spectroscopy is an optical technique that measures the reflectance of p\u2011polarized light at or near the Brewster angle to sensitively probe the optical properties (complex refractive index, weak absorption, resonances) and thicknesses of surfaces and thin films. Because the Fresnel p\u2011polarized reflection vanishes at the Brewster angle for nonabsorbing interfaces, small dispersive or absorptive features are detected with high contrast."},{"id":"57bab1b8-66b4-4a03-84f6-67914518aba5","l":"Brewster's angle","d":2,"p":"5ef62623-4765-4b21-8fce-7542d68c9faa","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Brewster's angle is the angle of incidence at which light reflected from the interface between two non-absorbing media is completely polarized perpendicular to the plane of incidence (the reflected and refracted rays are at 90\u00b0). It satisfies tan \u03b8B = n2/n1 (with n1 the incident-medium refractive index and n2 the transmitted-medium index), at which the parallel (p) component of reflection vanishes."},{"id":"a577028b-b641-4ccc-87da-10982ace21ec","l":"Brillouin scattering & spectroscopy","d":2,"p":"8156d7cb-6ab9-4097-be2b-4aa71aad93a3","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Brillouin scattering is the inelastic scattering of light by thermally excited acoustic phonons or density fluctuations in a material, which produces small frequency shifts (Brillouin shifts) related to the acoustic wavelength, sound velocity, and refractive index. Brillouin spectroscopy measures those frequency shifts and linewidths to probe elastic and viscoelastic properties, acoustic dispersion, and dynamic processes at gigahertz (hypersonic) frequencies."},{"id":"0ac2935f-9073-4bee-befd-2d0f09f3b26e","l":"Brownian dynamics","d":2,"p":"695da376-c44a-41dd-b968-d200c31e9d5e","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems","Plasma Physics"],"def":"Brownian dynamics is the stochastic description and simulation of particle motion in a viscous fluid where inertia is negligible, so particle velocities relax instantly and dynamics are governed by deterministic forces, viscous drag, and random thermal (Brownian) forces. It is implemented via the overdamped Langevin equation (or equivalent stochastic differential equation) and is used to model diffusive behavior of colloids, polymers, and biomolecules while satisfying the fluctuation\u2013dissipation relation."},{"id":"247815d0-7a1c-43e1-a5b2-bc1d2e22fa0a","l":"Brownmillerite","d":2,"p":"6af5be88-489f-4a50-9abf-998e8912254a","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Brownmillerite is an ordered oxygen\u2011deficient perovskite crystal structure (commonly A2B2O5) in which ordered oxygen vacancies produce alternating layers of corner\u2011linked BO6 octahedra and BO4 tetrahedra. This vacancy\u2011ordered arrangement gives rise to distinctive ionic, electronic and magnetic properties used in oxides chemistry and functional materials."},{"id":"d6f4cd63-92c6-4069-bb11-ab7923568ddf","l":"Brush polymer","d":2,"p":"2303d382-65bb-4b93-94b8-62a73502ec4b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"A brush polymer is a macromolecular architecture in which polymer chains (side chains) are densely grafted onto a linear backbone or a surface so that steric crowding forces the side chains to stretch away, forming a brush-like layer. This grafting-induced stretching strongly alters the molecule's mechanical, rheological and interfacial properties compared with linear polymers."},{"id":"c8c19391-f7a0-467e-9662-e9288f02e971","l":"Buckling","d":2,"p":"42ec6252-ac7f-42bd-9ddc-08ac45a4b4e9","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Buckling is an elastic instability in which a structural member (like a column, plate, or shell) subjected to compressive loading suddenly deforms laterally when the load exceeds a critical value. The critical load depends on the member's stiffness, geometry, length and boundary conditions (e.g., Euler's column formula for ideal slender columns)."},{"id":"00ecc3e9-c85c-40e4-bcbc-3642be5e1606","l":"Buneman waves","d":2,"p":"b39526d9-02d4-4caf-a280-518aed206059","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Buneman waves are short-wavelength electrostatic plasma waves driven by the Buneman (electron\u2013ion two-stream) instability that occurs when electrons drift sufficiently fast relative to ions. They grow rapidly on scales comparable to the electron Debye length and often evolve into nonlinear structures (e.g., electron holes) that produce strong electron heating and anomalous resistivity."},{"id":"2c65cc80-80de-42d8-85db-4d73e662c641","l":"Calcium signaling","d":2,"p":"c2ea9e2c-0e15-49ee-b336-111976b0b965","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Calcium signaling is the use of transient changes in intracellular calcium ion (Ca2+) concentration as a ubiquitous second messenger to transmit information and control cellular processes. Cells create spatiotemporal patterns (localized sparks, waves, oscillations) by regulated Ca2+ fluxes across plasma and organelle membranes, together with diffusion and buffering, which activate Ca2+-sensitive effectors for contraction, secretion, metabolism and gene expression."},{"id":"ce866ada-ee57-4c9c-81a0-937385d9ee88","l":"Calorimeters","d":2,"p":"be8bd934-5bc9-4a6d-ae73-f4753ce5c128","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Calorimeters (in particle physics) are detectors that measure the energy of incident particles by absorbing their energy and converting the deposited energy into a measurable signal (e.g., ionization, scintillation light, or heat), and are categorized as electromagnetic or hadronic and as homogeneous or sampling devices. More generally, a calorimeter in thermodynamics is an insulated apparatus that measures heat transfer by recording temperature changes of a material with known heat capacity."},{"id":"64f26e6b-2baf-45fa-bd50-974c3139e6bc","l":"Calorimetry","d":2,"p":"c2ea95f8-c209-44d7-b8f7-a6baf632c46a","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Calorimetry is the experimental measurement of heat exchanged during physical or chemical processes. It determines quantities such as heat capacities, reaction enthalpies, and phase-change energies by observing temperature changes in a calibrated calorimeter that relates those changes to transferred heat."},{"id":"48fd6047-01ff-43f4-b00d-757800d5876a","l":"Caloritronics","d":2,"p":"1d1e18e6-170c-403e-8786-d06fe58ddc94","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Caloritronics is the study and engineering of heat currents and thermal signals in materials and devices\u2014analogous to electronics but using thermal carriers (phonons, electrons, magnons) instead of charge. It encompasses control, measurement, and conversion of heat (e.g., thermoelectric and spin-caloritronic effects), especially at micro- and nanoscale for thermal management and information processing."},{"id":"864094ee-9e46-48e4-b267-5c07afbbdb14","l":"Cancer","d":2,"p":"bbcb2483-b22c-4bb6-bb81-beb53ff1b1ed","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Cancer is a collection of diseases in which cells acquire somatic genetic and epigenetic alterations that deregulate growth-control mechanisms, producing unchecked proliferation, local invasion, and often metastatic spread. It can be viewed as an evolutionary and ecological process in tissue, with altered signaling, mechanics, transport, and microenvironmental properties that are studied by physics-based approaches."},{"id":"75faf447-e157-46f1-b68c-8d2be5bbe346","l":"Canonical quantum gravity","d":2,"p":"6d5b9a32-cad0-4d9f-a837-b0f239f5cdc6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Canonical quantum gravity is an approach that quantizes general relativity by rewriting it in Hamiltonian (ADM) form and promoting the canonical phase-space variables of the spatial geometry and their momenta to operators whose commutators impose the theory's constraints.  The result is constraint equations (notably the Wheeler\u2013DeWitt equation) for a wavefunctional of the three-geometry, providing a nonperturbative, background\u2011independent route to quantum gravity (and forming the basis for loop quantum gravity)."},{"id":"522e8e48-cc1f-4b24-9a6e-9df7678cf8aa","l":"Capacitance spectroscopy","d":2,"p":"a38dc017-3236-4084-afc5-eb59c5de51e0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Capacitance spectroscopy is an experimental technique that measures a device's capacitance as a function of applied voltage, frequency, magnetic field, or temperature to probe the energy\u2011dependent charge distribution and density of electronic states. Variations in capacitance reveal occupation, charging transitions, and trap or quantum\u2011confined levels in semiconductors, heterostructures, and nanostructures."},{"id":"2434e95d-bec7-4636-952e-5b9df6989652","l":"Capacitors","d":2,"p":"2360e96e-a12b-4d21-9b65-36ed1177c0e6","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Capacitors are electrical components that store electric charge and energy by maintaining an electrostatic field between two conductors separated by an insulating dielectric; their ability to store charge is quantified by the capacitance C = Q/V. They are widely used to smooth voltages, block DC while passing AC, and form timing or filtering networks."},{"id":"113ab5da-6dd3-41ae-aa02-65a6a7bc650a","l":"Capsule & cell dynamics","d":2,"p":"12f6f3a9-8096-40e2-9170-e8f3a45d6bd9","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Capsule and cell dynamics is the study of the motion, deformation, and interactions of soft, membrane-enclosed particles (synthetic capsules or biological cells) suspended in fluids under applied flows, forces, or fields. Their behavior is governed by the coupling of membrane mechanics (elasticity, bending, tension), internal/external fluid viscosities and hydrodynamic interactions, and determines transport, rheology, and physiological or engineering function."},{"id":"089b087e-776a-47ca-b3c1-16c86030089e","l":"Carbohydrates","d":2,"p":"6c0e8bb5-a5a0-4f28-8d4a-4e7ace85a023","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Carbohydrates are organic biomolecules made of carbon, hydrogen, and oxygen, typically with the empirical formula Cn(H2O)m, and include simple sugars (monosaccharides), short chains (oligosaccharides), and long-chain polymers (polysaccharides). They function primarily as energy storage (e.g., glucose, glycogen, starch) and structural materials (e.g., cellulose) in living systems and have distinctive hydrophilic and hydrogen-bonding properties."},{"id":"2aeaa102-0ee1-4ae7-a52b-7230ec66deea","l":"Carbon capture","d":2,"p":"2e9e0c49-ce2d-4029-a89d-c5d0535574a2","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Carbon capture is the set of physical and chemical processes that remove carbon dioxide (CO2) from point-source emissions or directly from the atmosphere, using methods such as adsorption, absorption, membranes, or chemical reactions. The captured CO2 is then compressed and transported for long-term storage (e.g., geological sequestration) or for use in industrial processes to reduce net greenhouse-gas emissions."},{"id":"31f7839a-9a3c-4b8f-940b-8b2038c65af3","l":"Carbon-neutral fuels","d":2,"p":"2e9e0c49-ce2d-4029-a89d-c5d0535574a2","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Carbon-neutral fuels are fuels whose combustion emits CO2 that was previously removed from the atmosphere during their production (for example via biomass growth or carbon capture and conversion), so the net CO2 added over their lifecycle is zero. Whether a fuel is considered carbon-neutral depends on the chosen lifecycle boundaries and timescale of carbon uptake and release."},{"id":"64470342-2836-4044-a632-693f38e12b9f","l":"Carbon sequestration","d":2,"p":"2e9e0c49-ce2d-4029-a89d-c5d0535574a2","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Carbon sequestration is the removal of carbon \u2014 typically as carbon dioxide (CO2) \u2014 from the atmosphere and its transfer into long\u2011lived physical, chemical, or biological reservoirs to lower greenhouse\u2011gas concentrations. Methods include biological uptake (photosynthesis and soil/biomass storage), chemical/mineral carbonation, and engineered geological storage (injection into deep rock formations)."},{"id":"505b716e-8e82-444b-834c-e769c4d53e64","l":"Cardiac dynamics","d":2,"p":"bb9d205a-1327-40a1-a9f3-91d677ea5de3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Cardiac dynamics is the study of the time-dependent physical processes that govern heart function\u2014how electrical excitation, mechanical contraction, and blood flow interact as a coupled, multiscale nonlinear system\u2014producing wave propagation, oscillations, and instabilities such as arrhythmias."},{"id":"09a3322a-4d76-447c-9adf-6888f5d93aaf","l":"Casimir effect","d":2,"p":"ee82ad04-6c47-46a4-a70d-c9a7d7989f6a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"The Casimir effect is a quantum phenomenon in which material boundaries (e.g., conducting or dielectric surfaces) modify the allowed modes of the electromagnetic vacuum, producing a measurable force between neutral objects due to differences in zero-point (vacuum) energy. For example, two closely spaced parallel conductive plates experience an attractive force that increases as their separation decreases, though the sign and magnitude can depend on geometry and materials."},{"id":"745d2e58-7e0c-437c-9362-ba8ec0eed57e","l":"Cavity magnonics","d":2,"p":"3123cbf9-460d-467a-9306-b4fb316daddd","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Cavity magnonics is the study of the coherent interaction between magnons\u2014collective spin-wave excitations in magnetic materials\u2014and microwave photons confined in resonant cavities. It focuses on hybridization and strong-coupling phenomena (magnon\u2013photon polaritons) for controlling spin dynamics and enabling information transfer, sensing, and quantum transduction."},{"id":"f88f9d2e-9c55-48d2-aab4-c24fba575e45","l":"Cavity quantum electrodynamics","d":2,"p":"632f74d1-d16b-4b92-a295-b24729a93d8d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Cavity quantum electrodynamics (cavity QED) is the study of how quantized electromagnetic field modes confined in a resonator interact with atoms or other quantum emitters, and how that confinement alters light\u2013matter processes. It explores phenomena such as modified spontaneous emission rates (Purcell effect), coherent exchange of excitations in the strong-coupling regime (vacuum Rabi oscillations/splitting), and single\u2011photon quantum effects useful for quantum information."},{"id":"dd5ba9a6-ec6b-46ce-94ef-26ee41ff0db4","l":"Cavity resonators","d":2,"p":"a38dc017-3236-4084-afc5-eb59c5de51e0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Cavity resonators are structures that confine electromagnetic fields (by reflective or dielectric boundaries) so the fields form standing-wave modes at discrete resonant frequencies determined by the cavity geometry and boundary conditions. They store and selectively enhance electromagnetic energy and are characterized by mode patterns, resonant frequency, bandwidth (quality factor), and mode volume."},{"id":"80b30454-0457-4361-9703-68513ab85b8d","l":"Cell adhesion","d":2,"p":"c2ea9e2c-0e15-49ee-b336-111976b0b965","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Cell adhesion is the physical process by which a cell binds to another cell or to the extracellular matrix through specific receptor\u2013ligand bonds and nonspecific surface forces, creating stable contacts that transmit mechanical stress. It is governed by molecular binding kinetics and cell-generated forces (via the cytoskeleton), and thereby controls contact mechanics, tissue structure, and mechanochemical signaling."},{"id":"3c86e03c-b9be-4012-8875-b1c2364d6f8e","l":"Cell aggregation","d":2,"p":"c2ea9e2c-0e15-49ee-b336-111976b0b965","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Cell aggregation is the process by which individual cells come together to form clusters or multicellular assemblies due to intercellular adhesion, motility, chemical signaling and mechanical or hydrodynamic forces. In physics it is treated as active self-assembly or driven phase separation, describable by models that balance attractive and repulsive interactions, motile activity, and noise."},{"id":"82632346-da0a-485d-9c7f-aaa5b66b0872","l":"Cell assembly","d":2,"p":"c2ea9e2c-0e15-49ee-b336-111976b0b965","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"A cell assembly is a group of neurons with strengthened mutual synaptic connections that tend to become coactive and act as a functional unit\u2014originally proposed by Hebb to explain how memories and percepts are represented. In dynamical terms it is often modeled as a correlated, self\u2011sustaining firing pattern or attractor state in a recurrent neural network."},{"id":"3a8352ee-4435-41ea-9773-7e00a8303f8c","l":"Cell communication","d":2,"p":"c2ea9e2c-0e15-49ee-b336-111976b0b965","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Cell communication is the transfer of information between cells by chemical, electrical, or mechanical signals\u2014via diffusion and active transport of molecules, direct contact (receptors or gap junctions), or ionic currents\u2014so that one cell\u2019s state influences another\u2019s. From a physics perspective it is governed by transport processes, reaction\u2013kinetics, membrane potentials and mechanical coupling that set signal speed, range, and fidelity."},{"id":"32f64d06-8ebf-473e-8996-65a651e8f4c4","l":"Cell division","d":2,"p":"c2ea9e2c-0e15-49ee-b336-111976b0b965","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Cell division is the biological process by which a single parent cell splits into two or more daughter cells, involving duplication and segregation of the genome followed by physical partitioning of the cytoplasm (examples: mitosis for somatic cells, meiosis for gametes). It is driven by coordinated biochemical control and by mechanical forces such as spindle-driven chromosome motion and actomyosin contractility that reshape and divide the cell."},{"id":"fba8f315-e516-4e85-bedd-e067dc4a0ac3","l":"Cell fusion","d":2,"p":"c2ea9e2c-0e15-49ee-b336-111976b0b965","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Cell fusion is the process by which two distinct biological cells become one through the merging of their plasma membranes and the mixing of their cytoplasms. Physically it involves lipid bilayer rearrangements\u2014stalk and hemifusion intermediates followed by formation and expansion of a fusion pore\u2014driven by fusogenic proteins and by forces that overcome hydration, electrostatic, and membrane-elastic energy barriers."},{"id":"b6542f7b-a81e-4861-9856-17b8f8678f92","l":"Cell growth","d":2,"p":"c2ea9e2c-0e15-49ee-b336-111976b0b965","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Cell growth is the process by which a single cell increases its mass, volume, and molecular content through regulated uptake of nutrients and biosynthesis, driven by metabolic energy and constrained by transport, mechanics and thermodynamics. It is characterized quantitatively by growth rates, size-control laws, and the coupling between biochemical reactions and physical processes (diffusion, force balance, osmotic pressure)."},{"id":"aa7dd447-22f5-41d7-9033-5f115ac65e72","l":"Cell junctions","d":2,"p":"3e04a8a2-1199-4410-801c-ac848b0bbda8","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Cell junctions are specialized protein assemblies at interfaces between adjacent cells that mechanically link their cytoskeletons and/or form regulated seals or channels, enabling force transmission, selective transport, electrical coupling, and coordinated signaling across tissues. Common types include adhesive junctions (adherens, desmosomes), occluding/tight junctions, and communicating/gap junctions."},{"id":"a25a2941-9bcf-4be2-90e3-9dd72d0a33a9","l":"Cell locomotion","d":2,"p":"c2ea9e2c-0e15-49ee-b336-111976b0b965","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Cell locomotion is the active displacement of whole cells produced by internally generated mechanical forces and interactions with their surroundings. It includes crawling (actin polymerization-driven protrusions, myosin-mediated contraction and adhesion dynamics) and swimming (flagellar or ciliary beating), and is governed by force balance, adhesion kinetics, and low\u2013Reynolds-number hydrodynamics."},{"id":"589b5f45-5c4d-4d4e-a80e-612f2397152c","l":"Cell mechanics","d":2,"p":"c2ea9e2c-0e15-49ee-b336-111976b0b965","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Cell mechanics is the study of how living cells generate, transmit, and respond to mechanical forces, and how their structural components (membrane, cytoskeleton, adhesions, nucleus) determine deformation, stiffness, and viscoelastic behavior. It covers both passive material properties and active, energy-consuming processes that drive shape change, motility, and mechanotransduction from molecular to tissue scales."},{"id":"fd22f7de-66cb-47e6-9a4f-d4d421428168","l":"Cell membrane","d":2,"p":"9ccf11df-5b72-4ce0-92e6-e48f8dda172b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"The cell membrane (plasma membrane) is a nanometer-scale (~5 nm) fluid lipid bilayer studded with proteins and carbohydrates that encloses the cell and forms a mechanically flexible, chemically distinct interface between the interior and external environment. Its selective permeability, transport proteins, and ion gradients regulate molecular fluxes and generate membrane potentials used for signaling, energy transduction, and homeostasis."},{"id":"80fb6a46-7823-4068-8e0e-c3b317c807ab","l":"Cell membrane transport","d":2,"p":"c2ea9e2c-0e15-49ee-b336-111976b0b965","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Cell membrane transport is the movement of ions and molecules across a cell's lipid bilayer, driven by concentration and electrochemical gradients or by metabolic energy. It occurs by passive processes (simple and facilitated diffusion, osmosis) and active transport (carrier proteins and pumps using ATP), and controls ionic fluxes, membrane potential, and cellular homeostasis."},{"id":"b3e52f50-6654-4938-89f0-9c2e6f5977eb","l":"Cell migration","d":2,"p":"c2ea9e2c-0e15-49ee-b336-111976b0b965","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Cell migration is the active movement of cells through their environment produced by internal force generation and dynamic adhesion to substrates. It is driven by cytoskeletal processes (e.g., actin polymerization and myosin contractility) guided by chemical and mechanical cues, and underlies development, immune responses and cancer metastasis while serving as a model of active matter and mechanochemical coupling."},{"id":"25e62d0e-c610-4e57-ae10-4e8f7c349da8","l":"Cell nucleus","d":2,"p":"9ccf11df-5b72-4ce0-92e6-e48f8dda172b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Cell nucleus \u2014 the membrane-bound organelle in eukaryotic cells that houses the genome (packaged as chromatin) and the nucleolus; it is enclosed by a double lipid bilayer (the nuclear envelope) perforated by nuclear pores that control selective exchange of proteins and RNAs with the cytoplasm. It organizes DNA replication, transcription and ribosome assembly and has distinct mechanical and transport properties that couple genetic function to cytoskeletal forces."},{"id":"ed0cca7c-963b-4b09-b7a5-4fac19eebb0d","l":"Cell polarity","d":2,"p":"c2ea9e2c-0e15-49ee-b336-111976b0b965","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Cell polarity is a form of symmetry breaking in which a cell establishes spatially distinct domains of proteins, lipids and cytoskeletal structures (e.g., apical\u2013basal or front\u2013rear), enabling directional functions such as migration, division and targeted transport. It emerges and is maintained by coordinated biochemical gradients, active transport and mechanical feedback between molecular reactions and the cytoskeleton."},{"id":"8d53b475-1899-4fd3-975d-b4627f9abc88","l":"Cell signaling","d":2,"p":"c2ea9e2c-0e15-49ee-b336-111976b0b965","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Cell signaling is the process by which cells detect, transmit, and interpret information from their environment or neighboring cells via molecular interactions and physical cues. It consists of receptors, intracellular signal-transduction cascades (chemical messengers, ion fluxes, or mechanical forces), and effector mechanisms that convert signals into specific cellular responses."},{"id":"368fbc15-b05e-4b16-872a-d1434405e35a","l":"Cellular bilayers","d":2,"p":"c2ea9e2c-0e15-49ee-b336-111976b0b965","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Cellular bilayers are nanometer-thick, self-assembled sheets of amphiphilic lipid molecules that form the basic structural scaffold of cell membranes, with two opposing monolayers whose hydrophobic tails face inward and hydrophilic head groups face the aqueous exterior and interior. Their collective molecular interactions give rise to fluid, elastic, and selectively permeable behavior that controls transport, signaling, and membrane mechanics."},{"id":"1f84a3cb-c369-4882-baa3-a0aae44a63f8","l":"Cellular compartments","d":2,"p":"9ccf11df-5b72-4ce0-92e6-e48f8dda172b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Cellular compartments are distinct spatial regions within a cell\u2014either membrane-bound organelles (e.g., nucleus, mitochondria) or membrane-less biomolecular condensates\u2014whose specialized chemical environments and selective barriers concentrate specific molecules and reactions. They are maintained by physical mechanisms such as lipid bilayer membranes, active transport, and phase separation of macromolecules."},{"id":"808591fd-33d4-48e1-9f07-6a881fef4465","l":"Cell wall","d":2,"p":"9ccf11df-5b72-4ce0-92e6-e48f8dda172b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"A cell wall is a rigid or semi-rigid extracellular layer outside the plasma membrane of many cells (plants, fungi, bacteria, and algae) that provides mechanical support, maintains cell shape, and resists internal turgor pressure; its chemical composition varies by organism (e.g., cellulose in plants, chitin in fungi, peptidoglycan in bacteria)."},{"id":"559a4755-5e19-43c6-9696-00f37a44188c","l":"Cesium chloride structure","d":2,"p":"6af5be88-489f-4a50-9abf-998e8912254a","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The cesium chloride (CsCl) structure is a cubic ionic lattice in which one ion type sits at the cube corners and the other at the body center\u2014equivalently two interpenetrating simple-cubic sublattices displaced by (1/2,1/2,1/2)\u2014so each ion is surrounded by eight oppositely charged neighbors (coordination number 8); it has space group Pm-3m (B2)."},{"id":"1ebb7047-0c79-41e2-9a21-a6e76eacd55f","l":"Charge migration","d":2,"p":"339a8cdb-9337-4b8e-9138-cc7fcf112782","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Charge migration is the ultrafast, coherent redistribution of electronic charge density within a molecule, cluster, or nanostructure that follows a sudden perturbation (such as ionization or excitation), occurring on attosecond\u2013femtosecond timescales before significant nuclear motion. It is a purely electronic process driven by quantum coherence and electron correlation rather than by slower, classical carrier diffusion."},{"id":"6d57b8e3-e308-4a09-94e7-e552182a6352","l":"Charge order","d":2,"p":"979074fa-fadd-4ebe-acf1-0a9c92fb1848","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Charge order is a broken-symmetry electronic state in which the electron density becomes spatially modulated, producing a periodic or patterned arrangement of regions with higher and lower local charge. It typically arises from Coulomb interactions or Fermi-surface instabilities in correlated solids (e.g., charge density waves, stripe phases, or Wigner crystals) and strongly affects transport and competing orders like superconductivity."},{"id":"457680f3-90de-482b-a77e-c5caad895e24","l":"Charge-transfer collisions","d":2,"p":"7fb8ecb8-7f22-49e6-bd57-959dc9757c32","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Charge-transfer collisions (or charge exchange) are collisions between two particles\u2014typically an ion and a neutral atom or molecule\u2014in which one or more electrons are transferred from one partner to the other, changing their charge states. These inelastic collisions (resonant if the partners are the same species) play important roles in plasmas, astrophysics, and ion-beam physics by altering ionization balance and energy distribution."},{"id":"ef822552-b27e-40d0-80b1-e7a27606984a","l":"Chemical charge transfer","d":2,"p":"a8c79e8a-73ed-4190-8e3c-6fa1bef601d1","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Chemical charge transfer is the movement of electron density from one atom, molecule, or material (donor) to another (acceptor) during a chemical interaction, resulting in partial or complete electron transfer and often a change in oxidation states. It underlies redox reactions and charge-transfer complexes and strongly affects optical, electrical, and chemical properties of the species involved."},{"id":"c42bb2b7-41ca-4ac5-96c5-d6663b27ef6d","l":"Chemical lasers","d":2,"p":"cd4c012e-bacf-4bcf-8358-a6573a330ec4","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Chemical lasers are lasers in which a chemical reaction directly produces the excited atoms or molecules that provide the population inversion, so the stored chemical energy acts as the pump rather than electrical or optical excitation. They can deliver very high continuous or pulsed power (e.g., HF/DF and the chemical oxygen\u2013iodine laser COIL) and often operate in the infrared."},{"id":"8377b215-5432-4962-b384-44374d9a7e41","l":"Chemical separation","d":2,"p":"cb2f25c7-cb3c-4847-8c5e-0da35f6df26b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Chemical separation is the controlled isolation of components of a mixture by exploiting differences in their physical or chemical properties (e.g., volatility, solubility, density, charge, or affinity). Techniques such as distillation, filtration, chromatography, centrifugation, and extraction use those property differences to obtain pure substances or concentrated fractions."},{"id":"9af7b654-70b3-4add-ad23-619d3b98ba5a","l":"Chemical synthesis","d":2,"p":"c4ed35b0-1090-409c-9667-85f9ac03e9c0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Chemical synthesis is the controlled construction of molecules from simpler precursor substances via chemical reactions, using reagents, catalysts, and tuned thermodynamic and kinetic conditions to direct reaction pathways. It encompasses the arrangement of atoms and bonds, stoichiometry, reaction mechanisms, and the associated energy changes to achieve desired products and selectivity."},{"id":"4b6d892d-f8c5-4897-a555-8cb705fff0e7","l":"Chemical waves","d":2,"p":"8e35265c-459a-43fc-9eb8-73e1dd82b47c","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Nonlinear Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Chemical waves are self-sustaining, traveling concentration or reaction fronts that propagate through a reactive medium because local nonlinear chemical kinetics are coupled to transport (diffusion and/or advection). They occur in excitable or bistable reaction\u2013diffusion systems (e.g., the Belousov\u2013Zhabotinsky reaction) and produce pulses, fronts, and spatiotemporal patterns (spirals, targets) with speeds set by reaction rates and transport coefficients."},{"id":"ea4bddbd-ef94-4d52-b979-d22e179a8502","l":"Chemisorption","d":2,"p":"229eca6d-30f1-4c05-9a78-c352de8326b1","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Chemisorption is the adsorption of atoms or molecules onto a solid surface by formation of chemical bonds (covalent or ionic) between the adsorbate and surface atoms, giving strong, site-specific binding and often altering the surface electronic structure. It typically involves bond energies of order eV and may require an activation barrier, in contrast to weaker, nonbonding physisorption."},{"id":"4bb41cf8-01d2-4230-9932-b7a92b1d8c14","l":"Chemotaxis","d":2,"p":"c2ea9e2c-0e15-49ee-b336-111976b0b965","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Chemotaxis is the directed movement of motile cells, organisms, or active particles up or down a chemical-concentration gradient in response to attractant or repellent cues. In physics it is described as biased random walks or drift-diffusion of active agents arising from coupling between gradient sensing, intracellular signaling, and propulsion."},{"id":"7b5ca426-0545-44b8-b3b0-653ddc7d837e","l":"Chemotherapy","d":2,"p":"a24a2d8a-58a4-4cbc-81aa-4cd953e73f4b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Chemotherapy is a medical cancer treatment that uses chemical agents (cytotoxic or cytostatic drugs) to kill or inhibit the proliferation of malignant cells, typically delivered systemically. In physics and biophysics contexts it is analyzed through transport and diffusion, reaction kinetics, dosing and dose-response models, and interactions with tissue microenvironments and other therapies."},{"id":"9527142a-3609-4c45-ac29-2e0620a6eff9","l":"Cherenkov detectors","d":2,"p":"be8bd934-5bc9-4a6d-ae73-f4753ce5c128","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Cherenkov detectors are instruments that detect charged particles by collecting the Cherenkov radiation they emit when they travel through a dielectric medium faster than the speed of light in that medium (c/n). By measuring the light's intensity, emission angle, or timing (in threshold, ring-imaging, or time-of-flight variants) they provide particle detection, velocity measurement, and particle identification."},{"id":"1f7506e5-00a6-466f-9ea6-2737dc09bb75","l":"Cherenkov radiation","d":2,"p":"3123cbf9-460d-467a-9306-b4fb316daddd","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Cherenkov radiation is the electromagnetic light emitted when a charged particle travels through a dielectric medium faster than the phase velocity of light in that medium, producing a characteristic bluish glow. It results from coherent polarization and relaxation of the medium and is emitted on a cone with angle \u03b8 given by cos\u03b8 = c/(n v) (or cos\u03b8 = 1/(\u03b2 n)), where v is the particle speed and n the refractive index."},{"id":"c143add6-7561-450c-a6ca-a0ecfe981566","l":"Chern insulators","d":2,"p":"c9fb88dd-03ca-453d-b78c-5b9bb956df7f","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A Chern insulator is a two-dimensional band insulator whose occupied bands carry a nonzero Chern number \u2014 a topological invariant given by the Brillouin\u2011zone integral of the Berry curvature. This band topology (which requires broken time\u2011reversal symmetry) produces a quantized Hall conductance and robust chiral edge states without an external magnetic field (the quantum anomalous Hall effect)."},{"id":"ab682263-0d4d-4bb9-b696-56a6f2beae3e","l":"Chern number","d":2,"p":"9f7a4cce-0ded-4976-b8ad-cc75830297fa","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Chern number is an integer-valued topological invariant of a vector (or Bloch) bundle over a closed parameter space that measures the net \"twisting\" of the Berry connection. It equals (1/2\u03c0) times the integral of the Berry curvature over that manifold and determines quantized responses such as the integer quantum Hall conductance."},{"id":"9b6febed-f5fb-4a43-b464-24ac9b0b6dd3","l":"Chern-Simons gauge theory","d":2,"p":"a1083ad2-a154-449d-a9ff-59bf46db1c7f","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Chern-Simons gauge theory is a topological quantum field theory in (odd) three-dimensional spacetime whose action is the Chern-Simons 3-form built from a gauge connection A, typically written S_CS = (k/4\u03c0) \u222b Tr(A\u2227dA + (2/3)A\u2227A\u2227A), giving metric-independent bulk dynamics. Its quantized coupling (level k) yields integer-valued topological observables, underlies anyons and fractional statistics, computes knot and link invariants, and induces chiral Wess\u2013Zumino\u2013Witten edge theories on boundaries."},{"id":"7972e0ba-1a34-474c-afd4-5446c54d1b1e","l":"Chiral perturbation theory","d":2,"p":"74f884e0-6764-4962-a081-b198cca267c3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Chiral perturbation theory is the low-energy effective field theory of quantum chromodynamics that describes the interactions of the light pseudo\u2011Goldstone bosons (pions, kaons, eta) produced by spontaneous chiral symmetry breaking.  It organizes amplitudes as a systematic expansion in small momenta and light quark masses (divided by the chiral symmetry breaking scale), with a finite set of low\u2011energy constants encoding short\u2011distance QCD effects."},{"id":"db17b5e9-6a3a-4396-b68c-6e98e8f2e7ab","l":"Chiral symmetry","d":2,"p":"543d3af2-a8bc-462b-9d72-753b6de0d8ce","fl":["Properties","Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Chiral symmetry is the invariance of a fermionic theory under independent transformations of its left- and right\u2011handed (chirality) components, which coincide with helicity for massless particles. It is exact only for massless fermions and is broken explicitly by mass terms and often spontaneously (as in QCD), with spontaneous breaking producing light Nambu\u2013Goldstone modes such as the pions."},{"id":"4b5814b1-aa19-4b24-b8a8-9efb0e779159","l":"Chloroplasts","d":2,"p":"9ccf11df-5b72-4ce0-92e6-e48f8dda172b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Chloroplasts are membrane-bound organelles in plant and algal cells that capture light and convert it into chemical energy via photosynthesis. They contain chlorophyll in stacked thylakoid membranes where photon absorption drives electron transport and the production of ATP and NADPH used to fix carbon in the stroma."},{"id":"9086c67d-df7f-4f9f-b301-4369e5bf0c0f","l":"Chromatin","d":2,"p":"6c0e8bb5-a5a0-4f28-8d4a-4e7ace85a023","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Chromatin is the DNA\u2013protein polymer that compacts and organizes the eukaryotic genome in the nucleus, built from DNA wrapped around histone protein cores (nucleosomes) and folded into higher\u2011order structures. Its dynamic, hierarchical architecture controls access to genetic information and determines the mechanical and rheological properties of the nucleus."},{"id":"3cf17297-6021-46a2-ab57-634c174a823d","l":"Chromonic liquid crystals","d":2,"p":"b2a30868-6c3c-4e85-a4d9-73456ef016dc","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Chromonic liquid crystals are a class of lyotropic liquid crystals in which plank-like aromatic molecules (dyes, pigments or similar small molecules) stack face-to-face in water to form polydisperse columnar aggregates that align to give nematic or columnar liquid-crystalline phases. Their self-assembly and ordering are driven mainly by \u03c0\u2013\u03c0 stacking and hydrophobic/hydrophilic interactions and are strongly concentration- and temperature-dependent and reversible."},{"id":"9aeb9026-d61b-4725-9efb-58840a85a4b2","l":"Chromosomes","d":2,"p":"9ccf11df-5b72-4ce0-92e6-e48f8dda172b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Chromosomes are organized structures of long DNA molecules wrapped around histone and other proteins that package and carry an organism's genetic information. They compact and organize the genome to enable replication and faithful segregation during the cell cycle and exhibit mechanical, topological, and dynamic properties (e.g., stiffness, supercoiling, and phase behavior) studied in biophysics."},{"id":"ee3e7596-d302-4ea8-8907-e66ce139b203","l":"Cilia","d":2,"p":"9ccf11df-5b72-4ce0-92e6-e48f8dda172b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Cilia are slender, hairlike cellular appendages that produce rhythmic, motor-driven beating to generate fluid flow or propulsion at microscopic (low Reynolds number) scales. In physics they are modeled as active, flexible filaments whose hydrodynamic interactions and internal forcing can lead to propulsion, synchronization, and metachronal waves."},{"id":"76111435-ea0c-45ec-a78a-e736f8e58f2d","l":"Circadian rhythms","d":2,"p":"bb9d205a-1327-40a1-a9f3-91d677ea5de3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Circadian rhythms are endogenous, approximately 24-hour biological oscillations in behavior, physiology, and gene expression that persist without external cues. They arise from molecular transcription\u2013translation feedback loops and are well described as self-sustained nonlinear (limit-cycle) oscillators that entrain to external time cues (zeitgebers) like light and show temperature compensation."},{"id":"34c652fc-c268-4339-868f-508852ef3d9d","l":"Circuit quantum electrodynamics","d":2,"p":"632f74d1-d16b-4b92-a295-b24729a93d8d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Circuit quantum electrodynamics (cQED) is the study of quantum interactions between microwave photons confined in on\u2011chip superconducting resonators and engineered nonlinear electrical circuits (artificial atoms or qubits, typically Josephson\u2011junction devices). It implements cavity QED physics in electrical circuits to achieve strong light\u2013matter coupling for quantum control, readout, and information processing."},{"id":"f53811b7-7f5c-46d6-ab05-ae38aa1a2ab6","l":"Circular accelerators","d":2,"p":"226db421-ad24-4e1c-9fbf-5de508711fa2","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Circular accelerators are machines that accelerate charged particles around a closed loop by repeatedly applying electric fields (RF cavities) to raise their energy while magnetic fields (bending and focusing magnets) steer and confine the beam. By recycling the same accelerating structures on multiple turns, they can reach much higher particle energies than single-pass devices (examples: cyclotrons, synchrotrons, storage rings)."},{"id":"421e122f-4752-421c-8af6-88e0df808bb2","l":"Classical black holes","d":2,"p":"6287ac05-d956-4c3c-9882-8ea7a798f964","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Classical black holes are solutions of Einstein\u2019s general relativity describing regions of spacetime whose gravity is so strong that nothing, not even light, can escape\u2014this is enforced by an event horizon that causally isolates an interior (often containing a curvature singularity) from the exterior. In the classical (non\u2011quantum) theory they are characterized by a few global parameters (mass, angular momentum, electric charge) and satisfy uniqueness/no\u2011hair properties."},{"id":"8359ab1d-c3e9-4d53-bf7f-702075aaa8b0","l":"Classical electromagnetism","d":2,"p":"82f6c5fd-bbef-4ff9-a5f9-de62d3f5dfeb","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Classical electromagnetism is the field theory that describes electric and magnetic fields and their interactions with charges and currents, governed by Maxwell's equations and the Lorentz force law. It predicts electrostatics, magnetostatics, electromagnetic waves (including light), and is valid in the non\u2011quantum, continuum limit consistent with special relativity."},{"id":"8643591e-13b1-43a9-87c8-1d16fcfce10a","l":"Clathrates","d":2,"p":"84d35b4d-ab71-42a6-89e7-f25e73d8401f","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Clathrates are crystalline inclusion compounds in which a host lattice forms cage-like cavities that physically trap guest molecules without forming strong chemical bonds. A common example is gas hydrates, where a hydrogen-bonded water framework encloses small gas molecules (e.g., methane) stabilized by van der Waals interactions."},{"id":"837143cb-a746-4ccd-977d-774bda10dfd7","l":"Climate networks","d":2,"p":"6ec86497-6eda-4672-9845-336e089a5f69","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks","Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Climate networks are complex networks built from climate model or observational time series in which nodes represent spatial locations or climate variables and links encode statistical or causal dependencies (e.g., correlations, mutual information) between their dynamics. They provide a graph-theoretic framework to identify teleconnections, dominant dynamical modes, propagation pathways, and changes in connectivity relevant to climate variability and predictability."},{"id":"2c0e953d-44fd-4611-bfb0-3e39cfb9374a","l":"Coherent population trapping","d":2,"p":"70cba0b1-a5ec-474b-a023-2ab4c3e484b9","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Coherent population trapping is a quantum-interference effect in which an atom or molecule driven by two coherent optical fields is driven into a specific superposition of long-lived lower states (a \"dark state\") that is decoupled from the excited state, so the system no longer absorbs light. This produces suppressed excitation and fluorescence and preserves ground-state coherence, underpinning phenomena such as electromagnetically induced transparency and precision atomic clocks."},{"id":"cd61697a-a695-4144-851a-c1650b403794","l":"Coherent Raman electron spin resonance spectroscopy","d":2,"p":"62b17a2f-3001-4254-982f-0f01726f510b","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Coherent Raman electron spin resonance spectroscopy is a technique that uses phase-stable (two\u2011photon) Raman optical fields to drive and probe electron spin transitions, replacing or supplementing conventional microwave ESR with coherent optical control. By inducing and detecting Raman coherence between spin sublevels (typically split by a magnetic field), it provides high-resolution, optically addressable measurements of spin resonance and coherence."},{"id":"accb9505-d443-48a9-9ed2-0c637e798ef4","l":"Cold working","d":2,"p":"c4ed35b0-1090-409c-9667-85f9ac03e9c0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Cold working is the plastic deformation of a metal performed at temperatures below its recrystallization point (often at or near room temperature) by processes such as rolling, drawing, bending, or stamping. It increases dislocation density and produces strain (work) hardening, raising strength and hardness while reducing ductility."},{"id":"3cbbf1f7-9427-4812-a34b-410f229c0987","l":"Collective behavior models","d":2,"p":"6f3f5cf1-363e-4d04-ac63-ca4ae90b47fc","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Collective behavior models are theoretical and computational frameworks that describe how interactions among many individual agents or degrees of freedom give rise to emergent, system-level phenomena such as flocking, swarming, synchronization, pattern formation, and collective phase transitions. They employ tools from statistical physics, nonlinear dynamics, and agent-based simulations to identify order parameters, scaling laws, and universal behaviors."},{"id":"8c0fd3e1-467c-4feb-b802-905ba78daf8d","l":"Collective effects in clusters","d":2,"p":"cac7f673-4cd1-47b8-9de3-9f9785aea585","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Collective effects in clusters are emergent phenomena that arise when the constituents of a finite aggregate (atoms, molecules, or nanoparticles) interact coherently, producing system-wide behavior not present in isolated particles. Examples include collective electronic excitations (plasmons), cooperative magnetism, collective vibrational modes, and size-dependent changes in optical, electronic, or chemical properties."},{"id":"6efc577b-d290-4ed4-9279-77439ebc2d32","l":"Collective effects in quantum optics","d":2,"p":"632f74d1-d16b-4b92-a295-b24729a93d8d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Collective effects in quantum optics are phenomena that arise when many quantum emitters (atoms, molecules, or qubits) interact coherently via a shared electromagnetic field or mutual dipole\u2013dipole couplings, so their joint optical response differs qualitatively from the sum of independent emitters. Typical manifestations include superradiance and subradiance (enhanced or suppressed emission rates), cooperative frequency shifts, collective strong coupling and the formation of polaritonic modes."},{"id":"7b46c9f2-5627-4b1a-bba7-7cf74c4d4861","l":"Colliders","d":2,"p":"226db421-ad24-4e1c-9fbf-5de508711fa2","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Colliders are particle accelerators that steer two beams of particles to collide head-on so researchers can probe fundamental forces and create or study short-lived, high-mass particles at high center-of-mass energies. Detectors surrounding the collision points record the outgoing particles, allowing measurements of interaction rates, particle properties, and rare processes."},{"id":"23eefde2-f8e3-4637-a1e3-6442c5042e1e","l":"Color centers","d":2,"p":"7060bde3-9f16-411d-9d68-3328cf14b019","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Color centers are localized defects in a crystal lattice that trap electrons or holes and introduce discrete electronic states inside the band gap, giving rise to absorption of visible light and hence coloration. A common example is the F-center, an anion vacancy with a trapped electron, which produces characteristic optical transitions and altered electronic/optical properties."},{"id":"0b2e2d12-fe25-4c67-8086-edfad1177ee7","l":"Color deconfinement","d":2,"p":"b9110fa6-0c37-4c22-b39b-e90d8cacdc6f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Color deconfinement is the transition in quantum chromodynamics where color-charged quarks and gluons are no longer bound into color-neutral hadrons but form a deconfined medium (quark\u2013gluon plasma), enabled by color screening at sufficiently high temperature or baryon density."},{"id":"422f8f51-5fe3-4604-9ed3-5188e50a9d54","l":"Comb polymers","d":2,"p":"2303d382-65bb-4b93-94b8-62a73502ec4b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Comb polymers are branched macromolecules with a linear backbone onto which many side chains (grafts) are attached at regular intervals, giving a characteristic \"comb-like\" architecture. Their conformations and mechanical/rheological properties depend strongly on side\u2011chain length and grafting density, bridging the behavior between linear and highly branched (bottlebrush) polymers."},{"id":"551a7b72-2a25-49e4-be10-843849f6987d","l":"Communication networks","d":2,"p":"96ee91b1-3ba0-47ad-b628-6a89155e08e7","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Communication networks are systems of interconnected nodes and physical channels that exchange information-carrying signals according to routing and protocol rules. Their performance is governed by physical-layer constraints\u2014signal propagation, bandwidth, noise, and interference\u2014and by higher-level metrics such as capacity, latency, reliability, and topology."},{"id":"577daad3-7183-4f0b-9a5e-11bcd10276ef","l":"Compaction","d":2,"p":"c4ed35b0-1090-409c-9667-85f9ac03e9c0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Compaction is the process by which a material's volume decreases and its bulk density increases due to applied stress or other forcing that closes pore space, expels fluids, and causes grain rearrangement or deformation. It commonly describes densification of soils, powders, or sediments under compression, reducing porosity and increasing mechanical contact between particles."},{"id":"649554e5-8b05-415c-af99-96e13e9ad56a","l":"Compartmentalization","d":2,"p":"c2ea9e2c-0e15-49ee-b336-111976b0b965","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Compartmentalization is the division of a physical system into distinct regions or subsystems separated by boundaries that restrict the transfer of mass, energy, charge, or information, allowing different conditions or dynamics to be maintained in each region. It is used to sustain gradients, localize processes, reduce coupling between degrees of freedom, and enable emergent or controlled behavior."},{"id":"576f54ed-9a8f-4165-ba52-e5256ace1d1f","l":"Composite bosons","d":2,"p":"71e104e2-448f-42ec-a9be-bbaec6758896","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Composite bosons are bound states whose total spin is an integer (often formed from an even number of fermions), so they can occupy the same quantum state and largely obey Bose statistics. Because they have internal structure, their bosonic behavior is exact only in the long-wavelength/low-density limit and can show corrections from constituent exchange or overlap."},{"id":"e16ba2d3-fcb1-4f43-83df-7a5d527b9fe5","l":"Composite fermions","d":2,"p":"4fcc035e-7eb4-46f6-b347-5cc975e5ed95","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Composite fermions are emergent quasiparticles formed when electrons in a two-dimensional electron gas bind an even number of magnetic flux quanta (vortices), so they still obey Fermi statistics but feel a reduced effective magnetic field. This transformation maps interacting-electron fractional quantum Hall states onto integer quantum Hall states of composite fermions, explaining the observed fractional filling sequence."},{"id":"0d2c7741-1686-49bd-a316-206b1a8aca88","l":"Compressed air energy storage","d":2,"p":"7f29b291-aab9-4d63-a15c-1b98193cc0ca","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Compressed air energy storage (CAES) is a grid-scale energy storage method that stores electrical energy by using it to compress air into a high-pressure vessel or underground cavern, then later releases the stored air to expand through turbines to produce electricity. Its round-trip efficiency and capacity depend on storage pressure and thermal management (whether heat from compression is stored and reused or lost)."},{"id":"6f202a0d-dce9-4018-930c-69bdd8eccccd","l":"Compton scattering","d":2,"p":"3123cbf9-460d-467a-9306-b4fb316daddd","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Compton scattering is the inelastic collision of a photon with a (typically free) electron in which the photon transfers part of its energy and momentum to the electron and emerges with a longer wavelength. The amount of wavelength shift depends on the scattering angle and is described by the Compton formula, demonstrating the particle-like behavior of light."},{"id":"b846c92f-9322-4fb3-bbe6-a46a834cd7a0","l":"Conformal symmetry","d":2,"p":"543d3af2-a8bc-462b-9d72-753b6de0d8ce","fl":["Properties","Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Conformal symmetry is the invariance of a physical system under transformations that locally rescale the metric (x \u2192 x'(x) with g' = \u03a9(x)^2 g), preserving angles and the light-cone/causal structure but not absolute distances. In field theory it extends the Poincar\u00e9 symmetries by including dilations and special conformal transformations, imposing strong constraints on correlation functions and dynamics."},{"id":"b400a499-6ed8-4f65-8c61-4394a6be50d7","l":"Continuous percolation transition","d":2,"p":"08ee99a2-4964-4690-8cd4-213bbba951b5","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"A continuous percolation transition is a second-order phase transition in which, as the occupation probability or density reaches a critical value p_c, a system-spanning (infinite) cluster appears continuously and the fraction of sites in that cluster (the percolation order parameter) grows from zero. At p_c the correlation length diverges and observables show power-law scaling governed by universal critical exponents."},{"id":"543d3af2-a8bc-462b-9d72-753b6de0d8ce","l":"Continuous symmetries in condensed matter","d":2,"p":"eac8c4d5-07af-40a1-92d6-f32abca831a1","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Continuous symmetries in condensed matter are invariances of a system under transformations labeled by continuous parameters (e.g., spatial translations or rotations, and internal phase rotations like U(1)). Such symmetries imply conserved quantities via Noether's theorem, and when they are spontaneously broken by an ordered phase they produce gapless collective excitations (Goldstone modes) and characteristic topological defects."},{"id":"fec72dfb-0bb2-402a-810a-e515ffeaff8f","l":"Continuous-time random walk","d":2,"p":"b52b4f11-cad6-4cea-997f-36a603349fea","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"A continuous-time random walk (CTRW) is a renewal stochastic process in which a particle makes instantaneous spatial jumps at random times, with the waiting times between jumps and the jump lengths drawn from prescribed probability distributions. Non-exponential waiting-time or heavy-tailed step-size distributions can produce anomalous transport (sub- or superdiffusion) and, under scaling limits, give rise to fractional kinetic or generalized diffusion equations."},{"id":"4df8c87e-3d2c-4f9b-a45e-09913597d30e","l":"Cooper pairs","d":2,"p":"71e104e2-448f-42ec-a9be-bbaec6758896","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Cooper pairs are bound pairs of fermions (typically electrons) in a superconductor that form because an effective attractive interaction\u2014usually mediated by lattice vibrations (phonons)\u2014overcomes their Coulomb repulsion. These opposite-momentum, opposite-spin pairs behave coherently like bosons, condense into a collective ground state, and produce the superconducting energy gap and zero-resistance flow."},{"id":"425b5934-1208-4936-8029-7c7ca7201bce","l":"Core of giant planets","d":2,"p":"ea69605a-7cae-476a-a7da-28f3c186d709","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"The core of a giant planet is the central, highest-density region enriched in heavy elements (rocks, ices, and metals) that may exist as a compact solid or molten body or as a more diffuse \u201cfuzzy\u201d gradient mixed into the hydrogen\u2013helium envelope. Its mass, composition, and structure control the planet\u2019s interior density profile, gravitational moments, thermal evolution, and record of formation processes."},{"id":"00fd1487-ee50-4ab6-97bc-843eb91e8969","l":"Core-shell nanoparticles","d":2,"p":"4fd30a9d-7de5-44a0-82dd-d4b18d88721b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Core\u2013shell nanoparticles are nanoscale particles composed of an inner \"core\" material encapsulated by an outer \"shell\" of a different composition or phase, creating a distinct core\u2013shell interface. This architecture lets one independently tune size-dependent and surface properties\u2014optical, electronic, magnetic, chemical or thermal\u2014improving stability and enabling multifunctional behavior."},{"id":"df7aaedb-37e5-4c51-8423-d343a656c4d4","l":"Coronal loops","d":2,"p":"f712db32-2bc9-4e97-9f42-a16c12832ff8","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Coronal loops are arching magnetic flux tubes in the Sun\u2019s corona that trap and guide hot, ionized plasma, appearing as luminous loop-shaped structures in extreme-ultraviolet and X\u2011ray images and anchored in the photosphere. They trace the coronal magnetic-field topology and are primary sites of coronal heating, plasma flows, and dynamic activity such as flares."},{"id":"98d83e9f-21b5-47ee-9a00-a11b12bad90d","l":"Coronal mass ejections","d":2,"p":"f712db32-2bc9-4e97-9f42-a16c12832ff8","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Coronal mass ejections are large-scale eruptions from the Sun's corona that expel billions of tons of magnetized plasma and embedded magnetic fields into the heliosphere at speeds from hundreds to thousands of km/s. They result from destabilization or reconnection of stressed coronal magnetic fields (often involving flux-rope eruptions) and are major drivers of space weather and geomagnetic storms at Earth."},{"id":"83c417d5-efde-4d0b-82e9-997767f34217","l":"Correlated percolation","d":2,"p":"7f613135-db34-40bd-b19e-e520cf4b29f4","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Correlated percolation refers to percolation models in which the occupancy of sites or bonds is not independent but exhibits spatial correlations, so nearby occupation probabilities are statistically related. These correlations\u2014which can arise from interactions, disorder, or underlying fields\u2014change connectivity properties, percolation thresholds, and critical exponents compared with uncorrelated (Bernoulli) percolation."},{"id":"6c869c72-2ced-4e20-957d-a15a0cb6b969","l":"Cosmological constant","d":2,"p":"adf8950c-3d99-4e88-b7f3-76ecf2764c1e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"The cosmological constant, \u039b, is a constant term added to Einstein's field equations that represents a uniform vacuum energy density filling space and producing a constant negative pressure.  Physically it acts like the simplest form of dark energy (equation of state w = \u22121) and drives the accelerated expansion of the universe."},{"id":"ddef0ddc-6f3d-4d34-93c8-5c52964a1704","l":"CP symmetry","d":2,"p":"d0171af5-5603-4934-b23f-d95de0287b81","fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"CP symmetry is the combined operation of charge conjugation (C), which swaps particles with their antiparticles, and parity (P), which inverts spatial coordinates; a physical law is CP-symmetric if it is unchanged under that combined transformation. Many interactions (electromagnetic and strong) respect CP, but the weak interaction violates it\u2014a fact important for understanding the matter\u2013antimatter asymmetry of the universe."},{"id":"5e2e8380-514f-47f9-9cf8-0724e0351d6f","l":"CPT symmetry","d":2,"p":"d0171af5-5603-4934-b23f-d95de0287b81","fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"CPT symmetry is the invariance of a physical system under the combined operations of charge conjugation (swapping particles with antiparticles), parity (spatial inversion), and time reversal. The CPT theorem states that any local, Lorentz\u2011invariant quantum field theory with a Hermitian Hamiltonian must be invariant under this combined transformation, which implies, for example, equal masses and lifetimes for particles and their antiparticles."},{"id":"1bb2c49c-6855-405c-a5ac-4ef561c06162","l":"Cratering","d":2,"p":"42ec6252-ac7f-42bd-9ddc-08ac45a4b4e9","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Cratering is the formation of a depression in a surface caused by a high\u2011energy impact or explosion, produced by rapid excavation and ejection of target material followed by collapse and modification. Crater size and morphology depend on impact energy, angle, projectile and target properties (strength, porosity) and the local gravity field."},{"id":"deacc7ae-a243-460f-8072-56d5fd610cf3","l":"Creasing","d":2,"p":"42ec6252-ac7f-42bd-9ddc-08ac45a4b4e9","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Creasing is the nonlinear instability in a compressed surface or thin elastic layer that produces a sharp, localized fold where the material locally self-contacts, concentrating strain and stress. Unlike extended wrinkling, creasing is a highly localized singular deformation that appears once compressive strain exceeds a critical threshold (common in soft solids and thin films)."},{"id":"9f34773c-dd98-42cd-b96c-d310a9fddb2a","l":"Creep","d":2,"p":"77d491f8-55ad-47a6-a0a7-cd24ffce7602","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Creep is the slow, time-dependent, and usually permanent deformation of a solid under a sustained load or stress, often significant at high temperatures or over long times even when the applied stress is below the short-term yield strength. It arises from mechanisms such as diffusion, dislocation motion and grain-boundary sliding and typically shows primary (decelerating), secondary (steady-state) and tertiary (accelerating to failure) stages."},{"id":"5c05a702-8101-4aee-8dc6-4fd1a1aaa3a1","l":"Critical exponents","d":2,"p":"6faac3e6-cda0-43ca-be0f-4ea65fd16e81","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Networks","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Critical exponents are numbers that characterize the power-law behavior of thermodynamic and correlation functions near a continuous (second-order) phase transition \u2014 for example \u03be \u221d |T\u2212Tc|^{-\u03bd}, M \u221d |T\u2212Tc|^{\u03b2}, \u03c7 \u221d |T\u2212Tc|^{-\u03b3}, and C \u221d |T\u2212Tc|^{-\u03b1}. They are universal (depend only on symmetry and dimensionality, not microscopic details) and obey scaling relations that follow from the renormalization-group description of the critical point."},{"id":"61236974-2afd-45b8-9a5b-de81ff1f6c73","l":"Critical field","d":2,"p":"6faac3e6-cda0-43ca-be0f-4ea65fd16e81","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Networks","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Critical field: the magnitude of an applied external field (commonly magnetic or electric) at which a material undergoes a qualitative change or phase transition, for example the magnetic field Hc that destroys superconductivity and restores the normal state. The critical field typically depends on temperature and other thermodynamic variables and marks a boundary in the field\u2013temperature phase diagram."},{"id":"0c82313d-26e8-4119-85be-70b2a13c9a05","l":"Cross-linked polymers","d":2,"p":"2303d382-65bb-4b93-94b8-62a73502ec4b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Cross-linked polymers are materials in which individual polymer chains are joined at many junction points by covalent or strong physical bonds, forming a three-dimensional network. This network restricts chain mobility and gives the material enhanced mechanical strength, elasticity and resistance to melting or dissolution compared with linear polymers."},{"id":"680a4bac-3ffc-40d8-b936-2a3178d5f253","l":"Cross-phase modulation","d":2,"p":"bd03ea46-2406-4a24-8307-05b2b498fb4e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Cross-phase modulation is a third-order (\u03c7(3)) nonlinear optical effect in which the intensity of one light beam changes a medium\u2019s refractive index via the optical Kerr effect, thereby imposing an intensity-dependent phase shift on a different co-propagating beam. It is the inter-beam counterpart to self-phase modulation and can produce spectral broadening and cross-talk in guided-wave and fiber systems."},{"id":"35982b83-a2dc-42b2-8f56-84140939b6fa","l":"Crystal-field theory","d":2,"p":"3817eab7-2f24-46f8-ba4a-aa6433722b4a","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Crystal-field theory is a model that describes how the electrostatic field produced by surrounding ions or ligands breaks the degeneracy of an atom's orbitals\u2014especially the d orbitals of transition-metal ions\u2014producing characteristic energy splittings (e.g., t2g and eg in an octahedral field) that control optical, magnetic and spectroscopic behavior. It is an ionic, point-charge (or point-dipole) approximation that emphasizes electrostatic splitting (parameterized by \u0394) and neglects covalent metal\u2013ligand bonding."},{"id":"ecbbe074-7884-45a4-aa5c-d38b87af2514","l":"Crystal optics","d":2,"p":"dcf48409-af3f-4517-b58d-7ec6c95351f3","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Crystal optics is the study of how electromagnetic waves propagate through crystalline solids whose dielectric response is anisotropic and determined by the crystal symmetry. This anisotropy produces direction- and polarization-dependent refractive indices and phenomena such as birefringence, optical activity, and dispersion, which are described mathematically by the dielectric (or permittivity) tensor."},{"id":"8bd4e826-f5c0-46b1-9816-c43637b637a0","l":"C-symmetry","d":2,"p":"d0171af5-5603-4934-b23f-d95de0287b81","fl":["Properties","Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"C-symmetry, or charge-conjugation symmetry, is the discrete symmetry that replaces every particle by its antiparticle, reversing additive internal quantum numbers (e.g., electric charge, baryon/lepton number) and acting on fields via a charge-conjugation operator C. States that are unchanged or change sign under C are called C-even or C-odd, and this symmetry is exact in some interactions but violated by the weak interaction."},{"id":"98b74b27-7d20-4548-bf46-c1050ccfce37","l":"Cyclotron resonance","d":2,"p":"6fab7f86-2de1-4f52-9425-ed4f9aec008b","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Cyclotron resonance is the resonant absorption of electromagnetic radiation by charged particles in a magnetic field that occurs when the drive frequency matches the particle's cyclotron frequency, \u03c9_c = |q|B/m (or \u03c9_c = |q|B/m* in a crystal). It causes coherent circular motion and energy uptake and is widely used to measure carrier effective masses and scattering in plasmas and solids."},{"id":"fc03ac93-c0a9-421f-bb28-9478d611e24b","l":"Cyclotrons","d":2,"p":"4eec2798-da70-4fc2-8371-f6aa5f6180d7","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"A cyclotron is a circular particle accelerator that uses a uniform magnetic field to bend charged particles into a spiral path while an alternating (radio\u2011frequency) electric field applied across two D\u2011shaped electrodes (\"dees\") accelerates them each time they cross the gap. Nonrelativistically the particles remain in phase with the RF because the cyclotron frequency eB/m is constant, but relativistic mass increase breaks this resonance and limits the maximum energy unless modified designs (synchrocyclotrons, isochronous cyclotrons) are used."},{"id":"48635f62-5954-4923-a269-11b38dc8fc38","l":"Cytoskeleton","d":2,"p":"9ccf11df-5b72-4ce0-92e6-e48f8dda172b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"The cytoskeleton is a dynamic, intracellular network of protein filaments (primarily actin filaments, microtubules, and intermediate filaments) and associated proteins that gives cells shape, mechanical integrity, and the ability to generate and transmit forces. It behaves as an active, viscoelastic material whose polymerization dynamics and motor proteins drive processes such as intracellular transport, shape changes, and cell motility."},{"id":"135c6e79-d0ab-4747-8017-02aabae979b4","l":"Damping rings","d":2,"p":"f8f21b09-696c-40cb-a4d6-b7f8505fba32","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Damping rings are compact storage rings used in accelerator facilities to reduce the transverse and longitudinal emittance (beam size and divergence) of charged-particle bunches before injection into downstream accelerators. They do this by making the beam emit synchrotron radiation\u2014often enhanced with wigglers\u2014which damps particle oscillations while quantum excitation and RF re-acceleration set the final equilibrium emittance."},{"id":"1a24a4da-0abc-40c1-a76f-6452a2f4a87a","l":"Dark matter detectors","d":2,"p":"b144b8b6-c6a6-4178-81b5-8f63f6e17c20","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Dark matter detectors are instruments and experiments designed to find evidence of dark matter by measuring the tiny signals it would produce, either from direct interactions with detector targets or from secondary particles produced by its annihilation, decay, or production. They encompass laboratory setups (e.g., underground cryogenic detectors, liquid-noble time-projection chambers, bubble chambers, axion haloscopes), astrophysical telescopes for indirect searches (gamma rays, cosmic rays, neutrinos), and collider experiments that look for missing-energy signatures."},{"id":"a3016498-5216-44dd-a664-cfcec9fba0d1","l":"Dark matter direct detection","d":2,"p":"83481d17-bac2-4c24-b875-f19596028e90","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Dark matter direct detection refers to laboratory experiments that search for dark-matter particles from the Galactic halo by measuring the tiny, rare non-gravitational interactions they may have with detector target materials (e.g., nuclear or electron recoils). These searches use ultra-low-background, often underground detectors and look for characteristic signals such as recoil energy spectra, annual modulation, or directional anisotropy to distinguish signal from backgrounds."},{"id":"70c6fe98-6825-4fea-b015-c9df3d548c41","l":"Data mining","d":2,"p":"45a22032-e27f-4922-9d0c-10c4aa294db5","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Data mining is the automated extraction of useful patterns, correlations, and models from large datasets using statistical, machine-learning, and visualization techniques. In physics it is applied to experimental, observational, and simulation data to reveal underlying relationships, classify events, estimate parameters, and detect anomalies."},{"id":"3360d7a6-4446-4cc8-908f-a14751c31904","l":"Deep inelastic scattering","d":2,"p":"83c64883-5507-43f8-92bc-ae9f5ae03f02","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Deep inelastic scattering is the high-momentum-transfer collision of a lepton (or other probe) with a nucleon that breaks the target apart, producing a multi-particle hadronic final state. Because the momentum transfer Q^2 is large, the probe resolves the nucleon\u2019s pointlike constituents (partons), and measurements of structure functions as functions of Bjorken x and Q^2 reveal the parton distribution and test QCD."},{"id":"1861ce92-698d-456f-9af8-caa203f7ef1f","l":"Deep level transient spectroscopy","d":2,"p":"675773f0-6ff3-44de-8600-2e9034eea9f0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Deep level transient spectroscopy (DLTS) is an electrical characterization technique that detects and quantifies deep electronic trap states (defects) in the band gap of semiconductors by applying voltage or current pulses and measuring the resulting transient capacitance or current as trapped carriers are thermally emitted. By sweeping temperature and analyzing the emission-rate vs temperature (Arrhenius) behavior, DLTS yields trap activation energies, capture cross sections, and concentrations."},{"id":"224de10e-d45e-46fa-a05c-26636294752a","l":"Deep potential molecular dynamics","d":2,"p":"7e2374d5-2769-48f1-bd04-1d8f3a57e280","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Deep potential molecular dynamics is an atomistic simulation approach that uses deep neural-network interatomic potentials\u2014trained on quantum-mechanical (e.g., DFT) energies and forces\u2014to run molecular dynamics with near\u2013ab initio accuracy at much lower computational cost. These models enforce physical symmetries (translational, rotational, and permutational invariance) and encode local atomic environments so they can predict energies and forces for large, complex systems efficiently."},{"id":"4de39c8b-2d2b-4442-8c91-dda9c5c485ab","l":"Degenerative diseases","d":2,"p":"bbcb2483-b22c-4bb6-bb81-beb53ff1b1ed","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Degenerative diseases are conditions characterized by progressive, often irreversible, deterioration of cells, tissues, or organs that leads to a gradual loss of physiological function. They result from processes such as aging, genetic defects, protein misfolding, chronic inflammation, or cumulative mechanical/metabolic damage and include disorders like Alzheimer's, Parkinson's, osteoarthritis, and macular degeneration."},{"id":"2f031f28-5911-407e-8bce-149e70acf79b","l":"de Haas-van Alphen effect","d":2,"p":"3e0549b8-0105-4383-84a3-ba1f47652af0","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The de Haas\u2013van Alphen effect is the quantum oscillation of a metal's magnetization (or magnetic susceptibility) as a function of applied magnetic field, produced by Landau quantization of conduction-electron orbits and manifesting as oscillations periodic in 1/B. The oscillation frequencies are proportional to extremal cross-sectional areas of the Fermi surface (Onsager relation), so the effect is a precise probe of Fermi-surface geometry at low temperatures and high fields."},{"id":"442252b5-0145-46b7-a431-94f14e299497","l":"Dementia","d":2,"p":"bbcb2483-b22c-4bb6-bb81-beb53ff1b1ed","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Dementia is a clinical syndrome marked by progressive impairment of cognitive functions \u2014 memory, reasoning, language and visuospatial skills \u2014 severe enough to interfere with daily life. It reflects underlying brain damage from disorders such as Alzheimer\u2019s disease, vascular injury, Lewy body disease, or other neurodegenerative conditions and is not an inevitable part of normal aging."},{"id":"51a3c49f-9312-4b45-a82e-196e38914b95","l":"Demultiplexers","d":2,"p":"2360e96e-a12b-4d21-9b65-36ed1177c0e6","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A demultiplexer is a device that takes a single input signal and routes it to one of several output lines under the control of select (address) inputs, functioning as the inverse of a multiplexer. More generally, demultiplexing refers to separating a combined (multiplexed) signal into its individual channels (e.g., time-, frequency-, or wavelength-division) in communications and optics."},{"id":"6ea5e48b-1224-4d79-be71-3a811099ec33","l":"Dendritic polymers","d":2,"p":"2303d382-65bb-4b93-94b8-62a73502ec4b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Dendritic polymers are a class of macromolecules with a highly branched, tree\u2011like architecture\u2014ranging from regularly constructed dendrimers to more irregular hyperbranched polymers\u2014grown outward from a central core in repeated branching generations. Their controlled nanoscale structure produces a high density of peripheral functional groups and internal cavities, giving distinctive physical properties (size\u2011dependent transport, surface chemistry, and rheology) useful in nanoscience and materials."},{"id":"05d1f0ed-0bf5-4c6d-9e38-a9cf3a67eb5f","l":"Density functional theory","d":2,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Density functional theory (DFT) is a quantum-mechanical method that determines the ground-state properties of many-electron systems by using the electronic density as the fundamental variable instead of the full many-body wavefunction. The Hohenberg\u2013Kohn theorems guarantee a one-to-one mapping between density and ground-state observables, and the practical Kohn\u2013Sham formulation solves an auxiliary noninteracting problem while encoding electron-electron interactions in an approximate exchange\u2013correlation functional."},{"id":"bbc55f73-dd1f-4bb2-becb-af61690c5e03","l":"Density matrix methods","d":2,"p":"05a47dcc-71c0-4ebc-9d4e-79a40a191efb","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Density matrix methods use the density operator \u03c1 to represent both pure and mixed quantum states, allowing calculation of expectation values, probabilities, and correlations when a system is in an ensemble or entangled with an environment. Their dynamics are described by the von Neumann equation for closed systems and by master equations (e.g., Lindblad form) for open systems, with partial traces of \u03c1 giving reduced density matrices that quantify coherence and decoherence."},{"id":"0248af45-8a09-49c5-a9dc-17f4fd86b84a","l":"Density matrix renormalization group","d":2,"p":"50ba3f74-7f31-4857-8a75-b2b007348981","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields","Physics of Living Systems","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The density matrix renormalization group (DMRG) is a numerical variational method for obtaining highly accurate ground states and low-energy properties of one-dimensional and quasi-one-dimensional quantum many-body systems. It iteratively builds and truncates the many-body Hilbert space using the eigenstates of subsystem reduced density matrices to retain the most relevant degrees of freedom, an approach equivalent to optimizing a matrix product state."},{"id":"dab59f4b-8406-411a-a561-927f0145be6b","l":"Depinning transition","d":2,"p":"19f26688-0955-4fd9-85d8-aaf71c4a581f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Depinning transition: a nonequilibrium critical point where a driven object (e.g., an elastic interface, vortex lattice, or charge-density wave) in a disordered medium changes from a pinned state with zero long-time velocity to steady motion once the applied drive exceeds a threshold force. The transition exhibits scale-invariant avalanche dynamics and universal critical scaling of velocity, correlation length, and time near the threshold."},{"id":"5ee0cec3-a09c-483c-a40a-58c7e2edcaf6","l":"Developmental diseases","d":2,"p":"bbcb2483-b22c-4bb6-bb81-beb53ff1b1ed","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Developmental diseases are disorders caused by disruptions of normal growth, differentiation, or patterning during embryonic and early postnatal development, leading to congenital malformations, organ dysfunction, or neurodevelopmental deficits. They arise from genetic mutations, chromosomal or gene-regulatory changes, or environmental insults (e.g., infections, toxins, maternal illness) that perturb the programs controlling tissue and organ formation."},{"id":"ade47d97-1b9e-45c4-a708-8670ea246613","l":"Developmental pattern formation","d":2,"p":"93b8baac-44de-4a26-a175-357ad9d8744c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Developmental pattern formation is the process by which living tissues generate reproducible spatial and temporal organization (e.g., stripes, spots, segments, or cell-type arrangements) during embryogenesis and growth. It arises from the interplay of gene regulatory networks, diffusing morphogens, cell signaling and mechanics, and is analyzed with physical concepts such as reaction\u2013diffusion, symmetry breaking, instabilities, and nonlinear dynamics."},{"id":"ad0f86c8-f5cc-4cea-967e-c24bf848993d","l":"Device fabrication","d":2,"p":"2360e96e-a12b-4d21-9b65-36ed1177c0e6","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Device fabrication is the set of controlled processes used to convert device designs into physical, functional structures by patterning, depositing, removing and modifying materials at micro- to nanometer scales. Typical steps include lithography (optical or electron-beam), thin-film deposition, etching, doping/implantation, annealing and packaging, usually performed in cleanroom environments to achieve the required geometry, materials properties and performance."},{"id":"2b88f89f-3e4e-4b91-b3d1-c8d065e75d39","l":"Devices for digital logic, storage & processing","d":2,"p":"2360e96e-a12b-4d21-9b65-36ed1177c0e6","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Devices for digital logic, storage & processing are physical components that encode information in discrete states (typically binary) and implement Boolean operations, data retention, and computation by manipulating physical carriers such as charge, spin, or photons. Examples include transistors and logic gates for switching and computation, memory elements (flip\u2011flops, capacitors, magnetic or electronic memory) for storage, and integrated processors or specialized hardware (CMOS, spintronic, photonic, superconducting) that carry out processing tasks."},{"id":"b49beab9-dd95-471f-9188-cbf522cb1aa6","l":"Diagrammatic methods","d":2,"p":"05a47dcc-71c0-4ebc-9d4e-79a40a191efb","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Diagrammatic methods are graphical representations of terms in perturbative or combinatorial expansions that encode propagators, interaction vertices, and symmetry factors, allowing one to translate complex algebraic expressions into visual elements with precise rules for conversion back into formulas. They simplify bookkeeping and intuition for computing correlation functions, scattering amplitudes, or partition-function contributions\u2014examples include Feynman diagrams in quantum field theory and cluster/Mayer diagrams in statistical mechanics."},{"id":"b9f9441e-3970-4db6-8c8a-5801cb8dfde7","l":"Dialysis","d":2,"p":"a24a2d8a-58a4-4cbc-81aa-4cd953e73f4b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Dialysis is the transport-driven separation of solvent and small solute molecules through a semipermeable membrane that allows passage of small species but retains larger macromolecules. It proceeds by diffusion and osmotic flows driven by concentration (chemical potential) differences until equilibrium is reached."},{"id":"c63044ed-f3b9-4164-9cb2-5a9ae5f91252","l":"Diamagnetism","d":2,"p":"b6e4cc90-18f3-4427-841d-c667375bbca0","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Diamagnetism is the magnetic response in which an applied magnetic field induces tiny orbital currents in atoms or molecules that produce a magnetic moment opposing the field, giving a weak repulsive effect characterized by negative magnetic susceptibility (\u03c7 < 0). It is a universal property of all materials but is usually observable only when stronger paramagnetic or ferromagnetic contributions are absent."},{"id":"5d6ccda6-4646-44f3-86a2-bc5aabb0dafb","l":"Diamond","d":2,"p":"bfbc8007-4c38-4ccb-a698-eeb79bc1651c","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Diamond is the crystalline allotrope of carbon in which each atom is tetrahedrally bonded (sp3 hybridization) to four neighbors, forming the diamond-cubic lattice. This strong covalent network yields exceptional hardness, very high thermal conductivity, broad optical transparency and a wide electronic band gap (~5.5 eV), making diamond an electrical insulator unless doped."},{"id":"91938f5f-c884-46b3-8928-805b4ae49bfb","l":"Diamond structure","d":2,"p":"6af5be88-489f-4a50-9abf-998e8912254a","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Diamond structure: a cubic crystal lattice made of two interpenetrating face-centered cubic sublattices displaced by (1/4,1/4,1/4) of the cubic cell, so each atom is tetrahedrally coordinated to four nearest neighbors (sp3 bonding), forming a rigid three-dimensional covalent network."},{"id":"c934a16c-9df9-4ff3-a8f6-5e1275191c35","l":"Dicke model","d":2,"p":"cb4dcf82-80bb-4305-94f4-12a11959d840","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"The Dicke model describes N identical two-level atoms (treated as a collective spin) uniformly coupled to a single quantized electromagnetic mode, with a Hamiltonian that captures coherent atom\u2013field interactions.  When the full (non\u2011rotating\u2011wave) coupling is included the model exhibits collective phenomena such as superradiance and a zero\u2011temperature quantum phase transition to a superradiant phase."},{"id":"9c9d58c4-3f0d-415d-9448-784a41c227ad","l":"Dielectric constant measurements","d":2,"p":"675773f0-6ff3-44de-8600-2e9034eea9f0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Dielectric constant measurements determine a material's relative permittivity (the real part of its complex permittivity) by measuring its electrical response\u2014typically via capacitance or impedance techniques\u2014to an applied electric field, often as a function of frequency and temperature. They quantify how the material polarizes to reduce the internal field and how much electric energy is stored or dissipated."},{"id":"0e6cc628-b2ac-4b58-ba5b-d39d0f358cfa","l":"Dielectrics","d":2,"p":"74721827-c4e3-4da0-90ca-dba52158fc6b","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Dielectrics are insulating materials that become electrically polarized in an external electric field, producing bound charges that partially screen and modify the field. Their response is described by the polarization P and macroscopic parameters such as the permittivity (dielectric constant) and electric susceptibility, which determine how they store electrostatic energy."},{"id":"064cb603-a52e-4cdf-aa09-8e86bb3a8a4c","l":"Dielectric spectroscopy","d":2,"p":"62b17a2f-3001-4254-982f-0f01726f510b","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Dielectric spectroscopy is an experimental technique that measures a material's complex permittivity \u03b5*(\u03c9) as a function of frequency by applying oscillating electric fields. It reveals polarization mechanisms, relaxation times, and dielectric loss (including conductivity) across a broad range of timescales and frequencies."},{"id":"52f3dfc6-36cf-4fbd-8706-562f660f4a72","l":"Differential equations","d":2,"p":"b5ff1838-0ecd-41a5-a90b-bae4acb69fcf","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Differential equations are mathematical relations that link an unknown function to its derivatives, describing how a physical quantity changes with respect to one or more independent variables. They provide the framework for most physical laws\u2014ordinary differential equations involve derivatives in a single variable, while partial differential equations involve derivatives in multiple variables and require initial or boundary conditions to specify a unique solution."},{"id":"e946e564-0b5f-4f2a-a1cb-6b7402131e54","l":"Differential thermal analysis","d":2,"p":"c2ea95f8-c209-44d7-b8f7-a6baf632c46a","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Differential thermal analysis (DTA) is a thermal-analysis technique in which the temperature difference between a sample and an inert reference is recorded while both are subjected to a controlled temperature program; deviations in that temperature difference reveal endothermic or exothermic events (phase transitions, reactions, decomposition). It provides characteristic transition temperatures and qualitative or semi-quantitative information about associated heat effects."},{"id":"23f6330c-aba9-4c40-9153-5367f782db3e","l":"Diffractive production","d":2,"p":"97069ede-42dd-4f4d-b552-9fc8562cb02d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Diffractive production is a class of scattering processes in which particles interact via the exchange of a colorless object (e.g., a Pomeron or photon), so that one or both projectiles remain intact or are excited to a low-mass state while new particles are produced. It is characterized by small momentum transfer and large rapidity gaps (regions with no particle production) and is common in high-energy hadron collisions."},{"id":"087a4315-1a37-4e4c-8d3a-0ed8b6f324d0","l":"Diffusion & dynamics of clusters","d":2,"p":"cac7f673-4cd1-47b8-9de3-9f9785aea585","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"The diffusion and dynamics of clusters concern how small aggregates of atoms, molecules, or particles move and evolve in time\u2014including translational and internal rearrangements, coalescence, fragmentation, attachment\u2013detachment, and evaporation\u2013condensation. These processes are driven by thermal fluctuations and interparticle and substrate forces and determine cluster kinetics, stability, and growth pathways (e.g., surface diffusion, Brownian motion, Ostwald ripening)."},{"id":"c09b31e7-3cf9-4092-910c-cdecca6d3a98","l":"Dilute magnetic topological insulators","d":2,"p":"c9fb88dd-03ca-453d-b78c-5b9bb956df7f","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Dilute magnetic topological insulators are topological insulator materials in which a small concentration of magnetic dopants or magnetic proximity induces local magnetic moments that break time-reversal symmetry while the bulk remains insulating. This dilute magnetism gaps the Dirac surface states and can produce phenomena such as the quantum anomalous Hall effect and magnetically driven topological phase transitions."},{"id":"a7525540-a622-46fa-afad-aa9569b2d757","l":"Dilution refrigerator","d":2,"p":"22b17e02-28a0-4b88-ae3b-2520aa22cfb9","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A dilution refrigerator is a cryogenic refrigerator that attains continuous cooling to millikelvin temperatures by circulating a mixture of helium\u20113 and helium\u20114; cooling arises from the enthalpy absorbed when He\u20113 atoms cross the phase boundary and dilute into the He\u20114\u2011rich (dilute) phase."},{"id":"926cb610-c40b-4bd4-b069-14722b7251c7","l":"Diodes","d":2,"p":"2360e96e-a12b-4d21-9b65-36ed1177c0e6","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A diode is a two-terminal semiconductor device\u2014most commonly a p\u2013n junction\u2014that conducts current readily in one direction when forward-biased above a threshold voltage and strongly restricts current when reverse-biased (except for small leakage or breakdown currents). Variants (Zener, Schottky, LEDs, photodiodes) use different materials and structures to provide functions such as voltage regulation, fast switching, light emission, and photodetection."},{"id":"c5abdca0-778e-40cf-9c23-4180a017acb8","l":"Dipolar atoms","d":2,"p":"130b51f2-d186-4d8c-bfd2-24111492cf79","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Atoms that possess a sizable permanent dipole moment (typically a magnetic dipole in species like Cr, Dy, or Er) so that their mutual interactions are dominated by long-range, anisotropic dipole\u2013dipole forces. These 1/r^3 interactions, with angular dependence, strongly affect scattering, collective behavior, and many-body phases in ultracold atomic gases."},{"id":"59538962-3ec2-49e7-a2ee-9c8015f539b0","l":"Dipolar interaction","d":2,"p":"1f68adf2-72ef-4afa-8536-fd5094c2edd5","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Dipolar interaction is the long-range, anisotropic interaction between two electric or magnetic dipoles, arising from the field produced by one dipole acting on the other. Its potential energy scales as 1/r^3 and depends on the magnitudes and relative orientation and position of the dipole moments, producing angle-dependent attraction or repulsion."},{"id":"3c193441-3bab-41e1-b998-edb2d9656482","l":"Dipolar molecules","d":2,"p":"130b51f2-d186-4d8c-bfd2-24111492cf79","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Dipolar molecules are molecules that possess a permanent electric dipole moment\u2014i.e., an asymmetric spatial distribution of charge that gives the molecule distinct positive and negative ends. They interact via long-range, orientation-dependent (anisotropic) dipole\u2013dipole forces, which strongly affect their behavior in gases, liquids, and cold-molecule experiments."},{"id":"95f9eea9-adf3-4f2f-b476-b25a149052fc","l":"Dipolar Rydberg atoms","d":2,"p":"130b51f2-d186-4d8c-bfd2-24111492cf79","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Dipolar Rydberg atoms are atoms excited to very high principal quantum numbers whose electronic states carry large permanent or field-induced electric dipole moments. These dipoles give rise to strong, tunable long-range dipole\u2013dipole interactions (\u221d1/R^3), commonly produced by Stark or state-mixing of Rydberg levels and exploited in quantum information and many-body experiments."},{"id":"dfb220d0-afe4-4d25-aa37-8fce0d06cf9a","l":"Dirac equation","d":2,"p":"ee82ad04-6c47-46a4-a70d-c9a7d7989f6a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology","Particles & Fields"],"def":"The Dirac equation is the relativistic quantum wave equation for spin-1/2 fermions that is first-order in time and space derivatives, Lorentz covariant, and acts on a four-component spinor via 4\u00d74 gamma matrices. It naturally incorporates intrinsic spin, yields the correct fine structure and magnetic moment for the electron, and predicts the existence of antiparticles."},{"id":"35331cad-1c50-4499-a5a6-c6770b201385","l":"Dirac fermions","d":2,"p":"4fcc035e-7eb4-46f6-b347-5cc975e5ed95","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Dirac fermions are spin-1/2 particles described by the relativistic Dirac equation, whose quantum states are four-component spinors and which have distinct particle and antiparticle solutions while obeying Fermi\u2013Dirac statistics. In condensed-matter physics the term also denotes low-energy quasiparticles with linear (Dirac-like) dispersion and twofold spin/pseudospin structure that mimic relativistic Dirac particles."},{"id":"a51f5a16-9e50-4f10-8419-0c9d318bc93d","l":"Disclinations & dislocations","d":2,"p":"3331008f-47b2-4908-92ad-bc9bd7cd815e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Dislocations are line defects in a crystal that break translational order\u2014examples are edge dislocations (an extra half-plane of atoms) and screw dislocations (a helical mismatch)\u2014characterized by a Burgers vector and responsible for plastic (irreversible) deformation. Disclinations are defects that break orientational order, producing a wedge or twist in the lattice or director field (characterized by a Frank angle/vector), generally carry much higher elastic energy than dislocations, and appear in liquid crystals, grain boundaries, and other ordered media."},{"id":"f7b4456d-a184-49a5-b6d4-82c8df424d07","l":"Discontinuous percolation transition","d":2,"p":"08ee99a2-4964-4690-8cd4-213bbba951b5","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"A discontinuous percolation transition is a percolation phase change in which the order parameter\u2014the fraction of sites or bonds in the largest connected cluster\u2014jumps abruptly from zero (or vanishingly small) to a finite value at the percolation threshold, i.e., a first-order onset of macroscopic connectivity. Such abrupt transitions (seen in certain constrained or \"explosive\" percolation models) contrast with the smooth, scale-invariant growth characteristic of continuous percolation and are often accompanied by hysteresis or phase coexistence."},{"id":"847f8adc-79b0-4961-bc2a-d0494a37a24c","l":"Discrete symmetries in condensed matter","d":2,"p":"eac8c4d5-07af-40a1-92d6-f32abca831a1","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Discrete symmetries in condensed matter are symmetry operations that form a non\u2011continuous (discrete) group\u2014for example lattice translations, point\u2011group rotations and reflections, inversion, and internal operations such as time\u2011reversal or particle\u2011hole conjugation\u2014that leave the system Hamiltonian invariant. These symmetries constrain allowed Hamiltonian terms, protect degeneracies and band crossings, and play a central role in classifying phases and symmetry\u2011protected topological states."},{"id":"89a3e978-3f1f-4840-8622-57dd7e262105","l":"Disordered alloys","d":2,"p":"96973abf-f1b5-44c5-9549-9f0f4dfe234f","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Disordered alloys are solid solutions in which two or more atomic species occupy crystallographic sites without long-range periodic ordering, so site occupations are random or only short-range correlated. This configurational disorder (substitutional or interstitial) strongly modifies the alloy's electronic, magnetic, transport and mechanical properties compared with ordered compounds."},{"id":"dc3cbe0a-ecc1-4d41-990d-c2a9eda69355","l":"Dispersion engineering","d":2,"p":"3123cbf9-460d-467a-9306-b4fb316daddd","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Dispersion engineering is the deliberate design and control of a system's dispersion relation\u2014the frequency dependence of wave frequency versus wavevector\u2014to shape phase and group velocities. It uses material choice, geometry, periodic structures or metamaterials to tailor group-velocity dispersion and higher-order dispersion for tasks like pulse shaping, slow light, phase matching, and broadband frequency conversion."},{"id":"e6908b00-7734-4616-9873-6de09213b034","l":"Dissipative particle dynamics","d":2,"p":"695da376-c44a-41dd-b968-d200c31e9d5e","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems","Plasma Physics"],"def":"Dissipative particle dynamics (DPD) is a mesoscale, particle-based simulation method in which coarse-grained beads interact via pairwise conservative, dissipative (velocity-dependent) and stochastic forces that conserve momentum and obey a fluctuation\u2013dissipation relation. This momentum-conserving thermostat yields correct hydrodynamic behavior and thermal fluctuations for complex fluids and soft-matter systems while allowing larger time steps than atomistic molecular dynamics."},{"id":"bbdac54f-7bcd-4dc7-b02d-7ad7672d0832","l":"Domain walls","d":2,"p":"be0e6945-4b97-4b3d-8435-81912a4d3b07","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Domain walls are two-dimensional interfaces or topological defects that separate regions (domains) in which an order parameter or field sits in different degenerate ground states, with the order parameter changing across a narrow boundary. They carry an energy per unit area, can be topologically or energetically stabilized, and occur in magnets, ferroelectrics, liquid crystals and cosmological field theories."},{"id":"55dfbfe2-e452-4632-ad35-5d8c1419f776","l":"Dopants","d":2,"p":"7060bde3-9f16-411d-9d68-3328cf14b019","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Dopants are impurity atoms intentionally introduced in small concentrations into a host material to alter its electrical, optical, or structural properties; in semiconductors they act as donors or acceptors that create extra free electrons or holes and thereby control conductivity. They typically occupy substitutional or interstitial lattice sites and introduce localized energy levels within the band structure."},{"id":"6dcf8280-2ae3-4243-be61-782fc2770b58","l":"Doped semiconductors","d":2,"p":"12721ec5-11d3-484a-9ea9-a7bea1986722","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Doped semiconductors are semiconductor materials intentionally infused with small concentrations of impurity atoms (dopants) to modify their electrical properties. Donor dopants supply extra electrons to make n-type material, while acceptor dopants create holes to make p-type material, increasing carrier density and conductivity and shifting the Fermi level toward the conduction or valence band."},{"id":"49f03fd7-420c-4190-9659-8fd2133a5241","l":"Doppler broadening spectroscopy","d":2,"p":"d199d86e-97af-4dd1-9fe7-fdb00b298b96","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Doppler-broadening spectroscopy measures the widening of spectral lines produced by Doppler shifts from the thermal and bulk motions of emitting or absorbing particles along the line of sight. The shape and width of the resulting (typically Gaussian) profile are analyzed to infer the particles\u2019 velocity distribution, temperature, and flow or turbulence."},{"id":"e2eb1cbc-ceb4-47ea-84df-284074d16bb6","l":"Dosimetry","d":2,"p":"a24a2d8a-58a4-4cbc-81aa-4cd953e73f4b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Dosimetry is the measurement, calculation, and assessment of the absorbed dose from ionizing radiation \u2014 i.e., the energy deposited per unit mass (SI unit: gray) \u2014 in materials or biological tissue. It provides quantitative estimates used for radiation protection, medical treatment planning, and predicting radiobiological effects."},{"id":"cfcdff89-d672-490a-a06c-1d9cff147a1f","l":"Double beta decay","d":2,"p":"486fd198-240a-4fef-a8a8-60d4cb9ed665","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Double beta decay is a rare second-order weak nuclear process in which a nucleus increases its charge by two units by converting two neutrons into two protons and emitting two electrons and, in the Standard Model-allowed mode, two electron antineutrinos. A hypothetical neutrinoless mode, in which no neutrinos are emitted, would violate lepton number conservation and imply that neutrinos are Majorana particles, providing a probe of new physics and neutrino mass."},{"id":"7a46daf4-b19a-41af-827f-5e3069da7e6a","l":"Double quantum dots","d":2,"p":"709d46e1-304b-4708-a494-d27c13e9ca2c","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Two closely spaced quantum dots whose confined electrons are coupled by tunnel and electrostatic interactions, forming an \"artificial molecule\" with hybridized discrete energy levels. Double quantum dots are tunable by gate voltages and are used to study single-electron transport, Coulomb blockade, and coherent charge or spin qubit behavior."},{"id":"d5150f96-6a4b-461a-ae3d-c6948cfce991","l":"Dresselhaus coupling","d":2,"p":"4201a122-4a87-4b76-af1c-5e52bb109c78","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Dresselhaus coupling is a spin\u2013orbit interaction that arises from bulk inversion asymmetry in certain crystals (e.g., zinc\u2011blende semiconductors), producing a momentum\u2011dependent effective magnetic field that splits electronic spin bands. It enters the effective Hamiltonian as anisotropic k\u2011dependent terms\u2014e.g. the bulk form H_D = \u03b3[k_x(k_y^2\u2212k_z^2)\u03c3_x + cyclic] (with simpler linear\u2011in\u2011k forms in 2D)\u2014and is distinct from Rashba coupling, which originates from structural inversion asymmetry."},{"id":"209bc883-82c8-493a-a844-3637a51af21f","l":"Drude model","d":2,"p":"f4585613-4c82-4c78-a57d-515fa4774cb6","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Drude model is a classical theory that treats the conduction electrons in a metal as a gas of non\u2011interacting classical charged particles that undergo random scattering off the ionic lattice with an average relaxation time \u03c4. It yields simple transport results such as \u03c3 = n e^2 \u03c4 / m for electrical conductivity and basic predictions for the Hall and thermal conductivities, but neglects quantum effects (band structure, Fermi statistics) and electron\u2013electron interactions."},{"id":"409fb499-ed03-4204-9d9b-37a6fecd1644","l":"Dry active matter","d":2,"p":"ba8221e8-b186-4db2-abb4-bcb0cc7f5b9d","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Dry active matter is a collection of self-propelled particles (e.g., motile cells, driven grains, or flocking agents) moving on or coupled to a substrate where momentum is rapidly dissipated, so fluid-mediated momentum conservation and long-range hydrodynamic interactions are negligible. Because energy is injected locally by each particle, these systems exhibit intrinsically nonequilibrium collective phenomena such as flocking, phase separation, banding, and giant number fluctuations."},{"id":"c53a574f-48ce-4182-b627-df9cbd8751ac","l":"Duality","d":2,"p":"05a47dcc-71c0-4ebc-9d4e-79a40a191efb","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Duality is a correspondence between two different theoretical descriptions that produce the same physical predictions, so that the same phenomena can be described equivalently in different variables or regimes. Such mappings often exchange complementary pictures (e.g., particles \u2194 waves, weak \u2194 strong coupling, or gauge \u2194 gravity) and provide alternative, sometimes more tractable, ways to analyze a system."},{"id":"d41a81e9-bc29-4ff4-857c-b7d384406355","l":"Dye lasers","d":2,"p":"cd4c012e-bacf-4bcf-8358-a6573a330ec4","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Dye lasers are lasers that use organic dye molecules dissolved in a liquid (or embedded in a solid matrix) as the gain medium; when optically pumped they emit coherent light whose wavelength can be widely and continuously tuned because the dyes have a broad fluorescence bandwidth. Wavelength selection is achieved with intracavity wavelength\u2011selective elements (gratings, prisms, or etalons), and dye lasers can operate in pulsed or continuous modes."},{"id":"78b1b6ed-732d-4a1e-85da-75402f4399d4","l":"Dynamical localization","d":2,"p":"e8e055f8-8711-4305-9995-7b3789b1a63b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Nonlinear Dynamics"],"def":"Dynamical localization is the quantum suppression of classically expected diffusive spreading in a driven or classically chaotic system, where interference freezes the evolution so the wavefunction becomes exponentially localized in momentum (or sometimes position) space. It is analogous to Anderson localization in disordered lattices but arises from coherent time-dependent dynamics rather than static spatial disorder."},{"id":"8a8b969b-5dff-402d-9d74-39f97952be9a","l":"Dynamical tunneling","d":2,"p":"e8e055f8-8711-4305-9995-7b3789b1a63b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Nonlinear Dynamics"],"def":"Dynamical tunneling is a quantum-mechanical process in which a system transitions between regions of classical phase space that are disconnected by dynamical constraints (like invariant tori or conserved quantities) rather than by a coordinate-space potential barrier. Unlike ordinary barrier tunneling, it arises from the quantum overlap of states across phase-space separatrices and produces effects such as level splittings and transport between classically forbidden motions."},{"id":"3ce1a04d-9ff6-4494-8154-963ef04730ca","l":"Dynamic critical phenomena","d":2,"p":"6faac3e6-cda0-43ca-be0f-4ea65fd16e81","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Networks","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Dynamic critical phenomena are the universal, time-dependent behaviors of fluctuations near a continuous (second-order) phase transition, where correlation times diverge and relaxation becomes anomalously slow (critical slowing down). These dynamics obey scaling laws with dynamic critical exponents and are classified into universality classes set by the order-parameter symmetry and relevant conservation laws (e.g., the Hohenberg\u2013Halperin models)."},{"id":"a7e7d57f-4739-4c68-a882-c096b4a6fbb1","l":"Dynamic nuclear polarization","d":2,"p":"6fab7f86-2de1-4f52-9425-ed4f9aec008b","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Dynamic nuclear polarization is a method that increases nuclear spin polarization by transferring spin order from highly polarized electron spins (or other unpaired spins) to nearby nuclei, typically using microwave-driven electron spin resonance. The result is nuclear polarization far above the thermal equilibrium value, greatly enhancing NMR/MRI signal sensitivity."},{"id":"d0804355-e250-4c06-b827-494a629fe694","l":"Dynamics of phase separation","d":2,"p":"d0f6584f-c5d7-4011-99bb-2a370e8f2b99","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Dynamics of phase separation is the time-dependent process by which a homogeneous material, after a thermodynamic instability or rapid quench, evolves into spatially separated regions (phases) with distinct compositions or order parameters. It proceeds via mechanisms such as nucleation-and-growth or spinodal decomposition and subsequent coarsening, driven by diffusion, interfacial tension and hydrodynamics, and is described by continuum models (e.g., Cahn\u2013Hilliard, Model B/H) with characteristic scaling laws for domain growth."},{"id":"8a07b581-f540-48ea-b2f2-a20203ee9e1f","l":"Dzyaloshinskii-Moriya interaction","d":2,"p":"4201a122-4a87-4b76-af1c-5e52bb109c78","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Dzyaloshinskii\u2013Moriya interaction (DMI) is an antisymmetric exchange interaction between neighboring magnetic moments that arises from spin\u2013orbit coupling in crystals or interfaces lacking inversion symmetry and favors canted, chiral spin alignments. It is typically written H_DM = \u03a3_{ij} D_{ij} \u00b7 (S_i \u00d7 S_j), with the vector D_{ij} set by the spin\u2013orbit coupling and structural asymmetry and responsible for stabilizing spin spirals, chiral domain walls, and skyrmions."},{"id":"0554be63-d31c-4a95-832a-388b2f87f94a","l":"Ear","d":2,"p":"a32482f9-6a8e-410f-80c0-204520778cbf","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"The ear is the biological sensory organ that detects sound and head motion by converting airborne pressure waves and inertial forces into electrical nerve signals; its outer, middle, and inner parts provide acoustic coupling and impedance matching (pinna, canal, tympanic membrane, ossicles) and mechanoelectrical transduction in the cochlea and vestibular apparatus."},{"id":"e31ca7a8-41cc-44bb-9be6-86743f019eff","l":"Earthquakes","d":2,"p":"97bfddcd-43e2-47b5-9b1e-bfc0318f69a8","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Earthquakes are sudden releases of elastic strain energy in Earth's crust, usually caused by rapid slip on faults or by volcanic or man-made disturbances, that produce ground shaking. The released energy propagates as seismic waves (compressional P-waves, shear S-waves, and surface waves), characterized by parameters such as magnitude, focal depth, and fault mechanism."},{"id":"2c0a8549-3b19-447d-94b8-78b874a17b58","l":"Earth's interior","d":2,"p":"4db2768f-9069-4cad-901f-a8001738ab66","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Earth's interior is the layered inner structure of the planet \u2014 crust, mantle, liquid outer core, and solid inner core \u2014 distinguished by increasing pressure, temperature, composition changes, and phase (solid vs. liquid). Heat and material transport within these layers (e.g., mantle convection and the liquid-iron core dynamo) drive plate tectonics and generate Earth's magnetic field."},{"id":"54cb2b30-1fdc-4340-972a-a5eb87c8b310","l":"Ecological pattern formation","d":2,"p":"0f53e9c0-21db-4e21-9bf2-a3dcab5a14fc","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Ecological pattern formation is the spontaneous emergence of spatial or spatiotemporal structure\u2014patches, bands, waves, or mosaics\u2014in populations, resources, or community composition arising from local biological interactions and transport processes. These patterns result from nonlinear feedbacks and instabilities (e.g., reaction\u2013diffusion/Turing mechanisms, scale-dependent feedbacks, advection, or demographic noise) and can influence ecosystem function and signal regime shifts."},{"id":"aa6e6251-78da-4a3b-a270-cccc77b356e6","l":"Ecological population dynamics","d":2,"p":"0f53e9c0-21db-4e21-9bf2-a3dcab5a14fc","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Ecological population dynamics is the study of how the sizes and spatial distributions of biological populations change over time due to births, deaths, migration, and interactions such as predation, competition and cooperation. Physicists model these processes with deterministic and stochastic differential or difference equations and spatial/network models (e.g., logistic, Lotka\u2013Volterra, reaction\u2013diffusion) to analyze stability, oscillations, extinction events and emergent patterns."},{"id":"c06ade51-86ff-4167-ae3a-aa1ecc2b4937","l":"Edge localized mode","d":2,"p":"9bbcc3c5-16bb-4a7f-adea-c23acc893cde","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Edge localized mode (ELM) \u2014 a transient magnetohydrodynamic instability that occurs at the steep pressure-gradient \"pedestal\" near the edge of H\u2011mode tokamak plasmas and produces quasi\u2011periodic bursts that expel heat and particles. ELMs are typically driven by peeling\u2013ballooning instabilities and can rapidly erode or damage plasma\u2011facing components if not controlled."},{"id":"bd62fb96-9c63-41fd-b787-7fe0f62c014e","l":"Effective mass theory","d":2,"p":"05a47dcc-71c0-4ebc-9d4e-79a40a191efb","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Effective mass theory approximates electrons or holes in a periodic crystal as free particles whose inertia is modified by the lattice, so they respond to external forces as if they had an effective mass that encodes band-structure effects. For a Bloch band the effective mass is given by the inverse curvature of the energy dispersion, (m*^{-1})_{ij} = (1/\u0127^2) \u2202^2E/\u2202k_i\u2202k_j, reducing to m* = \u0127^2/(d^2E/dk^2) in one dimension."},{"id":"3ab8bde4-9e9e-497c-9262-2a77253d5ca0","l":"Effective medium approach","d":2,"p":"82f6c5fd-bbef-4ff9-a5f9-de62d3f5dfeb","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Effective-medium approach: a method that replaces a heterogeneous or microstructured material by an equivalent homogeneous medium with effective constitutive parameters (e.g., permittivity, conductivity, elastic moduli) chosen to reproduce the averaged macroscopic response. It is derived by homogenization or mean-field mixing rules and is valid when the probing wavelength or length scale is large compared with the microstructure, so that fine-scale scattering and nonlocal effects can be neglected."},{"id":"4dfb300a-0557-45f2-a9b4-ca554db6d652","l":"Efimov states","d":2,"p":"23dc1816-95ac-4954-b14c-f497299effe7","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Efimov states are a set of universal three-body bound states that can appear when the pairwise interactions are resonant (the two-body scattering length is much larger than the interaction range), even if no two-body bound states exist. They form a geometric spectrum with discrete scale invariance (for three identical bosons successive binding energies differ by a factor \u224822.7)."},{"id":"2940889f-926f-4022-8308-b366eed4c205","l":"Elastic deformation","d":2,"p":"77d491f8-55ad-47a6-a0a7-cd24ffce7602","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Elastic deformation is the reversible change in a material's shape or size produced by applied stress: when the stress is removed the material returns to its original form. Within the elastic limit this response is often linear (Hooke's law: stress \u221d strain) and stores energy as elastic potential energy."},{"id":"a2f6414e-1a5f-4911-89a7-a8ffeb2b1954","l":"Elastic modulus","d":2,"p":"e085ebce-3a96-4bf8-ad67-054c2a4f7e73","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Elastic modulus is a material property that quantifies stiffness by giving the proportionality constant between stress and strain in the linear elastic regime \u2014 mathematically the slope of the stress\u2013strain curve at small deformations. Common moduli are Young's (tensile), shear, and bulk modulus, and it is measured in pascals (Pa)."},{"id":"ae350a4a-781b-4833-8de2-bb1d93d7684a","l":"Elastic wave theory","d":2,"p":"82f6c5fd-bbef-4ff9-a5f9-de62d3f5dfeb","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Elastic wave theory studies how mechanical disturbances (stress and strain) propagate through elastic solids, using the elastodynamic (Navier\u2013Cauchy) equations from continuum mechanics. It predicts wave types (compressional P-waves, shear S-waves, and surface waves), their speeds, dispersion, and behaviors at interfaces such as reflection, refraction, and mode conversion."},{"id":"467f0788-e876-4bb8-964b-aae25db708e2","l":"Electrical transport techniques","d":2,"p":"675773f0-6ff3-44de-8600-2e9034eea9f0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Electrical transport techniques are experimental methods that characterize how charge (and related heat) carriers move in a material by measuring voltages, currents and their response to applied electric and magnetic fields and temperature gradients. Typical measurements \u2014 such as resistivity, Hall effect, magnetoresistance and impedance \u2014 yield conductivity, carrier density and mobility, scattering information, and access to classical and quantum transport phenomena."},{"id":"707a077a-b543-489a-86de-56c81f2a865b","l":"Electric dipole-dipole interactions","d":2,"p":"4dc047b5-2d27-4b49-b3d1-f8fa2fb4552d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Electric dipole-dipole interactions are the electrostatic forces and potential energy between two electric dipoles produced by each dipole's electric field; they are strongly orientation-dependent and fall off with separation roughly as 1/r^3. For point dipoles p1 and p2 separated by vector r (unit vector r\u0302), the interaction energy is U = (1/4\u03c0\u03b50 r^3)[p1\u00b7p2 \u2212 3(p1\u00b7r\u0302)(p2\u00b7r\u0302)], so the interaction can be attractive or repulsive depending on orientation."},{"id":"9314daec-43a0-4a1d-8a83-25d29c525bad","l":"Electricity & lightning","d":2,"p":"d4ce293a-8c78-4f5c-a4ec-9fd0f295d3ee","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Electricity and lightning encompass phenomena arising from electric charge and its motion: electricity includes static charge, electric fields, currents, and the associated electromagnetic forces and energy transfer, while lightning is a large-scale, transient atmospheric electrical discharge that neutralizes charge separation in clouds or between clouds and ground, producing a brief intense current, bright flash, and thunder."},{"id":"bfaaa316-f153-4d43-9b3c-5bc4379604e6","l":"Electric & magnetic plasma measurements","d":2,"p":"a5866236-c99c-4828-b499-4f50cccf7a2b","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Electric & magnetic plasma measurements are experimental diagnostics that determine the electric and magnetic fields, currents, and their fluctuations and waves inside a plasma using in\u2011situ probes (e.g., electrostatic probes, magnetic pickup coils), antennas, and remote electromagnetic techniques (e.g., polarimetry, interferometry). These measurements provide the amplitude, direction, and spatio\u2011temporal structure of fields needed to diagnose plasma parameters, wave behavior, transport, and stability."},{"id":"8f59f607-cd26-499f-b769-7e15cc7529c4","l":"Electrochemical pseudocapacitors","d":2,"p":"c6751f54-a0e7-44a2-aad8-cc8e5a5df651","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Electrochemical pseudocapacitors are energy-storage devices that store charge via fast, reversible Faradaic (redox or surface intercalation) reactions localized at or near the electrode surface, producing a capacitance-like, nearly linear relationship between stored charge and voltage. They bridge electrochemical double-layer capacitors and batteries by offering higher capacitance and energy density than EDLCs while retaining high power and rapid charge\u2013discharge kinetics, typically using materials like transition-metal oxides or conducting polymers."},{"id":"7bd4241f-24ae-4bb7-b20b-98ddd3380d36","l":"Electromagnetically induced transparency","d":2,"p":"70cba0b1-a5ec-474b-a023-2ab4c3e484b9","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Electromagnetically induced transparency (EIT) is a quantum-optical effect in which a strong control (coupling) laser coherently drives a multilevel (typically three-level \u039b) atomic system so that destructive interference between excitation pathways cancels absorption of a weak probe beam, opening a narrow transparency window. This coherent \"dark state\" also produces steep dispersion, enabling slowed light propagation and enhanced optical nonlinearities."},{"id":"ed224d79-ea1c-40d2-9a8d-0d1edeedaac6","l":"Electromagnetic therapy","d":2,"p":"a24a2d8a-58a4-4cbc-81aa-4cd953e73f4b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Electromagnetic therapy is the deliberate application of controlled electromagnetic fields\u2014static or time\u2011varying, typically non\u2011ionizing across ELF, radio and microwave bands\u2014to interact with biological tissue with the aim of producing therapeutic effects. The physical mechanisms include induced electric currents and voltages, dielectric (Joule) heating, magnetically driven stimulation of excitable cells, and forces on charged or magnetic constituents, with specific modalities (e.g., PEMF, radiofrequency diathermy, microwave ablation) distinguished by frequency, intensity and waveform."},{"id":"563e244d-b08e-437d-880a-3e03e3b08ca7","l":"Electromagnetic wave theory","d":2,"p":"82f6c5fd-bbef-4ff9-a5f9-de62d3f5dfeb","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Electromagnetic wave theory is the framework, based on Maxwell\u2019s equations, that describes how time-varying electric and magnetic fields propagate together as self-sustaining transverse waves (e.g., light, radio) that travel at speed c in vacuum and whose behavior in materials shows polarization, dispersion, reflection and refraction. It predicts the wave\u2019s energy and momentum flow (Poynting vector) and underlies optics, antenna theory and electromagnetic radiation phenomena."},{"id":"5c916789-4ee8-47a5-92d0-f5e5c4556416","l":"Electromigration","d":2,"p":"21992cc9-b289-4c96-8daa-2f131e29fb0c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Electromigration is the directed transport of atoms in a conductor caused by momentum transfer from a high-density flow of conduction electrons (\"electron wind\"), which induces biased atomic diffusion. It commonly leads to voids, hillocks, and eventual failure of metallic interconnects, with a rate that increases strongly with current density and temperature."},{"id":"4aaaeb0e-1324-43af-be4b-46b55703eb88","l":"Electron-correlation calculations","d":2,"p":"05a47dcc-71c0-4ebc-9d4e-79a40a191efb","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Electron-correlation calculations are computational many-body methods that go beyond independent-particle (mean-field) descriptions to account for the instantaneous Coulomb interactions and correlated motion of electrons. They are used to obtain accurate energies and properties of atoms, molecules, and solids, with common approaches including configuration interaction, coupled-cluster, and many-body perturbation theory."},{"id":"96992f41-1e10-44a6-a5aa-774f77c6879a","l":"Electron-cyclotron plasma waves","d":2,"p":"4f513e0c-b639-4505-9116-7a0613323cf4","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Electron-cyclotron plasma waves are wave modes in a magnetized plasma whose frequencies lie at or near the electron cyclotron frequency (and its harmonics), arising from the circular gyro-motion of electrons about the magnetic field. These waves\u2014which can be electromagnetic (e.g., extraordinary/whistler-like) or electrostatic (electron Bernstein modes)\u2014couple strongly to electrons via cyclotron resonance and play a key role in wave\u2013particle energy exchange, heating, and emission."},{"id":"d1402816-03a5-41ce-adad-a9f621e3ec0f","l":"Electron diffraction","d":2,"p":"b6850136-0dff-41c9-85da-b6bcf1518811","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Electron diffraction is the wave-like interference of electrons\u2014stemming from their de Broglie wavelength\u2014when they scatter from slits, surfaces, or crystal lattices, producing characteristic diffraction or Bragg patterns that reveal atomic-scale structure."},{"id":"8d9a5500-7204-4ae4-8a93-388da8f8d432","l":"Electron energy loss spectroscopy","d":2,"p":"b6850136-0dff-41c9-85da-b6bcf1518811","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Electron energy loss spectroscopy (EELS) measures the energy distribution of electrons after they have scattered from a sample, recording the discrete energy losses that correspond to excitations of electrons, phonons, plasmons, or core-level ionizations. These energy-loss spectra provide fingerprints of a material\u2019s elemental composition, electronic structure and chemical bonding and are often performed in transmission or scanning transmission electron microscopes for high spatial resolution."},{"id":"479d3c67-a551-4c5b-aedb-be4d794a2545","l":"Electronic & magnetic properties of clusters","d":2,"p":"cac7f673-4cd1-47b8-9de3-9f9785aea585","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"The electronic and magnetic properties of clusters concern how the finite size, atomic arrangement, and composition of small aggregates of atoms produce discrete electronic states, quantum\u2011confinement and shell effects that determine their conductivity, optical response, and chemical behavior. Their magnetic properties arise from spin-dependent interactions and electron correlations, giving cluster-specific magnetic moments, exchange and anisotropy effects, and phenomena like superparamagnetism that often differ markedly from bulk materials."},{"id":"8c80bf87-be16-49dd-8267-9660cfa5a049","l":"Electronic polarizability","d":2,"p":"bd03ea46-2406-4a24-8307-05b2b498fb4e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Electronic polarizability is the proportionality between an applied electric field and the induced electric dipole moment of an atom or molecule (p = \u03b1E), quantifying how easily the electron cloud is distorted. It is generally a tensor and can be frequency-dependent (dynamic polarizability), representing the fast electronic contribution to a material's overall response distinct from slower nuclear or orientational effects."},{"id":"20ab7bf9-c344-488d-b965-d86770676bc6","l":"Electronic susceptibility","d":2,"p":"bd03ea46-2406-4a24-8307-05b2b498fb4e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Electronic susceptibility is the linear response function that quantifies how a material's electronic charge distribution or polarization responds to an applied electric field or potential; in macroscopic form it is the (dimensionless) dielectric susceptibility \u03c7_e relating induced polarization P to field E via P = \u03b50 \u03c7_e E. Microscopically it is given by the density\u2013density response \u03c7(q,\u03c9) (e.g., the Lindhard function), which determines screening, the dielectric function, and collective electronic excitations."},{"id":"38eac340-8100-4e91-93f6-d332fd937555","l":"Electron-mediated pairing","d":2,"p":"57c8eac7-41d5-4fed-8ba5-d8cb38d1ad03","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Electron-mediated pairing is a mechanism in which two electrons form a Cooper pair because of an effective attractive interaction produced by the exchange of virtual electronic excitations (e.g., spin or charge fluctuations, plasmons) rather than by lattice vibrations. This electronically driven pairing is believed to underlie many unconventional superconductors and can yield non\u2011phononic pairing symmetries (such as d\u2011wave)."},{"id":"34773509-53cc-4885-a027-7bd3497f7157","l":"Electron microscopy","d":2,"p":"b6850136-0dff-41c9-85da-b6bcf1518811","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Electron microscopy is an imaging technique that uses a focused beam of high-energy electrons instead of light to produce much higher-resolution images of a specimen, allowing visualization down to sub-nanometer or atomic scales. Interactions between the electrons and the sample (transmission, scattering, secondary emission) generate contrast and give information about morphology, composition, and crystallography."},{"id":"07b5cdc1-f569-4721-a808-f8f9a546e27e","l":"Electron nuclear double resonance","d":2,"p":"62b17a2f-3001-4254-982f-0f01726f510b","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Electron nuclear double resonance (ENDOR) is a magnetic resonance technique in which nuclear magnetic transitions of nuclei coupled to an unpaired electron are detected indirectly by monitoring changes in the electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) signal. It provides high-resolution information on hyperfine interactions, nuclear identities, and the local electronic structure around paramagnetic centers."},{"id":"b10e8e8c-561b-4d1b-9ae9-6b56590fba1c","l":"Electron paramagnetic resonance","d":2,"p":"62b17a2f-3001-4254-982f-0f01726f510b","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR), or electron spin resonance (ESR), is a spectroscopic technique that detects resonant absorption of microwave radiation by unpaired electron spins subject to a static magnetic field, corresponding to transitions between Zeeman-split spin states. It is used to probe electronic structure and the local magnetic environment of paramagnetic species via parameters such as g-factors, hyperfine couplings, and zero-field splittings."},{"id":"2cb8570a-bc2f-41ab-a3f1-1467b288c3f0","l":"Electron-phonon coupling","d":2,"p":"a06c5357-8167-4e9e-8fef-1a0304cf08d2","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Electron-phonon coupling is the interaction between electrons and the quantized vibrations of a crystal lattice (phonons), whereby lattice displacements modulate electronic energies and scatter or transfer momentum and energy to electrons. It governs processes such as electrical resistivity, carrier energy relaxation, renormalization of electronic bands, and phonon-mediated (conventional) superconductivity."},{"id":"9dfe8ff5-a8ed-484c-a604-50ec54209dd1","l":"Electron spin resonance","d":2,"p":"62b17a2f-3001-4254-982f-0f01726f510b","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Electron spin resonance (ESR), also called electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR), is the resonant absorption of microwave radiation by unpaired electron spins in a static magnetic field when the photon energy matches the Zeeman splitting between spin states. It is used to probe electronic structure, g-factors, and spin interactions (e.g., hyperfine couplings) in paramagnetic atoms, molecules, and materials."},{"id":"24cdea16-1b38-4327-9634-d475c14587c5","l":"Electron thermal conductivity","d":2,"p":"1d1e18e6-170c-403e-8786-d06fe58ddc94","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Electron thermal conductivity is the portion of a material\u2019s thermal conductivity due to heat carried by mobile charge carriers (electrons and holes), and it typically dominates heat transport in metals and degenerate conductors. It depends on the carriers' specific heat, velocity (e.g., Fermi velocity) and scattering (mean free path or relaxation time), and in simple metals is often related to electrical conductivity by the Wiedemann\u2013Franz law k_e = L \u03c3 T (L = Lorenz number)."},{"id":"1205cc13-dd08-4eae-aec7-d11ffa5fd06d","l":"Electro-optic effects","d":2,"p":"bd03ea46-2406-4a24-8307-05b2b498fb4e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Electro-optic effects are changes in a material's optical properties (such as refractive index or absorption) produced by an applied electric field, which modify the phase, polarization, or intensity of light passing through the material. Common examples are the linear (Pockels) effect, where index change is proportional to the field, and the quadratic (Kerr) effect, where it is proportional to the field squared."},{"id":"5f27d035-18db-49d6-8e93-4286e478f7ba","l":"Electrophoresis","d":2,"p":"00960c4d-78bb-469c-8ffb-cda89f4fe20c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Electrophoresis is the directed motion of charged particles or macromolecules through a fluid or gel under an applied electric field. The steady velocity (electrophoretic mobility) depends on the particle's charge, size and shape and on the medium's viscosity and ionic conditions, which enables separation of species by charge-to-size characteristics."},{"id":"150c416a-1266-454f-8748-285c1d3cb3ae","l":"Electropolishing","d":2,"p":"c4ed35b0-1090-409c-9667-85f9ac03e9c0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Electropolishing is an electrochemical (anodic) machining process in which a metal workpiece is immersed in an electrolyte and made the anode so that controlled dissolution removes material to smooth and brighten the surface. Because current density is higher at microscopic peaks, those protrusions dissolve preferentially, yielding a leveled, low\u2011roughness, oxide\u2011free surface with improved appearance and corrosion resistance."},{"id":"2c149da2-1bb1-4e4f-b1a9-a991f4caa502","l":"Electroresistance","d":2,"p":"153de13f-2f91-40e7-8553-022f50949969","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Electroresistance is the change in a material\u2019s electrical resistance produced by an applied electric field or bias (or by current-driven effects), typically appearing as nonlinear conduction or as switching between distinct high- and low-resistance states. It is observed in systems such as oxide heterostructures, ferroelectric tunnel junctions, and memristive devices."},{"id":"df9155f2-e559-474c-a5c9-368216a301d9","l":"Electrospinning","d":2,"p":"c4ed35b0-1090-409c-9667-85f9ac03e9c0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Electrospinning is a fabrication technique that uses a strong electric field to draw a charged jet from a polymer solution or melt into continuous ultra-thin fibers (typically nanometers to micrometers in diameter). The applied voltage overcomes surface tension and induces stretching and whipping instabilities while solvent evaporates (or the melt solidifies), depositing a nonwoven fiber mat via coupled electrohydrodynamic, viscoelastic, and charge-transport effects."},{"id":"6e7d96ef-329b-4942-a6e5-7e684bac7a49","l":"Electrostatic accelerators","d":2,"p":"4eec2798-da70-4fc2-8371-f6aa5f6180d7","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Electrostatic accelerators are devices that accelerate charged particles by subjecting them to a static high-voltage electric potential, so the particle gains kinetic energy equal to its charge times the potential difference (qV). Common implementations include Van de Graaff and Cockcroft\u2013Walton machines, whose maximum particle energy is limited by the highest sustainable voltage and dielectric breakdown (tandem designs use ion stripping to effectively increase energy)."},{"id":"d61b787c-3f0e-4c2e-94b6-a3bb21fbf789","l":"Electrostatic double-layer capacitors","d":2,"p":"c6751f54-a0e7-44a2-aad8-cc8e5a5df651","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Electrostatic double-layer capacitors (EDLCs), often called supercapacitors, store energy by forming an electric double layer at the interface between a high-surface-area electrode (usually porous carbon) and an electrolyte: adsorbed ions in the electrolyte arrange opposite electronic charge in the electrode across nanometer-separated layers, yielding very large capacitance without relying on Faradaic (chemical) reactions."},{"id":"dba5475e-73d7-4522-b008-461c9a386c21","l":"Electrostatic double layer forces","d":2,"p":"ed385286-31ab-418f-bc3e-be9030396a19","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Electrostatic double layer forces are interactions between charged surfaces or particles in an ionic solution that arise from the electrical double layers\u2014surface charges plus their surrounding layer of counterions\u2014formed at each interface. When these diffuse ion clouds overlap the resulting screened Coulomb interaction (typically repulsive for like charges and decaying on the Debye screening length) can produce net repulsion or, in special cases, attraction due to ion correlations or specific adsorption."},{"id":"7584df11-595e-4afa-bd90-305dfa506396","l":"Elemental metals","d":2,"p":"721bc5e5-4148-408b-91b5-fb760c1278ab","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Elemental metals are chemical elements whose atoms form a crystalline solid held together by metallic bonding, characterized by a lattice of positive ions immersed in a sea of delocalized conduction electrons. They exhibit high electrical and thermal conductivity, ductility and malleability, and a metallic luster, and are distinct from alloys or compounds because they consist of a single element (e.g., Fe, Cu, Au)."},{"id":"14f2d37c-640c-4887-9945-11273baedfeb","l":"Elemental semiconductors","d":2,"p":"12721ec5-11d3-484a-9ea9-a7bea1986722","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Elemental semiconductors are materials made from a single chemical element (most commonly group\u2011IV elements such as silicon and germanium) whose crystal and electronic band structure produce a moderate band gap, giving them electrical conductivity between that of conductors and insulators. Their carrier concentration and conductivity can be tuned by temperature, doping, or applied fields, enabling their use in electronic devices."},{"id":"086d8ef4-e2c3-455a-91fb-eed5b8e7b485","l":"Elemental superconductors","d":2,"p":"2250b5f3-dfa1-4ca7-bedf-fff5d41bd6ea","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Elemental superconductors are pure chemical elements that, when cooled below a critical temperature Tc, exhibit zero electrical resistance and expel magnetic fields (the Meissner effect). Most are conventional, electron\u2013phonon (BCS) superconductors and can be classed as type I (e.g., Al, Pb) or type II (e.g., Nb, V) depending on their critical fields and coherence lengths."},{"id":"c6f039f8-0580-4488-a6e0-b8bf5455a86d","l":"Eliashberg theory","d":2,"p":"7299559c-15c4-47f3-b866-5633b1030453","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Eliashberg theory is a microscopic, strong\u2011coupling extension of BCS superconductivity that treats retarded electron\u2013phonon interactions and the resulting frequency\u2011dependent pairing gap and quasiparticle self\u2011energy.  It uses self\u2011consistent Green\u2019s\u2011function (Eliashberg) equations to compute Tc and gap features while typically neglecting vertex corrections under Migdal\u2019s theorem."},{"id":"4c4dcee7-86b9-4618-b705-2818166df95a","l":"Ellipsometry","d":2,"p":"a38dc017-3236-4084-afc5-eb59c5de51e0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Ellipsometry is a non\u2011destructive optical technique that measures the change in polarization of light upon reflection (or transmission) from a sample to determine its complex refractive index (or dielectric function) and thin\u2011film thickness. It does this by analyzing the amplitude ratio (\u03a8) and phase difference (\u0394) between p\u2011 and s\u2011polarized light."},{"id":"5ebd217a-f89f-41e1-a8a7-be7a8eb50edb","l":"Embedded atom model","d":2,"p":"3817eab7-2f24-46f8-ba4a-aa6433722b4a","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Embedded Atom Model (EAM) is a semi-empirical many-body interatomic potential for metals in which the total energy is given by a sum of pairwise interaction terms plus an embedding energy that depends on the local electron density contributed by neighboring atoms (the energy to place an atom into that host density). It captures metallic bonding and coordination effects and is widely used in molecular dynamics and atomistic materials modeling, with parameters fitted to experimental or first-principles data."},{"id":"2d827c68-8a43-4743-a8a3-7254ad3abafe","l":"Embryonic development","d":2,"p":"93b8baac-44de-4a26-a175-357ad9d8744c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Embryonic development is the process by which a single fertilized egg (zygote) undergoes successive cell divisions, pattern formation, and differentiation to build the tissues, organs, and body plan of a multicellular organism. It emerges from coordinated genetic programs, biochemical signaling, and mechanical forces that guide cell fate, shape, and organization."},{"id":"0e42f3a1-127b-4810-8d1d-6a13f4d6e212","l":"Endocytosis","d":2,"p":"c2ea9e2c-0e15-49ee-b336-111976b0b965","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Endocytosis is the cellular process in which the plasma membrane deforms and invaginates to engulf extracellular fluid, molecules, or particles and internalize them as membrane-bound vesicles. It is energy-dependent and protein-mediated (e.g., clathrin, caveolin, actin), with its mechanics governed by membrane bending, tension, and cytoskeletal forces."},{"id":"e6586efa-1454-4ae2-9407-f4ca0d9322da","l":"Energy applications","d":2,"p":"da2cf824-a50d-4c47-a974-efa09b98c860","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Energy applications are the practical use of physical principles and technologies to generate, convert, store, transmit, and utilize energy to perform work and provide services. They encompass power generation (fossil, nuclear, and renewable), energy conversion devices (engines, turbines, fuel cells), storage systems (batteries, capacitors, thermal storage), transmission/distribution, and efficiency and control technologies."},{"id":"ad1b4378-2c61-4eae-8b6b-0cea181aef18","l":"Energy-dispersive x-ray spectroscopy","d":2,"p":"18d10b28-37f1-434d-a95b-19cf71fc6f05","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) is an analytical technique that determines a material's elemental composition by measuring the energies of characteristic X-rays emitted when the sample is excited (typically by an electron beam); each X-ray energy corresponds to a specific atomic transition and thus a specific element. It is commonly paired with electron microscopes (SEM/TEM) and uses energy-sensitive detectors (e.g., Si(Li) or silicon drift detectors) to produce an X-ray energy spectrum for qualitative and quantitative analysis."},{"id":"7a3d4591-98e9-44a0-9d6f-b9e0d14419e0","l":"Energy-efficient buildings","d":2,"p":"02eb6e91-8409-4ef3-ad57-d76b9c554e4e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Energy-efficient buildings are structures engineered to minimize the net energy required for occupant services (heating, cooling, ventilation, lighting, and appliances) by reducing demand and losses and improving the efficiency of energy conversion and delivery. They achieve this through physics-based measures\u2014insulation and airtight envelopes, managed solar gains and thermal mass, heat-recovery and high-efficiency HVAC/lighting systems, smart controls, and integration of on-site or district renewables\u2014to optimize energy flows and lifecycle performance."},{"id":"f09bdce4-b46b-4b5e-81ed-043890b8f30e","l":"Energy-efficient industry","d":2,"p":"02eb6e91-8409-4ef3-ad57-d76b9c554e4e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Energy-efficient industry refers to industrial systems, technologies, and practices that minimize the energy input required to produce a given useful output by improving energy-conversion efficiency, reducing losses (e.g., heat, friction, electrical dissipation), and recovering waste energy. It applies thermodynamic and engineering principles\u2014such as maximizing useful work and minimizing exergy/entropy losses\u2014to lower energy use, costs, and emissions per unit of product."},{"id":"c243097d-7c1b-4cfd-8146-85a713d9d31a","l":"Energy-efficient transportation","d":2,"p":"02eb6e91-8409-4ef3-ad57-d76b9c554e4e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Energy-efficient transportation refers to vehicles and mobility systems designed to minimize the energy required to move people or goods per unit distance (e.g., per passenger\u2011km or ton\u2011km) by maximizing propulsion and powertrain conversion efficiency and minimizing dissipative losses such as aerodynamic drag, rolling resistance, and braking heat. Techniques span electrification and regenerative braking, improved aerodynamics and lightweight materials, and system-level measures like modal shift and traffic optimization."},{"id":"cb96f9d7-13ba-485d-ba02-07da9077c686","l":"Energy loss near-edge fine structure","d":2,"p":"b6850136-0dff-41c9-85da-b6bcf1518811","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Energy-loss near-edge structure (ELNES) are the fine-scale variations in an electron energy-loss spectrum that appear within a few tens of eV above a core-level ionization edge, produced by excitation of core electrons into unoccupied states. These near-edge features reflect the local electronic density of states, bonding, and chemical environment (oxidation state and coordination) and are measured with electron energy-loss spectroscopy."},{"id":"3040d6ba-d81f-4844-a016-342311b150ce","l":"Energy materials","d":2,"p":"80fe725c-ae9b-4701-a9f2-db29c094a8ea","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Energy materials are materials engineered to enable or improve the generation, conversion, storage, transport, or efficient use of energy\u2014for example in batteries, fuel cells, photovoltaics, thermoelectrics, catalysts, and phase-change media. Their functionality arises from tailored electronic, ionic, thermal, optical, and mechanical properties across micro- to nanoscale."},{"id":"5a3d8918-37ac-4345-88a5-2e0a1e4b4d2e","l":"Energy spectroscopy for chemical analysis","d":2,"p":"18d10b28-37f1-434d-a95b-19cf71fc6f05","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Energy spectroscopy for chemical analysis is the set of techniques that measure the energy distribution of emitted or absorbed particles (photons, electrons, or ions) from a sample to identify its elemental composition, chemical states, and electronic structure by using characteristic energies and fine-structure features. Common examples include X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS), X-ray absorption spectroscopy (XAS), electron energy-loss spectroscopy (EELS) and Auger electron spectroscopy (AES)."},{"id":"92597d78-ac9b-4e8b-8b10-23893c0c4716","l":"Entropic sampling methods","d":2,"p":"eb9bd2e1-eedd-4bd0-997d-58b44ffa3ebb","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics","Networks","Particles & Fields","Physics of Living Systems","Plasma Physics","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Entropic sampling methods are Monte Carlo algorithms that sample configurations with weights chosen to produce a nearly flat histogram in a chosen macroscopic variable (typically energy), enabling direct estimation of the density of states or entropy (the number of microstates as a function of energy). By adaptively building biasing weights (examples: Wang\u2013Landau, multicanonical sampling) they improve exploration of rare states and overcome free-energy barriers for accurate thermodynamic and free-energy calculations."},{"id":"dbd0abfb-c195-4629-be58-24a1f83e2e21","l":"Enzymes","d":2,"p":"6c0e8bb5-a5a0-4f28-8d4a-4e7ace85a023","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Enzymes are biological macromolecules (mostly proteins, occasionally RNA) that catalyze specific chemical reactions by providing alternative reaction pathways and lowering the activation energy, thereby increasing reaction rates without changing the equilibrium. Their catalytic power derives from precise molecular recognition and dynamic conformational changes that stabilize transition states and reaction intermediates."},{"id":"e25266db-f634-4f16-94ea-c7b816baf162","l":"Epidemic evolution","d":2,"p":"a1c60fe3-47ab-4c61-a925-5edab473b8f9","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Epidemic evolution is the time-dependent change in how an infectious disease spreads through a population\u2014its prevalence, spatial patterning, and timing\u2014together with concurrent genetic and phenotypic changes of the pathogen driven by mutation and selection. Physically, it is described by coupled dynamical and stochastic processes (e.g., SIR-type models, branching processes, network and spatial diffusion models) governed by transmission, recovery, immunity, and intervention effects."},{"id":"3e98849a-c0f0-4813-a372-9bbed0c398f2","l":"Epidemic growth","d":2,"p":"a1c60fe3-47ab-4c61-a925-5edab473b8f9","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Epidemic growth is the time evolution of the number of infected individuals in a population, typically showing an early exponential rise when each case generates on average more than one secondary infection (R>1), characterized by a growth rate r and a doubling time. As susceptibles are depleted or interventions act, the growth slows and crosses over to sub-exponential or logistic-like behavior described by compartmental (SIR) or branching-process models."},{"id":"d0de008f-b207-4f1e-ba11-5c5ba5282ac4","l":"Epidemic prevalence","d":2,"p":"a1c60fe3-47ab-4c61-a925-5edab473b8f9","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Epidemic prevalence is the proportion (or number per unit population) of individuals who are currently infected with a disease at a specified time. In epidemic models it is the instantaneous infected fraction I(t) (or its equivalent), used to quantify the current burden of disease."},{"id":"4a894fd1-e25b-4fca-b700-129b062043bd","l":"Epigenetics","d":2,"p":"8e083471-2fd1-49cc-88e4-8c35ddff33be","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Epigenetics is the study of heritable changes in gene expression and cellular phenotype that occur without altering the underlying DNA sequence, caused by biochemical modifications such as DNA methylation, histone modifications, and chromatin remodeling. These modifications regulate access to and interpretation of genetic information, producing stable or reversible regulatory states important for development, disease, and biophysical models of gene regulation."},{"id":"1c120976-a7e0-4978-9cc0-806fd4f243d7","l":"Epilepsy","d":2,"p":"bbcb2483-b22c-4bb6-bb81-beb53ff1b1ed","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Epilepsy is a neurological disorder characterized by a persistent predisposition to generate recurrent, unprovoked seizures\u2014transient episodes of abnormal, excessive, and hypersynchronous neuronal activity in the brain. Seizure manifestations and causes vary widely, from convulsive movements to brief sensory or cognitive disturbances, arising from genetic, structural, metabolic, or idiopathic factors."},{"id":"471b81e2-74ad-4d48-b2c0-a57b3c84bb81","l":"Epitaxy","d":2,"p":"c4ed35b0-1090-409c-9667-85f9ac03e9c0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Epitaxy is the ordered growth of a crystalline film on a crystalline substrate in which the deposited layer adopts a definite crystallographic orientation and lattice registry with the substrate. It includes homoepitaxy (same material) and heteroepitaxy (different materials), where lattice mismatch can produce strain, defects, or dislocations in the film."},{"id":"7d40854f-0c5c-41de-b042-29b2cd1ecd2d","l":"Epithelial cells","d":2,"p":"3e04a8a2-1199-4410-801c-ac848b0bbda8","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Epithelial cells are tightly connected cells that form continuous sheets lining body surfaces, organs, and cavities, characterized by apical\u2013basal polarity and specialized junctions that create selective barriers and enable regulated transport. Their collective mechanical properties and active force generation govern tissue integrity, morphogenesis, and repair."},{"id":"ef11c112-69f0-402d-9e59-a3fb0584b262","l":"Equations of state of nuclear matter","d":2,"p":"63a9c708-1efc-49bb-9b67-fb6a39269235","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"The equation of state of nuclear matter is the thermodynamic relation among pressure, energy density (or chemical potential), temperature, and composition (baryon density and isospin asymmetry) that characterizes bulk, strongly interacting nucleonic matter (usually idealized as infinite, uniform matter). It encodes the underlying nuclear forces and possible phase transitions (e.g., to hyperonic or quark matter, superfluid phases) and is the key input for neutron-star structure, supernovae, and heavy-ion collision dynamics."},{"id":"935c0a7f-c1a4-4b15-8fb8-0ff6481e1935","l":"Etching","d":2,"p":"c4ed35b0-1090-409c-9667-85f9ac03e9c0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Etching is the controlled removal of material from a solid surface by chemical or physical processes (e.g., wet chemical etchants, plasma/reactive-ion or ion-beam sputtering) to pattern or modify surface topography. It is typically selective (uses masks) and can be isotropic or anisotropic depending on the mechanism, making it a core technique in microfabrication and surface processing."},{"id":"01ea3d02-0aab-4e63-9211-f7ad429d6c9b","l":"Ettingshausen effect","d":2,"p":"6e14e903-9322-41db-b946-0b518ecafdf7","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Ettingshausen effect is a thermomagnetic phenomenon in which an electric current flowing through a conductor placed in a perpendicular magnetic field produces a transverse temperature gradient (or lateral heat flow) perpendicular to both the current and the field. It is the thermoelectric reciprocal of the Nernst effect, arising from Lorentz-force deflection of heat-carrying charge carriers."},{"id":"976f93bf-2e62-41e6-86ab-b7143e9eedab","l":"Eutactic polymers","d":2,"p":"20a16e31-69dd-4e1b-9a44-2df3b053d9fd","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Eutactic polymers are polymer chains or networks in which the directions of segments or bonds form a eutactic (literally \"well-arranged\") configuration: a set of unit vectors with positive weights whose weighted second-moment tensor is proportional to the identity. Such eutactic arrangements give isotropic and mechanically optimal packing or elastic-response properties in idealized models of polymer networks."},{"id":"052b7755-cc9a-475a-b3fe-45ff5bce22d1","l":"Evolving network models","d":2,"p":"6f3f5cf1-363e-4d04-ac63-ca4ae90b47fc","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Evolving network models are mathematical or computational frameworks in which the nodes and/or edges of a network change over time according to specified growth, deletion, or rewiring rules (often stochastic). They are used to study how microscopic dynamical mechanisms (e.g., preferential attachment, copying, rewiring) produce macroscopic structural and statistical properties such as degree distributions, clustering, and community structure in real-world networks."},{"id":"d4c7a190-1d1c-4c1c-a1b5-3a3515f59c93","l":"Exact solutions for many-body systems","d":2,"p":"05a47dcc-71c0-4ebc-9d4e-79a40a191efb","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Exact solutions for many-body systems are analytic or otherwise non\u2011approximate solutions that determine the full spectrum, eigenstates, and/or time evolution of an interacting multi\u2011particle model. Such solutions are rare\u2014typically available for special integrable models (e.g., via the Bethe ansatz, free\u2011fermion mappings, or conformal field theory)\u2014and serve as rigorous benchmarks and sources of insight into collective quantum behavior."},{"id":"2c1a565c-b46d-4999-8ee3-1b28af298353","l":"Exchange bias","d":2,"p":"1f68adf2-72ef-4afa-8536-fd5094c2edd5","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Exchange bias is the shift of a ferromagnet's magnetic hysteresis loop away from zero field caused by interfacial exchange coupling to an adjacent antiferromagnet (or ferrimagnet), producing a preferred (unidirectional) magnetization direction. It typically appears after cooling the bilayer through the antiferromagnet's ordering temperature in a magnetic field and is used to pin magnetization in spintronic devices."},{"id":"a4bcbb70-2dce-4000-ad77-e396039ea028","l":"Exchange interaction","d":2,"p":"1f68adf2-72ef-4afa-8536-fd5094c2edd5","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The exchange interaction is a quantum-mechanical effect that arises from the symmetry requirements of identical-fermion wavefunctions (Pauli exclusion) together with the Coulomb interaction, producing an energy that depends on the relative spin alignment of electrons.  It is the microscopic origin of effective spin\u2013spin couplings (modeled by the Heisenberg exchange term J S_i\u00b7S_j) and underlies ferromagnetism, antiferromagnetism, and related magnetic phenomena."},{"id":"45501391-01e6-4815-8ae7-50ec0ef918ec","l":"Excited-state quantum phase transitions","d":2,"p":"520d3361-6e3b-4612-895f-98a7259acfe3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Excited-state quantum phase transitions (ESQPTs) are nonanalytic changes in the spectrum and eigenstate structure that occur at particular critical energies of a quantum system, extending the idea of ground-state quantum phase transitions to excited levels and appearing as singularities in the density of states and sudden changes in expectation values. They are often tied to changes in the topology of classical phase-space trajectories (separatrices) in the semiclassical limit and are distinct from thermal phase transitions."},{"id":"c7f6de93-185f-433b-b9fe-cd25cb3f39e5","l":"Excitonic insulators","d":2,"p":"12721ec5-11d3-484a-9ea9-a7bea1986722","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"An excitonic insulator is a correlated electronic phase in which Coulomb attraction between electrons and holes drives spontaneous formation and condensation of bound electron\u2013hole pairs (excitons), producing an insulating gap. It occurs when the exciton binding energy exceeds the single-particle band gap (or the electron\u2013hole overlap in a semimetal), often accompanied by symmetry breaking such as a charge- or spin-density modulation."},{"id":"5d8aee2e-d655-44bf-a177-71af449baadf","l":"Exciton polariton","d":2,"p":"69adcacb-6270-4cfc-8cf5-60097ec74d8b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"An exciton polariton is a hybrid light\u2013matter quasiparticle created when a photon mode (for example in a semiconductor microcavity) is strongly coupled to an exciton, a bound electron\u2013hole pair. It inherits the photon\u2019s low effective mass and coherence together with the exciton\u2019s interactions, enabling bosonic collective phenomena like condensation and superfluid-like behavior."},{"id":"f5956fb3-0d47-471b-901a-d67016d9dabe","l":"Exclusion processes","d":2,"p":"3c88d04a-17da-443c-8428-781247d46a8a","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Exclusion processes are stochastic lattice models in which particles hop between neighboring sites but obey a hard-core exclusion rule (at most one particle per site), so dynamics are governed by both random motion and occupation constraints. They serve as paradigmatic models of driven diffusive systems and non\u2011equilibrium transport (e.g., SEP, ASEP, TASEP), displaying collective phenomena such as steady currents, shocks, and connections to KPZ universality."},{"id":"20b80c87-c994-40e0-af6a-a69bbf606be3","l":"Exocytosis","d":2,"p":"c2ea9e2c-0e15-49ee-b336-111976b0b965","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Exocytosis is the cellular process by which membrane-bound vesicles fuse with the plasma membrane to release their cargo into the extracellular space. Mechanically and energetically it involves protein-mediated membrane deformation and fusion-pore formation, often triggered by Ca2+ signals, to overcome membrane tension and curvature energy barriers."},{"id":"b35a4640-36cc-49f2-8458-0aac08158c51","l":"Exotic baryons","d":2,"p":"b0ebe8a2-ed86-4c14-bfdd-5860e15b18bc","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Exotic baryons are baryonic hadrons (baryon number 1) whose structure or quantum numbers cannot be described by the simple three-quark (qqq) picture, encompassing states such as pentaquarks (qqqq\u0304q), baryon\u2013meson molecular bound states, and hybrid baryons with excited gluonic degrees of freedom."},{"id":"b191db98-31a4-4d58-9ea7-55eefc6a43a3","l":"Exotic mesons","d":2,"p":"5400167e-3abc-44f8-b432-0dc88cab84cf","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Exotic mesons are hadronic states that cannot be described as a simple quark\u2013antiquark pair in the constituent quark model. They include particles with quantum numbers forbidden for q q\u0304 and structures such as glueballs (purely gluonic), hybrid mesons (q q\u0304 with excited gluonic field), tetraquarks, and hadronic molecules."},{"id":"d67c733a-2184-43d0-afd0-4eb8486f9a09","l":"Explosive burning","d":2,"p":"29903cc6-9078-49fa-8c8f-9416eb5644fb","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics","Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Explosive burning is a runaway combustion process in which fuel is consumed and energy is released so rapidly that the burning timescale is much shorter than the system's dynamical (hydrodynamic) timescale, producing large pressure rises, shocks, and rapid expansion. It applies to both chemical detonations/fast deflagrations on Earth and to thermonuclear flashes in astrophysics (e.g., novae and Type Ia supernovae)."},{"id":"dcb64bfd-15a0-401b-b99c-64435becb980","l":"Explosive percolation","d":2,"p":"7f613135-db34-40bd-b19e-e520cf4b29f4","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Explosive percolation refers to percolation processes on networks or lattices where adding edges or sites according to competitive rules that suppress early large-cluster growth produces a very sudden emergence of a macroscopic (giant) connected component. It was originally proposed as a discontinuous (first-order) percolation transition (e.g., Achlioptas processes) but is now understood in many cases as an unusually sharp continuous transition with atypical finite-size scaling."},{"id":"32f4e687-d066-4764-93b6-99ec01d07f81","l":"Extracellular matrix","d":2,"p":"3e04a8a2-1199-4410-801c-ac848b0bbda8","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"The extracellular matrix (ECM) is a cell-secreted, hydrated composite of fibrous proteins (e.g., collagen, elastin), adhesive glycoproteins, and proteoglycans that forms a viscoelastic scaffold in tissues. It provides structural support, sets tissue mechanical properties and force transmission, and presents biochemical cues that regulate cell adhesion, migration, and signaling."},{"id":"a1f541a0-a451-4514-a805-93e2bebb2a14","l":"Extreme event statistics","d":2,"p":"695da376-c44a-41dd-b968-d200c31e9d5e","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Nonlinear Dynamics","Plasma Physics","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Extreme event statistics is the branch of statistical physics and probability that studies the occurrence, size, and frequency of rare, large fluctuations (maxima or threshold exceedances) in stochastic systems by characterizing the tails of distributions and their scaling. It uses extreme value theory and related point-process or peak-over-threshold methods to predict return levels, exceedance probabilities, and rates of extremes in contexts such as climate, finance, turbulence, and materials failure."},{"id":"599500c0-9b0f-4d87-93e3-f24856613490","l":"Fabry-P\u00e9rot interferometry","d":2,"p":"d4074cef-1ff9-4a93-9cfa-dc4d7e4e111a","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Fabry\u2013P\u00e9rot interferometry uses multiple-beam interference in an optical cavity formed by two parallel, partially reflecting mirrors: light makes many round trips and interferes constructively when the round\u2011trip phase equals an integer multiple of 2\u03c0, producing sharp transmission (or reflection) resonances. The positions and widths of these resonances, set by the cavity length and mirror reflectivity, give high spectral and frequency resolution for wavelength measurement, spectroscopy, and laser stabilization."},{"id":"9752120a-a0c7-49de-91f5-d7395f9c110a","l":"Face-centered cubic","d":2,"p":"6af5be88-489f-4a50-9abf-998e8912254a","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Face-centered cubic (FCC) is a Bravais lattice in which lattice points sit at the eight cube corners and at the centers of the six faces of the cubic unit cell. It is a close-packed structure (coordination number 12, packing fraction \u22480.74048) found in many metals such as Al, Cu, Ag and Au, with close-packed {111} planes."},{"id":"3a9812ac-6c00-40cf-be5e-0d7253b8e134","l":"Fano resonance","d":2,"p":"99f32be9-888e-42c5-ba4a-1fdcea060307","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A Fano resonance is an asymmetric spectral feature that arises when a discrete (localized) resonance interferes with a continuum of nonresonant states, producing characteristic peak\u2013dip line shapes in scattering, absorption, or transport. The lineshape is described by Fano's formula, with a parameter q that encodes the relative amplitude and phase of the resonant and continuum pathways."},{"id":"b3adf52f-f690-48ba-8ffb-0c75d611a6ec","l":"Faraday effect","d":2,"p":"e5cd96a4-a0d5-4b3a-a477-8ed31cf31830","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Faraday effect (Faraday rotation) is a magneto-optical phenomenon in which the plane of polarization of linearly polarized light rotates as it travels through a material subject to a magnetic field component along the light's propagation direction. The rotation angle is proportional to the magnetic field strength and path length (\u03b8 = VBL), characterized by the material's Verdet constant, and is nonreciprocal (depends on propagation direction relative to the field)."},{"id":"3f5993c2-f864-4a0f-85b1-54b1d389f531","l":"Fatigue","d":2,"p":"77d491f8-55ad-47a6-a0a7-cd24ffce7602","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Fatigue is the progressive, localized damage and eventual fracture that occurs in a material subjected to repeated cyclic loading, often at stress amplitudes well below its static tensile strength. It typically proceeds by crack initiation (often at surfaces or defects) followed by crack propagation and final rapid failure, and is characterized empirically by S\u2013N curves and fracture-mechanics laws such as Paris' law."},{"id":"d151a5c5-901b-47dc-bd5d-a5b41d230ca7","l":"Fermi liquid theory","d":2,"p":"05a47dcc-71c0-4ebc-9d4e-79a40a191efb","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Fermi liquid theory is a phenomenological framework for interacting fermion systems at low temperature in which the low-energy excitations behave as long-lived \"quasiparticles\" in one-to-one correspondence with the states of a noninteracting Fermi gas, but with renormalized parameters (effective mass and Landau interaction parameters) that determine thermodynamic and transport properties. It therefore describes many metals and liquid 3He, but breaks down in one-dimensional or strongly correlated regimes that exhibit non-Fermi-liquid behavior."},{"id":"a871e32c-c9e5-42fe-876d-3d33b658e63f","l":"Ferrimagnetism","d":2,"p":"b6e4cc90-18f3-4427-841d-c667375bbca0","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Ferrimagnetism is a form of long-range magnetic order in which magnetic moments on two or more crystallographically distinct sublattices are aligned antiparallel by exchange interactions but have unequal magnitudes, yielding a nonzero spontaneous magnetization. It commonly occurs in ferrites and mixed-valence oxides and can show temperature-dependent behavior such as a compensation point where the net magnetization vanishes."},{"id":"77fc85af-9271-4360-8847-e0ce707a1d9f","l":"Ferroelectric domains","d":2,"p":"153de13f-2f91-40e7-8553-022f50949969","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Ferroelectric domains are spatial regions within a ferroelectric material in which the spontaneous electric polarization is uniformly oriented. They are separated by narrow domain walls, form to minimize the material's electrostatic and elastic energy, and can be reoriented by an applied electric field."},{"id":"a15886c3-d7df-4ad1-9f0a-3de1c7259930","l":"Ferroelectric phase transition","d":2,"p":"19f26688-0955-4fd9-85d8-aaf71c4a581f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A ferroelectric phase transition is the temperature-driven change from a high-temperature paraelectric phase with no spontaneous polarization to a low-temperature ferroelectric phase that develops a spontaneous, electrically switchable polarization due to a structural symmetry breaking (typically at the Curie temperature). The transition can be continuous (second-order, often via a soft optical phonon) or discontinuous (first-order) and is accompanied by large changes in dielectric, piezoelectric, and hysteresis behavior."},{"id":"6e6a0326-86c9-49f2-a044-8ee3ca248ee3","l":"Ferroelectrics","d":2,"p":"74721827-c4e3-4da0-90ca-dba52158fc6b","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Ferroelectrics are materials that exhibit a spontaneous electric polarization that can be reversed by applying an external electric field. This behavior arises from a noncentrosymmetric crystal structure and domain formation, produces a characteristic polarization\u2013electric field hysteresis, and typically disappears above a Curie temperature."},{"id":"4166ca2c-d6e2-4995-88fc-2b974d71668c","l":"Ferromagnetic resonance","d":2,"p":"0003fc3f-1522-4fca-bf3f-bcef0dcec405","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Ferromagnetic resonance is the resonant collective precession of a ferromagnet's net magnetization driven by a transverse oscillating magnetic field, with the resonance frequency set by the material's effective internal field, anisotropy and demagnetizing factors (given for the uniform mode by the Kittel relation). It is widely used to measure magnetic parameters such as anisotropy, gyromagnetic ratio and damping (Gilbert damping) of magnetic materials."},{"id":"b17ebd7e-3413-4450-9a9d-1bd7f334b312","l":"Ferromagnetism","d":2,"p":"b6e4cc90-18f3-4427-841d-c667375bbca0","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Ferromagnetism is a magnetic ordering in which atomic magnetic moments (primarily electron spins) align parallel over regions called domains due to quantum mechanical exchange interactions, producing a spontaneous net magnetization even without an applied field. This ordered state exists only below a material-specific Curie temperature and leads to strong permanent magnetism and hysteresis."},{"id":"e24b2e10-1a6d-4338-ab21-9ff46cf82c4c","l":"Fiber lasers","d":2,"p":"cd4c012e-bacf-4bcf-8358-a6573a330ec4","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"A fiber laser is a laser whose gain medium is an optical fiber doped with rare-earth ions (e.g., Er, Yb, Nd); pump light is coupled into the fiber and amplified as it propagates, with the resonator typically formed by fiber Bragg gratings or end mirrors. The waveguide confinement yields high gain per length, excellent beam quality and efficient thermal handling, enabling compact, high\u2011efficiency and high\u2011power sources for communications, sensing and materials processing."},{"id":"76313c60-aa6c-4757-80be-c2618571ee82","l":"Field emission & field-ion microscopy","d":2,"p":"1d433fa8-4cdd-4e66-ae51-df1797f18d56","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Field emission is the quantum-mechanical tunneling of electrons from a solid into vacuum under a very strong electric field that lowers the surface barrier (described by Fowler\u2013Nordheim theory), producing highly directional electron emission especially from sharp tips. Field\u2011ion microscopy is an atomic\u2011resolution imaging technique in which a sharply biased tip ionizes nearby imaging\u2011gas atoms by the intense local field and projects those ions onto a detector to map the surface atom positions."},{"id":"43f11413-ec8a-44bc-b0e2-72a21b584a7e","l":"Field-reversed plasma confinement configurations","d":2,"p":"5b4f3bb4-8db6-4156-a472-4c9988d6e313","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Field-reversed configurations (FRCs) are compact toroidal plasma states in which the plasma current reverses the applied axial magnetic field inside the plasma, producing closed poloidal magnetic field lines with little or no toroidal field. They confine plasma largely by the self-generated reversed field and pressure balance, are inherently high-beta, and are studied as compact, fusion-relevant confinement schemes."},{"id":"464661a4-e6ff-4198-8896-109098390cfa","l":"Filaments","d":2,"p":"9ccf11df-5b72-4ce0-92e6-e48f8dda172b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Filaments are elongated, thread-like structures in physical systems\u2014regions where mass, charge, current, vorticity, or magnetic flux is concentrated\u2014so that the system is effectively quasi-one-dimensional along the filament. Their dynamics and stability are governed by the forces that confine and stretch them (gravity, magnetic or surface tension, inertia) and by filamentation instabilities that create, bend, or break them."},{"id":"778678c1-03d6-402a-a039-4eaaeb999399","l":"Film deposition","d":2,"p":"c4ed35b0-1090-409c-9667-85f9ac03e9c0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Film deposition is the controlled creation of a thin solid layer on a substrate by delivering atoms, molecules, or clusters from a gas, liquid, or solid source and allowing them to adsorb, nucleate, and grow into a coherent film. Common techniques\u2014such as physical vapor deposition, chemical vapor deposition, sputtering and atomic-layer deposition\u2014are chosen to control the film\u2019s thickness, composition, structure and properties for electronic, optical or protective applications."},{"id":"b4dd5dd8-b88d-44bc-b30d-94b18a2b9a65","l":"Finite-difference time-domain method","d":2,"p":"82f6c5fd-bbef-4ff9-a5f9-de62d3f5dfeb","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The finite-difference time-domain (FDTD) method is an explicit numerical technique that solves time-dependent partial differential equations\u2014most commonly Maxwell\u2019s curl equations\u2014by replacing spatial and temporal derivatives with finite differences on a discrete (typically staggered, or Yee) grid and marching the fields forward in time. It directly simulates wave propagation and transients, with stability and accuracy controlled by the grid resolution and the Courant\u2013Friedrichs\u2013Lewy time-step condition."},{"id":"560ecb58-6715-4f0a-9756-37222ebc5244","l":"First order phase transitions","d":2,"p":"d2c655da-6260-45e7-bc14-26fba976f03b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A first-order phase transition is a thermodynamic change in which a first derivative of the free energy (such as entropy or volume) is discontinuous at the transition, so an order parameter jumps abruptly. It therefore involves latent heat, phase coexistence and nucleation-and-growth dynamics (examples: melting, boiling, and liquid\u2013gas transitions)."},{"id":"6080ec71-e7c6-45fd-9184-60285aafae40","l":"Fission","d":2,"p":"8c48a332-f38c-4fde-affa-82f036e2aa25","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics","Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Fission is the process in which a heavy atomic nucleus splits into two or more lighter nuclei, typically emitting neutrons and releasing a large amount of energy because the products have a higher binding energy per nucleon (mass defect converted to kinetic energy and radiation). It can occur spontaneously or be induced (e.g., by neutron absorption) and is the basis of nuclear reactors and weapons."},{"id":"da39ddc2-b711-4d2f-9d66-a88d82dad68b","l":"Fitness models","d":2,"p":"6f3f5cf1-363e-4d04-ac63-ca4ae90b47fc","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Fitness models are theoretical frameworks that assign each agent or network node an intrinsic \"fitness\" (a scalar quality or advantage) and use fitness-weighted probabilistic rules to determine link formation, replication, or competitive success. They are used in network science and evolutionary dynamics to generate heterogeneous degree or abundance distributions (examples: Bianconi\u2013Barab\u00e1si growth model and Caldarelli static fitness model)."},{"id":"defa146d-1394-47ad-9732-4ec3c759fd34","l":"Flavor changing neutral currents","d":2,"p":"83c64883-5507-43f8-92bc-ae9f5ae03f02","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Flavor-changing neutral currents are processes in which a fermion changes its flavor (type) without changing its electric charge, mediated by neutral interactions (e.g., Z, photon, gluon or other neutral particles). In the Standard Model they are forbidden at tree level and highly suppressed (occur only via loop diagrams through the GIM mechanism), making them rare and sensitive probes of new physics."},{"id":"c9d5cad5-c576-44e8-bafd-838d7904efff","l":"Flexible electronics","d":2,"p":"c765ac18-ded5-4ef0-a41a-22be13741559","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Flexible electronics are electronic circuits and devices designed to retain electrical functionality while bending, folding, or stretching, by placing active components on compliant substrates or making them ultrathin. They rely on flexible conductive and semiconducting materials (organics, graphene, metal nanowires, thin metal films) and mechanical designs (neutral mechanical plane, wavy or serpentine interconnects) that reduce strain on sensitive regions."},{"id":"5a5b8a33-d049-4c6b-8b22-b5eb243c3fa4","l":"Flexoelectrics","d":2,"p":"74721827-c4e3-4da0-90ca-dba52158fc6b","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Flexoelectrics is the linear coupling between electric polarization and a spatial gradient of mechanical strain: an inhomogeneous deformation (for example bending) induces an electric polarization, and conversely electric-field gradients produce mechanical stress. Unlike piezoelectricity, flexoelectricity can occur in any dielectric (including centrosymmetric ones) and becomes especially important at small length scales where large strain gradients arise."},{"id":"56ce8d79-02e5-49e1-adb7-496f94d811e9","l":"Flight networks","d":2,"p":"ca680354-e15b-45ba-add1-7f966f2c0ae2","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Flight networks are complex networks in which nodes represent airports (or airfields) and edges represent scheduled flights or air routes, often weighted by frequency, passenger/cargo flow, or capacity. Physicists study their topology, temporal and multilayer structure, and dynamical processes on them (transport, cascading delays, contagion and resilience) using tools from network theory and statistical physics."},{"id":"0f0cd98d-5753-4e9a-99e9-068f5528eafe","l":"Flow boundary effects","d":2,"p":"87e64a0f-c935-44dd-a3ef-ae868d3a6389","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Flow boundary effects are the ways in which walls, interfaces, or confining geometry alter a fluid\u2019s local and global motion by imposing kinematic and dynamic constraints (e.g., no\u2011slip or slip, roughness, wettability). These constraints create boundary layers, shear stresses, flow separation and secondary flows that strongly affect velocity profiles, drag, and heat/mass transport across scales."},{"id":"8bc7a692-1d92-4709-9fdd-154cb27c3da4","l":"Flow-vessel interactions","d":2,"p":"12f6f3a9-8096-40e2-9170-e8f3a45d6bd9","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Flow\u2013vessel interactions are the coupled physical processes by which a moving fluid and its confining structure mutually influence one another: the flow exerts pressure and shear that can deform, vibrate, erode, or chemically modify the vessel, while the vessel\u2019s geometry, compliance and surface properties alter the flow field and transport. This fluid\u2013structure coupling produces phenomena such as flow\u2011induced vibration, pressure\u2011wave propagation in compliant conduits, boundary\u2011layer changes, and transport\u2011limited deposition or erosion."},{"id":"4f042714-507d-4b80-a8b9-c3cdaee77ae3","l":"Fluid-gravity correspondence","d":2,"p":"6287ac05-d956-4c3c-9882-8ea7a798f964","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"The fluid-gravity correspondence is a holographic duality that maps long-wavelength, near-equilibrium solutions of gravity (typically Einstein equations with a black brane in an asymptotically anti\u2013de Sitter spacetime) to solutions of relativistic fluid dynamics on the lower-dimensional boundary. It provides a concrete way to derive hydrodynamic equations (e.g., Navier\u2013Stokes-like constitutive relations) and compute transport coefficients of the dual fluid from horizon geometry."},{"id":"2656ae44-ff76-4375-ad62-e2cc9ec869f8","l":"Fluids & classical fields in curved spacetime","d":2,"p":"6287ac05-d956-4c3c-9882-8ea7a798f964","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"The study of how continuous media (perfect or viscous fluids) and classical fields (e.g., scalar or electromagnetic) propagate and interact on a curved spacetime background, described by covariant equations such as relativistic hydrodynamics and wave/Maxwell equations.  These matter fields enter the Einstein equations through the stress\u2013energy tensor, giving rise to effects like gravitational redshift, frame dragging, accretion dynamics and cosmological evolution."},{"id":"d888a244-851c-4307-9315-f900a1332415","l":"Fluorescence spectroscopy","d":2,"p":"a38dc017-3236-4084-afc5-eb59c5de51e0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Fluorescence spectroscopy is an optical technique that excites atoms, molecules, or materials with short-wavelength light and measures the wavelength, intensity, and time dependence of the lower-energy light they emit as they relax from excited electronic states. By analyzing emission spectra and lifetimes (including the Stokes shift and quenching effects), it reveals electronic/vibrational structure, concentrations, environments, and excited-state dynamics."},{"id":"5c8c329d-6b2c-4c5d-9426-0b9cf2712859","l":"Fluorite structure","d":2,"p":"6af5be88-489f-4a50-9abf-998e8912254a","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The fluorite structure (CaF2 type) is a cubic crystal arrangement (space group Fm-3m) in which the cations form a face-centered cubic lattice and the anions occupy all tetrahedral sites, giving each cation eightfold (cubic) coordination and each anion fourfold (tetrahedral) coordination. It has stoichiometry AX2 and is adopted by compounds such as CaF2, UO2 and ThO2."},{"id":"2520fdae-e7fd-450c-8fc4-889d55a6b034","l":"Flux pinning","d":2,"p":"ed63100a-e62b-42e6-9405-93c81012cd30","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Flux pinning (or vortex pinning) is the immobilization of quantized magnetic flux lines (Abrikosov vortices) in a type-II superconductor by material defects, impurities, or inhomogeneities, which prevents vortex motion under applied currents or forces and thereby suppresses dissipative resistance. This effect allows the superconductor to trap magnetic fields, sustain higher critical currents, and enable stable magnetic levitation."},{"id":"c2f66e60-e32e-48eb-b14c-a21ad53a7d95","l":"Flywheel energy storage","d":2,"p":"7f29b291-aab9-4d63-a15c-1b98193cc0ca","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Flywheel energy storage stores energy as the rotational kinetic energy of a spinning rotor, with stored energy E = 1/2 I\u03c9^2, and energy is charged or extracted by coupling the rotor to a motor\u2013generator. High-speed rotors in low-friction bearings or vacuum enclosures give high power density, rapid charge/discharge, and long cycle life for short- to medium-term energy storage."},{"id":"f8ce1367-8901-4a03-8a8e-017bba4b95cc","l":"Focused ion beam","d":2,"p":"c4ed35b0-1090-409c-9667-85f9ac03e9c0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A focused ion beam (FIB) is an instrument that produces and focuses a narrow, energetic beam of ions (commonly Ga+, He+, or Ne+) to probe, image, mill, or deposit material with nanometer-scale precision. Material modification is achieved mainly by ion sputtering, implantation, and induced secondary electron/ion signals, making FIBs widely used for nanoscale fabrication and specimen preparation."},{"id":"ff38e530-df35-49fd-8e7d-2983ae15f6cb","l":"Fokker\u2013Planck equation","d":2,"p":"695da376-c44a-41dd-b968-d200c31e9d5e","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems","Plasma Physics"],"def":"The Fokker\u2013Planck equation is a partial differential equation that describes the time evolution of the probability density for a stochastic variable (in configuration or phase space) for Markov processes with continuous trajectories, expressing conservation of probability via first-order drift and second-order diffusion terms. It can be derived from a Langevin equation or as the continuous limit of a master equation and is equivalent to the forward Kolmogorov equation."},{"id":"3deb631f-0777-4832-b710-dd3ecc143824","l":"Food webs","d":2,"p":"01f18d6a-0e76-4d44-a8db-946934037ffc","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"A food web is a network representation of the feeding relationships in an ecosystem, with nodes for species or groups and directed links indicating who eats whom, thereby tracing flows of energy and matter. It is studied as a weighted, directed network to analyze dynamical properties like energy transfer, stability, resilience, and responses to perturbations."},{"id":"fb1f0128-d241-44b8-9846-49b3a370e1cc","l":"Force sensing","d":2,"p":"c2ea9e2c-0e15-49ee-b336-111976b0b965","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Force sensing is the detection and quantification of mechanical forces\u2014their magnitude, direction, and point of application\u2014by converting force-induced mechanical changes (strain, displacement, pressure) into measurable electrical or optical signals. Implementations range from contact transducers (strain gauges, piezoelectric, capacitive or MEMS sensors) to noncontact probes (optical or magnetic methods) and are characterized by sensitivity, dynamic range, bandwidth, and noise."},{"id":"18b3cb53-e41b-42c4-9b83-b7bc62bf8d27","l":"Fourier's law","d":2,"p":"ac80909d-0962-428a-b719-a7a28aa28a1d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Fourier's law states that the local heat flux density q is proportional to the negative temperature gradient: q = \u2212k \u2207T, where k is the thermal conductivity (a scalar for isotropic media or a tensor for anisotropic materials). It expresses that heat flows locally from regions of higher temperature to lower temperature."},{"id":"e440c22f-66d8-4c2d-bf97-f7ff1e23db30","l":"Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy","d":2,"p":"8156d7cb-6ab9-4097-be2b-4aa71aad93a3","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) is a spectroscopic technique that records an interferogram produced by varying the optical path difference in an interferometer (typically a Michelson) and uses a Fourier transform to convert that signal into intensity versus frequency (or wavenumber). It yields the infrared absorption, transmission, or reflection spectrum of a sample, revealing molecular vibrational and rotational transitions used for chemical identification and materials characterization."},{"id":"e8bd4ba0-aabf-4061-a241-4d8598393ec7","l":"Four-wave mixing","d":2,"p":"a38dc017-3236-4084-afc5-eb59c5de51e0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Four-wave mixing is a third-order (\u03c7^(3)) nonlinear optical process in which three electromagnetic waves interact in a medium to generate a fourth wave whose frequency and wavevector satisfy energy and momentum conservation (\u03c94 = \u03c91 + \u03c92 \u2212 \u03c93, k4 = k1 + k2 \u2212 k3). It underlies effects such as parametric frequency conversion, phase conjugation, amplification, and the generation of correlated or entangled photons."},{"id":"003c8f31-3e3a-4ed0-a19c-dac774934f19","l":"Fractional Brownian motion","d":2,"p":"6efb152f-e313-4269-b7dd-a830f5523c53","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Fractional Brownian motion (fBm) is a Gaussian, self-similar stochastic process with stationary increments parameterized by the Hurst exponent H\u2208(0,1); its mean-square displacement scales as \u27e8[B_H(t)\u2212B_H(0)]^2\u27e9 \u221d t^{2H}, with H=1/2 giving ordinary Brownian motion. Its increments are correlated\u2014positively for H>1/2 (persistent motion) and negatively for H<1/2 (anti-persistent motion)\u2014leading to anomalous diffusion."},{"id":"31b1e71d-7b76-4607-9f91-107bbc86787d","l":"Fragmentation into hadrons","d":2,"p":"97069ede-42dd-4f4d-b552-9fc8562cb02d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Fragmentation into hadrons (hadronization) is the nonperturbative QCD process by which energetic quarks and gluons produced in a high-energy collision evolve into color-neutral hadrons (mesons and baryons), typically observed as collimated jets. Because confinement prevents isolated colored partons, this low-energy transition is described phenomenologically using fragmentation functions and models rather than perturbative calculations."},{"id":"a58c1c6b-ef1e-4373-bd58-441cdd4a2c8b","l":"Free-electron lasers","d":2,"p":"7e4e985f-d2fb-46cf-9183-c6e4b4c1c61b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"A free-electron laser (FEL) is a tunable, high-brightness source of coherent electromagnetic radiation produced when a relativistic electron beam is forced to oscillate in a periodic magnetic structure (an undulator or wiggler). Interaction between the oscillating electrons and their emitted field causes microbunching and stimulated emission, yielding laser-like amplified pulses over a wide wavelength range (from microwave/IR up to X-rays) with free electrons as the gain medium."},{"id":"08359a66-5c8e-43f7-9e0f-4d34fe6bf78f","l":"Free-electron model","d":2,"p":"3817eab7-2f24-46f8-ba4a-aa6433722b4a","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The free-electron model treats the conduction electrons in a metal as a gas of noninteracting quantum particles confined by the sample and moving in a uniform (constant) potential, forming a degenerate Fermi gas. By neglecting electron\u2013electron and detailed lattice potentials (except via boundary conditions), it gives simple predictions for the Fermi energy, density of states, electrical conductivity and electronic heat capacity of simple metals."},{"id":"b49e8912-9ba2-4214-a765-d892199d9681","l":"Frequency combs & self-phase locking","d":2,"p":"dfb4980b-4281-4a8f-830b-8822ca3477ba","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams","Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Frequency combs are optical spectra consisting of a series of phase-coherent, equally spaced frequency lines (modes) produced by sources such as mode-locked lasers or microresonators, enabling precise measurement and synthesis of optical frequencies. Self-phase locking refers to the nonlinear coupling process that forces the relative phases of those modes (or of multiple oscillators) to a fixed relationship, stabilizing the comb and permitting formation of reproducible ultrashort pulses and a well-defined carrier\u2013envelope offset."},{"id":"9cfcc59c-683f-4a59-9e78-58cfe5bd01eb","l":"Frequency conversion","d":2,"p":"bd03ea46-2406-4a24-8307-05b2b498fb4e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Frequency conversion is the process of changing the frequency of a wave or signal so that output radiation or an electrical signal appears at a different frequency than the input. It is achieved by nonlinear or time-varying interactions\u2014for example harmonic generation, sum- and difference-frequency (parametric) conversion, stimulated scattering, or electronic mixing/heterodyning\u2014subject to energy conservation and, in optics, phase-matching (momentum conservation) constraints."},{"id":"e3f70dab-8f32-4ec3-b856-de646d4ba194","l":"Frustrated magnetism","d":2,"p":"b6e4cc90-18f3-4427-841d-c667375bbca0","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Frustrated magnetism occurs when magnetic moments (spins) in a material cannot satisfy all their pairwise interaction preferences simultaneously\u2014because of lattice geometry (e.g., triangles, kagome, pyrochlore) or competing exchange interactions\u2014leading to a large near-degenerate manifold of low-energy states. This frustration suppresses conventional long-range magnetic order and enhances fluctuations, often producing exotic phases such as spin liquids, spin ice, or complex noncollinear order."},{"id":"9d3ec67b-01ed-4ebe-8b7f-04613a3d5185","l":"Fuel cells","d":2,"p":"9320b25b-5182-484c-866d-c379c6650fb1","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Fuel cells are electrochemical devices that convert the chemical energy of a fuel and an oxidizer directly into electrical energy and heat via redox reactions across an electrolyte, typically involving an anode, cathode, and ion-conducting membrane. Unlike batteries, they produce continuous power as long as reactants are supplied; a common example is the hydrogen\u2013oxygen fuel cell, whose main byproduct is water."},{"id":"eef726dd-4221-441f-9ef1-ad38756ac86b","l":"Full counting statistics","d":2,"p":"695da376-c44a-41dd-b968-d200c31e9d5e","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems","Plasma Physics"],"def":"Full counting statistics is the complete probability distribution P(N,t) for the number N of particles, charges, or other quanta transferred through a system or detected in a time interval t, together with its generating (or cumulant-generating) function. It encodes all moments and correlations of the transfer process\u2014average current, noise, and higher-order cumulants\u2014and provides a framework (using counting fields) to analyze classical and quantum transport fluctuations."},{"id":"b755f66b-b30d-4ec1-a7a1-2e31e5f675cb","l":"Fullerenes","d":2,"p":"bfbc8007-4c38-4ccb-a698-eeb79bc1651c","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Fullerenes are a class of carbon allotropes whose atoms are sp2-bonded into closed, hollow cage-like molecules made of polygons (typically pentagons and hexagons); the prototypical example is C60, or buckminsterfullerene. Their curved, conjugated networks give them distinctive electronic, optical and chemical properties, such as high electron affinity and the ability to host atoms or molecules inside the cage."},{"id":"e9fb5664-55f2-407e-8195-39285176e4ef","l":"Functional analytical methods","d":2,"p":"b5ff1838-0ecd-41a5-a90b-bae4acb69fcf","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Functional analytical methods are mathematical techniques based on functional analysis\u2014the study of spaces of functions (e.g., Hilbert and Banach spaces), linear operators on them, and their spectra\u2014used to formulate and solve infinite-dimensional physics problems. They encompass tools such as spectral theory, operator semigroups, distribution theory and variational methods to analyse PDEs, quantum systems, stability and evolution problems rigorously."},{"id":"99ff0294-e6af-4167-9a72-39d01dcd5dad","l":"Functional magnetic resonance imaging","d":2,"p":"fee9296a-01ca-4b80-86bf-2c51f37c1871","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Networks","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Physics of Living Systems","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI) is a noninvasive MRI technique that maps brain activity by detecting local changes in blood oxygenation and flow that accompany neural activation. The measured signal is mainly the blood-oxygenation-level-dependent (BOLD) contrast, where differences in magnetic susceptibility between oxy- and deoxyhemoglobin alter the T2* MR signal, typically sampled with fast gradient-echo echo\u2011planar imaging."},{"id":"c7e6d548-0586-4dee-93e0-0a26c19baa8c","l":"Functional renormalization group","d":2,"p":"50ba3f74-7f31-4857-8a75-b2b007348981","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields","Physics of Living Systems","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The functional renormalization group is a nonperturbative formulation of the renormalization group that follows how a scale-dependent effective action (or generating functional) evolves as an infrared cutoff scale is varied. Implemented through a flow equation (e.g., the Wetterich equation), it interpolates between the microscopic bare action and the full quantum effective action, enabling controlled study of critical phenomena and strong-coupling effects."},{"id":"049df0a8-a968-4164-9a62-4d5013cb3b04","l":"Gamma decay","d":2,"p":"fd8cf2dd-2362-4994-a45c-a9b453fe508d","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Gamma decay is a nuclear process in which an excited nucleus relaxes to a lower energy state by emitting a high-energy photon (gamma ray), without changing its proton or neutron numbers. It typically follows other nuclear reactions or decays, carrying off energy and often angular momentum, and competes with internal conversion as a de-excitation mode."},{"id":"254ea127-03cf-4cd1-8c09-8df8fd3523fa","l":"Gamma ray spectroscopy","d":2,"p":"d199d86e-97af-4dd1-9fe7-fdb00b298b96","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Gamma-ray spectroscopy is the measurement and analysis of the energy distribution of gamma photons emitted by nuclear transitions, particle annihilation, and other high-energy processes. By recording photon energies and intensities with detectors (e.g., HPGe or scintillators) it identifies nuclear energy levels, decay schemes, and isotope composition in research, medicine, and security."},{"id":"d94b540a-e0d6-40ef-9e71-d39129278bd5","l":"Gaseous detectors","d":2,"p":"be8bd934-5bc9-4a6d-ae73-f4753ce5c128","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Gaseous detectors are instruments that detect ionizing radiation or charged particles by measuring the ionization they produce in a gas: primary electrons and ions drift in an applied electric field and are collected to give an electrical signal. Often the signal is amplified by gas multiplication (Townsend avalanches) to produce detectable pulses, as in proportional counters, Geiger\u2013M\u00fcller tubes, wire chambers, and time-projection chambers."},{"id":"92fc2740-caf0-4409-9af3-cac8d84b7d3f","l":"Gas lasers","d":2,"p":"cd4c012e-bacf-4bcf-8358-a6573a330ec4","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"A gas laser is a laser whose gain medium is a gas or gas mixture in which excited atoms, ions, or molecules produce stimulated emission and optical amplification. Population inversion is typically created by an electrical discharge, RF excitation, or optical pumping, and common examples include He\u2013Ne, CO2, excimer, and ion lasers spanning ultraviolet to infrared wavelengths."},{"id":"b64f00ec-a5b9-49e8-8c70-1953b3e793e3","l":"Gas-liquid interfaces","d":2,"p":"628f7190-cb71-4692-8cda-ebc544f9e9fb","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A gas\u2013liquid interface is the thin boundary layer between a gas and a liquid where density and molecular interactions change rapidly, producing an excess free energy per area (surface tension). Its curvature and gradients in composition or temperature drive phenomena such as capillarity, the Laplace pressure jump, wetting, interfacial waves, and Marangoni flows, and control mass, momentum and heat transfer across the boundary."},{"id":"113ec697-5c75-4106-b64f-bf2e8a4fcc6f","l":"Gauge symmetries","d":2,"p":"eac8c4d5-07af-40a1-92d6-f32abca831a1","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Gauge symmetries are continuous, position-dependent (local) transformations of a theory\u2019s field variables that leave all observable quantities unchanged, meaning they reflect a redundancy in the description rather than physical degrees of freedom. Requiring local gauge invariance forces the introduction of gauge fields (gauge bosons) and thereby organizes the fundamental interactions (e.g., U(1) for electromagnetism, SU(2) for the weak force, SU(3) for the strong force)."},{"id":"33401231-4a45-4b02-88e4-453749210d00","l":"Gauge theory techniques","d":2,"p":"a1083ad2-a154-449d-a9ff-59bf46db1c7f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Gauge theory techniques are the mathematical and computational methods used to formulate, quantize, and analyze physical theories with local (gauge) symmetries. They include procedures such as gauge fixing and Faddeev\u2013Popov/BRST quantization, path-integral and lattice formulations, Wilson loops, and renormalization-group and anomaly calculations to compute observables and handle redundant degrees of freedom."},{"id":"25d97106-cf28-49f0-b3b8-405f75d49a11","l":"Gelation","d":2,"p":"19f26688-0955-4fd9-85d8-aaf71c4a581f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Gelation is the process by which a fluid of polymers, colloids, or particles forms a sample-spanning network of bonds or crosslinks, causing a sol-to-gel transition. At the gel point an infinite connected cluster appears and the material develops a finite shear modulus and viscoelastic solid behavior; gelation can be driven by chemical crosslinking, physical aggregation, or phase separation and is often described by percolation and rheological scaling."},{"id":"180aa356-0bf3-4f20-864d-1dbee323ee2a","l":"Gene expression","d":2,"p":"8e083471-2fd1-49cc-88e4-8c35ddff33be","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Gene expression is the process by which information encoded in a gene (DNA) is used to produce a functional product, typically an RNA molecule or a protein. It comprises transcription (DNA \u2192 RNA) and, when applicable, translation (mRNA \u2192 protein) and is regulated at multiple steps to control timing and amount of the product."},{"id":"abd176b8-d5c8-4a8c-85dc-b80a4a77f485","l":"Generalized parton distributions","d":2,"p":"74f884e0-6764-4962-a081-b198cca267c3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Generalized parton distributions (GPDs) are functions that encode the simultaneous longitudinal-momentum and transverse-spatial distributions of quarks and gluons inside a hadron, interpolating between ordinary parton distribution functions and elastic form factors. They depend on the parton momentum fraction x, the skewness \u03be (longitudinal momentum transfer) and the momentum-transfer squared t, and are probed in hard exclusive reactions like deeply virtual Compton scattering."},{"id":"913cd3ee-a6c6-4c54-873c-08e0415a3d96","l":"General relativity equations & solutions","d":2,"p":"6287ac05-d956-4c3c-9882-8ea7a798f964","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"General relativity equations are the Einstein field equations, G_{\u03bc\u03bd}+\u039b g_{\u03bc\u03bd} = (8\u03c0G/c^4) T_{\u03bc\u03bd}, a set of nonlinear tensor equations that relate spacetime curvature (the Einstein tensor G_{\u03bc\u03bd}) to the distribution of energy and momentum (the stress\u2013energy tensor T_{\u03bc\u03bd}). Their solutions are metric tensors g_{\u03bc\u03bd} that satisfy these equations\u2014found exactly, numerically, or perturbatively\u2014and describe physical spacetimes such as black holes (Schwarzschild, Kerr), cosmological models (FLRW), and gravitational waves."},{"id":"501d73a3-d7e7-4514-b828-f49b1a88cea9","l":"General relativity formalism","d":2,"p":"6287ac05-d956-4c3c-9882-8ea7a798f964","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"The general relativity formalism is the differential\u2011geometric framework that describes gravity as the curvature of a four\u2011dimensional Lorentzian manifold, with the spacetime geometry encoded by the metric tensor and curvature tensors (Riemann, Ricci, scalar). Its dynamics are given by the Einstein field equations, which relate that curvature to the stress\u2013energy tensor, and free particles move along geodesics determined by the Levi\u2013Civita connection."},{"id":"8512e2be-2e7f-4392-888f-92e2ad94f41d","l":"Gene recombination","d":2,"p":"8e083471-2fd1-49cc-88e4-8c35ddff33be","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Gene recombination is the molecular process that reshuffles DNA segments between chromosomes or within DNA molecules\u2014most commonly by homologous crossover during meiosis but also by transposition or horizontal transfer\u2014producing new combinations of alleles that drive genetic diversity, repair, and evolution."},{"id":"378f86f0-e70a-4942-ad72-595cb7f788d3","l":"Gene repair","d":2,"p":"8e083471-2fd1-49cc-88e4-8c35ddff33be","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Gene repair refers to the cellular and engineered processes that detect and correct damage or mutations in a gene's DNA sequence to restore its normal sequence and function. It encompasses endogenous DNA-repair pathways (e.g., base- and nucleotide-excision, mismatch repair, homologous recombination, non-homologous end joining) as well as targeted interventions such as CRISPR- or enzyme-mediated gene editing used in gene therapy."},{"id":"2c37847b-5353-4057-b57d-9b4e5f7db07b","l":"Genetic diseases","d":2,"p":"bbcb2483-b22c-4bb6-bb81-beb53ff1b1ed","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Genetic diseases are disorders caused by changes in an individual's DNA\u2014point mutations, insertions/deletions, structural chromosomal abnormalities, or mitochondrial variants\u2014that disrupt normal gene function. They can be inherited or arise de novo and include single-gene (Mendelian) disorders, chromosomal syndromes, and complex polygenic conditions."},{"id":"426041d7-ba48-436b-bde9-45482e4fe33a","l":"Genomics","d":2,"p":"6b5443fa-9849-4d5b-966b-7d6e08113e36","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Genomics is the systems-level study of whole genomes\u2014the complete DNA content of an organism\u2014focusing on sequence, structural organization, variation, and regulatory interactions. It combines high-throughput sequencing and computational/statistical (and often physical) models to map gene networks, evolutionary dynamics, and genotype\u2013phenotype relationships."},{"id":"3873301d-c286-49f8-a26c-b6c7297bd647","l":"Geometrical & wave optics","d":2,"p":"42791fef-2a1a-4002-81dd-c04d56ae9618","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Geometrical and wave optics are the branches of optics that describe how light propagates and interacts with matter: geometrical optics models light as rays that travel in straight lines and undergo reflection and refraction to form images by lenses and mirrors when wavelengths are negligible, while wave optics treats light as an electromagnetic wave to explain interference, diffraction, polarization, and coherence effects that arise when the wavelength is comparable to system dimensions."},{"id":"8f8b77e5-7263-4333-a6a9-def3fd83e2f6","l":"Geometry","d":2,"p":"b5ff1838-0ecd-41a5-a90b-bae4acb69fcf","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Geometry is the branch of mathematics that studies the properties and relations of points, lines, surfaces, and solids, including distance, angle, area, and curvature. In physics it provides the language and structures (e.g., metrics, manifolds, and topologies) used to describe spatial configurations, boundary conditions, and the geometric nature of spacetime and fields."},{"id":"42d18014-e527-4082-971a-6ccde17ec358","l":"Geothermal heating","d":2,"p":"3e08aa2f-f8bd-4a09-ae45-3dddb833f61f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Geothermal heating is the transfer of thermal energy from the Earth's interior to the crust and surface, driven by the geothermal gradient and heat produced by radioactive decay and residual formation heat. This subsurface heat is transported by conduction and fluid convection and can be exploited directly for space and process heating or indirectly for electricity generation."},{"id":"8b139335-9e63-43fc-a051-03fcbb177a22","l":"Geothermal power","d":2,"p":"3e08aa2f-f8bd-4a09-ae45-3dddb833f61f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Geothermal power is the generation of usable heat and electricity by extracting thermal energy stored in the Earth's interior\u2014originating from radioactive decay and residual formation heat\u2014through wells, hot fluids, or hot rock. The extracted heat is converted to work (typically using steam-driven turbines or binary heat exchangers) for electricity generation or direct heating."},{"id":"21aac74b-6ecb-4c26-8769-658b268f17c0","l":"Glasses","d":2,"p":"373a7206-3c07-47bb-9078-efd80ed70556","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Glasses are amorphous, non-crystalline solids formed when a liquid is cooled (or otherwise constrained) so that structural relaxation becomes kinetically arrested before crystallization. They are metastable, mechanically rigid materials that lack long-range translational order and exhibit a glass transition\u2014a dramatic, continuous slowing of dynamics\u2014rather than a sharp melting point."},{"id":"36470f66-1f89-49a0-8e1e-376d61313005","l":"Glass transition","d":2,"p":"19f26688-0955-4fd9-85d8-aaf71c4a581f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The glass transition is the kinetic, non-equilibrium process by which a supercooled liquid progressively loses molecular mobility and becomes an amorphous solid (glass) as temperature is lowered, typically around a characteristic glass transition temperature Tg. Unlike a sharp thermodynamic phase change, it is marked by a continuous, dramatic increase in viscosity and relaxation times that depends on cooling rate and observation time."},{"id":"f4a415c8-2d62-4dc2-b4f3-786a95d1d5c7","l":"Glial cells","d":2,"p":"3e04a8a2-1199-4410-801c-ac848b0bbda8","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Glial cells are the non-neuronal cells of the nervous system that provide structural support, metabolic and ionic homeostasis, myelin insulation, and immune defense while modulating synaptic function and neuronal signaling. Major types include astrocytes, oligodendrocytes (and Schwann cells in the peripheral nervous system), microglia, and ependymal cells."},{"id":"5cf71b89-0934-4afb-9918-dfa39120db64","l":"Global warming","d":2,"p":"358b7565-3e15-46e9-859e-1254bd4a9e7f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics","Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Global warming is the long-term increase in Earth's average surface and lower-atmosphere temperature caused mainly by an enhanced greenhouse effect from increased atmospheric concentrations of greenhouse gases (e.g., CO2, CH4), which produce a positive radiative forcing and a net planetary energy imbalance."},{"id":"c6dfa073-68e6-427b-ba42-694be065a0ef","l":"Glow discharge","d":2,"p":"c4ed35b0-1090-409c-9667-85f9ac03e9c0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Glow discharge is a low-pressure plasma that forms between electrodes when an applied electric field accelerates electrons enough to ionize and excite gas atoms, producing a sustained current and visible emission. It typically occurs at voltages and currents below those of an arc and shows characteristic regions (cathode dark space, negative glow, positive column) with different ionization and excitation behavior."},{"id":"4464577e-902a-49ed-8498-eab013c5aa36","l":"Goldstone bosons","d":2,"p":"71e104e2-448f-42ec-a9be-bbaec6758896","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Goldstone bosons are gapless (massless) low-energy excitations that appear whenever a continuous global symmetry is spontaneously broken, with one bosonic mode for each broken symmetry generator as stated by Goldstone's theorem. In relativistic field theory they are massless scalar fields representing long-wavelength collective fluctuations of the order parameter, though if the symmetry is gauge rather than global these modes can be absorbed to give gauge fields mass (the Higgs mechanism)."},{"id":"39ce5938-8626-493a-b688-6a87f4a85804","l":"Grain boundaries","d":2,"p":"7060bde3-9f16-411d-9d68-3328cf14b019","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Grain boundaries are the interfaces between adjacent crystallites (grains) in a polycrystalline solid where the crystal lattice orientation changes; they consist of arrays of dislocations and other defects that accommodate the misorientation. These boundaries strongly influence a material\u2019s mechanical, thermal, electrical, and diffusion properties."},{"id":"11f6a8c9-16ef-4e4b-ac4f-76755d081c81","l":"Graphite","d":2,"p":"bfbc8007-4c38-4ccb-a698-eeb79bc1651c","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Graphite is a crystalline allotrope of carbon made of stacked hexagonal sheets of sp2-bonded carbon atoms, with strong covalent bonds within each plane and weak van der Waals forces between planes. Delocalized \u03c0 electrons within the sheets give graphite anisotropic electrical and thermal conductivity and semimetallic behavior."},{"id":"c7c02452-3387-49d3-a7e8-377d5b81ab40","l":"Graph theory","d":2,"p":"b5ff1838-0ecd-41a5-a90b-bae4acb69fcf","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Graph theory is the mathematical study of graphs\u2014collections of vertices (nodes) connected by edges\u2014that formalizes and analyzes the combinatorial and topological structure of discrete relationships (degree, paths, cycles, connectivity). In physics it provides a framework for modeling and studying networks, percolation, lattice structures, transport, and quantum/wave behavior on discrete connections."},{"id":"c6891af3-c2d3-40b2-8c23-e72f946cdc95","l":"Gravimetric adsorption measurements","d":2,"p":"229eca6d-30f1-4c05-9a78-c352de8326b1","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Gravimetric adsorption measurements determine the amount of gas or vapor taken up by a solid by monitoring the change in the sample's mass with a sensitive balance while varying pressure and temperature. After correcting for buoyancy and instrumental effects, the measured mass changes yield adsorption isotherms, uptake kinetics, and related thermodynamic information."},{"id":"6ec56f3f-01d3-46fe-99d4-7397cf024d01","l":"Gravitational wave detection","d":2,"p":"453f63ad-c881-451c-b024-5d2b5aab7199","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Gravitational wave detection is the measurement of tiny ripples in spacetime produced by accelerating masses, observed as differential tidal strains between separated, freely falling test masses. It is accomplished with instruments such as laser interferometers (e.g., LIGO/Virgo/KAGRA, LISA), pulsar timing arrays, or resonant detectors that extract these extremely small signals from noise using precise timing and correlation techniques."},{"id":"5a9d25ae-0e9e-4e63-90f4-6ad03aebebf7","l":"Gravitational wave sources","d":2,"p":"453f63ad-c881-451c-b024-5d2b5aab7199","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Gravitational wave sources are physical systems or events that generate propagating ripples in spacetime by accelerating mass\u2013energy in a non\u2011spherically symmetric (time\u2011varying quadrupole or higher) way, as predicted by general relativity. Common astrophysical sources include coalescing compact binaries (black holes and neutron stars), core\u2011collapse supernovae, rotating deformed neutron stars, and early\u2011Universe processes such as inflation or phase transitions."},{"id":"033162e2-40ae-4390-9472-cf780651a0ed","l":"Gravity batteries","d":2,"p":"7f29b291-aab9-4d63-a15c-1b98193cc0ca","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Gravity batteries are mechanical energy storage systems that store electrical energy by using motors to lift a heavy mass, converting the energy into gravitational potential, and later recover electricity by lowering the mass through a generator. They are analogous to pumped-hydro storage but use solid masses (e.g., concrete blocks, stacked weights, or mine shafts) for long-duration, low-degradation grid-scale storage."},{"id":"8c15e092-3b78-496f-96ae-e55153f7efbd","l":"Green's function methods","d":2,"p":"05a47dcc-71c0-4ebc-9d4e-79a40a191efb","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Green's function methods use a Green's function\u2014an impulse-response kernel for a linear operator\u2014to build solutions of linear differential or integral equations by superposing point-source responses, thereby imposing boundary conditions and solving boundary-value and scattering problems. In physics they are widely used to compute propagators and correlation functions (retarded, advanced, time-ordered) and to extract spectral, transport, and many-body properties."},{"id":"a3bc1bde-37e7-4063-8703-01ffe668cad8","l":"Grid computing","d":2,"p":"db0eac92-d73c-4c77-ad1b-6c2517418354","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Grid computing is a distributed-computing paradigm that federates geographically dispersed and heterogeneous processors, storage, and instruments into a single virtual computing system accessible to users and applications. It enables large-scale simulation, data analysis and collaborative experiments by providing middleware for job scheduling, data transfer, and secure resource sharing across institutions."},{"id":"b7fb7beb-2d98-4254-887c-092165de0a77","l":"Grid-integrated energy storage","d":2,"p":"b580bc4d-63fc-4636-9ef6-76e79ea68085","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Grid-integrated energy storage refers to devices or systems connected to the electrical power grid that store energy and can absorb, hold, and dispatch electricity on demand. They provide services such as load shifting, frequency and voltage regulation, reserve power and fast response for integrating variable renewables and improving grid reliability."},{"id":"b8cd55a2-21db-43b4-959b-fa4e6d1f4981","l":"Grid modernization","d":2,"p":"b580bc4d-63fc-4636-9ef6-76e79ea68085","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Grid modernization is the upgrade of the electric power system using advanced sensors, controls, communications, and power-electronic devices to improve reliability, resilience, efficiency, and the ability to manage variable generation and loads. It encompasses technologies like smart meters, synchrophasors, automated distribution, energy storage, microgrids, and cybersecurity to enable real-time monitoring and active control of power flows."},{"id":"0cac8122-b62b-434e-b870-91c68644326b","l":"Grid stability","d":2,"p":"b580bc4d-63fc-4636-9ef6-76e79ea68085","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Grid stability is the power system's ability to maintain synchronized operation and keep voltage and frequency within acceptable limits when subjected to disturbances, so that the system returns to a safe steady state rather than collapsing or losing synchronization. It encompasses transient, small-signal (steady\u2011state), frequency, and voltage stability issues that determine whether generators and loads stay coordinated after perturbations."},{"id":"def71b9e-01fe-4fb4-8973-467199737d13","l":"Gross-Neveu model","d":2,"p":"a1083ad2-a154-449d-a9ff-59bf46db1c7f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Gross\u2013Neveu model is a 1+1-dimensional quantum field theory of N species of massless Dirac fermions interacting via a quartic contact term (\u03c8\u0304\u03c8)^2. It is asymptotically free and exhibits nonperturbative phenomena such as dynamical mass generation and spontaneous (discrete) chiral symmetry breaking, and is exactly solvable in the large\u2011N limit, making it a standard toy model for studying strong-coupling effects."},{"id":"2d57856f-af7e-419a-9478-cfa7cfc7948e","l":"Group theory","d":2,"p":"b5ff1838-0ecd-41a5-a90b-bae4acb69fcf","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Group theory is the mathematical study of groups\u2014sets equipped with a single associative binary operation that is closed, has an identity element, and in which every element has an inverse\u2014and of their representations. In physics it provides the language to describe and classify symmetry operations, derive conservation laws and selection rules, and organize particle and phase classifications."},{"id":"91cd150d-1037-47f0-8d2f-55ab6339241e","l":"GW method","d":2,"p":"55bba34b-0d31-428f-a430-4f502667434a","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"The GW method is a many-body perturbation theory technique that computes electronic quasiparticle energies by approximating the electron self-energy as \u03a3 = iGW, where G is the one-particle Green\u2019s function and W is the dynamically screened Coulomb interaction. It provides dynamical, beyond-mean-field corrections to DFT-like single-particle energies and is widely used to predict band structures, ionization potentials, and electron affinities."},{"id":"333f5a80-40ab-417d-b88e-84c5dbc73892","l":"Hadron-hadron interactions","d":2,"p":"83c64883-5507-43f8-92bc-ae9f5ae03f02","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Hadron-hadron interactions are the forces and reactions between composite particles made of quarks (hadrons, e.g. baryons and mesons), governed primarily by the strong interaction (quantum chromodynamics). They encompass elastic and inelastic scattering, resonance formation and particle production, described at low energy by meson-exchange or effective potentials and at high energy by quark\u2013gluon (parton) dynamics."},{"id":"341af387-764f-4d45-92a5-4e36ea4fc215","l":"Hadronic decays","d":2,"p":"270c18a9-5bde-4c79-9f83-8453d38dc9c8","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Hadronic decays are decays of unstable particles whose final states contain hadrons (mesons or baryons). They proceed via the strong or weak interaction \u2014 when kinematically and quantum-number allowed they occur through the strong force and are typically rapid, involving the hadronization of quarks and gluons."},{"id":"54b448b7-95a7-45d4-a385-5c3d06a423a9","l":"Hadron mixing","d":2,"p":"5adb0193-f741-4713-8010-4270b7bf5d9c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Hadron mixing is the quantum-mechanical phenomenon whereby two or more hadronic states with the same conserved quantum numbers form linear combinations so that the interaction or flavor eigenstates differ from the mass eigenstates, causing oscillations and altered masses/decay properties. Examples include neutral meson oscillations (K0\u2194K\u03040, B0\u2194B\u03040) driven by weak interactions and flavor mixing like \u03b7\u2013\u03b7\u2032 or \u03c9\u2013\u03c6 due to quark-mass and interaction effects."},{"id":"6b01bfb7-7ac0-4fcf-8c75-77ef17a69e91","l":"Hadron production","d":2,"p":"97069ede-42dd-4f4d-b552-9fc8562cb02d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Hadron production is the creation of hadrons\u2014particles made of quarks bound by the strong force\u2014during high-energy processes such as particle collisions, decays, or jet fragmentation. It encompasses the perturbative production of quarks and gluons followed by nonperturbative hadronization/fragmentation governed by quantum chromodynamics, and is quantified by observables like cross sections and multiplicities."},{"id":"9b0eb802-f94a-49e6-82dc-c4a6f2cd656d","l":"Haemodynamics","d":2,"p":"12f6f3a9-8096-40e2-9170-e8f3a45d6bd9","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Haemodynamics is the study of the physical principles governing blood flow in the circulatory system, applying fluid mechanics\u2014pressure, flow, resistance and viscosity\u2014and vessel wall mechanics to quantify cardiac output, pressure gradients and shear stresses. It encompasses the pulsatile, often non-Newtonian behavior of blood, vessel compliance and geometry, and transitions between laminar and turbulent flow across scales from large arteries to capillaries."},{"id":"1765bfd4-ca02-4691-83e7-5a6feb672e53","l":"Hall magnetohydrodynamics","d":2,"p":"2747139a-722e-4167-a03a-e2671889b160","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Hall magnetohydrodynamics is a fluid plasma model derived from the two\u2011fluid (ion+electron) equations that retains the Hall term (J\u00d7B/(en)) in Ohm's law, giving a modified induction equation that distinguishes electron and ion motion. It is important at scales comparable to the ion skin depth or ion gyro-radius, producing dispersive whistler waves and enabling fast magnetic reconnection not captured by ideal MHD."},{"id":"540df786-01cd-4133-a90b-b9bc7d1419b4","l":"Hard-core bosons","d":2,"p":"71e104e2-448f-42ec-a9be-bbaec6758896","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Hard-core bosons are bosonic particles with an effectively infinite short-range repulsion that prohibits more than one particle occupying the same localized orbital or lattice site, so local occupation is restricted to 0 or 1. They obey bosonic commutation relations off-site but behave like spin-1/2 degrees of freedom (and in one dimension can be mapped to noninteracting fermions via the Jordan\u2013Wigner transformation), and are used as a simple model of strongly interacting bosons."},{"id":"4d9a66a1-5678-4093-a9a8-95f8b0662f24","l":"Hard x-ray photoelectron spectroscopy","d":2,"p":"18d10b28-37f1-434d-a95b-19cf71fc6f05","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Hard X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (HAXPES) is an X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy technique that uses high-energy (\"hard\") X-rays (typically a few keV to ~10\u201320 keV) to eject electrons and measure their kinetic energies, yielding binding-energy spectra of core and valence states. Because the higher photon energy increases the photoelectron inelastic mean free path, HAXPES is much more bulk-sensitive than conventional XPS and is especially useful for probing buried layers, interfaces, and deeper core levels for chemical and electronic-structure analysis."},{"id":"7cd899da-2ea4-4b7e-8da3-246542f85b57","l":"Hearing","d":2,"p":"7b59f715-a6b8-4141-b762-668edfb84dc3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Hearing is the physical process by which acoustic pressure waves in a medium are detected and transduced by a biological auditory system into neural signals for the brain to interpret. It involves mechanical-to-electrical conversion (e.g., eardrum, ossicles, cochlear hair cells) and is characterized by sensitivity to sound frequency, amplitude, timing, and spatial cues."},{"id":"e5b13792-7624-446a-89e8-220222c4ffa3","l":"Heart diseases","d":2,"p":"bbcb2483-b22c-4bb6-bb81-beb53ff1b1ed","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Heart diseases are conditions that impair the structure or function of the heart\u2014including coronary artery disease, heart attack, heart failure, valvular disease, arrhythmias, and congenital defects\u2014leading to reduced ability to pump blood and maintain circulation. They result from processes such as atherosclerosis, ischemia, infection, mechanical injury, or disturbed electrical conduction and are often described in terms of altered hemodynamics, tissue mechanics, and electrophysiology."},{"id":"a4da8014-2433-42a4-8994-60a4a1adfd37","l":"Heatbath algorithm","d":2,"p":"eb9bd2e1-eedd-4bd0-997d-58b44ffa3ebb","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics","Networks","Particles & Fields","Physics of Living Systems","Plasma Physics","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The heatbath algorithm is a Markov-chain Monte Carlo update that replaces a chosen degree of freedom by a value drawn exactly from its conditional Boltzmann (thermal) distribution given the rest of the system, guaranteeing detailed balance. It is widely used in spin and lattice simulations as an efficient way to thermalize individual variables and reduce autocorrelations compared with simple Metropolis moves."},{"id":"3d7118e0-4ee6-4f5c-b881-5b520b878bce","l":"Heat treatment","d":2,"p":"c4ed35b0-1090-409c-9667-85f9ac03e9c0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Heat treatment is the controlled heating and cooling of a material\u2014typically a metal or alloy\u2014to alter its microstructure and thereby change physical and mechanical properties such as hardness, strength, ductility, and toughness. It exploits phase transformations, diffusion, and precipitation processes (e.g., annealing, quenching, tempering, case hardening) to achieve the desired properties."},{"id":"3e39ec26-cbf5-485f-8dea-b59222446277","l":"Heavy baryons","d":2,"p":"b0ebe8a2-ed86-4c14-bfdd-5860e15b18bc","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Heavy baryons are baryons (three-quark hadrons) that contain at least one heavy quark\u2014typically charm (c) or bottom (b)\u2014together with light up, down, or strange quarks. Their masses, internal structure, and decay patterns are governed by quantum chromodynamics with distinctive heavy-quark dynamics (the top quark does not form baryons because it decays before hadronizing)."},{"id":"7cd84fc1-1dfc-4868-8269-40f1c7d73fb2","l":"Heavy-ion reaction mechanisms","d":2,"p":"7949bfe4-7221-4501-8608-7b5bd77fbb7a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Heavy-ion reaction mechanisms are the different dynamical pathways by which collisions between medium-to-heavy nuclei proceed, governed by beam energy, impact parameter and nuclear structure. They range from fast, peripheral direct processes (elastic/inelastic scattering, single- or multi-nucleon transfer, breakup, Coulomb excitation) through deep-inelastic and fragmentation, to slow, equilibrated compound-nucleus outcomes (fusion followed by fission or statistical particle emission), each distinguished by timescale and degree of equilibration."},{"id":"7d053db3-4dc3-41d0-bd8c-844bcdb802bc","l":"Heavy mesons","d":2,"p":"5400167e-3abc-44f8-b432-0dc88cab84cf","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Heavy mesons are quark\u2013antiquark bound states in which at least one constituent is a heavy quark (charm or bottom), giving them masses much larger than light mesons; examples include D and B mesons (heavy\u2013light) and quarkonia like J/\u03c8 and \u03d2 (heavy\u2013heavy). They exhibit characteristic spectroscopy and weak-decay behavior that make them valuable probes of QCD in the heavy-quark limit and of flavor/CP-violating weak interactions."},{"id":"f6577a8c-a6d7-49fe-bbda-818d3b15dac0","l":"Heavy quark effective theory","d":2,"p":"74f884e0-6764-4962-a081-b198cca267c3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Heavy Quark Effective Theory (HQET) is an effective field theory for hadrons containing a heavy quark (m_Q >> \u039b_QCD) that systematically expands QCD in powers of 1/m_Q, treating the heavy quark at leading order as a static color source. This expansion exposes heavy-quark spin\u2013flavor symmetries and organizes calculable subleading corrections to processes involving B and D mesons and other heavy hadrons."},{"id":"acd53620-d5ad-4471-b4ee-41d5c9ee52f1","l":"Helicoidal magnetic texture","d":2,"p":"1bd1a9ab-ec4f-40a8-9f81-9f1005587d58","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A helicoidal magnetic texture is a spatially periodic spin configuration in which the local magnetization of constant magnitude rotates continuously as one moves along a given direction, tracing a helical (spin\u2011spiral) pattern with a well\u2011defined pitch. Such textures are typically stabilized by competing exchange interactions or chiral Dzyaloshinskii\u2013Moriya couplings."},{"id":"86b1d1f3-b2fe-4cc4-bc7e-9c9b8cb4f15a","l":"Heliopause","d":2,"p":"7366f21e-d7d6-4f68-b1c2-d7642c6ea4bf","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"The heliopause is the outer boundary of the heliosphere where the outward-flowing solar wind's dynamic pressure is balanced by the pressure of the local interstellar medium, forming a contact discontinuity that separates solar plasma and magnetic field from interstellar plasma and field. Its distance from the Sun varies with solar and interstellar conditions\u2014typically around 100 astronomical units\u2014and is the frontier crossed by spacecraft entering interstellar space."},{"id":"a4f6aac1-33b2-44e9-ad56-bb68dd935b16","l":"Helioseismology","d":2,"p":"f712db32-2bc9-4e97-9f42-a16c12832ff8","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Helioseismology is the study of the Sun's internal structure and dynamics by observing and analyzing its surface oscillations\u2014primarily acoustic (p\u2011) modes excited by convection\u2014whose frequencies and spatial patterns reveal internal sound speed, rotation, flows, and composition."},{"id":"83e02edc-c3bd-40e8-ae65-ce723a4103e6","l":"Helium-3 superfluids","d":2,"p":"9284057b-d22f-4c66-bad2-a840fcc697c9","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Helium-3 superfluids are quantum phases of liquid 3He that form below a few millikelvin when fermionic 3He atoms pair into Cooper-like bound states and condense, producing frictionless (superfluid) flow. These phases\u2014most notably the A and B phases\u2014have spin-triplet, p-wave order parameters that break additional symmetries compared with conventional superfluids/superconductors, giving rise to rich phenomena such as quantized vortices and topological surface excitations."},{"id":"2cdac5c8-5ebd-4dd4-b328-a83e0dfd50e6","l":"Helium-4 superfluids","d":2,"p":"9284057b-d22f-4c66-bad2-a840fcc697c9","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Helium-4 superfluids are the low\u2011temperature phase of liquid 4He that appears below the lambda point (~2.17 K), in which a macroscopic fraction of atoms behaves as a coherent quantum fluid. They exhibit frictionless, irrotational flow with effectively zero viscosity, support quantized vortices and second sound, and are described phenomenologically by a two\u2011fluid model of coexisting normal and superfluid components."},{"id":"2f6d5fc5-d933-436a-ae1e-c599d0ce62e6","l":"Helix-coil transition","d":2,"p":"8da34010-6805-4ff1-93ed-6692da601029","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"The helix\u2013coil transition is the reversible, often cooperative conformational change of a polymer (especially polypeptides and nucleic acids) between an ordered helical secondary structure and a disordered random-coil state, driven by changes in temperature, solvent, pH, denaturants or applied force. It is modeled by statistical-mechanical theories (e.g., Zimm\u2013Bragg, Lifson\u2013Roig) that quantify helix nucleation and propagation and predict the fraction of helical residues and the sharpness of the transition."},{"id":"a0a856f1-da84-42ec-8a4f-6328d983a628","l":"Heterostructures","d":2,"p":"f111f126-342c-48fd-baa7-6124af4142eb","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Heterostructures are systems made by joining layers or regions of two or more different materials so that the resulting interfaces produce electronic, optical, magnetic, or mechanical properties distinct from those of the individual constituents. They are used to tailor band alignment, carrier confinement, strain, and interfacial phenomena in devices such as semiconductor quantum wells, superlattices, oxide interfaces, and van der Waals stacks."},{"id":"498d1139-cbf1-4d02-9dc5-02c4c71b189d","l":"Heusler alloy","d":2,"p":"96973abf-f1b5-44c5-9549-9f0f4dfe234f","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Heusler alloys are a family of ordered intermetallic compounds, typically with compositions X2YZ (full-Heusler) or XYZ (half-Heusler) and characteristic L21 or C1b crystal structures formed from transition metals and main\u2011group elements. They are notable for tunable magnetic and electronic properties\u2014such as ferromagnetism, half\u2011metallicity, shape\u2011memory behavior and topological phases\u2014even when the constituent elements are themselves nonmagnetic."},{"id":"be89f51a-7f1a-4928-bfe5-d22ce28b1342","l":"Hexagonal boron nitride","d":2,"p":"acc87e43-4992-4905-bf8d-10451a110790","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Hexagonal boron nitride (h-BN) is a layered, van der Waals crystal in which boron and nitrogen atoms alternate on a honeycomb lattice analogous to graphite, forming atomically flat two-dimensional sheets. It is a wide-band-gap (~5\u20136 eV) electrical insulator with high thermal conductivity, chemical and thermal stability, and is widely used as a dielectric substrate, lubricant, and insulating layer in nanoscale and electronic devices."},{"id":"9e1f6654-7347-4fbc-8357-ce7a8b17b1ef","l":"Hexagonal close-packed","d":2,"p":"6af5be88-489f-4a50-9abf-998e8912254a","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Hexagonal close-packed (HCP) is a crystal structure in which equal spheres are arranged in close-packed hexagonal layers with an ABAB... stacking sequence, producing hexagonal symmetry. Each atom has 12 nearest neighbors, an ideal c/a ratio of 1.633, and a packing efficiency of about 74%."},{"id":"92eac13b-f2fa-4e1f-a95c-8569cc911bcd","l":"Higher order beam dynamics","d":2,"p":"bb27c0c8-04af-471e-8122-24961354a177","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Higher-order beam dynamics is the study of charged-particle beam motion including nonlinear forces and field components beyond the linear (quadratic-Hamiltonian) approximation. These higher-order effects\u2014from sextupoles, octupoles, wakefields, space charge and beam\u2013beam interactions\u2014produce amplitude- and energy-dependent tune shifts, resonances, chaotic motion, emittance growth and limits on dynamic aperture and beam stability."},{"id":"d3a4e127-e2a1-4d1b-9637-522e2bf75e99","l":"High-harmonic generation","d":2,"p":"a38dc017-3236-4084-afc5-eb59c5de51e0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"High-harmonic generation is a nonlinear, nonperturbative process in which an intense ultrafast laser interacting with atoms, molecules, or solids converts photons into coherent radiation at high integer multiples of the driving frequency, often reaching extreme-ultraviolet or soft-x-ray energies. In gases it is commonly described by the three-step recollision picture\u2014tunnel ionization, acceleration in the laser field, and recombination emitting a high-energy photon\u2014and it is the basis for producing attosecond pulses and tabletop coherent short-wavelength sources."},{"id":"28a7fcb6-1e57-4f5e-a334-e99e2489926c","l":"High-intensity accelerators","d":2,"p":"4eec2798-da70-4fc2-8371-f6aa5f6180d7","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"High-intensity accelerators are particle accelerators engineered to deliver very large particle fluxes\u2014high beam current and high average beam power\u2014typically at moderate to high energies and high duty factor. They serve applications like spallation neutron sources, isotope production, neutrino beams and materials irradiation, and impose stringent requirements on beam-loss control, space-charge management, and target/component cooling."},{"id":"f731534d-8b7d-4d1d-8d6b-e31dab18e3fa","l":"High intensity laser-plasma interactions","d":2,"p":"2917e3bd-b25c-4e89-8796-5c7e77dfda5a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"High-intensity laser-plasma interactions are the nonlinear, often relativistic processes that occur when an ultrashort, tightly focused laser pulse (typically \u227310^18 W/cm^2, so the electron quiver motion is relativistic) ionizes matter and drives strong collective plasma motion via ponderomotive and electromagnetic forces. These interactions produce phenomena such as relativistic electron and ion acceleration, plasma waves and wakefields, high-order harmonic and X-ray emission, and a variety of kinetic and instability-driven effects."},{"id":"fd0f44b5-9098-4988-932d-dae0d0256746","l":"High-order harmonic generation","d":2,"p":"d858d084-693b-468e-b437-3df9c9c7a560","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"High-order harmonic generation is a nonlinear optical process in which an intense laser field driving atoms, molecules, or solids produces radiation at very high integer multiples of the fundamental frequency, reaching extreme ultraviolet and soft X-ray wavelengths. It is commonly explained by a three-step mechanism\u2014tunnel ionization, laser-driven acceleration, and recombination\u2014giving a characteristic plateau of harmonics and a sharp cutoff that enables attosecond pulse generation."},{"id":"389ca5a1-44bd-4ab7-8a7a-b5d23c1df064","l":"High-order harmonic spectroscopy","d":2,"p":"d8d9518f-91ee-40ab-9529-2c3f98431a0f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics"],"def":"High-order harmonic spectroscopy is an ultrafast probing technique that uses the coherent extreme-ultraviolet or soft-X-ray radiation produced by high-order harmonic generation\u2014when intense laser fields drive and re-collide electrons in atoms, molecules, or solids\u2014to interrogate electronic structure and dynamics with attosecond temporal and angstrom spatial sensitivity. By analyzing harmonic amplitudes, phases, polarization, and spectral features, it extracts information on molecular orbitals, photo-recombination cross sections, charge migration, and structural changes."},{"id":"cd90807e-8d7a-4766-96ea-79693d86033d","l":"High-pressure studies","d":2,"p":"8fa26c4d-d115-4c5c-9822-9e90b9248d1b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"High-pressure studies examine how materials respond when subjected to pressures far above ambient, revealing changes in structure, bonding, electronic, magnetic, and transport properties and enabling discovery of new phases. They employ static (diamond-anvil cells, multi-anvil presses) and dynamic (shock and ramp compression) techniques to reach pressures from gigapascals to terapascals for applications ranging from materials synthesis to modeling planetary interiors."},{"id":"a8106f54-62c8-4b1f-ac92-8dedec3561e7","l":"High strain-rate tests","d":2,"p":"b777b985-75b5-420a-9f13-21bb83461999","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"High strain-rate tests are experiments that subject materials to very rapid deformation to measure their dynamic mechanical response (stress\u2013strain behavior, strength, failure modes) under inertial and thermal effects. They typically probe strain rates from ~10^2 to 10^6 s^-1 using techniques such as split-Hopkinson (Kolsky) bars, plate-impact, and Taylor-impact tests to characterize rate-dependent constitutive behavior."},{"id":"b970019a-28f7-4608-bfac-1e736dc651a5","l":"High-throughput calculations","d":2,"p":"55bba34b-0d31-428f-a430-4f502667434a","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"High-throughput calculations are automated, large-scale computational workflows that run many standardized simulations (e.g., electronic-structure or molecular-dynamics calculations) in parallel to rapidly screen materials, molecules, or parameter spaces. They produce reproducible datasets and property databases to accelerate materials discovery, design, and theory validation."},{"id":"92325d9a-53bd-492c-91e3-06f1219f8ea9","l":"Hilbert-Schmidt speed measure","d":2,"p":"fce63f54-4c30-4bd4-b3b6-1a4d58bdaceb","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Hilbert\u2013Schmidt speed is the instantaneous rate at which a quantum state changes, defined by the Hilbert\u2013Schmidt norm of the density-matrix time derivative: S_HS = sqrt[Tr((d\u03c1/dt)^2)]. It is the geometric speed of state evolution in the space of density operators under the Hilbert\u2013Schmidt metric and quantifies dynamical distinguishability and coherence."},{"id":"2997a39f-6276-4d95-b3c1-3151690bd9b6","l":"Hinge states","d":2,"p":"eba4897b-35d4-4eed-9c36-a08ca406d79e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Hinge states are one-dimensional modes localized along the line-like intersections (hinges) between two gapped surfaces of a higher-order topological insulator or similar system. They are topologically protected, often forming robust chiral or helical conducting channels that persist so long as the protecting symmetry and bulk gap remain."},{"id":"24472ba6-068c-4f5d-bc0c-0a41ecf48825","l":"Histones","d":2,"p":"9ccf11df-5b72-4ce0-92e6-e48f8dda172b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Histones are small, positively charged proteins in eukaryotic cells that package DNA by forming nucleosomes\u2014segments of DNA wrapped around histone octamers. Their positioning and chemical modifications tune chromatin compaction and accessibility, thereby influencing transcription, replication, and repair."},{"id":"15455cef-0302-4e0f-be3b-45419e3e7fbb","l":"Holstein-Primakoff method","d":2,"p":"ba55d8a8-d049-4550-b264-530f74618214","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Holstein\u2013Primakoff method is a mapping that represents quantum spin-S operators in terms of bosonic creation and annihilation operators via a nonlinear (square-root) transformation, converting spin degrees of freedom into interacting bosons. This representation enables controlled expansions (e.g., 1/S) and spin-wave descriptions of collective excitations (magnons) in ordered magnetic systems."},{"id":"e35e4dc7-4f59-4f46-bea3-93077accdd34","l":"Homodyne & heterodyne detection","d":2,"p":"a38dc017-3236-4084-afc5-eb59c5de51e0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Homodyne detection is a coherent measurement technique that mixes a weak signal with a phase\u2011locked local oscillator at the same frequency so that the relative phase (field quadratures) is converted into a directly measurable amplitude or photocurrent difference, giving phase\u2011sensitive, high\u2011sensitivity readout. Heterodyne detection mixes the signal with a local oscillator at a different frequency, producing a beat (intermediate) frequency that carries the signal\u2019s amplitude and phase information and enables frequency translation, filtering, and sensitive detection."},{"id":"273474b6-f607-4b62-b5b6-8de8bca28814","l":"Honeycomb lattice","d":2,"p":"8bc71f5b-3de8-4994-8de2-1a300cec36b6","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A honeycomb lattice is a two-dimensional arrangement of sites forming a tessellation of regular hexagons, equivalently described as two interpenetrating triangular Bravais sublattices (i.e., a non\u2011Bravais lattice) with coordination number three. It is bipartite and is the lattice geometry of graphene and many model systems that exhibit Dirac-point band structures."},{"id":"aa77411b-bead-4473-896c-b89577c7e90d","l":"Hormones","d":2,"p":"6c0e8bb5-a5a0-4f28-8d4a-4e7ace85a023","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Hormones are chemical signaling molecules secreted by endocrine glands into the bloodstream or local fluids to reach specific target cells, where they bind receptors and modulate physiological processes such as metabolism, growth, reproduction, and homeostasis. They act at low concentrations with high specificity and can operate via endocrine, paracrine, or autocrine signaling."},{"id":"054da07a-bf9f-4b1b-9e14-b2fd2f80a368","l":"Host-pathogen interactions","d":2,"p":"02260c5f-466c-46d0-a687-826d188b22a4","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Host\u2013pathogen interactions are the dynamic processes by which infectious agents (viruses, bacteria, fungi, parasites) contact, invade, replicate within, and are controlled or transmitted by a host organism, determining infection outcome and disease progression. These interactions span molecular to population scales and are governed by physical mechanisms\u2014molecular recognition and binding, transport and diffusion, fluid and tissue mechanics, immune signaling, and evolutionary dynamics\u2014that are amenable to modeling with statistical physics, dynamical systems, and transport theory."},{"id":"2e1a712a-6c5f-4def-add2-6fdb7f732332","l":"H-polymer","d":2,"p":"2303d382-65bb-4b93-94b8-62a73502ec4b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"H\u2011polymer (homopolymer): a polymer whose chain is made up entirely of identical repeating monomer units; its macroscopic behavior is determined by chain length, conformation, and interactions rather than monomer-sequence heterogeneity."},{"id":"a0ce8ec5-e2c2-40f4-b53f-9fd9e2572289","l":"Hubbard model","d":2,"p":"a088bad1-4904-4f08-a625-a9840fdf32c7","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Hubbard model is a minimal lattice model of interacting electrons (fermions) that captures the competition between kinetic energy\u2014electrons hopping between neighboring sites with amplitude t\u2014and on-site Coulomb repulsion U when two electrons occupy the same site. It is a paradigmatic model for studying correlation-driven phenomena such as magnetism, Mott metal\u2013insulator transitions, and unconventional superconductivity."},{"id":"b334e77b-9216-4ef8-af85-4ffc023d9e2d","l":"Hugoniot measurements","d":2,"p":"3bbe5675-f826-44e6-8e86-f17066618893","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Hugoniot measurements are experiments that determine the locus of final thermodynamic states a material attains after single-shock compression (the Hugoniot curve) by measuring shock and particle velocities or pressure and density and applying the Rankine\u2013Hugoniot conservation relations (mass, momentum, energy) to infer pressure, density and internal-energy changes."},{"id":"250773f3-7403-47d6-aa15-9973bae77e67","l":"Hybrid capacitors","d":2,"p":"c6751f54-a0e7-44a2-aad8-cc8e5a5df651","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Hybrid capacitors are electrochemical energy-storage devices that combine a capacitor-like electrode (fast, non-faradaic double-layer storage) with a battery- or pseudocapacitor-type electrode (slower, faradaic charge storage) to achieve both high power and increased energy density. Examples include asymmetric supercapacitors and Li\u2011ion capacitors, which trade off some cycle life and power of pure capacitors for substantially higher energy than conventional capacitors."},{"id":"ba064101-4ae8-49b2-b947-4538e7064840","l":"Hybrid Monte Carlo algorithm","d":2,"p":"eb9bd2e1-eedd-4bd0-997d-58b44ffa3ebb","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics","Networks","Particles & Fields","Physics of Living Systems","Plasma Physics","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Hybrid (or Hamiltonian) Monte Carlo is a Markov-chain Monte Carlo method that samples a target distribution by augmenting variables with fictitious momenta and generating proposals via approximate Hamiltonian (symplectic) dynamics, then applying a Metropolis accept/reject step to correct discretization errors. This produces long, coherent moves that reduce random-walk behavior and greatly improve sampling efficiency in high-dimensional problems (e.g., lattice field theory and Bayesian inference)."},{"id":"10c6e308-9b27-4540-90f7-b445513d0ab2","l":"Hybrid quantum systems","d":2,"p":"632f74d1-d16b-4b92-a295-b24729a93d8d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Hybrid quantum systems are engineered devices that coherently couple distinct quantum platforms (e.g., superconducting qubits, spins, photons, and mechanical resonators) so each constituent\u2019s strengths\u2014long coherence, strong control, or convenient transduction\u2014can be combined to store, process, or convert quantum information."},{"id":"645d3fa0-5cfd-4a00-a877-673272fa21be","l":"Hydroelectric dams","d":2,"p":"4c12fcde-ed75-4e1c-bd19-253623703054","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Hydroelectric dams are engineered barriers that store water at elevation and convert its gravitational potential energy into mechanical energy by directing flow through turbines, which drive generators to produce electricity. The instantaneous electrical power extracted is approximately P \u2248 \u03c1 g Q h \u03b7, where \u03c1 is water density, g gravity, Q volumetric flow rate, h the hydraulic head, and \u03b7 the system efficiency."},{"id":"81236d07-2535-466b-93f2-e4d502bf5935","l":"Hydrogen production","d":2,"p":"7d448998-51f2-4304-867e-db5833197487","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Hydrogen production refers to the set of physical and chemical processes used to generate molecular hydrogen (H2) from feedstocks such as water, hydrocarbons, or biomass. Common methods include electrolysis, thermochemical steam-reforming and high\u2011temperature cycles, photochemical/biological conversion, and plasma or pyrolysis routes, which differ by energy source and emissions."},{"id":"f0761682-18fc-4076-9921-90eae238bc98","l":"Hydrogen storage","d":2,"p":"7d448998-51f2-4304-867e-db5833197487","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Hydrogen storage is the set of physical and chemical methods for containing hydrogen in a compact, safe, and retrievable form, characterized by gravimetric and volumetric energy densities and the thermodynamic and kinetic conditions required for uptake and release. Typical approaches\u2014compressed gas, cryogenic liquid, adsorption in porous materials, absorption in metal hydrides, and chemical hydrides\u2014are distinguished by their binding energies, phase behavior, and heat-management needs during charging and discharging."},{"id":"9ec242dd-6551-414b-b098-686bd2fdf979","l":"Hydrogen utilization","d":2,"p":"7d448998-51f2-4304-867e-db5833197487","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Hydrogen utilization is the conversion and use of hydrogen as an energy carrier or chemical feedstock\u2014via fuel cells, combustion, or catalytic processes\u2014to produce electricity, heat, or chemicals. It encompasses the physical and chemical processes, reaction kinetics, efficiencies, and system-level constraints (storage, delivery, and integration) that determine how effectively hydrogen's chemical energy is harnessed."},{"id":"36c3b23e-9800-4327-81b7-4363bf63c1a7","l":"Hyperbolic metamaterials","d":2,"p":"77fabbae-1f98-4300-996c-9158ab52e6fc","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Hyperbolic metamaterials are engineered, strongly anisotropic media whose principal components of the dielectric permittivity (or permeability) tensor have opposite signs, so that their isofrequency surface in k\u2011space is a hyperboloid rather than an ellipsoid. This \"indefinite\" dispersion allows propagation of very large wavevectors, giving a high photonic density of states and enabling effects like subwavelength imaging, enhanced spontaneous emission, and anomalous refraction."},{"id":"75cd06a8-fe97-4d94-90dc-82baafa69eb3","l":"Hyperons","d":2,"p":"b0ebe8a2-ed86-4c14-bfdd-5860e15b18bc","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Hyperons are baryons (three-quark hadrons) that carry nonzero strangeness because they contain one or more strange quarks, making them heavier than nucleons. Examples include the \u039b, \u03a3, \u039e and \u03a9 particles; they participate in the strong interaction but are typically unstable and decay via the weak interaction."},{"id":"51ee3dbe-32f4-46e1-8176-aa0f113de997","l":"Hypothetical particle production","d":2,"p":"97069ede-42dd-4f4d-b552-9fc8562cb02d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Hypothetical particle production is the creation of proposed, not-yet-observed particles (e.g., dark-matter candidates, supersymmetric states, axions) in high-energy interactions, decays, or astrophysical processes as predicted by theoretical extensions of the Standard Model. Physicists compute expected production rates and characteristic experimental or astrophysical signatures to guide searches and interpret observations."},{"id":"c4a3f7c2-0093-44f9-849d-4fcba4154486","l":"Ice","d":2,"p":"7b1d5a31-bb4e-4b73-ae62-1c68e9c6006e","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Ice is the solid phase of H2O in which molecules are arranged in hydrogen-bonded crystal lattices (the common form at ambient pressure is hexagonal ice Ih). Its open lattice makes it less dense than liquid water and gives rise to distinct thermal, mechanical, and surface properties, with many high\u2011pressure polymorphs exhibiting different structures and behaviors."},{"id":"ac816133-540a-4562-8130-584df553f46a","l":"Ideal-chain models","d":2,"p":"c267a3f3-0c34-4fc1-9825-947683caf4dd","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Ideal-chain models are simplified statistical descriptions of polymer chains that neglect excluded-volume and energetic interactions between monomers, treating the chain as a random walk of connected segments. As a result, segment orientations are effectively uncorrelated and the mean-square end-to-end distance scales linearly with chain length, yielding a Gaussian end-to-end distribution for long chains."},{"id":"07c38a95-0493-4a11-a22d-0fbe791957ad","l":"Image processing","d":2,"p":"dd01af76-b910-470d-b460-65d6ff3229d3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Image processing is the set of mathematical and algorithmic methods applied to digitally represented images (arrays of pixel or voxel values) to enhance, transform, or extract quantitative information. It includes operations such as filtering, denoising, deconvolution, segmentation, registration and feature extraction, often using Fourier/wavelet transforms and statistical inference to recover or analyze physical signals."},{"id":"70d9878d-c239-4e9c-b8bb-237aeff0612e","l":"Imaging & optical processing","d":2,"p":"a38dc017-3236-4084-afc5-eb59c5de51e0","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Imaging & optical processing is the study and application of how optical systems form, manipulate, and detect spatial distributions of light to create and analyze images. It encompasses lenses, mirrors, holography, Fourier and spatial filtering, and optical computing techniques used for image formation, enhancement, restoration, and pattern recognition."},{"id":"04b9587d-6b1e-41bc-9fd6-682d89321933","l":"Immune system diseases","d":2,"p":"bbcb2483-b22c-4bb6-bb81-beb53ff1b1ed","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Immune system diseases are disorders in which the immune system fails to protect the body correctly, either by being underactive (immunodeficiency), attacking the body's own tissues (autoimmunity), or overreacting to harmless substances (hypersensitivity/allergy). They arise from genetic, environmental, and stochastic perturbations that dysregulate immune cells, signaling networks, and molecular recognition processes."},{"id":"6a5ce358-6687-4078-818e-979f986ebce1","l":"Impact test","d":2,"p":"b777b985-75b5-420a-9f13-21bb83461999","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"An impact test is a mechanical test in which a sudden load (typically from a swinging pendulum or drop weight) strikes a specimen\u2014often notched\u2014and the energy absorbed or the force/time response is measured to assess fracture behavior under high strain-rate loading. Common forms (Charpy, Izod) quantify a material's notch toughness and its tendency to fail in a brittle versus ductile manner, including the ductile-to-brittle transition temperature."},{"id":"8528907b-9690-4c10-8cf7-f8496e5d6f0d","l":"Impurities","d":2,"p":"7060bde3-9f16-411d-9d68-3328cf14b019","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Impurities are atoms, ions, or structural defects in a material that differ from the ideal host lattice and break its perfect periodicity. They modify electronic, thermal, optical, and mechanical behavior by creating localized states, scattering centers, and strain\u2014e.g., intentional impurities (dopants) set carrier concentrations in semiconductors while unintentional impurities introduce disorder and recombination."},{"id":"ee3ed067-588c-427a-a95c-e6b753675cdd","l":"Incoherent beam instabilities","d":2,"p":"3934db16-fe69-4d69-82c8-9bc346490273","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Incoherent beam instabilities are growths in the amplitudes or energy of individual particles within a beam caused by single-particle effects (e.g., nonlinear fields, resonances with lattice imperfections, or broadband noise), rather than by collective motion of the bunch as a whole. They lead to emittance growth, halo formation and particle loss even when the beam centroid remains stable, and are often described in terms of resonant or diffusive single-particle dynamics."},{"id":"3228f572-a635-4497-973a-189d61c518eb","l":"Indentation","d":2,"p":"b777b985-75b5-420a-9f13-21bb83461999","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Indentation is the localized deformation (and often a residual impression) produced when a rigid indenter is pressed into a material under load, with the resulting contact area and depth reflecting elastic and/or plastic responses. It is widely used to probe mechanical properties such as hardness and elastic modulus."},{"id":"0f87b255-81c7-4b82-8de1-92ac98a019af","l":"Inelastic electron tunneling spectroscopy","d":2,"p":"b6850136-0dff-41c9-85da-b6bcf1518811","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Inelastic electron tunneling spectroscopy (IETS) is an experimental technique that probes elementary excitations (e.g., molecular vibrations, phonons, magnons) by detecting energy lost by electrons that tunnel through a barrier and excite those modes. The inelastic events produce characteristic changes in the tunneling conductance\u2014typically steps in dI/dV and peaks in d^2I/dV^2\u2014at bias voltages equal to the excitation energies, enabling identification of the excitations in junctions or STM measurements."},{"id":"43a66577-e319-49fc-9278-22f3d4d5f9ad","l":"Infinite-dimensional symmetries","d":2,"p":"fadc87f6-ee6b-49d6-9128-da9737e4d165","fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Symmetries whose transformation groups or algebras have an infinite number of independent generators, typically labeled by functions or modes rather than a finite set of parameters. They arise in many classical and quantum field theories (e.g., Virasoro, Kac\u2013Moody, and diffeomorphism algebras) and imply infinitely many conserved charges that strongly constrain \u2014 and sometimes exactly solve \u2014 the dynamics."},{"id":"003e2c86-f7d5-4c6c-bc0c-a6748f199578","l":"Infrared spectroscopy","d":2,"p":"8156d7cb-6ab9-4097-be2b-4aa71aad93a3","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Infrared spectroscopy is an analytical technique that measures how materials absorb, transmit, or emit infrared radiation as a function of wavelength or frequency, producing spectra that arise from molecular vibrational and low-energy rotational transitions. Because specific bonds and functional groups absorb at characteristic IR frequencies, these spectra are used to identify chemical composition and molecular structure."},{"id":"97432c80-71c7-4fe7-a27b-885be0a5cbbf","l":"Inhibitory synapses","d":2,"p":"205e6918-12a2-4f4c-9795-8f723f476866","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Inhibitory synapses are neuronal junctions where release of inhibitory neurotransmitters (e.g., GABA, glycine) generates postsynaptic currents that hyperpolarize the membrane or increase its conductance, thereby lowering the neuron's likelihood of firing an action potential. The resulting inhibitory postsynaptic potentials (IPSPs) can subtract from or shunt excitatory inputs, regulating network excitability and timing."},{"id":"6ceec497-12b4-4ec6-a643-268b2c1f161f","l":"Inner-shell processes","d":2,"p":"96384c37-66b9-4126-8aa1-d6e984cbe3c3","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Inner-shell processes are atomic events that involve the core (inner-shell) electrons \u2014 typically their excitation or removal (ionization) and the creation of a vacancy. The vacancy relaxes by radiative transitions (characteristic X\u2011ray emission) or nonradiative channels (Auger and Coster\u2013Kronig electron emission), governing X\u2011ray spectra, electron yields, and energy transfer in atoms and materials."},{"id":"2a71cbba-3674-42b1-8778-4b794f85a844","l":"Inorganic compounds","d":2,"p":"3502cc4a-fa49-4558-90ea-e743a42e00b3","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Inorganic compounds are chemical substances that typically do not contain carbon\u2013hydrogen (C\u2013H) bonds and include salts, metals and alloys, oxides, acids, bases, minerals and coordination complexes. (By convention some simple carbon-containing species such as CO, CO2 and carbonates are also treated as inorganic.)"},{"id":"8bde2d59-01a8-4dbf-83f9-47f332ce97d6","l":"Instantons","d":2,"p":"cf6fcf92-5d05-4722-a1b3-c076e8b22b9e","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Instantons are classical, finite-action solutions of the Euclidean (imaginary-time) field equations that are localized in spacetime and carry a topological charge. They describe nonperturbative tunneling between distinct vacuum sectors and enter the path integral with weight exp(\u2212S_inst/\u0127), producing effects such as vacuum structure, anomalous processes, and other quantum phenomena in gauge theories."},{"id":"b2bddb09-6925-4b5b-ae49-ad0db1bb74a2","l":"Integral equations","d":2,"p":"b5ff1838-0ecd-41a5-a90b-bae4acb69fcf","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Integral equations are equations in which the unknown function appears inside an integral sign, so the solution is a function that satisfies a relation involving integrals with a given kernel and source term. They commonly appear in physics (potential theory, scattering, boundary-value problems), can be linear or nonlinear, and include standard types such as Fredholm (fixed integration limits) and Volterra (variable upper limit) equations."},{"id":"31a5539e-a266-4242-8f21-0422ca28a85e","l":"Integrated optics","d":2,"p":"99f32be9-888e-42c5-ba4a-1fdcea060307","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Integrated optics is the field and technology of making compact on\u2011chip optical circuits that confine, guide, and manipulate light in integrated waveguides and components (couplers, modulators, lasers, detectors) on a common substrate. It enables miniaturized photonic systems for communications, sensing, and quantum technologies, analogous to electronic integrated circuits."},{"id":"67c97948-f373-43ff-a57c-7a8c3977f8e6","l":"Interface & surface thermodynamics","d":2,"p":"609a2adc-099f-463f-9e2a-dd85c0101044","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"The study of how thermodynamic variables (energy, entropy, chemical potential) are modified and localized at boundaries between phases, treating excess quantities associated with surfaces and interfaces (e.g., surface free energy or surface tension, adsorption, and line tension). It describes how these interfacial properties govern equilibrium shapes, capillarity, wetting and interfacial phase transitions and drive phenomena like Marangoni flows."},{"id":"f83f37c9-122a-4bb8-aee5-6ca3951f76bf","l":"Interference & diffraction of light","d":2,"p":"42791fef-2a1a-4002-81dd-c04d56ae9618","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Interference and diffraction of light are wave phenomena in which the superposition of coherent light waves produces spatially varying intensity: interference refers to the constructive and destructive combination of two or more waves with definite phase relationships, while diffraction describes the bending and spreading of a wavefront by an aperture or obstacle, with the resulting pattern arising from interference of the wavelets."},{"id":"b4a12fc5-f18b-4192-94e3-657cb1098bd1","l":"Intermetallic compounds","d":2,"p":"3502cc4a-fa49-4558-90ea-e743a42e00b3","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Intermetallic compounds are solid phases of two or more metallic elements that form an ordered, often stoichiometric crystal structure distinct from a random alloy. They exhibit characteristic mechanical and electronic properties (e.g., high melting points, hardness, sometimes brittleness) due to a mix of metallic and directional/covalent bonding."},{"id":"d881a57d-e82c-4e9f-bae7-59666c64c3e0","l":"Internal friction","d":2,"p":"77d491f8-55ad-47a6-a0a7-cd24ffce7602","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Internal friction is the irreversible dissipation of mechanical energy into heat within a material when it is deformed, arising from microscopic processes such as viscous flow, dislocation motion, and defect or grain-boundary relaxations. It appears as a phase lag between stress and strain (mechanical damping) and is quantified by measures like the loss angle or the inverse quality factor (Q^-1)."},{"id":"03d86469-9cd5-4193-a2d5-80071aa56a54","l":"Internet","d":2,"p":"96ee91b1-3ba0-47ad-b628-6a89155e08e7","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"The Internet is the global system of interconnected packet\u2011switched computer networks that use standardized protocols (notably TCP/IP) to route and exchange digital information. In physics terms it can be treated as a large\u2011scale complex network and communication medium whose signal propagation, queuing, and collective dynamics determine throughput, latency, and emergent behaviors."},{"id":"ebc61885-3aff-44a3-a5ef-7f422bc02c48","l":"Interstitials","d":2,"p":"7060bde3-9f16-411d-9d68-3328cf14b019","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Interstitials are point defects in a crystal where an extra atom (either a host atom \u2014 a self-interstitial \u2014 or an impurity) occupies a space between the regular lattice sites instead of a lattice position. They locally distort the lattice and affect the material's mechanical, electrical and diffusion properties."},{"id":"96574707-0abb-4b9c-a0b5-9c9e39029998","l":"Intersubband polariton","d":2,"p":"69adcacb-6270-4cfc-8cf5-60097ec74d8b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"An intersubband polariton is a hybrid light\u2013matter quasiparticle that forms when a cavity photon strongly couples to an intersubband electronic transition between quantized subbands in a semiconductor quantum well. The resulting mixed photonic\u2013electronic modes show Rabi splitting and inherit properties of both constituents (typically mid\u2011infrared/THz frequency, TM polarization, and collective many\u2011electron character)."},{"id":"8c3c4a35-9604-432d-b61f-e37b6abfd95f","l":"Intracellular signalling","d":2,"p":"c2ea9e2c-0e15-49ee-b336-111976b0b965","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Intracellular signalling is the set of biochemical and biophysical processes by which a cell transmits and processes information internally, converting external stimuli or internal states into coordinated functional responses. It proceeds via cascades of molecular interactions and transport\u2014ligand-receptor coupling, second messengers, protein phosphorylation, ion fluxes, conformational changes and diffusion/active transport\u2014governing metabolism, motility, gene expression and other cellular behaviors."},{"id":"077ec302-bc71-47d1-bedc-2f6b4c50aa5c","l":"Intracellular trafficking","d":2,"p":"c2ea9e2c-0e15-49ee-b336-111976b0b965","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Intracellular trafficking is the regulated transport and sorting of molecules, vesicles, organelles, and macromolecular complexes inside cells, carried out by both passive processes (diffusion, cytoplasmic flow) and active mechanisms (motor proteins moving along cytoskeletal tracks and membrane budding/fusion). It controls the spatial organization and timing of cellular functions such as secretion, endocytosis, organelle positioning, and signal transduction."},{"id":"88ccc6ea-2c99-46fb-863b-a73705da19bd","l":"Intracellular transport","d":2,"p":"c2ea9e2c-0e15-49ee-b336-111976b0b965","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Intracellular transport is the physical movement of proteins, vesicles and organelles inside cells enabled by passive diffusion and bulk flow as well as active, ATP-driven stepping of motor proteins along cytoskeletal filaments. It is a driven, out-of-equilibrium process shaped by molecular crowding, cytoskeletal architecture, and the viscoelastic rheology of the cytoplasm."},{"id":"e3d486ab-904a-40b3-8c79-a9a7dca631b2","l":"Invertebrate development","d":2,"p":"93b8baac-44de-4a26-a175-357ad9d8744c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Invertebrate development is the sequence of cellular, molecular, and morphogenetic events by which animals without a backbone (e.g., insects, mollusks, crustaceans, cnidarians, echinoderms) form from a fertilized egg into larvae and adults. It encompasses cell division, differentiation, pattern formation and morphogenesis driven by genetic programs, biochemical signaling and mechanical forces."},{"id":"dcbc0126-a9a6-4aea-8418-e1ccc3f68d71","l":"Invisible decays","d":2,"p":"270c18a9-5bde-4c79-9f83-8453d38dc9c8","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Invisible decays are particle decay modes whose final-state products do not produce detectable signals in an experiment because they interact too weakly (or not at all) with the detector. They are inferred indirectly from missing energy and momentum or from the kinematics of recoiling visible particles, with common examples being decays to neutrinos or to hypothetical dark-sector (dark matter) particles."},{"id":"1e17d158-54b4-4e04-8970-b1ddc803d306","l":"In-vitro methods","d":2,"p":"566341e3-164a-49b8-93ee-5881dfce1666","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"In\u2011vitro methods are experimental techniques performed on cells, tissues, or biomolecules outside their living organism\u2014typically in test tubes, Petri dishes, or microfluidic devices. They allow study of biological structure, dynamics, and interactions under well\u2011controlled, manipulable conditions, often used in biophysics to isolate specific physical effects."},{"id":"662411a8-5565-4f8d-a5e7-6a9cd3524103","l":"In-vivo methods","d":2,"p":"566341e3-164a-49b8-93ee-5881dfce1666","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"In-vivo methods are experimental techniques and measurements performed inside living organisms to observe, image, or manipulate biological and physical processes in their natural physiological context rather than in isolated cells or tissues. They include noninvasive or minimally invasive approaches such as MRI, PET, optical imaging, implanted sensors, tracers, and electrophysiology, and require attention to biocompatibility, physiological perturbation, and ethical/regulatory constraints."},{"id":"958ff91f-e195-45a6-8b3a-fb4caa321de5","l":"Ion-acoustic waves","d":2,"p":"b39526d9-02d4-4caf-a280-518aed206059","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Ion-acoustic waves are low-frequency, longitudinal electrostatic waves in a plasma in which the ions provide the inertia and the (usually warm) electrons provide the pressure restoring force, so they are the plasma analog of sound waves. Their phase speed for Ti \u226a Te is approximately c_s \u2248 sqrt(k_B T_e/m_i) and they propagate for wavelengths larger than the Debye length, with Landau damping set by particle thermal velocities."},{"id":"5a6cd404-0e5a-4cd1-af74-0f7667366ede","l":"Ion & atom channeling","d":2,"p":"7fb8ecb8-7f22-49e6-bd57-959dc9757c32","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Ion and atom channeling is the steering of energetic ions or neutral atoms through a single crystal along low-index crystallographic axes or planes by the averaged (continuum) potential of lattice rows or planes, which confines particles to channels between atoms and greatly reduces close nuclear collisions and scattering. This directional guidance (and its complement, blocking) governs penetration depths and angular distributions and underlies techniques like ion implantation and channeling analysis."},{"id":"1fc8f216-e853-4b59-bdb2-2ac001bd3ea2","l":"Ion beam analysis","d":2,"p":"1d433fa8-4cdd-4e66-ae51-df1797f18d56","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Ion beam analysis is a family of techniques in which a focused beam of energetic ions (keV\u2013MeV) is directed at a material and the resulting scattered ions, recoils, emitted X\u2011rays or gamma rays, or nuclear reaction products are detected to determine elemental composition, depth profiles, and structural information. Common implementations\u2014such as Rutherford backscattering, elastic recoil detection, particle\u2011induced X\u2011ray emission, and nuclear reaction analysis\u2014provide quantitative, often near\u2011surface, nondestructive analysis."},{"id":"3113bfec-6f9e-400c-9ece-b059ee42e3a5","l":"Ionic conductivity","d":2,"p":"21992cc9-b289-4c96-8daa-2f131e29fb0c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Ionic conductivity is the part of a material's electrical conductivity due to the motion of charged ions: it is the proportionality \u03c3_ionic between current density and electric field produced by ionic carriers, often written \u03c3_ionic = \u03a3_i n_i q_i \u03bc_i (S/m) where n_i, q_i and \u03bc_i are the density, charge and mobility of each ionic species."},{"id":"8e4d9f2c-45f2-4e5c-b910-33b1d819a545","l":"Ion implantation","d":2,"p":"c4ed35b0-1090-409c-9667-85f9ac03e9c0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Ion implantation is a materials-processing technique that introduces selected impurity atoms into a solid by accelerating ions and embedding them beneath the surface. The implanted depth and concentration profile are set by the ion species, energy and dose, and subsequent thermal annealing is usually used to repair lattice damage and electrically activate the dopants."},{"id":"f91bb300-b457-45fb-a396-dff856134f61","l":"Ion-induced tracks","d":2,"p":"7e57ef0c-07de-4919-8fd8-d27d99a1cd6d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Ion-induced tracks are narrow, often cylindrical regions of structural damage left in a solid or polymer by an energetic ion as it traverses the material, produced by localized energy deposition (electronic excitations and/or nuclear collisions) that melts, amorphizes, or displaces atoms along the ion path. These latent tracks can be revealed by chemical etching or microscopy and are exploited for particle detection, materials modification, and nanopore fabrication."},{"id":"2fbfe132-ab66-44c6-846d-0f947b52c8ca","l":"Ion-inertial confinement","d":2,"p":"fd233cf5-7ec1-4c82-90c6-2bad6ab2f982","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Ion-inertial confinement is confinement achieved primarily by the finite mass (inertia) of ions: material or plasma is compressed on a timescale short compared with the ion response time so the ions remain localized by their inertia rather than by steady magnetic or electrostatic fields. A common example is heavy\u2011ion driven inertial confinement fusion, where energetic ion beams rapidly implode a target before it can hydrodynamically disassemble."},{"id":"a310eb1c-8365-4b26-ac30-81c8d3b389da","l":"Ionized molecules","d":2,"p":"42e66168-abfd-4328-aa9d-f6fc3a077f75","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Ionized molecules are molecules that have lost or gained one or more electrons, giving them a net positive or negative electric charge (molecular cations or anions). Ionization alters their electronic structure and reactivity, and often produces radical ions when an unpaired electron remains."},{"id":"ccbebbca-3977-4d49-b4e1-dfb9daae39a0","l":"Irradiation","d":2,"p":"1d433fa8-4cdd-4e66-ae51-df1797f18d56","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Irradiation is the exposure of a material, device, or biological tissue to radiation (particles or electromagnetic waves), producing energy deposition that can cause heating, chemical or structural changes, or induced radioactivity. It is quantified by quantities such as fluence, flux, and absorbed dose."},{"id":"9867cb7d-b9ef-478b-85be-76fa029f4e65","l":"Island","d":2,"p":"d32ebb8f-d6ea-4fbe-8115-d00b2924ef90","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"An island is a small, spatially localized region within a larger system that is isolated or only weakly coupled to its surroundings and therefore exhibits physical behavior distinct from the bulk. Examples include metallic or quantum islands that can host quantized charge states (leading to Coulomb blockade) and islands of stability in phase space where trajectories remain confined."},{"id":"e10552da-abed-45f7-9bcd-a6210b844e68","l":"Isospin violation","d":2,"p":"d0171af5-5603-4934-b23f-d95de0287b81","fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Isospin violation is the breaking of the approximate SU(2) symmetry (isospin) that treats up and down quarks as identical under the strong interaction. It arises primarily from the up\u2013down quark mass difference and electromagnetic effects, and shows up as mass splittings and different decay or scattering rates among members of an isospin multiplet."},{"id":"597f9e94-c843-4191-b4b9-ce001fb240ff","l":"Isotactic polymers","d":2,"p":"20a16e31-69dd-4e1b-9a44-2df3b053d9fd","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Isotactic polymers are stereoregular polymers in which all chiral centers along the backbone have the same stereochemical configuration, so the pendant substituent groups are arranged on the same side of the chain. This regular stereochemistry promotes crystallinity and strongly affects the polymer\u2019s mechanical and thermal properties (e.g., isotactic polypropylene)."},{"id":"2d1f5769-b82c-432c-a7f0-c87af9f9ac44","l":"Isotope shift","d":2,"p":"7eb769ec-4d58-48b3-ac80-5b56c91008ec","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Isotope shift is the small difference in atomic (or ionic) spectral line frequencies between different isotopes of the same element. It arises mainly from changes in nuclear mass (mass shift, including normal and specific contributions) and from differences in nuclear charge distribution or size (field/volume shift) that slightly alter electronic energy levels."},{"id":"362579af-8fcc-44bb-8080-45c6015b20eb","l":"Jaynes-Cummings model","d":2,"p":"cb4dcf82-80bb-4305-94f4-12a11959d840","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"The Jaynes\u2013Cummings model is the quantum-mechanical description of a single two-level system (atom, qubit) coupled to a single mode of the quantized electromagnetic field, typically treated under the rotating-wave approximation. It predicts coherent, reversible exchange of excitations between atom and field and gives rise to entangled \"dressed\" states and phenomena such as vacuum Rabi oscillations and collapse-and-revival of Rabi oscillations."},{"id":"7a75d75d-af07-4747-8c95-74acc833ac2f","l":"Kagome lattice","d":2,"p":"8bc71f5b-3de8-4994-8de2-1a300cec36b6","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A Kagome lattice is a two-dimensional arrangement of points forming a regular pattern of corner-sharing equilateral triangles (equivalently a triangular Bravais lattice with a three-site basis), named after a Japanese woven pattern. Its geometry produces strong geometric frustration and a characteristic band structure with flat bands and Dirac points, giving rise to unusual magnetic and electronic/photonic behaviors."},{"id":"d565a9ce-d39d-41d8-9f6b-4902af924628","l":"Kagome metal","d":2,"p":"721bc5e5-4148-408b-91b5-fb760c1278ab","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A Kagome metal is a crystalline metal whose conducting atoms form a two-dimensional kagome lattice (a network of corner-sharing triangles), imprinting the electrons with characteristic band-structure features such as flat bands, Dirac crossings, and van Hove singularities. These features, together with geometric frustration and spin\u2013orbit coupling, often produce strong electronic correlations, unconventional magnetism, and topological transport or ordered phases."},{"id":"2f8b4de0-4269-4eed-a794-5fff7d59817f","l":"Kardar\u2013Parisi\u2013Zhang equation","d":2,"p":"695da376-c44a-41dd-b968-d200c31e9d5e","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems","Plasma Physics"],"def":"The Kardar\u2013Parisi\u2013Zhang (KPZ) equation is a nonlinear stochastic partial differential equation that models the time evolution of a fluctuating interface height h(x,t): \u2202_t h = \u03bd \u2207^2 h + (\u03bb/2)(\u2207h)^2 + \u03b7(x,t), where \u03bd is a surface tension, \u03bb is the strength of slope-dependent growth, and \u03b7(x,t) is spatiotemporal white noise. It defines the KPZ universality class of nonequilibrium surface growth and fluctuation scaling found in many driven stochastic systems."},{"id":"a8f7402a-6d62-410e-8a9f-b51a6ebfdddd","l":"k dot p method","d":2,"p":"3817eab7-2f24-46f8-ba4a-aa6433722b4a","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The k\u00b7p method is a perturbative technique for computing electronic band structure near a chosen Bloch wavevector k0 by expanding the crystal Hamiltonian in powers of (k\u2212k0) and using momentum-operator matrix elements between Bloch states at k0 to form an effective low-energy Hamiltonian. It provides analytic expressions for nearby band dispersions, effective masses, and band couplings (including spin\u2013orbit effects) around high-symmetry points."},{"id":"7c7a6987-9db9-4bef-9f95-9f2e7b22f57f","l":"Kekule distortion","d":2,"p":"f708cc58-38ab-4944-8e59-aba8cebdece7","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A Kekul\u00e9 distortion is a pattern of bond-length or bond-strength alternation on a hexagonal lattice\u2014originally the alternating single/double bonds proposed for benzene\u2014that triples the unit cell and breaks translational symmetry. In electronic systems such as graphene this bond-density wave couples the two Dirac valleys and typically opens a band gap (acts as a mass term)."},{"id":"1e8c4d80-00c2-42fe-bb79-836367759fe3","l":"Kekule lattice","d":2,"p":"8bc71f5b-3de8-4994-8de2-1a300cec36b6","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A Kekul\u00e9 lattice is a honeycomb (graphene-like) lattice with a periodic modulation of bond strengths (a Kekul\u00e9 distortion) that triples the unit cell and folds/couples the two Dirac points, typically opening a gap in the Dirac spectrum. This pattern thus generates a mass for Dirac fermions and can host topological defects with localized midgap states."},{"id":"0f7a2bd9-50af-4c94-b3be-c98ecdca2e0f","l":"Killing symmetry","d":2,"p":"8eec6905-8553-46b9-881f-5ba007405a6c","fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"A Killing symmetry is a continuous isometry of a (pseudo-)Riemannian manifold generated by a Killing vector field \u03be^\u03bc, satisfying L_\u03be g_{\u03bc\u03bd}=0 or equivalently \u2207_\u03bc \u03be_\u03bd + \u2207_\u03bd \u03be_\u03bc = 0. It preserves the metric (and geodesic structure) and gives rise to conserved quantities along geodesics via p_\u03bc \u03be^\u03bc."},{"id":"a5e662dd-e588-440d-80d3-72f52b8e14ac","l":"Knockout reactions","d":2,"p":"8f33b140-6e59-4b37-b124-bdd0633377d5","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Knockout reactions are direct nuclear collisions in which one or more nucleons are abruptly removed from a fast-moving nucleus (often in inverse kinematics), producing a residual nucleus and ejected nucleon(s). They are used as a spectroscopic tool to probe single-particle structure, shell occupancy, and momentum distributions of nucleons in nuclei."},{"id":"a5d3874d-8288-4339-aac1-0fe6d5cf7aed","l":"Kondo lattice model","d":2,"p":"a088bad1-4904-4f08-a625-a9840fdf32c7","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Kondo lattice model is a quantum many-body model of conduction electrons interacting via on-site antiferromagnetic exchange with a periodic array of localized magnetic moments (e.g., f-electron spins). It captures the competition between Kondo screening, which forms heavy quasiparticles, and RKKY-mediated intersite magnetic ordering, and is used to describe heavy-fermion behavior, magnetism, and quantum criticality in correlated materials."},{"id":"d24d477c-ee6f-4316-9d21-1994fc1bbe12","l":"Korringa-Kohn-Rostoker method","d":2,"p":"3817eab7-2f24-46f8-ba4a-aa6433722b4a","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Korringa\u2013Kohn\u2013Rostoker (KKR) method is an ab initio multiple-scattering Green's-function technique for solving the single-particle Schr\u00f6dinger or Kohn\u2013Sham equations in solids by treating atoms as scattering centers and matching spherical-wave solutions around each site (often using muffin\u2011tin potentials). It is used to calculate electronic band structures and densities of states and naturally handles impurities, alloys and surfaces."},{"id":"469fbbcc-69ab-4c4e-94dc-0dd320acd8e0","l":"Laboratory studies of gravity","d":2,"p":"ef090024-1dd2-47eb-8058-f7e000a440d0","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Laboratory studies of gravity are controlled experiments that probe the behavior of the gravitational interaction on terrestrial (micron-to-meter) scales using apparatus such as torsion balances, atom interferometers, optomechanical resonators, and cold-atom sensors. They aim to measure the Newtonian constant G, test the inverse-square law and the equivalence principle, search for short-range deviations or new forces, and explore quantum aspects of gravity."},{"id":"9c19d585-8eec-411b-af4c-0412fb91e670","l":"Landauer formula","d":2,"p":"f4585613-4c82-4c78-a57d-515fa4774cb6","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Landauer formula: In mesoscopic quantum transport, it relates the electrical conductance (or steady-state current) of a phase-coherent conductor to the quantum transmission probabilities of its scattering channels.  For example, at zero temperature a single channel gives G = (2e^2/h) T, and more generally I = (2e/h) \u222b T(E)[f_L(E)-f_R(E)] dE where T(E) is the transmission and f_{L,R} are the lead Fermi functions."},{"id":"b221bdf7-966c-4f78-8dc9-b9215ae57f6f","l":"Landau-Ginzburg-Devonshire theory","d":2,"p":"05a47dcc-71c0-4ebc-9d4e-79a40a191efb","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Landau\u2013Ginzburg\u2013Devonshire (LGD) theory is a phenomenological continuum thermodynamic framework for ferroelectric (and more generally ferroic) materials that models the free energy as a power-series expansion in the polarization order parameter and its spatial gradients, including electrostatic, elastic, and coupling terms. It is used to describe ferroelectric phase transitions, domain formation, size and boundary effects (e.g., in thin films), and the material's response to applied electric fields and stresses."},{"id":"9230f178-6216-4670-a372-eb98c91925ea","l":"Landau-Ginzburg theory","d":2,"p":"7299559c-15c4-47f3-b866-5633b1030453","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Landau\u2013Ginzburg theory is a phenomenological, coarse-grained field theory for continuous phase transitions that describes a spatially varying order parameter through a free-energy functional built from a local potential and gradient (and, where applicable, gauge) coupling terms. Minimizing this functional gives mean-field (Ginzburg\u2013Landau) equations that predict equilibrium order-parameter profiles, characteristic length scales (e.g., coherence length), and topological defects; the superconducting version uses a complex order parameter coupled to the electromagnetic vector potential."},{"id":"fbf29752-5d43-4b1f-b6d8-36564567004d","l":"Landau-Ginzburg-Wilson theory","d":2,"p":"ba55d8a8-d049-4550-b264-530f74618214","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Landau\u2013Ginzburg\u2013Wilson (LGW) theory is a coarse-grained field-theoretic framework for continuous phase transitions that extends Landau's order-parameter free energy to spatially varying fields and includes fluctuation effects via a local functional (e.g., a phi^4 free-energy). Wilson's renormalization-group treatment of this functional explains critical scaling, universality classes, and how microscopic details renormalize into universal critical behavior."},{"id":"17aed9b6-5b42-4def-8fd1-69a98e6afc5e","l":"Landau-Lifschitz-Gilbert equation","d":2,"p":"ba55d8a8-d049-4550-b264-530f74618214","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Landau-Lifshitz-Gilbert (LLG) equation is the phenomenological equation of motion for a ferromagnet's magnetization vector, describing its precession about an effective magnetic field together with a dissipative (Gilbert) damping that relaxes the magnetization toward the field direction. It combines a gyromagnetic-precession term and a damping term parameterized by a dimensionless Gilbert constant, and is the foundation of micromagnetic dynamics."},{"id":"442448fd-5f0a-4fbc-84b1-20d77cf44db5","l":"Landau-Lifshitz model","d":2,"p":"ba55d8a8-d049-4550-b264-530f74618214","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Landau\u2013Lifshitz model is a phenomenological continuum equation for the time evolution of the local magnetization in a ferromagnet: the magnetization vector precesses about an effective magnetic field and relaxes via a phenomenological damping torque. It underpins micromagnetics (and in the conservative limit reduces to the nonlinear sigma model for spin fields) and is commonly written in Landau\u2013Lifshitz or Landau\u2013Lifshitz\u2013Gilbert form."},{"id":"20a43606-a8fd-4c85-97a2-b7b127f2ba00","l":"Langevin algorithm","d":2,"p":"eb9bd2e1-eedd-4bd0-997d-58b44ffa3ebb","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics","Networks","Particles & Fields","Physics of Living Systems","Plasma Physics","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Langevin algorithm numerically integrates the Langevin equation\u2014Newton's law with a linear friction term and Gaussian white-noise force\u2014to simulate Brownian dynamics and thermalize systems. Its stochastic updates satisfy the fluctuation\u2013dissipation relation so that, at long times, trajectories sample the canonical (constant-temperature) ensemble."},{"id":"0ac46efd-0f43-4786-a998-8f9d0042ffd8","l":"Langevin equation","d":2,"p":"695da376-c44a-41dd-b968-d200c31e9d5e","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems","Plasma Physics"],"def":"The Langevin equation is a stochastic differential equation that describes the time evolution of a dynamical variable (e.g., a particle's velocity or position) under deterministic forces plus a random fluctuating force representing thermal noise, typically together with a dissipative (friction) term. For example, m dv/dt = \u2212\u03b3v + F_ext + \u03b7(t), where \u03b7(t) is zero-mean (often white) noise whose correlations are linked to \u03b3 by the fluctuation\u2013dissipation relation."},{"id":"c55cfd37-51bc-4655-906d-d22ffde417a8","l":"Language networks","d":2,"p":"18aaf314-921a-463e-aacb-1b7898708dc4","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Language networks are graph-based representations of linguistic units (words, phonemes, morphemes, texts) as nodes with edges encoding relationships such as co-occurrence, syntactic dependency, or semantic similarity. They are studied with complex-network and statistical-physics methods to reveal structural properties (e.g., small-worldness, scale-free degree distributions, community structure) and to model language processing, evolution, and information flow."},{"id":"55098dd9-c475-4c59-8f1d-f831c1f08ca4","l":"Lanthanides","d":2,"p":"aa36bec0-0f65-4f5d-bdf2-bc77986b4d0e","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Lanthanides are the 15 metallic elements with atomic numbers 57\u201371 (lanthanum through lutetium) that occupy the f\u2011block of the periodic table and are characterized by the progressive filling of 4f electron orbitals. They, often called rare-earth metals, have similar chemical behavior and strong magnetic, optical, and electronic properties used in magnets, catalysts, and phosphors."},{"id":"9fc47c39-70f6-4fab-a4b9-20ae21c44304","l":"Laser annealing","d":2,"p":"c4ed35b0-1090-409c-9667-85f9ac03e9c0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Laser annealing is a materials-processing technique that uses focused laser pulses or beams to rapidly heat and cool a localized region, causing recrystallization, defect reduction, and dopant activation while leaving the bulk substrate relatively cool. Its ultrafast, spatially selective heating produces non\u2011equilibrium microstructures and precise control of electrical and structural properties in semiconductors and thin films."},{"id":"58f3c112-5d65-4adc-a9fb-2da45d1fe505","l":"Laser applications","d":2,"p":"dfb4980b-4281-4a8f-830b-8822ca3477ba","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams","Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Laser applications are the practical uses of laser light that exploit its coherence, monochromaticity, directionality, and high intensity to perform controlled interactions with matter and information. They span precision manufacturing and materials processing, medical surgery and imaging, optical communication and data storage, spectroscopy and sensing, metrology, and research in nonlinear and quantum optics."},{"id":"79041d71-0f59-4e20-8f7f-12506b616967","l":"Laser dynamics","d":2,"p":"dfb4980b-4281-4a8f-830b-8822ca3477ba","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams","Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Laser dynamics is the study of the time-dependent behavior of lasers arising from the coupled evolution of the optical field and the gain medium (population inversion and polarization). It covers how lasers reach steady state or produce temporal phenomena such as relaxation oscillations, Q-switching, mode competition, mode-locking, pulse formation, and instabilities including chaos, typically modeled by rate equations or the Maxwell\u2013Bloch equations."},{"id":"d5bb259a-1abc-4006-a4c2-ea922c6f46e6","l":"Laser-induced demagnetization","d":2,"p":"466328b7-dffc-4b1c-ace1-668fe7f7dbb8","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Laser-induced demagnetization is the ultrafast reduction or quenching of a material's net magnetization caused by absorption of an intense laser pulse, typically occurring on femtosecond\u2013picosecond timescales. It arises from rapid transfer of energy and angular momentum out of the spin system into electrons, the lattice and external reservoirs via processes such as spin-flip scattering, spin\u2013orbit coupling and spin transport."},{"id":"50d3c708-16b8-483b-b4aa-a9784ee8692b","l":"Laser-induced magnetic fields in plasmas","d":2,"p":"2917e3bd-b25c-4e89-8796-5c7e77dfda5a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Magnetic fields produced when an intense laser pulse drives currents, plasma flows, or anisotropic particle distributions so that Amp\u00e8re's law and non\u2011ideal MHD effects create magnetic flux in the target. Typical generation mechanisms include the Biermann battery (\u2207n\u00d7\u2207T), hot\u2011electron and return currents, ponderomotive/relativistic forces and kinetic instabilities (e.g., Weibel); these laser\u2011induced fields can reach megagauss strengths and strongly modify transport and confinement."},{"id":"f4aeb6ff-0f55-4484-b714-8c35c6a54a84","l":"Laser inertial confinement","d":2,"p":"fd233cf5-7ec1-4c82-90c6-2bad6ab2f982","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Laser inertial confinement is a fusion approach in which intense, pulsed laser beams symmetrically heat and ablate the outer layer of a small fuel capsule (typically deuterium\u2013tritium), driving an inward implosion that compresses and heats the core to extreme densities and temperatures; the fuel's own inertia briefly confines the hot plasma long enough for thermonuclear reactions to occur."},{"id":"1168c67d-38f6-413f-9b21-30109dded59c","l":"Laser light absorption in plasmas","d":2,"p":"2917e3bd-b25c-4e89-8796-5c7e77dfda5a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Laser light absorption in plasmas is the transfer of electromagnetic energy from a laser beam to plasma particles, converting wave energy into particle kinetic and thermal energy and thereby attenuating the beam. This occurs by mechanisms such as collisional inverse bremsstrahlung, resonant absorption at density gradients or critical layers, and collisionless processes (e.g., vacuum heating, ponderomotive effects and Landau damping), with the dominant channel set by plasma density, temperature, laser intensity, wavelength and geometry."},{"id":"757a3d90-7e3a-43fd-be54-3ed9bb920cd5","l":"Laser-modified scattering","d":2,"p":"4054c254-9f83-4a0b-a8d1-1d2a99e11213","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Laser-modified scattering is a scattering process that occurs in the presence of a coherent laser field, where the external electromagnetic wave changes the interaction by dressing the particles, shifting their energies (ponderomotive and AC Stark effects), and permitting absorption or emission of laser photons.  These modifications produce new multiphoton channels, energy sidebands and altered scattering amplitudes and cross sections compared with field-free scattering."},{"id":"e0427998-6f32-4060-9db3-2081161f794d","l":"Laser spectroscopy","d":2,"p":"dfb4980b-4281-4a8f-830b-8822ca3477ba","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams","Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Laser spectroscopy uses coherent, tunable laser light to probe how atoms, molecules, or solids absorb, emit, or scatter light as a function of frequency or time. By analyzing those spectral signatures (absorption, fluorescence, Raman, dispersion, etc.) it yields high-resolution information on energy levels, composition, dynamics, and physical conditions."},{"id":"d0cca2f1-25dd-4239-a266-781aed19bd39","l":"Laser-system design","d":2,"p":"dfb4980b-4281-4a8f-830b-8822ca3477ba","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams","Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Laser-system design is the physics-based engineering of a complete laser, involving the selection and integration of a gain medium, pump source, optical resonator, beam-shaping optics, modulation and control electronics, and thermal/mechanical systems to produce specified optical outputs. It balances performance metrics (wavelength, power, beam quality, pulse duration/repetition rate, stability, efficiency) with constraints on reliability, safety, size, and cost."},{"id":"dedc6dbe-3651-4ad0-9d37-b260e8975adc","l":"Laser techniques","d":2,"p":"a38dc017-3236-4084-afc5-eb59c5de51e0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Laser techniques are experimental methods that exploit the coherence, monochromaticity, directionality, high intensity, and pulse control of laser light to probe, measure, manipulate, or modify matter and electromagnetic fields with high precision. They include approaches such as laser spectroscopy, interferometry, imaging, laser cooling and trapping, ultrafast pump\u2013probe measurements, and laser-based fabrication."},{"id":"9c6fd8a2-7200-4ac5-a615-c96ebc9b5748","l":"Lasing without inversion","d":2,"p":"70cba0b1-a5ec-474b-a023-2ab4c3e484b9","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Lasing without inversion is the production of optical gain and laser action in a medium even though the nominal upper laser level is not more populated than the lower one. It is achieved by creating quantum coherence and interference between multiple transition pathways (e.g., in multilevel atoms via electromagnetically induced transparency or coherent population trapping) that suppress absorption while leaving or enhancing stimulated emission."},{"id":"4301cb15-28d5-42d3-84fb-8935ddc0b754","l":"Lattice gases","d":2,"p":"c8cff238-7425-4c03-996c-41e9cc76aa4b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Lattice gases are statistical-mechanical models in which particles occupy the sites of a discrete spatial lattice and evolve under specified local rules (e.g., hopping, exclusion, creation/annihilation) that conserve or change particle number. They are used to study equilibrium and non\u2011equilibrium phenomena such as diffusion, transport and phase transitions, and in many cases map onto spin models (for example the hard\u2011core lattice gas \u2194 Ising model)."},{"id":"612f2818-37e6-4798-ac55-d67bb4053702","l":"Lattice gauge theory","d":2,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Lattice gauge theory is a formulation of gauge field theories on a discrete spacetime lattice in which gauge fields live on links (as group-valued link variables) and matter fields on sites, preserving exact gauge invariance at finite lattice spacing. It provides a nonperturbative, gauge-invariant regularization of quantum field theories\u2014most notably QCD\u2014enabling numerical evaluation of path integrals to study confinement, hadron spectra, and other strong-coupling phenomena, with the continuum theory recovered as the lattice spacing \u2192 0."},{"id":"3d61a6b2-fc2b-4fdf-b339-3215750643cd","l":"Lattice QCD","d":2,"p":"74f884e0-6764-4962-a081-b198cca267c3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Lattice QCD is a nonperturbative, first-principles formulation of quantum chromodynamics in which space\u2013time is discretized into a finite lattice and the QCD path integral (usually in Euclidean time) is evaluated numerically, typically by Monte Carlo simulation. Gauge fields live on lattice links and quark fields on sites, allowing ab initio calculations of hadron masses, matrix elements, and QCD thermodynamics with controlled continuum and infinite-volume extrapolations."},{"id":"908ef485-0c60-497a-9efb-49190e5dd258","l":"Lattices in beam optics","d":2,"p":"a9c20b4d-69a1-40f1-81c1-86b01859b80c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"A lattice in beam optics is the ordered arrangement of magnets and other beamline elements (dipoles, quadrupoles, sextupoles, RF cavities, etc.) that steer and focus a charged-particle beam along an accelerator or transport line. It defines the periodic transfer map and focusing properties (Twiss parameters, dispersion, betatron tune) that determine the beam envelope, stability, and phase advance through the machine."},{"id":"871a132a-42c7-4e59-8b33-e9fe5654440d","l":"Lattice thermal conductivity","d":2,"p":"1d1e18e6-170c-403e-8786-d06fe58ddc94","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Lattice thermal conductivity is the part of a material's thermal conductivity due to heat carried by lattice vibrations (phonons) rather than by electrons. Microscopically it is given by a sum over phonon modes of their heat capacity times squared group velocity times relaxation time (e.g., \u03ba_L \u2248 (1/3) C v \u2113 in kinetic theory) and typically dominates heat transport in insulators and semiconductors."},{"id":"058ed75d-2876-47c0-ad3b-d1b56d98ace5","l":"Layered semiconductors","d":2,"p":"12721ec5-11d3-484a-9ea9-a7bea1986722","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Layered semiconductors are materials made of strongly bonded atomic sheets stacked and held together by weak van der Waals forces, so their electronic band structure and band gap depend strongly on thickness and can persist down to a single monolayer. They show anisotropic transport and optical properties and include families such as transition-metal dichalcogenides (e.g., MoS2) and black phosphorus, widely used in nanoelectronics and optoelectronics."},{"id":"ff49e0cb-2610-46ab-a27e-ad4b26af23a5","l":"Lenses","d":2,"p":"dcf48409-af3f-4517-b58d-7ec6c95351f3","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Lenses are transparent optical elements with one or more curved surfaces that refract light to form or modify images. They are broadly classified as converging (convex) or diverging (concave) and are characterized by a focal length that determines how they focus or spread rays, with imaging described by the thin-lens equation."},{"id":"81656f64-b523-41ae-a472-d05617c2822b","l":"Lepton-hadron interactions","d":2,"p":"83c64883-5507-43f8-92bc-ae9f5ae03f02","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Lepton-hadron interactions are scattering or collision processes in which leptons (electrons, muons, neutrinos) interact with hadrons (protons, neutrons, nuclei), and are used as clean probes of hadronic structure. They proceed via electroweak exchanges (virtual photons or W/Z bosons) and are characterized by form factors at low momentum transfer and by parton distribution functions in deep inelastic scattering."},{"id":"00e25a44-c2f9-43fe-a15f-42597b64e665","l":"Leptonic, semileptonic & radiative decays","d":2,"p":"270c18a9-5bde-4c79-9f83-8453d38dc9c8","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Leptonic decays are processes in which a hadron decays entirely to leptons (typically a charged lepton and a neutrino) via the weak charged current, while semileptonic decays produce both leptons (a charged lepton and neutrino) and hadronic remnants, probing a combination of weak and strong (hadronic) dynamics. Radiative decays are decays that emit one or more photons (\u03b3), arising from electromagnetic radiation or radiative corrections to weak processes (e.g., B+\u2192\u2113+\u03bd, B\u2192D\u2113\u03bd, B\u2192K*\u03b3)."},{"id":"4a0d8991-1666-4f13-b745-e257217742f7","l":"Lepton-lepton interactions","d":2,"p":"83c64883-5507-43f8-92bc-ae9f5ae03f02","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Lepton-lepton interactions are processes in which two leptons exchange force carriers\u2014photons for charged leptons and W\u00b1/Z0 bosons for weak interactions\u2014producing scattering, annihilation, or pair\u2011production. Neutrinos interact only via the weak force while charged leptons also interact electromagnetically; these processes are described by quantum electrodynamics and the electroweak theory."},{"id":"c56503ef-aa50-411c-a77a-77b6e10bc38e","l":"Lepton production","d":2,"p":"97069ede-42dd-4f4d-b552-9fc8562cb02d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Lepton production is the creation of leptons (electrons, muons, taus and their neutrinos, plus antiparticles) in particle interactions or decays. It occurs through electromagnetic or weak processes\u2014examples include photon pair production, Drell\u2013Yan processes, and W/Z boson or heavy-hadron decays in high-energy collisions."},{"id":"98e51ea6-9105-474d-97af-52541d6150d1","l":"Levy flights","d":2,"p":"b52b4f11-cad6-4cea-997f-36a603349fea","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"L\u00e9vy flights are random walks whose step lengths are independent draws from a L\u00e9vy (\u03b1\u2011stable) heavy\u2011tailed distribution\u2014typically with a power\u2011law tail \u221d|x|^{\u22121\u2212\u03b1} (0<\u03b1<2)\u2014so the trajectory contains rare, arbitrarily large jumps and exhibits non\u2011Gaussian, superdiffusive behavior. For \u03b1=2 the distribution becomes Gaussian and the process reduces to ordinary Brownian motion, while \u03b1<2 gives infinite variance and anomalous transport."},{"id":"0a37f3c2-43fd-48fa-a630-85e5b860cda5","l":"Lifetimes & widths","d":2,"p":"7de7a12f-2bec-4394-98aa-26aebe36d88f","fl":["Properties","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Nuclear Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Lifetime and width describe unstable quantum states: the lifetime \u03c4 is the mean time before decay, and the width \u0393 is the energy (or mass) spread of the resonance, often given as the full width at half maximum of the spectral line. They are related by \u0393 = \u0127/\u03c4, a manifestation of the energy\u2013time uncertainty principle, so a shorter lifetime implies a broader energy width."},{"id":"0841db0c-24b2-4074-9ba3-a5b6243699b5","l":"Light mesons","d":2,"p":"5400167e-3abc-44f8-b432-0dc88cab84cf","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Light mesons are hadrons consisting of a quark\u2013antiquark pair made from the lightest quark flavors (up, down, and strange). They have masses set by the QCD confinement scale (hundreds of MeV) and include examples such as the pions, kaons, \u03b7 and the light vector mesons (\u03c1, \u03c9, \u03c6)."},{"id":"07396f85-99a2-483d-be5b-3725e182caa3","l":"Light propagation, transmission & absorption","d":2,"p":"42791fef-2a1a-4002-81dd-c04d56ae9618","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Light propagation, transmission & absorption describes how electromagnetic radiation travels through space and materials and how its amplitude, direction, phase and spectrum are altered by interactions with matter (reflection, refraction, scattering, dispersion). Transmission is the portion that passes through a medium, while absorption is the conversion of photon energy into other forms (electronic excitation, vibrational energy, heat), causing attenuation quantified by the absorption coefficient and often described by the Beer\u2013Lambert law."},{"id":"382676e6-fd8f-4fc6-b8e0-3abbc45c379c","l":"Light scattering","d":2,"p":"a38dc017-3236-4084-afc5-eb59c5de51e0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Light scattering is the process by which electromagnetic waves are deflected or redistributed in direction, intensity, phase, or frequency when they interact with particles, atoms, or refractive-index variations. Its angular and spectral characteristics depend on the scatterer size, shape and composition relative to the light wavelength, leading to elastic (e.g., Rayleigh, Mie) or inelastic (e.g., Raman, Compton) scattering."},{"id":"2d830d01-53ef-49ef-a211-3c553fe19a45","l":"Light stopping & storage","d":2,"p":"3123cbf9-460d-467a-9306-b4fb316daddd","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Light stopping and storage refers to techniques that dramatically reduce the group velocity of an optical pulse or coherently map its quantum state onto a long-lived excitation of a material medium so the pulse is effectively halted and later retrieved. This is typically done by coherent control of atomic ensembles (e.g., electromagnetically induced transparency or Raman protocols) to transfer photons to and from collective spin-wave excitations for optical buffering and quantum memory."},{"id":"f5025176-7b3c-435b-bc7e-231b608bfe9b","l":"Light therapy","d":2,"p":"a24a2d8a-58a4-4cbc-81aa-4cd953e73f4b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Light therapy is the deliberate application of specific wavelengths and intensities of electromagnetic radiation to biological tissue to produce therapeutic effects. Depending on wavelength and dose, mechanisms include photochemical/photobiomodulation (red/near\u2011IR for wound healing and pain), circadian entrainment by bright visible light (for mood and sleep disorders), and UV\u2011driven effects on skin or microbes."},{"id":"82d76c74-6a06-41db-96bc-469c4b9c9b19","l":"Limb development","d":2,"p":"93b8baac-44de-4a26-a175-357ad9d8744c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Limb development is the embryonic process that builds an animal limb by coordinating cell proliferation, differentiation and spatial patterning through gene regulatory networks and morphogen gradients (e.g., FGF, SHH) that specify axes and fate. Mechanical forces and growth dynamics interact with these biochemical signals to shape tissues and assemble skeletal, muscle and connective structures."},{"id":"2d000324-694a-4e7a-b5ac-9835da9e7cb0","l":"Linear accelerators","d":2,"p":"226db421-ad24-4e1c-9fbf-5de508711fa2","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Linear accelerators (linacs) are devices that accelerate charged particles in a straight line by passing them through a sequence of synchronized, time-varying electric fields (typically in RF cavities or drift tubes), increasing their kinetic energy each stage while magnetic elements focus and steer the beam. They produce high-energy electrons, protons, or ions for particle physics, medical radiotherapy, and industrial applications."},{"id":"4d50757e-0e54-4d04-ba84-7fac9ca8bf5f","l":"Linear polymer","d":2,"p":"2303d382-65bb-4b93-94b8-62a73502ec4b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"A linear polymer is a macromolecule composed of repeating monomer units covalently linked end-to-end in a single unbranched chain with two free ends and no branching or cross\u2011links. Its large-scale conformation and mechanics are characterized by parameters like degree of polymerization, contour length and persistence length and are often modeled as random\u2011walk (Gaussian or self\u2011avoiding) chains that can entangle in melts or solutions."},{"id":"21e4b843-87bb-44d1-ba7c-a6ccc0164fd2","l":"Line defects","d":2,"p":"3331008f-47b2-4908-92ad-bc9bd7cd815e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Line defects are one-dimensional, localized interruptions in the arrangement or order parameter of a material or field whose singular core extends along a line through the medium. Examples include dislocations and disclinations in crystals, vortex lines in superfluids and superconductors, and cosmic strings in field theory, and they often carry topological stability and long-range elastic or gauge fields."},{"id":"27c35343-1c27-4be9-b1a9-53eb9fac6712","l":"Lineshapes & shifts","d":2,"p":"e4236efc-fc7b-4d50-bc84-76ddf5e65856","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Lineshapes and shifts describe the profile and central-frequency displacement of spectral lines: the lineshape is the intensity versus frequency (or wavelength) distribution\u2014commonly Lorentzian (finite lifetime), Gaussian (Doppler), or a Voigt combination\u2014set by factors such as quantum lifetime, thermal motion, collisions and instrumental broadening. Shifts are changes of the line center produced by external fields, interactions or relative motion (e.g., Stark, Zeeman, pressure, Doppler, Lamb or cosmological redshift) and provide diagnostics of the emitter/absorber environment."},{"id":"3817026e-e4e2-4725-9742-f738536e5d7f","l":"Lipids","d":2,"p":"6c0e8bb5-a5a0-4f28-8d4a-4e7ace85a023","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Lipids are a class of predominantly hydrophobic or amphipathic organic molecules (e.g., fatty acids, triglycerides, phospholipids, sterols) that in water self\u2011assemble into micelles, bilayers and vesicles to form cell membranes and store energy. Their collective behavior is driven by the hydrophobic effect, van der Waals interactions and headgroup electrostatics, producing distinct mesoscopic phases and mechanical properties relevant to biophysics."},{"id":"858c439c-984d-429a-afa1-9e816eb0ccdb","l":"Liquid crystal lasers","d":2,"p":"cd4c012e-bacf-4bcf-8358-a6573a330ec4","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Liquid crystal lasers are lasers that use liquid-crystal materials either as the gain medium (for example dye-doped LCs) or as the resonant/feedback element, exploiting their anisotropy and reconfigurable order to control light amplification. A common example is chiral (cholesteric) liquid crystals, which form a self-assembled one-dimensional photonic crystal that provides wavelength-selective reflection and enables mirrorless, electrically or thermally tunable coherent emission."},{"id":"c2ebb1e2-8ef8-43dd-8711-b278b7b4ea86","l":"Liquid crystal phase transitions","d":2,"p":"19f26688-0955-4fd9-85d8-aaf71c4a581f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Liquid crystal phase transitions are transformations between different mesophases of anisotropic fluids\u2014e.g., nematic, smectic, cholesteric and isotropic\u2014marked by changes in molecular orientational and/or positional order. They are driven by temperature, concentration or external fields, described by order parameters (director or tensor fields), and can be first- or second-order transitions that break symmetries and involve characteristic critical behavior and topological defects."},{"id":"ee66f45e-7097-4dd2-b973-dd82c0d42ac0","l":"Liquid-gas phase transition","d":2,"p":"19f26688-0955-4fd9-85d8-aaf71c4a581f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The liquid\u2013gas phase transition is the thermodynamic transformation between a dense, condensed liquid and a low-density vapor, typically a first-order transition marked by a discontinuous jump in density and absorption or release of latent heat along a coexistence curve in the pressure\u2013temperature plane. This coexistence line ends at a critical point where the density difference vanishes and the transition becomes continuous, exhibiting critical fluctuations and universal scaling behavior."},{"id":"68bb3b4f-c58b-4efb-8500-325b23986472","l":"Liquid helium cooling","d":2,"p":"22b17e02-28a0-4b88-ae3b-2520aa22cfb9","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Liquid helium cooling is a cryogenic technique that uses liquid helium (typically 4He) as a refrigerant to reach and maintain temperatures near 4.2 K at atmospheric pressure and substantially lower (\u22481 K by pumping 4He, or millikelvin range using 3He/4He dilution) for experiments. It provides excellent thermal contact and low-temperature stability for studies of superconductivity, quantum fluids, and other low-temperature phenomena."},{"id":"6605a120-b9af-4162-b9b5-28f6575f8183","l":"Liquid-liquid interfaces","d":2,"p":"628f7190-cb71-4692-8cda-ebc544f9e9fb","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A liquid\u2013liquid interface is the narrow boundary region separating two coexisting liquid phases of different composition or density (for example oil and water), across which properties like composition and density change. Molecular interactions at the interface produce an interfacial tension and control phenomena such as capillarity, wetting, Marangoni flows, and selective mass transfer."},{"id":"1f768dc4-f406-4a68-ac8e-7a957c3fd741","l":"Liquid-liquid phase transition","d":2,"p":"19f26688-0955-4fd9-85d8-aaf71c4a581f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A liquid\u2013liquid phase transition is a reversible phase change in which a fluid of fixed composition transforms between two distinct liquid states (often differing in density and local structure, e.g., high\u2011density and low\u2011density liquids) as temperature or pressure is varied; it is typically first\u2011order with a coexistence line that can terminate at a liquid\u2011liquid critical point."},{"id":"5b2dc8be-322e-4f59-9763-f35ae2c8e1c7","l":"Liquid metals","d":2,"p":"721bc5e5-4148-408b-91b5-fb760c1278ab","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Liquid metals are metallic elements or alloys in the liquid state that retain metallic bonding and delocalized conduction electrons, giving them high electrical and thermal conductivity, characteristic reflectivity, high density, and relatively large surface tension. Examples include mercury, gallium, and Na\u2013K alloys, and they are widely used in heat transfer, cooling, and magnetohydrodynamic applications."},{"id":"4d4eef95-216c-4cb4-8194-829ce45909bf","l":"Liquid nitrogen cooling","d":2,"p":"22b17e02-28a0-4b88-ae3b-2520aa22cfb9","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Liquid nitrogen cooling is the use of liquid N2 as a cryogenic coolant to remove heat from objects by conduction, convection and especially by absorbing the latent heat of vaporization, bringing them down to near the liquid's boiling point (\u224877 K at 1 atm). Because cooling is governed by the boil-off temperature, it is widely used for cryopreservation, superconducting devices, low-temperature experiments, and industrial chilling."},{"id":"3f172ca2-7eb2-47a7-8beb-702e9855be1b","l":"Liquid-solid interfaces","d":2,"p":"628f7190-cb71-4692-8cda-ebc544f9e9fb","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A liquid\u2013solid interface is the boundary region where a liquid contacts a solid surface, often exhibiting molecular-scale ordering and thermodynamic and transport properties distinct from the bulk phases. Its interfacial energy, wetting (contact angle), slip, adsorption, and capillary forces govern adhesion, friction, heat and mass transfer, and heterogeneous nucleation at the interface."},{"id":"29018f04-ccd5-4474-9568-d6a0c6430720","l":"Liquid-solid phase transition","d":2,"p":"19f26688-0955-4fd9-85d8-aaf71c4a581f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Liquid\u2013solid phase transition is the change between a disordered, fluid liquid and a rigid solid driven by variations in thermodynamic variables (temperature, pressure, composition), most commonly at the melting/freezing point. It is typically a first\u2011order transition that involves latent heat, a discontinuous change in density and symmetry as the material orders into a crystal (or an amorphous solid), and proceeds kinetically by nucleation and growth of the solid phase."},{"id":"8fb12d19-b24e-4e37-b3d2-63abf78d4b4f","l":"Lithography","d":2,"p":"c4ed35b0-1090-409c-9667-85f9ac03e9c0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Lithography is a micro- and nanofabrication process that transfers a designed pattern onto a substrate by exposing a radiation- or particle-sensitive resist (via a mask or direct-write beam), developing the resist to reveal the pattern, and then etching or depositing to form physical structures. It encompasses methods such as photolithography, electron-beam, EUV, and nanoimprint lithography and is the core technique for making semiconductor devices and MEMS with feature sizes from micrometers down to nanometers."},{"id":"ec7bdbfa-adc2-4597-ac5f-47dbc90bb38c","l":"Local density of states","d":2,"p":"aaff2519-9891-4d4e-a067-1ea36b6b08d4","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The local density of states (LDOS) at position r and energy E is the number of quantum states per unit energy (and per unit volume for continuous systems) available at that location, weighted by the probability amplitude of those states; formally \u03c1(r,E)=\u2211n |\u03c8n(r)|^2 \u03b4(E\u2212En). Equivalently, in Green's-function language \u03c1(r,E)=\u2212(1/\u03c0)Im G(r,r;E+i0+), and the LDOS determines spatially resolved tunneling and spectral properties."},{"id":"c70be9dc-5b72-4658-8612-46285fccc67b","l":"London theory","d":2,"p":"7299559c-15c4-47f3-b866-5633b1030453","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"London theory is a phenomenological description of superconductivity, introduced by the London brothers, that relates the superconducting current to the electromagnetic fields through two linear \"London equations.\" It explains perfect conductivity and the Meissner effect by predicting that magnetic fields are expelled and decay exponentially inside a superconductor over the characteristic London penetration depth."},{"id":"f0026805-92f5-4d4d-8010-c5939dc373b8","l":"Loop quantum gravity","d":2,"p":"6d5b9a32-cad0-4d9f-a837-b0f239f5cdc6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Loop quantum gravity is a background-independent, nonperturbative approach to quantizing general relativity that represents quantum states of spacetime geometry as networks of discrete excitations (spin networks) and their histories (spin foams). It predicts that geometric quantities like area and volume have discrete spectra, implying a fundamentally granular structure of space at the Planck scale."},{"id":"e4a7e919-7780-4083-9f69-9037a6703d76","l":"Lorentz symmetry","d":2,"p":"8eec6905-8553-46b9-881f-5ba007405a6c","fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Lorentz symmetry is the invariance of physical laws under Lorentz transformations\u2014rotations and constant-velocity boosts that mix space and time and preserve the Minkowski spacetime interval. It is the mathematical expression of special relativity and underlies relativistic field theories (group SO(1,3)/O(1,3))."},{"id":"ad7c8f90-40a5-4310-a596-81368f4b7313","l":"Loschmidt echo","d":2,"p":"e8e055f8-8711-4305-9995-7b3789b1a63b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Nonlinear Dynamics"],"def":"The Loschmidt echo quantifies the sensitivity of a quantum evolution to perturbations by measuring the fidelity with which an initial state |\u03c8\u27e9 is recovered after forward evolution under Hamiltonian H for time t followed by imperfect time reversal under a perturbed Hamiltonian H\u2032. Mathematically L(t)=|\u27e8\u03c8| e^{iH\u2032t/\u0127} e^{-iHt/\u0127} |\u03c8\u27e9|^2, and it is used to study quantum irreversibility, decoherence, and the quantum\u2013classical correspondence."},{"id":"64ff1d76-484a-4548-a1c5-495094710fcc","l":"Lunar atmosphere","d":2,"p":"99668e65-fdae-43c5-b265-3b3af92ccddc","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"The lunar atmosphere is the Moon's extremely tenuous, surface-bounded exosphere composed of trace neutral atoms and molecules (e.g., He, Ar, Ne, Na, K, H, O) and occasional radioactive species. Its constituents are produced by solar-wind implantation, outgassing, sputtering and micrometeoroid vaporization and follow ballistic, collisionless trajectories, so the Moon effectively has a near-vacuum rather than a collisional atmosphere."},{"id":"a963330e-3018-400c-9ceb-4ff84b3e1d5d","l":"Lunar dust & plasma","d":2,"p":"99668e65-fdae-43c5-b265-3b3af92ccddc","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Lunar dust & plasma refers to the coupled system of the Moon's fine, electrostatically active regolith particles and the ambient space plasma (solar wind, photoelectrons) near the surface. Their interactions\u2014charging by solar UV and plasma, electrostatic levitation and transport, adhesion, and dusty-plasma dynamics\u2014govern dust mobilization, surface charging, and hazards to instruments and habitats."},{"id":"8e01f598-1218-403c-896b-bcb48bd89fb5","l":"Lunar magnetic field","d":2,"p":"99668e65-fdae-43c5-b265-3b3af92ccddc","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"The lunar magnetic field is the magnetic field observed at and around the Moon, arising mainly from remanent (fossil) magnetization locked in lunar crustal rocks and from weak, transient fields induced by interaction with the solar wind. Unlike Earth, the Moon today lacks a strong global dipole and instead exhibits patchy magnetic anomalies (typically tens to hundreds of nanotesla) that record past dynamo activity or impact-related magnetization."},{"id":"9d3798a1-53ad-49fc-8db2-35bb1e482356","l":"Luttinger\u2013Kohn model","d":2,"p":"3817eab7-2f24-46f8-ba4a-aa6433722b4a","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Luttinger\u2013Kohn model is a multiband k\u00b7p effective-mass Hamiltonian for carriers near the Brillouin-zone center of a semiconductor that captures band mixing and spin\u2013orbit coupling.  It parametrizes valence-band structure (giving heavy\u2011 and light\u2011hole dispersions and anisotropic effective masses) via a small set of Luttinger parameters."},{"id":"554a3641-e99f-440d-9b32-8f85a751372c","l":"Luttinger liquid model","d":2,"p":"05a47dcc-71c0-4ebc-9d4e-79a40a191efb","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Luttinger liquid model is the universal low-energy theory of interacting fermions (and bosons) in one dimension, in which excitations are collective bosonic density waves rather than Landau quasiparticles. It predicts spin\u2013charge separation and interaction-dependent power-law correlation functions set by one or more Luttinger parameters."},{"id":"840732ad-c7c9-473a-ba00-36d49bcb0cc2","l":"Luttinger's theorem","d":2,"p":"78d12677-c7dc-427e-acd7-cbff78a1713f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Luttinger's theorem states that for an interacting fermion system that does not break symmetries and is adiabatically connected to a noninteracting Fermi gas, the momentum-space volume enclosed by the Fermi surface (counting spin, modulo completely filled bands) is fixed by the total particle density and is unchanged by interactions. It is most naturally expressed via the zero-frequency single-particle Green's function (Luttinger\u2013Ward formalism) and can fail in certain non-Fermi-liquid or symmetry-broken phases."},{"id":"1df9e0ff-ebbd-4b34-8b7b-620228686906","l":"Mach-Zehnder atom interferometry","d":2,"p":"88cd30f5-62cf-441c-b7a3-8fd2f7f0fac5","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Mach\u2013Zehnder atom interferometry is the matter-wave analogue of the optical Mach\u2013Zehnder interferometer in which coherent atomic wavepackets are split, redirected (by light pulses or diffraction gratings) and recombined to produce interference that depends on the phase difference accumulated along the two paths. It is used to sensitively measure inertial effects, gravitational potentials, and external fields by converting those influences into measurable shifts of the interference fringes."},{"id":"2fa73daf-60e6-4b31-bb3e-14b07160a900","l":"Magnetic domains","d":2,"p":"1bd1a9ab-ec4f-40a8-9f81-9f1005587d58","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Magnetic domains are microscopic regions within ferromagnetic or ferrimagnetic materials in which the atomic magnetic moments are uniformly aligned by exchange interactions, producing a nearly uniform local magnetization. Adjacent domains point in different directions to reduce the material's total magnetic (demagnetizing and anisotropy) energy, and they are separated by narrow transition zones called domain walls."},{"id":"23c4db79-e8ca-4e08-bac8-35c4553d40b9","l":"Magnetic levitation","d":2,"p":"0003fc3f-1522-4fca-bf3f-bcef0dcec405","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Magnetic levitation is the suspension of an object above a surface by magnetic forces that counteract gravity, eliminating mechanical contact. It is realized using repulsive or attractive fields from permanent magnets, diamagnetic materials or superconductors (Meissner effect), or by active electromagnetic/eddy\u2011current techniques, and often requires active stabilization because static arrangements of ordinary magnets are unstable (Earnshaw\u2019s theorem)."},{"id":"2b9cd833-bb64-4a36-9781-4a62446bbfc9","l":"Magnetic mirrors & traps","d":2,"p":"5b4f3bb4-8db6-4156-a472-4c9988d6e313","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Magnetic mirrors are regions where a charged particle moving along a nonuniform magnetic field is reflected from stronger-field regions because its magnetic moment \u03bc = m v_\u22a5^2/(2B) is approximately conserved, causing the parallel velocity to reverse. Magnetic traps are engineered field configurations (mirror machines, magnetic bottles, or atom traps) that exploit this mirror effect or the force on magnetic moments in field gradients to confine charged particles or magnetically susceptible neutral atoms."},{"id":"3af8188d-9bbc-4acb-b259-18671f6714cd","l":"Magnetic nanoparticles","d":2,"p":"4fd30a9d-7de5-44a0-82dd-d4b18d88721b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Magnetic nanoparticles are particles with at least one dimension in the nanometer scale (\u22481\u2013100 nm) made from ferromagnetic or ferrimagnetic materials (e.g., Fe, Co, Ni, Fe3O4) whose small size produces single-domain behavior and strong surface- and finite-size effects. Thermal fluctuations can flip their net moment above a characteristic blocking temperature (superparamagnetism) while below it they behave as blocked single-domain particles, giving rise to pronounced size- and temperature-dependent magnetic properties used in data storage, biomedicine, and catalysis."},{"id":"a4491b18-7805-4bd3-8fe3-ea645cc727d9","l":"Magnetic reconnection","d":2,"p":"9bbcc3c5-16bb-4a7f-adea-c23acc893cde","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Magnetic reconnection is a plasma process in which magnetic field lines of opposite direction are brought together, break, and reconnect, changing the magnetic topology. This rapid rearrangement converts stored magnetic energy into plasma heating, bulk flows, and particle acceleration, and powers events like solar flares and magnetospheric substorms."},{"id":"1a42ad9f-ddc6-4181-bfd8-981f0bf84355","l":"Magnetic resonance imaging","d":2,"p":"fee9296a-01ca-4b80-86bf-2c51f37c1871","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Networks","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Physics of Living Systems","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is a noninvasive tomographic imaging technique that detects the nuclear magnetic resonance of atomic nuclei\u2014primarily hydrogen protons\u2014placed in a strong static magnetic field, excited by radio\u2011frequency pulses, and spatially encoded by magnetic field gradients. The measured signals, which depend on local proton density and relaxation times (T1, T2) and other contrast mechanisms, are Fourier\u2011reconstructed to form detailed images of internal tissues."},{"id":"51df7229-8061-48c4-8dd9-fa422ee4f7b5","l":"Magnetic semiconductors","d":2,"p":"12721ec5-11d3-484a-9ea9-a7bea1986722","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Magnetic semiconductors are materials that combine a semiconducting electronic band structure with long-range magnetic order, usually produced by magnetic ions (dopants) or intrinsic magnetic moments. Their magnetism is often carrier-mediated, coupling charge and spin degrees of freedom, which enables spin-dependent transport and underlies spintronic applications."},{"id":"6618a130-1865-4975-a405-5e92e799271c","l":"Magnetic storms, substorms","d":2,"p":"993587b9-4472-41fd-9896-2df938e75b92","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Magnetic storms are large-scale, hours-to-days disturbances of Earth's magnetosphere driven by enhanced solar wind input (e.g., CMEs or high-speed streams) that strengthen the ring current and global geomagnetic activity (seen as a Dst drop), producing expanded aurorae and ground-induced currents. Substorms are shorter (tens of minutes to a few hours) episodic releases of stored energy in the magnetotail\u2014via magnetic reconnection and current disruption\u2014that cause sudden auroral brightenings, local magnetic perturbations, and injections of energetic particles into the inner magnetosphere."},{"id":"81489c2f-bac4-4f0f-9572-9d2d77728783","l":"Magnetic thin films","d":2,"p":"08087671-73b2-4a66-a8ae-079c75cb9b33","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Magnetic thin films are layers of ferromagnetic, ferrimagnetic or antiferromagnetic material with thicknesses from a few atomic layers up to a few hundred nanometers, whose magnetic behavior is strongly affected by reduced dimensionality and surface or interface effects. These modifications (e.g., altered anisotropy, domain structure, coercivity, exchange coupling and spin transport) distinguish them from bulk magnets and make them central to magnetic recording and spintronics."},{"id":"4f5b6066-1008-4a3e-a54a-eadb7accb597","l":"Magnetic vortices","d":2,"p":"1bd1a9ab-ec4f-40a8-9f81-9f1005587d58","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Magnetic vortices are localized, topologically stable whirl-like configurations of magnetization or magnetic flux in condensed-matter systems. In ferromagnetic thin films they appear as in-plane magnetization curling around a small core with out-of-plane polarity, while in type-II superconductors they are quantized flux lines (Abrikosov vortices) with circulating supercurrents around a normal core."},{"id":"6445f83e-f405-4a82-a600-98f06865c0fa","l":"Magnetization measurements","d":2,"p":"0003fc3f-1522-4fca-bf3f-bcef0dcec405","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Magnetization measurements determine the magnetic moment per unit volume (magnetization M) or per sample as a function of external magnetic field, temperature, time, or frequency to quantify magnetic properties such as susceptibility, saturation, hysteresis, and remanence. They are performed with instruments like SQUID and vibrating-sample magnetometers (VSM) or torque magnetometers to characterize intrinsic and extrinsic magnetic behavior."},{"id":"1ae8b922-d896-41cc-bfff-6649f254bb1d","l":"Magnetization switching","d":2,"p":"1bd1a9ab-ec4f-40a8-9f81-9f1005587d58","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Magnetization switching is the process of reversing the net magnetization vector of a ferromagnet or nanomagnet from one stable orientation to another by applying an external stimulus (e.g., magnetic field, spin-polarized current, spin\u2013orbit torque, or heat) that overcomes anisotropy energy barriers. It is the fundamental operation for magnetic memory and spintronic devices and can proceed via mechanisms such as coherent rotation, domain-wall motion, or nucleation and growth of reversed domains."},{"id":"926dd7f3-8bc7-4111-94c9-e55d7ab1ebdd","l":"Magnetocaloric effect","d":2,"p":"492228d3-c5ce-4eac-922a-b8158c777c9b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The magnetocaloric effect is the reversible change in temperature (or equivalently entropy) of a magnetic material when an applied magnetic field is changed, caused by field-driven alignment or disordering of magnetic moments that alters the material's magnetic entropy. Under adiabatic conditions a field increase heats the material as spins align (reducing entropy), and large effects near magnetic phase transitions are exploited for magnetic refrigeration."},{"id":"1ac35d4c-ffe7-4297-b8a8-69e72faecbf6","l":"Magnetoelastic effect","d":2,"p":"492228d3-c5ce-4eac-922a-b8158c777c9b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Magnetoelastic effect is the coupling between a material\u2019s magnetic state and its mechanical deformation: changes in magnetization produce strain (magnetostriction) and conversely applied stress or strain changes magnetic properties (inverse magnetostriction or the Villari effect). It arises because the magnetic energy of a material depends on the lattice configuration and vice versa."},{"id":"6e1621f1-d2ad-4261-bc49-906171747b14","l":"Magnetoelectric effect","d":2,"p":"492228d3-c5ce-4eac-922a-b8158c777c9b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The magnetoelectric effect is the phenomenon in which an applied magnetic field induces an electric polarization in a material, or conversely an applied electric field induces a magnetization. In the linear regime it is described by a magnetoelectric tensor \u03b1ij and generally requires broken spatial inversion and time-reversal symmetries (as in multiferroics)."},{"id":"02c7cf6d-ba61-4941-b751-1aa847cc59fd","l":"Magneto-optical effect","d":2,"p":"492228d3-c5ce-4eac-922a-b8158c777c9b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Magneto-optical effect: any change in a material's optical response (polarization, phase velocity, or absorption) caused by an applied magnetic field or the material's magnetization. It includes phenomena such as Faraday rotation and the magneto-optic Kerr effect, which arise because the magnetic field makes the material's dielectric response polarization-dependent (e.g., different for left- and right-circular polarizations)."},{"id":"1c6daf36-c0ff-464f-963b-ac9673015bfc","l":"Magneto-optical Kerr effect","d":2,"p":"6cee676f-f03b-4091-8bd6-a0433baf26ba","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Magneto-optical Kerr effect: the rotation and change in ellipticity of the polarization of light reflected from a magnetized material, caused by magnetization-induced off-diagonal terms in the material's dielectric tensor that give different reflection amplitudes for left- and right-circular polarizations. It is widely used to probe surface and thin-film magnetization in polar, longitudinal, and transverse geometries."},{"id":"1bab4a54-5312-4e2e-b8e0-20ea5f08a8e1","l":"Magnetopause","d":2,"p":"993587b9-4472-41fd-9896-2df938e75b92","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"The magnetopause is the outer boundary of a planet\u2019s magnetosphere where the dynamic and thermal pressure of the incoming solar (or stellar) wind balances the magnetic pressure of the planetary field, separating the magnetically dominated, largely closed-field region from the shocked solar wind and often serving as a site of magnetic reconnection and plasma entry."},{"id":"23e22a76-a9ba-4593-8d52-4e5c98c47af3","l":"Magnetoresistance","d":2,"p":"0e380d9d-ccad-467d-abf7-7ab154e42c86","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Magnetoresistance is the change in a material's electrical resistance produced by an applied magnetic field. It arises because the field alters charge-carrier trajectories and scattering\u2014classically via the Lorentz force and, in magnetic or low-dimensional systems, via spin-dependent and quantum effects."},{"id":"de0938e9-3b60-4e53-97fc-093877c528d7","l":"Magnetosheath","d":2,"p":"993587b9-4472-41fd-9896-2df938e75b92","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"The magnetosheath is the region of shocked, slowed solar wind plasma that lies between a planet's bow shock and its magnetopause, formed as the supersonic solar wind is deflected around the magnetic obstacle. It is typically compressed, heated and turbulent, with magnetic fields draped and often intensified around the magnetosphere."},{"id":"e386920e-6778-4cda-849e-e1a1160090c7","l":"Magnetostriction","d":2,"p":"492228d3-c5ce-4eac-922a-b8158c777c9b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Magnetostriction is the change in shape or dimensions of a magnetic material (typically ferromagnetic) that occurs when its magnetization changes. It arises from magnetoelastic coupling\u2014reorientation of magnetic domains and spin\u2013orbit interactions that alter the lattice strain\u2014and has a reversible counterpart known as the Villari (or inverse magnetostrictive) effect."},{"id":"803e625f-ec39-41d3-b5f6-bba9b1c4a3c5","l":"Magnetotail","d":2,"p":"993587b9-4472-41fd-9896-2df938e75b92","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"The magnetotail is the elongated, nightside extension of a planet\u2019s magnetosphere formed when the solar wind stretches the planet\u2019s magnetic field into two oppositely directed lobes separated by a central plasma/current sheet. It stores magnetic energy and is the site of magnetic reconnection and particle acceleration that drive phenomena such as auroral substorms."},{"id":"5ec036a6-336a-4ec0-923a-a04fcd48bfc0","l":"Magnonic crystals","d":2,"p":"77fabbae-1f98-4300-996c-9158ab52e6fc","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Magnonic crystals are artificial magnetic materials with a periodic modulation of magnetic properties or geometry that controls the propagation of spin waves (magnons, the quanta of collective magnetization precession). Like photonic crystals for light, they produce magnon band structures and band gaps, enabling engineered filtering, guiding and manipulation of spin-wave signals for magnonic devices."},{"id":"e8cc2f3c-1229-4151-81a7-4074fb75bee4","l":"Magnonics","d":2,"p":"3e80884b-56d9-4ed5-9f94-7e835761cb6e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Magnonics is the study and engineering of spin waves\u2014collective oscillations of the magnetization whose quanta are called magnons\u2014in magnetic materials. It focuses on generating, guiding, manipulating, and detecting these excitations for information processing and signal transmission, exploiting their nanoscale wavelengths and typically low-dissipation GHz\u2013THz dynamics."},{"id":"b3ecb9a8-e74e-445a-9d05-21d86b298385","l":"Majorana bound states","d":2,"p":"74a2f68b-37df-4018-9750-8e16a9660c21","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Majorana bound states are localized zero-energy quasiparticle excitations in superconducting systems that are self-conjugate (\u03b3 = \u03b3\u2020), i.e., each excitation is its own antiparticle. They appear at edges, vortices or defects of topological superconductors and can exhibit non-Abelian exchange statistics, which makes them promising for fault-tolerant quantum computation."},{"id":"904cd29f-d48f-4042-9c4e-b1ce55e8092a","l":"Majorana fermions","d":2,"p":"4fcc035e-7eb4-46f6-b347-5cc975e5ed95","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Majorana fermions are fermionic particles whose particle and antiparticle are the same, i.e., their field equals its own charge conjugate and can be represented by a real-valued (Majorana) spinor. They may be realized as fundamental particles (possible Majorana neutrinos) or as emergent zero-energy quasiparticles in topological superconductors with exotic exchange statistics."},{"id":"875876d0-5cab-48e6-ab3d-26beba7fe826","l":"Many-body localization","d":2,"p":"7d9b7d96-c9e6-455f-9fa7-86088b7df853","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Many-body localization (MBL) is a phase of interacting quantum systems with strong disorder in which particles or excitations remain spatially localized and the system fails to thermalize, so it does not equilibrate or conduct despite interactions. MBL is characterized by emergent local integrals of motion and atypical entanglement in excited eigenstates, in violation of the eigenstate thermalization hypothesis."},{"id":"d74f237a-a2c6-43ff-88fe-f3025b1e298f","l":"Marine ecology","d":2,"p":"0f53e9c0-21db-4e21-9bf2-a3dcab5a14fc","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Marine ecology is the scientific study of interactions among marine organisms and between those organisms and their environment, focusing on the factors that determine the distribution, abundance, and dynamics of life in the ocean. It examines biological communities together with the physical and chemical drivers\u2014such as currents, light, temperature, and nutrient cycles\u2014that control energy flow and matter cycling in marine ecosystems."},{"id":"4ee22aaf-2d97-4caf-9aa8-4bcc34577f0b","l":"Markovian processes","d":2,"p":"695da376-c44a-41dd-b968-d200c31e9d5e","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems","Plasma Physics"],"def":"A Markovian process is a stochastic process that satisfies the Markov property: the conditional probability distribution of future states depends only on the system's present state (and current time) and is independent of its prior history. This memoryless property underlies Markov chains and Markovian master equations used to model diffusion, relaxation, and open-system dynamics."},{"id":"901531cd-56c5-438f-9b88-5096ac032655","l":"Martensitic phase transition","d":2,"p":"19f26688-0955-4fd9-85d8-aaf71c4a581f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A martensitic phase transition is a diffusionless, displacive first-order solid\u2013solid transformation in which the crystal lattice undergoes a coordinated shear and small atomic shuffles to change symmetry, producing twinned or variant domains called martensite. It is typically rapid and hysteretic (often athermal), driven by temperature or applied stress, and is central to steels and shape\u2011memory alloys."},{"id":"9b423457-1800-4009-8167-2a626fb4eb0f","l":"Massive compact halo objects","d":2,"p":"024cf552-b967-4a51-8b54-d209e10f0bc7","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Massive compact halo objects (MACHOs) are compact, nonluminous astrophysical bodies in a galactic halo\u2014such as brown dwarfs, faint white dwarfs, neutron stars, or black holes\u2014proposed as candidates for dark matter because their mass produces gravitational effects despite little or no emitted light. Their abundance has been constrained by microlensing surveys and other observations, ruling them out as the dominant form of dark matter except in limited mass ranges."},{"id":"38b065c4-10d9-4e16-8ace-63d1eb093d28","l":"Mass spectrometry","d":2,"p":"1d433fa8-4cdd-4e66-ae51-df1797f18d56","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Mass spectrometry is an analytical technique that ionizes atoms or molecules and measures the mass-to-charge ratio (m/z) of the resulting ions. By separating ions with electric/magnetic fields or time-of-flight methods and detecting them, it determines molecular masses, isotopic composition, and can provide structural or quantitative chemical information."},{"id":"e0952223-797e-4169-b2b6-48da54aab376","l":"Master equation","d":2,"p":"695da376-c44a-41dd-b968-d200c31e9d5e","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems","Plasma Physics"],"def":"A master equation is a differential equation that governs the time evolution of a system's probability distribution (in classical stochastic processes) or of its density matrix (in open quantum systems), describing transitions between states induced by stochastic events or coupling to an environment. It encodes transition rates or dissipative superoperators and thereby captures non\u2011unitary, irreversible dynamics such as relaxation and decoherence."},{"id":"754d5e77-be43-4243-aea6-ea8d4de70dd8","l":"Matrix product states","d":2,"p":"50ba3f74-7f31-4857-8a75-b2b007348981","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields","Physics of Living Systems","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Matrix product states (MPS) are a class of tensor-network wavefunctions for one-dimensional quantum many-body systems in which the amplitude of each basis configuration is written as a product of site-dependent matrices, with the matrix size (bond dimension) controlling how much entanglement the state can represent. They provide an efficient variational description of low\u2011entanglement (area\u2011law) states\u2014especially ground states of local gapped 1D Hamiltonians\u2014and are the formal framework behind DMRG."},{"id":"eaa9de3d-33f2-4e15-88a0-2251fd5b19cc","l":"Maxwell model","d":2,"p":"c267a3f3-0c34-4fc1-9825-947683caf4dd","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"The Maxwell model is the simplest linear viscoelastic model formed by a spring (elastic modulus G) and a dashpot (viscosity \u03b7) in series. It exhibits a single relaxation time \u03c4 = \u03b7/G, with exponential stress relaxation under imposed strain and viscous flow (creep) under sustained stress."},{"id":"2d89a076-7a54-4494-973a-ea562f654b8e","l":"Mean field theory","d":2,"p":"05a47dcc-71c0-4ebc-9d4e-79a40a191efb","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Mean-field theory approximates a many-body system by replacing the interactions of each particle with an average (mean) field produced by all the others, reducing the problem to a single-body (or few-body) problem in a self-consistently determined field. It often captures qualitative behavior and phase transitions but neglects fluctuations and spatial correlations, becoming exact only in high-coordination-number or infinite-dimensional limits."},{"id":"552fbc40-d35a-4aa2-b667-693eb2816579","l":"Mechanical effects of light on material media","d":2,"p":"3123cbf9-460d-467a-9306-b4fb316daddd","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Mechanical effects of light on material media are forces and torques produced when electromagnetic radiation transfers momentum and energy to matter\u2014via absorption, reflection, refraction, or spatial intensity gradients\u2014causing motion, stress, deformation, or rotation. Examples include radiation pressure and gradient/scattering forces used in optical tweezers, light-induced torques, photophoretic (heating-driven) forces, and optomechanical coupling in resonators."},{"id":"3ac6c674-22c4-461c-95da-eb36b75e9a65","l":"Mechanical metamaterials","d":2,"p":"77fabbae-1f98-4300-996c-9158ab52e6fc","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Mechanical metamaterials are engineered materials whose macroscopic mechanical properties are controlled primarily by their designed micro- or meso-scale geometry and architecture rather than by the chemistry of their constituents. By tailoring that structure they can exhibit unusual behaviors \u2014 for example negative Poisson\u2019s ratio, extreme anisotropy, tunable stiffness, programmable deformation, and elastic-wave band gaps \u2014 not typically found in natural materials."},{"id":"3bd9d833-8ba3-4d2b-93a9-3da2b0db0359","l":"Mechanisms of phase transition regulation & control in biology","d":2,"p":"5fddad64-3302-472f-9945-d256974cb278","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Mechanisms of phase transition regulation & control in biology are the molecular, biochemical and physical processes that determine where, when and how biomolecular phase transitions\u2014for example liquid\u2013liquid demixing that forms membraneless organelles\u2014nucleate, grow, persist or dissolve and what material properties they exhibit. These controls act by tuning interaction strengths and local concentrations (via transcription, sequestration, post\u2011translational modification, RNA binding, ions, temperature), providing spatial nucleation scaffolds, and using active, ATP\u2011driven processes (chaperones, enzymes, motor flows) to drive non\u2011equilibrium assembly and disassembly."},{"id":"caacbbef-0437-4dd8-9fa6-b0be0cdceae5","l":"Mechanotransduction","d":2,"p":"f5fed3b8-a2ea-4008-971e-62d179ae3b06","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Mechanotransduction is the process by which cells or tissues sense mechanical forces or deformations and convert them into biochemical or electrical signals that alter cellular activity. It typically involves force-sensitive proteins (e.g., ion channels, adhesion complexes) and the cytoskeleton coupling extracellular mechanics to intracellular signaling and gene regulation."},{"id":"7baed8e1-4a1c-48ba-9a68-b2c1c5b8b8dc","l":"Medical devices","d":2,"p":"a24a2d8a-58a4-4cbc-81aa-4cd953e73f4b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Medical devices are instruments, apparatus, machines, implants, or in vitro reagents used to diagnose, prevent, monitor, or treat disease and injury. They implement mechanical, electrical, optical, thermal, or chemical physical principles\u2014from sensors and imaging systems to prosthetics and implants\u2014and require attention to materials, biocompatibility, instrumentation, and safety."},{"id":"85108ad2-07c1-4e45-8a57-180aabe6d2e6","l":"Medical imaging","d":2,"p":"e42c3b84-6ccd-46ed-a6a6-56e8b3ee653f","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Medical imaging is the set of physics-based techniques for noninvasively visualizing the internal structure and function of the body by exploiting interactions between tissues and various forms of energy (X-rays, radiofrequency fields, ultrasound, gamma rays, etc.). It combines signal generation and detection with contrast mechanisms and computational image reconstruction to produce images used for diagnosis, treatment planning, and monitoring."},{"id":"ad6e0747-1da3-4858-a5f2-353aa0623027","l":"Melt-spinning","d":2,"p":"c4ed35b0-1090-409c-9667-85f9ac03e9c0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Melt-spinning is a rapid solidification process in which a jet of molten metal is ejected onto a rotating chilled (usually copper) wheel, forming thin ribbons that cool at very high rates (\u223c10^5\u201310^6 K/s). The extreme quench rates suppress equilibrium crystallization and produce amorphous or ultrafine-grained microstructures used for metallic glasses, soft magnets, and other non-equilibrium alloys."},{"id":"4e68d438-9022-4da6-ae03-4074370de48f","l":"Membranes","d":2,"p":"c2ea9e2c-0e15-49ee-b336-111976b0b965","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Membranes are thin, quasi-two-dimensional sheets or interfaces\u2014ranging from fluid lipid bilayers to elastic polymer or solid films\u2014that separate regions or bound volumes. In physics they are modeled by continuum surface mechanics (surface tension, bending rigidity and in-plane elasticity) and are studied for their mechanical response, thermal undulations and collective behaviors such as adhesion, fluctuations and phase transitions (e.g., flat versus crumpled states)."},{"id":"afbc4ef8-c257-4575-9c3a-6b189f51af6c","l":"Membrane structures","d":2,"p":"9ccf11df-5b72-4ce0-92e6-e48f8dda172b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Physics of Living Systems","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Membrane structures are thin, flexible two-dimensional continua that carry external loads mainly through in-plane tensile stresses (membrane forces) rather than bending moments, so their equilibrium shape is set by the applied loads and boundary conditions. Examples include soap films, inflated shells and tensile-fabric roofs, whose mechanics are described by membrane theory that neglects bending stiffness."},{"id":"c8842d65-34ad-4d91-a57b-b782a8489a46","l":"Memristors","d":2,"p":"2360e96e-a12b-4d21-9b65-36ed1177c0e6","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A memristor is a two-terminal circuit element whose instantaneous resistance depends on the time-integral of past current (or charge), so it \"remembers\" prior electrical activity as a nonvolatile change in conductance. Formally introduced by Chua as the element linking magnetic flux \u03c6 and charge q via M(q)=d\u03c6/dq, practical memristors are nanoscale devices where ionic or defect motion alters conductivity and produce a characteristic pinched hysteresis in their I\u2013V curves."},{"id":"a741e648-dd8d-4c84-9c0f-012a1233c7d2","l":"Metabolomics","d":2,"p":"6b5443fa-9849-4d5b-966b-7d6e08113e36","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Metabolomics is the comprehensive study of the metabolome\u2014the complete set of small-molecule metabolites, their concentrations, and fluxes in a biological system\u2014aimed at profiling and quantifying metabolic states and responses. It relies on analytical methods (e.g., mass spectrometry, NMR) and data analysis to relate metabolite patterns to physiology, disease, or environmental perturbations."},{"id":"5fbee489-3dc3-4259-a806-084cadf9066a","l":"Metagenomics","d":2,"p":"6b5443fa-9849-4d5b-966b-7d6e08113e36","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Metagenomics is the study of genetic material recovered directly from environmental samples, using high-throughput DNA sequencing to identify the organisms present and their genes without needing to culture them. It combines sequencing and computational analysis to infer community composition, metabolic potential, and ecological interactions."},{"id":"40d46fee-5bc3-43c7-8b86-47136e4ee1cc","l":"Metagratings","d":2,"p":"2a5345ea-2bd1-45f5-818c-bf056b4f6500","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Fluid Dynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Metagratings are engineered periodic arrays of discrete scatterers (usually a few per period) that control diffraction orders to steer, reflect, or refract waves with high efficiency by tailoring each element\u2019s scattering amplitude and phase. Unlike dense metasurfaces, metagratings rely on sparse, strategically placed elements and Floquet-mode engineering to suppress unwanted orders and produce anomalous beam shaping."},{"id":"06464e43-8795-4386-9402-829686fdd555","l":"Metal-insulator transition","d":2,"p":"19f26688-0955-4fd9-85d8-aaf71c4a581f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A metal\u2013insulator transition (MIT) is a change in a material\u2019s electronic state from a metallic phase\u2014with a Fermi surface and finite conductivity as temperature approaches zero\u2014to an insulating phase with vanishing low-temperature conductivity due to a charge gap or carrier localization. Such transitions can be driven by tuning parameters like carrier density (doping), pressure, temperature, disorder (Anderson localization) or strong electron\u2013electron interactions (Mott transition), and may involve structural or magnetic rearrangements."},{"id":"cb270e8a-d337-4cee-8330-b3595c3e94dd","l":"Metallic glasses","d":2,"p":"34548dbc-f720-4a9d-982f-aa10e01b74d9","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Metallic glasses (amorphous metals) are alloys whose atoms form a disordered, non\u2011crystalline structure because crystallization was avoided during solidification (e.g., by very rapid quenching or vapor deposition). They combine metallic bonding with glass\u2011like short\u2011range order, giving unusually high strength and elastic limits, good corrosion and soft\u2011magnetic properties, and deformation controlled by shear banding rather than by dislocation motion."},{"id":"c0a051f7-e121-4c01-b7bc-d42ce7adcb3f","l":"Metalloids","d":2,"p":"aa36bec0-0f65-4f5d-bdf2-bc77986b4d0e","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Metalloids are elements whose physical and chemical properties lie between those of metals and nonmetals, typically showing semiconducting or semimetallic electronic behavior with electrical conductivity that depends strongly on temperature and impurities. They occupy a diagonal zone of the periodic table (e.g., B, Si, Ge, As, Sb, Te) and tend to form covalent bonding networks rather than a delocalized metallic electron sea."},{"id":"85f82fe9-ff63-4cd8-aed1-42811ab1ea4f","l":"Metallurgical magnetohydrodynamics","d":2,"p":"c4ed35b0-1090-409c-9667-85f9ac03e9c0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Metallurgical magnetohydrodynamics is the study of how magnetic and electric fields interact with electrically conducting molten metals and alloys, where induced currents and Lorentz forces alter fluid flow, heat transfer, and solidification. It focuses on controlling processes in metallurgy\u2014such as electromagnetic stirring, braking, levitation, and refinement\u2014by exploiting these field\u2013flow couplings."},{"id":"b0c4ef48-e1bc-48db-bec1-434b20f2af00","l":"Metamagnetism","d":2,"p":"b6e4cc90-18f3-4427-841d-c667375bbca0","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Metamagnetism is the phenomenon in which a material\u2019s magnetization increases abruptly or very rapidly at a critical applied magnetic field, typically signaling a field-induced transition from a low-magnetization state (e.g., antiferromagnetic) to a high-magnetization state (e.g., ferromagnetic). It can appear as a first-order jump or a sharp crossover and arises from competing exchange interactions, magnetic anisotropy, or itinerant-electron correlation effects."},{"id":"dcc78937-ba2f-4066-9951-bb9eeba20994","l":"Metasurfaces","d":2,"p":"77fabbae-1f98-4300-996c-9158ab52e6fc","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Metasurfaces are ultrathin, planar arrays of subwavelength \"meta\u2011atoms\" that locally tailor the amplitude, phase, and polarization of incident waves (typically electromagnetic) to achieve engineered wavefront control\u2014such as beam steering, lensing, and holography\u2014in a compact, two\u2011dimensional form."},{"id":"9509a217-d06f-407a-82f2-410cbd47a9ca","l":"Metropolis algorithm","d":2,"p":"eb9bd2e1-eedd-4bd0-997d-58b44ffa3ebb","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics","Networks","Particles & Fields","Physics of Living Systems","Plasma Physics","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Metropolis algorithm is a Markov-chain Monte Carlo method for generating a sequence of system configurations sampled from a target probability distribution (commonly the Boltzmann distribution). It proposes random moves and accepts them with probability min(1, exp(\u2212\u0394E/kT)), a rule that enforces detailed balance and drives the chain toward equilibrium."},{"id":"1f72b9f1-0dd7-454f-861d-6c5927dfb128","l":"Micelles","d":2,"p":"9ccf11df-5b72-4ce0-92e6-e48f8dda172b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Micelles are nanoscale self-assembled aggregates of amphiphilic molecules in a solvent, in which hydrophobic tails cluster inward and hydrophilic heads face the solvent to form a core\u2013shell structure. They form above a characteristic critical micelle concentration and are central to surfactant behavior, solubilization, and soft-matter physics."},{"id":"5ad3550d-3c53-4107-a9a3-c1e1815550a6","l":"Microbial mats","d":2,"p":"af53b7f0-3191-45c2-9be4-e04b69db0666","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Microbial mats are multi\u2011layered, cohesive communities of microorganisms (often cyanobacteria and heterotrophs) that form thick biofilms on sediments or rocks, with cells embedded in extracellular polymeric substances (EPS) that trap and bind particles. They generate steep chemical and physical gradients (e.g., light, O2, sulfide), drive local biogeochemical fluxes, and produce characteristic sedimentary and mechanical structures relevant to transport, pattern formation, and surface stability."},{"id":"8af57475-285f-4750-a365-0209a0288034","l":"Microcavity & microdisk lasers","d":2,"p":"cd4c012e-bacf-4bcf-8358-a6573a330ec4","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Microcavity lasers are lasers whose optical resonator dimensions are comparable to the emission wavelength, producing very small mode volumes and often high quality factors that enhance light\u2013matter interaction and reduce lasing thresholds. A microdisk laser is a common microcavity geometry: a micron-scale circular dielectric disk that confines light in high\u2011Q whispering\u2011gallery modes around its rim, enabling compact, low\u2011threshold lasing and efficient coupling to nearby waveguides."},{"id":"00b7b3bc-d715-411f-ad64-78cab86be981","l":"Micromagnetic modeling","d":2,"p":"ba55d8a8-d049-4550-b264-530f74618214","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Micromagnetic modeling is the continuum computational approach that describes the spatial and temporal evolution of the magnetization vector field M(r,t) in ferromagnetic materials at sub-micron scales, resolving structures down to the exchange length. It predicts static and dynamic phenomena (domains, domain walls, switching, spin waves) by numerically solving the Landau\u2013Lifshitz\u2013Gilbert equation with contributions from exchange, anisotropy, magnetostatic, Zeeman and other effective fields."},{"id":"2a543597-0b44-440d-9941-14e4e8094d12","l":"Micromechanical devices","d":2,"p":"2360e96e-a12b-4d21-9b65-36ed1177c0e6","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Micromechanical devices are miniature mechanical systems\u2014beams, springs, gears, resonators and actuators\u2014whose characteristic dimensions are in the micrometer-to-millimeter range and that perform sensing, actuation or other mechanical functions. They are typically made by microfabrication (MEMS) and operate using elastic, inertial, electrostatic, piezoelectric or thermal forces, often integrated with electronics."},{"id":"1159b317-3be6-4ec5-bee1-deabdf35aaad","l":"Microparticles","d":2,"p":"6efb152f-e313-4269-b7dd-a830f5523c53","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Microparticles are discrete solid or liquid particles with characteristic sizes in the micrometer range (roughly 0.1\u20131000 \u03bcm), occupying the regime between molecular/colloidal scales and bulk materials. Their motion and interactions are governed by a mix of continuum hydrodynamics, surface forces and thermal (Brownian) fluctuations, and\u2014when charged or in plasmas\u2014electrostatic and long-range interactions."},{"id":"4a17608e-a85f-4f56-9c96-bebc3404fcb4","l":"Microphase separation","d":2,"p":"d0f6584f-c5d7-4011-99bb-2a370e8f2b99","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Microphase separation is the self-organization of a multicomponent material into stable, nanoscale-to-microscale domains (e.g., lamellae, cylinders, spheres, gyroids) when short-range immiscibility would drive phase separation but is frustrated by constraints such as covalent linking or competing long-range interactions. Unlike macrophase separation, it produces periodic mesostructures whose characteristic length scale is set by a balance of interfacial energy and opposing elastic or entropic costs (common in block copolymers and amphiphiles)."},{"id":"6f4c99a1-24e8-48f6-8091-4a111d0373ac","l":"Microrheology","d":2,"p":"2a7e97bc-0a5f-4b55-a310-75aae0ede940","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Microrheology is the measurement of local viscoelastic properties of complex fluids and soft solids by tracking the motion or response of micrometer- to nanometer-scale probe particles embedded in the material. Using passive (thermal-fluctuation) or active (externally driven) probes, it infers frequency-dependent mechanical moduli (e.g., the complex shear modulus) with spatial resolution at microscopic length scales."},{"id":"66d73f04-5784-47a0-b3c7-c8b15ee98de1","l":"Microswimmers","d":2,"p":"91278658-51b4-4d8e-a936-7cfad1266625","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Microswimmers are microscopic self-propelled particles or organisms that convert internal or ambient energy into directed motion through a fluid, typically operating at low Reynolds number where viscous forces dominate over inertia. Their propulsion mechanisms (e.g., flagellar beating, ciliary motion, or surface phoresis) and resulting hydrodynamic interactions produce nonequilibrium single-particle and collective behaviors distinct from passive Brownian tracers."},{"id":"737da8d9-50f1-4a87-9d13-996564f51af2","l":"Microtubules","d":2,"p":"9ccf11df-5b72-4ce0-92e6-e48f8dda172b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Microtubules are hollow cylindrical biopolymers (~25 nm outer diameter) assembled from \u03b1/\u03b2\u2011tubulin heterodimers that form the cell\u2019s cytoskeletal tracks, providing mechanical rigidity and polarized pathways for motor\u2011protein transport. They have large persistence lengths and exhibit non\u2011equilibrium \"dynamic instability\"\u2014stochastic, GTP\u2011regulated phases of growth and rapid shrinkage\u2014enabling force generation and reorganization during transport and cell division."},{"id":"f0273786-56c0-4981-9026-5a81411bcab6","l":"Mie scattering","d":2,"p":"3123cbf9-460d-467a-9306-b4fb316daddd","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Mie scattering is the exact solution of Maxwell's equations for how electromagnetic waves are scattered, absorbed, and extinguished by a homogeneous spherical particle whose size is comparable to the wavelength; it gives the full angular and polarization-dependent intensity as a function of the particle's size parameter and complex refractive index. It reduces to Rayleigh scattering for very small particles and approaches geometric-optics behavior for very large particles."},{"id":"87c87567-666f-409d-832d-37a10b1b5937","l":"Milky Way","d":2,"p":"022c3abb-9915-43bc-b7d9-0fe95ecf3d55","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"The Milky Way is the barred spiral galaxy that contains the Solar System, consisting of a rotating stellar and gaseous disk, a central bulge, and an extended dark-matter halo. It spans roughly 100,000 light\u2011years, contains on the order of 10^11\u201310^12 stars with a total mass \u224810^12 solar masses, and hosts the supermassive black hole Sagittarius A* at its center."},{"id":"9b80e2bd-a73f-47a3-ab50-1fe2547aecec","l":"Mitochondria","d":2,"p":"9ccf11df-5b72-4ce0-92e6-e48f8dda172b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Mitochondria are membrane-bound organelles in eukaryotic cells that produce most cellular ATP by oxidative phosphorylation, converting chemical energy into work via a proton-motive force across the inner membrane that drives ATP synthase. They contain their own genome and also participate in metabolism, calcium signaling, and programmed cell death."},{"id":"980335e1-1f6b-4f8e-ac0a-4e86f93a6561","l":"Mitosis","d":2,"p":"c2ea9e2c-0e15-49ee-b336-111976b0b965","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Mitosis is the stage of the eukaryotic cell cycle in which a duplicated genome is precisely segregated into two daughter nuclei so that two genetically identical daughter cells are produced. The process is mechanically driven by the mitotic spindle\u2014microtubules, associated proteins, and motor proteins\u2014that generate forces to align, separate chromosomes, and coordinate cytokinesis."},{"id":"f90a1ffa-2da2-4b9a-b3da-d6c48fd5ee48","l":"Mixtures of 3He & 4He","d":2,"p":"9284057b-d22f-4c66-bad2-a840fcc697c9","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Mixtures of 3He and 4He are cryogenic quantum fluids in which fermionic 3He atoms are dissolved in bosonic 4He, producing behavior governed by quantum statistics rather than classical thermodynamics. At low temperatures the mixture shows rich phenomena\u20144He Bose-condenses into a superfluid while 3He acts as a Fermi liquid (and only becomes a paired superfluid at millikelvin temperatures), the two isotopes phase-separate below roughly 0.87 K, and the enthalpy of mixing is exploited in dilution refrigerators."},{"id":"a92585bd-2c57-4257-bc4f-acdd545d10f6","l":"Modulation instability","d":2,"p":"bd03ea46-2406-4a24-8307-05b2b498fb4e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Modulation instability is the exponential growth of small amplitude perturbations on a continuous-wave background in a nonlinear dispersive (or diffractive) medium, caused by the interplay of nonlinearity and dispersion/diffraction and resulting in amplification of sidebands and breakup into localized structures. Also called the Benjamin\u2013Feir instability in fluids, it underlies phenomena such as optical supercontinuum generation and formation of soliton trains."},{"id":"d925d13b-628a-480e-a35d-78ce4271b6bf","l":"Moire materials","d":2,"p":"3bd34535-a0eb-4c20-9080-8877f534f6b5","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Moir\u00e9 materials are stacks of two-dimensional crystals twisted or slightly lattice-mismatched so that their interference creates a long-wavelength moir\u00e9 superlattice that modulates electronic and optical potentials. This moir\u00e9 reconstruction can produce narrow (flat) bands and enhanced interactions, enabling tunable correlated phenomena such as superconductivity, correlated insulators, and novel excitonic states."},{"id":"96147a1d-f5a5-48d1-8452-3133256d0842","l":"Molecular design","d":2,"p":"a24a2d8a-58a4-4cbc-81aa-4cd953e73f4b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Molecular design is the purposeful creation and optimization of molecules to achieve specified physical or functional properties by controlling composition, bonding, geometry, and supramolecular organization. It uses principles of quantum mechanics, thermodynamics, and materials science together with computational modeling and synthetic methods to predict and realize molecules with targeted electronic, optical, mechanical, or catalytic behavior."},{"id":"a39df5d0-663c-46cc-8967-535582bae42a","l":"Molecular junctions","d":2,"p":"f495cfcf-afde-4cee-8daa-3ffffaa0e3a6","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Molecular junctions are nanoscale devices in which a single molecule or a small assembly of molecules forms an electrical contact between two electrodes, creating a conductive bridge for charge and energy transport. They serve as probes and components for quantum transport phenomena (tunneling, quantized conductance, Coulomb blockade, electron\u2013vibration coupling) and for realizing molecular-scale electronic functions."},{"id":"81a45cbe-8d1f-4184-a578-40a17d5748e5","l":"Molecule trapping & guiding","d":2,"p":"0b6b113c-8f3d-421e-a149-940af26931cf","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Molecule trapping and guiding is the creation of spatially localized and controllable potentials\u2014using electric, magnetic, optical, or mechanical fields\u2014to confine molecules in space (trapping) or steer them along predefined paths (guiding). These techniques exploit molecular properties such as magnetic moments, electric dipoles, or polarizability to slow, hold, and transport molecules for spectroscopy, cold-collision studies, and quantum-state control."},{"id":"0a177748-958d-46aa-a4cf-55715c6a9807","l":"Monolayer films","d":2,"p":"08087671-73b2-4a66-a8ae-079c75cb9b33","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A monolayer film is a two-dimensional layer consisting of a single molecular or atomic layer adsorbed or chemically bound to a substrate, with thickness on the order of one molecular diameter. Because all constituents lie at an interface, monolayers show surface-dominated structural, electronic, and chemical properties and are widely used to modify and probe surfaces (e.g., Langmuir\u2013Blodgett films, self-assembled monolayers)."},{"id":"7b3cb40c-ec7c-45a3-97cc-f002aa96892e","l":"Monopoles","d":2,"p":"cf6fcf92-5d05-4722-a1b3-c076e8b22b9e","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Monopoles are particle-like excitations that carry a net magnetic charge\u2014an isolated north or south magnetic pole\u2014unlike ordinary dipolar magnets. They appear as theoretical solutions in quantum field theories (e.g., Dirac and \u2019t Hooft\u2013Polyakov monopoles), imply the Dirac quantization of electric charge, and have analogues as topological defects in condensed matter systems."},{"id":"ca92dcc0-3983-4bb7-8ab2-7973bde347b8","l":"Moon","d":2,"p":"79af0a5d-bb5e-41d4-ac34-634895fa02f5","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"A moon (natural satellite) is a celestial body gravitationally bound in orbit around a planet or minor planet, whose motion is governed by the planet\u2013moon two\u2011body (and multi\u2011body) gravitational dynamics. Moons range from small, irregular captured objects to large, geologically active bodies and often exhibit tidal interactions (including synchronous rotation) with their primary."},{"id":"52021ea9-52bc-4c58-b518-016cd494b063","l":"Morphogenesis","d":2,"p":"93b8baac-44de-4a26-a175-357ad9d8744c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Morphogenesis is the process by which biological shapes, structures and patterns emerge during development from initially undifferentiated material. In physics, it refers to the emergent pattern formation driven by interactions of growth, mechanical forces, chemical signaling and transport (e.g., reaction\u2013diffusion and instabilities)."},{"id":"39501eb9-cc8c-4c59-9bbf-424b8f16973a","l":"M\u00f6ssbauer spectroscopy","d":2,"p":"d199d86e-97af-4dd1-9fe7-fdb00b298b96","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"M\u00f6ssbauer spectroscopy is a recoil-free, resonant gamma\u2011ray absorption technique in solids that measures tiny energy shifts and splittings of nuclear gamma transitions caused by the local electronic and magnetic environment. Using a Doppler\u2011shifted source to scan resonance, it probes isomer shifts, electric quadrupole splittings, and magnetic hyperfine fields to reveal oxidation state, bonding, and magnetic order."},{"id":"ceb54253-541d-4846-aa8b-de23f3886707","l":"Motor system diseases","d":2,"p":"bbcb2483-b22c-4bb6-bb81-beb53ff1b1ed","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Motor system diseases are disorders of the neural and muscular components that generate and control voluntary movement, caused by dysfunction or damage to motor neurons, the basal ganglia or cerebellum, neuromuscular junctions, or muscle tissue. They produce symptoms such as weakness, spasticity, tremor, loss of coordination, and progressive paralysis, and include conditions like Parkinson\u2019s disease, amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, and muscular dystrophies."},{"id":"96f52bd9-df54-4629-9972-68b3c584e23b","l":"Mott superfluids","d":2,"p":"9284057b-d22f-4c66-bad2-a840fcc697c9","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"A Mott superfluid is a superfluid phase in a strongly correlated lattice system (for example the Bose\u2013Hubbard model) that occurs on the superfluid side of the interaction-driven Mott\u2013insulator transition: despite strong on-site repulsion and proximity to commensurate filling, the system develops long-range phase coherence and dissipationless flow. It therefore combines superfluid order with the reduced number fluctuations and correlation-driven localization tendencies characteristic of Mott physics."},{"id":"e0e5980d-d3e4-4350-9d95-57d881c3b5dd","l":"Mott-superfluid transition","d":2,"p":"19f26688-0955-4fd9-85d8-aaf71c4a581f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Mott\u2013superfluid transition is a zero-temperature quantum phase transition in interacting lattice boson systems (e.g., the Bose\u2013Hubbard model) between a Mott insulator \u2014 with integer site occupancy, suppressed number fluctuations and a gap to particle\u2013hole excitations \u2014 and a superfluid \u2014 with delocalized bosons, long\u2011range phase coherence and gapless (Goldstone) modes. It is driven by the competition between kinetic energy (hopping amplitude t) and on\u2011site repulsion (U), with superfluidity favored at large t/U and the Mott state at small t/U (for commensurate filling)."},{"id":"969a338e-66cd-4e77-9cda-15117b3b64fb","l":"Multiferroics","d":2,"p":"74721827-c4e3-4da0-90ca-dba52158fc6b","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Multiferroics are materials that exhibit two or more primary ferroic orders simultaneously \u2014 most commonly spontaneous electric polarization (ferroelectricity) and spontaneous magnetization or ordering (ferromagnetism/antiferromagnetism). They often show coupling between these orders, allowing electric-field control of magnetic properties (and vice versa), which is useful for multifunctional devices and sensing."},{"id":"2d8bfd31-006e-41d6-b59c-5378712ad5f3","l":"Multifractal systems","d":2,"p":"72e394dc-f8d4-44ff-ac08-c74d10d24d9a","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Multifractal systems are systems whose spatial or temporal measures show scale invariance but with many different local scaling behaviors, requiring a continuous spectrum of fractal dimensions rather than a single fractal dimension. They are quantified by the singularity spectrum f(\u03b1) or generalized (R\u00e9nyi) dimensions D_q and occur in phenomena such as turbulence, strange attractors, and fractal growth processes."},{"id":"0df0db6f-0578-416b-b284-c028796c6f21","l":"Multilayer graphene","d":2,"p":"a0106d33-6e0e-4b73-8190-a953b55d037a","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Multilayer graphene is a material made of two or more atomically thin graphene sheets stacked and bonded by weak van der Waals forces. Its electronic and mechanical properties depend sensitively on the number of layers and their stacking or twist\u2014giving behaviors that range from monolayer Dirac fermions to bilayer parabolic bands, tunable gaps for certain stackings, and moir\u00e9-induced flat bands and correlated states at small twist angles."},{"id":"2f08fc60-b62f-431c-90ba-75cc2c732b3f","l":"Multilayer thin films","d":2,"p":"08087671-73b2-4a66-a8ae-079c75cb9b33","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Multilayer thin films are engineered stacks of two or more nanometer- to micrometer-scale material layers deposited on a substrate, where each layer thickness and composition are chosen to produce desired combined properties. Their behavior arises from interference, interface and confinement effects (optical, electronic, thermal or mechanical), enabling applications such as high-reflectivity mirrors, anti-reflection coatings, filters, and barrier layers."},{"id":"0d10d1f4-84a2-4f98-8606-51df929ca2da","l":"Multiplexers","d":2,"p":"2360e96e-a12b-4d21-9b65-36ed1177c0e6","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A multiplexer (MUX) is a device that, under the control of binary selection signals, chooses one of several input channels and forwards that input to a single output. Multiplexers can be analog or digital and are used to combine multiple signals onto one line (and are the functional inverse of demultiplexers), enabling time-division or channel-sharing in communications and measurement systems."},{"id":"840235cc-68b1-4db7-badc-6f51e5dc013f","l":"Multipole order","d":2,"p":"979074fa-fadd-4ebe-acf1-0a9c92fb1848","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Multipole order denotes the classification of a spatial distribution of charges, currents, or localized moments by its multipole rank (monopole l=0, dipole l=1, quadrupole l=2, octupole l=3, \u2026), which specifies the angular symmetry of the field or source via spherical harmonics. In condensed-matter contexts it also denotes an ordered phase in which a particular multipole moment acquires a nonzero expectation value, breaking the symmetries associated with that rank."},{"id":"2aa60c9a-e26a-4bc7-8eb8-c6051a22fae2","l":"Multi-purpose particle detectors","d":2,"p":"be8bd934-5bc9-4a6d-ae73-f4753ce5c128","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Multi-purpose particle detectors are large, general-purpose experimental instruments designed to detect, identify, and precisely measure many types of particles and interactions across a wide range of physics processes. They combine layered subsystems\u2014tracking, calorimetry, muon detectors, and flexible trigger/data\u2011acquisition\u2014to provide broad angular acceptance, particle identification, and accurate energy/momentum measurements."},{"id":"1f7cf170-4313-4542-94eb-56a6c9a50917","l":"Muon accelerators & neutrino factories","d":2,"p":"226db421-ad24-4e1c-9fbf-5de508711fa2","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Muon accelerators are machines that produce, capture, rapidly cool and accelerate muons\u2014short\u2011lived, heavy cousins of the electron\u2014so they can be used for high\u2011energy collisions or as intense particle sources. A neutrino factory stores accelerated muons in a decay ring with long straight sections so their well\u2011understood decays yield intense, flavor\u2011controlled neutrino beams for precision oscillation and cross\u2011section experiments; both approaches require very fast acceleration and specialized cooling to counter the muon's short lifetime."},{"id":"7659a6d1-bbc1-470d-8a13-945894b75652","l":"Muon spin relaxation & rotation","d":2,"p":"0003fc3f-1522-4fca-bf3f-bcef0dcec405","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Muon spin relaxation and rotation (\u03bcSR) is an experimental technique in which spin\u2011polarized muons implanted into a material act as local magnetic probes: their spins precess (rotation) and lose coherence (relaxation) in response to static and fluctuating internal magnetic fields, revealing microscopic magnetic structure and dynamics. The time evolution of the muon spin is measured via the anisotropic emission of decay positrons, giving time\u2011resolved information on local field magnitude, distribution, and fluctuations."},{"id":"5789de09-ecb7-4356-9989-f25314dbbdf1","l":"Muon spin resonance","d":2,"p":"6fab7f86-2de1-4f52-9425-ed4f9aec008b","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Muon spin resonance (muSR) is an experimental technique in which spin\u2011polarized positive muons are implanted into a material and their spins precess and relax in the sample's local magnetic fields. The time\u2011dependent anisotropy of emitted decay positrons is measured to determine microscopic internal fields, magnetic order, and spin dynamics (on the muon lifetime timescale \u22482.2 \u03bcs)."},{"id":"c9118388-9237-4f60-a4f7-29316d3d1af7","l":"Nanomechanical devices","d":2,"p":"2360e96e-a12b-4d21-9b65-36ed1177c0e6","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Nanomechanical devices are mechanical structures with characteristic dimensions in the nanometer to sub-micrometer range that support controlled motion (vibrations, flexure, torsion) for sensing, actuation, signal processing, or probing fundamental physics. Their extremely small mass and high resonant frequencies give them very high sensitivity to forces, masses, and displacements and allow coupling to electrical, optical, and quantum degrees of freedom."},{"id":"03c374d7-5aca-44c9-ab5b-fbcf56ae0145","l":"Nano-oscillators","d":2,"p":"2360e96e-a12b-4d21-9b65-36ed1177c0e6","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Nano-oscillators are nanoscale devices or physical systems that produce or support sustained periodic motion or oscillatory responses (mechanical, electronic, magnetic, or optomechanical) with at least one characteristic dimension in the nanometer range. Their small mass and size give high resonance frequencies and accentuate surface, damping, and quantum effects, enabling applications in high\u2011frequency signal generation, sensing, and studies of mesoscopic dynamics."},{"id":"9624387a-3245-40d3-b2c2-60dcc76d065d","l":"Nanophotonics","d":2,"p":"99f32be9-888e-42c5-ba4a-1fdcea060307","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Nanophotonics is the study and engineering of light and light\u2013matter interactions on nanometer length scales\u2014comparable to or smaller than the wavelength\u2014where near-field, subwavelength confinement and quantum effects become important. It covers nanostructures such as plasmonic materials, photonic crystals, dielectric nanoresonators and quantum emitters used to control, guide and manipulate optical fields for sensing, imaging, communications and quantum technologies."},{"id":"ad0598aa-8664-48cf-b086-5d0b719997e2","l":"Narrow band gap systems","d":2,"p":"12721ec5-11d3-484a-9ea9-a7bea1986722","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Narrow band gap systems are materials whose electronic valence\u2013conduction band gap is very small (typically a few meV to a few hundred meV), so thermal or quantum fluctuations readily excite carriers across the gap. Consequently they exhibit high intrinsic carrier concentrations, strong temperature-dependent transport and optical responses, and often unusual thermoelectric or semimetal-like behavior."},{"id":"2e58d323-1467-4ca4-972e-bb88b6a363d6","l":"Negative refraction","d":2,"p":"5ef62623-4765-4b21-8fce-7542d68c9faa","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Negative refraction is the phenomenon where a wave crossing an interface is refracted to the same side of the normal as the incident ray, corresponding to an effective negative refractive index. In such media (e.g., certain metamaterials or photonic crystals) the phase velocity points opposite to the energy flow (Poynting vector), producing the inverted bending."},{"id":"873d9852-3e6c-4ebc-aee4-a09350c09780","l":"Nematic order","d":2,"p":"979074fa-fadd-4ebe-acf1-0a9c92fb1848","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Nematic order is an orientationally ordered state in which anisotropic constituents (molecules, spins, or electrons) align on average along a common axis (the director) while lacking long-range positional order, so rotational symmetry is broken but translational symmetry is not. It is apolar (head\u2013tail symmetric) and is commonly described by a traceless symmetric second-rank order parameter Qij whose principal eigenvector gives the director."},{"id":"bbcb2a3b-6cdf-4d31-96aa-1454cb9e42d5","l":"Nematic phase transition","d":2,"p":"19f26688-0955-4fd9-85d8-aaf71c4a581f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A nematic phase transition is the change from an isotropic (or less-ordered) state into a nematic state where anisotropic molecules or degrees of freedom develop long-range orientational order while preserving average translational symmetry. It breaks rotational symmetry and is described by a director (or a traceless symmetric Q-tensor) order parameter; the transition can be continuous or first-order depending on interactions and fluctuations."},{"id":"9fcc87df-7e31-4052-a49d-1b3f096ac7f8","l":"Nernst effect","d":2,"p":"6e14e903-9322-41db-b946-0b518ecafdf7","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Nernst effect is a transverse thermoelectric phenomenon in which a magnetic field applied perpendicular to a temperature gradient induces an electric field orthogonal to both; quantitatively E = N B \u00d7 \u2207T, where N is the Nernst coefficient. It originates from the magnetic deflection of heat-driven charge carriers (or of superconducting vortices) and provides information on carrier entropy and scattering."},{"id":"b80b0695-3ad7-4617-9b06-9787823eb6ea","l":"Nerve cells","d":2,"p":"3e04a8a2-1199-4410-801c-ac848b0bbda8","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Nerve cells (neurons) are specialized excitable cells that generate and propagate electrical signals\u2014changes in membrane potential and action potentials\u2014by controlled flows of ions through membrane channels. They integrate inputs via dendrites and soma and transmit information along axons to other neurons or effector cells through chemical or electrical synapses."},{"id":"207ef9e7-f687-4813-a6db-5a44887e04ea","l":"Nesting","d":2,"p":"78d12677-c7dc-427e-acd7-cbff78a1713f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Nesting (in condensed matter) is the condition where large portions of the Fermi surface can be mapped onto other portions by a single wavevector Q, which strongly enhances particle\u2013hole susceptibilities and promotes collective instabilities such as charge- or spin-density waves (and can influence superconductivity)."},{"id":"2c814a4c-abc6-4729-8893-298956b99582","l":"Network inference","d":2,"p":"7b9db112-8475-42b4-ad22-f00f6b8635f8","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Network inference is the process of reconstructing a system's connectivity\u2014which nodes are linked and with what coupling strengths\u2014from observational data (e.g., time series, correlations, or response measurements). It uses statistical and computational methods (inverse statistical mechanics, sparse regression, Bayesian/maximum-likelihood estimation, information-theoretic or causality tools) to identify the topology and interaction parameters that best explain the observed dynamics."},{"id":"e6ace51b-6ddc-4425-a0c7-14dcd1b4bd8c","l":"Neural encoding","d":2,"p":"ce1c6c0a-58ee-41a7-a3b2-83d268ffbda6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Neural encoding is the mapping by which external stimuli or internal variables are represented as patterns of neural activity (e.g., spike trains, firing rates, or population codes). It is typically treated as a stimulus\u2192response transformation, often probabilistic, that characterizes which features of the input are reliably conveyed by neurons."},{"id":"e5246ca5-f13e-49eb-a63f-7e37d07a9cbd","l":"Neural information","d":2,"p":"5151d67c-cd9b-47c1-8098-5d41f48d8bf0","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Neural information is the information carried, processed, and transmitted by the states and activity of neural systems\u2014e.g., spike trains, firing rates, and synaptic configurations\u2014typically quantified with information-theoretic measures such as entropy and mutual information relating neural activity to stimuli, actions, or internal variables. It is constrained by physical factors like noise, dynamics, and metabolic cost, which set limits on coding fidelity and channel capacity."},{"id":"e41b5a7f-9076-4dd6-9e29-d884f8143d00","l":"Neuromorphic computing","d":2,"p":"4c886248-148a-41d4-888c-de09e60e1e87","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Neuromorphic computing is an approach to designing hardware and algorithms that mimic the structure and dynamics of biological neural networks\u2014neurons, synapses and spikes\u2014to perform computation in a non\u2013von Neumann, event-driven way. By co\u2011locating memory and processing and using devices such as spiking CMOS circuits, memristors, photonic or spintronic elements that implement synaptic plasticity, neuromorphic systems aim for massively parallel, low\u2011latency, energy\u2011efficient learning and perception."},{"id":"e67acefe-5a09-46b3-8d2c-8412b7df877a","l":"Neuronal avalanches","d":2,"p":"205e6918-12a2-4f4c-9795-8f723f476866","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Neuronal avalanches are cascades of spiking or local-field-potential activity in neuronal networks that occur as bursts separated by quiescent intervals and whose sizes and durations follow power-law (scale-invariant) distributions. Their statistics and propagation are consistent with dynamics near a critical branching point, suggesting the brain operates close to a critical state."},{"id":"3a68853d-8031-4222-9eb4-ca56627c40cd","l":"Neuronal network models","d":2,"p":"7b9db112-8475-42b4-ad22-f00f6b8635f8","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Neuronal network models are mathematical and computational descriptions of populations of neurons and their synaptic connections that capture how activity propagates, organizes, and adapts in brain-like circuits. They range from biophysically detailed spiking-neuron formulations to abstract rate- or mean-field models and typically include network topology, synaptic dynamics, external inputs, and plasticity rules to study dynamics, coding, and learning."},{"id":"391ab427-b471-438c-aa41-02b301fffc62","l":"Neuroplasticity","d":2,"p":"ce1c6c0a-58ee-41a7-a3b2-83d268ffbda6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Neuroplasticity is the nervous system's ability to adapt its structure and function\u2014by altering synaptic strengths, forming or pruning connections, and remodeling neurons\u2014to support learning, memory, and recovery from injury. It operates across multiple timescales, from rapid changes in synaptic efficacy (milliseconds\u2013hours) to slower structural and circuit-level rewiring (days\u2013months)."},{"id":"9557aa49-72bb-404d-afe9-76ca9e5e5ef2","l":"Neutrino detection","d":2,"p":"be8bd934-5bc9-4a6d-ae73-f4753ce5c128","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Neutrino detection is the set of experimental techniques used to observe neutrinos\u2014very light, electrically neutral particles that interact only via the weak force\u2014by registering the rare interactions they cause in matter. Detectors infer neutrino events from the secondary charged particles or nuclear changes those interactions produce (e.g., via Cherenkov or scintillation light, ionization tracks, or radiochemical transmutations) to measure neutrino flux, energy, and flavor."},{"id":"bf8ac618-dcbe-4098-91c1-f781ef555e5d","l":"Neutrino interactions","d":2,"p":"83c64883-5507-43f8-92bc-ae9f5ae03f02","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Neutrino interactions are the weak-force processes by which neutrinos scatter off or are absorbed/emitted by other particles\u2014typically electrons, nucleons, or nuclei\u2014mediated by W\u00b1 (charged-current, which produces a charged lepton) or Z0 (neutral-current, which leaves the neutrino's flavor unchanged) bosons. These interactions have very small cross sections, making them rare but central to neutrino detection and to studies in particle physics and astrophysics."},{"id":"d3bfaca4-3f7c-4619-b15c-1522f85d3c89","l":"Neutrinoless double beta decay","d":2,"p":"bf8ac618-dcbe-4098-91c1-f781ef555e5d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Neutrinoless double beta decay is a hypothetical second-order weak nuclear transition in which a nucleus emits two electrons (beta particles) but no neutrinos, thereby changing its charge by two while violating total lepton number by two units. Its observation would imply that neutrinos are Majorana particles and reveal physics beyond the Standard Model, constraining the absolute neutrino mass scale."},{"id":"47a1bb2e-c1ae-4b2c-a48d-b75767137d82","l":"Neutrino mass","d":2,"p":"a164d72c-84d8-4332-a3b1-a9199a4f50ad","fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Neutrino mass is the (very small) rest mass of the neutrino mass eigenstates \u2014 a nonzero inertial mass that enables neutrinos to change flavor (neutrino oscillations). Oscillation experiments determine differences of squared masses (\u0394m^2) while the absolute mass scale is set by direct kinematic measurements (beta-decay endpoints) and cosmological limits."},{"id":"60981b34-f6ea-4914-8ffc-71959d12ea29","l":"Neutrino mixing","d":2,"p":"5adb0193-f741-4713-8010-4270b7bf5d9c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Neutrino mixing is the quantum mechanical phenomenon in which the neutrino states produced and detected by the weak interaction (flavor eigenstates \u03bde, \u03bd\u03bc, \u03bd\u03c4) are linear superpositions of the neutrino mass eigenstates (\u03bd1, \u03bd2, \u03bd3), related by a unitary mixing matrix (the PMNS matrix). Because the mass eigenstates propagate with different phases, a neutrino's flavor composition changes with time and distance, producing neutrino oscillations."},{"id":"703e2e58-467b-463a-949c-c451ad620e52","l":"Neutrino oscillations","d":2,"p":"5adb0193-f741-4713-8010-4270b7bf5d9c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Neutrino oscillations are a quantum-mechanical phenomenon in which a neutrino produced with a definite flavor (electron, muon, or tau) can change into a different flavor as it propagates. This occurs because the flavor states are coherent superpositions of nondegenerate mass eigenstates, so the relative phases evolve with distance and energy, giving flavor-change probabilities determined by mass-squared differences and mixing angles."},{"id":"01421c08-8671-4d10-8fad-3f514f51033e","l":"Neutron capture","d":2,"p":"fd8cf2dd-2362-4994-a45c-a9b453fe508d","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Neutron capture is the process in which a nucleus absorbs a free neutron to form a heavier, often excited, compound nucleus that typically de-excites by emitting gamma rays (radiative capture). If the excitation energy is sufficient the nucleus may instead emit other particles (e.g., proton or \u03b1), so capture cross sections (which depend strongly on neutron energy and nuclear structure) govern reactions important in reactors, nucleosynthesis, and activation analysis."},{"id":"8b217c72-a730-4357-b137-03ebc83a72e1","l":"Neutron diffraction","d":2,"p":"fd8cf2dd-2362-4994-a45c-a9b453fe508d","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Neutron diffraction is the coherent elastic scattering of a neutron beam from the periodic arrangement of atomic nuclei and magnetic moments in a material, producing interference patterns that reveal the crystal and magnetic structure. Because neutrons are electrically neutral and carry a magnetic moment, they penetrate deeply, have wavelengths comparable to interatomic spacings, and provide unique sensitivity to light elements, isotopes, and magnetic order not easily accessed by X-rays."},{"id":"1515b875-7014-43c8-b872-65e6551b38f0","l":"Neutron imaging","d":2,"p":"fd8cf2dd-2362-4994-a45c-a9b453fe508d","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Neutron imaging is a non\u2011destructive imaging technique that forms 2D or 3D images by transmitting a beam of neutrons through a sample and mapping spatial variations in neutron attenuation and scattering. Because neutrons interact strongly with certain light elements and isotopes but weakly with heavy metals, it provides complementary contrast to X\u2011rays for revealing internal features, hydrogenous materials, and isotope distributions."},{"id":"bf26f14c-735e-46f0-a8aa-f2bd740f005e","l":"Neutron irradiation","d":2,"p":"fd8cf2dd-2362-4994-a45c-a9b453fe508d","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Neutron irradiation is the exposure of a material or device to a flux of free neutrons (from a reactor, spallation source, or accelerator), causing nuclear reactions such as activation and transmutation. Those neutron-induced processes produce atomic displacements, defects, heating, and changes in mechanical, electrical, and isotopic properties of the irradiated material."},{"id":"a98e9efc-f257-4da1-a29d-1e6918850e82","l":"Neutron pair-distribution function analysis","d":2,"p":"fd8cf2dd-2362-4994-a45c-a9b453fe508d","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Neutron pair-distribution function (PDF) analysis is a technique that obtains the real-space distribution of interatomic distances by Fourier transforming the total neutron scattering intensity (including Bragg and diffuse scattering) to produce G(r) or g(r), the probability of finding atom pairs separated by distance r. It reveals local atomic arrangements, short-range order, and disorder in crystalline, amorphous, or nanoscale materials, with particular sensitivity to light elements and isotopic contrast because neutrons scatter from nuclei."},{"id":"bb241a59-336d-4f3c-9eb0-a96d526e294f","l":"Neutron reflectometry","d":2,"p":"fd8cf2dd-2362-4994-a45c-a9b453fe508d","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Neutron reflectometry is an experimental technique that measures the specular reflection of a neutron beam at grazing incidence from planar surfaces and interfaces to determine the depth-dependent scattering length density\u2014i.e., composition, layering, roughness, and magnetic profiles\u2014on nanometer length scales. By recording reflectivity versus momentum transfer and using modeling (including isotope contrast and polarized neutrons), one extracts layer thicknesses, interfacial structure, and magnetization depth profiles."},{"id":"d9057958-4bba-4f0f-ae7d-ef5fb8dc1f36","l":"Neutrons","d":2,"p":"b0ebe8a2-ed86-4c14-bfdd-5860e15b18bc","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Neutrons are electrically neutral, spin-1/2 baryons made of one up quark and two down quarks that, together with protons, form atomic nuclei and determine isotopic properties and nuclear binding. Free neutrons are unstable and undergo beta decay into a proton, electron, and antineutrino with a mean lifetime of about 880 seconds, though they are stabilized by the strong interaction when bound in nuclei."},{"id":"c74c7b81-5dee-4820-a500-6ab6f1b48cb1","l":"Neutron scattering","d":2,"p":"fd8cf2dd-2362-4994-a45c-a9b453fe508d","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Neutron scattering is the interaction of a beam of neutrons with the nuclei and magnetic moments in a material, where neutrons are deflected either elastically (revealing atomic and magnetic structure) or inelastically (probing excitations and dynamics such as phonons and magnons). Because neutrons are electrically neutral and carry a magnetic moment, they penetrate deeply and provide isotope- and spin-sensitive information about materials."},{"id":"f8282e55-d0a9-498a-ac57-7508a391c80f","l":"Neutron spallation facilities","d":2,"p":"4eec2798-da70-4fc2-8371-f6aa5f6180d7","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Neutron spallation facilities are research centers that produce intense neutron beams by bombarding a heavy-metal target (e.g., tungsten, mercury) with high\u2011energy protons, causing nuclear spallation in which many neutrons are knocked out of target nuclei. Those neutrons are then moderated and channeled to instruments for neutron scattering, imaging, materials research, and isotope production."},{"id":"3ce0b7aa-0081-4dfb-9c30-a5335b38f150","l":"Neutron spin echo spectroscopy","d":2,"p":"fd8cf2dd-2362-4994-a45c-a9b453fe508d","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Neutron spin echo (NSE) spectroscopy is a neutron scattering technique that encodes energy transfers as phase shifts of the neutron spin via controlled Larmor precession in magnetic fields, achieving very high energy (\u03bceV) resolution independent of beam monochromaticity. It directly measures the intermediate scattering function in the time domain, making it ideal for probing slow dynamical processes such as diffusion, polymer and membrane dynamics, and relaxational motions."},{"id":"033b7123-5ac3-430f-8c2e-70837a53fb00","l":"Neutron star crust","d":2,"p":"c17001d7-c0bd-41d6-b970-ea4269e7ec06","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"The neutron star crust is the outer, roughly 0.5\u20131 km thick, solid layer of a neutron star consisting of a Coulomb lattice of increasingly neutron-rich nuclei embedded in a degenerate electron gas and capable of supporting shear stresses. Deeper in the inner crust nuclei become so neutron-rich that neutrons \"drip\" out to form a superfluid background and, near the crust\u2013core boundary, matter can arrange into exotic non-spherical \"nuclear pasta\" phases before transitioning to uniform nuclear matter in the core."},{"id":"466c100a-86af-414a-81c6-b8d82ab9febe","l":"Noise measurements","d":2,"p":"675773f0-6ff3-44de-8600-2e9034eea9f0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Noise measurements are experimental determinations of the random, unwanted fluctuations in electrical, optical, mechanical, or other physical signals and systems. They quantify those fluctuations with statistical descriptors (RMS, variance, power spectral density, noise temperature, noise figure) and time- or frequency-domain techniques (spectrum analyzers, FFTs, cross-correlation) to identify sources and set detection or precision limits."},{"id":"38ff1f3b-73c4-4075-82a2-868e22a06753","l":"Non-Abelian gauge theories","d":2,"p":"7e256d71-43dd-4793-87f0-7c8de704416c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Non-Abelian gauge theories are field theories whose local gauge symmetry is described by a non-commutative (non-Abelian) Lie group, so gauge transformations do not commute. As a result the gauge fields carry the group charge and interact nonlinearly with each other via terms proportional to the group's structure constants (examples include Yang\u2013Mills theory and quantum chromodynamics)."},{"id":"369130ef-d38b-4c26-8a4a-117f84912492","l":"Non-Abelian models","d":2,"p":"a1083ad2-a154-449d-a9ff-59bf46db1c7f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Non-Abelian models are theoretical frameworks whose symmetries are described by non-commutative (non-Abelian) groups, meaning symmetry operations do not commute. This noncommutativity produces richer structure and dynamics\u2014e.g., self-interacting gauge fields\u2014and underlies theories like Yang\u2013Mills and quantum chromodynamics (SU(2), SU(3))."},{"id":"e6390bcf-ccc0-4722-bb8b-532b07949ba7","l":"Noncentrosymmetric materials","d":2,"p":"6af5be88-489f-4a50-9abf-998e8912254a","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Noncentrosymmetric materials are solids whose crystal structure does not have a center of inversion \u2014 the operation r \u2192 \u2212r is not a symmetry of the lattice \u2014 so spatial inversion symmetry is broken. This symmetry breaking permits phenomena forbidden in centrosymmetric crystals, such as piezoelectricity and bulk polarization, even-order nonlinear optics (e.g., second-harmonic generation), and antisymmetric spin\u2013orbit effects (Rashba/Dresselhaus)."},{"id":"cd403d00-d86b-41dc-a6fb-72bc3a302627","l":"Nonequilibrium & irreversible thermodynamics","d":2,"p":"609a2adc-099f-463f-9e2a-dd85c0101044","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"The study of thermodynamic systems driven away from equilibrium, focusing on irreversible processes that produce entropy and generate fluxes (heat, mass, charge, momentum) in response to thermodynamic forces (gradients of temperature, chemical potential, etc.). It encompasses macroscopic frameworks\u2014local equilibrium, linear response and Onsager reciprocity\u2014as well as nonlinear and stochastic approaches to describe relaxation, transport, and nonequilibrium steady states."},{"id":"175d7ede-663a-458e-9cc0-0de53c91520c","l":"Non-Fermi-liquid theory","d":2,"p":"05a47dcc-71c0-4ebc-9d4e-79a40a191efb","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Non-Fermi-liquid theory describes interacting fermion systems whose low-energy behavior cannot be captured by Landau's Fermi liquid: the notion of long-lived fermionic quasiparticles breaks down (quasiparticle residue vanishes or is ill-defined) and thermodynamic and transport properties show anomalous, non-quadratic scaling. Such behavior occurs in systems like one-dimensional Luttinger liquids, quantum critical metals, and \"strange\" heavy-fermion or cuprate compounds."},{"id":"ccb5afcb-29c8-48d3-849c-f5bc325b8105","l":"Nonlinear optical materials","d":2,"p":"dcf48409-af3f-4517-b58d-7ec6c95351f3","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Nonlinear optical materials are media whose induced polarization depends nonlinearly on the applied electric field of light, so at sufficiently high intensities the optical response cannot be described by a single, field-independent refractive index. This nonlinearity (described by higher-order susceptibilities \u03c7^(2), \u03c7^(3), ...) produces effects such as harmonic generation, sum/difference-frequency mixing, self-phase modulation, and an intensity-dependent refractive index (optical Kerr effect)."},{"id":"3e4edab1-417b-4db6-9a42-7bff12ef67c8","l":"Nonlinear optical susceptibility","d":2,"p":"bd03ea46-2406-4a24-8307-05b2b498fb4e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Nonlinear optical susceptibility is a set of frequency-dependent tensor coefficients that relate a material's induced polarization to higher-order products of the applied electric field (P = \u03b50[\u03c7(1)E + \u03c7(2):EE + \u03c7(3)\u22ef]), quantifying how the medium generates new frequencies and intensity-dependent refractive effects. Its magnitude and symmetry determine the efficiency of nonlinear processes such as second-harmonic generation, sum-frequency mixing, and the optical Kerr effect."},{"id":"300a7ec9-588f-45a6-ba93-47f80340107c","l":"Nonlinear resonance","d":2,"p":"bd03ea46-2406-4a24-8307-05b2b498fb4e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Nonlinear resonance is the strong excitation of a nonlinear oscillator or mode by periodic forcing when the drive frequency (or a combination of drive frequencies) locks to an amplitude-dependent natural frequency or an internal modal frequency. Unlike linear resonance, it produces amplitude-dependent frequency shifts, asymmetric resonance peaks, sub- and superharmonic responses, multistability and hysteresis, and enhanced modal energy transfer."},{"id":"0804ec3a-906d-428e-adba-aa03de620aa2","l":"Nonlinear sigma model","d":2,"p":"6f92bd2f-a884-4762-b3c4-2d9c011a118f","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A nonlinear sigma model is a field theory whose basic degrees of freedom are maps \u03c6(x) from spacetime into a curved target manifold M, with an action given primarily by the pullback of the metric on M so that the fields are constrained to lie on a nonlinear (non\u2011vector) manifold. These models describe e.g. continuum limits of spin systems, string worldsheet dynamics and patterns of symmetry breaking, and often include topological terms and nontrivial renormalization-group behavior."},{"id":"3fe81fb7-5b74-4ee4-84e2-966ee01a55b4","l":"Nonlinear waveguides","d":2,"p":"bd03ea46-2406-4a24-8307-05b2b498fb4e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Nonlinear waveguides are guiding structures (optical, microwave, or acoustic) whose material response depends on the local wave amplitude, so the governing wave equation becomes nonlinear. This intensity-dependent behavior produces effects such as self-phase modulation, self-focusing or defocusing, soliton formation, harmonic generation, and nonlinear mode coupling."},{"id":"6e1e2c55-5018-44bb-af02-f8907952187a","l":"Nonlocality","d":2,"p":"d599eade-2dc5-4572-84ac-54f7a980fbfb","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Nonlocality is the property of a theory or phenomenon in which the state or dynamics at one location depend directly on variables or events at spatially separated locations, rather than only on local fields and their derivatives. In quantum mechanics it refers specifically to entangled correlations that violate Bell inequalities and cannot be explained by local hidden\u2011variable models, even though they do not permit faster\u2011than\u2011light signaling."},{"id":"04034da4-5062-459a-b1a9-cb2ff33c877a","l":"Nonlocal nonlinear media","d":2,"p":"dcf48409-af3f-4517-b58d-7ec6c95351f3","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Nonlocal nonlinear media are materials whose constitutive response (for example a refractive-index change) depends nonlinearly on the field amplitude and also on the field distribution at other points, so the induced change at a given location is a nonlinear functional of the field over an extended region. This spatial nonlocality\u2014seen in thermal, electrostrictive, and photorefractive media\u2014alters wave dynamics (e.g., soliton stability and modulational instability) compared with purely local nonlinearities."},{"id":"25a98813-4461-40e1-97be-e5e2dd8fb570","l":"Non-Markovian processes","d":2,"p":"695da376-c44a-41dd-b968-d200c31e9d5e","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems","Plasma Physics"],"def":"Non-Markovian processes are stochastic or dynamical processes whose future evolution depends on the system's past history, not solely on its current state, and therefore exhibit memory effects. They are characterized by time-correlated (colored) noise and nonlocal-in-time evolution (memory kernels), leading to non-exponential relaxation and, in quantum systems, possible information backflow between system and environment."},{"id":"5cedebeb-56df-4037-a9f7-803a10b9e13c","l":"Nonrelativistic QCD","d":2,"p":"74f884e0-6764-4962-a081-b198cca267c3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Nonrelativistic QCD (NRQCD) is an effective field theory of quantum chromodynamics tailored to heavy-quark systems (like charmonium or bottomonium) in which the heavy quark velocity v is much less than c. It is obtained by integrating out the hard scale ~m and expanding in powers of 1/m and v, providing a systematic power-counting and factorization framework that separates short-distance perturbative coefficients from long-distance nonperturbative matrix elements for spectroscopy, decays, and production."},{"id":"4861b950-93ec-45de-9598-db0a06582a80","l":"Nonsequential double (or multiple) ionization","d":2,"p":"4054c254-9f83-4a0b-a8d1-1d2a99e11213","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Nonsequential double (or multiple) ionization is the correlated removal of two or more electrons from an atom or molecule in a single interaction event, where electron-electron correlations (e.g., recollision or shake-off) directly eject additional electrons rather than by independent, time-separated single-ionization steps. It therefore produces correlated electron emission on the same ultrafast timescale and with characteristic joint energy and angular distributions."},{"id":"7c64589b-99c1-4f11-8592-a6e0c273f589","l":"Nuclear charge radii","d":2,"p":"f837d92b-2b47-45d3-8803-a555715c1b78","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"The nuclear charge radius is a measure of the spatial extent of a nucleus's electric-charge distribution (i.e., the protons), usually quantified by the root-mean-square (rms) charge radius r_ch = sqrt(<r^2>_charge). It is typically on the order of 1 femtometer and is determined experimentally from electron scattering, muonic-atom spectroscopy, and optical isotope shifts."},{"id":"e57ab27c-9c45-4697-92b7-680cee4591df","l":"Nuclear decay","d":2,"p":"8c48a332-f38c-4fde-affa-82f036e2aa25","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Nuclear decay (radioactive decay) is the spontaneous transformation of an unstable atomic nucleus into a more stable nucleus or nuclear state, accompanied by emission of particles (e.g., alpha, beta) and/or photons (gamma) and sometimes by fission. It is a random process with a constant decay probability per nucleus, giving an exponential decrease of activity characterized by a half-life."},{"id":"047c9d16-460f-4d03-b991-42f412b9c272","l":"Nuclear fusion","d":2,"p":"8c48a332-f38c-4fde-affa-82f036e2aa25","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics","Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Nuclear fusion is the process in which two light atomic nuclei collide and join to form a heavier nucleus, releasing energy because the products have a higher binding energy per nucleon (so their total mass is slightly less than the reactants). It requires very high temperatures and/or pressures to overcome the Coulomb repulsion between nuclei and is the primary energy source of stars and fusion reactors."},{"id":"fee9296a-01ca-4b80-86bf-2c51f37c1871","l":"Nuclear magnetic resonance","d":2,"p":"6fab7f86-2de1-4f52-9425-ed4f9aec008b","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Nuclear magnetic resonance is the resonant absorption and re\u2011emission of radio\u2011frequency electromagnetic radiation by nuclei with nonzero spin when placed in a static magnetic field, occurring at the Larmor frequency set by the field and the nucleus' gyromagnetic ratio. It is used to probe local magnetic environments and molecular structure via shifts, spin\u2013spin couplings, and relaxation behavior."},{"id":"d7a8e4ea-0682-4c0a-b452-0eb01289b956","l":"Nuclear quadrupole resonance","d":2,"p":"6fab7f86-2de1-4f52-9425-ed4f9aec008b","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Nuclear quadrupole resonance (NQR) is a radiofrequency spectroscopic technique that probes nuclei with electric quadrupole moments (spin \u2265 1) through their interaction with the local electric field gradient, causing characteristic energy-level splittings and resonant transitions even in the absence of an external magnetic field. It is used to reveal local charge distribution, bonding and symmetry in solids and molecules."},{"id":"fe162bea-3cd4-46d7-a1b1-41f456d94796","l":"Nuclear spin resonance","d":2,"p":"6fab7f86-2de1-4f52-9425-ed4f9aec008b","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Nuclear spin resonance is the resonant absorption or emission of radiofrequency electromagnetic radiation by nuclear magnetic moments in a static magnetic field, occurring when the radiation frequency matches the nuclei's Larmor precession frequency and induces transitions between Zeeman-split spin states. It underlies techniques such as nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) and MRI, which probe local magnetic and chemical environments of nuclei."},{"id":"870d415a-ceb3-4e49-a475-798d07177a83","l":"Nucleic acids","d":2,"p":"6c0e8bb5-a5a0-4f28-8d4a-4e7ace85a023","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Nucleic acids are biological polymers (DNA and RNA) made of nucleotide monomers consisting of a sugar-phosphate backbone and nitrogenous bases whose sequence encodes genetic information. Physically, they behave as charged, semiflexible polymers whose base-pairing and stacking interactions produce structures such as the DNA double helix and determine their mechanical and thermodynamic properties."},{"id":"694bca67-2e42-4933-9952-76dd646e0e8f","l":"Nucleons","d":2,"p":"b0ebe8a2-ed86-4c14-bfdd-5860e15b18bc","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Nucleons are the proton and neutron, the spin-1/2 baryons that constitute atomic nuclei. Each is a composite particle of three quarks bound by the strong interaction, and nucleons themselves are held together in nuclei by the residual strong (nuclear) force."},{"id":"33f199a2-1b0f-4e00-a922-a42ef08688db","l":"Nucleon spin structure","d":2,"p":"8fc05421-d713-43be-b560-4bca5de50eab","fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"The nucleon spin structure is the decomposition of a proton's or neutron's total spin-1/2 into the intrinsic spins of quarks and gluons and their orbital angular momenta, plus the dynamical correlations among these components. It is explored experimentally with polarized scattering and described theoretically by spin-dependent parton distributions, generalized/TMD distributions, and QCD spin sum rules."},{"id":"d0a164f0-57c8-431f-8402-e699a8a7dd9b","l":"Numerical relativity","d":2,"p":"ef675df4-aadf-4446-af41-0955eee689fb","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Numerical relativity is the use of computational algorithms to solve Einstein\u2019s nonlinear field equations for dynamical spacetimes that cannot be treated analytically. It typically casts the equations in an initial-value (3+1) form and evolves them with discretization or spectral methods to simulate phenomena such as black-hole and neutron-star mergers, gravitational-wave emission, and gravitational collapse."},{"id":"e7d6f15d-3877-42c1-bab5-2c3d5fa480d5","l":"Numerical Renormalization Group","d":2,"p":"50ba3f74-7f31-4857-8a75-b2b007348981","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields","Physics of Living Systems","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Numerical Renormalization Group (NRG) is a nonperturbative computational method that implements Wilson's renormalization idea by logarithmically discretizing a continuum bath, mapping it onto a one-dimensional chain with exponentially decaying hoppings, and iteratively diagonalizing while keeping only the low-energy states at each step. It is widely used to obtain accurate low-temperature and low-frequency properties (thermodynamics, spectral functions, and fixed-point behavior) of quantum impurity and related strongly correlated systems."},{"id":"1fcee116-6a2e-4a03-9719-94ebcee57082","l":"Olfaction","d":2,"p":"7b59f715-a6b8-4141-b762-668edfb84dc3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Olfaction is the sensory process of detecting and interpreting airborne or dissolved chemical molecules (odorants) that interact with olfactory receptors to produce neural signals perceived as smell. Physically, it depends on the transport of odorant molecules by diffusion and advection to receptor sites and the stochastic binding and transduction events that convert chemical interactions into electrical signals."},{"id":"a07d946b-68bd-49b3-8caa-d6e7a9d904e0","l":"Olivines","d":2,"p":"6af5be88-489f-4a50-9abf-998e8912254a","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Olivines are a group of orthorhombic nesosilicate minerals with general formula (Mg,Fe)2SiO4, forming a solid solution between the magnesium endmember forsterite and the iron endmember fayalite, and characterized by isolated SiO4 tetrahedra with Mg/Fe cations in octahedral sites. They are the dominant minerals of Earth's upper mantle and many meteorites and thus strongly influence the elastic, thermal and phase\u2011transition behavior of planetary interiors."},{"id":"656ee730-4186-4197-8d82-7073b500fdaf","l":"Online social networks","d":2,"p":"18aaf314-921a-463e-aacb-1b7898708dc4","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Online social networks are socio-technical graphs whose nodes are users (or accounts) and whose links represent digital ties such as friendships, follows, messages or co-participation; these links can be directed, weighted and multiplex, and the networks evolve in time. Physicists study their large-scale topology and dynamics using network science and statistical-physics tools to understand information spreading, contagion, influence, and emergent collective behavior."},{"id":"22f674ce-3670-4b64-be34-1dc2152c5fea","l":"Optical bistability","d":2,"p":"bd03ea46-2406-4a24-8307-05b2b498fb4e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Optical bistability is the property of an optical system (typically a resonator with an intensity-dependent refractive index or absorption) to admit two distinct, stable output (or intracavity) intensities for the same input intensity because of the interplay between optical nonlinearity and feedback, producing hysteresis when the input is varied."},{"id":"ecf6e836-240a-4093-9d1c-46680eeeccad","l":"Optical chaos","d":2,"p":"bd03ea46-2406-4a24-8307-05b2b498fb4e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Optical chaos is the occurrence of deterministic chaotic behavior in optical systems, arising from nonlinearity and feedback and manifesting as aperiodic, sensitive dependence on initial conditions in lasers, cavities, and nonlinear photonic devices. It is characterized by broadband, noise\u2011like spectra and positive Lyapunov exponents despite being generated by purely deterministic equations."},{"id":"88881b13-215a-42f5-aa11-c46ba5343090","l":"Optical cloaking","d":2,"p":"77fabbae-1f98-4300-996c-9158ab52e6fc","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Optical cloaking is the set of techniques that make an object appear invisible by controlling the propagation of light so that the field leaving the cloaked region matches the field that would be present if the object were not there. Realizations use approaches such as transformation optics and metamaterials, scattering-cancellation, or active wavefront shaping to steer or cancel scattered light, and are generally limited in bandwidth, viewing angles, and loss."},{"id":"830c747a-630a-4511-9ebd-e2a91ed40337","l":"Optical computing","d":2,"p":"a38dc017-3236-4084-afc5-eb59c5de51e0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Optical computing is the use of light\u2014photons and optical components\u2014to represent, transmit, and process information, performing logical and arithmetic operations via linear and nonlinear optical effects, interference, and diffraction instead of electronic charge flow. It exploits the high bandwidth, low latency, and intrinsic parallelism of optical signals in free-space, fiber, or integrated photonic circuits for computation and signal processing."},{"id":"9bb26fbc-67b8-4a8f-b87f-550ff19924ff","l":"Optical conformal mapping","d":2,"p":"82f6c5fd-bbef-4ff9-a5f9-de62d3f5dfeb","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Optical conformal mapping uses complex-analytic, angle-preserving (conformal) coordinate transformations to design spatially varying refractive-index profiles that steer light as if the geometry of space were deformed. In two-dimensional wave problems this produces isotropic, scalar-index media (avoiding the anisotropy of general transformation optics) and is used to make devices such as cloaks, beam shifters, and lenses."},{"id":"f075546e-d81b-4169-8ea5-3fca491e04bb","l":"Optical desorption spectroscopy","d":2,"p":"a38dc017-3236-4084-afc5-eb59c5de51e0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Optical desorption spectroscopy is a surface-analysis technique in which light (typically a laser) is used to eject adsorbed atoms or molecules from a surface and the yield of desorbed species is measured as a function of photon energy, fluence, or time. By analyzing how desorption depends on wavelength and intensity, the method reveals information about adsorbate binding energies, electronic or vibrational excitation pathways, and surface desorption dynamics."},{"id":"f7cf6ebf-ce2c-4b70-8e90-bc74a1729337","l":"Optical fibers","d":2,"p":"dcf48409-af3f-4517-b58d-7ec6c95351f3","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Optical fibers are thin, flexible dielectric waveguides\u2014typically glass or plastic\u2014consisting of a higher\u2011index core surrounded by a lower\u2011index cladding that confines and transmits light by total internal reflection. They guide discrete optical modes (single\u2011 or multi\u2011mode) for communications and sensing, with performance limited by attenuation and dispersion."},{"id":"13376217-db71-43eb-abb8-252e043aa99a","l":"Optical interactions with surfaces","d":2,"p":"3123cbf9-460d-467a-9306-b4fb316daddd","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Optical interactions with surfaces describe how light is reflected, transmitted, absorbed or scattered at a material boundary, determined by electromagnetic boundary conditions and the surface's optical properties (refractive index, absorption, roughness, coatings). They encompass specular and diffuse reflection, refraction, thin-film interference, diffraction, evanescent coupling and surface-confined excitations (e.g., surface plasmons), with outcomes that depend on wavelength, angle and polarization."},{"id":"ca9988fc-ff8d-4c19-ae21-68f7ba12e0e7","l":"Optical interferometry","d":2,"p":"a38dc017-3236-4084-afc5-eb59c5de51e0","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Optical interferometry is a technique that combines coherent light beams so their phase-dependent superposition produces interference fringes; the fringe pattern encodes tiny differences in optical path length, enabling precise measurement of distances, surface shapes, refractive-index changes, and wavefront aberrations with sub\u2011wavelength sensitivity."},{"id":"3fadef04-0d0b-4ca7-bdc5-9409eb481be0","l":"Optical isolators","d":2,"p":"dcf48409-af3f-4517-b58d-7ec6c95351f3","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Optical isolators are nonreciprocal optical devices that transmit light freely in one direction while strongly attenuating back-propagating light; they typically use a magneto\u2011optic Faraday rotator between polarizers to produce a direction-dependent polarization rotation that breaks reciprocity and protects lasers and sensitive optics from reflections."},{"id":"95b999dd-b903-4e07-9b42-6b395558b48c","l":"Optical lattices & traps","d":2,"p":"4c4f4d87-e837-4d65-970d-f5323698a5f1","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Optical lattices are periodic potentials for neutral atoms formed by the interference of laser beams, whose AC-Stark (light-shift) energy landscape localizes atoms at intensity maxima or minima and creates a tunable, crystal-like array. Optical traps more generally use focused or structured laser light to confine and manipulate atoms, molecules or microscopic particles via optical dipole (gradient) and scattering forces for cooling, precision measurement and quantum simulation."},{"id":"006cd748-6a9a-40d2-97e0-6fb2d8b3e905","l":"Optical levitation","d":2,"p":"4474a071-3610-4651-a0e2-5a399dd970fb","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Optical levitation is the suspension of matter against gravity using forces exerted by light\u2014primarily radiation pressure and optical-gradient forces from focused or resonant electromagnetic fields\u2014to trap atoms, droplets, microspheres, or nanoparticles in free space. Methods range from balancing an upward laser beam's scattering force to using optical tweezers or cavity fields that create stable gradient traps for manipulation and precision measurement."},{"id":"7f0abc75-583e-4934-aea5-9d0675b93a82","l":"Optically detected magnetic resonance","d":2,"p":"0003fc3f-1522-4fca-bf3f-bcef0dcec405","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Optically detected magnetic resonance (ODMR) is a spectroscopic technique in which magnetic-resonance transitions of electron or nuclear spins are driven by microwave or radiofrequency fields and read out indirectly via changes in an optical signal (fluorescence, absorption, or luminescence) that depend on the spin state. This enables sensitive, often spatially resolved detection of spin dynamics and environments without direct electrical detection, and is widely used in solid-state qubits, color centers, and atomic systems."},{"id":"03fd617c-bf7a-458e-88fc-472bcd5a9254","l":"Optical metallography","d":2,"p":"a38dc017-3236-4084-afc5-eb59c5de51e0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Optical metallography is the examination and characterization of the microstructure of metals and alloys by observing polished (and often chemically etched) surfaces with reflected visible-light microscopy to reveal grains, phases, inclusions, and defects that relate to processing and properties."},{"id":"5802dce1-0079-4688-9f4e-a79871191efc","l":"Optical microcavities","d":2,"p":"dcf48409-af3f-4517-b58d-7ec6c95351f3","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Optical microcavities are small resonant structures that trap and store light in discrete optical modes by multiple reflections or interference within a volume comparable to the wavelength. They are characterized by high quality factors and small mode volumes, which enhance and control light\u2013matter interactions for applications in lasers, sensors, and cavity quantum electrodynamics."},{"id":"a40cc113-e132-471a-bb1b-2d29d8248a64","l":"Optical microscopy","d":2,"p":"a38dc017-3236-4084-afc5-eb59c5de51e0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Optical microscopy is the use of visible (and near-UV/IR) light together with lenses and optical elements to form magnified images of small specimens, exploiting absorption, scattering, phase, or fluorescence contrast. Its spatial resolution is set by wave optics (the diffraction limit, roughly half the wavelength \u2248200 nm for visible light) unless one uses specialized super-resolution or nonlinear techniques that circumvent that limit."},{"id":"6d66ed0c-5f88-42bf-822b-6ca6391b3c6c","l":"Optical nanoscopy","d":2,"p":"a38dc017-3236-4084-afc5-eb59c5de51e0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Optical nanoscopy is a family of optical microscopy techniques that achieve spatial resolution well below the conventional Abbe diffraction limit (typically tens of nanometers or better). These methods extract subwavelength detail by using near\u2011field probes, nonlinear optical effects, patterned or time\u2011modulated illumination, or precise single\u2011molecule localization to encode and recover information at the nanoscale."},{"id":"e1596a2e-6470-42ac-8a18-6f7030f68aed","l":"Optical parametric oscillators & amplifiers","d":2,"p":"dcf48409-af3f-4517-b58d-7ec6c95351f3","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Optical parametric amplifiers (OPAs) and oscillators (OPOs) are nonlinear-optical devices that use parametric three-wave mixing in a medium with a second-order nonlinearity (\u03c7^(2)) to convert pump photons into signal and idler photons, providing gain and coherent frequency conversion while conserving energy and momentum (phase matching). An OPA gives single-pass amplification of an injected signal, whereas an OPO includes a resonant cavity so signal/idler fields build up and produce self\u2011sustained, tunable oscillation once the pump exceeds a threshold."},{"id":"eda3782d-bea8-440d-b146-496ce7370741","l":"Optical phonons","d":2,"p":"684c842a-625c-4cd3-bf2a-c64bbcbc1c24","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Optical phonons are lattice vibration modes in which atoms within the crystal basis oscillate against each other (out of phase), giving a nonzero frequency even at zero wavevector. In polar crystals these relative motions create macroscopic electric fields and therefore couple strongly to electromagnetic radiation (infrared absorption and Raman scattering)."},{"id":"196cd709-11cf-4d85-b531-c82c050f3b51","l":"Optical plasma measurements","d":2,"p":"a5866236-c99c-4828-b499-4f50cccf7a2b","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Optical plasma measurements are minimally invasive diagnostic techniques that use light\u2014emitted, absorbed, or scattered by the plasma\u2014or injected laser beams to infer its properties. Examples include passive spectroscopy, laser-induced fluorescence, Thomson scattering, and interferometry, which provide electron/ion densities, temperatures, flows, composition, and field information."},{"id":"270a99c1-e216-4048-9814-5f80212428de","l":"Optical properties of clusters","d":2,"p":"cac7f673-4cd1-47b8-9de3-9f9785aea585","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Optical properties of clusters describe how small aggregates of atoms or nanoparticles absorb, scatter, and emit electromagnetic radiation. These properties depend strongly on size, shape, composition and environment, showing quantum-confinement and surface effects (discrete electronic transitions) as well as collective excitations such as localized surface plasmon resonances that differ from bulk materials."},{"id":"48060ba5-3772-446b-a0cd-c58da79462c6","l":"Optical solitons","d":2,"p":"bd03ea46-2406-4a24-8307-05b2b498fb4e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Optical solitons are self\u2011reinforcing light pulses or beams that propagate without changing shape because dispersive spreading (group\u2011velocity dispersion or diffraction) is exactly balanced by nonlinear self\u2011phase modulation/self\u2011focusing (typically the Kerr effect). They are solitary\u2011wave solutions of the nonlinear Schr\u00f6dinger equation and appear in optical fibers, waveguides, and bulk nonlinear media."},{"id":"91c254a4-9edc-4bb7-b1bf-a47ec136efd2","l":"Optical sources & detectors","d":2,"p":"dcf48409-af3f-4517-b58d-7ec6c95351f3","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Optical sources are devices that generate electromagnetic radiation in the optical spectrum (ultraviolet\u2013visible\u2013infrared), such as lasers, LEDs and lamps, and are characterized by emission wavelength, power, coherence, and modulation bandwidth. Optical detectors are devices that convert incident optical radiation into an electrical signal (current or voltage)\u2014for example photodiodes, photomultiplier tubes and CCD/CMOS imagers\u2014characterized by responsivity, quantum efficiency, noise, bandwidth and dynamic range."},{"id":"efe40972-7a4f-4a87-80ef-ae39472ce875","l":"Optical spectroscopy","d":2,"p":"d8d9518f-91ee-40ab-9529-2c3f98431a0f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics"],"def":"Optical spectroscopy is the set of experimental techniques that use visible, ultraviolet, and near\u2011infrared light to probe matter by measuring how it absorbs, emits, or scatters photons. Analysis of the resulting wavelengths, intensities, and line shapes reveals electronic and vibrational energy levels, composition, temperature, and dynamical processes in atoms, molecules, and solids."},{"id":"c7d219bc-3c17-440b-a847-c10c46cff8fd","l":"Optical tests of quantum theory","d":2,"p":"632f74d1-d16b-4b92-a295-b24729a93d8d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Optical tests of quantum theory are laboratory experiments that use light (single photons, entangled photon pairs, lasers and interferometers) to probe and quantify foundational quantum phenomena\u2014such as superposition, entanglement, nonlocality, contextuality and complementarity\u2014and to test quantum predictions against classical or alternative models. Typical implementations include Bell-inequality tests, interference and delayed-choice experiments, quantum tomography, weak measurements, and precision tests of decoherence or collapse models."},{"id":"3284d3ff-ea72-4711-a99a-310e37d181dd","l":"Optical vortices","d":2,"p":"7fc8e960-d2c3-425f-9bc5-2990bf2b3fa1","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Optical vortices are structured light fields that contain phase singularities where the optical phase winds azimuthally around a line (or point) and the intensity goes to zero at the core, producing a helical wavefront. Each vortex is labeled by an integer topological charge equal to the number of 2\u03c0 phase twists around the core and carries orbital angular momentum proportional to that charge."},{"id":"03dc6715-06ab-41a4-9640-15809f90e47a","l":"Orbital order","d":2,"p":"979074fa-fadd-4ebe-acf1-0a9c92fb1848","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Orbital order is a long-range pattern in a solid in which electrons preferentially occupy particular atomic orbitals (typically degenerate d or f states), breaking the orbital degeneracy and spatial symmetry. It often arises from electron-electron interactions and electron\u2013lattice coupling (e.g., Jahn\u2013Teller effects) and leads to anisotropic electronic, magnetic, and structural properties."},{"id":"0cc941ef-b5e3-4f7f-99a3-de3f9cb4dd61","l":"Organelle","d":2,"p":"9ccf11df-5b72-4ce0-92e6-e48f8dda172b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"An organelle is a specialized subcellular structure\u2014often membrane-bound but sometimes formed by protein assemblies\u2014within a eukaryotic cell that performs a specific biochemical function. Examples include the nucleus (genetic storage), mitochondria (ATP production) and chloroplasts (photosynthesis); organelles are typically micrometer-scale and enable spatial compartmentalization of cellular processes."},{"id":"101b99f7-34cb-4ea9-8f26-b5d649d98640","l":"Organic compounds","d":2,"p":"3502cc4a-fa49-4558-90ea-e743a42e00b3","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Organic compounds are chemical compounds whose molecules are built on carbon frameworks, typically containing carbon\u2013hydrogen bonds and often also O, N, S, P or halogens. They span simple hydrocarbons to complex biomolecules and polymers and form the subject matter of organic chemistry."},{"id":"1e313b95-4f7a-4441-9a75-8db8d6066529","l":"Organic microcavities","d":2,"p":"dcf48409-af3f-4517-b58d-7ec6c95351f3","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Organic microcavities are optical resonators formed by placing an organic material (mye.g., molecular dyes, polymers or organic semiconductors) between reflective mirrors so that confined cavity photons interact with molecular excitons.  This confinement can modify spontaneous emission and, in the strong\u2011coupling regime, produce hybrid light\u2013matter states (exciton\u2013polaritons) with distinctive transport, lasing, and condensation behaviors."},{"id":"3edf35c2-58d3-40f7-b0a0-f4fa24717edb","l":"Organic sensors","d":2,"p":"c215fb56-b919-42c5-9267-13846d715223","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Organic sensors are devices that use carbon\u2011based materials (molecules, polymers, or organic semiconductors) to transduce physical, chemical, or biological stimuli into electrical or optical signals via changes in conductivity, charge density, fluorescence, or electrochemical potential. They are valued for mechanical flexibility, low\u2011cost solution processing, chemical tunability, and often enhanced biocompatibility for wearable and biointerfacing applications."},{"id":"95e2a8bb-1dd2-41b9-964a-f2c73de502d6","l":"Organic transistors","d":2,"p":"c215fb56-b919-42c5-9267-13846d715223","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Organic transistors are electronic switching devices in which the semiconducting channel is made from carbon-based (small-molecule or polymer) organic materials; a gate electrode modulates the carrier density in that organic layer to control current between source and drain. They are often solution-processable and mechanically flexible, with charge transport governed by hopping or band-like motion depending on molecular order, and are used for flexible electronics, displays and sensors."},{"id":"798ca642-d768-4735-88dc-0e0b803676e4","l":"Orthoexcitons","d":2,"p":"29e56ec8-6039-4a4b-9832-1f48fa943ea1","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Orthoexcitons are excitons in which the electron and hole spins combine to a triplet state (total spin S = 1), so the pair is threefold-degenerate. Due to electron\u2013hole exchange splitting they have a different energy and radiative behavior (often optically allowed) compared with the singlet \u201cparaexciton\u201d state."},{"id":"0f323354-f6c6-4599-b1a5-fe21d3d6b0ca","l":"Oscillator strength","d":2,"p":"e4236efc-fc7b-4d50-bc84-76ddf5e65856","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Oscillator strength is a dimensionless measure of how strongly a quantum transition (typically electronic) couples to electromagnetic radiation, proportional to the square of the transition dipole matrix element and to the integrated absorption cross-section. It quantifies the probability or strength of absorption or emission for that transition, with the sum over all transitions satisfying the Thomas\u2013Reiche\u2013Kuhn sum rule."},{"id":"0f0d4ce2-a2c1-49d1-98fc-bd0ba2f0836a","l":"Osmosis","d":2,"p":"c2ea9e2c-0e15-49ee-b336-111976b0b965","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Osmosis is the net flow of solvent through a semipermeable membrane from a region of lower solute concentration (higher solvent chemical potential) to a region of higher solute concentration, driven by the chemical potential difference. At equilibrium the flow is stopped by an opposing hydrostatic pressure difference, the osmotic pressure."},{"id":"9c72c141-cbd4-46eb-92a1-4f027bd5fa0a","l":"Paraelectrics","d":2,"p":"74721827-c4e3-4da0-90ca-dba52158fc6b","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Paraelectrics are insulating materials that do not have a spontaneous electric polarization in the absence of an external field but become polarized when an external electric field is applied. They are typically the high\u2011temperature, nonpolar phase of ferroelectrics, showing a linear, reversible dielectric response (often following the Curie\u2013Weiss law) and no hysteresis."},{"id":"e0ffa695-2cff-4ca2-a55d-04f70580e102","l":"Paraexcitons","d":2,"p":"29e56ec8-6039-4a4b-9832-1f48fa943ea1","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Paraexcitons are excitons in which the bound electron and hole form a spin-singlet (total spin zero) state, typically lying lower in energy than the spin-triplet (orthoexciton) due to exchange splitting. Because radiative recombination is often spin-forbidden for this singlet configuration, paraexcitons are frequently optically inactive (dark) and thus long-lived, making them important for exciton dynamics and Bose\u2013Einstein condensation studies."},{"id":"487cecd1-f37f-41b2-bf5d-09926f6b9e04","l":"Parafermions","d":2,"p":"4fcc035e-7eb4-46f6-b347-5cc975e5ed95","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Parafermions are exotic quasiparticles that generalize Majorana zero modes to Z_n symmetry: they obey generalized (parafermionic) commutation relations and exhibit fractionalized, non\u2011Abelian exchange statistics. They arise in strongly interacting low\u2011dimensional systems (e.g., fractional quantum Hall edges proximitized by superconductors or Z_n clock/parafermion chains) and can produce topologically protected ground\u2011state degeneracy useful for quantum information."},{"id":"d2595eab-0fc4-4761-b2fb-9275c5ab6d2f","l":"Paramagnetism","d":2,"p":"b6e4cc90-18f3-4427-841d-c667375bbca0","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Paramagnetism is the behavior of materials that contain unpaired atomic or ionic magnetic moments which tend to align with an applied magnetic field, producing a small positive magnetization but no spontaneous magnetization when the field is removed. Thermal agitation randomizes the moments, so the (usually small) magnetic susceptibility is positive and for noninteracting moments typically follows Curie's law, \u03c7 \u221d 1/T."},{"id":"f3be8b9d-7566-46f4-b4a4-8d7cf394dc37","l":"Parametric resonance","d":2,"p":"bd03ea46-2406-4a24-8307-05b2b498fb4e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Parametric resonance is the instability and rapid growth of an oscillator's amplitude that occurs when a system parameter (for example, a spring constant or pendulum length) is varied periodically in time at a frequency related to the oscillator's natural frequency\u2014most commonly about twice that frequency. It arises because the time-dependent parameter pumps energy directly into the mode, producing exponential amplitude increase in the absence of sufficient damping (mathematically seen as instability bands of equations with periodic coefficients, e.g., the Mathieu equation)."},{"id":"ee4fde11-6055-48ef-958c-3cc63fc9b6f8","l":"Particle plasma measurements","d":2,"p":"a5866236-c99c-4828-b499-4f50cccf7a2b","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Particle plasma measurements are diagnostic techniques used to determine the properties of the particles in a plasma\u2014electrons, ions, and neutrals\u2014such as species composition, number density, temperature, velocity/energy distributions, and charge states. They employ in-situ probes (e.g., Langmuir probes, Faraday cups, electrostatic analyzers), remote optical methods (spectroscopy, Thomson scattering, laser-induced fluorescence), and mass/charge-resolved detectors to reconstruct distribution functions and flows."},{"id":"edc1099a-36c7-42d9-9364-842b964617bb","l":"Parton distribution functions","d":2,"p":"74f884e0-6764-4962-a081-b198cca267c3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Parton distribution functions (PDFs) f_i(x,Q^2) are probability densities that give the likelihood to find a parton (quark, antiquark, or gluon) of type i carrying a fraction x of a fast-moving hadron's longitudinal momentum when probed at a resolution (hard scale) Q^2. They are nonperturbative, universal inputs to QCD factorization theorems and evolve with Q^2 according to perturbative DGLAP evolution equations."},{"id":"4aa3944f-a8af-4118-bf0a-5d094d91801e","l":"Patch clamping","d":2,"p":"427070f0-a28d-4ad3-a3ae-961d1c1dfffb","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Patch clamping is an electrophysiological technique that uses a glass micropipette sealed to a small patch of cell membrane to measure and control ionic currents with single\u2011channel or whole\u2011cell resolution. By forming a high\u2011resistance \"gigaseal\" and operating in modes such as voltage\u2011clamp or current\u2011clamp (and configurations like cell\u2011attached, whole\u2011cell, inside\u2011out, outside\u2011out), it enables detailed studies of ion channel kinetics, conductance, and pharmacology."},{"id":"7f1887e2-e66f-453e-bb84-c1e1ebd6101f","l":"Path-integral Monte Carlo","d":2,"p":"eb9bd2e1-eedd-4bd0-997d-58b44ffa3ebb","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics","Networks","Particles & Fields","Physics of Living Systems","Plasma Physics","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Path-integral Monte Carlo is a numerical method that computes quantum equilibrium properties by combining Feynman's path-integral (imaginary-time) representation of the thermal density matrix with stochastic Monte Carlo sampling of particle worldlines weighted by the Euclidean action. It effectively maps quantum particles onto classical ring polymers to capture quantum and thermal fluctuations and is widely used for bosons and distinguishable particles, though it is hampered for fermions by the sign problem."},{"id":"d6072ccd-5d49-4a8c-a032-efa240964575","l":"Pathogenic infections","d":2,"p":"bbcb2483-b22c-4bb6-bb81-beb53ff1b1ed","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Pathogenic infections occur when disease-causing organisms (bacteria, viruses, fungi, protozoa, or prions) invade, replicate in, and disrupt the normal function of a host organism. They produce symptoms and immune responses and are characterized by host\u2013pathogen interactions, growth dynamics, and transmission processes (e.g., contact, droplets, vectors, or contaminated media)."},{"id":"37df7597-0065-44e9-a472-be834180e67c","l":"Peltier effect","d":2,"p":"6e14e903-9322-41db-b946-0b518ecafdf7","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Peltier effect is the reversible heating or cooling that occurs at the junction of two dissimilar conductors or semiconductors when an electric current passes, with heat absorbed or released proportional to the current and the difference in the materials' Peltier coefficients. It underlies thermoelectric cooling/heating devices and is distinct from irreversible Joule heating."},{"id":"af069b35-0aac-45bc-b4f0-8020d8d5d61c","l":"Penetration depth","d":2,"p":"78017460-1d24-4041-bc27-e547620d05b7","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Penetration depth is the characteristic distance over which a field, wave amplitude, or particle flux falls to 1/e of its value at a surface due to absorption, scattering, or screening; quantitatively it is the inverse of the attenuation coefficient.  Specific contexts use special names (e.g., skin depth for EM waves in conductors, London penetration depth for magnetic fields in superconductors)."},{"id":"81fe795c-d39b-4036-8401-e8ddd7b2d781","l":"Penning traps","d":2,"p":"0b6b113c-8f3d-421e-a149-940af26931cf","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"A Penning trap is a device that confines charged particles in three dimensions by combining a strong, uniform magnetic field (which forces cyclotron motion for radial confinement) with a static electric quadrupole potential (which provides axial restoring forces). Penning traps are widely used for precision mass and frequency measurements, ion storage, antimatter studies, and quantum information experiments."},{"id":"fcb9ee4f-ec32-448f-aaf3-a5936996807b","l":"Peptides","d":2,"p":"6c0e8bb5-a5a0-4f28-8d4a-4e7ace85a023","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Peptides are polymers of two or more amino acids linked by peptide (amide) bonds, formed by a condensation reaction between an amino group's nitrogen and a carboxyl group's carbon. Typically shorter than proteins (often fewer than ~50 residues), their amino-acid sequence determines their three-dimensional conformation and biological function."},{"id":"da5a7af0-f0c0-43b9-915b-931cd597806e","l":"Periodic-orbit theory","d":2,"p":"c5b596fc-84f5-4464-b9ae-bc11ff6d2b0a","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Periodic-orbit theory is a semiclassical framework that relates quantum spectral and wave properties to sums over the classical system's periodic trajectories, with each periodic orbit contributing an oscillatory term to quantities like the density of states. It underlies methods such as the Gutzwiller trace formula and cycle expansions of dynamical zeta functions, and is widely used to quantize classically chaotic systems and compute dynamical averages."},{"id":"0af13c28-1aac-43f3-8ccb-d8073044fb6d","l":"Permalloy","d":2,"p":"96973abf-f1b5-44c5-9549-9f0f4dfe234f","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Permalloy is a soft ferromagnetic nickel\u2013iron alloy (typically about 80% Ni and 20% Fe) characterized by very high magnetic permeability, low coercivity and near-zero magnetostriction, making it ideal for magnetic shielding, transformer cores, sensors and recording heads."},{"id":"f28f4f1e-8eef-4fce-8661-07f5751ad90a","l":"Perovskites","d":2,"p":"6af5be88-489f-4a50-9abf-998e8912254a","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Perovskites are materials with the ABX3 (commonly ABO3) crystal structure in which B-site cations sit at the centers of corner-sharing BX6 octahedra and larger A-site cations occupy the interstitial cavities of the lattice (ideal form is cubic). Because this framework tolerates extensive chemical substitution and structural distortion, perovskites exhibit a wide range of electronic, magnetic, optical, ionic, and ferroelectric behaviors and underpin technologies from superconductors and magnetoresistors to solar cells and LEDs."},{"id":"1d281972-785b-42bb-944c-e35a91320b05","l":"Perturbative QCD","d":2,"p":"74f884e0-6764-4962-a081-b198cca267c3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Perturbative QCD is the use of perturbation theory in quantum chromodynamics, exploiting asymptotic freedom to expand scattering amplitudes and other observables in powers of the strong coupling at high momentum transfer where the coupling is small. It underlies precision calculations of hard processes, parton evolution and jet formation via factorization and renormalization-group methods, but fails at low energies where the coupling becomes large and nonperturbative confinement effects dominate."},{"id":"dc6fd646-f7b9-4085-bb05-9e9ef4ca4116","l":"Perturbed-angular correlation spectroscopy","d":2,"p":"d199d86e-97af-4dd1-9fe7-fdb00b298b96","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Perturbed-angular correlation (PAC) spectroscopy is a hyperfine technique that measures time-dependent changes in the angular correlation between successive gamma rays emitted by radioactive probe nuclei, where local electric-field gradients or magnetic fields at the probe site perturb the correlation. Analysis of the perturbation gives site-specific information on local symmetry, electric-field gradients, magnetic fields, and dynamic processes (e.g., diffusion or relaxation) in solids and materials."},{"id":"f8a66602-83f1-4a9b-b61d-fe385d7fb428","l":"Phase-change materials","d":2,"p":"050430d9-e7b1-4e3f-bbb3-975081181c3c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Phase-change materials are substances that can reversibly switch between distinct structural phases (most commonly amorphous and crystalline) when driven by heat, light, or electrical pulses. These transitions involve latent heat and produce large, repeatable changes in optical, electrical, and thermal properties, enabling applications in thermal energy storage and nonvolatile memory."},{"id":"36932cf6-44f8-4508-8a6a-20a0e46f6c8f","l":"Phase oscillator models","d":2,"p":"7b9db112-8475-42b4-ad22-f00f6b8635f8","fl":["Physical Systems","Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Phase oscillator models represent each self-sustained oscillator by a single angular (phase) variable on the circle, describing only its timing while neglecting amplitude dynamics, with interactions given by coupling functions of phase differences. They arise from phase-reduction of limit-cycle oscillators (e.g., the Kuramoto model) and are used to analyze synchronization, entrainment, and collective rhythms."},{"id":"472e1c82-acd7-4941-bfdd-13e4b53f5a96","l":"Phase shift","d":2,"p":"d72773f6-a8d3-4130-a6cd-b070d4487dcd","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Phase shift is the change in the phase angle of a periodic oscillation or wave relative to a chosen reference, corresponding to a time or spatial displacement of the waveform and typically measured in radians or degrees. It arises from propagation delays, reflections, interactions or system response and determines how peaks and troughs of one signal align with another."},{"id":"1cf824c7-0d79-44df-9d04-099e1d771679","l":"Phase space dynamics","d":2,"p":"695da376-c44a-41dd-b968-d200c31e9d5e","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems","Plasma Physics"],"def":"Phase space dynamics is the description of how a system's state\u2014represented as a point in phase space (the space of all coordinates and conjugate momenta or other state variables)\u2014evolves in time under its equations of motion. It analyzes the resulting flow, trajectories and invariant structures (fixed points, limit cycles, attractors) and their stability, including features like conserved phase-volume in Hamiltonian systems (Liouville's theorem) or chaos in dissipative systems."},{"id":"daa209c8-4197-48fd-8086-8a6d169f3228","l":"Phase transitions in cellular systems","d":2,"p":"5fddad64-3302-472f-9945-d256974cb278","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Phase transitions in cellular systems are abrupt or continuous changes in the collective mechanical or dynamical state of cell assemblies\u2014for example from fluid-like to solid-like behavior, from disordered to coherent migration, or from unjammed to jammed\u2014characterized by changes in suitable order parameters. These transitions are driven by control variables such as cell density, adhesion, contractility, motility, and signaling, and are analyzed using concepts from statistical physics and active-matter theory because living cells are inherently driven, nonequilibrium units."},{"id":"b4ca610b-81c4-4917-9c92-66d0763e675d","l":"Phase transitions in clusters","d":2,"p":"cac7f673-4cd1-47b8-9de3-9f9785aea585","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Phase transitions in clusters are the finite-size analogs of bulk phase changes occurring in small aggregates of atoms or molecules, where the system undergoes structural or energetic reorganization (e.g., melting, evaporation, or solid\u2013solid isomerization) as temperature, energy, or other parameters vary. Because clusters are finite, these transitions are broadened and strongly size-dependent, showing rounded heat-capacity peaks, phase coexistence, and microcanonical anomalies such as negative heat capacity and shifted transition points."},{"id":"532cc5e3-72e1-4e3b-a0f2-a35d100ad20e","l":"Phase transitions in protein & nucleic acid assemblies","d":2,"p":"5fddad64-3302-472f-9945-d256974cb278","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Phase transitions in protein and nucleic acid assemblies are cooperative changes in macromolecular organization\u2014driven by concentration, temperature, ionic conditions and multivalent sequence-dependent interactions\u2014that convert a homogeneous solution into condensed states such as liquid-like droplets, viscoelastic gels, or solid aggregates/crystals. These transitions set the material properties, transport dynamics, and biological functions of biomolecular condensates and can show thermodynamic and kinetic phenomena (criticality, nucleation, gelation, aging)."},{"id":"444ca8a2-51fd-4293-8d18-1c86a274637d","l":"Phenomics","d":2,"p":"6b5443fa-9849-4d5b-966b-7d6e08113e36","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Phenomics is the high\u2011throughput, quantitative study of phenotypes\u2014an organism\u2019s observable physical, biochemical, and behavioral traits\u2014aimed at mapping how genotype, environment, and their interactions produce form and function. It uses automated imaging and sensing, statistical and computational modeling, and other quantitative tools (often borrowed from physics) to measure, analyze, and predict trait variation."},{"id":"2b86a860-1b6a-4613-979b-03d3ae84f0d6","l":"Phononic crystals","d":2,"p":"2a5345ea-2bd1-45f5-818c-bf056b4f6500","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Fluid Dynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Phononic crystals are engineered materials with a periodic arrangement of elastic properties or mass density that produces a band structure for mechanical (acoustic or elastic) waves, including frequency ranges (band gaps) where wave propagation is forbidden. They enable control of sound, vibration, and phonon transport for applications such as waveguides, filters, and thermal management."},{"id":"c791cc5e-a27b-4b07-9700-271653a27b6a","l":"Phonon polariton","d":2,"p":"69adcacb-6270-4cfc-8cf5-60097ec74d8b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A phonon polariton is a hybrid quasiparticle formed by the strong coupling of infrared electromagnetic waves (photons) with optical phonons in a polar crystal, producing collective modes that carry both photon and lattice-vibration character. These modes exist in the material's Reststrahlen band (between the transverse and longitudinal optical phonon frequencies) and exhibit strong dispersion and subwavelength electromagnetic confinement."},{"id":"92df9837-8bf4-4d0c-b033-db6e21b908b0","l":"Photoabsorption","d":2,"p":"90223563-a9ed-483d-ad68-68ab7c3b3161","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Photoabsorption is the quantum process in which a photon is absorbed by an atom, molecule, solid, or nucleus, transferring its energy to promote the system to an excited state or to cause ionization, dissociation, or other electronic/structural changes. The likelihood of absorption at a given photon energy is characterized by the photoabsorption cross section (or the macroscopic absorption coefficient)."},{"id":"a6e09dc2-49f1-4144-ad7e-89b74b623afb","l":"Photoacoustics","d":2,"p":"53354177-c126-47af-94c7-a4cf5764b664","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics"],"def":"Photoacoustics is the study and application of acoustic waves generated when pulsed or modulated electromagnetic radiation is absorbed by a material, producing rapid local heating and thermoelastic expansion that launches pressure waves. It is the basis for sensing and imaging techniques (e.g., photoacoustic spectroscopy and tomography) that infer optical absorption and spatial structure from the detected acoustic signals."},{"id":"0a5e3a73-ce1c-4388-a12d-9dd67740f019","l":"Photocatalysis","d":2,"p":"03d36887-85f0-4045-a51f-93c21e08df23","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics","Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Photocatalysis is a light-driven catalytic process in which a material (typically a semiconductor) absorbs photons to produce excited electron\u2013hole pairs that initiate and drive chemical reactions at its surface. The photo-generated charges mediate redox transformations (e.g., pollutant decomposition, water splitting) while the catalyst itself remains unchanged, enabling light to lower activation barriers and steer reaction pathways."},{"id":"fb5959a2-85d6-4eb9-b0fe-132e7237fd57","l":"Photocurrent spectroscopy","d":2,"p":"d8d9518f-91ee-40ab-9529-2c3f98431a0f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics"],"def":"Photocurrent spectroscopy is an optical characterization technique in which a sample is illuminated with tunable-wavelength light and the resulting photocurrent is measured as a function of photon energy. The spectral dependence of the photocurrent reveals electronic and optoelectronic properties such as bandgaps, optical transitions (including excitons), defect states, built-in fields, and carrier-collection efficiency."},{"id":"d46a1247-2115-4e6c-a262-38c57fcbd391","l":"Photodetectors","d":2,"p":"dcf48409-af3f-4517-b58d-7ec6c95351f3","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Photodetectors are devices that sense incident light and convert photons into an electrical signal (current, voltage or digital counts). They operate via mechanisms such as the photoelectric/photo\u2011voltaic effect, internal photoemission, photoconductivity or thermal absorption, and are characterized by parameters like quantum efficiency, responsivity, noise and bandwidth."},{"id":"af632568-a953-4829-ac7f-5e3f3dad68ba","l":"Photoelectron diffraction","d":2,"p":"90223563-a9ed-483d-ad68-68ab7c3b3161","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Photoelectron diffraction is the interference of electron waves produced by photoemission and subsequently scattered by neighboring atoms, which produces angular and energy-dependent intensity patterns. These diffraction patterns (observed in X-ray/angle-resolved photoelectron spectroscopy) encode local atomic structure and are used to determine surface and short-range order."},{"id":"17c93c15-6e47-417e-b15a-d2d5b7f874c6","l":"Photoelectron emission microscopy","d":2,"p":"90223563-a9ed-483d-ad68-68ab7c3b3161","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Photoelectron emission microscopy (PEEM) is a surface-sensitive imaging technique that forms a magnified real-space image by illuminating a sample with UV or X-ray photons and collecting the emitted photoelectrons with electron optics. Image contrast comes from local variations in work function, composition, electronic states or electric/magnetic fields, giving sub-micron to nanometer-scale maps of surface chemistry and dynamics."},{"id":"40082853-5a77-4bce-bb7b-a6b598d04d93","l":"Photoemission spectroscopy","d":2,"p":"90223563-a9ed-483d-ad68-68ab7c3b3161","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Photoemission spectroscopy is an experimental technique that uses photons to eject electrons from a material via the photoelectric effect and measures the emitted electrons' kinetic energies (and often angles). From those spectra one can determine electronic structure, band dispersions, binding energies, chemical states, and surface-sensitive information about the sample."},{"id":"63113db0-b6bd-4288-8702-8e42a023cce8","l":"Photoexcitation","d":2,"p":"a38dc017-3236-4084-afc5-eb59c5de51e0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Photoexcitation is the process in which a photon is absorbed by an atom, molecule, or solid and promotes an electron from a lower to a higher electronic energy level without ejecting it from the system. It produces excited electronic states (or bound electron\u2013hole pairs called excitons) that subsequently relax by radiative or nonradiative processes and can lead to charge transfer or chemical reactions."},{"id":"c412179d-aaae-467a-9c07-86fac565d769","l":"Photogalvanic effect","d":2,"p":"57934ca6-f76c-4b88-9513-12b414e4e105","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Photogalvanic effect: the generation of a steady (dc) electric current or voltage in a homogeneous material under uniform illumination, arising from asymmetric photoexcitation or scattering in a medium lacking inversion symmetry and occurring without an applied bias or built-in junction. It is a second-order (quadratic) nonlinear optical response and includes polarization-sensitive forms such as the circular and linear photogalvanic effects."},{"id":"9098e351-0e96-4435-b04b-a0fa64abbf64","l":"Photoionization","d":2,"p":"a38dc017-3236-4084-afc5-eb59c5de51e0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Photoionization is the process in which an atom, molecule, or ion absorbs a photon with energy greater than an electron\u2019s binding energy, causing that electron to be ejected and leaving a more highly charged ion. Its likelihood depends on photon energy and target structure and is quantified by the photoionization cross section, often showing resonant features from intermediate bound states."},{"id":"6bdea32e-2fdf-4421-b982-41a2a011a363","l":"Photoluminescence","d":2,"p":"a38dc017-3236-4084-afc5-eb59c5de51e0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Photoluminescence is the emission of light from a material that occurs after it absorbs photons, produced when excited electronic (or excitonic) states relax radiatively to lower-energy states; it includes fast fluorescence and longer-lived phosphorescence."},{"id":"15e95a27-eaeb-48f8-bca8-15afcaa74224","l":"Photon & charged-lepton interactions with hadrons","d":2,"p":"83c64883-5507-43f8-92bc-ae9f5ae03f02","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Photon and charged-lepton interactions with hadrons are scattering processes in which real or virtual photons\u2014or charged leptons such as electrons or muons used as probes\u2014exchange energy and momentum with hadronic targets, revealing the targets' internal charge, current and partonic structure. Depending on kinematics they are elastic, inelastic or deep-inelastic and are described experimentally by form factors and structure functions and theoretically by QED electroweak currents combined with QCD dynamics."},{"id":"6bdec497-a29b-4c9d-b1ce-9eb63250cc50","l":"Photon counting","d":2,"p":"a38dc017-3236-4084-afc5-eb59c5de51e0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Photon counting is a detection technique that measures light by recording individual photon arrival events, giving the number and timing of photons in a signal. It permits measurements at the single-photon (quantum) level\u2014revealing photon statistics and enabling low-light imaging and quantum-optical experiments\u2014using detectors like photomultiplier tubes, single-photon avalanche diodes, or superconducting nanowire detectors."},{"id":"67eeeb44-b5fa-4af0-b186-157a81da451f","l":"Photonic crystal lasers","d":2,"p":"cd4c012e-bacf-4bcf-8358-a6573a330ec4","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Photonic crystal lasers are lasers whose resonator and feedback are provided by a periodic, wavelength-scale modulation of the refractive index (a photonic crystal) that creates bandgaps and localized defect or band-edge modes. By confining and controlling light with the crystal\u2019s modes, they produce high\u2011Q, low\u2011threshold, and often highly directional nanoscale lasing."},{"id":"f4d2acb3-55a5-41f0-bd7e-e87fbd811944","l":"Photonic crystal waveguide","d":2,"p":"d9fbef9a-71f6-4881-9f69-8d81c5749cff","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A photonic crystal waveguide is an optical waveguide formed by introducing a linear defect into a photonic crystal\u2014a periodic dielectric structure\u2014so that guided electromagnetic modes are confined along the defect and trapped transversely by the crystal's photonic bandgap (or index contrast). These waveguides provide wavelength-scale confinement and engineered dispersion, enabling precise control of light propagation and slow-light effects."},{"id":"6b2cc514-2c2d-42de-9ee0-04b80aeeb50b","l":"Photon pairs & parametric down-conversion","d":2,"p":"632f74d1-d16b-4b92-a295-b24729a93d8d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Parametric down-conversion is a second-order nonlinear optical process in which a pump photon inside a nonlinear crystal is converted into two lower-energy photons (signal and idler) while conserving energy and momentum (phase matching). In its spontaneous form this process produces correlated or entangled photon pairs that are widely used as sources in quantum optics and quantum information experiments."},{"id":"2a49cb39-937f-42fa-8a4e-94b2d5adc5dd","l":"Photon production","d":2,"p":"97069ede-42dd-4f4d-b552-9fc8562cb02d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Photon production is the generation of real photons when energy is converted into electromagnetic radiation by processes such as atomic electronic transitions, thermal emission, bremsstrahlung from accelerated charges, particle decays or annihilation, and stimulated emission. In high-energy physics it more specifically denotes reactions or scattering events that yield one or more photons in the final state (e.g., pp \u2192 \u03b3 + X), used to probe electroweak and QCD dynamics."},{"id":"0082dae7-d213-4ca7-b629-6f6681b2f175","l":"Photon statistics","d":2,"p":"632f74d1-d16b-4b92-a295-b24729a93d8d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Photon statistics is the probability distribution and set of correlation functions that describe the fluctuations in the number of photons detected from a light field. It is quantified by moments (mean, variance) and correlation functions such as g^(2)(\u03c4), which distinguish classical (Poissonian or super-Poissonian) from nonclassical (sub\u2011Poissonian, antibunched) light."},{"id":"878297d0-7ed9-4aee-8439-56bcf340edf6","l":"Photorefractive & Kerr effects","d":2,"p":"bd03ea46-2406-4a24-8307-05b2b498fb4e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Photorefractive effect: In certain crystals and glasses, illumination generates mobile charge carriers that redistribute to form a space\u2011charge field; that internal field modifies the local refractive index via the electro\u2011optic effect, producing light\u2011induced, often slow, refractive\u2011index gratings used in holography and beam coupling.\n\nKerr effect: An instantaneous third\u2011order nonlinear optical response in which the refractive index changes proportional to the square of the electric field (equivalently to the light intensity, n = n0 + n2I), causing phenomena such as self\u2011phase modulation and self\u2011focusing."},{"id":"eda9bd18-e9e3-4ce4-a8ce-2b2c3c0b301f","l":"Photosphere","d":2,"p":"f712db32-2bc9-4e97-9f42-a16c12832ff8","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"The photosphere is the thin, outer visible layer of a star from which most of the star's visible light escapes \u2014 it is defined approximately by the region where the optical depth \u03c4 \u2248 1 so photons can freely escape. For the Sun this gaseous \"surface\" is a few hundred kilometers thick and has a temperature of about 5800 K."},{"id":"4dccb392-8ef9-4f3a-83a4-7018fb2ca102","l":"Photovoltaic effect","d":2,"p":"57934ca6-f76c-4b88-9513-12b414e4e105","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The photovoltaic effect is the generation of an electric voltage or current in a material when it absorbs light: incident photons create electron\u2013hole pairs that are separated and collected by an internal electric field (for example at a p\u2013n junction or interface), converting photon energy directly into electrical energy."},{"id":"1816597c-51d0-46b4-b414-87f113a21181","l":"Phylogenetics reconstruction","d":2,"p":"346899f4-4f01-4d98-87a3-1668696a3d48","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Phylogenetic reconstruction is the computational inference of the evolutionary relationships among organisms, genes, or sequences, typically represented as a branching tree or network that hypothesizes common ancestry and divergence. It uses molecular or morphological data together with statistical and algorithmic methods (e.g., distance methods, parsimony, maximum likelihood, Bayesian inference) and explicit models of evolution to estimate topology, branch lengths, and sometimes divergence times."},{"id":"4452dd6b-0cb8-46ac-b4d7-aac7b315a181","l":"Physisorption","d":2,"p":"229eca6d-30f1-4c05-9a78-c352de8326b1","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Physisorption is the adsorption of atoms or molecules on a surface via weak, nonchemical interactions (mainly van der Waals/dispersion forces) without significant charge transfer or covalent bond formation. It is typically reversible with low adsorption enthalpy (\u223c10\u2013100 meV per adsorbate) and can form multilayers, in contrast to stronger, often irreversible chemisorption."},{"id":"fc4acbc9-f553-4042-b796-c105467649e1","l":"Piezoelectric devices","d":2,"p":"3bab53f4-af17-4278-95a1-fa1b42b9231c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Piezoelectric devices are components that exploit the piezoelectric effect\u2014certain crystals, ceramics, or polymers generate electric charge when mechanically strained and conversely deform when an electric field is applied. They convert between mechanical and electrical energy and are used as sensors, actuators, transducers, frequency-control elements, and energy harvesters."},{"id":"5917e11b-ac3c-49f2-8cec-20ef36800b02","l":"Piezoelectrics","d":2,"p":"74721827-c4e3-4da0-90ca-dba52158fc6b","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Piezoelectrics are materials that convert mechanical stress to electric polarization (the direct piezoelectric effect) and conversely produce mechanical strain when an electric field is applied (the converse effect); this linear electromechanical coupling occurs only in non\u2011centrosymmetric structures."},{"id":"13bb7469-f4cb-4cbc-8da2-a11b45c294ba","l":"Planetary atmosphere","d":2,"p":"ea69605a-7cae-476a-a7da-28f3c186d709","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"The planetary atmosphere is the layer of gases gravitationally bound to a planet, extending from the surface (or cloud tops) outward and characterized by altitude-dependent pressure, temperature, and composition. Its structure and evolution are governed by radiative heating and cooling, chemistry and fluid dynamics, and exchanges of mass and energy with the surface, magnetosphere, and stellar wind."},{"id":"6040c824-b403-479c-a36b-237e0858d1d7","l":"Planetary bow shock","d":2,"p":"ea69605a-7cae-476a-a7da-28f3c186d709","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"A planetary bow shock is a standing, curved shock wave that forms upstream of a planet where the supersonic solar (or stellar) wind meets the obstacle of the planet's magnetosphere or ionosphere and is abruptly decelerated, compressed, heated and deflected into a subsonic flow. It is a collisionless plasma shock whose shape and location depend on the solar-wind conditions and whether the planet is magnetized or has an induced magnetic obstacle."},{"id":"7e8a1736-3b2b-48b4-b85b-f8c8119a7ff7","l":"Planetary magnetosphere","d":2,"p":"ea69605a-7cae-476a-a7da-28f3c186d709","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"A planetary magnetosphere is the region around a planet where the planet\u2019s magnetic field\u2014intrinsic or induced\u2014dominates the motion of charged particles and creates a magnetic cavity that deflects the solar wind. Its size and shape are set by the interaction between that field and the solar wind and include features such as the bow shock, magnetopause, magnetotail, and radiation belts."},{"id":"dd077f89-993b-4e5c-8835-8fb65e40a1a2","l":"Plane-wave expansion","d":2,"p":"82f6c5fd-bbef-4ff9-a5f9-de62d3f5dfeb","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A plane-wave expansion represents a spatial function (e.g., a wavefunction, field, or potential) as a linear superposition \u2014 sum or integral \u2014 of plane-wave basis functions e^{i k\u00b7r}, with amplitudes given by its Fourier coefficients. It is widely used to solve linear PDEs, exploit translational symmetry (periodic solids, scattering), and represent momentum eigenstates in quantum mechanics and electromagnetism."},{"id":"75160755-0e10-4f67-a059-da3125244331","l":"Plant development","d":2,"p":"93b8baac-44de-4a26-a175-357ad9d8744c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Plant development is the time evolution of a plant\u2019s form and function produced by patterns of cell division, expansion and differentiation under genetic programs and environmental cues. It arises from coupled biochemical signaling, transport and mechanical forces that coordinate structure and morphogenesis across molecular to whole-plant scales."},{"id":"fc9aab40-7bcd-49b8-a415-8903c07897f0","l":"Plant ecology","d":2,"p":"0f53e9c0-21db-4e21-9bf2-a3dcab5a14fc","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Plant ecology is the branch of ecology that studies how plants interact with their physical environment and with other organisms, determining their distribution, abundance, growth, and survival. It examines processes such as resource uptake and competition, reproduction and succession, and the roles of plants in energy flow and nutrient cycling across population, community, and ecosystem scales."},{"id":"3f71ccf5-16ff-425f-b7b3-4791f9503b59","l":"Plasma macroinstabilities","d":2,"p":"9bbcc3c5-16bb-4a7f-adea-c23acc893cde","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Plasma macroinstabilities are large-scale collective instabilities whose wavelengths are comparable to the size of the plasma system, driven by gradients or stored free energy in pressure, current, flow, or magnetic-field geometry. They grow (linearly and then nonlinearly) to reorganize global fields and flows\u2014examples include kink, tearing, interchange/ballooning and sawtooth modes\u2014and can cause rapid energy release, enhanced transport, or catastrophic disruptions in laboratory and astrophysical plasmas."},{"id":"bdf4c29f-ad02-43b5-b39b-47d3de04aaa9","l":"Plasma medicine","d":2,"p":"a24a2d8a-58a4-4cbc-81aa-4cd953e73f4b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Plasma Physics"],"def":"Plasma medicine is the interdisciplinary field that applies cold, non\u2011thermal ionized gases (cold atmospheric plasmas) to biological tissues and cells for therapeutic and diagnostic purposes. Therapeutic effects arise from a mixture of reactive oxygen and nitrogen species, charged particles, electric fields, UV photons and small thermal effects, enabling applications like sterilization, wound healing, and selective cancer-cell inactivation."},{"id":"b094f9fa-ebc7-4e4d-8957-0a7303f6e91e","l":"Plasma microinstabilities","d":2,"p":"9bbcc3c5-16bb-4a7f-adea-c23acc893cde","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Plasma microinstabilities are rapidly growing collective instabilities that occur at kinetic length and time scales in a plasma (comparable to the particle Larmor radius, Debye length, or cyclotron/plasma frequency). Driven by free-energy sources such as temperature or velocity gradients, pressure anisotropies, or currents, they excite waves and turbulence that enhance transport, heating, and wave\u2013particle interactions beyond collisional expectations."},{"id":"4909099f-e479-4384-9843-f0d4566d1d7a","l":"Plasma sheet","d":2,"p":"993587b9-4472-41fd-9896-2df938e75b92","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"The plasma sheet is the central layer of a planet's magnetotail containing relatively hot, tenuous plasma and a strong cross\u2011tail current that separates the tail lobes. It acts as a reservoir and transport region for particles that supply the inner magnetosphere and power auroral activity."},{"id":"1e55a06f-33e7-43e5-8903-9839ce7a28c9","l":"Plasma spectroscopy","d":2,"p":"a5866236-c99c-4828-b499-4f50cccf7a2b","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Plasma spectroscopy is the study of the electromagnetic radiation that plasmas emit, absorb, or scatter, using the resulting spectral lines, continua and polarization to diagnose plasma properties such as temperature, density, composition, ionization state, velocities, and electric or magnetic fields."},{"id":"bc34c509-781e-4a9b-afbc-76453a3b8671","l":"Plasmasphere","d":2,"p":"993587b9-4472-41fd-9896-2df938e75b92","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"The plasmasphere is the torus-shaped region of relatively cold, dense plasma in Earth\u2019s inner magnetosphere, supplied by the ionosphere and largely co-rotating with the planet. It is bounded by the plasmapause, a sharp outer density gradient whose location shifts with geomagnetic activity."},{"id":"cdde4175-aa1b-46ab-be82-f661d9dc10de","l":"Plastic deformation","d":2,"p":"77d491f8-55ad-47a6-a0a7-cd24ffce7602","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Plastic deformation is the permanent, nonrecoverable change in shape or size of a material that occurs when applied stress exceeds its elastic limit (yield strength). In crystalline solids it results mainly from irreversible processes like dislocation motion, slip, and twinning, while in amorphous materials it occurs via shear transformations or viscous flow."},{"id":"f03f5fbb-39e8-470e-b07f-1b1da663212f","l":"Point defects","d":2,"p":"3331008f-47b2-4908-92ad-bc9bd7cd815e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Point defects are localized disruptions of a crystal lattice confined to one or a few atomic sites\u2014examples include vacancies, interstitials, and substitutional impurities (and paired types like Frenkel and Schottky defects). These defects strongly influence a material\u2019s electrical, optical, mechanical, and diffusion properties."},{"id":"b950c2f0-8a43-4a12-b6fa-f47f36a9d7ca","l":"Poisson ratio","d":2,"p":"e085ebce-3a96-4bf8-ad67-054c2a4f7e73","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Poisson ratio \u03bd is the negative of the ratio of lateral (transverse) strain to axial (longitudinal) strain produced by uniaxial stress: \u03bd = \u2212\u03b5_transverse/\u03b5_axial (the minus sign accounts for lateral contraction when a material is stretched). For isotropic elastic solids \u03bd typically ranges between \u22121 and 0.5, with most engineering materials near 0.2\u20130.35."},{"id":"e113d477-3252-4d9c-a6a8-6ff354294756","l":"Polarization in interactions & scattering","d":2,"p":"83c64883-5507-43f8-92bc-ae9f5ae03f02","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Polarization in interactions and scattering is the orientation or quantum state of a particle's internal degrees of freedom (for example the electric-field direction of a photon or the spin alignment of a fermion) that characterizes how it responds to and mediates forces. It affects and can be measured through polarization-dependent cross sections, angular asymmetries, and transfer or analyzing powers in scattering and reaction processes."},{"id":"805af495-94b0-499f-a6d6-ac1200439aa0","l":"Polarization of light","d":2,"p":"42791fef-2a1a-4002-81dd-c04d56ae9618","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Polarization of light describes the orientation and relative phase of the transverse electric-field oscillations in an electromagnetic wave as it propagates. Common types are linear (field oscillates in a fixed direction), circular (field vector rotates at constant magnitude), and elliptical (the general case)."},{"id":"5a300c5c-69de-42b3-8f4c-9f01c12b2a0f","l":"Polarization vortices","d":2,"p":"153de13f-2f91-40e7-8553-022f50949969","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Polarization vortices are topological defects in a vector polarization field where the polarization direction rotates (winds) continuously around a central core or singularity, characterized by a nonzero winding number. They appear in contexts such as optical vector-vortex beams (spatially varying light polarization) and nanoscale ferroelectric materials (closed-loop polarization domains)."},{"id":"d92d4a4a-f9c0-4b1f-8691-50285ed23537","l":"Polymer knots","d":2,"p":"8da34010-6805-4ff1-93ed-6692da601029","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Polymer knots are topological configurations in which a polymer chain forms a nontrivial knot that cannot be undone without cutting the chain. They appear in closed (circular) polymers or as localized knotted regions in open chains and strongly affect the polymer's static and dynamic properties (size, diffusion, elasticity, and folding/unfolding)."},{"id":"32cdcf0a-2165-4a69-9ca7-b706497b7bec","l":"Polymer-nanoparticle composites","d":2,"p":"910b0011-1066-4b4c-871a-357e24b7243d","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Polymer-nanoparticle composites are materials in which nanoscale particles (typically <100 nm, e.g., inorganic oxides, metals, or carbon nanomaterials) are dispersed within a polymer matrix. The particles' size, surface chemistry, and spatial distribution strongly alter the composite's mechanical, thermal, electrical, and optical properties through interfacial interactions, polymer-chain confinement, and collective/percolation effects."},{"id":"208d6035-66f5-450f-bf30-ed758e8edce1","l":"Polymer networks","d":2,"p":"2303d382-65bb-4b93-94b8-62a73502ec4b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Polymer networks are materials in which long polymer chains are joined by permanent or reversible crosslinks to form a continuous, percolating network; the network topology, crosslink density, and chain flexibility control the material's elasticity, viscoelasticity, swelling, and transport behavior. Examples include rubbers, hydrogels and elastomers widely studied in soft-matter physics and materials science."},{"id":"40dacc09-061f-457e-8f0d-c57a2dc17da1","l":"Polymer scaling theory","d":2,"p":"c267a3f3-0c34-4fc1-9825-947683caf4dd","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Polymer scaling theory is a framework that uses self-similarity and universal scaling exponents to relate macroscopic polymer properties (size, free energy, structure factor, osmotic pressure) to chain length N and external conditions, collapsing detailed chemistry into a few exponents and crossover functions. For example, the typical chain size scales as R \u221d N^\u03bd with \u03bd = 1/2 for an ideal chain and \u03bd \u2248 0.588 for a swollen chain in a good solvent, with the blob concept describing crossover regimes."},{"id":"d0ca8cf0-d4c9-4d11-911f-d72b776ec03d","l":"Ponderomotive effects","d":2,"p":"15b0e018-46e8-4acf-8e67-9d54fc70c1b1","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Ponderomotive effects are the time-averaged nonlinear forces and resulting motions experienced by charged particles in a rapidly oscillating electromagnetic field, arising because the particle's quiver motion in the field samples spatial gradients and produces a net push toward lower field intensity.  In the high-frequency limit this is described by a ponderomotive potential (U_p \u221d E^2/\u03c9^2) that expels particles from high-field regions and plays a central role in laser\u2013plasma and strong-field phenomena."},{"id":"08b53e02-8779-41b1-ae1e-beeef0ce31c9","l":"Population genetics","d":2,"p":"346899f4-4f01-4d98-87a3-1668696a3d48","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Population genetics is the quantitative study of how evolutionary forces\u2014natural selection, mutation, genetic drift, and migration\u2014change the frequencies of alleles and genotypes within and between populations over time. It uses deterministic and stochastic mathematical models (e.g., diffusion approximations, Wright\u2013Fisher and Moran models, and fitness landscapes) to predict dynamics of genetic variation and adaptation."},{"id":"fd184070-f49e-405c-9e66-42f7ee162475","l":"Positron annihilation spectroscopy","d":2,"p":"d199d86e-97af-4dd1-9fe7-fdb00b298b96","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Positron annihilation spectroscopy is a nondestructive technique in which low-energy positrons are implanted into a material and their annihilation with electrons produces 511 keV gamma rays; analysis of the annihilation lifetime, Doppler broadening, or angular correlation of those photons reveals local electron density and open-volume defects. It is widely used to detect vacancies, vacancy clusters, pore sizes, and other microstructural or electronic inhomogeneities in solids and thin films."},{"id":"3434789b-73bf-46b2-90e8-f47e54d523fc","l":"Powder metallurgy","d":2,"p":"c4ed35b0-1090-409c-9667-85f9ac03e9c0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Powder metallurgy is a set of manufacturing techniques in which metal (or alloy) powders are shaped by compaction and then consolidated by heating and/or pressure\u2014typically sintering\u2014below the melting point to form solid components. It enables near\u2011net\u2011shape production with controlled composition, microstructure and porosity."},{"id":"4f2c2ce8-fafb-4331-848c-e4b56e3f3eb7","l":"Power grid networks","d":2,"p":"96ee91b1-3ba0-47ad-b628-6a89155e08e7","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Power grid networks are interconnected systems of generators, transmission lines, transformers, substations and loads that transport electrical energy from producers to consumers, forming a complex electrical network whose currents and voltages are governed by Kirchhoff's laws and electromagnetic principles. Their behavior is described by AC power\u2011flow and dynamics (frequency/phase synchronization, stability, and cascades) subject to line impedances, thermal limits and control actions."},{"id":"42fc3426-c55d-4458-8eaf-ce6febad3f97","l":"Precipitation","d":2,"p":"c4ed35b0-1090-409c-9667-85f9ac03e9c0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Precipitation is the atmospheric process in which water vapor undergoes phase change to form liquid droplets or ice crystals that grow by condensation, collision\u2013coalescence or ice\u2011phase processes and become heavy enough to fall to the surface under gravity (e.g., rain, snow, sleet, hail)."},{"id":"64545bf5-eb34-4338-9a4b-e369a2733114","l":"Preferential attachment models","d":2,"p":"6f3f5cf1-363e-4d04-ac63-ca4ae90b47fc","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Preferential attachment models are stochastic network-growth rules in which new nodes connect to existing nodes with probability proportional to those nodes' current degree (or intrinsic attractiveness), producing \"rich\u2011get\u2011richer\" dynamics that typically yield heavy\u2011tailed, often power\u2011law, degree distributions. They are used to explain the emergence of scale\u2011free structure in social, biological, and technological networks."},{"id":"dace9f34-5b49-4130-969d-9d22426daa2b","l":"Price's model","d":2,"p":"6f3f5cf1-363e-4d04-ac63-ca4ae90b47fc","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Price's model, introduced by Derek J. de Solla Price, is a stochastic growth model for directed networks (originally citation networks) in which each new node links to existing nodes with probability proportional to their current in-degree plus a constant \"initial attractiveness\" (cumulative advantage). This preferential-attachment rule leads to a scale-free power-law in-degree distribution characteristic of many real-world citation and information networks."},{"id":"58916f48-51e5-4e33-94e0-bed8c275dda8","l":"Probability theory","d":2,"p":"b5ff1838-0ecd-41a5-a90b-bae4acb69fcf","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Probability theory is the mathematical framework for quantifying and manipulating uncertainty and random events, assigning numbers between 0 and 1 to outcomes that obey axioms for combining and conditioning. It provides concepts such as probability distributions, expectation values, variances, and stochastic processes that underpin statistical mechanics, quantum mechanics, and the theory of noise and diffusion."},{"id":"783a11f2-a7a4-4246-8bf9-e635a6a0fe7b","l":"Projected entangled pair states","d":2,"p":"50ba3f74-7f31-4857-8a75-b2b007348981","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields","Physics of Living Systems","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Projected entangled pair states (PEPS) are a class of tensor-network quantum states for lattice many-body systems obtained by placing maximally entangled virtual pairs on the lattice bonds and applying a local linear map (projection) at each site to map the virtual indices into the site's physical Hilbert space. They generalize one-dimensional matrix product states to two and higher dimensions, efficiently encoding area-law entanglement and serving as variational ans\u00e4tze for ground states of local Hamiltonians."},{"id":"149b07d4-403a-40c2-afcb-6aff2bd646f3","l":"Projective symmetries","d":2,"p":"eac8c4d5-07af-40a1-92d6-f32abca831a1","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Projective symmetries are symmetry operations that act on quantum states only up to an overall phase or internal gauge transformation, i.e., they form projective (rather than ordinary linear) representations of the physical symmetry group. They commonly appear for fractionalized excitations (spinons, anyons) or in systems with gauge redundancy, where the symmetry algebra closes only modulo a central phase or gauge element."},{"id":"8c0cd30a-5be8-4913-9220-907afd4bceb8","l":"Proteins","d":2,"p":"6c0e8bb5-a5a0-4f28-8d4a-4e7ace85a023","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Proteins are biological macromolecules made of linear chains of amino acids that fold into specific three-dimensional structures stabilized by covalent bonds and noncovalent interactions (hydrogen bonding, electrostatics, van der Waals, hydrophobic effect). Their sequence-dependent structure and conformational dynamics determine a wide range of cellular functions such as catalysis, signaling, transport, and structural support."},{"id":"9f4a90a9-5c37-4ba4-99ee-6cf2878294eb","l":"Protein structure prediction","d":2,"p":"6e1bbe22-c6fb-42b5-8ff6-1f61db13fdc4","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Protein structure prediction is the computational determination of a protein's three-dimensional arrangement of atoms (secondary, tertiary and quaternary structure) from its amino-acid sequence. It uses physics-based energy models, statistical potentials and data-driven methods (e.g., homology modeling or machine learning) to identify the low\u2013free-energy native fold and its stability."},{"id":"b3a24a83-7c2b-4f94-8b18-bf123a8752ee","l":"Proteomics","d":2,"p":"6b5443fa-9849-4d5b-966b-7d6e08113e36","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Proteomics is the large-scale study of the proteome\u2014the complete set of proteins expressed by a cell, tissue, or organism\u2014focusing on their identities, abundances, post-translational modifications, interactions and dynamics. It combines analytical techniques (especially mass spectrometry) with computational analysis to measure and characterize proteins in a systems-level, often time-resolved way."},{"id":"42975037-b654-4ceb-b89e-7f942f7427ae","l":"Protons","d":2,"p":"b0ebe8a2-ed86-4c14-bfdd-5860e15b18bc","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"A proton is a positively charged subatomic baryon (spin-1/2) made of two up quarks and one down quark bound by the strong force via gluons, carrying electric charge +e and a magnetic moment; together with neutrons it forms atomic nuclei. Its mass is about 1.6726\u00d710^-27 kg (\u2248938.27 MeV/c^2), and protons are effectively stable in ordinary matter."},{"id":"488f834e-365c-4954-a53f-c1701374a1e5","l":"Proton therapy","d":2,"p":"a24a2d8a-58a4-4cbc-81aa-4cd953e73f4b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Proton therapy is a form of external-beam radiotherapy that uses accelerated protons to irradiate tumors; because protons deposit most of their energy at the Bragg peak near the end of their range, it enables highly localized dose delivery with reduced exit dose to surrounding healthy tissue. Beams from cyclotrons or synchrotrons are energy\u2011modulated and scanned to create a spread\u2011out Bragg peak that covers the tumor volume."},{"id":"a3e1e2d0-9720-4be8-8410-abe172ff6ea6","l":"Pseudogap","d":2,"p":"8ef7f47d-a47b-4652-967c-4176ffb5a96c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A pseudogap is a partial, temperature-dependent suppression of the electronic density of states near the Fermi level that appears above the temperature where a true ordered phase (such as superconductivity) sets in. Unlike a full gap produced by long-range order, a pseudogap is incomplete and anisotropic, often arising from precursor pairing, fluctuations, or competing orders in strongly correlated materials (notably cuprate superconductors)."},{"id":"79063d38-a4e8-415e-aff8-63a46dcf6572","l":"Pseudo Jahn-Teller effect","d":2,"p":"9f8fee62-cbe8-4e7e-b0f5-c47a7cc9f70c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The pseudo Jahn\u2013Teller effect is a vibronic instability in which a nondegenerate electronic ground state is driven to a lower-symmetry geometry by electron\u2013vibration (vibronic) coupling to nearby excited electronic states, so that off-diagonal mixing lowers the total energy. It generalizes the Jahn\u2013Teller effect by producing symmetry-breaking distortions through second-order (nondegenerate) state mixing that can make a vibrational mode\u2019s force constant negative (imaginary frequency)."},{"id":"91027edc-1335-4304-87e8-437a350d768d","l":"Pseudopods","d":2,"p":"9ccf11df-5b72-4ce0-92e6-e48f8dda172b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Pseudopods (pseudopodia) are temporary protrusions of a eukaryotic cell\u2019s membrane and cytoplasm used for locomotion and engulfing material, formed by actin\u2011cytoskeleton remodeling. Their dynamics are governed by actin polymerization that pushes the membrane outward, cortical contractility and membrane tension that retract or shape the protrusion, and associated intracellular fluid flows."},{"id":"d5490963-e2d4-4fa8-9764-3e2f347e5b8c","l":"P-symmetry","d":2,"p":"d0171af5-5603-4934-b23f-d95de0287b81","fl":["Properties","Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"P-symmetry (parity symmetry) is the invariance of a physical system under spatial inversion, r \u2192 \u2212r, implemented by the parity operator which assigns states definite parity eigenvalues (+1 or \u22121). It expresses mirror symmetry of the laws of physics and is conserved by electromagnetic and strong interactions but is violated by the weak interaction."},{"id":"ccad126b-1f5a-410f-828c-fd0d1fd90de4","l":"PT-symmetry","d":2,"p":"d0171af5-5603-4934-b23f-d95de0287b81","fl":["Properties","Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"PT-symmetry is invariance of a system or Hamiltonian under the combined operations of parity (spatial reflection) and time reversal (an antiunitary operation that complex-conjugates amplitudes and reverses momenta).  In quantum and wave systems this allows certain non-Hermitian Hamiltonians to have entirely real spectra and lossless-like dynamics in an \"unbroken\" PT phase, with a transition to complex eigenvalues when PT symmetry is spontaneously broken."},{"id":"bf3b309e-dd81-45a3-9b2e-6e52a6e177b1","l":"Pulmonary fluid mechanics","d":2,"p":"12f6f3a9-8096-40e2-9170-e8f3a45d6bd9","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Pulmonary fluid mechanics is the application of fluid-mechanical principles to flows and transport processes in the respiratory system. It encompasses airflow through branching, compliant airways and alveoli, liquid-film and mucus transport, aerosol and particle deposition, surfactant dynamics, and pulmonary blood flow, including the coupled fluid\u2013structure interactions that govern breathing and gas exchange."},{"id":"23b5ea80-bba8-4aa7-9321-dd86af99df26","l":"Pulse compression","d":2,"p":"bd03ea46-2406-4a24-8307-05b2b498fb4e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Pulse compression is a signal-processing technique that encodes a long transmitted pulse (e.g., with a linear frequency chirp or phase code) and then uses matched filtering or dispersion compensation to convert it into a much shorter, higher\u2011peak\u2011power received pulse, improving temporal/range resolution and SNR without raising transmitter peak power. It is widely used in radar, sonar, and ultrafast optics (e.g., chirped\u2011pulse amplification)."},{"id":"f2c49627-6555-4323-a97f-958f47d01a2e","l":"Pulse shaping","d":2,"p":"2fd5a7a1-32f9-4efa-b4d9-368e9619d7ed","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Pulse shaping is the deliberate modification of an electromagnetic pulse's temporal waveform\u2014and equivalently its spectral amplitude and phase\u2014to produce a desired envelope, duration, chirp or spectral profile. It is realized with devices such as modulators, phase masks, dispersive optics or arbitrary waveform generators to optimize signal transmission, spectroscopy, nonlinear interactions, or quantum control."},{"id":"5ddb2c82-443e-4a9d-b7d2-9c3a4c5a35ac","l":"Pumped-storage hydroelectricity","d":2,"p":"4c12fcde-ed75-4e1c-bd19-253623703054","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Pumped-storage hydroelectricity is a large-scale energy storage method that converts electrical energy into gravitational potential by pumping water to an elevated reservoir and later generates electricity by releasing that water through turbines back down to a lower reservoir. It uses reversible pump-turbines for charge/discharge and provides grid-scale load balancing and short- to medium-term energy storage with typical round-trip efficiencies around 70\u201385%."},{"id":"830a8137-6b9a-447a-b09a-4eeb6ece6f31","l":"Pyrochlores","d":2,"p":"6af5be88-489f-4a50-9abf-998e8912254a","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Pyrochlores are materials with the formula A2B2O7 that crystallize in a cubic structure where one cation sublattice forms a network of corner-sharing tetrahedra. This geometry produces strong geometric frustration and consequently a variety of unusual magnetic, electronic, and thermal behaviors (e.g., spin ice, spin liquids, metal\u2013insulator transitions)."},{"id":"a9344dd6-82ed-462d-a89c-f1227b41943b","l":"Pyroelectrics","d":2,"p":"74721827-c4e3-4da0-90ca-dba52158fc6b","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Pyroelectrics are materials that develop a temporary change in electric polarization (and therefore a surface charge or voltage) when their temperature changes, because their spontaneous polarization is temperature-dependent. They are polar, noncentrosymmetric crystals (often also piezoelectric or ferroelectric) used in infrared detectors and thermal sensors."},{"id":"c70cbab0-8cf3-4573-aab7-d0c0932f43b9","l":"Pyrometry","d":2,"p":"c2ea95f8-c209-44d7-b8f7-a6baf632c46a","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Pyrometry is the measurement of temperature\u2014especially high temperatures\u2014by detecting the thermal radiation emitted by an object and converting the intensity or spectral distribution into a temperature using Planck\u2019s law. Practical pyrometry is usually non-contact and requires accounting for the object's emissivity and the instrument's spectral response."},{"id":"6bd7d694-d5ae-4ba4-910d-1f427bc874fa","l":"QCD phenomenology","d":2,"p":"74f884e0-6764-4962-a081-b198cca267c3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"QCD phenomenology is the area of particle physics that connects the theory of Quantum Chromodynamics to experiment by developing calculational tools and models to predict and interpret observable strong-interaction phenomena. It spans perturbative methods for high-energy processes (jets, hard scattering, structure functions) and nonperturbative approaches (lattice QCD, effective field theories, hadronization and spectroscopy) to describe confinement and hadron dynamics."},{"id":"32c9b7ae-6182-4ee6-96a1-2f51b3b65b3a","l":"Quantum algorithms","d":2,"p":"2ddffb19-8fa7-4c61-83a8-af6b9be80b5a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum algorithms are computational procedures designed to run on quantum processors that exploit quantum-mechanical resources\u2014superposition, entanglement and interference\u2014to solve problems more efficiently or in qualitatively different ways than classical algorithms. They are implemented as sequences of quantum gates or controlled evolutions that manipulate amplitudes to amplify correct outcomes (e.g., Shor's factoring, Grover's search, or quantum simulation algorithms)."},{"id":"12f97a90-dcdf-4500-82cb-1f2cc15670c2","l":"Quantum anomalous Hall effect","d":2,"p":"99c1e71a-1639-42a5-8e00-6e258fc0b4f5","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The quantum anomalous Hall effect is a zero\u2011magnetic\u2011field quantum Hall state in which a two\u2011dimensional insulator with broken time\u2011reversal symmetry exhibits a precisely quantized Hall conductance \u03c3_xy = C e^2/h (C an integer Chern number). It arises from a topologically nontrivial band structure (typically involving spin\u2013orbit coupling and intrinsic magnetization) and is accompanied by dissipationless chiral edge states."},{"id":"824c5bb5-1d38-4fff-9104-1fdc765ad06b","l":"Quantum aspects of black holes","d":2,"p":"6d5b9a32-cad0-4d9f-a837-b0f239f5cdc6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Quantum aspects of black holes are the phenomena and theoretical frameworks that arise when quantum mechanics is applied to black hole spacetimes, treating quantum fields on curved backgrounds and seeking quantum descriptions of the geometry. They include Hawking radiation and black hole thermodynamics (entropy and temperature), microscopic accounts of black hole microstates (e.g., in string theory and holography), and conceptual challenges such as the information-loss paradox and the fate of singularities."},{"id":"df3dd36a-c63c-4a89-bcf1-8b6d011f2f0f","l":"Quantum atomic crystals","d":2,"p":"daf1bbbc-2b32-42e3-89b6-e280ffa32030","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Quantum atomic crystals are crystalline phases formed by atoms whose lattice structure and dynamics are dominated by quantum effects\u2014such as zero-point motion, tunneling, and quantum statistics\u2014rather than by classical thermal motion. Examples include solid helium and ordered ultracold-atom arrays in optical lattices, which can show quantum melting, supersolidity, and other nonclassical behaviors."},{"id":"8d2a7943-d0ae-4e32-ab4f-6d6388a5a779","l":"Quantum benchmarking","d":2,"p":"2ddffb19-8fa7-4c61-83a8-af6b9be80b5a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum benchmarking is the collection of experimental and statistical protocols used to quantify the performance and noise of quantum devices and operations by producing standardized metrics (e.g., average gate fidelities or error rates). Common approaches\u2014such as randomized benchmarking, interleaved or cycle benchmarking, gate-set tomography, and cross-entropy benchmarking\u2014are designed to give scalable, robust estimates of device quality while minimizing sensitivity to state-preparation and measurement (SPAM) errors."},{"id":"cc1bd67c-f42f-4789-9e02-6b24f2396e45","l":"Quantum cavities","d":2,"p":"dcf48409-af3f-4517-b58d-7ec6c95351f3","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Quantum cavities are electromagnetic resonators that confine and quantize field modes so that individual photons can interact coherently with atoms, quantum emitters, or superconducting circuits. By providing discrete, high\u2011Q modes and engineered coupling, they enhance light\u2013matter interactions for cavity QED experiments, quantum information processing, and sensing."},{"id":"3cc6785c-c788-47ae-911c-5b1683fbca6d","l":"Quantum channels","d":2,"p":"8ec9d84c-4a4c-499f-81f9-e6e703c50033","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum channels are the mathematical description of physical transformations of quantum states: they are linear, completely positive, trace-preserving maps that send density operators on one Hilbert space to density operators on another. They model unitary evolution, decoherence, measurements and noise, and can be represented by Kraus operator sums or as a unitary on a larger system (Stinespring dilation)."},{"id":"bc3272d1-4ae7-46d9-b068-a5006a9a231a","l":"Quantum chaotic transport","d":2,"p":"e8e055f8-8711-4305-9995-7b3789b1a63b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Nonlinear Dynamics"],"def":"Quantum chaotic transport is the study of how quantum particles or waves propagate through systems whose classical dynamics are chaotic, especially in open structures (cavities, quantum dots, waveguides). It emphasizes how quantum coherence and interference alter the classically chaotic flow, producing characteristic mesoscopic phenomena and universal statistical behavior (e.g., conductance fluctuations, weak localization, shot noise, and dynamical localization)."},{"id":"ae589249-804f-4a7c-94d7-f882ecc129be","l":"Quantum chemistry methods","d":2,"p":"55bba34b-0d31-428f-a430-4f502667434a","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Quantum chemistry methods are computational techniques that apply quantum mechanics to predict the electronic structure, energies, spectra, and properties of atoms, molecules, and materials by approximately solving the electronic Schr\u00f6dinger equation; common approaches include Hartree\u2013Fock, post\u2011Hartree\u2011Fock correlated methods, density functional theory, and semiempirical models that trade off accuracy and computational cost."},{"id":"f9456df5-5ca2-4a8a-8b11-d5ffd9d96921","l":"Quantum computing models","d":2,"p":"2ddffb19-8fa7-4c61-83a8-af6b9be80b5a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum computing models are theoretical frameworks that specify how information is encoded in quantum systems (qubits, qudits, or continuous variables) and how computational steps\u2014unitary gates, Hamiltonian evolution, and/or measurements\u2014are implemented to process that information and characterize resources, complexity, and error behavior. Common examples include the quantum circuit model, adiabatic/annealing and Hamiltonian-based models, measurement-based (cluster-state) computing, topological quantum computing, and sampling models such as boson sampling."},{"id":"22dbe828-c8c7-4b61-abb0-20c7d36f3d01","l":"Quantum criticality","d":2,"p":"520d3361-6e3b-4612-895f-98a7259acfe3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Quantum criticality describes the universal, scale-invariant behavior of a many-body system near a continuous zero-temperature (quantum) phase transition, where quantum \u2014 rather than thermal \u2014 fluctuations drive singular changes in the ground state as a control parameter (pressure, doping, field, etc.) is varied. At the quantum critical point correlation lengths and times diverge and observables follow power-law scaling, often producing a finite-temperature \"quantum critical\" regime with anomalous transport and thermodynamic properties."},{"id":"8f03d659-8cea-4e87-ab36-4e3dc6e1a2ec","l":"Quantum cryptography","d":2,"p":"8ec9d84c-4a4c-499f-81f9-e6e703c50033","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum cryptography uses quantum-mechanical principles to perform cryptographic tasks\u2014most famously quantum key distribution (QKD)\u2014in which secure encryption keys are generated and shared with security guaranteed by phenomena like the no\u2011cloning theorem and measurement-induced disturbance. Its security rests on physical laws rather than computational hardness, so eavesdropping on quantum signals necessarily introduces detectable changes."},{"id":"0d8c3864-a060-4baf-a6a0-ad0b455410cc","l":"Quantum description of light-matter interaction","d":2,"p":"3123cbf9-460d-467a-9306-b4fb316daddd","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"The quantum description of light\u2013matter interaction treats both the electromagnetic field and the material degrees of freedom as quantum systems coupled by an interaction Hamiltonian (commonly the minimal-coupling or electric-dipole form), so photons can be absorbed, emitted, or scattered by quantized atomic, molecular, or solid-state states. This framework predicts uniquely quantum phenomena such as spontaneous emission, vacuum-induced energy shifts, nonclassical photon statistics, and entanglement between light and matter."},{"id":"d4adc3eb-a5e8-4d92-a9eb-ec587ba89bf8","l":"Quantum discord","d":2,"p":"d599eade-2dc5-4572-84ac-54f7a980fbfb","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum discord is a measure of nonclassical correlations in a bipartite quantum state that go beyond entanglement: it equals the difference between the quantum mutual information (total correlations) and the maximal classical correlations obtainable by local measurements. A nonzero discord signals that local measurements disturb the joint state and that even some separable (unentangled) mixed states can contain genuinely quantum correlations."},{"id":"929a0cb8-681b-4acb-ade6-376718a2b7e5","l":"Quantum dot molecules","d":2,"p":"709d46e1-304b-4708-a494-d27c13e9ca2c","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Quantum dot molecules are systems of two or more closely spaced semiconductor quantum dots whose electronic and excitonic states hybridize through tunnel coupling and Coulomb interactions, producing molecular-like bonding and antibonding levels. They act as \"artificial molecules\" with tunable energy spectra and optical/electronic properties set by dot size, spacing, and external fields."},{"id":"5de0faa9-608e-450b-8d04-5aa4219dad8d","l":"Quantum entanglement","d":2,"p":"d599eade-2dc5-4572-84ac-54f7a980fbfb","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum entanglement is a nonclassical correlation in a composite quantum system where the joint state cannot be expressed as a product of individual subsystem states, so the subsystems have interdependent measurement outcomes. These correlations persist regardless of spatial separation, underpin quantum information tasks (e.g., teleportation) but do not allow faster\u2011than\u2011light signaling."},{"id":"2eb80948-a4d0-4b3c-b196-1e722da4c96f","l":"Quantum fields in curved spacetime","d":2,"p":"6287ac05-d956-4c3c-9882-8ea7a798f964","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Quantum fields in curved spacetime is the framework for doing quantum field theory when the background metric is a fixed, curved (general-relativistic) spacetime\u2014gravity is treated classically while matter fields are quantized. Curvature alters the vacuum and particle concept (leading to effects like particle creation, the Unruh effect, and Hawking radiation) and the quantum expectation value of the stress\u2013energy can feed back into semiclassical Einstein equations."},{"id":"f2f44396-379b-459c-8561-6686ba232d28","l":"Quantum Fisher information","d":2,"p":"53cd9599-5951-4fa6-af4e-2ff2c0e53fe4","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum Fisher information is the quantum generalization of Fisher information that quantifies how sensitively a quantum state depends on a parameter and thus sets the ultimate precision limit for estimating that parameter via the quantum Cram\u00e9r\u2013Rao bound. It is computed from the state's symmetric logarithmic derivative and is proportional to the infinitesimal Bures distance between nearby quantum states."},{"id":"5604ac5d-c1e3-41b0-8ed2-03b2867cbf1f","l":"Quantum fluctuations & noise","d":2,"p":"632f74d1-d16b-4b92-a295-b24729a93d8d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Quantum fluctuations are the intrinsic, random variations of physical observables that persist even in a system's ground state, arising from the Heisenberg uncertainty principle and zero-point energy. Quantum noise is the observable manifestation of those fluctuations (and related quantum-statistical randomness) in measurements and signals\u2014an irreducible source of error such as shot noise, vacuum fluctuations, and measurement back-action that limits precision in quantum and classical devices."},{"id":"e4ec4cec-9285-4a0f-b46a-e41838df98af","l":"Quantum gates","d":2,"p":"2ddffb19-8fa7-4c61-83a8-af6b9be80b5a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum gates are the elementary operations in quantum computing, represented mathematically by unitary operators that act on the state space of one or more qubits to change their amplitudes and phases. Unlike classical logic gates, they are reversible and can create and manipulate superposition and entanglement; sequences of quantum gates form quantum circuits that implement algorithms."},{"id":"1d626944-7dd8-4abd-8400-1e1af764ab46","l":"Quantum geometry","d":2,"p":"9f7a4cce-0ded-4976-b8ad-cc75830297fa","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Quantum geometry is the study of geometric structures when described by quantum theory \u2014 where distances, areas, connections or metrics become operators or state-dependent quantities with quantum fluctuations, discreteness, or noncommutativity; examples include quantized spacetime in quantum gravity and the quantum metric and Berry curvature of electronic bands."},{"id":"34f6ade4-130f-4fca-bd2e-651b4a617405","l":"Quantum graphs","d":2,"p":"e8e055f8-8711-4305-9995-7b3789b1a63b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Nonlinear Dynamics"],"def":"A quantum graph is a metric graph whose edges are treated as one-dimensional wires carrying a quantum wavefunction governed by a differential operator (typically the Schr\u00f6dinger or Laplace operator), with vertex boundary conditions (e.g., continuity and current conservation) that couple the edge solutions. It is a simplified model for quantum transport, scattering, spectral statistics, and wave dynamics on network-like structures."},{"id":"9ce95d6c-b894-4c18-b005-3e74f18b6f1d","l":"Quantum group symmetries","d":2,"p":"fadc87f6-ee6b-49d6-9128-da9737e4d165","fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Quantum group symmetries are generalized symmetry algebras \u2014 Hopf algebras that q\u2011deform classical Lie groups and Lie algebras into noncommutative, co\u2011algebraic structures which act on quantum systems. They underlie integrable models, braided/anyonic statistics and q\u2011deformed representation theory, reducing to ordinary group symmetries when the deformation parameter q \u2192 1."},{"id":"9017d068-f9d7-42f8-b083-a385e9b14c0b","l":"Quantum Hall effect","d":2,"p":"99c1e71a-1639-42a5-8e00-6e258fc0b4f5","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Quantum Hall effect is a quantum phenomenon in two-dimensional electron systems, where the transverse (Hall) conductance becomes precisely quantized in units of e^2/h when subjected to low temperatures and a strong perpendicular magnetic field. This quantization\u2014appearing as integer or interaction-induced fractional plateaus\u2014stems from Landau-level quantization and topological bulk properties that produce dissipationless edge currents."},{"id":"6e1c9aa8-2e35-4fcf-ba26-5fcc93a4aed4","l":"Quantum information architectures & platforms","d":2,"p":"2c450d36-1070-4190-8d54-aa17a4ef7f8b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum information architectures & platforms are the combined logical designs and physical implementations for encoding, processing, protecting, and communicating quantum information\u2014covering qubit encodings, gate sets, error-correction schemes, control and interconnect topologies. They include the hardware technologies (e.g., superconducting circuits, trapped ions, photonics, spins, neutral atoms, topological devices) and their supporting infrastructure (control electronics, cryogenics, and networking) that determine performance, scalability, and integration."},{"id":"55c3fe8b-3121-4488-a8a6-4e2876ab1113","l":"Quantum information processing with continuous variables","d":2,"p":"2c450d36-1070-4190-8d54-aa17a4ef7f8b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum information processing with continuous variables studies encoding, manipulating, and measuring quantum information stored in degrees of freedom with continuous spectra\u2014typically the quadratures of bosonic modes (e.g., optical fields) or position and momentum\u2014so that states live in infinite\u2011dimensional Hilbert spaces. It uses Gaussian states and operations (squeezing, beam splitters, homodyne detection) together with non\u2011Gaussian resources to implement teleportation, computation, communication, and metrology protocols."},{"id":"85d77fd2-8d59-4b15-8bc7-a3246ac268f0","l":"Quantum interconnects","d":2,"p":"2c450d36-1070-4190-8d54-aa17a4ef7f8b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum interconnects are physical channels and interface devices that transmit quantum information\u2014quantum states or entanglement\u2014between separate quantum systems while preserving coherence and quantum correlations. They include links (optical fibers, microwave waveguides), transducers that convert between different carriers (e.g., microwave\u2194optical), and network elements (switches, repeaters) needed to scale quantum processors and build quantum communication networks."},{"id":"f464ad01-629c-41f3-98bc-195c920e4d42","l":"Quantum measurements","d":2,"p":"01f70d47-cbac-4b5a-8ff9-0768e722511b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum measurements are physical processes that produce classical outcomes from a quantum system, with outcome probabilities determined by the Born rule and associated to measurement operators or eigenvalues of an observable. The act of measurement typically changes the system's state (the projection or \"collapse\" for ideal measurements), and more generally is described by POVMs and quantum instruments to account for partial, noisy, or weak measurements and their back-action."},{"id":"cd384806-a109-4fdd-9efc-c15bde603baa","l":"Quantum memories","d":2,"p":"2c450d36-1070-4190-8d54-aa17a4ef7f8b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum memories are physical systems that can store and retrieve arbitrary quantum states (qubits or photonic states) while preserving their coherence and entanglement. They serve to synchronize and buffer quantum information in applications such as quantum communication and computation and are characterized by storage time, fidelity, efficiency, and bandwidth."},{"id":"36dfd7c2-1779-4d5e-b0ad-3236103434e4","l":"Quantum molecular crystals","d":2,"p":"daf1bbbc-2b32-42e3-89b6-e280ffa32030","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Quantum molecular crystals are solids composed of discrete molecules on a lattice whose structural and dynamical behavior is governed by quantum effects\u2014notably large zero\u2011point motion, tunneling, and quantized rotational states of the constituent molecules. These effects lead to unusual phases and properties (e.g., orientationally disordered \"plastic\" phases, proton tunneling, quantum paraelectricity) and strong isotope dependence."},{"id":"9dc2ee1a-ff51-438a-b7c7-1045cd385cfc","l":"Quantum Monte Carlo","d":2,"p":"eb9bd2e1-eedd-4bd0-997d-58b44ffa3ebb","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics","Networks","Particles & Fields","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Physics of Living Systems","Plasma Physics","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Quantum Monte Carlo (QMC) is a family of stochastic numerical methods that solve quantum many-body problems by Monte Carlo sampling of wavefunctions or path integrals to estimate energies and expectation values with controllable statistical error. Common variants\u2014variational, diffusion (projector), and path-integral Monte Carlo\u2014can be highly accurate for bosons and correlated electrons, though fermionic calculations typically require approximations (e.g., the fixed-node approximation) to mitigate the sign problem."},{"id":"f8539a02-81ce-440e-b63a-535195a24901","l":"Quantum networks","d":2,"p":"8ec9d84c-4a4c-499f-81f9-e6e703c50033","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology","Networks"],"def":"Quantum networks are systems of spatially separated quantum nodes (quantum processors, memories, or sensors) connected by quantum channels (typically photonic links) that distribute and process quantum states and entanglement across distances. They enable tasks such as secure communication, entanglement-based teleportation, distributed quantum computing, and enhanced sensing, and rely on components like quantum repeaters to mitigate loss and decoherence."},{"id":"5ba9ba41-67e3-4588-86b5-6cc8052e0847","l":"Quantum optics with artificial atoms","d":2,"p":"632f74d1-d16b-4b92-a295-b24729a93d8d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Quantum optics with artificial atoms studies the interaction of quantized electromagnetic fields with engineered, discrete-level quantum systems (artificial atoms) such as superconducting qubits, semiconductor quantum dots, or color centers. These tunable, solid\u2011state emitters enable circuit/cavity QED, strong light\u2013matter coupling, single\u2011photon devices, quantum nonlinear optics, and components for quantum information processing."},{"id":"88a1e90b-09a3-405c-b12a-5bcdc0c8d1e8","l":"Quantum repeaters","d":2,"p":"8ec9d84c-4a4c-499f-81f9-e6e703c50033","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum repeaters are devices and protocols that enable long-distance quantum communication by overcoming loss and decoherence in photonic channels. They do this by creating entanglement over short links and extending it stepwise via entanglement swapping, purification (error correction), and quantum memories to store and synchronize quantum states."},{"id":"84501895-7551-48a1-848a-2ecc00f30308","l":"Quantum scars","d":2,"p":"e8e055f8-8711-4305-9995-7b3789b1a63b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Nonlinear Dynamics"],"def":"Quantum scars are anomalous enhancements of probability density in certain quantum eigenstates of classically chaotic systems, appearing as wavefunction intensity concentrated along unstable classical periodic orbits. They produce localized, non\u2011ergodic structures that deviate from random\u2011matrix/ergodic expectations, arising from constructive interference of semiclassical trajectories."},{"id":"9b6aaf50-04bd-4aaa-b4c3-d6fa9b7f7303","l":"Quantum sensing","d":2,"p":"53cd9599-5951-4fa6-af4e-2ff2c0e53fe4","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum sensing uses engineered quantum states and measurements\u2014exploiting superposition, entanglement, squeezing and other nonclassical resources\u2014to detect and quantify physical quantities (fields, forces, time, displacement, etc.) with sensitivities or resolutions beyond what classical sensors can achieve, often reaching quantum-limited (standard-quantum or Heisenberg) precision."},{"id":"3248f582-d541-4ffd-93ec-48a886abd31d","l":"Quantum software","d":2,"p":"2ddffb19-8fa7-4c61-83a8-af6b9be80b5a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum software is the collection of programs, languages, compilers, simulators, and control/verification tools used to design, optimize, and execute quantum algorithms on quantum hardware or emulators. It encompasses high-level algorithms, circuit and pulse control, noise models, error-correction/mitigation, and benchmarking/verification frameworks."},{"id":"99ed3492-0248-44a8-9278-4709ff9cffd5","l":"Quantum spin Hall effect","d":2,"p":"99c1e71a-1639-42a5-8e00-6e258fc0b4f5","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The quantum spin Hall effect is a two-dimensional topological insulating phase, protected by time-reversal symmetry, in which the bulk is insulating while the edges host counterpropagating, spin-polarized (helical) modes with spin locked to momentum. These helical edge states support dissipationless spin currents (and robust charge transport) without an external magnetic field and are characterized by a Z2 topological invariant."},{"id":"ad0341ba-4e83-4a2e-8732-5e9eb51a4e5f","l":"Quantum state engineering","d":2,"p":"632f74d1-d16b-4b92-a295-b24729a93d8d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Quantum state engineering is the deliberate preparation and control of specific quantum states\u2014pure or mixed, localized or entangled\u2014by tailoring interactions, Hamiltonians, measurements, and environments. It employs techniques such as coherent pulses, measurement and feedback, and reservoir (dissipative) engineering to create, stabilize, or transform nonclassical states for quantum computing, communication, sensing, and simulation."},{"id":"fa660751-b084-419b-9861-223d75821bb0","l":"Quantum states of light","d":2,"p":"632f74d1-d16b-4b92-a295-b24729a93d8d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Quantum states of light are the quantum-mechanical descriptions of the electromagnetic field, specified by a state vector or density operator in the field's Hilbert space and determining the probabilities for photon number, phase, and correlations. Examples include Fock (fixed photon number), coherent (classical-like laser light), squeezed (reduced noise in one quadrature), thermal, and entangled photon states, which can exhibit nonclassical features such as superposition, squeezing, and entanglement."},{"id":"20982911-c2b4-4fb9-ac94-68f3336e10b4","l":"Quantum teleportation","d":2,"p":"830eaa37-9813-4972-9351-1846c659b20b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum teleportation is a protocol that transfers an unknown quantum state from one system to another at a distant location by consuming a shared entangled pair and sending a finite number of classical bits, without physically moving the original particle. The sender's measurement destroys the original state and the receiver reconstructs it with local operations using the classical message, so the process does not allow faster-than-light signaling."},{"id":"8e3130c3-9c84-483a-9907-a9d304524b5d","l":"Quantum thermodynamics","d":2,"p":"609a2adc-099f-463f-9e2a-dd85c0101044","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Quantum thermodynamics is the study of how thermodynamic concepts\u2014energy, heat, work, entropy, and temperature\u2014apply to and emerge from systems governed by quantum mechanics. It investigates thermalization, fluctuation relations, and the roles of quantum coherence, correlations, and entanglement in energy exchange and the performance of microscopic heat engines."},{"id":"98af60b6-b51d-4e6c-b52b-ec471b54a87d","l":"Quantum tomography","d":2,"p":"830eaa37-9813-4972-9351-1846c659b20b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum tomography is the set of experimental and statistical methods used to reconstruct a quantum state, process, or measurement operator from the outcome statistics of measurements performed on many identically prepared systems. It infers objects such as the density matrix or process (Choi) matrix\u2014typically via linear inversion, maximum-likelihood, or Bayesian estimation\u2014and is inherently statistical because single copies cannot reveal full quantum information."},{"id":"5ea60796-89ce-43f1-8194-e4568f4d6d66","l":"Quantum verification","d":2,"p":"830eaa37-9813-4972-9351-1846c659b20b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum verification is the set of theoretical and experimental methods used to certify that quantum states, operations, or computations are correct and performed as claimed, even in the presence of noise or untrusted devices. It includes laboratory tools like state/process tomography, randomized benchmarking and self\u2011testing, as well as cryptographic and complexity\u2011theoretic protocols (e.g., interactive proofs and the class QMA) for verifying quantum computations and proofs."},{"id":"fb2f621b-f402-4184-b6d5-a2db367ee6ea","l":"Quantum walks","d":2,"p":"e8e055f8-8711-4305-9995-7b3789b1a63b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology","Fluid Dynamics","Nonlinear Dynamics"],"def":"Quantum walks are the quantum-mechanical analogue of classical random walks in which a quantum particle coherently explores the sites of a graph or lattice, evolving in superposition and producing interference between paths. They occur in discrete-time (with an internal \"coin\" degree of freedom) and continuous-time forms, exhibit ballistic rather than diffusive spreading, and underpin quantum algorithms and models of quantum transport."},{"id":"51cfc384-c54c-4e7d-92d3-ba8dabde7cc4","l":"Quark & gluon jets","d":2,"p":"0b08c33f-27b1-4bbf-90f9-1eb1bf63d1eb","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Quark and gluon jets are collimated sprays of hadrons observed in high-energy collisions, produced when an energetic quark or gluon undergoes QCD radiation (parton showering) followed by hadronization. Because gluons carry a larger color charge than quarks, gluon jets typically radiate more, making them broader, with higher particle multiplicity and softer fragment energies than quark jets."},{"id":"49a168c5-ca4b-492d-aba2-cfe9d31ccd33","l":"Quark mixing","d":2,"p":"5adb0193-f741-4713-8010-4270b7bf5d9c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Quark mixing is the fact that the quark states that participate in weak charged-current interactions are not the same as the quark mass eigenstates, so weak decays couple linear combinations of up- and down-type quarks. This misalignment is described by the unitary Cabibbo\u2013Kobayashi\u2013Maskawa (CKM) matrix, which sets the amplitudes for flavor-changing weak transitions and contains the CP-violating phase."},{"id":"acc11cac-50ec-41cf-b918-9df5ef23c15d","l":"Quarkonia","d":2,"p":"5400167e-3abc-44f8-b432-0dc88cab84cf","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Quarkonia are mesons formed by a heavy quark bound to its own antiquark (for example, charmonium c c\u0304 and bottomonium b b\u0304). Their largely nonrelativistic bound-state spectra, decays, and production mechanisms provide sensitive probes of quantum chromodynamics."},{"id":"844905af-98de-43dd-b6d8-a5792680fea4","l":"Qubits","d":2,"p":"2ddffb19-8fa7-4c61-83a8-af6b9be80b5a","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A qubit is the basic unit of quantum information: a two-level quantum system described by a normalized state vector in a two-dimensional Hilbert space (commonly labeled |0> and |1>). Unlike a classical bit, a qubit can exist in coherent superpositions of |0> and |1> and can become entangled with other qubits, while measurement yields a classical 0 or 1 with probabilities set by the state's amplitudes."},{"id":"20f30721-e043-4448-a25f-16db6860f77a","l":"Qudits","d":2,"p":"2ddffb19-8fa7-4c61-83a8-af6b9be80b5a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"A qudit is a d-level quantum system (d \u2265 2) that generalizes the qubit: its state is a superposition of d orthogonal basis states |0\u27e9,\u2026,|d\u22121\u27e9 and serves as the basic unit of quantum information in a d-dimensional Hilbert space. Compared with qubits, qudits provide larger per-particle information capacity and richer entanglement and gate structures useful for quantum computing and communication."},{"id":"dfbf780b-81e5-4126-a90d-9493d9e792b9","l":"Rabi model","d":2,"p":"cb4dcf82-80bb-4305-94f4-12a11959d840","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"The Rabi model describes a two-level quantum system (spin-1/2 or qubit) dipole-coupled to a single-mode harmonic oscillator (a quantized field), typically written H = \u03c9 a\u2020a + (\u03b5/2)\u03c3_z + g \u03c3_x(a + a\u2020). It is the simplest fully quantum model of light\u2013matter interaction that captures Rabi oscillations including counter\u2011rotating terms and reduces to the Jaynes\u2013Cummings model under the rotating\u2011wave approximation."},{"id":"afa65577-2d57-41ce-acfe-dc2aa330f8a2","l":"Radiation belts","d":2,"p":"993587b9-4472-41fd-9896-2df938e75b92","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Radiation belts are regions around a magnetized planet or moon where energetic charged particles (electrons and ions) are trapped by the body's magnetic field, forming concentrated zones of high-energy radiation (for example, Earth's Van Allen belts). They arise from solar wind, cosmic rays, and magnetospheric acceleration processes, vary with geomagnetic activity, and pose hazards to spacecraft and living organisms."},{"id":"bf27e0d6-72a8-4c08-81d1-8aa81b9ba40e","l":"Radiation damage","d":2,"p":"b9ef303e-f2a9-4a3b-8b81-23a6799d7823","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Radiation damage is the change in a material\u2019s atomic structure and macroscopic properties caused by energy deposited from energetic particles or photons, which produces atomic displacements, ionization, defect clusters and sometimes nuclear transmutation. It leads to defects such as vacancies, interstitials, dislocation loops and gas bubbles and alters mechanical, thermal and electrical behavior, commonly quantified by dose measures like displacements per atom (dpa)."},{"id":"61428653-e07b-4eb0-850e-5befec07a0d0","l":"Radiation from moving charges","d":2,"p":"7e4e985f-d2fb-46cf-9183-c6e4b4c1c61b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Radiation from moving charges is the emission of electromagnetic waves produced whenever a charged particle's motion creates time\u2011varying electromagnetic fields\u2014most fundamentally when the charge accelerates (with emitted power given by the Larmor formula nonrelativistically and its Li\u00e9nard generalization relativistically). In media or at boundaries, similar radiation can arise even for uniform motion (e.g., Cherenkov and transition radiation), and the spectrum and directionality are set by the charge's trajectory and the surrounding material."},{"id":"f4eb2e27-2920-46a3-bc06-3a13f7384dc2","l":"Radiation therapy","d":2,"p":"a24a2d8a-58a4-4cbc-81aa-4cd953e73f4b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Radiation therapy is the medical use of ionizing radiation to control or destroy disease\u2014most commonly cancer\u2014by depositing energy in tissues to induce DNA damage and cell death. It is delivered as external beams (photons, electrons, protons, heavier ions) or internal sources (brachytherapy) with treatment planning to concentrate dose in the target while sparing healthy tissue."},{"id":"7ecf5917-cd16-436d-b8d3-f6ac5afcb57d","l":"Radioactive waste","d":2,"p":"d9e32a6d-d42e-430a-9665-10aaf0776e26","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Radioactive waste is material (solid, liquid, or gas) that contains radioactive nuclides produced by nuclear reactors, medical, industrial, or research activities and is hazardous because it emits ionizing radiation. Its radiological risk and required management (shielding, containment, storage or disposal) depend on the waste's activity, radionuclide composition, and half-lives."},{"id":"e7e2469f-19ea-473e-bb88-70226b308f95","l":"Radio-frequency & microwave plasma measurements","d":2,"p":"a5866236-c99c-4828-b499-4f50cccf7a2b","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Radio-frequency and microwave plasma measurements are diagnostic techniques that use RF (kHz\u2013hundreds of MHz) and microwave (\u2248GHz\u2013hundreds of GHz) electromagnetic waves to probe plasmas. By analyzing transmitted, reflected, scattered or emitted signals (e.g., interferometry, reflectometry, scattering, ECE) these methods determine plasma parameters such as electron density and temperature, collision frequency, dielectric properties, wave dispersion and fluctuation spectra."},{"id":"d3438a74-fcf7-4d82-8da4-8d5b439da313","l":"Radiofrequency reflectometry","d":2,"p":"a38dc017-3236-4084-afc5-eb59c5de51e0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Radiofrequency reflectometry is a measurement technique that injects an RF signal into a resonant or impedance-matched circuit containing a device and detects the amplitude and phase of the reflected wave. Changes in the device's impedance (due to charge motion, tunneling, state transitions, etc.) alter the reflection coefficient or resonance, enabling fast, sensitive readout of electrical or quantum states."},{"id":"6574e719-c243-4d56-bf4b-d1df568bb323","l":"Raman lasers","d":2,"p":"cd4c012e-bacf-4bcf-8358-a6573a330ec4","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Raman lasers are lasers that use stimulated Raman scattering as the gain mechanism: a strong pump laser transfers energy to a lower-frequency (Stokes) optical field by inelastic scattering from vibrational or rotational modes of the gain medium. They produce coherent light shifted from the pump by the medium's Raman frequency, providing tunable output and often operating without electronic population inversion in crystals, gases, or optical fibers."},{"id":"8c2c45c3-8b11-425e-b5d8-9faec4b6dcfd","l":"Raman spectroscopy","d":2,"p":"8156d7cb-6ab9-4097-be2b-4aa71aad93a3","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Raman spectroscopy is an optical technique that measures the inelastic scattering of monochromatic light by matter, where scattered photons are shifted in energy (Stokes and anti\u2011Stokes lines) by vibrational, rotational, or lattice excitations. The measured wavelength shifts provide fingerprints of molecular structure, chemical composition, and phonon modes."},{"id":"3d19765a-35bb-4e02-b80f-cc0628e0d760","l":"Random attacks","d":2,"p":"f014e3f5-6876-4e32-a7b6-c7ca1c6eb27b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Random attacks are perturbations in which elements of a system (e.g., nodes or links in a network) are removed or disabled uniformly at random, modeling non-adaptive failures. They are used to study system robustness, percolation thresholds, and the likelihood of cascading breakdowns compared with targeted (deliberate) attacks."},{"id":"a5122c7d-e33f-48e5-b638-b22d6214df9b","l":"Random lasers","d":2,"p":"cd4c012e-bacf-4bcf-8358-a6573a330ec4","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Random lasers are light-amplifying systems in which optical feedback is provided by multiple scattering in a disordered gain medium rather than by mirrors or a conventional cavity. When gain overcomes losses, amplified spontaneous emission and interference of the scattered waves produce lasing emission with spectrally and spatially complex (often speckled or localized) modes and a threshold behavior."},{"id":"5400cdbb-53f8-47c7-b672-6ba1edef9e87","l":"Rare decays","d":2,"p":"270c18a9-5bde-4c79-9f83-8453d38dc9c8","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Rare decays are processes in which an unstable particle decays into a specific final state with an exceptionally small branching fraction, typically suppressed by symmetries, limited phase space, tiny couplings, or loop-level dynamics. Because their Standard Model rates are both tiny and precisely predicted, measurements of rare decays provide sensitive probes of virtual effects and potential new physics."},{"id":"452ccb3d-f576-421e-b9e4-a090c68a1a54","l":"Rare-earth alloys","d":2,"p":"96973abf-f1b5-44c5-9549-9f0f4dfe234f","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Rare-earth alloys are metallic materials that contain one or more rare-earth elements (primarily the lanthanides, often including yttrium or scandium) combined with other metals. Their partially filled 4f electron shells produce localized magnetic moments, strong spin\u2013orbit and crystal-field effects, and thus distinctive magnetic, electronic, and optical properties useful for permanent magnets, lasers, and other technologies."},{"id":"2bd34e35-3d0d-4479-8111-185e6a4bea0f","l":"Rare-isotope facilities","d":2,"p":"4eec2798-da70-4fc2-8371-f6aa5f6180d7","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Rare-isotope facilities are accelerator-based laboratories that produce, separate, and deliver short-lived nuclei far from the valley of stability as beams or samples. They use techniques such as in-flight fragmentation, spallation, and isotope-separation-on-line (ISOL) to provide radioactive ion beams for experiments in nuclear structure, nuclear astrophysics, fundamental symmetries, and applied sciences."},{"id":"36a39358-f9b2-43d8-8cfc-e76935b4bac7","l":"Rashba coupling","d":2,"p":"4201a122-4a87-4b76-af1c-5e52bb109c78","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Rashba coupling is a spin\u2013orbit interaction that appears in systems with structural inversion asymmetry (e.g., surfaces or semiconductor heterostructures), causing a momentum-dependent splitting of electronic spin bands and spin\u2013momentum locking. In two dimensions it is described by the Rashba Hamiltonian H_R = \u03b1_R (k_y \u03c3_x \u2212 k_x \u03c3_y), where \u03b1_R is the Rashba coefficient and \u03c3 are the Pauli matrices."},{"id":"0eeff6c2-2b0f-4163-b617-e76cd625e623","l":"Rayleigh scattering","d":2,"p":"8156d7cb-6ab9-4097-be2b-4aa71aad93a3","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Rayleigh scattering is the elastic scattering of electromagnetic waves by particles or density fluctuations much smaller than the wavelength, in which the scattered intensity scales approximately as 1/\u03bb^4. This wavelength dependence makes shorter (blue) light scatter far more than longer (red) light, explaining effects like the blue sky and red sunsets and producing polarization of the scattered light."},{"id":"aa4842a6-9109-4517-b1c2-5bbc24e970fc","l":"Rayleigh scattering in plasmas","d":2,"p":"2917e3bd-b25c-4e89-8796-5c7e77dfda5a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Rayleigh scattering in plasmas is the elastic scattering of electromagnetic waves by particles or refractive-index/density fluctuations that are much smaller than the radiation wavelength, so the scatterers act as oscillating dipoles. In a plasma this mechanism can arise from bound electrons, tiny dust grains or microscopic density fluctuations and (in the dipole limit) gives a strong wavelength dependence (\u221d\u03bb\u207b\u2074), distinct from wavelength-independent Thomson scattering by free electrons or collective plasma scattering."},{"id":"b52a63dc-1811-4ec3-bbb7-a57a18ae0d0d","l":"Reactivity of clusters","d":2,"p":"cac7f673-4cd1-47b8-9de3-9f9785aea585","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Reactivity of clusters is the propensity of small aggregates of atoms or molecules to undergo chemical reactions, governed by their size, composition, geometric and electronic structure, and surface coordination. Because clusters have high surface-to-volume ratios, discrete electronic states and often fluxional geometries, their reaction rates and pathways can differ markedly from bulk materials and vary nonmonotonically with cluster size."},{"id":"c38c003f-e0a0-4aeb-9769-a3a2d3801385","l":"Reactor fuels & coolants","d":2,"p":"d9e32a6d-d42e-430a-9665-10aaf0776e26","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Reactor fuels are materials (typically enriched uranium, plutonium-bearing compounds, or fissile salts) whose nuclei undergo controlled fission to produce the heat and neutrons that sustain a nuclear chain reaction. Reactor coolants are fluids (e.g., light or heavy water, helium, liquid metals, or molten salts) circulated through the core to remove and transport heat, influence the neutron spectrum, and provide chemical and thermal stability for safe operation."},{"id":"6b8cec18-fe24-4997-9c52-0ec84c560462","l":"Reactor instrumentation","d":2,"p":"d9e32a6d-d42e-430a-9665-10aaf0776e26","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Reactor instrumentation comprises the sensors, signal-processing equipment, and control interfaces used to measure and monitor a nuclear reactor\u2019s neutronic, thermal-hydraulic, and radiological state, providing real-time data for operation, control and safety systems. Typical instruments include neutron detectors, thermocouples, pressure and flow sensors and radiation monitors together with the electronics and logic that drive regulation and protective actions."},{"id":"766cdcd9-f515-4656-afc1-7eae7766ec6c","l":"Real & complex analysis","d":2,"p":"b5ff1838-0ecd-41a5-a90b-bae4acb69fcf","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Real and complex analysis are branches of mathematical analysis: real analysis studies the rigorous behavior of real-valued functions of real variables (limits, continuity, differentiation, integration, sequences/series and measure theory), while complex analysis studies functions of a complex variable with special properties like holomorphicity, contour integration, residues and analytic continuation. Together they provide the foundational techniques used across physics for solving differential equations, evaluating integrals, and describing waves, fields and quantum systems."},{"id":"f9f3737e-faa1-4bb6-9a17-6e30c1b8e818","l":"Reduced magnetohydrodynamics","d":2,"p":"2747139a-722e-4167-a03a-e2671889b160","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Reduced magnetohydrodynamics (RMHD) is a simplified set of MHD equations used for strongly magnetized plasmas with a large, uniform \"guide\" field, obtained by ordering fluctuations that are small perpendicular to the field and vary slowly along it. It describes incompressible, low\u2011frequency perpendicular velocity and magnetic\u2011flux dynamics (dominantly shear\u2011Alfv\u00e9n behavior) while filtering out compressible and fast magnetosonic modes."},{"id":"2d9d8831-55f4-48d1-b48a-639618697882","l":"Reflectivity","d":2,"p":"a38dc017-3236-4084-afc5-eb59c5de51e0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Reflectivity is the fraction of incident electromagnetic power (or intensity) that a surface or interface reflects, a dimensionless quantity (often denoted R) between 0 and 1 that generally depends on wavelength, angle of incidence, polarization and surface properties. For a smooth interface and a plane wave, R equals the squared magnitude of the complex Fresnel amplitude reflection coefficient (R = |r|^2), while rough or scattering surfaces require separating specular and diffuse components."},{"id":"0957ffab-e896-4a67-926d-c02aa975e14e","l":"Regeneration","d":2,"p":"93b8baac-44de-4a26-a175-357ad9d8744c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Regeneration is the phenomenon in neutral-meson systems (notably neutral kaons) where a beam of the long-lived state (e.g., K_L) acquires a short-lived component (e.g., K_S) after passing through matter, producing a renewed coherent admixture. It arises because the particle and antiparticle flavor components scatter differently in the material, causing interference that reconstructs the short-lived eigenstate."},{"id":"3f06aca0-cecb-4a75-8063-7a7a79e35425","l":"Relativistic hydrodynamics","d":2,"p":"5512c79b-f40a-44ca-ae16-8ceef3dda771","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Relativistic hydrodynamics is the continuum theory of fluid and plasma flow when velocities, energies, or gravitational fields require special or general relativity, formulated by local conservation of the energy\u2013momentum tensor and any conserved currents together with Lorentz\u2011invariant constitutive relations. It generalizes nonrelativistic Navier\u2013Stokes to include relativistic kinematics, thermodynamics, and causal dissipative corrections, and is used in astrophysics and high\u2011energy nuclear physics."},{"id":"f9c1d5ee-5cb8-4c51-9eb5-952dd60bd072","l":"Relativistic magnetohydrodynamics","d":2,"p":"2747139a-722e-4167-a03a-e2671889b160","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Relativistic magnetohydrodynamics is the macroscopic theory that couples Maxwell\u2019s equations to relativistic fluid dynamics to describe the motion and electromagnetic fields of conducting fluids or plasmas when velocities, energies, or gravitational fields are relativistic. It generalizes ideal MHD using the relativistic energy\u2013momentum tensor (including fields and matter), Lorentz factors, and optionally curved spacetime, and is used to model systems such as jets, pulsar winds, and accretion flows."},{"id":"0046b7d2-677a-4782-b146-9c4cedb0a578","l":"Renner-Teller effect","d":2,"p":"2f9b8e36-83be-438b-83e8-0321500dfa28","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"The Renner\u2013Teller effect is a vibronic coupling phenomenon in linear polyatomic molecules where a degenerate electronic state (e.g., a \u03a0 state) is split and mixed by bending vibrations. It arises from nonadiabatic interaction between electronic orbital angular momentum and the vibrational (bending) motion, causing energy-level splitting and breakdown of the simple Born\u2013Oppenheimer separation."},{"id":"7275d884-8e4f-4312-96a7-f3583963f990","l":"Replica theory","d":2,"p":"695da376-c44a-41dd-b968-d200c31e9d5e","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems","Plasma Physics"],"def":"Replica theory is a method in statistical physics for treating quenched disorder by introducing n identical copies (\"replicas\") of a system, computing the disorder-averaged nth moment of the partition function, and using analytic continuation to the limit n \u2192 0 to obtain the disorder-averaged (quenched) free energy. It is widely used in studies of spin glasses and random optimization problems, where it can reveal phenomena such as replica symmetry breaking and many metastable states."},{"id":"a6da5248-3e96-4e1f-9be3-326072eaba3c","l":"Resonant piezoelectric spectroscopy","d":2,"p":"a38dc017-3236-4084-afc5-eb59c5de51e0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Resonant piezoelectric spectroscopy is an experimental technique that uses piezoelectric coupling to excite and detect a sample's mechanical resonances and measure their frequencies and linewidths. By monitoring shifts in resonance frequency and quality factor as a function of temperature, frequency, or field, it probes the sample's elastic moduli, internal friction (dissipation), and relaxation or phase-transition dynamics."},{"id":"25c16621-8ab8-460b-a0bf-2e9f779623c9","l":"Resonating valence bond theory","d":2,"p":"7299559c-15c4-47f3-b866-5633b1030453","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Resonating valence bond (RVB) theory describes a quantum state of interacting electrons in which neighboring electrons form singlet \"valence bonds\" and the ground state is a coherent superposition of many different valence-bond configurations, so the bonds continually \"resonate\" between arrangements. This yields a spin-liquid with no long-range magnetic order and, when doped with charge carriers, provides a proposed mechanism for unconventional (e.g., high-Tc) superconductivity and fractionalized excitations."},{"id":"3295017e-c1c7-447e-b7cb-6810fa1fcac7","l":"Resource theories","d":2,"p":"d599eade-2dc5-4572-84ac-54f7a980fbfb","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Resource theories are formal operational frameworks that define a set of allowed (free) operations and free states so that any state or operation outside that set is treated as a resource whose creation, interconversion, and consumption are studied under those constraints. They provide quantitative monotones and conversion rules used to analyze and compare resources such as entanglement, coherence, athermality, and reference frames in quantum information and thermodynamics."},{"id":"6c094599-92c9-40ba-8003-2abdaa37c05d","l":"Retina","d":2,"p":"fc65882e-b316-4cf1-a96a-ec8557624d80","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"The retina is the light-sensitive, multilayered neural tissue lining the back of the eye that receives the optical image formed by the cornea and lens. Its photoreceptors (rods and cones) convert incident photons into electrical signals that are processed by retinal circuits and sent to the brain via the optic nerve."},{"id":"1a6b9799-905c-4c1d-a8cc-d8c348eb6baf","l":"RF energy harvesting devices","d":2,"p":"3bab53f4-af17-4278-95a1-fa1b42b9231c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"RF energy harvesting devices capture ambient radio\u2011frequency electromagnetic waves and convert them into usable DC electrical power using an antenna (often resonant), impedance\u2011matching networks, a rectifier (a \"rectenna\") and power\u2011management or storage components. They provide small amounts of power to low\u2011power sensors and electronics, with available energy and efficiency set by transmit power, frequency, distance and regulatory limits."},{"id":"0dabd201-b49f-4261-8747-243f266ea9d2","l":"RF, microwave, & terahertz sources","d":2,"p":"a38dc017-3236-4084-afc5-eb59c5de51e0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"RF, microwave, and terahertz sources are devices that generate electromagnetic radiation in the radio\u2011frequency (~kHz\u2013MHz\u2013GHz), microwave (~300 MHz\u2013300 GHz) and terahertz (~0.1\u201310 THz) bands, producing continuous or pulsed, coherent or incoherent signals. They employ electronic, vacuum, or photonic mechanisms (oscillators, amplifiers, klystrons/magnetrons, Gunn diodes, frequency multipliers, photomixers, quantum\u2011cascade lasers, etc.) for applications in communication, sensing, imaging and spectroscopy."},{"id":"bb8d20b9-98b0-406c-8a12-dab644ed86cc","l":"Rigorous coupled-wave analysis","d":2,"p":"82f6c5fd-bbef-4ff9-a5f9-de62d3f5dfeb","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Rigorous coupled-wave analysis (RCWA), also called the Fourier modal method, is a frequency\u2011domain technique for solving Maxwell's equations in periodic (grating-like) structures by expanding the electromagnetic fields and the permittivity into Fourier harmonics and converting the problem into a set of coupled ordinary differential/eigenvalue equations for those harmonics. Solving these equations layer-by-layer with appropriate boundary conditions yields exact diffraction, reflection and transmission amplitudes within the accuracy set by the number of retained harmonics."},{"id":"24977262-0ec3-406f-88bf-5bc9fb700cca","l":"Ring polymers","d":2,"p":"2303d382-65bb-4b93-94b8-62a73502ec4b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Ring polymers (cyclic polymers) are macromolecules in which the monomer chain is joined end-to-end to form a closed loop with no free ends. Their cyclic topology and resulting topological constraints (knots and inter-ring linking) give them distinct conformations, dynamics, and rheological properties compared with linear polymers."},{"id":"d3d66936-fc59-4c19-a956-d58d21db7d5e","l":"River networks","d":2,"p":"6ec86497-6eda-4672-9845-336e089a5f69","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks","Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"River networks are branched networks of channels and tributaries that collect and route surface water from a drainage basin to an outlet. Their topology and geometry emerge from interacting processes of surface flow, sediment transport and erosion, and often show scaling laws, fractal-like self-similarity, and statistical network regularities (e.g., Horton\u2013Strahler ordering, Hack's law)."},{"id":"406540a6-00a4-4b58-9176-f415370d86be","l":"RKKY interaction","d":2,"p":"1f68adf2-72ef-4afa-8536-fd5094c2edd5","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The RKKY (Ruderman\u2013Kittel\u2013Kasuya\u2013Yosida) interaction is an indirect exchange coupling between localized magnetic moments in a metal mediated by conduction electrons: one moment spin-polarizes the electron sea, producing an oscillatory spin density that couples to another moment. In three dimensions the coupling falls off as ~cos(2k_F r)/r^3 (k_F the Fermi wavevector), so its sign alternates with distance and it can be ferromagnetic or antiferromagnetic."},{"id":"c55b9676-6832-44d2-a0ac-1817a6a2b352","l":"Road networks","d":2,"p":"ca680354-e15b-45ba-add1-7f966f2c0ae2","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Road networks are spatial graphs in which nodes represent intersections or terminals and edges represent road segments connecting them. Their topology and geometry determine traffic flow, congestion, routing and resilience, and they are analyzed using network theory, transport models, percolation, and dynamical/flow equations."},{"id":"11165b22-f80e-45b1-bc82-2a4a4008c1ea","l":"Rocks","d":2,"p":"4db2768f-9069-4cad-901f-a8001738ab66","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Rocks are naturally occurring solid aggregates of one or more minerals (and sometimes glass or organic material) that make up the crust of Earth and other planetary bodies. Their macroscopic physical behavior\u2014elasticity, strength, thermal conductivity, porosity and permeability\u2014is determined by mineral composition, grain size and shape, fabrics and fractures, and governs processes like deformation, seismic wave propagation, and heat transport."},{"id":"40fd3077-767b-403b-9a94-1b924553d541","l":"Rodlike polymer","d":2,"p":"f3ad2159-b3a9-4c96-bd88-6b789b7d6a37","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"A rodlike polymer is a polymer whose backbone is so stiff that it behaves like a straight, rigid rod over the length scales of interest\u2014its persistence length is comparable to or larger than its contour length. Such polymers show little bending flexibility and therefore have distinct orientational order, entropic elasticity, and rheological properties compared with flexible chains."},{"id":"f57ff7c7-0fbb-460b-9337-42bedf279576","l":"Rotation sensing","d":2,"p":"2d7a7b42-9db0-4027-8b95-43b276a198c0","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Rotation sensing is the measurement of angular motion\u2014changes in orientation, angular velocity, or accumulated rotation\u2014of an object or reference frame. It is performed by instruments (gyroscopes, optical or matter-wave interferometers, MEMS sensors) that detect rotation-induced effects such as Coriolis forces or Sagnac-phase shifts to provide inertial reference information."},{"id":"b5f4d103-166b-4ed8-85b5-90befd1927c3","l":"Rouse model","d":2,"p":"c267a3f3-0c34-4fc1-9825-947683caf4dd","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"The Rouse model is a coarse-grained dynamical model of an ideal polymer chain that represents the polymer as N beads connected by harmonic springs undergoing Brownian motion with viscous friction, while neglecting hydrodynamic interactions and excluded-volume effects. It yields Gaussian chain statistics and predicts internal normal modes with relaxation times \u03c4_p \u221d p^\u22122 and center-of-mass diffusion D \u221d 1/N for unentangled polymers."},{"id":"21b2f19f-e126-45ea-b221-c5a44ac62834","l":"rp process","d":2,"p":"29903cc6-9078-49fa-8c8f-9416eb5644fb","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics","Nuclear Physics"],"def":"The rp-process (rapid proton-capture process) is a nucleosynthesis pathway in explosive, proton-rich astrophysical environments (e.g., Type I X-ray bursts and novae) in which nuclei rapidly capture protons and undergo \u03b2+ (positron) decays to build heavier, proton-rich isotopes up to roughly A\u2248100. Its progress depends on high temperature and proton flux and is limited by slow \u03b2-decay \u201cwaiting points\u201d and photodisintegration cycles."},{"id":"7b743095-26c0-432e-9eab-45c856d4ce29","l":"r process","d":2,"p":"29903cc6-9078-49fa-8c8f-9416eb5644fb","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics","Nuclear Physics"],"def":"The r-process (rapid neutron-capture process) is a nucleosynthesis mechanism in which atomic nuclei capture neutrons so quickly that multiple captures occur before unstable nuclei can beta-decay, requiring extremely high neutron fluxes and short timescales. It operates in explosive, neutron-rich astrophysical environments (e.g., neutron-star mergers and some core-collapse supernovae) and produces the heaviest neutron-rich isotopes, including the lanthanides and actinides."},{"id":"64d463fb-f047-4356-ba40-dd1f450bfbba","l":"Rumor evolution","d":2,"p":"df25e539-cf94-40a1-a90f-53453eed4846","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Rumor evolution is the dynamical process by which a piece of information (a rumor) changes in content and prevalence as it spreads through a population, due to imperfect transmission, selective amplification, and forgetting. It is modeled using tools from statistical physics, network theory, and evolutionary dynamics to capture mutation, selection, and propagation on social networks."},{"id":"26257b12-2d08-44bf-8258-ba0afe00f3f8","l":"Rumor growth","d":2,"p":"df25e539-cf94-40a1-a90f-53453eed4846","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Rumor growth is the time evolution of the number of individuals who have heard or are transmitting a rumor, typically modeled by epidemic-like, branching-process, or network diffusion frameworks. It often shows an initial exponential increase followed by slowdown and saturation due to finite population size, stifling (loss of interest), or network-structure effects."},{"id":"55a182aa-5668-46c3-91b2-6cb90d84071e","l":"Rumor prevalence","d":2,"p":"df25e539-cf94-40a1-a90f-53453eed4846","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Rumor prevalence is the fraction (or density) of individuals in a population who at a given time are aware of and/or actively transmitting a rumor. In epidemic-style spreading models it is the instantaneous proportion of \"infected\" nodes (with cumulative prevalence referring to the total fraction ever informed)."},{"id":"714642d8-35fe-454b-8403-c3563f1e87bb","l":"Run-of-river hydroelectricity","d":2,"p":"4c12fcde-ed75-4e1c-bd19-253623703054","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Run-of-river hydroelectricity is a form of hydropower that converts the gravitational potential and kinetic energy of a river's natural flow and elevation drop directly into electricity by routing part or all of the stream through turbines and generators without a large impounding reservoir. Because it relies on instantaneous river discharge rather than stored water, its power output closely follows seasonal and short-term variations in flow and has limited ability to regulate supply."},{"id":"0c144fd8-34f5-4d95-b0f2-02a9fe6faf57","l":"Sachdev-Ye-Kitaev model","d":2,"p":"a088bad1-4904-4f08-a625-a9840fdf32c7","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Sachdev\u2013Ye\u2013Kitaev (SYK) model is a zero\u2011dimensional quantum model of N fermions (commonly Majorana) with random all\u2011to\u2011all q\u2011body interactions whose coupling constants are drawn from a Gaussian distribution and become solvable in the large\u2011N limit. Its low\u2011energy physics is a strongly correlated, non\u2011Fermi\u2011liquid state with emergent conformal symmetry, extensive ground\u2011state entropy, maximal quantum chaos, and a holographic connection to nearly\u2011AdS2/Schwarzian gravity."},{"id":"b05e45ee-0cfe-4b2d-83a8-eaf64578c13c","l":"Sagnac effect","d":2,"p":"2d7a7b42-9db0-4027-8b95-43b276a198c0","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"The Sagnac effect is the shift in interference phase (or difference in travel time) between two coherent waves sent in opposite directions around a closed loop on a rotating platform, caused by the rotation changing the effective path lengths. The phase shift is proportional to the loop area and the angular velocity and is the basis for ring-laser and fiber\u2011optic gyroscopes."},{"id":"54310559-5808-47cd-81fb-80a07180a065","l":"Sandpile models","d":2,"p":"3c88d04a-17da-443c-8428-781247d46a8a","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Sandpile models are simple lattice cellular automata for self\u2011organized criticality in which grains are added locally and sites that exceed a threshold topple, redistributing grains to neighbors and triggering cascades (avalanches). Despite their simple, local, often conservative rules (e.g., the Abelian/Bak\u2013Tang\u2013Wiesenfeld model), they evolve to a critical state with scale\u2011invariant avalanche statistics and power\u2011law distributions without tuning parameters."},{"id":"bb0a871d-bf5c-44db-be68-9bd8ed4febe9","l":"Scanning probe microscopy","d":2,"p":"a0889633-1e24-48ce-80d9-98b0139e10cb","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Scanning probe microscopy is a family of techniques that obtain images and local measurements of surfaces at nanometer-to-atomic resolution by raster-scanning a sharp physical probe (tip) close to the sample and monitoring tip\u2013sample interactions (e.g., tunneling current, forces, magnetic or thermal signals). Examples include scanning tunneling microscopy (STM) and atomic force microscopy (AFM)."},{"id":"c33a7211-ebcd-4336-917d-a75ecb1e50c4","l":"Scanning tunneling microscopy","d":2,"p":"a0889633-1e24-48ce-80d9-98b0139e10cb","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Scanning tunneling microscopy (STM) is a scanning-probe technique that images and probes conductive surfaces with atomic resolution by rastering an ultra-sharp conducting tip a few \u00e5ngstr\u00f6ms above the sample and measuring the quantum tunneling current between tip and surface. Because the tunneling current depends exponentially on tip\u2013sample separation and on the local electronic density of states, STM maps surface topography and electronic structure and can perform local spectroscopy and atom manipulation."},{"id":"d6476a85-9182-4287-90e8-9f22b6ea045e","l":"Scanning tunneling spectroscopy","d":2,"p":"a0889633-1e24-48ce-80d9-98b0139e10cb","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Scanning tunneling spectroscopy (STS) is an extension of scanning tunneling microscopy in which the tunneling current I(V) is measured as a function of tip\u2013sample bias and position and its derivative dI/dV is used to probe the local electronic density of states of a surface with atomic-scale spatial and meV-scale energy resolution. Under standard tunneling approximations (Tersoff\u2013Hamann) the dI/dV signal is approximately proportional to the sample's local density of states, enabling mapping of surface states, band edges, and quasiparticle excitations."},{"id":"b25e61da-2d24-40c9-8075-2ba3a874f11a","l":"Schottky barriers","d":2,"p":"f495cfcf-afde-4cee-8daa-3ffffaa0e3a6","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A Schottky barrier is the energy barrier that forms at a metal\u2013semiconductor contact when their Fermi levels equilibrate, creating a depletion region that impedes carrier flow and yields a rectifying (diode-like) interface. Its effective height\u2014ideally set by the metal work function and semiconductor electron affinity but often modified by interface states and Fermi-level pinning\u2014controls current transport mechanisms such as thermionic emission."},{"id":"645b6143-f550-4fbd-9800-3a1da372088f","l":"Schroedinger equation","d":2,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"The Schr\u00f6dinger equation is the fundamental linear partial differential equation of nonrelativistic quantum mechanics that determines how a system\u2019s wavefunction\u2014whose squared magnitude gives probability densities\u2014evolves in time. In its time-dependent form it reads i\u0127 \u2202\u03c8/\u2202t = \u0124\u03c8, while stationary states satisfy the time-independent eigenvalue equation \u0124\u03c8 = E\u03c8, which yields quantized energy levels."},{"id":"9d47badb-073f-41a9-91ce-ea87843908ad","l":"Schwinger boson method","d":2,"p":"ba55d8a8-d049-4550-b264-530f74618214","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Schwinger boson method represents a spin-S operator at each site by two boson species a and b via S^+ = a\u2020b, S^- = b\u2020a, S^z = (a\u2020a \u2212 b\u2020b)/2 together with the local constraint a\u2020a + b\u2020b = 2S. This bosonic mapping is used to construct mean-field and large\u2011N treatments of quantum magnets, valence-bond states, and spin-liquid behavior."},{"id":"e9746ace-f51c-4982-9db7-6843c9284205","l":"Scintillators","d":2,"p":"be8bd934-5bc9-4a6d-ae73-f4753ce5c128","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Scintillators are materials that emit brief pulses of visible or ultraviolet light when ionizing radiation or energetic particles deposit energy in them, converting that energy into photons via excitation and radiative de-excitation of luminescent centers. They are used in radiation detectors by coupling the light output to photodetectors (e.g., photomultiplier tubes or photodiodes) to measure particle presence, energy, and timing."},{"id":"74a28d17-3b2e-4adc-ba94-b48f168d3642","l":"Second order nonlinear optical processes","d":2,"p":"bd03ea46-2406-4a24-8307-05b2b498fb4e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Second-order nonlinear optical processes are light\u2013matter interactions in which the medium's polarization contains a term quadratic in the electric field, P^(2)=\u03b50 \u03c7^(2):EE, so the material mixes optical frequencies and generates new ones (e.g., second-harmonic generation, sum- and difference-frequency generation, optical rectification, and parametric amplification). These effects require a noncentrosymmetric medium (\u03c7^(2) \u2260 0) and efficient conversion typically depends on phase-matching (momentum conservation) as well as energy conservation."},{"id":"f4517494-264d-46f7-bdcb-4921ec22589f","l":"Second order phase transitions","d":2,"p":"d2c655da-6260-45e7-bc14-26fba976f03b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A second-order (or continuous) phase transition is a thermodynamic change in which the order parameter varies continuously and no latent heat is released, so first derivatives of the free energy (e.g., entropy, volume) remain continuous while second derivatives (e.g., heat capacity, susceptibility, compressibility) become singular or diverge. Such transitions occur at a critical point and are characterized by a diverging correlation length and universal critical exponents."},{"id":"ed257437-ebc5-4a6a-949a-7f246a9ecb60","l":"Second quantization","d":2,"p":"05a47dcc-71c0-4ebc-9d4e-79a40a191efb","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Second quantization is a formalism for many\u2011body quantum systems that promotes single\u2011particle wavefunctions or classical fields to operator\u2011valued creation and annihilation operators acting on Fock space, so states are described by occupation numbers rather than individual particle coordinates. It naturally enforces bosonic or fermionic statistics via commutation or anticommutation relations and greatly simplifies the treatment of interactions and processes with variable particle number."},{"id":"0512c42d-e057-4c4a-a38a-1034cd29163b","l":"Second sound","d":2,"p":"95b916ee-a0ff-4079-9e14-69d436d0e6db","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Second sound is a propagating wave of temperature or entropy in which heat is transported ballistically like an elastic wave rather than by diffusion. It occurs in systems describable by two-fluid hydrodynamics\u2014most famously superfluid helium II and some low-temperature crystals\u2014where opposing flow of the normal and superfluid (or phonon) components carries the oscillating entropy."},{"id":"38a251c2-8390-4bb8-904e-43c5f76729b6","l":"Seebeck effect","d":2,"p":"6e14e903-9322-41db-b946-0b518ecafdf7","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Seebeck effect is the generation of an electric voltage across a conductor or between two different conductors when their ends are held at different temperatures. It arises from thermally driven diffusion of charge carriers and is quantified by the Seebeck coefficient S = \u2212\u0394V/\u0394T."},{"id":"c13cb991-9d42-41c4-a93c-9df6d15efc09","l":"Self-consistent field theory","d":2,"p":"c267a3f3-0c34-4fc1-9825-947683caf4dd","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Self-consistent field theory is a mean-field method in which the effective potential or field experienced by particles depends on the particle density or wavefunction it produces, and is determined by iteratively adjusting the field until the field and the resulting distribution are mutually consistent. It underlies approaches such as Hartree\u2013Fock/Kohn\u2013Sham methods in quantum many-body theory and analogous field-theory formulations for polymers and soft condensed matter."},{"id":"6fd2ade8-8feb-450a-9a53-4bc85f433903","l":"Self-diffusion","d":2,"p":"96cf4e19-746a-4d9b-b2ef-b76773008bbc","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Self-diffusion is the spontaneous, thermally driven motion of identical particles within a homogeneous material, describing how individual atoms or molecules exchange places in the absence of a concentration gradient. It is characterized by a self-diffusion coefficient, which can be measured from the particles' mean-square displacement or from the velocity-autocorrelation (Green\u2013Kubo) integral."},{"id":"a9f5a206-d10c-443c-949e-27cb40de3135","l":"Self-focusing","d":2,"p":"bd03ea46-2406-4a24-8307-05b2b498fb4e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Self-focusing is a nonlinear effect in which an intense electromagnetic beam changes the medium's refractive index (for example via the intensity-dependent Kerr effect), so the medium acts like a lens that counteracts diffraction and narrows the beam. If the beam power exceeds a medium-dependent critical value this focusing can lead to collapse or to sustained filamentation; other mechanisms (thermal, plasma/relativistic) can produce similar behavior."},{"id":"be41aa06-017b-4e12-9a11-eac4cae44acf","l":"Self-focusing & filamentation in plasmas","d":2,"p":"2917e3bd-b25c-4e89-8796-5c7e77dfda5a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Self-focusing in plasmas is the nonlinear narrowing of an intense electromagnetic beam as the beam itself alters the plasma refractive index\u2014primarily through relativistic electron mass increase and/or ponderomotive expulsion of electrons\u2014so the beam is guided or collapses to a smaller spot. Filamentation is the transverse breakup of that beam into multiple narrow, high-intensity channels (filaments), driven by modulational and transverse instabilities and often sustained by self-generated plasma density depressions."},{"id":"39be65ae-f60a-4b4a-8a97-454ac827d044","l":"Self-healing","d":2,"p":"b7ae9785-f32b-4c42-a5f3-727b6f30a208","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Self-healing is the intrinsic ability of a physical system\u2014such as a material, fluid, or wavefield\u2014to restore its original structure or function after local damage or perturbation, using internal dynamics or built-in repair mechanisms without external intervention."},{"id":"36e1d549-9909-4881-a2d8-0a501a300a26","l":"Self-propelled particles","d":2,"p":"ba8221e8-b186-4db2-abb4-bcb0cc7f5b9d","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Self-propelled particles are particles that convert internal or ambient energy into sustained directed motion, producing persistent active trajectories. As fundamental units of active matter they operate out of thermal equilibrium and can exhibit collective behaviors such as swarming, clustering, and spontaneous flow."},{"id":"1a3a4756-6041-42f7-ab5e-5010d8e2c456","l":"Semiconductor Bloch equations","d":2,"p":"3123cbf9-460d-467a-9306-b4fb316daddd","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"The semiconductor Bloch equations are a set of coupled quantum kinetic equations that describe the time evolution of carrier distributions and interband polarizations in a semiconductor, extending the optical Bloch equations to many-particle solid-state systems. They include band structure, light\u2013matter coupling, carrier\u2013carrier Coulomb interactions and scattering, and are used to model coherent optical response, exciton dynamics, and ultrafast carrier relaxation."},{"id":"e25a5c89-6132-4bbd-8efb-4a426458c900","l":"Semiconductor compounds","d":2,"p":"12721ec5-11d3-484a-9ea9-a7bea1986722","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Semiconductor compounds are crystalline materials made from two or more elements whose electronic band structure includes a band gap that enables controlled electrical conductivity. Their chemical composition and crystal structure (e.g., III\u2013V, II\u2013VI, IV\u2013IV families such as GaAs, CdTe, SiC) determine band gap, carrier mobility and optical properties, which are exploited in electronic and optoelectronic devices."},{"id":"dcd3f376-bd39-4f17-bb33-56c96dd943e6","l":"Semiconductor microcavities","d":2,"p":"dcf48409-af3f-4517-b58d-7ec6c95351f3","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Semiconductor microcavities are optical resonators built in semiconductor heterostructures that confine photons to a small volume using mirrors or index contrast (e.g., distributed Bragg reflectors or etched cavities). By strongly enhancing the interaction between confined photons and electronic excitations (excitons, quantum-well or quantum-dot transitions), they enable modified spontaneous emission, cavity quantum-electrodynamics effects and hybrid light\u2013matter quasiparticles such as exciton\u2013polaritons."},{"id":"b906be48-c729-48b7-ac18-dfaf8c7901de","l":"Semiconductor quantum optics","d":2,"p":"632f74d1-d16b-4b92-a295-b24729a93d8d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Semiconductor quantum optics is the study of quantum light\u2013matter interactions in semiconductor materials and nanostructures (quantum wells, wires, dots), where quantum confinement and electronic excitations (excitons, electron\u2013hole pairs) couple to quantized electromagnetic fields. It encompasses phenomena and technologies such as cavity quantum electrodynamics, exciton\u2013polaritons, single-photon sources, quantum coherence and entanglement, and nonlinear quantum optical effects in semiconductors."},{"id":"3f343ffa-b300-4a03-a6a9-9a76421fa262","l":"Sequencing analysis","d":2,"p":"06acf1f4-0f88-4c17-bc06-52d0e4a3dfbd","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Sequencing analysis is the application of statistical and computational methods to ordered data (time series, event logs, or symbolic strings) to identify patterns, correlations, state transitions, and underlying dynamics. It encompasses tools such as autocorrelation and spectral methods, hidden Markov models, information-theoretic measures, and sequence-alignment techniques to infer structure and generating processes."},{"id":"a6758855-6e21-49d9-851d-3c18fa579e85","l":"Shape coexistence","d":2,"p":"f7596119-caf1-4d2d-9f87-7a727621e77c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Shape coexistence is the phenomenon in atomic nuclei where two or more distinct intrinsic shapes (e.g., spherical, prolate, oblate, or triaxial) exist at nearly the same energy in a single nucleus, giving rise to competing low-lying configurations and characteristic excited 0+ states and rotational bands. It results from competing shell effects and many-body correlations that create multiple minima in the nuclear potential-energy surface and can be identified by spectroscopy (e.g., E0 transitions, band structures)."},{"id":"d989b751-363d-49a0-bcc8-5d7304c77187","l":"Shape development","d":2,"p":"f7596119-caf1-4d2d-9f87-7a727621e77c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Shape development is the time evolution of an object's geometry driven by physical, chemical, or biological mechanisms. It encompasses processes such as growth, deformation, self\u2011assembly, phase change and pattern formation that reshape bodies via forces, material transport, reactions, or instabilities."},{"id":"a335b762-2f79-434e-9b50-42bdf4d91b52","l":"Shear deformation","d":2,"p":"77d491f8-55ad-47a6-a0a7-cd24ffce7602","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Shear deformation is the change of shape in a material caused by forces acting parallel to a surface, which make adjacent layers slide past one another so originally right angles become oblique. It is characterized by the shear strain (usually denoted \u03b3), the relative lateral displacement per unit thickness, and is produced by an applied shear stress."},{"id":"9fffec69-8e96-42d0-b6bf-42d1234858eb","l":"Shift current","d":2,"p":"57934ca6-f76c-4b88-9513-12b414e4e105","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Shift current is a DC photocurrent produced in noncentrosymmetric crystals when interband optical excitation causes a coherent real-space shift of electronic wavepackets, giving a second-order (\u03c7^(2)) bulk photovoltaic response. It originates from band-to-band transition matrix elements and Berry-connection (phase) differences, and therefore does not require p\u2013n junctions or built-in electric fields."},{"id":"1ae82140-c94a-46bb-8274-ad7311e44112","l":"Shock waves & discontinuities in plasma","d":2,"p":"9bbcc3c5-16bb-4a7f-adea-c23acc893cde","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Shock waves in a plasma are nonlinear propagating fronts across which density, pressure, flow speed and magnetic field change abruptly on scales of the mean free path or gyro-radius, converting bulk kinetic energy into heat and often accelerating particles via dissipative or kinetic processes. Discontinuities are ideal-MHD jump surfaces (e.g., contact, tangential, rotational) that permit abrupt changes in some quantities without net dissipation, with the allowed jumps determined by the Rankine\u2013Hugoniot relations."},{"id":"894b4f64-f17f-418b-ba2f-5caf090b896b","l":"Shot noise","d":2,"p":"38e21fa2-d4a0-4206-a623-ab3eb9ba82a8","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Shot noise is the fundamental noise arising from the discrete, random arrival of charge carriers (or photons) that produces fluctuations in a steady current or count rate. For uncorrelated (Poisson) arrivals it is \"white\" and has current spectral density S_I = 2 e I (more generally S = 2 q I), with deviations when correlations (Fano factor \u22601) are present."},{"id":"c1059a78-47d7-4f70-9c3a-c40e32acf475","l":"Shubnikov-de Haas effect","d":2,"p":"3e0549b8-0105-4383-84a3-ba1f47652af0","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Shubnikov\u2013de Haas effect is the appearance of oscillations in a material's electrical resistivity (magnetoresistance) as a function of applied magnetic field, caused by Landau quantization of the electronic energy levels and the resulting periodic modulation of the density of states at the Fermi level. These quantum oscillations, periodic in 1/B and seen at low temperatures and high fields, are used to extract Fermi-surface parameters such as effective mass, carrier density, and scattering rates."},{"id":"5235df37-8a92-4148-af3c-27923efd3e8e","l":"Signatures with bottom quarks","d":2,"p":"66ce7b40-3091-4fb4-a468-0170ee73cbed","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Signatures with bottom quarks are experimental final-state patterns that indicate the production of b quarks, typically seen as b\u2011tagged jets, displaced secondary vertices, and leptons from semileptonic b\u2011hadron decays. Because b hadrons have a relatively long lifetime and large mass, these distinctive features are used to identify b quarks for precision Standard Model measurements and searches for new physics."},{"id":"97321d61-17ac-431e-8229-649693390d49","l":"Signatures with Higgs bosons","d":2,"p":"66ce7b40-3091-4fb4-a468-0170ee73cbed","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Signatures with Higgs bosons are the experimentally observable final states and kinematic patterns produced when one or more Higgs bosons are created and decay in a detector \u2014 for example a diphoton invariant-mass peak, b\u2011quark pairs, four-lepton resonances, associated vector bosons or jets, missing transverse energy, or displaced vertices. These signatures are used to identify Higgs production modes, measure its couplings and properties, and to search for deviations from the Standard Model."},{"id":"6e8f7daa-032b-4f15-9731-2eba67d1bb55","l":"Signatures with jets","d":2,"p":"66ce7b40-3091-4fb4-a468-0170ee73cbed","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Event topologies in collider experiments that include one or more reconstructed hadronic jets\u2014collimated sprays of particles produced by the fragmentation of energetic quarks or gluons. Such \"signatures with jets\" are used to identify QCD processes and to search for or characterize Standard Model and beyond-Standard-Model phenomena (e.g., jets + missing transverse energy or jets + leptons)."},{"id":"b317da66-be0c-4388-b052-6654a38445dd","l":"Signatures with light leptons","d":2,"p":"66ce7b40-3091-4fb4-a468-0170ee73cbed","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Signatures with light leptons are experimental event topologies that include electrons and muons (and often their associated neutrinos) among the final-state particles; these charged leptons are relatively easy to identify in detectors and thus provide clean probes of Standard Model processes and potential new physics. They are characterized by lepton multiplicity, kinematic variables (e.g., momentum and invariant mass), isolation criteria, and often missing transverse energy when neutrinos are produced."},{"id":"db0f67df-75a2-4506-9a17-417515f72862","l":"Signatures with missing energy","d":2,"p":"66ce7b40-3091-4fb4-a468-0170ee73cbed","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Signatures with missing energy are experimental observations in which the vector sum of the measured final-state energy or momentum (typically transverse momentum in colliders) is nonzero, indicating one or more invisible or undetected particles\u2014such as neutrinos or dark-matter candidates\u2014carried away energy. They are inferred from momentum/energy imbalance in a detector and are a primary tool in searches for weakly interacting or invisible particles."},{"id":"9b86dcd5-fc4b-4b3f-80f8-676ca9d1168b","l":"Signatures with new bosons","d":2,"p":"66ce7b40-3091-4fb4-a468-0170ee73cbed","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Signatures with new bosons are experimental observables indicating the existence of additional bosonic particles (e.g., new scalars, vectors like Z' or dark photons, or other force carriers) beyond the Standard Model. They typically appear as resonance peaks or excesses in invariant-mass or cross-section measurements, anomalous angular or kinematic distributions, displaced vertices from long-lived decays, or missing-energy signals when the new boson decays invisibly."},{"id":"e685d0d5-ea5f-4a78-9cce-d79c241b979b","l":"Signatures with new fermions","d":2,"p":"66ce7b40-3091-4fb4-a468-0170ee73cbed","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Signatures with new fermions are the observable experimental effects produced when hypothetical fermionic particles beyond the Standard Model (e.g., heavy neutral leptons, vector-like quarks, or dark-sector fermions) are produced or mediate interactions. These signatures include distinctive collider features (resonant bumps, displaced vertices, long-lived charged tracks, or unusual missing transverse energy), altered precision measurements, and novel astrophysical or direct-detection signals."},{"id":"cef5ef6c-1ad6-4153-8dfd-c333a763d16c","l":"Signatures with photons","d":2,"p":"66ce7b40-3091-4fb4-a468-0170ee73cbed","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Signatures with photons are experimental observables in which one or more detected photons provide a characteristic signal of an underlying physical process, used to identify and study particle decays, interactions, or astrophysical sources. They are classified by photon multiplicity and kinematics (e.g., single-photon, diphoton, photon+jets, photon+missing-energy) and by features such as energy, direction, timing, and isolation."},{"id":"4563d5a7-3390-4bc6-b78e-8246d818b9bd","l":"Signatures with specific particles","d":2,"p":"66ce7b40-3091-4fb4-a468-0170ee73cbed","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"An experimental signature with specific particles is an identifiable pattern of detected final-state particles \u2014 for example electrons, muons, photons, jets, or missing energy \u2014 together with their kinematic properties that characterizes a class of events in a detector. Such signatures are used to select and classify events, separate signal from background, and search for or measure underlying physical processes."},{"id":"8cc860c5-f9d8-4541-8753-d0033eb084b8","l":"Signatures with tau leptons","d":2,"p":"66ce7b40-3091-4fb4-a468-0170ee73cbed","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Signatures with tau leptons are experimental final states in particle collisions that include one or more tau leptons, identified either via their leptonic decays (an electron or muon plus neutrinos) or hadronic decays (narrow, low-track-multiplicity jets plus neutrinos). Because the tau's short lifetime and neutrino-rich decays produce missing energy and sometimes displaced vertices, these distinctive but challenging signatures are used to study Standard Model processes (e.g., Higgs \u2192 \u03c4\u03c4, electroweak tests) and to search for new physics."},{"id":"8026a205-fcbc-40e0-93a6-725a0e19f591","l":"Signatures with top quarks","d":2,"p":"66ce7b40-3091-4fb4-a468-0170ee73cbed","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Signatures with top quarks are characteristic experimental patterns from the production and decay of top quarks in high-energy collisions, typically identified by b\u2011tagged jets, energetic leptons or hadronic jets, missing transverse energy, and invariant-mass combinations that reconstruct the top mass. They are used to measure top\u2011quark properties and to search for deviations from the Standard Model or for new particles that couple to or decay into top quarks."},{"id":"9e4f4969-3810-4818-b121-ab7b02e87abc","l":"Signatures with W or Z bosons","d":2,"p":"66ce7b40-3091-4fb4-a468-0170ee73cbed","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Signatures with W or Z bosons are experimental event patterns in particle collisions where a W\u00b1 or Z0 boson is reconstructed from its decay products\u2014for example, a charged lepton plus missing transverse energy for W\u2192\u2113\u03bd, or an opposite-sign same-flavor lepton pair with invariant mass near the Z for Z\u2192\u2113+\u2113\u2212. These signatures (including hadronic decays to jets) are used both to test Standard Model predictions and as clean channels to search for new particles or interactions."},{"id":"316326db-9f0b-42cd-ae41-3a580c6a5ec9","l":"Simple cubic","d":2,"p":"6af5be88-489f-4a50-9abf-998e8912254a","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Simple cubic (SC) is the primitive cubic crystal lattice with lattice points at the eight corners of a cube; each unit cell effectively contains one atom, has coordination number 6, and a packing efficiency of about 52.4%."},{"id":"5a3e040e-ef6f-4ae6-b8e9-39da105b9446","l":"Simulated annealing","d":2,"p":"eb9bd2e1-eedd-4bd0-997d-58b44ffa3ebb","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics","Networks","Particles & Fields","Physics of Living Systems","Plasma Physics","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Simulated annealing is a stochastic optimization algorithm that mimics thermal annealing: it explores configuration space via random trial moves accepted with Boltzmann-like probability exp(\u2212\u0394E/(kB T)) so that at high \"temperature\" uphill moves can escape local minima and as T is slowly lowered the system converges toward a global minimum of the objective (energy or cost) function."},{"id":"8428d57b-6044-43d6-962c-538be583a26c","l":"Single-electron devices","d":2,"p":"2360e96e-a12b-4d21-9b65-36ed1177c0e6","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Single-electron devices are electronic components that control and detect the transfer of individual electrons by exploiting Coulomb blockade and single-electron tunneling through small-capacitance islands separated by tunnel barriers (examples include the single-electron transistor and charge pump). They function when the island's charging energy exceeds thermal energy (kBT), typically requiring low temperatures or very small capacitances, and are used in precision metrology, ultra-low-power electronics, and quantum devices."},{"id":"c33b545a-458f-4340-98af-08d61af6681e","l":"Single impurity model","d":2,"p":"a088bad1-4904-4f08-a625-a9840fdf32c7","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A single impurity model describes a single localized degree of freedom (e.g., an atomic orbital or a localized spin) coupled to a continuum of host electrons or other excitations. It is used to study impurity-induced scattering, local spectral properties, and many-body phenomena such as Kondo screening and Anderson impurity physics."},{"id":"14b70282-fde1-45bd-bb02-bd1249443b06","l":"Single-molecule magnets","d":2,"p":"3f3d275f-3133-4b36-990a-59ca878fa208","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Single-molecule magnets (SMMs) are individual molecules that behave as discrete nanomagnets, showing magnetic bistability and slow relaxation (hysteresis) below a blocking temperature due to a large spin ground state and strong magnetic anisotropy. They are typically transition-metal or lanthanide clusters and can exhibit quantum tunneling of magnetization."},{"id":"283378ff-c379-4fec-99c5-66a8bc5692db","l":"Single-photon detectors","d":2,"p":"a38dc017-3236-4084-afc5-eb59c5de51e0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Single-photon detectors are devices that register the absorption of individual photons by converting each photon into a discrete electrical (or thermal) signal, enabling counting and precise timing of single-photon events. Common implementations include photomultiplier tubes, avalanche photodiodes, superconducting nanowire detectors, and transition-edge sensors, and they are characterized by quantum efficiency, dark-count rate, timing jitter, dead time, and (for some) photon-number resolution."},{"id":"26ef5ca9-b059-4dbd-9ef3-00c8944a7e47","l":"Single photon sources","d":2,"p":"632f74d1-d16b-4b92-a295-b24729a93d8d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Single-photon sources are devices or quantum emitters engineered to produce light pulses containing one\u2014and only one\u2014photon at a time, either on-demand or probabilistically with a herald signal, exhibiting antibunched (sub-Poissonian) photon statistics. They are fundamental resources for quantum communication, computing, sensing and tests of quantum optics."},{"id":"ac15776a-3741-4dfb-baad-ee2918f8e6e6","l":"Singularities In general relativity","d":2,"p":"6287ac05-d956-4c3c-9882-8ea7a798f964","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"In general relativity, a singularity is a region or boundary of spacetime where the classical description fails \u2014 typically signaled either by geodesic incompleteness (timelike or null geodesics end after finite proper time) or by the divergence of curvature invariants (e.g., the Kretschmann scalar). Such behavior, as in black-hole interiors or the Big Bang, indicates a breakdown of GR and the need for a quantum-gravity description."},{"id":"d459260e-f76f-4982-aefd-d42e619b5c14","l":"Sintering","d":2,"p":"c4ed35b0-1090-409c-9667-85f9ac03e9c0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Sintering is the thermally activated consolidation of powdered or particulate materials in which heating (usually below the bulk melting point) drives mass transport\u2014by diffusion, viscous flow, or evaporation\u2013condensation\u2014so particles bond, reduce surface area, and densify into a coherent solid. It is driven by reduction of surface and interface energies and is widely used in ceramics, metallurgy, and powder-based manufacturing."},{"id":"e56ddca7-4557-4d64-a77d-286fffb72afc","l":"Skyrmions","d":2,"p":"1bd1a9ab-ec4f-40a8-9f81-9f1005587d58","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Skyrmions are topological solitons\u2014stable, localized field configurations carrying an integer-valued winding (topological) charge that measures how the order-parameter field wraps its target space. In condensed-matter physics they commonly appear as nanoscale, vortex-like magnetic spin textures stabilized by chiral interactions (e.g., Dzyaloshinskii\u2013Moriya) and are robust against continuous deformations and current-driven motion."},{"id":"93a1876e-55bc-446b-ad63-8e356e642d43","l":"Slave bosons","d":2,"p":"05a47dcc-71c0-4ebc-9d4e-79a40a191efb","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Slave bosons are auxiliary bosonic fields introduced in theoretical treatments of strongly correlated electron systems to enforce local constraints (for example, no double occupancy) by factorizing an electron operator into a fermionic part (spinon) and a bosonic \"slave\" field. This representation enables mean-field and large-N approximations of models like the Hubbard and Anderson lattice but introduces a gauge redundancy and unphysical states that must be projected out."},{"id":"86fe21d2-55da-4e10-b71b-5129f58fd130","l":"Small-world model","d":2,"p":"6f3f5cf1-363e-4d04-ac63-ca4ae90b47fc","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"A small-world model is a network model (most commonly the Watts\u2013Strogatz model) obtained by randomly rewiring a fraction of edges in a regular lattice, producing graphs that combine high local clustering with short average path lengths (scaling logarithmically with system size). It captures the \"six degrees of separation\" property seen in many real-world social, biological, and technological networks."},{"id":"ef41b9c0-c5a2-4728-9a87-d114abfb3659","l":"Smart materials","d":2,"p":"da2cf824-a50d-4c47-a974-efa09b98c860","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Smart materials are materials that sense and respond reversibly to external stimuli (temperature, stress, electric or magnetic fields, light, chemical environment) by changing one or more physical properties, enabling sensing, actuation, or adaptive behavior. Examples include piezoelectrics, shape\u2011memory alloys, magnetostrictive and electrochromic materials, which convert specific inputs into measurable or functional outputs."},{"id":"dc7de380-7398-4003-b1ae-2dfd7b5ff77a","l":"S-matrix method in transport","d":2,"p":"f4585613-4c82-4c78-a57d-515fa4774cb6","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The S-matrix method in transport is a scattering formalism that relates asymptotic incoming and outgoing wave amplitudes through a scattering matrix S, whose elements are the transmission and reflection amplitudes for each channel.  From these amplitudes one obtains transmission probabilities and hence transport observables (currents, electrical or thermal conductance via the Landauer\u2013B\u00fcttiker type relations), naturally accounting for multichannel quantum interference and phase coherence."},{"id":"0b4849cd-d5f0-402c-8d7f-8a5c9171456f","l":"Social system dynamics","d":2,"p":"dc706191-bd70-4d20-8944-0217b12cd7d9","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Social system dynamics studies how interacting individuals, groups, and institutions evolve over time to produce collective patterns and macroscopic behavior. It uses tools from dynamical systems, statistical physics and network theory to model processes such as opinion formation, contagion, cooperation, and systemic transitions driven by nonlinear interactions and multiscale feedback."},{"id":"f8f5813f-fe1f-4354-a65c-f8a5ecafb6bd","l":"Sodium chloride structure","d":2,"p":"6af5be88-489f-4a50-9abf-998e8912254a","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The sodium chloride (rock-salt) structure is a cubic ionic crystal in which one ion species (e.g., Cl\u2212) occupies a face-centered cubic lattice and the opposite ions (Na+) fill all octahedral sites, so every ion has six nearest neighbors of opposite charge (coordination number 6) and the lattice belongs to space group Fm-3m."},{"id":"86bccae1-413d-4f0c-9e35-7c6c31519cc5","l":"Soft magnets","d":2,"p":"dadd0f02-6a35-463b-997f-67d64338d639","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Soft magnets are ferromagnetic materials with low coercivity and high permeability, so they can be magnetized and demagnetized easily by small applied fields. They exhibit low remanence and hysteresis losses, making them suitable for transformer cores, inductors and other AC magnetic applications."},{"id":"580109ed-a8ed-4964-898c-80fedc129ae0","l":"Solar concentrators","d":2,"p":"d2b62966-4eb1-4cbd-8ea1-4b34ea070e52","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Solar concentrators are optical systems\u2014using mirrors, lenses, or arrays thereof\u2014that collect sunlight over a large aperture and focus it onto a much smaller receiver, thereby increasing the local solar irradiance (the concentration ratio). They are used to raise working temperatures or power density for thermal engines or photovoltaic cells and typically employ sun-tracking to maintain high concentration."},{"id":"e18327a4-0daf-47fd-9dcb-e88e2f05a2af","l":"Solar corona","d":2,"p":"f712db32-2bc9-4e97-9f42-a16c12832ff8","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"The solar corona is the Sun's outermost atmospheric layer \u2014 an extended, low-density ionized plasma that stretches millions of kilometers into space and is visible as a faint halo during total solar eclipses. Its temperature (\u223c1\u20133 million K or higher) far exceeds the underlying photosphere, is shaped by the Sun's magnetic field, and is the source of the solar wind and strong UV/X\u2011ray emission."},{"id":"9eeeb146-30a5-4fcf-a7c4-1f9f2389a8c0","l":"Solar cycles","d":2,"p":"f712db32-2bc9-4e97-9f42-a16c12832ff8","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Solar cycles are the roughly 11-year quasi-periodic variations in the Sun\u2019s magnetic activity\u2014manifested by changes in sunspot number, flares, coronal mass ejections, and small changes in irradiance\u2014whose progression culminates in a reversal of the Sun\u2019s global magnetic polarity. These cycles arise from the solar dynamo, the interaction of differential rotation and convective motions that amplify and reorganize magnetic fields (the full magnetic or Hale cycle spans about 22 years)."},{"id":"a28df2a4-cd81-4c29-864d-227baab328e4","l":"Solar energy harvesting devices","d":2,"p":"3bab53f4-af17-4278-95a1-fa1b42b9231c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Solar energy harvesting devices are engineered systems that capture incident solar radiation and convert it into usable energy\u2014most commonly electrical power via the photovoltaic effect, thermal energy via solar-thermal collectors, or chemical fuels via photoelectrochemical reactions. They typically combine an optical absorber/collector with a conversion mechanism and often include energy storage or power-conditioning components."},{"id":"ad2f244c-6755-4f69-a355-4171b9d2dc5e","l":"Solar flares","d":2,"p":"f712db32-2bc9-4e97-9f42-a16c12832ff8","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Solar flares are sudden, intense releases of magnetic energy in the Sun\u2019s atmosphere\u2014driven by magnetic reconnection in active regions\u2014that produce rapid bursts of electromagnetic radiation across the spectrum and accelerate energetic particles. They typically last minutes to hours and can disturb the heliosphere and Earth's space weather."},{"id":"de25c76f-9242-441c-945d-6aa09665ed94","l":"Solar interior","d":2,"p":"f712db32-2bc9-4e97-9f42-a16c12832ff8","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Solar interior: the region of the Sun beneath the visible photosphere, comprising the core, radiative zone, and convective zone, where hydrogen fusion in the core produces the Sun\u2019s energy and that energy is transported outward by radiation and then convection. Its temperature, density, composition, rotation, and magnetic structure vary with depth and are studied through solar models, helioseismology, and neutrino observations."},{"id":"d598f15a-ed6f-4072-b83d-0bf49ff1c638","l":"Solar radiation management","d":2,"p":"1a3b4d60-b5e0-4a92-9631-9c79d27566d1","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Solar radiation management (SRM) is a class of deliberate interventions that alter Earth's shortwave radiative balance\u2014e.g., by increasing planetary albedo\u2014to offset anthropogenic radiative forcing and lower global temperatures. Examples include stratospheric aerosol injection, marine cloud brightening, and space-based reflectors; SRM reduces incoming solar heating but does not remove greenhouse gases or cure associated problems like ocean acidification."},{"id":"c68b92c7-8efb-4c8e-a4c6-cfd0240eae12","l":"Solar thermal energy","d":2,"p":"d2b62966-4eb1-4cbd-8ea1-4b34ea070e52","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Solar thermal energy is the capture and conversion of sunlight into heat by absorbing solar radiation and converting photon energy into thermal energy in a collector, working fluid, or solid. That thermal energy is used directly for space or water heating and industrial processes or to produce electricity via heat engines (e.g., concentrating solar power)."},{"id":"4184e251-4eb4-4a26-b037-3b7e99862f1c","l":"Solar wind","d":2,"p":"f712db32-2bc9-4e97-9f42-a16c12832ff8","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"The solar wind is a continuous, supersonic outflow of ionized gas (plasma)\u2014primarily electrons, protons, and alpha particles\u2014escaping the Sun's hot corona and carrying the Sun's magnetic field into interplanetary space. This streaming plasma varies with solar activity and drives space weather by interacting with planetary magnetospheres and atmospheres."},{"id":"5a590aac-40d8-47d2-9ca9-3d2c5bf3544b","l":"Sol-gel process","d":2,"p":"c4ed35b0-1090-409c-9667-85f9ac03e9c0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The sol\u2013gel process is a wet-chemical route in which a liquid colloidal suspension (sol) of molecular or nanoparticulate precursors undergoes hydrolysis and condensation to form a three-dimensional solid network (gel). After drying and heat treatment the gel converts into porous or dense inorganic materials (glasses, ceramics, thin films or powders) with tunable composition and nanostructure."},{"id":"19c4b961-ea7f-4c27-9cc4-e3a017b10d2b","l":"Solid-gas interfaces","d":2,"p":"628f7190-cb71-4692-8cda-ebc544f9e9fb","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The solid\u2013gas interface is the boundary region where a solid and a gas meet, characterized by atomic-scale surface structure and thermodynamic properties distinct from the bulk phases. It controls adsorption/desorption, heterogeneous reactions, heat/mass and momentum exchange, and phenomena such as catalysis, corrosion and nucleation."},{"id":"2c89df20-03b8-4b8c-b224-6fd4926b4f1a","l":"Solidification","d":2,"p":"c4ed35b0-1090-409c-9667-85f9ac03e9c0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Solidification is the phase transition in which a liquid becomes a solid as temperature falls below its melting point, typically a first-order process that releases latent heat. It usually proceeds by nucleation and growth of a crystalline phase, though rapid cooling can yield an amorphous (glassy) solid if ordering is kinetically suppressed."},{"id":"8c13d88a-a3d4-40bd-8c91-af56c1c92773","l":"Solid-solid interfaces","d":2,"p":"628f7190-cb71-4692-8cda-ebc544f9e9fb","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Solid\u2013solid interfaces are the boundaries where two solid materials\u2014or two regions (grains or phases) of the same solid with different crystallographic orientation or composition\u2014meet, including grain boundaries, phase boundaries, and heterointerfaces. Their atomic structure, mismatch strain, and chemistry strongly modify transport, mechanical, thermal, and electronic properties and thus control the behavior of polycrystalline and composite materials."},{"id":"b5fd2a0f-4151-4fdf-9fec-b82180188a68","l":"Solid-solid transformations","d":2,"p":"19f26688-0955-4fd9-85d8-aaf71c4a581f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Solid\u2013solid transformations are changes in the crystal structure or atomic arrangement of a material in which one solid phase converts into another without passing through a liquid state. They occur by mechanisms ranging from long-range atomic diffusion (nucleation and growth, order\u2013disorder changes) to cooperative, diffusionless lattice displacements (martensitic/displacive transformations) and are driven by changes in temperature, pressure, or composition."},{"id":"0fd584d6-fc75-468f-a3c2-503d35444fe5","l":"Solid solutions","d":2,"p":"96973abf-f1b5-44c5-9549-9f0f4dfe234f","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Solid solutions are single-phase crystalline solids in which two or more atomic species are mutually incorporated into the same crystal lattice, either by substituting for host atoms (substitutional) or occupying interstitial sites. They form a continuous range of compositions that preserve the parent crystal structure, with physical properties that vary smoothly with composition."},{"id":"a6c1f405-466a-4fb3-877e-e9549c927712","l":"Solid-state detectors","d":2,"p":"2360e96e-a12b-4d21-9b65-36ed1177c0e6","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Solid-state detectors are devices that detect ionizing radiation or particles by converting energy deposited in a solid\u2014typically a semiconductor such as silicon or germanium\u2014into mobile charge carriers (electron\u2013hole pairs), whose drift under an applied field produces a measurable electrical signal. Examples include PIN diodes, CCDs, avalanche photodiodes and germanium gamma-ray detectors, valued for compactness, high energy/spatial resolution, and fast response."},{"id":"371ef245-60f3-45d9-b265-ed7abd84f07f","l":"Solid state lasers","d":2,"p":"cd4c012e-bacf-4bcf-8358-a6573a330ec4","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Solid-state lasers are lasers whose gain medium is a solid host (crystal or glass) doped with active ions (rare-earth or transition-metal) that provide the stimulated-emission transition. They are typically optically pumped (e.g., by laser diodes or flashlamps) and can produce high-quality continuous-wave or pulsed output \u2014 examples include ruby, Nd:YAG, and Ti:sapphire."},{"id":"57e4b4d6-da52-4b4a-9873-85c3a7046201","l":"Sonar","d":2,"p":"d794d6b2-dac3-40e5-906e-24fc867d825f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Sonar (sound navigation and ranging) is a technique that uses acoustic waves propagated through a fluid (typically water) to detect, locate, and characterize objects by measuring returned echoes. Active sonar emits pulses and uses echo time-of-flight and Doppler shifts to determine range and relative velocity, while passive sonar listens to sounds produced by sources."},{"id":"75fbdc90-f97b-4688-a84e-86afaafc0296","l":"Sound generation","d":2,"p":"eae7e56e-ad28-455e-9169-552cf35f1508","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Sound generation is the process by which a physical source creates time-varying pressure disturbances in a compressible medium that propagate away as acoustic waves. These disturbances originate from oscillatory motions, rapid pressure changes, or unsteady flows (e.g., vibrating solids, turbulent eddies, or shocks), with the emitted sound\u2019s strength and spectrum set by the source dynamics, geometry, and coupling to the medium."},{"id":"e5171280-b16e-4dc4-a7e1-8ee2f9f6d4e2","l":"Space charge in beams","d":2,"p":"3934db16-fe69-4d69-82c8-9bc346490273","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Space charge in beams is the collective self-electric (and, for moving beams, accompanying magnetic) field produced by the finite density of charged particles, which exerts forces back on the particles themselves. These self-fields lead to Coulomb repulsion (and partial magnetic pinch at relativistic speeds), causing beam expansion, emittance growth, tune shifts and setting limits on current and beam quality."},{"id":"67ea4925-0fee-4ee7-8599-24280499d733","l":"Spacetime topology & causal structure","d":2,"p":"6287ac05-d956-4c3c-9882-8ea7a798f964","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Spacetime topology and causal structure refers to the manifold's continuous connectivity (which points are near or separate) together with the light-cone\u2013determined ordering of events that dictates which events can influence which others (timelike, lightlike, or spacelike separation).  Their interplay determines global features such as horizons and Cauchy surfaces, and whether pathological behaviors like closed timelike curves or violations of global hyperbolicity can occur."},{"id":"1a0102fc-c8e6-47d7-a9d5-1e8a46c30d13","l":"Spatial light modulators","d":2,"p":"dcf48409-af3f-4517-b58d-7ec6c95351f3","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Spatial light modulators (SLMs) are electronically addressable optical devices that impose a programmable, spatially varying modulation of an incident light field \u2014 typically in phase, amplitude, or polarization \u2014 on a pixel-by-pixel basis. Common implementations include liquid-crystal arrays and MEMS micro-mirror (DMD) arrays, and they are used for beam shaping, holography, adaptive optics, and optical trapping."},{"id":"f6ec8025-4d3a-4b0b-b05e-0a5e9db1733b","l":"Spatial modeling","d":2,"p":"695da376-c44a-41dd-b968-d200c31e9d5e","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems","Plasma Physics"],"def":"Spatial modeling is the construction and use of mathematical and computational representations of how physical quantities, materials, or structures vary and interact across space. It combines geometry, boundary conditions and appropriate numerical or analytical methods (e.g., discretization and PDE solvers) to predict spatial distributions and dynamics at relevant scales."},{"id":"122cca65-2681-4875-a54d-95f0611201cb","l":"Spatial profiles of optical beams","d":2,"p":"dfb4980b-4281-4a8f-830b-8822ca3477ba","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams","Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Spatial profiles of optical beams describe how the optical field's amplitude, phase (and often polarization) vary across the beam's transverse cross-section and along the propagation direction. These transverse field patterns\u2014e.g., Gaussian, Hermite\u2013Gaussian, Laguerre\u2013Gaussian, and Bessel modes\u2014determine diffraction, focusing, beam quality, and modal interactions."},{"id":"661293d0-1ffe-42d0-87fa-4c769446ecf8","l":"Special relativity","d":2,"p":"6287ac05-d956-4c3c-9882-8ea7a798f964","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"Special relativity is Einstein\u2019s 1905 theory of space and time for observers in uniform (non\u2011accelerating) motion, based on the principles that the laws of physics are the same in all inertial frames and that the speed of light in vacuum is constant for all observers. It replaces Galilean transformations with Lorentz transformations and implies effects such as time dilation, length contraction, relativity of simultaneity, and mass\u2013energy equivalence (E=mc^2)."},{"id":"816fad41-e621-48cc-b377-1e2c65b63074","l":"Specific heat measurements","d":2,"p":"c2ea95f8-c209-44d7-b8f7-a6baf632c46a","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Specific heat measurements are experimental techniques (commonly calorimetry) used to determine a material's heat capacity per unit mass or per mole by measuring the heat required to change its temperature, typically as a function of temperature. They probe thermodynamic properties such as phase transitions, entropy changes, and microscopic excitations (e.g., lattice, electronic, or magnetic contributions)."},{"id":"5c06f660-eee5-4254-9387-e1b2954d30b4","l":"Spectral shaping","d":2,"p":"bd03ea46-2406-4a24-8307-05b2b498fb4e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Spectral shaping is the deliberate modification of a signal's frequency\u2011domain amplitude and phase (and sometimes polarization) distribution to produce desired temporal, spatial, or spectral behavior. It is implemented with filters, modulators or pulse shapers to control bandwidth, pulse duration, noise, or interference in optics, electronics, and acoustics."},{"id":"dd68898b-d68a-45df-899c-ca1478f6a185","l":"Spectroscopy of clusters","d":2,"p":"cac7f673-4cd1-47b8-9de3-9f9785aea585","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Spectroscopy of clusters is the study of the electronic, vibrational and structural energy levels and dynamics of finite aggregates of atoms or molecules (clusters) by measuring their absorption, emission, scattering and photoelectron spectra. By tracking how spectral features change with cluster size, composition and geometry it reveals quantum\u2011size, surface and cooperative effects that bridge single molecules and bulk matter."},{"id":"4bdb49fb-fff6-4c45-8de5-14a145d87803","l":"Speech","d":2,"p":"eae7e56e-ad28-455e-9169-552cf35f1508","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Speech is the production of structured acoustic waveforms by the vocal apparatus that convey linguistic information. Physically, it arises from airflow-driven oscillations of the vocal folds (the sound source) filtered by the resonant shape of the vocal tract, producing time-varying pressure waves characterized by pitch, formants, amplitude envelope, and transients that propagate as sound."},{"id":"23dc00ca-a664-4243-91ad-a4ea33108402","l":"Sperimagnetism","d":2,"p":"b6e4cc90-18f3-4427-841d-c667375bbca0","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Sperimagnetism is a non-collinear magnetic ordering in which atomic moments are canted by a similar polar angle around a common axis, with random azimuthal orientations (i.e., distributed on a cone), so transverse components cancel while axial components produce a net magnetization; it commonly occurs in canted ferrimagnets and certain rare-earth\u2013transition-metal compounds."},{"id":"8f3c43fb-6878-4ce9-a41b-116f6ff0a983","l":"Spermatozoa","d":2,"p":"3e04a8a2-1199-4410-801c-ac848b0bbda8","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Spermatozoa are the motile male gametes of animals that carry a haploid complement of paternal DNA and are specialized to reach and fertilize an oocyte, typically composed of a head (nucleus plus acrosome), a mitochondria-rich midpiece, and a tail. The tail contains a 9+2 microtubule axoneme whose ATP-driven dynein motors produce microtubule sliding and bending waves to propel the cell, operating in the low-Reynolds-number (viscous-dominated) regime."},{"id":"93d43578-13d0-4766-a96f-c229c0e29260","l":"Speromagnetism","d":2,"p":"b6e4cc90-18f3-4427-841d-c667375bbca0","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Speromagnetism is a frozen, noncollinear magnetic state in which local magnetic moments retain their magnitude but point in random directions so that there is no macroscopic long-range order or net magnetization. It typically arises in systems with strong random local anisotropy or structural disorder (e.g., amorphous ferromagnets or ensembles of randomly oriented single-domain particles) and is distinct from conventional ferromagnetic or antiferromagnetic order."},{"id":"827645cb-0968-44d2-9f65-056d692fa471","l":"Spheromaks","d":2,"p":"5b4f3bb4-8db6-4156-a472-4c9988d6e313","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"A spheromak is a self-organized, magnetically confined plasma in a compact, simply connected (roughly spheroidal) shape whose magnetic field lines wrap in both toroidal and poloidal directions, producing internal currents and magnetic flux without a central conductor. It is often described as a compact torus in a relaxed (approximately force-free) state, typically characterized by J \u221d B."},{"id":"f37bf9ba-5b81-40a7-89b4-acdb9181a88e","l":"Spiking neurons","d":2,"p":"205e6918-12a2-4f4c-9795-8f723f476866","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Spiking neurons are models of excitable cells that produce brief, all-or-none voltage pulses (action potentials or \"spikes\") whenever the membrane potential crosses a threshold. They are described by nonlinear dynamical equations (e.g., Hodgkin\u2013Huxley, integrate-and-fire) and communicate information via the timing and patterns of those spikes together with synaptic coupling and refractory effects."},{"id":"fcca5cbd-948f-4480-8590-77cddddcdb7f","l":"Spin accumulation","d":2,"p":"29a5aa4f-d2c0-43ce-98a8-21cbbf2ae916","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Spin accumulation is a nonequilibrium local imbalance between spin-up and spin-down electron populations, usually described as a difference in their electrochemical potentials (a \"spin voltage\"). It is produced where spin currents are injected or generated (e.g., by spin injection or the spin Hall effect) and decays over the material's spin diffusion length due to spin\u2011flip relaxation."},{"id":"6950be70-706e-4cf6-99c8-bc9c0b5f8dc2","l":"Spin caloritronics","d":2,"p":"29a5aa4f-d2c0-43ce-98a8-21cbbf2ae916","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Spin caloritronics is the study of how temperature gradients and heat currents generate, manipulate, and are affected by electron and magnon spin currents, combining principles of spintronics and thermoelectrics. It examines spin-dependent Seebeck and Peltier effects and the coupling among electrons, magnons, and phonons that convert thermal energy into spin or charge signals and vice versa."},{"id":"10d9a24e-ca66-446e-b534-fb044a4d50ee","l":"Spin coherence","d":2,"p":"29a5aa4f-d2c0-43ce-98a8-21cbbf2ae916","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Spin coherence is the preservation of a well-defined relative quantum phase between components of a spin state (or between spins in an ensemble), which allows superposition and interference of spin states. Its loss\u2014spin decoherence, often quantified by the transverse relaxation time T2\u2014erases those phase relations and limits spin-based sensing and quantum information."},{"id":"0ed84e54-12b1-4906-8906-632d48bb90e9","l":"Spin current","d":2,"p":"29a5aa4f-d2c0-43ce-98a8-21cbbf2ae916","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Spin current is the transport of spin angular momentum through a material, described by both a flow direction and the spin polarization carried. It can be carried by electrons, magnons, or other quasiparticles and may occur with or without an accompanying charge current (a \"pure\" spin current has zero net charge flow)."},{"id":"1bd2a2b2-d164-4a28-b457-a42083195632","l":"Spin density waves","d":2,"p":"1bd1a9ab-ec4f-40a8-9f81-9f1005587d58","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A spin density wave is a collective magnetic state in which the local spin magnetization oscillates periodically in space with a well-defined wavevector, producing a sinusoidally or helically modulated magnetic order. It commonly occurs in itinerant-electron (often quasi\u2013low-dimensional) systems as the spin analogue of a charge density wave, frequently driven by Fermi-surface nesting and can be commensurate or incommensurate with the crystal lattice."},{"id":"7d5e894f-597c-480f-95cb-92d623942472","l":"Spin diffusion","d":2,"p":"29a5aa4f-d2c0-43ce-98a8-21cbbf2ae916","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Spin diffusion is the transport process by which a non\u2011equilibrium spin polarization spreads through a material due to carrier motion and scattering, driven by gradients in spin density. It is described by a diffusion equation and is limited by spin\u2011relaxation (spin\u2011flip) processes, with propagation set by the spin diffusion coefficient and the spin diffusion length."},{"id":"5aa70cf6-b7ef-4502-a5aa-eb3a92770f26","l":"Spinels","d":2,"p":"6af5be88-489f-4a50-9abf-998e8912254a","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Spinels are a family of crystalline oxides with general formula AB2O4 that adopt the cubic spinel lattice (space group Fd-3m), in which A-site cations occupy tetrahedral interstices and B-site cations occupy octahedral sites; the degree of cation inversion (normal vs. inverse spinel) strongly affects their magnetic, electronic, and optical properties. They are important model systems and functional materials in magnetism, electronics, catalysis, and energy storage."},{"id":"b0910e94-72dc-4bdc-9b28-7230c1d114fa","l":"Spin filtering","d":2,"p":"29a5aa4f-d2c0-43ce-98a8-21cbbf2ae916","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Spin filtering is the production or enhancement of spin polarization by selectively removing or transmitting particles of one spin orientation through spin-dependent interactions, so the remaining ensemble becomes polarized. In practice this is achieved by repeated spin-dependent scattering or absorption (for example a stored beam repeatedly interacting with a polarized target) that preferentially depletes one spin state."},{"id":"6fa907b2-6ea6-4095-b649-c38800eb22ef","l":"Spin fluctuations","d":2,"p":"57c8eac7-41d5-4fed-8ba5-d8cb38d1ad03","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Spin fluctuations are time- and space-dependent variations of the orientations and magnitudes of microscopic magnetic moments (spins) caused by thermal agitation and quantum zero-point motion. They determine a material\u2019s magnetic response, control critical behavior near magnetic phase transitions, and can mediate interactions such as unconventional superconductivity."},{"id":"257ff504-8723-46c7-aea0-ec7d55a0dc6f","l":"Spin generation","d":2,"p":"29a5aa4f-d2c0-43ce-98a8-21cbbf2ae916","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Spin generation is the creation of a net spin angular-momentum imbalance (spin polarization) or a spin current in a material, i.e., a non-equilibrium population of carriers with preferred spin orientation. It is produced by external stimuli such as optical excitation (optical orientation), electrical injection, spin\u2013orbit effects (spin Hall or Edelstein effects), thermal gradients (spin Seebeck) or dynamic magnetization (spin pumping)."},{"id":"bb4a3b78-a738-4e19-abd6-1feea6226ea1","l":"Spin Hall effect","d":2,"p":"99c1e71a-1639-42a5-8e00-6e258fc0b4f5","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Spin Hall effect is the generation of a transverse spin current and resulting spin accumulation at the lateral edges of a conductor or semiconductor when an electric field or charge current is applied, caused by spin\u2013orbit coupling that deflects electrons with opposite spin in opposite transverse directions. It can arise from intrinsic band-structure (Berry-curvature) effects or from extrinsic scattering mechanisms such as skew scattering and side-jump."},{"id":"e9c1b6a7-5fa0-4e03-ba40-c835aaeee544","l":"Spin injection","d":2,"p":"29a5aa4f-d2c0-43ce-98a8-21cbbf2ae916","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Spin injection is the process of creating a nonequilibrium population of spin-polarized charge carriers in a material by transferring spin angular momentum across an interface, typically from a ferromagnet into a nonmagnetic metal or semiconductor. It is achieved electrically (spin-polarized currents) or optically, producing spin currents and spin accumulation that enable spintronic functionality, with efficiency limited by interface properties and spin relaxation."},{"id":"6ff84248-428b-4704-ae9f-e55346a4ce60","l":"Spin lattice models","d":2,"p":"a088bad1-4904-4f08-a625-a9840fdf32c7","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Spin lattice models are theoretical models in which classical or quantum spin variables sit on the sites of a spatial lattice and interact via local exchange, anisotropy, and external-field terms specified by a Hamiltonian (examples include the Ising, Heisenberg, and XY models). They are used to study collective magnetic behavior, ordering, excitations, and phase transitions in many-body systems."},{"id":"6888cd56-6c89-469b-a6cf-79ed1bfeb5ad","l":"Spinless fermions","d":2,"p":"4fcc035e-7eb4-46f6-b347-5cc975e5ed95","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Spinless fermions are fermionic particles or effective quasiparticles that obey Fermi\u2013Dirac statistics and the Pauli exclusion principle but carry no internal spin degree of freedom. In practice the term is used when the spin is frozen out or neglected (e.g., fully spin\u2011polarized electrons or simplified lattice models), while the many\u2011body wavefunction remains antisymmetric under particle exchange."},{"id":"6d515c94-1309-4f53-8a2d-eaa6ae444f89","l":"Spin noise spectroscopy","d":2,"p":"0003fc3f-1522-4fca-bf3f-bcef0dcec405","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Spin noise spectroscopy is a nonperturbative technique that detects spontaneous, equilibrium fluctuations of a system's spin polarization\u2014typically via optical (Faraday/Kerr rotation) or electrical noise measurements. By analyzing the noise power spectrum (using the fluctuation\u2013dissipation relation) one extracts intrinsic spin properties such as relaxation times, g\u2011factors, and interaction effects without optically or electrically pumping the spins."},{"id":"6328a096-2259-4dbb-93bd-f6f4250c1a78","l":"Spinodal decomposition","d":2,"p":"1f768dc4-f406-4a68-ac8e-7a957c3fd741","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Spinodal decomposition is the spontaneous, barrierless phase separation of a uniform mixture that occurs when the system is quenched into the spinodal region of its phase diagram, where the homogeneous free energy curvature is negative (d^2F/d\u03c6^2 < 0). In that region small composition fluctuations grow by \"uphill\" diffusion, producing an interconnected domain pattern whose early-stage dynamics are described by the Cahn\u2013Hilliard theory."},{"id":"686c0d62-36cb-4ef8-9e01-1c253cdaf18f","l":"Spin optoelectronics","d":2,"p":"3d71d9d8-fab6-4338-be83-7624e12f1438","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Spin optoelectronics is the study and engineering of devices that couple the quantum spin of charge carriers with optical fields, using light to generate, manipulate, and read out spin polarization and using carrier spin to control light emission and polarization. It merges spintronics and optoelectronics to enable components such as spin-LEDs, spin lasers and spin-sensitive photodetectors that exploit spin-dependent optical selection rules and magnetic contacts."},{"id":"2b1a89d4-6f49-4c03-9549-6fe3da7a5004","l":"Spinor Bose-Einstein condensates","d":2,"p":"4254adf6-872d-4868-9931-a2a3e84b566e","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Spinor Bose-Einstein condensates are Bose\u2013Einstein condensed gases of atoms whose internal (hyperfine) spin degrees of freedom remain active, so the macroscopic wavefunction is a multi-component spinor rather than a single scalar. Spin-dependent interactions and external fields produce coherent spin-mixing, magnetic ordering (e.g., ferromagnetic or polar phases), and rich spin textures and topological defects not found in scalar condensates."},{"id":"66969bb5-5f13-44a2-9ac9-a4ddaf711c3e","l":"Spin polarization","d":2,"p":"29a5aa4f-d2c0-43ce-98a8-21cbbf2ae916","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Spin polarization is the degree to which the intrinsic angular momenta (spins) of particles in a system are preferentially aligned along a chosen direction, producing a net magnetic moment. It is commonly quantified as the normalized population imbalance P = (n\u2191 \u2212 n\u2193)/(n\u2191 + n\u2193), ranging from \u22121 to 1, and applies to electrons, nuclei, atoms or electronic states in solids."},{"id":"bdc5ffd1-66e1-4bc4-953c-07e290cf2701","l":"Spin relaxation","d":2,"p":"29a5aa4f-d2c0-43ce-98a8-21cbbf2ae916","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Spin relaxation is the process by which a non\u2011equilibrium ensemble of quantum spins returns to thermal equilibrium through interactions that randomize their orientations or phases. It is commonly characterized by a longitudinal relaxation time T1 (population or energy exchange with the environment) and a transverse relaxation time T2 (loss of phase coherence among spins)."},{"id":"36b1df63-f80c-4483-a31f-56a69281602c","l":"Spin reorientation transition","d":2,"p":"19f26688-0955-4fd9-85d8-aaf71c4a581f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A spin reorientation transition is a magnetic phase transition in which the preferred direction (easy axis) of a material's atomic spins rotates from one orientation to another as external parameters (temperature, film thickness, applied field, pressure, or composition) change. It occurs when competing anisotropies (magnetocrystalline, shape, surface/interface, strain) alter their relative strengths and can be continuous or discontinuous."},{"id":"fc8aa7de-ba57-4179-8c97-aed96475e82b","l":"Spin state transition","d":2,"p":"92927025-018a-4b9f-b617-c4a768530654","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A spin state transition is a change in a quantum system\u2019s electronic-spin configuration between distinct spin eigenstates (different total spin or spin multiplicity). Such transitions\u2014often called spin crossover in coordination complexes\u2014are driven by external stimuli (temperature, pressure, light, or magnetic field) and typically produce accompanying changes in magnetic, structural, and optical properties."},{"id":"2c94e9cb-6047-4621-816e-26add1b9dbe3","l":"Spin texture","d":2,"p":"1bd1a9ab-ec4f-40a8-9f81-9f1005587d58","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Spin texture is the spatial pattern of spin orientations in a material, described by a vector field of local spins or magnetization that can vary continuously to form domain walls, vortices, helices or topological objects such as skyrmions. The term applies both to real-space configurations of magnetic moments and to momentum-space spin distributions (e.g., spin\u2013momentum locking in electronic bands)."},{"id":"f1c78ef6-4185-4a87-9ee6-a89e7ba783b6","l":"Spin torque","d":2,"p":"29a5aa4f-d2c0-43ce-98a8-21cbbf2ae916","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Spin torque is the mechanical-like torque exerted on a magnetic moment or the magnetization of a material when a flow or accumulation of electron spin angular momentum (e.g., from a spin-polarized current or spin current) transfers angular momentum to the local moments. This transfer\u2014mediated by exchange and spin-orbit interactions\u2014can drive magnetization precession, damping changes, or switching."},{"id":"e27c2c2b-1771-4f5c-93e5-684185202266","l":"Spin waves","d":2,"p":"29a5aa4f-d2c0-43ce-98a8-21cbbf2ae916","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Spin waves are collective, wavelike oscillations of the orientations of magnetic moments in an ordered magnet, in which neighboring spins precess coherently and a disturbance in the magnetization propagates through the material; their quanta are called magnons. Their frequency\u2013wavelength (dispersion) is set by exchange and dipolar interactions and governs the low\u2011energy magnetic dynamics."},{"id":"b9629923-ee75-46d5-b428-544c3473d555","l":"Spin wave spectroscopy","d":2,"p":"0003fc3f-1522-4fca-bf3f-bcef0dcec405","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Spin-wave spectroscopy is the set of experimental methods used to excite and detect spin waves (magnons)\u2014the collective precessional modes of magnetic moments\u2014in order to measure their frequencies, dispersion relations, lifetimes, damping and interactions. Common implementations include ferromagnetic resonance, Brillouin light scattering, inelastic neutron scattering, spin-polarized electron energy-loss and time-resolved magneto-optical or microwave probes."},{"id":"23e0894e-316f-4146-a50d-24d702922db6","l":"Spin wave theory","d":2,"p":"ba55d8a8-d049-4550-b264-530f74618214","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Spin wave theory describes the collective low-energy excitations of an ordered magnetic system as coherent precessional oscillations of spins (magnons); it linearizes a spin Hamiltonian (e.g., Heisenberg) about the ordered ground state and treats these excitations as bosonic quasiparticles with a characteristic dispersion. The theory yields magnon spectra, temperature-dependent reduction of magnetization, and a framework for including magnon interactions beyond the linear approximation."},{"id":"9b6caf4a-e524-424f-8d8b-f2fe9440772c","l":"Spiral & scroll waves","d":2,"p":"8e35265c-459a-43fc-9eb8-73e1dd82b47c","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Nonlinear Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Spiral and scroll waves are self-organized rotating wave patterns in two- and three-dimensional excitable or reaction\u2013diffusion media: a spiral wave is a planar rotating wavefront that winds around a phase singularity (an unexcited core), while a scroll wave is its 3D analogue whose rotation centers form a filament. They arise from nonlinear wave propagation and can persist, meander, interact, or break up, appearing in systems from chemical oscillations to cardiac tissue."},{"id":"20743cc2-b9b6-42a3-9a19-8f00876b1188","l":"Split-ring resonators","d":2,"p":"77fabbae-1f98-4300-996c-9158ab52e6fc","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Split-ring resonators are subwavelength metallic structures\u2014typically one or two concentric conductive rings with a small gap\u2014whose split provides capacitance so that circulating currents produce an LC resonance and a strong effective magnetic dipole. They are used as metamaterial building blocks to engineer magnetic permeability and exotic electromagnetic responses (including negative \u03bc) across microwave to optical frequencies."},{"id":"a781d742-17e9-49c2-8abf-87e01638317f","l":"Spreading models","d":2,"p":"7b9db112-8475-42b4-ad22-f00f6b8635f8","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Spreading models are mathematical descriptions of how some quantity or state (e.g., particles, heat, chemicals, fires, epidemics, or information) propagates in space and time. They range from deterministic diffusion and reaction\u2013diffusion equations to stochastic percolation, branching processes, and network-based compartmental models, and are used to predict front speeds, thresholds, and spatiotemporal patterns."},{"id":"002b6f36-b205-41ef-8810-90d621c01e9a","l":"Sputtering","d":2,"p":"c4ed35b0-1090-409c-9667-85f9ac03e9c0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Sputtering is the process by which atoms, molecules, or clusters are ejected from a solid surface when energetic particles (typically ions) strike it and transfer momentum to near-surface atoms. The sputter yield depends on projectile energy, mass and angle and on the target's binding energy and structure, and the effect is exploited in thin-film deposition, etching and surface analysis."},{"id":"db4fd634-2189-4fc0-84db-ecb4da41cbd6","l":"Square lattice","d":2,"p":"8bc71f5b-3de8-4994-8de2-1a300cec36b6","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A square lattice is a two-dimensional Bravais lattice whose lattice points lie at the vertices of a regular grid of equal-length, orthogonal primitive vectors (spacing a), so the repeating unit is a square. Each site has four nearest neighbors and the lattice is a common simple model in condensed-matter and statistical physics."},{"id":"3696b5b4-d797-4cee-8c53-610e16e25eac","l":"Squeezing of quantum noise","d":2,"p":"632f74d1-d16b-4b92-a295-b24729a93d8d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Squeezing of quantum noise is the redistribution of quantum fluctuations so that the variance of one observable (for example, an electromagnetic field quadrature) is reduced below the vacuum or shot-noise level while the conjugate observable's variance increases, consistent with the Heisenberg uncertainty principle. Such squeezed states are nonclassical and are used to improve precision measurements and quantum-information tasks."},{"id":"44ab84e3-5d57-4d8b-b204-83c7af40fabd","l":"Stability of clusters","d":2,"p":"cac7f673-4cd1-47b8-9de3-9f9785aea585","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Stability of clusters is the propensity of a finite aggregate of particles (atoms, molecules, or colloids) to remain bound and retain its structure rather than dissociate or rearrange. It is quantified by binding or dissociation energies and energy barriers on the potential-energy surface (thermodynamic vs kinetic stability) and depends on size, composition, interparticle interactions, electronic-shell effects, and temperature."},{"id":"a8d8fed9-a94a-4b2d-b6c7-6fabc2e4afe8","l":"Stacking faults","d":2,"p":"7060bde3-9f16-411d-9d68-3328cf14b019","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A stacking fault is a planar crystallographic defect where the regular sequence of closely packed atomic layers is locally interrupted or shifted (for example, an error in the ABCABC stacking of an FCC crystal). Such faults\u2014classified as intrinsic or extrinsic\u2014are typically bounded by partial dislocations and can significantly affect a material's mechanical and electronic properties."},{"id":"39bc26de-f07f-4241-8635-eaeec699bb81","l":"Star polymers","d":2,"p":"2303d382-65bb-4b93-94b8-62a73502ec4b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Star polymers are branched macromolecules in which several linear polymer chains (\"arms\") radiate from a common central core or branch point; their arm number and length (functionality and molecular weight) strongly determine their solution conformation, dynamics, and viscoelastic behavior, distinguishing them from linear polymers."},{"id":"c251c461-50eb-42cd-bad9-5f92ee725f80","l":"Stellar atmosphere","d":2,"p":"9b591609-822d-4be9-93a8-dff9f7f3722c","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"The stellar atmosphere is the outer gaseous envelope of a star\u2014those layers above the opaque interior from which most of the emergent radiation and spectral lines originate. It includes the photosphere, chromosphere and corona, with steep density and temperature gradients and ionization changes that set the observed spectrum and drive mass loss (winds)."},{"id":"43584f0b-2102-449a-bb59-2aa50d8b919d","l":"Stellarators & toroidal confinement devices","d":2,"p":"5b4f3bb4-8db6-4156-a472-4c9988d6e313","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Toroidal confinement devices are magnetic-fusion machines that confine hot plasma in a doughnut-shaped (toroidal) chamber using magnetic field lines that spiral around the torus to keep the plasma away from material walls. Stellarators are a class of toroidal devices that create this helical (rotational-transform) field primarily with externally shaped, three-dimensional coils so they can operate steadily without relying on a large toroidal plasma current."},{"id":"96ca3b2c-d95a-4ffd-8d1b-1ea7dc2fe6d6","l":"Stellar interior","d":2,"p":"9b591609-822d-4be9-93a8-dff9f7f3722c","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"The stellar interior is the region of a star beneath its photosphere, including the core and overlying shells, where the local temperature, density, pressure, and composition determine the star\u2019s structure. It is the site of energy generation (usually nuclear fusion) and energy transport (by radiation, convection, or conduction) under hydrostatic and thermal equilibrium, with these quantities changing strongly with radius."},{"id":"930ae4ca-6b04-4a30-95a5-22bd20f5c06c","l":"Stellar wind","d":2,"p":"9b591609-822d-4be9-93a8-dff9f7f3722c","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Stellar wind is a continuous outflow of plasma (electrons, protons, and ions) streaming away from a star\u2019s outer atmosphere, driven by thermal pressure, radiation pressure, magnetic forces, and/or dust acceleration. It carries mass, momentum, and magnetic field into the circumstellar and interstellar environment, affecting stellar evolution, angular momentum loss, and planetary atmospheres."},{"id":"3b7cff28-7457-42cf-865d-eb8fa3a1a4b4","l":"Stem cells","d":2,"p":"3e04a8a2-1199-4410-801c-ac848b0bbda8","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Stem cells are undifferentiated cells that both self-renew (produce more stem cells) and differentiate into specialized cell types. Their developmental potency ranges from totipotent and pluripotent (broad potential) to multipotent (restricted lineages), enabling embryonic development, tissue maintenance, and regenerative therapies."},{"id":"a15558dd-3575-4b7c-9b22-4365f468c460","l":"Stimulated Brillouin & Raman scattering in plasmas","d":2,"p":"2917e3bd-b25c-4e89-8796-5c7e77dfda5a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Stimulated Brillouin scattering (SBS) in plasmas is a three-wave parametric instability in which an intense electromagnetic pump wave decays into a scattered electromagnetic wave and a low-frequency ion-acoustic (density) wave, producing a small frequency shift set by the ion-acoustic mode. Stimulated Raman scattering (SRS) is the analogous three-wave process in which the pump decays into a scattered electromagnetic wave and a high-frequency electron plasma (Langmuir) wave, giving a larger frequency downshift near the electron plasma frequency and strongly influencing energy transfer and hot-electron generation in laser\u2013plasma interactions."},{"id":"e4c4c0af-5486-4833-88ff-e347f070d04d","l":"Stimulated Raman adiabatic passage","d":2,"p":"70cba0b1-a5ec-474b-a023-2ab4c3e484b9","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Stimulated Raman adiabatic passage (STIRAP) is a coherent control technique that transfers population between two quantum states of a three-level system by adiabatically following a non-absorbing \"dark\" superposition created with two time\u2011delayed laser pulses applied in a counterintuitive order (Stokes before pump). It achieves near\u2011unit, robust transfer while keeping the lossy intermediate state essentially unpopulated."},{"id":"da18b93f-430a-4ea1-8831-966d9bb6a0fc","l":"Stimulated Raman scattering","d":2,"p":"bd03ea46-2406-4a24-8307-05b2b498fb4e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Stimulated Raman scattering is a nonlinear optical process in which an intense pump beam coherently amplifies photons whose frequencies are shifted by a material\u2019s vibrational (or rotational) modes: an incident photon is inelastically scattered into a lower-energy Stokes (or higher-energy anti\u2011Stokes) photon while creating (or annihilating) a vibrational excitation. The presence of the shifted field stimulates further inelastic scattering, producing exponential gain of the frequency\u2011shifted light and a coherent molecular polarization at the vibrational frequency."},{"id":"1b17688e-1135-4ec0-831e-c499e0d80c1d","l":"Stochastic analysis","d":2,"p":"695da376-c44a-41dd-b968-d200c31e9d5e","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems","Plasma Physics"],"def":"Stochastic analysis is the mathematical study of systems driven by randomness, focusing on the properties and time evolution of stochastic processes and stochastic differential equations that model noise\u2011driven dynamics. It provides tools such as It\u00f4/Stratonovich calculus, martingale theory, and Fokker\u2013Planck/Kolmogorov equations to compute statistical behavior, sample\u2011path properties, and long\u2011time limits."},{"id":"474c166e-ba13-450a-952d-fed7bf8ef77b","l":"Stochastic differential equations","d":2,"p":"695da376-c44a-41dd-b968-d200c31e9d5e","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Stochastic differential equations are differential equations in which one or more terms are random processes (typically modeled as white noise or increments of a Wiener/Brownian process), so the solution is a stochastic process rather than a deterministic function. They require stochastic calculus (e.g., It\u00f4 or Stratonovich interpretations) and are used to model the time evolution of physical systems subject to random fluctuations, such as in Langevin dynamics and noisy transport."},{"id":"e19e8fa3-f61f-41e2-bfa4-167c78cd06e9","l":"Stochastic processes & statistics","d":2,"p":"b5ff1838-0ecd-41a5-a90b-bae4acb69fcf","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Stochastic processes are mathematical descriptions of quantities that evolve in time or space with intrinsic randomness, characterized by probability distributions, correlations, and transition rules (e.g., Brownian motion, Markov processes). Statistics provides the tools to estimate, test, and infer those probabilistic properties from data, quantify uncertainty, and relate microscopic fluctuations to macroscopic behavior."},{"id":"feb4ab40-aa9b-42d9-9c4d-07395fc943d9","l":"Stochastic thermodynamics","d":2,"p":"609a2adc-099f-463f-9e2a-dd85c0101044","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Stochastic thermodynamics is a theoretical framework that extends classical thermodynamics to small systems and nonequilibrium processes by treating microscopic degrees of freedom as stochastic trajectories (e.g., Langevin or master-equation dynamics) and assigning trajectory-level quantities such as work, heat, and entropy production. It yields exact relations\u2014like fluctuation theorems and generalized second-law inequalities\u2014that quantify the role of thermal fluctuations in energy and information exchanges."},{"id":"30cbff6a-f9e1-40ff-9471-e28ea0071607","l":"Stopping power","d":2,"p":"7fb8ecb8-7f22-49e6-bd57-959dc9757c32","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Stopping power is the average energy loss per unit path length suffered by a charged particle traveling through a material, defined as S = \u2212dE/dx (e.g., MeV/cm). It arises mainly from inelastic collisions with electrons (ionization and excitation) and, at high energies, from radiative processes, and depends on the particle's charge and energy and on the target material."},{"id":"1b8cdb74-7017-48c1-a0d2-b726c073d8b8","l":"Strain","d":2,"p":"77d491f8-55ad-47a6-a0a7-cd24ffce7602","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Strain is the dimensionless measure of deformation of a body, quantifying how much its shape or size changes relative to the original configuration. Common examples are normal (engineering) strain \u03b5 = \u0394L/L0 for a change in length, shear strain given by a small angular change (in radians), and volumetric strain \u0394V/V0 for change in volume."},{"id":"99645aa2-38c8-4a54-b02c-6e6ae1116626","l":"Strain engineering","d":2,"p":"c4ed35b0-1090-409c-9667-85f9ac03e9c0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Strain engineering is the intentional application or control of mechanical deformation in a material to alter its crystal lattice and thereby tune its electronic, optical, magnetic, and mechanical properties. It is implemented via methods like epitaxial mismatch, substrate bending, patterned stressors or pressure, and is widely used in thin films, heterostructures and nanodevices to modify band structure, carrier mobility, phase transitions and other functional behaviors."},{"id":"384b8e00-eb9c-4497-b5fa-ad2975f0638f","l":"Stress propagation","d":2,"p":"4fb63b7b-53b3-49e8-923f-9b158d32ef0e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Stress propagation is the transmission of mechanical stress (internal force per area) through a material following a disturbance. In elastic solids this occurs as stress waves that travel at speeds set by the elastic moduli and density, while in viscous or viscoelastic media stress instead relaxes or diffuses over time."},{"id":"9413f345-56f8-47f5-b146-35fc466bc174","l":"String theory techniques in condensed matter","d":2,"p":"a1083ad2-a154-449d-a9ff-59bf46db1c7f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The use of mathematical tools and dualities developed in string theory to model and analyze condensed-matter systems.  Typical techniques include holographic (AdS/CFT) duality, conformal and topological field-theory methods, and brane constructions, which provide nonperturbative ways to study strongly correlated, topological and quantum-critical phenomena such as transport, entanglement, and phase structure."},{"id":"585115b3-f622-4877-8245-cd791d5bfdb8","l":"Stripes","d":2,"p":"979074fa-fadd-4ebe-acf1-0a9c92fb1848","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Stripes are quasi-one-dimensional, spatially modulated patterns of charge and/or spin density in strongly correlated materials, forming alternating linear regions (domains) with different electronic order. They break translational/rotational symmetry and arise from competing interactions (e.g., Coulomb repulsion and kinetic energy), often influencing or competing with superconductivity and other collective phases."},{"id":"4a65171b-cadb-4d0a-9a5f-a2f102379a87","l":"Stroke","d":2,"p":"bbcb2483-b22c-4bb6-bb81-beb53ff1b1ed","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Stroke: the linear distance a reciprocating or oscillating component travels between its extreme positions during one cycle (total travel). For a crank\u2011driven piston, the stroke equals twice the crank radius and, together with bore, sets the engine's swept displacement."},{"id":"d029ac4c-6eb6-4375-8137-18453990e255","l":"Strong-field-induced spectra","d":2,"p":"2007366a-9fc5-499c-90bd-02ba78a14762","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Strong-field-induced spectra are the frequency-resolved signals produced when matter is driven by very intense electromagnetic fields that modify energy levels and transition pathways beyond the linear (perturbative) regime, producing features such as high harmonics, above-threshold ionization peaks, Stark-shifted lines, and light-induced sidebands. These spectra reflect field-dressed, often ultrafast, electron dynamics and ionization/recombination processes and are used to probe and control microscopic motion."},{"id":"50e1b8c1-9292-4a21-881d-b13c9e018672","l":"Strong field ionization & excitation","d":2,"p":"2007366a-9fc5-499c-90bd-02ba78a14762","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Strong-field ionization and excitation are nonperturbative processes that occur when an intense electromagnetic field (typically from an ultrashort laser pulse) strongly distorts an atom\u2019s or molecule\u2019s binding potential so that electrons tunnel through or are driven over the barrier, or are promoted to high-lying excited states. The regime is characterized by the Keldysh parameter and gives rise to phenomena such as tunneling vs. over-the-barrier ionization, above-threshold ionization, recollision-driven excitation, and high-harmonic generation."},{"id":"432d687e-342c-49cb-874d-f44fb396f42a","l":"Structural order parameter","d":2,"p":"979074fa-fadd-4ebe-acf1-0a9c92fb1848","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A structural order parameter is a scalar, vector, or tensor quantity (often a coarse-grained field) that measures the degree and character of positional or symmetry-breaking structural order in a material, being zero in the high\u2011symmetry (disordered) phase and nonzero in the low\u2011symmetry (ordered) phase. It quantifies the amplitude and symmetry of a lattice distortion or bond/orientational ordering (for example the displacement amplitude in a martensitic transition or the amplitude of a density-wave) and is used in Landau and continuum theories to describe phase transitions and domain formation."},{"id":"01416268-be92-4712-8850-347275bcc8ff","l":"Structural phase transition","d":2,"p":"19f26688-0955-4fd9-85d8-aaf71c4a581f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A structural phase transition is a change in the arrangement and symmetry of atoms in a solid\u2014i.e., its crystal lattice and unit cell\u2014caused by variation of external parameters (temperature, pressure, composition) that moves the system between different free-energy minima. Such transitions can be continuous or discontinuous and typically alter mechanical, electronic, optical, or magnetic properties."},{"id":"7707f36f-d313-4a62-9df9-cb2573b4bf00","l":"Sun","d":2,"p":"0f2ae25e-a9ef-412f-ac42-2158123660ca","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics"],"def":"The Sun is a G2V main-sequence star \u2014 a hot, mostly hydrogen plasma ball that provides the dominant gravitational and radiative influence in the Solar System. It generates energy by nuclear fusion of hydrogen into helium in its core, producing electromagnetic radiation and a continuous outflow of charged particles (the solar wind)."},{"id":"a3cc729c-98ea-421a-ac1b-df399f62721d","l":"Superconducting devices","d":2,"p":"2250b5f3-dfa1-4ca7-bedf-fff5d41bd6ea","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Superconducting devices are electrical components and circuits that exploit superconductivity\u2014the zero electrical resistance and exclusion of magnetic flux (Meissner effect)\u2014to achieve behaviors unattainable with normal conductors. Examples include superconducting magnets, SQUIDs, Josephson-junction qubits, microwave resonators and detectors, which provide lossless current transport, ultrahigh sensitivity and low-noise performance when operated at cryogenic temperatures."},{"id":"91ed8eb4-208e-4fd8-a258-aeae8213de49","l":"Superconducting order parameter","d":2,"p":"ca8ddaad-441c-481c-93b3-65f46125fb93","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The superconducting order parameter is a complex-valued macroscopic field equal to the quantum expectation value of the Cooper-pair creation operator, which is nonzero in the superconducting state and zero in the normal state. Its magnitude measures the condensate (pair) density or gap amplitude and its phase gives the macroscopic quantum phase whose gradients produce supercurrents and whose winding numbers create vortices, reflecting spontaneous U(1) symmetry breaking."},{"id":"480737bf-e2eb-4385-8850-b23964f5eb1b","l":"Superconducting phase transition","d":2,"p":"ca8ddaad-441c-481c-93b3-65f46125fb93","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The superconducting phase transition is the thermodynamic change that occurs when a material is cooled below its critical temperature (or driven below its critical field/current) and abruptly enters a superconducting state with zero electrical resistance and expulsion of magnetic flux (Meissner effect). Microscopically it marks the formation of a coherent condensate of Cooper pairs and the onset of long-range phase coherence (broken U(1) symmetry), often occurring as a continuous (second-order) transition in conventional superconductors."},{"id":"88eb1f2f-c0a6-4e29-b761-b172957f50dd","l":"Superconducting quantum optics","d":2,"p":"632f74d1-d16b-4b92-a295-b24729a93d8d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Superconducting quantum optics is the study and implementation of quantum-optical phenomena using superconducting circuits\u2014Josephson-junction-based \"artificial atoms\" coupled to on\u2011chip microwave resonators\u2014where microwave photons and qubits interact in regimes analogous to cavity QED. It exploits strong, tunable light\u2013matter coupling and low dissipation to generate and control nonclassical states for fundamental studies and quantum information processing."},{"id":"1c7ace25-c6b5-4f32-ab12-13fe90d0e8cc","l":"Superconductor-insulator transition","d":2,"p":"19f26688-0955-4fd9-85d8-aaf71c4a581f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The superconductor\u2013insulator transition is a phase transition (often a quantum phase transition at T \u2192 0) in which a material or film is tuned from a zero\u2011resistance superconducting state to an insulating state by changing parameters such as disorder, magnetic field, carrier density, or thickness. Microscopically it corresponds to the loss of long\u2011range superconducting phase coherence and superfluid stiffness and the localization of charge carriers (either Cooper pairs in the bosonic scenario or single electrons in fermionic scenarios), with characteristic critical scaling near the transition."},{"id":"9d5b9d9b-e3ca-44f9-aee3-f64a9afdbed8","l":"Supercontinuum generation","d":2,"p":"bd03ea46-2406-4a24-8307-05b2b498fb4e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Supercontinuum generation is a nonlinear optical process in which an intense, typically ultrashort, laser pulse propagating through a dispersive nonlinear medium is transformed into an extremely broadband, near-continuous spectrum, often spanning hundreds of nanometers to multiple octaves. The dramatic spectral broadening results from combined effects such as self-phase modulation, four-wave mixing, soliton dynamics (including fission) and stimulated Raman scattering under appropriate dispersion and nonlinearity conditions."},{"id":"48cbad61-9656-4c65-8dab-3ac0580bd0f2","l":"Supercooled liquid","d":2,"p":"93461c37-4b2d-4092-9572-0faf35224cb9","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A supercooled liquid is a metastable liquid that has been cooled below its equilibrium freezing (melting) temperature without undergoing crystallization. Its disordered, fluid structure persists while viscosity and relaxation times grow (due to suppressed nucleation), eventually leading either to crystallization or to a glass transition."},{"id":"439d4fc1-0cef-4119-8847-d81322192cb7","l":"Superparamagnetism","d":2,"p":"b6e4cc90-18f3-4427-841d-c667375bbca0","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Superparamagnetism is the behavior of single-domain ferromagnetic or ferrimagnetic nanoparticles whose magnetic moment can thermally flip as a whole because the thermal energy (kB T) is comparable to or larger than the magnetic anisotropy energy barrier (K\u00b7V), so on measurement timescales the ensemble shows no remanence or coercivity and behaves like a very large paramagnet. Below a characteristic blocking temperature Tb (where K\u00b7V \u2273 kB T) the moments become \"blocked\" and the particles recover stable single-domain hysteresis."},{"id":"201f2354-95c5-49c1-a126-7790267a5ed8","l":"Surface acoustic wave","d":2,"p":"29146d99-215f-436f-9dbc-6dd06cd063c4","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A surface acoustic wave (SAW) is an elastic wave that propagates along the free surface of a solid with its amplitude decaying exponentially into the bulk (the common Rayleigh wave combines longitudinal and vertical shear motion). SAWs confine mechanical energy near the surface and can be generated, detected, and used in devices (filters, sensors, delay lines), especially on piezoelectric materials."},{"id":"ca58f976-78ce-4279-b515-7e8118060c94","l":"Surface differential reflectance spectroscopy","d":2,"p":"8156d7cb-6ab9-4097-be2b-4aa71aad93a3","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Surface differential reflectance spectroscopy is an optical technique that measures the small, wavelength-dependent change in a surface's reflectance (\u0394R/R) by comparing a modified surface state (adsorbate, ultrathin film, temperature- or field-modulated state) to a reference. By recording this differential spectrum\u2014often with polarization and lock-in/modulation detection\u2014it sensitively reveals surface electronic and vibrational transitions, thin-film growth, optical anisotropies, and changes in surface optical constants while suppressing the large bulk reflectance background."},{"id":"72ad545c-2aa9-47ac-971b-5b09d8d2e56c","l":"Surface-driven phase separation","d":2,"p":"d0f6584f-c5d7-4011-99bb-2a370e8f2b99","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Surface-driven phase separation is phase separation in a multicomponent system whose initiation, morphology, or kinetics are controlled by a boundary or interface that preferentially attracts one component. Such surfaces alter local thermodynamics and boundary conditions, producing composition gradients, wetting layers, or surface-directed spinodal decomposition near the interface."},{"id":"a4e5b8b0-38ad-44ad-a2dc-7a305721c5f7","l":"Surface-enhanced Raman spectroscopy","d":2,"p":"8156d7cb-6ab9-4097-be2b-4aa71aad93a3","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Surface-enhanced Raman spectroscopy (SERS) is a technique that greatly amplifies the weak inelastic (Raman) scattering of molecules adsorbed on or near nanostructured metal surfaces\u2014typically silver or gold\u2014by exploiting intense, localized electromagnetic fields produced by localized surface plasmon resonances and, to a lesser extent, charge-transfer (\u201cchemical\u201d) interactions with the metal. The plasmonic enhancement can increase Raman cross-sections by many orders of magnitude, enabling highly sensitive (even single-molecule) vibrational detection."},{"id":"7dc981b4-af43-438f-b618-bea48d59f640","l":"Surface phonon polaritons","d":2,"p":"69adcacb-6270-4cfc-8cf5-60097ec74d8b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Surface phonon polaritons are electromagnetic surface waves formed by strong coupling between infrared photons and optical phonons at the surface or interface of a polar dielectric, with fields evanescently confined perpendicular to the interface while propagating along it. Analogous to surface plasmon polaritons but involving lattice vibrations instead of free electrons, they occur near the material\u2019s Reststrahlen band and enable subwavelength mid-IR/THz light\u2013matter interactions."},{"id":"df6f637e-a45a-4607-a1b5-e5f75827d500","l":"Surface plasmon polariton","d":2,"p":"69adcacb-6270-4cfc-8cf5-60097ec74d8b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A surface plasmon polariton is an electromagnetic surface wave formed by the strong coupling between photons and collective oscillations of conduction electrons (plasmons) at a metal\u2013dielectric interface. It propagates along the interface with TM polarization, having fields that decay evanescently normal to the surface and a dispersion set by the materials' dielectric functions."},{"id":"37132a04-c784-4f13-aaf4-d75163a8b144","l":"Surface plasmons","d":2,"p":"0b46d855-14dd-4d47-8df1-76e3e60ebfd3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Surface plasmons are coherent collective oscillations of conduction electrons localized at a metal\u2013dielectric interface that couple to electromagnetic fields to form surface-bound electromagnetic waves whose fields decay evanescently away from the interface. Their dispersion differs from bulk plasmons, enabling strong subwavelength field confinement used in sensing and nanophotonics."},{"id":"7600fee4-52d4-46ff-bca9-7439ac394273","l":"Surface scattering","d":2,"p":"1d433fa8-4cdd-4e66-ae51-df1797f18d56","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Surface scattering is the process by which particles or waves interacting with a material boundary are deflected, absorbed, or re-emitted because of the surface's microscopic structure and electromagnetic or atomic properties, causing changes in direction, phase, and sometimes energy. It ranges from specular reflection at smooth interfaces to diffuse or inelastic scattering from roughness, defects, or surface excitations, and strongly influences optical, electronic, thermal, and neutron/phonon transport near interfaces."},{"id":"c21c30b0-2219-4793-9841-d9e9d3d0dd2c","l":"Susceptibility measurements","d":2,"p":"0003fc3f-1522-4fca-bf3f-bcef0dcec405","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Susceptibility measurements are experimental determinations of a material's susceptibility \u03c7, the linear response coefficient relating an induced polarization or magnetization to an applied electric or magnetic field (often as a function of frequency, temperature, or field). They quantify how strongly a system responds to external fields and are used to characterize dielectric and magnetic properties, phase transitions, and dynamical behavior."},{"id":"2ac8fe5c-f63f-4ae6-9b8e-753e85cb9c52","l":"Su-Schrieffer-Heeger model","d":2,"p":"a088bad1-4904-4f08-a625-a9840fdf32c7","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Su\u2013Schrieffer\u2013Heeger (SSH) model is a one-dimensional tight-binding model with a two-site unit cell and alternating (dimerized) hopping amplitudes that realizes two distinct insulating phases distinguished by a topological winding number. It was introduced to describe solitons in polyacetylene and is a paradigmatic minimal model of a symmetry-protected 1D topological insulator supporting robust zero-energy edge or domain-wall states."},{"id":"78696d1f-9f22-4dcd-aade-94c56d24db96","l":"Symmetry energy","d":2,"p":"63a9c708-1efc-49bb-9b67-fb6a39269235","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"Symmetry energy is the component of the nuclear binding energy (or nuclear equation of state) that quantifies the energy cost of neutron\u2013proton imbalance (isospin asymmetry). It appears in the semi-empirical mass formula as a term \u221d(N\u2212Z)^2/A and, as a density-dependent quantity in infinite matter, strongly influences the structure of neutron-rich nuclei and neutron stars."},{"id":"1d7ae980-38ba-4734-9ae4-35f53be4df73","l":"Synapses","d":2,"p":"ce1c6c0a-58ee-41a7-a3b2-83d268ffbda6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Synapses are specialized junctions between neurons that enable signal transmission: in chemical synapses an action potential triggers neurotransmitter release across a narrow cleft to bind postsynaptic receptors and modulate ion channels, while electrical synapses use gap junctions to pass ionic currents directly, allowing near\u2011instantaneous electrical coupling."},{"id":"8db6714b-7455-4b42-9b6d-0234c154f21f","l":"Synchrotron radiation facilities","d":2,"p":"7e4e985f-d2fb-46cf-9183-c6e4b4c1c61b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Synchrotron radiation facilities are large-scale research centers that produce intense, highly collimated, and tunable electromagnetic radiation (from infrared to hard X-rays) by steering relativistic electron beams around storage rings and through magnetic insertion devices (bending magnets, wigglers, undulators). These bright, stable photon beams are delivered to beamlines for high-resolution imaging, spectroscopy, and structural studies in physics, chemistry, materials science, and biology."},{"id":"0dd85e6e-270f-421f-aae6-1d6635b1fd58","l":"Syndiotactic polymers","d":2,"p":"20a16e31-69dd-4e1b-9a44-2df3b053d9fd","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Syndiotactic polymers are stereoregular polymers in which the substituent (pendant) groups on successive repeat units alternate in stereochemical configuration (\u2026R, S, R, S\u2026), so the pendants lie on alternating sides of the backbone. This alternating tacticity promotes regular packing and crystallinity and therefore alters the polymer\u2019s thermal and mechanical properties compared with atactic material."},{"id":"e0f079e8-0778-414c-bf16-19426bd5c240","l":"Synthetic antiferromagnetic multilayers","d":2,"p":"ff65e95b-d8de-402b-a6cb-4f266682e4c6","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Synthetic antiferromagnetic multilayers are engineered stacks of ferromagnetic layers separated by thin spacer layers (typically nonmagnetic metals) in which adjacent ferromagnetic moments are coupled antiparallel\u2014usually via indirect interlayer (RKKY) exchange\u2014so the stack behaves like an antiferromagnet with tunable coupling strength. They produce low net magnetization and reduced stray fields while retaining the properties of ferromagnets, making them useful in spintronic devices (sensors, MRAM) and fast magnetization dynamics."},{"id":"ea7945e9-6d62-4645-aaa8-ba7c953af711","l":"Targeted attacks","d":2,"p":"f014e3f5-6876-4e32-a7b6-c7ca1c6eb27b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Targeted attacks are deliberate, focused actions that aim to compromise, disrupt, or extract information from a specific component or element of a physical system by exploiting its known vulnerabilities. They are designed to maximize impact on the chosen target while minimizing collateral effects or detection, and occur in contexts from experimental sabotage to cybersecurity and adversarial interventions in quantum and classical systems."},{"id":"0275b3b8-2b29-4df9-9f1c-489e76689fa7","l":"Tavis-Cummings model","d":2,"p":"cb4dcf82-80bb-4305-94f4-12a11959d840","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"The Tavis\u2013Cummings model is a quantum-optical Hamiltonian describing N identical two-level atoms (or spins) collectively coupled to a single quantized bosonic mode under the rotating-wave approximation; it conserves total excitation number and reduces to the Jaynes\u2013Cummings model for N=1. It captures collective phenomena such as superradiance, collective Rabi oscillations and cooperative frequency shifts and is widely used in cavity and circuit QED."},{"id":"2653ebb8-5a11-44df-a2ad-dcffbf92f30f","l":"Tension tests","d":2,"p":"b777b985-75b5-420a-9f13-21bb83461999","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Tension tests (tensile tests) are materials tests in which a standardized specimen is subjected to a controlled uniaxial pulling (tensile) force until yield and often until fracture, while measuring load and elongation. The resulting stress\u2013strain data yield key mechanical properties such as Young\u2019s modulus, yield strength, ultimate tensile strength, elongation at break, and measures of ductility."},{"id":"70623632-e9d5-4eaa-b6b3-95c6b0ce0b9c","l":"Tensor network methods","d":2,"p":"05a47dcc-71c0-4ebc-9d4e-79a40a191efb","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Tensor network methods are numerical and analytical techniques that represent quantum many-body states or operators as networks of interconnected tensors whose graph structure encodes the pattern of entanglement, enabling compact approximations by truncating internal \"bond\" dimensions. They provide efficient ways to simulate and analyze strongly correlated quantum systems and perform entanglement-aware coarse-graining (examples include matrix product states, projected entangled pair states, and the multiscale entanglement renormalization ansatz)."},{"id":"8f3b4a45-4924-4fd8-a9a4-e53880feae29","l":"Tensor network renormalization","d":2,"p":"50ba3f74-7f31-4857-8a75-b2b007348981","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields","Physics of Living Systems","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Tensor network renormalization is a numerical coarse-graining procedure for tensor-network representations of many-body quantum states or classical partition functions that systematically contracts and compresses tensors to reduce degrees of freedom while preserving long-range correlations. By removing short-range entanglement at each step it drives the network toward scale-invariant fixed-point tensors, enabling extraction of universal data (e.g., critical exponents, central charge) and efficient simulation of critical and topological phases."},{"id":"d47bba71-117d-4810-a4e0-e6528994d02e","l":"Terahertz spectroscopy","d":2,"p":"07ec0e31-6cd2-4c32-97e1-ba06f99a5d87","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Terahertz spectroscopy uses electromagnetic radiation in the terahertz band (roughly 0.1\u201310 THz) to probe low-energy excitations in materials\u2014such as phonons, molecular rotations, intermolecular modes and charge-carrier dynamics. By measuring the amplitude and phase (or absorption and dispersion) of transmitted or reflected THz pulses or continuous waves, it yields the material's complex dielectric function or conductivity and reveals structural and dynamical properties on picosecond timescales."},{"id":"e585d49b-d45d-4ddf-b7c1-4f15ff757377","l":"Tethered particle motion","d":2,"p":"d21f7e96-ea6a-4c54-bed5-d62e78986605","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Tethered particle motion (TPM) is an experimental technique in which a microscopic bead is attached to a surface by a flexible tether (e.g., a polymer or DNA) and the bead's constrained Brownian motion is tracked; the amplitude and statistics of the bead's excursions reflect the tether's length, stiffness, and any molecular binding or conformational changes. It is widely used to infer single-molecule kinetics and mechanical properties without applying an external force."},{"id":"902c2351-569a-494d-bdf7-7e2996ff8f84","l":"Theoretical evolution","d":2,"p":"346899f4-4f01-4d98-87a3-1668696a3d48","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Theoretical evolution is the formulation and analysis of how physical systems change in time according to theoretical models and dynamical laws. It encompasses the mathematical evolution equations and formalisms\u2014e.g., Newtonian/Hamiltonian/Lagrangian dynamics, Schr\u00f6dinger or von Neumann equations, master equations, and renormalization-group flows\u2014used to predict the time dependence of states and observables."},{"id":"c8d96fc7-7105-414b-8cc1-e4c90c8c04a7","l":"Theory, design & simulation of reactors","d":2,"p":"d9e32a6d-d42e-430a-9665-10aaf0776e26","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics"],"def":"The theory, design, and simulation of reactors is the discipline that develops physical models and engineering concepts to predict, optimize, and ensure the performance and safety of energy-generating reactors (primarily fission and fusion). It combines reactor physics (neutronics), thermal\u2011hydraulics, materials and fuel\u2011cycle modeling, control and safety analysis into computational tools used for design, licensing, and operation."},{"id":"db2d0bec-0cda-4c1b-96cd-9a9771de4fe0","l":"Thermal conductivity of fluids","d":2,"p":"1d1e18e6-170c-403e-8786-d06fe58ddc94","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Thermal conductivity of fluids is the material property k that quantifies a fluid's ability to conduct heat by molecular energy transport; it appears in Fourier's law q = \u2212k\u2207T as the proportionality between heat flux and temperature gradient, with SI units W\u00b7m\u22121\u00b7K\u22121. It depends on temperature, pressure and composition (molecular collisions, diffusion, and intermolecular forces) and is generally much lower in gases than in solids or liquids."},{"id":"a5d028c8-ac70-402d-9247-6906807ac178","l":"Thermal cross-sections","d":2,"p":"fd8cf2dd-2362-4994-a45c-a9b453fe508d","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Thermal cross-sections are microscopic reaction cross-sections averaged over a thermal (Maxwell\u2013Boltzmann) energy distribution of incident particles\u2014typically neutrons\u2014at a specified temperature, representing the effective probability per target nucleus for processes like absorption, scattering or fission. They are temperature-dependent, commonly quoted for room-temperature neutrons (0.0253 eV, corresponding to 2200 m/s) and expressed in barns."},{"id":"766e5998-b1ad-4a2d-8def-fe5c8565eb98","l":"Thermal energy harvesting devices","d":2,"p":"3bab53f4-af17-4278-95a1-fa1b42b9231c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Thermal energy harvesting devices convert thermal energy\u2014from spatial temperature gradients or time-varying temperatures\u2014into usable electrical or mechanical power by exploiting physical effects such as the Seebeck (thermoelectric), pyroelectric, thermophotovoltaic, thermionic, or heat-engine (Stirling) principles. They are used to scavenge ambient or waste heat to power low-power sensors, electronics, or to improve overall system energy efficiency."},{"id":"ea633c66-c6b0-4e14-acaa-97e33637ab10","l":"Thermal Hall effect","d":2,"p":"1d1e18e6-170c-403e-8786-d06fe58ddc94","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The thermal Hall effect is the appearance of a heat current (or transverse temperature difference) perpendicular to an applied longitudinal temperature gradient when time-reversal symmetry is broken (for example by a magnetic field or intrinsic chirality). It is the heat-transport analogue of the electrical Hall effect and is quantified by an off-diagonal thermal conductivity \u03ba_xy arising from carriers such as electrons, phonons, or magnons."},{"id":"c661b646-27b5-4d52-bbc4-6185158fd692","l":"Thermal transport","d":2,"p":"1d1e18e6-170c-403e-8786-d06fe58ddc94","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Thermal transport is the transfer of thermal energy driven by temperature differences within and between systems. It occurs by conduction (microscopic heat carriers such as phonons and electrons in solids), by convection (bulk motion of fluids), and by thermal radiation (electromagnetic emission)."},{"id":"c6fb182e-9ea8-4f92-9062-0b063bce1659","l":"Thermodilatometry","d":2,"p":"c2ea95f8-c209-44d7-b8f7-a6baf632c46a","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Thermodilatometry is a thermal analysis technique that measures the change in an object\u2019s linear or volumetric dimensions as a function of temperature under controlled heating or cooling. It is used to determine coefficients of thermal expansion and to detect phase transitions, glass transitions, sintering, or other temperature\u2011dependent structural changes."},{"id":"1ae2d731-3bea-434d-afbe-62fd5a79fea6","l":"Thermodynamics of computation","d":2,"p":"609a2adc-099f-463f-9e2a-dd85c0101044","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Thermodynamics of computation studies the fundamental thermodynamic costs and limits of information processing, relating logical operations to physical quantities such as work, heat, and entropy production. It quantifies minimal energetic costs (e.g., Landauer's principle: erasing one bit requires at least k_B T ln 2 of dissipated heat) and motivates approaches like reversible computing and information engines to approach those limits."},{"id":"d72f7b98-2241-4ee0-ac5d-71934b12a3bf","l":"Thermodynamics of mixing","d":2,"p":"609a2adc-099f-463f-9e2a-dd85c0101044","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Thermodynamics of mixing describes the energetic and entropic changes when two or more components are combined, characterized by the enthalpy, entropy, and Gibbs free energy of mixing (\u0394G_mix = \u0394H_mix \u2212 T\u0394S_mix). It determines spontaneity, miscibility and phase behavior through changes in chemical potentials and activity coefficients, with ideal solutions having \u0394H_mix \u2248 0 and a positive combinatorial entropy of mixing."},{"id":"98437b05-f0c0-47aa-aba8-d73081519e1b","l":"Thermoelectrics","d":2,"p":"af9f5a05-6917-40cf-a2ff-5520ff506935","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Thermoelectrics are the phenomena, materials, and devices that convert heat differences directly into electric voltage (and vice versa) via coupled charge and heat transport in solids, principally through the Seebeck, Peltier, and Thomson effects. Their efficiency is set by material properties \u2014 Seebeck coefficient, electrical and thermal conductivity \u2014 typically combined into the dimensionless figure of merit ZT, and they are used for solid\u2011state power generation and refrigeration."},{"id":"cf87d589-c85d-44c1-b47c-bb16a13449b2","l":"Thermogravimetric analysis","d":2,"p":"c2ea95f8-c209-44d7-b8f7-a6baf632c46a","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) is an experimental technique that measures a sample\u2019s mass continuously as a function of temperature or time while the sample is subjected to a controlled heating program and atmosphere. It yields a thermogram (mass vs temperature/time) used to assess thermal stability, composition, decomposition, volatile loss, oxidation, and related mass-changing processes."},{"id":"091a904e-4721-44ff-a15b-52a6b8a5a26c","l":"Thermomechanical analysis","d":2,"p":"c2ea95f8-c209-44d7-b8f7-a6baf632c46a","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Thermomechanical analysis is the experimental study of how a material's mechanical response\u2014dimensional change, strain, stiffness, or creep\u2014varies with temperature, typically measured while applying a controlled load. It is used to determine properties such as the coefficient of thermal expansion, glass-transition or softening temperatures, and thermally driven deformation behavior."},{"id":"148f9078-177e-4e4e-b028-9806070d439e","l":"Thermometry","d":2,"p":"c2ea95f8-c209-44d7-b8f7-a6baf632c46a","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Thermometry is the science and practice of measuring temperature using thermometers and related methods. It covers the design and calibration of instruments, the use of temperature scales and reference points, and the characterization of measurement uncertainty and traceability to primary standards."},{"id":"928700ef-1305-43ae-ab15-117d0a461239","l":"Thermopower","d":2,"p":"6e14e903-9322-41db-b946-0b518ecafdf7","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Thermopower (Seebeck coefficient) is the proportionality S = -\u0394V/\u0394T (units V/K) that quantifies the voltage generated across a material per unit temperature difference. It arises from charge carriers diffusing under a thermal gradient, with the sign and magnitude reflecting carrier type and the energy dependence of transport."},{"id":"1709c324-0db6-4ba7-aff6-0d41456e5331","l":"Thermoreflectance","d":2,"p":"c2ea95f8-c209-44d7-b8f7-a6baf632c46a","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Thermoreflectance is the dependence of a material\u2019s optical reflectivity on its temperature, so small temperature changes produce measurable changes in reflected intensity (quantified by dR/dT). It is used as a non\u2011contact, high\u2011resolution optical thermometry and pump\u2013probe technique to map temperature and extract thermal properties of micro- and nanoscale structures."},{"id":"2d892cc0-2136-4996-a5a8-fa6c20172e44","l":"Theta pinch","d":2,"p":"5b4f3bb4-8db6-4156-a472-4c9988d6e313","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"A theta pinch is a plasma-confinement/compression scheme in which a rapidly changing axial magnetic field (Bz) induces an azimuthal (\u03b8-direction) current in the plasma; the resulting J\u03b8 \u00d7 Bz Lorentz force radially compresses or \"pinches\" the plasma. Unlike a Z-pinch, where an axial current produces the pinching azimuthal magnetic field, the theta-pinch relies on induced azimuthal currents from a time-varying axial field."},{"id":"6f143a0e-1eaa-4456-9ff0-908ed485147d","l":"Thiele equation","d":2,"p":"ba55d8a8-d049-4550-b264-530f74618214","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Thiele equation is an effective steady-state equation of motion for rigid magnetization textures (domain walls, vortices, skyrmions) obtained by projecting the Landau\u2013Lifshitz\u2013Gilbert dynamics onto collective coordinates; it expresses a balance between a gyrotropic (Magnus-like) force, dissipative forces, and conservative/external forces. It is commonly written in compact form as G \u00d7 v + D\u00b7v + F = 0, where G is the gyrovector, D the dissipation tensor, v the texture velocity, and F the net external force."},{"id":"864f97a3-75c3-43f4-b8b2-2ce1ef9d955a","l":"Third order nonlinear optical processes","d":2,"p":"bd03ea46-2406-4a24-8307-05b2b498fb4e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Third-order nonlinear optical processes are light\u2013matter interactions governed by the cubic term in the medium's polarization, P^(3) = \u03b50 \u03c7^(3) E^3, so the induced response scales with the cube of the driving electric field. They produce phenomena such as third-harmonic generation, four-wave mixing, the optical Kerr effect (intensity-dependent refractive index), self-phase modulation, and two-photon absorption, and are the lowest-order nonlinearity in centrosymmetric materials (where \u03c7^(2) = 0)."},{"id":"228e3971-f910-4f2c-9a84-ca221b8d90ab","l":"Thomas\u2013Fermi model","d":2,"p":"05a47dcc-71c0-4ebc-9d4e-79a40a191efb","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Thomas\u2013Fermi model is a semiclassical, statistical approximation that describes electrons in atoms, molecules, or solids by replacing the many-electron wavefunction with a local electron density determined self-consistently from the electrostatic potential, treating electrons as a degenerate Fermi gas. It yields a nonlinear Thomas\u2013Fermi equation for the density and gives approximate energies and charge distributions (accurate for large Z) while neglecting shell structure and detailed exchange\u2013correlation effects."},{"id":"94cd76e0-c4c8-49a6-b47c-aa18c2f85d8e","l":"Tidal hydroelectricity","d":2,"p":"4c12fcde-ed75-4e1c-bd19-253623703054","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Tidal hydroelectricity is the generation of electricity by converting the kinetic and potential energy of ocean tides\u2014driven by the gravitational pull of the Moon and Sun and Earth's rotation\u2014into electrical power using devices such as tidal barrages, lagoons, and in-stream turbines. It exploits predictable periodic sea-level differences and tidal currents to drive turbines or pumps that produce power."},{"id":"5e2e9bde-5b3f-4239-80bf-378c5e279976","l":"Tight-binding model","d":2,"p":"3817eab7-2f24-46f8-ba4a-aa6433722b4a","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The tight-binding model is a quantum lattice model that describes electrons in a crystal by expanding states in localized atomic-like orbitals and treating electronic motion as tunneling (hopping) between lattice sites. Its simple Hamiltonian\u2014on-site energies plus hopping matrix elements\u2014yields electronic energy bands and is especially useful for narrow-band and strongly localized systems."},{"id":"d18459ad-10a6-46ae-9d4e-89b232ac16d6","l":"Time crystals","d":2,"p":"050430d9-e7b1-4e3f-bbb3-975081181c3c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Time crystals are phases of matter that spontaneously break time-translation symmetry, exhibiting persistent, rigidly periodic motion in time instead of static spatial order. Experimentally realized as \"discrete\" or Floquet time crystals, they appear in driven many-body systems as robust subharmonic oscillations (periods that are integer multiples of the drive) stabilized by interactions and disorder."},{"id":"5d2efa87-c4b7-464b-b208-2d7475462205","l":"Time-dependent Ginzburg-Landau theory","d":2,"p":"f4585613-4c82-4c78-a57d-515fa4774cb6","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Time-dependent Ginzburg-Landau (TDGL) theory is a phenomenological, coarse-grained description of the nonequilibrium dynamics of an order parameter near a continuous phase transition (most commonly superconductivity), obtained by adding a first-order-in-time relaxation equation to the static Ginzburg\u2013Landau free-energy functional. It typically includes coupling to electromagnetic potentials and stochastic noise to model dissipative processes such as vortex motion, flux flow and phase-ordering kinetics."},{"id":"39f2a83e-a96f-407c-b1cf-0ef0c436d0d7","l":"Time-differential perturbed-angular correlation spectroscopy","d":2,"p":"d199d86e-97af-4dd1-9fe7-fdb00b298b96","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Time-differential perturbed-angular correlation (TDPAC) spectroscopy is a nuclear hyperfine technique that measures the time-dependent change in the angular correlation between successive \u03b3-rays emitted in a radioactive decay cascade, caused by local electric and magnetic fields at the probe nucleus. Analysis of the oscillation and damping of this time-dependent correlation yields hyperfine parameters (electric field gradients, magnetic fields and their fluctuation rates), giving atomic-scale information on the local electronic environment, structure and dynamics."},{"id":"dc2b1b8f-7c92-426c-92f5-e5d537e838c9","l":"Time-of-flight neutron spectroscopy","d":2,"p":"fd8cf2dd-2362-4994-a45c-a9b453fe508d","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Time-of-flight neutron spectroscopy is an experimental technique that determines neutron energies by measuring the time it takes neutrons from a pulsed source (or after a chopper) to travel a known flight path to a detector, allowing conversion of arrival times into neutron energies. By recording energy- and angle-resolved scattering from a sample, it provides momentum- and energy-dependent information about structural correlations and excitations (S(Q,\u03c9))."},{"id":"4b7bcf85-8b1f-409b-8d0a-dae40b4e3b6e","l":"Time-resolved infrared spectroscopy","d":2,"p":"8156d7cb-6ab9-4097-be2b-4aa71aad93a3","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Time-resolved infrared spectroscopy is an experimental technique that measures how a sample's infrared absorption or emission changes as a function of time after a triggering event (for example, optical excitation or a rapid chemical perturbation). By monitoring transient vibrational signatures on timescales from femtoseconds to milliseconds it reveals molecular structural changes, energy redistribution, and reaction kinetics."},{"id":"ba4c38b9-3608-4fd8-969f-5ae0dfbe90a4","l":"t-J model","d":2,"p":"a088bad1-4904-4f08-a625-a9840fdf32c7","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The t-J model is an effective lattice model for strongly correlated electrons derived from the large\u2011U limit of the Hubbard model, with a nearest\u2011neighbor electron hopping term (t) and an antiferromagnetic superexchange term (J). It is defined in a Hilbert space with no double occupancy, capturing the competition between kinetic energy and spin exchange in doped Mott insulators and widely used to study magnetism and unconventional superconductivity."},{"id":"cb2077a9-744c-49b6-980f-1992b7ccb4f8","l":"Tokamaks","d":2,"p":"5b4f3bb4-8db6-4156-a472-4c9988d6e313","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"A tokamak is a doughnut-shaped (toroidal) magnetic-confinement device that traps and heats plasma to conditions suitable for controlled thermonuclear fusion. It uses strong toroidal magnetic fields from external coils together with a large toroidal plasma current that produces a poloidal field, creating helical field lines that stabilize and confine the plasma."},{"id":"91dc0639-6f1c-438a-907b-22864452c003","l":"Topological defects","d":2,"p":"3331008f-47b2-4908-92ad-bc9bd7cd815e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Topological defects are stable, localized structures in a field or ordered medium where the order parameter cannot be made continuous everywhere due to the nontrivial topology of the space of allowed configurations. They typically form during symmetry-breaking transitions and are classified by conserved topological charges; examples include domain walls, vortices (strings), monopoles (points) and skyrmions (textures)."},{"id":"17172c5a-689d-48e0-9052-8e65ec6b9bf3","l":"Topological effects in photonic systems","d":2,"p":"99f32be9-888e-42c5-ba4a-1fdcea060307","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Topological effects in photonic systems are phenomena where light behavior is determined by global, discrete topological invariants of the system's band structure (e.g., Chern numbers or Zak phase), producing boundary or defect modes whose existence and properties are robust against local disorder. They are realized in photonic crystals, waveguide arrays, resonator lattices and metamaterials that emulate electronic topological phases\u2014yielding, for example, unidirectional, backscattering-immune edge transport and protected localization."},{"id":"cfdb482b-3fa7-4d4d-886b-e6cc39da64d1","l":"Topological Hall effect","d":2,"p":"99c1e71a-1639-42a5-8e00-6e258fc0b4f5","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Topological Hall effect is an additional Hall voltage generated when conduction electrons traverse noncoplanar magnetic textures (e.g., skyrmions), acquiring a real\u2011space Berry phase that acts like an emergent magnetic field. It appears as an extra contribution to the Hall resistivity, proportional to the texture's scalar spin chirality, distinct from ordinary and anomalous Hall effects."},{"id":"7181ba39-9925-4736-9b20-c05b03675b33","l":"Topological metamaterials","d":2,"p":"77fabbae-1f98-4300-996c-9158ab52e6fc","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Topological metamaterials are engineered, subwavelength-structured materials whose designed unit-cell geometry produces topologically nontrivial band structures, yielding boundary or defect modes that propagate robustly (e.g., without backscattering) against disorder and imperfections. They can be realized for electromagnetic, acoustic, mechanical, or elastic waves and exploit topological band theory to achieve protected waveguiding and localization."},{"id":"4f65979f-8a52-4ebb-bb0c-29db6fb979d0","l":"Topological tools","d":2,"p":"7b9db112-8475-42b4-ad22-f00f6b8635f8","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Topological tools are mathematical methods from topology used in physics to classify and quantify global, deformation\u2011invariant properties of systems\u2014features that remain unchanged under smooth deformations. Common examples include homotopy and homology groups, fiber bundles and characteristic classes (e.g., Chern numbers), and K\u2011theory/index theorems, which are used to characterize defects, topological phases, and protected boundary modes."},{"id":"b99dc135-7e94-41c6-af54-e7ca2fbcd379","l":"Topology","d":2,"p":"b5ff1838-0ecd-41a5-a90b-bae4acb69fcf","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Topology is the branch of mathematics that studies properties of geometric objects that are unchanged by continuous deformations (stretching or bending but not tearing or gluing), such as connectedness and the number of holes. In physics, topology is used to classify global features of fields, defects, and phases via topological invariants (e.g., winding numbers, Chern numbers), which lead to robust, quantized phenomena like topological insulators and protected edge states."},{"id":"e121608d-18d2-40e2-9913-babe6fd6bc8d","l":"Top quark production","d":2,"p":"97069ede-42dd-4f4d-b552-9fc8562cb02d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Top quark production is the creation of top quarks in high-energy particle collisions, typically at hadron colliders. It occurs mainly via strong-interaction pair production (gluon fusion and quark\u2013antiquark annihilation) or via electroweak single-top channels (t-channel, s-channel, and W-associated) and is used to probe the top's properties and beyond\u2013Standard-Model physics."},{"id":"d1d678cf-f841-45c5-908c-a0cf14bb182b","l":"Torque magnetometry","d":2,"p":"0003fc3f-1522-4fca-bf3f-bcef0dcec405","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Torque magnetometry is an experimental technique that determines a sample's magnetic moment and anisotropy by measuring the mechanical torque \u03c4 produced when the sample in a magnetic field experiences \u03c4 = \u03bc \u00d7 B (or \u03c4 = V M \u00d7 \u03bc0H). The tiny torque is detected via torsion balances or cantilever deflection, giving high-sensitivity information about magnetization, susceptibility anisotropy, and quantum oscillations."},{"id":"d1e4737b-53e6-48f9-8ba4-a9d44fcaaea0","l":"Touch","d":2,"p":"7b59f715-a6b8-4141-b762-668edfb84dc3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Touch is the physical interaction that occurs when two bodies come into contact at their surfaces, producing surface forces such as the normal force and friction. At the microscopic level it is mediated by electromagnetic interactions (electron cloud repulsion and interatomic potentials) and mechanical deformation rather than true point-to-point occupancy."},{"id":"dbd70901-ad16-451f-8947-2b8cb777cdb9","l":"Trade networks","d":2,"p":"d93a03e0-cbd7-4a22-8232-591d01e6a765","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Trade networks are networks whose nodes represent economic agents or locations and whose edges represent trade relationships or flows of goods, services, money, or information; edges are typically weighted and directed and can change over time. In physics, such networks are modeled with tools from network theory and statistical mechanics to study transport, flow dynamics, resilience, contagion, and emergent macroscopic patterns like inequality or market stability."},{"id":"e8e0410b-a629-447f-87a9-1974d9b2d98f","l":"Traffic models","d":2,"p":"3c88d04a-17da-443c-8428-781247d46a8a","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Traffic models are theoretical and computational descriptions of how many individual agents (vehicles or pedestrians) move and interact on roads or networks, used to predict flow, congestion, and emergent collective phenomena. They range from microscopic car-following and cellular-automaton/agent-based models to mesoscopic kinetic and macroscopic hydrodynamic descriptions, and capture features like fundamental diagrams, shock waves, and phase transitions to jammed states."},{"id":"70bf6387-5e34-4427-960b-cb40d070e693","l":"Transcriptomics","d":2,"p":"6b5443fa-9849-4d5b-966b-7d6e08113e36","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Transcriptomics is the large-scale study of the transcriptome\u2014the complete set of RNA molecules (messenger and noncoding RNAs) produced by a cell or tissue under particular conditions. It quantifies gene expression levels and splicing using high-throughput methods (e.g., RNA sequencing or microarrays) to reveal regulatory states and cellular responses."},{"id":"c8eef81a-e82d-47df-96e0-db4b1a9d9df4","l":"Transducers","d":2,"p":"2360e96e-a12b-4d21-9b65-36ed1177c0e6","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A transducer is a device that converts one form of energy or physical quantity into another, typically translating a measurand (mechanical, thermal, optical, chemical, etc.) into an electrical signal (sensor) or converting electrical energy into a physical effect (actuator). It provides a measurable input\u2013output relationship (transfer function) used for sensing, measurement, or control."},{"id":"237d2027-53a4-4ad2-a220-d78e7d1fd4d6","l":"Transfer matrix calculations","d":2,"p":"695da376-c44a-41dd-b968-d200c31e9d5e","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems","Plasma Physics"],"def":"Transfer matrix calculations use a matrix to relate the state of a linear system (for example wave amplitudes and their derivatives or forward/backward traveling components) at one position to its state at another, and compute the effect of propagation and interfaces by multiplying the matrices for successive regions. By concatenating these matrices one obtains overall transmission, reflection, and stability or band-structure properties for layered, periodic, or scattering systems."},{"id":"0acf1020-b2c9-4ef5-b0ab-7f59f809a3c6","l":"Transfer matrix method","d":2,"p":"82f6c5fd-bbef-4ff9-a5f9-de62d3f5dfeb","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The transfer matrix method is a linear-algebra technique that represents how a system's local state (for example wave amplitudes, fields, or partial partition-function contributions) changes from one position or layer to the next by multiplication with a transfer matrix. By composing these matrices for successive regions one can compute overall transmission/reflection, eigenmodes, or stability properties of one-dimensional or layered systems."},{"id":"acaa8bae-20ad-41df-8791-c124361f4ca1","l":"Transformation optics","d":2,"p":"8359ab1d-c3e9-4d53-bf7f-702075aaa8b0","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Transformation optics is a design framework that uses coordinate transformations of Maxwell\u2019s equations to compute spatially varying permittivity and permeability tensors that steer electromagnetic fields along prescribed paths. Realizing those effective material parameters (typically with metamaterials) enables devices such as cloaks, beam shifters, and unusual lenses."},{"id":"ce85a590-748b-47e4-a10b-a25c874f3bdf","l":"Transistors","d":2,"p":"2360e96e-a12b-4d21-9b65-36ed1177c0e6","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A transistor is a three\u2011terminal semiconductor device that controls, switches, or amplifies electrical signals by regulating the flow of charge carriers. Common types\u2014bipolar junction transistors (BJT) and field\u2011effect transistors (FET)\u2014operate via carrier injection across p\u2013n junctions or by modulating a conductive channel with an electric field (gate)."},{"id":"1233e97e-8593-44af-8651-24253580a5ac","l":"Transition metal alloys","d":2,"p":"96973abf-f1b5-44c5-9549-9f0f4dfe234f","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Transition metal alloys are metallic mixtures in which one or more principal components are transition (d\u2011block) elements; the partially filled d orbitals of these atoms strongly influence bonding and electronic structure. As a result they exhibit characteristic mechanical strength, electrical and thermal transport, magnetic behavior, and complex phase behavior (solid solutions, ordered intermetallics) that depend sensitively on composition and processing."},{"id":"8d99ef2a-53df-4d8d-ba33-e13adb7fb1e7","l":"Trial wave functions","d":2,"p":"05a47dcc-71c0-4ebc-9d4e-79a40a191efb","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Trial wave functions are parametrized, approximate wavefunctions used as educated guesses for a quantum system\u2019s state in variational and related methods. Their adjustable parameters are optimized (typically by minimizing the Hamiltonian expectation value via the variational principle) to produce upper-bound estimates of ground-state energies and approximate observables while respecting known symmetries and boundary conditions."},{"id":"3922ad9d-d6c8-41f8-8142-a0d7d7b1e317","l":"Triangular lattice","d":2,"p":"8bc71f5b-3de8-4994-8de2-1a300cec36b6","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A triangular lattice is a two-dimensional Bravais lattice whose points lie at the vertices of a regular tiling by equilateral triangles, so each lattice site has six nearest neighbors and primitive vectors separated by 60\u00b0. It is the densest 2D Bravais packing (often called the 2D hexagonal or equilateral triangular lattice)."},{"id":"5816d77f-6cf9-42f7-ab6f-3e354fd82b20","l":"T-symmetry","d":2,"p":"d0171af5-5603-4934-b23f-d95de0287b81","fl":["Properties","Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Time-reversal symmetry (T-symmetry) is the invariance of physical laws under reversal of the direction of time (t \u2192 \u2212t), which flips velocities, momenta and angular momenta and in quantum mechanics is implemented by an antiunitary time-reversal operator. While many microscopic equations are T-symmetric, certain quantum weak interactions violate T-symmetry, and macroscopic irreversibility (the thermodynamic arrow of time) arises from statistical boundary conditions rather than fundamental T-violation."},{"id":"bf33401a-fd7c-433e-903d-284762e06df1","l":"Tube theories","d":2,"p":"c267a3f3-0c34-4fc1-9825-947683caf4dd","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Tube theories are effective models that describe systems whose degrees of freedom are confined to narrow, quasi\u2013one\u2011dimensional regions (tubes). Examples include the Doi\u2013Edwards \"tube\" model of polymer entanglement (reptation) and flux\u2011tube/string models of confinement in gauge theories."},{"id":"e664d01a-c9cf-4af2-9b65-28f8267f5620","l":"Tunable lasers","d":2,"p":"cd4c012e-bacf-4bcf-8358-a6573a330ec4","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Tunable lasers are laser systems whose emission wavelength (or frequency) can be adjusted in a controlled way over a specified spectral range, either continuously or in discrete steps. Tuning is achieved by changing wavelength-selective elements or the gain medium conditions (for example: grating or etalon angle, cavity length, temperature, or injection current)."},{"id":"a89e3d02-d932-4cf8-8e77-eb919431c2a7","l":"Tunnel diode resonance","d":2,"p":"6fab7f86-2de1-4f52-9425-ed4f9aec008b","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Tunnel diode resonance is the quantum-mechanical effect in a tunneling junction (especially a double-barrier or Esaki-type diode) where the transmission probability and resulting current show sharp peaks when carrier energies align with discrete quasi-bound states or opposite-side energy levels, producing enhanced tunneling. This resonant alignment is responsible for the diode's peak current and associated negative differential resistance, enabling high-frequency oscillations."},{"id":"6f9f6f47-3cea-43a9-9739-d594e6448ec6","l":"Tunnel junctions","d":2,"p":"f495cfcf-afde-4cee-8daa-3ffffaa0e3a6","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A tunnel junction is an electrical contact formed by two conductors separated by an ultrathin insulating or nonconducting barrier through which charge carriers pass by quantum tunneling, producing a measurable current despite classically forbidden transmission. Such junctions underlie devices like tunnel diodes, Josephson junctions, magnetic tunnel junctions, and the scanning tunneling microscope, and show I\u2013V characteristics strongly sensitive to barrier thickness, materials, and quantum coherence."},{"id":"5cf50e0b-bcbc-491b-9fb3-4684dc9a20e5","l":"Turbulent mixing","d":2,"p":"062f0300-c33d-4782-b24c-43996b0469f3","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Nonlinear Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Turbulent mixing is the rapid stirring and homogenization of scalars (e.g., heat, mass) and momentum by chaotic, multi-scale eddies in a turbulent flow, producing much faster transport than molecular diffusion. Energy and scalar variance are transferred from large to smaller eddies until viscous and molecular processes at the smallest scales irreversibly mix the fields."},{"id":"9fec756e-3c66-4d30-a450-eedceb58cdd3","l":"Twinning","d":2,"p":"7060bde3-9f16-411d-9d68-3328cf14b019","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Twinning is the formation within a crystal of two or more regions (twins) whose lattices are related by a specific symmetry operation\u2014typically a reflection or simple rotation\u2014across a coherent planar interface called a twin boundary (or twin plane). It occurs during growth, phase transformations, or mechanical deformation and provides a distinct mechanism for shape change and property modification in materials."},{"id":"982e157e-15b4-4ad0-8745-0cd5760cfac6","l":"Twist deformation","d":2,"p":"77d491f8-55ad-47a6-a0a7-cd24ffce7602","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Twist deformation is a mechanical deformation in which material elements rotate about a common axis so that cross-sections turn relative to one another along the body\u2019s length (torsion), producing shear strains and shear stresses rather than pure extension or bending. It is characterized by the angle of twist per unit length (or total twist) of the object."},{"id":"80e8d55b-69ad-4f7f-aae8-aef2fec22c18","l":"Twisted bilayer graphene","d":2,"p":"a0106d33-6e0e-4b73-8190-a953b55d037a","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Twisted bilayer graphene is two sheets of graphene stacked with a small relative rotation, producing a long-wavelength moir\u00e9 superlattice that strongly modifies the electronic band structure. At certain \"magic\" twist angles the moir\u00e9 bands become very flat, quenching kinetic energy and enabling strong-correlation phenomena such as correlated insulating states and unconventional superconductivity."},{"id":"197978c3-3c88-49bf-a1bf-e7ac3b3ae5a7","l":"Twisted bilayers","d":2,"p":"3bd34535-a0eb-4c20-9080-8877f534f6b5","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Twisted bilayers are two stacked two-dimensional crystalline sheets (for example graphene) rotated by a finite angle relative to each other, producing a long-wavelength moir\u00e9 superlattice that alters interlayer hybridization. The twist angle strongly reshapes the electronic and optical band structure\u2014at certain \u201cmagic\u201d angles it can produce nearly flat bands and enhanced electron correlations, giving rise to superconductivity, correlated insulating states, and other emergent phenomena."},{"id":"b2e7a01e-4277-4515-a454-56b9477c9a13","l":"Twisted heterostructures","d":2,"p":"3bd34535-a0eb-4c20-9080-8877f534f6b5","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Twisted heterostructures are stacks of two-dimensional crystalline layers (typically van der Waals materials) rotated with respect to one another so their lattices produce a long-wavelength moir\u00e9 superlattice. The moir\u00e9 potential drastically alters electronic and optical band structures in an angle-dependent way, enabling tunable phenomena such as flat bands, correlated insulating and superconducting states, and novel excitonic or topological behavior."},{"id":"345bc492-1944-418f-a806-21a93b59145c","l":"Twisted multilayer graphene","d":2,"p":"a0106d33-6e0e-4b73-8190-a953b55d037a","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Twisted multilayer graphene refers to two or more graphene sheets stacked with a relative rotational misalignment (a twist angle) that produces a moir\u00e9 superlattice. The resulting interference pattern dramatically alters the electronic bands\u2014often producing flat bands and enhanced electron correlations\u2014leading to emergent phenomena such as correlated insulators, superconductivity, and nontrivial topology that depend sensitively on twist angle and layer count."},{"id":"a5c7649b-3b6b-424a-af3a-540b511d8d5c","l":"Twisted multilayers","d":2,"p":"3bd34535-a0eb-4c20-9080-8877f534f6b5","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Twisted multilayers are stacks of atomically thin crystalline sheets (e.g., graphene or transition\u2011metal dichalcogenides) in which one or more layers are rotated by a finite twist angle relative to the others, producing a long\u2011wavelength moir\u00e9 superlattice. The moir\u00e9 modulation strongly reshapes electronic, optical and mechanical properties\u2014tuning band dispersions, creating flat bands, and enabling correlated and topological phases."},{"id":"0421be99-33ef-411a-8c39-9cdbc7bb8f4a","l":"Two-dimensional electron gas","d":2,"p":"8fc60c42-47b7-46f9-85a7-8c09b9e3cdbd","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A two-dimensional electron gas (2DEG) is a layer of conduction electrons confined so they can move freely in two spatial dimensions while their motion is quantized in the perpendicular direction, typically formed at semiconductor heterojunctions, quantum wells, or oxide interfaces. This confinement produces discrete subbands and often high mobility, underpinning phenomena such as the quantum Hall effect and mesoscopic transport."},{"id":"4240c627-0c28-4c58-9c5d-614bc2d09f02","l":"Uhlmann phase","d":2,"p":"9f7a4cce-0ded-4976-b8ad-cc75830297fa","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Uhlmann phase is a geometric phase for mixed quantum states, defined as the holonomy acquired by purifications of a density operator under Uhlmann parallel transport along a path in state space. It generalizes the Berry phase to density matrices (reducing to the Berry phase for pure states) and is constructed from the Uhlmann connection/Fidelity structure on the bundle of purifications."},{"id":"b363ddc7-599d-4308-8b6b-f51ce783360f","l":"Ultrafast magnetization dynamics","d":2,"p":"3e80884b-56d9-4ed5-9f94-7e835761cb6e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Ultrafast magnetization dynamics are the time-dependent changes of a material\u2019s magnetization occurring on femtosecond-to-picosecond timescales, typically induced by ultrashort optical pulses, THz fields, or fast electrical currents. These dynamics arise from rapid electronic excitation and energy/ angular-momentum exchange among electrons, spins and the lattice, producing phenomena such as ultrafast demagnetization, coherent spin precession, and sub-picosecond magnetic switching."},{"id":"c35dddca-b514-4561-954c-e3b4ad2f1912","l":"Ultrashort pulses","d":2,"p":"2007366a-9fc5-499c-90bd-02ba78a14762","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Ultrashort pulses are electromagnetic pulses whose temporal duration is extremely brief\u2014typically in the picosecond-to-attosecond range (most commonly femtoseconds to attoseconds), often spanning only a few to thousands of optical cycles. Their very short duration yields a broad spectral bandwidth and high peak power, allowing time-resolved probing and control of ultrafast electronic, molecular, and atomic dynamics."},{"id":"0a3aa840-b81e-4a59-a119-b91cb6514b6d","l":"Ultrasonography","d":2,"p":"d794d6b2-dac3-40e5-906e-24fc867d825f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Ultrasonography is a non\u2011ionizing imaging technique that uses pulses of high\u2011frequency sound (ultrasound, typically 1\u201320 MHz) emitted and detected by a piezoelectric transducer; echoes produced at acoustic\u2011impedance boundaries are time\u2011of\u2011flight and amplitude\u2011analyzed to form real\u2011time images of internal structures. Doppler ultrasonography measures frequency shifts of the echoes to quantify motion such as blood flow."},{"id":"011df754-7d14-4d82-85d4-d33b3166ccf6","l":"Ultrasound techniques","d":2,"p":"29146d99-215f-436f-9dbc-6dd06cd063c4","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Ultrasound techniques employ high-frequency acoustic waves (typically above 20 kHz and often in the kHz\u2013MHz range) generated and detected by transducers to probe internal structure, motion, and material properties by measuring reflected, transmitted, scattered, or Doppler-shifted signals. By analyzing time-of-flight, amplitude, frequency shift and phase information, these methods enable imaging (e.g., medical sonography), flow measurement, and nondestructive testing."},{"id":"5cd15dc4-372e-459d-a18f-7e4d15e8705c","l":"Ultrathin films","d":2,"p":"08087671-73b2-4a66-a8ae-079c75cb9b33","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Ultrathin films are continuous solid layers whose thickness is comparable to a few atomic layers up to a few nanometers, so that surface and interface effects and quantum confinement strongly modify their structural, electronic, optical, and magnetic properties compared with the bulk. They are produced by techniques like molecular-beam epitaxy, sputtering, or atomic-layer deposition and are central to nanoscale devices and studies of low-dimensional physics."},{"id":"88f9ae06-e804-4e9d-9c58-f14782bd53c7","l":"Uncertainty relation","d":2,"p":"48672257-b4c7-4558-b1ed-49a79dfe913e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Uncertainty relation: In quantum mechanics, certain pairs of observables (for example position x and momentum p) cannot both be sharply defined for a given state; their standard deviations satisfy \u0394x\u00b7\u0394p \u2265 \u0127/2. More generally, for any two operators A and B the uncertainties obey \u0394A\u00b7\u0394B \u2265 (1/2)|\u27e8[A,B]\u27e9|, a consequence of operator noncommutativity rather than mere measurement disturbance."},{"id":"a1507851-4650-442c-ab00-f7a24d27e6fb","l":"Uncorrelated percolation","d":2,"p":"7f613135-db34-40bd-b19e-e520cf4b29f4","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Uncorrelated percolation is the standard percolation model in which each site or bond of a lattice (or point in continuum percolation) is occupied independently with probability p, so there are no spatial correlations between occupation variables. As p is varied the system exhibits a geometric phase transition at a critical p_c where an infinite connected cluster first appears and the correlation length and cluster-size distribution show universal critical scaling."},{"id":"c4d611dc-c5a0-41bd-8329-b437a2097bc4","l":"Undulators & wigglers","d":2,"p":"7e4e985f-d2fb-46cf-9183-c6e4b4c1c61b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Undulators and wigglers are periodic magnetic insertion devices in accelerators that force relativistic electrons into transverse oscillations, causing them to emit intense synchrotron radiation. In an undulator the magnetic strength is low so emissions from many periods interfere constructively to give narrow, quasi\u2011monochromatic harmonics, while a wiggler has stronger fields that produce large-angle oscillations and a broader, incoherent spectrum like many independent bend magnets."},{"id":"2a1e1a09-f7d8-44d8-8560-9efdbd01a032","l":"Upconversion","d":2,"p":"bd03ea46-2406-4a24-8307-05b2b498fb4e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Upconversion is a process in which two or more lower-energy (longer-wavelength) photons are absorbed either simultaneously or sequentially and their energies combine to produce emission of a single higher-energy (shorter-wavelength) photon (an anti\u2011Stokes process). It can occur via nonlinear optical mixing (e.g., sum-frequency generation or two-photon absorption) or via energy-transfer upconversion in doped materials such as rare\u2011earth ions."},{"id":"ed4c3f12-282b-4fac-af5b-d2b3f1dad53e","l":"Upper & lower hybrid plasma waves","d":2,"p":"4f513e0c-b639-4505-9116-7a0613323cf4","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Upper-hybrid waves are high-frequency, predominantly electrostatic oscillations in a magnetized plasma in which electron plasma oscillations couple to electron cyclotron motion; their resonance frequency is \u03c9_uh = sqrt(\u03c9_pe^2 + \u03c9_ce^2) (\u03c9_pe and \u03c9_ce are the electron plasma and cyclotron frequencies). Lower-hybrid waves are lower-frequency, mainly electrostatic modes produced by coupling between electron and ion motions for nearly perpendicular propagation, with a characteristic frequency approximately \u03c9_lh \u2248 (\u03c9_ci \u03c9_ce)^{1/2} (\u03c9_ci is the ion cyclotron frequency)."},{"id":"946ab036-e7b7-482b-83ba-ed94d162909d","l":"Usadel equation","d":2,"p":"7299559c-15c4-47f3-b866-5633b1030453","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Usadel equation is a diffusion-like nonlinear differential equation for the momentum-averaged (isotropic) quasiclassical Green's function that describes how superconducting pair correlations and the order parameter vary in disordered (diffusive, \"dirty\") superconductors and normal metals. Derived from the Eilenberger equations by impurity averaging, it is widely used to model proximity effects, Josephson transport, and spectral/thermal properties in superconducting heterostructures."},{"id":"39f369f1-f48a-4dce-858c-8337d41ec94d","l":"Vacancies","d":2,"p":"7060bde3-9f16-411d-9d68-3328cf14b019","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Vacancies are point defects in a crystalline solid where one or more lattice sites are unoccupied by atoms. They affect material properties by enabling atomic diffusion and altering local electronic, optical, and mechanical behavior."},{"id":"7f4db98c-7148-4a1b-b88f-51d55b9e2f38","l":"Vacuum interfaces","d":2,"p":"628f7190-cb71-4692-8cda-ebc544f9e9fb","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A vacuum interface is the boundary between a region of vacuum and an adjacent medium (solid, liquid, plasma or field-filled region) across which material properties and boundary conditions change. It governs surface phenomena\u2014reflection, emission, surface electronic states, and vacuum-fluctuation effects (e.g., Casimir forces)\u2014by setting the matching conditions for fields and particles at the surface."},{"id":"859c1c76-bd2c-446c-88f8-e4d90dd33bc0","l":"Valence","d":2,"p":"7c6cdbf5-0145-41f4-b17c-5725b4146ab4","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Valence (valency) is the combining capacity of an atom or ion, usually given by the number of electrons it can lose, gain, or share to form chemical bonds. It typically equals the number of unpaired electrons in the atom\u2019s outer (valence) shell and thus determines how many bonds the atom commonly forms."},{"id":"a5d4ce92-0e19-4cf5-bfd8-8ac4d0195c03","l":"van der Waals systems","d":2,"p":"f111f126-342c-48fd-baa7-6124af4142eb","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Van der Waals systems are materials or assemblies whose structure and binding are governed primarily by van der Waals (dispersion) forces\u2014weak, noncovalent attractive interactions arising from correlated instantaneous charge fluctuations. Examples include molecular solids, colloids, physisorbed films and layered two-dimensional materials (van der Waals heterostructures) whose components are held together without strong chemical bonds."},{"id":"ae26b7a8-0016-4431-ab95-a06d85739ab2","l":"Van Hove singularity","d":2,"p":"aaff2519-9891-4d4e-a067-1ea36b6b08d4","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A Van Hove singularity is a non\u2011analytic feature in the density of states of a crystal that occurs at energies where the band dispersion has a critical (stationary) point\u2014i.e., where the group velocity \u2207kE(k) vanishes. Depending on dimensionality and the type of critical point (maximum, minimum, or saddle) it appears as a divergence, logarithmic peak, or discontinuity and strongly enhances electronic, optical, or vibrational responses."},{"id":"5516bedc-1b8e-4b93-b3a2-926781651332","l":"Variational wave functional methods","d":2,"p":"3817eab7-2f24-46f8-ba4a-aa6433722b4a","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Variational wave functional methods are techniques in quantum many-body and field theory where one chooses a parameterized trial wave functional \u03a8[\u03c6(x)]\u2014a functional that assigns amplitudes to field or configuration profiles\u2014and optimizes its parameters (usually by minimizing the expectation value of the Hamiltonian) to approximate the ground state or low-energy properties. Time-dependent versions use a similar ansatz and a variational principle on the action to derive effective equations of motion for the ansatz parameters."},{"id":"9ab2b3dd-ba31-4ddf-8b04-e72cc06a3003","l":"Vertebrate development","d":2,"p":"93b8baac-44de-4a26-a175-357ad9d8744c","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Vertebrate development is the sequence of embryonic and early-life processes by which a fertilized egg builds a vertebrate body plan, forming germ layers, body axes, organs, and specialized tissues. It is driven by regulated cell division, migration and differentiation under genetic control and by physical mechanisms such as morphogen gradients, mechanical forces, and tissue mechanics that shape morphogenesis and growth."},{"id":"fb9b38ab-fde2-49ea-a5b1-3adb6f323135","l":"Vertex copying models","d":2,"p":"6f3f5cf1-363e-4d04-ac63-ca4ae90b47fc","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Vertex copying models are stochastic network-growth rules in which a newly introduced vertex chooses an existing \"prototype\" vertex and creates edges by copying some or all of that prototype's neighbors (often with probabilistic additions or deletions). These dynamics naturally produce high clustering and heavy-tailed degree distributions and are used to model the structure of web, citation, and biological networks."},{"id":"96b688f3-33b0-4f02-a6f1-9e5b455dbceb","l":"Vesicles","d":2,"p":"c2ea9e2c-0e15-49ee-b336-111976b0b965","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Vesicles are closed, fluid-filled compartments bounded by a thin lipid bilayer membrane\u2014either biological (e.g., endosomes, synaptic vesicles) or synthetic (liposomes). Their shapes and dynamics are governed by membrane mechanics (bending rigidity and tension), interfacial curvature, and coupling to enclosed and external fluids, leading to characteristic responses to forces, flows, and osmotic gradients."},{"id":"df30acff-987c-4b88-b32c-58b4e3887f8e","l":"Vesicle structures","d":2,"p":"9ccf11df-5b72-4ce0-92e6-e48f8dda172b","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Vesicle structures are closed, membrane-bound compartments formed by the self-assembly of amphiphilic molecules (e.g., lipids or block copolymers) into a bilayer that encloses a volume of fluid. Their shapes and dynamics are controlled by membrane mechanics (bending rigidity, spontaneous curvature, surface tension) and hydrodynamics, producing thermal fluctuations, shape transitions, budding and fusion."},{"id":"5923ceb4-c0e8-4989-a5a7-74f3e7555870","l":"Vibronic effects","d":2,"p":"ff5d90d2-4959-4829-b1ac-b56c5dc765df","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Vibronic effects are phenomena that arise from coupling between a molecule's (or solid's) electronic states and its nuclear vibrational motions, so that electronic transitions are accompanied by changes in vibrational excitation and electronic and vibrational degrees of freedom are mixed. They reflect nonadiabatic behavior (breakdown of the Born\u2013Oppenheimer approximation) and crucially shape optical spectra, transition intensities, relaxation, and charge/energy transfer processes."},{"id":"51bdc1d8-0b2c-4ff1-9b35-c11ffcf21f62","l":"Vibronic spectroscopy","d":2,"p":"d8d9518f-91ee-40ab-9529-2c3f98431a0f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics"],"def":"Vibronic spectroscopy is the study of molecular spectra arising from combined electronic and vibrational transitions, which reveals how changes in electronic state couple to vibrational modes and produces characteristic vibronic progressions governed by the Franck\u2013Condon principle and related coupling mechanisms."},{"id":"8343b1db-7af3-45de-b5f6-124cb4969000","l":"Vicsek model","d":2,"p":"d7c049d9-95bf-40ab-804d-a50f21faaf04","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"The Vicsek model is a minimal, discrete-time model of active matter in which point particles move at constant speed and at each step align their direction to the average heading of neighbors within a finite radius, subject to random angular noise. It exhibits a nonequilibrium phase transition from disordered motion to coherent flocking, illustrating how local alignment rules produce large-scale collective motion."},{"id":"d4f25f6b-c9e3-4af9-8be7-ecbfc836fbb4","l":"Virial theorem","d":2,"p":"695da376-c44a-41dd-b968-d200c31e9d5e","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems","Plasma Physics"],"def":"The virial theorem relates the long-time (or ensemble) average of a bound system's kinetic energy to its forces or potential energy: in general 2\u27e8T\u27e9 = \u27e8r\u00b7\u2207V\u27e9, and for a potential homogeneous of degree k it gives 2\u27e8T\u27e9 = k\u27e8V\u27e9. For the inverse-square gravitational or Coulomb potential (k = \u22121) this reduces to 2\u27e8T\u27e9 + \u27e8V\u27e9 = 0."},{"id":"0a93dde2-1bbf-44d2-8a5d-983a23d52489","l":"Viruses","d":2,"p":"91278658-51b4-4d8e-a936-7cfad1266625","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Viruses are submicroscopic (typically 20\u2013300 nm) infectious particles made of nucleic acid (DNA or RNA) enclosed in a protein capsid and sometimes a lipid envelope. They lack cellular structure and metabolic machinery and can reproduce only by hijacking a host cell's molecular machinery."},{"id":"ec2bfc95-97d4-42e2-b826-81c40ea63440","l":"Viscometers","d":2,"p":"2a7e97bc-0a5f-4b55-a310-75aae0ede940","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Viscometers are instruments that measure a fluid\u2019s viscosity\u2014its resistance to shear or flow\u2014by quantifying how the fluid responds to a known force or deformation (e.g., shear stress versus shear rate or flow under gravity). Different designs yield dynamic or kinematic viscosity values, with common types including capillary, rotational (cone-and-plate or Couette), falling-sphere, and oscillatory/damped viscometers."},{"id":"b1cff8a3-aa14-4433-871f-d2c49bb8d261","l":"Volumetric adsorption measurements","d":2,"p":"229eca6d-30f1-4c05-9a78-c352de8326b1","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Volumetric adsorption measurements are laboratory methods (often called manometric or pressure\u2011change techniques) that quantify the amount of gas a solid adsorbent takes up at controlled temperature by measuring pressure or volume changes in a calibrated gas-handling system. From the equilibrium pressures and known dead volumes the adsorbed quantity is calculated (typically via the ideal gas law), producing adsorption isotherms used to characterize surface area, porosity and adsorption thermodynamics."},{"id":"f5c31d1b-ba98-4dd3-982f-85539bb5044b","l":"Vortex lattices","d":2,"p":"ed63100a-e62b-42e6-9405-93c81012cd30","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Vortex lattices are ordered arrays of vortices\u2014localized cores of circulating flow or quantized magnetic flux\u2014that form in rotating superfluids, Bose\u2013Einstein condensates, and type-II superconductors. Each vortex carries quantized circulation (or one flux quantum in superconductors), and the lattice spacing and symmetry (commonly a triangular/Abrikosov pattern) are set by the rotation rate or applied magnetic field and by interactions that minimize the system's free energy."},{"id":"cde93d0d-5450-4f6f-8fa9-38a7d22aa56c","l":"Vortices in field theory","d":2,"p":"cf6fcf92-5d05-4722-a1b3-c076e8b22b9e","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Vortices in field theory are localized, topologically nontrivial solutions of the field equations where a complex order parameter winds by an integer multiple of 2\u03c0 around a core in which the field magnitude vanishes, producing quantized circulation and, in gauge theories, confined magnetic flux. They appear as pointlike defects in two dimensions or linelike defects in three dimensions (e.g., Abrikosov vortices in superconductors and Nielsen\u2013Olesen vortices in the Abelian\u2013Higgs model) and play important roles in condensed matter and cosmology."},{"id":"b35990fc-9fc9-490b-86ec-e6c53407c5fa","l":"Wannier function methods","d":2,"p":"3817eab7-2f24-46f8-ba4a-aa6433722b4a","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Wannier function methods construct an orthonormal set of spatially localized basis functions (Wannier functions) by unitary transformation of the crystal Bloch eigenstates, yielding a compact real-space representation of electronic states. They are used to derive tight-binding models, efficiently interpolate band structures, and compute electronic response and topological properties (e.g., Berry phases, polarization, and transport)."},{"id":"f049647a-30f7-4c29-96ba-0216c7c96bb9","l":"Water","d":2,"p":"93461c37-4b2d-4092-9572-0faf35224cb9","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Water is the molecular substance H2O, made of two hydrogens covalently bound to an oxygen; its polar molecules form extensive hydrogen bonds that give it distinctive physical properties (high specific heat, surface tension, dielectric constant, and a density maximum near 4 \u00b0C) and enable it to occur commonly as liquid, solid (ice), and vapor."},{"id":"7fe4ef17-3503-44ef-ab52-57fcf3529d77","l":"Watts-Strogatz model","d":2,"p":"6f3f5cf1-363e-4d04-ac63-ca4ae90b47fc","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"The Watts\u2013Strogatz model is a generative network model that starts from a regular ring lattice of N nodes each connected to k nearest neighbors and randomly rewires each edge with probability p to interpolate between order and randomness. For intermediate p it produces \"small-world\" networks that combine high local clustering with short average path lengths, matching structural features of many real-world systems."},{"id":"59e4f802-0901-44d3-a830-6fff59de997c","l":"Wave energy","d":2,"p":"6a87a113-159a-46b4-8ebb-ebc179f00384","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Wave energy is the energy carried and transported by a propagating disturbance\u2014mechanical or electromagnetic\u2014arising from oscillations of the medium (stored as kinetic and potential energy) or of the fields. For linear waves the local energy density and the energy flux (intensity) scale with the square of the amplitude and are conveyed at the wave's group velocity."},{"id":"12a67a34-1391-42e9-b91a-e8f45b4b04ae","l":"Waveguide arrays","d":2,"p":"1198f0b2-1f1a-416c-94f0-5e80ea9bc191","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Waveguide arrays are engineered lattices of closely spaced optical (or electromagnetic) waveguides in which evanescent coupling lets light hop between neighbors, producing discrete diffraction and a photonic band structure analogous to electrons in a crystal. They provide a platform for controlling light propagation and studying phenomena such as localization, nonlinear solitons, and topological photonics."},{"id":"e73da731-be57-4947-9f54-7d1c9900c8b1","l":"Weak antilocalization","d":2,"p":"7d9b7d96-c9e6-455f-9fa7-86088b7df853","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Weak antilocalization is a quantum-interference effect in disordered conductors with strong spin\u2013orbit coupling whereby time-reversed electron trajectories interfere destructively, reducing backscattering and producing a small enhancement of the low\u2011temperature conductivity. This interference is destroyed by a magnetic field, giving a characteristic suppression of the conductivity (a low-field magnetoresistance feature)."},{"id":"0677d705-89b4-4e49-bb4d-ecc8d57d6063","l":"Weak boson production","d":2,"p":"97069ede-42dd-4f4d-b552-9fc8562cb02d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Weak boson production is the creation of the electroweak gauge bosons W\u00b1 and Z^0 in high-energy particle interactions, typically via processes such as quark\u2013antiquark annihilation (Drell\u2013Yan), vector-boson fusion, or associated production. Measurements of these production rates and kinematics at colliders test the Standard Model, probe electroweak couplings, and constrain parton distribution functions."},{"id":"25ffa683-96b5-4212-981a-988786784565","l":"Weak links","d":2,"p":"f495cfcf-afde-4cee-8daa-3ffffaa0e3a6","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Weak links are localized regions of reduced superconducting order or coupling between superconducting regions (for example constrictions, grain boundaries, or thin insulating barriers) that permit Josephson tunneling of Cooper pairs and sustain a phase difference and supercurrent across the connection. Because their coupling is weaker than the bulk, weak links limit the critical current and are sites for phase slips, dissipation, and altered vortex dynamics."},{"id":"72273560-4fac-43d8-9a6c-59313e7bba70","l":"Weak localization","d":2,"p":"7d9b7d96-c9e6-455f-9fa7-86088b7df853","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Weak localization is a quantum-interference effect in disordered conductors where constructive interference between time-reversed electron scattering paths enhances backscattering, producing a small reduction in conductance (increase in resistance) at low temperatures. It requires phase coherence and is suppressed by dephasing, magnetic fields, or strong spin\u2013orbit scattering."},{"id":"65b6d8ee-955e-49db-8b9d-c0c8a7c27ddc","l":"Wear","d":2,"p":"4e823153-8e04-4bdb-a7ca-0f608ecd9453","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Wear is the progressive removal, transfer, or deformation of material from a solid surface produced by mechanical interaction with another surface, particles, or a fluid, leading to changes in surface topography and properties. Common mechanisms include adhesive, abrasive, corrosive/chemical, and fatigue wear, and it controls component lifetime, friction, and efficiency."},{"id":"15844c28-c28a-49af-ae5c-e83e7c4a41bc","l":"Welding","d":2,"p":"c4ed35b0-1090-409c-9667-85f9ac03e9c0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Welding is the process of permanently joining materials\u2014typically metals or thermoplastics\u2014by applying localized heat, pressure, or both so the surfaces coalesce via melting and solidification or by solid\u2011state diffusion/plastic flow. It involves controlled energy transfer (e.g., arc, flame, laser, friction, ultrasonic), phase changes and metallurgical bonding, and may use filler material to form a continuous joint."},{"id":"362c905e-258e-4aef-b297-54c2b1ea1a6d","l":"Weyl fermions","d":2,"p":"4fcc035e-7eb4-46f6-b347-5cc975e5ed95","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Weyl fermions are massless spin-1/2 particles described by two-component solutions of the Weyl (massless Dirac) equation that have definite chirality (left- or right-handed). In condensed-matter systems they appear as low-energy quasiparticles in Weyl semimetals, acting as sources or sinks of Berry curvature in momentum space and giving rise to topological surface Fermi-arc states."},{"id":"50b8a226-083c-43fc-8073-5586ce8dec84","l":"Whistler plasma waves","d":2,"p":"4f513e0c-b639-4505-9116-7a0613323cf4","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Whistler plasma waves are a right-hand circularly polarized branch of electromagnetic waves in a magnetized plasma that propagate along magnetic field lines with strong dispersion (higher frequencies travel faster), producing the characteristic descending \"whistling\" tones seen from lightning and in space plasmas. They typically occur in the very-low-frequency range between the ion and electron cyclotron frequencies (i.e., below the electron cyclotron frequency)."},{"id":"635e2238-22e0-4354-aa85-e6ec02f7471f","l":"Wide band gap systems","d":2,"p":"12721ec5-11d3-484a-9ea9-a7bea1986722","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Wide band gap systems are solids whose electronic valence\u2013conduction band gap is much larger than in conventional semiconductors (typically \u22732\u20133 eV), so thermal excitation of carriers is strongly suppressed at room temperature. This large gap yields low intrinsic carrier density, high breakdown fields and thermal/chemical robustness, making such materials (e.g., GaN, SiC, diamond, AlN, BN) valuable for high\u2011power, high\u2011frequency and UV optoelectronic applications."},{"id":"6d5dbc9a-27db-41d9-9672-27ea8002048f","l":"Wigner crystal","d":2,"p":"8fc60c42-47b7-46f9-85a7-8c09b9e3cdbd","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A Wigner crystal is a phase in which electrons (or other charged particles) localize at regularly spaced positions to form a lattice when their mutual Coulomb repulsion dominates their kinetic energy. It appears at very low densities or temperatures (large interaction parameter rs) and competes with the delocalized Fermi-liquid state in systems like 2D electron gases or electrons on helium."},{"id":"a9491c4b-d1d9-48ee-b5ff-901ab68c4236","l":"Wire medium","d":2,"p":"77fabbae-1f98-4300-996c-9158ab52e6fc","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A wire medium is an artificial electromagnetic material composed of a dense, subwavelength periodic array of parallel metallic wires embedded in a dielectric. It behaves as an anisotropic, plasmonic metamaterial whose effective permittivity along the wires is dispersive (can become negative below a geometry-dependent plasma frequency) and exhibits strong spatial dispersion, supporting longitudinal/TEM-like modes."},{"id":"e7f3808a-cdf8-40b0-9bb7-d60335032d62","l":"Work theorems","d":2,"p":"082e0bd7-290e-4ff7-9cdb-0a838c18ee8e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Work theorems are principles that relate the work done by forces to changes in a system's energy or motion. The prototypical result\u2014the work\u2013energy theorem\u2014says the net work done on a particle equals its change in kinetic energy, with related results (virtual work, work\u2013power relation, virial theorem) extending the idea to constrained systems, power flow, and ensemble averages."},{"id":"57f33950-d481-44dd-8afa-2a05911b9cbf","l":"World wide web","d":2,"p":"96ee91b1-3ba0-47ad-b628-6a89155e08e7","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"The World Wide Web is a global information system of interlinked hypertext documents and resources, identified by URLs and accessed over the Internet using protocols such as HTTP, enabling retrieval and navigation of web pages through web browsers and other clients."},{"id":"940cd3dc-9c75-4d88-b175-10639d9654d4","l":"Wormlike chain","d":2,"p":"f3ad2159-b3a9-4c96-bd88-6b789b7d6a37","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"A wormlike chain (WLC) is a continuum model of a semiflexible polymer represented as an inextensible, differentiable space curve whose bending energy is given by the Kratky\u2013Porod form (energy \u221d \u222b(curvature)^2 ds), characterized by its contour length and a persistence length lp. The tangent\u2013tangent correlation decays exponentially with arc-length separation, \u27e8t(s)\u00b7t(0)\u27e9 = exp(\u2212s/lp), so lp quantifies the crossover from rigid-rod to flexible-coil behavior."},{"id":"e5dd2c2f-e1db-431f-8a06-06a807d0bcf4","l":"Wrinkling","d":2,"p":"42ec6252-ac7f-42bd-9ddc-08ac45a4b4e9","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Wrinkling is the formation of small-amplitude, short-wavelength undulations in a thin elastic sheet, membrane, or interface that appear when compressive stresses exceed a threshold and the material relieves in-plane compression by out-of-plane buckling. The wrinkle wavelength and amplitude are set by a balance between bending stiffness and competing restoring forces (in-plane stretching, substrate stiffness, or applied tension)."},{"id":"47cfac98-a969-498e-99dc-d734d82eefa9","l":"Wurtzite","d":2,"p":"6af5be88-489f-4a50-9abf-998e8912254a","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Wurtzite is a hexagonal crystal structure (space group P6_3mc) in which two atomic species form an ABAB stacking of close-packed layers with each atom tetrahedrally coordinated to four neighbors. It is noncentrosymmetric and polar along the c-axis and is the common structure for materials like ZnO and GaN, giving rise to piezoelectric and spontaneous polarization effects."},{"id":"6d708986-9ffd-4743-9dbb-a13c95fa5414","l":"X-ray absorption spectroscopy","d":2,"p":"18d10b28-37f1-434d-a95b-19cf71fc6f05","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"X-ray absorption spectroscopy (XAS) measures how a material\u2019s X-ray absorption coefficient varies with photon energy across an element\u2019s core-level absorption edge, providing element-specific information. The near-edge region (XANES) is sensitive to oxidation state and unoccupied electronic states, while the extended fine structure (EXAFS) yields local atomic distances and coordination around the absorbing atom."},{"id":"32de7669-68ef-4f3e-9598-2a70300ea01e","l":"X-ray diffraction","d":2,"p":"18d10b28-37f1-434d-a95b-19cf71fc6f05","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"X-ray diffraction is the coherent scattering of X-rays by the periodic electron density in a material, producing constructive and destructive interference that yields a characteristic diffraction pattern. By analyzing the angles and intensities of the diffracted beams (often using Bragg's law), one can determine the crystal lattice, interatomic spacings, and atomic arrangement."},{"id":"baf4d613-5f2f-47b9-bbea-36e18adb8c6c","l":"X-ray emission spectroscopy","d":2,"p":"18d10b28-37f1-434d-a95b-19cf71fc6f05","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"X-ray emission spectroscopy (XES) measures the energies and intensities of X-ray photons emitted when electrons decay into core-level vacancies, providing element-specific information about the occupied electronic states. It is used to probe chemical bonding, oxidation and spin states, and the local electronic and geometric environment of atoms."},{"id":"c1af3df9-0333-4760-9a43-e6b6c304981d","l":"X-ray fluorescence holography","d":2,"p":"18d10b28-37f1-434d-a95b-19cf71fc6f05","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"X-ray fluorescence holography is an element-specific, lensless imaging method that records interference between coherent X-ray fluorescence emitted by selected atoms (object wave) and a known reference wave to form a hologram encoding the three-dimensional arrangement of nearby atoms. Reconstructing that interference pattern produces a real-space image of the local atomic structure around the fluorescent atom with sub-\u00e5ngstr\u00f6m sensitivity, complementary to diffraction and EXAFS."},{"id":"1a94ea0f-0a72-43b4-ad8c-7ee963b7d191","l":"X-ray & gamma ray plasma measurements","d":2,"p":"a5866236-c99c-4828-b499-4f50cccf7a2b","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"X-ray and gamma-ray plasma measurements are diagnostics that use the high-energy photons emitted by plasmas to infer their physical conditions. By analyzing the spectra, images, timing, and polarization of photons produced by processes such as thermal bremsstrahlung, line and recombination emission, synchrotron and inverse-Compton radiation, and nuclear reactions, these measurements constrain temperature, density, composition, ionization state, nonthermal particle populations, and magnetic-field-related effects."},{"id":"42e3a839-5b63-4011-b3b2-aee1f129aac6","l":"X-ray holography","d":2,"p":"18d10b28-37f1-434d-a95b-19cf71fc6f05","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"X-ray holography is a lensless imaging technique that records the interference between a coherent reference x-ray wave and x-rays scattered from a sample so that both amplitude and phase information are captured and the object's structure can be numerically reconstructed. It enables high-resolution (often nanometer-to-atomic scale) and potentially three-dimensional or element-sensitive imaging using inline or off-axis geometries."},{"id":"5e0af9df-1b70-430a-9342-1bc732fec476","l":"X-ray imaging","d":2,"p":"18d10b28-37f1-434d-a95b-19cf71fc6f05","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"X-ray imaging is a non\u2011invasive technique that forms images of an object's internal structure by transmitting X\u2011ray photons through it and detecting spatial variations in transmitted intensity caused by differential attenuation (absorption and scattering). Contrast in the resulting radiographs or tomographic reconstructions arises from differences in electron density and atomic number, enabling projection images (radiography) or three\u2011dimensional reconstructions (computed tomography)."},{"id":"d03a9a05-6cbb-40c9-b754-81f248538cf0","l":"X-ray lasers","d":2,"p":"cd4c012e-bacf-4bcf-8358-a6573a330ec4","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"X-ray lasers are sources of coherent electromagnetic radiation in the x-ray wavelength range (roughly 0.01\u201310 nm) produced by stimulated, collective emission processes. They are realized in plasma-based media or with free-electron-laser accelerators to generate ultrashort, high\u2011brightness x\u2011ray pulses for atomic-scale imaging and time\u2011resolved studies."},{"id":"a967f4ed-b836-453e-b29b-638047562576","l":"X-ray magnetic circular dichroism","d":2,"p":"18d10b28-37f1-434d-a95b-19cf71fc6f05","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"X-ray magnetic circular dichroism (XMCD) is the difference in x\u2011ray absorption between left- and right\u2011circularly polarized x\u2011rays in a magnetized material, caused by the coupling of photon helicity to electronic spin and orbital magnetic moments. Measured at core\u2011level absorption edges, XMCD is element\u2011 and orbital\u2011specific and can be analyzed (via sum rules) to quantify spin and orbital contributions to the magnetization."},{"id":"6c0dfaf1-778e-440f-b99a-ad8cbf63c92a","l":"X-ray magnetic linear dichroism","d":2,"p":"18d10b28-37f1-434d-a95b-19cf71fc6f05","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"X-ray magnetic linear dichroism (XMLD) is the difference in x-ray absorption for linearly polarized x-rays whose polarization vectors are oriented along different directions with respect to a material's magnetic axis. It arises from magnetically induced anisotropy of the unoccupied electronic states (via spin\u2013orbit coupling and exchange interactions) and is especially sensitive to magnetic anisotropy and antiferromagnetic or noncollinear spin order."},{"id":"5dfe3229-8e68-499f-97aa-66d04a28d6b3","l":"X-ray pair-distribution function analysis","d":2,"p":"18d10b28-37f1-434d-a95b-19cf71fc6f05","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"X-ray pair-distribution function (PDF) analysis is a real-space structural method that obtains the distribution of interatomic distances by Fourier-transforming total X-ray scattering (Bragg plus diffuse) to produce G(r), the probability of finding atom pairs separated by distance r. It reveals local and intermediate-range order in crystalline, amorphous, and nanoscale materials, making it sensitive to defects, disorder, and short-range correlations that conventional crystallography can miss."},{"id":"1a3823f6-9b7e-409c-acc8-e7a321e0a1b7","l":"X-ray photoelectron diffraction","d":2,"p":"18d10b28-37f1-434d-a95b-19cf71fc6f05","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"X-ray photoelectron diffraction (XPD) is a structural probe in which X-rays eject core-level electrons whose angular distribution shows interference between the directly emitted electron waves and those elastically scattered by neighboring atoms. Analysis of these element-specific diffraction patterns reveals local atomic geometry, surface structure, and adsorption sites."},{"id":"d9af0237-9064-4875-aa7e-1293ffa03f4e","l":"X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy","d":2,"p":"18d10b28-37f1-434d-a95b-19cf71fc6f05","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) is a surface-sensitive analytical technique that uses X-ray photons to eject core-level electrons via the photoelectric effect and measures their kinetic energies to determine their binding energies. From those binding energies and peak intensities one obtains elemental composition, chemical (oxidation) states, and electronic-structure information from the top few nanometers of a material."},{"id":"74869800-4a85-4550-b358-388246e95c6b","l":"X-ray photoemission electron microscopy","d":2,"p":"18d10b28-37f1-434d-a95b-19cf71fc6f05","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"X-ray photoemission electron microscopy (XPEEM) is a surface-sensitive imaging technique that forms real-space images by collecting and projecting photoelectrons emitted from a sample illuminated with X-rays. Using tunable X-rays (often from a synchrotron) it provides element- and chemical-state-specific (and, with polarization contrast, magnetic) information with lateral resolution typically in the 10\u2013100 nm range."},{"id":"4a5f7aa7-39bc-461b-ade6-ca30428f9471","l":"X-ray photon correlation spectroscopy","d":2,"p":"18d10b28-37f1-434d-a95b-19cf71fc6f05","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"X-ray photon correlation spectroscopy (XPCS) is a coherent scattering technique that records the speckle pattern produced by coherent X-rays scattered from a sample and analyzes temporal correlations of the speckle intensity to extract the time-dependent intermediate scattering function. By computing intensity autocorrelation functions at a given wavevector q, XPCS noninvasively probes nanoscale dynamics (diffusion, relaxations) in materials over a wide range of timescales."},{"id":"0db9ea9c-e374-4d31-9795-aa684e33f437","l":"X-ray reflectivity","d":2,"p":"18d10b28-37f1-434d-a95b-19cf71fc6f05","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"X-ray reflectivity is the measurement of the fraction of an incident X-ray beam that is specularly reflected from a surface as a function of incidence angle or momentum transfer. The angular dependence (interference fringes) arises from electron-density contrasts at interfaces and is used to extract thin-film thickness, density and interface roughness."},{"id":"1d3e6a42-9d7e-4c5f-b0ef-c5fb489e8336","l":"X-ray scattering","d":2,"p":"18d10b28-37f1-434d-a95b-19cf71fc6f05","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"X-ray scattering is the process by which incident X-ray photons are deflected by electrons or nuclei in a material, causing a change in the photons' direction (and sometimes energy). Elastic (coherent) scattering produces diffraction patterns that reveal atomic-scale structure, while inelastic scattering (e.g., Compton) transfers energy to electrons and probes electronic excitations."},{"id":"2f76e1c6-edbc-4c5d-bca4-d997611f28ef","l":"X-ray standing waves","d":2,"p":"18d10b28-37f1-434d-a95b-19cf71fc6f05","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"X-ray standing waves are stationary interference patterns produced when incident X-ray waves coherently interfere with reflected or Bragg-diffracted X-rays near a surface or inside a crystal, creating spatially periodic intensity maxima and minima. Because the nodes and antinodes modulate absorption or fluorescence from specific atoms, they provide a nondestructive, element-specific probe of atomic positions and interface structure."},{"id":"6d702873-996a-41f9-a4c9-3a348fc488c0","l":"X-ray thermal diffuse scattering","d":2,"p":"18d10b28-37f1-434d-a95b-19cf71fc6f05","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"X-ray thermal diffuse scattering (TDS) is the diffuse, temperature-dependent component of X-ray scattering produced by thermally excited lattice vibrations (phonons), seen as broad intensity distributed around and between Bragg peaks. Originating from inelastic one- and multi-phonon processes, TDS carries information about phonon dispersions, polarization patterns, and anharmonic lattice dynamics."},{"id":"e881be61-7487-47d8-9502-39a8af785317","l":"X-ray tomography","d":2,"p":"18d10b28-37f1-434d-a95b-19cf71fc6f05","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"X-ray tomography (or computed tomography, CT) is a noninvasive imaging technique that reconstructs the internal spatial distribution of X-ray attenuation (related to density and composition) from many projection measurements taken at different angles to produce cross-sectional slices or a 3D volume. Reconstruction is performed by mathematical algorithms such as filtered back-projection or iterative methods to recover the object's internal structure."},{"id":"920ace69-29da-4335-94db-060815e71492","l":"Yukawa coupling","d":2,"p":"83c64883-5507-43f8-92bc-ae9f5ae03f02","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"A Yukawa coupling is the interaction strength between a scalar field and a fermion bilinear in a quantum field theory, typically written y \u03c8\u0304\u03c8\u03c6 in the Lagrangian. In the Standard Model these couplings of the Higgs field to fermions generate the fermion masses after electroweak symmetry breaking (m_f = y_f v/\u221a2)."},{"id":"6f5f3704-ca30-40eb-bbc0-779d3c265d92","l":"Zeolites","d":2,"p":"84d35b4d-ab71-42a6-89e7-f25e73d8401f","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Zeolites are crystalline, microporous aluminosilicate frameworks built from corner-sharing SiO4 and AlO4 tetrahedra that form regular networks of nanometer-scale pores and channels. The Al substitution gives the framework a negative charge balanced by exchangeable cations, producing high surface area materials with selective adsorption, ion-exchange and catalytic properties."},{"id":"da29de2b-207a-4fab-8aa0-41234f977325","l":"Zimm model","d":2,"p":"c267a3f3-0c34-4fc1-9825-947683caf4dd","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Zimm model: a bead-and-spring theory for dilute polymer chains that extends the Rouse model by including long-range hydrodynamic interactions mediated by the solvent (commonly treated via a preaveraged Oseen tensor). It yields distinct dynamic scaling laws\u2014e.g., diffusion D \u221d N^{-\u03bd} and longest relaxation time \u03c4 \u221d N^{3\u03bd}, where \u03bd is the Flory swelling exponent."},{"id":"008b6eb7-1058-4086-b592-5f19b81366cc","l":"Zinc-blende structure","d":2,"p":"6af5be88-489f-4a50-9abf-998e8912254a","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The zinc-blende structure is a cubic crystal lattice (space group F-43m) made of two interpenetrating face-centered-cubic sublattices offset by (1/4,1/4,1/4), giving AB stoichiometry with each atom tetrahedrally coordinated to four atoms of the other type. It is realized by ZnS and many III\u2013V semiconductors such as GaAs."},{"id":"98a7e146-90cf-446c-a690-14b24ce07aef","l":"Z-pinches","d":2,"p":"fd233cf5-7ec1-4c82-90c6-2bad6ab2f982","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"A Z\u2011pinch is a plasma confinement configuration in which a strong electric current flowing along the z\u2011axis produces an azimuthal magnetic field whose J\u00d7B (Lorentz) force radially compresses or \"pinches\" the plasma. It is used in pulsed\u2011power, fusion and X\u2011ray sources but is limited by magnetohydrodynamic instabilities (kink and sausage) that disrupt stable confinement."},{"id":"90ec95f3-c6a6-4327-98ef-833f86289335","l":"\u03c0-\u03c0 stacking","d":2,"p":"7b287382-c7f9-480a-bc80-3ac6fe07b665","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"\u03c0-\u03c0 stacking is a noncovalent attractive interaction between aromatic rings caused by favorable overlap and mutual arrangement of their delocalized \u03c0-electron clouds. It originates from a combination of London dispersion and electrostatic/quadrupole effects, produces preferred face-to-face, offset-parallel (slipped), or T-shaped geometries, and typically contributes on the order of a few kcal/mol (\u22484\u201320 kJ/mol) to stability."},{"id":"8c7d508a-8f2b-449e-8692-fc00c18cc0f3","l":"AC susceptibility measurements","d":3,"p":"c21c30b0-2219-4793-9841-d9e9d3d0dd2c","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"AC susceptibility measurements probe a material's complex magnetic susceptibility \u03c7(\u03c9) by applying a small oscillating magnetic field and detecting the induced magnetization or pickup-coil voltage. The in-phase (real) component \u03c7' reflects the reversible response and phase transitions, while the out-of-phase (imaginary) component \u03c7'' quantifies dissipation and magnetic relaxation dynamics as a function of frequency and temperature."},{"id":"9ccb5cf4-5532-4cdd-a1c7-4003a9a4feaa","l":"Actin","d":3,"p":"8c0cd30a-5be8-4913-9220-907afd4bceb8","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Actin is a highly conserved globular (G\u2011actin) protein that polymerizes into filamentous (F\u2011actin) networks forming a major structural and dynamic component of the eukaryotic cytoskeleton. These ATP\u2011dependent filaments provide mechanical support, transmit and generate forces (often via myosin), and reorganize by polymerization/depolymerization and treadmilling to drive cell shape changes, division, and motility."},{"id":"bf5ac386-7932-4269-b37b-db76dc45b2bb","l":"Active Brownian particles","d":3,"p":"36e1d549-9909-4881-a2d8-0a501a300a26","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Active Brownian particles are a minimal model of self-propelled (energy-consuming) particles that move at a roughly constant speed along an internal orientation that undergoes rotational diffusion while also experiencing translational (thermal) noise. They break detailed balance and capture generic nonequilibrium phenomena of active matter such as persistent motion, enhanced diffusion, clustering, and motility-induced phase separation."},{"id":"41f9017d-cc28-4664-8844-f8310c565a0f","l":"Active defects","d":3,"p":"f3ad8cfa-a536-49e0-bf56-ff47aeb98e3f","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Active defects are topological defects in ordered materials composed of internally driven (active) constituents whose dynamics are maintained by local energy consumption rather than thermal equilibrium. Because of this activity such defects can self-propel, generate hydrodynamic flows, and drive nonequilibrium phenomena like sustained creation\u2013annihilation cycles and active turbulence (e.g., motile \u00b11/2 defects in active nematics)."},{"id":"8f47d367-938e-44e3-8df7-eed42223999f","l":"Active nematic gels","d":3,"p":"7e15b2f2-fbff-4712-b89b-5946e4320e24","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Active nematic gels are soft, nonequilibrium materials in which an elastic (often crosslinked) polymer network is coupled to orientable, rodlike units that consume energy to exert forces, so the system combines nematic orientational order with gel elasticity and internal activity. They exhibit spontaneous flows, self-driven defect creation and motion, and viscoelastic responses that are described by active gel hydrodynamic theories coupling the nematic order parameter, elastic stresses, and active stresses."},{"id":"92d1c0c3-c0e8-4e34-bcac-229e8c16fad9","l":"Active polar gels","d":3,"p":"7e15b2f2-fbff-4712-b89b-5946e4320e24","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Active polar gels are viscoelastic materials composed of orientable (polar) filaments or units that continuously consume internal energy (for example ATP) to generate forces and stresses. Their combination of gel-like elasticity, polar orientational order, and internally driven (active) stresses leads to nonequilibrium phenomena such as spontaneous flows, contractility, and pattern formation, and is described by active hydrodynamic theories of polar liquid crystals."},{"id":"09d08dc4-f460-4ea1-84d5-ac9d1e4477d1","l":"Actuating materials","d":3,"p":"ef41b9c0-c5a2-4728-9a87-d114abfb3659","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Actuating materials are materials that transduce an external stimulus (electric or magnetic fields, temperature, light, chemical environment, humidity, etc.) into mechanical motion, force, or a controlled change of shape by coupling the stimulus to internal structural, phase, or volume changes. Common examples include piezoelectrics, electroactive and dielectric elastomers, shape\u2011memory alloys, magnetostrictive alloys, and stimulus\u2011responsive hydrogels."},{"id":"f4eda6e0-9440-429d-814e-2e368b9b5f8a","l":"Adiabatic quantum optimization","d":3,"p":"f9456df5-5ca2-4a8a-8b11-d5ffd9d96921","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Adiabatic quantum optimization is a quantum computing method that encodes an optimization problem into the ground state of a problem Hamiltonian and finds the solution by slowly evolving the system from the known ground state of a simple initial Hamiltonian to that final Hamiltonian, using the adiabatic theorem to keep the system in its instantaneous ground state. Its success and required runtime are set by the minimum energy gap to excited states during the evolution\u2014very small gaps can force very slow, long computations to avoid nonadiabatic transitions."},{"id":"a50e8ff6-ceef-409e-992a-b17cc2541ca6","l":"Aggregation","d":3,"p":"9988d7c1-f3e7-4fec-b85f-8fa6df6487bb","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Aggregation is the process by which individual particles or smaller clusters collide and stick together to form larger assemblies (aggregates), often with irregular or fractal structure. It is driven by interparticle forces, Brownian or shear-driven collisions, and external fields, and determines the morphology and transport properties of colloids, aerosols, granular materials and astrophysical dust."},{"id":"ebed35ee-2ec2-4036-8785-e9d20c88afbe","l":"Alkali metals","d":3,"p":"7584df11-595e-4afa-bd90-305dfa506396","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Alkali metals are the chemical elements in Group 1 of the periodic table (Li, Na, K, Rb, Cs, Fr) characterized by a single valence electron that gives them low ionization energies and a strong tendency to form +1 cations. They are soft, low-density metals with low melting points and high chemical reactivity, notably reacting vigorously with water to produce hydroxides and hydrogen."},{"id":"0ea946b9-6d14-4328-9232-e4b0a4a0dbf2","l":"Alkaline earth metals","d":3,"p":"7584df11-595e-4afa-bd90-305dfa506396","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Alkaline earth metals are the Group 2 elements of the periodic table (Be, Mg, Ca, Sr, Ba, Ra) characterized by two valence electrons that they readily lose to form +2 cations. They are shiny, silvery metals with metallic bonding, moderate chemical reactivity that increases down the group, and typical chemistry forming oxides, hydroxides and salts."},{"id":"78fde4c7-f999-47d0-9b48-2e0ae4ab1994","l":"Alkaline fuel cells","d":3,"p":"9d3ec67b-01ed-4ebe-8b7f-04613a3d5185","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Alkaline fuel cells are electrochemical devices that generate electricity by oxidizing hydrogen at the anode and reducing oxygen at the cathode in an aqueous alkaline electrolyte (typically KOH), with hydroxide ions (OH\u2212) conducting charge through the electrolyte while electrons flow through an external circuit. They offer high efficiency and low activation losses but are sensitive to CO2, which forms carbonates and degrades performance."},{"id":"73f8b483-c0c2-4d31-824c-d8d85068167a","l":"Anderson impurity model","d":3,"p":"c33b545a-458f-4340-98af-08d61af6681e","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Anderson impurity model is a quantum many-body model that describes a single localized electronic level (an impurity) with strong on-site Coulomb repulsion hybridized with a continuum of conduction electrons. It captures how charge fluctuations and electron correlations produce localized magnetic moments and their screening (e.g., the Kondo effect) in metals and quantum dots."},{"id":"a4074854-ac2f-4771-9451-0ce9bbad43ce","l":"Angle-resolved photoemission spectroscopy","d":3,"p":"40082853-5a77-4bce-bb7b-a6b598d04d93","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Angle-resolved photoemission spectroscopy (ARPES) is an experimental technique that measures the kinetic energy and emission angle of electrons photoemitted from a material to directly determine their energy\u2013momentum dispersion and Fermi-surface structure. Based on the photoelectric effect and momentum conservation parallel to the surface, ARPES provides surface-sensitive information about band structure, quasiparticle properties, and many-body interactions."},{"id":"03f082a4-0a5c-4be2-9879-953479491089","l":"Angular-dependent magnetoresistance","d":3,"p":"23e22a76-a9ba-4593-8d52-4e5c98c47af3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Angular-dependent magnetoresistance (AMR) is the change in a material\u2019s electrical resistance as the orientation of an applied magnetic field is rotated relative to the current direction and crystallographic axes. This anisotropic response, caused by angle-dependent Lorentz-force deflection, spin-orbit coupling, and scattering, is used to probe electronic structure, Fermi-surface geometry, and magnetic anisotropy."},{"id":"0691e67d-a662-4911-b645-7d587620004b","l":"Anisotropic magnetoresistance","d":3,"p":"23e22a76-a9ba-4593-8d52-4e5c98c47af3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Anisotropic magnetoresistance (AMR) is the dependence of a ferromagnetic material's electrical resistivity on the angle between the electric current and the magnetization direction, caused by spin\u2013orbit-coupling\u2013induced anisotropic scattering of charge carriers (often following a cos^2\u03b8-like angular dependence)."},{"id":"18bdf1ba-c0cb-4c21-89c0-3cd3c6ff4235","l":"Annular dark-field imaging","d":3,"p":"c33a7211-ebcd-4336-917d-a75ecb1e50c4","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Annular dark-field imaging is a scanning transmission electron microscopy (STEM) mode that forms images by collecting electrons scattered away from the forward beam with a ring-shaped (annular) detector, excluding the unscattered beam to produce dark-field contrast. In the high-angle variant (HAADF) the signal is largely incoherent and roughly scales with atomic number (Z-contrast), making it useful for atomic-resolution structure and composition mapping."},{"id":"322012a1-fa3b-47b6-8fd1-e43cc74170b2","l":"Antiferroelectrics","d":3,"p":"6e6a0326-86c9-49f2-a044-8ee3ca248ee3","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Antiferroelectrics are insulating crystals in which neighboring electric dipoles (or polar sublattices) are arranged in an antiparallel, ordered pattern so the net macroscopic polarization cancels. Under a sufficiently strong external electric field they can undergo a field-induced transition to a polar (ferroelectric-like) state, producing a characteristic double hysteresis loop in polarization versus field."},{"id":"1e9da9e1-574f-4234-81ef-1c0b4dcbe80e","l":"Assortativity","d":3,"p":"4f65979f-8a52-4ebb-bb0c-29db6fb979d0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Assortativity in networks quantifies the tendency of nodes to connect to others that are similar according to some attribute (commonly degree). The standard measure is the assortativity coefficient\u2014the Pearson correlation of node attributes (or degrees) at the ends of edges\u2014ranging from \u22121 (disassortative) through 0 (no correlation) to +1 (assortative)."},{"id":"41edb15a-5197-43de-ba6d-42d47f07fee5","l":"Atmospheric neutrino oscillations","d":3,"p":"703e2e58-467b-463a-949c-c451ad620e52","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Atmospheric neutrino oscillations are the quantum-mechanical flavor changes experienced by neutrinos produced in cosmic-ray\u2013induced air showers as they travel through the atmosphere and Earth, so that muon, electron, and tau neutrino compositions vary with path length and energy. Measurements of these flavor changes (notably the disappearance of upward-going muon neutrinos) provide direct evidence for neutrino mass and mixing and are sensitive to oscillation parameters and matter effects."},{"id":"72ec65a9-c9c8-49e7-ab17-71549bdb7959","l":"ATP","d":3,"p":"dbd0abfb-c195-4629-be58-24a1f83e2e21","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"ATP (adenosine triphosphate) is the primary biochemical energy carrier: a nucleotide that stores free energy in its terminal phosphate bonds and supplies that energy to cellular processes when hydrolyzed to ADP or AMP. Under typical cellular conditions ATP hydrolysis releases on the order of 20\u201350 kJ/mol of usable free energy (\u2248 \u221230.5 kJ/mol under standard biochemical conditions), enabling molecular machines and metabolic work."},{"id":"1a335bc8-c10f-41f1-9ddf-b49dcf89bc26","l":"Attosecond laser irradiation","d":3,"p":"dedc6dbe-3651-4ad0-9d37-b260e8975adc","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Attosecond laser irradiation is the exposure of matter to laser pulses with durations on the order of attoseconds (10^-18 s), typically produced by high-harmonic generation and often lying in the extreme-ultraviolet to soft x-ray range. Such irradiation provides sub-femtosecond temporal resolution and high peak fields, enabling initiation and time-resolved probing of electron dynamics, strong-field ionization, and other ultrafast processes."},{"id":"ad2fef27-53cf-48b1-8f02-e714bcd7d5d5","l":"Attosecond laser spectroscopy","d":3,"p":"dedc6dbe-3651-4ad0-9d37-b260e8975adc","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Attosecond laser spectroscopy is a family of time-resolved techniques that use ultrashort light pulses on the attosecond (10^-18 s) timescale\u2014usually produced by high-harmonic generation\u2014to initiate and probe electronic motion and ultrafast processes in atoms, molecules, and solids. It provides sub-femtosecond temporal resolution to measure phenomena such as photoemission delays, tunneling dynamics, and charge migration."},{"id":"cb5aea90-16ab-4d55-b675-ff8da3e62bcc","l":"Axons","d":3,"p":"b80b0695-3ad7-4617-9b06-9787823eb6ea","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Axons are long, thin projections of neurons that carry electrical signals (action potentials) away from the cell body toward other neurons or muscle cells. Their signal transmission is determined by ion-channel dynamics and passive cable properties, and in many animals is sped up by myelination enabling saltatory conduction."},{"id":"c85266b7-f864-46ff-b800-0c561be9593b","l":"Bacterial infections","d":3,"p":"d6072ccd-5d49-4a8c-a032-efa240964575","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Bacterial infections occur when pathogenic bacteria colonize and proliferate within a host organism, damaging tissues directly or via toxins and eliciting immune responses that produce disease symptoms. In physics and biophysics they are often treated through models of population dynamics, transport and chemotaxis, biofilm mechanics, and stochastic spread."},{"id":"df668498-c0bb-4430-ae64-6adda8dc93bd","l":"Ballooning instability","d":3,"p":"3f71ccf5-16ff-425f-b7b3-4791f9503b59","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Ballooning instability is a pressure-driven ideal-MHD instability in magnetically confined plasmas (especially toroidal devices like tokamaks) in which plasma locally \u201cbulges\u201d outward along magnetic field lines in regions of unfavorable curvature. It typically has short perpendicular and long parallel wavelengths, grows rapidly, and often limits the maximum plasma pressure (beta) achievable in confinement."},{"id":"3aa1f62d-ba58-416a-9eaa-5d162b472e5f","l":"Batteries","d":3,"p":"80fe725c-ae9b-4701-a9f2-db29c094a8ea","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Batteries are electrochemical devices that convert chemical energy into electrical energy via redox reactions between two electrodes (anode and cathode) separated by an electrolyte, producing an electromotive force (voltage) and supplying current to external circuits. They have internal resistance and finite capacity that set their deliverable power and energy, and are classified as primary (single-use) or secondary (rechargeable) cells."},{"id":"9db56d09-561a-4566-8f7e-90791060acd2","l":"BCS-BEC crossover","d":3,"p":"95b999dd-b903-4e07-9b42-6b395558b48c","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The BCS\u2013BEC crossover is the smooth evolution of a fermionic many-body system from the Bardeen\u2013Cooper\u2013Schrieffer (BCS) regime of weakly attractive, overlapping Cooper pairs to the Bose\u2013Einstein condensate (BEC) regime of tightly bound, bosonic molecule-like pairs as interaction strength or density is varied. It is marked by a continuous change in pair size and coherence length, a shift of the chemical potential from near the Fermi energy to negative binding energies, and is realized in ultracold Fermi gases and some superconductors."},{"id":"82a171ca-847a-4a8a-a0b5-5bad9a5458e9","l":"Bead-spring models","d":3,"p":"ac816133-540a-4562-8130-584df553f46a","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Bead-spring models are coarse-grained representations of polymer chains in which segments (beads) are connected by elastic springs that encode chain connectivity and entropic elasticity. They are used in theory and simulation (with thermal noise and optionally hydrodynamic or excluded\u2011volume interactions) to study polymer conformations, dynamics, and rheology."},{"id":"9740edef-8fa4-4b91-95dc-bee92c55283e","l":"Beam-plasma instability","d":3,"p":"b094f9fa-ebc7-4e4d-8957-0a7303f6e91e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"A beam-plasma instability occurs when a directed stream of charged particles travels through a plasma and resonantly amplifies plasma waves. Small perturbations grow by extracting free energy from the beam's nonthermal velocity distribution (e.g., two\u2011stream or bump\u2011on\u2011tail), producing wave growth, beam scattering and plasma heating."},{"id":"8abddf0b-ea8e-4c03-af96-883903427079","l":"Berry curvature","d":3,"p":"1d626944-7dd8-4abd-8400-1e1af764ab46","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Berry curvature is a gauge-invariant geometric quantity defined as the curl of the Berry connection in a system's parameter space (e.g., momentum space) that measures the local twisting of the quantum eigenstate bundle. It acts like an effective magnetic field in parameter space, determining the accumulation of Berry phase, anomalous velocities in semiclassical dynamics, and topological invariants such as Chern numbers."},{"id":"6904d01d-76bf-48dd-84db-55e7e648d1b9","l":"Beta-detected nuclear magnetic resonance","d":3,"p":"fee9296a-01ca-4b80-86bf-2c51f37c1871","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Beta-detected nuclear magnetic resonance (\u03b2-NMR) is an NMR-like technique in which spin-polarized radioactive probe nuclei are implanted into a sample and their Larmor precession is monitored via the anisotropic angular distribution of emitted beta particles from their decay. This yields a highly sensitive, depth-resolved probe of local magnetic fields, hyperfine interactions, and spin dynamics in surfaces, thin films, and bulk materials."},{"id":"91fa8496-5046-48f6-af39-cb99c33f7b7b","l":"Bethe ansatz","d":3,"p":"d4c7a190-1d1c-4c1c-a1b5-3a3515f59c93","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Bethe ansatz is a nonperturbative method for constructing exact eigenstates of certain one-dimensional quantum many-body (integrable) systems by writing the wavefunction as a superposition of plane waves whose momenta (rapidities) are related by algebraic \"Bethe equations\" that encode two-body scattering phase shifts and boundary conditions. Introduced by Hans Bethe for the spin-1/2 Heisenberg chain, it applies to models such as the XXZ chain and the Lieb\u2013Liniger Bose gas."},{"id":"e6ba62b3-4bed-4034-81ae-892d7ea6a620","l":"Betweenness","d":3,"p":"4f65979f-8a52-4ebb-bb0c-29db6fb979d0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Betweenness is the ternary relation that a point or event B lies between two others A and C, meaning B sits on or within the line segment or geodesic joining A and C. In metric contexts it is often expressed by d(A,C)=d(A,B)+d(B,C), while in spacetime/causal settings it means B lies in the causal interval between A and C."},{"id":"7cfd1a71-fa27-4840-94c6-583be63c5887","l":"Bimolecular conformation","d":3,"p":"9fe8a5bc-b93d-46bc-9faa-208a23f0d923","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Bimolecular conformation is the three-dimensional arrangement and relative orientation of two molecules (or two subunits of a dimer) when they form an encounter complex or bound pair. This spatial geometry\u2014specified by separation, angles, and torsions\u2014determines their intermolecular interaction energy, reaction pathways, and spectroscopic signatures."},{"id":"4b3604f3-c502-4fed-ae35-054ffeee8dd9","l":"Biofuel cells","d":3,"p":"9d3ec67b-01ed-4ebe-8b7f-04613a3d5185","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Biofuel cells are electrochemical devices that convert chemical energy in organic (biological) fuels into electrical energy by driving redox reactions at electrodes catalyzed by biological agents such as enzymes or microorganisms. They operate under mild conditions with electron transfer occurring directly or via mediators between the biological catalyst and the electrode, enabling applications from biosensors and implantable power to renewable energy generation."},{"id":"bf039ac6-3727-477a-8cf1-1575d5282b47","l":"Biological aging","d":3,"p":"aa6e6251-78da-4a3b-a270-cccc77b356e6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Biological aging is the progressive, time-dependent decline in physiological function and increased probability of death that arises as organisms accumulate molecular and cellular damage and lose homeostatic and repair capacity. It is an emergent property of complex, far-from-equilibrium biological systems driven by stochastic damage, reduced energy allocation to maintenance, and altered signaling and repair pathways."},{"id":"d0fc908a-b959-4924-a0b9-dba5908c7b50","l":"Bioluminescence","d":3,"p":"9c437e89-cf6a-4250-8738-b43ded1eb4ff","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Bioluminescence is the production of light by living organisms via biochemical reactions that generate electronically excited molecules which emit photons as they relax. It is a form of chemiluminescence\u2014a direct conversion of chemical energy into visible or near-visible light, typically mediated by substrates (e.g., luciferin) and enzymes (e.g., luciferase)."},{"id":"ccd75205-8903-4b5c-8f03-b05a43efce17","l":"Blood plasma","d":3,"p":"356040c7-f8a2-4afa-a9e4-c6f22fd80281","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Blood plasma is the liquid component of blood (\u224855% by volume), a straw\u2011colored fluid of mostly water containing dissolved electrolytes, proteins (albumin, globulins, fibrinogen), nutrients, hormones and gases that suspends and transports blood cells and solutes. Physically it is a complex colloidal electrolyte solution with viscosity slightly above water and serves as the primary medium for mass, heat and charge transport in the circulatory system."},{"id":"acab8a03-b89b-4d98-a16e-32ac98626471","l":"Borides","d":3,"p":"2a71cbba-3674-42b1-8778-4b794f85a844","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Borides are compounds in which boron combines with another element (usually a metal) to form binary or ternary materials featuring boron-rich clusters, chains, or covalent networks bonded to metal atoms. They are typically refractory and hard, with a wide range of electronic properties\u2014from metallic conductivity and superconductivity (e.g., MgB2) to semiconducting or insulating\u2014depending on their structure and composition."},{"id":"f99ebc4a-3c39-4248-8540-5345d085896c","l":"Bose-Hubbard model","d":3,"p":"a0ce8ec5-e2c2-40f4-b53f-9fd9e2572289","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Bose\u2013Hubbard model is a minimal lattice Hamiltonian for interacting bosons that hop between neighboring sites and experience local (on-site) interactions; the competition between tunneling (kinetic energy) and on-site repulsion drives a quantum phase transition between a superfluid and a Mott insulator. It is characterized by parameters for nearest-neighbor hopping, on-site interaction strength, and chemical potential and is widely used to describe ultracold atoms in optical lattices and other strongly correlated bosonic systems."},{"id":"bb2b3838-0712-4217-b31a-f46e4a88e7e6","l":"Bottom baryons","d":3,"p":"3e39ec26-cbf5-485f-8dea-b59222446277","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Bottom baryons are baryons (three-quark hadrons with baryon number 1) in which one or more constituent quarks is a bottom (beauty, b) quark. They are comparatively heavy, unstable particles that decay via the weak interaction and are studied to probe heavy-quark dynamics and QCD in the nonperturbative regime."},{"id":"f3c1d5a7-ea62-4454-8b73-9de673608585","l":"Bottom mesons","d":3,"p":"7d053db3-4dc3-41d0-bd8c-844bcdb802bc","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Bottom mesons (or B/beauty mesons) are hadrons made of one bottom (b) quark or antiquark bound with a lighter quark or antiquark (examples include B+, B0, Bs, Bc and their antiparticles). Their large mass and weak-interaction decays make them important probes of flavor physics, CP violation, and quark mixing."},{"id":"b1258dc8-11b9-441e-b5fa-17d95fd32e05","l":"Buneman instability","d":3,"p":"b094f9fa-ebc7-4e4d-8957-0a7303f6e91e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Buneman instability: an electrostatic, kinetic two\u2011stream instability that occurs when a sufficiently large relative drift (current) between electrons and ions in a collisionless plasma drives resonant amplification of short-wavelength electrostatic waves. It leads to rapid growth of electron\u2013ion density fluctuations, electron trapping and anomalous resistivity."},{"id":"c02bdf57-d862-4713-909b-02ca418573c0","l":"Carbides","d":3,"p":"2a71cbba-3674-42b1-8778-4b794f85a844","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Carbides are compounds of carbon with metals or metalloids, in which carbon bonds range from ionic (e.g., CaC2) and covalent (e.g., SiC, B4C) to interstitial/metallic (e.g., TiC, WC). They are typically very hard and refractory with high melting points, and exhibit a wide span of electronic behavior from insulating/semiconducting to metallic."},{"id":"554dae70-b590-4636-8826-7a779fb852b8","l":"Centrality","d":3,"p":"4f65979f-8a52-4ebb-bb0c-29db6fb979d0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Centrality (in heavy-ion collisions) quantifies the collision geometry \u2014 the degree of overlap of the two nuclei \u2014 usually characterized by the impact parameter or by experimental proxies such as percentiles of charged-particle multiplicity or spectator energy. Central collisions have small impact parameter and many participating nucleons, while peripheral collisions have large impact parameter and few participants."},{"id":"b0b49d96-33a9-4d5f-9aa1-6ce479ffcc01","l":"Ceramics","d":3,"p":"2a71cbba-3674-42b1-8778-4b794f85a844","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Ceramics are inorganic, nonmetallic solids\u2014typically oxides, carbides, nitrides, or silicates\u2014formed by high\u2011temperature processing and held together mainly by ionic and/or covalent bonds. They typically exhibit high hardness and melting points, low electrical and thermal conductivity, and brittle fracture behavior, which makes them useful as structural materials, insulators, and thermal barriers."},{"id":"eff89dbf-95f8-4cde-b017-976a395f7a3f","l":"Chalcogenides","d":3,"p":"2a71cbba-3674-42b1-8778-4b794f85a844","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Chalcogenides are compounds containing one or more chalcogen elements (the group-16 elements O, S, Se, Te, and Po) bonded to more electropositive elements. In condensed-matter physics they form a broad materials class\u2014from insulators to metals and superconductors\u2014whose layered and crystalline members (e.g., transition-metal dichalcogenides, Ge\u2013Sb\u2013Te alloys) show important electronic, optical, thermoelectric and phase-change properties."},{"id":"09dc0029-8dcf-45a5-bee6-9c63935807b0","l":"Charmed baryons","d":3,"p":"3e39ec26-cbf5-485f-8dea-b59222446277","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Charmed baryons are baryons (three-quark hadrons) in which one or more constituent quarks is a charm quark, so they carry nonzero charm quantum number. Examples include \u039bc+, \u03a3c, \u039ec and doubly-charmed \u039ecc states; because of the heavy charm quark they are heavier than light baryons and are valuable probes of heavy-quark dynamics and QCD."},{"id":"c2dc657c-f9d2-496f-a3a7-a8df411aed90","l":"Charmed mesons","d":3,"p":"7d053db3-4dc3-41d0-bd8c-844bcdb802bc","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Charmed mesons are hadronic mesons that contain at least one charm (c) quark or charm antiquark, e.g., open\u2011charm states like the D and Ds families (c q\u0304 or c\u0304 q) and hidden\u2011charm states such as charmonium (c c\u0304). They are heavy-light or heavy-heavy quark bound states used to study QCD dynamics, weak decays, and CP violation in the charm sector."},{"id":"ea69dc6e-9587-4b51-b9e8-b699efbaeafd","l":"Chemical beam epitaxy","d":3,"p":"471b81e2-74ad-4d48-b2c0-a57b3c84bb81","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Chemical beam epitaxy (CBE) is a low-pressure, beam-based epitaxial growth method in which directed molecular beams of volatile chemical precursors impinge on a heated crystalline substrate and thermally decompose at the surface to form atomically ordered thin films. It combines the vacuum-directionality and in situ monitoring of molecular beam epitaxy with the use of organometallic/chemical precursors typical of chemical vapor deposition, enabling precise control of composition and doping for semiconductor layers."},{"id":"fe6212f5-2762-4253-9267-54b97b955a02","l":"Chemical deposition","d":3,"p":"778678c1-03d6-402a-a039-4eaaeb999399","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Chemical deposition is the formation of a solid film or coating on a substrate by chemical reactions of precursor species in a gas or liquid phase, with reaction at or near the surface producing nucleation and growth while volatile byproducts are removed. Common forms include chemical vapor deposition, electrodeposition, and atomic-layer deposition, and the process is governed by transport, reaction kinetics, and thermodynamics."},{"id":"a59b2f4e-cfd9-4618-b65a-4ed6cc9072bd","l":"Chemical hydrogen storage","d":3,"p":"f0761682-18fc-4076-9921-90eae238bc98","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Chemical hydrogen storage is storing hydrogen by chemically binding it into compounds (e.g., metal hydrides, complex hydrides, or liquid organic hydrogen carriers) rather than as a compressed gas or liquid. Hydrogen is released later by a chemical, thermal, or catalytic reaction; this approach can give higher volumetric/gravimetric density but requires energy and/or reagent steps to liberate and regenerate the carrier."},{"id":"d6271a55-4ee5-43f9-bacf-cf0ceef16009","l":"Chirp","d":3,"p":"d3a4e127-e2a1-4d1b-9637-522e2bf75e99","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"A chirp is a waveform whose instantaneous frequency varies continuously with time\u2014typically sweeping upward (up-chirp) or downward (down-chirp); the frequency sweep can be linear or nonlinear and is widely used in radar, sonar, communications and optics."},{"id":"c66f670b-1cc2-4ce6-87c3-80f0e7960bde","l":"Cluster expansion","d":3,"p":"a0dcc4fb-34a0-4892-a60a-cfb14c0f73e8","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Cluster expansion is a systematic representation of a partition function, correlation function, or observable as a sum of contributions from connected clusters (finite groups) of interacting particles or degrees of freedom, where each term (cluster integral or cumulant/Ursell function) isolates the genuine many\u2011body correlation of that group. It is used to compute virial coefficients, equations of state, and controlled many\u2011body corrections in classical and quantum statistical mechanics."},{"id":"c34b5516-5f96-4518-be69-93be5d192054","l":"Clustering coefficient & triangles","d":3,"p":"4f65979f-8a52-4ebb-bb0c-29db6fb979d0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"A triangle in a network is a set of three nodes all mutually connected (a 3\u2011cycle). The clustering coefficient measures the tendency of nodes to form such triangles: the local coefficient for node i is C_i = 2T_i/[k_i(k_i\u22121)] where T_i is the number of triangles through i and k_i its degree, while global clustering is often taken as the average of C_i or as (3 \u00d7 number of triangles)/(number of connected triples)."},{"id":"1dcb04b9-c828-4028-86e8-b3e8c62a2003","l":"Cluster methods","d":3,"p":"2d89a076-7a54-4494-973a-ea562f654b8e","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Cluster methods are a class of many-body computational techniques that approximate an extended or interacting system by selecting a finite \"cluster\" of particles or lattice sites to treat exactly (or with high accuracy) while representing the remainder of the system via an embedding, bath, or truncated expansion. They include approaches such as cluster dynamical mean-field theory, cluster perturbation/expansion, and coupled-cluster methods used in condensed matter, nuclear physics, and quantum chemistry."},{"id":"6cd2c5cb-e229-4095-ba27-a564840b871c","l":"Cochlea","d":3,"p":"0554be63-d31c-4a95-832a-388b2f87f94a","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"The cochlea is the spiral, fluid-filled organ in the inner ear that mechanically decomposes incoming sound into a spatial frequency map by supporting traveling waves on the basilar membrane. Hair cells convert those local vibrations into electrical signals for the nervous system, with outer hair cells providing active, nonlinear amplification that sharpens frequency tuning."},{"id":"afe2131a-c1b0-4b3c-bd58-61dedcb4824e","l":"Coherent potential approximation","d":3,"p":"8c15e092-3b78-496f-96ae-e55153f7efbd","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The coherent potential approximation (CPA) is a single-site effective-medium theory for disordered solids that replaces a random, substitutional potential by a self-consistently determined \"coherent\" potential so that an electron (or wave) sees no net scattering on average. It gives an effective Green's function and band structure for alloys and other random systems, while neglecting short-range correlation and multisite scattering."},{"id":"2b315a5c-8a84-4c29-b1a0-b97a413acce5","l":"Coherent X-ray scattering","d":3,"p":"1d3e6a42-9d7e-4c5f-b0ef-c5fb489e8336","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Coherent X-ray scattering is the elastic scattering of X-rays from a sample using a beam with well-defined phase relationships so the scattered waves interfere, producing speckle or diffraction patterns. Those interference patterns encode nanoscale spatial structure and temporal fluctuations, enabling imaging and correlation measurements (e.g., coherent diffraction imaging and X-ray photon correlation spectroscopy)."},{"id":"fafb123d-34f3-41d5-9eca-ff9370641d97","l":"Collagen","d":3,"p":"8c0cd30a-5be8-4913-9220-907afd4bceb8","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Collagen is a family of structural proteins whose three\u2011polypeptide chains form a characteristic triple helix that self\u2011assembles into fibrils and fibers in the extracellular matrix, giving tissues their primary tensile strength. Its hierarchical organization and crosslinking produce nonlinear elastic and time\u2011dependent (viscoelastic) mechanical behavior central to biomechanics and biomaterials."},{"id":"92a80ba9-bbe9-445a-8fe5-6d45157cda9c","l":"Collisionless shock in plasma","d":3,"p":"1ae82140-c94a-46bb-8274-ad7311e44112","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"A collisionless shock in a plasma is a shock wave whose sharp transition and energy dissipation are produced not by binary particle collisions but by collective electromagnetic fields, plasma instabilities, and wave\u2013particle interactions. Such shocks have thicknesses much smaller than the particle mean free path and efficiently convert bulk kinetic energy into heating, turbulence, and particle acceleration (e.g., Earth's bow shock, supernova remnants)."},{"id":"dfbac5cf-ad17-4e82-9851-c599864d3844","l":"Colossal magnetoresistance","d":3,"p":"23e22a76-a9ba-4593-8d52-4e5c98c47af3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Colossal magnetoresistance is the very large (often orders-of-magnitude) change in electrical resistance produced in certain materials\u2014most famously doped manganite perovskites\u2014by an applied magnetic field. It reflects a field-driven switching between competing electronic/magnetic phases (insulating, charge/orbital-ordered states and ferromagnetic metallic states) due to strong coupling among spin, charge, orbital and lattice degrees of freedom."},{"id":"180b51bc-73e1-45ee-90e6-a820e89e0da1","l":"Community detection algorithms","d":3,"p":"4f65979f-8a52-4ebb-bb0c-29db6fb979d0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Community detection algorithms are computational methods for partitioning a network into groups (communities or modules) whose nodes have denser connections within the group than to nodes outside it, thereby revealing the network's mesoscale structure. They include approaches such as modularity maximization, spectral clustering, and stochastic block models, and are used to uncover functional or structural subdivisions in physical, biological, and social systems."},{"id":"af1e794b-6772-46b8-8c2f-39ec3c33ec5b","l":"Compartmental model","d":3,"p":"3a68853d-8031-4222-9eb4-ca56627c40cd","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"A compartmental model represents a system by dividing it into a set of discrete, internally homogeneous \"compartments\" whose contents exchange with one another according to specified transfer rates; the dynamics are described by coupled rate equations (usually ordinary differential equations). It is used to capture macroscopic flows or state transitions (e.g., mass, energy, or populations/disease states) without resolving spatial detail."},{"id":"4a23167a-cfb3-47f2-82b9-d5de1385d088","l":"Complex Langevin dynamics","d":3,"p":"0ac46efd-0f43-4786-a998-8f9d0042ffd8","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems","Plasma Physics"],"def":"Complex Langevin dynamics is an extension of stochastic quantization where the real degrees of freedom are analytically continued to complex variables and evolved by a Langevin equation with a complex drift, allowing numerical sampling of path integrals with a complex action (the sign problem). It aims to compute expectation values by a complexified stochastic process, but its correctness requires conditions (e.g., holomorphic observables and sufficiently localized probability distributions) and is not guaranteed in all cases."},{"id":"6fc7bcaa-baba-4c80-b8f0-512572201d87","l":"Compressed hydrogen","d":3,"p":"f0761682-18fc-4076-9921-90eae238bc98","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Compressed hydrogen is hydrogen (usually H2) whose density has been increased by applying high pressure. It may refer to high-pressure gas used for storage and transport or to condensed high-pressure phases studied for pressure-driven phenomena such as molecular dissociation and metallization."},{"id":"26e5d799-3bb4-4a63-9861-943bde6efc25","l":"Configuration interaction","d":3,"p":"ae589249-804f-4a7c-94d7-f882ecc129be","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Configuration interaction (CI) is a quantum many-body method that approximates the electronic wavefunction as a linear combination of Slater determinants (electronic configurations) and obtains correlated energies by diagonalizing the Hamiltonian in that determinant basis, thereby including electron correlation beyond a single-determinant (Hartree\u2013Fock) description. Practical CI calculations truncate the determinant space (e.g., CISD), which reduces cost but can introduce size-consistency errors unless the full CI limit is reached."},{"id":"41b65115-0898-4699-b403-a7122728398f","l":"Conformation changes","d":3,"p":"deef9592-7e3e-4949-9bca-ffa9fa9d1e46","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Conformational changes are structural rearrangements of a molecule\u2014especially a polymer or biomacromolecule\u2014produced by rotations about single bonds or domain movements that do not break covalent bonds. Physically, they correspond to transitions between different minima on the molecule's potential-energy landscape and can be driven by thermal fluctuations, ligand binding, mechanical forces, or chemical modification, often altering the molecule's function."},{"id":"0fbfabc4-13a9-48b5-9fe4-dde689a4ced3","l":"Consensus & non-consensus models","d":3,"p":"3cbbf1f7-9427-4812-a34b-410f229c0987","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Consensus models are dynamical models of interacting agents or degrees of freedom whose local update rules drive the system toward a single shared macroscopic state (e.g., unanimous opinion or global phase alignment). Non-consensus models, by contrast, are interaction rules or parameter regimes that prevent global agreement and instead produce persistent disagreement, coexistence of multiple stable states, polarization, or fragmented clusters."},{"id":"711fad0e-53da-4c0f-ba8b-1b3341ee9a47","l":"Continuous Gaussian chain model","d":3,"p":"ac816133-540a-4562-8130-584df553f46a","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"The continuous Gaussian chain model represents a flexible polymer as a continuous space curve r(s) (0 \u2264 s \u2264 L) whose configurations are weighted by a Gaussian (Wiener) measure\u2014equivalently an energy quadratic in the local derivative, e.g. \u221d \u222b|dr/ds|^2 ds\u2014so segment orientations are uncorrelated and the end-to-end vector is Gaussian. It is the continuum limit of the freely-jointed chain, neglects excluded-volume and intrinsic stiffness beyond a Kuhn length, and is used to predict mean-squared size and other ideal-polymer statistics."},{"id":"955db8d1-4fa6-4a09-b918-d6c111224553","l":"Conventional linacs","d":3,"p":"2d000324-694a-4e7a-b5ac-9835da9e7cb0","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Conventional linacs are linear particle accelerators that use a sequence of metallic radio\u2011frequency cavities or drift tubes in vacuum to incrementally accelerate charged particles by synchronized oscillating electromagnetic fields, employing normal\u2011conducting or superconducting structures and external focusing elements rather than plasma- or laser-based accelerating media."},{"id":"66e7b8e9-d055-49d4-a275-88f9fd168d2c","l":"Convergent beam electron diffraction","d":3,"p":"d1402816-03a5-41ce-adad-a9f621e3ec0f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Convergent-beam electron diffraction (CBED) is a transmission electron microscopy technique in which a focused, convergent electron probe illuminates a small region of a crystal, producing diffraction discs rather than sharp spots. The fine intensity distribution within those discs (including higher\u2011order Laue zone features) encodes local crystal symmetry, lattice parameters, strain, thickness and defect information at nanometer spatial resolution."},{"id":"afe8a790-f3c8-4262-92e4-315c228b1a3d","l":"Coupled cluster","d":3,"p":"ae589249-804f-4a7c-94d7-f882ecc129be","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Coupled cluster is a quantum many-body method that builds a correlated wavefunction by applying an exponential cluster operator to a reference state (usually a single Slater determinant), compactly summing contributions from single, double, triple, ... excitations. It provides a systematic, size-extensive hierarchy of approximations (e.g., CCSD, CCSD(T)) that yields highly accurate energies for interacting fermion systems in chemistry and nuclear physics."},{"id":"88cb8a23-42a4-4615-ba96-62e5ea7c2095","l":"Coupled mode theory","d":3,"p":"a0dcc4fb-34a0-4892-a60a-cfb14c0f73e8","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Coupled mode theory is a perturbative framework that describes how distinct wave eigenmodes (in optical fibers, resonators, waveguides, etc.) exchange energy through weak interaction, reducing the full wave problem to a set of coupled ordinary differential equations for the modal amplitudes. The coupling coefficients, determined by mode overlap or a small perturbation to the system, govern the rate and phase of energy transfer and can include effects of loss, gain, and dispersion."},{"id":"c49518ff-424b-4a7b-9eac-613e4451b51c","l":"CP violation","d":3,"p":"ddef0ddc-6f3d-4d34-93c8-5c52964a1704","fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"CP violation is the failure of a physical law to be invariant under the combined operations of charge conjugation (C), which swaps particles with antiparticles, and parity (P), which inverts spatial coordinates \u2014 i.e., a process behaves differently from its CP-mirror image. It is observed in weak interactions (e.g., K and B meson decays), requires complex phases in the underlying theory (such as the CKM matrix), and is a necessary ingredient for generating the universe's matter\u2013antimatter asymmetry."},{"id":"78855062-19be-4a72-9393-4e848f839e5f","l":"Crystallization","d":3,"p":"9988d7c1-f3e7-4fec-b85f-8fa6df6487bb","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Crystallization is the phase-transition process by which atoms, ions, or molecules in a disordered phase (liquid, vapor, or supersaturated solution) organize into a solid with long-range periodic order (a crystal lattice). It typically occurs by nucleation followed by crystal growth and is accompanied by the release of latent heat."},{"id":"f437e02e-b860-450d-8a1c-72152a756f1d","l":"Cylindrical micelles","d":3,"p":"1f72b9f1-0dd7-454f-861d-6c5927dfb128","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Cylindrical micelles are self-assembled, elongated aggregates of amphiphilic molecules in solution in which the hydrophobic tails pack into a cylindrical core and the hydrophilic headgroups face the solvent. Their existence and dimensions are set by molecular packing constraints and interactions, and long flexible (wormlike) cylindrical micelles can entangle and give rise to distinctive viscoelastic and flow behavior."},{"id":"4ce24fb5-6229-4c16-98e2-6953f591504d","l":"Dark-field microscopy","d":3,"p":"a40cc113-e132-471a-bb1b-2d29d8248a64","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Dark-field microscopy is an optical imaging technique in which illumination is delivered as a high\u2011angle hollow cone and the central, unscattered light is blocked so that only light scattered or diffracted by the specimen enters the objective. This produces bright features on a dark background, greatly enhancing contrast for small, transparent, or weakly absorbing structures."},{"id":"d38e30b8-9518-41b3-8592-7fe6b52fff45","l":"Dark-field optical spectroscopy","d":3,"p":"efe40972-7a4f-4a87-80ef-ae39472ce875","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics"],"def":"Dark-field optical spectroscopy is a method that measures the wavelength-dependent light scattered by a sample while excluding the directly transmitted or reflected beam, by using oblique or annular illumination and a collection geometry that captures only scattered light. It enhances contrast for weak scatterers (e.g., nanoparticles, defects) and yields scattering spectra that reveal optical resonances, size, and local dielectric environment."},{"id":"6e829c75-cad5-47b7-82f0-0aa11e506090","l":"DC susceptibility measurements","d":3,"p":"c21c30b0-2219-4793-9841-d9e9d3d0dd2c","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"DC susceptibility measurements determine how a material's magnetization M responds to a static (time-independent) applied magnetic field H by directly measuring M as a function of H or temperature. The magnetic susceptibility \u03c7 = dM/dH (or M/H in the linear regime) quantifies whether the sample is diamagnetic, paramagnetic, ferromagnetic, and reveals magnetic phase transitions and Curie\u2013Weiss behavior."},{"id":"0aed3e0e-4a2b-4c5f-a1dd-51f4acfb6b09","l":"Decision making models","d":3,"p":"3cbbf1f7-9427-4812-a34b-410f229c0987","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Decision-making models are mathematical and computational frameworks that describe how agents or systems choose among alternatives by integrating evidence, preferences, constraints, and uncertainty. They range from normative (Bayesian, utility-maximizing, optimal-control) to descriptive/stochastic dynamical approaches (drift\u2013diffusion, Markov decision processes, reinforcement learning, game theory) used to predict individual and collective choice behavior."},{"id":"5c567aa1-5c30-4feb-867d-3685a6a7c71d","l":"Density functional approximations","d":3,"p":"05d1f0ed-0bf5-4c6d-9e38-a9cf3a67eb5f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Density functional approximations are practical approximations to the unknown exact functionals in density functional theory\u2014most importantly the exchange\u2013correlation functional\u2014expressed as explicit or semi-local functions of the electron density (and sometimes its gradients, kinetic-energy density, or orbitals) to produce energies and potentials for electronic-structure calculations. Common classes include LDA, GGA, meta-GGA and hybrid or range-separated functionals, chosen to trade off accuracy against computational cost."},{"id":"50f22423-51dd-440c-a869-bb9c157808bb","l":"Density functional calculations","d":3,"p":"05d1f0ed-0bf5-4c6d-9e38-a9cf3a67eb5f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Density functional calculations are computational electronic-structure methods based on density functional theory that determine the ground-state properties of many-electron systems by using the electron density as the central variable instead of the full many-body wavefunction. In practice they solve effective single-particle Kohn\u2013Sham equations with approximate exchange\u2013correlation functionals to predict energies, structures, and response properties."},{"id":"71518a9c-fa1a-4603-8a45-62976bec13bc","l":"Density functional theory development","d":3,"p":"05d1f0ed-0bf5-4c6d-9e38-a9cf3a67eb5f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Density functional theory development refers to the research and engineering of new approximations, algorithms, and theoretical extensions that improve the accuracy, efficiency, and scope of density functional theory\u2014a quantum-mechanical framework that describes many-electron systems using the electronic density rather than the full many-body wavefunction. It encompasses creating better exchange\u2013correlation functionals, nonlocal and time-dependent formalisms, numerical and scaling improvements, and hybrid or embedding methods to extend DFT to broader classes of materials and phenomena."},{"id":"bc60fcb7-3e83-4bb5-80c4-f288799b5b55","l":"Differential scanning calorimetry","d":3,"p":"64f26e6b-2baf-45fa-bd50-974c3139e6bc","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Differential scanning calorimetry (DSC) is a thermoanalytical technique that measures the difference in heat flow into a sample and an inert reference as they are heated or cooled under a controlled temperature program, providing heat-capacity data and enthalpies and temperatures of phase transitions and reactions (e.g., melting, crystallization, glass transitions)."},{"id":"b9e02b47-e089-442d-ad1c-a29f2e65c709","l":"Diffusion quantum Monte Carlo","d":3,"p":"9dc2ee1a-ff51-438a-b7c7-1045cd385cfc","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics","Networks","Particles & Fields","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Physics of Living Systems","Plasma Physics","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Diffusion quantum Monte Carlo (DMC) is a projector quantum Monte Carlo algorithm that stochastically evolves an ensemble of \"walkers\" according to the imaginary-time Schr\u00f6dinger equation\u2014using diffusion, importance-sampling drift, and branching\u2014to project out the many-body ground-state wavefunction and energy. For fermionic systems it typically uses the fixed-node approximation to enforce antisymmetry, so computed energies are accurate up to controllable time-step and fixed-node errors."},{"id":"ea7709e2-5c56-4cde-be5c-062e8cf4089a","l":"Dilute magnetic semiconductors","d":3,"p":"51df7229-8061-48c4-8dd9-fa422ee4f7b5","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Dilute magnetic semiconductors are semiconducting materials in which a small fraction (typically a few percent) of the host atoms are substituted by magnetic ions, introducing localized magnetic moments into the crystal. Those moments interact via the semiconductor's charge carriers (electrons or holes), producing tunable, often carrier-mediated magnetic order and enabling combined control of spin and charge for spintronic applications."},{"id":"98df1e80-9659-40a8-9d2e-3a4541e918e6","l":"Diode lasers","d":3,"p":"371ef245-60f3-45d9-b265-ed7abd84f07f","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Diode lasers are semiconductor p\u2013n junction devices that produce coherent light when an injected current creates stimulated emission in an active region. Optical feedback from the device facets or internal gratings (e.g., Fabry\u2013P\u00e9rot or distributed-feedback cavities) selects the lasing wavelength, which is chiefly determined by the semiconductor bandgap and cavity design, yielding compact, efficient light sources across infrared to visible wavelengths."},{"id":"db7f4ead-5152-4992-ab52-35070979824d","l":"Dipole quantum emitters","d":3,"p":"dd5ba9a6-ec6b-46ce-94ef-26ee41ff0db4","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Dipole quantum emitters are single quantum systems (atoms, molecules, quantum dots, color centers, etc.) whose optical transitions can be modeled as point electric (or sometimes magnetic) dipoles characterized by a transition dipole moment. This dipole moment determines the emitter's polarization, angular radiation pattern and coupling to nearby electromagnetic modes (e.g., cavities or plasmonic structures), governing emission rates and selection rules."},{"id":"26346c2a-8c52-49e7-b58d-cd91183e792e","l":"Discrete dipole approximation","d":3,"p":"a0dcc4fb-34a0-4892-a60a-cfb14c0f73e8","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The discrete dipole approximation (DDA) is a numerical method for computing scattering and absorption of electromagnetic waves by particles of arbitrary shape and composition by replacing the target with a finite array of polarizable point dipoles. The induced dipoles are solved self-consistently including their mutual interactions via the electromagnetic Green\u2019s function to yield far\u2011field cross sections and near\u2011field distributions, with accuracy set by making the dipole spacing small compared to the wavelength and structural features."},{"id":"7cfdaeec-195a-4ab4-b184-40c5c1ff3f4e","l":"Disk micelles","d":3,"p":"1f72b9f1-0dd7-454f-861d-6c5927dfb128","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Disk micelles are self-assembled, disk-shaped aggregates of amphiphilic molecules in a selective solvent, with hydrophobic parts sequestered inside and hydrophilic heads exposed to the solvent. Their flat geometry is set by molecular packing and by rim-stabilizing species (as in bicelles), making them important membrane-mimetic and nanoscale soft-matter structures."},{"id":"327ef5cc-47a5-4abf-83d3-958f6fa9b598","l":"DNA","d":3,"p":"870d415a-ceb3-4e49-a475-798d07177a83","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Physics of Living Systems","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"DNA (deoxyribonucleic acid) is a biological polymer of nucleotide monomers that forms a right-handed double helix with a sugar\u2011phosphate backbone and complementary base pairs (A\u2013T, G\u2013C). Its linear sequence of bases encodes genetic information and underlies replication, transcription, and heredity."},{"id":"46dd9789-2257-434d-86ca-0ef092f34e31","l":"DNA-protein interactions","d":3,"p":"b9a7848b-4a90-4912-94b2-4d9c05b0a736","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"DNA\u2013protein interactions are the physical contacts and binding events between proteins and DNA that enable molecular recognition, processing, and structural organization of the genome. They result from specific and nonspecific forces (electrostatic, hydrogen bonding, van der Waals) and are governed by kinetics and thermodynamics, producing effects such as sequence-specific binding, bending, looping, sliding, and regulation of transcription, replication and repair."},{"id":"786d1afc-bea6-4885-839d-17debd6a7b75","l":"Domain wall motion","d":3,"p":"bbdac54f-7bcd-4dc7-b02d-7ad7672d0832","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Domain wall motion is the time-dependent displacement of the thin interface separating regions (domains) with different orientations of an order parameter (e.g., magnetization in ferromagnets or polarization in ferroelectrics). It is driven by external stimuli (magnetic/electric fields, stress, or spin-polarized currents) and governed by material parameters, damping, pinning by defects, and microscopic torques (e.g., spin-transfer or spin-orbit torques)."},{"id":"6f68d252-72cf-411e-9abd-30987340f58f","l":"Doppler ultrasonography","d":3,"p":"0a3aa840-b81e-4a59-a119-b91cb6514b6d","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Doppler ultrasonography is an ultrasound technique that uses the Doppler effect on waves reflected from moving scatterers (typically blood cells) to detect and quantify their velocity and flow direction. The measured frequency shift is converted into velocity information and displayed as spectral waveforms, color-flow maps, or power-Doppler images for diagnostic imaging."},{"id":"a734a289-c8b5-4071-880e-44dc3fe472f6","l":"Drift, ion- or electron-cyclotron instability","d":3,"p":"b094f9fa-ebc7-4e4d-8957-0a7303f6e91e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"A drift ion- or electron-cyclotron instability is a microinstability in a magnetized plasma in which cross-field drift motions (caused by density or temperature gradients or E\u00d7B flows) resonate with the cyclotron motion of ions or electrons. This resonant coupling excites waves near the species' cyclotron frequency and converts drift free energy into growing oscillations, enhancing transport and heating."},{"id":"e23ba3c3-a1bd-418d-906e-6282fd941525","l":"d-wave","d":3,"p":"91ed8eb4-208e-4fd8-a258-aeae8213de49","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"d-wave denotes a spatial symmetry with orbital angular momentum l = 2 (associated with d-type spherical harmonics or atomic d orbitals). In superconductors a d-wave pairing state (e.g. d_{x^2\u2212y^2}) has a gap function that changes sign under 90\u00b0 rotation and contains nodes\u2014directions in momentum space where the superconducting gap vanishes."},{"id":"0774515d-9691-4527-84c5-ec2340cf4404","l":"Dynamical diffraction","d":3,"p":"32de7669-68ef-4f3e-9598-2a70300ea01e","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Dynamical diffraction is the wave-theory description of scattering from a periodic medium that treats multiple coherent scattering events self-consistently, so the scattered waves inside the material are coupled rather than arising from a single scattering event. It is necessary for describing diffraction from nearly perfect crystals (X\u2011rays, electrons, neutrons) and predicts phenomena such as intensity redistribution, Pendell\u00f6sung oscillations, extinction and the finite Darwin angular/energy width of Bragg peaks."},{"id":"9cb6d520-c858-440f-8e4e-c1e78734140f","l":"Dynamical mean field theory","d":3,"p":"2d89a076-7a54-4494-973a-ea562f654b8e","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Dynamical mean field theory (DMFT) is a nonperturbative computational framework for strongly correlated lattice systems that maps the full lattice problem onto a self-consistent quantum impurity model, thereby retaining local (time-dependent) quantum fluctuations while approximating nonlocal spatial correlations. It is exact in the infinite coordination limit and is widely used to study phenomena like the Mott metal\u2013insulator transition and correlated electron dynamics."},{"id":"798b8073-f864-4a93-af14-3831d282f6a5","l":"Dynamics of nonlinear optical systems","d":3,"p":"ecf6e836-240a-4093-9d1c-46680eeeccad","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Dynamics of nonlinear optical systems is the study of how electromagnetic fields evolve in time and space when the medium's response depends nonlinearly on the field amplitude, so that dispersion, diffraction, gain/loss and nonlinear polarization interact. It encompasses phenomena such as solitons, modulational instability, pattern formation, bistability, mode-locking and optical chaos, typically modeled by equations like the nonlinear Schr\u00f6dinger, Maxwell\u2013Bloch or coupled-mode equations."},{"id":"5c0655b9-1ca6-415c-b8ef-04ed1a9c8f38","l":"Dynamic spin injection","d":3,"p":"e9c1b6a7-5fa0-4e03-ba40-c835aaeee544","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Dynamic spin injection is the transfer of spin angular momentum into a material driven by time-dependent magnetization dynamics (for example, a precessing ferromagnet), typically producing a pure spin current or spin accumulation in an adjacent nonmagnetic layer. It is commonly realized by spin pumping during ferromagnetic resonance, where the oscillating magnetization injects spins across an interface without net charge flow."},{"id":"c36ff5fb-4144-458b-9f26-d20acc8b2aea","l":"Earthquake detection & measurement","d":3,"p":"e31ca7a8-41cc-44bb-9be6-86743f019eff","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Earthquake detection & measurement is the set of techniques and instruments (seismometers, accelerometers, and seismic networks) used to record seismic waves, identify earthquake events, and distinguish them from noise. It quantifies source properties\u2014origin time, hypocenter/epicenter, depth, magnitude (e.g., moment magnitude), focal mechanism\u2014and the intensity and frequency content of ground shaking from waveform analysis."},{"id":"f7ceeb08-47c1-4834-bf9c-ad31a197e2f7","l":"Earthquake statistics & scaling","d":3,"p":"e31ca7a8-41cc-44bb-9be6-86743f019eff","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Earthquake statistics & scaling is the study of the statistical patterns and scaling laws that describe how earthquake occurrence, sizes, durations, and spatial extent are distributed in time and space. It includes empirical laws such as the Gutenberg\u2013Richter power law for frequency versus magnitude, Omori's law for aftershock decay, and scaling relations linking seismic moment, rupture length, and duration, reflecting self-similarity and critical-like behavior of fault systems."},{"id":"bf582a64-42a5-4009-a9af-e9a1100f5de6","l":"Elastic back scattering spectroscopy","d":3,"p":"1fc8f216-e853-4b59-bdb2-2ac001bd3ea2","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Elastic back scattering spectroscopy is an analytical technique in which energetic probe particles (commonly ions in Rutherford backscattering) are scattered elastically from a sample and the energy and angle distribution of particles scattered back toward the source are measured. From the kinematic energy shifts and energy loss in the material one extracts elemental identity, relative concentration and depth profiling of near-surface layers."},{"id":"88e709b5-d686-4d69-a431-3b3ef2249553","l":"Elastic recoil detection","d":3,"p":"1fc8f216-e853-4b59-bdb2-2ac001bd3ea2","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Elastic recoil detection is an ion-beam surface-analysis technique in which energetic ions elastically scatter from atoms in a target and eject (recoil) those target atoms into a detector. By measuring the recoils' energies (or time-of-flight) and counting rates one obtains element-specific near-surface composition and depth profiles, with particular sensitivity to light elements such as hydrogen."},{"id":"ef10775b-ded9-4e62-b477-a77c782fca69","l":"Electrical generation of spin carriers","d":3,"p":"257ff504-8723-46c7-aea0-ec7d55a0dc6f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Electrical generation of spin carriers is the creation of charge carriers (electrons or holes) that possess a net spin polarization by applying electrical currents or voltages. Typical mechanisms include spin injection from ferromagnetic contacts, the spin Hall and Rashba\u2013Edelstein effects, and spin\u2013orbit torques, enabling transport and manipulation of spin angular momentum in spintronic devices."},{"id":"c19bdc1b-a173-4731-97b6-7ed4e548fba1","l":"Electrical properties of membranes","d":3,"p":"4e68d438-9022-4da6-ae03-4074370de48f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Electrical properties of membranes are the characteristics that determine how a thin dielectric barrier separating ionic solutions sustains and controls electric fields and ionic currents across it. Key measurable quantities are the transmembrane potential, membrane capacitance, resistance (or conductance), surface charge, and the dynamics set by ion channels, pumps, and electrodiffusion."},{"id":"9dd0ce9a-037b-4ba1-9270-169c681cf28e","l":"Electrical spin injection","d":3,"p":"e9c1b6a7-5fa0-4e03-ba40-c835aaeee544","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Electrical spin injection is the creation of a non-equilibrium population of spin-polarized charge carriers in a material by driving an electrical current from a spin-polarized source (e.g., a ferromagnet) across an interface into a nonmagnetic conductor or semiconductor, producing spin accumulation and a spin current. It is the primary means of transferring and detecting spin information in spintronic devices, and its efficiency depends on interface properties and spin relaxation."},{"id":"5d1749fd-5090-4e69-8da9-d571fbb361ff","l":"Electrocatalysis","d":3,"p":"4aabe9ae-12ad-4c2e-a578-fb7239d3b285","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Electrocatalysis is the catalytic acceleration of electrochemical reactions at an electrode, where a catalyst at the electrode\u2013electrolyte interface facilitates electron transfer, lowers activation barriers and reduces the required overpotential. It proceeds via adsorption-mediated reaction pathways that control reaction rates and selectivity in technologies such as fuel cells, water electrolysis, and CO2 reduction."},{"id":"75ffdbb6-e062-498e-902f-b2847a437e4a","l":"Electroencephalography","d":3,"p":"422ebc8c-e0eb-4e3c-ba60-bf7becf9371d","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Electroencephalography (EEG) is a noninvasive electrophysiological technique that records voltage fluctuations at the scalp generated by synchronized extracellular currents in large populations of cortical neurons (primarily pyramidal cells). It provides high temporal (millisecond) resolution of brain activity\u2014used to study oscillatory rhythms, event-related potentials, and pathological patterns\u2014though with relatively coarse spatial localization."},{"id":"583c1101-8653-42c7-91b1-b9390d4b715a","l":"Electrolysis of water","d":3,"p":"81236d07-2535-466b-93f2-e4d502bf5935","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Electrolysis of water is the electrochemical process in which an external electric current drives the non-spontaneous decomposition of liquid water into hydrogen and oxygen gases at two electrodes. At the cathode water is reduced to produce H2 and at the anode it is oxidized to produce O2; the process requires an electrolyte and an applied voltage that exceeds the thermodynamic decomposition potential plus any overpotentials."},{"id":"3c901cbb-2a04-4205-87c9-dda8463ab2dc","l":"Electromagnetic calorimeters","d":3,"p":"ce866ada-ee57-4c9c-81a0-937385d9ee88","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Electromagnetic calorimeters are particle detectors that measure the energy (and often position) of electrons, positrons, and photons by absorbing them and converting the resulting electromagnetic showers into measurable signals (scintillation light or ionization). They use combinations of dense absorber and active sensing media, are sized in units of radiation length to contain showers, and their homogeneous or sampling design determines energy resolution and particle identification."},{"id":"b09100da-62de-4a00-93de-c2706fea5be7","l":"Electron dipole spin resonance","d":3,"p":"9dfe8ff5-a8ed-484c-a604-50ec54209dd1","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Electron dipole spin resonance (EDSR) is a technique for driving resonant transitions of an electron's spin using an oscillating electric field instead of a direct ac magnetic field. The electric field couples to the spin indirectly via the electron's orbital motion\u2014typically through spin\u2013orbit interaction or a spatially varying magnetic field (e.g., a micromagnet)\u2014allowing coherent spin rotations at the electron spin resonance frequency."},{"id":"541e8dcf-6bfe-44c3-960e-d16c7eb5ed17","l":"Electron driven instability","d":3,"p":"b094f9fa-ebc7-4e4d-8957-0a7303f6e91e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"An electron-driven instability is a plasma instability in which free energy in the electron distribution (e.g., beams, currents, temperature anisotropy, or a bump-on-tail) is transferred by resonant interaction to electromagnetic or electrostatic waves, causing exponential growth of perturbations. Such instabilities (for example two-stream, bump-on-tail, whistler or electron-cyclotron instabilities) alter particle distributions and fields in laboratory and space plasmas."},{"id":"3377f39d-0259-4911-a1a6-6066a7b926d3","l":"Electron magnetic circular dichroism","d":3,"p":"8d9a5500-7204-4ae4-8a93-388da8f8d432","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Electron magnetic circular dichroism (EMCD) is the electron-microscopy analogue of X-ray magnetic circular dichroism: a technique that detects the magnetization-dependent difference in electron energy-loss spectra recorded with opposite scattering geometries in a transmission electron microscope. The dichroic signal, arising from spin\u2013orbit\u2013coupling\u2013sensitive core-level excitations, provides element-specific information on spin and orbital magnetic moments with nanometer spatial resolution."},{"id":"881b156b-3c81-4163-a39a-e9e40f2eada0","l":"Electron probe micro-analysis","d":3,"p":"34773509-53cc-4885-a027-7bd3497f7157","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Electron probe microanalysis (EPMA) is a microanalytical technique in which a focused electron beam bombards a small region of a solid to generate characteristic X-rays; the measured X-ray intensities (using wavelength- or energy-dispersive spectrometers) are used to identify and quantitatively determine the elemental composition with micrometer-scale spatial resolution. It is widely used in geology, materials science and metallurgy for quantitative, often nondestructive, elemental analysis and mapping."},{"id":"20ebb1a7-d36c-4742-b0a8-e689f24ca9ed","l":"Electron spin resonance scanning tunneling microscopy","d":3,"p":"c33a7211-ebcd-4336-917d-a75ecb1e50c4","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Electron spin resonance scanning tunneling microscopy (ESR-STM) is a technique that combines scanning tunneling microscopy with electron spin resonance to detect, image, and coherently manipulate individual electron spins on surfaces with atomic spatial resolution. An RF magnetic or electric field applied via the STM tip drives spin transitions at the Zeeman resonance, which are read out as changes or modulations in the tunneling current or differential conductance."},{"id":"dbd7f2c2-5a2c-4a8d-9991-d3f453748a55","l":"Energy recovery linacs","d":3,"p":"2d000324-694a-4e7a-b5ac-9835da9e7cb0","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"An energy recovery linac (ERL) is a linear accelerator in which an accelerated charged-particle beam is returned through the accelerating structures out of phase so that its kinetic energy is transferred back into the RF fields and recovered for reuse. By decelerating spent high\u2011current, high\u2011brightness beams and reusing their energy, ERLs provide efficient, high-average-current sources for light sources, free-electron lasers, and accelerator-driven experiments while greatly reducing required RF power."},{"id":"2391532a-95f1-4c6d-a4ae-fadd54a03043","l":"Entanglement detection","d":3,"p":"5de0faa9-608e-450b-8d04-5aa4219dad8d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Entanglement detection is the set of experimental and theoretical methods used to determine whether a composite quantum state is nonseparable (entangled) rather than a statistical mixture of product states. Common techniques include violation of Bell inequalities, entanglement witnesses, the positive partial transpose (PPT) criterion, and quantum state tomography combined with separability tests."},{"id":"24680aeb-3710-4132-8221-f37921bf708f","l":"Entanglement manipulation","d":3,"p":"5de0faa9-608e-450b-8d04-5aa4219dad8d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Entanglement manipulation is the set of quantum operations and protocols used to create, transform, distribute, and preserve entanglement between subsystems\u2014often subject to constraints such as local operations and classical communication (LOCC). Common tasks include entanglement generation, swapping, purification (distillation), concentration/dilution, and protection against decoherence."},{"id":"17411eb6-bb56-4169-aaa2-01827a8ace87","l":"Entanglement measures","d":3,"p":"5de0faa9-608e-450b-8d04-5aa4219dad8d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Entanglement measures are quantitative functions that assign a nonnegative number to a quantum state to indicate how much quantum correlation (entanglement) it contains, vanishing for separable (unentangled) states. They are constructed to be nonincreasing under local operations and classical communication (LOCC) and include examples such as entanglement entropy (for pure states), concurrence, entanglement of formation, and logarithmic negativity."},{"id":"95477339-c975-4a6d-a24a-be92cb8f03bd","l":"Entanglement production","d":3,"p":"5de0faa9-608e-450b-8d04-5aa4219dad8d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Entanglement production is the process by which quantum evolution, interactions, or measurements generate nonclassical correlations between parts of a composite system so that the joint state cannot be written as a product of individual states. It is quantified by measures such as entanglement entropy, concurrence, or negativity and can be characterized operationally by the entangling power or production rate of a dynamical process or quantum gate."},{"id":"1aa71c79-cce3-4eed-943c-5a6c7da01df1","l":"Entropy production","d":3,"p":"cd403d00-d86b-41dc-a6fb-72bc3a302627","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Entropy production is the amount (or rate) of entropy generated inside a system by irreversible processes (e.g., heat flow, viscosity, chemical reactions), quantifying the degree of irreversibility and energy dissipation. By the second law it is nonnegative and can be written in continuum/nonequilibrium thermodynamics as a sum of products of thermodynamic fluxes and their conjugate forces."},{"id":"6232be3b-e875-4196-9786-98184a53b973","l":"Epitaxial strain","d":3,"p":"1b8cdb74-7017-48c1-a0d2-b726c073d8b8","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Epitaxial strain is the elastic distortion a crystalline thin film acquires when it is grown coherently on a substrate with a different lattice constant, forcing the film's in-plane lattice to match the substrate and producing tensile or compressive strain. This strain modifies the film's structural, electronic, magnetic, and optical properties and can relax by forming defects (e.g., misfit dislocations) once the film exceeds a critical thickness."},{"id":"db6e35c3-c64a-45c9-b0f7-5b5aac85be63","l":"Evanescent waves","d":3,"p":"07396f85-99a2-483d-be5b-3725e182caa3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Evanescent waves are localized wave solutions whose amplitude decays exponentially with distance from a boundary or region where the perpendicular component of the wavevector is imaginary. They arise in situations like total internal reflection, waveguide cutoff, or tunneling, remaining confined near the interface and not carrying energy away into the bulk perpendicular to that interface."},{"id":"96bbb76c-5300-45a8-a2c0-651160e0f0c9","l":"Exact diagonalization","d":3,"p":"d4c7a190-1d1c-4c1c-a1b5-3a3515f59c93","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Exact diagonalization is a numerical method in quantum many-body physics where the system Hamiltonian is represented as a matrix in a finite basis and then diagonalized numerically to obtain exact eigenvalues and eigenvectors for that finite-size Hilbert space. It gives exact (within the chosen basis) energies and correlation functions but is limited to small systems or symmetry-reduced sectors because the Hilbert space grows exponentially."},{"id":"fcf1f7ab-78db-4578-9509-9339d465a3d5","l":"Exceptional Lie algebra symmetries","d":3,"p":"543d3af2-a8bc-462b-9d72-753b6de0d8ce","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Exceptional Lie algebra symmetries are continuous symmetries whose Lie algebras belong to the five exceptional simple Lie algebras (G2, F4, E6, E7, E8), a finite set that does not fit into the classical A\u2013D series and is distinguished by unique root systems and representation structures. They arise in physics where these rare, highly constrained symmetry groups govern dynamics or geometry, for example in certain grand-unified models, string compactifications, and special-holonomy manifolds."},{"id":"11598af2-484c-44e2-b795-7b98492778cb","l":"Extended Hubbard model","d":3,"p":"a0ce8ec5-e2c2-40f4-b53f-9fd9e2572289","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The extended Hubbard model is a lattice model of interacting electrons that generalizes the Hubbard model by adding nonlocal interactions (typically nearest-neighbor Coulomb repulsion V and sometimes longer-range or correlated-hopping terms) to the usual on-site interaction U and nearest-neighbor hopping t. It is used to study how the competition between kinetic energy, local correlations, and intersite interactions produces phases such as charge-density waves, superconductivity, and Mott and magnetic transitions."},{"id":"847fb2b1-93d7-4bf5-bcb4-d049d395b76c","l":"Femtosecond laser irradiation","d":3,"p":"dedc6dbe-3651-4ad0-9d37-b260e8975adc","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Femtosecond laser irradiation is the exposure of matter to ultrashort laser pulses with durations on the order of 10^-15 seconds, producing very high peak intensities that drive nonlinear absorption and rapid electronic excitation. Because the pulse deposits energy faster than heat can diffuse, it produces nonequilibrium electronic and structural dynamics (e.g., multiphoton ionization, plasma formation, ultrafast phase changes) useful for precision material processing and ultrafast studies."},{"id":"4f05e51d-a3d3-4c89-bbeb-bbfa122e6791","l":"Femtosecond laser spectroscopy","d":3,"p":"dedc6dbe-3651-4ad0-9d37-b260e8975adc","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Femtosecond laser spectroscopy is a time-resolved optical technique that uses ultrashort (~10^\u221215 s) laser pulses as pump and probe to initiate and then monitor electronic, vibrational, and structural dynamics in atoms, molecules, and condensed matter. By varying the delay between pulses it reconstructs transient states and reaction pathways, enabling direct measurement of processes that occur on femtosecond timescales."},{"id":"8f0c82d2-6deb-4c23-9c22-7b83dece782b","l":"Few-cycle light pulses","d":3,"p":"c35dddca-b514-4561-954c-e3b4ad2f1912","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Few-cycle light pulses are ultrashort optical pulses whose temporal duration spans only a few oscillations of the carrier electromagnetic field (typically 1\u201310 cycles), corresponding to durations of a few femtoseconds at visible/near-IR wavelengths. Their brevity implies a very broad spectral bandwidth and makes the carrier\u2013envelope phase crucial for time-resolved and strong-field phenomena."},{"id":"6d593cba-6afb-4099-a31b-fbf0eed249a5","l":"Fibril formation","d":3,"p":"9988d7c1-f3e7-4fec-b85f-8fa6df6487bb","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Fibril formation is the self\u2011assembly process by which small monomeric molecules (often proteins or peptides) organize into long, ordered fibrous aggregates stabilized by specific intermolecular interactions and conformational rearrangements. It typically proceeds by nucleation\u2011dependent polymerization, with distinct lag, growth and saturation phases that are described by kinetic and thermodynamic models."},{"id":"f6f3fe36-54ce-45f1-86a3-ad41516135c3","l":"Field-effect transistors","d":3,"p":"ce85a590-748b-47e4-a10b-a25c874f3bdf","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A field-effect transistor (FET) is a voltage-controlled semiconductor device in which an electric field from a gate electrode modulates the conductivity of a channel between source and drain to control current flow. FETs (e.g., MOSFETs, JFETs) have high input impedance and are widely used as amplifiers and switches in electronic circuits."},{"id":"cfc4d494-9c93-4476-ba86-007a1ecac746","l":"Filamentation in plasmas","d":3,"p":"3f71ccf5-16ff-425f-b7b3-4791f9503b59","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Filamentation in plasmas is the spontaneous formation of narrow, elongated channels of enhanced current, density, or electromagnetic field that arise when transverse perturbations grow\u2014typically via the Weibel or current\u2011filamentation instability\u2014so that charged particles are magnetically pinched into filaments. It occurs in laser\u2013plasma interactions, beam\u2013plasma systems, and astrophysical shocks and strongly affects transport, magnetic\u2011field generation, and radiation."},{"id":"0ce5fe9d-416a-4c9a-ace7-9acedd3b2f66","l":"Fire-hose instability","d":3,"p":"3f71ccf5-16ff-425f-b7b3-4791f9503b59","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"The fire-hose instability is a magnetized-plasma instability that occurs when the pressure along the magnetic field exceeds the perpendicular pressure so much that magnetic tension can no longer stabilize perturbations, causing field lines to \"whip\" and Alfv\u00e9n\u2011like transverse modes to grow. In fluid (CGL/MHD) form the instability sets in when p_parallel \u2212 p_perp > B^2/\u03bc0, i.e., when the effective magnetic tension becomes negative."},{"id":"de53d02f-91de-4093-9d5d-abe30bc2d58a","l":"First passage problems","d":3,"p":"a1f541a0-a451-4514-a805-93e2bebb2a14","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Nonlinear Dynamics","Plasma Physics","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"First passage problems concern the statistics of when (or where) a stochastic process first reaches a specified state, threshold, or boundary (an absorbing point); they ask for quantities like the first-passage-time distribution, survival probability, and splitting probabilities for random walks, diffusion, and escape or reaction processes."},{"id":"ab9eaaf7-5280-4687-8cba-f29edf7b90ea","l":"Fixed-field alternating gradient accelerators","d":3,"p":"f53811b7-7f5c-46d6-ab05-ae38aa1a2ab6","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"A fixed-field alternating-gradient (FFAG) accelerator is a circular accelerator that uses static (time-independent) magnetic fields together with alternating-gradient (strong focusing) magnet arrangements to accelerate particles over a large momentum range without changing magnet strengths. The alternating focusing and defocusing sectors keep transverse motion stable while particle orbits shift radially with energy, enabling high repetition rates and large energy acceptance (with scaling and non-scaling design variants)."},{"id":"3b66172c-b80b-47f5-85cc-8ee5c2e80fe4","l":"Flagella","d":3,"p":"66d73f04-5784-47a0-b3c7-c8b15ee98de1","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Fluid Dynamics","Physics of Living Systems","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Flagella are slender, whip\u2011like cellular appendages that propel cells through fluids by generating forces along their length. Bacterial flagella are helical filaments rotated by a molecular motor, while eukaryotic flagella produce traveling bending waves; both operate in low\u2011Reynolds\u2011number hydrodynamics where viscous forces dominate."},{"id":"7c671d6d-555c-41a8-b908-38f3edeccffa","l":"Flexomagnetism","d":3,"p":"1ac35d4c-ffe7-4297-b8a8-69e72faecbf6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Flexomagnetism is the linear coupling between magnetization and spatial gradients of strain: a nonuniform mechanical deformation (strain gradient) induces a magnetization, and conversely magnetization or its gradients produce stress. It is the magnetic analogue of flexoelectricity and becomes especially important in nanoscale structures, domain walls, and centrosymmetric crystals where uniform magnetostrictive or piezomagnetic responses are symmetry-forbidden."},{"id":"ce4604c4-fbad-4ef4-b8fd-e2acff7e1869","l":"Flow boundary instabilities","d":3,"p":"0f0cd98d-5753-4e9a-99e9-068f5528eafe","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Flow boundary instabilities are hydrodynamic instabilities that originate at boundaries or interfaces in a flow\u2014such as solid walls, free surfaces, or fluid\u2013fluid interfaces\u2014where strong gradients in velocity, density, or stress make small perturbations grow. They include mechanisms like Tollmien\u2013Schlichting waves in boundary layers, Kelvin\u2013Helmholtz shear instabilities at interfaces, and Rayleigh\u2013Taylor buoyancy-driven overturning, and often lead to transition to turbulence or enhanced mixing."},{"id":"580982af-615d-4d2e-8b84-4df6d28a4416","l":"Flow confinement","d":3,"p":"0f0cd98d-5753-4e9a-99e9-068f5528eafe","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Flow confinement is the restriction of fluid or particle motion to a limited spatial region by physical boundaries or applied fields, which alters transport, velocity profiles, and stability compared with unconfined flow. Examples include channel or pipe flows and porous-media flows, as well as magnetic or electrostatic confinement of plasmas."},{"id":"2f1f7cb2-1b29-4d1a-a13e-3768c1f582e0","l":"Fluctuations","d":3,"p":"deef9592-7e3e-4949-9bca-ffa9fa9d1e46","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Fluctuations are spontaneous, typically random deviations of a physical quantity from its mean value in time or space, caused by microscopic degrees of freedom, thermal agitation, or quantum uncertainty. They are described statistically (e.g., by variance and correlation functions) and underlie phenomena such as noise, Brownian motion, and critical behavior."},{"id":"9b656cab-64ae-4b47-ba61-845c2beda40b","l":"Fluorescence","d":3,"p":"6bdea32e-2fdf-4421-b982-41a2a011a363","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Fluorescence is the prompt emission of light by a molecule or material that has absorbed electromagnetic radiation: an electron is excited to a higher electronic state and then returns radiatively to a lower state, emitting a photon typically of lower energy (longer wavelength) than the absorbed one (Stokes shift). It usually involves spin-allowed transitions and occurs on nanosecond or faster timescales while the excitation is present."},{"id":"254edc62-1b8e-4ea2-9c6c-4133e2f52f76","l":"Fluorescent biomolecules","d":3,"p":"9c437e89-cf6a-4250-8738-b43ded1eb4ff","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Fluorescent biomolecules are biological molecules that absorb photons to reach an electronic excited state and then re-emit part of that energy as longer-wavelength fluorescent light (a process characterized by a Stokes shift). They can be intrinsic chromophores (e.g., aromatic amino acids, GFP) or externally attached fluorophores and are described by excitation/emission spectra, quantum yield, fluorescence lifetime, and photostability, properties exploited in imaging and spectroscopy."},{"id":"6c882ee4-606b-4a0d-af30-406061e22be7","l":"Folding","d":3,"p":"deef9592-7e3e-4949-9bca-ffa9fa9d1e46","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Folding is the process by which an extended body or structure adopts a compacted shape through localized bending and the formation of creases, hinges, or loops, converting applied stress or thermal/entropic forces into curvature. It occurs across scales\u2014from thin elastic sheets and geological strata to polymers and proteins\u2014and is governed by mechanical instabilities, boundary conditions, and energetic minimization."},{"id":"d92dc6e4-38f7-4458-bb3c-857fe7dbef88","l":"Fractional quantum Hall effect","d":3,"p":"9017d068-f9d7-42f8-b083-a385e9b14c0b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Fractional quantum Hall effect: a phenomenon in two-dimensional electron systems at low temperature and strong perpendicular magnetic field where the Hall conductance is quantized at fractional values (filling factors \u03bd = p/q) of e^2/h instead of integers. It results from strong electron\u2013electron interactions that produce incompressible, topologically ordered quantum liquids (e.g., Laughlin and hierarchical states) whose quasiparticle excitations carry fractional electric charge and obey anyonic exchange statistics."},{"id":"bade8808-168e-44b0-bf2a-161d31ed4ba8","l":"Fragmentation functions","d":3,"p":"edc1099a-36c7-42d9-9364-842b964617bb","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Fragmentation functions are nonperturbative probability densities D_i^h(z, \u03bc^2) that describe the likelihood for a high-energy parton i (quark or gluon) to hadronize into a specific hadron h carrying a fraction z of the parton's momentum. They are universal inputs to QCD factorization theorems and evolve with the factorization scale \u03bc according to DGLAP-type equations."},{"id":"faf89c76-5520-4281-8530-097abffc736f","l":"Freely-jointed chain","d":3,"p":"ac816133-540a-4562-8130-584df553f46a","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"A freely-jointed chain is an idealized polymer model of N rigid segments of fixed length joined by frictionless hinges that allow each link to adopt any orientation independently (no bending energy or excluded-volume). Its end-to-end vector performs a random walk, with zero mean and mean-squared length \u27e8R^2\u27e9 = N b^2 for segment length b."},{"id":"2a5449e4-be35-4eec-b96d-68bed85b8c07","l":"Freely-rotating chain","d":3,"p":"ac816133-540a-4562-8130-584df553f46a","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"A freely-rotating chain is a polymer model in which successive bonds have fixed lengths and a fixed bond angle between adjacent bonds, while the dihedral (torsional) angles are free to rotate independently; this yields short-range orientational correlations along the chain but overall flexible conformations."},{"id":"ec2de03c-c5fd-49b1-9ad7-f01419d70561","l":"Friedberg-Lee neutrino mass model","d":3,"p":"47a1bb2e-c1ae-4b2c-a48d-b75767137d82","fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"The Friedberg\u2013Lee neutrino mass model postulates a global \"Friedberg\u2013Lee\" translational family symmetry under which neutrino fields shift by flavor-dependent constant spinors, which constrains the neutrino mass matrix to a special texture with a vanishing eigenvalue. This symmetry-based construction can naturally produce one massless neutrino and specific relations among masses and mixing parameters that aim to account for observed neutrino oscillation data."},{"id":"7134b210-84c1-4aef-af11-de8e0158f84d","l":"Fusion evaporation reactions","d":3,"p":"047c9d16-460f-4d03-b991-42f412b9c272","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics","Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Fusion-evaporation reactions are nuclear processes in which a projectile nucleus fuses with a target to form a highly excited compound nucleus that cools by \"evaporating\" light particles (neutrons, protons, \u03b1 particles) and \u03b3 rays, leaving a residual nucleus. The types and numbers of evaporated particles depend on the compound nucleus's excitation energy and angular momentum, and thus determine which final isotope is produced."},{"id":"00dc7f5b-ac28-43f7-b325-8f34a2fcfc04","l":"f-wave","d":3,"p":"91ed8eb4-208e-4fd8-a258-aeae8213de49","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"f-wave denotes a superconducting or superfluid pairing symmetry whose Cooper-pair orbital wavefunction carries orbital angular momentum \u2113 = 3, so the gap (order parameter) transforms like an \u2113 = 3 spherical/cubic harmonic. Because \u2113 is odd, f-wave states are odd under spatial inversion (usually implying spin-triplet pairing) and typically have a multi-lobed angular dependence with nodes on the Fermi surface."},{"id":"7ff88f45-f9d8-45b2-b06d-68f97962c8c6","l":"Game theory models","d":3,"p":"3cbbf1f7-9427-4812-a34b-410f229c0987","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Game theory models are mathematical frameworks that describe strategic interactions among agents (players) who choose actions to maximize payoffs, specified by strategy sets and payoff functions and analyzed via solution concepts like Nash equilibrium. In physics they are used to study collective, evolutionary, and networked dynamics where individual decision rules produce emergent, often statistical, behavior."},{"id":"1135dc6a-829e-4d64-9884-892265275337","l":"Geological CO2 storage","d":3,"p":"64470342-2836-4044-a632-693f38e12b9f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Geological CO2 storage is the engineered injection and long-term containment of carbon dioxide into deep subsurface rock formations\u2014such as depleted oil and gas reservoirs, deep saline aquifers, or unmineable coal seams\u2014to prevent its release to the atmosphere. At depths typically around or greater than 800 m CO2 becomes supercritical and is immobilized by structural trapping under caprock, residual trapping in pore spaces, dissolution into formation fluids, and slow mineralization, with safety relying on seal integrity, pressure management, and monitoring."},{"id":"0e60c98a-6b58-45ac-9a0c-3624ef778ae5","l":"Ghost imaging","d":3,"p":"70d9878d-c239-4e9c-b8bb-237aeff0612e","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Ghost imaging is an indirect imaging technique that reconstructs an object's spatial image by correlating measurements between two light beams: one that illuminates the object and is measured only by a non-spatial \"bucket\" detector, and a reference beam that does not interact with the object but is measured with spatial resolution. The image emerges from the intensity or photon-count correlations and can be produced using entangled photon pairs or classically correlated/thermal light."},{"id":"b3e44e4a-95e5-46d9-a1cf-77a29510d1ea","l":"Giant magnetoresistance","d":3,"p":"23e22a76-a9ba-4593-8d52-4e5c98c47af3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Giant magnetoresistance (GMR) is the large change in electrical resistance that occurs in multilayer structures of ferromagnetic and nonmagnetic metals when an applied magnetic field changes the relative orientation of the magnetizations of adjacent ferromagnetic layers. The effect arises from spin-dependent scattering of conduction electrons: antiparallel layer alignment produces higher resistance while parallel alignment gives lower resistance."},{"id":"205e612b-d72a-4142-a1cc-50bc92e37ef6","l":"Giant vesicles","d":3,"p":"df30acff-987c-4b88-b32c-58b4e3887f8e","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Giant vesicles are closed, self-assembled bilayer sacs (typically lipid) with diameters of order micrometers (generally >1 \u00b5m), making them cell-sized model membranes. They encapsulate an aqueous volume and are widely used to study membrane mechanics, phase behavior, and transport processes accessible by optical microscopy."},{"id":"41d72fef-5543-4ba7-9213-d07af7c02ed1","l":"Glueballs","d":3,"p":"b191db98-31a4-4d58-9ea7-55eefc6a43a3","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Glueballs are hypothetical color-singlet bound states made entirely of gluons, predicted by quantum chromodynamics (QCD) because gluons self-interact in a non\u2011Abelian gauge theory. They carry no valence quarks, no flavor or electric charge, have a discrete mass spectrum (studied in lattice QCD), and are difficult to identify experimentally because they mix with ordinary mesons."},{"id":"6ab2b6b8-a1f2-4da6-b8ec-f650a397e494","l":"Grazing-incidence fast atom diffraction","d":3,"p":"3c9c01fe-fe82-4861-a59d-3da21432adb7","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Grazing-incidence fast atom diffraction (GIFAD) is a surface-analysis technique in which high-energy (typically keV) neutral atoms strike a crystalline surface at very shallow angles and scatter coherently from the topmost atomic rows, producing interference (diffraction) patterns. The angular distribution of the diffracted atoms encodes the surface periodicity and the lateral variation of the atom\u2013surface potential, allowing sensitive measurements of surface structure and dynamics."},{"id":"49953cb9-129b-476f-b701-1536b31ab7af","l":"Grazing-incidence small-angle x-ray scattering","d":3,"p":"1d3e6a42-9d7e-4c5f-b0ef-c5fb489e8336","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Grazing-incidence small-angle X-ray scattering (GISAXS) is a surface-sensitive scattering technique in which a collimated X-ray beam impinges at an angle near the critical angle for total external reflection, producing small-angle scattering that probes nanoscale lateral and vertical structure of thin films, surfaces, and interfaces. By limiting penetration depth and exploiting interference from the surface/interface, GISAXS yields statistical information on particle size, shape, arrangement, and vertical profiles over nanometer length scales."},{"id":"d4e6f31f-7d0f-4dee-87db-ef8dfae56546","l":"Grazing-incidence X-ray diffraction","d":3,"p":"32de7669-68ef-4f3e-9598-2a70300ea01e","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Grazing-incidence X-ray diffraction is an X-ray scattering technique in which the beam strikes a sample at a very small angle near or below the critical angle for total external reflection, creating a shallow penetration depth and an evanescent wave that enhances sensitivity to surfaces and thin films. By measuring the diffracted intensity as a function of angle and in-plane momentum transfer, it reveals the crystallographic structure, orientation, strain, and morphology of surface and near-surface layers while suppressing bulk scattering."},{"id":"d5de184b-e051-46d9-8102-94baf90623b4","l":"Gutzwiller approximation","d":3,"p":"a0dcc4fb-34a0-4892-a60a-cfb14c0f73e8","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Gutzwiller approximation is a variational mean-field scheme that evaluates the Gutzwiller-projected many-body wavefunction by replacing the projection with site- and occupation-dependent renormalization factors, thus approximating how local electronic correlations suppress double occupancy and reduce hopping amplitudes. It becomes exact in the infinite-coordination (infinite-dimension) limit and is widely used to describe correlation-driven band narrowing and the Mott metal\u2013insulator transition."},{"id":"ff50fad4-8d01-4043-8591-e60769e24fa8","l":"Hadron colliders","d":3,"p":"7b46c9f2-5627-4b1a-bba7-7cf74c4d4861","fl":["Physical Systems","Techniques"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Hadron colliders are particle accelerators that accelerate beams of hadrons (composite particles such as protons or heavy ions) and bring them into head-on collisions at very high energies. Their energetic collisions produce short-lived particles and probe the strong, electroweak, and potential beyond\u2013Standard-Model interactions, making them central tools for high-energy physics."},{"id":"77677633-2ae6-468e-b010-ff1da0a7ac70","l":"Hadronic calorimeters","d":3,"p":"ce866ada-ee57-4c9c-81a0-937385d9ee88","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Hadronic calorimeters are particle detectors that measure the energy of hadrons (protons, neutrons, pions, kaons) by fully absorbing them and sampling the ensuing hadronic showers produced by strong nuclear interactions in dense material. They are typically built from alternating layers of dense absorber and active sensing material (or homogeneous absorbers) that convert shower energy into electrical or optical signals, and are much thicker than electromagnetic calorimeters because hadronic showers penetrate farther and involve nuclear processes."},{"id":"382d0bbd-44c4-4654-af59-d50457310ff2","l":"Half-Heusler alloy","d":3,"p":"498d1139-cbf1-4d02-9dc5-02c4c71b189d","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Half-Heusler alloys are ternary intermetallic compounds with formula XYZ that crystallize in the cubic C1b (MgAgAs-type, space group F-43m) structure, essentially derived from full Heusler X2YZ by leaving one sublattice vacant. Their band filling and chemical composition give rise to diverse electronic and magnetic behaviors \u2014 from semiconducting and high-performance thermoelectric to topological and magnetic phases."},{"id":"656e4d8e-3483-4adc-8f66-8e19c8ae7014","l":"Hall bar","d":3,"p":"467f0788-e876-4bb8-964b-aae25db708e2","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A Hall bar is a rectangular thin-film or wire-shaped sample with current contacts at its ends and voltage probes along its length and across its width, designed to measure the transverse (Hall) voltage and the longitudinal voltage when a magnetic field is applied perpendicular to the sample. It is commonly used to determine carrier density, mobility and to observe Hall and quantum Hall effects."},{"id":"43744bc4-ba33-470f-817c-315be56cf759","l":"Hartree-Fock methods","d":3,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Hartree\u2013Fock methods are a family of mean-field approximations for solving the many-electron Schr\u00f6dinger equation by representing the N-electron wavefunction as a single Slater determinant of one-electron orbitals and using the variational principle to derive coupled one-electron equations (the Hartree\u2013Fock or Roothaan equations) that are solved self-consistently. They treat exchange exactly within the single-determinant picture but neglect dynamic electron correlation beyond that mean-field approximation."},{"id":"243ebf5e-d251-49c3-b8b6-fc6a3d29442e","l":"Heisenberg model","d":3,"p":"6ff84248-428b-4704-ae9f-e55346a4ce60","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Heisenberg model is a lattice model of interacting magnetic moments (spins) with Hamiltonian H = \u2211_{ij} J_{ij} S_i\u00b7S_j, where S_i are spin operators or vectors and the exchange constants J_{ij} (and their signs) determine ferromagnetic or antiferromagnetic coupling. It is isotropic under spin rotations (global SU(2) symmetry) and serves as a paradigmatic model for magnetism, spin waves, and quantum phase transitions."},{"id":"149763ee-5622-485e-8715-8d4c03a24da5","l":"Helicity distribution functions","d":3,"p":"33f199a2-1b0f-4e00-a922-a42ef08688db","fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Helicity distribution functions \u0394f(x, Q^2) are polarized parton distribution functions that give, for a parton type f (quark, antiquark, gluon), the difference between the number densities with helicity aligned minus anti-aligned with a longitudinally polarized parent hadron as a function of the parton's momentum fraction x (and factorization scale Q^2). They encode the hadron's longitudinal spin structure and enter spin-dependent cross sections and sum rules for the proton's spin."},{"id":"5df754f3-2e3b-416b-a958-d3f17105f973","l":"High-resolution electron energy loss spectroscopy","d":3,"p":"8d9a5500-7204-4ae4-8a93-388da8f8d432","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"High-resolution electron energy loss spectroscopy (HREELS) is a surface-sensitive technique that measures the energy lost by a beam of low-energy electrons scattered from a sample to reveal its vibrational, electronic, and collective excitations. Using electron monochromators and analyzers it achieves meV-scale energy resolution, allowing detection of surface phonons, adsorbate vibrations, plasmons, and related fine spectral features."},{"id":"8f673cae-6dc7-41ef-8344-e6da9556d0f7","l":"High-resolution electron microscopy","d":3,"p":"34773509-53cc-4885-a027-7bd3497f7157","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"High-resolution electron microscopy is an imaging technique that uses focused beams of high-energy electrons in instruments such as TEM and STEM to resolve structural detail at sub-nanometer to atomic scales. By exploiting the short electron de Broglie wavelength and advanced electron optics (e.g., aberration correction, phase-contrast methods), it enables direct visualization of atomic arrangements, defects, interfaces, and nanoscale structure in thin specimens."},{"id":"ee6a7985-5dde-4bf8-9b7b-859f03f7c524","l":"High-resolution transmission electron microscopy","d":3,"p":"34773509-53cc-4885-a027-7bd3497f7157","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"High-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) is a mode of TEM that forms images from the coherent interference of electrons transmitted through an ultra-thin specimen, enabling direct visualization of atomic-scale structure and lattice planes. Modern, aberration-corrected HRTEM achieves sub-angstrom resolution, though image contrast depends on defocus, thickness and aberrations and often requires simulation for quantitative interpretation."},{"id":"d85b4b78-1f70-48bf-942f-c10f818328f3","l":"Hindered rotation model","d":3,"p":"ac816133-540a-4562-8130-584df553f46a","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Hindered rotation model describes the rotational motion of a molecular subunit (for example a methyl group) that is constrained by a periodic potential from its chemical environment, typically represented by a torsional potential V(\u03c6) with discrete minima. It captures how the barrier to rotation modifies a free-rotor spectrum\u2014producing quantized torsional levels, barrier-dependent splittings, and possible quantum tunneling between equivalent orientations."},{"id":"03dd43af-44df-4f86-a923-d06bafa16899","l":"Hodgkin-Huxley model","d":3,"p":"3a68853d-8031-4222-9eb4-ca56627c40cd","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"The Hodgkin\u2013Huxley model is a biophysically grounded mathematical description of excitable cell membranes that represents the membrane as an electrical circuit with voltage-dependent sodium and potassium conductances plus a leak. It uses a coupled set of nonlinear ordinary differential equations for the membrane potential and gating variables to quantitatively reproduce action potential generation and propagation."},{"id":"82f5eb72-01e1-44b6-9a3e-46ab097e7e65","l":"Holography","d":3,"p":"70d9878d-c239-4e9c-b8bb-237aeff0612e","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Holography (optical) is a technique for recording and later reconstructing the full amplitude and phase of a wavefront\u2014usually by interfering coherent light beams\u2014so that a three-dimensional image of the original object is produced. In theoretical physics, holography (the holographic principle) is the idea that the information content of a volume of space can be fully encoded by degrees of freedom on its lower-dimensional boundary, exemplified by the AdS/CFT correspondence."},{"id":"e1c14817-2a26-49fb-9f28-ff69a699db05","l":"Holstein model","d":3,"p":"a0ce8ec5-e2c2-40f4-b53f-9fd9e2572289","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Holstein model is a lattice model of electrons that hop between sites and interact locally with dispersionless (Einstein) optical phonons, with the coupling proportional to the on-site electron density times the phonon displacement. It is a minimal model used to study polaron formation, charge-density waves and electron-phonon\u2013mediated superconductivity."},{"id":"34311689-6044-4a0c-997e-f9135357b95e","l":"Hopfield model","d":3,"p":"3a68853d-8031-4222-9eb4-ca56627c40cd","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"The Hopfield model is a fully connected recurrent neural network of binary units (spins \u00b11) with symmetric synaptic weights, designed so that prescribed patterns become attractors of the dynamics; asynchronous updates decrease a global Lyapunov (energy) function and the network relaxes to stable fixed points that implement associative memory. Mathematically it is equivalent to an Ising spin glass with Hebbian learning rules used to imprint memories."},{"id":"2086df13-fad1-4cb5-8391-791ece479131","l":"Hybrid mesons","d":3,"p":"b191db98-31a4-4d58-9ea7-55eefc6a43a3","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Hybrid mesons are hadrons made of a quark\u2013antiquark pair together with an explicit excitation of the gluonic field (i.e., a valence gluonic degree of freedom). Predicted by quantum chromodynamics, they can show properties and J^PC quantum numbers (including so-called \"exotic\" ones) that ordinary quark\u2013antiquark mesons cannot."},{"id":"57c5b6fb-0d61-4cce-9d84-505f5d6322e4","l":"Hybrid perovskites","d":3,"p":"f28f4f1e-8eef-4fce-8661-07f5751ad90a","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Hybrid perovskites are crystalline materials with the perovskite ABX3 structure in which the A-site is occupied by an organic molecular cation (e.g., methylammonium) while B is a metal (commonly Pb2+ or Sn2+) and X is a halide. Their hybrid organic\u2013inorganic lattice yields tunable electronic bandgaps, strong light absorption, long carrier lifetimes and soft ionic dynamics, making them important for high\u2011efficiency solar cells and optoelectronic devices."},{"id":"d7371ac0-e80d-4546-857b-b69c685fe2d8","l":"Hydrides","d":3,"p":"2a71cbba-3674-42b1-8778-4b794f85a844","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Hydrides are chemical compounds in which hydrogen is chemically bonded to another element; they occur as ionic (saline) hydrides with hydride anions (H\u2212), covalent molecular hydrides (e.g., H2O, CH4), or metallic/interstitial hydrides where hydrogen occupies lattice sites in a metal. These classes differ in bonding and properties and are important for hydrogen storage, catalysis, and superconductivity under pressure."},{"id":"6a3a5182-5be2-4cdc-80b8-7644a39af64b","l":"Hydrodynamic instabilities","d":3,"p":"3f71ccf5-16ff-425f-b7b3-4791f9503b59","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Hydrodynamic instabilities are fluid-flow phenomena in which small disturbances to an otherwise steady or laminar state grow because the flow can convert available energy (from shear, buoyancy, pressure gradients, or surface tension) into perturbation energy. Such growth produces new flow patterns, enhanced mixing, or transition to turbulence \u2014 examples include Kelvin\u2013Helmholtz, Rayleigh\u2013Taylor, and Taylor\u2013Couette instabilities."},{"id":"4e3db254-f992-4829-a77b-39a2f48f0908","l":"Hydrogen evolution reaction","d":3,"p":"81236d07-2535-466b-93f2-e4d502bf5935","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) is the electrochemical reduction of protons (or water in neutral/alkaline media) at a cathode to produce molecular hydrogen, serving as the cathodic half-reaction in water electrolysis and many electrochemical cells. Its rate is controlled by surface-mediated steps (Volmer adsorption, Heyrovsky electrochemical desorption, or Tafel recombination), so electrocatalysts are used to lower the overpotential and improve hydrogen-production efficiency."},{"id":"9ca80152-a892-40a5-a67d-9c6375dc0dee","l":"Hydrogen fuel cells","d":3,"p":"9ec242dd-6551-414b-b098-686bd2fdf979","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Hydrogen fuel cells are electrochemical devices that convert the chemical energy of hydrogen and an oxidant (typically oxygen) directly into electricity, heat, and water by separating electron and ion flows. They operate by oxidizing hydrogen at the anode and reducing oxygen at the cathode, with ions transported through an electrolyte (e.g., a proton-exchange membrane) and electrons delivered through an external circuit, producing no carbon emissions at the point of use."},{"id":"fb4cfd7b-691e-43d7-b2c9-dd7560244f4b","l":"Hydrogen storage materials","d":3,"p":"3040d6ba-d81f-4844-a016-342311b150ce","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Materials (solids or liquids) engineered to store hydrogen by adsorption, absorption, or chemical binding so it can be safely transported and released on demand. Their performance is judged by gravimetric and volumetric hydrogen capacity, binding thermodynamics and release temperature, kinetics, reversibility, and cycling stability; common classes include metal and complex hydrides, porous solids (MOFs, carbons), and liquid/chemical carriers."},{"id":"e0b739fe-4296-4a25-ac7f-e7ece6adf461","l":"Hydromagnetic instability","d":3,"p":"3f71ccf5-16ff-425f-b7b3-4791f9503b59","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Hydromagnetic instability is an instability of a conducting fluid or plasma caused by the coupling between fluid motions and magnetic fields, as described by magnetohydrodynamics (MHD). Small perturbations grow when magnetic tension, pressure, and flow forces are imbalanced, leading to reconfiguration of the field and flow and often to conversion of magnetic energy into kinetic and thermal energy."},{"id":"3c391c62-6f7d-4ed1-8e63-ff4195b62185","l":"Hyperentanglement","d":3,"p":"5de0faa9-608e-450b-8d04-5aa4219dad8d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Hyperentanglement is when two or more quantum systems are entangled simultaneously in more than one independent degree of freedom (for example, polarization and spatial mode of the same photon pair). This produces a composite, higher-dimensional entangled state that increases information capacity and enables tasks like complete Bell-state discrimination and more robust quantum-information protocols."},{"id":"cb91fc50-4e07-460e-8c15-fd30401cae1d","l":"III-V semiconductors","d":3,"p":"e25a5c89-6132-4bbd-8efb-4a426458c900","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"III-V semiconductors are compound materials formed by combining elements from group III (e.g., Al, Ga, In) with elements from group V (e.g., N, P, As, Sb) of the periodic table. They typically have direct band gaps and high carrier mobilities, making them key materials for optoelectronics and high-speed electronic devices (e.g., GaAs, InP, GaN)."},{"id":"263176b9-698c-482f-9cd9-28dfc6c88997","l":"II-VI semiconductors","d":3,"p":"e25a5c89-6132-4bbd-8efb-4a426458c900","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"II\u2013VI semiconductors are binary compounds composed of a group\u2011II element (e.g., Zn, Cd, Hg) and a group\u2011VI chalcogen (O, S, Se, Te), typically crystallizing in zincblende or wurtzite lattices with predominantly ionic\u2013covalent bonding. They often have relatively wide, frequently direct band gaps and strong excitonic effects, making them useful in optoelectronics (LEDs, lasers, photodetectors) and infrared/photovoltaic applications, with properties tunable by alloying and doping."},{"id":"c4808123-37c2-4338-b80a-8fc96a2f0f35","l":"Impulsive stimulated Raman scattering","d":3,"p":"da18b93f-430a-4ea1-8831-966d9bb6a0fc","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Impulsive stimulated Raman scattering is an ultrafast nonlinear optical process in which an intense laser pulse shorter than a vibrational period impulsively modulates a material's polarizability and thereby launches a coherent vibration (molecular vibration or phonon). Unlike spontaneous Raman scattering, the strong, short pulse coherently drives and phase-locks the Raman-active mode, producing macroscopic oscillations observable in pump\u2013probe experiments."},{"id":"2d659124-48a5-442f-80d3-a5814f009850","l":"Inelastic light scattering","d":3,"p":"382676e6-fd8f-4fc6-b8e0-3abbc45c379c","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Inelastic light scattering is the process in which incident photons exchange energy (and momentum) with excitations in a material so that the scattered photons have a different frequency than the incident ones. The resulting energy shifts (Stokes or anti\u2011Stokes lines) reveal properties of vibrational, electronic, magnetic or collective modes (examples: Raman, Brillouin, and Compton scattering)."},{"id":"2499c825-4d14-4491-b8be-1eb8cd7167b2","l":"Inelastic neutron scattering","d":3,"p":"c74c7b81-5dee-4820-a500-6ab6f1b48cb1","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Inelastic neutron scattering is a spectroscopic technique in which neutrons exchange energy and momentum with a sample during collision, so the scattered neutrons emerge with different energies and directions than the incident beam. By measuring those energy and momentum transfers\u2014mediated by nuclear and magnetic interactions\u2014one directly probes collective excitations (phonons, magnons, etc.), diffusive dynamics, and their dispersion relations in the material."},{"id":"24098ed4-b73f-4eb2-bd1a-df209728659f","l":"Inelastic X-ray scattering","d":3,"p":"1d3e6a42-9d7e-4c5f-b0ef-c5fb489e8336","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Inelastic X\u2011ray scattering is the process in which an X\u2011ray photon exchanges energy and momentum with a material and therefore emerges with a changed energy and direction, having created or absorbed electronic, vibrational or collective excitations. Measuring the energy and momentum transfer yields the dynamic structure factor S(q,\u03c9), allowing one to probe phonons, plasmons, crystal-field and other excitations in solids and liquids."},{"id":"3f4f484a-93a7-4d39-a72a-5eaa33c60512","l":"Infinite dimensional symmetries","d":3,"p":"543d3af2-a8bc-462b-9d72-753b6de0d8ce","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Infinite-dimensional symmetries are symmetry groups or algebras that have infinitely many independent generators, typically acting on functions or field configurations rather than on a finite set of coordinates. They occur in many field theories (e.g., the Virasoro and Kac\u2013Moody algebras, diffeomorphism groups) and, by imposing infinitely many constraints, strongly restrict dynamics and give rise to features like integrability, conserved currents, central extensions, and quantum anomalies."},{"id":"256add35-7ff1-43ad-aefa-ce53d75b3976","l":"Information thermodynamics","d":3,"p":"8e3130c3-9c84-483a-9907-a9d304524b5d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Information thermodynamics studies how information processing (measurement, feedback, storage, erasure) couples to energy, work, and entropy, treating information-theoretic quantities like Shannon or mutual information as thermodynamic resources or constraints. It formalizes results such as Landauer\u2019s principle (erasing one bit costs at least kB T ln 2 of heat) and generalized second-law inequalities that include information flow, with applications to Maxwell\u2019s demon, computation, and small fluctuating systems."},{"id":"3fb1eb5f-4f56-4de2-ba29-95395826bba6","l":"Integer quantum Hall effect","d":3,"p":"9017d068-f9d7-42f8-b083-a385e9b14c0b","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The integer quantum Hall effect is the phenomenon in which a two-dimensional electron system at low temperature and strong perpendicular magnetic field exhibits exact plateaus of Hall conductance \u03c3_xy = \u03bd e^2/h with \u03bd an integer; these plateaus arise from filled Landau levels and topologically protected chiral edge states (Chern numbers), making the quantization robust to disorder."},{"id":"470fc610-f9a6-44b3-8e02-236b910a52aa","l":"Integrate-and-fire model","d":3,"p":"3a68853d-8031-4222-9eb4-ca56627c40cd","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Integrate-and-fire model: a minimal spiking neuron model in which the membrane potential accumulates incoming currents (often with a leak term) and, when it reaches a fixed threshold, produces an instantaneous spike and is reset (typically with an optional refractory period), used to study single-neuron and network dynamics."},{"id":"24b63cd7-d85b-4aae-bc69-271c2ab5ecaa","l":"Intermetallic semiconductors","d":3,"p":"e25a5c89-6132-4bbd-8efb-4a426458c900","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Intermetallic semiconductors are ordered compounds of two or more metallic elements whose combined electronic structure produces a band gap and semiconducting (rather than metallic) electrical behavior. They often have complex crystal structures and mixed covalent\u2013metallic bonding, giving narrow, composition-tunable band gaps useful for thermoelectric, optoelectronic, and topological applications."},{"id":"e5391c73-aefe-44c4-a5f5-b5cbbaf856d0","l":"Inverse photoemission spectroscopy","d":3,"p":"40082853-5a77-4bce-bb7b-a6b598d04d93","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Inverse photoemission spectroscopy (IPES) is a surface-sensitive technique that probes a material\u2019s unoccupied electronic states by injecting low-energy electrons into the sample and detecting photons emitted when those electrons fill empty states above the Fermi level. Measuring the emitted photon (or incident electron) energy gives an energy-resolved map of conduction-band states and complements photoemission, which probes occupied states."},{"id":"99dbd7a1-59c4-4389-b22e-03d2f7acb0bc","l":"Inverse spin Hall effect","d":3,"p":"bb4a3b78-a738-4e19-abd6-1feea6226ea1","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Inverse spin Hall effect: the conversion of a spin current or non-equilibrium spin polarization into a transverse charge current or voltage in a material with strong spin\u2013orbit coupling, caused by spin-dependent deflection of charge carriers. It is the Onsager-reciprocal counterpart of the spin Hall effect and is widely used to electrically detect spin currents."},{"id":"2d6cd977-010b-4f2f-ab14-1dbffb3a0574","l":"Inversion symmetry","d":3,"p":"847f8adc-79b0-4961-bc2a-d0494a37a24c","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Inversion symmetry (or parity) is the invariance of a system under spatial inversion through a point, i.e., the transformation r \u2192 \u2212r; if the system's Hamiltonian or equations of motion are unchanged by this operation, it is said to possess inversion symmetry. In quantum mechanics this implies states can be labeled by parity eigenvalues (+1 or \u22121)."},{"id":"11d03e84-aa0c-4fc3-831c-13daddd395c8","l":"Ion-acoustic instability","d":3,"p":"b094f9fa-ebc7-4e4d-8957-0a7303f6e91e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Ion-acoustic instability is the spontaneous exponential growth of ion-acoustic waves\u2014low-frequency, longitudinal plasma oscillations supported by ion inertia and electron pressure\u2014when free energy from relative drifts or beams (e.g., an electron current or ion beam) overcomes damping. It is commonly triggered when the electron drift speed relative to ions approaches or exceeds the ion-acoustic speed cs \u2248 (kBTe/mi)1/2 and is easiest to excite when Te >> Ti."},{"id":"d78dbe98-f8bb-40f9-a835-7d74c1a0a745","l":"Ionic solids","d":3,"p":"2a71cbba-3674-42b1-8778-4b794f85a844","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Ionic solids are crystals composed of oppositely charged ions arranged on a regular lattice and held together predominantly by Coulomb (electrostatic) attraction. They typically have high melting points, are hard but brittle, and conduct electricity only when molten or dissolved."},{"id":"edd306f7-e7cb-49a2-8ff4-1ee37cf261b0","l":"Ion scattering from surfaces","d":3,"p":"7600fee4-52d4-46ff-bca9-7439ac394273","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Ion scattering from surfaces is the interaction in which energetic ions strike a solid surface and are elastically or inelastically deflected, may exchange charge with the target, or eject surface atoms (sputtering). Measuring the energies, angles, and charge states of the scattered particles provides surface-sensitive information about the topmost atomic layers' composition, structure, and electronic properties."},{"id":"3800079a-5d09-4035-9b0f-b9eead0bdfeb","l":"Iridescence","d":3,"p":"9c437e89-cf6a-4250-8738-b43ded1eb4ff","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Iridescence is angle-dependent coloration produced when light undergoes wavelength-selective interference or diffraction from microstructured surfaces (thin films, multilayers, gratings, or photonic crystals), causing reflected colors to change with viewing or illumination angle. It is a form of structural coloration rather than pigment-based color."},{"id":"60c0e911-44c3-4224-89b1-0c5a46dc82c3","l":"Ising model","d":3,"p":"695da376-c44a-41dd-b968-d200c31e9d5e","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Networks","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"The Ising model is a simple statistical-mechanical model of binary \"spins\" s_i = \u00b11 placed on the sites of a lattice, with energy H = \u2212J \u2211_{\u27e8i,j\u27e9} s_i s_j \u2212 h \u2211_i s_i describing (typically) nearest-neighbor coupling J and an external field h. It is a paradigmatic model for ferromagnetism and phase transitions, exhibiting collective order and critical behavior in higher dimensions."},{"id":"1bb6c922-68ec-4ff4-9c63-4e79a313864d","l":"Josephson junctions","d":3,"p":"a3cc729c-98ea-421a-ac1b-df399f62721d","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A Josephson junction is a device formed by two superconductors separated by a very thin insulating barrier or weak link, allowing Cooper pairs to tunnel coherently between them and produce a dissipationless supercurrent set by the phase difference of the superconducting order parameter. It exhibits the dc Josephson effect (a zero\u2011voltage supercurrent up to a critical current) and the ac Josephson effect (an applied voltage drives an oscillating supercurrent with frequency proportional to the voltage)."},{"id":"c0f06e0b-ac22-4e2a-badb-25832bfa4f4b","l":"Kaons","d":3,"p":"0841db0c-24b2-4074-9ba3-a5b6243699b5","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Kaons are pseudoscalar mesons containing a strange quark paired with an up or down antiquark (or their antiparticles), occurring as charged (K+, K\u2212) and neutral (K0, K\u03040) states. They decay via the weak interaction and were key to the discovery of strangeness and of CP violation in neutral-kaon mixing."},{"id":"553f6fe0-d536-4fbd-a496-bfdd52a802f5","l":"Kinesiology","d":3,"p":"d56b5277-e328-4221-a086-720804ca6adf","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Kinesiology is the scientific study of human (and animal) movement, focusing on how muscles, joints, and the nervous system generate and control motion. It applies principles of physics\u2014kinematics, kinetics, and energetics\u2014together with physiology to analyze forces, motions, and energy transfer in living systems."},{"id":"c3927831-272a-4717-9188-55375ebd547f","l":"Kink instability","d":3,"p":"3f71ccf5-16ff-425f-b7b3-4791f9503b59","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Kink instability is an MHD instability in a current-carrying plasma or magnetic flux tube that causes the column to bend into a helical (kinked) shape when the magnetic field lines are twisted beyond a critical threshold. It is primarily the m=1 mode (Kruskal\u2013Shafranov criterion) and can lead to magnetic reconnection and disruptive reconfiguration in devices and astrophysical plasmas."},{"id":"019c379e-c262-4f9d-9ac6-bc478de08095","l":"Kitaev model","d":3,"p":"6ff84248-428b-4704-ae9f-e55346a4ce60","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Kitaev model is a two-dimensional spin-1/2 lattice model (famously on the honeycomb lattice) with bond-dependent anisotropic Ising interactions that renders it exactly solvable by a mapping of spins to Majorana fermions coupled to a static Z2 gauge field. Its ground states are quantum spin liquids with fractionalized excitations\u2014itinerant Majorana fermions and Z2 vortices\u2014and can realize both Abelian and non-Abelian topological phases relevant to fault-tolerant quantum computation."},{"id":"5b64e7f0-e98c-4f11-8048-893d8f8d039f","l":"Kuramoto model","d":3,"p":"36932cf6-44f8-4508-8a6a-20a0e46f6c8f","fl":["Physical Systems","Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"The Kuramoto model is a paradigmatic system of N coupled phase oscillators with intrinsic frequencies \u03c9_i whose dynamics follow\nd\u03b8_i/dt = \u03c9_i + (K/N) \u03a3_{j=1}^N sin(\u03b8_j \u2212 \u03b8_i); \nas the coupling K increases it shows a transition from incoherence to partial synchronization, quantified by the complex order parameter r e^{i\u03c8} = (1/N) \u03a3_{j=1}^N e^{i\u03b8_j}."},{"id":"f979101b-3ab8-4299-bb9f-39b748823b3d","l":"Laser ablation","d":3,"p":"dedc6dbe-3651-4ad0-9d37-b260e8975adc","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Laser ablation is the removal of material from a surface by focused laser irradiation that rapidly deposits optical energy into the target. The absorbed energy drives localized heating, vaporization, plasma formation, or direct photochemical bond breaking\u2014depending on pulse duration and intensity\u2014causing material ejection used in micromachining, thin-film deposition, and analytical techniques."},{"id":"a6342e89-ed94-4f16-9f13-fd0f541f66a5","l":"Laser-beam impact phenomena","d":3,"p":"dedc6dbe-3651-4ad0-9d37-b260e8975adc","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Laser-beam impact phenomena are the collection of physical processes that occur when a laser beam deposits energy onto a target, producing responses such as heating, melting, vaporization/ablation, shock waves, material ejection and plasma formation. The specific outcomes and timescales depend on laser parameters (intensity, pulse duration, wavelength, repetition rate) and the target's material properties, leading to regimes from gentle heating to ultrafast nonthermal damage."},{"id":"7a43744a-c898-40fc-960e-647ec0e47e67","l":"LEDs","d":3,"p":"c215fb56-b919-42c5-9267-13846d715223","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Light-emitting diode (LED): a semiconductor p\u2013n junction that produces light by electroluminescence when forward biased \u2014 photons are emitted as injected electrons and holes recombine radiatively, with the emission wavelength set by the material bandgap and device quantum-well design. LEDs convert electrical energy to light efficiently, with output governed by carrier injection, radiative vs. nonradiative recombination, and optical extraction."},{"id":"0d8ba0c6-b6b2-44d0-bff7-9e753ed4ea9b","l":"Lepton colliders","d":3,"p":"7b46c9f2-5627-4b1a-bba7-7cf74c4d4861","fl":["Physical Systems","Techniques"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Lepton colliders are particle accelerators that bring beams of leptons (typically electrons and positrons, or occasionally muons) into head\u2011on collisions to study fundamental interactions. Because leptons are elementary and have well\u2011defined initial states, these machines provide very clean experimental conditions for high\u2011precision measurements of electroweak processes, the Higgs boson, and searches for new physics."},{"id":"c5ad858c-cb0d-4435-af1b-b005b72e7241","l":"Lepton-hadron colliders","d":3,"p":"7b46c9f2-5627-4b1a-bba7-7cf74c4d4861","fl":["Physical Systems","Techniques"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Lepton-hadron colliders are particle accelerators that collide a beam of leptons (usually electrons or positrons, sometimes muons) with a beam of hadrons (protons or nuclei) to probe the internal partonic structure of hadrons via deep inelastic scattering. They combine the clean, well-understood electroweak probe provided by leptons with the high energies and complex QCD dynamics of hadrons, enabling precision studies of parton distribution functions, QCD, and electroweak interactions."},{"id":"bbbcaf8b-d891-40ce-a58e-5500103fb97d","l":"Lidar","d":3,"p":"70d9878d-c239-4e9c-b8bb-237aeff0612e","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Lidar (Light Detection and Ranging) is an active remote\u2011sensing technique that emits short laser pulses and measures the time delay (time\u2011of\u2011flight) and angle of returned scattered light to determine distances to targets. By scanning the beam and/or analyzing return intensity and Doppler shifts, lidar produces high\u2011resolution 3D maps and can provide information on surface reflectivity, motion, and atmospheric composition."},{"id":"109740e4-dc39-4311-b31d-7e77a1648efa","l":"Lifetime of molecular or ionic species","d":3,"p":"0a37f3c2-43fd-48fa-a630-85e5b860cda5","fl":["Properties","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Nuclear Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"The lifetime of a molecular or ionic species is the average time a representative particle survives before being removed by reaction, decay, photodissociation, or transport \u2014 i.e., the e\u2011folding time of its abundance under loss processes. For a first\u2011order loss with rate constant k, \u03c4 = 1/k (equivalently \u03c4 = t1/2/ln2); in general \u03c4 is the mean residence time derived from the species' decay probability distribution and depends on the local environment and loss mechanisms."},{"id":"bfbd8932-0b37-4f8a-aab1-5022c5ec52d2","l":"Light propagation in random media","d":3,"p":"3873301d-c286-49f8-a26c-b6c7297bd647","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Light propagation in random media is the study of how electromagnetic waves travel through materials whose refractive index varies randomly in space, so that repeated scattering, absorption and interference determine the transport of energy and coherence. It covers descriptions from radiative-transfer and diffusion approximations to wave-interference phenomena such as speckle, coherent backscattering and Anderson localization."},{"id":"fd3758de-3ff7-452e-98a2-8f503e7bafb5","l":"Linear colliders","d":3,"p":"7b46c9f2-5627-4b1a-bba7-7cf74c4d4861","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Linear colliders are particle accelerators that accelerate two opposing beams of particles along straight trajectories and bring them into head\u2011on collisions at interaction points. By avoiding circular motion (and thus large synchrotron radiation losses for light charged particles like electrons), they enable high center\u2011of\u2011mass energies and very clean, precise measurements for studies of fundamental interactions and searches for new physics."},{"id":"16a6a5d2-ae10-49f4-9963-aceca75da6d9","l":"Linear spin wave theory","d":3,"p":"23e0894e-316f-4146-a50d-24d702922db6","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Linear spin wave theory is a semiclassical approximation for the low-energy excitations of an ordered magnet in which small deviations of spins from their classical ground-state directions are mapped to bosonic variables and linearized (keeping only leading/quadratic terms), producing noninteracting magnon modes and their dispersion relations. It assumes small quantum fluctuations (e.g., large spin S or low temperature) and neglects magnon\u2013magnon interactions."},{"id":"e378b7c3-67f8-4c3f-9279-0af088a5b8b6","l":"Liquid epitaxy","d":3,"p":"471b81e2-74ad-4d48-b2c0-a57b3c84bb81","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Liquid epitaxy (liquid-phase epitaxy, LPE) is a crystal-growth technique in which a molten or supersaturated liquid containing the film constituents is brought into contact with a crystalline substrate and cooled or otherwise driven to precipitate a solid layer that adopts the substrate's lattice orientation. It enables low-temperature deposition of high-quality epitaxial semiconductor or oxide layers, with thickness and composition controlled by temperature, solute concentration, and diffusion."},{"id":"de2cebe6-26f9-4f51-b41c-03ca1b56d419","l":"Liquified hydrogen","d":3,"p":"f0761682-18fc-4076-9921-90eae238bc98","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Liquified hydrogen (liquid hydrogen, LH2) is hydrogen gas (primarily diatomic H2) cooled and/or pressurized into its cryogenic liquid phase. It is a colorless, very low-density fluid with a boiling point of about 20.3 K at 1 atm (density \u224870.8 kg/m3), widely used as a rocket propellant and cryogenic coolant."},{"id":"74e68502-450a-4f2d-aba2-e105b7f6cc72","l":"Locomotion","d":3,"p":"d56b5277-e328-4221-a086-720804ca6adf","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Locomotion is the process by which a body produces net displacement through its environment by converting internal energy into forces and torques that overcome inertia, drag, and friction. It encompasses the mechanics, energetics, and control of movement and the body\u2013environment interactions (contact, fluid, or granular) that determine speed, efficiency, and stability."},{"id":"0cee8f96-5288-42bb-b1fb-92633fd76876","l":"Lorentz microscopy","d":3,"p":"34773509-53cc-4885-a027-7bd3497f7157","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Lorentz microscopy is a transmission electron microscopy technique for imaging magnetic structures in thin specimens by detecting the small deflections of the electron beam produced by the Lorentz force from in-plane magnetic induction. Contrast mechanisms include Fresnel (defocus) and Foucault (aperture) modes, and quantitative mapping of magnetic induction can be obtained using off-axis electron holography or differential phase-contrast methods."},{"id":"ddf0fdb5-bc3c-4a4d-b9bf-473550f3c0a4","l":"Loss-cone instability","d":3,"p":"b094f9fa-ebc7-4e4d-8957-0a7303f6e91e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Loss-cone instability is a kinetic plasma instability that occurs when particles with small pitch angles escape along magnetic field lines (the \"loss cone\"), leaving an anisotropic velocity-space deficit. That anisotropy produces a resonant positive slope in the distribution function which drives growth of waves (e.g., cyclotron/whistler modes) by extracting particle energy."},{"id":"eef6c8e0-113c-40e9-a981-db01b2fe257e","l":"Low-energy electron diffraction","d":3,"p":"d1402816-03a5-41ce-adad-a9f621e3ec0f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Low-energy electron diffraction (LEED) is a surface-sensitive diffraction technique in which a beam of low-energy electrons (typically 20\u2013300 eV) is elastically scattered from a crystalline surface to produce a characteristic spot pattern. Because these electrons penetrate only the top few atomic layers, analysis of the LEED pattern reveals the surface lattice periodicity, reconstructions, and atomic registry."},{"id":"08bcda4a-f198-4ef7-8127-75e741ccc138","l":"Low-energy electron microscopy","d":3,"p":"34773509-53cc-4885-a027-7bd3497f7157","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Low-energy electron microscopy (LEEM) is a surface-sensitive imaging technique that forms real-time, high-resolution images by reflecting low-energy electrons (typically 0\u2013100 eV) from a sample in ultra-high vacuum. Because those electrons probe only the topmost atomic layers, LEEM yields nanometer-scale maps of surface morphology and dynamics and can be combined with diffraction/IV spectroscopy to reveal surface structure and electronic properties."},{"id":"eaca2279-c165-4d8d-9d90-993209240f9e","l":"Low Reynolds number swimmers","d":3,"p":"66d73f04-5784-47a0-b3c7-c8b15ee98de1","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Low Reynolds number swimmers are microscale organisms or engineered particles that move in fluids where Re \u226a 1, so viscous forces dominate inertial forces and the flow is governed by the linear, time-reversible Stokes equations. Because time-reversible strokes produce no net motion (Purcell's scallop theorem), these swimmers propel themselves by nonreciprocal shape changes or force cycles\u2014e.g., rotating flagella, beating cilia, or asymmetric stroke sequences."},{"id":"ad4f68fe-3688-43f1-af5c-7cd5f8184930","l":"Lyotroptic active nematics","d":3,"p":"f3ad8cfa-a536-49e0-bf56-ff47aeb98e3f","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Lyotropic active nematics are nematic liquid-crystalline phases formed by rod-like, self-driven particles suspended in a solvent whose orientational order is controlled by particle concentration (lyotropic). Because the constituents exert active stresses, these systems operate far from equilibrium, exhibiting spontaneous flows, continual creation and annihilation of topological defects, and strong coupling between orientational order and concentration or phase separation."},{"id":"5f042072-e281-48f0-8092-ea2fe2c29c26","l":"Machine learning","d":3,"p":"2226691f-74b8-410b-b732-715bb1c8964b","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology","Fluid Dynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Machine learning is a collection of computational and statistical methods that automatically infer patterns and predictive models from data by training parameterized algorithms rather than coding rules explicitly. In physics it is widely used for tasks such as regression, classification, dimensionality reduction, surrogate modeling, and solving inverse or high\u2011dimensional problems."},{"id":"cb0dfbaa-d411-420c-abde-f8b8d4ebf6c2","l":"Macroscopic fluctuation theory","d":3,"p":"cd403d00-d86b-41dc-a6fb-72bc3a302627","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Interdisciplinary Physics"],"def":"Macroscopic fluctuation theory is a theoretical framework for describing large-scale fluctuations and rare events in driven diffusive systems and nonequilibrium steady states by treating density and current fields hydrodynamically and assigning them probabilities via a large-deviation (action) functional. It yields variational (optimal-path) principles for fluctuation histories and predicts fluctuation-response relations, dynamical phase transitions, and current statistics."},{"id":"c003635d-9ddb-49e3-88fb-41f3ffb91cdd","l":"Magnetic force microscopy","d":3,"p":"f2622e6a-5f55-4f27-804c-aed3aebdbe02","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Magnetic force microscopy (MFM) is a scanning probe technique that images magnetic stray fields and domain structure at the nanoscale by measuring the magnetic force or force gradient between a magnetized cantilever tip and the sample as the tip is raster-scanned. Variations in the cantilever\u2019s oscillation (phase, frequency, or amplitude) are recorded to produce a map of the sample\u2019s magnetic landscape."},{"id":"391d8598-aef3-4799-9fdb-fc950d2a6fc4","l":"Magnetic refrigeration","d":3,"p":"e6586efa-1454-4ae2-9407-f4ca0d9322da","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Magnetic refrigeration is a cooling technology that exploits the magnetocaloric effect, in which a magnetic material changes temperature and entropy when exposed to a changing magnetic field (heating on magnetization, cooling on demagnetization). Practical devices cycle magnetic field application and heat exchange\u2014often using an active magnetic regenerator\u2014providing refrigeration without vapor-compression and eliminating traditional gaseous refrigerants."},{"id":"356a5e27-1e83-46fb-a678-b35d8f244f2e","l":"Magnetic tunnel junctions","d":3,"p":"6f9f6f47-3cea-43a9-9739-d594e6448ec6","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A magnetic tunnel junction (MTJ) is a nanoscale spintronic device made of two ferromagnetic electrodes separated by a thin insulating barrier, where electrons quantum-mechanically tunnel between the magnets and the tunneling probability depends on their spin. This spin-dependent tunneling produces tunneling magnetoresistance (TMR)\u2014a large change in resistance between parallel and antiparallel magnetization alignments\u2014used in sensors and MRAM."},{"id":"72d12154-97d7-48e7-be3a-9a49aebca52a","l":"Mammography","d":3,"p":"85108ad2-07c1-4e45-8a57-180aabe6d2e6","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Mammography is a clinical X\u2011ray imaging technique that uses low\u2011energy photons (typically ~20\u201330 keV) to produce high\u2011resolution projection images of the breast for screening and diagnosis. It exploits differential X\u2011ray attenuation between glandular, fatty tissue and lesions, and uses breast compression, anti\u2011scatter measures, and specialized detectors to maximize contrast and spatial resolution while minimizing dose."},{"id":"89b8ec15-5f55-480d-9e18-195f3dc0411c","l":"Maxwell's equations","d":3,"p":"8359ab1d-c3e9-4d53-bf7f-702075aaa8b0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Maxwell's equations are four coupled partial differential equations that govern classical electrodynamics by relating the electric field E and magnetic field B to their sources (charge density \u03c1 and current density J) and to each other.  They comprise Gauss's law for electricity, Gauss's law for magnetism, Faraday's law of induction, and the Amp\u00e8re\u2013Maxwell law, and together imply the existence of electromagnetic waves that propagate at the speed of light."},{"id":"170cd9f4-59f1-4cf5-b25f-2d522780945a","l":"Measurement-based quantum computing","d":3,"p":"f9456df5-5ca2-4a8a-8b11-d5ffd9d96921","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Measurement-based quantum computing (MBQC), or the one-way quantum computer, is a model in which a fixed highly entangled resource state (typically a cluster or graph state) is prepared and computation is carried out by successive local single-qubit measurements whose bases are chosen adaptively using classical feedforward of earlier outcomes. This measurement-driven protocol is universal and equivalent in power to the circuit model of quantum computation."},{"id":"03ea23c0-a2b0-4198-9a17-980c1f9d91de","l":"Mechanical properties of membranes","d":3,"p":"4e68d438-9022-4da6-ae03-4074370de48f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Mechanical properties of membranes are the material parameters and response behaviors that determine how thin, two-dimensional sheets (biological lipid bilayers or synthetic films) deform under forces, e.g., bending rigidity, spontaneous curvature, surface (tension) and area-compression (stretch) moduli, shear resistance (for solid membranes), and in-plane viscosity. These properties set membrane shape, thermal fluctuations, stability, and dynamical responses to stresses and flows."},{"id":"cc847ef9-eb86-4c97-b54c-3d7151ccc48f","l":"Medical image processing","d":3,"p":"85108ad2-07c1-4e45-8a57-180aabe6d2e6","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Medical image processing uses computational and mathematical methods to reconstruct, enhance, analyze, and visualize images produced by medical imaging systems (X\u2011ray, CT, MRI, ultrasound, PET) to support diagnosis, treatment planning, and image\u2011guided procedures. Typical tasks include physics\u2011based image reconstruction, denoising, segmentation, registration, and quantitative feature extraction."},{"id":"b25351a4-fd4e-448d-9452-bd84379d265d","l":"Medical image quality","d":3,"p":"85108ad2-07c1-4e45-8a57-180aabe6d2e6","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Medical image quality is the degree to which an imaging system and its images enable accurate detection, characterization, and measurement of anatomical or functional features for a specific clinical task. It is assessed by both objective metrics (spatial resolution, contrast, noise/SNR, CNR, artifact level, temporal resolution, and quantitative accuracy) and subjective observer performance, and depends on acquisition parameters and dose\u2013benefit trade-offs."},{"id":"5f9094b0-d801-4b62-a637-6951e2976cd9","l":"Medical imaging safety","d":3,"p":"85108ad2-07c1-4e45-8a57-180aabe6d2e6","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Medical imaging safety is the set of principles, procedures, and engineering controls that minimize harm and maximize diagnostic benefit from imaging technologies by managing physical and biological hazards. It encompasses radiation protection (ALARA, shielding, dose monitoring and optimization), MRI and implanted-device screening, contrast-agent and acoustic/optical bioeffect management, equipment maintenance, personnel training, and regulatory quality-assurance practices."},{"id":"d6fcd8b9-4d50-4824-ad6d-71e2b16afcf4","l":"Medical magnetic resonance imaging","d":3,"p":"85108ad2-07c1-4e45-8a57-180aabe6d2e6","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Medical magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is a noninvasive diagnostic technique that uses nuclear magnetic resonance of hydrogen nuclei: a strong static magnetic field aligns proton spins, radiofrequency pulses tip those spins, and spatially varying magnetic field gradients encode the emitted RF signals for image reconstruction. The resulting images reflect local proton density and relaxation properties (T1, T2), giving high soft-tissue contrast without ionizing radiation."},{"id":"6b156cbb-39ac-4db9-8ecf-50b5e3646acb","l":"Medical X-ray imaging","d":3,"p":"85108ad2-07c1-4e45-8a57-180aabe6d2e6","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Medical X-ray imaging uses penetrating X\u2011ray photons passed through the body to form images of internal structures by measuring the differential attenuation of the beam caused by varying tissue composition and density. Detectors (film, digital panels, or CT scanners) record transmitted or scattered X\u2011rays and convert those signals into planar radiographs or reconstructed tomographic slices."},{"id":"3eb82ff9-45b5-4441-a628-aa681529dc2f","l":"Meiosis","d":3,"p":"32f64d06-8ebf-473e-8996-65a651e8f4c4","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Meiosis is a specialized eukaryotic cell division that reduces the chromosome number by half (diploid \u2192 haploid) to produce gametes or spores. It involves one round of DNA replication followed by two sequential divisions (meiosis I and II), with homologous chromosome pairing and recombination generating genetic diversity."},{"id":"d2352d48-b3ef-42d6-9dd2-c8270b2bb962","l":"Membrane assembly","d":3,"p":"4e68d438-9022-4da6-ae03-4074370de48f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Membrane assembly is the physical process by which amphiphilic molecules (lipids and membrane proteins) self\u2011organize into two\u2011dimensional bilayers or closed vesicles, driven by the hydrophobic effect and minimization of free energy. It includes the kinetics and mechanics of bilayer formation, fusion, budding and protein incorporation that determine membrane composition, curvature and mechanical properties."},{"id":"2ff7c9e3-6ff5-4d4b-955f-5dfbb837a92c","l":"Membrane dynamics","d":3,"p":"4e68d438-9022-4da6-ae03-4074370de48f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Membrane dynamics is the study of the time-dependent behavior of thin, quasi-two-dimensional interfaces\u2014such as lipid bilayers, cell membranes, or fluid films\u2014including their shape changes, thermal fluctuations, in-plane flows, and transport of embedded components. These dynamics are governed by membrane elasticity (bending rigidity and tension), membrane and bulk viscosities, hydrodynamic coupling to surrounding fluids, and possible active forces from proteins or external fields."},{"id":"c1faf87c-765e-41fa-ac02-3eab8858bdf9","l":"Membrane fluctuations","d":3,"p":"4e68d438-9022-4da6-ae03-4074370de48f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Membrane fluctuations are the spontaneous, thermally driven deviations of a soft membrane (e.g., a lipid bilayer or thin elastic sheet) from its mean shape, including out-of-plane undulations and local thickness or area variations. Their amplitude and wavelength dependence result from a competition between thermal energy and elastic restoring forces\u2014primarily bending rigidity and surface tension\u2014and are commonly described by the Helfrich elastic model."},{"id":"86c34563-0b72-46af-930a-389f79331162","l":"Membrane formation","d":3,"p":"4e68d438-9022-4da6-ae03-4074370de48f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Membrane formation is the process by which molecules or materials organize into a thin, continuous two-dimensional surface that separates two regions or phases\u2014examples include lipid bilayers forming cell membranes, soap films spanning a frame, or polymer films coating an interface. This assembly is driven by minimization of free energy, balancing forces such as hydrophobic/hydrophilic interactions, surface tension and bending elasticity to produce a mechanically and chemically distinct interface."},{"id":"07203494-ef0d-4ff6-baee-4c2e6e0b4b3c","l":"Membrane structure","d":3,"p":"4e68d438-9022-4da6-ae03-4074370de48f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"A membrane structure is a thin, quasi-two-dimensional surface whose mechanics are governed mainly by in-plane tensile and shear stresses with negligible bending stiffness, so it carries loads primarily by tension rather than bending. The term applies across scales \u2014 from macroscopic tensile constructions and soap films to molecular membranes (e.g., lipid bilayers) that also govern transport and surface phenomena."},{"id":"a05fc982-55a1-4a97-bbda-baa4ed33a7a6","l":"Metal-insulator-metal diodes","d":3,"p":"926cb610-c40b-4bd4-b069-14722b7251c7","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Metal\u2013insulator\u2013metal (MIM) diodes are two-terminal devices consisting of two metal electrodes separated by a nanometer-scale insulating layer, where current flows primarily by quantum tunneling through the barrier rather than by semiconductor junction mechanisms. Their nonlinear, often asymmetric I\u2013V response (adjustable by electrode materials or barrier shape) makes them useful as ultrafast rectifiers and detectors in microwave-to-terahertz applications."},{"id":"d0ae988a-9713-477c-afbe-ba128fe87844","l":"Mineralogical CO2 storage","d":3,"p":"64470342-2836-4044-a632-693f38e12b9f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Mineralogogical CO2 storage (mineral carbonation) is the permanent immobilization of carbon dioxide by chemical reaction with reactive silicate or oxide minerals to form stable carbonate solids (e.g., calcite, magnesite). It occurs naturally and can be engineered by injecting CO2 (gas or dissolved) into reactive rocks or industrial residues, where dissolved CO2 forms carbonic acid and then precipitates as carbonate minerals."},{"id":"e8a39c06-7a9b-4a30-8284-11e953b230ee","l":"Minkowski momentum","d":3,"p":"0d8c3864-a060-4baf-a6a0-ad0b455410cc","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Minkowski momentum is the electromagnetic momentum density in a material medium defined by g_M = D \u00d7 B (SI units), where D is the electric displacement and B the magnetic induction. For a homogeneous, nondispersive dielectric it implies a photon momentum p = n\u0127\u03c9/c and stems from the Minkowski form of the electromagnetic energy\u2013momentum tensor (one of the two canonical momentum definitions in the Abraham\u2013Minkowski debate)."},{"id":"c5d8ca90-e81b-45da-885b-bc6c06f0084c","l":"Mirror instability","d":3,"p":"3f71ccf5-16ff-425f-b7b3-4791f9503b59","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Mirror instability is a kinetic, nonpropagating instability in a magnetized plasma driven by pressure anisotropy with perpendicular temperature exceeding parallel temperature (T\u22a5 > T\u2225), which produces stationary, highly oblique magnetic field depressions or peaks called mirror modes. It typically occurs in high\u2011\u03b2 plasmas and has the rough threshold (T\u22a5/T\u2225 \u2212 1) \u2273 1/\u03b2\u2225, where \u03b2\u2225 is the plasma beta based on the parallel pressure."},{"id":"08e6b4ed-ea89-432f-9034-732c3c4e8035","l":"Molecular beam epitaxy","d":3,"p":"471b81e2-74ad-4d48-b2c0-a57b3c84bb81","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Molecular beam epitaxy (MBE) is a thin-film growth technique in which beams of atoms or molecules from thermal sources travel in ultra-high vacuum and deposit on a heated crystalline substrate, producing epitaxial, layer-by-layer growth with atomic-scale control of thickness and composition. It is widely used to fabricate high-purity semiconductor heterostructures and quantum devices."},{"id":"a3e8c7ca-d654-481f-8154-23ec06a6b403","l":"Molecular orbital theory","d":3,"p":"ae589249-804f-4a7c-94d7-f882ecc129be","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Molecular orbital theory is a quantum-mechanical description in which atomic orbitals combine (often via linear combinations) to form molecular orbitals\u2014delocalized wavefunctions that extend over an entire molecule. Electrons occupy these MOs according to the Pauli principle and energy ordering, and the relative occupancy of bonding versus antibonding MOs determines molecular structure, stability, and many spectroscopic properties."},{"id":"088acf0f-7e63-4f08-8a24-8651d8df730d","l":"Molecular scattering from surfaces","d":3,"p":"7600fee4-52d4-46ff-bca9-7439ac394273","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Molecular scattering from surfaces is the study of how gas-phase molecules collide with and are deflected by solid or liquid surfaces, exchanging energy and momentum and possibly adsorbing, trapping, or undergoing chemical reactions. Measurements of the angular and energy distributions of scattered molecules reveal information about surface structure, dynamics, and interaction potentials."},{"id":"5fbbd0b9-5d6d-4aa7-baed-5c853210011c","l":"Molten carbonate fuel cells","d":3,"p":"9d3ec67b-01ed-4ebe-8b7f-04613a3d5185","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Molten carbonate fuel cells (MCFCs) are high\u2011temperature electrochemical devices that use a molten carbonate salt mixture as the electrolyte to conduct carbonate ions (CO3^2\u2212) between electrodes, converting the chemical energy of hydrogen or hydrocarbon fuels and oxygen into electricity, heat and CO2. Operating typically at 600\u2013700 \u00b0C, their elevated temperature allows internal fuel reforming and high efficiency but requires corrosion\u2011resistant materials."},{"id":"0d9fc0d0-fda1-4621-8e62-1387f3c632f2","l":"M\u00f6ssbauer emission spectroscopy","d":3,"p":"39501eb9-cc8c-4c59-9bbf-424b8f16973a","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"M\u00f6ssbauer emission spectroscopy is a high-resolution nuclear gamma-ray technique that records recoil-free (M\u00f6ssbauer) gamma photons emitted by nuclei in a solid to obtain their precise transition energies. By measuring small energy shifts and splittings of the emitted lines it probes hyperfine interactions (isomer shifts, quadrupole and magnetic splittings) and thus the local electronic, chemical, and magnetic environment of the emitting nuclei."},{"id":"43822279-9251-4705-8e15-c287c2e101fa","l":"Motor proteins","d":3,"p":"8c0cd30a-5be8-4913-9220-907afd4bceb8","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Motor proteins are nanoscale biomolecular machines that transduce chemical energy from ATP hydrolysis into mechanical work to produce force and directed motion along cytoskeletal filaments (microtubules and actin). Examples\u2014kinesin and dynein on microtubules and myosin on actin\u2014drive intracellular transport, muscle contraction, and cell-division processes via coordinated mechanochemical stepping."},{"id":"11abe4f0-0e20-4ee5-bb4c-7ce1d0bfbf35","l":"Multiferroic tunnel junctions","d":3,"p":"6f9f6f47-3cea-43a9-9739-d594e6448ec6","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Multiferroic tunnel junctions are nanoscale tunnel junctions in which the ultrathin tunnel barrier (or one electrode) is a multiferroic material that simultaneously carries ferroelectric polarization and magnetic order. The coexistence and magnetoelectric coupling of polarization and magnetization enable electric- or magnetic-field control of spin-dependent tunneling, producing nonvolatile tunneling electroresistance and magnetoresistance useful for memory and spintronic devices."},{"id":"a26a8dba-90de-420e-bbd8-586b1914112a","l":"Multi-wire proportional & drift chambers","d":3,"p":"d94b540a-e0d6-40ef-9e71-d39129278bd5","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Multi-wire proportional chambers are gas-filled detectors made of many parallel sense wires that collect avalanches of ionization electrons produced by a traversing charged particle, giving localized proportional electrical signals for fast tracking and triggering. Drift chambers are similar multi-wire devices that determine particle positions more precisely by measuring the drift time of ionization electrons to the sense wires (using a known electron drift velocity) to reconstruct tracks."},{"id":"a0dfe635-22c0-4f38-a45b-3907f7ad6eb0","l":"Mutation","d":3,"p":"08b53e02-8779-41b1-ae1e-beeef0ce31c9","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Mutation \u2014 a stochastic change in an organism's hereditary information (e.g., DNA or RNA sequence) that alters its genotype. In physics-based models of evolution and population dynamics, mutation is treated as a random transition process with a characteristic rate that injects variation and influences the balance of selection, drift, and adaptation."},{"id":"f1615b74-e249-40af-b9e0-e2bd3ac84ba5","l":"Natural language processing","d":3,"p":"2226691f-74b8-410b-b732-715bb1c8964b","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology","Fluid Dynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Natural language processing (NLP) is the interdisciplinary field that develops computational methods\u2014drawing on linguistics, statistics, and machine learning\u2014to analyze, model, and generate human language for tasks such as parsing, translation, summarization, and question answering. Modern NLP emphasizes probabilistic models and deep neural networks trained on large text corpora to capture syntax, semantics, and pragmatic context."},{"id":"68fc713c-6b28-4a8f-ad9a-5c535d4e6a3a","l":"Near-field optical spectroscopy","d":3,"p":"efe40972-7a4f-4a87-80ef-ae39472ce875","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics"],"def":"Near-field optical spectroscopy is a set of techniques that measure a material's spectral (frequency-dependent) optical response with spatial resolution far below the diffraction limit by probing evanescent fields within a subwavelength distance of the sample. A nanoscale tip or aperture converts local near-field interactions into detectable scattered or emitted light, enabling mapping of local absorption, emission, refractive index, and plasmonic/phononic resonances with nanometer-scale accuracy."},{"id":"64f466f9-d7b4-4c8b-b595-8adff8e74e9d","l":"Neuronal imaging","d":3,"p":"85108ad2-07c1-4e45-8a57-180aabe6d2e6","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Neuronal imaging is the family of experimental techniques that visualize the structure and real-time activity of neurons and neural circuits by detecting physical signals produced or modulated by neural tissue (optical fluorescence/voltage signals, electrical potentials, magnetic resonance, radioactive tracers, etc.). Different modalities span scales from single-cell microscopy (two\u2011photon, calcium/voltage imaging) to whole\u2011brain methods (fMRI, PET), with characteristic tradeoffs in spatial and temporal resolution and invasiveness."},{"id":"e1d2c9a5-598e-4b39-af07-d13d4e3a4a79","l":"Neutron tomography","d":3,"p":"1515b875-7014-43c8-b872-65e6551b38f0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Neutron tomography is a non\u2011destructive imaging technique that reconstructs an object's three\u2011dimensional internal structure from multiple neutron transmission projections using tomographic reconstruction algorithms. Because neutrons interact with atomic nuclei rather than electron clouds, they penetrate metals well and provide contrast complementary to X\u2011rays\u2014being especially sensitive to light elements (e.g., hydrogen) and certain isotopes\u2014so they are used to image internal features, fluids, and embedded components."},{"id":"de7df369-187c-4025-8b07-8af412d6e82c","l":"Nitrides","d":3,"p":"2a71cbba-3674-42b1-8778-4b794f85a844","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Nitrides are compounds in which nitrogen is chemically bonded to a less electronegative element (typically a metal or metalloid), forming binary or multinary materials with bonding that can range from ionic to covalent. They often have high hardness and thermal stability and include important semiconductors (e.g., GaN, AlN) used in LEDs, high-power electronics, and hard coatings."},{"id":"7d5f1f31-b6fc-4fb8-9a59-2931c6306e5c","l":"Noble metals","d":3,"p":"7584df11-595e-4afa-bd90-305dfa506396","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Noble metals are metallic elements (notably gold, silver, and the platinum-group metals) that are unusually resistant to oxidation and corrosion under normal conditions, and therefore chemically inert compared with typical metals. They typically have filled or nearly filled d-bands, high work functions and electrical conductivity, and distinct optical and catalytic properties."},{"id":"6061ae5e-a425-456f-af55-c1ff5830de7b","l":"Nonclassical interferometry","d":3,"p":"ca9988fc-ff8d-4c19-ae21-68f7ba12e0e7","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Nonclassical interferometry is interferometry that employs quantum (nonclassical) states and measurements\u2014such as squeezed states, entangled photons (NOON states), or single-photon/Fock inputs\u2014to obtain phase or displacement sensitivities and measurement capabilities that exceed classical limits. It exploits quantum correlations and reduced quantum noise to beat the shot-noise limit and approach the Heisenberg limit for precision."},{"id":"6ebd5a59-e5ef-4670-a1fd-0475cf68304d","l":"Noncommutative geometry","d":3,"p":"8f8b77e5-7263-4333-a6a9-def3fd83e2f6","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Noncommutative geometry is a mathematical framework that generalizes ordinary geometry by replacing the commutative algebra of functions on a space with a noncommutative algebra, so that coordinates become operators that do not necessarily commute. It provides a way to describe \"spaces\" with intrinsic quantum or discrete structure and is used in formulations of quantum mechanics, quantum field theory, and approaches to quantum gravity."},{"id":"cc37023d-0bed-4caa-8cea-484ab1d0a87c","l":"Noncontact atomic force microscopy","d":3,"p":"f2622e6a-5f55-4f27-804c-aed3aebdbe02","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Noncontact atomic force microscopy (NC-AFM) is an AFM operating mode in which a sharp tip oscillates a few \u00e5ngstr\u00f6ms to nanometers above a sample without making mechanical contact; forces between tip and surface produce measurable shifts in the cantilever's resonance frequency, amplitude, or phase. By mapping those force-induced signals (often using frequency-modulation detection), NC-AFM resolves surface topography and local chemical/force contrast with atomic-scale resolution."},{"id":"5318b579-e3c8-464e-abfb-f28dd1889bd3","l":"Non-diffracting waves","d":3,"p":"07396f85-99a2-483d-be5b-3725e182caa3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Non-diffracting waves are wave solutions whose transverse amplitude or intensity profile remains essentially unchanged as they propagate, avoiding the usual diffraction-induced spreading. Ideal examples (e.g., Bessel beams) are propagation-invariant solutions of the wave equation with formally infinite energy, while real-world implementations are finite-energy approximations (including Airy and Mathieu beams) that maintain a diffraction-free form over a long but limited distance and often exhibit self-healing after obstacles."},{"id":"69e286a8-4297-43c0-b7bc-5aa09e9a2982","l":"Nonequilibrium Green's function","d":3,"p":"8c15e092-3b78-496f-96ae-e55153f7efbd","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Nonequilibrium Green's functions are correlation functions that describe the propagation and statistical correlations of particles in quantum many-body systems driven away from thermal equilibrium. Formulated on the Keldysh time contour (with retarded/advanced and lesser/greater components), they provide a practical framework for computing time-dependent expectation values, steady-state transport, and interaction or reservoir effects in open quantum systems."},{"id":"af16c992-ba43-4399-bedd-ae18036b5dfb","l":"Non-gaussian quantum states","d":3,"p":"fa660751-b084-419b-9861-223d75821bb0","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Non-Gaussian quantum states are quantum states whose phase-space quasi-probability distribution (e.g., the Wigner function) or quadrature statistics are not Gaussian, so they cannot be fully described by just first and second moments and exhibit nonzero higher-order correlations. They include Fock states, Schr\u00f6dinger cat states and photon\u2011subtracted/added states, and are a necessary resource for tasks such as entanglement distillation and universal continuous\u2011variable quantum computation."},{"id":"d2a30132-6138-4e06-98d9-d71dd696cbf7","l":"Nuclear magnetic resonance relaxation rate","d":3,"p":"fee9296a-01ca-4b80-86bf-2c51f37c1871","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) relaxation rate is the inverse of the characteristic time scale(s) over which perturbed nuclear spin magnetization returns to equilibrium \u2014 specifically R1 = 1/T1 for longitudinal (spin\u2013lattice) relaxation and R2 = 1/T2 for transverse (spin\u2013spin) dephasing. These rates are set by interactions with fluctuating local magnetic fields (other spins, lattice motions) and thus probe molecular dynamics and the spin environment."},{"id":"c16a05ce-2d70-4ae6-b335-317404e599d0","l":"Nuclear medicine imaging","d":3,"p":"85108ad2-07c1-4e45-8a57-180aabe6d2e6","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Nuclear medicine imaging is a diagnostic technique that administers radiolabeled tracers which emit gamma photons or positrons, allowing visualization and quantitative measurement of physiological and molecular processes in vivo. Detectors such as gamma cameras and PET scanners register the emitted photons (or coincidence pairs) and use tomographic reconstruction (planar, SPECT, or PET) to form images of tracer distribution and function."},{"id":"d73b620d-16e7-401d-9d82-0f8ddc43076a","l":"Nuclear reaction analysis","d":3,"p":"1fc8f216-e853-4b59-bdb2-2ac001bd3ea2","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Nuclear reaction analysis is an ion-beam materials characterization technique in which a beam of energetic ions induces specific nuclear reactions in a target and the emitted reaction products (charged particles or gamma rays) are measured to identify and quantify elements. By using known reaction cross sections and the energy loss of the beam in matter, the measured yields as a function of beam energy provide elemental concentration and depth-profile information."},{"id":"2f7c0d81-b895-4834-86a6-1a2185246030","l":"NV centers","d":3,"p":"39f369f1-f48a-4dce-858c-8337d41ec94d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"NV centers (nitrogen-vacancy centers) are atomic-scale point defects in diamond consisting of a substitutional nitrogen atom adjacent to a lattice vacancy. Their electronic spin (S = 1) is optically addressable and coherently controllable with long coherence times\u2014even at room temperature\u2014making them useful for quantum sensing, magnetometry, and quantum information."},{"id":"996cc3c0-b4c2-4a3d-9433-7bcc1917b65e","l":"O(N) symmetry","d":3,"p":"543d3af2-a8bc-462b-9d72-753b6de0d8ce","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"O(N) symmetry means a system is invariant under the orthogonal group O(N), i.e., all N\u00d7N real transformations that preserve the Euclidean dot product (hence lengths and angles) and include rotations and reflections. In physics it typically acts on an N-component real field or order parameter; the connected subgroup of pure rotations is SO(N)."},{"id":"5b8299b9-4fc3-421e-ba07-ad6a5fd07dab","l":"Optical absorption spectroscopy","d":3,"p":"efe40972-7a4f-4a87-80ef-ae39472ce875","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics"],"def":"Optical absorption spectroscopy is an experimental technique that measures how the intensity of light transmitted through or reflected from a sample varies with wavelength or frequency. The wavelength-dependent attenuation reveals resonant electronic, vibrational, or rotational transitions and, via the absorption coefficient (Beer\u2013Lambert law), yields transition energies, band gaps and concentrations of absorbing species."},{"id":"c9690e5d-cac9-4f75-be56-51fc776b51e4","l":"Optical activity","d":3,"p":"878297d0-7ed9-4aee-8439-56bcf340edf6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Optical activity is the property of certain media (typically chiral molecules or noncentrosymmetric crystals) to rotate the plane of polarization of linearly polarized light as it propagates through them. This rotation arises because the medium has different refractive indices (and sometimes different absorptions) for left- and right-circularly polarized components, a phenomenon called circular birefringence (and circular dichroism)."},{"id":"8096fc1e-c526-4bd8-868d-6fea7abf29d7","l":"Optical generation of spin carriers","d":3,"p":"257ff504-8723-46c7-aea0-ec7d55a0dc6f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Optical generation of spin carriers is the creation of spin-polarized charge carriers (electrons and/or holes) in a material by absorption of light that transfers angular momentum, e.g., circularly polarized photons driving spin-selective interband transitions (optical orientation) or exciton formation. This non-contact method injects and controls spin populations for studying spin dynamics and for spintronic applications."},{"id":"f5f255a0-4135-4a6f-9bb4-bbe9d5690c01","l":"Optical instabilities","d":3,"p":"ecf6e836-240a-4093-9d1c-46680eeeccad","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Optical instabilities are dynamical regimes in optical systems where small perturbations of the electromagnetic field grow rather than decay, producing self\u2011oscillations, spatial patterns, filamentation or chaotic light. They arise from the interplay of gain or feedback, medium nonlinearities, dispersion and diffraction (e.g., laser relaxation oscillations, modulational instability, transverse pattern formation)."},{"id":"b36b3f96-501f-4748-bcf4-a201a8b1f55d","l":"Optical-pump terahertz-probe spectroscopy","d":3,"p":"d47bba71-117d-4810-a4e0-e6528994d02e","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Optical-pump terahertz-probe spectroscopy is a time-resolved pump\u2013probe technique in which an ultrafast optical pulse excites (pumps) a sample and a delayed broadband terahertz pulse probes the resulting transient changes in the transmitted or reflected terahertz waveform. From the change in amplitude and phase of the terahertz field one extracts the sample's time-dependent complex conductivity and dielectric response, revealing carrier dynamics, scattering rates, and low-energy excitations on sub-picosecond to nanosecond timescales."},{"id":"3f3c14af-f4ee-4098-abe7-d077b362704e","l":"Optical quantum information processing","d":3,"p":"6e1c9aa8-2e35-4fcf-ba26-5fcc93a4aed4","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Optical quantum information processing is the encoding, transmission, manipulation, and measurement of quantum information using photons and optical elements, exploiting quantum superposition and entanglement of light. It underlies tasks such as quantum computing, secure quantum communication, and quantum-enhanced sensing and metrology."},{"id":"fc74677d-67b3-4be5-984a-30630ebcb46d","l":"Optical rogue waves","d":3,"p":"48060ba5-3772-446b-a0cd-c58da79462c6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Optical rogue waves are rare, spontaneous extreme-intensity light pulses in nonlinear optical systems whose peak amplitudes greatly exceed the typical background (commonly defined by a threshold such as twice the significant intensity). They arise from nonlinear dynamics\u2014e.g., modulation instability, soliton interactions, and noise amplification\u2014and exhibit heavy-tailed statistics analogous to oceanic rogue waves."},{"id":"1c34a735-46ab-4b59-bca0-c03f0c76d289","l":"Optical second-harmonic generation","d":3,"p":"d3a4e127-e2a1-4d1b-9637-522e2bf75e99","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Optical second-harmonic generation (SHG) is a second-order nonlinear optical process in which two photons at frequency \u03c9 combine in a medium to produce a single coherent photon at frequency 2\u03c9, with efficiency set by the material's second-order susceptibility \u03c7^(2). Efficient SHG requires broken inversion symmetry (bulk noncentrosymmetric crystals or symmetry-breaking interfaces) and appropriate phase-matching or quasi-phase-matching to conserve momentum."},{"id":"fd217aaf-b276-452a-b3a5-f8c748b0f98f","l":"Optical spin injection","d":3,"p":"e9c1b6a7-5fa0-4e03-ba40-c835aaeee544","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Optical spin injection is the generation of a non-equilibrium spin polarization in a material by absorption of circularly polarized light. Photon angular momentum is transferred to electrons or holes via spin-dependent optical selection rules, producing spin-polarized carrier populations or spin currents for spintronic and ultrafast control applications."},{"id":"0d68bfc9-8cf3-46cf-b51d-c91e1e052bd4","l":"Optical tweezers","d":3,"p":"dedc6dbe-3651-4ad0-9d37-b260e8975adc","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Optical tweezers use a tightly focused laser beam to trap and manipulate microscopic dielectric particles: the beam's intensity gradient pulls particles toward the focus while scattering (radiation-pressure) forces push them along the beam axis, producing a stable three-dimensional optical trap. They can apply and measure piconewton-scale forces and are widely used to manipulate biological molecules, colloids, and micromechanical systems."},{"id":"e84e0eff-599e-46e3-856a-af07fafb61bb","l":"Organic LEDs","d":3,"p":"926cb610-c40b-4bd4-b069-14722b7251c7","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Organic light-emitting diodes (OLEDs) are electroluminescent devices in which thin films of organic semiconductor molecules or polymers, sandwiched between electrodes, emit light when injected electrons and holes recombine to form excitons that decay radiatively. Because the emissive layer is carbon-based, OLEDs operate at low voltage and can be made lightweight, flexible, and color-tunable, making them useful for displays and solid-state lighting."},{"id":"8725a823-2f22-4e9a-b371-25929a4f5a36","l":"Organic semiconductors","d":3,"p":"101b99f7-34cb-4ea9-8f26-b5d649d98640","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Organic semiconductors are materials composed of \u03c0\u2011conjugated molecules or polymers whose electronic states form a semiconductor-like energy gap, allowing controlled injection, transport and recombination of charge carriers. Charge transport occurs via delocalized \u03c0-electrons or by thermally activated hopping of polarons and excitons between weakly coupled molecules, making these materials useful for OLEDs, organic photovoltaics and field\u2011effect transistors."},{"id":"b780936a-06f1-4e85-872d-cbcfd412b035","l":"Organic superconductors","d":3,"p":"101b99f7-34cb-4ea9-8f26-b5d649d98640","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Organic superconductors are crystalline materials in which superconductivity is carried by \u03c0-electron networks of large organic molecules (rather than a conventional metallic lattice). They are typically low-dimensional (quasi-1D or quasi-2D), strongly correlated, and often show unconventional pairing, sensitivity to pressure, and competition with charge/spin-density-wave or Mott-insulating phases."},{"id":"39d93223-4007-49a6-8084-c551ffb8b9cf","l":"Organometallic materials","d":3,"p":"101b99f7-34cb-4ea9-8f26-b5d649d98640","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Organometallic materials are compounds, polymers, or extended solids in which metal centers are bonded directly to carbon-containing ligands, producing hybrid inorganic\u2013organic structures whose structural, electronic, magnetic, and optical properties arise from metal\u2013ligand interactions. They range from discrete complexes to coordination polymers and frameworks and are widely used in catalysis, molecular electronics, optoelectronics, and magnetic materials."},{"id":"6fdf4bb3-246f-40a9-b756-d416629bdd26","l":"Oxides","d":3,"p":"2a71cbba-3674-42b1-8778-4b794f85a844","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Oxides are compounds in which oxygen is chemically bonded to one or more other elements, often appearing as oxide ions (O^2\u2212) in ionic solids or as covalently bonded oxygen in molecular and network structures. They span a wide range of materials\u2014from insulating ceramics to conductive and correlated transition\u2011metal oxides\u2014and exhibit diverse structural, electronic, magnetic and optical properties important in condensed\u2011matter physics and materials science."},{"id":"5d719067-c723-4c97-869d-a60c26c64272","l":"Parasitic diseases","d":3,"p":"d6072ccd-5d49-4a8c-a032-efa240964575","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Parasitic diseases are illnesses caused by parasites\u2014organisms such as protozoa, helminths (worms), or ectoparasites\u2014that live on or inside a host and derive nutrients at the host\u2019s expense. They are transmitted by vectors (e.g., mosquitoes), contaminated food or water, or direct contact and can cause acute or chronic harm (examples include malaria, schistosomiasis, and intestinal worm infections)."},{"id":"6fc8813f-a669-421b-8003-93d983e9e384","l":"Particle-induced x-ray emission","d":3,"p":"1fc8f216-e853-4b59-bdb2-2ac001bd3ea2","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Particle-induced X-ray emission (PIXE) is an analytical technique in which energetic charged particles (typically MeV-range protons or alpha particles) ionize inner atomic shells of a target, and the subsequent electronic transitions emit characteristic X-rays whose energies identify the elements present. Because those X-ray energies are element-specific, PIXE provides sensitive, non-destructive, and quantitative elemental analysis."},{"id":"8b7ed72b-81ae-473a-9b90-9bdb8b668dca","l":"Paths","d":3,"p":"4f65979f-8a52-4ebb-bb0c-29db6fb979d0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"A path is a parameterized curve in physical or configuration space that specifies the sequence of positions or states of a system as a function of a parameter (usually time)\u2014i.e., the system's trajectory. In quantum mechanics and field theory, \u201cpaths\u201d denote the possible histories summed over in the path-integral formulation, each contributing with a phase weight exp(iS/\u0127)."},{"id":"9483eb29-38b6-42ff-990e-ba426446d4eb","l":"Phase contrast optical microscopy","d":3,"p":"a40cc113-e132-471a-bb1b-2d29d8248a64","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Phase-contrast optical microscopy is an imaging technique that converts spatial variations in the optical phase of light\u2014produced by transparent specimens that alter the optical path length\u2014into intensity contrast by interfering the unscattered (reference) and scattered wavefronts. It uses an annular condenser and a phase plate in the objective to introduce a controlled phase shift between these components, making otherwise nearly transparent structures visible without staining."},{"id":"b1a74b14-bcf5-47ff-81a7-256555c36f5d","l":"Phosphorescence","d":3,"p":"6bdea32e-2fdf-4421-b982-41a2a011a363","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Phosphorescence is photoluminescence in which a material continues to emit light after the excitation source is removed because excited electrons are trapped in long\u2011lived metastable states (e.g., triplet states or defect traps) that decay radiatively on slow timescales. Unlike fluorescence, the emitting transitions are spin\u2011 or symmetry\u2011forbidden (or involve trapped carriers), producing a delayed \"afterglow\" that can last from microseconds to hours."},{"id":"d68619b1-3023-4aee-88df-1dfc3f183f80","l":"Phosphoric acid fuel cells","d":3,"p":"9d3ec67b-01ed-4ebe-8b7f-04613a3d5185","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Phosphoric acid fuel cells (PAFCs) are a class of fuel cell that use concentrated liquid phosphoric acid as the electrolyte between platinum-catalyzed electrodes to oxidize hydrogen and produce electricity. Operating at intermediate temperatures (~150\u2013200 \u00b0C), PAFCs offer greater tolerance to CO and are commonly used for stationary power and cogeneration."},{"id":"2b5bdcab-7c76-45e4-aa6a-8b350792e105","l":"Photodiodes","d":3,"p":"926cb610-c40b-4bd4-b069-14722b7251c7","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A photodiode is a semiconductor p\u2013n or p\u2013i\u2013n junction device that converts incident photons into an electrical current by generating electron\u2013hole pairs when light is absorbed. Operated typically under reverse bias for faster, more linear response, photodiodes are widely used as fast, sensitive optical detectors and light sensors."},{"id":"02f22b8d-0fd5-4f7b-826c-26f8b43ca3f1","l":"Photoionization of molecules","d":3,"p":"9098e351-0e96-4435-b04b-a0fa64abbf64","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Photoionization of molecules is the process in which a molecule absorbs a photon and ejects one or more electrons, producing a positively charged molecular ion and free electron(s). The detailed outcome\u2014direct ionization, autoionization, or dissociative ionization\u2014and the resulting electronic, vibrational and rotational state of the ion depend on the photon energy and the orbital from which the electron is removed, and are characterized by photoionization cross sections and photoelectron spectra."},{"id":"96b78beb-a88f-4c6b-90ae-d5e96821326a","l":"Photon correlation spectroscopy","d":3,"p":"382676e6-fd8f-4fc6-b8e0-3abbc45c379c","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Photon correlation spectroscopy (also called dynamic light scattering) is an optical technique that measures temporal fluctuations of scattered light intensity to probe the microscopic motion of scatterers (e.g., Brownian particles) in a sample. By computing the intensity autocorrelation function g2(\u03c4) and using the Siegert relation to obtain the field correlation g1(\u03c4), PCS extracts decay rates that yield diffusion coefficients and hence particle size or dynamic information."},{"id":"7f3141df-7d5f-4160-8bdf-805a081574b3","l":"Photosystems","d":3,"p":"8c0cd30a-5be8-4913-9220-907afd4bceb8","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Photosystems are membrane-bound pigment\u2013protein complexes (notably photosystem I and II) in chloroplasts and cyanobacteria that capture sunlight and funnel excitation energy through networks of pigments to a specialized reaction center. At the reaction center a photon-induced exciton produces charge separation that drives an electron-transport chain, converting light energy into chemical potential (NADPH, ATP and a proton gradient) for photosynthesis."},{"id":"258d6104-3e21-4b28-9599-fa4cb50c028a","l":"Photovoltaic absorbers","d":3,"p":"c215fb56-b919-42c5-9267-13846d715223","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Photovoltaic absorbers are semiconductor materials in solar cells that absorb sunlight and convert photons into mobile electron\u2013hole pairs which can be collected as electrical current. Their performance is determined by properties such as band gap (to match the solar spectrum), high optical absorption, long carrier lifetime and diffusion length, low recombination, and chemical/thermal stability."},{"id":"977cf3f6-ab87-440b-afe2-cf08e8fcd916","l":"Physical deposition","d":3,"p":"778678c1-03d6-402a-a039-4eaaeb999399","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Physical deposition is the formation of a solid film or coating on a substrate by physically transporting atoms, molecules, or clusters from a source to the surface, where they condense and accumulate. Common techniques\u2014such as thermal evaporation, sputtering, pulsed-laser deposition and molecular-beam epitaxy\u2014rely on physical ejection or vapor transport (often in vacuum) rather than chemical reactions at the substrate."},{"id":"ee35c003-4a4c-444d-9d68-8da5cb77dec8","l":"Physical hydrogen storage","d":3,"p":"f0761682-18fc-4076-9921-90eae238bc98","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Physical hydrogen storage is the containment of molecular hydrogen by physical means rather than by forming chemical bonds, for example as compressed gas, cryogenic liquid, or as H2 physisorbed/condensed in porous materials. It relies on pressure, temperature and weak van der Waals or confinement forces to hold and release hydrogen, and is distinct from storage in chemical carriers or metal hydrides."},{"id":"cda21e99-72bf-4feb-9508-6f0df7cdd9b0","l":"Piezoresponse force microscopy","d":3,"p":"f2622e6a-5f55-4f27-804c-aed3aebdbe02","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Piezoresponse force microscopy (PFM) is an atomic\u2011force\u2011microscopy-based scanning probe technique that images and quantifies local electromechanical (converse piezoelectric/ferroelectric) response with nanoscale spatial resolution. A conductive AFM tip applies an AC (and optionally DC) bias to the sample and the resulting tip/cantilever vertical and lateral vibrations are detected as amplitude and phase signals, which map piezoelectric coefficients, domain orientation, and switching behavior."},{"id":"8413a24a-3f33-4f1e-acb6-041b49c6e82d","l":"Pions","d":3,"p":"0841db0c-24b2-4074-9ba3-a5b6243699b5","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Pions (\u03c0 mesons) are the lightest mesons\u2014spin-0, pseudoscalar particles made of up and down quark\u2013antiquark pairs that occur in three charge states (\u03c0+, \u03c00, \u03c0\u2212) with masses \u2248135\u2013140 MeV/c^2. They are unstable (decaying via electromagnetic or weak interactions) and, in low-energy nuclear physics, mediate the residual strong force between nucleons (Yukawa picture)."},{"id":"68897869-397d-4ae5-8b23-e134879724a4","l":"Plasma etching","d":3,"p":"935c0a7f-c1a4-4b15-8fb8-0ff6481e1935","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Plasma etching is a dry microfabrication process that removes material from a substrate by exposing it to a low\u2011pressure plasma whose reactive radicals chemically react with the surface and whose energetic ions physically sputter or assist directional removal. By combining chemical reactivity and ion bombardment, it enables controlled, often anisotropic pattern transfer with high selectivity used in semiconductor and MEMS fabrication."},{"id":"ba201151-9b30-4a72-8c7f-f78db4cd6901","l":"Platelets","d":3,"p":"356040c7-f8a2-4afa-a9e4-c6f22fd80281","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Platelets are small (\u22482\u20134 \u03bcm), anuclear cytoplasmic fragments shed from bone marrow megakaryocytes that circulate in blood and mediate hemostasis by adhering to vascular injury sites, aggregating, and releasing clotting and signaling molecules to form and stabilize blood clots. They typically circulate for about 7\u201310 days before being removed."},{"id":"fb9f2062-4e41-440f-9057-f75d55f1182c","l":"Pnictides","d":3,"p":"2a71cbba-3674-42b1-8778-4b794f85a844","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Pnictides are compounds that contain one or more pnictogen elements (group 15: N, P, As, Sb, Bi). In condensed-matter physics the term most often refers to layered iron-pnictide materials (e.g., FeAs-based) that exhibit magnetic ordering and unconventional superconductivity."},{"id":"af1fa8f5-5fde-4d5b-a199-679652c2580b","l":"Polarization-dependent photoemission spectroscopy","d":3,"p":"40082853-5a77-4bce-bb7b-a6b598d04d93","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Polarization-dependent photoemission spectroscopy is a photoemission technique in which the polarization of the incident photons is varied while measuring the energy and angular distribution of emitted electrons to map the electronic structure. Because photoemission intensities follow matrix-element selection rules that depend on light polarization and orbital symmetry, this method selectively reveals the orbital character, symmetry properties, and related textures of electronic states."},{"id":"ef122523-2bad-4ba6-9ab6-eb30a6e88101","l":"Polarized optical microscopy","d":3,"p":"a40cc113-e132-471a-bb1b-2d29d8248a64","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Polarized optical microscopy is an optical imaging technique that illuminates a sample with polarized light and analyzes the light after it passes through (or is reflected by) the sample using a polarizer and analyzer to detect changes in polarization. These polarization changes\u2014caused by birefringence or anisotropic structure\u2014produce contrast and interference colors that reveal crystallographic orientation, internal stress, molecular ordering, and other anisotropic properties."},{"id":"7c56deec-3d56-4312-ad9b-e175e5daf3a5","l":"Potts model","d":3,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Networks","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Potts model is a lattice spin model that generalizes the Ising model by assigning each site one of q discrete states and giving neighboring sites a lower energy when they share the same state (typically \u2212J for equal states and 0 otherwise). It is a paradigmatic model of statistical mechanics that exhibits diverse phase transitions and critical behavior (reducing to the Ising model at q=2 and relating to percolation in the q\u21921 limit)."},{"id":"827fdf24-fbdc-46eb-8b8e-7141a15e13e1","l":"Prion diseases","d":3,"p":"d6072ccd-5d49-4a8c-a032-efa240964575","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Prion diseases are fatal neurodegenerative disorders caused by an abnormally folded form of the prion protein (PrP) that templates conversion of the normal cellular PrP into the misfolded, aggregation-prone form, leading to progressive brain damage. They are unusual infectious diseases composed solely of protein (no nucleic acid), often have long incubation times, and include Creutzfeldt\u2013Jakob disease, kuru, and bovine spongiform encephalopathy."},{"id":"fab04dd2-7adb-4dc9-a932-ea5eebb5c91a","l":"Prisoner's Dilemma","d":3,"p":"3cbbf1f7-9427-4812-a34b-410f229c0987","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"The Prisoner's Dilemma is a two-player game-theory model in which each player can cooperate or defect, and the payoffs are arranged so that defection is a dominant strategy for each player but the mutual-defection outcome is worse for both than mutual cooperation. It illustrates the tension between individual rationality (the Nash equilibrium of mutual defection) and collective optimality (the Pareto-superior outcome of mutual cooperation)."},{"id":"75a0accd-d320-406e-9ffc-d90a2be278ba","l":"Protein-ligand interactions","d":3,"p":"b9a7848b-4a90-4912-94b2-4d9c05b0a736","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Protein-ligand interactions are the reversible binding of a small molecule (ligand) to a specific site on a protein, mediated by complementary shape and noncovalent forces (hydrogen bonds, electrostatics, van der Waals, and the hydrophobic effect). Their behavior is characterized by thermodynamic affinity (\u0394G, Kd) and kinetic on/off rates, often coupled to protein conformational changes that determine specificity and biological function."},{"id":"840f005d-7faa-4f5f-8322-ecbcbe03fb65","l":"Protein-membrane interactions","d":3,"p":"b9a7848b-4a90-4912-94b2-4d9c05b0a736","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Protein-membrane interactions are the physical processes by which proteins adsorb onto, insert into, or scaffold and remodel lipid bilayers. They are governed by hydrophobic and electrostatic forces, specific molecular recognition, and membrane mechanics (elasticity, curvature, lateral diffusion), and thereby control protein localization, membrane shape, transport, and signaling."},{"id":"39253822-1d1f-4d80-941a-ad2502ea849f","l":"Protein-protein interactions","d":3,"p":"b9a7848b-4a90-4912-94b2-4d9c05b0a736","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Protein-protein interactions are the specific physical contacts and associations between two or more protein molecules, formed by complementary shapes and noncovalent forces (hydrogen bonds, electrostatics, van der Waals forces, and the hydrophobic effect). They produce transient or stable complexes that are characterized by binding affinity, kinetics, and structural complementarity and underlie most cellular processes."},{"id":"a9d32435-4a66-44c2-b501-fbc8e8321e13","l":"Protein-solvent interactions","d":3,"p":"b9a7848b-4a90-4912-94b2-4d9c05b0a736","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Protein-solvent interactions are the physical forces and energetic effects between a protein and the surrounding solvent molecules (usually water and dissolved ions) that determine the organization and dynamics of the solvation shell. They encompass hydrogen bonding, electrostatic screening, van der Waals and hydrophobic forces and associated entropic contributions, and thereby control protein folding, stability, conformational dynamics, and reaction kinetics."},{"id":"c03fd525-1198-4149-911b-a12d8125ff05","l":"Protein structure","d":3,"p":"6e1bbe22-c6fb-42b5-8ff6-1f61db13fdc4","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Protein structure is the three-dimensional arrangement of amino acid residues in a polypeptide, described at hierarchical levels\u2014primary (sequence), secondary (\u03b1\u2011helices and \u03b2\u2011sheets), tertiary (overall fold), and quaternary (assembly of subunits)\u2014and stabilized by covalent bonds and noncovalent interactions (hydrogen bonds, ionic interactions, van der Waals forces, and the hydrophobic effect). The native folded structure is a low\u2013free\u2011energy ensemble that determines the protein\u2019s functional properties and dynamics."},{"id":"5c324065-94d1-48e6-acfa-f9dfa7f13704","l":"Protein targeting","d":3,"p":"deef9592-7e3e-4949-9bca-ffa9fa9d1e46","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Protein targeting is the cellular process that directs newly synthesized proteins to their correct subcellular destinations\u2014such as the nucleus, mitochondria, endoplasmic reticulum, membranes, or secretion\u2014so they can perform their functions. It is mediated by short amino\u2011acid signal sequences and recognition factors that guide proteins via co\u2011 or post\u2011translational translocation across membranes, vesicular trafficking, and chaperone\u2011assisted delivery."},{"id":"c63c569f-2139-458f-b7f5-2aaf22340fd6","l":"Proton-exchange membrane fuel cells","d":3,"p":"9d3ec67b-01ed-4ebe-8b7f-04613a3d5185","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Proton-exchange membrane fuel cells (PEMFCs) are electrochemical devices that convert hydrogen and oxygen directly into electricity, water, and heat by oxidizing hydrogen at an anode and reducing oxygen at a cathode, with electrons flowing through an external circuit. A solid polymer electrolyte (the proton-exchange membrane) selectively conducts protons from anode to cathode while blocking electrons and gases, enabling low-temperature, high-power-density operation with catalyst-coated electrodes."},{"id":"7726d58b-3c3a-4fcf-bdaa-c13eb8dc154d","l":"Pulse characterization","d":3,"p":"c35dddca-b514-4561-954c-e3b4ad2f1912","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Pulse characterization is the set of measurements and analysis methods used to determine a transient pulse's properties\u2014primarily its temporal and spectral amplitude and phase, and often its spatial profile, polarization, energy and timing. For example, techniques such as autocorrelation, frequency-resolved optical gating (FROG) and SPIDER reconstruct a pulse's waveform, duration and chirp."},{"id":"e182c548-24f2-45a8-99ce-816acead5bf5","l":"Pump-probe spectroscopy","d":3,"p":"dedc6dbe-3651-4ad0-9d37-b260e8975adc","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Pump\u2013probe spectroscopy is a time-resolved technique in which an initial \"pump\" pulse excites a sample and a time-delayed \"probe\" pulse measures its transient response, with the delay scanned to map the evolution of excited-state populations, coherences, or structural changes. By recording changes in absorption, reflectivity, emission, photoemission, or scattering as a function of delay, it reveals ultrafast dynamical processes (e.g., relaxation, energy transfer, carrier dynamics) on femtosecond\u2013nanosecond timescales."},{"id":"ad026de2-595a-4710-b48e-6ac5781b929c","l":"p-wave","d":3,"p":"91ed8eb4-208e-4fd8-a258-aeae8213de49","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A p-wave is a quantum-mechanical wave or partial-wave component with orbital angular momentum quantum number l = 1, whose angular dependence is described by the spherical harmonics Y1,m (m = \u22121, 0, 1) and which has odd spatial parity. It appears as atomic p orbitals, the l = 1 channel in scattering, and as odd-parity (l = 1) pairing symmetry in superconductors."},{"id":"33098fcf-ee2f-4639-a1ac-62461df9d808","l":"Quantum algorithms for chemical calculations","d":3,"p":"32c9b7ae-6182-4ee6-96a1-2f51b3b65b3a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum algorithms for chemical calculations are computational procedures run on quantum processors that encode many-body molecular wavefunctions into qubits and exploit superposition and entanglement to simulate electronic structure, reaction dynamics, and molecular properties with potentially greater efficiency than classical methods. Examples include quantum phase estimation for high-precision energy eigenvalues and variational quantum eigensolvers tailored for noisy, near-term devices."},{"id":"ce77e5ab-2a5f-47c0-8888-64728f10f637","l":"Quantum effects in biological systems","d":3,"p":"7b165f78-b636-495d-aea2-01023f34a353","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Quantum effects in biological systems are instances where intrinsically quantum-mechanical behaviors\u2014such as coherence, tunneling, superposition, entanglement, or discrete energy levels\u2014directly influence the structure, dynamics, or function of biomolecules and cellular processes. These effects can enable or modulate phenomena like electron/proton transfer, exciton transport in photosynthesis, and spin-dependent magnetoreception, often occurring transiently despite warm, noisy biological environments."},{"id":"4512bef4-205b-4ea8-95ed-06601b9e1e87","l":"Quantum information with atoms & light","d":3,"p":"6e1c9aa8-2e35-4fcf-ba26-5fcc93a4aed4","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum information with atoms & light studies the use of atoms (or atomic ensembles) and photons to encode, process, store and transmit quantum information. It exploits coherent light\u2013matter interactions (e.g., trapped ions/atoms, cavity QED, atomic ensembles and single photons) to generate and distribute entanglement, implement quantum gates, and realize quantum memories and networks."},{"id":"be565264-cf45-45a5-b885-b3ab66a6de88","l":"Quantum information with hybrid systems","d":3,"p":"6e1c9aa8-2e35-4fcf-ba26-5fcc93a4aed4","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum information with hybrid systems is the study and use of heterogeneous quantum platforms\u2014e.g., discrete qubits and continuous-variable modes, photons and spins, or superconducting circuits coupled to mechanical resonators\u2014tied together to store, process, and transmit quantum information. By interfacing different physical degrees of freedom and employing quantum transduction and entanglement between them, hybrid architectures combine complementary strengths (long coherence, fast control, good communication) for sensing, computation, and networking."},{"id":"493f057a-0e3b-4ada-b5df-7b6833605ce8","l":"Quantum information with solid state qubits","d":3,"p":"6e1c9aa8-2e35-4fcf-ba26-5fcc93a4aed4","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum information with solid-state qubits uses quantum two-level systems encoded in condensed-matter devices (e.g., superconducting circuits, semiconductor or donor spins, NV centers, and topological anyons) to store, process, and transmit quantum information. These platforms enable chip-scale integration and fast control but require materials engineering and error correction to mitigate decoherence and noise from the solid-state environment."},{"id":"59043017-efdd-4608-9c12-dbccbcc0719a","l":"Quantum information with trapped ions","d":3,"p":"6e1c9aa8-2e35-4fcf-ba26-5fcc93a4aed4","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum information with trapped ions is the use of individual atomic ions confined in electromagnetic traps as qubits, with quantum information encoded in their internal electronic or hyperfine states. Coherent control and entangling gates are implemented with laser or microwave-driven transitions that couple through the ions' shared motional modes, enabling high-fidelity quantum computation, error correction, and quantum simulation."},{"id":"489ff58d-a79f-416b-9919-d0f017e21757","l":"Quantum macroscopicity","d":3,"p":"5de0faa9-608e-450b-8d04-5aa4219dad8d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum macroscopicity is a quantitative measure of how \"macroscopic\" and nonclassical a quantum state or experiment is \u2014 i.e., the extent to which quantum superposition, coherence, or entanglement involve large numbers of particles, large mass/size, or macroscopically distinct outcomes. Different proposed measures operationalize this by counting effective particle number or mass, by the distinguishability of superposed branches, or by the state's sensitivity to decoherence and classicalizing noise."},{"id":"82fdaa19-9dec-4512-9d89-82cb9877d0aa","l":"Quantum metric","d":3,"p":"1d626944-7dd8-4abd-8400-1e1af764ab46","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The quantum metric (or quantum metric tensor) is a Riemannian metric on the manifold of quantum states that quantifies the infinitesimal distance (state distinguishability or fidelity susceptibility) between nearby states as system parameters are varied; mathematically it is the real part of the quantum geometric tensor (whose imaginary part is the Berry curvature). It captures how rapidly a state changes with parameters and appears in physical contexts such as quantum phase transitions, topology, and geometric contributions to transport (e.g., superfluid weight)."},{"id":"010a2a45-bfe3-4616-a72e-d9ac5e37ce9a","l":"Quantum nondemolition measurement","d":3,"p":"f464ad01-629c-41f3-98bc-195c920e4d42","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"A quantum nondemolition (QND) measurement is one that extracts information about a particular observable without perturbing its future evolution, so repeated measurements yield the same outcome aside from technical noise. Technically, it requires the measured operator to commute with the system-plus-measurement interaction (or for O(t1) and O(t2) to commute), so the measurement back-action is transferred to conjugate variables rather than the measured quantity."},{"id":"d8bba384-a60a-468e-8249-ca70f634548f","l":"Quantum superposition","d":3,"p":"5de0faa9-608e-450b-8d04-5aa4219dad8d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Quantum superposition is the principle that a quantum system can exist in a linear combination of two or more basis states at once, described by a wavefunction whose complex amplitudes encode both probabilities and relative phases. Upon measurement the system is found in one of the component states, with the probability given by the squared magnitude of that state's amplitude."},{"id":"04a58101-3e56-4101-b228-c5af6d059ef7","l":"Quasi elastic neutron scattering","d":3,"p":"c74c7b81-5dee-4820-a500-6ab6f1b48cb1","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Quasi-elastic neutron scattering (QENS) is a neutron spectroscopy technique that detects small, continuous energy transfers\u2014seen as a broadening of the elastic peak\u2014caused by stochastic motions such as molecular translations, rotations, and relaxations. By analyzing the energy and momentum dependence of that broadening (typically \u03bceV\u2013meV energy range, pico\u2013nanosecond time scales and \u00e5ngstr\u00f6m length scales), QENS yields microscopic diffusion coefficients and relaxation times."},{"id":"243ce9ed-ba9b-4fab-bb55-bc370e3ac7e4","l":"Radar","d":3,"p":"70d9878d-c239-4e9c-b8bb-237aeff0612e","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Radar (radio detection and ranging) is a remote-sensing system that transmits radio-frequency electromagnetic waves and analyzes their echoes\u2014using time delay for range, Doppler shift for velocity, and antenna directionality for angle\u2014to detect, locate, and characterize distant objects."},{"id":"08498102-3cae-47c5-974a-166a14d5ea94","l":"Radiative neutrino mass models","d":3,"p":"47a1bb2e-c1ae-4b2c-a48d-b75767137d82","fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Radiative neutrino mass models are extensions of the Standard Model in which neutrino masses arise only via quantum loop (radiative) processes rather than at tree level, enforced by new symmetries and particles that forbid direct mass terms. The loop suppression and heavy new states naturally produce tiny neutrino masses and often link neutrino mass generation to phenomena like dark matter, lepton-number violation, or observable collider signatures."},{"id":"dfadf91e-b07e-4081-9498-25b87e6d5eb9","l":"Random field spin models","d":3,"p":"6ff84248-428b-4704-ae9f-e55346a4ce60","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Random-field spin models are lattice spin systems (e.g., Ising, XY, Heisenberg) in which each spin feels, besides exchange interactions, a quenched (frozen-in) spatially varying random external field that breaks symmetry locally. They are used to study how frozen disorder alters magnetic ordering, domain formation and critical behavior\u2014e.g., the random-field Ising model exhibits glassy energy landscapes and can suppress long-range order in low dimensions (Imry\u2013Ma effect)."},{"id":"efcb74a9-574f-44cb-ae57-4b9a1cec44df","l":"Random phase approximation","d":3,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Random phase approximation (RPA) is a self-consistent mean-field method for the linear response of interacting many-body systems that approximates the dielectric (or susceptibility) by summing an infinite series of noninteracting particle\u2013hole polarization \"bubble\" diagrams, thereby capturing long-range screening and collective modes (e.g., plasmons) while neglecting exchange and short-range correlations. Physically it corresponds to assuming the phases of different excitations are uncorrelated so their interference averages to zero."},{"id":"182426dd-9bb6-4597-b803-11946b863972","l":"Red blood cells","d":3,"p":"356040c7-f8a2-4afa-a9e4-c6f22fd80281","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Red blood cells (erythrocytes) are ~6\u20138 \u03bcm biconcave, nucleus-free blood cells that transport oxygen in vertebrates by binding it to hemoglobin. Their membrane, cytoskeleton and viscous interior make them highly deformable, determining their mechanical behavior, flow (rheology) and oxygen delivery in the microcirculation."},{"id":"9cff1080-b63e-4bf7-94dc-5f3031babef7","l":"Reflection high-energy electron diffraction","d":3,"p":"d1402816-03a5-41ce-adad-a9f621e3ec0f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Reflection high-energy electron diffraction (RHEED) is a surface-sensitive technique in which a beam of high-energy electrons strikes a crystalline surface at a grazing angle and the diffracted/reflected electrons produce a characteristic pattern that reveals the surface crystallography, morphology, and growth dynamics. It is widely used for in situ monitoring of thin-film deposition (e.g., molecular beam epitaxy), where changes in streaks, spots, and intensity oscillations indicate surface ordering and layer-by-layer growth."},{"id":"79f4c730-68b7-4a82-b303-e8a001287704","l":"Relaxor ferroelectrics","d":3,"p":"6e6a0326-86c9-49f2-a044-8ee3ca248ee3","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Relaxor ferroelectrics are disordered ferroelectric materials that do not form conventional long-range polar order but instead show a broad, frequency-dependent peak in dielectric permittivity and slow, glasslike polarization dynamics. Their behavior is attributed to nanoscale polar regions embedded in a chemically/structurally disordered matrix with strong random fields, producing exceptionally large dielectric and electromechanical responses."},{"id":"764cff67-9726-441a-a165-e8baeb7f1cc6","l":"Remote sensing","d":3,"p":"552fbc40-d35a-4aa2-b667-693eb2816579","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Remote sensing is the measurement of objects or surfaces from a distance by detecting and analyzing energy (usually electromagnetic radiation, or active signals like radar/lidar) that they emit, reflect, or scatter. By interpreting these remotely measured signals from platforms such as satellites, aircraft, or ground sensors, one can infer physical, chemical, and structural properties of the target without direct contact."},{"id":"5d069596-e362-4ea5-92e2-f1aac4b37463","l":"Replication","d":3,"p":"deef9592-7e3e-4949-9bca-ffa9fa9d1e46","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Replication is the repetition of an experiment, observation, or simulation under the same or equivalent conditions to see whether the same results are obtained; successful replication by the original or independent groups establishes the reliability and robustness of a finding and helps rule out random errors, biases, or methodological flaws."},{"id":"e25d6300-d023-4190-a0c7-10873f94e494","l":"Resistive wall modes","d":3,"p":"3f71ccf5-16ff-425f-b7b3-4791f9503b59","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Resistive wall modes are global magnetohydrodynamic instabilities in toroidally confined plasmas that would be suppressed by a perfectly conducting surrounding wall but grow when the wall has finite electrical resistivity, which allows magnetic flux to diffuse through and the mode to evolve. They typically grow on the much slower resistive\u2011wall time scale (longer than the Alfv\u00e9n time) and are important for confinement control, often requiring plasma rotation or active feedback to stabilize."},{"id":"0e1491f9-ac6a-4d41-81e1-86d8a51dfa32","l":"Resistivity measurements","d":3,"p":"467f0788-e876-4bb8-964b-aae25db708e2","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Resistivity measurements are experimental techniques for determining a material's intrinsic electrical resistivity \u03c1 (\u03a9\u00b7m) by measuring the voltage produced by a known current and converting the measured resistance using the sample geometry. Common methods\u2014such as two- or four-point probe, van der Pauw, and impedance spectroscopy\u2014are chosen to minimize contact and lead resistances and to control temperature, frequency, or magnetic-field effects."},{"id":"ebb9014c-84db-40a2-bf87-dfe6f01d6c97","l":"Resonant elastic x-ray scattering","d":3,"p":"1d3e6a42-9d7e-4c5f-b0ef-c5fb489e8336","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Resonant elastic x-ray scattering is an x-ray diffraction technique in which the incident photon energy is tuned to a core-level absorption edge so that the elastic (no-energy-loss) scattering is resonantly enhanced by electronic transitions. This resonant enhancement provides element-, orbital- and electronic-state-specific contrast, enabling momentum-resolved probes of charge, orbital and magnetic order and symmetry in materials."},{"id":"c7e84b76-a41c-4f3d-8b50-0b7959c1b0d6","l":"Resonant photoemission spectroscopy","d":3,"p":"40082853-5a77-4bce-bb7b-a6b598d04d93","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Resonant photoemission spectroscopy is a photoelectron technique in which the photon energy is tuned to an atomic absorption edge so that a core-level excitation resonantly interferes with direct photoemission, enhancing and selectively probing emission from particular elements and orbitals via the intermediate core-excited state and its Auger-like decay. It therefore provides element- and orbital-specific information about valence electronic structure, hybridization, and many-body effects such as satellites and multiplet splitting."},{"id":"9aa7b2d0-6514-41db-af3a-3307c284e110","l":"RNA","d":3,"p":"870d415a-ceb3-4e49-a475-798d07177a83","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"RNA (ribonucleic acid) is a biopolymer made of ribonucleotide monomers that carries genetic information and can perform catalytic and regulatory functions in cells. Its typically single\u2011stranded sequence folds into specific secondary and tertiary structures via hydrogen bonding and base stacking, making RNA a subject of study in thermodynamics, kinetics, and polymer/soft-matter physics."},{"id":"552ea8ca-3d9b-40a6-a22d-e2d6484c8880","l":"RNA-protein interactions","d":3,"p":"b9a7848b-4a90-4912-94b2-4d9c05b0a736","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"RNA\u2013protein interactions are noncovalent, sequence- and structure-dependent contacts between RNA molecules and proteins that assemble into functional ribonucleoprotein complexes. They are governed by physical forces (electrostatics, hydrogen bonding, base stacking and van der Waals) and are characterized by thermodynamics and kinetics that determine binding affinity, specificity and conformational dynamics."},{"id":"770e0a9d-99a8-4cbc-96b6-664b85361aaa","l":"RNA structure","d":3,"p":"9fe8a5bc-b93d-46bc-9faa-208a23f0d923","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"RNA structure is the specific spatial arrangement of a ribonucleic acid molecule determined by its nucleotide sequence and by intramolecular base pairing and stacking, organized hierarchically into primary (sequence), secondary (stems, loops, hairpins) and tertiary (3D folds) motifs. These structures are stabilized by hydrogen bonds, base stacking, and electrostatic/ion-mediated interactions, are thermally and kinetically dynamic, and largely determine the molecule's biochemical function."},{"id":"3e36b888-fcd3-4e7f-b96e-2b9eb0632515","l":"Ruddlesden-Popper compounds","d":3,"p":"f28f4f1e-8eef-4fce-8661-07f5751ad90a","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Ruddlesden\u2013Popper compounds are layered oxide materials with general formula A_{n+1}B_nO_{3n+1}, built from n contiguous perovskite (ABO3) layers separated by extra rock-salt AO sheets. Their quasi-two-dimensional stacking produces tunable electronic, magnetic, and structural properties (dependent on n) important for superconductivity, magnetism, ferroelectricity, and catalysis."},{"id":"7b8f181e-b12f-41e6-af8c-516ac0bb478b","l":"Rutherford back scattering spectroscopy","d":3,"p":"1fc8f216-e853-4b59-bdb2-2ac001bd3ea2","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Rutherford backscattering spectroscopy (RBS) is an ion-beam analysis technique in which energetic light ions (typically 1\u20133 MeV He+) are directed at a sample and the energy spectrum of ions scattered back toward the detector is measured. From the elastic Coulomb (Rutherford) scattering kinematics and cross sections, the backscattered energy and yield give quantitative information on elemental composition and depth profiles near the surface."},{"id":"9668bf9a-2bc4-4ba2-8f18-94381c11aa71","l":"Satellite communication networks","d":3,"p":"551a7b72-2a25-49e4-be10-843849f6987d","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Satellite communication networks are systems of orbiting satellites and ground terminals that exchange information via directed radio or microwave electromagnetic links, forming a routed network in space and on the ground. Their performance is governed by orbital dynamics and geometry (line-of-sight, Doppler), finite light-speed latency, free-space path loss and atmospheric effects, and is optimized using antenna beamforming, frequency allocation, modulation and coding to control bandwidth, interference, and reliability."},{"id":"4d734e8f-2c3b-42a0-a4cb-8f453aeae99d","l":"Scanning electron microscopy","d":3,"p":"a0889633-1e24-48ce-80d9-98b0139e10cb","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) is an imaging technique in which a tightly focused beam of electrons is raster-scanned across a specimen and the electrons and other signals emitted by the sample are detected to form high-resolution images. By collecting secondary and backscattered electrons (and often X-rays), SEM reveals surface topography, composition contrasts, and other local properties with much finer spatial resolution than optical microscopy."},{"id":"d4663f7e-ae3d-43d2-82bf-e55638fa4922","l":"Scanning near-field optical microscopy","d":3,"p":"bb0a871d-bf5c-44db-be68-9bd8ed4febe9","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Scanning near-field optical microscopy (SNOM) is a scanning-probe technique that achieves optical resolution beyond the diffraction limit by bringing a nanoscopic probe or subwavelength aperture within a few to tens of nanometers of the sample to excite and collect evanescent (near-field) optical fields. It maps local optical properties (intensity, phase, spectrum, polarization) at subwavelength spatial scales."},{"id":"2344c13c-dcdb-44f5-afad-7c4f273664da","l":"Scanning transmission electron microscopy","d":3,"p":"34773509-53cc-4885-a027-7bd3497f7157","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Scanning transmission electron microscopy (STEM) is an electron-microscopy technique in which a finely focused electron probe is scanned across a thin specimen while transmitted electrons are collected by one or more detectors to form images. By combining different detectors (bright-field, annular dark-field/Z-contrast) and spectrometers (EELS, EDX), STEM provides high\u2013/atomic-scale structural and compositional mapping."},{"id":"0a0f9e2d-cd3c-4d96-9ece-21f4988fdc80","l":"Scission point","d":3,"p":"6080ec71-e7c6-45fd-9184-60285aafae40","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics","Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Scission point (in nuclear fission) is the configuration along the fission pathway at which a deforming compound nucleus actually splits into two (or more) separate fragments \u2014 effectively the moment the connecting neck vanishes. It marks the transition from a single nucleus to distinct fragments and strongly influences the fragments' mass, charge, kinetic energies, and excitation."},{"id":"993ee86e-3933-4d04-b955-1f637c224ed1","l":"Secondary ion mass spectrometry","d":3,"p":"38b065c4-10d9-4e16-8ace-63d1eb093d28","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Secondary ion mass spectrometry (SIMS) is a surface-analysis technique in which a focused primary ion beam sputters atoms and molecules from a sample surface, producing ionized fragments (\"secondary ions\"). Those secondary ions are collected and analyzed in a mass spectrometer (e.g., time-of-flight or magnetic sector) to determine elemental and isotopic composition and depth profiles with high sensitivity and spatial resolution."},{"id":"041382ec-7a44-4ad6-a175-9f0d194171c2","l":"Secondary structure","d":3,"p":"9fe8a5bc-b93d-46bc-9faa-208a23f0d923","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Secondary structure is the local, regularly repeating arrangement of a polymer or polypeptide backbone\u2014such as \u03b1\u2011helices, \u03b2\u2011sheets, and turns\u2014stabilized mainly by hydrogen bonds between backbone atoms. It describes structural motifs distinct from the primary sequence and the overall tertiary fold."},{"id":"52614c10-d121-4b01-95f4-52a63fb3e9d7","l":"Seesaw mechanism","d":3,"p":"703e2e58-467b-463a-949c-c451ad620e52","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"The seesaw mechanism is a framework in which very small masses for light particles arise because they mix with much heavier states, so that diagonalizing the mass matrix produces a light eigenvalue suppressed roughly by the ratio (light\u2013heavy mixing)^2/ heavy mass. A canonical example is the Type I seesaw for neutrinos, where light left-handed neutrino masses are \u2248 m_D^2 / M_R when their Dirac mass m_D mixes with heavy right\u2011handed Majorana masses M_R."},{"id":"b83bd293-b7e7-4c2d-b03e-d09e5e617662","l":"Self-healing systems","d":3,"p":"ef41b9c0-c5a2-4728-9a87-d114abfb3659","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Self-healing systems are physical systems that autonomously detect damage or faults and restore their original structure or function without external intervention. They achieve this via internal repair mechanisms such as reversible bonds, transport of healing agents, mechanical reconfiguration, redundancy, or feedback control to recover performance."},{"id":"2fbd3cb2-47c6-42c7-8561-e8b7acc44c65","l":"Self-propelled janus particles","d":3,"p":"36e1d549-9909-4881-a2d8-0a501a300a26","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Self-propelled Janus particles are colloidal \u201ctwo-faced\u201d particles whose surfaces have different chemical or physical properties on each side, causing asymmetric interactions with their surroundings. By locally converting energy (e.g., from chemical reactions, light, or heat) into surface gradients or flows (phoretic, electrophoretic, or bubble recoil), they autonomously propel themselves and serve as simple active-matter units driven out of equilibrium."},{"id":"6ccf54a5-f729-4aef-9a24-1bac1b45bf3e","l":"Semiclassical methods","d":3,"p":"b96dac97-ab85-4320-892d-9b245caf097f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Nonlinear Dynamics","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics"],"def":"Semiclassical methods are approximation schemes that bridge quantum and classical physics by either treating some degrees of freedom classically while keeping others quantum, or by asymptotically expanding full quantum mechanics in powers of \u0127 so that quantum amplitudes are expressed in terms of classical trajectories and actions. They yield leading-order quantum behavior and corrections (e.g., WKB, stationary-phase/path-integral, Van Vleck/Gutzwiller/EBK approaches) and are valid when typical actions are large compared with \u0127."},{"id":"095b6ba0-9ab0-479a-8120-4ebc9468fb0a","l":"Semiconductor lasers","d":3,"p":"371ef245-60f3-45d9-b265-ed7abd84f07f","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Semiconductor lasers are electrically driven lasers in which carrier injection into a semiconductor gain region (typically a p\u2013n junction or heterostructure) creates a population inversion and stimulated emission, with optical feedback from a cavity producing coherent laser output. Their emission wavelength is set by the semiconductor bandgap, and common forms include edge-emitting diode lasers and vertical-cavity surface-emitting lasers (VCSELs)."},{"id":"efb1e897-88b5-44d0-9835-78c50ca405cb","l":"Shape-memory materials","d":3,"p":"ef41b9c0-c5a2-4728-9a87-d114abfb3659","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Shape-memory materials are solids that can be plastically deformed and later recover a preset shape when triggered (usually by heating) because they undergo a reversible, diffusionless solid\u2011state phase transformation. In metals (e.g., NiTi) this is a martensitic\u2194austenitic transformation that also produces superelastic behavior, while polymers show analogous thermally or stimulus\u2011driven mechanisms."},{"id":"ae966583-9c5e-42e2-bd58-52c86269c389","l":"Signal transduction","d":3,"p":"2d827c68-8a43-4743-a8a3-7254ad3abafe","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Signal transduction is the process by which a cell detects an external or internal stimulus and converts it into a specific biochemical response via receptors, second messengers, and downstream molecular cascades that encode, amplify, and integrate information. Physically, it involves conformational changes, diffusion, phosphorylation and transport that transmit signals across molecular and cellular length- and time-scales."},{"id":"0954c75e-fd16-4bbb-a676-4bc700186930","l":"Silicon vacancies","d":3,"p":"39f369f1-f48a-4dce-858c-8337d41ec94d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Silicon vacancies are intrinsic point defects in crystalline silicon where a silicon atom is missing from its lattice site. They introduce localized electronic states in the band gap, act as charge traps and recombination centers, can migrate at elevated temperatures, and form complexes with impurities that strongly affect silicon's electrical and optical properties."},{"id":"97ac4ade-dfd3-4233-b066-e971a55c97f5","l":"Single-electron transistors","d":3,"p":"ce85a590-748b-47e4-a10b-a25c874f3bdf","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A single-electron transistor (SET) is a three-terminal nanodevice in which a small conducting island is connected to source and drain by tunnel junctions and a gate electrode controls the island potential so that current flows by discrete single-electron tunneling events governed by Coulomb blockade. Operation requires the island's charging energy to exceed thermal energy (hence typically low temperatures), enabling quantized charge transfer useful for ultrasensitive electrometry and single-electron control."},{"id":"2fadabca-7c58-48a1-af05-25bdd7251e30","l":"SIR model","d":3,"p":"a781d742-17e9-49c2-8abf-87e01638317f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"The SIR model is a compartmental epidemiological model that divides a fixed population into Susceptible (S), Infected (I), and Recovered (R) groups and uses coupled ordinary differential equations to describe infection and recovery dynamics. Its behavior is set by the infection and recovery rates (\u03b2 and \u03b3), with the basic reproduction number R0 = \u03b2/\u03b3 determining whether an outbreak grows (R0>1) or dies out."},{"id":"79397a34-8875-466f-adbc-03d73de72680","l":"SIRS model","d":3,"p":"a781d742-17e9-49c2-8abf-87e01638317f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"The SIRS model is a compartmental epidemiological model that divides a population into Susceptible, Infected, and Recovered classes with recovered individuals losing immunity and returning to the susceptible class. It is typically described by coupled ODEs (or stochastic analogs) with transmission, recovery, and immunity\u2011loss rates and can produce endemic states or oscillatory outbreaks depending on parameters like the basic reproduction number."},{"id":"5ebcb77a-26bf-4834-81c8-82d85bcdc07a","l":"SIS model","d":3,"p":"a781d742-17e9-49c2-8abf-87e01638317f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"The SIS (Susceptible\u2013Infectious\u2013Susceptible) model is a simple epidemic model in which individuals cycle between susceptible and infectious states, with infected persons recovering back to the susceptible class (no lasting immunity). It is formulated as deterministic rate equations or stochastic processes (often on networks) and exhibits a threshold in transmission versus recovery rates that separates disease extinction from an endemic steady state."},{"id":"80109603-f480-4e31-bc31-ebd80ea1980f","l":"Skutterudites","d":3,"p":"6af5be88-489f-4a50-9abf-998e8912254a","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Skutterudites are a family of cubic, cage-like crystalline compounds based on the CoAs3 structure (typically MX3 with M a transition metal and X a pnictogen) that can host additional \"filler\" atoms in their voids. The ability of these cages to accommodate rattling atoms gives skutterudites unusually low lattice thermal conductivity and a wide range of electronic behaviors (useful in thermoelectrics, heavy-fermion physics, magnetism and superconductivity)."},{"id":"f1a3e49a-bcd2-4cd8-aa2d-6d4dbbeec303","l":"Slip boundary effects","d":3,"p":"0f0cd98d-5753-4e9a-99e9-068f5528eafe","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Slip boundary effects occur when a fluid does not satisfy the usual no-slip condition at a solid surface, so the fluid has a nonzero tangential velocity relative to the boundary; this is commonly modeled by a Navier slip condition that relates slip velocity to the local shear via a slip length. Such slip alters near-wall velocity profiles, shear stresses and drag, and becomes important in micro/nanofluidics, on hydrophobic or gas-covered surfaces, and in rarefied gases."},{"id":"5c08cdf4-5d6f-42f3-9b47-d45f6ea9ac79","l":"Sl(N) symmetry","d":3,"p":"543d3af2-a8bc-462b-9d72-753b6de0d8ce","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"SL(N) symmetry is invariance under the special linear group SL(N) of invertible N\u00d7N matrices with unit determinant, whose transformations preserve oriented volume in an N-dimensional vector space. Its Lie algebra sl(N) is the set of traceless N\u00d7N matrices, which generate the infinitesimal symmetry transformations."},{"id":"4d962a16-95f9-4b0e-8ef5-2c952d497a98","l":"Small angle neutron scattering","d":3,"p":"c74c7b81-5dee-4820-a500-6ab6f1b48cb1","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Small-angle neutron scattering (SANS) is an elastic neutron-scattering technique that measures scattered intensity at very small angles (small momentum transfer q) to determine the size, shape and spatial correlations of structures on roughly 1\u2013100 nm length scales. It exploits neutrons' sensitivity to nuclear and magnetic contrast and is widely used for soft matter, biological macromolecules, colloids, and magnetic materials."},{"id":"df4299d8-c6df-41d8-96f5-46e062fd5b05","l":"Small-angle x-ray scattering","d":3,"p":"1d3e6a42-9d7e-4c5f-b0ef-c5fb489e8336","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Small-angle x-ray scattering (SAXS) is an elastic scattering technique in which x-rays are measured at very small angles to probe electron-density inhomogeneities in a sample. The angular dependence of scattered intensity (as a function of scattering vector q) yields statistical information about the size, shape and spatial correlations of structures on roughly 1\u2013100 nm length scales."},{"id":"c88f2853-42f8-42dd-ad4e-a83fcb2eab3a","l":"Solar cells","d":3,"p":"e6586efa-1454-4ae2-9407-f4ca0d9322da","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Solar cells are semiconductor devices that convert sunlight directly into electrical energy via the photovoltaic effect: photons with energy above the material band gap create electron\u2013hole pairs which are separated by an internal electric field (e.g., at a p\u2013n junction), producing a DC current and voltage."},{"id":"6148df1d-df65-467c-9a90-427d326326e0","l":"Solid acid fuel cells","d":3,"p":"9d3ec67b-01ed-4ebe-8b7f-04613a3d5185","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Solid acid fuel cells are electrochemical devices that convert chemical energy to electricity using a solid acid (e.g., CsHSO4, CsH2PO4) as the proton-conducting electrolyte between anode and cathode. These materials undergo a superprotonic phase transition at intermediate temperatures (roughly 100\u2013250 \u00b0C) that creates a highly conductive hydrogen-bond network, enabling efficient proton transport and fuel-cell operation without liquid electrolytes."},{"id":"ca670454-d9fc-4383-a903-d40a6313bc02","l":"Solid oxide fuel cells","d":3,"p":"9d3ec67b-01ed-4ebe-8b7f-04613a3d5185","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Solid oxide fuel cells (SOFCs) are high-temperature (typically 600\u20131000 \u00b0C) electrochemical devices that convert chemical energy of a fuel directly into electricity using a dense ceramic oxide electrolyte (commonly yttria-stabilized zirconia) that conducts oxide ions (O2\u2212) between porous electrodes. Oxygen is reduced at the cathode to O2\u2212, which migrates through the electrolyte to oxidize fuel at the anode, producing heat, water/CO2 and an external electric current."},{"id":"4b58c664-c089-4304-8767-503856ad696e","l":"Solid-state refrigeration","d":3,"p":"e6586efa-1454-4ae2-9407-f4ca0d9322da","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Solid-state refrigeration is cooling produced by reversible thermodynamic effects in solids\u2014for example the thermoelectric (Peltier), magnetocaloric, electrocaloric, and elastocaloric effects\u2014where applied electric, magnetic fields or mechanical stress change a material's entropy and temperature without circulating fluids. It enables compact, low-maintenance cooling systems that avoid gaseous refrigerants, though practical performance depends on material properties and device design."},{"id":"f23576c3-4f74-4081-a875-4fd94b502760","l":"SO(N) symmetry","d":3,"p":"543d3af2-a8bc-462b-9d72-753b6de0d8ce","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"SO(N) symmetry is invariance under the special orthogonal group SO(N), the Lie group of N\u00d7N real matrices R with R^T R = I and det R = +1 that represent rotations preserving lengths and orientation in an N\u2011dimensional Euclidean space. In physics, an SO(N) symmetry of a system means its laws are unchanged by those rotations, typically implying conserved angular\u2011momentum\u2013type quantities via Noether\u2019s theorem."},{"id":"dadb5d96-fc29-4c93-8428-3335c7ad1b2a","l":"Sound detection","d":3,"p":"7cd899da-2ea4-4b7e-8da3-246542f85b57","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Sound detection is the sensing and measurement of acoustic (pressure) waves propagating in a medium, performed by transducers that register local pressure or particle-velocity fluctuations. Those transducers\u2014e.g., microphones or the ear\u2014convert the mechanical pressure variations into electrical or neural signals for analysis of amplitude, frequency, phase and direction."},{"id":"eaae9484-9863-4004-8f4b-8e5aebd837e7","l":"Sound discrimination","d":3,"p":"7cd899da-2ea4-4b7e-8da3-246542f85b57","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Sound discrimination is the ability to distinguish between different acoustic signals by detecting differences in their physical properties\u2014such as frequency (pitch), intensity (loudness), spectral content (timbre), timing, or spatial origin. It is quantified by thresholds like the just-noticeable difference (JND) for frequency or intensity, which set the smallest detectable change in a given acoustic parameter."},{"id":"a17c2d23-a811-47eb-8cdc-be0f3659b0a4","l":"Sound recognition","d":3,"p":"7cd899da-2ea4-4b7e-8da3-246542f85b57","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Sound recognition is the identification and classification of acoustic signals by analyzing their physical signatures\u2014frequency content, amplitude envelope, and temporal structure\u2014to infer the sound source or event. It is performed by extracting features from the waveform and matching them to templates or statistical/learning models using signal-processing and pattern-analysis techniques."},{"id":"35f5af50-0c0e-4d58-b5d6-4045b3f851c6","l":"Sound source perception","d":3,"p":"7cd899da-2ea4-4b7e-8da3-246542f85b57","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Sound source perception is the auditory process of inferring a sound-emitting object's location, distance, motion, and identity from the received acoustic signal by extracting physical cues such as interaural time and level differences, spectral filtering by the head and pinnae (HRTF), reverberation, and temporal structure. It links sound-propagation physics with neural and cognitive processing to reconstruct source properties from the waveform."},{"id":"03690979-d1b3-4d0c-b584-0a36b9458abe","l":"Sp(2N) symmetry","d":3,"p":"543d3af2-a8bc-462b-9d72-753b6de0d8ce","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Sp(2N) symmetry is invariance under the symplectic group Sp(2N), the Lie group of 2N\u00d72N linear transformations that preserve a fixed nondegenerate antisymmetric bilinear (symplectic) form. Its compact real form (often written USp(2N)) has rank N and dimension N(2N+1) and commonly appears in physics as a gauge or global symmetry with a 2N\u2011dimensional pseudo\u2011real fundamental representation."},{"id":"9de8f682-d6bf-473d-b38b-20640d20f0f3","l":"Spatial domain thermoreflectance","d":3,"p":"1709c324-0db6-4ba7-aff6-0d41456e5331","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Spatial-domain thermoreflectance is a noncontact optical technique that maps local surface temperature by measuring spatially resolved changes in a material's reflectivity (dR/dT) across the sample. It produces high-resolution temperature or heat-flux images by scanning or imaging the reflectivity response, often with modulation and lock-in detection for improved sensitivity."},{"id":"e0f89438-f8db-496b-9d86-6d5a9ca16c5a","l":"Spectro-temporal characterization","d":3,"p":"d3a4e127-e2a1-4d1b-9637-522e2bf75e99","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Spectro-temporal characterization is the measurement and analysis of a signal's simultaneous spectral (frequency) and temporal (time) properties\u2014including amplitude and phase as functions of both variables\u2014to fully describe its time\u2013frequency structure and any correlations between frequency and arrival time. It is used, for example, to reconstruct ultrafast optical pulses and other time-varying signals via time\u2013frequency representations (spectrograms) and specialized retrieval methods."},{"id":"95495fe1-ba9d-4e42-8ca8-f51a39cc5efd","l":"Speech aerodynamics","d":3,"p":"4bdb49fb-fff6-4c45-8de5-14a145d87803","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Speech aerodynamics is the study of the airflow and air-pressure patterns in the respiratory tract and vocal tract that produce and shape speech sounds. It examines how quantities like subglottal pressure, transglottal flow, and flow-induced turbulence interact with the vocal folds and articulators to create voicing, consonant bursts, and other speech phenomena."},{"id":"a43cf705-cc21-412c-aaaf-37db0439cb28","l":"Speech perception","d":3,"p":"4bdb49fb-fff6-4c45-8de5-14a145d87803","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Speech perception is the physical and neural process by which time-varying acoustic signals produced by speech are analyzed and decoded into phonetic and linguistic units. It relies on extraction of spectral and temporal cues (formant trajectories, pitch, amplitude envelope) and is shaped by auditory transduction and propagation effects such as noise, masking, and reverberation."},{"id":"5669f367-4b2b-4748-a109-99d4f1fa3883","l":"Speech processing","d":3,"p":"4bdb49fb-fff6-4c45-8de5-14a145d87803","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Speech processing is the study and application of physical and computational methods for generating, transmitting, recording, and analyzing human speech as an acoustic signal. It combines acoustics (vocal source and vocal-tract filter, wave propagation, noise) with signal-processing techniques (time\u2013frequency analysis, feature extraction, synthesis and recognition) to extract, transmit, modify, or interpret linguistic and paralinguistic information."},{"id":"8cf44df7-d351-4557-9654-fae28ed2fd03","l":"Speech production","d":3,"p":"4bdb49fb-fff6-4c45-8de5-14a145d87803","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Speech production is the physical process by which respiratory airflow is converted into time-varying acoustic waves by vibration of the vocal folds and dynamic shaping of the vocal tract. It is commonly modeled by source\u2013filter theory, with a glottal source (voicing) filtered by resonances of the vocal tract formed by articulators (tongue, lips, velum, jaw), under aerodynamic, acoustic, and neuromuscular control."},{"id":"c42b101f-8f96-49f4-87be-c43b0754aefe","l":"Spherical micelles","d":3,"p":"1f72b9f1-0dd7-454f-861d-6c5927dfb128","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Spherical micelles are roughly spherical aggregates of amphiphilic molecules (surfactants or block copolymers) in a solvent, with hydrophobic tails sequestered in an internal core and hydrophilic head groups exposed to the solvent. They form above a critical micelle concentration as a free\u2011energy\u2013minimizing structure driven by the hydrophobic effect and headgroup repulsion, with size set by molecular geometry (packing parameter) and solution conditions."},{"id":"9829c045-5615-4cdf-bfc7-43d62381ebca","l":"Spiking neuron models","d":3,"p":"3a68853d-8031-4222-9eb4-ca56627c40cd","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Spiking neuron models are dynamical mathematical models that describe a neuron's continuous membrane-potential evolution and the generation of discrete, stereotyped action potentials (spikes) when a threshold is crossed. They range from biophysically detailed descriptions of ion-channel kinetics (e.g., Hodgkin\u2013Huxley) to simplified effective rules (e.g., integrate-and-fire, FitzHugh\u2013Nagumo) and are used to study spike timing, coding, and network dynamics."},{"id":"f867ec69-9307-42b6-a242-bd9076e41398","l":"Spin-boson model","d":3,"p":"6ff84248-428b-4704-ae9f-e55346a4ce60","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The spin-boson model describes a quantum two-level system (spin-1/2 or qubit) coupled linearly to a continuum of harmonic oscillators that represent its bosonic environment, providing a minimal microscopic model of dissipation and decoherence. Its dynamics and steady states depend on the bath spectral density (Ohmic, sub-Ohmic, or super-Ohmic), and it is a paradigmatic model for environmental-induced relaxation, suppression of tunneling, and quantum phase transitions."},{"id":"595b37ed-b7d8-457e-be59-5224c6dead59","l":"Spin chains","d":3,"p":"6ff84248-428b-4704-ae9f-e55346a4ce60","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Spin chains are one-dimensional lattices of localized spins (magnetic moments) that interact through exchange couplings and are modeled by Hamiltonians such as the Heisenberg, Ising, or XY models. They serve as minimal platforms for studying quantum and classical magnetism, collective excitations (magnons, spinons), quantum phase transitions, and often exactly solvable (integrable) many-body behavior."},{"id":"47a722cd-0867-4ef2-9574-9e6bfc2206da","l":"Spin-dependent parton distribution functions","d":3,"p":"edc1099a-36c7-42d9-9364-842b964617bb","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Spin-dependent parton distribution functions are probability densities that describe how the momentum fraction x carried by a quark or gluon in a fast-moving hadron depends on the relative orientation of the parton\u2019s spin and the hadron\u2019s spin (often expressed as the difference between aligned and anti-aligned probabilities) and which evolve with the probe scale Q^2.  Specific examples are the helicity distributions (longitudinal spin correlation) and transversity distributions (transverse spin correlation), which enter the formulas for polarized high-energy scattering observables."},{"id":"6a67d9c3-a76a-4786-a418-0ecec4ba0b19","l":"Spin echo small angle neutron scattering","d":3,"p":"3ce0b7aa-0081-4dfb-9c30-a5335b38f150","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Spin-echo small-angle neutron scattering (SESANS) is a neutron scattering technique that uses controlled Larmor precession of neutron spins before and after a sample to encode scattering-angle information into the transmitted beam's spin-echo polarization. By measuring the polarization loss as a function of spin-echo length, SESANS directly yields real-space pair-correlation (projected) information about structures on length scales from tens of nanometers up to micrometers, extending conventional SANS range."},{"id":"67c26071-097d-4a29-94a0-aaf9aa61a706","l":"Spin Hall magnetoresistance","d":3,"p":"23e22a76-a9ba-4593-8d52-4e5c98c47af3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Spin Hall magnetoresistance is a magnetoresistance effect in bilayers of a heavy nonmagnetic metal and an adjacent magnetic material in which the metal's electrical resistance depends on the magnetization direction of the neighbor. It arises because a charge current in the metal generates a transverse spin accumulation via the spin Hall effect, and the amount of spin current absorbed or reflected at the interface (and converted back to charge by the inverse spin Hall effect) depends on the magnetization orientation, changing the measured resistance."},{"id":"c8bfbcdd-31b6-4898-923d-4e7f6b38bc21","l":"Spin ice","d":3,"p":"12590779-553a-4c0b-80ab-4798eefd261f","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Spin ice is a class of frustrated magnetic materials, usually with Ising-like moments on a pyrochlore lattice of corner-sharing tetrahedra, whose spins obey a local \"two-in, two-out\" rule analogous to the proton arrangement in water ice, giving a macroscopically degenerate ground state with residual entropy. Its elementary excitations behave like emergent, mobile magnetic monopoles."},{"id":"3fef8adb-a80a-489d-a165-e46a30aa4174","l":"Spin ladders","d":3,"p":"6ff84248-428b-4704-ae9f-e55346a4ce60","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Spin ladders are quasi-one-dimensional magnetic systems of spins arranged on parallel chains (\"legs\") coupled by transverse links (\"rungs\"), usually modeled by Heisenberg exchange interactions along legs and rungs. Their low-energy behavior depends on the number of legs\u2014odd-leg ladders behave like gapless 1D chains while even-leg ladders typically form a gapped spin-liquid of rung singlets\u2014making them key models for quantum magnetism and doped-correlated phases."},{"id":"e2ab085b-23f1-4b85-8449-9d7ae6cd2489","l":"Spin liquid","d":3,"p":"e3f70dab-8f32-4ec3-b856-de646d4ba194","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A quantum magnetic ground state in which interacting spins do not develop conventional long-range order even as temperature approaches zero, because strong quantum fluctuations (often aided by geometric frustration) produce a highly entangled, disordered state. Spin liquids frequently exhibit long-range entanglement, fractionalized quasiparticle excitations, and emergent gauge-like or topological properties instead of symmetry-breaking magnetic order."},{"id":"7d20bbff-9e45-461c-b603-22a527cf0b67","l":"Spin-orbit torque","d":3,"p":"f1c78ef6-4185-4a87-9ee6-a89e7ba783b6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Spin-orbit torque is the torque exerted on a ferromagnet\u2019s magnetization by spin currents or non-equilibrium spin accumulation produced via spin\u2013orbit coupling when a charge current flows in an adjacent layer or interface. It transfers angular momentum to the magnetization (via mechanisms such as the spin Hall or Rashba\u2013Edelstein effects) and can drive switching or coherent precession, typically with damping-like and field-like components."},{"id":"2af30043-7d0f-4b32-983b-97ff6a118f1c","l":"Spin Peltier effect","d":3,"p":"6950be70-706e-4cf6-99c8-bc9c0b5f8dc2","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Spin Peltier effect is the generation of a heat current (local heating or cooling) when a spin current\u2014a flow of spin angular momentum, often with little net charge\u2014 is injected into a magnetic material or across a ferromagnet/normal-metal interface. It is the thermoelectric reciprocal of the spin Seebeck effect, arising from spin-dependent Peltier coefficients and spin-dependent heat transport."},{"id":"4b310b66-f5c7-460e-b827-9b3888172aa2","l":"Spin-polarized scanning tunneling microscopy","d":3,"p":"c33a7211-ebcd-4336-917d-a75ecb1e50c4","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Spin-polarized scanning tunneling microscopy (SP-STM) is a scanning tunneling microscope technique that uses a magnetic, spin-polarized tip to detect spin-dependent tunneling currents, providing atomic-scale imaging and spectroscopy of local magnetic order and electronic spin polarization via contrast that depends on the relative tip\u2013sample magnetization."},{"id":"abbb2e9b-e4ac-48ab-a10a-113643dad34c","l":"Spin pumping","d":3,"p":"257ff504-8723-46c7-aea0-ec7d55a0dc6f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Spin pumping is the emission of a pure spin current from a magnet whose magnetization is driven to precess (for example by ferromagnetic resonance) into an adjacent nonmagnetic material, transferring angular momentum across the interface and thereby enhancing the magnet's damping. It is governed by the interfacial spin-mixing conductance and is often detected electrically via the inverse spin Hall effect in the adjacent layer."},{"id":"5db65708-48a5-4281-a032-6f878f763b07","l":"Spin-resolved photoemission spectroscopy","d":3,"p":"40082853-5a77-4bce-bb7b-a6b598d04d93","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Spin-resolved photoemission spectroscopy is an experimental technique in which photons eject electrons from a material and the energy, momentum, and spin polarization of the emitted electrons are measured, providing a spin-resolved map of the occupied electronic band structure. It is used to probe spin distributions of surface and bulk states and to study magnetism, spin\u2013orbit coupling, and topological electronic phases."},{"id":"8845d956-74f5-4743-adb9-bfff0fbf44e5","l":"Spin Seebeck effect","d":3,"p":"6950be70-706e-4cf6-99c8-bc9c0b5f8dc2","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Spin Seebeck effect: the generation of a spin current or spin accumulation in a magnetic material induced by an applied temperature gradient, driven by thermally excited magnons and spin-dependent carrier transport. It is typically detected by converting that spin flow to a measurable voltage via the inverse spin Hall effect in an adjacent nonmagnetic metal and differs from the ordinary Seebeck effect by transporting spin rather than net charge."},{"id":"d88f1794-8203-4531-bccd-b5bd36c0595f","l":"Spin spiral","d":3,"p":"2c94e9cb-6047-4621-816e-26add1b9dbe3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A spin spiral is a noncollinear magnetic structure in which the direction of local magnetic moments rotates continuously and periodically in space with a fixed pitch and propagation wavevector, forming a helical or cycloidal modulation. Such spirals typically arise from competing symmetric exchange interactions or from antisymmetric Dzyaloshinskii\u2013Moriya coupling; if the rotation axis is parallel to the propagation direction it is a proper screw (helical) spiral, while if it is perpendicular it is cycloidal."},{"id":"b0b31456-f6d8-41fb-98e8-54b679b42090","l":"Spin torque nano-oscillators","d":3,"p":"03c374d7-5aca-44c9-ab5b-fbcf56ae0145","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Spin-torque nano-oscillators are nanoscale magnetic devices that convert a DC spin-polarized current into sustained, microwave-frequency precession of a ferromagnet\u2019s magnetization via spin-transfer or spin\u2013orbit torque. Implemented in magnetic multilayers (e.g., spin valves or magnetic tunnel junctions), they act as tunable, compact sources and detectors of GHz-range electrical signals."},{"id":"24440caa-2866-4cc0-a821-ac3e888c3d72","l":"Spin transfer torque","d":3,"p":"f1c78ef6-4185-4a87-9ee6-a89e7ba783b6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Spin-transfer torque is the torque exerted on a ferromagnet's local magnetization by a spin-polarized electric current, arising when conduction electrons transfer their spin angular momentum to the magnetic texture. It enables current-driven switching of magnetic layers and domain-wall motion in nanoscale spintronic devices without applied magnetic fields."},{"id":"f88780fa-d4e0-4199-90d2-e7d5eb2c13cc","l":"SQUID","d":3,"p":"a3cc729c-98ea-421a-ac1b-df399f62721d","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A SQUID (Superconducting Quantum Interference Device) is a superconducting loop containing one or two Josephson junctions that measures magnetic flux by using quantum interference of the superconducting wavefunction, producing a periodic voltage or current response with period equal to the flux quantum (\u03a60 = h/2e). It is an extremely sensitive magnetometer used to detect tiny magnetic fields and weak electromagnetic signals in applications from biomagnetism to fundamental quantum experiments."},{"id":"c5537a69-a43a-4730-a0fd-6cd84d71c61d","l":"Static light scattering","d":3,"p":"382676e6-fd8f-4fc6-b8e0-3abbc45c379c","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Static light scattering (SLS) is an experimental technique that measures the time\u2011averaged intensity of light scattered by a sample as a function of scattering angle (or scattering vector) to determine static properties such as molecular weight, radius of gyration, and particle form/structure factors. Unlike dynamic light scattering, SLS analyzes the steady-state scattered intensity rather than temporal fluctuations of that intensity."},{"id":"5e63b98a-fa35-4b3c-b569-cbe00e18cf9b","l":"Stimuli-responsive materials","d":3,"p":"ef41b9c0-c5a2-4728-9a87-d114abfb3659","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Stimuli-responsive materials are materials that undergo controlled, often reversible, changes in structure or physical properties (mechanical, optical, electrical, chemical) when exposed to external stimuli such as temperature, pH, light, electric or magnetic fields, mechanical stress, or chemical agents. These responses arise from mechanisms like phase transitions, molecular conformational changes, swelling/deswelling, ion transport, or electronic/ferroelectric switching, and are exploited in sensors, actuators, drug delivery, and adaptive systems."},{"id":"b33d5f76-2c72-4bd2-96e4-2b36a10395dc","l":"Storage rings","d":3,"p":"f53811b7-7f5c-46d6-ab05-ae38aa1a2ab6","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Accelerators & Beams"],"def":"Storage rings are circular accelerator structures that confine and circulate beams of charged particles (electrons, protons, or ions) for extended times using bending and focusing magnets together with RF cavities to maintain orbit and energy. They are used for colliders and continuous sources of synchrotron radiation, relying on ultra\u2011high vacuum, beam cooling, and feedback systems to preserve beam quality and lifetime."},{"id":"fff3cc47-e750-4de1-be7d-ea35a728c32e","l":"SU(N) symmetries","d":3,"p":"543d3af2-a8bc-462b-9d72-753b6de0d8ce","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"SU(N) symmetries are invariances under the special unitary group SU(N), the Lie group of N\u00d7N unitary matrices with determinant one (dimension N^2\u22121). In physics they act as internal (global or gauge) symmetry groups whose generators correspond to conserved charges via Noether\u2019s theorem and underlie examples like isospin SU(2) and color SU(3)."},{"id":"6beb8b63-e263-43d9-a1f6-2d9ddead05e5","l":"Superconducting qubits","d":3,"p":"844905af-98de-43dd-b6d8-a5792680fea4","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Superconducting qubits are quantum bits implemented in lithographically patterned superconducting circuits that use the nonlinearity of Josephson junctions to create anharmonic, macroscopic quantum energy levels that function as two-level systems. They operate at millikelvin temperatures and are manipulated and read out with microwave signals, forming a leading solid-state platform for quantum computing."},{"id":"9398f516-2afe-44ee-ab80-433065e21a57","l":"Superlattices","d":3,"p":"a0a856f1-da84-42ec-8a4f-6328d983a628","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Superlattices are artificially engineered, nanoscale periodic structures made by stacking alternating layers of different materials (typically semiconductors or dielectrics) so that carriers or waves see a new periodic potential. This engineered periodicity produces minibands, band gaps and altered electronic, optical and phononic transport properties compared with the bulk constituents."},{"id":"f143ca2d-b1d3-44bb-b092-ed0f6c617651","l":"Superradiance & subradiance","d":3,"p":"6efc577b-d290-4ed4-9279-77439ebc2d32","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"Superradiance is the collective, coherent emission from an ensemble of indistinguishable emitters in which constructive interference of their dipoles produces a much faster decay and enhanced peak intensity (in the ideal Dicke limit scaling \u221d N^2). Subradiance is the complementary effect where destructive interference produces long-lived, weakly radiating collective states with strongly suppressed decay rates."},{"id":"7afc8a46-4a16-4509-8db6-2bfa23521cc4","l":"Surface code quantum computing","d":3,"p":"f9456df5-5ca2-4a8a-8b11-d5ffd9d96921","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Surface code quantum computing is a fault-tolerant architecture that encodes logical qubits in a two-dimensional lattice of physical qubits and protects them by repeatedly performing local stabilizer (parity) measurements to detect and correct errors. It uses topological, nonlocal encoding (via extended logical operators, defects or boundaries) and local nearest-neighbor operations to realize high error thresholds and implement logical gates through code deformation, braiding of defects, or lattice surgery."},{"id":"06b3c81e-43b2-4057-ace2-fdc2602c74cb","l":"Surface magneto-optical Kerr effect","d":3,"p":"1c6daf36-c0ff-464f-963b-ac9673015bfc","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"The surface magneto-optical Kerr effect is the rotation and induced ellipticity of polarized light reflected from a magnetized material, produced by magnetization-dependent off-diagonal components of the dielectric tensor. It is inherently surface-sensitive (probing the top few nanometers) and is widely used to detect and image surface or thin-film magnetization, hysteresis, and domain structure."},{"id":"5bf19a3b-6683-440a-b0c2-d150377bff1f","l":"Survival strategies","d":3,"p":"aa6e6251-78da-4a3b-a270-cccc77b356e6","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Survival strategies are the behavioral, physiological or structural mechanisms by which organisms or dynamical systems increase their probability of persisting under stress or fluctuating environments. In physics-oriented treatments they are modeled and analyzed as stochastic optimization or evolutionary/game-theoretic processes balancing energy, information and risk (examples: dormancy, bet\u2011hedging, dispersal, repair)."},{"id":"8704dd9e-d470-40d1-990d-ecf6e51b0189","l":"s-wave","d":3,"p":"91ed8eb4-208e-4fd8-a258-aeae8213de49","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"An s-wave is a quantum state or partial wave with zero orbital angular momentum (l = 0), so its angular dependence is isotropic (spherically symmetric) and has no angular nodes. In contexts like scattering it is the lowest-order (most isotropic) contribution, and in superconductivity \"s-wave\" denotes an isotropic Cooper-pair wavefunction and energy gap."},{"id":"ab1e2db7-995a-4fbe-81e0-b759c39372d8","l":"Symmetry breaking states","d":3,"p":"5de0faa9-608e-450b-8d04-5aa4219dad8d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Symmetry-breaking states are configurations of a physical system that do not share the full symmetry of the underlying laws or Hamiltonian; in spontaneous symmetry breaking this occurs when symmetric equations admit multiple degenerate ground states and the system picks one, producing a nonzero order parameter and associated collective excitations."},{"id":"2e781078-85ac-45e5-a0ba-c67f431ef1fe","l":"Synaptic transmission model","d":3,"p":"3a68853d-8031-4222-9eb4-ca56627c40cd","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"A synaptic transmission model is a quantitative description of how an electrical signal in a presynaptic neuron is converted into a chemical signal at the synapse and back into an electrical response in the postsynaptic neuron. It represents processes such as vesicle release, neurotransmitter diffusion and receptor binding, and the resulting postsynaptic conductance and membrane potential changes, typically using kinetic rate equations, diffusion equations and conductance-based or stochastic formulations."},{"id":"eb6b3b96-d689-4c18-b145-8f78c4c4be08","l":"Tapping mode atomic force microscopy","d":3,"p":"f2622e6a-5f55-4f27-804c-aed3aebdbe02","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Tapping-mode atomic force microscopy is an AFM imaging technique where the cantilever is driven to oscillate near its resonance so the tip intermittently \"taps\" the sample surface; a feedback loop maintains a set oscillation amplitude to follow topography while minimizing lateral forces and sample damage. Changes in the oscillation amplitude and phase relative to the drive provide contrast sensitive to local mechanical and adhesive properties."},{"id":"f4de4acc-c72d-4cfb-83b1-67ec806083a0","l":"Tearing instability","d":3,"p":"3f71ccf5-16ff-425f-b7b3-4791f9503b59","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Tearing instability is an instability of a magnetized plasma current sheet in which small perturbations grow and cause magnetic field lines to \"tear\" and reconnect into magnetic islands (plasmoids), allowing rapid conversion of magnetic energy to kinetic and thermal energy. It is driven by equilibrium current gradients and requires non-ideal effects (e.g., resistivity, Hall or kinetic physics) to break the ideal frozen\u2011in constraint."},{"id":"47a89c20-280b-4156-ae48-872cbbcbdfa4","l":"Terahertz sources","d":3,"p":"07ec0e31-6cd2-4c32-97e1-ba06f99a5d87","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Terahertz sources are devices that generate electromagnetic radiation in the terahertz frequency band (roughly 0.1\u201310 THz), the spectral region between microwaves and infrared. They include electronic oscillators, photonic ultrafast-laser emitters (e.g., photoconductive antennas, optical rectification) and semiconductor quantum-cascade lasers, and are used for spectroscopy, imaging and communications despite the technical challenges of the so-called \"THz gap.\""},{"id":"c23e31e0-d517-429f-90a9-2f6e6189492f","l":"Terahertz time-domain spectroscopy","d":3,"p":"d47bba71-117d-4810-a4e0-e6528994d02e","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Terahertz time-domain spectroscopy (THz-TDS) is a coherent technique that measures the time-dependent electric field of broadband, single-cycle terahertz pulses transmitted or reflected from a sample\u2014typically generated and detected with ultrafast lasers. Fourier transforming the recorded waveform yields amplitude and phase across the THz band (\u22480.1\u201310 THz), enabling direct extraction of the sample's complex refractive index, dielectric function, and conductivity without separate Kramers\u2013Kronig analysis."},{"id":"f76737f2-4ee4-4c74-a4d1-63ce8af0ea05","l":"Tertiary structure","d":3,"p":"9fe8a5bc-b93d-46bc-9faa-208a23f0d923","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Tertiary structure is the overall three-dimensional arrangement of a single polypeptide chain, describing how its secondary-structure elements and side chains fold into a compact shape. It is stabilized by hydrophobic packing, hydrogen bonds, ionic interactions, van der Waals forces and disulfide bridges, and largely determines the protein's functional properties."},{"id":"21153288-2cc1-447b-9714-d79cc06a734f","l":"Thermoelectric cooling","d":3,"p":"98437b05-f0c0-47aa-aba8-d73081519e1b","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Thermoelectric cooling is a solid-state refrigeration method that uses the Peltier effect: an electrical current flowing through junctions of dissimilar conductors or semiconductors absorbs heat at one junction and releases it at the other, creating a temperature difference. It provides compact, reversible cooling with no moving parts, but its performance is limited by the materials' thermoelectric figure of merit (ZT)."},{"id":"e4e5953c-3b25-4901-bb5a-a703af4c47e3","l":"Thermoelectric generators","d":3,"p":"98437b05-f0c0-47aa-aba8-d73081519e1b","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Thermolectric generators are solid\u2011state devices that convert a temperature difference directly into electrical power by exploiting the Seebeck effect, typically using paired n\u2011 and p\u2011type semiconductor legs arranged electrically in series and thermally in parallel. Their practical efficiency is set by the materials' thermoelectric figure of merit (ZT) and is ultimately bounded by the Carnot limit."},{"id":"367a1b45-c8f5-4fda-b5fe-e5112e92cc39","l":"Thermoelectric heating","d":3,"p":"98437b05-f0c0-47aa-aba8-d73081519e1b","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Thermoelectric heating is the generation (or absorption) of heat when an electric current and a temperature gradient interact in a conductive material or at junctions of dissimilar conductors, chiefly via the Peltier and Thomson effects. In practice it appears in thermoelectric devices as reversible Peltier/Thomson heating or cooling at interfaces, together with irreversible resistive (Joule) heating in the bulk."},{"id":"1d64ed65-d710-42df-9a0a-abdf32e095db","l":"Threshold & cascade models","d":3,"p":"3cbbf1f7-9427-4812-a34b-410f229c0987","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Threshold and cascade models describe dynamics on networks in which an element changes state (e.g., activates, fails, adopts) when its input\u2014such as the number or fraction of active neighbors or received load\u2014exceeds a local threshold, causing possible chain reactions. These models capture avalanches and phase-transition\u2013like behavior (branching processes, percolation) and are used to study contagion, systemic risk, and failure propagation, with outcomes sensitive to threshold distributions and network structure."},{"id":"083ca210-0b71-4ee8-882b-053297428820","l":"Time & angle resolved photoemission spectroscopy","d":3,"p":"40082853-5a77-4bce-bb7b-a6b598d04d93","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Time- and angle-resolved photoemission spectroscopy (tr-ARPES) is a pump\u2013probe extension of angle-resolved photoemission that uses an ultrafast pump to excite a sample and a time-delayed probe to photoemit electrons, measuring their kinetic energy and emission angle to produce femtosecond-resolved, momentum- and energy-resolved maps of the transient electronic band structure and occupations. It directly reveals non-equilibrium quasiparticle dynamics, lifetimes, and many-body interactions in solids."},{"id":"298275ec-94c5-486b-b78e-c7da9c0482db","l":"Time-of-flight mass spectrometry","d":3,"p":"38b065c4-10d9-4e16-8ace-63d1eb093d28","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Time-of-flight mass spectrometry (TOF-MS) is a technique that separates ions by mass-to-charge ratio by accelerating them to the same kinetic energy and measuring the time they take to travel a known distance to a detector\u2014lighter ions arrive earlier than heavier ones. Because the flight time scales as the square root of m/z (t \u221d \u221a(m/z)), the measured arrival times are converted into a mass spectrum."},{"id":"c4c44678-c6fb-4c48-8399-0f777109e437","l":"Time-projection chambers","d":3,"p":"d94b540a-e0d6-40ef-9e71-d39129278bd5","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"A time projection chamber (TPC) is a particle detector that records three-dimensional charged-particle tracks by drifting ionization electrons, produced along a particle\u2019s path in a gas or liquid, to a segmented readout plane; the electron arrival time gives the coordinate along the drift direction while the readout position gives the transverse coordinates. TPCs provide fine-grained spatial tracking and ionization (dE/dx) measurements for particle identification and momentum reconstruction (often used with a magnetic field)."},{"id":"8f54b19f-f0bb-4350-9f62-fb65f08f6f36","l":"Time-resolved light scattering spectroscopy","d":3,"p":"382676e6-fd8f-4fc6-b8e0-3abbc45c379c","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Time-resolved light scattering spectroscopy measures how the light scattered from a sample changes in time after pulsed or modulated illumination, recording time-dependent intensity, phase, polarization or spectrum to reveal dynamic processes. By analyzing time-of-flight distributions or intensity autocorrelation functions of the scattered photons, it extracts information on particle motion, relaxation times, transport properties and ultrafast structural or electronic dynamics."},{"id":"09acb30d-fe38-4733-8528-4f3b6214146b","l":"Time-resolved photoluminescence","d":3,"p":"6bdea32e-2fdf-4421-b982-41a2a011a363","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Time-resolved photoluminescence is an optical spectroscopy technique that records how the intensity and spectrum of photoluminescence change in time after a short excitation pulse, revealing carrier recombination lifetimes and dynamic processes such as radiative vs. nonradiative decay, trapping, and energy transfer. It is typically implemented with pulsed excitation and fast detection (e.g., time-correlated single-photon counting or streak cameras) to extract decay curves and lifetimes."},{"id":"21435647-75b6-47d2-9ede-51467b456f47","l":"Time-resolved reflection spectroscopy","d":3,"p":"efe40972-7a4f-4a87-80ef-ae39472ce875","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics"],"def":"Time-resolved reflection spectroscopy is a pump\u2013probe technique that measures how a sample's reflected light intensity (and thus its reflectivity or complex refractive index) changes as a function of time after a short excitation pulse. By recording reflectance at controlled time delays, it reveals ultrafast electronic, structural, and optical dynamics such as carrier relaxation, phase transitions, and coherent excitations."},{"id":"4965b0fe-590f-49fd-8f3d-9f89cf0bb140","l":"Time-resolved two-photon photoemission spectroscopy","d":3,"p":"40082853-5a77-4bce-bb7b-a6b598d04d93","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Time-resolved two-photon photoemission spectroscopy is an ultrafast pump\u2013probe technique in which an initial (pump) photon excites electrons into unoccupied or intermediate states and a time-delayed probe photon promotes those electrons above the vacuum level so their kinetic energy and emission angle can be measured. By scanning the pump\u2013probe delay and analyzing the energy and momentum of the emitted electrons, it provides femtosecond-resolved information on excited-state lifetimes, relaxation pathways, and surface or interface electronic structure."},{"id":"56afcd05-c7d8-4562-8270-40a3208a03f1","l":"Tomography","d":3,"p":"70d9878d-c239-4e9c-b8bb-237aeff0612e","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Tomography is a set of imaging techniques that reconstruct an object's internal structure from multiple external projections or slices by mathematically inverting those measurements (e.g., via the Radon transform). It is used in many fields\u2014X\u2011ray CT, electron tomography, and seismic tomography being common examples\u2014to produce noninvasive cross\u2011sectional or 3D images."},{"id":"363a7585-1775-4b5d-bf38-1dd9bd87c1f9","l":"Topological quantum computing","d":3,"p":"f9456df5-5ca2-4a8a-8b11-d5ffd9d96921","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Topological quantum computing is an approach to quantum information processing that encodes qubits in nonlocal topological degrees of freedom (for example, non\u2011Abelian anyons or Majorana zero modes) and performs quantum gates by braiding and fusing these quasiparticles. Because the quantum information is stored in global topology rather than local microscopic details, operations are intrinsically protected against many forms of local noise, offering built\u2011in fault tolerance."},{"id":"e6b04765-18dc-43d5-8d15-558431526b67","l":"Transcription","d":3,"p":"deef9592-7e3e-4949-9bca-ffa9fa9d1e46","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Transcription is the cellular process in which RNA polymerase reads a DNA template strand and synthesizes a complementary RNA molecule, thereby converting genetic information from DNA into functional RNA (e.g., mRNA, tRNA, rRNA). It proceeds through initiation, elongation, and termination and constitutes the first step of gene expression."},{"id":"a7031030-7922-4b4d-b232-6d0b2c27b6d2","l":"Transition-metal rare-earth alloys","d":3,"p":"1233e97e-8593-44af-8651-24253580a5ac","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Transition-metal rare-earth alloys are metallic alloys or intermetallic compounds formed by combining a transition metal (d\u2011electron element) with a rare\u2011earth (lanthanide or related) element. Their electronic and magnetic behavior is dominated by the interplay of localized rare\u2011earth 4f moments and itinerant transition\u2011metal d electrons, giving rise to strong magnetic anisotropy, complex ordering, and rich phenomena (e.g., RKKY exchange, Kondo/heavy\u2011fermion effects, and useful magnetocaloric/permanent\u2011magnet properties)."},{"id":"da6707f1-6146-40bb-948f-38cd8ae1e3eb","l":"Transition metals","d":3,"p":"7584df11-595e-4afa-bd90-305dfa506396","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Transition metals are the d-block elements whose atoms or common ions have an incomplete d subshell (partially filled d orbitals), which leads to characteristic properties such as multiple accessible oxidation states, colored compounds, magnetism, and good electrical/thermal conductivity."},{"id":"fb6137e3-27d0-4a1b-91c9-fa44a0723303","l":"Translation","d":3,"p":"deef9592-7e3e-4949-9bca-ffa9fa9d1e46","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Translation is motion in which every point of an object undergoes the same displacement (and thus has the same velocity and acceleration) so the object moves without rotating. For a rigid body, pure translation is the motion of the entire body as a whole, equivalent to the motion of its center of mass."},{"id":"5573b86c-324b-45ea-832a-145da2b82de3","l":"Translocation","d":3,"p":"deef9592-7e3e-4949-9bca-ffa9fa9d1e46","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Translocation is the process by which a particle or macromolecule (commonly a polymer like DNA) passes through a constriction such as a pore, channel, or membrane. Its dynamics are governed by driving forces (e.g., electric fields or chemical gradients), entropic barriers, frictional dissipation, and interactions with the confining geometry."},{"id":"920dbc21-fc92-4c14-925f-ab495c6b1c82","l":"Transmission electron microscopy","d":3,"p":"34773509-53cc-4885-a027-7bd3497f7157","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) is an imaging technique in which a beam of high-energy electrons is transmitted through an ultrathin specimen and electromagnetic lenses project the transmitted and scattered electrons to form an image. Contrast and diffraction from electron scattering and phase shifts reveal internal structure, composition, and crystallography with spatial resolution down to the atomic scale."},{"id":"e0615e8e-044c-4159-b841-90dd3c7ed08c","l":"Transposable elements","d":3,"p":"426041d7-ba48-436b-bde9-45482e4fe33a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Transposable elements (or \"jumping genes\") are DNA sequences that can move or copy themselves to new locations within a genome, either by a cut-and-paste DNA transposition mechanism or by an RNA intermediate (retrotransposition). Their activity can alter gene function, create mutations, and reshape genome structure and size."},{"id":"d13a722e-8271-44f2-9035-ce64a864682c","l":"Transposases","d":3,"p":"426041d7-ba48-436b-bde9-45482e4fe33a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Transposases are enzymes that catalyze the movement of transposable DNA elements within a genome by excising a segment and inserting it at a new site, enabling \"cut\u2011and\u2011paste\" or \"copy\u2011and\u2011paste\" transposition. They recognize specific terminal sequences, form a protein\u2013DNA complex to cleave and join DNA strands, and thereby alter genome structure and function."},{"id":"9826d5fb-dd71-4137-ad11-51746c0309a1","l":"Transposon activity","d":3,"p":"426041d7-ba48-436b-bde9-45482e4fe33a","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Transposon activity is the movement of transposable genetic elements (\u201ctransposons\u201d) within a genome, carried out either by DNA cut\u2011and\u2011paste transposases or by RNA\u2011mediated copy\u2011and\u2011paste (retrotransposition), which can create insertions, deletions, rearrangements, and alter gene expression. It is modulated by cellular and epigenetic controls and influences genome evolution and disease."},{"id":"f2939fef-1916-4c9f-9653-5fbf94a7f130","l":"Transverse momentum dependent distribution","d":3,"p":"edc1099a-36c7-42d9-9364-842b964617bb","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"A transverse momentum dependent distribution (TMD) is a parton distribution function that gives the probability density to find a parton inside a fast-moving hadron with a specified longitudinal momentum fraction x and intrinsic transverse momentum k_T, including possible spin and correlation information. TMDs generalize ordinary (collinear) PDFs and appear in TMD factorization formulas for processes sensitive to small transverse momenta of final-state particles."},{"id":"532ce441-0fc6-45b5-ab1d-7d7bba53645a","l":"Transversity distribution functions","d":3,"p":"33f199a2-1b0f-4e00-a922-a42ef08688db","fl":["Properties"],"dl":["Nuclear Physics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Transversity distribution functions (h1(x)) are leading-twist parton distribution functions that quantify the difference in number density of quarks whose spin is polarized transverse to the nucleon momentum and aligned versus anti-aligned with a transversely polarized nucleon. They are chiral-odd, so unlike helicity or unpolarized PDFs they cannot be measured in inclusive DIS and must be accessed in reactions that involve another chiral-odd partner (e.g., Drell\u2013Yan or semi-inclusive DIS with the Collins fragmentation function)."},{"id":"84a08e27-e075-4749-8940-19c517579bc2","l":"Tubulin","d":3,"p":"8c0cd30a-5be8-4913-9220-907afd4bceb8","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Tubulin is a globular protein that forms alpha\u2013beta heterodimers which bind GTP and polymerize head-to-tail into protofilaments to build microtubules. Microtubules are ~25 nm hollow cylindrical filaments with significant bending stiffness and GTP-dependent dynamic instability, enabling cellular shape maintenance, force generation, intracellular transport, and mitotic spindle function."},{"id":"3a040c83-a7ba-428e-9f59-dd50bfa3fa4b","l":"Tunneling anisotropic magnetoresistance","d":3,"p":"23e22a76-a9ba-4593-8d52-4e5c98c47af3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Tunneling anisotropic magnetoresistance (TAMR) is the change in the electrical resistance of a tunnel junction that occurs when the magnetization direction of a ferromagnetic electrode is rotated, arising from spin\u2013orbit coupling which makes the tunneling density of states and tunneling probability anisotropic with respect to crystallographic axes or current direction. Unlike conventional tunneling magnetoresistance, TAMR can appear with a single ferromagnetic electrode and is observed as a magnetization-angle-dependent variation in tunneling conductance."},{"id":"8990a6dc-a356-4deb-8841-2de7846edcfe","l":"Tunneling electroresistance","d":3,"p":"2c149da2-1bb1-4e4f-b1a9-a991f4caa502","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Tunneling electroresistance (TER) is the change in electrical resistance of a tunnel junction produced by reversing the ferroelectric polarization of the insulating barrier: polarization switching modifies the barrier potential and interfacial charge screening, thereby changing the electron tunneling probability. TER underlies nonvolatile resistive switching in ferroelectric tunnel junctions."},{"id":"60bf0f7e-63b1-4b9d-92a9-a9d6048519b3","l":"Tunneling magnetoresistance","d":3,"p":"23e22a76-a9ba-4593-8d52-4e5c98c47af3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Tunneling magnetoresistance (TMR) is the change in electrical resistance of a magnetic tunnel junction\u2014two ferromagnetic electrodes separated by a thin insulating barrier\u2014when the relative orientation of their magnetizations is switched. It arises from spin-dependent quantum tunneling, giving higher conductance for parallel magnetizations than for antiparallel ones."},{"id":"7e9646f6-7b1b-4bd5-9b0d-262de05f0b16","l":"Two-dimensional coherent spectroscopy","d":3,"p":"efe40972-7a4f-4a87-80ef-ae39472ce875","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics"],"def":"Two-dimensional coherent spectroscopy is an ultrafast, phase\u2011controlled nonlinear spectroscopy that measures a material\u2019s coherent third\u2011order response using sequences of femtosecond pulses and Fourier transforms along two time axes. The resulting 2D spectra correlate excitation and detection frequencies to reveal couplings, energy transfer, coherence dynamics, and to separate homogeneous from inhomogeneous broadening."},{"id":"17efc391-a78d-42e7-b9cb-841c6310aa7c","l":"Two-photon photoelectron spectroscopy","d":3,"p":"40082853-5a77-4bce-bb7b-a6b598d04d93","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Two-photon photoelectron spectroscopy (2PPE) is a nonlinear photoemission technique in which one photon promotes an electron from an occupied state into an intermediate unoccupied state and a second photon ionizes it into the vacuum so the emitted electron's kinetic energy and angle can be measured. By using ultrafast (pump\u2013probe) pulses, 2PPE reveals the energies, momenta and femtosecond-scale lifetimes and relaxation dynamics of unoccupied electronic states at surfaces and interfaces."},{"id":"b40f710f-b90b-4ed8-845f-26fc41991fd3","l":"Two-photon polymerization","d":3,"p":"dedc6dbe-3651-4ad0-9d37-b260e8975adc","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Two-photon polymerization is a nonlinear optical 3D microfabrication technique in which a photoinitiator in a photosensitive resin simultaneously absorbs two lower-energy photons from a tightly focused ultrafast laser pulse, triggering polymerization only at the focal volume. Because the two-photon absorption probability scales with the square of the intensity, the reaction is confined to a sub-diffraction-limited voxel, enabling arbitrary high-resolution (micro\u2011 to nanoscale) 3D structuring inside the bulk material."},{"id":"4396648f-c801-43a4-95e2-a216f39f9cf4","l":"Two-stream instability","d":3,"p":"b094f9fa-ebc7-4e4d-8957-0a7303f6e91e","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"Two-stream instability is a kinetic plasma instability that occurs when two interpenetrating charged-particle streams (for example a beam and a background plasma) have a relative drift, causing small electrostatic or electromagnetic perturbations to grow exponentially. The instability arises from resonant energy transfer between particles and waves, leading to wave amplification and eventual momentum and energy redistribution (beam slowing and plasma heating)."},{"id":"5c4ccdd5-f963-4d29-a1cf-b96d96314c2b","l":"Ultrafast demagnetization","d":3,"p":"b363ddc7-599d-4308-8b6b-f51ce783360f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Ultrafast demagnetization is the rapid reduction of a material's magnetization occurring on femtosecond to picosecond time scales after an ultrafast perturbation (commonly an ultrashort laser pulse). It results from nonequilibrium transfers of energy and angular momentum among electrons, spins and the lattice\u2014mediated by processes such as spin-flip scattering, spin-orbit coupling and ultrafast spin transport."},{"id":"69ac5176-b884-4de3-af51-15f44a244a35","l":"Ultrasound spectroscopy","d":3,"p":"011df754-7d14-4d82-85d4-d33b3166ccf6","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Ultrasound spectroscopy is the technique of probing a material's mechanical and structural properties by sending high-frequency sound (ultrasound, typically kHz\u2013GHz) and measuring the frequency-dependent transmission, reflection, scattering, absorption, or resonance. Analysis of the resulting spectra of sound speed, attenuation, and resonant modes reveals elastic moduli, viscosity, microstructure, and dynamical or phase-change behavior."},{"id":"a3b7b852-2443-4d36-b8d8-4011d5130b7a","l":"Ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy","d":3,"p":"40082853-5a77-4bce-bb7b-a6b598d04d93","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) is an experimental technique in which ultraviolet photons eject valence electrons from a sample and the kinetic energies of those photoelectrons are measured. From the known photon energy these spectra give electron binding energies and thereby map the valence electronic structure, work function, and surface-sensitive chemical information."},{"id":"15cb359f-2dba-4eaa-baa0-8b8f6b92f559","l":"Unfolding","d":3,"p":"deef9592-7e3e-4949-9bca-ffa9fa9d1e46","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Unfolding is the data-analysis procedure of correcting measured distributions for distortions introduced by the measurement process (detector resolution, inefficiency, and acceptance) in order to estimate the true underlying physical distribution. Mathematically it is an inverse problem (deconvolution) using the detector response matrix, typically requiring regularization to suppress amplification of statistical fluctuations."},{"id":"63ace35d-85ee-4b5f-bc58-9bbd520986a9","l":"Unilamellar vesicles","d":3,"p":"df30acff-987c-4b88-b32c-58b4e3887f8e","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Unilamellar vesicles are closed, roughly spherical compartments bounded by a single lipid bilayer that separates an enclosed aqueous interior from the external solution, distinguishing them from multilamellar vesicles with multiple concentric bilayers. They range in size from ~20 nm (small unilamellar vesicles) to tens of micrometers (giant unilamellar vesicles) and are widely used as model systems for membrane mechanics, transport, and self-assembly."},{"id":"d6df2a66-7d7e-49dc-a32e-fe4b2866a4ed","l":"Unruh effect","d":3,"p":"2eb80948-a4d0-4b3c-b196-1e722da4c96f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Gravitation, Cosmology & Astrophysics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"The Unruh effect is the prediction that a detector undergoing uniform proper acceleration a through the Minkowski vacuum will perceive a thermal bath of particles with temperature T = \u0127 a / (2\u03c0 c k_B), even though inertial observers see no particles. It demonstrates that the notion of particles in quantum field theory depends on the observer\u2019s motion and is closely related to Hawking radiation."},{"id":"ac63bd99-eabc-4647-a98a-e2147cedc9a2","l":"Unsharp measurements","d":3,"p":"5de0faa9-608e-450b-8d04-5aa4219dad8d","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Unsharp measurements are generalized quantum measurements described by positive operator-valued measures (POVMs) whose measurement operators are not projectors, representing intrinsically imprecise or \"fuzzy\" observations of an observable that yield partial information while causing less disturbance than an ideal projective (sharp) measurement."},{"id":"c602689e-10b1-4f8a-bd12-da685ac1119f","l":"U(N) symmetry","d":3,"p":"543d3af2-a8bc-462b-9d72-753b6de0d8ce","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"U(N) symmetry is invariance under the group U(N) of N\u00d7N unitary matrices (M\u2020M = I), a compact Lie group of dimension N^2 whose transformations mix N internal complex degrees of freedom without changing norms. Its Lie algebra u(N) consists of Hermitian generators and decomposes as u(1) \u2295 su(N), so such a symmetry implies N^2 conserved generators and can be implemented as either a global or a local (gauge) symmetry in field theories."},{"id":"a06f6c62-03c0-4084-bb03-a169845aad44","l":"Variational approach","d":3,"p":"a0dcc4fb-34a0-4892-a60a-cfb14c0f73e8","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A variational approach is a method that formulates a physical problem as finding stationary points (typically minima) of a functional and derives the governing equations or optimal solutions by varying the function(s) on which the functional depends. Practical implementations include the calculus of variations (yielding Euler\u2013Lagrange equations, e.g., the principle of least action) and trial-function methods like Rayleigh\u2013Ritz used to obtain approximate eigenvalues and ground states."},{"id":"d729814e-38d9-4fe5-b3db-b4d77a4f3d06","l":"VCSELs","d":3,"p":"371ef245-60f3-45d9-b265-ed7abd84f07f","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"VCSELs (vertical-cavity surface-emitting lasers) are semiconductor lasers whose optical cavity is perpendicular to the wafer surface so they emit light out of the top of the chip. They use a very short vertical cavity with high-reflectivity distributed Bragg reflector mirrors around an active quantum-well region, giving low threshold currents, circular single-transverse-mode beams, easy wafer-scale testing and dense arrays for communications and sensing."},{"id":"712921d6-3441-404e-ac25-51885edffe8f","l":"Vector mesons","d":3,"p":"0841db0c-24b2-4074-9ba3-a5b6243699b5","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Particles & Fields"],"def":"Vector mesons are mesons (quark\u2013antiquark bound states) with total spin J = 1, so they transform like spatial vectors and typically have parity P = \u22121; neutral ones also carry charge-conjugation C = \u22121, giving J^{PC}=1^{--}. Examples include the \u03c1, \u03c9, \u03c6, J/\u03c8 and \u03d2."},{"id":"69f2656d-2e39-4f98-8636-d62057de767f","l":"Viral diseases","d":3,"p":"d6072ccd-5d49-4a8c-a032-efa240964575","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Viral diseases are illnesses caused by viruses\u2014nanometer-scale obligate intracellular agents made of nucleic acid enclosed in a protein capsid (and sometimes a lipid envelope)\u2014that hijack host cellular machinery to replicate, damage tissues, and spread between hosts."},{"id":"0e378245-c424-4d52-bd5d-81039b2c9cc4","l":"Vision","d":3,"p":"7b59f715-a6b8-4141-b762-668edfb84dc3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Vision is the physical process by which visible electromagnetic radiation is collected and shaped by optical components to form spatial light patterns (images). Those patterns are transduced by photodetectors\u2014biological photoreceptors or electronic sensors\u2014into electrical signals that are then processed to extract intensity, color, depth and motion information."},{"id":"9c5c49ce-2069-49f9-bfd8-94d4f85b0e7f","l":"Voter model","d":3,"p":"3cbbf1f7-9427-4812-a34b-410f229c0987","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"The voter model is a stochastic interacting-particle model of opinion dynamics in which each agent on a lattice or network holds one of two states and, at random times, adopts the state of a randomly chosen neighbor; this simple imitation rule produces domain coarsening and can lead to consensus (an absorbing state) or to persistent fluctuations depending on dimension and network topology."},{"id":"703a9d8c-f20e-4b6c-8840-5d80a681c821","l":"Wall slip","d":3,"p":"0f0cd98d-5753-4e9a-99e9-068f5528eafe","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Wall slip is the relative motion between a fluid and a solid boundary in which the fluid has a nonzero tangential velocity at the wall (i.e., the no-slip condition is violated), often caused by a thin low-viscosity or depleted layer at the interface. It is quantified by a slip velocity or a slip length \u2014 the distance inside the wall at which the extrapolated flow profile would reach the wall velocity."},{"id":"cd565159-a944-468c-b561-2718ad594a73","l":"Weibel instability","d":3,"p":"3f71ccf5-16ff-425f-b7b3-4791f9503b59","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Plasma Physics"],"def":"The Weibel instability is a kinetic electromagnetic instability in a collisionless plasma driven by anisotropy in the particle velocity (or temperature) distribution, where small transverse magnetic perturbations grow and produce current filaments. It converts free energy from the anisotropy into magnetic fields and particle scattering, and is important for spontaneous field generation in astrophysical shocks, laser plasmas, and beam\u2013plasma systems."},{"id":"e15e7952-4ed1-46e4-81dd-01e3723604af","l":"Whispering gallery mode resonators","d":3,"p":"dd5ba9a6-ec6b-46ce-94ef-26ee41ff0db4","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Whispering gallery mode resonators are dielectric cavities (e.g., microspheres, microdisks, or rings) that trap electromagnetic waves by continuous total internal reflection along their curved boundary, producing circulating resonant modes localized near the perimeter. These modes have very high quality factors and strong evanescent fields, making them useful for sensing, low-threshold lasers, and nonlinear-optical applications."},{"id":"3738fa94-d0d1-463e-b8fc-0dd7982de64f","l":"White blood cells","d":3,"p":"356040c7-f8a2-4afa-a9e4-c6f22fd80281","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"White blood cells (leukocytes) are the immune cells circulating in blood and lymph whose primary roles are detecting, neutralizing, and removing pathogens and damaged or foreign cells. They arise from bone marrow and include specialized types (neutrophils, lymphocytes, monocytes, eosinophils, basophils) that perform functions such as phagocytosis, antigen presentation, inflammation mediation, and antibody production."},{"id":"9fb26ff7-3cea-4731-8317-483692f5a36a","l":"Wireless communication networks","d":3,"p":"551a7b72-2a25-49e4-be10-843849f6987d","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Networks"],"def":"Wireless communication networks are systems of spatially distributed nodes (transmitters, receivers, relays) that exchange information by transmitting and receiving electromagnetic waves through the air or space without physical connectors. Their performance is governed by electromagnetic propagation effects (path loss, diffraction, scattering, multipath, Doppler), interference and noise, spectrum allocation, and signal-processing techniques such as modulation, coding, and antenna design."},{"id":"8b16fd40-9ac3-45b5-a389-7543ee26bb8c","l":"Worm-like chain model","d":3,"p":"ac816133-540a-4562-8130-584df553f46a","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"The worm-like chain (WLC) model is a continuum model of a semiflexible polymer that treats the molecule as an inextensible elastic filament whose bending costs energy proportional to the square of curvature (also called the Kratky\u2013Porod model). Its key parameter, the persistence length, sets the distance over which tangent directions remain correlated, giving an exponential decay of orientational correlations and describing the elastic behavior of biopolymers like DNA and actin."},{"id":"de07dc02-fb0a-4fdb-8d0f-544f975831bb","l":"X-ray absorption fine structure spectroscopy","d":3,"p":"6d708986-9ffd-4743-9dbb-a13c95fa5414","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"X-ray absorption fine structure (XAFS) spectroscopy is an element-specific technique that measures the energy-dependent oscillations in an atom's x-ray absorption coefficient near and above a core-level absorption edge to probe its local electronic and atomic environment. Analysis of the near-edge (XANES) and extended (EXAFS) regions reveals information on oxidation state and coordination geometry, as well as interatomic distances and structural disorder."},{"id":"4de81efe-38c4-4aca-bac1-a76737433dc9","l":"X-ray absorption near-edge spectroscopy","d":3,"p":"6d708986-9ffd-4743-9dbb-a13c95fa5414","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"X-ray absorption near-edge spectroscopy (XANES) is the portion of x-ray absorption spectroscopy that examines the fine structure within roughly 10\u201350 eV of an element's absorption edge, arising from core-electron excitations and multiple scattering of the emitted photoelectron. These near-edge features are sensitive to the absorber's unoccupied electronic states, oxidation state, and local coordination geometry, so XANES is used to probe chemical and electronic structure at specific atomic sites."},{"id":"d6140738-325c-4c18-b909-7b6cab088086","l":"X-ray diffraction topography","d":3,"p":"32de7669-68ef-4f3e-9598-2a70300ea01e","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"X-ray diffraction topography is an imaging technique that records the spatial distribution of x-ray intensity diffracted from a crystal to produce a \"topograph\" showing defects, strain and local lattice orientation. The contrast in the image arises from local variations in lattice spacing, tilt or disorder that modify the Bragg diffraction condition (commonly in transmission or back-reflection geometries)."},{"id":"d45ade7d-8b97-4566-a733-1c8d84a30b18","l":"X-ray diffuse scattering","d":3,"p":"1d3e6a42-9d7e-4c5f-b0ef-c5fb489e8336","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"X-ray diffuse scattering is the continuous X-ray intensity in reciprocal space outside the sharp Bragg peaks, produced by deviations from perfect periodicity\u2014such as thermal vibrations (phonons), point/line defects, short-range order, or strain\u2014whose spatial correlations scatter X-rays into non-Bragg directions. It provides information on correlated disorder and lattice dynamics (e.g., phonon-related thermal diffuse scattering) complementary to the average-structure information from Bragg diffraction."},{"id":"528c8088-7367-43ee-8987-bcb487dd8209","l":"X-ray powder diffraction","d":3,"p":"32de7669-68ef-4f3e-9598-2a70300ea01e","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"X-ray powder diffraction is an experimental technique in which monochromatic X-rays scatter from a polycrystalline or powdered sample and the diffracted intensity is recorded as a function of angle; constructive interference from many randomly oriented crystallites produces a pattern of Bragg peaks determined by the crystal lattice spacings (Bragg's law). The resulting diffraction pattern is used to identify phases, determine unit-cell parameters, and extract structural information about the material."},{"id":"9f0c8bb8-8b7d-4a6d-8274-50542131c1c6","l":"X-ray resonant magnetic scattering","d":3,"p":"1d3e6a42-9d7e-4c5f-b0ef-c5fb489e8336","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"X-ray resonant magnetic scattering is a polarization-sensitive x-ray diffraction technique in which the incident photon energy is tuned to an atomic absorption edge so that the scattering amplitude is resonantly enhanced and becomes sensitive to the local magnetic (spin and orbital) moments. Because the resonance is element- and orbital-specific, the method yields detailed information on magnetic ordering, spin orientation, chirality and domain structure with high reciprocal-space and spatial resolution."},{"id":"adaeeffe-82ec-4c5f-b4cd-15a31329275a","l":"XUV generation","d":3,"p":"50e1b8c1-9292-4a21-881d-b13c9e018672","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical"],"def":"XUV generation is the production of electromagnetic radiation in the extreme-ultraviolet spectral region (roughly 10\u2013124 nm, corresponding to photon energies of order 10\u2013124 eV). It is produced by converting optical or kinetic energy into short-wavelength photons via processes such as high-harmonic generation in gases or solids, laser-produced plasmas, synchrotron emission, or free-electron lasers."},{"id":"45954983-3d55-4d9d-9cdd-75f14447175a","l":"XY model","d":3,"p":"695da376-c44a-41dd-b968-d200c31e9d5e","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Networks","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"The XY model is a lattice spin model of planar (two-component) unit-length spins whose energy favors alignment, usually written H = \u2212J \u03a3\u27c2cos(\u03b8_i \u2212 \u03b8_j) with a continuous U(1) rotational symmetry. In two dimensions it is notable for a topological Kosterlitz\u2013Thouless transition driven by binding and unbinding of vortex\u2013antivortex pairs."},{"id":"cc151ab5-b599-4dd0-bb77-9f4fd82dac69","l":"XYZ model","d":3,"p":"6ff84248-428b-4704-ae9f-e55346a4ce60","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The XYZ model is a quantum spin model of a lattice in which neighboring spins interact anisotropically through different coupling strengths Jx, Jy, and Jz for the SxSx, SySy and SzSz terms; its Hamiltonian is H = \u03a3\u27e8i,j\u27e9(Jx Si^x Sj^x + Jy Si^y Sj^y + Jz Si^z Sj^z). It generalizes the XXZ/XY/Ising models and serves as a paradigmatic model of anisotropic magnetism and quantum phase transitions (in 1D it is integrable and exactly solvable)."},{"id":"f55c4133-0b52-48be-947e-eea1a4fece33","l":"Z_2 symmetry","d":3,"p":"847f8adc-79b0-4961-bc2a-d0494a37a24c","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Z_2 symmetry is the discrete two-element group {e, g} with g^2 = e, i.e., an order\u20112 symmetry whose nontrivial operation applied twice gives the identity. In physics it typically manifests as a sign flip or binary exchange (for example an Ising spin flip, parity/inversion, or a particle\u2013hole exchange), often represented by multiplication by \u00b11."},{"id":"4d874502-a0c3-4455-860e-6dfb1abd1598","l":"Angiography","d":4,"p":"6b156cbb-39ac-4db9-8ecf-50b5e3646acb","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Angiography is an imaging technique for visualizing the anatomy and flow of blood vessels by introducing a contrast agent that alters the imaging signal (e.g., X\u2011ray attenuation or MR susceptibility) and acquiring images with modalities such as X\u2011ray/CT, MRI, or ultrasound. Digital subtraction angiography (DSA) with X\u2011rays is a common high\u2011resolution method that subtracts background tissue signal to highlight the contrast\u2011filled vascular lumen and dynamics."},{"id":"3d20e485-92f3-41f6-822e-1267ffa2cf54","l":"Angle-resolved inverse photoemission spectroscopy","d":4,"p":"e5391c73-aefe-44c4-a5f5-b5cbbaf856d0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Angle-resolved inverse photoemission spectroscopy (ARIPES) is an experimental technique that maps the energy\u2013momentum dispersion of unoccupied electronic states above the Fermi level by injecting low-energy electrons into a sample and detecting the photons emitted when those electrons fill empty states. By resolving the photon (or incident-electron) angle, ARIPES provides momentum-resolved information about the conduction-band structure and serves as the inverse counterpart to angle-resolved photoemission spectroscopy (ARPES)."},{"id":"7e7e3af7-dd33-4157-beb8-4d46b3cfbc83","l":"Artificial spin ice","d":4,"p":"c8bfbcdd-31b6-4898-923d-4e7f6b38bc21","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Artificial spin ice are engineered arrays of interacting single-domain nanomagnets (or other bistable elements) patterned on lattices (e.g., square, kagome) so that their mutual interactions reproduce the geometric frustration and \"ice rules\" (e.g., two-in/two-out) of natural spin-ice materials. These designer systems allow controlled study and direct imaging of frustrated states, dynamics, and emergent excitations such as magnetic monopole\u2013like defects."},{"id":"852b5d68-528f-4fcb-8b33-c172db07041a","l":"Binocular vision","d":4,"p":"0e378245-c424-4d52-bd5d-81039b2c9cc4","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Binocular vision is the use of two spatially separated eyes to produce two slightly different images of a scene. The geometric disparity (parallax) between those images is fused by the visual system to yield depth perception (stereopsis) and improved spatial resolution via triangulation and vergence cues."},{"id":"23a31dd5-45a3-4768-9bef-d60d7f0eac4c","l":"Bone densitometry","d":4,"p":"6b156cbb-39ac-4db9-8ecf-50b5e3646acb","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Bone densitometry is the quantitative measurement of bone mineral density (BMD) and content used to assess bone strength and fracture risk. Physically it employs imaging modalities\u2014most commonly dual\u2011energy X\u2011ray absorptiometry (DXA), which uses differential X\u2011ray attenuation at two energies to separate bone from soft tissue\u2014while alternatives include quantitative CT and ultrasound."},{"id":"8cd08234-051c-43a8-8799-efeb7d7ce9f2","l":"Chemical solution deposition","d":4,"p":"fe6212f5-2762-4253-9267-54b97b955a02","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Chemical solution deposition is a wet-chemical thin\u2011film fabrication method in which a solution of molecular or colloidal precursors is deposited on a substrate (by spin\u2011coating, dip\u2011coating, inkjet, etc.) and then thermally or chemically processed to remove solvents/organics and convert the deposit into a continuous inorganic or oxide film. It is commonly used to make functional oxide, electronic and superconducting films with composition and microstructure controlled by precursor chemistry and heat treatment."},{"id":"3d75077d-969a-45d8-82f9-5dcf697b3d79","l":"Chemical vapor deposition","d":4,"p":"fe6212f5-2762-4253-9267-54b97b955a02","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Chemical vapor deposition (CVD) is a thin-film and coating growth technique in which volatile precursor gases are delivered to a substrate and chemically react or decompose at the surface to form a solid material while gaseous byproducts are swept away. Control of gas-phase transport, surface reaction kinetics, and thermal or plasma activation determines the film's composition, structure, and growth rate."},{"id":"ca4b9a34-476a-4d03-a192-660ebada3734","l":"Classical spin chains","d":4,"p":"595b37ed-b7d8-457e-be59-5224c6dead59","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Classical spin chains are one-dimensional lattice models in which each site carries a classical magnetic moment (a fixed-length vector or Ising \u00b11 variable) that interacts with neighboring moments via exchange, anisotropy, and external-field terms. Their equilibrium and dynamical behavior\u2014domain walls, solitons, thermalization and chaos\u2014is described by classical energy functionals and equations of motion (e.g., the Landau\u2013Lifshitz equation) rather than quantum spin operators."},{"id":"13e1d422-491e-40f9-95cd-9655dbfe5e25","l":"Cobaltates","d":4,"p":"6fdf4bb3-246f-40a9-b756-d416629bdd26","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Cobaltates are transition-metal oxides in which cobalt ions occupy lattice sites (often in layered or perovskite structures) and can adopt multiple oxidation and spin states. These materials show strong electron\u2013lattice\u2013spin coupling and host phenomena such as large thermoelectric response, spin-state and metal\u2013insulator transitions, charge ordering, magnetism, and, in hydrated layered forms (e.g., NaxCoO2\u00b7yH2O), unconventional superconductivity."},{"id":"3f8bf87b-51df-4918-b2bb-14be65415c0f","l":"Color detection","d":4,"p":"0e378245-c424-4d52-bd5d-81039b2c9cc4","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Color detection is the measurement and discrimination of the spectral composition of incident visible light, i.e., determining wavelength-dependent intensity or chromaticity. It is performed by devices (photodetectors, color sensors, spectrometers) or biological receptors (cones) that map the spectral power distribution into color coordinates or signals."},{"id":"91a0d08d-33ce-413f-bdab-c73e4a93a58a","l":"Complex oxides","d":4,"p":"6fdf4bb3-246f-40a9-b756-d416629bdd26","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Complex oxides are crystalline materials made of oxygen and two or more different cations (often transition metals) whose crystal structures (e.g., perovskite, spinel) produce strong coupling among charge, spin, orbital, and lattice degrees of freedom. This coupling yields a wide range of emergent electronic and magnetic behaviors\u2014ferroelectricity, magnetism, metal\u2013insulator transitions, colossal magnetoresistance, and high\u2011Tc superconductivity\u2014making them central to correlated-electron physics and oxide electronics."},{"id":"db558ee8-ce44-45ef-b762-ef6d96dd2f4b","l":"Cryo-scanning electron microscopy","d":4,"p":"4d734e8f-2c3b-42a0-a4cb-8f453aeae99d","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Cryo-scanning electron microscopy is a scanning-electron technique in which samples are rapidly frozen and kept at cryogenic temperatures (typically ~77 K) in vacuum so their native, hydrated structures are preserved and beam-induced damage is reduced. The frozen surface is then imaged with an electron beam\u2014often after fracturing or gentle sublimation\u2014yielding high-resolution morphological and topographical information for biological and soft-matter specimens."},{"id":"86d53aed-ec98-4254-a556-6182dbaa6e2a","l":"Cryo-transmission electron microscopy","d":4,"p":"2344c13c-dcdb-44f5-afad-7c4f273664da","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Cryo-transmission electron microscopy (cryo-TEM) is transmission electron microscopy performed on thin specimens that have been rapidly vitrified and kept at cryogenic temperatures so their hydrated biological or soft-matter structures are preserved close to the native state. By transmitting an electron beam through the frozen sample and minimizing radiation damage, cryo-TEM enables high-resolution imaging and 3D structural reconstruction (e.g., single-particle analysis or tomography)."},{"id":"6f4b4f90-bede-474c-bec9-22b271efd04f","l":"Cuprates","d":4,"p":"6fdf4bb3-246f-40a9-b756-d416629bdd26","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Cuprates are copper\u2013oxide ceramic compounds with quasi-two-dimensional CuO2 planes that, when carrier-doped, host strongly correlated electrons and high-temperature (unconventional, typically d-wave) superconductivity. They are derived from doped Mott insulators and also display competing phenomena such as antiferromagnetism and the pseudogap."},{"id":"2a203809-ba10-4c32-a96b-774ce2b4d64c","l":"Current-voltage low-energy electron diffraction","d":4,"p":"eef6c8e0-113c-40e9-a981-db01b2fe257e","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Current\u2013voltage low-energy electron diffraction (I\u2013V LEED) is a surface analysis technique in which the intensities of LEED diffraction beams are measured as the incident electron energy (beam accelerating voltage) is varied to produce I\u2013V spectra. These energy-dependent intensities are highly sensitive to the surface atomic positions and potential, and comparison with multiple\u2011scattering calculations yields quantitative surface structural and chemical information."},{"id":"58d9c0f8-d2ea-40ad-83d8-56978aa7f655","l":"Cytoskeletal motor proteins","d":4,"p":"43822279-9251-4705-8e15-c287c2e101fa","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Cytoskeletal motor proteins are nanoscale mechanochemical enzymes (e.g., kinesin, dynein, myosin) that convert chemical energy from ATP hydrolysis into mechanical work by taking directed steps along cytoskeletal filaments (microtubules or actin), generating forces and carrying cargo for intracellular transport, force generation, and cell motility."},{"id":"74399720-5754-4470-9474-8940c09603b3","l":"DFT+DMFT","d":4,"p":"5c567aa1-5c30-4feb-867d-3685a6a7c71d","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"DFT+DMFT is a computational approach that combines density functional theory (DFT) for ab initio band-structure with dynamical mean-field theory (DMFT) to include strong, local, frequency-dependent electron correlations. It works by mapping correlated lattice sites onto a self-consistently solved quantum impurity problem, enabling realistic treatment of Mott physics, quasiparticle renormalization, and finite-temperature spectral properties."},{"id":"ffcb7c9c-2fd2-4caa-ac0e-9832b20fc351","l":"DFT+U","d":4,"p":"5c567aa1-5c30-4feb-867d-3685a6a7c71d","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"DFT+U is a practical extension of density functional theory (commonly LDA+U or GGA+U) that adds a Hubbard-like on-site Coulomb correction (U, often with an effective U\u2013J) to better treat strongly correlated, localized electrons (e.g., transition-metal d or f orbitals). The extra mean-field energy term penalizes fractional orbital occupancy and reduces self-interaction and spurious delocalization in standard DFT, improving band gaps, magnetic moments, and electron localization."},{"id":"9632c044-c274-4218-9568-e8638315bcee","l":"Dichalcogenides","d":4,"p":"eff89dbf-95f8-4cde-b017-976a395f7a3f","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Dichalcogenides are compounds with the stoichiometry MX2, where a metal (often a transition metal) is bonded to two chalcogen atoms (S, Se, Te), typically forming layered van der Waals crystals. They are important in physics as tunable two-dimensional materials that can be semiconducting, metallic, or superconducting and often show strong spin\u2013orbit coupling and valley-dependent electronic effects."},{"id":"77465d33-022a-41c5-bbd6-a4c3e54c06f8","l":"Digital mammography","d":4,"p":"72d12154-97d7-48e7-be3a-9a49aebca52a","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Digital mammography is an x\u2011ray imaging method for the breast that uses electronic detectors (direct photoconductors or indirect scintillator\u2011photodiode/CCD panels) to convert transmitted x rays into digital signals instead of film. The digital acquisition provides wider dynamic range and image processing/quantitative analysis capabilities, enabling dose optimization and advanced techniques like digital breast tomosynthesis."},{"id":"6944ba20-4445-4ec6-9f76-4619ac24fda2","l":"Dip coating","d":4,"p":"977cf3f6-ab87-440b-afe2-cf08e8fcd916","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Dip coating is a technique for depositing a thin liquid film on a solid substrate by immersing it in a coating solution and withdrawing it at a controlled speed. The final film thickness is set by a balance of viscous entrainment, capillary forces and gravity (and later by solvent evaporation and drainage), often described by the Landau\u2013Levich\u2013Derjaguin scaling with withdrawal speed, viscosity and surface tension."},{"id":"d97af63e-7a21-4db5-a362-e32cccfeb1e0","l":"DNA folding","d":4,"p":"6c882ee4-606b-4a0d-af30-406061e22be7","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"DNA folding is the physical process by which a long double\u2011helical DNA polymer adopts compact, higher\u2011order three\u2011dimensional conformations (e.g., supercoils, loops, nucleosome\u2011packed chromatin) from its extended coil state. These conformations arise from a balance of bending and torsional elasticity, electrostatic screening, protein binding and molecular crowding, and they control genome organization and accessibility over nanometer\u2013micron scales."},{"id":"562c76d9-02eb-4582-9d33-536bc9402994","l":"DNA origami","d":4,"p":"6c882ee4-606b-4a0d-af30-406061e22be7","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"DNA origami is a DNA nanotechnology technique that folds a long single-stranded DNA scaffold into prescribed two- or three-dimensional shapes by using many short synthetic \"staple\" strands that selectively hybridize to chosen sites, enabling programmable self-assembly of complex, addressable nanostructures with nanometer-scale precision."},{"id":"361b9d63-ce04-46c1-ba5f-95150567e983","l":"DNA unfolding","d":4,"p":"15cb359f-2dba-4eaa-baa0-8b8f6b92f559","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"DNA unfolding is the transition of DNA from its folded, base\u2011paired or higher\u2011order compact conformations (double helix, hairpins, nucleoprotein complexes) to a more extended or single\u2011stranded state (denatured or unzipped), driven by thermal, chemical, ionic, or mechanical perturbations. In physics it is treated as a cooperative conformational transition of a polymer and analyzed using energy\u2011landscape and statistical\u2011mechanics/force\u2011spectroscopy frameworks."},{"id":"4a7f1f86-7981-41d1-bfbb-6d0d4e28db65","l":"Entanglement entropy","d":4,"p":"17411eb6-bb56-4169-aaa2-01827a8ace87","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Entanglement entropy is the von Neumann entropy of a subsystem's reduced density matrix, S_A = \u2212Tr(\u03c1_A ln \u03c1_A), which quantifies the amount of quantum correlations (entanglement) between that subsystem and the rest of the system. For a pure global state it satisfies S_A = S_B and vanishes for separable (unentangled) states."},{"id":"0923a4a2-4e56-4240-9fa9-b74351ac4bd0","l":"Environmental scanning electron microscopy","d":4,"p":"4d734e8f-2c3b-42a0-a4cb-8f453aeae99d","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Environmental scanning electron microscopy (ESEM) is a variant of scanning electron microscopy that images specimens in a controlled gaseous or variable-pressure chamber rather than in high vacuum, enabling observation of wet, non\u2011conductive, or volatile samples. It uses differential pumping and gas\u2011mediated detection of secondary electrons to prevent charging and avoid conductive coatings, permitting in situ and dynamic sample studies."},{"id":"8e7d00a3-54d7-4e39-ba96-0ac54deff1f6","l":"Epidemic spreading","d":4,"p":"5ebcb77a-26bf-4834-81c8-82d85bcdc07a","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Epidemic spreading is the dynamical process by which an infectious state (disease, information, or failure) propagates through a population or contact network via stochastic transmission and recovery events, commonly modeled by compartmental rules such as SIS and SIR. In physics it is treated as a non-equilibrium statistical process exhibiting thresholds and critical behavior and is often mapped to percolation, branching processes, and reaction\u2013diffusion phenomena."},{"id":"af3185ef-c371-4a84-946a-b1dee9137066","l":"Equilibrium fluctuations","d":4,"p":"2f1f7cb2-1b29-4d1a-a13e-3768c1f582e0","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Equilibrium fluctuations are the spontaneous microscopic temporal and spatial variations of observables in a system that is on average stationary at thermodynamic equilibrium, caused by thermal (or quantum) motion even though macroscopic averages remain constant. Their statistical magnitudes and correlations are fixed by the equilibrium ensemble and are related to linear response via fluctuation\u2013dissipation relations (e.g., variances scale with kB T times the appropriate susceptibility)."},{"id":"7dcaf534-e28a-494e-881e-65c1c13693ba","l":"Evolving communities","d":4,"p":"180b51bc-73e1-45ee-90e6-a820e89e0da1","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Evolving communities are groups of interacting elements in a complex system whose membership and internal/external connections change over time. They are studied with temporal-network models and dynamic community-detection methods to characterize processes such as formation, growth, merging, splitting, and dissolution."},{"id":"3ebe12c8-7b3a-49da-88ea-7d9973faa7b6","l":"Exponential integrate-and-fire model","d":4,"p":"470fc610-f9a6-44b3-8e02-236b910a52aa","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"A single-compartment spiking neuron model that extends the leaky integrate-and-fire equation by adding an exponential voltage-dependent term which produces a rapid, threshold-like upstroke of the membrane potential. When the membrane potential crosses a cutoff the model emits a spike and the potential is reset (often with a refractory period), giving a simple, computationally efficient description of spike initiation dynamics."},{"id":"10ec7cb5-d181-4c25-835c-fde996f3f4de","l":"Ferrites","d":4,"p":"6fdf4bb3-246f-40a9-b756-d416629bdd26","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Ferrites are ceramic iron\u2011oxide\u2013based magnetic materials (typically spinel MFe2O4 or hexagonal BaFe12O19 structures) that exhibit ferrimagnetism due to oppositely aligned magnetic sublattices. Their high electrical resistivity and tunable permeability make them useful as soft magnetic cores for transformers, inductors and RF components and, in other compositions, as hard ferrite permanent magnets."},{"id":"1919a2b6-a80a-42ae-a3c8-7e6ec37303b7","l":"Film mammography","d":4,"p":"72d12154-97d7-48e7-be3a-9a49aebca52a","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Film mammography is a projection X\u2011ray technique for imaging the breast using low\u2011energy X\u2011rays (typically ~18\u201330 keV) optimized for soft\u2011tissue contrast; the transmitted X\u2011rays are converted by intensifying screens and recorded on photographic film in a cassette to produce a high\u2011resolution analog radiograph for detecting masses and microcalcifications."},{"id":"666ef756-42d3-41ff-8706-3d0d764b0ed0","l":"Flow batteries","d":4,"p":"3aa1f62d-ba58-416a-9eaa-5d162b472e5f","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Flow batteries are rechargeable electrochemical systems in which energy is stored in liquid electrolytes containing redox-active species that are held in external tanks and pumped through a central cell stack. In the stack reversible redox reactions at electrodes and ion transport across a membrane convert chemical energy to electricity, with power determined by the stack and energy capacity by the electrolyte volume."},{"id":"444242e0-35d6-4170-a057-03f59e852745","l":"Fluorescence recovery after photobleaching","d":4,"p":"9b656cab-64ae-4b47-ba61-845c2beda40b","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Fluorescence recovery after photobleaching (FRAP) is an optical technique in which a focused, intense light pulse irreversibly photobleaches fluorophores in a defined region of a sample and the subsequent time-dependent return of fluorescence\u2014caused by molecular diffusion and/or binding\u2013unbinding events\u2014 is monitored to quantify mobility (e.g., diffusion coefficients), mobile fraction, and kinetic rates."},{"id":"21d843e0-d42a-40be-8844-335103a988c6","l":"Fluoroscopy","d":4,"p":"6b156cbb-39ac-4db9-8ecf-50b5e3646acb","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Fluoroscopy is an X\u2011ray imaging technique that uses a continuous or pulsed X\u2011ray beam and an image intensifier or flat\u2011panel detector to produce real\u2011time moving radiographic images of internal structures. It enables dynamic studies and guidance of interventional procedures (often with contrast agents) and requires careful dose management because of prolonged exposure."},{"id":"f2029804-38ee-489d-bd90-0b4abae2e0e6","l":"GGA","d":4,"p":"5c567aa1-5c30-4feb-867d-3685a6a7c71d","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"GGA (generalized gradient approximation) is an approach within density functional theory that models the exchange\u2013correlation energy as a functional of the local electron density and its gradient, thereby accounting for density inhomogeneities and improving accuracy over the local density approximation; common GGA forms include PBE and PW91."},{"id":"ce332f59-5dbd-4cd5-83df-585bd90e98a8","l":"Hybrid functionals","d":4,"p":"5c567aa1-5c30-4feb-867d-3685a6a7c71d","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Hybrid functionals are exchange\u2013correlation approximations used in Kohn\u2013Sham density functional theory that combine a fraction of nonlocal exact (Hartree\u2013Fock) exchange with a local or semilocal density-based exchange\u2013correlation functional, giving improved accuracy for band gaps, reaction barriers, and thermochemistry. Variants include global hybrids with a fixed mixing fraction and range-separated hybrids that partition exchange by interelectronic distance."},{"id":"05a0acc2-fb43-4fde-9610-aa253a417189","l":"Iridates","d":4,"p":"6fdf4bb3-246f-40a9-b756-d416629bdd26","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Iridates are oxides of the heavy transition metal iridium that host 5d electrons. Because iridium's strong spin\u2013orbit coupling competes with electron correlations and crystal-field effects, iridates exhibit unusual electronic and magnetic states\u2014e.g., spin\u2013orbit\u2013entangled Jeff = 1/2 Mott insulators, topological phases, and candidate Kitaev quantum spin liquids."},{"id":"a7c7c809-7b1b-489a-81ad-715a798e3882","l":"Kitaev-Heisenberg model","d":4,"p":"243ebf5e-d251-49c3-b8b6-fc6a3d29442e","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The Kitaev\u2013Heisenberg model is a spin-1/2 lattice Hamiltonian that combines bond-dependent, Ising-like Kitaev exchange with conventional isotropic Heisenberg coupling, typically studied on the honeycomb lattice. The competition between the anisotropic Kitaev interactions and Heisenberg magnetism produces a rich phase diagram including quantum spin-liquid and various magnetically ordered phases, and it models low-energy magnetism in spin\u2013orbit\u2013coupled Mott insulators (e.g., iridates and \u03b1-RuCl3)."},{"id":"77397021-b0aa-4e53-97ba-7dbfe8ef4b0a","l":"Langmuir-Blodgett deposition","d":4,"p":"977cf3f6-ab87-440b-afe2-cf08e8fcd916","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Langmuir\u2013Blodgett deposition is a method for transferring an ordered monolayer (typically of amphiphilic molecules) from the liquid\u2013air interface of a Langmuir trough onto a solid substrate by controlled vertical dipping or withdrawal. By compressing the floating film with movable barriers to set the surface pressure, it produces well-controlled monolayer or multilayer thin films with defined thickness and molecular orientation."},{"id":"da8c111b-fd31-499f-a19f-4b3ce0159b1c","l":"LDA","d":4,"p":"5c567aa1-5c30-4feb-867d-3685a6a7c71d","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"LDA (Local Density Approximation) is an approximation in density functional theory that models the exchange\u2013correlation energy density at each point in an electronic system by that of a homogeneous electron gas with the same local electron density. It is the simplest common functional\u2014accurate for slowly varying densities but less reliable for strongly inhomogeneous systems or where nonlocal correlations are important."},{"id":"e1751f46-2f7e-4913-9fd0-5dd343c8d7a0","l":"Leaky integrate-and-fire model","d":4,"p":"470fc610-f9a6-44b3-8e02-236b910a52aa","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"The leaky integrate-and-fire model is a simple point-neuron model in which the membrane potential evolves like a leaky RC circuit (\u03c4 dV/dt = \u2212(V \u2212 V_rest) + R I(t)), so it integrates input currents while exponentially decaying toward a resting value. When the potential reaches a fixed threshold the model emits a spike and the voltage is reset (often with a brief refractory period), providing a minimal description of neuronal spiking."},{"id":"bb72e576-c04b-44d7-a312-46411b228ad7","l":"Light detection","d":4,"p":"0e378245-c424-4d52-bd5d-81039b2c9cc4","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Light detection is the measurement of electromagnetic radiation by converting incident photons into a measurable electrical or chemical signal through their interaction with matter, allowing determination of intensity, spectrum, polarization, timing, and spatial direction. Common detectors and methods include photodiodes, photomultiplier tubes, CCD/CMOS sensors, and spectrometers."},{"id":"36605f3a-251d-4dd6-ac96-f52c669d6536","l":"Light sheet fluorescence microscopy","d":4,"p":"9b656cab-64ae-4b47-ba61-845c2beda40b","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Light sheet fluorescence microscopy (LSFM), also called selective plane illumination microscopy (SPIM), is an imaging technique that excites fluorescence only within a thin planar \"sheet\" of light that intersects the sample while a detection objective placed orthogonally images that plane. By restricting excitation to a single optical section it enables fast, high-contrast 3D imaging with much lower photobleaching and phototoxicity than widefield or confocal methods."},{"id":"52c8e1d0-df68-4b26-a81f-c7c6bc4378e7","l":"Lithium-alternative batteries","d":4,"p":"3aa1f62d-ba58-416a-9eaa-5d162b472e5f","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Lithium-alternative batteries are rechargeable electrochemical cells that replace lithium with other active ions or redox couples (e.g., sodium, magnesium, zinc, aluminum, metal\u2013air, or flow chemistries), using different electrode and electrolyte materials to store and release electrical energy. They aim to offer lower cost, greater resource abundance or improved safety and different power/energy trade-offs compared with lithium-ion systems, often with corresponding compromises in energy density or commercial maturity."},{"id":"6bcd2a14-13c4-4f1f-8a5e-24b8c6ffbb21","l":"Lithium batteries","d":4,"p":"3aa1f62d-ba58-416a-9eaa-5d162b472e5f","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Lithium batteries are electrochemical cells that store and deliver energy by reversible redox reactions in which lithium ions shuttle between anode and cathode through an electrolyte while electrons flow through an external circuit. In common lithium\u2011ion designs the ions intercalate into host materials (e.g., graphite anode and transition\u2011metal\u2011oxide cathode), giving high energy density and cell voltages around 3\u20134 V."},{"id":"5835d6de-78a5-4e5c-bab0-f5c890cd89d7","l":"Machine learning categories","d":4,"p":"5f042072-e281-48f0-8092-ea2fe2c29c26","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology","Fluid Dynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Machine learning categories group algorithms by the type of data and feedback they use and by the tasks they solve. Common categories include supervised learning (learn mappings from labeled examples), unsupervised learning (discover structure in unlabeled data), semi\u2011supervised learning (combine a few labels with many unlabeled examples), and reinforcement learning (learn actions or policies from reward signals); related distinctions include generative vs discriminative models and online vs batch training."},{"id":"76fed82e-b866-42da-9f69-0ccf38c3a785","l":"Machine learning models","d":4,"p":"5f042072-e281-48f0-8092-ea2fe2c29c26","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology","Fluid Dynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Machine learning models are parameterized algorithms (e.g., neural networks, decision trees, kernel machines) that learn statistical mappings from inputs to outputs by adjusting internal parameters to minimize a chosen loss function. Once trained on data, they generalize those learned correlations to make predictions, classify or cluster data, discover structure, or serve as surrogate models for complex physical systems without explicit programming of the underlying rules."},{"id":"d1bb7901-511a-4ecd-8855-3a8685e35a73","l":"Manganates","d":4,"p":"6fdf4bb3-246f-40a9-b756-d416629bdd26","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Manganates are inorganic salts or oxyanions containing the Mn(VI) ion MnO4^2\u2212 (for example K2MnO4), typically green in color. They are chemically intermediate between permanganate (Mn(VII)O4\u2212) and MnO2 and tend to disproportionate in acid to give permanganate and manganese dioxide."},{"id":"b2464ec3-d883-41dd-be63-010094f435d3","l":"Manganites","d":4,"p":"6fdf4bb3-246f-40a9-b756-d416629bdd26","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Manganites are manganese-containing transition-metal oxides\u2014typically mixed-valence perovskites (e.g., La1\u2212xCaxMnO3)\u2014in which strong coupling among charge, spin, orbital and lattice degrees of freedom produces rich phase diagrams, metal\u2013insulator transitions and dramatic effects such as colossal magnetoresistance."},{"id":"16857fe9-fb4b-4ef0-abba-2546d9c142e2","l":"Measurement back action","d":4,"p":"24680aeb-3710-4132-8221-f37921bf708f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Measurement back action is the perturbation a measurement process imposes on the system being measured. In quantum physics it is unavoidable\u2014manifested by state collapse and uncertainty relations as added noise or altered subsequent evolution\u2014while in classical contexts it arises from coupling to the probe and can often be made negligible."},{"id":"c3373e13-4ddc-4a37-97e4-18d804398602","l":"Measures of continuous variables entanglement","d":4,"p":"17411eb6-bb56-4169-aaa2-01827a8ace87","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology"],"def":"Measures of continuous-variable entanglement are quantitative functions that assign a nonnegative number to a quantum state of systems with continuous degrees of freedom (e.g., optical modes or position/momentum) to indicate the strength of nonclassical correlations and which vanish for separable states.  Useful measures\u2014required to be monotonic under local operations and classical communication\u2014include entanglement entropy for pure states and operationally computable quantities for Gaussian states such as logarithmic negativity and Gaussian R\u00e9nyi/entanglement of formation, which are typically evaluated from the state's covariance matrix and its symplectic eigenvalues."},{"id":"5a4983b9-c022-4296-b3da-4a2d5440f13c","l":"Meta-GGA","d":4,"p":"5c567aa1-5c30-4feb-867d-3685a6a7c71d","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Meta-GGA (meta-generalized gradient approximation) is a class of semilocal exchange\u2013correlation functionals in Kohn\u2013Sham density-functional theory that depend not only on the electron density and its gradient (as in GGA) but also on an additional local ingredient such as the Kohn\u2013Sham kinetic-energy density or the density Laplacian. This extra dependence allows meta-GGAs to capture more of the electronic-structure physics (improving energies, barriers, and bond descriptions) while remaining computationally efficient."},{"id":"42bd2e4c-7912-4c45-8566-bc3443a14a3b","l":"MOSFET","d":4,"p":"f6f3fe36-54ce-45f1-86a3-ad41516135c3","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A MOSFET (metal\u2013oxide\u2013semiconductor field-effect transistor) is a three\u2011terminal semiconductor device in which a voltage applied to a gate electrode\u2014separated from the semiconductor by a thin insulating oxide\u2014creates or depletes a conductive channel between source and drain, thereby controlling current flow. It acts as a voltage\u2011controlled switch or amplifier and is the fundamental building block of modern integrated circuits."},{"id":"346a840b-b41b-4238-ba7f-f61577ccb2a1","l":"Motion detection","d":4,"p":"0e378245-c424-4d52-bd5d-81039b2c9cc4","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Motion detection is the sensing and measurement of a change in an object's position, velocity, or acceleration relative to a chosen reference frame over time. It is implemented by comparing successive measurements from sensors (optical, acoustic, electromagnetic, or inertial) to infer displacement, speed, and direction."},{"id":"7e47ebfd-7df6-4333-9b12-c4be4335fa1d","l":"Multichannel Anderson model","d":4,"p":"73f8b483-c0c2-4d31-824c-d8d85068167a","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"The multichannel Anderson model is a generalization of the single-impurity Anderson model in which a localized impurity level (with orbital or spin degeneracy and on-site Coulomb repulsion) hybridizes with several independent conduction-electron channels. It captures Kondo screening and charge fluctuations and, for more channels than impurity degeneracy, can produce overscreened, non\u2011Fermi\u2011liquid behavior relevant to heavy-fermion and quantum\u2011critical systems."},{"id":"65920eff-f184-41cd-9ec0-b33bb0679565","l":"Nitrogen vacancy centers in diamond","d":4,"p":"2f7c0d81-b895-4834-86a6-1a2185246030","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Nitrogen-vacancy (NV) centers in diamond are atomic-scale defects consisting of a substitutional nitrogen atom next to a carbon vacancy that host a localized spin-1 electronic system with spin-dependent optical transitions. The NV spin can be initialized, manipulated and read out optically at room temperature and has long coherence times, making NV centers useful as solid-state qubits and nanoscale sensors of magnetic, electric and thermal fields."},{"id":"d87e7b24-5781-4b0a-bd2c-a37fdc8ef16a","l":"Nonequilibrium fluctuations","d":4,"p":"2f1f7cb2-1b29-4d1a-a13e-3768c1f582e0","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Nonequilibrium fluctuations are spontaneous temporal or spatial variations of physical quantities in systems driven away from thermodynamic equilibrium. Unlike equilibrium fluctuations, they can exhibit non-thermal statistics, sustained probability currents, long-range correlations, and violation of detailed balance and the equilibrium fluctuation\u2013dissipation relation."},{"id":"c40aaad2-e8d4-4610-be78-3b26ed0275dd","l":"Non-overlapping communities","d":4,"p":"180b51bc-73e1-45ee-90e6-a820e89e0da1","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Non-overlapping communities (also called disjoint communities) are a partition of a network's nodes into mutually exclusive groups such that nodes within each group have denser connections to one another than to nodes in other groups. In this structure every node is assigned to exactly one community, in contrast to overlapping communities where nodes can belong to multiple groups."},{"id":"90fe4f6a-8a53-4bde-9d66-af64556260c8","l":"Nonresonant inelastic x-ray scattering","d":4,"p":"24098ed4-b73f-4eb2-bd1a-df209728659f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Nonresonant inelastic x-ray scattering (NIXS) is the process in which incident x-rays scatter from a material with finite energy and momentum transfer while the photon energy is not tuned to any atomic absorption edge, so no core-hole resonance is involved. The measured spectrum is proportional to the dynamic structure factor S(q,\u03c9) of the electron density and thus provides bulk-sensitive information on charge, valence and collective excitations (including higher-order multipole transitions accessible at large momentum transfer)."},{"id":"7751c8be-8aa5-4e54-8364-49c898c17572","l":"Organic-inorganic hybrid perovskites","d":4,"p":"57c5b6fb-0d61-4cce-9d84-505f5d6322e4","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Organic\u2013inorganic hybrid perovskites are crystalline materials with the ABX3 perovskite lattice in which the A-site is an organic cation (e.g., methylammonium or formamidinium), the B-site a metal cation (commonly Pb or Sn), and X a halide anion. They combine ionic and covalent bonding in a soft, polar lattice that yields tunable bandgaps, strong light absorption, long carrier diffusion lengths, and ionic/structural dynamical behavior, making them prominent for photovoltaics and optoelectronics."},{"id":"08ae680b-f3a4-40f0-b45d-182fc0bb9491","l":"Overlapping communities","d":4,"p":"180b51bc-73e1-45ee-90e6-a820e89e0da1","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Overlapping communities are groups of nodes in a network whose membership is not mutually exclusive, so individual nodes can belong to multiple densely connected modules at once. They generalize disjoint community structure and capture multi-functional or cross-cutting organization common in social, biological, and technological systems."},{"id":"9bf06d71-942b-47ec-872c-0ddcec3e4c00","l":"Physical vapor deposition","d":4,"p":"977cf3f6-ab87-440b-afe2-cf08e8fcd916","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Physical vapor deposition (PVD) is a family of vacuum-based processes in which material is physically vaporized from a source and transported in the gas phase to condense as a thin solid film on a substrate, with no chemical reaction required at the surface. Common PVD techniques include thermal evaporation, sputtering, and cathodic arc deposition, enabling precise control of film thickness, composition, and microstructure."},{"id":"d3063979-72db-440e-a0bc-b295a2d0e72e","l":"Plating","d":4,"p":"fe6212f5-2762-4253-9267-54b97b955a02","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Plating is the process of depositing a thin metallic or other functional layer onto a substrate to modify its surface properties. It is achieved by electrochemical reduction (electroplating), chemical reduction (electroless plating), or physical/vapor-deposition techniques to provide corrosion protection, wear resistance, conductivity, or improved appearance."},{"id":"d3835c6e-6178-498b-b7ee-97586a73e40a","l":"Positron emission tomography","d":4,"p":"c16a05ce-2d70-4ae6-b335-317404e599d0","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Positron emission tomography (PET) is a medical nuclear-imaging technique that maps the three-dimensional distribution of injected positron-emitting radiotracers to image functional processes such as metabolism or receptor binding. When a positron annihilates with an electron it produces two ~511 keV gamma photons emitted nearly 180\u00b0 apart, and PET uses coincidence detection of these photons and tomographic reconstruction algorithms to localize the tracer activity."},{"id":"dbd20f34-3917-446f-9ffa-1c49e2c7f5e8","l":"Protein folding","d":4,"p":"6c882ee4-606b-4a0d-af30-406061e22be7","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Protein folding is the process by which a linear polypeptide chain spontaneously acquires its functional three-dimensional native structure. It is governed by intramolecular interactions and solvent effects that drive the system toward a free-energy minimum and is often modeled as movement on a rugged energy landscape with intermediates and kinetic traps."},{"id":"9eb3af40-21fa-47e7-a778-47c088a7a774","l":"Protein translocation","d":4,"p":"5573b86c-324b-45ea-832a-145da2b82de3","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Protein translocation is the process by which polypeptides are moved across or into cellular membranes and organelles through specialized channels (translocons) guided by signal sequences. It occurs co\u2011 or post\u2011translationally and is driven by energy sources such as ATP/GTP hydrolysis or membrane potential (e.g., proton motive force)."},{"id":"96de60cc-9213-4e81-98b4-a13ec75f48cc","l":"Protein unfolding","d":4,"p":"15cb359f-2dba-4eaa-baa0-8b8f6b92f559","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Protein unfolding is the process by which a protein's specific folded (native) three-dimensional structure is destabilized and lost, converting it into a disordered, extended polypeptide chain when external perturbations (heat, chemical denaturants, pH shifts, or mechanical force) overcome stabilizing intramolecular interactions. Physically, unfolding is a conformational transition on the protein's free-energy landscape\u2014often cooperative\u2014accompanied by changes in enthalpy, entropy, and kinetic barriers between folded and unfolded states."},{"id":"627b34e8-3176-4fa8-982a-cb8a93bfd584","l":"Pseudopotentials","d":4,"p":"5c567aa1-5c30-4feb-867d-3685a6a7c71d","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Pseudopotentials are effective, simplified potentials that replace a complicated true interaction (for example the nuclear Coulomb potential plus tightly bound core electrons) by a gentler potential acting on valence electrons or low-energy particles, constructed so that scattering and bound-state properties outside a chosen core region are preserved. They are used to reduce computational complexity in electronic-structure calculations and in low-energy scattering problems (e.g., Fermi or Huang\u2013Yang contact pseudopotentials)."},{"id":"3ee18eec-73be-44b3-b5f1-99ca6f2f51d1","l":"Quantum cascade lasers","d":4,"p":"095b6ba0-9ab0-479a-8120-4ebc9468fb0a","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Quantum cascade lasers are semiconductor lasers in which electrons undergo radiative intersubband transitions within a repeated stack of engineered quantum wells and barriers, emitting a photon at each stage as they \"cascade\" through the structure. Because the emission energy is set by the engineered subband spacings rather than the material bandgap, QCLs provide efficient, widely tunable light sources in the mid\u2011infrared to terahertz range."},{"id":"7619f9b9-6359-4319-839b-484ec254ae1c","l":"Quantum spin chains","d":4,"p":"595b37ed-b7d8-457e-be59-5224c6dead59","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Quantum spin chains are one-dimensional lattice models of interacting quantum spins (e.g., Ising, XY, Heisenberg) that serve as minimal systems for studying quantum magnetism, many-body correlations, excitations, and quantum phase transitions."},{"id":"2494575b-0813-412b-a05c-c3210ed5e434","l":"Quantum spin liquid","d":4,"p":"e2ab085b-23f1-4b85-8449-9d7ae6cd2489","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A quantum spin liquid is a magnetic phase in which interacting spins fail to freeze into any conventional long-range order even at zero temperature because strong quantum fluctuations and/or frustration produce a highly entangled ground state. Such states typically exhibit long-range entanglement, topological order, and fractionalized excitations (e.g., spinons) rather than conventional magnons."},{"id":"ac10ce0a-9a1b-4824-b651-7f2dc483a884","l":"Radiography","d":4,"p":"6b156cbb-39ac-4db9-8ecf-50b5e3646acb","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Radiography is an imaging technique that uses penetrating ionizing radiation (typically X-rays or gamma rays) transmitted through an object and recorded on a detector or film to reveal its internal structure; image contrast arises from spatial variations in attenuation and scattering. It is widely used in medical diagnostics and nondestructive testing of materials and components."},{"id":"1d5be62d-d32d-4599-83fa-52a8ac62e866","l":"Radiopharmaceuticals","d":4,"p":"c16a05ce-2d70-4ae6-b335-317404e599d0","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Radiopharmaceuticals are radioactive compounds that combine a radionuclide with a biological carrier molecule to target specific organs, tissues, or molecular processes for use in nuclear medicine. They are used either as tracers for diagnostic imaging (gamma or positron emitters for SPECT/PET) or to deliver therapeutic radiation (beta, alpha, or Auger emitters) to diseased tissue."},{"id":"0598f8cc-ca97-4376-b799-388c1558537a","l":"Random bond Ising model","d":4,"p":"60c0e911-44c3-4224-89b1-0c5a46dc82c3","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Networks","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Random-bond Ising model: an Ising lattice model in which the nearest-neighbor exchange couplings J_{ij} are quenched random variables drawn from a specified distribution (e.g., \u00b1J or Gaussian), so the bond strengths vary from link to link. It is used to study how spatial randomness in interactions modifies phase transitions and critical behavior and, if signs are mixed, can produce frustration and spin-glass phases."},{"id":"6a2e2c81-9842-486e-933e-1b6f8dd8ce77","l":"Random field Ising model","d":4,"p":"dfadf91e-b07e-4081-9498-25b87e6d5eb9","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Networks","Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"The random field Ising model (RFIM) is an Ising spin system with ferromagnetic nearest-neighbor coupling and quenched, site-dependent random external fields h_i that couple to each spin S_i = \u00b11, typically described by H = \u2212J \u03a3_{\u27e8ij\u27e9} S_iS_j \u2212 \u03a3_i h_i S_i. It is a minimal model for how quenched disorder (e.g., impurities) affects magnetic ordering, domain formation, hysteresis, and disorder-driven phase transitions."},{"id":"d69c3767-0e9e-4114-a2b9-a88624200cb1","l":"Random field XY model","d":4,"p":"dfadf91e-b07e-4081-9498-25b87e6d5eb9","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Random-field XY model: a classical lattice model of two-component (U(1)) spins or phases with ferromagnetic coupling subject to quenched, spatially varying external fields that locally favor particular spin orientations. It captures how static disorder competes with phase coherence, leading to pinning, domain formation and suppression or glassy modification of long-range order."},{"id":"81e98b33-4dad-4a65-91ce-c257de3b86f6","l":"Resonant inelastic x-ray scattering","d":4,"p":"24098ed4-b73f-4eb2-bd1a-df209728659f","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Resonant inelastic x\u2011ray scattering (RIXS) is a photon-in\u2013photon-out spectroscopic technique in which incident x\u2011rays are tuned to an atomic absorption edge to resonantly excite a core electron, and the energy- and momentum-resolved measurement of the emitted photon records the energy lost to elementary excitations (electronic, magnetic, orbital, or lattice) in the material. The resonant core-hole intermediate state enhances the scattering cross section and gives element- and orbital-specific sensitivity with momentum resolution."},{"id":"679c1ede-0e24-4359-a84e-f70c157984cd","l":"RNA folding","d":4,"p":"6c882ee4-606b-4a0d-af30-406061e22be7","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"RNA folding is the process by which a single-stranded RNA polymer self-organizes from a linear sequence into specific secondary (stems, loops) and tertiary (coaxial stacking, pseudoknots) structures. It is driven and constrained by sequence-dependent base pairing and stacking, backbone conformational energetics, electrostatic interactions and ion screening, and by competing thermodynamic and kinetic pathways in a thermal solvent."},{"id":"7263fe66-08e6-42e2-afb7-275fb5668d72","l":"RNA unfolding","d":4,"p":"15cb359f-2dba-4eaa-baa0-8b8f6b92f559","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"RNA unfolding is the process by which an RNA molecule loses its native secondary and tertiary structure\u2014breaking base pairs and tertiary contacts\u2014and converts to an extended or disordered state under thermal fluctuations, mechanical force, or chemical denaturants. Physically it is described as a barrier-crossing conformational transition on a sequence-dependent free-energy landscape, with characteristic kinetics and cooperativity."},{"id":"9c3e94b0-dd2b-4f35-bb02-7586b81b76ac","l":"Rumor spreading","d":4,"p":"5ebcb77a-26bf-4834-81c8-82d85bcdc07a","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Networks"],"def":"Rumor spreading is the dynamical process by which information (a rumor) propagates through a population or network of interacting agents, driven by stochastic transmission, stifling, and forgetting events. It is studied with epidemic-like and percolation models (e.g., Daley\u2013Kendall, Maki\u2013Thompson) to predict spreading speed, final reach, and threshold behavior."},{"id":"116f828e-3007-4ad2-b5ef-b0b179b67c38","l":"Ruthenates","d":4,"p":"6fdf4bb3-246f-40a9-b756-d416629bdd26","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Ruthenates are transition-metal oxides containing ruthenium ions (e.g., Sr2RuO4, Ca2RuO4) that exhibit strong electron correlations and substantial spin\u2013orbit coupling, giving rise to a rich range of electronic and magnetic ground states such as unconventional superconductivity, Mott insulating behavior, and complex magnetic orders. They are widely studied as model systems for the interplay of electron correlations, spin\u2013orbit effects, and lattice degrees of freedom in correlated oxides."},{"id":"9a5f6c44-1098-4651-967f-349bef5e52ae","l":"Single photon emission computed tomography","d":4,"p":"c16a05ce-2d70-4ae6-b335-317404e599d0","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Single-photon emission computed tomography (SPECT) is a nuclear imaging technique that produces three-dimensional maps of physiological function by detecting gamma rays emitted singly from injected radiotracers (e.g., 99mTc or 123I) with rotating, collimated gamma cameras. Tomographic reconstruction of the acquired projections yields slice images of tracer distribution, distinguishing SPECT from PET which relies on coincident annihilation photons."},{"id":"50e40c8e-0131-453e-acd3-aa59abe69272","l":"SNS junctions","d":4,"p":"1bb6c922-68ec-4ff4-9c63-4e79a313864d","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"An SNS junction is a superconducting weak link consisting of two superconductors separated by a normal (non\u2011superconducting) conducting region. Superconducting correlations penetrate the normal metal via the proximity effect, enabling a Josephson supercurrent\u2014carried by Andreev bound states and controlled by the phase difference between the superconductors."},{"id":"8d3325a6-e98e-4e69-a335-016380a3ccab","l":"Solid-state batteries","d":4,"p":"3aa1f62d-ba58-416a-9eaa-5d162b472e5f","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Solid-state batteries are rechargeable electrochemical cells that replace the conventional liquid or gel electrolyte with a solid ionic conductor, so ions (typically Li+) migrate between electrodes through a solid phase. This architecture can boost energy density and safety\u2014enabling lithium\u2011metal anodes and reducing flammability\u2014but faces challenges such as lower room\u2011temperature ionic conductivity and high solid\u2013solid interfacial resistance."},{"id":"4b477756-a9b6-488e-b461-28b29f2b3c2a","l":"Spin coating","d":4,"p":"977cf3f6-ab87-440b-afe2-cf08e8fcd916","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Spin coating is a technique for forming uniform thin films by depositing a liquid solution on a substrate and rapidly rotating it so centrifugal and viscous forces spread and thin the liquid while solvent evaporation arrests the flow. The final film thickness and uniformity are controlled by parameters such as solution viscosity and concentration, spin speed and duration, and evaporation rate."},{"id":"e474eddf-b8ab-4f35-9c9b-f0e07cf3a5df","l":"Spin helix","d":4,"p":"d88f1794-8203-4531-bccd-b5bd36c0595f","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A spin helix is a spatially periodic spin texture in which the local spin polarization rotates continuously along a direction, forming a helical pattern. In spintronics it often refers to the \"persistent spin helix\" that appears in two-dimensional electron systems when Rashba and Dresselhaus spin\u2013orbit couplings are balanced, yielding a long-lived spin pattern protected by an SU(2)-like symmetry."},{"id":"c9cfa8ec-e999-4aa1-902a-7052a2dc6817","l":"Spin photodiodes","d":4,"p":"2b5bdcab-7c76-45e4-aa6a-8b350792e105","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Spin photodiodes are photodetectors that convert absorbed photons\u2014especially circularly polarized light\u2014into spin-polarized electrical currents by generating carriers with a preferential spin and separating/collecting them via spin-dependent transport or magnetic contacts. They combine optics and spintronics to detect light polarization, inject or read out spin currents, and study spin dynamics in semiconductors."},{"id":"075be4f4-ce0e-4e38-bf72-d9e4648724bb","l":"Spin-resolved inverse photoemission spectroscopy","d":4,"p":"e5391c73-aefe-44c4-a5f5-b5cbbaf856d0","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Spin-resolved inverse photoemission spectroscopy is an experimental technique that measures the spin-dependent unoccupied electronic states of a material by injecting a spin-polarized electron beam and detecting the photons emitted when those electrons occupy empty states above the Fermi level. By correlating the incident-electron spin, emitted-photon energy and angle, it yields the spin-resolved unoccupied density of states complementary to spin-resolved photoemission (which probes occupied states)."},{"id":"e95f6d55-95a4-4f25-81ff-ca0333817fed","l":"Spot-profile analysis LEED","d":4,"p":"eef6c8e0-113c-40e9-a981-db01b2fe257e","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter"],"def":"Spot-profile analysis LEED (SPA-LEED) is a high-resolution variant of low-energy electron diffraction in which the detailed shape and intensity distribution of individual diffraction spots are measured and analyzed to obtain quantitative information about a surface's morphology, domain sizes, defect densities, step distributions, strain, and atomic relaxations. By studying the spot profiles in reciprocal space (and deconvolving instrumental resolution), one extracts real-space correlation lengths and disorder characteristics of the surface."},{"id":"a0eb610d-e104-4cac-83f3-115124abc8f7","l":"Tandem photovoltaics","d":4,"p":"258d6104-3e21-4b28-9599-fa4cb50c028a","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Tandem photovoltaics are solar-cell architectures that stack two or more subcells with different semiconductor bandgaps so each absorbs a different portion of the solar spectrum, reducing thermalization and transmission losses and thereby enabling efficiencies above the single\u2011junction Shockley\u2013Queisser limit. Implementations include monolithic (two\u2011terminal) series-connected stacks and mechanically or optically coupled multi\u2011terminal designs."},{"id":"016a4ed2-bd01-4644-9999-a830f02ae172","l":"Thermal batteries","d":4,"p":"3aa1f62d-ba58-416a-9eaa-5d162b472e5f","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Thermal batteries are devices that store and later release thermal energy or convert heat into electrical power; the term can refer both to thermal\u2011energy storage systems (using sensible, latent, or chemical heat storage) and to thermally\u2011activated electrochemical cells (molten\u2011salt \u201creserve\u201d batteries that are inert until heated and then produce current)."},{"id":"7160ac44-83c4-41fe-971a-e26136a19047","l":"Thermal photovoltaics","d":4,"p":"258d6104-3e21-4b28-9599-fa4cb50c028a","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Thermal photovoltaics (thermophotovoltaics) are devices that convert heat to electricity by using a hot emitter to radiate photons which are absorbed and converted by a photovoltaic cell. Efficiency is maximized by tailoring the emitter spectrum and optical filters to match the cell bandgap and by using photon-recycling or near-field radiative transfer techniques to increase usable photon flux."},{"id":"7b79ca56-6194-4286-804c-34ce14298bd6","l":"Thin-film photovoltaics","d":4,"p":"258d6104-3e21-4b28-9599-fa4cb50c028a","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Thin-film photovoltaics are solar cells in which the photoactive semiconductor layers are deposited as micrometer- to nanometer-scale films on rigid or flexible substrates and convert sunlight to electricity via the photovoltaic effect. By using much thinner absorber layers than wafer silicon, thin films (e.g., amorphous Si, CdTe, CIGS, and metal-halide perovskites) reduce material use and enable lightweight, flexible, and potentially lower-cost modules, with distinct optical, electronic, and stability trade-offs."},{"id":"3832d910-2919-412b-adbc-46991a922260","l":"Time-dependent DFT","d":4,"p":"5c567aa1-5c30-4feb-867d-3685a6a7c71d","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Time-dependent density functional theory (TDDFT) is the extension of ground-state DFT to time-dependent quantum many-body systems, using the time-dependent electron density as the fundamental variable and evolving noninteracting Kohn\u2013Sham orbitals under a time-dependent Kohn\u2013Sham potential. Based on the Runge\u2013Gross theorem, TDDFT provides an (in principle) exact framework for real-time electronic dynamics and excitation spectra, but practical calculations require approximations to the time-dependent exchange\u2013correlation potential and its response kernel."},{"id":"e2bd4da8-2699-4421-8169-5b88615ab409","l":"Tractor beams","d":4,"p":"0d68bfc9-8cf3-46cf-b51d-c91e1e052bd4","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Tractor beams are directed electromagnetic or acoustic fields engineered to produce a net attractive force that pulls an object toward the source, enabling non\u2011contact manipulation. They are realized by shaping phase, intensity, or interference patterns (e.g., Bessel or vortex beams) so that gradient and scattering forces combine to produce a net inward\u2014or \u201cnegative radiation\u2011pressure\u201d\u2014force on the target."},{"id":"2a9cae2b-9797-41ca-91c2-08aaeffffcdd","l":"Transient absorption spectroscopy","d":4,"p":"e182c548-24f2-45a8-99ce-816acead5bf5","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Transient absorption spectroscopy is a pump\u2013probe optical technique that measures the time-dependent change in a sample\u2019s absorption spectrum after an excitation (pump) pulse by recording the probe beam\u2019s differential transmission or absorption as a function of wavelength and pump\u2013probe delay. It reveals excited-state populations, relaxation pathways, energy transfer and other ultrafast dynamics on timescales from femtoseconds to milliseconds."},{"id":"220659d8-380e-4952-b916-494295ff4fc5","l":"Transition metal oxides","d":4,"p":"6fdf4bb3-246f-40a9-b756-d416629bdd26","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Transition metal oxides are compounds formed from oxygen and transition metals (elements with partially filled d orbitals). Their variable oxidation states and strongly interacting d electrons produce rich electronic and magnetic behavior\u2014including strong correlations and Mott metal\u2013insulator transitions, magnetism, superconductivity, and complex charge, spin, and orbital ordering."},{"id":"cb0ed0fb-00db-49ce-9422-9e6a55999c8b","l":"Transparent conducting oxides","d":4,"p":"6fdf4bb3-246f-40a9-b756-d416629bdd26","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Transparent conducting oxides (TCOs) are wide-bandgap oxide materials that combine substantial electrical conductivity with high optical transparency in the visible range by supplying free carriers (via doping or defects) while keeping interband absorption suppressed. They are used as transparent electrodes in devices such as solar cells, flat-panel displays and LEDs, with common examples including indium tin oxide (ITO), fluorine-doped tin oxide (FTO) and aluminum-doped zinc oxide (AZO)."},{"id":"ade677cb-7124-424f-8223-4908384d91fa","l":"Ultrafast femtosecond pump probe","d":4,"p":"e182c548-24f2-45a8-99ce-816acead5bf5","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Ultrafast femtosecond pump\u2013probe is a time-resolved spectroscopy technique in which an ultrashort (\u223c10^\u221215 s) \"pump\" pulse excites a sample and a time-delayed \"probe\" pulse interrogates its transient response, allowing observation of electronic, vibrational, or structural dynamics. By varying the pump\u2013probe delay and measuring changes in absorption, reflectivity, emission, scattering, or diffraction, the evolving nonequilibrium state is mapped with femtosecond temporal resolution."},{"id":"15d242bf-4a11-41e3-9ba6-016d0d9e2f0f","l":"Ultrafast pump-probe spectroscopy","d":4,"p":"e182c548-24f2-45a8-99ce-816acead5bf5","fl":["Techniques"],"dl":["Atomic, Molecular & Optical","Plasma Physics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Ultrafast pump-probe spectroscopy is a time-resolved technique in which an initial \"pump\" laser pulse impulsively excites a sample and a time-delayed \"probe\" pulse measures transient changes in its optical, electronic, or structural response. By scanning the pump\u2013probe delay with femtosecond to picosecond resolution, it reveals ultrafast processes such as electronic relaxation, energy transfer, vibrational coherences, and chemical reaction dynamics."},{"id":"3ca68d95-2d58-4a20-ba8e-487b34c95d58","l":"Vanadates","d":4,"p":"6fdf4bb3-246f-40a9-b756-d416629bdd26","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Vanadates are compounds containing vanadium\u2013oxygen anions (vanadate ions like VO4^3\u2212 or larger polyvanadate clusters), typically with vanadium in a high oxidation state. They form a broad class of oxides and salts whose crystal structures and vanadium d\u2011electron configurations give rise to diverse electronic, magnetic, catalytic and optical properties studied in condensed\u2011matter physics."},{"id":"1cbb48a0-6549-48c7-96a6-8ecf5c8f16c9","l":"Vision defects","d":4,"p":"0e378245-c424-4d52-bd5d-81039b2c9cc4","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Vision defects are optical imperfections of the eye in which the image formed by the cornea and lens is not sharply focused on the retina, due to mismatches between the eye's refractive power and axial length or to irregularities in corneal/lens curvature. These refractive errors\u2014including myopia, hyperopia, astigmatism and presbyopia\u2014cause blurred or distorted vision and are modeled and corrected using lenses, contact optics, or surgical reshaping."},{"id":"e11d309d-bba0-40eb-a280-1f6b8260a8af","l":"Astigmatism","d":5,"p":"1cbb48a0-6549-48c7-96a6-8ecf5c8f16c9","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Astigmatism is an optical aberration in which rays from a point source focus at different distances in orthogonal meridional planes, producing two line foci (and an intermediate blurred disk) instead of a single point. It results from anisotropic curvature or differing refractive power across an optical surface (for example, a toric lens or the eye's cornea)."},{"id":"348a3524-1a30-4ce1-a233-1eb962bcdcff","l":"Bayesian networks","d":5,"p":"76fed82e-b866-42da-9f69-0ccf38c3a785","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Networks","Particles & Fields","Quantum Information, Science & Technology","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A Bayesian network is a probabilistic graphical model that represents a set of random variables and their conditional dependencies with a directed acyclic graph (DAG), where the joint probability distribution factorizes as the product of each variable\u2019s conditional probability given its parent nodes. It encodes conditional independence relations and enables efficient probabilistic inference, learning, and causal reasoning under uncertainty."},{"id":"19dcd8cd-8d4d-4868-ab9a-41d0eec57296","l":"Belief functions","d":5,"p":"76fed82e-b866-42da-9f69-0ccf38c3a785","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology","Fluid Dynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Belief functions (from Dempster\u2013Shafer evidence theory) are set-valued measures that assign to each proposition (subset of a hypothesis space) a number in [0,1] representing the amount of justified belief supported by available evidence. Unlike ordinary probabilities they need not be additive\u2014mass can be assigned to sets rather than only to single outcomes\u2014so they naturally represent partial or imprecise epistemic uncertainty (with a dual plausibility function bounding the belief)."},{"id":"ee1b4cc2-bdd7-414f-a29d-c125938e7e9a","l":"Color blindness","d":5,"p":"1cbb48a0-6549-48c7-96a6-8ecf5c8f16c9","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Color blindness (color vision deficiency) is a reduced or altered ability to distinguish between colors that differ in wavelength, arising from absence, altered spectral sensitivity, or malfunction of one or more types of cone photoreceptors in the retina or from downstream neural processing defects. Common forms are red\u2013green deficiencies (protanopia, deuteranopia) and blue\u2013yellow deficiency (tritanopia), as well as complete monochromacy."},{"id":"4ceecfa9-2962-47f3-ad09-c81b6c6900bc","l":"Decision trees","d":5,"p":"76fed82e-b866-42da-9f69-0ccf38c3a785","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Networks","Particles & Fields","Quantum Information, Science & Technology","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Decision trees are supervised machine-learning models that make predictions by recursively partitioning the input feature space into regions using simple conditional rules (binary or multiway splits), with each terminal node (leaf) assigning a class label or numeric prediction. They are nonparametric and interpretable tools for classification or regression, though single trees can overfit without pruning or ensembling."},{"id":"cfc3a160-a6ae-4258-ae39-513eba1aff1c","l":"Deep learning","d":5,"p":"5835d6de-78a5-4e5c-bab0-f5c890cd89d7","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology","Fluid Dynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Deep learning is a class of machine learning that uses artificial neural networks with many stacked layers to automatically learn hierarchical features and approximate complex, nonlinear mappings from data. Networks are trained by adjusting weights (typically with gradient-based backpropagation) to perform tasks such as classification, regression, surrogate modeling, and physics-informed inference."},{"id":"c22274c3-0f09-440d-a852-ced8af74b43f","l":"Dimensionality reduction","d":5,"p":"5835d6de-78a5-4e5c-bab0-f5c890cd89d7","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology","Fluid Dynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Dimensionality reduction is the process of mapping high-dimensional data or state spaces onto a lower-dimensional representation that preserves the essential structure, variability, or effective degrees of freedom. It is used to simplify models, remove redundancy, and aid visualization and analysis, with common methods including linear techniques (e.g., PCA) and nonlinear manifold-learning approaches."},{"id":"8a56e261-4160-4c17-a604-44716d21ec7c","l":"Dynein","d":5,"p":"58d9c0f8-d2ea-40ad-83d8-56978aa7f655","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Dynein is a large, multi-subunit molecular motor (an AAA+ ATPase) that converts chemical energy from ATP hydrolysis into mechanical work to walk along microtubules toward their minus ends, generating forces for intracellular cargo transport and the bending of cilia and flagella."},{"id":"a13395f9-9957-41ac-a323-7ff3ecc69c01","l":"Genetic algorithms","d":5,"p":"45a22032-e27f-4922-9d0c-10c4aa294db5","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology","Fluid Dynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Genetic algorithms are stochastic optimization and search methods inspired by natural selection that evolve a population of candidate solutions by iteratively applying selection, recombination (crossover), and mutation. Candidates are evaluated by a fitness (objective) function, and over generations the population tends to produce higher-quality solutions for complex or high-dimensional problems."},{"id":"7dbccf41-abe3-4350-88ff-260f1fe67d61","l":"Hyperopia","d":5,"p":"1cbb48a0-6549-48c7-96a6-8ecf5c8f16c9","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Hyperopia (farsightedness) is a refractive error in which the eye's optical power is too weak or its axial length too short, so incoming light is focused behind the retina, producing blurred near vision while distant objects may be clearer. It is corrected by converging (positive) lenses or by increased ocular accommodation."},{"id":"1875d305-0881-476b-b827-962710017024","l":"Kinesin","d":5,"p":"58d9c0f8-d2ea-40ad-83d8-56978aa7f655","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Kinesin is a family of ATP-powered, dimeric molecular motor proteins that walk processively along microtubule filaments\u2014typically toward the microtubule plus end\u2014converting ATP hydrolysis into ~8-nm steps and generating forces of a few picoNewtons to transport intracellular cargo."},{"id":"8813446d-37c5-42c7-8bba-b4db64b9bb7d","l":"Lithium-air batteries","d":5,"p":"6bcd2a14-13c4-4f1f-8a5e-24b8c6ffbb21","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Lithium\u2013air (Li\u2013air) batteries are metal\u2013air electrochemical cells that store energy by oxidizing lithium metal at the anode and reducing oxygen from the air at a porous cathode, forming lithium oxides or peroxides during discharge. They offer very high theoretical specific energy compared with Li\u2011ion batteries but are limited in practice by sluggish oxygen reaction kinetics, electrolyte and electrode degradation, and poor cycle life."},{"id":"a7ab8f74-905c-429f-884d-1f576b43f4fe","l":"Lithium-ion batteries","d":5,"p":"6bcd2a14-13c4-4f1f-8a5e-24b8c6ffbb21","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Lithium-ion batteries are rechargeable electrochemical cells that store energy by reversibly shuttling lithium ions between a negative electrode (commonly graphite) and a positive electrode (e.g., layered lithium transition\u2011metal oxides) through a nonaqueous electrolyte and separator, while electrons travel through the external circuit to provide power. Charging and discharging proceed via intercalation/deintercalation redox reactions that convert chemical free energy into electrical work, yielding high energy density but also long\u2011term degradation (SEI formation, electrode structural changes)."},{"id":"8b42c421-53e4-4d48-8c7d-a5d2bf758fc1","l":"Lithium metal batteries","d":5,"p":"6bcd2a14-13c4-4f1f-8a5e-24b8c6ffbb21","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Lithium metal batteries are electrochemical cells that use metallic lithium as the negative electrode (anode), delivering much higher specific energy than conventional intercalation-based anodes. They include both primary and emerging rechargeable designs but are limited by lithium dendrite growth and unstable solid\u2013electrolyte interphases that cause poor cycle life and safety risks."},{"id":"7d1c0e34-760f-4d53-b069-a96aa9b1c695","l":"Lithium polymer batteries","d":5,"p":"6bcd2a14-13c4-4f1f-8a5e-24b8c6ffbb21","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Lithium\u2011polymer (LiPo) batteries are rechargeable lithium\u2011ion electrochemical cells that use a solid or gel-like polymer electrolyte or polymer\u2011impregnated separator instead of a free-flowing liquid electrolyte, and typically employ lithium\u2011intercalation electrodes in thin, flexible pouch packaging to achieve high energy density and low weight. They are widely used in portable electronics and drones, offering versatile form factors but requiring careful charging and thermal management due to safety risks."},{"id":"47b40e91-cfa8-4e9f-8536-2f067b43035e","l":"Lithium-sulfur batteries","d":5,"p":"6bcd2a14-13c4-4f1f-8a5e-24b8c6ffbb21","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas"],"dl":["Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Energy Science & Technology"],"def":"Lithium\u2013sulfur (Li\u2013S) batteries are rechargeable electrochemical cells that use a lithium metal anode and a sulfur-based cathode, storing energy through the multi-electron conversion of sulfur to lithium sulfides (e.g., S8 + 16Li \u2192 8Li2S). They offer very high theoretical gravimetric energy density (~2.5\u20132.6 kWh/kg) but in practice face challenges from sulfur\u2019s low electrical conductivity, large volume changes, and soluble lithium polysulfide intermediates that cause capacity fade."},{"id":"e1ccc86b-0745-449e-a5b3-bbcea73f5eca","l":"Myopia","d":5,"p":"1cbb48a0-6549-48c7-96a6-8ecf5c8f16c9","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Myopia (nearsightedness) is a refractive error of the eye where light from distant objects is focused in front of the retina\u2014usually because the eyeball is too long or the optical power of the cornea/lens is too great\u2014so distant scenes appear blurred while near ones are clear. It is optically corrected by adding negative (diverging) lenses or reducing the eye's converging power (e.g., via refractive surgery)."},{"id":"2596b0fc-05b7-44b4-aa92-45ad5d0f93a6","l":"Myosin","d":5,"p":"58d9c0f8-d2ea-40ad-83d8-56978aa7f655","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Myosin is a family of ATP-driven molecular motor proteins that convert chemical energy from ATP hydrolysis into mechanical work by undergoing cyclic conformational changes (power strokes) and stepping along actin filaments. These motors generate force and directed motion for processes such as muscle contraction, cell motility, and intracellular cargo transport."},{"id":"d60931c4-736f-473b-960a-f89ca7353993","l":"Nickelates","d":5,"p":"220659d8-380e-4952-b916-494295ff4fc5","fl":["Physical Systems"],"dl":["Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Nickelates are transition-metal oxides containing nickel and oxygen (commonly perovskite RNiO3 or layered NiO2-based compounds) in which nickel ions occupy octahedral or planar sites. They exhibit strong electron correlations that produce metal\u2013insulator transitions, charge and orbital ordering, and complex magnetism, making them important model systems for correlated-electron physics and analogs to the cuprate superconductors."},{"id":"118110e3-4f4b-4164-b471-e4f3c94d0af8","l":"Protein folding pathways","d":5,"p":"dbd20f34-3917-446f-9ffa-1c49e2c7f5e8","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Physics of Living Systems"],"def":"Protein folding pathways are the sequences of conformational states and transitions by which a polypeptide chain progresses from its unfolded ensemble to its native three-dimensional structure. They are characterized on an energy landscape\u2014with folding funnels, intermediates, transition states, and kinetic barriers\u2014that determine the rates and probabilities of different routes and the possibility of multiple parallel pathways."},{"id":"564f8499-91ad-46ba-8a44-3342e0724284","l":"Regression analysis","d":5,"p":"76fed82e-b866-42da-9f69-0ccf38c3a785","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology","Fluid Dynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Regression analysis is a collection of statistical methods for fitting a mathematical model that relates a dependent variable to one or more independent variables, estimating the model parameters from data (commonly by least squares) and quantifying their uncertainty. Physicists use it to identify trends, test hypotheses, calibrate measurements, and make predictions from experimental or simulated data."},{"id":"460ec2ba-c95d-44e0-b833-dfc16ab5132b","l":"Reinforcement learning","d":5,"p":"5835d6de-78a5-4e5c-bab0-f5c890cd89d7","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology","Fluid Dynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Reinforcement learning is a branch of machine learning in which an agent learns by trial and error to take actions in an environment so as to maximize a scalar cumulative reward. It is typically formalized with Markov decision processes and relies on balancing exploration and exploitation using methods such as temporal-difference learning or policy gradients."},{"id":"7628000d-0b40-4c66-a44f-becba8dcf442","l":"Semi-supervised learning","d":5,"p":"5835d6de-78a5-4e5c-bab0-f5c890cd89d7","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology","Fluid Dynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Semi-supervised learning is a machine-learning paradigm that trains models using a small set of labeled examples together with a larger set of unlabeled data, exploiting assumed structure in the unlabeled data (e.g., clustering or low-dimensional manifolds) to improve generalization. In physics it is often used when labeling is expensive or impractical, for example in classifying experimental events, analyzing simulations, or mapping between simulation and measurement."},{"id":"cc6132f9-e5cb-413e-bb25-2e7732c94c8f","l":"Supervised learning","d":5,"p":"5835d6de-78a5-4e5c-bab0-f5c890cd89d7","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology","Fluid Dynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Supervised learning is a class of machine learning in which a model is trained on labeled input\u2013output pairs to learn a mapping from inputs to outputs. The model parameters are adjusted by minimizing a loss function so the trained model can predict outputs (for regression) or labels (for classification) on new data."},{"id":"44409c6d-cfa8-4116-9f15-fbbc09a9c0c4","l":"Support-vector machines","d":5,"p":"76fed82e-b866-42da-9f69-0ccf38c3a785","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Networks","Particles & Fields","Quantum Information, Science & Technology","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Support-vector machines are supervised learning algorithms that find the hyperplane in feature space which maximizes the margin between labeled classes, with the classifier defined by a small set of critical training points called support vectors. They are formulated as a convex quadratic optimization and can be kernelized to produce nonlinear decision boundaries or adapted for regression."},{"id":"e7006e14-1a8d-4059-ae95-269eb9f3b233","l":"Training models","d":5,"p":"76fed82e-b866-42da-9f69-0ccf38c3a785","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology","Fluid Dynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Training models is the process of adjusting a model's parameters using data and an optimization algorithm (e.g., gradient descent) to minimize a loss function or maximize likelihood so the model captures underlying patterns and can make predictions. It also involves practices like regularization and cross-validation to avoid overfitting and assess generalization to new data."},{"id":"22c7f1fc-4593-4674-9b9f-30f874fdee76","l":"Unsupervised learning","d":5,"p":"5835d6de-78a5-4e5c-bab0-f5c890cd89d7","fl":["Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Quantum Information, Science & Technology","Fluid Dynamics","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics","Particles & Fields"],"def":"Unsupervised learning is a class of machine\u2011learning methods that discovers structure, patterns, or low\u2011dimensional representations directly from unlabeled data by optimizing objectives like clustering, density estimation, or reconstruction. Common techniques\u2014e.g., PCA, k\u2011means, Gaussian mixture models, autoencoders, and manifold learning\u2014are used in physics for feature extraction, phase identification, anomaly detection, and data compression."},{"id":"f1ec050f-abfa-46d3-8bcf-cdf070fcdcbf","l":"Valence bond solid","d":5,"p":"2494575b-0813-412b-a05c-c3210ed5e434","fl":["Research Areas"],"dl":["Statistical Physics & Thermodynamics","Polymers & Soft Matter","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"A valence bond solid is a quantum ground state of a spin system in which local spins pair into singlets (\"valence bonds\") that form a spatially ordered pattern breaking the lattice symmetry; it is nonmagnetic and typically has a spin gap."},{"id":"0a9a17ee-a48d-4731-b8f4-77722430f49a","l":"Artificial neural networks","d":6,"p":"76fed82e-b866-42da-9f69-0ccf38c3a785","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Networks","Particles & Fields","Quantum Information, Science & Technology","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Artificial neural networks are computational models made of layers of simple processing units (\"neurons\") connected by adjustable weights, where each neuron nonlinearly transforms a weighted sum of its inputs to produce an output. Networks learn to approximate complex functions or mappings by adjusting those weights using training algorithms (e.g., gradient descent/backpropagation) and are widely used for regression, classification, surrogate modeling, and data-driven discovery in physics."},{"id":"7c2a6982-6d72-4cd8-bba6-2fccb06a7011","l":"Random forests","d":6,"p":"4ceecfa9-2962-47f3-ad09-c81b6c6900bc","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Networks","Particles & Fields","Quantum Information, Science & Technology","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Random forests are an ensemble learning method that builds many decision trees from bootstrap-resampled subsets of the data with random feature selection, then combines their outputs (majority vote for classification, average for regression). This aggregation reduces variance and overfitting, while providing robust predictions and simple measures of feature importance for complex, nonlinear datasets."},{"id":"dea8a5db-5c9e-4ee3-b8b8-722679810a4e","l":"Convolutional neural networks","d":7,"p":"0a9a17ee-a48d-4731-b8f4-77722430f49a","fl":["Physical Systems","Research Areas","Techniques"],"dl":["Fluid Dynamics","Networks","Particles & Fields","Quantum Information, Science & Technology","Condensed Matter, Materials & Applied Physics"],"def":"Convolutional neural networks (CNNs) are deep learning architectures that apply trainable convolutional filters across input arrays to detect local, spatially correlated features and build hierarchical representations through stacked layers and pooling. Their weight sharing and local receptive fields make them translation-robust and especially effective for grid-structured data (images, fields), so they are widely used for pattern recognition, surrogate modeling, and analyzing spatial/temporal data in physics."}];